window.HANDBOOK_CONTENT = {"01-architecture-request-lifecycle": "# 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle\n\n> **In one line:** Django is a stack of small, well-defined layers — server gateway, handler, middleware onion, URL resolver, view, ORM and template engine — and if you can trace one HTTP request through every layer and back, you can debug almost anything Django does in production and answer the most common opening question in a Django interview.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\nEvery Django interview, at every level, opens with some version of the same question: *\"What happens when a request hits a Django application?\"* Junior candidates answer \"the URL goes to a view, the view returns a template.\" Senior candidates answer with the gateway interface, the handler class, the order in which middleware runs, what happens when no URL matches, where exceptions become responses, and when the database connection is closed. The difference between those two answers is the difference between someone who has *used* Django and someone who can *operate* it.\n\nThe problem Django solves is old and unglamorous. A web application has to parse raw HTTP, decide which code should handle each URL, protect itself against forged requests, talk to a database without SQL injection, render HTML safely, manage sessions and authentication, and do all of that the same way in every endpoint. The **naive approach** — one big script that reads `environ`, branches on the path with `if/elif`, builds SQL with string formatting and concatenates HTML — fails fast. Cross-cutting concerns (auth, CSRF, compression, logging) get copy-pasted into every branch, one forgotten check becomes a security hole, and nobody can find where a header was set. Frameworks exist to put each concern in exactly one place.\n\nDjango's answer is **\"batteries included\"**: a single, coherent framework that ships with an ORM, migrations, a template engine, forms, an admin, authentication, sessions, caching, security middleware and a test runner, all designed to work together. Its architecture is usually described as **MTV (Model–Template–View)**, sometimes written MVT. The moving parts are:\n\n- a **gateway interface** (WSGI or ASGI) that turns bytes from the web server into a Python call,\n- a **handler** (`WSGIHandler` / `ASGIHandler`, both subclasses of `BaseHandler`) that builds a request object and runs it through\n- a **middleware chain** — an onion of wrappers around the view,\n- a **URL resolver** that maps the path to a view function,\n- the **view**, your code, which typically uses\n- the **ORM** to read and write models and\n- the **template engine** to render a response,\n- and then the response travels back out through the middleware and the gateway.\n\nThis chapter is the map for the whole handbook. Each layer gets its own chapter later; here you learn how they fit together and what really happens inside `BaseHandler`.\n\n> **Why this matters:** most production incidents in Django — a missing header, a 500 that should be a 404, a request that holds a database connection too long, a middleware that silently swallows an exception — are easiest to diagnose once you know exactly which layer of the lifecycle owns the behaviour.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **MTV / MVT** — Django's naming of the classic MVC split. The **Model** is the data layer (ORM classes); the **Template** is presentation (what MVC calls the *view*); the **View** is the request-handling logic (what MVC calls the *controller*). The URLconf plus the framework itself act as the dispatcher. *Why it matters:* interviewers love the \"MVC vs MVT\" question, and the honest answer is \"same idea, different names, with the framework acting as the front controller.\"\n- **WSGI** — the synchronous Python gateway standard (PEP 3333): the server calls `application(environ, start_response)` once per request. Django's entry point is `django.core.handlers.wsgi.WSGIHandler`, exposed as `application` in your project's `wsgi.py`. *Why it matters:* Gunicorn, uWSGI and mod_wsgi all speak WSGI; one request occupies one worker thread for its full duration.\n- **ASGI** — the asynchronous successor: `await application(scope, receive, send)`. Django's entry point is `django.core.handlers.asgi.ASGIHandler` in `asgi.py`. *Why it matters:* needed for async views, WebSockets (via Channels) and long-lived connections; served by Uvicorn, Daphne or Hypercorn.\n- **`BaseHandler`** — the shared core of both handlers. It builds the middleware chain once at startup (`load_middleware`) and processes each request in `get_response` / `get_response_async`. *Why it matters:* this is \"the request lifecycle\" in code.\n- **`HttpRequest`** — the object your view receives (`WSGIRequest` or `ASGIRequest`): method, path, headers in `META`, `GET`, `POST`, `FILES`, `COOKIES`, plus attributes added by middleware such as `user` and `session`.\n- **`HttpResponse`** — what every view must return (or an exception it raises). Middleware can inspect and modify it on the way out.\n- **Middleware** — callables that wrap the view: code before `get_response(request)` runs on the way in, code after runs on the way out. Ordered by the `MIDDLEWARE` setting. *Why it matters:* order is semantics; `AuthenticationMiddleware` needs the session that `SessionMiddleware` loads.\n- **URLconf / resolver** — the module named by `ROOT_URLCONF`, containing `urlpatterns`. `URLResolver.resolve(path)` returns a `ResolverMatch` (view function, args, kwargs, url name, namespaces).\n- **ORM** — Django's object-relational mapper: models, managers and lazy QuerySets that compile to parameterized SQL.\n- **Template engine** — the Django Template Language (DTL) or Jinja2, configured in `TEMPLATES`, with autoescaping on by default.\n- **Signals in the lifecycle** — `request_started`, `request_finished` and `got_request_exception`. Django itself connects `close_old_connections` to `request_started` and `request_finished`, which is how per-request database connection management works.\n- **Batteries included** — the contrib apps (`admin`, `auth`, `contenttypes`, `sessions`, `messages`, `staticfiles`) and built-ins that cover what most web apps need. *Why it matters:* faster delivery and a consistent security baseline, at the cost of some flexibility.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 From socket to Python call\n\nDjango never touches a socket. A web server or application server (Gunicorn, Uvicorn, uWSGI) accepts the TCP connection, parses the HTTP request, and calls your **application object**. With WSGI that object is created in `wsgi.py`:\n\n```python\n# mysite/wsgi.py\nimport os\nfrom django.core.wsgi import get_wsgi_application\n\nos.environ.setdefault(\"DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE\", \"mysite.settings\")\napplication = get_wsgi_application()\n```\n\n`get_wsgi_application()` does two things: it calls `django.setup()` (configure logging, populate the app registry — import every app's `AppConfig`, then its models, then call each `ready()`), and it returns a `WSGIHandler()` instance. Creating the handler calls `load_middleware()`, which builds the middleware chain **once per process**. That is why a syntax error in a middleware crashes the worker at boot rather than on the first request, and why middleware `__init__` is a good place for one-time configuration.\n\n### 3.2 What `WSGIHandler.__call__` does\n\nFor each request the server calls `handler(environ, start_response)`. Simplified from Django 5.2's source, the handler:\n\n1. calls `set_script_prefix(get_script_name(environ))` so `reverse()` produces correct URLs when the site is mounted under a sub-path;\n2. sends the **`request_started`** signal (Django's own receiver closes database connections that have exceeded `CONN_MAX_AGE` or are unusable);\n3. builds `request = WSGIRequest(environ)` — headers are lazily parsed, `GET`/`POST`/`FILES`/`COOKIES` are computed on first access;\n4. calls `response = self.get_response(request)`;\n5. calls `start_response(status, headers)` and returns the response (an iterable of bytes) to the server;\n6. when the server finishes sending and calls `response.close()`, Django sends **`request_finished`**, which again runs `close_old_connections` and closes any file objects.\n\n### 3.3 `BaseHandler.get_response` and the middleware chain\n\n`get_response(request)` sets the URLconf for the thread (`set_urlconf(settings.ROOT_URLCONF)`), calls `self._middleware_chain(request)`, and logs any 4xx/5xx response through the `django.request` logger. The chain itself is constructed by `load_middleware()`:\n\n```python\n# Simplified from django/core/handlers/base.py\ndef load_middleware(self, is_async=False):\n    get_response = self._get_response_async if is_async else self._get_response\n    handler = convert_exception_to_response(get_response)\n    for middleware_path in reversed(settings.MIDDLEWARE):\n        middleware = import_string(middleware_path)\n        mw_instance = middleware(handler)            # new-style: __init__(get_response)\n        if hasattr(mw_instance, \"process_view\"):\n            self._view_middleware.insert(0, mw_instance.process_view)\n        if hasattr(mw_instance, \"process_template_response\"):\n            self._template_response_middleware.append(mw_instance.process_template_response)\n        if hasattr(mw_instance, \"process_exception\"):\n            self._exception_middleware.append(mw_instance.process_exception)\n        handler = convert_exception_to_response(mw_instance)\n    self._middleware_chain = handler\n```\n\nRead that loop carefully, because it explains the **onion**. It iterates `MIDDLEWARE` **in reverse**, wrapping each middleware around the previous handler. So the *first* entry in `MIDDLEWARE` ends up as the *outermost* layer: it sees the request first and the response last. Every layer is wrapped in `convert_exception_to_response`, which means an exception raised anywhere inside is converted to an HTTP response right there, so the outer middleware always receives a response object, never a raw exception.\n\n### 3.4 `_get_response`: resolve, process_view, call the view\n\nThe innermost callable is `_get_response(request)`:\n\n1. **Resolve the URL.** `resolve_request()` uses `request.urlconf` if a middleware set one, otherwise `ROOT_URLCONF`, calls `get_resolver(urlconf).resolve(request.path_info)`, and stores the `ResolverMatch` on `request.resolver_match`. Patterns are tried **in order; the first match wins**. No match raises `Resolver404` (a subclass of `Http404`).\n2. **Run `process_view` hooks** in `MIDDLEWARE` order with `(request, view_func, view_args, view_kwargs)`. If one returns a response, the view is skipped. This is how `CsrfViewMiddleware` checks the token *after* it knows which view will run (so `@csrf_exempt` on the view can be honoured).\n3. **Wrap in a transaction** if any database has `ATOMIC_REQUESTS=True` (`make_view_atomic`).\n4. **Call the view** with `(request, *args, **kwargs)`.\n5. **On exception**, run `process_exception` hooks (in reverse `MIDDLEWARE` order); the first one to return a response wins, otherwise the exception re-raises and `convert_exception_to_response` handles it.\n6. **Check the response.** If the view returned `None`, Django raises `ValueError: The view ... didn't return an HttpResponse object. It returned None instead.`\n7. **Deferred rendering.** If the response has a `render()` method (a `TemplateResponse`), the `process_template_response` hooks run, then `response.render()` is called.\n\n### 3.5 Exceptions become responses\n\n`convert_exception_to_response` calls `response_for_exception`, which maps:\n\n| Exception | Response |\n|---|---|\n| `Http404` (incl. `Resolver404`) | 404 — `technical_404_response` when `DEBUG=True`, otherwise `handler404` |\n| `PermissionDenied` | 403 via `handler403` |\n| `BadRequest`, `SuspiciousOperation`, `MultiPartParserError` | 400 via `handler400` (suspicious ops are also logged to `django.security.*`) |\n| anything else | 500 — sends `got_request_exception`, then `technical_500_response` (DEBUG) or `handler500` |\n\nThe diagram below puts the whole path on one page.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 430\" width=\"100%\" height=\"430\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj01a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj01b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">One request through Django (WSGI path)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"110\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"75\" y=\"75\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Client</text>\n  <text x=\"75\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">GET /orders/42/</text>\n  <rect x=\"160\" y=\"50\" width=\"130\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nginx + Gunicorn</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">parse HTTP, pick worker</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"101\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">application(environ, sr)</text>\n  <rect x=\"320\" y=\"50\" width=\"160\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"400\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">WSGIHandler.__call__</text>\n  <text x=\"400\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">request_started signal</text>\n  <text x=\"400\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">WSGIRequest(environ)</text>\n  <rect x=\"510\" y=\"50\" width=\"160\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"590\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">BaseHandler.get_response</text>\n  <text x=\"590\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">set_urlconf(ROOT_URLCONF)</text>\n  <text x=\"590\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">_middleware_chain(request)</text>\n  <path d=\"M130,80 L156,80\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj01a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M290,80 L316,80\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj01a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M480,80 L506,80\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj01a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"510\" y=\"140\" width=\"350\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Middleware (request phase, top to bottom)</text>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"178\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Security, Session, Common, Csrf, Authentication, Message, XFrame</text>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">any layer may return a response and short-circuit</text>\n  <path d=\"M590,110 L590,136\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj01a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"510\" y=\"240\" width=\"350\" height=\"66\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">_get_response</text>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"277\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">resolve(path_info) to ResolverMatch, 404 if none</text>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"292\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">process_view hooks, ATOMIC_REQUESTS wrap, call view</text>\n  <path d=\"M685,210 L685,236\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj01a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"510\" y=\"336\" width=\"160\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"590\" y=\"358\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">View (your code)</text>\n  <text x=\"590\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Order.objects.get(pk=42)</text>\n  <text x=\"590\" y=\"390\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">render(request, tpl, ctx)</text>\n  <rect x=\"700\" y=\"336\" width=\"160\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"780\" y=\"358\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ORM + Templates</text>\n  <text x=\"780\" y=\"375\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">SQL to PostgreSQL</text>\n  <text x=\"780\" y=\"390\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">DTL render, autoescape</text>\n  <path d=\"M685,306 L620,332\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj01a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M670,371 L696,371\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj01a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"240\" width=\"460\" height=\"166\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f0fdf4\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"262\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Response path (bottom to top)</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"284\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">1. View returns HttpResponse (None raises ValueError)</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"304\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">2. TemplateResponse: process_template_response, then render()</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"324\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">3. Middleware response phase runs in REVERSE order</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"344\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">4. Exceptions at any layer become 404/403/400/500 responses</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"364\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">5. start_response(status, headers), body iterable to server</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"384\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">6. close(): request_finished signal, close_old_connections</text>\n  <path d=\"M506,371 L484,371\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj01b)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.6 Why MTV and not MVC?\n\nIn classic MVC the *controller* receives input and picks a *view* to render the *model*. In Django the framework itself is the front controller (handler + URLconf), your **view function** is the request-specific controller logic, and the **template** is the presentation. Django's FAQ states it plainly: what MVC calls a \"view\" is a Django template, and what MVC calls a \"controller\" is the framework plus the view function. The names are different; the separation of concerns is the same. In an API-only project (DRF), the \"template\" is replaced by a serializer and a renderer, which is why people say Django REST projects are \"MV + serializer\".\n\n### 3.7 ASGI: the same core, a different front door\n\n`ASGIHandler.__call__(scope, receive, send)` is a coroutine. For an `http` scope it reads the request body from `receive()` into a spooled temporary file, builds an `ASGIRequest`, awaits `get_response_async`, and streams the response back with `send()` messages (`http.response.start`, then one or more `http.response.body`). The middleware chain is built in async mode; sync-only middleware and sync views are adapted with `sync_to_async` (thread-sensitive by default), and async middleware under WSGI is adapted with `async_to_sync`. Each adaptation costs a thread hop, so a mixed stack under ASGI is slower than a pure one. The details belong in [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi).\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 The middleware onion\n\nNew-style middleware (since Django 1.10) is a callable factory. Everything before `self.get_response(request)` is the request phase; everything after is the response phase:\n\n```python\nclass TimingMiddleware:\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response      # the next layer inward; runs once per process\n\n    def __call__(self, request):\n        start = time.perf_counter()           # request phase (outer to inner)\n        response = self.get_response(request)\n        response[\"Server-Timing\"] = f\"app;dur={(time.perf_counter() - start) * 1000:.1f}\"\n        return response                       # response phase (inner to outer)\n```\n\nBecause layers wrap each other, the recommended order in Django's own `startproject` template is meaningful:\n\n```python\nMIDDLEWARE = [\n    \"django.middleware.security.SecurityMiddleware\",          # HTTPS redirect, HSTS, nosniff: first\n    \"django.contrib.sessions.middleware.SessionMiddleware\",   # loads request.session\n    \"django.middleware.common.CommonMiddleware\",              # APPEND_SLASH, DISALLOWED_USER_AGENTS\n    \"django.middleware.csrf.CsrfViewMiddleware\",              # process_view token check\n    \"django.contrib.auth.middleware.AuthenticationMiddleware\",# request.user (needs session)\n    \"django.contrib.messages.middleware.MessageMiddleware\",   # needs session\n    \"django.middleware.clickjacking.XFrameOptionsMiddleware\", # X-Frame-Options: DENY\n]\n```\n\nThe next diagram shows the onion, and what a short-circuit looks like.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj01c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj01d\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj01e\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">The middleware onion: first in MIDDLEWARE = outermost layer</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"560\" height=\"340\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"58\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">SecurityMiddleware</text>\n  <rect x=\"50\" y=\"68\" width=\"500\" height=\"300\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">SessionMiddleware</text>\n  <rect x=\"80\" y=\"96\" width=\"440\" height=\"260\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">CommonMiddleware</text>\n  <rect x=\"110\" y=\"124\" width=\"380\" height=\"220\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">CsrfViewMiddleware</text>\n  <rect x=\"140\" y=\"152\" width=\"320\" height=\"180\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">AuthenticationMiddleware</text>\n  <rect x=\"200\" y=\"200\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"228\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">URL resolve + view</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"246\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">_get_response</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"262\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">process_view hooks run here</text>\n  <path d=\"M160,190 L196,220\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj01c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"186\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"9\">request in</text>\n  <path d=\"M404,270 L444,300\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj01d)\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"318\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"9\">response out</text>\n  <rect x=\"610\" y=\"40\" width=\"250\" height=\"150\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"735\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Normal flow</text>\n  <text x=\"625\" y=\"84\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Request: Security, Session, Common,</text>\n  <text x=\"625\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Csrf, Auth ... then the view</text>\n  <text x=\"625\" y=\"122\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Response: ... Auth, Csrf, Common,</text>\n  <text x=\"625\" y=\"138\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Session (saves cookie), Security</text>\n  <text x=\"625\" y=\"164\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Session must wrap Auth: request.user</text>\n  <text x=\"625\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">is read from request.session</text>\n  <rect x=\"610\" y=\"210\" width=\"250\" height=\"170\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"735\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Short-circuit</text>\n  <text x=\"625\" y=\"254\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">CommonMiddleware with PREPEND_WWW</text>\n  <text x=\"625\" y=\"270\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">sees example.com/orders/ and</text>\n  <text x=\"625\" y=\"286\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">returns a 301 to www. itself.</text>\n  <text x=\"625\" y=\"310\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Csrf, Auth and the view never run;</text>\n  <text x=\"625\" y=\"326\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Session and Security still see the</text>\n  <text x=\"625\" y=\"342\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">response on the way out.</text>\n  <path d=\"M520,110 L604,290\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj01e)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 4.2 Sequence view\n\nThe same journey as a sequence, including the cleanup that happens after the bytes are sent:\n\n```text\nClient      Gunicorn     WSGIHandler      Middleware chain          Resolver/View        DB\n  |  GET /orders/42/ |           |                 |                        |               |\n  |----------------->|           |                 |                        |               |\n  |                  | app(env)  |                 |                        |               |\n  |                  |---------->| request_started |                        |               |\n  |                  |           | WSGIRequest     |                        |               |\n  |                  |           |---------------->| Security..Auth (in)    |               |\n  |                  |           |                 |----------------------->| resolve()     |\n  |                  |           |                 |                        | process_view  |\n  |                  |           |                 |                        | view()------->| SELECT ...\n  |                  |           |                 |                        |<--------------|\n  |                  |           |                 |<-----------------------| HttpResponse  |\n  |                  |           |<----------------| Auth..Security (out)   |               |\n  |                  |<----------| start_response  |                        |               |\n  |<-----------------| bytes     | close(): request_finished -> close_old_connections       |\n```\n\n### 4.3 Where each concern lives\n\n| Concern | Layer | Chapter |\n|---|---|---|\n| Worker processes, timeouts, TLS termination | Gunicorn/Uvicorn + Nginx | [Ch 35 · Deployment](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production) |\n| Settings, app registry, `ready()` | `django.setup()` | [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings) |\n| HTTPS redirect, sessions, CSRF, `request.user` | Middleware | [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) |\n| Path to view mapping | URLconf | [Ch 03 · URL Routing](topic.html?p=03-urls-routing) |\n| Business logic, status codes | Views | [Ch 04 · Views](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses) |\n| Data access | ORM | [Ch 10 · QuerySets](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) |\n| HTML rendering | Templates | [Ch 05 · Templates](topic.html?p=05-templates) |\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example: watch the lifecycle happen\n\nCreate a throwaway project and add a middleware that prints each phase.\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\"\ndjango-admin startproject mysite .\npython manage.py startapp shop\n```\n\n```python\n# shop/views.py\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse, Http404\n\ndef hello(request, name):\n    print(\"  [view] running for\", name)\n    if name == \"ghost\":\n        raise Http404(\"No such person\")\n    return HttpResponse(f\"Hello, {name}!\")\n```\n\n```python\n# mysite/urls.py\nfrom django.urls import path\nfrom shop import views\n\nurlpatterns = [\n    path(\"hello/<str:name>/\", views.hello, name=\"hello\"),\n]\n```\n\n```python\n# shop/middleware.py\nclass TraceMiddleware:\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response\n        print(\"[init] TraceMiddleware built once per process\")\n\n    def __call__(self, request):\n        print(f\"[in ] {request.method} {request.path}\")\n        response = self.get_response(request)\n        print(f\"[out] status={response.status_code}\")\n        return response\n\n    def process_view(self, request, view_func, view_args, view_kwargs):\n        print(f\"  [process_view] {view_func.__name__} kwargs={view_kwargs}\")\n        return None   # continue to the view\n```\n\nAdd `\"shop.middleware.TraceMiddleware\"` as the **last** entry of `MIDDLEWARE` and `\"shop\"` to `INSTALLED_APPS`, then run the server and hit three URLs.\n\n```bash\npython manage.py runserver\ncurl -s localhost:8000/hello/ada/\ncurl -s localhost:8000/hello/ghost/ -o /dev/null\ncurl -s localhost:8000/nope/ -o /dev/null\n```\n\nThe console shows the order of the phases, and how a URL miss never reaches `process_view`.\n\n```text\n[init] TraceMiddleware built once per process\n[in ] GET /hello/ada/\n  [process_view] hello kwargs={'name': 'ada'}\n  [view] running for ada\n[out] status=200\n[in ] GET /hello/ghost/\n  [process_view] hello kwargs={'name': 'ghost'}\n  [view] running for ghost\n[out] status=404\n[in ] GET /nope/\n[out] status=404\n```\n\nNotice two things. First, the `Http404` raised in the view was converted into a response *before* it reached `TraceMiddleware`'s response phase — that is `convert_exception_to_response` at work. Second, for `/nope/` the resolver raised `Resolver404` before `process_view` ran, so the middleware saw only the request and the 404 response.\n\n### 5.2 Real-world example: request ID, timing and structured logging\n\nIn production you want every log line from one request tied together, a latency number per endpoint, and the correlation ID returned to the client so support can find the logs. This is a classic interview \"design a middleware\" task.\n\n```python\n# core/middleware.py\nimport logging\nimport time\nimport uuid\nfrom contextvars import ContextVar\n\nrequest_id_var: ContextVar[str] = ContextVar(\"request_id\", default=\"-\")\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\"core.request\")\n\n\nclass RequestIdFilter(logging.Filter):\n    \"\"\"Inject the current request id into every log record.\"\"\"\n    def filter(self, record):\n        record.request_id = request_id_var.get()\n        return True\n\n\nclass RequestContextMiddleware:\n    header = \"HTTP_X_REQUEST_ID\"     # set by the load balancer if present\n\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response\n\n    def __call__(self, request):\n        rid = request.META.get(self.header) or uuid.uuid4().hex\n        token = request_id_var.set(rid)\n        request.request_id = rid\n        start = time.perf_counter()\n        try:\n            response = self.get_response(request)\n        finally:\n            request_id_var.reset(token)\n        elapsed_ms = (time.perf_counter() - start) * 1000\n        match = getattr(request, \"resolver_match\", None)\n        logger.info(\n            \"request\",\n            extra={\n                \"method\": request.method,\n                \"path\": request.path,\n                \"route\": match.route if match else None,\n                \"view\": match.view_name if match else None,\n                \"status\": response.status_code,\n                \"ms\": round(elapsed_ms, 1),\n            },\n        )\n        response[\"X-Request-ID\"] = rid\n        return response\n```\n\nWire it in near the top of the stack so its timing includes the other middleware, and attach the filter to your log handler:\n\n```python\n# settings.py (excerpt)\nMIDDLEWARE = [\n    \"django.middleware.security.SecurityMiddleware\",\n    \"core.middleware.RequestContextMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.sessions.middleware.SessionMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.common.CommonMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.csrf.CsrfViewMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.auth.middleware.AuthenticationMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.messages.middleware.MessageMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.clickjacking.XFrameOptionsMiddleware\",\n]\n\nLOGGING = {\n    \"version\": 1,\n    \"disable_existing_loggers\": False,\n    \"filters\": {\"request_id\": {\"()\": \"core.middleware.RequestIdFilter\"}},\n    \"formatters\": {\n        \"plain\": {\"format\": \"%(asctime)s %(levelname)s [%(request_id)s] %(name)s %(message)s\"},\n    },\n    \"handlers\": {\n        \"console\": {\"class\": \"logging.StreamHandler\", \"filters\": [\"request_id\"], \"formatter\": \"plain\"},\n    },\n    \"root\": {\"handlers\": [\"console\"], \"level\": \"INFO\"},\n    \"loggers\": {\"django.request\": {\"level\": \"WARNING\"}},\n}\n```\n\nUsing `request.resolver_match.route` (for example `orders/<int:pk>/`) instead of `request.path` is what makes the metric useful: a dashboard grouped by route has a handful of rows, while one grouped by raw path has one row per order ID. A `ContextVar` rather than a thread-local is chosen deliberately so the same code works under ASGI, where many requests share one thread.\n\n### 5.3 A tiny view touching every layer\n\n```python\n# shop/views.py\nfrom django.shortcuts import get_object_or_404, render\nfrom .models import Order\n\ndef order_detail(request, pk):\n    order = get_object_or_404(\n        Order.objects.select_related(\"customer\"),   # one JOIN instead of two queries\n        pk=pk, customer__user=request.user,          # request.user from AuthenticationMiddleware\n    )\n    return render(request, \"shop/order_detail.html\", {\"order\": order})\n```\n\nThe ORM issues one parameterized query; nothing is interpolated into the SQL string:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"shop_order\".\"id\", \"shop_order\".\"customer_id\", \"shop_order\".\"total\", ...,\n       \"shop_customer\".\"id\", \"shop_customer\".\"user_id\", ...\nFROM \"shop_order\"\nINNER JOIN \"shop_customer\" ON (\"shop_order\".\"customer_id\" = \"shop_customer\".\"id\")\nWHERE (\"shop_order\".\"id\" = %s AND \"shop_customer\".\"user_id\" = %s)\nLIMIT 21\n```\n\n`LIMIT 21` is how `get()` detects `MultipleObjectsReturned` cheaply and still reports \"more than 20\" in the error message. The `render()` shortcut loads the template through the configured loaders, renders it with autoescaping, and wraps the result in an `HttpResponse`.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Aspect | Django's choice | Benefit | Cost |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Scope | Batteries included | Auth, admin, ORM, migrations, security in one coherent package | Heavier than micro-frameworks; opinions you must follow |\n| Pattern | MTV with central dispatcher | Clear place for each concern | Business logic tends to bloat views/models without discipline |\n| Cross-cutting code | Middleware onion | One place for auth, CSRF, headers, logging | Runs on *every* request; order mistakes are subtle |\n| Concurrency | Sync-first, async optional | Simple mental model, mature libraries | Long I/O ties up a worker under WSGI |\n| Data | Built-in ORM | Safe SQL, migrations, portability | Easy to write N+1 queries; complex SQL needs escape hatches |\n| Errors | Exceptions mapped to responses | `raise Http404` anywhere | Swallowed exceptions in custom middleware hide bugs |\n\n### When to use Django\n\n- Database-backed products with users, permissions, an admin back office and forms — the sweet spot.\n- APIs with DRF where you want auth, throttling, pagination and an ORM out of the box.\n- Teams that value convention and security defaults over assembling libraries.\n\n### When NOT to use Django\n\n- A single tiny endpoint or a pure proxy where startup time and footprint dominate (a micro-framework or a function-as-a-service handler is lighter).\n- Workloads that are almost entirely long-lived connections at very high fan-out with no relational data; Django Channels can do it, but a purpose-built async stack may be simpler.\n- When you need a non-relational primary store and the ORM, admin and migrations would be unused weight.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**Mistake: thinking the URL resolver runs first.** People assume Django picks the view and then runs middleware. In fact the full request phase of every middleware's `__call__` runs *before* URL resolution; only `process_view` runs after it. Consequence: in `__call__` you cannot rely on `request.resolver_match` before calling `get_response`. Use `process_view` if you need the view.\n\n**Mistake: wrong middleware order.** Placing `AuthenticationMiddleware` above `SessionMiddleware` raises `ImproperlyConfigured` (\"The Django authentication middleware requires session middleware to be installed\") on the first request, and `manage.py check` flags it for the admin. Note too that `APPEND_SLASH` works in `CommonMiddleware`'s *response* phase (it turns a 404 into a 301), so a middleware further out that replaces 404 responses will hide it. Start from the `startproject` order and insert your own middleware deliberately.\n\n**Mistake: returning `None` from a view.** A missing `return` in one branch produces `ValueError: ... didn't return an HttpResponse object`. Make every branch return, and test the unhappy paths.\n\n```python\n# Before\ndef publish(request, pk):\n    if request.method == \"POST\":\n        ...\n        return redirect(\"post-detail\", pk=pk)\n    # GET falls through: returns None -> 500\n\n# After\nfrom django.views.decorators.http import require_POST\n\n@require_POST                               # GET now gets 405 Method Not Allowed\ndef publish(request, pk):\n    ...\n    return redirect(\"post-detail\", pk=pk)\n```\n\n**Mistake: catching all exceptions in middleware.** A `try/except Exception: return HttpResponse(status=500)` around `get_response` hides tracebacks from Sentry and from `got_request_exception`. Let Django's exception handling run, or log with `logger.exception` and re-raise.\n\n**Mistake: per-request work in middleware `__init__`, or per-process work in `__call__`.** `__init__` runs once per process; `__call__` runs per request. Compiling regexes or reading settings belongs in `__init__`; anything about the user belongs in `__call__`.\n\n**Mistake: storing request state on the middleware instance.** One instance serves every request in the process (and every thread in it). `self.current_user = request.user` is a data leak between concurrent requests. Put state on `request` or in a `ContextVar`.\n\n**Best practices**\n\n- Keep views thin: parse input, call a service/model method, return a response.\n- Raise `Http404`, `PermissionDenied`, `BadRequest` instead of hand-building error responses — they flow through the custom error handlers consistently.\n- Know your gateway: WSGI for classic sync apps, ASGI only when you have async views or WebSockets that benefit.\n- Use `request.resolver_match.route` for metrics, never raw paths.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: slow requests that are not slow in the view.** *Symptom:* p99 latency is high but view timing looks fine. *Root cause:* time spent in middleware (a session backed by a slow DB table, a middleware calling an external service), or in the response phase (`GZipMiddleware` on large bodies). *Fix:* put a timing middleware at the top and another just above the view; compare. Django Debug Toolbar's *Timer* and *SQL* panels show where time goes in development.\n\n**Pitfall: worker timeouts under Gunicorn.** *Symptom:* `[CRITICAL] WORKER TIMEOUT` and 502s from Nginx. *Root cause:* a sync worker is blocked for longer than Gunicorn's `--timeout` (30 s default) — usually an external HTTP call without a timeout or a huge query. *Fix:* set timeouts on every outbound call, move slow work to Celery ([Ch 31](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks)), add `statement_timeout` on PostgreSQL.\n\n**Pitfall: database connections pile up.** *Symptom:* `FATAL: too many connections` on PostgreSQL. *Root cause:* each worker thread holds its own connection; with `CONN_MAX_AGE=None` connections persist forever, multiplied by workers × threads × pods. *Fix:* size the pool consciously, use Django 5.1+'s native psycopg pool or PgBouncer — see [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases).\n\n**Pitfall: `DEBUG=True` in production.** *Symptom:* tracebacks with settings shown to users; every connection keeps a log of recent SQL queries (`connection.queries`), which costs memory and CPU on every request. *Fix:* `DEBUG=False`, set `ALLOWED_HOSTS`, and run `python manage.py check --deploy` in CI.\n\n**Pitfall: 500 instead of 404 for missing objects.** `Order.objects.get(pk=pk)` raises `Order.DoesNotExist`, which is *not* an `Http404`, so users see a 500 and your error tracker fills up. Use `get_object_or_404`.\n\n**Debugging toolkit**\n\n- `django.db.connection.queries` (DEBUG only) and `len(connection.queries)` for query counts; `QuerySet.explain()` for plans.\n- `python manage.py shell` + `from django.urls import resolve; resolve(\"/orders/42/\")` to see which view a path maps to.\n- Logging: the `django.request` logger records every 4xx/5xx with the request attached; `django.db.backends` at DEBUG logs every SQL statement.\n- Sentry's Django integration hooks `got_request_exception` and adds request data and breadcrumbs.\n\n**Scaling notes.** Django scales horizontally: the request lifecycle is stateless as long as sessions, cache and files live in shared services (database/Redis/S3). Capacity per instance is roughly *workers × threads* concurrent requests under WSGI; under ASGI one process can hold many idle connections but CPU-bound work still serializes on the event loop. The usual ladder: fix queries → cache → more workers/pods → read replicas → offload to tasks.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What happens, step by step, when a request hits a Django application?**\nA: The web server (Nginx) forwards to an application server such as Gunicorn, which calls Django's WSGI application, a `WSGIHandler`. The handler sends `request_started`, builds a `WSGIRequest` and calls `BaseHandler.get_response`, which runs the middleware chain. Each middleware's request phase runs top to bottom; the innermost `_get_response` resolves the URL against `ROOT_URLCONF` (first match wins, 404 otherwise), runs `process_view` hooks, and calls the view. The view uses the ORM and usually renders a template into an `HttpResponse`. The response then passes back through middleware in reverse order, the handler calls `start_response`, and when the server closes the response `request_finished` fires and stale DB connections are closed.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between MVC and Django's MVT?**\nA: They are the same separation of concerns with different names. Django's *model* is the MVC model; Django's *template* is what MVC calls the view (presentation); Django's *view* holds the request-handling logic MVC assigns to the controller. The framework itself — the handler plus the URLconf — is the front controller that dispatches requests. So \"where is the controller in Django?\" is answered by \"the framework and the view function together.\"\n\n**Q: What is the difference between WSGI and ASGI, and which does Django use?**\nA: WSGI is a synchronous calling convention: one call per request, `application(environ, start_response)`, returning an iterable body, which ties up a worker thread for the whole request. ASGI is its async successor: `await application(scope, receive, send)`, supporting long-lived connections such as WebSockets and many concurrent requests per process. Django supports both through `WSGIHandler` and `ASGIHandler`, which share `BaseHandler`. Use WSGI for classic sync apps and ASGI when you have async views, streaming or WebSockets via Channels.\n\n**Q: In which order does middleware run?**\nA: On the request, in the order listed in `MIDDLEWARE` (top to bottom); on the response, in reverse (bottom to top). That follows from `load_middleware` wrapping the list in reverse, so the first entry is the outermost layer. `process_view` runs top to bottom just before the view, while `process_exception` and `process_template_response` run bottom to top. If a middleware returns a response without calling `get_response`, inner layers and the view are skipped but outer layers still process the response.\n\n**Q: Why must `SessionMiddleware` come before `AuthenticationMiddleware`?**\nA: `AuthenticationMiddleware` sets `request.user` lazily by reading the user ID stored in `request.session`. That attribute only exists after `SessionMiddleware` has run its request phase. If the order is reversed, Django's auth middleware fails its check that session middleware is installed, or `request.user` cannot be loaded. The same applies to `MessageMiddleware`, whose default storage falls back to the session.\n\n**Q: How does Django turn an exception raised in a view into an HTTP response?**\nA: Every layer of the middleware chain is wrapped by `convert_exception_to_response`. When an exception escapes, `response_for_exception` maps `Http404` to 404, `PermissionDenied` to 403, `BadRequest`/`SuspiciousOperation` to 400, and anything else to 500. With `DEBUG=True` you get the technical 404/500 pages; otherwise the `handler404`/`handler500` views (customizable in the root URLconf). For 500s Django also sends `got_request_exception` and logs to `django.request`, which is what Sentry hooks into. Before that, middleware `process_exception` hooks get a chance to return their own response.\n\n**Q: What does \"batteries included\" mean, and what is the downside?**\nA: Django ships the things most web apps need — ORM, migrations, auth, admin, sessions, forms, templates, caching, security middleware, test tooling — designed to work together and maintained on one release schedule. That gives fast delivery and a consistent security baseline. The downside is weight and opinion: you work within Django's patterns (its ORM, its project layout), swapping a component is harder than in a micro-framework, and unused pieces still cost startup time and memory.\n\n**Q: What happens if a view returns `None`?**\nA: After calling the view, `_get_response` checks the result and raises `ValueError` saying the view didn't return an `HttpResponse` object. It becomes a 500. The usual cause is a branch without a `return`, for example handling POST but forgetting GET. Decorators like `@require_POST` and making every branch return a response fix it.\n\n**Q: When is the URL resolved relative to middleware, and why does it matter?**\nA: The middleware request phases (`__call__` code before `get_response`) run before URL resolution; resolution happens in `_get_response`, followed by `process_view`. So `request.resolver_match` is not available in a middleware's `__call__` before it calls the next layer, but it is available in `process_view` and in the response phase. This matters for middleware that wants to act per view, such as CSRF exemption or per-route metrics.\n\n**Q: How does Django manage database connections across requests?**\nA: Connections are per thread and opened lazily on the first query. Django connects `close_old_connections` to the `request_started` and `request_finished` signals; it closes a connection if it has exceeded `CONN_MAX_AGE` or is in an unusable state. With the default `CONN_MAX_AGE=0`, every request opens and closes its own connection; with a positive value connections persist across requests in the same thread; `CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS` (4.1+) validates reused connections. Django 5.1 added native connection pooling for PostgreSQL with psycopg 3.\n\n**Q: Your API's p99 latency doubled after a deploy but the view code didn't change. How do you find the cause? (Senior)**\nA: I'd first split the time by layer instead of guessing: a timing middleware at the top of the stack versus one just above the view (or APM spans) tells me whether the time is in middleware, the view, or after the response, such as streaming or GZip. Then I check what the deploy changed outside the view — a new middleware, a reordered `MIDDLEWARE`, session engine or cache settings, `CONN_MAX_AGE` (connection setup on every request costs real time with TLS to the DB), or Gunicorn worker/thread counts. Query counts per request via logging or `assertNumQueries` in tests catch a new N+1 hidden in a context processor or middleware that touches `request.user`. Finally I look at saturation: if workers are all busy, latency is queueing time in the Gunicorn backlog, not handler time.\n\n**Q: How would you design a middleware that adds a correlation ID to every log line and response, and works under both WSGI and ASGI? (Senior)**\nA: Read an incoming `X-Request-ID` from the load balancer or generate a UUID, store it in a `contextvars.ContextVar` (not a thread-local, because under ASGI many requests share a thread and the context is copied per task), attach a `logging.Filter` that injects it into each record, and set the response header on the way out. Place it near the top of `MIDDLEWARE` so its timing and logs cover other middleware. For a native async stack, implement both sync and async paths by setting `sync_capable`/`async_capable` and checking `iscoroutinefunction(get_response)`, or use `@sync_and_async_middleware`, to avoid a thread hop per request. Also propagate the ID to Celery tasks in headers so background work is traceable.\n\n**Q: Why might running a fully sync Django app under ASGI make it slower, not faster? (Senior)**\nA: Under ASGI every sync view and sync-only middleware must be adapted with `sync_to_async`, which by default is thread-sensitive and runs in a single shared thread, so you add a context switch per call and can serialize work that a threaded WSGI worker would have run in parallel. The ORM is still synchronous underneath; the async ORM methods themselves run queries in a thread. You only gain when views spend time awaiting I/O with genuinely async clients, or when you hold many idle connections such as WebSockets or long polling. For classic request/response CRUD, Gunicorn sync or threaded workers are simpler and at least as fast.\n\n**Q: Where would you put code that must run once at startup versus once per request?**\nA: One-time initialization that needs the app registry goes in `AppConfig.ready()` — registering signal handlers, checks — and must be idempotent and must not query the database. Per-process middleware setup goes in the middleware's `__init__`, which runs when the handler builds the chain. Per-request logic goes in `__call__` or a view. Doing database queries at import time or in `ready()` is a classic mistake that breaks migrations and tests.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Stage | Who | Key detail |\n|---|---|---|\n| Accept + parse HTTP | Nginx / Gunicorn / Uvicorn | Django never sees the socket |\n| Entry point | `WSGIHandler` / `ASGIHandler` | built once; `load_middleware()` at init |\n| Start | `request_started` signal | closes stale DB connections |\n| Middleware in | `MIDDLEWARE` top to bottom | may short-circuit with a response |\n| Resolve | `URLResolver.resolve(path_info)` | ordered, first match wins, else 404 |\n| `process_view` | middleware, top to bottom | CSRF check lives here |\n| View | your code | must return `HttpResponse` |\n| Template response | `process_template_response`, `render()` | deferred rendering |\n| Middleware out | bottom to top | headers, cookies, compression |\n| Errors | `convert_exception_to_response` | 404/403/400/500 mapping |\n| Finish | `request_finished` signal | connection cleanup |\n\n- MTV: template = presentation, view = controller logic, framework + URLconf = dispatcher.\n- First middleware in the list is the outermost layer: first in, last out.\n- Middleware `__init__` runs once per process; `__call__` per request; never store request state on `self`.\n- The URL is resolved after the middleware request phases, before `process_view`.\n- Exceptions are converted to responses at every layer; `DoesNotExist` is not `Http404`.\n- `SessionMiddleware` before `AuthenticationMiddleware` and `MessageMiddleware`; `SecurityMiddleware` first.\n- WSGI = one request per worker thread; ASGI = event loop, worth it for async I/O and WebSockets.\n- DB connections are per thread, managed by `close_old_connections` on request start/finish.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up once: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" && django-admin startproject lab . && python manage.py startapp core`.\n\n1. **Trace the onion.** Write two middleware classes, `A` and `B`, that print on the way in and out. Put them at positions 1 and 3 of `MIDDLEWARE`, hit a view and confirm the order. Swap them and predict the output before running.\n2. **Short-circuit.** Make `B` return `HttpResponse(\"maintenance\", status=503)` when a setting `MAINTENANCE=True`. Verify that `A` still sees the response but the view and `process_view` do not run.\n3. **Exception mapping.** Create views that raise `Http404`, `PermissionDenied`, `BadRequest`, `Order.DoesNotExist` and `ZeroDivisionError`. Record the status codes with `DEBUG=True` and `DEBUG=False` (set `ALLOWED_HOSTS=[\"localhost\"]`).\n4. **Resolver in the shell.** In `python manage.py shell` call `resolve(\"/hello/ada/\")` and inspect `func`, `kwargs`, `url_name`, `route`. Then call `resolve(\"/missing/\")` and catch `Resolver404`.\n5. **Signals.** Connect receivers to `request_started` and `request_finished` that log `connection.connection is None`. Change `CONN_MAX_AGE` between 0 and 60 and observe connection reuse.\n\n**Mini project — Lifecycle profiler.** Build a `core` app with a middleware that records, per request: route, view name, status, total time, time inside the view (measure in `process_view` plus the response phase), and number of SQL queries (use `connection.execute_wrapper` to count). Log it as one JSON line and expose an `X-Request-ID` header. Add a test with `RequestFactory` and `override_settings(MIDDLEWARE=...)` that asserts the header is set.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings) (what `django.setup()` loads), [Ch 03 · URL Routing & URLconf](topic.html?p=03-urls-routing) (the resolver in depth), [Ch 04 · Views, HttpRequest & HttpResponse](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses), [Ch 05 · Django Template Language](topic.html?p=05-templates), [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) (writing and ordering middleware), [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi), [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production), [Ch 36 · Django Internals](topic.html?p=36-django-internals).\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · HTTP Fundamentals](../rest-api/topic.html?p=02-http-fundamentals), [REST API · Status Codes](../rest-api/topic.html?p=07-status-codes), [System Design · Load Balancing](../system-design/topic.html?p=09-load-balancing), [System Design · Proxies & Gateways](../system-design/topic.html?p=10-proxies-gateway).\n\n- **Request and response objects** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the authoritative reference for `HttpRequest`/`HttpResponse` attributes. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/request-response/>\n- **Middleware** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · the official explanation of the onion, hooks and ordering. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/http/middleware/>\n- **How to deploy with WSGI / ASGI** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · what `wsgi.py`/`asgi.py` do and how servers load them. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/deployment/>\n- **Django FAQ: \"Django appears to be a MVC framework...\"** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the official MTV vs MVC answer. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/faq/general/>\n- **django/core/handlers/base.py** — GitHub · *Advanced* · read `load_middleware` and `_get_response` yourself; it is short. <https://github.com/django/django/blob/stable/5.2.x/django/core/handlers/base.py>\n- **PEP 3333 — Python WSGI** — python.org · *Intermediate* · the spec every WSGI server implements. <https://peps.python.org/pep-3333/>\n- **ASGI specification** — asgi.readthedocs.io · *Advanced* · scope, receive and send explained. <https://asgi.readthedocs.io/en/latest/>\n- **Django Debug Toolbar** — GitHub · *Beginner* · see timing, SQL and templates for each request. <https://github.com/django-commons/django-debug-toolbar>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 01.*\n", "02-projects-apps-settings": "# 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings\n\n> **In one line:** A Django *project* is configuration — settings, the root URLconf and the WSGI/ASGI entry points — while *apps* are reusable units of models, views and commands that the project activates through `INSTALLED_APPS`; understanding how settings load lazily and how the app registry boots is what lets you structure large codebases, keep secrets out of git, and avoid the classic `AppRegistryNotReady` and \"works on my machine\" failures.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle) — where `wsgi.py` calls `django.setup()` before the first request.\n\nThe first two commands anyone runs in Django are `django-admin startproject` and `python manage.py startapp`, and the first interview question about them is: *\"What is the difference between a project and an app?\"* It sounds trivial, but the answer exposes whether you understand how Django is assembled at runtime.\n\nThe underlying problem is configuration and modularity. A real backend has dozens of settings that differ between a developer laptop, CI, staging and production: database URLs, secret keys, cache servers, allowed hosts, email backends, feature flags. It also has many feature areas — accounts, catalogue, orders, billing — that should be separable, testable and sometimes reusable across projects.\n\nThe **naive approaches** both fail. Hard-coding settings in one `settings.py` and editing it per environment means the production `SECRET_KEY` ends up in git, a developer accidentally ships `DEBUG=True`, and nobody knows which config is live. Putting all models and views in one giant app means circular imports, 3,000-line `models.py` files, and migrations nobody can reason about.\n\nDjango's answer has three parts:\n\n1. **Projects vs apps.** The project is the container of configuration; apps are Python packages that contribute models, views, templates, static files, admin registrations, signals and management commands. A project activates apps by listing them in `INSTALLED_APPS`.\n2. **The app registry.** At startup `django.setup()` imports every installed app in a defined order, builds a registry (`django.apps.apps`) of `AppConfig` objects and models, and then calls each app's `ready()` hook. Almost everything — the ORM, admin autodiscovery, signal receivers, template loaders — depends on this registry being ready.\n3. **Lazy settings.** `django.conf.settings` is a lazy proxy that loads the module named by `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE` the first time any setting is read, layered on top of Django's defaults in `django.conf.global_settings`. Because the settings module is just Python, you can read environment variables, split by environment, and validate at import time.\n\nFinally, `manage.py` is the project's command-line front door, and **custom management commands** are how you run operational jobs — backfills, cleanups, imports — with the full Django environment loaded.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Project** — the directory created by `startproject`: `manage.py` plus a configuration package with `settings.py`, `urls.py`, `wsgi.py`, `asgi.py`. *Why it matters:* it is deployment-specific glue, not where business logic lives.\n- **App** — a Python package with an `apps.py` (`AppConfig`) and usually `models.py`, `views.py`, `admin.py`, `migrations/`, `templates/`, `static/`, `management/commands/`. *Why it matters:* the unit of models, migrations and reuse; migrations are per app.\n- **`INSTALLED_APPS`** — the ordered list of app configs or packages the project activates. *Why it matters:* only installed apps get their models registered, migrations run, templates and static files discovered and commands exposed; **order matters** for template, static and command overrides.\n- **`AppConfig`** — the class describing an app: `name` (full dotted path), `label` (short unique name, default last component of `name`), `verbose_name`, `default_auto_field`, and the `ready()` hook. Since Django 3.2 it is discovered automatically from `apps.py`.\n- **App registry (`django.apps.apps`)** — the global object holding every `AppConfig` and model. `apps.get_model(\"orders\", \"Order\")` looks models up by label. *Why it matters:* accessing models before it is ready raises `AppRegistryNotReady`.\n- **`ready()`** — called once per process after all models are loaded. The place to connect signal receivers, register checks and import modules for their side effects. *Why it matters:* the documented home for signal registration.\n- **`DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE`** — the environment variable naming the settings module (`config.settings.prod`). `manage.py`, `wsgi.py` and `asgi.py` set a default with `os.environ.setdefault`.\n- **`LazySettings`** — the `LazyObject` behind `django.conf.settings`; it builds a `Settings` instance on first attribute access. *Why it matters:* you must import `from django.conf import settings`, never the settings module directly.\n- **`global_settings`** — Django's defaults for every setting; your module only overrides what it changes. `manage.py diffsettings` prints the differences.\n- **Environment variables / twelve-factor config** — keeping secrets and per-environment values outside the code, read at startup. *Why it matters:* no secrets in git; the same image runs everywhere.\n- **`SECRET_KEY` and `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS`** — the key used for signing (sessions with signed cookies, password reset tokens, `signing`, messages). Fallbacks (4.1+) let you rotate keys without invalidating every signed value at once.\n- **`manage.py` / `django-admin`** — command-line utilities; `manage.py` is `django-admin` with `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE` preset and the project on `sys.path`.\n- **Management command** — a `BaseCommand` subclass in `<app>/management/commands/<name>.py`, run as `python manage.py <name>`. *Why it matters:* the standard way to script operational jobs with the ORM available.\n- **System checks** — the framework (`manage.py check`, `check --deploy`) that validates models, settings and security configuration; runs automatically before most commands.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 Project vs app, precisely\n\nDjango's docs define it crisply: *an app is a web application that does something; a project is a collection of configuration and apps for a particular website.* A project can contain many apps; an app can be in many projects. `django.contrib.auth` is an app; so is `rest_framework`; so is your `orders` package.\n\nWhat makes a package an app is **not** its folder structure but its presence in `INSTALLED_APPS`. Once listed, Django will:\n\n- import its `models` module and register every model class with the registry;\n- include its `migrations/` in the migration graph;\n- search its `templates/` directory (when `APP_DIRS=True`) and `static/` directory (via `AppDirectoriesFinder`);\n- expose its `management/commands/*.py` as commands;\n- autodiscover its `admin.py` (through `django.contrib.admin`'s `ready()`);\n- call its `AppConfig.ready()`.\n\n### 3.2 How the app registry boots: `apps.populate()`\n\n`django.setup()` configures logging from `LOGGING`, sets the URL script prefix, and calls `apps.populate(settings.INSTALLED_APPS)`. Populate runs in three strict phases, and each phase completes for **all** apps before the next begins:\n\n1. **Import app configs.** For each entry, import the package (or the dotted `AppConfig` path), find its `AppConfig` — the single subclass in `apps.py`, the one marked `default = True` if there are several, or a plain `AppConfig` otherwise — and instantiate it. Labels must be unique, or you get `ImproperlyConfigured: Application labels aren't unique`. After this phase `apps.apps_ready` is true.\n2. **Import models.** For each app config, import its `models` module. Defining a model class runs the `ModelBase` metaclass, which calls `apps.register_model()`. After this phase `apps.models_ready` is true and relations between models (string references such as `\"orders.Order\"`) can be resolved.\n3. **Run `ready()`.** Call each `AppConfig.ready()` in `INSTALLED_APPS` order. Then `apps.ready` is true.\n\nIf code tries to use models during phase 1 or 2 — for example a module-level `get_user_model()` call in an app's `__init__.py`, or importing `models` from another app's `apps.py` at module level — Django raises `AppRegistryNotReady: Apps aren't loaded yet.` (or `Models aren't loaded yet.`). The fix is always the same: move the import inside a function or into `ready()`.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 380\" width=\"100%\" height=\"380\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj02a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">django.setup() and apps.populate(): three phases, each over ALL apps</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">django.setup()</text>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">read settings (lazy load)</text>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">configure LOGGING</text>\n  <path d=\"M200,79 L236,79\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj02a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"240\" y=\"44\" width=\"190\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"335\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Phase 1: app configs</text>\n  <text x=\"252\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">import each package</text>\n  <text x=\"252\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">find AppConfig in apps.py</text>\n  <text x=\"252\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">check labels are unique</text>\n  <text x=\"252\" y=\"148\" fill=\"#1d4ed8\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">apps_ready = True</text>\n  <text x=\"252\" y=\"172\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"9\">models not usable yet</text>\n  <path d=\"M430,119 L466,119\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj02a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"470\" y=\"44\" width=\"190\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"565\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Phase 2: models</text>\n  <text x=\"482\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">import app.models</text>\n  <text x=\"482\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">ModelBase metaclass runs</text>\n  <text x=\"482\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">apps.register_model()</text>\n  <text x=\"482\" y=\"148\" fill=\"#6d28d9\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">models_ready = True</text>\n  <text x=\"482\" y=\"172\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">lazy relations resolved</text>\n  <path d=\"M660,119 L696,119\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj02a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"700\" y=\"44\" width=\"160\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"780\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Phase 3: ready()</text>\n  <text x=\"712\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">AppConfig.ready() in</text>\n  <text x=\"712\" y=\"104\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">INSTALLED_APPS order</text>\n  <text x=\"712\" y=\"122\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">connect signals, checks</text>\n  <text x=\"712\" y=\"148\" fill=\"#15803d\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">apps.ready = True</text>\n  <text x=\"712\" y=\"172\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">no DB queries here</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"220\" width=\"840\" height=\"140\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"242\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Where AppRegistryNotReady comes from</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"266\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">orders/__init__.py:   from .models import Order      runs in phase 1, before models are ready</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"286\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">accounts/apps.py:     User = get_user_model()       at module level, phase 1</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"306\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">script.py:            from orders.models import Order   without django.setup() first</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"332\" fill=\"#15803d\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fix: import inside functions or ready(); standalone scripts call django.setup() or use a management command</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.3 How settings load: `LazySettings`\n\n`django/conf/__init__.py` defines `settings = LazySettings()`. `LazySettings` is a `LazyObject`: until something reads an attribute, it holds nothing. On the first access (`settings.DEBUG`), `_setup()` reads `os.environ[\"DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE\"]` — raising `ImproperlyConfigured: Requested setting DEBUG, but settings are not configured` if it is missing — and constructs `Settings(module_name)`:\n\n1. Copy every UPPERCASE name from `django.conf.global_settings` (the defaults).\n2. `importlib.import_module(module_name)` — your settings file executes now, top to bottom.\n3. Copy every UPPERCASE name from your module, overriding the defaults. Lowercase names are ignored, which is why helper variables like `env = environ.Env()` don't leak into `settings`.\n4. Validate a few things (for example tuple/list types for some settings) and remember which settings were explicitly set (used by `diffsettings`).\n\nTwo consequences matter in interviews. First, **importing your settings module directly** (`from config import settings`) bypasses this: you skip defaults, you bypass `override_settings` in tests, and you can import the wrong environment's module. Always use `from django.conf import settings`. Second, settings are read **once per process**. Changing an environment variable after boot does nothing until workers restart.\n\nFor scripts without a settings module (rare — mostly libraries' test suites), `settings.configure(DEBUG=True, ...)` populates settings directly, followed by `django.setup()`.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 330\" width=\"100%\" height=\"330\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj02b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">From environment to settings.DEBUG: layered, lazy, once per process</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"46\" width=\"200\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Process environment</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">config.settings.prod</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">DATABASE_URL, SECRET_KEY ...</text>\n  <rect x=\"260\" y=\"46\" width=\"200\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">first settings.X access</text>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">LazySettings._setup()</text>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Settings(module_name)</text>\n  <path d=\"M220,83 L256,83\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj02b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"500\" y=\"46\" width=\"360\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"680\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">django.conf.settings (the proxy you import)</text>\n  <text x=\"680\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">UPPERCASE names only; cached for the process lifetime</text>\n  <text x=\"680\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">override_settings swaps the wrapped object in tests</text>\n  <path d=\"M460,83 L496,83\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj02b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Layers merged inside Settings (later wins)</text>\n  <rect x=\"60\" y=\"164\" width=\"240\" height=\"140\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"186\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. global_settings</text>\n  <text x=\"76\" y=\"208\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">DEBUG = False</text>\n  <text x=\"76\" y=\"226\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">ALLOWED_HOSTS = []</text>\n  <text x=\"76\" y=\"244\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">SESSION_COOKIE_AGE = 1209600</text>\n  <text x=\"76\" y=\"262\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">every setting has a default</text>\n  <rect x=\"320\" y=\"164\" width=\"240\" height=\"140\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"186\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. settings/base.py</text>\n  <text x=\"336\" y=\"208\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">INSTALLED_APPS, MIDDLEWARE</text>\n  <text x=\"336\" y=\"226\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">TEMPLATES, AUTH_USER_MODEL</text>\n  <text x=\"336\" y=\"244\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">shared by every environment</text>\n  <rect x=\"580\" y=\"164\" width=\"240\" height=\"140\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"700\" y=\"186\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">3. settings/prod.py</text>\n  <text x=\"596\" y=\"208\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">from .base import *</text>\n  <text x=\"596\" y=\"226\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">SECRET_KEY = env(\"SECRET_KEY\")</text>\n  <text x=\"596\" y=\"244\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">DATABASES from DATABASE_URL</text>\n  <text x=\"596\" y=\"262\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">HTTPS, HSTS, cookie flags</text>\n  <path d=\"M300,234 L316,234\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj02b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M560,234 L576,234\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj02b)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.4 What `manage.py` actually does\n\n`manage.py` is five meaningful lines: set a default `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE`, then `execute_from_command_line(sys.argv)`. That creates a `ManagementUtility`, which:\n\n1. parses `--settings` and `--pythonpath` early (so they apply before setup);\n2. calls `django.setup()`;\n3. builds the command map with `get_commands()` — Django's built-in commands, then each installed app's `management/commands/` scanned **in reverse `INSTALLED_APPS` order**, so an app listed earlier overrides a same-named command from an app listed later (this is how `django.contrib.staticfiles` overrides `runserver`);\n4. loads the command class and calls `run_from_argv()` → `execute()` → system checks (unless `requires_system_checks = []`) → your `handle()`.\n\n`django-admin` is the same utility without the preset settings module, which is why `django-admin migrate` fails with \"settings are not configured\" unless you pass `--settings` or export the variable.\n\n### 3.5 Order in `INSTALLED_APPS` is behaviour\n\nBecause the registry and finders walk `INSTALLED_APPS` in order:\n\n- **Templates:** the `app_directories` loader returns the *first* matching template, so putting your app above `django.contrib.admin` lets you override `admin/base_site.html`.\n- **Static files:** `AppDirectoriesFinder` also takes the first match.\n- **Commands:** first-listed app wins (see above).\n- **`ready()`:** runs in order, so an app relying on another's `ready()` side effects must come after it.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 Recommended layout for a real project\n\n```text\nrepo/\n├── manage.py\n├── pyproject.toml\n├── config/                     # the \"project\" package: configuration only\n│   ├── __init__.py\n│   ├── settings/\n│   │   ├── __init__.py\n│   │   ├── base.py             # shared, no secrets\n│   │   ├── dev.py              # DEBUG, debug toolbar, console email\n│   │   ├── test.py             # fast hasher, in-memory email\n│   │   └── prod.py             # security headers, real services\n│   ├── urls.py                 # ROOT_URLCONF\n│   ├── wsgi.py\n│   └── asgi.py\n└── apps/                       # domain apps, one per bounded area\n    ├── accounts/               # custom user model lives here (day one)\n    ├── catalog/\n    ├── orders/\n    │   ├── apps.py\n    │   ├── models.py\n    │   ├── services.py         # business logic, called by views/commands/tasks\n    │   ├── views.py\n    │   ├── urls.py\n    │   ├── admin.py\n    │   ├── signals.py\n    │   ├── migrations/\n    │   ├── management/commands/expire_unpaid_orders.py\n    │   ├── templates/orders/...\n    │   └── tests/\n    └── core/                   # shared utilities, base models, middleware\n```\n\nTwo layouts are common for settings: **a settings package split by environment** (shown above, selected by `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE`) or **a single settings module driven entirely by environment variables** (twelve-factor style, `DEBUG=env.bool(\"DEBUG\", False)`). The split works well when environments differ structurally (dev adds the debug toolbar to `INSTALLED_APPS`); the single file works well when every difference is a value. Many teams combine them: `base.py` reads env vars for values, and `dev.py`/`prod.py` hold only structural differences.\n\n### 4.2 Startup to first request\n\n```text\ngunicorn config.wsgi:application\n   │\n   ├─ import config.wsgi\n   │     os.environ.setdefault(\"DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE\", \"config.settings.prod\")\n   │     get_wsgi_application()\n   │        ├─ django.setup()\n   │        │     ├─ settings._setup()          -> import config.settings.prod (reads env vars)\n   │        │     ├─ logging.config.dictConfig(LOGGING)\n   │        │     └─ apps.populate(INSTALLED_APPS)\n   │        │           ├─ phase 1: AppConfigs\n   │        │           ├─ phase 2: models\n   │        │           └─ phase 3: ready()  -> signals connected, admin autodiscovered\n   │        └─ WSGIHandler()  -> load_middleware()\n   └─ fork workers (or with --preload: load once, then fork)\n```\n\nWith Gunicorn's `--preload`, the app is imported in the master and workers are forked from it — faster boot and shared memory pages, but database connections or threads opened during import would be shared by forked children, which is another reason not to touch the database in `ready()`.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example: a project with one app\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\"\ndjango-admin startproject config .      # trailing dot: no extra nesting\npython manage.py startapp blog\n```\n\n```python\n# blog/apps.py (generated)\nfrom django.apps import AppConfig\n\nclass BlogConfig(AppConfig):\n    default_auto_field = \"django.db.models.BigAutoField\"\n    name = \"blog\"\n```\n\nAdd the app to the project. Listing the package name is enough since Django 3.2; the single `AppConfig` subclass in `apps.py` is picked up automatically.\n\n```python\n# config/settings.py (excerpt)\nINSTALLED_APPS = [\n    \"django.contrib.admin\",\n    \"django.contrib.auth\",\n    \"django.contrib.contenttypes\",\n    \"django.contrib.sessions\",\n    \"django.contrib.messages\",\n    \"django.contrib.staticfiles\",\n    \"blog\",                       # or \"blog.apps.BlogConfig\"\n]\nDEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD = \"django.db.models.BigAutoField\"\n```\n\nCheck what you changed relative to Django's defaults:\n\n```bash\npython manage.py diffsettings          # settings that differ from global_settings; \"###\" marks names Django has no default for\npython manage.py check                 # system checks\npython manage.py shell -c \"from django.apps import apps; print([a.label for a in apps.get_app_configs()])\"\n```\n\n```text\n['admin', 'auth', 'contenttypes', 'sessions', 'messages', 'staticfiles', 'blog']\n```\n\n### 5.2 Registering signals in `ready()`\n\n```python\n# orders/apps.py\nfrom django.apps import AppConfig\n\nclass OrdersConfig(AppConfig):\n    default_auto_field = \"django.db.models.BigAutoField\"\n    name = \"apps.orders\"\n    label = \"orders\"              # keeps table names orders_order, and \"orders.Order\" references\n    verbose_name = \"Orders\"\n\n    def ready(self):\n        # Imported for side effects: @receiver decorators connect handlers.\n        from . import signals  # noqa: F401\n```\n\n```python\n# orders/signals.py\nfrom django.db.models.signals import post_save\nfrom django.dispatch import receiver\nfrom .models import Order\n\n@receiver(post_save, sender=Order, dispatch_uid=\"orders.order_created_audit\")\ndef audit_order_created(sender, instance, created, **kwargs):\n    if created:\n        ...\n```\n\n`dispatch_uid` makes the connection idempotent: Django's docs note `ready()` may run more than once in some test setups, and without a unique ID you would get duplicate receivers. Signals in depth are in [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals).\n\n### 5.3 Real-world example: environment-driven settings\n\nThis settings package keeps secrets out of git, fails fast when a required variable is missing, and keeps per-environment differences small. It uses `django-environ`, but `python-decouple` or plain `os.environ` work the same way.\n\n```python\n# config/settings/base.py\nfrom pathlib import Path\nimport environ\n\nBASE_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent.parent\nenv = environ.Env()\nenviron.Env.read_env(BASE_DIR / \".env\", overwrite=False)   # dev convenience; real envs set vars\n\nSECRET_KEY = env(\"SECRET_KEY\")                       # no default: crash if missing\nSECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS = env.list(\"SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS\", default=[])\nDEBUG = env.bool(\"DEBUG\", default=False)            # default to the safe value\nALLOWED_HOSTS = env.list(\"ALLOWED_HOSTS\", default=[])\n\nINSTALLED_APPS = [\n    \"django.contrib.admin\",\n    \"django.contrib.auth\",\n    \"django.contrib.contenttypes\",\n    \"django.contrib.sessions\",\n    \"django.contrib.messages\",\n    \"django.contrib.staticfiles\",\n    \"apps.accounts\",\n    \"apps.catalog\",\n    \"apps.orders\",\n    \"apps.core\",\n]\nAUTH_USER_MODEL = \"accounts.User\"                    # set before the first migrate\n\nDATABASES = {\"default\": env.db(\"DATABASE_URL\")}     # postgres://user:pass@host:5432/db\nDATABASES[\"default\"][\"CONN_MAX_AGE\"] = env.int(\"DB_CONN_MAX_AGE\", default=60)\nDATABASES[\"default\"][\"CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS\"] = True\n\nCACHES = {\n    \"default\": {\n        \"BACKEND\": \"django.core.cache.backends.redis.RedisCache\",\n        \"LOCATION\": env(\"REDIS_URL\", default=\"redis://localhost:6379/0\"),\n    }\n}\n\nROOT_URLCONF = \"config.urls\"\nWSGI_APPLICATION = \"config.wsgi.application\"\nDEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD = \"django.db.models.BigAutoField\"\nSTATIC_URL = \"static/\"\nSTATIC_ROOT = BASE_DIR / \"staticfiles\"\nTIME_ZONE = \"UTC\"\nUSE_TZ = True\n```\n\n```python\n# config/settings/dev.py\nfrom .base import *  # noqa: F401,F403\n\nDEBUG = True\nALLOWED_HOSTS = [\"localhost\", \"127.0.0.1\"]\nINSTALLED_APPS += [\"debug_toolbar\"]\nMIDDLEWARE = [\"debug_toolbar.middleware.DebugToolbarMiddleware\", *MIDDLEWARE]\nINTERNAL_IPS = [\"127.0.0.1\"]\nEMAIL_BACKEND = \"django.core.mail.backends.console.EmailBackend\"\n```\n\n```python\n# config/settings/prod.py\nfrom .base import *  # noqa: F401,F403\n\nDEBUG = False\nSECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER = (\"HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO\", \"https\")   # behind a TLS-terminating proxy\nSECURE_SSL_REDIRECT = True\nSECURE_HSTS_SECONDS = 60 * 60 * 24 * 365\nSECURE_HSTS_INCLUDE_SUBDOMAINS = True\nSESSION_COOKIE_SECURE = True\nCSRF_COOKIE_SECURE = True\nCSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS = env.list(\"CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS\")          # e.g. https://app.example.com\nSTORAGES = {\n    \"default\": {\"BACKEND\": \"storages.backends.s3.S3Storage\"},\n    \"staticfiles\": {\"BACKEND\": \"whitenoise.storage.CompressedManifestStaticFilesStorage\"},\n}\n```\n\n```python\n# config/settings/test.py\nfrom .base import *  # noqa: F401,F403\n\nPASSWORD_HASHERS = [\"django.contrib.auth.hashers.MD5PasswordHasher\"]  # fast tests only\nEMAIL_BACKEND = \"django.core.mail.backends.locmem.EmailBackend\"\nCACHES = {\"default\": {\"BACKEND\": \"django.core.cache.backends.locmem.LocMemCache\"}}\n```\n\nSelect the environment explicitly at every entry point:\n\n```bash\nexport DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=config.settings.prod\npython manage.py check --deploy          # flags missing HSTS, insecure cookies, DEBUG, weak SECRET_KEY\ngunicorn config.wsgi:application\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** `MIDDLEWARE` and `INSTALLED_APPS` from `base` are lists; `+=` in `dev.py` mutates the same list object imported from `base`. That is harmless when only one settings module is ever imported per process, but rebuild with `[*X, ...]` if anything else imports `base` too.\n\n### 5.4 Real-world example: a production management command\n\nA nightly job cancels orders that were never paid. It must be safe to rerun, report progress, support a dry run and process in batches so it never holds a long transaction or loads a million rows into memory.\n\n```python\n# apps/orders/management/commands/expire_unpaid_orders.py\nfrom datetime import timedelta\n\nfrom django.core.management.base import BaseCommand, CommandError\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.utils import timezone\n\nfrom apps.orders.models import Order\n\n\nclass Command(BaseCommand):\n    help = \"Cancel orders that stayed PENDING longer than --hours.\"\n\n    def add_arguments(self, parser):\n        parser.add_argument(\"--hours\", type=int, default=24)\n        parser.add_argument(\"--batch-size\", type=int, default=500)\n        parser.add_argument(\"--dry-run\", action=\"store_true\")\n\n    def handle(self, *args, hours, batch_size, dry_run, **options):\n        if hours < 1:\n            raise CommandError(\"--hours must be at least 1\")      # exit code 1, clean message\n\n        cutoff = timezone.now() - timedelta(hours=hours)\n        stale = Order.objects.filter(status=Order.Status.PENDING, created_at__lt=cutoff)\n        total = stale.count()\n        self.stdout.write(f\"{total} stale orders older than {cutoff:%Y-%m-%d %H:%M}\")\n        if dry_run or total == 0:\n            return\n\n        cancelled = 0\n        while True:\n            with transaction.atomic():\n                ids = list(\n                    stale.order_by(\"pk\")\n                    .select_for_update(skip_locked=True)          # don't fight a concurrent payment\n                    .values_list(\"pk\", flat=True)[:batch_size]\n                )\n                if not ids:\n                    break\n                cancelled += Order.objects.filter(\n                    pk__in=ids, status=Order.Status.PENDING      # re-check: idempotent\n                ).update(status=Order.Status.CANCELLED, updated_at=timezone.now())\n            self.stdout.write(f\"  cancelled {cancelled}/{total}\")\n\n        self.stdout.write(self.style.SUCCESS(f\"Done: {cancelled} orders cancelled\"))\n```\n\n```bash\npython manage.py expire_unpaid_orders --hours 48 --dry-run\npython manage.py expire_unpaid_orders --hours 48 --batch-size 1000\n```\n\nThe `update()` call issues one statement per batch; the `status` re-check in the `WHERE` clause makes a rerun harmless:\n\n```sql\nUPDATE \"orders_order\"\nSET \"status\" = 'cancelled', \"updated_at\" = '2026-10-25T02:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz\nWHERE (\"orders_order\".\"id\" IN (101, 102, 103, ...) AND \"orders_order\".\"status\" = 'pending');\n```\n\nNote that `update()` bypasses `save()` and does not send `post_save`; if other code relies on that signal, call a service function instead (see [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization)). Test the command with `call_command`:\n\n```python\nfrom io import StringIO\nfrom django.core.management import call_command\nfrom django.test import TestCase\n\nclass ExpireUnpaidOrdersTests(TestCase):\n    def test_dry_run_changes_nothing(self):\n        out = StringIO()\n        call_command(\"expire_unpaid_orders\", \"--dry-run\", stdout=out)\n        self.assertIn(\"stale orders\", out.getvalue())\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Choice | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| Many small apps | Clear ownership, smaller migrations, reusable | Cross-app FKs and circular imports if boundaries are wrong |\n| One big app | No boundary decisions, simple imports | Huge files, one migration stream, hard to split later |\n| Settings split per environment | Structural differences are explicit | Drift between files; `import *` hides where a value came from |\n| Single env-driven settings | One file, same code everywhere (twelve-factor) | Many env vars to manage; structural differences need conditionals |\n| `.env` file in dev | Convenient | Easy to commit by accident; never use in production images |\n| Secrets manager (Vault, AWS SM) | Rotation, audit, no plaintext on disk | More infrastructure; startup depends on it |\n| Management command | Full Django env, testable with `call_command` | Runs in its own process; long jobs need batching and locking |\n| Celery task for the same job | Retries, scheduling, distribution | More moving parts; see [Ch 31](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks) |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Create a new app when a feature area owns its own models and could be reasoned about alone (accounts, billing, catalogue).\n- Use environment variables for every value that differs between deploys or is secret.\n- Use a management command for operator-run or cron-scheduled jobs that need the ORM.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Don't create an app per model or per view; you get dozens of apps with tangled FKs.\n- Don't put business logic in `settings.py` or query the database there or in `ready()`.\n- Don't use management commands for work triggered by user requests — enqueue a task instead.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**Committing `SECRET_KEY` and credentials.** The `startproject` template generates an insecure key in `settings.py`. Once in git history it is compromised; anyone with it can forge signed cookies and password-reset tokens. Read it from the environment, give it no default, and rotate it using `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS` (old key in fallbacks, new key as `SECRET_KEY`, remove the fallback after sessions and tokens expire).\n\n**`DEBUG` defaulting to `True`.**\n\n```python\n# Before: one missing env var and production shows tracebacks\nDEBUG = os.environ.get(\"DEBUG\", True)          # also: any non-empty string is truthy!\n\n# After\nDEBUG = env.bool(\"DEBUG\", default=False)\n```\n\n**Importing the settings module directly.** `from config.settings import base` gives you the raw module, not Django's merged, overridable settings. Use `from django.conf import settings`.\n\n**Querying the database in `ready()` or at import time.** It runs during `migrate` before tables exist, in every test process, and in every Gunicorn worker on boot. Django 5.0+ emits a `RuntimeWarning` when the database is accessed during app initialization. Load lazily on first use instead.\n\n**Changing `AUTH_USER_MODEL` later.** It must be set before the first `migrate`; changing it afterwards means rewriting migrations and data. Create a custom user model on day one — see [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user).\n\n**Renaming an app's label casually.** The label is baked into table names (`orders_order`), content types, permissions and migration dependencies. Renaming requires careful migrations; set `label` explicitly when the package path changes but you want to keep the label.\n\n**Circular imports between apps.** `orders.models` imports `catalog.models` which imports `orders.models`. Use string references (`models.ForeignKey(\"catalog.Product\", ...)`) and `apps.get_model` inside functions.\n\n**Best practices**\n\n- One config package (`config/`), domain apps under `apps/`, a `core` app for shared base classes.\n- Fail fast: required settings have no defaults; run `check --deploy` in CI against production settings.\n- Keep `ready()` small: import signal modules, register checks, nothing else.\n- Make management commands idempotent, batched, with `--dry-run`, and test them with `call_command`.\n- Never print secrets; `diffsettings` output and debug pages can leak them (Django masks names containing `KEY`, `SECRET`, `PASS`, `TOKEN` and similar on debug pages, but not in your own logs).\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: wrong settings module in production.** *Symptom:* debug toolbar appears in production, or `DisallowedHost` errors. *Root cause:* the container's `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE` was unset, so `manage.py`'s `setdefault` picked `config.settings.dev`. *Fix:* make the default the *safe* one (prod) or have no default and fail; log the active module at startup.\n\n**Pitfall: settings changed but nothing happened.** Settings are read once per process. After changing an env var or a ConfigMap you must restart or roll the workers. A feature flag that must change without restart belongs in the database or cache, not in settings.\n\n**Pitfall: `AppRegistryNotReady` in a Celery worker or script.** A standalone script imports models without calling `django.setup()`. *Fix:* set `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE` and call `django.setup()` before importing models (Celery's Django integration does this in `celery.py`), or turn the script into a management command.\n\n**Pitfall: cron job runs twice.** Two pods each run the scheduled command, or a job overlaps the previous run. *Fix:* use a Kubernetes CronJob with `concurrencyPolicy: Forbid`, a database advisory lock, or `select_for_update(skip_locked=True)` batches as above, and make the work idempotent.\n\n**Pitfall: memory blow-up in a command.** `for order in Order.objects.all():` caches every row in the QuerySet. *Fix:* `iterator(chunk_size=2000)` or keyset batching by primary key.\n\n**Debugging**\n\n- `python manage.py diffsettings --all` to see the effective value of every setting; `python manage.py shell -c \"from django.conf import settings; print(settings.SETTINGS_MODULE)\"`.\n- `python manage.py check --deploy --settings=config.settings.prod` in CI.\n- `python -X importtime manage.py check` to find slow imports that hurt boot and test start-up.\n- For `ready()` issues, set a breakpoint in `django/apps/registry.py:populate` and watch which app fails.\n\n**Scaling notes.** Large codebases (50+ apps) benefit from explicit app boundaries: a service layer per app, cross-app calls through functions not model internals, and import-linter rules to forbid cycles. Boot time matters at scale: every worker and every test process pays for imports, so heavy optional imports should happen lazily inside functions.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between a Django project and a Django app?**\nA: A project is the configuration container for one site: `settings`, the root URLconf and the WSGI/ASGI entry points, plus `manage.py`. An app is a Python package that provides a feature — models, views, templates, static files, admin, commands — and is activated by listing it in `INSTALLED_APPS`. A project contains many apps, and a well-designed app can be reused across projects, the way `django.contrib.auth` or `rest_framework` are. What makes a package an app is its presence in `INSTALLED_APPS`, not its folder layout.\n\n**Q: What does `INSTALLED_APPS` do, and does its order matter?**\nA: It tells Django which apps to load into the app registry, so their models are registered, migrations are included, templates and static files are discovered, admin modules are autodiscovered and management commands are available. Order matters: template and static finders return the first match, so an app listed earlier can override another's templates; the first-listed app wins for same-named management commands; and `ready()` runs in that order. A common example is placing your app above `django.contrib.admin` to override admin templates.\n\n**Q: What is `AppConfig.ready()` used for, and what should you never do in it?**\nA: `ready()` runs once per process after all models are loaded, so it is the safe place to connect signal receivers (usually by importing a `signals` module), register system checks, or wire up anything that needs models. You should not run database queries there: it executes during `migrate` before tables may exist, in every test run and in every worker on boot, and recent Django versions warn about it. It can also run more than once in some test setups, so registrations should be idempotent (`dispatch_uid`).\n\n**Q: How do you manage settings for different environments?**\nA: Keep a shared base module and either environment-specific modules selected by `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE` (`config.settings.dev`, `.prod`, `.test`), or a single module that reads every varying value from environment variables, or a combination. Secrets and per-deploy values come from the environment or a secrets manager, with safe defaults (`DEBUG=False`) and no defaults for required secrets so a misconfiguration fails at boot. Validate production configuration with `manage.py check --deploy` in CI.\n\n**Q: Why should you import `from django.conf import settings` rather than your settings module?**\nA: `django.conf.settings` is a lazy proxy that merges Django's `global_settings` defaults with your module and respects `override_settings` in tests and the `--settings` flag. Importing your module directly gives you only what that file defines, may load a different environment's file than the one active, and bypasses test overrides. It also couples code to a project path, which breaks reusable apps.\n\n**Q: What causes `AppRegistryNotReady` and how do you fix it?**\nA: It means code tried to use models or the registry before `apps.populate()` finished — usually a model import in an app's `__init__.py` or at module level in `apps.py`, calling `get_user_model()` at import time, or a standalone script that imports models without calling `django.setup()`. The fix is to delay the import: inside a function, inside `ready()`, or use string model references such as `\"orders.Order\"`. Scripts should set `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE` and call `django.setup()`, or become management commands.\n\n**Q: How do you write a custom management command?**\nA: Create `<app>/management/commands/<name>.py` (with `__init__.py` files in `management` and `commands`) containing `class Command(BaseCommand)`. Declare options in `add_arguments(parser)` using argparse, implement `handle(self, *args, **options)`, write output with `self.stdout.write` and `self.style.SUCCESS`, and raise `CommandError` for user errors. Test it with `django.core.management.call_command`. For production jobs, add `--dry-run`, batch the work, and make it idempotent.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `manage.py` and `django-admin`?**\nA: They run the same `ManagementUtility`. `manage.py` sets `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE` to your project's settings by default and puts the project directory on `sys.path`, so commands work without extra flags. `django-admin` needs `--settings` or the environment variable for anything that requires settings; it is mostly used for `startproject`.\n\n**Q: How should secrets like `SECRET_KEY` and database passwords be handled, and what is `SECRET_KEY` used for?**\nA: Read them from environment variables or a secrets manager at startup, never commit them, and give required secrets no default so a missing value crashes the deploy instead of silently using an insecure key. `SECRET_KEY` is used for cryptographic signing — signed cookies and the signed-cookie session backend, password-reset tokens, the messages cookie, and `django.core.signing` — not for password hashing. Leaking it lets an attacker forge those signed values.\n\n**Q: How do you rotate `SECRET_KEY` without logging everyone out? (Senior)**\nA: Since Django 4.1, set the new key as `SECRET_KEY` and put the old one in `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS`. Signing uses the new key, while verification also accepts values signed with fallback keys, so existing sessions, password-reset links and signed cookies keep working. After the longest-lived signed artifact expires — for example `SESSION_COOKIE_AGE` and `PASSWORD_RESET_TIMEOUT` — remove the old key from the fallbacks. If the key was leaked, you skip the grace period and accept forced logouts, because keeping a compromised key as a fallback keeps the hole open.\n\n**Q: How would you structure a large Django codebase with 40+ apps and several teams? (Senior)**\nA: Organize apps around business capabilities rather than technical layers, each owning its models, migrations, services, URLs and tests. Enforce boundaries: other apps call a public service module, not another app's models internals; use string FK references to avoid import cycles; and enforce the dependency graph with a tool like import-linter in CI. Keep the config package thin, put shared base models and middleware in a `core` app, and avoid generic \"utils\" apps that everything depends on. Watch boot time and migration graph complexity, and squash migrations periodically.\n\n**Q: A nightly management command occasionally processes records twice and sometimes runs out of memory. How do you fix it? (Senior)**\nA: The double processing usually comes from overlapping runs (a slow run still going when the next starts, or two pods running the same cron) combined with non-idempotent work. I'd prevent overlap with a CronJob `concurrencyPolicy: Forbid` or a PostgreSQL advisory lock, claim rows inside `transaction.atomic()` with `select_for_update(skip_locked=True)`, and make updates conditional (`WHERE status = 'pending'`) so retries are harmless. The memory problem is the QuerySet result cache on a full table scan; I'd batch by primary key or use `.iterator(chunk_size=...)` and `values_list` for IDs. Finally I'd add metrics and a dry-run mode so operators can see what a run will do.\n\n**Q: What is `DEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD` and why does it matter?**\nA: It sets the primary-key type for models that don't declare one. New projects since Django 3.2 use `BigAutoField` (64-bit), which avoids exhausting a 32-bit integer key on high-volume tables. Each `AppConfig` can override it with `default_auto_field`. Changing it on an existing project generates migrations that alter primary keys and every foreign key referencing them, which can lock large tables, so decide early.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Thing | What to remember |\n|---|---|\n| `startproject config .` | config package + `manage.py`; trailing dot avoids nesting |\n| `startapp orders` | app skeleton with `apps.py`, `models.py`, `migrations/` |\n| `INSTALLED_APPS` | activates apps; order affects template/static/command overrides |\n| `apps.populate` | configs, then models, then `ready()` |\n| `AppConfig` | `name`, `label`, `verbose_name`, `default_auto_field`, `ready()` |\n| `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE` | which module `LazySettings` imports on first access |\n| `global_settings` | defaults; `diffsettings` shows your overrides |\n| `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS` | rotate keys without invalidating signatures (4.1+) |\n| `check --deploy` | security checklist for production settings |\n| Custom command | `management/commands/x.py`, `BaseCommand`, `add_arguments`, `handle`, `CommandError` |\n| `call_command` | run commands from code and tests |\n\n- A package is an app because it is in `INSTALLED_APPS`.\n- Settings are lazy, merged with defaults, and read once per process.\n- Always `from django.conf import settings`.\n- Required secrets have no default; `DEBUG` defaults to `False`.\n- `AppRegistryNotReady` means you imported models too early.\n- `ready()`: connect signals, no queries, idempotent.\n- Set `AUTH_USER_MODEL` before the first migrate.\n- Management commands: batched, idempotent, `--dry-run`, tested with `call_command`.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" django-environ && django-admin startproject config . && python manage.py startapp blog`.\n\n1. **Split the settings.** Convert `config/settings.py` into `config/settings/{base,dev,prod}.py`. Make `SECRET_KEY` required from the environment and confirm `python manage.py check` fails with a clear error when it is unset.\n2. **Watch populate.** Add `print()` calls to `blog/__init__.py`, `blog/apps.py` (module level and inside `ready()`) and `blog/models.py`. Run `python manage.py check` and explain the order. Then add `from .models import *` to `blog/__init__.py` and reproduce `AppRegistryNotReady`.\n3. **Template override by order.** Create `blog/templates/admin/base_site.html` that changes the admin title. Confirm it applies only when `blog` is listed above `django.contrib.admin`.\n4. **`diffsettings` and `check --deploy`.** Run both against `prod.py` and fix every warning `check --deploy` reports.\n5. **Override a command.** Create a `blog` command named `check_links` and a second app with the same command name; verify which one runs depending on `INSTALLED_APPS` order.\n\n**Mini project — Ops command suite.** In a `shop` app with an `Order` model, write `expire_unpaid_orders` (as in §5.4) and `export_orders --since YYYY-MM-DD --out file.csv` that streams rows with `iterator()` and `values_list`. Add `--dry-run`, progress output, `CommandError` on bad input, and tests using `call_command` that assert counts and idempotency (running twice changes nothing the second time).\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle), [Ch 03 · URL Routing & URLconf](topic.html?p=03-urls-routing) (`ROOT_URLCONF`), [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations) (per-app migration graphs), [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals) (receivers in `ready()`), [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user) (`AUTH_USER_MODEL`), [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security) (`check --deploy`, HTTPS settings), [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production), [Ch 36 · Django Internals](topic.html?p=36-django-internals) (`LazySettings`, registry).\n\n**Other handbooks:** [Docker · Environment & Configuration](../docker/topic.html?p=15-env-config), [Docker · Secrets](../docker/topic.html?p=24-secrets), [Kubernetes · ConfigMaps & Secrets](../kubernetes/topic.html?p=05-configmaps-secrets), [Kubernetes · Jobs & CronJobs](../kubernetes/topic.html?p=08-jobs-cronjobs).\n\n- **Applications** — Django docs · *Beginner* · `AppConfig`, the registry and the initialization process explained officially. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/applications/>\n- **Settings** — Django docs · *Beginner* · how settings load, `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE`, `settings.configure()`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/settings/>\n- **Settings reference** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · every setting and its default. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/settings/>\n- **How to create custom django-admin commands** — Django docs · *Beginner* · `BaseCommand`, arguments, output, testing. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/custom-management-commands/>\n- **Deployment checklist** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · the settings to review before going live. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/deployment/checklist/>\n- **The Twelve-Factor App: Config** — 12factor.net · *Beginner* · why configuration belongs in the environment. <https://12factor.net/config>\n- **django-environ** — GitHub · *Beginner* · typed env vars and database/cache URLs for settings. <https://github.com/joke2k/django-environ>\n- **django/apps/registry.py** — GitHub · *Advanced* · read `Apps.populate` to see the three phases in code. <https://github.com/django/django/blob/stable/5.2.x/django/apps/registry.py>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 02.*\n", "03-urls-routing": "# 03 · URL Routing & URLconf\n\n> **In one line:** A URLconf is an ordered list of patterns that Django walks top to bottom — descending into `include()`d URLconfs by prefix — until the first one matches, converts the captured values, and hands them to a view; naming every route and using `reverse()` with namespaces is what lets you change URLs without breaking links.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle) (where resolution happens in `_get_response`) and [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings) (`ROOT_URLCONF` and per-app modules).\n\nEvery web framework has to answer one question for every request: *which code handles this path?* The **naive approach** is a chain of `if path == \"/orders/\": ... elif path.startswith(\"/orders/\"): ...` inside one function. It works for five endpoints and collapses at fifty: parameter parsing is repeated and inconsistent (`int(path.split(\"/\")[2])` that crashes on `/orders/abc/`), there is no way to generate a URL from its name so templates hard-code strings, and two teams editing the same function step on each other.\n\nDjango separates routing into a declarative **URLconf**: a Python module with a `urlpatterns` list. Each entry maps a route to a view, optionally captures typed parameters with **path converters**, and has a **name** used to build URLs in reverse. Apps own their own URLconfs, which the project mounts with `include()` under a prefix and optionally a **namespace**, so `orders:detail` and `blog:detail` never collide.\n\nInterviewers ask about routing because it reveals whether you understand three subtle things: **order** (first match wins, so a greedy pattern can shadow later ones), **reversing** (`reverse()` and `{% url %}` instead of hard-coded paths, and why `reverse_lazy` exists), and **what happens when nothing matches** (the 404 path, `APPEND_SLASH` redirects, custom `handler404`).\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **URLconf** — a module defining `urlpatterns`; the root one is `settings.ROOT_URLCONF`, overridable per request by setting `request.urlconf` in middleware. *Why it matters:* it is the routing table for the whole site.\n- **`path(route, view, kwargs=None, name=None)`** — matches a route string with optional `<converter:name>` segments. Routes have no leading slash. *Why it matters:* the default, readable way to declare routes.\n- **`re_path(regex, view, ...)`** — matches a Python regular expression; named groups become kwargs (as strings). *Why it matters:* for patterns converters can't express, and legacy URLs.\n- **Path converter** — a class with `regex`, `to_python()` and `to_url()`. Built-ins: `str` (default; any non-empty text without `/`), `int` (zero or positive integers, converted to `int`), `slug`, `uuid`, `path` (any non-empty text, including `/`). *Why it matters:* validation and type conversion at the routing layer.\n- **`register_converter(cls, \"name\")`** — makes a custom converter usable in `path()`.\n- **`include(module_or_list, namespace=None)`** — mounts another URLconf under a prefix. *Why it matters:* apps own their routes; the project composes them.\n- **URL name** — the `name=` argument, used by `reverse(\"name\")`, `{% url \"name\" %}` and `redirect(\"name\")`.\n- **Application namespace (`app_name`)** — declared in an app's `urls.py`; prefixes every name (`orders:detail`).\n- **Instance namespace (`include(..., namespace=\"...\")`)** — distinguishes multiple mounts of the same app (two admin sites, an API mounted twice).\n- **`reverse()` / `reverse_lazy()`** — build a path from a name and arguments; `reverse_lazy` defers until the string is used, for module- or class-level attributes evaluated at import time.\n- **`ResolverMatch`** — the result of resolution, stored on `request.resolver_match`: `func`, `args`, `kwargs`, `url_name`, `route`, `namespaces`, `view_name`.\n- **`Resolver404` / `NoReverseMatch`** — raised when a path matches nothing, or a name/arguments combination can't be reversed.\n- **`APPEND_SLASH`** — when `True` (default) and a path without a trailing slash doesn't match but the slashed version does, `CommonMiddleware` issues a permanent redirect.\n- **`handler404`, `handler500`, `handler403`, `handler400`** — root-URLconf variables naming custom error views.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 How `path()` compiles\n\n`path(\"orders/<int:pk>/\", views.detail, name=\"detail\")` builds a `URLPattern` whose pattern is a `RoutePattern`. On first use, the route string is translated into a regex by replacing each `<converter:name>` with a named group using the converter's `regex`:\n\n```text\n\"orders/<int:pk>/\"   ->   ^orders/(?P<pk>[0-9]+)/\\Z\n\"blog/<slug:slug>/\"  ->   ^blog/(?P<slug>[-a-zA-Z0-9_]+)/\\Z\n\"files/<path:rest>\"  ->   ^files/(?P<rest>.+)\\Z\n```\n\nWhen the regex matches, each captured string is passed through the converter's `to_python()`. If `to_python()` raises `ValueError`, Django treats the pattern as **not matching** and moves on to the next one — so a custom converter can reject values (an unknown year, an invalid checksum) and let another pattern try.\n\n`re_path()` builds a `RegexPattern` from your regex directly. Named groups become string kwargs; if there are no named groups, unnamed groups are passed as positional args (mixing them is not supported — named groups win).\n\n### 3.2 How resolution walks the tree\n\nThe root URLconf becomes a `URLResolver` (cached per URLconf by `get_resolver()`). `URLResolver.resolve(path)`:\n\n1. Matches its own prefix (the root's is `^/`), strips it, and keeps the remainder.\n2. Iterates `url_patterns` **in order**. For a `URLPattern`, it tries the full-match regex on the remainder. For a nested `URLResolver` (an `include()`), it tries the include's prefix; only if the prefix matches does it recurse with the rest of the path.\n3. Returns a `ResolverMatch` for the **first** match, merging captured kwargs from every level (`include` prefixes can capture too) plus any extra `kwargs` dict passed in `path()`.\n4. If nothing matches, raises `Resolver404` carrying the list of tried patterns — that list is what the yellow debug 404 page shows when `DEBUG=True`.\n\nBecause includes are pruned by prefix, a request for `/api/orders/42/` never evaluates the hundreds of patterns under `/admin/`. But *within* a level, matching is linear and order-sensitive.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 420\" width=\"100%\" height=\"420\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj03a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj03b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#94a3b8\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Resolving GET /api/orders/42/ : ordered, prefix-pruned, first match wins</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"230\" height=\"350\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"135\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ROOT_URLCONF (config/urls.py)</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"82\" width=\"198\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">1. \"admin/\"  include(admin.urls)</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"114\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"9\">prefix fails: subtree skipped</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"132\" width=\"198\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">2. \"\" (home view)</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"164\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"9\">full match fails on \"api/orders/42/\"</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"182\" width=\"198\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">3. \"api/\"  include(api.urls)</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"214\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"9\">prefix matches: recurse on \"orders/42/\"</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"232\" width=\"198\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"250\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"10\">4. \"&lt;path:page&gt;\" catch-all</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"264\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"9\">never tried: earlier match won</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"300\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Nothing matches at any level:</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"316\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Resolver404 with \"tried\" list</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"332\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">DEBUG: technical 404 page</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"348\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">else: handler404 view</text>\n  <path d=\"M234,202 L296,202\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj03a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"300\" y=\"44\" width=\"260\" height=\"250\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">api/urls.py  (app_name = \"api\")</text>\n  <rect x=\"316\" y=\"82\" width=\"228\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"326\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">a. \"orders/\"  name=\"order-list\"</text>\n  <text x=\"326\" y=\"114\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"9\">full match fails (extra \"42/\")</text>\n  <rect x=\"316\" y=\"132\" width=\"228\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"326\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">b. \"orders/new/\"</text>\n  <text x=\"326\" y=\"164\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"9\">literal \"new\" does not match \"42\"</text>\n  <rect x=\"316\" y=\"182\" width=\"228\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"326\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">c. \"orders/&lt;int:pk&gt;/\"</text>\n  <text x=\"326\" y=\"214\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"9\">regex ^orders/(?P&lt;pk&gt;[0-9]+)/\\Z matches</text>\n  <text x=\"326\" y=\"226\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"9\">to_python(\"42\") gives 42</text>\n  <text x=\"316\" y=\"260\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Put \"orders/new/\" BEFORE &lt;str:slug&gt;</text>\n  <text x=\"316\" y=\"276\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">patterns, or \"new\" is captured as a slug</text>\n  <path d=\"M544,207 L596,207\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj03a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"600\" y=\"140\" width=\"260\" height=\"154\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"730\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ResolverMatch</text>\n  <text x=\"614\" y=\"184\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">func       = order_detail</text>\n  <text x=\"614\" y=\"202\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">kwargs     = {\"pk\": 42}</text>\n  <text x=\"614\" y=\"220\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">url_name   = \"order-detail\"</text>\n  <text x=\"614\" y=\"238\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">namespaces = [\"api\"]</text>\n  <text x=\"614\" y=\"256\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">view_name  = \"api:order-detail\"</text>\n  <text x=\"614\" y=\"274\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">route      = \"api/orders/&lt;int:pk&gt;/\"</text>\n  <rect x=\"600\" y=\"310\" width=\"260\" height=\"84\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"730\" y=\"330\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Then</text>\n  <text x=\"614\" y=\"350\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">request.resolver_match = match</text>\n  <text x=\"614\" y=\"368\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">process_view(...) hooks</text>\n  <text x=\"614\" y=\"386\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">order_detail(request, pk=42)</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.3 How `reverse()` works\n\nReversing is the inverse lookup: from a view name and arguments to a path. The resolver lazily builds a **reverse dictionary** (`_populate()`) mapping each name — and each view callable — to its possible patterns, including the prefixes of every enclosing `include()`. `reverse(\"api:order-detail\", kwargs={\"pk\": 42})`:\n\n1. Splits the name on `:` into namespaces and the final name.\n2. Resolves each namespace: if it is an application namespace with several instances, it picks the instance from `current_app` (or `request.current_app` / `request.resolver_match.namespace` in templates), falling back to the **default instance** — the one whose instance namespace equals the app name — and then to the last deployed instance.\n3. Finds candidate patterns for the name, and for each, checks that the supplied args/kwargs fit the pattern's parameters. Each value is converted with the converter's `to_url()` and must match the converter's regex.\n4. Returns the first candidate that fits, with the script prefix (`FORCE_SCRIPT_NAME` or the WSGI `SCRIPT_NAME`) prepended and the result percent-quoted. If none fits: `NoReverseMatch`, whose message lists the patterns tried.\n\n> **Version note:** Django 5.2 added `query` and `fragment` keyword arguments to `reverse()` and `reverse_lazy()`, so `reverse(\"search\", query={\"q\": \"django\"})` yields `/search/?q=django`. On 4.2 you append `\"?\" + urlencode(...)` yourself.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 360\" width=\"100%\" height=\"360\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj03c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Namespaces: app_name (application) vs include(namespace=...) (instance)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"260\" height=\"140\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">config/urls.py</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">path(\"api/v1/\", include(\"api.urls\",</text>\n  <text x=\"54\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">namespace=\"v1\"))</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">path(\"api/v2/\", include(\"api.urls\",</text>\n  <text x=\"54\" y=\"146\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">namespace=\"v2\"))</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">same app mounted twice, two instances</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"200\" width=\"260\" height=\"140\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">api/urls.py</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"246\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">app_name = \"api\"</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"266\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">urlpatterns = [</text>\n  <text x=\"44\" y=\"284\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">path(\"orders/&lt;int:pk&gt;/\", ...,</text>\n  <text x=\"64\" y=\"300\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">name=\"order-detail\"),</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"318\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">]</text>\n  <rect x=\"320\" y=\"44\" width=\"540\" height=\"296\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"590\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">How a name is reversed</text>\n  <rect x=\"336\" y=\"80\" width=\"508\" height=\"52\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"348\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">reverse(\"v2:order-detail\", kwargs={\"pk\": 7})</text>\n  <text x=\"348\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">instance namespace named explicitly: /api/v2/orders/7/</text>\n  <rect x=\"336\" y=\"142\" width=\"508\" height=\"70\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"348\" y=\"162\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">reverse(\"api:order-detail\", kwargs={\"pk\": 7}, current_app=\"v1\")</text>\n  <text x=\"348\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">application namespace + current instance: /api/v1/orders/7/</text>\n  <text x=\"348\" y=\"198\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">in templates, current_app comes from request.resolver_match.namespace</text>\n  <rect x=\"336\" y=\"222\" width=\"508\" height=\"52\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"348\" y=\"242\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">reverse(\"api:order-detail\", ...) with no current app</text>\n  <text x=\"348\" y=\"260\" fill=\"#92400e\" font-size=\"10\">default instance (namespace == \"api\") if any, else the LAST deployed one</text>\n  <rect x=\"336\" y=\"284\" width=\"508\" height=\"46\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"348\" y=\"304\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">reverse(\"order-detail\")  without namespace</text>\n  <text x=\"348\" y=\"320\" fill=\"#b91c1c\" font-size=\"10\">NoReverseMatch: names inside an app_name are only reachable namespaced</text>\n  <path d=\"M280,110 L332,106\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj03c)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.4 The 404 path and `APPEND_SLASH`\n\nWhen resolution fails, `Resolver404` propagates up and is converted into a 404 response — the technical page with the tried patterns when `DEBUG=True`, otherwise your `handler404` (default `django.views.defaults.page_not_found`, which renders `404.html`).\n\n`CommonMiddleware` then inspects 404 responses on the way out. If `APPEND_SLASH=True`, the path doesn't end in `/`, and the path *with* a slash would resolve, it replaces the 404 with a **301** redirect to the slashed URL (preserving the query string). It cannot preserve a POST body across a redirect, so with `DEBUG=True` it raises a `RuntimeError` explaining the problem for POST/PUT/PATCH requests instead of silently redirecting and losing data. This is why API clients that post to `/api/orders` (no slash) suddenly \"lose\" their payload in production: they get a 301, follow it as a GET, and hit the list endpoint.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 Project URLconf composed from app URLconfs\n\n```text\nconfig/urls.py  (ROOT_URLCONF)\n├── \"\"             -> pages.urls        app_name=\"pages\"\n├── \"accounts/\"    -> django.contrib.auth.urls   (login, logout, password_reset...)\n├── \"shop/\"        -> shop.urls         app_name=\"shop\"\n│                    ├── \"\"                         name=\"product-list\"\n│                    ├── \"p/<slug:slug>/\"           name=\"product-detail\"\n│                    └── \"cart/\"                    name=\"cart\"\n├── \"api/v1/\"      -> api.urls          namespace=\"v1\"  (app_name=\"api\")\n├── \"admin/\"       -> admin.site.urls   (namespace \"admin\")\n└── handler404 = \"pages.views.not_found\"\n```\n\nEach app knows only its own relative routes. The project decides where it is mounted. Moving the shop from `/shop/` to `/store/` is a one-line change, and every `{% url \"shop:product-detail\" slug=p.slug %}` keeps working.\n\n### 4.2 Request-time and template-time flows\n\n```text\nREQUEST TIME                                   RENDER / REDIRECT TIME\nGET /shop/p/blue-mug/                          {% url \"shop:product-detail\" slug=p.slug %}\n  -> get_resolver(ROOT_URLCONF) (cached)          -> reverse(\"shop:product-detail\",\n  -> \"shop/\" prefix ok, recurse \"p/blue-mug/\"            kwargs={\"slug\": \"blue-mug\"},\n  -> \"p/<slug:slug>/\" matches                            current_app=request namespace)\n  -> ResolverMatch(func=product_detail,           -> to_url(\"blue-mug\") checked vs slug regex\n                   kwargs={\"slug\": \"blue-mug\"})   -> \"/shop/p/blue-mug/\"\n  -> product_detail(request, slug=\"blue-mug\")  redirect(\"shop:cart\") -> 302 Location: /shop/cart/\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example: an app URLconf with converters and names\n\n```python\n# shop/urls.py\nfrom django.urls import path\nfrom . import views\n\napp_name = \"shop\"\n\nurlpatterns = [\n    path(\"\", views.product_list, name=\"product-list\"),\n    path(\"p/new/\", views.product_create, name=\"product-create\"),          # before the slug route\n    path(\"p/<slug:slug>/\", views.product_detail, name=\"product-detail\"),\n    path(\"orders/<int:pk>/\", views.order_detail, name=\"order-detail\"),\n    path(\"orders/<uuid:token>/receipt/\", views.receipt, name=\"receipt\"),\n    path(\"docs/<path:page>\", views.docs, name=\"docs\"),                    # page may contain \"/\"\n]\n```\n\n```python\n# config/urls.py\nfrom django.contrib import admin\nfrom django.urls import include, path\n\nurlpatterns = [\n    path(\"admin/\", admin.site.urls),\n    path(\"shop/\", include(\"shop.urls\")),         # namespace defaults to app_name \"shop\"\n]\n```\n\nThe view receives converted values, not strings:\n\n```python\n# shop/views.py\nfrom django.shortcuts import get_object_or_404, redirect, render\nfrom .models import Order, Product\n\ndef order_detail(request, pk: int):\n    order = get_object_or_404(Order, pk=pk, user=request.user)\n    return render(request, \"shop/order_detail.html\", {\"order\": order})\n\ndef product_create(request):\n    ...\n    product = Product.objects.create(...)\n    return redirect(\"shop:product-detail\", slug=product.slug)     # 302 to /shop/p/<slug>/\n```\n\nReverse from Python, templates and models:\n\n```python\nfrom django.urls import reverse\nreverse(\"shop:order-detail\", kwargs={\"pk\": 42})            # '/shop/orders/42/'\nreverse(\"shop:docs\", args=[\"guides/setup\"])                 # '/shop/docs/guides/setup'\nreverse(\"shop:product-list\", query={\"page\": 2})             # '/shop/?page=2'  (Django 5.2+)\n```\n\n```html\n<a href=\"{% url 'shop:product-detail' slug=product.slug %}\">{{ product.name }}</a>\n{% url 'shop:order-detail' pk=order.pk as order_url %}\n<a href=\"{{ order_url }}\">View order</a>\n```\n\n```python\n# shop/models.py\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    slug = models.SlugField(unique=True)\n    ...\n    def get_absolute_url(self):\n        return reverse(\"shop:product-detail\", kwargs={\"slug\": self.slug})\n```\n\n`get_absolute_url()` is used by the admin's \"View on site\" link, by `redirect(product)` (passing a model instance) and by generic views' default `success_url`, so defining it once removes URL-building from views.\n\nExplore resolution in the shell:\n\n```python\n>>> from django.urls import resolve\n>>> m = resolve(\"/shop/orders/42/\")\n>>> m.func.__name__, m.kwargs, m.view_name, m.route\n('order_detail', {'pk': 42}, 'shop:order-detail', 'shop/orders/<int:pk>/')\n>>> resolve(\"/shop/orders/abc/\")\nTraceback (most recent call last):\ndjango.urls.exceptions.Resolver404: {'tried': [...], 'path': 'shop/orders/abc/'}\n```\n\n### 5.2 Custom converter\n\nA converter for four-digit years, and one for comma-separated ID lists used in a bulk endpoint:\n\n```python\n# core/converters.py\nclass YearConverter:\n    regex = r\"[0-9]{4}\"\n\n    def to_python(self, value: str) -> int:\n        year = int(value)\n        if not 1990 <= year <= 2100:\n            raise ValueError(\"year out of range\")       # -> this pattern doesn't match; try the next\n        return year\n\n    def to_url(self, value: int) -> str:\n        if not 1990 <= int(value) <= 2100:\n            raise ValueError(\"year out of range\")       # -> NoReverseMatch\n        return f\"{int(value):04d}\"\n\n\nclass IdListConverter:\n    regex = r\"[0-9]+(?:,[0-9]+){0,99}\"                  # cap at 100 IDs in the URL itself\n\n    def to_python(self, value: str) -> list[int]:\n        return [int(x) for x in value.split(\",\")]\n\n    def to_url(self, value) -> str:\n        return \",\".join(str(int(x)) for x in value)\n```\n\n```python\n# blog/urls.py\nfrom django.urls import path, register_converter\nfrom core.converters import IdListConverter, YearConverter\nfrom . import views\n\nregister_converter(YearConverter, \"yyyy\")\nregister_converter(IdListConverter, \"ids\")\n\napp_name = \"blog\"\nurlpatterns = [\n    path(\"archive/<yyyy:year>/\", views.archive, name=\"archive\"),\n    path(\"posts/<ids:ids>/\", views.posts_bulk, name=\"posts-bulk\"),\n]\n```\n\n```python\nreverse(\"blog:archive\", kwargs={\"year\": 2026})           # '/blog/archive/2026/'\nreverse(\"blog:posts-bulk\", kwargs={\"ids\": [3, 5, 8]})   # '/blog/posts/3,5,8/'\nreverse(\"blog:archive\", kwargs={\"year\": 1800})          # NoReverseMatch\n```\n\n`re_path` is still the right tool for legacy URLs you must keep alive:\n\n```python\nfrom django.urls import re_path\nfrom django.views.generic import RedirectView\n\nurlpatterns += [\n    # old PHP-era links: /article.php?id=12 is handled by the query string; path part only here\n    re_path(r\"^archive/(?P<year>[0-9]{4})/(?P<month>0[1-9]|1[0-2])/$\", views.month_archive),\n    re_path(r\"^old-shop/(?P<rest>.*)$\",\n            RedirectView.as_view(url=\"/shop/%(rest)s\", permanent=True)),\n]\n```\n\n### 5.3 Real-world example: a versioned, namespaced project URLconf\n\n```python\n# config/urls.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.contrib import admin\nfrom django.urls import include, path\nfrom django.views.generic import TemplateView\n\nadmin.site.site_header = \"Acme Ops\"\n\napi_v1 = [\n    path(\"orders/\", include(\"orders.api_urls\")),       # app_name = \"orders-api\"\n    path(\"catalog/\", include(\"catalog.api_urls\")),\n]\n\nurlpatterns = [\n    path(\"\", TemplateView.as_view(template_name=\"home.html\"), name=\"home\"),\n    path(\"accounts/\", include(\"django.contrib.auth.urls\")),\n    path(\"shop/\", include(\"shop.urls\")),\n    path(\"api/v1/\", include((api_v1, \"api\"), namespace=\"v1\")),  # (patterns, app_name) tuple\n    path(\"healthz/\", include(\"core.health_urls\")),\n    path(\"ops-7f3a/\", admin.site.urls),                         # obscure admin path\n]\n\nif settings.DEBUG:\n    import debug_toolbar\n    from django.conf.urls.static import static\n    urlpatterns = [path(\"__debug__/\", include(debug_toolbar.urls)), *urlpatterns]\n    urlpatterns += static(settings.MEDIA_URL, document_root=settings.MEDIA_ROOT)\n\nhandler404 = \"core.views.not_found\"\nhandler500 = \"core.views.server_error\"\n```\n\n```python\n# core/views.py\nfrom django.shortcuts import render\n\ndef not_found(request, exception):\n    return render(request, \"errors/404.html\", {\"path\": request.path}, status=404)\n\ndef server_error(request):\n    # Keep it dependency-free: no DB queries, no context processors that could fail again.\n    return render(request, \"errors/500.html\", status=500)\n```\n\nThings to notice:\n\n- `include((api_v1, \"api\"), namespace=\"v1\")` passes an inline list with an application namespace; names become `v1:orders-api:list` style chains when the included URLconfs declare their own `app_name`.\n- The admin is moved off `/admin/` to reduce automated brute-force noise (security by obscurity is not a control, but it cuts log spam).\n- `handler404` takes `(request, exception)`; `handler500` takes only `(request)`. These are only used when `DEBUG=False`.\n- `static()` only works when `DEBUG=True`; it returns an empty list otherwise. Media in production is served by Nginx, a CDN or S3 — see [Ch 20 · Static & Media Files](topic.html?p=20-static-media-files).\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Option | Pros | Cons | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `path()` + built-in converters | Readable, typed, validated | Limited expressiveness | Almost everything |\n| Custom converter | Reusable validation and typing, works both ways | Must keep `regex`/`to_python`/`to_url` consistent | Domain IDs, dates, encoded lists |\n| `re_path()` | Full regex power | Harder to read; captures are strings; easy to forget anchors | Legacy URLs, complex constraints |\n| `include()` per app | Apps own routes; prefix pruning | Namespace setup needed | All multi-app projects |\n| Instance namespaces | Same app mounted twice | `current_app` subtleties | Multiple admin sites, API versions |\n| Hard-coded URLs in templates | Nothing to learn | Breaks on every URL change | Never |\n| DRF routers | Generate CRUD routes for viewsets | Less explicit | APIs; see [Ch 22](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers) |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Use `path()` with converters by default, a custom converter whenever the same parameter shape appears in several routes, and `re_path()` only for the leftovers.\n- Use `app_name` in every app URLconf and refer to routes as `app:name` everywhere.\n- Use `reverse_lazy()` for URLs in class attributes, settings (`LOGIN_URL` can be a name) and module-level constants.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Don't do routing in views (`if kwargs[\"action\"] == \"delete\": ...`) — give each action its own route and view.\n- Don't put validation that needs the database in a converter (e.g. \"does this product exist?\"); converters run for every request that reaches that pattern and cannot return a 404 cleanly. Do lookups in the view.\n- Don't encode large or sensitive data in paths; URLs end up in logs, browser history and `Referer` headers.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**A greedy pattern shadows later ones.**\n\n```python\n# Before: \"new\" is captured as a slug; product_create is unreachable\nurlpatterns = [\n    path(\"p/<slug:slug>/\", views.product_detail),\n    path(\"p/new/\", views.product_create),\n]\n\n# After: specific routes first\nurlpatterns = [\n    path(\"p/new/\", views.product_create),\n    path(\"p/<slug:slug>/\", views.product_detail),\n]\n```\n\nThe same applies to a catch-all `<path:page>` route for a CMS: it must be **last**.\n\n**Leading slash in a route.** `path(\"/orders/\", ...)` never matches, because Django strips the leading slash before matching. `manage.py check` warns (`urls.W002`).\n\n**`reverse()` at import time.**\n\n```python\n# Before: raises ImproperlyConfigured / circular import, because URLconfs aren't loaded yet\nclass OrderCreate(CreateView):\n    success_url = reverse(\"shop:order-list\")\n\n# After\nclass OrderCreate(CreateView):\n    success_url = reverse_lazy(\"shop:order-list\")\n```\n\n**Namespace without `app_name`.** `include(\"shop.urls\", namespace=\"shop\")` when `shop/urls.py` has no `app_name` raises `ImproperlyConfigured: Specifying a namespace in include() without providing an app_name is not supported`. Set `app_name` in the module or pass a `(patterns, app_name)` tuple.\n\n**Forgetting `$` / `\\Z` in `re_path`.** `re_path(r\"^orders/(?P<pk>\\d+)/\", ...)` without an end anchor also matches `/orders/42/anything/else`. `path()` routes are always fully matched; regexes aren't unless you anchor them.\n\n**Using `\\d` in regexes and expecting ASCII digits.** In Python 3 `\\d` matches any Unicode digit (Arabic-Indic, Devanagari...). `int()` will accept many of those, but they produce surprising URLs. Prefer `[0-9]`, which is what the built-in `int` converter uses.\n\n**Trailing-slash mismatch for APIs.** Clients posting to `/api/orders` get a 301 and lose the body. Pick one convention (DRF routers default to trailing slashes; `DefaultRouter(trailing_slash=False)` drops them) and make clients follow it; consider `APPEND_SLASH=False` for pure APIs so they get a clear 404.\n\n**Best practices**\n\n- Name every route; never hard-code paths in templates, views, emails or tests.\n- Keep URLs nouns (`/orders/42/`) and actions as HTTP methods where possible.\n- Define `get_absolute_url()` on models with a canonical page.\n- Test routing explicitly: `self.assertEqual(resolve(\"/shop/\").func, views.product_list)` and `reverse()` round-trips.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: POST data lost after deploy.** *Symptom:* a form or webhook \"succeeds\" but nothing is created; logs show a 301 followed by a GET. *Root cause:* the client posts to a URL without a trailing slash; `CommonMiddleware` redirects; the client follows with GET. *Fix:* fix the URL at the client, or add a route without a slash for that webhook, or disable `APPEND_SLASH` for the API.\n\n**Pitfall: 500s from `NoReverseMatch` after a refactor.** A renamed route or a changed parameter breaks a `{% url %}` buried in a rarely visited template; only users find it. *Fix:* render every template in tests, grep for old names, and keep old names alive (or add redirects) during migrations.\n\n**Pitfall: broken URLs behind a path prefix.** The app is deployed at `https://example.com/app/` but generated links point to `/orders/`. *Root cause:* the proxy strips the prefix without setting `SCRIPT_NAME`. *Fix:* pass `SCRIPT_NAME` from the proxy or set `FORCE_SCRIPT_NAME=\"/app\"` (and adjust `STATIC_URL`).\n\n**Pitfall: 404 page that crashes.** A custom `handler404` template extends a base template that queries the database or uses `request.user`; if the DB is the reason for the error (for 500s) you get a second error. Keep `500.html` static and simple; Django renders `handler500` with a minimal context for this reason.\n\n**Pitfall: per-tenant URLconfs.** Setting `request.urlconf` in middleware for multi-tenant routing works, but every distinct URLconf gets its own cached resolver; generating them dynamically per tenant leaks memory. Use a small fixed set of URLconfs.\n\n**Debugging**\n\n- `python manage.py show_urls` (from `django-extensions`) lists every route, name and view.\n- `resolve(path)` and `reverse(name, kwargs=...)` in `manage.py shell`.\n- The DEBUG 404 page lists every pattern tried in order — the fastest way to spot shadowing.\n- Log `request.resolver_match.route` in access logs and metrics; group latency by route, not by path.\n\n**Scaling notes.** Resolution cost is tiny compared to a database query, but it is linear in the number of patterns at each level. Very large flat URLconfs (thousands of generated patterns, common with naive per-tenant or per-language generation) show up in profiles; group them with `include()` prefixes so whole subtrees are skipped. Resolvers are cached per process, so the first request after a deploy pays the population cost for `reverse()`.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `path()` and `re_path()`?**\nA: `path()` uses a simple route syntax with converters like `<int:pk>` and `<slug:slug>`; it is always matched against the whole remaining path and converts captured values to Python types. `re_path()` takes a regular expression; named groups become keyword arguments but are passed as strings, and you must anchor the pattern yourself. Use `path()` by default and `re_path()` for patterns converters can't express or legacy URLs.\n\n**Q: In what order are URL patterns matched, and what happens if two match?**\nA: Django walks `urlpatterns` top to bottom and uses the first pattern that matches; later patterns are never tried. For an `include()`, only its prefix is checked first, and if it matches Django recurses into the included list. So more specific routes such as `p/new/` must come before generic ones such as `p/<slug:slug>/`, and catch-alls must be last. If nothing matches anywhere, a `Resolver404` becomes a 404 response.\n\n**Q: What are path converters and how do you write a custom one?**\nA: A converter defines how a URL segment is matched and converted: a `regex` class attribute, `to_python(value)` to convert the matched string for the view, and `to_url(value)` to convert a Python value back when reversing. Built-ins are `str`, `int`, `slug`, `uuid` and `path`. Register a custom one with `register_converter(MyConverter, \"name\")` and use `<name:param>`. Raising `ValueError` in `to_python` means \"this pattern doesn't match\", and in `to_url` it causes `NoReverseMatch`.\n\n**Q: Why use `reverse()` or `{% url %}` instead of hard-coding URLs?**\nA: Names decouple the code from the URL structure: you can change `/shop/` to `/store/` or alter a pattern in one place, and every link, redirect and test that uses the name keeps working. `reverse()` also applies converters' `to_url`, quoting and the script prefix, so links are correct behind a sub-path deployment. Hard-coded strings break silently and only fail when a user clicks them.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `reverse()` and `reverse_lazy()`?**\nA: `reverse()` computes the URL immediately, which requires the URLconf to be loaded. `reverse_lazy()` returns a lazy object evaluated when converted to a string. You need the lazy version wherever the expression is evaluated at import time — class attributes like `success_url` on generic views, module-level constants, decorators' arguments — because URLconfs import views, so reversing while views are being imported causes circular-import or improperly-configured errors.\n\n**Q: What are URL namespaces and what is the difference between `app_name` and `namespace`?**\nA: Namespaces prevent name collisions between apps: with `app_name = \"shop\"` in `shop/urls.py`, routes are referenced as `shop:detail`. `app_name` defines the application namespace, shared by every deployment of that app. The `namespace=` argument of `include()` defines an instance namespace, which distinguishes multiple mounts of the same app — for example two admin sites or `v1`/`v2` of an API. When reversing an application namespace, Django uses `current_app` to pick the instance, else the default instance, else the last one deployed.\n\n**Q: What does `APPEND_SLASH` do and when does it cause problems?**\nA: With `APPEND_SLASH=True` (the default) and `CommonMiddleware` installed, a request for a path without a trailing slash that doesn't match, but would match with one, is redirected with a 301 to the slashed URL. The problem is non-GET requests: a redirect can't carry a POST body, so clients following it send a GET and the data is lost — in DEBUG mode Django raises a `RuntimeError` to warn you. APIs often choose a strict convention, disable it, or use routers without trailing slashes.\n\n**Q: How do you customize the 404 and 500 pages?**\nA: With `DEBUG=False`, Django renders `404.html` and `500.html` from your templates by default. For custom logic, set `handler404 = \"app.views.not_found\"` (signature `(request, exception)`) and `handler500` (signature `(request)`) in the root URLconf. Keep the 500 page simple and free of database access, because it may be rendered while the database is the cause of the failure. With `DEBUG=True` you see the technical pages instead, so test custom handlers with `DEBUG=False`.\n\n**Q: How do you pass extra data to a view from the URLconf?**\nA: Captured parameters become keyword arguments, and `path()` accepts a third argument, a dict of extra kwargs, such as `path(\"feed/\", views.feed, {\"format\": \"rss\"})`. Extra kwargs override captured ones with the same name. You can also pass kwargs to `include()`, which applies them to every pattern in the included URLconf. The view reads them as normal function arguments, and they appear in `request.resolver_match.extra_kwargs`.\n\n**Q: How would you design URLs for a versioned public API with Django? (Senior)**\nA: I'd put the version in the URL prefix (`/api/v1/`) for clarity and cacheability, mounting the same or a forked set of app URLconfs with instance namespaces (`v1`, `v2`), or use DRF's `NamespaceVersioning` so `request.version` comes from the namespace. Shared views branch on `request.version` only for small differences, and genuinely different contracts get separate serializers. I'd pick one trailing-slash convention, name every route, document deprecation with headers, and route by `resolver_match.route` in metrics to watch traffic per version before removing one. Header-based versioning is an alternative, but URL versions are easier to debug, cache and share as links.\n\n**Q: A pattern you just added never gets called; requests go to a different view. How do you debug it? (Senior)**\nA: That is almost always ordering or prefix shadowing. I'd call `resolve(\"/the/path/\")` in the shell to see which view and `route` actually match, and look at the DEBUG 404 or `show_urls` output for the full ordered list. Typical culprits are a generic converter earlier in the same list (`<slug:slug>` before `new/`), an `include()` prefix earlier in the root URLconf that captures the path, a catch-all CMS route that isn't last, or a missing end anchor on a `re_path`. I'd fix the order and add a test that asserts `resolve(path).func` so the regression can't recur.\n\n**Q: How would you implement multi-tenant routing where each tenant has its own subdomain and some tenant-specific URLs? (Senior)**\nA: A middleware resolves the tenant from the `Host` header (validated against `ALLOWED_HOSTS` and a tenant table, cached), attaches it to `request.tenant`, and can set `request.urlconf` to one of a small fixed set of URLconfs, such as `public_urls` versus `tenant_urls`. Resolvers are cached per URLconf module, so I avoid generating a URLconf per tenant. Tenant-specific behaviour lives in views reading `request.tenant`, not in per-tenant routes. `reverse()` uses the request's URLconf when called with `urlconf=request.urlconf`, and absolute URLs for emails must include the tenant's host, built with `request.build_absolute_uri()` or a helper.\n\n**Q: What does `request.resolver_match` contain and how is it useful?**\nA: It is the `ResolverMatch` produced by URL resolution: the view `func`, `args`, `kwargs`, `url_name`, `app_names`, `namespaces`, `view_name` (the namespaced name), and `route` (the matched route pattern). It is useful for highlighting the active menu item in templates, for picking the current namespace when reversing, and especially for per-route metrics and logging, because `route` groups `/orders/1/` and `/orders/2/` together.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Tool | Syntax | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| Route | `path(\"orders/<int:pk>/\", view, name=\"detail\")` | no leading slash; full match |\n| Regex | `re_path(r\"^x/(?P<y>[0-9]{4})/$\", view)` | anchor it; strings captured |\n| Converters | `str int slug uuid path` | `path` includes `/` |\n| Custom | `regex`, `to_python`, `to_url`; `register_converter` | `ValueError` = no match / `NoReverseMatch` |\n| Include | `path(\"shop/\", include(\"shop.urls\"))` | prefix pruned |\n| Namespaces | `app_name = \"shop\"`; `include(..., namespace=\"v1\")` | `shop:detail` |\n| Reverse | `reverse(\"shop:detail\", kwargs={\"pk\": 1})` | 5.2: `query=`, `fragment=` |\n| Lazy | `reverse_lazy(...)` | class attrs, import time |\n| Template | `{% url \"shop:detail\" pk=o.pk %}` | `as var` form |\n| Redirect | `redirect(\"shop:detail\", pk=1)` / `redirect(obj)` | 302; `permanent=True` for 301 |\n| Errors | `handler404(request, exception)`, `handler500(request)` | only when `DEBUG=False` |\n\n- First match wins; specific before generic; catch-all last.\n- `include()` checks its prefix before descending.\n- Every route gets a name; every app gets an `app_name`.\n- `reverse_lazy` for anything evaluated at import time.\n- `APPEND_SLASH` redirects lose POST bodies.\n- `Http404`/`Resolver404` become 404; `DoesNotExist` does not.\n- Use `resolver_match.route` for metrics.\n- Test routing with `resolve()` and `reverse()`.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" && django-admin startproject config . && python manage.py startapp shop`.\n\n1. **Shadowing.** Add `p/<slug:slug>/` above `p/new/`, request `/shop/p/new/`, and read the DEBUG 404 or the view that answers. Fix the order and add a test with `resolve()`.\n2. **Custom converter.** Implement the `YearConverter` from §5.2. Show that `/blog/archive/1800/` falls through to the next pattern (or 404) and that `reverse()` with 1800 raises `NoReverseMatch`.\n3. **Namespaces.** Mount `shop.urls` twice (`shop/` with `namespace=\"shop\"` and `outlet/` with `namespace=\"outlet\"`). Render a template that uses `{% url \"shop:product-list\" %}` from each mount and observe which instance is chosen with and without `current_app`.\n4. **APPEND_SLASH.** With `DEBUG=True`, POST to `/shop/cart` (no slash) using `curl -X POST` and read the `RuntimeError`. Set `DEBUG=False` and observe the 301.\n5. **Error handlers.** Add `handler404`/`handler500` views, set `DEBUG=False` and `ALLOWED_HOSTS=[\"localhost\"]`, and trigger both.\n\n**Mini project — Link-safe blog.** Build a `blog` app with list, detail (`<yyyy:year>/<slug:slug>/`), tag (`tag/<slug:tag>/`), and archive routes, all named under `app_name=\"blog\"`. Give `Post` a `get_absolute_url()`, use only `{% url %}` and `reverse()` for links, add a legacy `re_path` redirect from `/posts/<id>/` to the canonical URL, and write tests that reverse every route and resolve every path back to the right view.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle), [Ch 04 · Views, HttpRequest & HttpResponse](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses) (what the resolved view receives), [Ch 05 · Django Template Language](topic.html?p=05-templates) (`{% url %}`), [Ch 17 · Class-Based & Generic Views](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views) (`as_view()` in URLconfs), [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) (`CommonMiddleware`, `request.urlconf`), [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers), [Ch 24 · DRF Pagination, Filtering, Versioning & Docs](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning).\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · Resource Modeling & URI Design](../rest-api/topic.html?p=05-resource-modeling-uri-design), [REST API · Naming Conventions](../rest-api/topic.html?p=09-naming-conventions), [REST API · API Versioning](../rest-api/topic.html?p=31-api-versioning).\n\n- **URL dispatcher** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the complete guide: converters, namespaces, reversing, error handlers. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/http/urls/>\n- **django.urls functions** — Django docs · *Beginner* · reference for `path`, `re_path`, `include`, `register_converter`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/urls/>\n- **django.urls utility functions** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `reverse`, `reverse_lazy`, `resolve`, `ResolverMatch`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/urlresolvers/>\n- **Writing your first Django app, part 3** — Django docs · *Beginner* · URLs, names and namespacing in the official tutorial. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/intro/tutorial03/>\n- **django/urls/resolvers.py** — GitHub · *Advanced* · `URLResolver.resolve` and `_reverse_with_prefix` in a few hundred lines. <https://github.com/django/django/blob/stable/5.2.x/django/urls/resolvers.py>\n- **django-extensions (show_urls)** — GitHub · *Beginner* · list every route and its view from the command line. <https://github.com/django-extensions/django-extensions>\n- **Cool URIs don't change** — W3C (Tim Berners-Lee) · *Beginner* · why stable, named URLs matter. <https://www.w3.org/Provider/Style/URI>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 03.*\n", "04-views-requests-responses": "# 04 · Views, HttpRequest & HttpResponse\n\n> **In one line:** A Django view is just a callable that takes an `HttpRequest` and returns an `HttpResponse` (or raises an exception Django maps to one); mastering what lives on the request — and which parts are parsed lazily, limited, or spoofable — and choosing the right response class for buffered, JSON, streamed or file output is what separates a view that works in a demo from one that survives production traffic.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle) (where views sit in the handler) and [Ch 03 · URL Routing & URLconf](topic.html?p=03-urls-routing) (how the view and its kwargs are chosen).\n\nOnce the URL resolver has picked a view, your code runs. The contract is tiny — *request in, response out* — and that minimalism is deliberate. A view can be a plain function (a **function-based view**, FBV), a class whose `as_view()` returns a function ([Ch 17 · Class-Based Views](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views)), or a DRF `APIView` ([Ch 22](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers)). Underneath they all obey the same contract, so this chapter is the foundation for all of them.\n\nThe **naive approach** to request handling is to treat the request as a dictionary of strings and the response as a string: read `request.POST[\"qty\"]`, `int()` it, build HTML with f-strings, return it. That fails in many small ways at once. `request.POST` is empty for JSON bodies. `KeyError` on a missing field becomes a 500 instead of a 400. A GET handler that changes data is cached, prefetched by browsers and replayed. A 2 GB CSV export built in memory kills the worker. An IP read from `X-Forwarded-For` is whatever the attacker typed. Refreshing the page after a POST resubmits the order.\n\nDjango gives you structured tools for each of these: a rich `HttpRequest` (`GET`, `POST`, `body`, `FILES`, `headers`, `META`, `COOKIES`, plus `user` and `session` from middleware), a family of response classes (`HttpResponse`, redirects, `JsonResponse`, `StreamingHttpResponse`, `FileResponse`, `TemplateResponse`), shortcuts (`render`, `redirect`, `get_object_or_404`), decorators that declare behaviour (`require_POST`, `login_required`, `cache_control`), and exceptions (`Http404`, `PermissionDenied`, `BadRequest`) that become correct status codes.\n\nInterview questions in this area are concrete: *\"What's the difference between `request.POST` and `request.body`?\"*, *\"How do you return JSON?\"*, *\"How would you stream a large file?\"*, *\"What is the Post/Redirect/Get pattern?\"*, *\"How do you get the client's IP behind a load balancer?\"*\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **View** — any callable `view(request, *args, **kwargs) -> HttpResponse`. *Why it matters:* everything else (CBVs, DRF) compiles down to this.\n- **`HttpRequest`** — `WSGIRequest` or `ASGIRequest`; attributes are mostly read-only and many are **parsed lazily** on first access.\n- **`request.method`** — uppercase string (`\"GET\"`, `\"POST\"`). *Why it matters:* branch on it, or better, restrict it with decorators.\n- **`request.GET`** — a `QueryDict` of query-string parameters, for any method. **`request.POST`** — a `QueryDict` of form fields, populated **only** for `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` and `multipart/form-data` bodies. *Why it matters:* JSON requests have an empty `request.POST`.\n- **`QueryDict`** — an immutable multi-value dict: `get()` returns the last value, `getlist()` all values; `.copy()` returns a mutable copy.\n- **`request.body`** — the raw request body as bytes. *Why it matters:* the place to read JSON or webhooks' signed payloads.\n- **`request.FILES`** — a `MultiValueDict` of `UploadedFile` objects, only for `multipart/form-data` POSTs from a form with `enctype=\"multipart/form-data\"`.\n- **`request.headers`** — a case-insensitive, read-only mapping of HTTP headers (`request.headers[\"X-Request-ID\"]`). **`request.META`** — the full WSGI environ: headers as `HTTP_*`, plus `REMOTE_ADDR`, `SERVER_NAME`, `CONTENT_LENGTH`.\n- **`request.COOKIES`** — dict of cookie strings; **`get_signed_cookie()`** validates signed ones.\n- **`request.get_host()` / `build_absolute_uri()` / `is_secure()`** — host and scheme helpers that respect `ALLOWED_HOSTS`, `USE_X_FORWARDED_HOST` and `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER`.\n- **`HttpResponse`** — buffered content (bytes), `status`, `headers`; `set_cookie()`, `delete_cookie()`.\n- **Response subclasses** — `HttpResponseRedirect` (302), `HttpResponsePermanentRedirect` (301), `HttpResponseNotModified` (304), `HttpResponseBadRequest` (400), `HttpResponseForbidden` (403), `HttpResponseNotFound` (404), `HttpResponseNotAllowed` (405, takes the allowed methods), `HttpResponseGone` (410), `HttpResponseServerError` (500).\n- **`JsonResponse`** — serializes with `DjangoJSONEncoder` (dates, decimals, UUIDs); `safe=True` by default, which requires a `dict`.\n- **`StreamingHttpResponse`** — content is an iterator consumed while sending; no `.content`, no `Content-Length` by default.\n- **`FileResponse`** — a streaming response optimized for files (uses `wsgi.file_wrapper` when available), sets `Content-Type`, `Content-Length` and `Content-Disposition`.\n- **`TemplateResponse`** — keeps template and context until the end of the response phase, so decorators and middleware can modify them.\n- **Shortcuts** — `render()`, `redirect()`, `get_object_or_404()`, `get_list_or_404()`.\n- **View decorators** — `require_http_methods`, `require_GET`, `require_POST`, `require_safe`, `login_required`, `permission_required`, `csrf_exempt`, `ensure_csrf_cookie`, `cache_page`, `never_cache`, `cache_control`, `vary_on_headers`, `condition`/`etag`/`last_modified`.\n- **Error exceptions** — `Http404` (404), `PermissionDenied` (403), `BadRequest` (400), `SuspiciousOperation` (400, logged as a security event).\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 How an `HttpRequest` is built, lazily\n\n`WSGIHandler` constructs `WSGIRequest(environ)`. The constructor does very little: it sets `path`, `path_info`, `method`, `META = environ`, and reads `CONTENT_TYPE`/`CONTENT_LENGTH` metadata. Everything expensive is deferred:\n\n- `request.GET` is a cached property that parses `QUERY_STRING` into a `QueryDict` on first access.\n- `request.COOKIES` parses the `Cookie` header on first access.\n- `request.POST` and `request.FILES` are built together by `_load_post_and_files()` on first access. For `multipart/form-data` Django runs the `MultiPartParser`, which streams the body through **upload handlers**: files up to `FILE_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE` (2.5 MB default) stay in memory (`InMemoryUploadedFile`), larger ones are written to a temp file (`TemporaryUploadedFile`). For URL-encoded forms it parses `request.body`. For any other content type, `POST` is an empty `QueryDict`.\n- `request.body` reads the whole input stream into memory the first time, enforcing `DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE` (2.5 MB default; file data in multipart uploads is excluded from that limit). Exceeding it raises `RequestDataTooBig`, a `SuspiciousOperation` that becomes a 400.\n\nThat design has a famous consequence. The input stream can be read **once**. If the multipart parser has already consumed it (because something accessed `request.POST`), then accessing `request.body` raises `RawPostDataException: You cannot access body after reading from request's data stream`. Conversely, if you read `request.body` first, Django can still parse `POST` from the buffered bytes. So when a view needs the raw body — e.g. to verify a webhook HMAC — read `request.body` *before* anything touches `request.POST`, including middleware such as `CsrfViewMiddleware` (which reads `POST` for the token on unsafe methods unless the view is exempt).\n\nOther protective limits: `DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_NUMBER_FIELDS` (1000 by default) caps the number of GET/POST parameters, and `DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_NUMBER_FILES` (100) caps files per multipart request; exceeding either is a 400 (`TooManyFieldsSent` / `TooManyFilesSent`).\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj04a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj04b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">WSGIRequest: what is parsed when (the body stream can be read once)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"200\" height=\"120\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">WSGI environ</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">REQUEST_METHOD, PATH_INFO</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"104\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">QUERY_STRING, HTTP_COOKIE</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"120\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">CONTENT_TYPE, CONTENT_LENGTH</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"136\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">HTTP_* headers, REMOTE_ADDR</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"152\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">wsgi.input (body stream)</text>\n  <rect x=\"260\" y=\"44\" width=\"200\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Eager (constructor)</text>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">method, path, path_info, META</text>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"97\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">headers view over META</text>\n  <path d=\"M220,74 L256,74\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj04a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"260\" y=\"120\" width=\"200\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Lazy, cheap</text>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">GET from QUERY_STRING</text>\n  <text x=\"360\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">COOKIES from HTTP_COOKIE</text>\n  <path d=\"M220,140 L256,148\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj04a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"500\" y=\"44\" width=\"360\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"680\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Lazy, reads wsgi.input: POST / FILES / body</text>\n  <text x=\"514\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">multipart/form-data</text>\n  <text x=\"514\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">MultiPartParser streams through upload handlers</text>\n  <text x=\"514\" y=\"122\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">small files in memory, large ones to temp files</text>\n  <text x=\"514\" y=\"144\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">application/x-www-form-urlencoded</text>\n  <text x=\"514\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">reads body (limit DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE), parses</text>\n  <text x=\"514\" y=\"182\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">application/json, anything else</text>\n  <text x=\"514\" y=\"198\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">POST stays EMPTY; use json.loads(request.body)</text>\n  <text x=\"514\" y=\"226\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Limits: 1000 fields, 100 files, 2.5 MB non-file data</text>\n  <path d=\"M220,152 L496,152\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj04a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"270\" width=\"410\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"292\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Order trap (multipart request)</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"314\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">1. something reads request.POST  (e.g. CSRF check)</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"332\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">2. multipart parser consumes wsgi.input</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"350\" fill=\"#b91c1c\" font-size=\"10\">3. request.body raises RawPostDataException</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"368\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">non-multipart bodies are buffered, so body still works there</text>\n  <rect x=\"450\" y=\"270\" width=\"410\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"292\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Safe order (webhook)</text>\n  <text x=\"464\" y=\"314\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">1. @csrf_exempt view (signature replaces CSRF)</text>\n  <text x=\"464\" y=\"332\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">2. raw = request.body   verify HMAC over raw bytes</text>\n  <text x=\"464\" y=\"350\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">3. payload = json.loads(raw)</text>\n  <text x=\"464\" y=\"368\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">request.POST can still be parsed from the buffered body</text>\n  <path d=\"M680,244 L680,266\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj04b)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.2 What a response really is\n\n`HttpResponse` holds a list of byte chunks; setting `content` encodes strings with the response charset (`DEFAULT_CHARSET`, UTF-8). Headers live in `response.headers`, a case-insensitive mapping (since 3.2; `response[\"X-Foo\"]` still works). When the handler returns it, the WSGI layer calls `start_response(f\"{status} {reason}\", headers + cookies)` and iterates the response to send bytes. Because the content is fully built before sending, middleware can read and rewrite it (`GZipMiddleware`, `ConditionalGetMiddleware` computing an ETag), and Django sets `Content-Length` when it can.\n\n`StreamingHttpResponse` instead wraps an iterator in `streaming_content`. Nothing is generated until the server iterates, so memory stays flat — but there is no `.content`, middleware that needs the full body must skip it (`GZipMiddleware` compresses streams chunk by chunk; ETag computation is skipped), and under a WSGI sync worker **the worker is occupied for the whole duration of the stream**. Since Django 4.2 the iterator may be async; serving a sync iterator under ASGI (or an async one under WSGI) works but Django warns and has to adapt it, consuming it in a thread or buffering.\n\n`FileResponse(open(path, \"rb\"), as_attachment=True, filename=\"report.pdf\")` is a `StreamingHttpResponse` specialized for files: it sets the type from the filename, the length from the file, the `Content-Disposition` header, and lets the WSGI server use `wsgi.file_wrapper` (e.g. `sendfile`) for efficiency. The file is closed when the response is closed.\n\n`TemplateResponse` defers rendering: it stores `template_name` and `context_data` and renders when the handler calls `render()` after `process_template_response` hooks. That's why CBVs return it — tests can assert on `response.context_data`, and decorators can swap the template.\n\n### 3.3 Decorators wrap views like onion layers\n\nA decorator returns a new function wrapping the view, so stacked decorators apply **bottom-up** and run **top-down**:\n\n```python\n@require_POST          # runs first: 405 for GET before anything else\n@login_required        # then: redirect anonymous users to LOGIN_URL\n@transaction.atomic    # then: open a transaction\ndef checkout(request): ...\n```\n\nEquivalent to `checkout = require_POST(login_required(transaction.atomic(checkout)))`. Order matters: put cheap, rejecting checks outermost. Since Django 5.0 most built-in view decorators (method, cache, vary, CSRF, clickjacking) also accept `async def` views, and `login_required` and the other auth decorators followed in 5.1.\n\nThe diagram below summarizes the response-class choice from §3.2.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 350\" width=\"100%\" height=\"350\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj04c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Choosing a response class: buffered vs streamed</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"400\" height=\"290\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Buffered: HttpResponse family</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"80\" width=\"360\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"52\" y=\"98\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">HttpResponse / render()</text>\n  <text x=\"52\" y=\"114\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">HTML, small text; content in memory, Content-Length set</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"132\" width=\"360\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"52\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">JsonResponse</text>\n  <text x=\"52\" y=\"166\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">DjangoJSONEncoder; safe=True needs a dict</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"184\" width=\"360\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"52\" y=\"202\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">HttpResponseRedirect / redirect()</text>\n  <text x=\"52\" y=\"218\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">302 (301 permanent); Location header; PRG pattern</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"236\" width=\"360\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"52\" y=\"254\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">TemplateResponse</text>\n  <text x=\"52\" y=\"270\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">render deferred; middleware can alter template/context</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"306\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Middleware sees the full body (GZip, ETag).</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"322\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Memory = size of the body, per request.</text>\n  <rect x=\"460\" y=\"44\" width=\"400\" height=\"290\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"660\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Streamed: StreamingHttpResponse family</text>\n  <rect x=\"480\" y=\"80\" width=\"360\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"492\" y=\"98\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">StreamingHttpResponse(iterator)</text>\n  <text x=\"492\" y=\"114\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">CSV/NDJSON exports, SSE; chunks generated on demand</text>\n  <text x=\"492\" y=\"128\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">no .content, usually no Content-Length</text>\n  <rect x=\"480\" y=\"144\" width=\"360\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"492\" y=\"162\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">FileResponse(file, as_attachment=True)</text>\n  <text x=\"492\" y=\"178\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">sets type, length, Content-Disposition</text>\n  <text x=\"492\" y=\"192\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">wsgi.file_wrapper; file closed with the response</text>\n  <rect x=\"480\" y=\"208\" width=\"360\" height=\"72\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"492\" y=\"226\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Cost</text>\n  <text x=\"492\" y=\"242\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">a sync worker is busy until the last byte is sent</text>\n  <text x=\"492\" y=\"258\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">exceptions mid-stream cannot change the status code</text>\n  <text x=\"492\" y=\"272\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">big private files: X-Accel-Redirect or signed S3 URL</text>\n  <text x=\"480\" y=\"306\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Memory stays flat regardless of total size.</text>\n  <text x=\"480\" y=\"322\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Proxies may buffer: X-Accel-Buffering: no for SSE.</text>\n  <path d=\"M420,190 L456,190\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj04c)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.4 Errors are exceptions, not return values\n\nInside a view you can `raise Http404(\"...\")`, `raise PermissionDenied`, `raise BadRequest(\"...\")`. The handler's `response_for_exception` turns them into 404/403/400 responses rendered by `handler404`/`handler403`/`handler400` (or the technical pages in DEBUG). An uncaught `Model.DoesNotExist`, `KeyError` or `ValueError` is a 500. So the discipline is: validate input and raise the right exception, and let `get_object_or_404` convert missing rows into 404s.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 The Post/Redirect/Get pattern\n\n```text\nBrowser                       Django view                        Database\n   |  GET /orders/new/            |                                  |\n   |----------------------------->| render empty form (200)          |\n   |<-----------------------------|                                  |\n   |  POST /orders/new/ (form)    |                                  |\n   |----------------------------->| validate; create Order --------->| INSERT\n   |                              |<---------------------------------|\n   |<-----------------------------| 302 Location: /orders/42/        |\n   |  GET /orders/42/             |                                  |\n   |----------------------------->| render detail (200)              |\n   |<-----------------------------|                                  |\n   |  user presses F5             |  -> repeats the harmless GET, not the POST\n```\n\nReturning HTML directly from a successful POST means a refresh re-submits the form (\"Confirm form resubmission\"), creating a duplicate order. Redirecting after a successful POST — and re-rendering the form with errors (status 200 or 400) on failure — is the standard pattern.\n\n### 4.2 Anatomy of a well-behaved FBV\n\n```text\n@decorators                -> method restriction, auth, caching headers\ndef view(request, **kw):\n    1. parse + validate input   -> 400 (BadRequest / form errors) on bad input\n    2. load objects             -> get_object_or_404, scoped to request.user (403/404)\n    3. do the work              -> service function, transaction.atomic where needed\n    4. build response           -> render / redirect / JsonResponse / FileResponse\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example: reading the request\n\n```python\n# shop/views.py\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse, JsonResponse\nfrom django.views.decorators.http import require_GET\n\n@require_GET\ndef inspect(request):\n    return JsonResponse({\n        \"method\": request.method,\n        \"path\": request.path,\n        \"q\": request.GET.get(\"q\", \"\"),                 # last value or default\n        \"tags\": request.GET.getlist(\"tag\"),            # ?tag=a&tag=b -> [\"a\", \"b\"]\n        \"user_agent\": request.headers.get(\"User-Agent\"),\n        \"accepts_json\": request.accepts(\"application/json\"),\n        \"host\": request.get_host(),                    # validated against ALLOWED_HOSTS\n        \"secure\": request.is_secure(),\n        \"absolute\": request.build_absolute_uri(\"/shop/\"),\n        \"cookies\": sorted(request.COOKIES),\n        \"user\": str(request.user),                     # set by AuthenticationMiddleware\n    })\n```\n\n```bash\ncurl -s \"localhost:8000/inspect/?q=mug&tag=a&tag=b\" -H \"Accept: application/json\"\n```\n\n```text\n{\"method\": \"GET\", \"path\": \"/inspect/\", \"q\": \"mug\", \"tags\": [\"a\", \"b\"],\n \"user_agent\": \"curl/8.7.1\", \"accepts_json\": true, \"host\": \"localhost:8000\",\n \"secure\": false, \"absolute\": \"http://localhost:8000/shop/\", \"cookies\": [], \"user\": \"AnonymousUser\"}\n```\n\n`QueryDict` is immutable, which surprises people building \"next page\" links:\n\n```python\nparams = request.GET.copy()          # mutable copy\nparams[\"page\"] = 2\nnext_url = f\"{request.path}?{params.urlencode()}\"\n```\n\n### 5.2 Handling forms, JSON and files\n\nA form POST with Post/Redirect/Get (forms in depth in [Ch 06](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation)):\n\n```python\nfrom django.contrib import messages\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required\nfrom django.shortcuts import redirect, render\nfrom django.views.decorators.http import require_http_methods\nfrom .forms import AddressForm\n\n@login_required\n@require_http_methods([\"GET\", \"POST\"])\ndef edit_address(request):\n    if request.method == \"POST\":\n        form = AddressForm(request.POST, instance=request.user.address)\n        if form.is_valid():\n            form.save()\n            messages.success(request, \"Address saved.\")\n            return redirect(\"accounts:address\")          # PRG\n    else:\n        form = AddressForm(instance=request.user.address)\n    return render(request, \"accounts/address.html\", {\"form\": form})\n```\n\nA JSON endpoint without DRF — parsing, validating and returning proper status codes:\n\n```python\nimport json\nfrom decimal import Decimal, InvalidOperation\nfrom django.core.exceptions import BadRequest\nfrom django.http import JsonResponse\nfrom django.views.decorators.http import require_POST\n\n@require_POST\ndef quote(request):\n    if request.content_type != \"application/json\":\n        return JsonResponse({\"error\": \"expected application/json\"}, status=415)\n    try:\n        data = json.loads(request.body)\n        qty = int(data[\"qty\"])\n        price = Decimal(str(data[\"unit_price\"]))\n    except (json.JSONDecodeError, KeyError, ValueError, TypeError, InvalidOperation):\n        raise BadRequest(\"qty and unit_price are required numbers\")\n    if qty <= 0:\n        return JsonResponse({\"error\": \"qty must be positive\"}, status=422)\n    return JsonResponse({\"qty\": qty, \"total\": price * qty})   # Decimal -> \"12.50\" via DjangoJSONEncoder\n```\n\nNote this view still requires a CSRF token when called from a browser session, because `CsrfViewMiddleware` protects every POST; token-authenticated APIs normally use DRF, where CSRF applies only to session authentication ([Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security)).\n\nReturning a list needs `safe=False`, because JSON arrays at the top level were once an attack vector in very old browsers and Django keeps the explicit opt-in:\n\n```python\nreturn JsonResponse([{\"id\": 1}, {\"id\": 2}], safe=False)\n```\n\nAn upload view:\n\n```python\nfrom django.core.exceptions import BadRequest\n\n@login_required\n@require_POST\ndef upload_avatar(request):\n    f = request.FILES.get(\"avatar\")                     # needs enctype=\"multipart/form-data\"\n    if f is None:\n        raise BadRequest(\"avatar file missing\")\n    if f.size > 2 * 1024 * 1024 or f.content_type not in {\"image/png\", \"image/jpeg\"}:\n        raise BadRequest(\"PNG/JPEG under 2 MB only\")   # content_type is client-supplied: re-check\n    request.user.profile.avatar.save(f.name, f, save=True)  # storage backend handles chunks\n    return redirect(\"accounts:profile\")\n```\n\n### 5.3 Real-world example: a streaming CSV export and a protected download\n\nExporting hundreds of thousands of orders as CSV must not load them all into memory or hold one huge string. Stream rows directly from a server-side cursor:\n\n```python\n# reports/views.py\nimport csv\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import permission_required\nfrom django.http import StreamingHttpResponse\nfrom django.utils import timezone\nfrom django.views.decorators.http import require_GET\nfrom orders.models import Order\n\n\nclass Echo:\n    \"\"\"File-like object whose write() returns the value, so csv.writer yields lines.\"\"\"\n    def write(self, value):\n        return value\n\n\ndef order_rows(qs):\n    writer = csv.writer(Echo())\n    yield writer.writerow([\"id\", \"created_at\", \"customer_email\", \"status\", \"total\"])\n    rows = qs.values_list(\"id\", \"created_at\", \"customer__email\", \"status\", \"total\")\n    for row in rows.iterator(chunk_size=2000):         # server-side cursor on PostgreSQL\n        yield writer.writerow(row)\n\n\n@require_GET\n@permission_required(\"orders.view_order\", raise_exception=True)   # 403, not a login redirect\ndef export_orders(request):\n    since = request.GET.get(\"since\")\n    qs = Order.objects.order_by(\"id\")\n    if since:\n        qs = qs.filter(created_at__date__gte=since)\n    filename = f\"orders-{timezone.now():%Y%m%d}.csv\"\n    response = StreamingHttpResponse(order_rows(qs), content_type=\"text/csv\")\n    response[\"Content-Disposition\"] = f'attachment; filename=\"{filename}\"'\n    response[\"Cache-Control\"] = \"private, no-store\"\n    return response\n```\n\nThe SQL is one query streamed in chunks, not 2000-row pages:\n\n```sql\nDECLARE \"_django_curs_...\" NO SCROLL CURSOR WITH HOLD FOR\nSELECT \"orders_order\".\"id\", \"orders_order\".\"created_at\", \"accounts_user\".\"email\",\n       \"orders_order\".\"status\", \"orders_order\".\"total\"\nFROM \"orders_order\"\nINNER JOIN \"accounts_user\" ON (\"orders_order\".\"customer_id\" = \"accounts_user\".\"id\")\nORDER BY \"orders_order\".\"id\" ASC;\nFETCH FORWARD 2000 FROM \"_django_curs_...\";\n```\n\nTwo production caveats. A sync Gunicorn worker is busy for the entire download, so very large or frequent exports belong in a Celery task that writes a file to object storage and emails a signed link ([Ch 31](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks)). And because headers are sent before the first row, an exception halfway through cannot turn into a 500 — the client just gets a truncated file; log errors inside the generator.\n\nFor private files (invoices), check authorization in Django but let Nginx send the bytes:\n\n```python\nfrom django.http import FileResponse, HttpResponse\nfrom django.shortcuts import get_object_or_404\n\n@login_required\ndef invoice_pdf(request, pk):\n    invoice = get_object_or_404(Invoice, pk=pk, customer__user=request.user)  # 404, not 403: don't leak existence\n    if settings.USE_X_ACCEL:\n        response = HttpResponse(content_type=\"application/pdf\")\n        response[\"X-Accel-Redirect\"] = f\"/protected/invoices/{invoice.file.name}\"   # internal Nginx location\n        response[\"Content-Disposition\"] = f'attachment; filename=\"invoice-{invoice.number}.pdf\"'\n        return response\n    return FileResponse(invoice.file.open(\"rb\"), as_attachment=True,\n                        filename=f\"invoice-{invoice.number}.pdf\")\n```\n\n### 5.4 Cookies, headers and caching decorators\n\n```python\nfrom django.views.decorators.cache import cache_control, never_cache\nfrom django.views.decorators.vary import vary_on_headers\n\n@cache_control(public=True, max_age=300)\n@vary_on_headers(\"Accept-Language\")\ndef pricing(request):\n    response = render(request, \"pricing.html\")\n    response.set_cookie(\n        \"seen_pricing\", \"1\",\n        max_age=60 * 60 * 24 * 30, secure=True, httponly=True, samesite=\"Lax\",\n    )\n    return response\n\n@never_cache                      # Cache-Control: max-age=0, no-cache, no-store, must-revalidate, private\n@login_required\ndef account_dashboard(request):\n    ...\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Response | Memory | Middleware can modify body | Worker held | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `HttpResponse` / `render` | whole body | yes | until built | HTML pages, small payloads |\n| `JsonResponse` | whole body | yes | until built | small JSON APIs without DRF |\n| `TemplateResponse` | whole body | yes, before render | until built | CBVs, testable context |\n| `StreamingHttpResponse` | one chunk | chunk-wise only | whole stream | exports, NDJSON, SSE (prefer ASGI) |\n| `FileResponse` | one block | no | whole download | local files, small-to-medium downloads |\n| `X-Accel-Redirect` / signed URL | none | n/a | milliseconds | large private files |\n\n| FBV | CBV |\n|---|---|\n| Explicit, linear, easy to read and decorate | Reuse via inheritance and mixins, generic CRUD in a few lines |\n| Repetition across similar views | Control flow hidden across MRO; harder for newcomers |\n\n### When to use\n\n- FBVs for one-off, logic-heavy endpoints, webhooks, and anything whose flow you want visible top to bottom.\n- `JsonResponse` for a handful of internal JSON endpoints; DRF once you need auth schemes, serializers, pagination or browsable docs.\n- Streaming for large generated output where a background job would be overkill.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Don't use `StreamingHttpResponse` for small responses — you lose `Content-Length`, ETags and simple error handling.\n- Don't serve big files through Django workers at scale; offload to Nginx, a CDN or pre-signed storage URLs.\n- Don't put business logic directly in views if commands, tasks and APIs need the same logic; extract a service function.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**Reading JSON from `request.POST`.** It is empty for `application/json`. Use `json.loads(request.body)` (or DRF's `request.data`).\n\n**Changing state on GET.** Links get prefetched, crawled and cached; a \"delete\" link called by a GET is a CSRF and data-loss bug (CSRF protection only covers unsafe methods). Restrict with `@require_POST` and use forms or buttons.\n\n**`KeyError` on input.**\n\n```python\n# Before: missing field -> 500, logged as a server error\nqty = int(request.POST[\"qty\"])\n\n# After: explicit validation -> 400 with a message (or use a Form)\ntry:\n    qty = int(request.POST.get(\"qty\", \"\"))\nexcept ValueError:\n    raise BadRequest(\"qty must be an integer\")\n```\n\n**Rendering after a successful POST.** Leads to duplicate submissions on refresh. Redirect (PRG).\n\n**Trusting client-supplied metadata.** `request.FILES[\"f\"].content_type` and `.name` come from the client; `X-Forwarded-For` can be forged. Validate file contents, sanitize names (storage backends generate safe names), and read the client IP only from the header your own proxy sets — typically the right-most entry appended by your trusted proxy, not the left-most.\n\n**Using `.get()` and getting a 500.** `Order.objects.get(pk=pk)` raises `DoesNotExist` → 500. Use `get_object_or_404`, and scope the query to the current user to avoid insecure direct object references.\n\n**`HttpResponseRedirect` to user input.** `redirect(request.GET[\"next\"])` is an open redirect. Validate with `url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme(next_url, allowed_hosts={request.get_host()}, require_https=request.is_secure())`.\n\n**Best practices**\n\n- Restrict methods with decorators; return 405 automatically rather than branching manually.\n- Validate input with forms/serializers; raise `BadRequest` or return form errors.\n- Scope every object lookup to the user or tenant (`get_object_or_404(Order, pk=pk, user=request.user)`).\n- Prefer 404 over 403 when revealing existence is itself a leak.\n- Use the right response class; set `Cache-Control` explicitly for private data.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: `RequestDataTooBig` / 400s on large forms.** *Symptom:* big admin formsets or bulk-edit pages fail with 400. *Root cause:* `DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE` or `DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_NUMBER_FIELDS` exceeded. *Fix:* raise the limit only for the affected flow (or redesign it); these limits protect against memory and hash-collision DoS — don't set them to `None` globally.\n\n**Pitfall: uploads fill the disk or memory.** Large uploads stream to `FILE_UPLOAD_TEMP_DIR`; a container with a tiny `/tmp` fails. Set Nginx `client_max_body_size`, validate size early, and for big files upload straight to S3 with pre-signed URLs.\n\n**Pitfall: workers exhausted by slow clients or streams.** A sync worker serving a slow mobile client or a long CSV is blocked. Nginx buffering protects you from slow *uploads* and slow *readers* of normal responses; streams and SSE are not buffered meaningfully. Offload large exports to tasks; run long-lived streams on ASGI.\n\n**Pitfall: wrong client IP / wrong scheme behind a load balancer.** `REMOTE_ADDR` is the proxy's address, `request.is_secure()` is false, and `build_absolute_uri()` generates `http://` links in emails. *Fix:* set `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER = (\"HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO\", \"https\")` only if your proxy always sets/overwrites that header; use a vetted middleware (or `django-ipware`) configured for your proxy count to read the client IP.\n\n**Pitfall: `DisallowedHost` noise.** Scanners hit your IP with random `Host` headers; `get_host()` raises `DisallowedHost` → 400 and a `django.security.DisallowedHost` log. Configure Nginx to drop unknown hosts and route that logger to a lower level.\n\n**Debugging**\n\n- `request.META` dump in a debug view (never in production) to see what the proxy actually sends.\n- Django Debug Toolbar's *Request* and *Headers* panels.\n- `RequestFactory` in tests to build requests with arbitrary headers: `RequestFactory().post(\"/x/\", data, content_type=\"application/json\", headers={\"X-Signature\": \"...\"})`.\n- `curl -v` to see redirects, `Set-Cookie` and caching headers exactly.\n\n**Scaling notes.** Views should be fast, stateless and bounded: no unbounded queries (paginate), no unbounded bodies (limits), no unbounded outbound calls (timeouts). Measure p95 per route; anything consistently above a few hundred milliseconds is a candidate for caching ([Ch 29](topic.html?p=29-caching)), query optimization ([Ch 13](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization)) or background processing.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is a Django view, minimally?**\nA: Any callable that accepts an `HttpRequest` as its first argument (plus URL kwargs) and returns an `HttpResponse`, or raises an exception that Django converts into one, such as `Http404`. Function-based views are plain functions; class-based views produce such a function through `as_view()`; DRF views do the same with their own request and response wrappers. Returning `None` raises a `ValueError` and becomes a 500.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `request.POST` and `request.body`?**\nA: `request.body` is the raw request body as bytes, read from the input stream on first access and limited by `DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE`. `request.POST` is a parsed `QueryDict` of form fields, populated only for `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` and `multipart/form-data` requests — it is empty for JSON. For JSON you parse `request.body` yourself (or use DRF's `request.data`). For multipart requests, once the parser has consumed the stream via `request.POST`, accessing `request.body` raises `RawPostDataException`.\n\n**Q: What is a `QueryDict` and why is it immutable?**\nA: It is a dictionary subclass that can hold several values per key, because query strings and forms can repeat keys (`?tag=a&tag=b`). `get()` returns the last value and `getlist()` returns all of them. The instances on the request are immutable so that one piece of code can't silently change what later code sees as the client's input; call `.copy()` to get a mutable version, for example to build a pagination link.\n\n**Q: How do you return JSON from a plain Django view?**\nA: Use `JsonResponse(data)`, which serializes with `DjangoJSONEncoder` (handling datetimes, decimals and UUIDs) and sets the content type. By default `safe=True` requires a dict at the top level; pass `safe=False` to return a list. For serious APIs — authentication schemes, validation, pagination, content negotiation — DRF is the better choice, but `JsonResponse` is fine for a few internal endpoints.\n\n**Q: What does `get_object_or_404` do and why use it?**\nA: It calls `get()` on a model, manager or QuerySet with the given filters and raises `Http404` if the object doesn't exist, instead of letting `DoesNotExist` bubble up as a 500. Passing a QuerySet lets you add `select_related` or scope the lookup, e.g. `get_object_or_404(Order.objects.filter(user=request.user), pk=pk)`, which also prevents users from reading other users' objects. It still raises `MultipleObjectsReturned` if the filter isn't unique.\n\n**Q: What is the Post/Redirect/Get pattern and why use it?**\nA: After a successful POST, the view returns a redirect (302/303) to a GET page instead of rendering HTML directly. The browser then loads that page with GET, so refreshing or pressing back repeats a harmless GET instead of re-submitting the form and creating a duplicate. On validation failure you re-render the form with errors without redirecting. Django's `redirect()` shortcut and the generic editing views implement this by default.\n\n**Q: How would you stream a large CSV file to the user?**\nA: Use `StreamingHttpResponse` with a generator that yields CSV lines — `csv.writer` over a pseudo-buffer whose `write` returns the line — and iterate the QuerySet with `.values_list(...).iterator(chunk_size=...)` so rows are fetched through a server-side cursor without filling the QuerySet cache. Set `Content-Disposition: attachment`. Memory stays flat, but the worker is busy for the whole download and mid-stream errors can't change the status code, so for very large exports a background task that writes to object storage and sends a link scales better.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `HttpResponse`, `StreamingHttpResponse` and `FileResponse`?**\nA: `HttpResponse` holds the full body in memory, so middleware can inspect or rewrite it and `Content-Length` is known. `StreamingHttpResponse` wraps an iterator consumed while sending; it has no `.content`, middleware must handle it chunk by chunk, and the worker is occupied until the stream ends. `FileResponse` is a streaming subclass for file objects that sets content type, length and `Content-Disposition` and can use the server's `wsgi.file_wrapper` for efficient file sending.\n\n**Q: How do decorators like `@login_required` and `@require_POST` work, and does their order matter?**\nA: Each decorator wraps the view in a new function that runs a check before (and sometimes after) calling the original. They are applied bottom-up and run top-down, so the decorator closest to `def` is the innermost. Order matters: putting `@require_POST` outermost rejects a GET with 405 before the login redirect; putting `@login_required` outermost redirects anonymous GETs to login first. Put cheap rejecting checks outermost, and put `@transaction.atomic` innermost so it covers only the real work.\n\n**Q: How do you return an error status from a view?**\nA: Raise `Http404`, `PermissionDenied` or `BadRequest` (or `SuspiciousOperation`) and Django renders the configured handler with 404, 403 or 400. Alternatively return a response with an explicit status, such as `JsonResponse({...}, status=422)` or `HttpResponseNotAllowed([\"POST\"])`. Raising is preferable for standard cases because the response goes through your custom error templates and logging consistently, while explicit statuses are right for API-specific errors with a body.\n\n**Q: How do you securely serve private, user-specific files that may be large? (Senior)**\nA: Authorize in Django — look up the file scoped to `request.user`, returning 404 to avoid leaking existence — but don't stream the bytes through a Python worker. Either return an empty response with `X-Accel-Redirect` (Nginx) or `X-Sendfile` pointing at an internal-only location, so the web server sends the file, or redirect to a short-lived pre-signed S3/GCS URL. Set `Content-Disposition` with a safe filename, `Cache-Control: private, no-store`, and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, and never serve user uploads from the same origin as the app with an HTML content type. `FileResponse` is fine for small files or low traffic.\n\n**Q: How do you get the client's real IP address when Django runs behind a load balancer, and what's the risk? (Senior)**\nA: `REMOTE_ADDR` will be the proxy's address. The client IP is in `X-Forwarded-For`, but that header is a comma-separated list the client can pre-populate, so the left-most value is attacker-controlled. You must take the address added by your own trusted proxy: count trusted hops from the right, or have the edge proxy overwrite a dedicated header such as `X-Real-IP`. Implement that once in middleware or with a configured library like `django-ipware`, and use it consistently for rate limiting and audit logs — getting it wrong lets attackers bypass IP-based throttles.\n\n**Q: A webhook endpoint fails signature verification intermittently and sometimes raises `RawPostDataException`. What's going on? (Senior)**\nA: The signature must be computed over the exact raw bytes, so the view needs `request.body`. `RawPostDataException` means something read `request.POST` first on a multipart request, consuming the stream — commonly CSRF middleware on a non-exempt view, or a logging middleware that touches `POST`. I'd mark the webhook `@csrf_exempt` (the HMAC is the authenticity check), read `request.body` first, verify with `hmac.compare_digest` against the provider's header, and only then parse JSON. The intermittent signature failures are often from re-serializing the JSON before hashing, a different encoding, or a timestamp tolerance window, so verify the raw bytes and the provider's timestamp header exactly as documented.\n\n**Q: When does `request.user` get populated and what is it for anonymous visitors?**\nA: `AuthenticationMiddleware` attaches `request.user` as a lazy object that loads the user from the session on first access, so views that never touch it don't pay for the query. For visitors who aren't logged in it is an `AnonymousUser` instance, whose `is_authenticated` is `False`. It requires `SessionMiddleware` earlier in the stack, and in DRF, `request.user` is instead resolved by the configured authentication classes.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Need | Use |\n|---|---|\n| Query params | `request.GET.get(\"q\")`, `getlist(\"tag\")` |\n| Form fields | `request.POST` (urlencoded/multipart only) |\n| JSON body | `json.loads(request.body)` |\n| Files | `request.FILES[\"f\"]` + `enctype=\"multipart/form-data\"` |\n| Headers | `request.headers[\"X-Foo\"]`; raw: `request.META[\"HTTP_X_FOO\"]` |\n| Host/scheme | `get_host()`, `is_secure()`, `build_absolute_uri()` |\n| HTML | `render(request, tpl, ctx)` |\n| Redirect | `redirect(\"app:name\", pk=1)` / `redirect(obj)` |\n| JSON | `JsonResponse(dict)`, `safe=False` for lists |\n| Big output | `StreamingHttpResponse(gen)`, `FileResponse(f)` |\n| 404 | `get_object_or_404(qs, ...)`, `raise Http404` |\n| 400/403 | `raise BadRequest`, `raise PermissionDenied` |\n| Methods | `@require_POST`, `@require_http_methods([...])`, `@require_safe` |\n| Caching headers | `@cache_control(...)`, `@never_cache`, `@vary_on_headers(...)` |\n\n- View = callable(request) -> HttpResponse.\n- `request.POST` is empty for JSON.\n- The body stream is read once; read `request.body` before `POST` on multipart if you need raw bytes.\n- `DoesNotExist` is a 500; use `get_object_or_404`.\n- PRG after successful POSTs.\n- Decorators apply bottom-up, run top-down.\n- Streaming saves memory, costs a worker and error handling.\n- Never trust `X-Forwarded-For`'s left-most value or a file's `content_type`.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" && django-admin startproject config . && python manage.py startapp shop`.\n\n1. **Request explorer.** Build the `inspect` view from §5.1. Call it with `curl` using a query string with repeated keys, a custom header and a cookie; then POST JSON and form data and compare `request.POST` and `request.body`.\n2. **The body trap.** Write a view that reads `request.POST` and then `request.body`; POST multipart data with `curl -F` and reproduce `RawPostDataException`. Reverse the order and show it works.\n3. **PRG.** Implement a tiny \"create note\" form without redirect, submit, and refresh the page. Add `redirect()` and observe the difference.\n4. **Streaming.** Create 200,000 rows with `bulk_create`, then export them with `StreamingHttpResponse` and with a naive `HttpResponse` built from a list; compare peak memory with `tracemalloc`.\n5. **Decorator order.** Stack `@require_POST` and `@login_required` in both orders and record the responses for an anonymous GET.\n\n**Mini project — Webhook receiver.** Build `POST /hooks/payments/` that is `@csrf_exempt`, verifies an HMAC-SHA256 signature over `request.body` using a secret from settings (`hmac.compare_digest`), rejects stale timestamps, parses JSON, records the event ID in a table with a unique constraint to ignore duplicates, and returns 200 quickly (enqueue the real work). Write tests with `RequestFactory` and `Client` for valid, tampered, replayed and malformed requests.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 03 · URL Routing & URLconf](topic.html?p=03-urls-routing), [Ch 05 · Django Template Language](topic.html?p=05-templates) (`render()` and `TemplateResponse`), [Ch 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation), [Ch 17 · Class-Based & Generic Views](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views), [Ch 20 · Static & Media Files](topic.html?p=20-static-media-files) (uploads and storage), [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers), [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security), [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi) (async views and streaming).\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · HTTP Methods & Idempotency](../rest-api/topic.html?p=06-http-methods-idempotency), [REST API · Status Codes](../rest-api/topic.html?p=07-status-codes), [REST API · HTTP Caching & ETags](../rest-api/topic.html?p=25-http-caching-etags), [REST API · Streaming, SSE & WebSockets](../rest-api/topic.html?p=30-streaming-sse-websockets).\n\n- **Request and response objects** — Django docs · *Beginner* · every attribute of `HttpRequest`, `QueryDict` and the response classes. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/request-response/>\n- **Writing views** — Django docs · *Beginner* · views, errors and custom error handlers. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/http/views/>\n- **Django shortcut functions** — Django docs · *Beginner* · `render`, `redirect`, `get_object_or_404`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/http/shortcuts/>\n- **View decorators** — Django docs · *Beginner* · method, conditional, caching and vary decorators. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/http/decorators/>\n- **File uploads** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · upload handlers, memory vs temp files, limits. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/http/file-uploads/>\n- **How to create CSV output** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · the official streaming CSV recipe. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/outputting-csv/>\n- **MDN: HTTP overview** — MDN Web Docs · *Beginner* · methods, headers and status codes the request/response objects model. <https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP>\n- **django-ipware** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · correct client-IP extraction behind proxies. <https://github.com/un33k/django-ipware>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 04.*\n", "05-templates": "# 05 · Django Template Language\n\n> **In one line:** The Django Template Language (DTL) is a deliberately limited, autoescaping presentation layer: templates are found by loaders, compiled once into a tree of nodes, and rendered against a context stack — so they are safe by default, fast when cached, and dangerous mainly in two places: when you switch escaping off, and when a loop quietly triggers a database query per row.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 04 · Views, HttpRequest & HttpResponse](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses) (`render()` and `TemplateResponse`) and [Ch 03 · URL Routing & URLconf](topic.html?p=03-urls-routing) (`{% url %}`).\n\nServer-rendered HTML is still how most Django back offices, admin screens, emails, PDFs and many public sites are produced — and with HTMX-style partial rendering it is enjoying a revival. The problem templates solve is separating **presentation** from **logic**: designers and front-end developers edit markup without touching Python, the same layout is shared by hundreds of pages, and user-supplied data is inserted without opening an XSS hole.\n\nThe **naive approach** — building HTML with f-strings in views — fails on every count. Every page repeats the `<head>` and navigation. One forgotten `html.escape()` around a username lets an attacker inject `<script>`. Logic and markup interleave until nobody can change either safely.\n\nDjango's template system answers with a small set of ideas:\n\n- **Engines and loaders** — the `TEMPLATES` setting configures one or more engines (DTL by default, Jinja2 optionally) and where templates are found (project `DIRS`, each app's `templates/`).\n- **Compilation** — a template's source is tokenized and parsed once into a tree of `Node`s; the cached loader keeps compiled templates in memory.\n- **Context** — rendering resolves `{{ variables }}` against a stack of dictionaries, filled by the view and by **context processors**.\n- **Inheritance** — `{% extends %}` and `{% block %}` let child templates override named regions of a base layout; `{% include %}` composes fragments.\n- **Filters and tags** — `{{ value|date:\"Y-m-d\" }}` transforms values; `{% for %}`, `{% if %}`, `{% url %}` control output; you can write your own.\n- **Autoescaping** — every variable is HTML-escaped unless explicitly marked safe.\n\nThe DTL is intentionally *not* a programming language: no arbitrary Python expressions, no function calls with arguments. Interviewers probe whether you understand why (keep logic in views, make templates safe to hand to non-programmers), how inheritance and context processors work, how autoescaping protects you and where it doesn't, and how templates cause N+1 queries.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Template engine / backend** — `django.template.backends.django.DjangoTemplates` or `django.template.backends.jinja2.Jinja2`, configured in `TEMPLATES`. *Why it matters:* `render()` and `get_template()` iterate the engines in order.\n- **`DIRS` and `APP_DIRS`** — project-level template directories, and whether to look in each installed app's `templates/` folder. *Why it matters:* lookup order decides which template wins on name collisions.\n- **Loader** — finds and loads template source: `filesystem.Loader` (DIRS), `app_directories.Loader` (apps), wrapped by `cached.Loader`. *Why it matters:* the cached loader avoids re-reading and re-parsing on every request.\n- **Template name namespacing** — store app templates at `app/templates/app/x.html` and refer to `\"app/x.html\"`. *Why it matters:* otherwise two apps' `detail.html` collide and the first in `INSTALLED_APPS` silently wins.\n- **`Template`, `NodeList`, `Node`** — the compiled form: `TextNode`, `VariableNode`, `IfNode`, `ForNode`, `ExtendsNode`, `BlockNode`, `IncludeNode`...\n- **`Context` / `RequestContext`** — a stack of dicts used for lookup; `RequestContext` also runs context processors. `render(request, ...)` always uses the request.\n- **Context processor** — a function `processor(request) -> dict` listed in `OPTIONS[\"context_processors\"]`, merged into every `RequestContext`. Defaults include `request`, `auth` (`user`, `perms`) and `messages`.\n- **Variable lookup** — `{{ a.b }}` tries dictionary lookup, then attribute lookup, then list-index lookup; callables are called with no arguments.\n- **Filter** — a function transforming a value: `{{ name|lower }}`, `{{ price|floatformat:2 }}`.\n- **Tag** — logic or output control: `{% for %}`, `{% if %}`, `{% url %}`, `{% static %}`, `{% csrf_token %}`, `{% include %}`, `{% with %}`, `{% cache %}`.\n- **Template inheritance** — `{% extends \"base.html\" %}`, `{% block name %}`, `{{ block.super }}`.\n- **Custom template library** — `app/templatetags/<lib>.py` with `register = template.Library()`, loaded with `{% load lib %}`.\n- **Autoescaping** — conversion of `<`, `>`, `'`, `\"` and `&` to HTML entities in variable output. **`SafeString`** — a string marked as already safe (`mark_safe`, `|safe`, `format_html`).\n- **`json_script`** — filter that safely embeds data for JavaScript in a `<script type=\"application/json\">` element.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 Finding a template\n\n`render(request, \"orders/detail.html\", ctx)` calls `loader.render_to_string`, which asks each configured engine for `get_template(name)`. For the DTL engine, the template loaders are tried in order. With `APP_DIRS=True` and no explicit `loaders` option, Django uses:\n\n```python\n[(\"django.template.loaders.cached.Loader\", [\n    \"django.template.loaders.filesystem.Loader\",       # each dir in DIRS, in order\n    \"django.template.loaders.app_directories.Loader\",  # each app's templates/, INSTALLED_APPS order\n])]\n```\n\nThe first loader to find the name wins; if none does, `TemplateDoesNotExist` lists every path tried (visible on the DEBUG page's \"Template-loader postmortem\").\n\n> **Version note:** since Django 4.1 the cached loader is used even when `DEBUG=True`, with the development server's autoreloader clearing it when template files change. On 4.0 and earlier it was only enabled automatically with `DEBUG=False`.\n\n### 3.2 Compiling: lexer, parser, node tree\n\n`Template(source)` compiles immediately:\n\n1. **Lexer** — a regex splits the source into tokens: `TEXT`, `VAR` (`{{ ... }}`), `BLOCK` (`{% ... %}`) and `COMMENT` (`{# ... #}`).\n2. **Parser** — walks the tokens. Text becomes a `TextNode`; a variable becomes a `VariableNode` wrapping a `FilterExpression` (the variable plus its filter chain, filters looked up and argument counts checked at compile time); a block token looks up the tag's compilation function in the loaded libraries (built-ins plus anything `{% load %}`ed), which consumes tokens up to its end tag (`{% endfor %}`) and returns a node such as `ForNode`.\n3. The result is a `NodeList` stored on the `Template`. Syntax errors (unknown tag, unclosed block, unknown filter) raise `TemplateSyntaxError` here — at compile time, not render time.\n\nWith the cached loader, steps 1–2 happen **once per template per process**; afterwards each render only walks the node tree.\n\n### 3.3 Rendering: the context stack and variable resolution\n\n`template.render(context)` calls `nodelist.render(context)`, concatenating each node's output. A `Context` is a **stack of dicts**: tags like `{% for %}` and `{% with %}` `push()` a new layer for their variables and `pop()` it at the end, so inner names shadow outer ones without leaking. `RequestContext` first pushes the output of every context processor, then the view's dict on top.\n\nResolving `{{ order.customer.name }}` splits on dots and, for each bit, tries in order:\n\n1. dictionary lookup — `obj[\"customer\"]`;\n2. attribute lookup — `obj.customer`;\n3. list-index lookup — `obj[int(\"customer\")]` (only for numeric bits).\n\nIf the result is callable it is **called with no arguments** — which is how `{{ user.get_full_name }}` and `{{ order.items.all }}` work — unless it has `do_not_call_in_templates = True`, or has `alters_data = True` (Django sets this on `save()` and `delete()`), in which case it renders as invalid. A missing variable renders as `string_if_invalid` (empty by default) instead of raising. Exceptions with `silent_variable_failure = True` (for example `ObjectDoesNotExist`) are swallowed the same way. That forgiving behaviour is convenient but hides typos — set `string_if_invalid` to something visible in development.\n\nEvery value is then passed through `render_value_in_context`, which converts it to a string, localizes it and **escapes it** if autoescaping is on and the value is not a `SafeString`.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj05a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">From render() to HTML: load once, compile once, render per request</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"190\" height=\"100\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"115\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">render(request, name, ctx)</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">engines tried in order</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">get_template(name)</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">RequestContext(request, ctx)</text>\n  <path d=\"M210,94 L246,94\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj05a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"250\" y=\"44\" width=\"190\" height=\"100\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"345\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">cached.Loader</text>\n  <text x=\"262\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">hit: return compiled Template</text>\n  <text x=\"262\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">miss: filesystem (DIRS), then</text>\n  <text x=\"262\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">app_directories (app order)</text>\n  <path d=\"M440,94 L476,94\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj05a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"480\" y=\"44\" width=\"380\" height=\"100\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"670\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Compile (on cache miss only)</text>\n  <text x=\"492\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Lexer: TEXT | VAR {{ }} | BLOCK {% %} | COMMENT {# #}</text>\n  <text x=\"492\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Parser: tag functions build nodes, filters resolved</text>\n  <text x=\"492\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">TemplateSyntaxError raised here, not at render</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"170\" width=\"430\" height=\"210\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"192\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">NodeList (compiled tree)</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"206\" width=\"180\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"223\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">TextNode \"&lt;h1&gt;Order \"</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"240\" width=\"180\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"257\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">VariableNode order.number</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"274\" width=\"180\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"291\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">ForNode item in order.items.all</text>\n  <rect x=\"70\" y=\"308\" width=\"150\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"325\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">VariableNode item.name</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"342\" width=\"180\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"359\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">IfNode / URLNode / ...</text>\n  <text x=\"240\" y=\"222\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">render(context) walks nodes</text>\n  <text x=\"240\" y=\"240\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">and concatenates output</text>\n  <text x=\"240\" y=\"268\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">ForNode pushes a context</text>\n  <text x=\"240\" y=\"286\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">layer per loop (forloop, item)</text>\n  <text x=\"240\" y=\"314\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">order.items.all runs a QUERY</text>\n  <text x=\"240\" y=\"332\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">item.product.name in the loop</text>\n  <text x=\"240\" y=\"350\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">= one query per row (N+1)</text>\n  <path d=\"M450,275 L486,275\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj05a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"490\" y=\"170\" width=\"370\" height=\"210\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"675\" y=\"192\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Resolving {{ order.customer.name }}</text>\n  <text x=\"502\" y=\"216\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">for each dotted bit, try in order:</text>\n  <text x=\"514\" y=\"236\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">1. dict lookup        obj[\"customer\"]</text>\n  <text x=\"514\" y=\"254\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">2. attribute lookup   obj.customer</text>\n  <text x=\"514\" y=\"272\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">3. list index         obj[int(bit)]</text>\n  <text x=\"502\" y=\"296\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">callable? call with no args (unless alters_data /</text>\n  <text x=\"502\" y=\"312\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">do_not_call_in_templates)</text>\n  <text x=\"502\" y=\"334\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">missing: string_if_invalid (\"\" by default)</text>\n  <text x=\"502\" y=\"356\" fill=\"#15803d\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">then escape unless SafeString</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.4 Inheritance: how blocks resolve\n\n`{% extends \"base.html\" %}` must be the **first tag** in a template. When the child renders, its `ExtendsNode` loads the parent (which may itself extend another template), collects every `{% block %}` from the child and all ancestors into a **block context** (child definitions first), and renders the *root* ancestor. Each `BlockNode` in the root renders the most-derived definition of that block; `{{ block.super }}` renders the next definition up the chain. Content in a child outside any block is ignored. Because the parent name can be a variable (`{% extends layout %}`), you can switch layouts per request.\n\n`{% include \"x.html\" %}` is different: it renders another template **with the current context** (or only the given variables with `{% include \"x.html\" with a=1 only %}`) and inserts the output. Blocks inside an included template do not participate in the including template's inheritance.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 360\" width=\"100%\" height=\"360\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj05b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Three-level inheritance: the most-derived block wins, block.super walks up</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"260\" height=\"290\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">base.html (root)</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"80\" width=\"228\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"98\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">block title: \"Acme\"</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"114\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">html, head, nav, footer</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"134\" width=\"228\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"152\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">block sidebar: default links</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"188\" width=\"228\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"206\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">block content: empty</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"242\" width=\"228\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"260\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">block scripts: core.js</text>\n  <rect x=\"310\" y=\"44\" width=\"260\" height=\"290\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">shop/base_shop.html</text>\n  <text x=\"326\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">extends \"base.html\"</text>\n  <rect x=\"326\" y=\"134\" width=\"228\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"336\" y=\"152\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">block sidebar: categories</text>\n  <rect x=\"326\" y=\"242\" width=\"228\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"336\" y=\"260\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">block scripts: block.super</text>\n  <text x=\"336\" y=\"276\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">+ cart.js</text>\n  <rect x=\"600\" y=\"44\" width=\"260\" height=\"290\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"730\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">shop/product_detail.html</text>\n  <text x=\"616\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">extends \"shop/base_shop.html\"</text>\n  <rect x=\"616\" y=\"100\" width=\"228\" height=\"26\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"626\" y=\"117\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">block title: product.name</text>\n  <rect x=\"616\" y=\"188\" width=\"228\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"626\" y=\"206\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">block content: product page</text>\n  <path d=\"M600,113 L268,102\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj05b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M310,156 L268,156\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj05b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M600,210 L268,210\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj05b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M310,264 L268,264\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj05b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"306\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Root template renders; each block slot</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"322\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">is filled by the deepest override (dashed)</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Output: title=product.name, sidebar=categories, content=product page, scripts=core.js + cart.js</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.5 Autoescaping: what it protects and what it doesn't\n\nWith autoescaping on (the default for DTL), `{{ comment.body }}` containing `<script>alert(1)</script>` renders as `&lt;script&gt;alert(1)&lt;/script&gt;`. Escaping converts the five characters `<`, `>`, `'`, `\"` and `&`, which makes values safe in **HTML element content** and in **quoted attribute values**.\n\nEscaping is **switched off** by `|safe`, `{% autoescape off %}`, `mark_safe()` in Python, and by any filter/tag that returns a `SafeString`. And it is **insufficient** in some contexts even when on:\n\n- inside `<script>` blocks or `on*=` event handler attributes — HTML entities are not JavaScript escaping;\n- in unquoted attributes — `<div class={{ cls }}>` lets a space start a new attribute;\n- in `href`/`src` — escaping doesn't stop `javascript:` URLs;\n- in CSS contexts.\n\nFor data that JavaScript needs, use `{{ data|json_script:\"order-data\" }}`, which emits a `<script type=\"application/json\" id=\"order-data\">` with `<`, `>` and `&` escaped as Unicode escapes, then `JSON.parse(document.getElementById(\"order-data\").textContent)`. For building HTML in Python, use `format_html()`, which escapes its arguments and marks the result safe. XSS in depth is in [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security).\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 Where templates live\n\n```text\nproject/\n├── templates/                 # DIRS: project-wide layouts and overrides\n│   ├── base.html\n│   ├── errors/404.html, 500.html\n│   ├── emails/base_email.html\n│   └── admin/base_site.html   # overrides the admin's template (DIRS searched first)\n└── apps/\n    └── shop/\n        ├── templates/shop/    # namespaced: \"shop/product_list.html\"\n        │   ├── base_shop.html\n        │   ├── product_list.html\n        │   └── partials/_product_card.html\n        └── templatetags/\n            ├── __init__.py\n            └── shop_tags.py   # {% load shop_tags %}\n```\n\n### 4.2 What happens on each render\n\n```text\nview: render(request, \"shop/product_list.html\", {\"products\": qs, \"page\": page})\n  -> engine.get_template()        cached loader: compiled Template (compile only on first use)\n  -> RequestContext(request)      push: csrf + every context processor\n                                    request -> {\"request\": request}\n                                    auth    -> {\"user\": SimpleLazyObject, \"perms\": PermWrapper}\n                                    messages-> {\"messages\": storage, \"DEFAULT_MESSAGE_LEVELS\": ...}\n                                  push: view context {\"products\": qs, \"page\": page}\n  -> template.render(context)     ExtendsNode -> base.html; blocks resolved\n                                  ForNode iterates qs (QuerySet evaluated here: 1 query)\n                                  IncludeNode \"_product_card.html\" per product (cached compile)\n  -> HttpResponse(html)\n```\n\nTwo performance facts fall out of this. QuerySets passed to templates are **lazy**, so the query runs during rendering — and any relation accessed per row in a loop runs another query unless the view used `select_related`/`prefetch_related`. And context processors run on **every** `RequestContext` render, so a processor that queries the database taxes every page.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example: settings, a base layout and a child page\n\n```python\n# config/settings.py (excerpt)\nTEMPLATES = [\n    {\n        \"BACKEND\": \"django.template.backends.django.DjangoTemplates\",\n        \"DIRS\": [BASE_DIR / \"templates\"],\n        \"APP_DIRS\": True,\n        \"OPTIONS\": {\n            \"context_processors\": [\n                \"django.template.context_processors.request\",\n                \"django.contrib.auth.context_processors.auth\",\n                \"django.contrib.messages.context_processors.messages\",\n                \"core.context_processors.site_settings\",       # our own, see §5.3\n            ],\n        },\n    },\n]\n```\n\n```html\n<!-- templates/base.html -->\n{% load static %}\n<!doctype html>\n<html lang=\"en\">\n<head>\n  <meta charset=\"utf-8\">\n  <title>{% block title %}Acme{% endblock %}</title>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"{% static 'css/site.css' %}\">\n</head>\n<body>\n  <nav>\n    <a href=\"{% url 'home' %}\">Home</a>\n    {% if user.is_authenticated %}\n      Hi, {{ user.get_short_name|default:user.username }}\n      <form method=\"post\" action=\"{% url 'logout' %}\">{% csrf_token %}<button>Log out</button></form>\n    {% else %}\n      <a href=\"{% url 'login' %}?next={{ request.path|urlencode }}\">Log in</a>\n    {% endif %}\n  </nav>\n  {% for message in messages %}\n    <div class=\"alert alert-{{ message.tags }}\">{{ message }}</div>\n  {% endfor %}\n  <main>{% block content %}{% endblock %}</main>\n  {% block scripts %}<script src=\"{% static 'js/core.js' %}\" defer></script>{% endblock %}\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n```html\n<!-- shop/templates/shop/product_list.html -->\n{% extends \"base.html\" %}\n{% load humanize shop_tags %}\n\n{% block title %}Products · {{ block.super }}{% endblock %}\n\n{% block content %}\n  <h1>Products</h1>\n  <ul>\n    {% for product in products %}\n      {% include \"shop/partials/_product_card.html\" with product=product only %}\n    {% empty %}\n      <li>No products yet.</li>\n    {% endfor %}\n  </ul>\n  <p>{{ products|length }} product{{ products|length|pluralize }}, updated {{ updated_at|naturaltime }}</p>\n{% endblock %}\n```\n\n```html\n<!-- shop/templates/shop/partials/_product_card.html -->\n<li class=\"card {% if forloop.first %}first{% endif %}\">\n  <a href=\"{{ product.get_absolute_url }}\">{{ product.name }}</a>\n  <span>{{ product.price|floatformat:2 }} {{ product.currency }}</span>\n  {% if product.description %}<p>{{ product.description|truncatewords:20 }}</p>{% endif %}\n</li>\n```\n\nNote `only` on the include: the partial sees just `product`, not `forloop` — so `forloop.first` there is empty. That's the price of isolation; pass what you need explicitly (`with product=product first=forloop.first only`).\n\n```python\n# shop/views.py\nfrom django.shortcuts import render\nfrom django.utils import timezone\nfrom .models import Product\n\ndef product_list(request):\n    products = Product.objects.filter(active=True).order_by(\"name\")\n    return render(request, \"shop/product_list.html\", {\n        \"products\": products,\n        \"updated_at\": timezone.now(),\n    })\n```\n\n`humanize` requires `\"django.contrib.humanize\"` in `INSTALLED_APPS`.\n\n### 5.2 Custom filters and tags\n\n```python\n# shop/templatetags/shop_tags.py\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom django import template\nfrom django.utils.html import format_html\n\nregister = template.Library()\n\n\n@register.filter\ndef money(value, currency=\"USD\"):\n    \"\"\"{{ product.price|money:\"EUR\" }} -> 'EUR 12.50' (plain text; autoescaped normally).\"\"\"\n    if value in (None, \"\"):\n        return \"\"\n    return f\"{currency} {Decimal(value):,.2f}\"\n\n\n@register.simple_tag(takes_context=True)\ndef active(context, url_name):\n    \"\"\"{% active 'shop:product-list' %} -> 'active' when the current route matches.\"\"\"\n    match = getattr(context[\"request\"], \"resolver_match\", None)\n    return \"active\" if match and match.view_name == url_name else \"\"\n\n\n@register.simple_tag\ndef badge(text, level=\"info\"):\n    # format_html escapes arguments and returns a SafeString.\n    return format_html('<span class=\"badge badge-{}\">{}</span>', level, text)\n\n\n@register.inclusion_tag(\"shop/partials/_cart_summary.html\", takes_context=True)\ndef cart_summary(context):\n    cart = context[\"request\"].session.get(\"cart\", {})\n    return {\"count\": sum(cart.values()), \"user\": context.get(\"user\")}\n```\n\n```html\n{% load shop_tags %}\n<a class=\"{% active 'shop:product-list' %}\" href=\"{% url 'shop:product-list' %}\">Shop</a>\n{{ product.price|money:\"EUR\" }}\n{% badge \"New\" \"success\" %}\n{% cart_summary %}\n```\n\nThe library module must live in a `templatetags` package (with `__init__.py`) inside an **installed app**, and the server must be restarted after creating a new library. Simple tags' return values are autoescaped unless they are `SafeString`s — which is why `badge` uses `format_html` instead of an f-string and `mark_safe`.\n\n> **Version note:** Django 5.2 adds `@register.simple_block_tag`, which makes block tags such as `{% card title=\"Orders\" %}...{% endcard %}` as easy to write as simple tags (the content between the tags is passed as `content`). Before 5.2 you write a compilation function and a `Node` subclass by hand. Django 5.1 added the built-in `{% querystring %}` tag for building query strings (`{% querystring page=page.next_page_number %}`) while preserving other `GET` parameters.\n\n### 5.3 Real-world example: a fast, safe product page\n\nA product detail page shows the product, its variants, reviews with authors, related products, a sidebar of categories that changes rarely, and passes data to a JavaScript widget. Three production concerns: no N+1 queries, no XSS, and no heavy work on every render.\n\n```python\n# shop/views.py\nfrom django.db.models import Prefetch\nfrom django.shortcuts import get_object_or_404, render\nfrom .models import Product, Review\n\ndef product_detail(request, slug):\n    product = get_object_or_404(\n        Product.objects.select_related(\"brand\", \"category\").prefetch_related(\n            \"variants\",\n            Prefetch(\"reviews\",\n                     queryset=Review.objects.select_related(\"author\").order_by(\"-created_at\")[:20],\n                     to_attr=\"recent_reviews\"),\n        ),\n        slug=slug, active=True,\n    )\n    widget_data = {\n        \"productId\": product.pk,\n        \"variants\": [{\"sku\": v.sku, \"price\": str(v.price), \"stock\": v.stock} for v in product.variants.all()],\n    }\n    return render(request, \"shop/product_detail.html\", {\"product\": product, \"widget_data\": widget_data})\n```\n\n```html\n<!-- shop/templates/shop/product_detail.html -->\n{% extends \"shop/base_shop.html\" %}\n{% load cache shop_tags %}\n\n{% block title %}{{ product.name }} · {{ block.super }}{% endblock %}\n\n{% block sidebar %}\n  {% cache 600 category_sidebar product.category_id %}\n    {% include \"shop/partials/_category_tree.html\" %}\n  {% endcache %}\n{% endblock %}\n\n{% block content %}\n  <h1>{{ product.name }}</h1>\n  <p class=\"brand\">{{ product.brand.name }}</p>            {# select_related: no extra query #}\n  <div class=\"description\">{{ product.description_html|safe }}</div>  {# sanitized on save, see below #}\n\n  <h2>Reviews</h2>\n  {% for review in product.recent_reviews %}              {# to_attr list: no query, no re-filter #}\n    <article>\n      <strong>{{ review.author.display_name }}</strong>   {# escaped: user-controlled #}\n      <p>{{ review.body|linebreaksbr }}</p>\n    </article>\n  {% empty %}\n    <p>No reviews yet.</p>\n  {% endfor %}\n\n  <div id=\"variant-picker\"></div>\n  {{ widget_data|json_script:\"variant-data\" }}\n{% endblock %}\n\n{% block scripts %}\n  {{ block.super }}\n  <script>\n    const data = JSON.parse(document.getElementById(\"variant-data\").textContent);\n    VariantPicker.mount(document.getElementById(\"variant-picker\"), data);\n  </script>\n{% endblock %}\n```\n\nThe rendered `json_script` output escapes HTML-significant characters so that a variant SKU like `</script><script>evil()` cannot break out:\n\n```html\n<script id=\"variant-data\" type=\"application/json\">{\"productId\": 7, \"variants\": [{\"sku\": \"</script>...\", \"price\": \"12.50\", \"stock\": 3}]}</script>\n```\n\nThe page issues a fixed number of queries regardless of how many reviews or variants exist:\n\n```sql\n-- 1: product + brand + category (JOINs)\nSELECT ... FROM \"shop_product\"\nINNER JOIN \"shop_brand\" ON (...) INNER JOIN \"shop_category\" ON (...)\nWHERE (\"shop_product\".\"active\" AND \"shop_product\".\"slug\" = 'blue-mug') LIMIT 21;\n-- 2: variants for that product\nSELECT ... FROM \"shop_variant\" WHERE \"shop_variant\".\"product_id\" IN (7);\n-- 3: recent reviews with authors (sliced Prefetch uses a window function on 4.2+)\nSELECT ... FROM \"shop_review\" INNER JOIN \"accounts_user\" ON (...) WHERE ... \"product_id\" IN (7) ...;\n```\n\n`description_html|safe` is acceptable **only** because the HTML is produced by trusted staff and sanitized with an allow-list sanitizer (for example `nh3` or `bleach`) when saved; marking raw user input safe is the classic Django XSS bug. The category sidebar, identical for every product in a category, is fragment-cached for 10 minutes keyed by category ([Ch 29 · Caching](topic.html?p=29-caching)).\n\nA context processor that adds site-wide settings cheaply, using the cache rather than querying on every render:\n\n```python\n# core/context_processors.py\nfrom django.core.cache import cache\nfrom .models import SiteSettings\n\ndef site_settings(request):\n    data = cache.get_or_set(\"site_settings:v1\", lambda: SiteSettings.load().as_dict(), 300)\n    return {\"site\": data}\n```\n\n### 5.4 Rendering outside a request: emails\n\n```python\nfrom django.core.mail import EmailMultiAlternatives\nfrom django.template.loader import render_to_string\n\ndef send_order_confirmation(order):\n    ctx = {\"order\": order, \"site_url\": \"https://shop.example.com\"}\n    text = render_to_string(\"emails/order_confirmed.txt\", ctx)\n    html = render_to_string(\"emails/order_confirmed.html\", ctx)   # no request: no context processors\n    msg = EmailMultiAlternatives(f\"Order {order.number} confirmed\", text, to=[order.email])\n    msg.attach_alternative(html, \"text/html\")\n    msg.send()\n```\n\nWithout a request there is no `RequestContext`, so `user`, `request` and `messages` are not in the context, and `{% url %}` produces relative paths — prefix absolute links yourself.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Aspect | DTL | Jinja2 (`django.template.backends.jinja2.Jinja2`) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Expressiveness | Intentionally limited; no calls with arguments | Python-like expressions, macros, calls with args |\n| Safety | Autoescape on; logic stays in Python | Autoescape on in Django's backend by default; more rope |\n| Speed | Fine with cached loader | Generally faster rendering (compiles to Python bytecode) |\n| Ecosystem | Admin, third-party apps ship DTL templates | Need a custom `environment` to expose `url`, `static`; context processors via the backend's `context_processors` option |\n| Learning curve | Small | Small for Python devs |\n\n| Server-rendered templates | SPA + JSON API |\n|---|---|\n| One codebase, SEO-friendly, fast first paint, simple auth (sessions + CSRF) | Rich client interactivity, separate deployable front end |\n| Partial updates need HTMX/Turbo-style tooling | Two codebases, API versioning, token auth, more JS |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Admin tools, back-office apps, content sites, emails and PDFs — templates are the fastest path.\n- HTMX-style apps where the server returns HTML fragments for partial page updates.\n- Jinja2 when you have a template-heavy site and measurable rendering cost, or need macros.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Don't put business logic, permissions decisions or queries in templates — compute in the view and pass simple data.\n- Don't use DTL to generate non-HTML formats where escaping is wrong (e.g. CSV or JSON); use the proper serializer, or `{% autoescape off %}` with great care for plain-text emails.\n- Don't build complex interactive UIs purely in templates if they need lots of client state; a front-end framework fits better.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**N+1 queries from templates.**\n\n```python\n# Before: 1 query for orders + 1 per order for customer + 1 per order for items\norders = Order.objects.filter(status=\"paid\")\n```\n\n```html\n{% for o in orders %}{{ o.customer.name }} ({{ o.items.count }}){% endfor %}\n```\n\n```python\n# After: 1 query with JOIN + an annotation instead of per-row COUNT\nfrom django.db.models import Count\norders = (Order.objects.filter(status=\"paid\")\n          .select_related(\"customer\")\n          .annotate(item_count=Count(\"items\")))\n```\n\n```html\n{% for o in orders %}{{ o.customer.name }} ({{ o.item_count }}){% endfor %}\n```\n\nDetails in [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization).\n\n**`|safe` on user input.** `{{ comment.body|safe }}` is an XSS vulnerability. Sanitize with an allow-list on input if you must accept HTML; otherwise let autoescaping work and use `linebreaksbr`/`urlize` for formatting.\n\n**Data in `<script>` via `{{ }}`.** `<script>var name = \"{{ user.name }}\";</script>` is not safe even with autoescape (and `escapejs` output must still be quoted correctly). Use `json_script`.\n\n**Un-namespaced app templates.** `shop/templates/detail.html` collides with `blog/templates/detail.html`; whichever app is earlier wins everywhere. Always use `app/templates/app/...`.\n\n**Heavy context processors.** A processor that runs `Category.objects.all()` or counts notifications executes on every page, including error pages and AJAX fragments. Make it lazy (return a `SimpleLazyObject` or a callable) or cache it.\n\n**Silent typos.** `{{ ordr.total }}` renders nothing. In development set `\"string_if_invalid\": \"INVALID[%s]\"` in `OPTIONS` (but not in production — the admin and some apps rely on empty strings), and render templates in tests.\n\n**Calling methods that change data.** Django blocks `save()`/`delete()` (they have `alters_data = True`), but your own `def send_reminder(self)` will happily run when a template outputs `{{ order.send_reminder }}`. Mark such methods `alters_data = True` or don't expose those objects.\n\n**Best practices**\n\n- Keep templates dumb: prepare data in the view; prefer `annotate()` to template arithmetic.\n- One base layout, a small number of intermediate layouts, many small partials named `_x.html`.\n- Use `{% url %}` and `{% static %}` everywhere; never hard-code paths.\n- Use `format_html` for HTML in Python; `json_script` for JS data; never `mark_safe` on external input.\n- Fragment-cache expensive, shared regions with a key that includes everything they depend on.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: slow pages with fast views.** *Symptom:* view code takes 20 ms, response takes 800 ms. *Root cause:* lazy QuerySets and relations evaluated during rendering (N+1), or expensive template tags. *Fix:* Django Debug Toolbar's SQL panel attributes queries to the template line that triggered them; fix with `select_related`/`prefetch_related`/`annotate`. In tests, wrap the client call in `self.assertNumQueries(n)` so the template's queries are counted too.\n\n**Pitfall: `TemplateDoesNotExist` only in production.** *Root cause:* an app missing from production `INSTALLED_APPS`, case-sensitive file systems (macOS dev is case-insensitive by default, Linux isn't), or templates excluded from the Docker image. *Fix:* the error's postmortem lists every path tried; add a test that renders every template.\n\n**Pitfall: stale templates after deploy.** With the cached loader, each process keeps compiled templates in memory until restart. Rolling deploys replace processes, so this is only a problem if you hot-swap template files without restarting.\n\n**Pitfall: fragment cache leaks data between users.** `{% cache 600 header %}` around a block containing `{{ user.username }}` shows the first user's name to everyone. Include the varying values in the key: `{% cache 600 header request.user.pk %}`, or keep per-user parts outside the cached fragment.\n\n**Pitfall: XSS through translations or admin-edited content.** Strings from `{% blocktranslate %}` and admin-managed \"HTML snippets\" are often marked safe. Treat staff-entered HTML as untrusted unless it's sanitized; set a Content Security Policy (via `django-csp` on 5.2) as defence in depth.\n\n**Debugging**\n\n- DEBUG error page: template source with the failing line highlighted and the loader postmortem.\n- `OPTIONS[\"debug\"]` follows `DEBUG` by default; it adds origin info used by debug pages.\n- Debug Toolbar's *Templates* panel: which templates rendered, with their contexts; *SQL* panel: queries with template stack traces.\n- `python manage.py shell -c \"from django.template.loader import get_template; print(get_template('shop/x.html').origin)\"` to see which file won.\n\n**Scaling notes.** DTL rendering is CPU work in the worker; for most pages it is small compared to queries. When it isn't: cache fragments that are identical across users, cache whole pages for anonymous users ([Ch 29](topic.html?p=29-caching)), reduce `{% include %}` inside huge loops (each include has overhead — inline or use a custom inclusion tag), and consider Jinja2 for rendering-heavy pages. Move rendering of bulk emails or PDFs to Celery workers so web workers stay free.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: How does Django find a template when you call `render()`?**\nA: It iterates the engines in `TEMPLATES`; for the DTL engine, it tries the configured loaders in order. By default that's the cached loader wrapping the filesystem loader (each directory in `DIRS`) and then the app-directories loader (each installed app's `templates/` folder, in `INSTALLED_APPS` order). The first match wins, and if nothing matches `TemplateDoesNotExist` lists every path tried. That ordering is why you namespace app templates (`shop/templates/shop/...`) and how you override third-party templates, such as the admin's, from `DIRS` or an earlier app.\n\n**Q: How does template inheritance work?**\nA: A child template starts with `{% extends \"base.html\" %}` and overrides named `{% block %}` regions; the parent defines the layout and default block content. At render time Django loads the chain of ancestors, collects all block definitions, and renders the root template, filling each block with the most-derived definition. `{{ block.super }}` inserts the parent's version of the block. `extends` must be the first tag, and content outside blocks in a child is ignored.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `{% include %}` and `{% extends %}`?**\nA: `extends` establishes an inheritance relationship: the child customizes slots in a parent layout, and there can be only one `extends` per template. `include` inserts the rendered output of another template at that point, using the current context (or only explicit variables with `with ... only`). Blocks inside an included template don't override anything in the including template. Use `extends` for layouts and `include` for reusable fragments.\n\n**Q: What are context processors and when should you write one?**\nA: A context processor is a function that takes the request and returns a dict merged into the context of every template rendered with a `RequestContext` — which `render(request, ...)` always uses. Built-ins add `request`, `user` and `perms`, and `messages`. Write one for data genuinely needed on almost every page, such as site settings or the current tenant. Keep them cheap and lazy, because they run on every render including error pages, and a database query there taxes every request.\n\n**Q: How does Django's autoescaping prevent XSS, and how do you disable it?**\nA: By default every variable output is HTML-escaped: `<`, `>`, `'`, `\"` and `&` become entities, so user input renders as text instead of markup. It's disabled per variable with the `|safe` filter, per region with `{% autoescape off %}`, or for a value by marking it safe in Python with `mark_safe` or `format_html`. You should only mark content safe when you generated it or sanitized it with an allow-list.\n\n**Q: Where does autoescaping not protect you?**\nA: HTML escaping is correct for element content and quoted attribute values, but not for JavaScript contexts (inside `<script>` or `on*` attributes), unquoted attributes, URL attributes (`href=\"{{ url }}\"` with a `javascript:` URL), or CSS. For JavaScript data use `json_script`; always quote attributes; validate URLs' schemes. Anything marked safe — `|safe`, `mark_safe`, custom tags returning `SafeString` — bypasses escaping entirely.\n\n**Q: How do you write a custom template filter or tag?**\nA: Create a `templatetags` package in an installed app, add a module with `register = template.Library()`, and decorate functions: `@register.filter` for filters, `@register.simple_tag` for tags that return a value, `@register.inclusion_tag(\"tpl.html\")` for tags that render a template with their own context, with `takes_context=True` to access the current context. Load it with `{% load module_name %}`. Return plain strings to keep autoescaping, or use `format_html` to return safe HTML. Fully custom block tags need a compilation function and a `Node` subclass, or `simple_block_tag` on Django 5.2.\n\n**Q: Why can't you call methods with arguments in Django templates?**\nA: The DTL is deliberately restricted so that templates stay presentation-only and safe to hand to non-programmers; logic belongs in views, model methods, or custom tags and filters. Variable lookups can call zero-argument callables (`{{ user.get_full_name }}`), and filters take one argument. Django also refuses to call methods flagged `alters_data`, like `save()` and `delete()`, from templates. If you need more expressiveness, write a filter or tag, or use the Jinja2 backend.\n\n**Q: How do templates cause N+1 queries and how do you prevent them?**\nA: QuerySets are lazy, so a QuerySet passed to a template is evaluated during rendering, and every relation accessed inside `{% for %}` — `{{ order.customer.name }}`, `{{ order.items.count }}` — can run another query per row. Prevent it in the view with `select_related` for foreign keys and one-to-one, `prefetch_related` for reverse and many-to-many relations, and `annotate` for counts and sums. Detect it with Debug Toolbar's SQL panel and lock it in with `assertNumQueries` in tests.\n\n**Q: What does the cached template loader do?**\nA: It wraps the other loaders and keeps compiled `Template` objects in memory per process, so each template is read and parsed once rather than on every render. It has been enabled by default when `loaders` isn't specified, including in development since Django 4.1, with the autoreloader clearing it when files change. In production that means template changes need a process restart, which rolling deploys handle.\n\n**Q: A page's response time grew from 100 ms to 2 s as data grew, but the view's code is trivial. How do you investigate? (Senior)**\nA: Growth with data size points to per-row work during rendering. I'd open the page with Debug Toolbar or APM and look at the query count and the template stack traces attached to each query — typically a relation access or `.count` inside a loop, or a custom tag that queries per call. I'd fix it in the view with `select_related`, `prefetch_related` (with a `Prefetch` and `to_attr` for filtered relations) and `annotate`, and paginate if the list is unbounded. Then I'd check render CPU itself: thousands of `{% include %}` calls or heavy filters in a loop, and fragment-cache shared regions. Finally I'd add an `assertNumQueries` test so the number of queries stays constant as data grows.\n\n**Q: How would you safely pass server data to a JavaScript component in a Django template? (Senior)**\nA: Use the `json_script` filter: `{{ data|json_script:\"widget-data\" }}` emits a `<script type=\"application/json\">` element whose content escapes `<`, `>` and `&` as Unicode escapes, so a value like `</script>` can't terminate the element; the script then reads it with `JSON.parse(el.textContent)`. Avoid interpolating `{{ }}` inside inline scripts or `on*` attributes, because HTML escaping isn't JavaScript escaping. Serialize only the fields the client needs — not whole model dicts with private fields — and add a Content Security Policy to reduce the impact of any mistake. For larger or dynamic data, an authenticated JSON endpoint is cleaner.\n\n**Q: How would you design a reusable UI component system on top of DTL for a large team? (Senior)**\nA: I'd keep one base layout and a few section layouts, and build components as namespaced partials (`components/_card.html`) exposed through inclusion tags or Django 5.2's `simple_block_tag`, so each component has a clear, explicit set of arguments instead of depending on whatever is in the context. Components return escaped output by default and build HTML with `format_html`. I'd document them in a living style guide page and render every component in tests. For HTMX-style partial updates I'd structure pages so fragments render standalone, with libraries like django-template-partials or django-cotton as options. Performance-wise, avoid deep include chains inside large loops and cache shared fragments.\n\n**Q: What happens when a template references a variable that doesn't exist?**\nA: Django doesn't raise; it renders the engine's `string_if_invalid` value, an empty string by default, and filters are applied to it. The same happens for exceptions marked `silent_variable_failure`, such as `ObjectDoesNotExist`. That is forgiving in production but hides typos, so set `string_if_invalid` to a visible marker in development and render templates in tests. Errors in tag syntax are different: they raise `TemplateSyntaxError` when the template is compiled.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Feature | Syntax | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| Variable | `{{ order.customer.name }}` | dict, attr, index; callables called |\n| Filter | `{{ price\\|floatformat:2 }}` | one argument max |\n| Default | `{{ name\\|default:\"Anon\" }}` | falsy values |\n| Loop | `{% for x in xs %}...{% empty %}...{% endfor %}` | `forloop.counter`, `.first`, `.last` |\n| Condition | `{% if a and not b %}` | `==`, `in`, `is` supported |\n| Inherit | `{% extends \"base.html\" %}` + `{% block %}` | first tag; `{{ block.super }}` |\n| Include | `{% include \"x.html\" with a=1 only %}` | current context unless `only` |\n| URL | `{% url \"app:name\" pk=o.pk %}` | reverse by name |\n| Static | `{% load static %}{% static \"css/a.css\" %}` | uses `STORAGES[\"staticfiles\"]` |\n| CSRF | `{% csrf_token %}` | inside every POST form |\n| Cache | `{% load cache %}{% cache 600 key var %}` | include varying values in key |\n| JS data | `{{ data\\|json_script:\"id\" }}` | never `{{ }}` inside `<script>` |\n| Query string | `{% querystring page=2 %}` | Django 5.1+ |\n| Custom | `templatetags/x.py`, `Library()`, `@register.filter/simple_tag/inclusion_tag` | `{% load x %}` |\n\n- Loaders: DIRS first, then apps in `INSTALLED_APPS` order; first match wins.\n- Namespace app templates: `app/templates/app/...`.\n- Compile once (cached loader), render per request by walking the node tree.\n- Context is a stack; `RequestContext` adds context processors.\n- Autoescape is on; `|safe`/`mark_safe` turn it off — only for trusted or sanitized HTML.\n- Autoescape doesn't cover JS, CSS, unquoted attributes or `javascript:` URLs.\n- Templates evaluate lazy QuerySets: optimize relations in the view.\n- Missing variables render as `\"\"` silently.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" django-debug-toolbar && django-admin startproject config . && python manage.py startapp shop`.\n\n1. **Inheritance chain.** Build `base.html` → `shop/base_shop.html` → `shop/product_detail.html` as in §3.4, using `{{ block.super }}` in the scripts block. Predict the output before rendering.\n2. **Loader order.** Create `templates/shop/product_list.html` and `shop/templates/shop/product_list.html` with different text. Which wins? Use `get_template(...).origin` to confirm, then remove the project-level one.\n3. **XSS lab.** Save a product description containing `<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>`. Render it with `{{ }}`, with `|safe`, and inside a `<script>` string; then fix the script case with `json_script`.\n4. **N+1 hunt.** Create 50 orders with customers and items. Render a list showing customer names and item counts; count queries with Debug Toolbar or `assertNumQueries`; reduce it to 1–2 queries.\n5. **Custom tags.** Implement `money`, `active` and `badge` from §5.2; write tests that render `Template(\"{% load shop_tags %}{% badge '<b>' %}\")` and assert the `<b>` is escaped.\n\n**Mini project — Storefront templates.** Build a small shop UI: a base layout with nav and messages, a product list with pagination using `{% querystring %}`, a product page with reviews and a JS variant picker fed by `json_script`, a fragment-cached category sidebar, an inclusion tag for the cart summary, and an HTML + text order confirmation email rendered with `render_to_string`. Add tests that render every template, assert query counts for list and detail pages, and assert that a malicious product name is escaped everywhere.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 04 · Views, HttpRequest & HttpResponse](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses), [Ch 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation) (rendering forms and errors), [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization), [Ch 17 · Class-Based & Generic Views](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views) (`get_context_data`, `TemplateResponse`), [Ch 20 · Static & Media Files](topic.html?p=20-static-media-files) (`{% static %}`), [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security) (XSS and CSP), [Ch 29 · Caching in Django](topic.html?p=29-caching) (fragment caching).\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · Validation & Input Sanitization](../rest-api/topic.html?p=17-validation-input-sanitization), [REST API · OWASP API Security](../rest-api/topic.html?p=23-owasp-api-security), [System Design · CDN](../system-design/topic.html?p=11-cdn).\n\n- **Templates (topic guide)** — Django docs · *Beginner* · engines, loaders, `TEMPLATES` and the DTL overview. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/templates/>\n- **The Django template language** — Django docs · *Beginner* · variables, filters, tags, inheritance and autoescaping for template authors. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/templates/language/>\n- **Built-in template tags and filters** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the complete reference. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/templates/builtins/>\n- **How to create custom template tags and filters** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · filters, simple/inclusion tags, autoescaping rules, writing `Node`s. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/custom-template-tags/>\n- **The Django template language: for Python programmers** — Django docs · *Advanced* · `Context`, `RequestContext`, loaders and the rendering API. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/templates/api/>\n- **Security in Django: XSS protection** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · what autoescaping covers and its limits. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/security/>\n- **OWASP XSS Prevention Cheat Sheet** — OWASP · *Intermediate* · output encoding per context (HTML, attribute, JS, URL, CSS). <https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Cross_Site_Scripting_Prevention_Cheat_Sheet.html>\n- **django-template-partials** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · reusable named partials, handy for HTMX fragment rendering. <https://github.com/carltongibson/django-template-partials>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 05.*\n", "06-forms-validation": "# 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation\n\n> **In one line:** A Django form is a validation pipeline with a rendering layer on top: untrusted strings go in, typed and checked `cleaned_data` comes out, and every step (`to_python` → `validate` → validators → `clean_<field>` → `clean` → model validation) has a precise order you must know to put each rule in the right place.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 04 · Views, HttpRequest & HttpResponse](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses) (where `request.POST` and `request.FILES` come from) and [Ch 05 · Django Template Language](topic.html?p=05-templates) (how forms render and autoescape).\n\nEvery web application eventually accepts input from people it does not trust. The browser sends you a `QueryDict` of **strings** — `\"42\"`, `\"2026-10-25\"`, `\"on\"`, `\"\"` — and your code wants an `int`, a `date`, a `bool` and a decision about whether \"empty\" is allowed. In between sit a dozen jobs: converting types, rejecting nonsense, enforcing business rules that span several fields, showing errors next to the right input, re-filling the form so the user does not retype everything, and protecting against people who post fields you never rendered.\n\nThe naive approach is to do this by hand in the view:\n\n```python\ndef signup(request):\n    if request.method == \"POST\":\n        age = int(request.POST[\"age\"])          # ValueError on \"abc\", KeyError if missing\n        email = request.POST.get(\"email\", \"\")\n        if \"@\" not in email:\n            return HttpResponse(\"bad email\")    # where does this error show? which field?\n        User.objects.create(email=email, age=age)\n```\n\nIt crashes on bad input (a 500 instead of a friendly error), mixes parsing with business rules, cannot re-render the form with the user's values, and has to be rewritten for every view. Worse, the rules live in one view — the API endpoint, the admin and the management command all re-implement (or forget) them.\n\nDjango's **forms framework** solves this with three ideas. A **Form class** declares fields declaratively, like a model. Each **Field** knows how to turn a string into a Python value and how to validate it. And a fixed, documented **validation pipeline** runs when you call `is_valid()`, collecting *all* errors (not just the first) into `form.errors`, keyed by field. A **ModelForm** goes further and derives the fields from a model, then runs the model's own validation (`full_clean()`, uniqueness, constraints) before you save. The same Form object renders itself to HTML, carries the user's submitted values back into the inputs, and escapes everything.\n\n> **Why this matters:** \"Where do I put this validation rule?\" is the single most common forms question in interviews and code review. The answer depends on the pipeline order: single-value type checks go in the Field or a validator, rules about one field go in `clean_<field>()`, cross-field rules go in `clean()`, and invariants that must hold *everywhere* (admin, API, shell) belong on the model. This chapter makes that order second nature.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **`Form`** — a class whose class attributes are `Field` instances; the metaclass `DeclarativeFieldsMetaclass` collects them into `base_fields`, and each instance deep-copies them into `self.fields`. *Why it matters:* you can mutate `self.fields` per instance (e.g. limit a queryset to the current user) without affecting other requests.\n- **Bound vs unbound form** — a form is **bound** if you passed `data` (and optionally `files`), even an empty dict; otherwise it is **unbound** and only renders `initial`. *Why it matters:* only bound forms validate; `is_valid()` on an unbound form is always `False` with no errors.\n- **`Field`** — converts and validates one value: `forms.CharField`, `IntegerField`, `EmailField`, `DateField`, `ChoiceField`, `ModelChoiceField`, `FileField`, `ImageField`… *Why it matters:* the field, not your view, owns type conversion.\n- **`Widget`** — the HTML representation of a field (`TextInput`, `Select`, `CheckboxInput`, `ClearableFileInput`) and the code that reads its value back out of the POST data (`value_from_datadict`). *Why it matters:* field = *what* the data is; widget = *how* it is displayed and extracted.\n- **`cleaned_data`** — the dict of validated, typed values. Exists only after validation; contains only fields that passed. *Why it matters:* never read `request.POST` after a form validated it — read `cleaned_data`.\n- **`form.errors`** — an `ErrorDict` mapping field name → `ErrorList`; cross-field errors live under `NON_FIELD_ERRORS` (`\"__all__\"`) and are returned by `form.non_field_errors()`. *Why it matters:* this is what the template shows and what tests assert on.\n- **`ValidationError`** — the exception every validation step raises; carries `message`, `code` and `params`. *Why it matters:* using `code=` lets callers (and tests, and `error_messages`) identify errors without parsing English.\n- **Validator** — a callable that takes a value and raises `ValidationError` (`MinValueValidator`, `RegexValidator`, `FileExtensionValidator`, or your own function). *Why it matters:* validators are reusable across form fields **and** model fields.\n- **`clean_<fieldname>()`** — a form method for single-field rules that need form context (the current user, other DB lookups). Must **return** the cleaned value.\n- **`clean()`** — the form-wide hook for rules involving several fields; runs even if some fields failed.\n- **`ModelForm`** — a form generated from a model via `class Meta: model = ...; fields = [...]`; its `save()` creates or updates `self.instance`.\n- **`initial`** — values shown on an unbound form (and used to compute `has_changed()`); *not* a default that is applied on submit.\n- **Formset** — a collection of the same form repeated N times, coordinated by a hidden **management form** (`TOTAL_FORMS`, `INITIAL_FORMS`, `MIN_NUM_FORMS`, `MAX_NUM_FORMS`).\n- **Inline formset** — a model formset bound to a parent object through a ForeignKey (order + order lines).\n- **`request.FILES`** — uploaded files, populated only when the form is `enctype=\"multipart/form-data\"` and the method is POST.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### The validation pipeline, step by step\n\nCalling `form.is_valid()` checks `self.is_bound and not self.errors`. Accessing `self.errors` triggers `full_clean()` the first time (the result is cached on the instance). `Form.full_clean()` does three things, in order:\n\n1. **`_clean_fields()`** — for each field in declaration order:\n   1. Read the raw value through the widget: `field.widget.value_from_datadict(data, files, prefixed_name)` (disabled fields use their initial value instead — the browser value is ignored, which is why `disabled=True` is a safe way to show read-only data).\n   2. `value = field.clean(value)`, which is itself three steps:\n      - **`to_python(value)`** — convert the string to the Python type (`\"42\"` → `42`); raises `ValidationError(code=\"invalid\")` if it cannot.\n      - **`validate(value)`** — field-intrinsic checks that are not validators, chiefly `required` (raises `code=\"required\"` for empty values) and, for `ChoiceField`, \"is this one of the choices\".\n      - **`run_validators(value)`** — runs every validator in `field.validators` (including those implied by `max_length`, `min_value`…), **collecting all their errors** rather than stopping at the first.\n   3. Store `cleaned_data[name] = value`.\n   4. If the form defines **`clean_<name>()`**, call it; its **return value** replaces `cleaned_data[name]`.\n   5. Any `ValidationError` in steps 2–4 goes to `self.add_error(name, e)`, which also **removes** the name from `cleaned_data`.\n2. **`_clean_form()`** — call **`self.clean()`**. If it returns a dict, that becomes `cleaned_data` (returning `None` keeps the existing one). A `ValidationError` raised here becomes a non-field error.\n3. **`_post_clean()`** — a hook that does nothing on `Form` but is where **`ModelForm`** builds the instance and runs **model validation**.\n\nTwo consequences fall straight out of that order. First, `clean_<field>()` only runs if the field's own `clean()` succeeded, so inside it `self.cleaned_data[name]` is guaranteed to exist and be typed. Second, `clean()` runs **even if fields failed**, so inside it you must use `self.cleaned_data.get(\"x\")` — a failed field is simply absent.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 430\" width=\"100%\" height=\"430\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj06a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj06b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">form.is_valid() &#8594; full_clean(): the exact order</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"120\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">raw POST string</text>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">\"42\" / \"\" / \"abc\"</text>\n  <path d=\"M142,69 L170,69\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"172\" y=\"36\" width=\"520\" height=\"150\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#eff6ff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"432\" y=\"54\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e40af\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. _clean_fields() &#8212; per field, in declaration order</text>\n  <rect x=\"186\" y=\"64\" width=\"110\" height=\"46\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"241\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">widget</text>\n  <text x=\"241\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">value_from_datadict</text>\n  <path d=\"M298,87 L312,87\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"314\" y=\"64\" width=\"100\" height=\"46\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"364\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">to_python()</text>\n  <text x=\"364\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">\"42\" &#8594; 42</text>\n  <path d=\"M416,87 L430,87\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"432\" y=\"64\" width=\"100\" height=\"46\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"482\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">validate()</text>\n  <text x=\"482\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">required, choices</text>\n  <path d=\"M534,87 L548,87\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"550\" y=\"64\" width=\"128\" height=\"46\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"614\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">run_validators()</text>\n  <text x=\"614\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">collects ALL errors</text>\n  <text x=\"432\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e40af\" font-size=\"10\">field.clean(value) = to_python &#8594; validate &#8594; run_validators &#8594; cleaned_data[name]</text>\n  <rect x=\"314\" y=\"138\" width=\"364\" height=\"38\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"496\" y=\"154\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">clean_&lt;name&gt;() &#8212; only if field.clean passed</text>\n  <text x=\"496\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"9\">must RETURN the value; it replaces cleaned_data[name]</text>\n  <path d=\"M432,188 L432,212\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"172\" y=\"214\" width=\"520\" height=\"56\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"432\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#92400e\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. _clean_form() &#8594; form.clean()</text>\n  <text x=\"432\" y=\"254\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"10\">cross-field rules; ALWAYS runs; failed fields are absent &#8594; use cleaned_data.get()</text>\n  <path d=\"M432,272 L432,296\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"172\" y=\"298\" width=\"520\" height=\"66\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"432\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#5b21b6\" font-weight=\"bold\">3. _post_clean() &#8212; ModelForm only</text>\n  <text x=\"432\" y=\"338\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6d28d9\" font-size=\"10\">construct_instance() &#8594; instance.full_clean(exclude=...)</text>\n  <text x=\"432\" y=\"354\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#6d28d9\" font-size=\"10\">model field validators, Model.clean(), unique checks, Meta.constraints</text>\n  <rect x=\"712\" y=\"120\" width=\"150\" height=\"120\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"787\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#991b1b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ValidationError</text>\n  <text x=\"787\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">add_error(name, e)</text>\n  <text x=\"787\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">removes name from</text>\n  <text x=\"787\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">cleaned_data</text>\n  <text x=\"787\" y=\"214\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">clean() errors &#8594;</text>\n  <text x=\"787\" y=\"228\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">\"__all__\"</text>\n  <path d=\"M694,110 L710,130\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M694,242 L710,232\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"384\" width=\"842\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"441\" y=\"405\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"11\">is_valid() = is_bound and not errors &#183; errors computed once and cached &#183; every step runs; you get ALL errors, not the first</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### How Django implements it internally\n\n- `django.forms.forms.BaseForm.errors` is a property: `if self._errors is None: self.full_clean()`. That is why calling `is_valid()` twice is cheap, and why mutating `data` after validation does nothing.\n- `Field.clean()` lives in `django/forms/fields.py`; `run_validators` loops over validators, catches each `ValidationError`, extends a list, and raises one combined error at the end. That is the \"collect all errors\" behaviour.\n- **Empty values**: each field has `empty_values` (by default `(None, \"\", [], (), {})`). `validate()` raises `required` if the value is in that set, and `run_validators` *skips* validators for empty values — so a `MinLengthValidator` never fires on an optional empty field.\n- `CharField.to_python` strips whitespace (`strip=True` by default) and returns `empty_value` (`\"\"` by default) for empty input — so `\"   \"` becomes `\"\"` and fails `required`.\n- In Django 5.x, `_clean_fields` iterates `self._bound_items()` and reads each value through `BoundField` (`bf.data` or `bf.initial` for disabled fields).\n\n### Where a rule belongs\n\n| Rule | Put it in | Why |\n|---|---|---|\n| \"Must be an integer 1–100\" | Field options (`IntegerField(min_value=1, max_value=100)`) | Built-in, gives proper HTML attributes too |\n| \"Must match our SKU format\" (reusable) | A validator function | Shareable with model fields and DRF |\n| \"Username must not be taken\" | `clean_username()` (or model `unique=True`) | Needs a DB lookup; tied to one field |\n| \"End date after start date\" | `Form.clean()` | Involves two fields |\n| \"Coupon valid for *this* user\" | `clean_coupon()` with user injected in `__init__` | Needs request context |\n| \"Order total never negative, anywhere\" | Model `clean()` + `CheckConstraint` | Must hold for admin, API, shell — not just this form |\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### The request cycle around a form\n\nA form view handles two requests. On **GET** you build an **unbound** form (optionally with `initial`) and render it. On **POST** you build a **bound** form from `request.POST`/`request.FILES` and call `is_valid()`. If it fails, you **re-render the same bound form** — it carries the user's values and the errors back into the HTML. If it passes, you act on `cleaned_data` and **redirect** (Post/Redirect/Get), so that refreshing the result page does not resubmit the POST.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 380\" width=\"100%\" height=\"380\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj06c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj06d\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj06e\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">One form, two requests: GET renders, POST validates, success redirects (PRG)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"110\" height=\"290\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"75\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Browser</text>\n  <rect x=\"380\" y=\"50\" width=\"200\" height=\"290\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"480\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">View</text>\n  <path d=\"M132,96 L376,96\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">GET /signup/</text>\n  <rect x=\"396\" y=\"84\" width=\"168\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"480\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">SignupForm() &#8212; UNBOUND</text>\n  <path d=\"M376,128 L134,128\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">200 HTML (initial values)</text>\n  <path d=\"M132,170 L376,170\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"164\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">POST + csrf token</text>\n  <rect x=\"396\" y=\"156\" width=\"168\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"480\" y=\"174\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">SignupForm(request.POST)</text>\n  <text x=\"480\" y=\"190\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#5b21b6\" font-size=\"10\">BOUND &#8594; is_valid()</text>\n  <path d=\"M566,178 L640,140\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06e)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"642\" y=\"110\" width=\"220\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"752\" y=\"132\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#991b1b\" font-weight=\"bold\">invalid</text>\n  <text x=\"752\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">re-render SAME bound form</text>\n  <text x=\"752\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">200 (or 422/400 for APIs) + errors</text>\n  <path d=\"M566,190 L640,240\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"642\" y=\"214\" width=\"220\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"752\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">valid</text>\n  <text x=\"752\" y=\"252\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">use cleaned_data / form.save()</text>\n  <text x=\"752\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">then redirect()</text>\n  <path d=\"M376,296 L134,296\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06d)\"/>\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\">302 Location: /welcome/</text>\n  <path d=\"M132,324 L376,324\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj06c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"250\" y=\"318\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">GET /welcome/ (refresh is safe)</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Components and who owns what\n\n```text\nForm class ─┬─ base_fields (class-level, built by DeclarativeFieldsMetaclass)\n            └─ instance: fields (deep copy) ── BoundField per field (form[\"email\"])\n                                     │                 ├── value(), errors, label_tag()\nField ─── to_python / validate / validators            └── widget.render(name, value, attrs)\nWidget ── value_from_datadict(data, files, name)  ← reads the POST\n          render() via form renderer (templates: django/forms/widgets/*.html)\nModelForm ── ModelFormMetaclass → fields_for_model(model, fields, exclude, widgets...)\n          └─ _post_clean → construct_instance → instance.full_clean → validate_unique\n```\n\nRendering is template-based (since 4.0, via `FORM_RENDERER`). In **Django 5.0+** the default form template is the div-based one (`{{ form }}` equals `{{ form.as_div }}`), and `{{ form.email.as_field_group }}` renders label, help text, errors and widget for one field — handy for custom layouts without hand-writing every `<label>`.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: a contact form\n\n```python\n# contact/forms.py\nfrom django import forms\nfrom django.core.exceptions import ValidationError\n\n\nclass ContactForm(forms.Form):\n    name = forms.CharField(max_length=100)\n    email = forms.EmailField()\n    subject = forms.ChoiceField(choices=[(\"sales\", \"Sales\"), (\"support\", \"Support\")])\n    message = forms.CharField(widget=forms.Textarea, min_length=20)\n    cc_myself = forms.BooleanField(required=False)\n\n    def clean_name(self):\n        name = self.cleaned_data[\"name\"]          # guaranteed present and stripped\n        if name.lower() == \"admin\":\n            raise ValidationError(\"That name is reserved.\", code=\"reserved\")\n        return name.title()                        # MUST return the value\n\n    def clean(self):\n        cleaned = super().clean()\n        subject, message = cleaned.get(\"subject\"), cleaned.get(\"message\")  # .get(): may be absent\n        if subject == \"sales\" and message and \"price\" not in message.lower():\n            self.add_error(\"message\", \"Sales enquiries should mention pricing.\")\n        return cleaned\n```\n\n```python\n# contact/views.py\nfrom django.shortcuts import redirect, render\nfrom .forms import ContactForm\n\n\ndef contact(request):\n    if request.method == \"POST\":\n        form = ContactForm(request.POST)\n        if form.is_valid():\n            send_contact_email(**form.cleaned_data)    # typed values, never request.POST\n            return redirect(\"contact-thanks\")          # PRG\n    else:\n        form = ContactForm(initial={\"subject\": \"support\"})\n    return render(request, \"contact/form.html\", {\"form\": form})\n```\n\n```html\n<!-- contact/templates/contact/form.html -->\n<form method=\"post\" novalidate>\n  {% csrf_token %}\n  {{ form.non_field_errors }}\n  {{ form.name.as_field_group }}\n  {{ form.email.as_field_group }}\n  {{ form.subject.as_field_group }}\n  {{ form.message.as_field_group }}\n  {{ form.cc_myself.as_field_group }}\n  <button type=\"submit\">Send</button>\n</form>\n```\n\nTry it in the shell to see the pipeline in action:\n\n```python\n>>> f = ContactForm({\"name\": \"admin\", \"email\": \"nope\", \"subject\": \"x\", \"message\": \"short\"})\n>>> f.is_bound, f.is_valid()\n(True, False)\n>>> f.errors.as_data()\n```\n\n```text\n{'name': [ValidationError(['That name is reserved.'])],\n 'email': [ValidationError(['Enter a valid email address.'])],\n 'subject': [ValidationError(['Select a valid choice. x is not one of the available choices.'])],\n 'message': [ValidationError(['Ensure this value has at least 20 characters (it has 5).'])]}\n```\n\nEvery field failed independently and all four errors were collected. `clean()` still ran, found nothing in `cleaned_data` for `subject`/`message`, and added nothing.\n\n### Reusable validators\n\n```python\n# core/validators.py\nimport re\nfrom django.core.exceptions import ValidationError\nfrom django.utils.deconstruct import deconstructible\n\nSKU_RE = re.compile(r\"^[A-Z]{3}-\\d{4}$\")\n\n\ndef validate_sku(value):\n    if not SKU_RE.match(value):\n        raise ValidationError(\"%(value)s is not a valid SKU (AAA-9999).\",\n                              code=\"invalid_sku\", params={\"value\": value})\n\n\n@deconstructible   # required if a model field uses it, so migrations can serialize it\nclass MaxFileSizeValidator:\n    def __init__(self, max_bytes):\n        self.max_bytes = max_bytes\n\n    def __call__(self, f):\n        if f.size > self.max_bytes:\n            raise ValidationError(\"File too large (max %(max)s bytes).\",\n                                  code=\"file_too_large\", params={\"max\": self.max_bytes})\n\n    def __eq__(self, other):\n        return isinstance(other, MaxFileSizeValidator) and self.max_bytes == other.max_bytes\n```\n\nThe same `validate_sku` can sit on `models.CharField(validators=[validate_sku])` and on a form field — and because ModelForm runs model validation, you get it in forms, the admin and DRF `ModelSerializer` for free.\n\n### Real-world example: a ModelForm with request context, files and an inline formset\n\nThe scenario: a seller creates a `Product` with an image and several `Variant` rows (size/colour/stock) on one page. The category dropdown must only show the seller's own categories, the SKU must be unique per seller, and the whole thing saves atomically.\n\n```python\n# catalog/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.core.validators import FileExtensionValidator, MinValueValidator\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom core.validators import validate_sku, MaxFileSizeValidator\n\n\nclass Category(models.Model):\n    owner = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE)\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=80)\n\n\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    seller = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE)\n    category = models.ForeignKey(Category, on_delete=models.PROTECT)\n    sku = models.CharField(max_length=8, validators=[validate_sku])\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2,\n                                validators=[MinValueValidator(0)])\n    sale_price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2, null=True, blank=True)\n    image = models.ImageField(\n        upload_to=\"products/%Y/%m/\", blank=True,\n        validators=[FileExtensionValidator([\"jpg\", \"jpeg\", \"png\", \"webp\"]),\n                    MaxFileSizeValidator(5 * 1024 * 1024)],\n    )\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [\n            models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"seller\", \"sku\"], name=\"uniq_seller_sku\"),\n        ]\n\n    def clean(self):\n        # Model-level invariant: holds for forms, admin, and full_clean() callers.\n        if self.sale_price is not None and self.sale_price >= self.price:\n            from django.core.exceptions import ValidationError\n            raise ValidationError({\"sale_price\": \"Sale price must be below the price.\"})\n\n\nclass Variant(models.Model):\n    product = models.ForeignKey(Product, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"variants\")\n    size = models.CharField(max_length=10)\n    colour = models.CharField(max_length=30)\n    stock = models.PositiveIntegerField(default=0)\n```\n\n```python\n# catalog/forms.py\nfrom django import forms\nfrom django.forms import inlineformset_factory, BaseInlineFormSet\nfrom .models import Category, Product, Variant\n\n\nclass ProductForm(forms.ModelForm):\n    class Meta:\n        model = Product\n        fields = [\"category\", \"sku\", \"title\", \"price\", \"sale_price\", \"image\"]  # explicit allow-list\n        widgets = {\"title\": forms.TextInput(attrs={\"placeholder\": \"e.g. Linen shirt\"})}\n        error_messages = {\"sku\": {\"invalid_sku\": \"Use the format ABC-1234.\"}}\n\n    def __init__(self, *args, user, **kwargs):\n        super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)\n        self.user = user\n        # Restrict choices per request; also VALIDATES (a forged id fails \"invalid_choice\").\n        self.fields[\"category\"].queryset = Category.objects.filter(owner=user)\n        # 'seller' is not a form field, so set it before _post_clean so the\n        # (seller, sku) UniqueConstraint can be validated against the right seller.\n        self.instance.seller = user\n\n\nclass BaseVariantFormSet(BaseInlineFormSet):\n    def clean(self):\n        super().clean()\n        seen = set()\n        for form in self.forms:\n            if not form.cleaned_data or form.cleaned_data.get(\"DELETE\"):\n                continue\n            key = (form.cleaned_data[\"size\"], form.cleaned_data[\"colour\"])\n            if key in seen:\n                raise forms.ValidationError(\"Each size/colour combination must be unique.\")\n            seen.add(key)\n\n\nVariantFormSet = inlineformset_factory(\n    Product, Variant, formset=BaseVariantFormSet,\n    fields=[\"size\", \"colour\", \"stock\"], extra=2, can_delete=True,\n    min_num=1, validate_min=True, max_num=50, validate_max=True,\n)\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** constraint and unique validation in ModelForm skips any field that is not in the form. Because `seller` is excluded, Django cannot know which seller to check against unless you set `self.instance.seller` *before* validation runs. If you only set it after `form.save(commit=False)`, the duplicate slips through validation and you get an `IntegrityError` (a 500) at `save()` time instead of a friendly form error.\n\n```python\n# catalog/views.py\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.shortcuts import get_object_or_404, redirect, render\nfrom .forms import ProductForm, VariantFormSet\nfrom .models import Product\n\n\n@login_required\ndef product_edit(request, pk=None):\n    product = get_object_or_404(Product, pk=pk, seller=request.user) if pk else Product()\n    if request.method == \"POST\":\n        form = ProductForm(request.POST, request.FILES, instance=product, user=request.user)\n        formset = VariantFormSet(request.POST, instance=product, prefix=\"variants\")\n        if form.is_valid() and formset.is_valid():      # evaluate BOTH so both show errors\n            with transaction.atomic():\n                product = form.save()                    # instance.seller already set\n                formset.instance = product\n                formset.save()\n            return redirect(\"product-detail\", pk=product.pk)\n    else:\n        form = ProductForm(instance=product, user=request.user)\n        formset = VariantFormSet(instance=product, prefix=\"variants\")\n    return render(request, \"catalog/product_form.html\", {\"form\": form, \"formset\": formset})\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** `form.is_valid() and formset.is_valid()` short-circuits — if the form is invalid, the formset never validates and its errors do not show on the first round trip. Some teams write `all([form.is_valid(), formset.is_valid()])` so both always run.\n\n```html\n<!-- catalog/templates/catalog/product_form.html -->\n<form method=\"post\" enctype=\"multipart/form-data\">\n  {% csrf_token %}\n  {{ form }}\n  {{ formset.management_form }}\n  {{ formset.non_form_errors }}\n  <table>\n    {% for f in formset %}\n      <tr>{% for field in f.visible_fields %}<td>{{ field }} {{ field.errors }}</td>{% endfor %}\n          {% for hidden in f.hidden_fields %}{{ hidden }}{% endfor %}</tr>\n    {% endfor %}\n  </table>\n  <button>Save</button>\n</form>\n```\n\nThe SQL issued by a successful create (PostgreSQL, two variants) shows the work ModelForm validation does before anything is written:\n\n```sql\n-- ModelChoiceField validation: the submitted category id must be in the restricted queryset\nSELECT \"catalog_category\".\"id\", \"catalog_category\".\"owner_id\", \"catalog_category\".\"name\"\nFROM \"catalog_category\"\nWHERE (\"catalog_category\".\"owner_id\" = 7 AND \"catalog_category\".\"id\" = 3) LIMIT 21;\n\n-- UniqueConstraint(seller, sku) validated by Model.validate_constraints()\nSELECT 1 AS \"a\" FROM \"catalog_product\"\nWHERE (\"catalog_product\".\"seller_id\" = 7 AND \"catalog_product\".\"sku\" = 'SHI-0042') LIMIT 1;\n\n-- then, inside transaction.atomic()\nINSERT INTO \"catalog_product\" (\"seller_id\", \"category_id\", \"sku\", \"title\", \"price\", \"sale_price\", \"image\")\nVALUES (7, 3, 'SHI-0042', 'Linen shirt', 49.00, NULL, 'products/2026/10/shirt.webp') RETURNING \"catalog_product\".\"id\";\nINSERT INTO \"catalog_variant\" (\"product_id\", \"size\", \"colour\", \"stock\") VALUES (51, 'M', 'white', 10) RETURNING \"catalog_variant\".\"id\";\nINSERT INTO \"catalog_variant\" (\"product_id\", \"size\", \"colour\", \"stock\") VALUES (51, 'L', 'white', 4) RETURNING \"catalog_variant\".\"id\";\n```\n\nNote the validation-time uniqueness check is a **separate SELECT** — a race between two concurrent submissions can still pass both checks, so the database `UniqueConstraint` remains the real guarantee. Catch `IntegrityError` at save time if you expect contention (see [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency)).\n\n### `save(commit=False)` and many-to-many\n\n```python\nform = ArticleForm(request.POST)\nif form.is_valid():\n    article = form.save(commit=False)   # instance built, NOT saved; M2M not saved\n    article.author = request.user\n    article.save()\n    form.save_m2m()                      # required: tags can only be set once article has a pk\n```\n\nForgetting `save_m2m()` after `commit=False` is a classic silent bug — the article saves, its tags vanish.\n\n### File uploads in brief\n\nUploaded files only appear in `request.FILES` if the form has `enctype=\"multipart/form-data\"` and you pass `request.FILES` to the form. Files up to `FILE_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE` (2.5 MB by default) are held in memory (`InMemoryUploadedFile`); larger ones stream to a temp file (`TemporaryUploadedFile`). `ImageField` uses Pillow to verify the content is a real image; `FileField` only checks a file was sent. **Never trust `uploaded.content_type` or the filename** — both come from the client. Storage, `MEDIA_ROOT`, S3 and serving are covered in [Ch 20 · Static & Media Files](topic.html?p=20-static-media-files).\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Approach | Strengths | Weaknesses | Best for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Plain `Form` | Total control of fields; not tied to a model | You map fields to models yourself | Search, contact, login, multi-model wizards |\n| `ModelForm` | Fields, widgets, model validation, unique/constraint checks and `save()` for free | Couples UI to schema; mass-assignment risk with `__all__` | CRUD on one model |\n| Formsets / inline formsets | Many rows in one submit with add/delete | Management-form fiddliness; heavy on large N; JS needed to add rows | Order lines, variants, addresses |\n| DRF serializers | JSON, nested data, content negotiation | Separate from forms; does not run `Model.clean()` | APIs ([Ch 21 · DRF Serializers](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers)) |\n| Client-only validation (HTML5/JS) | Instant feedback | Trivially bypassed | UX only — always validate server-side too |\n| Model-level validation + DB constraints | One source of truth for every entry point | `save()` does not call `full_clean()` automatically | Invariants that must always hold |\n\n### When to use\n- Any server-rendered HTML input — forms give you escaping, CSRF integration, error display and value re-population.\n- ModelForm for straightforward create/edit of one model, with an explicit `fields` list.\n- Plain `Form` as a **validation object** even outside HTML: management commands, CSV imports, webhook payloads — `MyForm(data=row).is_valid()` is a perfectly good validator.\n\n### When NOT to use\n- JSON APIs — use DRF serializers (or Pydantic/ninja schemas); forms do not handle nested JSON well.\n- Highly dynamic SPA UIs where the server only returns JSON.\n- Enormous editable grids (thousands of rows) — formsets render and validate every row; use a bulk-edit API or CSV import.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. `fields = \"__all__\"` (or `exclude`) on a ModelForm.** It feels convenient, but any field you later add to the model — `is_staff`, `balance`, `approved` — becomes user-editable instantly. An attacker just adds `&is_staff=on` to the POST. *Instead:* an explicit `fields = [...]` allow-list. Django requires you to choose `fields` or `exclude` precisely because of this.\n\n**2. Forgetting to return from `clean_<field>()`.**\n\n```python\n# Wrong: cleaned_data[\"email\"] becomes None\ndef clean_email(self):\n    email = self.cleaned_data[\"email\"].lower()\n    if User.objects.filter(email=email).exists():\n        raise ValidationError(\"Taken.\")\n\n# Right\ndef clean_email(self):\n    email = self.cleaned_data[\"email\"].lower()\n    if User.objects.filter(email__iexact=email).exists():\n        raise ValidationError(\"Taken.\", code=\"duplicate\")\n    return email\n```\n\n**3. Indexing `cleaned_data[\"x\"]` in `clean()`.** If `x` failed its own validation it is not in `cleaned_data`, and you get a `KeyError` — a 500 on bad input. *Instead:* `cleaned_data.get(\"x\")`, and only compare when both values are present.\n\n**4. Overriding `ModelForm.clean()` without `super().clean()`.** `BaseModelForm.clean()` sets the flag that makes `_post_clean` run unique validation. Skip it and duplicates reach the database as `IntegrityError`. Always start with `cleaned = super().clean()`.\n\n**5. `Form(request.POST or None)`.** A POST with zero fields (e.g. all checkboxes unticked) gives an empty, falsy `QueryDict`, so the form is unbound and the submission is silently ignored. *Instead:* branch on `request.method == \"POST\"`.\n\n**6. Re-rendering a fresh form on error.** Writing `form = MyForm()` in the invalid branch throws away the user's input and the errors. Re-render the bound form.\n\n**7. Not redirecting after success.** Rendering a template on a successful POST means F5 re-submits — duplicate orders, double emails. Use PRG.\n\n**8. Business invariants only in the form.** The admin, the API, a Celery task or `shell` will all bypass them. Put invariants in `Model.clean()` **and** back them with constraints; let forms add UX-specific rules on top.\n\n**9. Calling `Model.save()` and assuming validation ran.** `save()` never calls `full_clean()`. If you build instances outside forms, call `obj.full_clean()` explicitly (see [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields)).\n\n**10. Mutating `self.fields` at class level or sharing querysets.** Changing `ProductForm.base_fields[\"category\"].queryset` affects every future request. Do per-request changes in `__init__` on `self.fields`.\n\n**Best practices:** pass request-scoped context (user, tenant) as explicit keyword arguments to `__init__`; give every `ValidationError` a `code`; use `error_messages` for wording; use `disabled=True` for read-only fields (the posted value is ignored); add `novalidate` during development so you test server-side errors, not the browser's.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**N+1 queries from ModelChoiceField rendering.** A form with a `ModelChoiceField` whose labels use `__str__` that touches a relation (`f\"{self.city.name} — {self.name}\"`) runs one query per option. Symptom: a \"slow edit page\" with hundreds of queries in debug-toolbar. Fix: `self.fields[\"store\"].queryset = Store.objects.select_related(\"city\")`, or a custom `label_from_instance`. In formsets, each form gets its own copy of the field, so the choices query runs **once per form** — 50 rows means 50 identical queries. Fix: compute the choices once and assign them to each form's field, or switch to a lightweight `ChoiceField`.\n\n**Huge dropdowns.** A `ModelChoiceField` over 200 000 customers renders a multi-megabyte `<select>` and runs a full-table query on every page load. Use an autocomplete widget backed by an endpoint (the admin's `autocomplete_fields` does exactly this; see [Ch 07 · The Django Admin](topic.html?p=07-django-admin)).\n\n**Uniqueness races.** Two users submit the same username in the same second; both pass `clean_username()`'s `exists()` check; one insert raises `IntegrityError`. Root cause: check-then-act across two statements. Fix: keep the DB constraint, wrap the save in `try/except IntegrityError` and convert to `form.add_error(\"username\", ...)`.\n\n**Upload limits and DoS.** `DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE` (2.5 MB, excludes file data) and `DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_NUMBER_FIELDS` (1000) raise `RequestDataTooBig`/`TooManyFieldsSent` (a 400) when exceeded; Django 4.2 added `DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_NUMBER_FILES`. Large file bodies are bounded by your reverse proxy (`client_max_body_size` in Nginx) — set it, or a 2 GB upload ties up a worker. Symptom of a too-small proxy limit: users see a bare Nginx 413 page, not your form error.\n\n**Formset tampering.** The management form is client-controlled. Setting `TOTAL_FORMS=100000` used to be a memory DoS; Django caps it with `absolute_max` (defaults derived from `max_num`). Missing or tampered management data now produces a non-form error rather than an exception (since 3.2).\n\n**Debugging validation.** In a shell or test: `form.is_valid(); form.errors.as_json()`; `form.errors.as_data()` shows the `ValidationError` objects with their codes; `form.cleaned_data` shows what survived. In views, log `form.errors.get_json_data()` on invalid submits to spot fields users systematically get wrong. In tests, prefer `self.assertFormError(form, \"email\", \"Taken.\")` (5.x signature takes the form instance) or check `form.has_error(\"email\", code=\"duplicate\")`.\n\n**Scaling notes.** Form instantiation deep-copies all fields — cheap for normal forms but measurable for giant dynamic forms rendered per row. Template-based widget rendering costs a template lookup per widget; the cached template loader (on by default when `DEBUG=False`) keeps this fast.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between a bound and an unbound form?**\nA: A form is bound when you pass it data (`MyForm(request.POST)`), even an empty dict, and unbound when you do not (`MyForm()` or `MyForm(initial=...)`). Only bound forms can be validated; `is_valid()` on an unbound form returns `False` without errors. Unbound forms render `initial` values; bound forms render the submitted values so the user does not lose input. You check with `form.is_bound`. The typical view creates an unbound form on GET and a bound one on POST.\n\n**Q: Walk me through what happens when you call form.is_valid().**\nA: `is_valid()` returns `is_bound and not self.errors`; accessing `errors` runs `full_clean()` once and caches the result. `full_clean()` calls `_clean_fields()`, which for each field reads the raw value via the widget and calls `field.clean()` — `to_python()`, then `validate()`, then `run_validators()` — then calls `clean_<field>()` if defined. Next `_clean_form()` calls `form.clean()` for cross-field logic. Finally `_post_clean()` runs, which for a ModelForm builds the instance and calls `instance.full_clean()` plus unique and constraint validation. Every error is collected into `form.errors` rather than stopping at the first.\n\n**Q: Where do you put validation that involves two fields, and what must you be careful about?**\nA: In `Form.clean()`, because it runs after all fields have been individually cleaned. Be careful that it runs even when some fields failed, so those fields are missing from `cleaned_data` — use `.get()` and guard against `None`. Call `super().clean()` first, especially in a ModelForm where it enables unique validation. To attach the error to a specific field rather than the form, use `self.add_error(\"end_date\", \"...\")` instead of raising. Return `cleaned_data` (or `None`, which keeps it).\n\n**Q: What is the difference between clean_<field>() and a validator?**\nA: A validator is a stand-alone callable that receives just the value and raises `ValidationError`; it is reusable across form fields, model fields and DRF. `clean_<field>()` is a form method with access to `self` — the other cleaned values that came before it, request context injected in `__init__`, the instance — and it can *transform* the value because its return value replaces `cleaned_data[field]`. Validators run inside `field.clean()`; `clean_<field>()` runs after it, and only if it succeeded. Use validators for pure value rules and `clean_<field>` for contextual ones.\n\n**Q: Does Model.save() run validation? Does a ModelForm?**\nA: `Model.save()` does not call `full_clean()`; it writes whatever you give it, and only database constraints stop bad data. A ModelForm does run model validation: in `_post_clean()` it calls `instance.full_clean(exclude=...)`, which runs model field validators, `Model.clean()`, and then unique and constraint checks. Fields not on the form are excluded from that validation. So if you create objects in code outside forms and want model rules enforced, you must call `full_clean()` yourself.\n\n**Q: Why is fields = \"__all__\" on a ModelForm risky?**\nA: It is a mass-assignment vulnerability waiting to happen. Every model field becomes editable through the form, including ones added later like `is_staff`, `price` or `owner`, and an attacker only needs to add the field to the POST body — they do not need it to be rendered. Django refuses to build a ModelForm with neither `fields` nor `exclude` precisely to force this decision. An explicit allow-list makes schema changes safe by default. `exclude` has the same problem as `__all__` because new fields are included automatically.\n\n**Q: What does form.save(commit=False) do and when do you need save_m2m()?**\nA: `commit=False` builds the model instance from `cleaned_data` but does not save it, so you can set fields that are not in the form (owner, tenant) before calling `instance.save()`. Many-to-many data cannot be saved until the instance has a primary key, so with `commit=False` Django attaches a `save_m2m()` method to the form for you to call after saving the instance. Forgetting it silently drops tags or groups. With `commit=True` (the default) Django saves the instance and the M2M data in one go.\n\n**Q: How do you limit a ModelChoiceField to the current user's objects, and does that also protect against forged IDs?**\nA: Pass the user into the form's `__init__` and set `self.fields[\"category\"].queryset = Category.objects.filter(owner=user)`. Yes, it also protects: `ModelChoiceField.to_python` looks the submitted pk up in that queryset, so a forged id for someone else's category fails with `invalid_choice`. That lookup is the `SELECT ... WHERE owner_id = ... AND id = ...` you see in the query log. Do the assignment per instance — modifying `base_fields` would leak across requests.\n\n**Q: What are formsets and what is the management form?**\nA: A formset manages a list of identical forms submitted together, created with `formset_factory`, `modelformset_factory` or `inlineformset_factory` (for children of a parent via FK). The management form is a set of hidden inputs — `TOTAL_FORMS`, `INITIAL_FORMS`, `MIN_NUM_FORMS`, `MAX_NUM_FORMS` — that tells Django how many forms to read back from the POST. You must render `{{ formset.management_form }}`; without it you get a \"ManagementForm data is missing or has been tampered with\" error. Options like `extra`, `can_delete`, `min_num`/`validate_min` and `max_num`/`validate_max` control behaviour, and `formset.clean()` is the hook for rules across rows.\n\n**Q: Why might file uploads silently not arrive in your form?**\nA: The two usual causes are a missing `enctype=\"multipart/form-data\"` on the `<form>` element and forgetting to pass `request.FILES` as the second argument to the form. Without the enctype the browser sends only filenames as text, and `request.FILES` is empty. Uploads also require POST. Beyond that, size limits at the proxy (Nginx 413) or Django's upload settings can reject the request before your view runs.\n\n**Q: A user reports a 500 error when two people register the same username at the same moment, even though you check uniqueness in clean_username(). Why, and how do you fix it? (Senior)**\nA: The check and the insert are two separate statements, so two concurrent requests can both run the `exists()` query before either inserts — classic check-then-act race. The second insert hits the database unique constraint and raises `IntegrityError`, which nothing catches. The form-level check is still valuable for the common case's UX, but the database constraint is the real guarantee. Fix: keep `unique=True`/`UniqueConstraint`, and wrap the save in `try: with transaction.atomic(): form.save() except IntegrityError: form.add_error(\"username\", \"Taken.\")`, then re-render. The inner `atomic()` matters so the failed insert does not poison an outer transaction such as `ATOMIC_REQUESTS`.\n\n**Q: How would you design validation so the same business rules apply to an HTML form, the admin, a DRF API and a CSV import? (Senior)**\nA: Push invariants down to the lowest common layer. Pure value rules become reusable validators attached to model fields; multi-field invariants go in `Model.clean()`; and anything that must never be violated is also a `UniqueConstraint`/`CheckConstraint` in the database. ModelForms and the admin get this automatically via `full_clean()`. DRF `ModelSerializer` runs field validators and unique validators but not `Model.clean()`, so either call `full_clean()` in the serializer's `validate()`/`create()` or move logic into a service function that every entry point calls. The CSV import should use a form or call `full_clean()` per row. Form-specific concerns — password confirmation, captcha — stay in the form.\n\n**Q: An edit page with a 30-row inline formset is slow and debug-toolbar shows 600 queries. What is going on and how do you fix it? (Senior)**\nA: Each form in a formset gets its own deep copy of every field, so a `ModelChoiceField` runs its choices query once per row, and if option labels touch related objects you multiply again by the number of options. Initial data for model formsets can also lazily load related objects per row. Fixes: give the formset a queryset with `select_related`/`prefetch_related`; evaluate choices once (e.g. build a list of `(pk, label)` and assign `field.choices` in the formset's `get_form_kwargs`/`_construct_form`), or use `ModelChoiceField` with an `iterator` override; use autocomplete widgets for large choice sets. Then lock it in with `assertNumQueries` in a test.\n\n**Q: What are the main pitfalls when accepting user file uploads in production? (Senior)**\nA: Never trust the client-supplied filename or `content_type`; validate the extension with `FileExtensionValidator`, verify content (Pillow for images, `python-magic` for others) and enforce size limits both in Django and at the proxy. Store uploads outside your code directory, ideally on object storage, with generated names to avoid path tricks and overwrites. Serve user content from a separate domain or with `Content-Disposition: attachment` so an uploaded HTML/SVG file cannot run script on your origin. Large files should stream to temporary files (Django does this above `FILE_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE`) or go direct-to-S3 via presigned URLs so web workers are not tied up. Scan for malware asynchronously if users download each other's files.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between initial and a model field default?**\nA: `initial` is purely a display value for an unbound form; if the user clears the input and submits, `initial` is not applied — the field is empty. It is also used to compute `has_changed()`. A model `default` is applied when a model instance is created without that value, and a ModelForm uses it to pre-fill the unbound form. If you need a value applied server-side regardless of what was posted, set it in `clean_<field>`, `save(commit=False)` or make the field `disabled`.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Step | Method | Runs when | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Read value | `widget.value_from_datadict` | Always | Extracting from POST/FILES |\n| Convert | `field.to_python` | Always | `\"42\"` → `42` |\n| Field checks | `field.validate` | Always | `required`, valid choice |\n| Validators | `field.run_validators` | Value not empty | Length, range, regex, custom |\n| Field hook | `clean_<name>()` | Field passed | Context-aware single-field rule; must return |\n| Form hook | `clean()` | Always | Cross-field rules; use `.get()` |\n| Model | `_post_clean` → `full_clean` | ModelForm only | Model validators, `Model.clean`, unique, constraints |\n\n- `is_valid()` = `is_bound and not errors`; errors computed once, cached.\n- All errors are collected; nothing short-circuits except per-field (`clean_<x>` skipped if `x` failed).\n- `cleaned_data` is the only thing to read after validation.\n- `add_error(field, msg)` removes the field from `cleaned_data`; `None` field = non-field error.\n- `Model.save()` never validates; ModelForm does via `full_clean()`.\n- Explicit `fields = [...]` on every ModelForm.\n- `commit=False` → `instance.save()` → `form.save_m2m()`.\n- Uploads need `enctype=\"multipart/form-data\"` **and** `request.FILES`.\n- PRG: redirect after a successful POST.\n- Validation-time unique checks race; the DB constraint is the guarantee.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up a throwaway project:\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\" pillow\ndjango-admin startproject formlab . && python manage.py startapp shop\n```\n\n1. **Trace the pipeline.** Write a form with a field that has a validator, a `clean_<field>`, and a `clean()`, each printing a line. Submit valid and invalid data in the shell and confirm the order, including that `clean()` runs when a field fails but `clean_<field>` does not.\n2. **Mass assignment.** Build a `ProfileForm` with `fields=\"__all__\"` on a model that has `is_verified`. POST `is_verified=on` with `curl` and see it saved. Switch to an allow-list and repeat.\n3. **Race the unique check.** Add `clean_username()` with an `exists()` check, then use two threads (or `TransactionTestCase` with two connections) to submit the same name. Observe the `IntegrityError`; add the `try/except` and convert it to a form error.\n4. **Inline formset.** Implement the Product + Variant page from §5 with `can_delete`. Add JavaScript (or extra rows) to submit five variants; tamper with `TOTAL_FORMS` and observe the non-form error.\n5. **Uploads.** Accept an avatar with `ImageField`; try uploading a text file renamed to `.png` and see Pillow reject it. Add a 1 MB size validator.\n6. **Query count.** Render a 20-row model formset with a `ModelChoiceField` and count queries with `assertNumQueries`; then reduce it.\n\n**Mini project — \"Event registration\":** an `Event` with capacity, a `RegistrationForm` (name, email, ticket type, dietary notes, optional file for a student ID). Rules: email unique per event (constraint + friendly error), student tickets require the file (cross-field), registration closes when capacity is reached (check in `clean()`, enforce with `select_for_update` at save). Add an inline formset for \"additional guests\" (max 3) and tests for every validation path.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 04 · Views, HttpRequest & HttpResponse](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses) · [Ch 05 · Django Template Language](topic.html?p=05-templates) · [Ch 07 · The Django Admin](topic.html?p=07-django-admin) · [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) (`full_clean`, `null` vs `blank`) · [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) · [Ch 17 · Class-Based & Generic Views](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views) (`FormView`, `CreateView.form_valid`) · [Ch 20 · Static & Media Files](topic.html?p=20-static-media-files) · [Ch 21 · DRF Serializers](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers) · [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security) (CSRF, XSS)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · Validation & Input Sanitization](../rest-api/topic.html?p=17-validation-input-sanitization) · [REST API · Error Handling & Problem Details](../rest-api/topic.html?p=16-error-handling-problem-details) · [SQL · Keys & Constraints](../sql/topic.html?p=29-keys-constraints)\n\n- **Working with forms** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the official walkthrough of forms, views and templates. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/forms/>\n- **Form and field validation** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · the authoritative description of the cleaning order used in this chapter. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/forms/validation/>\n- **Creating forms from models** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · ModelForm, `save()`, `commit=False` and model formsets. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/forms/modelforms/>\n- **Formsets** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · management form, `extra`, `can_delete`, `min_num`/`max_num`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/forms/formsets/>\n- **File Uploads** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · upload handlers, memory vs temp files and limits. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/http/file-uploads/>\n- **Validators** — Django docs · *Beginner* · built-in validators and writing your own. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/validators/>\n- **django-crispy-forms** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · the most popular layout helper when hand-written templates get repetitive. <https://github.com/django-crispy-forms/django-crispy-forms>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 06.*\n", "07-django-admin": "# 07 · The Django Admin\n\n> **In one line:** The admin is a full CRUD application generated at runtime from your models and `ModelAdmin` classes — superb as an internal back-office tool if you control its queries and permissions, and a liability if you let it run unbounded `COUNT(*)`s, N+1 list pages and customer-facing workflows.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation) (every admin add/change page is a generated ModelForm) and [Ch 03 · URL Routing & URLconf](topic.html?p=03-urls-routing) (the admin mounts its own URLconf). Models and relations are covered in [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) and [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships).\n\nEvery product needs a back office: support staff look up a customer, finance refunds an order, ops toggles a feature flag, a content editor fixes a typo. Building those screens by hand — list pages with search, filters, pagination, edit forms with validation, audit history, permissions — is weeks of unglamorous work for every model. Teams that skip it end up running SQL against production, which is how \"I accidentally updated every row\" incidents happen.\n\nDjango's answer is **`django.contrib.admin`**: register a model and you get a searchable, filterable, paginated list view, add/change/delete forms with validation, a history log and per-model permissions, all driven by introspecting `Model._meta`. You customise it declaratively through a **`ModelAdmin`** class — `list_display`, `list_filter`, `search_fields`, `inlines`, `actions` — and imperatively by overriding hooks like `get_queryset()`, `save_model()` and `has_change_permission()`.\n\nThe naive use is `admin.site.register(Order)` and nothing else. It works beautifully on a 500-row dev database. In production with 20 million orders, the same page runs a full-table `COUNT(*)`, renders a `<select>` with every customer for the FK field, and issues one query per row for each related column in the list. The admin did not get slower; your data got bigger and the defaults were tuned for convenience, not scale. This chapter teaches both halves: how to build a genuinely useful admin, and how to keep it fast and safe.\n\n> **Why this matters:** interviewers ask \"how would you customise the admin\" to see whether you know `ModelAdmin` beyond `list_display` — and they ask \"the admin page for orders times out, what do you do\" to see whether you understand the queries it generates.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **`AdminSite`** — the object that holds the registry (`site._registry: {Model: ModelAdmin instance}`) and serves the index, login, and app pages. `admin.site` is the default instance. *Why it matters:* you can create several sites (e.g. a restricted support site) with different models.\n- **Autodiscovery** — `django.contrib.admin`'s `AdminConfig.ready()` calls `autodiscover()`, which imports every installed app's `admin.py`. *Why it matters:* registrations run at startup; an import error in any `admin.py` breaks the whole project boot.\n- **`ModelAdmin`** — the per-model configuration and view controller; one instance per registered model, shared across all requests. *Why it matters:* never store per-request state on `self`.\n- **`@admin.register(Model)`** — decorator form of `admin.site.register(Model, ModelAdmin)`.\n- **Changelist** — the list page, implemented by `ModelAdmin.changelist_view()` and the `ChangeList` class (`django.contrib.admin.views.main`) that builds the queryset.\n- **`list_display`** — columns on the changelist: field names, callables, or `ModelAdmin` methods (decorate with `@admin.display(description=..., ordering=..., boolean=...)`).\n- **`list_filter`** — sidebar filters: field names, `SimpleListFilter` subclasses, or `(\"field\", RelatedOnlyFieldListFilter)`.\n- **`search_fields`** — fields searched by the search box, with prefixes `^` (startswith), `=` (iexact), `@` (full-text search, on PostgreSQL); default is `icontains`.\n- **`list_select_related`** — controls `select_related()` on the changelist queryset. *Why it matters:* the main N+1 lever.\n- **Inlines** — `TabularInline` / `StackedInline` edit child rows (FK to the parent) on the parent's page; they are inline formsets.\n- **Actions** — functions applied to selected rows of the changelist (`delete_selected` is built in); declared with `@admin.action`.\n- **`raw_id_fields` / `autocomplete_fields`** — replace a giant `<select>` for FK/M2M with an ID input or an AJAX autocomplete. *Why it matters:* the cure for multi-megabyte change pages.\n- **`readonly_fields`**, **`fieldsets`**, **`fields`**, **`exclude`** — what the change form shows and how.\n- **Permissions** — the admin checks `is_staff` to let a user in, then `has_view/add/change/delete_permission` per model, which by default map to `app.view_model` etc. Superusers pass everything.\n- **`LogEntry`** — the `django_admin_log` table recording each add/change/delete made through the admin. *Why it matters:* a free (partial) audit trail.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### How the admin builds a page\n\nThe admin is just Django: URLs, views, ModelForms and templates. When you include `admin.site.urls`, `AdminSite.get_urls()` generates, for each registered model, five URL patterns pointing at the ModelAdmin's views:\n\n```text\n/admin/<app>/<model>/                     → changelist_view   (list, filters, search, actions)\n/admin/<app>/<model>/add/                 → add_view          (changeform_view, object_id=None)\n/admin/<app>/<model>/<pk>/change/         → change_view       (changeform_view)\n/admin/<app>/<model>/<pk>/delete/         → delete_view       (confirmation + collector preview)\n/admin/<app>/<model>/<pk>/history/        → history_view      (LogEntry rows)\n```\n\nEach view is wrapped by `AdminSite.admin_view()`, which enforces login + `is_staff`, adds `csrf_protect`, and marks the response as never cached. Inside, the view checks the relevant `has_*_permission(request, obj)` and returns 403 if it fails.\n\nFor a **change page**, `changeform_view` calls `get_object()` (via `get_queryset()`, so your restrictions apply), then `get_form()` — which calls `modelform_factory` with your `fields`/`fieldsets`/`exclude`/`readonly_fields`/`formfield_overrides` — and `get_formsets_with_inlines()` for the inline formsets. On POST it validates form and formsets exactly as in Chapter 6, then calls `save_model()`, `save_related()` (which saves M2M and inline formsets) and `log_change()` inside a transaction.\n\nFor the **changelist**, `changelist_view` builds a `ChangeList` object, and that object assembles the queryset in a fixed order. This is where almost every admin performance problem originates, so it is worth knowing precisely.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 420\" width=\"100%\" height=\"420\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj07a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">ChangeList.get_queryset(): how one admin list page becomes SQL</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"190\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"115\" y=\"63\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ModelAdmin.get_queryset()</text>\n  <text x=\"115\" y=\"78\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">your annotate / tenant filter</text>\n  <path d=\"M212,66 L236,66\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj07a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"238\" y=\"44\" width=\"190\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"333\" y=\"63\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">list_filter</text>\n  <text x=\"333\" y=\"78\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">each filter's queryset() &#8594; WHERE</text>\n  <path d=\"M430,66 L454,66\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj07a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"456\" y=\"44\" width=\"190\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"551\" y=\"63\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">get_search_results()</text>\n  <text x=\"551\" y=\"78\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">search_fields &#8594; ILIKE / UPPER LIKE</text>\n  <path d=\"M648,66 L672,66\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj07a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"674\" y=\"44\" width=\"186\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"767\" y=\"63\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ordering</text>\n  <text x=\"767\" y=\"78\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">+ pk tiebreak (deterministic)</text>\n  <path d=\"M767,90 L767,116\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj07a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"560\" y=\"118\" width=\"300\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"710\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">apply_select_related()</text>\n  <text x=\"710\" y=\"155\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"9\">list_select_related, or FKs named directly in list_display</text>\n  <path d=\"M558,143 L512,143\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj07a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"118\" width=\"490\" height=\"96\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"265\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#991b1b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Paginator: the expensive part on big tables</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"158\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">1. SELECT COUNT(*) FROM ... WHERE &lt;filters&gt;      (result count)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"176\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">2. SELECT COUNT(*) FROM ... unfiltered       (if show_full_result_count)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"194\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">3. SELECT ... ORDER BY ... LIMIT 100 OFFSET n   (the page)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"208\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"9\">PostgreSQL COUNT(*) scans all matching rows &#8212; seconds on 10M+ rows</text>\n  <path d=\"M265,216 L265,242\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj07a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"244\" width=\"840\" height=\"76\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"264\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#92400e\" font-weight=\"bold\">Render: one row per object, one cell per list_display entry</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"284\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"10\">field name \"customer\" &#8594; covered by auto select_related &#183; callable obj.customer.email &#8594; NOT detected &#8594; 1 query per row</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"302\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"10\">method doing obj.items.count() &#8594; 1 COUNT per row &#8594; replace with annotate(Count(\"items\")) in get_queryset()</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"334\" width=\"840\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"354\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#5b21b6\" font-weight=\"bold\">Sidebar extras that also query</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"374\" fill=\"#6d28d9\" font-size=\"10\">list_filter on a FK &#8594; loads every related object as a choice &#183; date_hierarchy &#8594; DISTINCT dates query</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"392\" fill=\"#6d28d9\" font-size=\"10\">show_facets (5.0+) &#8594; extra COUNTs per filter option when facets are shown</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### How `list_select_related` really works\n\n`ChangeList.apply_select_related()` follows three rules. If `list_select_related = True`, it calls `select_related()` with no arguments (follows all non-null FKs — can create big joins). If it is a list/tuple, it calls `select_related(*fields)`. If it is `False` (the default), Django **inspects `list_display`** and adds `select_related()` for any entry that is directly a ForeignKey field. The catch: a callable or method that does `obj.customer.email` is opaque to that inspection, so it triggers one query per row. Explicit `list_select_related = [\"customer\", \"customer__company\"]` is the reliable form.\n\n### How search is compiled\n\n`get_search_results()` splits the search string into terms (respecting quotes). Each term must match **at least one** search field (OR across fields), and **all** terms must match (AND across terms). With the default `icontains` lookup on PostgreSQL that becomes `UPPER(col::text) LIKE UPPER('%term%')`, which **cannot use a normal B-tree index** — a leading wildcard forces a sequential scan. Searching across a to-many relation (`items__sku`) can produce duplicate rows; the admin detects this (`lookup_spawns_duplicates`) and de-duplicates the result, which adds cost.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 380\" width=\"100%\" height=\"380\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj07b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Admin components: registry &#8594; ModelAdmin &#8594; views &#8594; forms &#8594; DB + LogEntry</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"180\" height=\"120\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Startup</text>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">AdminConfig.ready()</text>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">autodiscover()</text>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">imports each app's admin.py</text>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">@admin.register &#8594; site._registry</text>\n  <path d=\"M202,110 L236,110\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj07b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"238\" y=\"50\" width=\"190\" height=\"120\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"333\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e40af\" font-weight=\"bold\">AdminSite</text>\n  <text x=\"333\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">get_urls(): index, login,</text>\n  <text x=\"333\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">5 URLs per registered model</text>\n  <text x=\"333\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">admin_view(): login + is_staff</text>\n  <text x=\"333\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">+ csrf_protect + never_cache</text>\n  <path d=\"M430,110 L464,110\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj07b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"466\" y=\"50\" width=\"394\" height=\"120\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"663\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#5b21b6\" font-weight=\"bold\">ModelAdmin (one shared instance per model)</text>\n  <text x=\"482\" y=\"94\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">changelist_view &#8594; ChangeList (filters, search, paging, actions)</text>\n  <text x=\"482\" y=\"112\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">changeform_view &#8594; get_form() + inline formsets</text>\n  <text x=\"482\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">delete_view &#8594; Collector preview of cascades</text>\n  <text x=\"482\" y=\"148\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">has_view/add/change/delete_permission(request, obj)</text>\n  <path d=\"M663,172 L663,206\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj07b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"466\" y=\"208\" width=\"394\" height=\"80\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"663\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">POST on change page (one transaction)</text>\n  <text x=\"482\" y=\"250\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">form.is_valid() + formsets valid &#8594; save_model() &#8594; save_related()</text>\n  <text x=\"482\" y=\"268\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">&#8594; log_change() writes LogEntry &#8594; message + redirect</text>\n  <path d=\"M464,248 L380,248\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj07b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"160\" y=\"208\" width=\"218\" height=\"80\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"269\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#92400e\" font-weight=\"bold\">Database</text>\n  <text x=\"269\" y=\"250\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"10\">your tables</text>\n  <text x=\"269\" y=\"268\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"10\">django_admin_log (LogEntry)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"306\" width=\"840\" height=\"58\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"326\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#991b1b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Bypasses to remember</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"346\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">delete_selected uses queryset.delete() (no Model.delete() override) &#183; list_editable saves via formset &#183; actions see the raw queryset</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### The request workflow for a bulk action\n\n```text\nPOST /admin/shop/order/   action=mark_shipped  _selected_action=[12, 15, 19]\n  → changelist_view → response_action()\n      → checks the action's required permissions (@admin.action(permissions=[\"change\"]))\n      → queryset = cl.get_queryset().filter(pk__in=selected)   # your get_queryset applies\n      → action(modeladmin, request, queryset)\n          → returns None  → redirect back to changelist (messages shown)\n          → returns HttpResponse → rendered as-is (intermediate page / CSV download)\n```\n\n### Customisation layers (cheapest first)\n\n1. **Declarative options** on `ModelAdmin` — `list_display`, `list_filter`, `search_fields`, `readonly_fields`, `fieldsets`, `autocomplete_fields`.\n2. **Hook methods** — `get_queryset`, `get_readonly_fields`, `get_list_display`, `get_search_results`, `save_model`, `delete_queryset`, `has_*_permission`, `get_form`.\n3. **Custom forms** — `form = OrderAdminForm` for validation specific to staff.\n4. **Template overrides** — `templates/admin/<app>/<model>/change_form.html` extending the stock template and overriding a block.\n5. **Custom views** — `get_urls()` to add pages, wrapped in `self.admin_site.admin_view()`.\n6. **Your own app** — when you find yourself at layer 5 for most screens, it is time to build a real internal tool.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example\n\n```python\n# blog/admin.py\nfrom django.contrib import admin\nfrom .models import Author, Post\n\n\n@admin.register(Author)\nclass AuthorAdmin(admin.ModelAdmin):\n    list_display = [\"name\", \"email\"]\n    search_fields = [\"name\", \"email\"]          # required for autocomplete_fields elsewhere\n\n\n@admin.register(Post)\nclass PostAdmin(admin.ModelAdmin):\n    list_display = [\"title\", \"author\", \"status\", \"published_at\", \"is_recent\"]\n    list_filter = [\"status\", \"published_at\"]\n    search_fields = [\"title\", \"^author__name\"]  # ^ = istartswith (can use an index with the right opclass)\n    date_hierarchy = \"published_at\"\n    prepopulated_fields = {\"slug\": [\"title\"]}\n    autocomplete_fields = [\"author\"]\n    readonly_fields = [\"created_at\"]\n    ordering = [\"-published_at\"]\n\n    @admin.display(boolean=True, description=\"Recent?\", ordering=\"published_at\")\n    def is_recent(self, obj):\n        from django.utils import timezone\n        return obj.published_at and (timezone.now() - obj.published_at).days < 7\n```\n\n```bash\npython manage.py createsuperuser\npython manage.py runserver   # http://127.0.0.1:8000/admin/\n```\n\nBecause `author` is an FK named directly in `list_display`, the changelist SQL already joins it:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"blog_post\".\"id\", \"blog_post\".\"title\", \"blog_post\".\"status\", \"blog_post\".\"published_at\",\n       \"blog_author\".\"id\", \"blog_author\".\"name\", \"blog_author\".\"email\"\nFROM \"blog_post\"\nINNER JOIN \"blog_author\" ON (\"blog_post\".\"author_id\" = \"blog_author\".\"id\")\nORDER BY \"blog_post\".\"published_at\" DESC, \"blog_post\".\"id\" DESC\nLIMIT 100;\n```\n\n### Real-world example: an orders back office\n\nRequirements: support staff see orders with customer email, item count and total; they filter by status and \"high value\"; they search by order number or customer email; they can mark orders shipped in bulk and export CSV; they can edit line items inline; only the finance group may change totals; the table has tens of millions of rows.\n\n```python\n# shop/admin.py\nimport csv\nfrom django.contrib import admin, messages\nfrom django.db.models import Count, Sum\nfrom django.http import StreamingHttpResponse\nfrom django.utils import timezone\nfrom .models import Order, OrderItem\n\n\nclass OrderItemInline(admin.TabularInline):\n    model = OrderItem\n    extra = 0\n    autocomplete_fields = [\"product\"]          # never a 100k-option <select>\n    readonly_fields = [\"line_total\"]\n    fields = [\"product\", \"quantity\", \"unit_price\", \"line_total\"]\n\n    def get_queryset(self, request):\n        return super().get_queryset(request).select_related(\"product\")\n\n\nclass HighValueFilter(admin.SimpleListFilter):\n    title = \"value\"\n    parameter_name = \"value\"\n\n    def lookups(self, request, model_admin):\n        return [(\"high\", \"≥ 1,000\"), (\"normal\", \"< 1,000\")]\n\n    def queryset(self, request, queryset):\n        if self.value() == \"high\":\n            return queryset.filter(total__gte=1000)\n        if self.value() == \"normal\":\n            return queryset.filter(total__lt=1000)\n        return queryset\n\n\nclass Echo:\n    def write(self, value):\n        return value\n\n\n@admin.register(Order)\nclass OrderAdmin(admin.ModelAdmin):\n    list_display = [\"number\", \"customer_email\", \"status\", \"item_count\", \"total\", \"created_at\"]\n    list_filter = [\"status\", HighValueFilter, (\"created_at\", admin.DateFieldListFilter)]\n    search_fields = [\"=number\", \"=customer__email\"]   # exact matches: index-friendly\n    list_select_related = [\"customer\"]\n    list_per_page = 50\n    show_full_result_count = False                    # skip the unfiltered COUNT(*)\n    raw_id_fields = [\"customer\"]\n    readonly_fields = [\"number\", \"created_at\"]\n    inlines = [OrderItemInline]\n    actions = [\"mark_shipped\", \"export_csv\"]\n    fieldsets = [\n        (None, {\"fields\": [\"number\", \"customer\", \"status\"]}),\n        (\"Money\", {\"fields\": [\"total\", \"currency\"], \"classes\": [\"collapse\"]}),\n        (\"Audit\", {\"fields\": [\"created_at\"]}),\n    ]\n\n    def get_queryset(self, request):\n        return super().get_queryset(request).annotate(_item_count=Count(\"items\"))\n\n    @admin.display(description=\"Customer\", ordering=\"customer__email\")\n    def customer_email(self, obj):\n        return obj.customer.email                     # covered by list_select_related\n\n    @admin.display(description=\"Items\", ordering=\"_item_count\")\n    def item_count(self, obj):\n        return obj._item_count                        # from the annotation, no per-row query\n\n    def get_readonly_fields(self, request, obj=None):\n        ro = list(super().get_readonly_fields(request, obj))\n        if not request.user.groups.filter(name=\"finance\").exists():\n            ro += [\"total\", \"currency\"]\n        return ro\n\n    @admin.action(description=\"Mark selected orders as shipped\", permissions=[\"change\"])\n    def mark_shipped(self, request, queryset):\n        updated = queryset.filter(status=Order.Status.PAID).update(\n            status=Order.Status.SHIPPED, shipped_at=timezone.now()\n        )\n        self.message_user(request, f\"{updated} orders marked shipped.\", messages.SUCCESS)\n\n    @admin.action(description=\"Export selected to CSV\", permissions=[\"view\"])\n    def export_csv(self, request, queryset):\n        writer = csv.writer(Echo())\n        rows = queryset.values_list(\"number\", \"customer__email\", \"status\", \"total\").iterator(chunk_size=2000)\n        header = [\"number\", \"email\", \"status\", \"total\"]\n        stream = (writer.writerow(r) for r in [header, *rows])\n        resp = StreamingHttpResponse(stream, content_type=\"text/csv\")\n        resp[\"Content-Disposition\"] = 'attachment; filename=\"orders.csv\"'\n        return resp\n\n    def save_model(self, request, obj, form, change):\n        if not change:\n            obj.created_by = request.user\n        super().save_model(request, obj, form, change)\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** `queryset.update()` in `mark_shipped` skips `Order.save()` and `post_save` signals. That is usually what you want for speed — but if a signal sends the \"your order shipped\" email, nothing is sent. Either loop and `save(update_fields=[...])`, or (better) enqueue the notification explicitly with `transaction.on_commit`.\n\nThe resulting page SQL (with a status filter and no search) is three statements, none per-row:\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS \"__count\" FROM \"shop_order\" WHERE \"shop_order\".\"status\" = 'paid';\n\nSELECT \"shop_order\".\"id\", \"shop_order\".\"number\", \"shop_order\".\"status\", \"shop_order\".\"total\",\n       \"shop_order\".\"created_at\", COUNT(\"shop_orderitem\".\"id\") AS \"_item_count\",\n       \"accounts_customer\".\"id\", \"accounts_customer\".\"email\"\nFROM \"shop_order\"\nLEFT OUTER JOIN \"shop_orderitem\" ON (\"shop_order\".\"id\" = \"shop_orderitem\".\"order_id\")\nINNER JOIN \"accounts_customer\" ON (\"shop_order\".\"customer_id\" = \"accounts_customer\".\"id\")\nWHERE \"shop_order\".\"status\" = 'paid'\nGROUP BY \"shop_order\".\"id\", \"accounts_customer\".\"id\"\nORDER BY \"shop_order\".\"id\" DESC\nLIMIT 50;\n```\n\nCompare the naive version (`list_display = [\"number\", \"customer_email_naive\", \"items_naive\"]` where the methods do `obj.customer.email` and `obj.items.count()`): 1 COUNT + 1 page query + 50 customer queries + 50 item counts = **102 queries** per page load.\n\n### Taming COUNT(*) on huge tables\n\n`show_full_result_count = False` removes one COUNT, but the paginator still counts the filtered set. For very large tables, a custom paginator that uses PostgreSQL's planner estimate when there are no filters is a common pattern:\n\n```python\n# core/admin_paginator.py\nfrom django.core.paginator import Paginator\nfrom django.db import connection\nfrom django.utils.functional import cached_property\n\n\nclass EstimatedCountPaginator(Paginator):\n    @cached_property\n    def count(self):\n        query = self.object_list.query\n        if not query.where:                      # unfiltered: use pg_class estimate\n            with connection.cursor() as cur:\n                cur.execute(\"SELECT reltuples::bigint FROM pg_class WHERE relname = %s\",\n                            [query.model._meta.db_table])\n                estimate = cur.fetchone()[0]\n            if estimate > 100_000:\n                return estimate\n        return super().count\n```\n\n```python\nclass OrderAdmin(admin.ModelAdmin):\n    paginator = EstimatedCountPaginator\n```\n\n### A restricted second admin site\n\n```python\n# support/admin.py\nfrom django.contrib import admin\nfrom shop.models import Order\n\n\nclass SupportSite(admin.AdminSite):\n    site_header = \"Support Console\"\n    site_title = \"Support\"\n\n    def has_permission(self, request):\n        return request.user.is_active and request.user.groups.filter(name=\"support\").exists()\n\n\nsupport_site = SupportSite(name=\"support\")\n\n\n@admin.register(Order, site=support_site)\nclass SupportOrderAdmin(admin.ModelAdmin):\n    list_display = [\"number\", \"status\"]\n    search_fields = [\"=number\"]\n\n    def has_delete_permission(self, request, obj=None):\n        return False\n```\n\n```python\n# config/urls.py\nfrom django.contrib import admin\nfrom django.urls import path\nfrom support.admin import support_site\n\nurlpatterns = [\n    path(\"ops-7f3a/\", admin.site.urls),     # not /admin/: cuts noise from scanners (not real security)\n    path(\"support/\", support_site.urls),\n]\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Aspect | Admin | Custom internal tool (Django views / React + DRF) |\n|---|---|---|\n| Time to first screen | Minutes | Days to weeks |\n| CRUD, search, filters, pagination | Built in | You build them |\n| Validation | Model + ModelForm validation automatically | You wire it |\n| Permissions | Model-level out of the box; object-level via hooks | Whatever you design |\n| Audit | `LogEntry` (no field-level diff) | Whatever you design |\n| UX for complex workflows | Poor (multi-step, approvals, dashboards) | Good |\n| Performance at scale | Needs tuning (COUNT, N+1, selects) | Designed in from the start |\n| Exposure risk | Powerful UI that bypasses product rules | Only what you expose |\n\n### When to use\n- Internal back-office for trusted staff: support lookups, content editing, configuration tables, data fixes.\n- Early-stage products, where an admin buys months of runway before you need custom tooling.\n- Read-mostly inspection of production data (with `has_change_permission` returning `False` for most staff).\n\n### When NOT to use\n- As a customer-facing UI or a self-service portal — its look, permissions model and URL structure are not designed for untrusted users.\n- For workflows with state machines, approvals, or multi-step wizards — fighting the admin costs more than building a view.\n- For bulk operations on millions of rows — write a management command or a Celery job.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Callables in `list_display` that touch relations.** `obj.customer.email` or `obj.items.count()` cause per-row queries. *Instead:* `list_select_related` for FKs and `annotate()` in `get_queryset()` for counts/sums; expose annotations through `@admin.display(ordering=\"_alias\")` so the column is also sortable.\n\n**2. FK dropdowns over large tables.** The default widget for a FK is a `<select>` containing every row of the target table. *Instead:* `raw_id_fields` or `autocomplete_fields` (the latter needs `search_fields` on the related model's admin).\n\n**3. `list_filter` on a high-cardinality FK.** `list_filter = [\"customer\"]` loads all customers into the sidebar. *Instead:* use `RelatedOnlyFieldListFilter`, a `SimpleListFilter` with a small set of options, or search.\n\n**4. Leading-wildcard search on big tables.** Default `icontains` across five text columns is a sequential scan on every keystroke-search. *Instead:* `=` or `^` prefixes for identifiers, a trigram index (`pg_trgm` GIN) for fuzzy text, or `@` full-text on PostgreSQL (see [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres)).\n\n**5. Assuming `delete_selected` calls your `Model.delete()`.** It calls `delete_queryset()`, which does `queryset.delete()`. Overridden `delete()` methods (soft delete!) are skipped; signals do fire. *Instead:* override `delete_queryset()` and `delete_model()` on the ModelAdmin, or disable the action with `actions` / `get_actions()`.\n\n**6. Giving everyone `is_superuser`.** It is the path of least resistance and it means every support agent can delete production data and change permissions. *Instead:* groups with exact model permissions, `has_*_permission` overrides for row-level rules, and a handful of named superusers.\n\n**7. Storing request state on the ModelAdmin.** `self.current_user = request.user` in one method and reading it in another is a race between concurrent requests, since the instance is shared. Pass `request` through instead.\n\n**8. Business logic only in `save_model()`.** It runs only for admin saves. Put invariants in the model or a service layer; use `save_model()` for admin-specific concerns (setting `created_by`).\n\n**Best practices:** register every model you might need for debugging, but with conservative permissions; always set `list_display`, `search_fields`, `list_select_related` and `raw_id_fields`/`autocomplete_fields` on large models; write a test that loads each changelist with `assertNumQueries` so new columns cannot reintroduce N+1.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Changelist timeouts on large tables.** In production the orders admin takes 30 s and Gunicorn kills the worker. Root cause, usually in this order: the paginator's `COUNT(*)` over millions of rows, a leading-wildcard search, ordering by an unindexed column (the default `-pk` is fine; `ordering = [\"-created_at\"]` needs an index), and N+1 columns. Fix with `show_full_result_count = False`, an estimating paginator, indexed `ordering`, exact search prefixes and annotations.\n\n**Change page loads megabytes.** Symptom: the edit page for a single order is slow even though the list is fine. Root cause: FK/M2M `<select>` widgets enumerating whole tables, or inlines with hundreds of child rows. Fix: `raw_id_fields`/`autocomplete_fields`, paginate or cap inlines (`max_num`), or link to a filtered changelist instead of inlining.\n\n**Delete confirmation page hangs.** `delete_view` runs the deletion **Collector** to show everything that will cascade — on an object with millions of dependants that is millions of rows loaded into memory. Fix: block admin deletion for such models (`has_delete_permission` → `False`), use soft delete, or delete in batches from a background job.\n\n**Security incidents.** The admin is the most valuable target in your app: one phished staff password equals full data access. Mitigations: 2FA (e.g. `django-otp`), SSO, restrict the admin path at the proxy (VPN / IP allow-list), separate staff accounts from customer accounts, short session age for staff, and alerting on `LogEntry` deletions. Changing the URL from `/admin/` only reduces scanner noise. See [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security).\n\n**Debugging.** Install django-debug-toolbar in staging and open the changelist — the SQL panel shows duplicates per row instantly. In tests:\n\n```python\nfrom django.test import TestCase\nfrom django.urls import reverse\n\n\nclass OrderAdminQueryTest(TestCase):\n    def test_changelist_query_count_is_constant(self):\n        self.client.force_login(make_superuser())\n        make_orders(5)\n        with self.assertNumQueries(7):     # session, user, count, page, ... - measure once, then lock it\n            self.client.get(reverse(\"admin:shop_order_changelist\"))\n        make_orders(50)\n        with self.assertNumQueries(7):     # same number with 10x the rows → no N+1\n            self.client.get(reverse(\"admin:shop_order_changelist\"))\n```\n\nAdmin URL names follow `admin:<app>_<model>_<changelist|add|change|delete|history>`, which is how you `reverse()` to them from your own code and templates.\n\n**Scaling notes.** Point heavy read-only admin traffic at a replica with a custom `get_queryset().using(\"replica\")` only where stale data is acceptable (see [Ch 34 · Scaling Django](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases)). Long-running actions (exports of millions of rows, re-indexing) should enqueue a Celery task and return immediately ([Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks)).\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: How does the admin know which models to show?**\nA: Models must be registered with an `AdminSite`, either via `admin.site.register(Model, ModelAdmin)` or the `@admin.register(Model)` decorator, usually in the app's `admin.py`. At startup `django.contrib.admin`'s `AdminConfig.ready()` calls `autodiscover()`, which imports every installed app's `admin` module so those registrations run. The site keeps a `_registry` dict of model → ModelAdmin instance and generates URLs for each entry. If you use `SimpleAdminConfig` instead, autodiscovery is off and you must import modules yourself.\n\n**Q: What are the most useful ModelAdmin options?**\nA: For the list page: `list_display`, `list_filter`, `search_fields`, `list_select_related`, `date_hierarchy`, `ordering`, `list_per_page`, `list_editable` and `actions`. For the edit page: `fields`/`fieldsets`, `readonly_fields`, `raw_id_fields` or `autocomplete_fields`, `prepopulated_fields`, `inlines` and a custom `form`. Hooks such as `get_queryset`, `save_model`, `get_readonly_fields` and `has_*_permission` let you change behaviour per request. Knowing which options add queries — filters, counts, FK selects — is what separates basic from production use.\n\n**Q: How do you show a computed column in list_display and make it sortable?**\nA: Add a ModelAdmin method that takes `obj` and returns the value, and list its name in `list_display`. Decorate it with `@admin.display(description=\"Items\", ordering=\"_item_count\")`. Sorting needs a database expression, so compute the value with `annotate()` in `get_queryset()` and point `ordering` at the annotation; the method then just returns `obj._item_count`. This also avoids a per-row query, which a method calling `obj.items.count()` would cause.\n\n**Q: What are inlines and when would you use TabularInline vs StackedInline?**\nA: Inlines let you edit related child objects (those with a ForeignKey to the parent) on the parent's change page; internally they are inline formsets. `TabularInline` renders each child as a table row — compact, good for many small records like order lines. `StackedInline` renders each child as a full fieldset — better for few, field-heavy children like a profile. Options include `extra`, `max_num`, `fk_name` (when there are several FKs to the parent) and `autocomplete_fields`.\n\n**Q: How do you write a custom admin action?**\nA: Define a function or ModelAdmin method taking `(modeladmin, request, queryset)` (or `self, request, queryset`), decorate it with `@admin.action(description=\"...\", permissions=[\"change\"])`, and add it to `actions`. The queryset is the selected rows filtered through your `get_queryset()`. Return `None` to redirect back to the list with any `message_user` notices, or return an `HttpResponse` for an intermediate confirmation page or a file download. Prefer `queryset.update()` for speed but remember it skips `save()` and signals.\n\n**Q: Why might the admin list page cause an N+1 problem, and how do you fix it?**\nA: Each row's `list_display` entries are evaluated in Python; any entry that follows a relation triggers a query per row unless it was loaded up front. Django auto-applies `select_related` only for FK fields named directly in `list_display`, not for callables like `obj.customer.email`. Fix with `list_select_related = [\"customer\"]` for to-one relations and `annotate()` in `get_queryset()` for counts or sums over to-many relations. Prove it with debug-toolbar or a test that asserts a constant query count as rows grow.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between raw_id_fields and autocomplete_fields?**\nA: Both replace the default `<select>` widget for ForeignKey and ManyToMany fields, which would otherwise load every row of the related table. `raw_id_fields` shows a plain text input for the primary key plus a lookup popup — zero queries for options, but not very friendly. `autocomplete_fields` uses an AJAX Select2-style widget that queries the related model's admin as you type; it requires that admin to define `search_fields`, and it respects that admin's permissions and `get_search_results`. Use either on any relation to a table with more than a few hundred rows.\n\n**Q: How does admin permission checking work?**\nA: To enter the admin at all, the user must be active and `is_staff` (checked by `AdminSite.has_permission`). Then, per model, the ModelAdmin's `has_view_permission`, `has_add_permission`, `has_change_permission` and `has_delete_permission` decide what they see and can do; by default these call `user.has_perm(\"app.change_model\")` and friends, which come from the auto-created model permissions assigned to users or groups. Superusers pass all checks. The methods receive `obj`, so you can override them for object-level rules, and `get_queryset` can hide rows entirely.\n\n**Q: Does the admin's \"delete selected\" call Model.delete()?**\nA: No. The built-in `delete_selected` action calls `ModelAdmin.delete_queryset()`, which runs `queryset.delete()` — a bulk delete through the collector. Your overridden `Model.delete()` (say, a soft delete) is not called, though `pre_delete`/`post_delete` signals do fire per object unless the fast-delete path applies. Deleting a single object from its change page calls `delete_model()`, which does call `obj.delete()`. To make both paths consistent, override `delete_queryset` and `delete_model`, or remove the action.\n\n**Q: The orders admin page takes 20 seconds on a table of 50 million rows. Walk me through diagnosing and fixing it. (Senior)**\nA: First measure with debug-toolbar or `connection.queries` in staging against a production-sized copy, and `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` the slowest statements. Typical culprits are the paginator's `COUNT(*)` (PostgreSQL must scan all matching rows), the extra unfiltered count from `show_full_result_count`, ordering on an unindexed column, leading-wildcard `icontains` search across several columns, and N+1 list columns. Fixes: `show_full_result_count = False`, a paginator that uses the `pg_class.reltuples` estimate when unfiltered, an index matching `ordering`, exact (`=`) or prefix (`^`) search on identifiers plus trigram or full-text indexes for fuzzy fields, `list_select_related` and annotations. Also make the default view narrower — e.g. a default filter for the last 30 days — so most loads touch a small slice. Finally lock it with `assertNumQueries` tests.\n\n**Q: How would you implement row-level (tenant) restrictions in the admin so staff of tenant A cannot see tenant B's data? (Senior)**\nA: Override `get_queryset(request)` to filter by the user's tenant; every admin view — changelist, change, delete, actions, history — fetches objects through it, so cross-tenant URLs 404. Override `has_change_permission(request, obj)` and friends for finer rules. Restrict FK choices with `formfield_for_foreignkey` so a staff member cannot attach another tenant's object, and set the tenant in `save_model` rather than exposing it as a field. Autocomplete endpoints go through the related admin's `get_search_results`, so restrict those too. Add tests that hit each URL as a user of another tenant and expect 403/404.\n\n**Q: When would you stop using the Django admin and build a custom internal tool instead? (Senior)**\nA: When the screens become workflows rather than CRUD: multi-step approvals, state machines with guarded transitions, dashboards joining many models, or operations that need idempotency and background processing. Another signal is that most of your ModelAdmins override templates and `get_urls()` heavily — you are building a custom app inside a framework that fights you. Also when non-technical or external users need access, because the admin's permission model and UX are designed for trusted staff. A common middle path is to keep the admin for data inspection and fixes, and build purpose-built views or a small React + DRF tool for the high-traffic workflows.\n\n**Q: How do you add a custom page (for example, a \"refund\" form) to the admin? (Senior)**\nA: Override `get_urls()` on the ModelAdmin and prepend your own `path(\"<int:pk>/refund/\", self.admin_site.admin_view(self.refund_view), name=\"shop_order_refund\")`. Wrapping with `admin_view` enforces login, `is_staff`, CSRF protection and no-cache headers; inside the view you still check `has_change_permission(request, obj)`. Render with `TemplateResponse` and `self.admin_site.each_context(request)` so the admin chrome and sidebar work, and add a button by overriding `change_form.html` for that model. Do the refund in a service function inside `transaction.atomic()` and log it with `log_change` so it appears in history.\n\n**Q: What does the admin's LogEntry record, and is it a sufficient audit log?**\nA: Each add, change and delete performed through the admin writes a `LogEntry` row with the user, content type, object id, a string representation, the action flag and a change message listing which fields changed. It does not record old and new values, and changes made outside the admin — API, shell, `update()` calls, Celery jobs — are not logged at all. For compliance-grade auditing, use a model-history package (e.g. django-simple-history) or database-level auditing, and treat `LogEntry` as a convenience view.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Need | Option / hook |\n|---|---|\n| Columns | `list_display` + `@admin.display(description, ordering, boolean)` |\n| Avoid N+1 on list | `list_select_related`, `annotate()` in `get_queryset()` |\n| Filters | `list_filter` (fields, `SimpleListFilter`, `RelatedOnlyFieldListFilter`) |\n| Search | `search_fields` with `^` `=` `@`; override `get_search_results` |\n| Big FK dropdown | `raw_id_fields` / `autocomplete_fields` (needs related `search_fields`) |\n| Avoid extra COUNT | `show_full_result_count = False`, custom `paginator` |\n| Child rows | `inlines = [TabularInline/StackedInline]` |\n| Bulk ops | `actions` + `@admin.action(permissions=[...])` |\n| Per-user restrictions | `get_queryset`, `has_*_permission`, `get_readonly_fields`, `formfield_for_foreignkey` |\n| Admin-only save logic | `save_model`, `save_related`, `delete_model`, `delete_queryset` |\n| Custom pages | `get_urls()` + `admin_site.admin_view()` |\n| Reverse a URL | `admin:<app>_<model>_changelist` / `_change` |\n\n- The admin is generated ModelForms + views + templates; nothing magic.\n- ModelAdmin instances are shared — no per-request state on `self`.\n- Auto `select_related` only covers FK fields named directly in `list_display`.\n- Paginator `COUNT(*)` is the #1 cost on huge tables.\n- `icontains` search = sequential scan without a trigram index.\n- `delete_selected` → `queryset.delete()`; your `Model.delete()` is skipped.\n- `update()` in actions skips `save()` and signals.\n- Delete confirmation runs the cascade collector — can be enormous.\n- Lock admin query counts with `assertNumQueries`.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\" django-debug-toolbar\ndjango-admin startproject adminlab . && python manage.py startapp shop\n```\n\n1. **Find the N+1.** Create `Customer`, `Order`, `OrderItem`; seed 5 000 orders with a management command. Add `list_display` methods for customer email and item count, open the changelist with debug-toolbar and count queries. Fix with `list_select_related` and `annotate`.\n2. **Big dropdown.** Seed 50 000 customers and open an order's change page; note the response size. Switch to `autocomplete_fields` and compare.\n3. **Search plan.** Seed a million orders (use `bulk_create`), search by email with default `icontains`, and run the generated SQL under `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`. Switch to `=customer__email` with an index and compare.\n4. **Soft delete trap.** Give `Order` a `delete()` override that sets `deleted_at`. Delete via the bulk action and confirm the rows are really gone; fix by overriding `delete_queryset`.\n5. **Tenant isolation.** Add a `tenant` FK, restrict `get_queryset` by `request.user.tenant`, and write tests that a user of tenant B gets 404 on tenant A's change URL and does not see A's customers in autocomplete.\n\n**Mini project — \"Support console\":** a second `AdminSite` at `/support/` for a `support` group with read-only access to orders and customers, a custom \"Resend receipt\" action that enqueues a task via `on_commit`, a custom \"Refund\" page via `get_urls()`, an estimated-count paginator, and a query-count test for every changelist.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation) · [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) · [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships) · [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) · [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres) · [Ch 26 · Permissions, Groups & Authorization](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization) · [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security) · [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [SQL · Indexes](../sql/topic.html?p=19-indexes) · [SQL · Query Optimization](../sql/topic.html?p=21-query-optimization) · [SQL · Execution Plans](../sql/topic.html?p=22-execution-plans)\n\n- **The Django admin site** — Django docs · *Beginner → Intermediate* · the complete ModelAdmin reference: every option and hook in this chapter. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/contrib/admin/>\n- **Admin actions** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · writing actions, permissions and intermediate pages. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/contrib/admin/actions/>\n- **Tutorial part 7: Customizing the admin** — Django docs · *Beginner* · gentle introduction to list_display, filters and inlines. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/intro/tutorial07/>\n- **django-debug-toolbar** — GitHub · *Beginner* · the fastest way to see admin SQL and duplicate queries. <https://github.com/django-commons/django-debug-toolbar>\n- **django-simple-history** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · full field-level history when LogEntry is not enough. <https://github.com/jazzband/django-simple-history>\n- **django-otp** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · two-factor authentication for admin logins. <https://github.com/django-otp/django-otp>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 07.*\n", "08-models-fields": "# 08 · Models & Fields\n\n> **In one line:** A Django model is a Python class that the `ModelBase` metaclass turns into a table definition, a validation schema and a row-to-object mapper at once — and almost every model bug (two kinds of \"empty\", silent invalid saves, overflowing IDs, unenforced choices) comes from confusing which of those three layers an option belongs to.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings) (models live in apps listed in `INSTALLED_APPS`, and `DEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD` is a setting) and [Ch 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation) (where `blank` and model validation surface to users).\n\nYour application's data has rules: an order has a customer, a price is a decimal with two places, a status is one of five values, an email is unique. Those rules have to be expressed in three places that are easy to let drift apart — the **database schema** (`CREATE TABLE`, `NOT NULL`, `UNIQUE`, types), the **validation layer** (what a form or API will accept), and the **Python objects** your code manipulates. The naive approach writes SQL DDL by hand, a separate validation layer, and hand-rolled row mappers; within months the schema says one thing, the forms another, and the objects a third.\n\nDjango's **model** is a single declaration that drives all three. You write:\n\n```python\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    sku = models.CharField(max_length=20, unique=True)\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2)\n```\n\nand Django derives the table (via migrations — [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations)), the validation (`full_clean()`, ModelForms, the admin, DRF ModelSerializers), and an object mapper (`Product.objects.get(...)` returns a `Product` with a `Decimal` price).\n\nThe catch is that the field options you pass are **not all the same kind of thing**. `null=True` changes the schema; `blank=True` changes only validation; `choices` changes validation and display but **not** the database; `default` is applied in Python, while `db_default` (5.0+) is applied by the database; `auto_now` is applied in `save()` and therefore *not* by `update()`. Knowing which layer each option lives in is exactly what the classic interview questions — \"null vs blank\", \"does save() validate?\", \"why is this CharField both NULL and empty string?\" — are testing.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Model** — a subclass of `django.db.models.Model`; one class ≈ one table, one instance ≈ one row. *Why it matters:* it is the source of truth for schema and validation.\n- **`ModelBase`** — the metaclass that processes the class body at import time: creates `_meta`, calls each field's `contribute_to_class`, adds the default manager and the `DoesNotExist`/`MultipleObjectsReturned` exceptions, and registers the model in the app registry.\n- **`_meta` (`Options`)** — the model's metadata: fields, table name, ordering, constraints, relations. *Why it matters:* the admin, forms, serializers and migrations are all built by reading `_meta`.\n- **Field** — a class attribute instance (`CharField`, `DecimalField`, …) that knows its DB column type (`db_type()`), how to convert values to and from the DB (`get_prep_value`, `from_db_value`, `to_python`) and its validation rules.\n- **`null`** — **database-level**: column allows `NULL`. Default `False` (`NOT NULL`).\n- **`blank`** — **validation-level**: empty value accepted by `full_clean()` / forms. Default `False`.\n- **`default`** — value (or callable) used by Python when an instance is created without that field. *Why it matters:* not in the database schema (Django drops the DB default after a migration), so raw SQL inserts do not get it.\n- **`db_default`** (5.0+) — a database-side default (literal or expression like `Now()`), present in the schema. *Why it matters:* applies to inserts from any client and helps zero-downtime column adds.\n- **`choices`** — limits valid values for validation and provides `get_<field>_display()`; best expressed with `TextChoices`/`IntegerChoices`. *Why it matters:* the DB does **not** enforce it.\n- **`unique` / `db_index`** — create a unique constraint / an index. `unique=True` implies an index.\n- **`Meta`** — inner class for model-level options: `db_table`, `ordering`, `indexes`, `constraints`, `verbose_name`, `abstract`, `managed`, `get_latest_by`, `default_related_name`, `permissions`.\n- **Primary key** — every model has exactly one; if you do not declare one Django adds `id` of type `DEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD` (`BigAutoField` in new projects). Django 5.2 also supports **composite primary keys** via `CompositePrimaryKey`.\n- **`save()`** — persists the instance: INSERT for new rows, UPDATE for existing ones; sends `pre_save`/`post_save`; does **not** validate.\n- **`full_clean()`** — runs `clean_fields()`, `clean()`, `validate_unique()` and `validate_constraints()` (4.1+); raises `ValidationError` with a dict of errors.\n- **`_state`** — per-instance `adding` (not yet saved) and `db` (the alias it was loaded from). *Why it matters:* `save()` uses it to decide between INSERT and UPDATE.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### What happens when Python imports `models.py`\n\nWhen Python executes `class Product(models.Model): ...`, the metaclass `ModelBase.__new__` runs:\n\n1. It separates \"contributable\" attributes (anything with a `contribute_to_class` method — fields, managers, `Meta`) from plain attributes.\n2. It creates `Options` from the inner `Meta` and attaches it as `_meta`. The default `db_table` is `<app_label>_<modelname lowercased>`.\n3. It calls `field.contribute_to_class(cls, name)` for each field: the field learns its `name`, `attname` (`customer_id` for an FK named `customer`) and `column`, adds itself to `_meta`, and, for some fields, installs descriptors on the class (`DeferredAttribute` for normal fields, relation descriptors for FKs).\n4. If no field has `primary_key=True`, `_meta` adds an auto field named `id` using the app's `default_auto_field` or `DEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD`.\n5. It adds `objects = Manager()` if you defined no manager, creates `Product.DoesNotExist` and `Product.MultipleObjectsReturned`, and registers the model in `apps` (the app registry).\n\nThat is why model definitions must be importable without side effects and why an app must be in `INSTALLED_APPS` — the registry is how Django finds every model for migrations, relations by string (`\"shop.Order\"`), and the admin.\n\n### Two empty values: `null` vs `blank`\n\n`null` is about the column; `blank` is about validation. They combine into four cases:\n\n| `null` | `blank` | Schema | `full_clean()` with empty | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| False | False | `NOT NULL` | error \"This field cannot be blank.\" | Required value (the default) |\n| False | True | `NOT NULL` | allowed — stores `\"\"` for strings | Optional text (`CharField`, `TextField`) |\n| True | True | `NULL` allowed | allowed — stores `NULL` | Optional non-string (`DateField`, `IntegerField`, FK) |\n| True | False | `NULL` allowed | error in forms; code can still save `None` | \"Required in UI, but legacy rows may be NULL\" |\n\nThe Django convention for string fields is **`blank=True` without `null=True`**: empty is `\"\"`, and there is only one way to be empty. With `null=True` on a `CharField` you get two — `NULL` and `\"\"` — and now `filter(nickname=\"\")` misses half the \"empty\" rows and `filter(nickname__isnull=True)` misses the other half. The one exception: an **optional unique** string (e.g. an optional external id). Multiple `\"\"` values would violate `UNIQUE`, but multiple `NULL`s do not (NULLs are distinct in a SQL unique constraint), so use `null=True, blank=True, unique=True` and make sure forms convert empty to `None` (`empty_value=None` on the form field).\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj08a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Which layer does each field option live in?</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"270\" height=\"250\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e40af\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Database schema (DDL)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#1e293b\">null=True &#8594; column allows NULL</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#1e293b\">unique=True &#8594; UNIQUE constraint</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#1e293b\">db_index=True &#8594; CREATE INDEX</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#1e293b\">max_length &#8594; varchar(n)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#1e293b\">db_default (5.0+) &#8594; DEFAULT expr</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#1e293b\">db_column, db_comment, db_table</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"210\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Meta.indexes / Meta.constraints</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"236\" fill=\"#1e40af\" font-size=\"10\">Enforced for EVERY writer:</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"252\" fill=\"#1e40af\" font-size=\"10\">ORM, update(), raw SQL, other services</text>\n  <rect x=\"305\" y=\"44\" width=\"270\" height=\"250\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15803d\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Validation (full_clean / forms)</text>\n  <text x=\"321\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#1e293b\">blank=True &#8594; empty accepted</text>\n  <text x=\"321\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#1e293b\">choices &#8594; value must be listed</text>\n  <text x=\"321\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#1e293b\">validators=[...]</text>\n  <text x=\"321\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#1e293b\">max_length (also checked here)</text>\n  <text x=\"321\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#1e293b\">editable=False &#8594; not in forms</text>\n  <text x=\"321\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Model.clean() cross-field rules</text>\n  <text x=\"321\" y=\"210\" fill=\"#1e293b\">unique/constraints re-checked by SELECT</text>\n  <text x=\"321\" y=\"236\" fill=\"#15803d\" font-size=\"10\">Runs ONLY when someone calls</text>\n  <text x=\"321\" y=\"252\" fill=\"#15803d\" font-size=\"10\">full_clean(): ModelForm, admin, you</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"44\" width=\"270\" height=\"250\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#92400e\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Python / save() behaviour</text>\n  <text x=\"606\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#1e293b\">default=callable &#8594; set in __init__</text>\n  <text x=\"606\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#1e293b\">auto_now / auto_now_add &#8594; pre_save</text>\n  <text x=\"606\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#1e293b\">to_python / from_db_value</text>\n  <text x=\"606\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#1e293b\">get_FOO_display() from choices</text>\n  <text x=\"606\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#1e293b\">verbose_name, help_text</text>\n  <text x=\"606\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#1e293b\">save() INSERT vs UPDATE logic</text>\n  <text x=\"606\" y=\"210\" fill=\"#1e293b\">pre_save / post_save signals</text>\n  <text x=\"606\" y=\"236\" fill=\"#92400e\" font-size=\"10\">Skipped by QuerySet.update(),</text>\n  <text x=\"606\" y=\"252\" fill=\"#92400e\" font-size=\"10\">bulk_create/bulk_update, raw SQL</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"312\" width=\"840\" height=\"74\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"334\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#991b1b\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Interview traps that come straight from this picture</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"354\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">save() does not call full_clean() &#183; choices are not a DB constraint (add CheckConstraint) &#183; default is not in the schema (use db_default)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"372\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">update() does not touch auto_now fields &#183; null=True on CharField gives two empties &#183; blank=True alone does not allow NULL</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### What `save()` actually does\n\n`Model.save()` → `save_base()` → `_save_table()`. Step by step:\n\n1. Send **`pre_save`** (unless `raw`).\n2. For each field, call `field.pre_save(instance, add)` — this is where `auto_now`/`auto_now_add` set timestamps and `FileField` commits the file to storage.\n3. Decide INSERT vs UPDATE. If the instance has a pk value **and** is not in the \"adding with a pk default\" case, try an **UPDATE … WHERE pk = %s**. If that UPDATE matched **zero rows**, fall back to **INSERT**. If there is no pk value, INSERT directly. Since Django 3.0, when `_state.adding` is true and the pk field has a `default` (e.g. `UUIDField(default=uuid4)`), Django skips the UPDATE attempt and inserts straight away — saving a pointless query.\n4. On INSERT, PostgreSQL returns the generated id via `RETURNING`, which is assigned back to `instance.pk`.\n5. Set `_state.adding = False`, `_state.db = using`, and send **`post_save`** with `created=True/False`.\n\nNotably absent: **validation**. `save()` writes what you give it; only database constraints can stop it. And because the UPDATE writes **every** concrete field, two processes that load the same row and save different fields will overwrite each other's changes (a lost update). `save(update_fields=[\"status\"])` limits the UPDATE to the named columns — cheaper and much safer under concurrency.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj08b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">obj.save(): from Python object to SQL statement</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"150\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">obj.save()</text>\n  <path d=\"M172,64 L196,64\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj08b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"198\" y=\"44\" width=\"150\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"273\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">pre_save signal</text>\n  <text x=\"273\" y=\"76\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#5b21b6\" font-size=\"9\">synchronous, same txn</text>\n  <path d=\"M350,64 L374,64\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj08b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"376\" y=\"44\" width=\"200\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"476\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">field.pre_save() for each field</text>\n  <text x=\"476\" y=\"76\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#92400e\" font-size=\"9\">auto_now timestamps, file commit</text>\n  <path d=\"M578,64 L602,64\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj08b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"604\" y=\"44\" width=\"256\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#991b1b\" font-weight=\"bold\">NO full_clean() here</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"76\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"9\">only DB constraints can reject</text>\n  <path d=\"M440,86 L440,112\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj08b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"300\" y=\"114\" width=\"280\" height=\"50\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"134\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">pk set? and not (adding with pk default)?</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">also: force_insert / force_update / update_fields</text>\n  <path d=\"M300,140 L196,190\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj08b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">no pk</text>\n  <path d=\"M580,140 L684,190\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj08b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"640\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">pk present</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"192\" width=\"330\" height=\"66\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"185\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">INSERT INTO ... RETURNING id</text>\n  <text x=\"185\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">generated pk assigned to obj.pk</text>\n  <text x=\"185\" y=\"246\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">created=True</text>\n  <rect x=\"530\" y=\"192\" width=\"330\" height=\"66\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"695\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e40af\" font-weight=\"bold\">UPDATE ... SET all columns WHERE id = %s</text>\n  <text x=\"695\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">or only update_fields=[...]</text>\n  <text x=\"695\" y=\"246\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">rows matched = 0 &#8594; fall back to INSERT</text>\n  <path d=\"M528,240 L352,240\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj08b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M440,262 L440,288\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj08b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"250\" y=\"290\" width=\"380\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"314\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">_state.adding=False, _state.db set &#8594; post_save(created=...)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"344\" width=\"840\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"362\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#92400e\" font-size=\"10\">Full-row UPDATE overwrites columns another process changed since you loaded the row (lost update).</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"378\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#92400e\" font-size=\"10\">Use update_fields=[...] for targeted writes, or F() / select_for_update() for counters and contention (Ch 14).</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Value conversion\n\nEach field mediates between three representations. `to_python()` converts arbitrary input (form strings, deserialised JSON) to the Python type; `get_prep_value()` turns the Python value into something the DB adapter accepts; `get_db_prep_value()` adds backend-specific tweaks; `from_db_value()` (if defined, as on custom fields and `JSONField`) converts what the database driver returns back to Python when loading. Custom fields implement exactly these hooks.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### From class to table to object\n\n```text\nmodels.py (class)\n   │  import → ModelBase.__new__ → _meta (Options), fields.contribute_to_class, registry\n   ▼\nmakemigrations → autodetector compares models' state vs migration state → 0001_initial.py\n   ▼\nmigrate → SchemaEditor.create_model() → CREATE TABLE ... (field.db_type(), NULL/NOT NULL, UNIQUE)\n   ▼\nruntime:  Product(...).save()        → INSERT/UPDATE (get_db_prep_value)\n          Product.objects.get(pk=1)  → SELECT → Model.from_db() → instance (from_db_value)\n          product.full_clean()       → validators, clean(), unique & constraint SELECTs\n```\n\nYou can see the DDL a model produces without touching the database:\n\n```bash\npython manage.py makemigrations shop\npython manage.py sqlmigrate shop 0001\n```\n\n### Choosing a primary key\n\n| Option | Column (PostgreSQL) | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `AutoField` | `integer GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY` | Small, ordered | Overflows at ~2.1 billion |\n| `BigAutoField` (default since 3.2) | `bigint` identity | Effectively never overflows; ordered | 8 bytes; enumerable in URLs |\n| `UUIDField(primary_key=True, default=uuid.uuid4)` | `uuid` | Unguessable, generate anywhere (offline, client) | 16 bytes; random v4 inserts scatter across the B-tree (worse locality, bigger indexes) |\n| Time-ordered UUID (v7 via a library) | `uuid` | Unguessable-ish and index-friendly | Leaks creation time |\n| `CompositePrimaryKey(\"tenant_id\", \"id\")` (5.2+) | composite `PRIMARY KEY` | Matches legacy/multi-tenant schemas | Young feature: limited admin support, FKs to it not supported |\n\n> **Version note:** on PostgreSQL, Django 4.1+ creates auto fields as `GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY` columns rather than `serial`. Projects created before 3.2 may have `DEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD` unset, which triggers system check `models.W042` and keeps 32-bit `AutoField` ids — set it explicitly in settings or per app (`AppConfig.default_auto_field`).\n\nA common production pattern is a `BigAutoField` primary key for joins and indexes plus a separate `public_id = UUIDField(default=uuid.uuid4, unique=True, editable=False)` used in URLs and APIs — internal efficiency, external opacity.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example\n\n```python\n# library/models.py\nfrom django.db import models\n\n\nclass Book(models.Model):\n    class Genre(models.TextChoices):\n        FICTION = \"fic\", \"Fiction\"\n        SCIENCE = \"sci\", \"Science\"\n        HISTORY = \"his\", \"History\"\n\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    subtitle = models.CharField(max_length=200, blank=True)          # optional text: \"\" not NULL\n    isbn = models.CharField(max_length=13, unique=True)\n    genre = models.CharField(max_length=3, choices=Genre, default=Genre.FICTION)  # 5.0+: pass the enum\n    pages = models.PositiveIntegerField(null=True, blank=True)       # optional number: NULL\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=8, decimal_places=2)      # money: Decimal, never Float\n    published_on = models.DateField(null=True, blank=True, db_index=True)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n    updated_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        ordering = [\"title\"]\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return self.title\n```\n\n```sql\n-- python manage.py sqlmigrate library 0001 (PostgreSQL, abridged)\nCREATE TABLE \"library_book\" (\n    \"id\" bigint NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY,\n    \"title\" varchar(200) NOT NULL,\n    \"subtitle\" varchar(200) NOT NULL,\n    \"isbn\" varchar(13) NOT NULL UNIQUE,\n    \"genre\" varchar(3) NOT NULL,\n    \"pages\" integer NULL CHECK (\"pages\" >= 0),\n    \"price\" numeric(8, 2) NOT NULL,\n    \"published_on\" date NULL,\n    \"created_at\" timestamp with time zone NOT NULL,\n    \"updated_at\" timestamp with time zone NOT NULL\n);\nCREATE INDEX \"library_book_isbn_..._like\" ON \"library_book\" (\"isbn\" varchar_pattern_ops);\nCREATE INDEX \"library_book_published_on_...\" ON \"library_book\" (\"published_on\");\n```\n\nRead the DDL closely: `subtitle` is `NOT NULL` despite being optional (that is `blank=True`); `genre` has **no** CHECK constraint and **no** DEFAULT (choices and `default` live in Python); `PositiveIntegerField` does add a CHECK; `unique=True` on a `CharField` also gets a `_like` index for `LIKE 'abc%'` queries on PostgreSQL.\n\nExercising validation in the shell:\n\n```python\n>>> b = Book(title=\"Dune\", isbn=\"9780441013593\", genre=\"xyz\", price=\"9.999\")\n>>> b.save()                     # succeeds! save() never validates; genre \"xyz\" is stored\n>>> b.full_clean()\n```\n\n```text\nValidationError: {'genre': [\"Value 'xyz' is not a valid choice.\"],\n                  'price': ['Ensure that there are no more than 2 decimal places.']}\n```\n\nThe row with an invalid genre is already in the database — `save()` accepted it. Notice that `isbn` is *not* reported even though a row with that ISBN exists: `validate_unique()` excludes the instance's own primary key, so it only complains when a *different* row has the value. Running `full_clean()` **before** `save()` is the pattern for code paths outside forms.\n\n### Real-world example: an order model with invariants at every layer\n\n```python\n# shop/models.py\nimport uuid\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.core.exceptions import ValidationError\nfrom django.core.validators import MinValueValidator\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.db.models import Q\nfrom django.db.models.functions import Now\nfrom django.urls import reverse\n\n\nclass Order(models.Model):\n    class Status(models.TextChoices):\n        PENDING = \"pending\", \"Pending\"\n        PAID = \"paid\", \"Paid\"\n        SHIPPED = \"shipped\", \"Shipped\"\n        CANCELLED = \"cancelled\", \"Cancelled\"\n\n    public_id = models.UUIDField(default=uuid.uuid4, unique=True, editable=False)\n    customer = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.PROTECT,\n                                 related_name=\"orders\")\n    status = models.CharField(max_length=16, choices=Status, default=Status.PENDING,\n                              db_default=Status.PENDING)\n    subtotal = models.DecimalField(max_digits=12, decimal_places=2,\n                                   validators=[MinValueValidator(Decimal(\"0\"))])\n    discount = models.DecimalField(max_digits=12, decimal_places=2, default=Decimal(\"0\"))\n    coupon_code = models.CharField(max_length=32, blank=True)\n    external_ref = models.CharField(max_length=64, null=True, blank=True, unique=True)  # optional unique\n    notes = models.JSONField(default=dict, blank=True)      # callable default: never {} literal\n    placed_at = models.DateTimeField(db_default=Now())       # DB clock; works for raw inserts too\n    shipped_at = models.DateTimeField(null=True, blank=True)\n    updated_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        ordering = [\"-placed_at\"]\n        indexes = [models.Index(fields=[\"customer\", \"-placed_at\"], name=\"order_cust_recent_idx\")]\n        constraints = [\n            models.CheckConstraint(\n                condition=Q(status__in=[\"pending\", \"paid\", \"shipped\", \"cancelled\"]),\n                name=\"order_status_valid\",\n            ),\n            models.CheckConstraint(condition=Q(discount__lte=models.F(\"subtotal\")),\n                                   name=\"order_discount_lte_subtotal\"),\n            models.CheckConstraint(\n                condition=Q(status=\"shipped\", shipped_at__isnull=False) | ~Q(status=\"shipped\"),\n                name=\"order_shipped_has_timestamp\",\n            ),\n        ]\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return f\"Order {self.public_id} ({self.get_status_display()})\"\n\n    def get_absolute_url(self):\n        return reverse(\"order-detail\", kwargs={\"public_id\": self.public_id})\n\n    @property\n    def total(self):\n        return self.subtotal - self.discount\n\n    def clean(self):\n        # Cross-field rule with a friendly message; the CheckConstraint is the backstop.\n        if self.discount and not self.coupon_code:\n            raise ValidationError({\"coupon_code\": \"A discount requires a coupon code.\"})\n\n    def mark_shipped(self):\n        from django.utils import timezone\n        self.status = self.Status.SHIPPED\n        self.shipped_at = timezone.now()\n        self.save(update_fields=[\"status\", \"shipped_at\", \"updated_at\"])  # include auto_now field\n```\n\n> **Version note:** `CheckConstraint(condition=...)` is the Django 5.1+ spelling; on 4.2 the argument is `check=...` (deprecated in 5.1). `choices=Status` (passing the enum class directly) works from 5.0; on 4.2 write `choices=Status.choices`. `db_default` requires 5.0+.\n\nBecause `Meta.constraints` are validated by `full_clean()` since 4.1, a ModelForm on `Order` reports constraint violations as form errors. And because they are real database constraints, `Order.objects.filter(...).update(status=\"bogus\")` is rejected by PostgreSQL too — the layer that `update()` cannot bypass.\n\nThe `mark_shipped` UPDATE is narrow:\n\n```sql\nUPDATE \"shop_order\"\nSET \"status\" = 'shipped', \"shipped_at\" = '2026-10-25T10:14:03+00:00'::timestamptz,\n    \"updated_at\" = '2026-10-25T10:14:03+00:00'::timestamptz\nWHERE \"shop_order\".\"id\" = 1042;\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** `auto_now` fields are only updated when included in `update_fields`. Leave `updated_at` out of the list and it silently stops changing.\n\n### Overriding `save()` correctly\n\n```python\nclass Article(models.Model):\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    slug = models.SlugField(max_length=220, unique=True)\n\n    def save(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        if not self.slug:\n            from django.utils.text import slugify\n            self.slug = slugify(self.title)[:200]\n            update_fields = kwargs.get(\"update_fields\")\n            if update_fields is not None:            # keep update_fields callers correct\n                kwargs[\"update_fields\"] = {\"slug\"}.union(update_fields)\n        super().save(*args, **kwargs)                # ALWAYS call super, pass args through\n```\n\nRemember that this override does not run for `QuerySet.update()`, `bulk_create()` or `bulk_update()` — derive values in a way that does not depend on `save()` if those paths exist.\n\n### Custom field (when built-ins do not fit)\n\n```python\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom django.db import models\n\n\nclass CentsField(models.BigIntegerField):\n    \"\"\"Stores money as integer cents; exposes Decimal in Python.\"\"\"\n\n    def from_db_value(self, value, expression, connection):\n        return None if value is None else Decimal(value) / 100\n\n    def to_python(self, value):\n        if value is None or isinstance(value, Decimal):\n            return value\n        return Decimal(str(value))\n\n    def get_prep_value(self, value):\n        value = self.to_python(value)\n        return None if value is None else int(value * 100)\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Decision | Option A | Option B | Guidance |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Optional string | `blank=True` (`\"\"`) | `null=True, blank=True` | A, unless the field is also `unique` |\n| Default value | `default=` (Python) | `db_default=` (DB, 5.0+) | `db_default` when other writers exist or for safe column adds |\n| Enumerations | `choices` only | `choices` + `CheckConstraint` | Add the constraint when data integrity matters |\n| Money | `DecimalField` | `FloatField` / integer cents | Decimal (or cents); never float |\n| PK | `BigAutoField` | `UUIDField` | Big int internally; UUID as a public id |\n| Timestamps | `auto_now(_add)` | `default=timezone.now` / `db_default=Now()` | `auto_now` is not editable and skipped by `update()` |\n| Uniqueness | `unique=True` | `UniqueConstraint` (with `condition=`, expressions) | `UniqueConstraint` for multi-column/partial |\n| Default ordering | `Meta.ordering` | explicit `order_by()` | Meta ordering adds ORDER BY to every query — keep it cheap or omit |\n\n### When to use\n- Models as the single source of truth for schema and validation for any relational data Django owns.\n- `full_clean()` in service functions, imports and tasks that create objects outside forms.\n- Database constraints for every invariant that would corrupt data if violated.\n\n### When NOT to use\n- A model for data you do not own and cannot migrate — use `managed = False` (read-only mapping to an existing table or view) instead of letting migrations touch it.\n- Fat models with business workflows that call external services in `save()` — use a service layer.\n- `JSONField` as an excuse to skip modelling relational data you will filter and join on.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. `null=True` on `CharField`/`TextField`.** Two empty states, inconsistent filters, and `NOT NULL` migrations later. *Instead:* `blank=True` only (except optional-unique).\n\n**2. Expecting `blank=True` to allow `None`.** Saving `pages=None` on `IntegerField(blank=True)` raises `IntegrityError: null value in column \"pages\"`. *Instead:* non-string optional fields need `null=True, blank=True`.\n\n**3. Mutable defaults.** `default={}` or `default=[]` shares one object across instances (and migrations warn). *Instead:* `default=dict`, `default=list`, or a function.\n\n**4. Calling the default.** `default=timezone.now()` evaluates once at import time — every row gets the server start time. *Instead:* `default=timezone.now` (no parentheses).\n\n**5. Trusting `choices` for integrity.** A script or `update()` can write any value. *Instead:* add a `CheckConstraint`.\n\n**6. Assuming `save()` validates.**\n\n```python\n# Before: invalid data reaches the DB\nOrder(customer=u, subtotal=Decimal(\"-5\")).save()\n\n# After: validate first in non-form code paths\norder = Order(customer=u, subtotal=Decimal(\"-5\"))\norder.full_clean()      # ValidationError: subtotal must be >= 0\norder.save()\n```\n\n**7. `FloatField` for money.** `0.1 + 0.2 != 0.3`. *Instead:* `DecimalField` with explicit `max_digits`/`decimal_places`, and `Decimal` arithmetic in Python.\n\n**8. Expensive `Meta.ordering`.** `ordering = [\"-created_at\"]` without an index on a large table makes every unordered query (including admin and `first()`) sort. *Instead:* index the ordering column or drop `Meta.ordering` and order explicitly.\n\n**9. Forgetting `__str__`.** The admin, shell and error messages show `Order object (1042)`. Always define it — cheaply, without touching relations (it is called in lists and dropdowns).\n\n**10. Overriding `save()` without `*args, **kwargs`.** Breaks `using=`, `update_fields=` and `force_insert=` for callers. Always pass them through.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Integer PK overflow.** A 32-bit `AutoField` on an event or log table hits 2,147,483,647 and every insert fails with `integer out of range`. Symptom: sudden total write outage on one table. Root cause: legacy `DEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD = AutoField`. Fix: migrate to `BigAutoField` — on a large table that rewrites the column and its FKs, so plan it (or do it early). Check old projects for warning `W042`.\n\n**Adding a NOT NULL column to a big table.** Adding `status = CharField(default=\"active\")` historically made Django set a DB default, backfill, then drop the default; long-running on huge tables and locking. With `db_default` (5.0+) the default stays in the schema and PostgreSQL 11+ can add a column with a constant default as a metadata-only change. See [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations).\n\n**Lost updates from full-row saves.** Two workers load an order; one sets `status`, the other sets `notes`; the second `save()` writes back the stale `status`. Symptom: \"the status flipped back by itself\". Fix: `update_fields`, `F()` expressions, or `select_for_update()` ([Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency)).\n\n**Naive vs aware datetimes.** With `USE_TZ = True` (the default since 5.0, and in every generated settings file long before) Django stores UTC and expects aware datetimes; assigning `datetime.now()` raises a `RuntimeWarning` and stores the wrong instant. Use `django.utils.timezone.now()`.\n\n**Slow `__str__`.** A `__str__` that follows a FK (`f\"{self.customer.name} – {self.id}\"`) causes N+1 in admin dropdowns and log lines. Keep `__str__` to local columns.\n\n**Debugging.** `python manage.py sqlmigrate app NNNN` for DDL; `Model._meta.get_fields()` to introspect; `connection.queries` (DEBUG) or debug-toolbar for what `save()` emits; `python manage.py check` for model system checks (`fields.E…`, `models.W…`).\n\n```python\n>>> from django.db import connection, reset_queries\n>>> reset_queries(); o = Order.objects.get(pk=1); o.discount = 5; o.save()\n>>> [q[\"sql\"] for q in connection.queries]\n```\n\n**Scaling notes.** Narrow tables load faster; move rarely used large columns (`TextField` blobs, big JSON) into a OneToOne side table or use `defer()` ([Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization)). UUIDv4 primary keys on very hot insert tables bloat indexes; prefer bigint PK + UUID public id.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between null=True and blank=True?**\nA: `null=True` is database-level: the column is created without `NOT NULL`, so it can store `NULL`. `blank=True` is validation-level: `full_clean()`, ModelForms and the admin accept an empty value. They are independent; for string fields the convention is `blank=True` alone so empty is stored as `\"\"`, avoiding two kinds of empty. For non-string optional fields (dates, numbers, FKs) you need both, because there is no \"empty string\" for those types. The exception is an optional unique string, which needs `null=True` because multiple NULLs do not violate a unique constraint but multiple `\"\"` values do.\n\n**Q: Why should you avoid null=True on CharField?**\nA: Because then a \"no value\" can be represented both as `NULL` and as `\"\"`. Queries must check both (`Q(x=\"\") | Q(x__isnull=True)`), forms produce `\"\"` while code may write `None`, and uniqueness and indexing behave differently for each. Django's own convention is to store empty strings for text. The only common exception is a field that is optional and also `unique=True`.\n\n**Q: Does Model.save() call full_clean()?**\nA: No. `save()` sends `pre_save`, lets fields run `pre_save()` (auto timestamps), performs the INSERT or UPDATE and sends `post_save` — but never validates. Validation runs when you call `full_clean()`, which ModelForms and the admin do for you. That design keeps `save()` fast and predictable, but it means scripts, tasks and API code must call `full_clean()` themselves or rely on database constraints. DRF ModelSerializers run field and unique validators but not `Model.clean()`.\n\n**Q: What does full_clean() do?**\nA: It runs four steps and aggregates the errors into one `ValidationError` keyed by field: `clean_fields()` (each field's `to_python` and validators, including `blank` and `choices`), `clean()` (your cross-field hook), `validate_unique()` (SELECTs for `unique`, `unique_together` and similar), and, since Django 4.1, `validate_constraints()` for `Meta.constraints`. You can pass `exclude=` to skip fields, and `validate_unique=False` / `validate_constraints=False`. The unique and constraint checks are separate queries, so they are not race-proof — the database constraint is the real guarantee.\n\n**Q: How does Django decide whether save() should INSERT or UPDATE?**\nA: If the instance has no primary key value, it INSERTs. If it has one, Django tries an `UPDATE ... WHERE pk = %s`, and if no rows matched it falls back to an INSERT. Since 3.0, if the instance is still `_state.adding` and the pk field has a default (like a UUID), Django skips the UPDATE attempt and inserts directly. You can force behaviour with `force_insert=True` or `force_update=True`, and restrict columns with `update_fields`. After an insert on PostgreSQL the generated id comes back via `RETURNING` and is set on the instance.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between default and db_default?**\nA: `default` is applied in Python when the model instance is created without that value; it is not part of the database schema, so raw SQL inserts or other services do not get it. `db_default`, added in Django 5.0, sets a `DEFAULT` clause in the table definition and can be an expression such as `Now()`, so every writer gets it and the database computes it. If both are set, Python uses `default` for instances it creates. `db_default` is also useful for adding columns to large tables safely.\n\n**Q: Are choices enforced by the database?**\nA: No. `choices` drive validation in `full_clean()`/forms, the select widget, and `get_<field>_display()`, but the column is a plain `varchar` or `integer` with no constraint. Anything that bypasses validation — `update()`, `bulk_create`, raw SQL, a bug — can store an invalid value. If integrity matters, add a `CheckConstraint(condition=Q(status__in=[...]))`. Use `TextChoices`/`IntegerChoices` for readable enums.\n\n**Q: What is DEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD and why did Django change it to BigAutoField?**\nA: It is the field type Django uses for the implicit `id` primary key when a model does not declare one; it can be overridden per app via `AppConfig.default_auto_field`. A 32-bit `AutoField` overflows at around 2.1 billion rows, which high-volume tables do reach, and migrating a PK column on a huge table is painful. Since Django 3.2 new projects set `BigAutoField` (64-bit). Older projects that do not set it get warning `models.W042` and keep `AutoField` until they migrate.\n\n**Q: When would you use a UUID primary key, and what are the costs?**\nA: When ids must be unguessable in URLs, generated outside the database (offline clients, merging data from several systems), or not reveal volume. The costs are size (16 bytes, which also multiplies in every FK and index) and, for random UUIDv4, poor insert locality: new keys land all over the B-tree, causing more page splits and a less cache-friendly index. A common compromise is a `BigAutoField` primary key plus a unique `UUIDField` public id, or time-ordered UUIDs (v7). Remember `default=uuid.uuid4` without parentheses.\n\n**Q: Why do auto_now fields sometimes not update?**\nA: `auto_now` is implemented in the field's `pre_save()`, which runs only inside `Model.save()`. `QuerySet.update()`, `bulk_update()` and raw SQL never call it, so the timestamp stays stale. With `save(update_fields=[...])`, the field is only written if you include it in the list. If you need DB-level guarantees, use a trigger or set the value explicitly in updates (`update(status=..., updated_at=Now())`).\n\n**Q: A colleague overrides save() to compute a slug, but slugs are missing for some rows. What happened? (Senior)**\nA: Some code path is creating or updating rows without calling `Model.save()` — typically `bulk_create()`, `QuerySet.update()`, `bulk_update()`, `loaddata` fixtures (which call `save_base(raw=True)` and bypass your `save()`), or raw SQL from another service. Overridden `save()` methods and `pre_save`/`post_save` signals are skipped by all of these. Another possibility is `save(update_fields=[...])` from a caller that does not include `slug`, so the computed value is never written. Fixes: compute the slug in the creating code or a service function used everywhere, merge into `update_fields` inside the override, enforce non-empty with a `CheckConstraint` so violations fail loudly, and backfill with a data migration.\n\n**Q: How would you enforce a business invariant like \"discount cannot exceed subtotal\" so that it holds no matter how data is written? (Senior)**\nA: Express it in the database as `CheckConstraint(condition=Q(discount__lte=F(\"subtotal\")), name=...)`, which PostgreSQL enforces for every writer including `update()` and raw SQL. Add it to `Model.clean()` or rely on `validate_constraints()` (4.1+) for a friendly message in forms and the admin. Call `full_clean()` in service functions and DRF serializers that create orders. Ship the constraint with a migration that first fixes existing violating rows (or adds it `NOT VALID` then validates, via a custom operation) so the migration does not fail on deploy. Tests should cover the constraint raising `IntegrityError` when bypassing validation.\n\n**Q: Two Celery workers update different fields of the same order and one change disappears. Why, and how do you prevent it? (Senior)**\nA: Each worker loaded the full row, changed one attribute and called `save()`, which issues an UPDATE of every column with the values from its in-memory copy. The later save writes back the stale value of the field the other worker changed — a lost update. Fix options: `save(update_fields=[\"field\"])` so each worker only writes its column; atomic `QuerySet.update()` with `F()` for counters; `select_for_update()` inside `transaction.atomic()` when the change depends on current state; or optimistic locking with a `version` column checked in the UPDATE's WHERE clause. See the transactions chapter for the trade-offs.\n\n**Q: What does the ModelBase metaclass do? (Senior)**\nA: When the model class body executes at import time, `ModelBase.__new__` creates the `Options` object (`_meta`) from the inner `Meta`, then calls `contribute_to_class` on every field and manager so they register with `_meta` and install descriptors (for example `DeferredAttribute` for plain fields and relation descriptors for FKs). It adds an auto primary key if none was declared, a default `objects` manager if there are no managers, per-model `DoesNotExist`/`MultipleObjectsReturned` exceptions, handles abstract and proxy inheritance, and registers the model with the app registry. Everything else — forms, the admin, migrations, serializers — introspects `_meta`.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Option | Layer | Note |\n|---|---|---|\n| `null=True` | DB | Column allows NULL; avoid on strings |\n| `blank=True` | Validation | Empty accepted by forms/`full_clean` |\n| `default=` | Python | Callable for mutables/timestamps; not in schema |\n| `db_default=` | DB (5.0+) | `DEFAULT` in DDL; expressions like `Now()` |\n| `choices=` | Validation/display | Not enforced by DB; `get_FOO_display()` |\n| `unique=True` | DB + validation | Implies an index |\n| `db_index=True` | DB | Single-column index |\n| `editable=False` | Forms/admin | Hidden from ModelForms |\n| `auto_now(_add)` | `save()` | Skipped by `update()`/bulk ops |\n| `validators=` | Validation | Run by `full_clean()` only |\n\n- `save()` never validates; `full_clean()` = fields → `clean()` → unique → constraints.\n- `save()` = UPDATE-then-INSERT if pk set; `update_fields` narrows the UPDATE.\n- `update()`, `bulk_create`, `bulk_update` skip `save()` and save signals.\n- New projects: `BigAutoField`; old ones: fix W042.\n- Money is `DecimalField`; datetimes are aware (`timezone.now`).\n- `default=dict`, `default=timezone.now` — callables, no parentheses.\n- Real invariants → `Meta.constraints`; friendly messages → `clean()`.\n- 5.2: `CompositePrimaryKey`; 5.0: `db_default`, `GeneratedField`, enum `choices=`; 5.1: `CheckConstraint(condition=)`.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"psycopg[binary]\"\ndjango-admin startproject modellab . && python manage.py startapp library\n```\n\n1. **Read the DDL.** Create the `Book` model from §5, run `makemigrations` and `sqlmigrate`, and annotate which options appear in SQL and which do not.\n2. **Two empties.** Add `nickname = CharField(null=True, blank=True)`, create rows with `None` and `\"\"`, and write the query that finds \"all rows without a nickname\". Then fix the model and write a data migration converting NULLs to `\"\"`.\n3. **save() vs full_clean().** Save a `Book` with an invalid `genre` and a negative price; observe what the DB accepts and what `full_clean()` reports. Add a `CheckConstraint` and repeat with `update()`.\n4. **Lost update.** In two shells load the same order, change different fields, save both; observe the overwrite. Repeat with `update_fields`.\n5. **INSERT vs UPDATE.** Log SQL while saving a model with a `UUIDField(primary_key=True, default=uuid4)` and one with a `BigAutoField`; confirm the UUID model skips the UPDATE attempt.\n6. **Custom field.** Implement `CentsField`, store `Decimal(\"19.99\")`, and verify the raw column value with `connection.cursor()`.\n\n**Mini project — \"Invoice model\":** model `Invoice` and `InvoiceLine` with public UUID ids, `TextChoices` status with a matching `CheckConstraint`, `db_default=Now()` issue date, `DecimalField` amounts with non-negative constraints, a `clean()` rule that due date ≥ issue date (plus a constraint), a `mark_paid()` method using `update_fields`, and tests proving each invariant holds both through `full_clean()` and through raw `update()`.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation) · [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships) · [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) · [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations) · [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) · [Ch 15 · Custom Managers, QuerySets & Model Inheritance](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance) · [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres) · [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals) · [Ch 36 · Django Internals](topic.html?p=36-django-internals)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [SQL · Data Types](../sql/topic.html?p=04-data-types) · [SQL · Keys & Constraints](../sql/topic.html?p=29-keys-constraints) · [SQL · Normalization](../sql/topic.html?p=28-normalization) · [Database Design · Schema Evolution](../database-design/topic.html?p=27-schema-evolution)\n\n- **Models** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the topic guide to defining models, fields and Meta. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/models/>\n- **Model field reference** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · every field type and option, including `null`, `blank`, `db_default`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/fields/>\n- **Model instance reference** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `save()`, `full_clean()`, `refresh_from_db()` and how saving works. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/instances/>\n- **Model Meta options** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `ordering`, `indexes`, `constraints`, `managed`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/options/>\n- **Constraints reference** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `CheckConstraint` and `UniqueConstraint` with conditions and expressions. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/constraints/>\n- **Writing custom model fields** — Django docs · *Advanced* · the conversion hooks behind every field. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/custom-model-fields/>\n- **Django 5.2 release notes** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · composite primary keys and other model changes in the current LTS. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/releases/5.2/>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 08.*\n", "09-relationships": "# 09 · Relationships: ForeignKey, ManyToMany & OneToOne\n\n> **In one line:** Django relations are database foreign keys plus Python descriptors — a `ForeignKey` is an indexed `<name>_id` column with an accessor on each side, `on_delete` is a policy Django's deletion collector runs in Python, and `related_name` is the name of the reverse door — so modelling relations well means knowing both the SQL they create and the queries their accessors fire.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) (fields, `null`/`blank`, `_meta`). Querying across relations and the N+1 problem are developed in [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) and [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization).\n\nReal data is connected. An order belongs to a customer, a book has many authors, a user has exactly one profile, a comment can be attached to a post *or* a photo *or* a video, a category has subcategories. In SQL you express these with foreign keys and junction tables; in Python you want to write `order.customer.email` and `book.authors.all()`.\n\nThe naive approach stores ids as plain integers (`customer_id = IntegerField()`) and looks things up by hand. You lose referential integrity (ids pointing at deleted rows), you write the same lookup code everywhere, and nothing tells you what should happen to orders when a customer is deleted. The opposite naive approach is to use relations without understanding them: every `order.customer` access in a loop is a query, deleting one user silently cascades through a dozen tables, and two `ForeignKey(User)` fields on one model refuse to start because their reverse accessors clash.\n\nDjango's three relation fields cover the shapes you need:\n\n- **`ForeignKey`** — many-to-one. Many orders → one customer. Creates a column with a database FK constraint and an index.\n- **`ManyToManyField`** — many-to-many. Books ↔ authors. Creates a hidden junction (\"through\") table, or uses one you define to hold extra data.\n- **`OneToOneField`** — one-to-one. User ↔ profile. A ForeignKey with `unique=True` whose reverse side returns a single object.\n\nEach field installs **descriptors** on both models so you can traverse the relation from either side, and each needs a deletion policy (`on_delete`) and, often, a **`related_name`** for the reverse side. Add `GenericForeignKey` for polymorphic \"attach to anything\" relations and `\"self\"` for trees and graphs, and you have the whole toolbox — along with its sharp edges.\n\n> **Why this matters:** \"Explain `on_delete` options\", \"what is `related_name`\", \"how do you add extra fields to a many-to-many\", \"what is the difference between `ForeignKey(unique=True)` and `OneToOneField`\" and \"when would you use a GenericForeignKey\" are staples of every Django interview.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Forward relation** — accessed from the model that declares the field (`order.customer`). *Why it matters:* it loads one object, and the result is cached on the instance.\n- **Reverse relation** — accessed from the target model (`customer.orders.all()`). *Why it matters:* it is a **manager**, i.e. a fresh query each time you call `.all()` unless prefetched.\n- **`attname` (`customer_id`)** — the raw column value on the instance. *Why it matters:* reading `order.customer_id` costs no query; `order.customer.id` costs one if the object is not loaded.\n- **`on_delete`** — what happens to rows that reference an object being deleted: `CASCADE`, `PROTECT`, `RESTRICT`, `SET_NULL`, `SET_DEFAULT`, `SET(...)`, `DO_NOTHING`. Required on every FK and O2O.\n- **Collector** — `django.db.models.deletion.Collector`, which walks relations in Python, applies `on_delete`, sends signals and issues the DELETE/UPDATE statements. *Why it matters:* Django emulates `ON DELETE`; the database constraint itself is plain.\n- **`related_name`** — name of the reverse accessor on the target (`customer.orders` instead of `customer.order_set`). `\"+\"` disables it.\n- **`related_query_name`** — name used in reverse *lookups* (`Customer.objects.filter(order__total__gt=100)`); defaults to `related_name` if set, else the model name.\n- **Through model** — the junction table of an M2M; auto-created (`app_book_authors`) or explicit via `through=` to carry extra columns (role, date joined, position).\n- **`symmetrical`** — for M2M to `\"self\"`: whether the relation is mirrored (friends) or directed (followers).\n- **`limit_choices_to`** — restricts choices in forms/admin for the relation.\n- **`to_field`** — point the FK at a unique non-pk column.\n- **`db_constraint=False`** — create the column and index without a database FK constraint (legacy, cross-database, or sharded data). *Why it matters:* integrity becomes your problem.\n- **Lazy reference** — refer to a model by string (`\"catalog.Product\"`, `\"self\"`, `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL`) to avoid circular imports and swappable-model problems.\n- **GenericForeignKey** — `content_type` FK + `object_id` + `GenericForeignKey()`, from `django.contrib.contenttypes`; points at any model. `GenericRelation` gives the reverse side.\n- **Related descriptors** — `ForwardManyToOneDescriptor`, `ReverseManyToOneDescriptor`, `ManyToManyDescriptor`, `ForwardOneToOneDescriptor`, `ReverseOneToOneDescriptor`: the classes that implement attribute access.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### What a ForeignKey becomes\n\n```python\nclass Order(models.Model):\n    customer = models.ForeignKey(\"accounts.Customer\", on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"orders\")\n```\n\nAt class creation `ForeignKey.contribute_to_class` sets `name=\"customer\"`, `attname=\"customer_id\"`, `column=\"customer_id\"` and installs a `ForwardManyToOneDescriptor` at `Order.customer`. Once both models are loaded (relations by string are resolved lazily through the app registry), `contribute_to_related_class` installs a `ReverseManyToOneDescriptor` at `Customer.orders`. Migrations produce:\n\n```sql\nALTER TABLE \"shop_order\" ADD COLUMN \"customer_id\" bigint NOT NULL\n    CONSTRAINT \"shop_order_customer_id_fk_accounts_customer_id\"\n    REFERENCES \"accounts_customer\"(\"id\") DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED;\nCREATE INDEX \"shop_order_customer_id_...\" ON \"shop_order\" (\"customer_id\");\n```\n\nThree things to notice. The column type matches the target's primary key (`bigint` here). Django **indexes FK columns by default** (`db_index=True`), because nearly every reverse access and join uses them. And on PostgreSQL the constraint is `DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED` — checked at commit time, not per statement — which is what lets Django insert or delete related rows in any order inside a transaction. Note what is **absent**: no `ON DELETE CASCADE`. The database only enforces \"the referenced row must exist\"; the cascade is Django's job.\n\n### How the accessors behave\n\n- **`order.customer`** — `ForwardManyToOneDescriptor.__get__` looks in the instance's field cache (`order._state.fields_cache`); on a miss it runs `SELECT ... FROM accounts_customer WHERE id = %s` and caches the result. Second access is free. `select_related(\"customer\")` fills the cache via a JOIN.\n- **`order.customer = c`** — sets both the cache and `order.customer_id = c.pk`. Assigning an unsaved object raises a `ValueError` on save.\n- **`customer.orders`** — returns a `RelatedManager` (built by `create_reverse_many_to_one_manager`) whose base queryset is `Order.objects.filter(customer=customer)`. Every `.all()` / `.filter()` is a new query, unless `prefetch_related(\"orders\")` populated `_prefetched_objects_cache` — and even then, `.filter()` on the manager re-queries.\n- **`book.authors`** — a `ManyRelatedManager` querying through the junction table; supports `add()`, `remove()`, `set()`, `clear()`, `create()`.\n- **`user.profile`** (reverse O2O) — `ReverseOneToOneDescriptor` runs a query and returns one object, or raises `User.profile.RelatedObjectDoesNotExist` (a subclass of both `Profile.DoesNotExist` and `AttributeError`, so `hasattr(user, \"profile\")` works).\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 420\" width=\"100%\" height=\"420\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj09a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj09b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">One ForeignKey, two doors: forward descriptor vs reverse manager</text>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"50\" width=\"300\" height=\"170\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"180\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e40af\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">shop_order</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"96\" fill=\"#1e293b\">id            bigint PK</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"116\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">customer_id   bigint NOT NULL  (indexed)</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"136\" fill=\"#1e293b\">total         numeric(12,2)</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"164\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">FK constraint: REFERENCES accounts_customer(id)</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">no ON DELETE clause &#8212; Django's Collector does it</text>\n  <rect x=\"550\" y=\"50\" width=\"300\" height=\"170\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"700\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#5b21b6\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">accounts_customer</text>\n  <text x=\"568\" y=\"96\" fill=\"#1e293b\">id            bigint PK</text>\n  <text x=\"568\" y=\"116\" fill=\"#1e293b\">email         varchar UNIQUE</text>\n  <text x=\"568\" y=\"136\" fill=\"#1e293b\">name          varchar</text>\n  <path d=\"M330,116 L548,96\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj09a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e40af\" font-size=\"10\">many &#8594; one</text>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"240\" width=\"400\" height=\"160\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"262\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e40af\" font-weight=\"bold\">Forward: order.customer</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"284\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">ForwardManyToOneDescriptor.__get__</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"302\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">1. in order._state.fields_cache? &#8594; return (0 queries)</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"320\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">2. else SELECT ... WHERE id = order.customer_id, cache it</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"338\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">order.customer_id &#8594; plain attribute, never a query</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"360\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">select_related(\"customer\") fills the cache via JOIN</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"382\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">in a loop without it: 1 query per order (N+1)</text>\n  <rect x=\"450\" y=\"240\" width=\"400\" height=\"160\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"650\" y=\"262\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#5b21b6\" font-weight=\"bold\">Reverse: customer.orders (related_name)</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"284\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">ReverseManyToOneDescriptor &#8594; RelatedManager</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"302\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">base queryset: Order.objects.filter(customer=customer)</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"320\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">each .all() / .count() / .filter() = new query</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"338\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">default name without related_name: order_set</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"360\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">prefetch_related(\"orders\"): 1 extra query, IN (...)</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"382\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">.filter() on a prefetched manager re-queries</text>\n  <path d=\"M180,222 L180,238\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj09a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M700,222 L700,238\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj09b)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### `on_delete`: the options and what they really do\n\n| Option | Effect when the referenced object is deleted | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|\n| `CASCADE` | Delete the referencing rows too (recursively) | Children that cannot exist alone: order lines, comments |\n| `PROTECT` | Refuse: raise `ProtectedError` | Financial/history data: orders → customer, invoices → product |\n| `RESTRICT` (3.1+) | Refuse, **unless** the referencing row is itself being deleted by a CASCADE in the same operation; raises `RestrictedError` | Complex graphs where a parent cascade should win |\n| `SET_NULL` | Set the FK to NULL (requires `null=True`) | Optional links: \"assigned_to\" a staff member who leaves |\n| `SET_DEFAULT` | Set to the field's `default` | Rare; a \"unknown/system\" sentinel row |\n| `SET(value_or_callable)` | Set to a value or the result of a callable | Reassign to a sentinel user: `SET(get_deleted_user)` |\n| `DO_NOTHING` | Django does nothing; the DB constraint decides (usually an `IntegrityError` at commit) | Legacy schemas with real DB-level `ON DELETE`, or managed elsewhere |\n\n`RESTRICT` vs `PROTECT` is the subtle one. Suppose `Artist` → `Album` (CASCADE) and `Song` has FKs to both `Album` (CASCADE) and `Artist` (RESTRICT). Deleting the artist cascades to albums, which cascades to songs — since every song is already being removed via a cascade path, `RESTRICT` allows it. Deleting a single song's artist *directly*, when that song would not otherwise be deleted, is blocked. `PROTECT` would block the artist deletion in both cases.\n\n### How the Collector deletes\n\n`instance.delete()` and `QuerySet.delete()` both create a `Collector`:\n\n1. `collector.collect(objs)` — for each object, inspect every reverse relation (`_meta.related_objects`). For `CASCADE`, query the related rows and collect them recursively; for `PROTECT`/`RESTRICT`, gather offenders and raise; for `SET_*`, record field updates.\n2. **Fast deletes** — when a related model has no signals connected, no further cascades and no generic relations, Django skips loading those rows into Python and issues a single `DELETE ... WHERE fk_id IN (...)`.\n3. `collector.delete()` — inside a transaction: send `pre_delete` for each collected object, run the `SET_NULL`/`SET(...)` UPDATEs, DELETE in dependency order (children first), send `post_delete`, and clear pks on the Python objects. Returns `(total, {\"app.Model\": count, ...})`.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj09c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">customer.delete(): Django's Collector applies on_delete in Python</text>\n  <rect x=\"340\" y=\"40\" width=\"200\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#991b1b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Customer #7 .delete()</text>\n  <path d=\"M380,82 L150,124\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj09c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M440,82 L440,124\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj09c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M500,82 L730,124\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj09c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"126\" width=\"240\" height=\"80\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#92400e\" font-weight=\"bold\">Address.customer CASCADE</text>\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"10\">no signals, no further relations</text>\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"186\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"10\">&#8594; FAST delete: one DELETE ... IN</text>\n  <rect x=\"320\" y=\"126\" width=\"240\" height=\"80\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e40af\" font-weight=\"bold\">Ticket.assignee SET_NULL</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">collected as a field update</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"186\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">&#8594; UPDATE ticket SET assignee_id = NULL</text>\n  <rect x=\"610\" y=\"126\" width=\"240\" height=\"80\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"730\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#5b21b6\" font-weight=\"bold\">Order.customer PROTECT</text>\n  <text x=\"730\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">any orders exist?</text>\n  <text x=\"730\" y=\"186\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">&#8594; raise ProtectedError, delete nothing</text>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"226\" width=\"820\" height=\"96\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"248\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">If nothing is protected: collector.delete() in one transaction</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"270\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">1. pre_delete signal for each loaded object (skipped for fast-deleted rows)</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"288\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">2. UPDATEs for SET_NULL / SET_DEFAULT / SET(...)   3. DELETE children first, then parents (dependency order)</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"306\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">4. post_delete signals, instance.pk = None &#8594; returns (total, {\"app.Model\": n, ...})</text>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"338\" width=\"820\" height=\"50\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"358\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#991b1b\" font-size=\"10\">Cost: CASCADE over objects with signals loads every dependant into memory. Deleting a user with 2M events = 2M objects.</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"376\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#991b1b\" font-size=\"10\">DB-level ON DELETE is not used, so raw SQL DELETEs bypass on_delete entirely and hit the FK constraint.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n> **Version note:** Django 6.0 adds an option for database-level on-delete behaviour; in 5.2 LTS, `on_delete` is always emulated by the collector as described here.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### Many-to-many and the through table\n\n```python\nclass Book(models.Model):\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    authors = models.ManyToManyField(\"Author\", related_name=\"books\")\n```\n\nMigrations create a junction table with two FKs and a unique pair:\n\n```sql\nCREATE TABLE \"library_book_authors\" (\n    \"id\" bigint NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY,\n    \"book_id\" bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES \"library_book\" (\"id\") DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED,\n    \"author_id\" bigint NOT NULL REFERENCES \"library_author\" (\"id\") DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED,\n    CONSTRAINT \"library_book_authors_book_id_author_id_..._uniq\" UNIQUE (\"book_id\", \"author_id\")\n);\n```\n\n`book.authors.add(a1, a2)` inserts junction rows. When the through model is auto-created and nobody listens to `m2m_changed`, Django can use `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING` directly; otherwise it first SELECTs existing pairs to avoid duplicates, then bulk-inserts the rest, sending `m2m_changed` (`pre_add`/`post_add`). `remove()` deletes junction rows (not authors), `clear()` deletes all of them for this book, and `set([...])` computes the diff and calls remove/add.\n\nWhen the relationship itself has data — the author's **role** on a book, the **date** a user joined a group, the **position** of a track in a playlist — declare an explicit **through model**:\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 330\" width=\"100%\" height=\"330\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj09d\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">ManyToMany with an explicit through model</text>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"60\" width=\"200\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"130\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e40af\" font-weight=\"bold\">Project</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">id, name</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"126\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">members = M2M(User,</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"142\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">  through=\"Membership\")</text>\n  <rect x=\"340\" y=\"50\" width=\"200\" height=\"140\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">Membership (through)</text>\n  <text x=\"356\" y=\"96\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">project_id  FK CASCADE</text>\n  <text x=\"356\" y=\"114\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">user_id     FK CASCADE</text>\n  <text x=\"356\" y=\"132\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">role        varchar</text>\n  <text x=\"356\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">joined_at   timestamptz</text>\n  <text x=\"356\" y=\"172\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">UniqueConstraint(project, user)</text>\n  <rect x=\"650\" y=\"60\" width=\"200\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"750\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#5b21b6\" font-weight=\"bold\">User</text>\n  <text x=\"666\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">id, email</text>\n  <text x=\"666\" y=\"126\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">reverse: user.projects</text>\n  <text x=\"666\" y=\"142\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">reverse: user.memberships</text>\n  <path d=\"M338,104 L232,104\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj09d)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M542,118 L648,118\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj09d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"210\" width=\"820\" height=\"104\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"232\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">How you use it</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"254\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">project.members.add(u, through_defaults={\"role\": \"editor\"})     &#8594; INSERT into membership (2.2+)</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"272\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Membership.objects.create(project=p, user=u, role=\"owner\")      &#8594; when you need full control</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"290\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">project.members.filter(membership__role=\"owner\")                 &#8594; JOIN through the through table</text>\n  <text x=\"46\" y=\"306\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">project.memberships.select_related(\"user\")                       &#8594; read role + user in one query</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Choosing the relation shape\n\n```text\nDoes each B belong to exactly one A?          → ForeignKey on B  (B.a = FK(A))\nCan each B belong to many A and vice versa?   → ManyToManyField (through= if the link has data)\nIs it exactly one B per A, optional extension? → OneToOneField on B (B.a = O2O(A, primary_key=True?))\nCan B point at many different model types?    → GenericForeignKey (or several nullable FKs + CheckConstraint)\nDoes A point at other A rows (tree, graph)?   → ForeignKey(\"self\") / ManyToManyField(\"self\", symmetrical=...)\n```\n\n### Naming, clashes and `related_query_name`\n\nTwo FKs from one model to the same target both want the default reverse accessor `message_set`, so `manage.py check` fails with `fields.E304`/`E305` (reverse accessor/query name clashes). Give each a distinct `related_name`:\n\n```python\nclass Message(models.Model):\n    sender = models.ForeignKey(User, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"sent_messages\")\n    recipient = models.ForeignKey(User, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"received_messages\")\n```\n\nReverse **lookups** use `related_query_name`, which defaults to `related_name`: `User.objects.filter(sent_messages__subject__icontains=\"invoice\")`. On abstract base classes use placeholders so each concrete child gets a unique name: `related_name=\"%(app_label)s_%(class)s_set\"`. Use `related_name=\"+\"` to suppress the reverse accessor entirely (e.g. `created_by` on 40 models — you do not want 40 accessors on `User`).\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: a blog\n\n```python\n# blog/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\n\n\nclass Category(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=80)\n    parent = models.ForeignKey(\"self\", null=True, blank=True, on_delete=models.CASCADE,\n                               related_name=\"children\")            # self-referential tree\n\n\nclass Tag(models.Model):\n    name = models.SlugField(unique=True)\n\n\nclass Post(models.Model):\n    author = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.PROTECT,\n                               related_name=\"posts\")\n    category = models.ForeignKey(Category, null=True, blank=True, on_delete=models.SET_NULL,\n                                 related_name=\"posts\")\n    tags = models.ManyToManyField(Tag, blank=True, related_name=\"posts\")   # blank: optional in forms\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n\n\nclass AuthorProfile(models.Model):\n    user = models.OneToOneField(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE,\n                                primary_key=True, related_name=\"author_profile\")\n    bio = models.TextField(blank=True)\n```\n\n```python\n>>> post = Post.objects.get(pk=1)        # 1 query\n>>> post.author_id                        # 0 queries: raw column\n3\n>>> post.author.username                  # 1 query, then cached\n>>> post.author.username                  # 0 queries\n>>> user = post.author\n>>> user.posts.count()                    # SELECT COUNT(*) ... WHERE author_id = 3\n>>> user.posts.filter(title__startswith=\"D\")   # new query each call\n>>> post.tags.add(python, django)         # junction INSERTs; m2m_changed fires\n>>> post.tags.set([django])               # removes python, keeps django\n>>> hasattr(user, \"author_profile\")       # False if no profile row (no exception)\n>>> Category.objects.get(name=\"Web\").children.all()   # immediate subcategories\n```\n\nNote `null=True` on `ManyToManyField` does nothing (there is no column) — use `blank=True` to make it optional in forms. And `primary_key=True` on the O2O makes the profile share the user's id: no extra id column, one less index.\n\nThe SQL behind `user.posts.filter(title__startswith=\"D\")`:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"blog_post\".\"id\", \"blog_post\".\"author_id\", \"blog_post\".\"category_id\", \"blog_post\".\"title\"\nFROM \"blog_post\"\nWHERE (\"blog_post\".\"author_id\" = 3 AND \"blog_post\".\"title\"::text LIKE 'D%');\n```\n\nAnd a reverse lookup across the M2M — \"users who wrote posts tagged django\":\n\n```python\nUser.objects.filter(posts__tags__name=\"django\").distinct()\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT DISTINCT \"auth_user\".\"id\", \"auth_user\".\"username\", ...\nFROM \"auth_user\"\nINNER JOIN \"blog_post\" ON (\"auth_user\".\"id\" = \"blog_post\".\"author_id\")\nINNER JOIN \"blog_post_tags\" ON (\"blog_post\".\"id\" = \"blog_post_tags\".\"post_id\")\nINNER JOIN \"blog_tag\" ON (\"blog_post_tags\".\"tag_id\" = \"blog_tag\".\"id\")\nWHERE \"blog_tag\".\"name\" = 'django';\n```\n\nThe `.distinct()` is needed because filtering across to-many relations returns one row per matching join path — a user with three django posts would appear three times. (Multi-valued filter semantics are developed in [Ch 10](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups).)\n\n### Real-world example: a SaaS project tool\n\nRequirements: projects have members with roles and join dates; tasks belong to a project, may be assigned to a member (unassign if the user is deleted), and have subtasks; deleting a project must remove its tasks; users who authored audit records must never be hard-deleted silently; comments can attach to tasks or projects.\n\n```python\n# work/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.contrib.contenttypes.fields import GenericForeignKey, GenericRelation\nfrom django.contrib.contenttypes.models import ContentType\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.utils import timezone\n\n\ndef get_sentinel_user():\n    from django.contrib.auth import get_user_model\n    return get_user_model().objects.get_or_create(username=\"deleted-user\")[0]\n\n\nclass Comment(models.Model):\n    content_type = models.ForeignKey(ContentType, on_delete=models.CASCADE)\n    object_id = models.PositiveBigIntegerField()\n    target = GenericForeignKey(\"content_type\", \"object_id\")\n    author = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.SET(get_sentinel_user),\n                               related_name=\"+\")\n    body = models.TextField()\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(default=timezone.now)\n\n    class Meta:\n        indexes = [models.Index(fields=[\"content_type\", \"object_id\"])]   # GFK lookups need this\n\n\nclass Project(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=120)\n    owner = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.PROTECT,\n                              related_name=\"owned_projects\")\n    members = models.ManyToManyField(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, through=\"Membership\",\n                                     related_name=\"projects\")\n    comments = GenericRelation(Comment, related_query_name=\"project\")   # reverse + cascade\n\n\nclass Membership(models.Model):\n    class Role(models.TextChoices):\n        OWNER = \"owner\"\n        EDITOR = \"editor\"\n        VIEWER = \"viewer\"\n\n    project = models.ForeignKey(Project, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"memberships\")\n    user = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE,\n                             related_name=\"memberships\")\n    role = models.CharField(max_length=10, choices=Role, default=Role.VIEWER)\n    joined_at = models.DateTimeField(default=timezone.now)\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"project\", \"user\"], name=\"uniq_membership\")]\n\n\nclass Task(models.Model):\n    project = models.ForeignKey(Project, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"tasks\")\n    parent = models.ForeignKey(\"self\", null=True, blank=True, on_delete=models.CASCADE,\n                               related_name=\"subtasks\")\n    assignee = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, null=True, blank=True,\n                                 on_delete=models.SET_NULL, related_name=\"assigned_tasks\",\n                                 limit_choices_to={\"is_active\": True})\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    comments = GenericRelation(Comment, related_query_name=\"task\")\n```\n\nUsing it:\n\n```python\np = Project.objects.create(name=\"Launch\", owner=alice)\np.members.add(alice, through_defaults={\"role\": Membership.Role.OWNER})\np.members.add(bob, carol, through_defaults={\"role\": Membership.Role.EDITOR})\n\n# Who are the editors? (JOIN through the through table)\np.members.filter(memberships__role=\"editor\", memberships__project=p)\n\n# Role + user in one query, via the through model\nfor m in p.memberships.select_related(\"user\"):\n    print(m.user.email, m.role, m.joined_at)\n\n# Generic relation: comments on any object\nComment.objects.create(target=task, author=bob, body=\"On it\")\ntask.comments.all()                                    # via GenericRelation\nComment.objects.filter(task__project=p)                # related_query_name makes this possible\n\n# Deleting\nbob.delete()        # tasks assigned to bob → assignee NULL; his memberships deleted;\n                    # his comments reassigned to the sentinel user\nalice.delete()      # ProtectedError: she owns projects\np.delete()          # tasks, subtasks, memberships AND comments (via GenericRelation) deleted\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** without `GenericRelation` on `Task`, deleting a task leaves its comments orphaned — a GFK has no database FK, so nothing cascades. `GenericRelation` is what teaches the collector about the dependency.\n\nThe delete of a project, as captured by `connection.queries` (abridged), shows the collector at work:\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"work_task\" WHERE \"work_task\".\"project_id\" IN (12);            -- collect tasks\nSELECT ... FROM \"work_task\" WHERE \"work_task\".\"parent_id\" IN (40, 41, 42);    -- recurse into subtasks\nDELETE FROM \"work_comment\" WHERE (\"work_comment\".\"content_type_id\" = 18\n       AND \"work_comment\".\"object_id\" IN (40, 41, 42));                       -- fast delete via GenericRelation\nDELETE FROM \"work_membership\" WHERE \"work_membership\".\"project_id\" IN (12);    -- fast delete\nDELETE FROM \"work_task\" WHERE \"work_task\".\"id\" IN (42, 41, 40);\nDELETE FROM \"work_comment\" WHERE (\"work_comment\".\"content_type_id\" = 17 AND \"work_comment\".\"object_id\" IN (12));\nDELETE FROM \"work_project\" WHERE \"work_project\".\"id\" IN (12);\n```\n\n### Self-referential M2M: followers vs friends\n\n```python\nclass Profile(models.Model):\n    user = models.OneToOneField(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE)\n    friends = models.ManyToManyField(\"self\", blank=True)                    # symmetrical=True default\n    following = models.ManyToManyField(\"self\", symmetrical=False, blank=True,\n                                       related_name=\"followers\")\n```\n\n`a.friends.add(b)` also makes `b.friends` contain `a` (Django writes both rows). `a.following.add(b)` is one-way, and `b.followers.all()` gives the reverse.\n\n### Trees in practice\n\n`ForeignKey(\"self\")` (adjacency list) is simple and writes are cheap, but reading a whole subtree needs one query per level — or a recursive CTE via raw SQL ([SQL · Recursive CTEs](../sql/topic.html?p=12-recursive-cte)). Libraries such as django-treebeard (materialized path / nested sets) or django-mptt make subtree reads a single indexed query at the cost of more expensive writes.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Choice | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| `ForeignKey` | Real DB integrity, indexed, joins well | Must choose `on_delete` carefully |\n| Auto M2M | Zero boilerplate, `add/remove/set` | No extra data; hard to migrate to a through model later |\n| Explicit through model | Extra columns, own constraints, own admin | More code; create rows via the through model or `through_defaults` |\n| `OneToOneField` | Splits optional/large data from a hot table; extends third-party models | Reverse access raises if missing; an extra join |\n| `GenericForeignKey` | One table for \"attach to anything\" | No FK integrity, no direct joins, needs `GenericRelation` for cascades, index on `(content_type, object_id)` |\n| Several nullable FKs + `CheckConstraint` (exactly one set) | Real integrity, normal joins | One column per target type |\n| `db_constraint=False` | Works across DBs / huge tables / legacy | Integrity is your problem; orphans happen |\n\n### When to use\n- `PROTECT` for anything with financial, legal or audit meaning; `CASCADE` for true composition (lines of an order).\n- An explicit through model **from the start** whenever a relationship could plausibly gain attributes.\n- `OneToOneField(User, primary_key=True)` for profiles instead of modifying the user table (if you did not make a custom user model).\n\n### When NOT to use\n- `GenericForeignKey` for core relations you query and join constantly — use concrete FKs.\n- `CASCADE` from users to business records; deleting an account should not erase invoices.\n- Very deep `ForeignKey(\"self\")` hierarchies read as whole subtrees on every request — use a tree library or CTE.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Reflexive `on_delete=models.CASCADE`.** Copy-pasted everywhere, it turns one admin delete into data loss across the schema. *Instead:* decide per relation; prefer `PROTECT` for business data and soft-delete users.\n\n**2. Hard-coding `User`.** `ForeignKey(\"auth.User\")` or `from django.contrib.auth.models import User` breaks with a custom user model. *Instead:* `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL` in model fields and `get_user_model()` in code ([Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user)).\n\n**3. `obj.fk.id` instead of `obj.fk_id`.** The former loads the related object to read a value you already have. *Instead:* `order.customer_id`, and `filter(customer_id=...)`.\n\n**4. Missing `related_name`.** Default `order_set` names are vague and clash when two FKs share a target. *Instead:* always name reverse accessors in plural business terms.\n\n**5. Treating a prefetched manager like a list.**\n\n```python\n# Before: prefetch is wasted, one query per project\nprojects = Project.objects.prefetch_related(\"tasks\")\nfor p in projects:\n    open_tasks = p.tasks.filter(done=False)          # new query each time\n\n# After: filter in the prefetch, store on an attribute\nfrom django.db.models import Prefetch\nprojects = Project.objects.prefetch_related(\n    Prefetch(\"tasks\", queryset=Task.objects.filter(done=False), to_attr=\"open_tasks\"))\nfor p in projects:\n    p.open_tasks                                     # list, no query\n```\n\n**6. `null=True` on `ManyToManyField`.** Has no effect and triggers a system check warning. Use `blank=True`.\n\n**7. Reverse O2O without handling absence.** `request.user.profile` raises `RelatedObjectDoesNotExist` for users without a profile → 500. *Instead:* `getattr(user, \"profile\", None)` / `hasattr`, or guarantee creation in the signup service.\n\n**8. GFK without an index.** Lookups by `(content_type_id, object_id)` sequential-scan the comments table. Add `Index(fields=[\"content_type\", \"object_id\"])`.\n\n**9. Changing an auto M2M to a through model by editing the field.** Django cannot migrate that automatically and wants to drop and recreate the table. *Instead:* `SeparateDatabaseAndState` to adopt the existing table as the through model ([Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations)).\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**The cascading delete that took the site down.** Support deletes a spam account; the account has 3 million `Event` rows with a `post_delete` receiver. The collector loads every event into memory to send signals, the request runs for minutes, holds locks and the worker gets OOM-killed. Symptom: one admin click → memory spike and lock waits on `events`. Fix: soft-delete users; delete dependants in batches in a background task; remove signal receivers on high-volume models so fast-delete applies; or `on_delete=DO_NOTHING` with a database-level cleanup job.\n\n**`ProtectedError` surfaced as 500.** A view calls `obj.delete()` on something referenced via `PROTECT`. Catch `ProtectedError`/`RestrictedError` and return a 409/form error explaining what depends on the object (the exception carries `protected_objects`).\n\n**Raw SQL bypasses `on_delete`.** A data engineer runs `DELETE FROM accounts_customer WHERE ...` — Django's cascades never run, the FK constraint fails at commit, or, with `db_constraint=False`, orphans appear silently. Document that the DB has no `ON DELETE`; add constraints/scripts accordingly.\n\n**Unindexed through/GFK lookups.** Auto M2M through tables get indexes on both FKs plus the unique pair; hand-written through models get FK indexes automatically, but if you query `Membership.objects.filter(user=..., role=...)` heavily, add a composite index.\n\n**FK targets changing type.** Switching a target's pk from `int` to `bigint` (e.g. fixing `W042`) also alters every FK column pointing at it — on large tables that is a long rewrite. Plan it as a dedicated migration.\n\n**Debugging relations.** `Model._meta.get_fields()` lists forward and reverse relations (`include_hidden=True` shows `+` ones). `Model._meta.related_objects` shows what will be considered on delete. To preview a delete without running it:\n\n```python\nfrom django.db import router\nfrom django.db.models.deletion import Collector\n\ncollector = Collector(using=router.db_for_write(Customer))\ncollector.collect([customer])\nprint({model._meta.label: len(objs) for model, objs in collector.data.items()})\nprint(collector.fast_deletes)        # querysets that will be deleted without loading\n```\n\nThe admin's delete confirmation page uses the same machinery (`NestedObjects`) to show what will cascade.\n\n**Scaling notes.** Very high-cardinality reverse relations (a user's events) should not be traversed with `user.events.all()` in requests — paginate or aggregate. M2M `prefetch_related` over thousands of parents builds a large `IN (...)` list; batch it or use `iterator(chunk_size=...)` with prefetch. Partitioned tables and sharding may force `db_constraint=False` ([Database Design · Sharding](../database-design/topic.html?p=12-sharding)).\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: Explain the on_delete options for ForeignKey.**\nA: `CASCADE` deletes referencing rows along with the target; `PROTECT` refuses the deletion with `ProtectedError`; `RESTRICT` (3.1+) also refuses, raising `RestrictedError`, except when the referencing object is itself being deleted through another CASCADE path in the same operation; `SET_NULL` sets the FK to NULL and requires `null=True`; `SET_DEFAULT` sets the field's default; `SET(value_or_callable)` sets a given value such as a sentinel user; and `DO_NOTHING` leaves it to the database constraint, which normally raises an `IntegrityError`. Importantly, Django emulates these in Python with its deletion collector rather than creating `ON DELETE` clauses in the schema. Choose per relation: CASCADE for composition, PROTECT for business and financial records.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between PROTECT and RESTRICT?**\nA: Both prevent deleting an object that is still referenced. `PROTECT` always blocks, even if the referencing rows would themselves be deleted by another cascade in the same delete. `RESTRICT` allows the deletion when every referencing object is already being removed via a CASCADE from somewhere else in the same operation, and blocks it otherwise. It is useful in graphs where a parent's cascade should win but direct deletion of the restricted target should not. Both are enforced by Django's collector, not the database.\n\n**Q: What is related_name and why should you set it?**\nA: It names the reverse accessor that a `ForeignKey`, `OneToOneField` or `ManyToManyField` installs on the target model, for example `customer.orders` instead of the default `customer.order_set`. It also becomes the default `related_query_name` used in reverse filters like `Customer.objects.filter(orders__total__gt=100)`. You must set it when two relations from one model point at the same target, or `check` fails with a clash error. Setting it to `\"+\"` disables the reverse relation, and on abstract models you use `%(app_label)s` / `%(class)s` placeholders to keep names unique.\n\n**Q: How do you store extra data on a many-to-many relationship?**\nA: Declare an explicit through model with two ForeignKeys and the extra fields, and point the M2M at it: `members = ManyToManyField(User, through=\"Membership\")`. Add a `UniqueConstraint` on the pair if duplicates are not allowed. Since Django 2.2 you can still use `add()`, `create()` and `set()` with `through_defaults={\"role\": \"editor\"}`, or create `Membership` rows directly. Query extra fields by filtering across the through model (`members__memberships__role`) or by iterating `project.memberships.select_related(\"user\")`. Decide early, because converting an auto M2M to a through model requires a `SeparateDatabaseAndState` migration.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between ForeignKey(unique=True) and OneToOneField?**\nA: At the database level they are identical: a FK column with a unique constraint. The difference is in the Python API. With `OneToOneField` the reverse accessor returns a single object (`user.profile`) and raises `RelatedObjectDoesNotExist` if missing; with `ForeignKey(unique=True)` the reverse accessor is still a manager (`user.profile_set.get()`), which is clumsy. Django even emits a system check warning suggesting `OneToOneField`. O2O is also what multi-table inheritance uses as the parent link.\n\n**Q: Why does accessing order.customer inside a loop slow down a page?**\nA: The forward accessor queries the related object the first time it is accessed on each instance and caches it only on that instance. Iterating 100 orders and touching `order.customer` therefore issues 100 extra queries — the N+1 problem. The fix is `select_related(\"customer\")`, which loads customers with a JOIN in the same query, or `prefetch_related` for reverse and many-to-many relations. If you only need the id, read `order.customer_id`, which never queries.\n\n**Q: What happens in the database when you call book.authors.add(author)?**\nA: Django inserts a row into the junction table linking `book_id` and `author_id`. If the through table is auto-created and no `m2m_changed` receivers exist, Django can insert with `ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING`; otherwise it first SELECTs which pairs already exist and inserts only the missing ones. It sends `m2m_changed` with `pre_add` and `post_add` actions. The book must already be saved, since the junction row needs its primary key. `save()` and `post_save` of Book are not involved at all — which is why signal handlers on M2M changes must listen to `m2m_changed`.\n\n**Q: What is a GenericForeignKey and what are its downsides?**\nA: It is a polymorphic relation from `django.contrib.contenttypes`: a `content_type` FK to `ContentType`, an `object_id` column and a `GenericForeignKey(\"content_type\", \"object_id\")` descriptor that resolves to an object of any model. It is handy for comments, tags, likes or audit records that attach to many types. Downsides: no database foreign key, so no referential integrity and orphans unless you add `GenericRelation` on each target (which also enables cascades and reverse queries); you cannot filter or `select_related` through the GFK itself; and you need an index on `(content_type, object_id)`. `prefetch_related` does support GFKs, and Django 5.0 adds `GenericPrefetch` for per-type querysets.\n\n**Q: Why should you use settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL in ForeignKeys?**\nA: Because the user model is swappable: projects can define their own and set `AUTH_USER_MODEL`. A FK to `\"auth.User\"` or an imported `User` class hard-wires the default model, breaking reusable apps and making migration to a custom user model painful. `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL` is a lazy string reference that migrations record as a swappable dependency. In runtime code use `get_user_model()`.\n\n**Q: A support agent deletes one user in the admin and the database CPU spikes, the request times out and other requests stall. What happened and how would you prevent it? (Senior)**\nA: The deletion collector followed every reverse relation with `CASCADE`. For related models that have signal receivers or further cascades, it loads all dependent rows into Python to send `pre_delete`/`post_delete`, then deletes them in one transaction, holding row locks for the duration — with millions of events or logs that is minutes of work and huge memory. Prevention: soft-delete accounts (an `is_active`/`deleted_at` flag) instead of hard deletes; use `PROTECT` on business data so a hard delete fails fast; remove signal receivers from high-volume child models so fast delete (a single `DELETE ... IN`) applies; and perform necessary purges asynchronously in batches. Disable the admin delete for such models with `has_delete_permission`.\n\n**Q: When would you choose a GenericForeignKey versus separate nullable foreign keys, and how would you keep integrity? (Senior)**\nA: A GFK fits when the set of target types is open-ended or plugin-driven, reads are mostly \"comments of this object\", and you can live without DB-level integrity. If the targets are a small fixed set (say tasks and projects) that you query and join often, separate nullable FKs — `task = FK(null=True)`, `project = FK(null=True)` — give real foreign keys, normal joins, `select_related` and cascades. Enforce \"exactly one is set\" with a `CheckConstraint` such as `Q(task__isnull=False, project__isnull=True) | Q(task__isnull=True, project__isnull=False)`. For GFKs, keep integrity by declaring `GenericRelation` on every target, indexing `(content_type, object_id)`, and running periodic orphan-detection jobs.\n\n**Q: How do you model hierarchical data like categories or org charts in Django, and what are the trade-offs? (Senior)**\nA: The simplest is an adjacency list, `parent = ForeignKey(\"self\", null=True, on_delete=...)`: cheap writes and moves, but fetching a full subtree needs one query per level or a recursive CTE via raw SQL. Materialized path (django-treebeard's `MP_Node`) stores the path string, so a subtree is one indexed `LIKE 'path%'` query, at the cost of rewriting paths on moves. Nested sets (treebeard `NS_Node`, django-mptt) make subtree reads very fast but make inserts and moves expensive because many rows are renumbered. Choose by read/write ratio and tree depth, and consider PostgreSQL's `ltree` for path queries. Also pick `on_delete` deliberately: CASCADE on `parent` deletes whole subtrees.\n\n**Q: How would you migrate an existing auto-created ManyToMany to an explicit through model without data loss? (Senior)**\nA: Changing `through=` on an existing field makes the autodetector want to drop and recreate the table, losing rows. Instead, create a through model whose `db_table` is the existing junction table name and whose two FK fields match its columns, then write a migration using `SeparateDatabaseAndState`: in `state_operations`, create the through model and alter the field to use it; in `database_operations`, do nothing (or only rename). In a follow-up migration, add the new columns (role, joined_at) with defaults normally. Test on a copy of production and check `sqlmigrate` to confirm no destructive SQL.\n\n**Q: Why does User.objects.filter(posts__tags__name=\"django\") return duplicates?**\nA: Filtering across a to-many relation produces a JOIN, and the result has one row per matching combination — a user with three posts tagged django appears three times. Django does not de-duplicate automatically. Add `.distinct()`, or better, express it as `filter(Exists(Post.objects.filter(author=OuterRef(\"pk\"), tags__name=\"django\")))`, which never duplicates and is often faster on large tables. The same issue affects `count()` and aggregates over such joins.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Relation | Declare on | DB | Forward | Reverse |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| FK | the \"many\" side | `<name>_id` + FK constraint + index | `obj.fk` (object, cached) | `target.<related_name>` manager (`<model>_set`) |\n| O2O | the extension side | FK + UNIQUE | `obj.o2o` | `target.<related_name>` single object or raises |\n| M2M | either side | junction table, unique pair | manager | manager |\n| M2M through | either side | your model | manager | manager + `through` reverse |\n| GFK | the attaching model | `content_type_id` + `object_id` (no FK) | object of any type | `GenericRelation` |\n\n- `on_delete` is emulated by the Collector in Python; no DB `ON DELETE` in 5.2.\n- `PROTECT` for business data; `CASCADE` for composition; `SET_NULL` needs `null=True`.\n- `obj.fk_id` = no query; `obj.fk` = query once (or `select_related`).\n- Reverse managers query every time; `.filter()` defeats a prefetch — use `Prefetch(to_attr=...)`.\n- `related_name` on every relation; `\"+\"` to disable; `%(class)s` on abstract models.\n- M2M: `add/remove/set/clear`, `through_defaults`, `m2m_changed` (not `post_save`).\n- `null` on M2M is meaningless; use `blank=True`.\n- FK to users: `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL`.\n- Filtering across to-many can duplicate rows: `distinct()` or `Exists()`.\n- GFK needs an index and `GenericRelation` for cascades.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"psycopg[binary]\" django-debug-toolbar\ndjango-admin startproject rellab . && python manage.py startapp work\n```\n\n1. **DDL inspection.** Create `Project`, `Task`, `Membership` from §5 and read `sqlmigrate` output; find the FK constraints, the indexes, and confirm there is no `ON DELETE`.\n2. **on_delete lab.** Create one user with owned projects, assigned tasks and comments. Try deleting them under `PROTECT`, then switch to `SET(get_sentinel_user)`; preview deletes with the `Collector` snippet before running them.\n3. **RESTRICT vs PROTECT.** Build the Artist/Album/Song example and show the case where `RESTRICT` allows deletion and `PROTECT` does not.\n4. **Through model.** Use `add(..., through_defaults=...)`, then query all projects where a user is an `owner` and list members with roles in one query.\n5. **Duplicates.** Write the \"users with posts tagged django\" query with and without `distinct()`, then with `Exists`; compare SQL and `EXPLAIN`.\n6. **GFK integrity.** Remove `GenericRelation` from `Task`, delete a task and find the orphaned comments; add it back and repeat.\n\n**Mini project — \"Team workspace\":** organisations → teams → members (through model with role), projects with tasks and subtasks, comments via either GFK or a constrained pair of FKs (justify your choice in a README), soft-deleted users with a sentinel for authored content, a \"delete organisation\" Celery task that removes data in batches, and tests asserting delete behaviour and query counts for a project page.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) · [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) · [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations) · [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) · [Ch 15 · Custom Managers, QuerySets & Model Inheritance](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance) · [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals) (`m2m_changed`, delete signals) · [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user) · [Ch 36 · Django Internals](topic.html?p=36-django-internals) (related descriptors)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [SQL · Joins](../sql/topic.html?p=05-joins) · [SQL · Keys & Constraints](../sql/topic.html?p=29-keys-constraints) · [SQL · Recursive CTEs](../sql/topic.html?p=12-recursive-cte) · [SQL · Schema Design](../sql/topic.html?p=30-schema-design)\n\n- **Making queries — related objects** — Django docs · *Beginner* · forward and reverse access, related managers and lookups across relations. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/queries/>\n- **Model field reference — relationship fields** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · every option of ForeignKey, ManyToManyField and OneToOneField, including all `on_delete` values. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/fields/>\n- **Many-to-many relationships** — Django docs · *Beginner* · worked examples of `add`, `remove`, `set` and through models. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/examples/many_to_many/>\n- **Related objects reference** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · the related manager API in detail. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/relations/>\n- **The contenttypes framework** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · GenericForeignKey, GenericRelation and their caveats. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/contrib/contenttypes/>\n- **django-treebeard** — GitHub · *Advanced* · materialized path and nested set trees for Django. <https://github.com/django-treebeard/django-treebeard>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 09.*\n", "10-querysets-lookups": "# 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions\n\n> **In one line:** A QuerySet is a lazy, immutable description of a SQL query that runs only when you ask for its results and then caches them — so writing good ORM code means knowing exactly when a QuerySet is evaluated, how lookups and `Q`/`F` objects compile into the `WHERE` clause, and which methods run a query you did not expect.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) and [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships) (the fields and relations you query across). Aggregation and subqueries continue in [Ch 12 · Aggregation, Annotation, Subqueries & Window Functions](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries); N+1 and performance tooling in [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization).\n\nEvery web request in a Django app ends up asking the database questions: *the 20 most recent published posts*, *orders over 100 from customers in India placed this month*, *does this email already exist?*, *decrement stock by one*. The naive approaches are both bad. Writing raw SQL strings everywhere gives up portability, composability and protection against injection. Loading everything into Python and filtering there (`[o for o in Order.objects.all() if o.total > 100]`) moves millions of rows over the network to discard most of them.\n\nThe Django ORM gives you the **QuerySet**: a Python object that *describes* a query and can be refined step by step — `Order.objects.filter(status=\"paid\").exclude(customer__country=\"US\").order_by(\"-placed_at\")[:20]` — without touching the database. Only when you actually need rows (iterate, `list()`, `len()`, `bool()`, print it) does Django compile the description into one SQL statement, execute it, and cache the results on that QuerySet.\n\nThat laziness is the source of both the ORM's power and its most common bugs. Power: you can build a query across functions, add filters conditionally, pass it to a template, and still send a single, precise `SELECT`. Bugs: a QuerySet evaluated twice runs twice; `if qs:` loads every row just to check emptiness; a \"small\" `print(qs)` in production fetches 21 rows; `obj.balance = obj.balance - 10; obj.save()` races with other requests where `F(\"balance\") - 10` would not. Interviewers love this territory — \"are QuerySets lazy?\", \"get vs filter\", \"Q vs F\", \"exists vs count\", \"what does this generate in SQL?\" — because the answers reveal whether you understand what your code sends to the database.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **QuerySet** — a lazy, chainable representation of a query over one model. Each method like `filter()` returns a **new** QuerySet (a clone). *Why it matters:* building is free; evaluation is the cost.\n- **Manager** — the entry point (`Model.objects`) that hands out QuerySets. *Why it matters:* custom managers change the default starting query ([Ch 15](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance)).\n- **Evaluation** — the moment SQL runs: iteration, `list()`, `len()`, `bool()`, `repr()`, slicing with a step, pickling, `x in qs`. *Why it matters:* everything before that is just Python object construction.\n- **Result cache** — `qs._result_cache`, filled by `_fetch_all()` on first evaluation; later iterations of the *same* QuerySet object reuse it. *Why it matters:* reuse the evaluated object; a new chain (`qs.all()`, `qs.filter()`) means a new query.\n- **Lookup** — the `__op` suffix in a filter keyword: `exact`, `iexact`, `contains`, `icontains`, `in`, `gt`, `gte`, `lt`, `lte`, `startswith`, `endswith`, `range`, `isnull`, `regex`. The default is `exact`.\n- **Transform** — a suffix that changes the value before the lookup: `__date`, `__year`, `__month`, `__lower`, `__len` (on some fields). Chainable: `placed_at__date__gte`.\n- **Spanning relations** — `__` follows relationships and becomes JOINs: `customer__country__code=\"IN\"`.\n- **`Q` object** — encapsulates a condition so you can combine with `|` (OR), `&` (AND), `~` (NOT), `^` (XOR, 4.2+). *Why it matters:* keyword arguments can only express AND.\n- **`F` expression** — a reference to a column's value **in the database**, used in filters, updates and annotations. *Why it matters:* compare two columns, or update atomically without a read-modify-write race.\n- **`values()` / `values_list()`** — return dicts / tuples instead of model instances (`flat=True` for one field, `named=True` for namedtuples). *Why it matters:* cheaper, and the gateway to `GROUP BY`.\n- **`get()`** — exactly one object or an exception (`DoesNotExist`, `MultipleObjectsReturned`).\n- **`exists()` / `count()`** — `SELECT 1 ... LIMIT 1` vs `SELECT COUNT(*)`: presence check vs number.\n- **`iterator()`** — streams rows without filling the result cache, using server-side cursors on PostgreSQL. *Why it matters:* bounded memory for large scans.\n- **`sql.Query` / `SQLCompiler`** — the internal objects that hold the query tree and turn it into SQL. *Why it matters:* `str(qs.query)` and `qs.explain()` are how you inspect what will run.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### Laziness, cloning and evaluation — step by step\n\n```python\nqs = Order.objects.filter(status=\"paid\")      # 1. no SQL\nqs = qs.exclude(total__lt=10)                  # 2. no SQL (new clone)\nqs = qs.order_by(\"-placed_at\")                 # 3. no SQL\nfirst_page = qs[:20]                           # 4. no SQL: adds LIMIT 20\nfor order in first_page:                       # 5. SQL runs NOW, once\n    ...\nfor order in first_page:                       # 6. no SQL: result cache\n    ...\n```\n\nInternally, a `QuerySet` wraps a `django.db.models.sql.Query` object (`qs.query`) — a tree of the model, joins (`alias_map`), the `where` node, ordering, limits and selected columns. `filter(**kwargs)` calls `_filter_or_exclude()`, which **clones** the QuerySet (`_chain()` → `_clone()`, copying the `Query`) and calls `query.add_q(Q(**kwargs))`. `add_q` resolves each keyword: it splits `customer__country__code` on `__`, walks `_meta` to find relations (`setup_joins`, adding JOINs to the alias map), finds the final field, applies any transforms, picks the lookup class (`Exact`, `IContains`, `GreaterThan`…) and adds it to the `WhereNode`. Nothing is sent to the database.\n\nEvaluation goes through `_fetch_all()`: if `_result_cache` is `None`, it does `self._result_cache = list(self._iterable_class(self))`. The default iterable is `ModelIterable`, which calls `query.get_compiler(using).execute_sql()`; `SQLCompiler.as_sql()` renders the tree into SQL text with `%s` placeholders and a separate params list (the driver substitutes them safely — this is why ORM filters are immune to SQL injection), and `ModelIterable` turns each row into an instance via `Model.from_db()`. `values()` swaps in `ValuesIterable`; `values_list()` uses `ValuesListIterable` or `FlatValuesListIterable`.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 420\" width=\"100%\" height=\"420\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj10a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj10b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">A QuerySet's life: build lazily, evaluate once, cache</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"840\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#eff6ff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e40af\" font-weight=\"bold\">BUILD phase &#8212; pure Python, zero queries; every call returns a clone</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"78\" width=\"170\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"121\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Order.objects</text>\n  <text x=\"121\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Manager.get_queryset()</text>\n  <text x=\"121\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Query(model=Order)</text>\n  <path d=\"M208,108 L232,108\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj10a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"234\" y=\"78\" width=\"190\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"329\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">.filter(customer__country=\"IN\")</text>\n  <text x=\"329\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">_chain() + query.add_q(Q(...))</text>\n  <text x=\"329\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">setup_joins &#8594; JOIN; lookup &#8594; WhereNode</text>\n  <path d=\"M426,108 L450,108\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj10a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"452\" y=\"78\" width=\"180\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"542\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">.order_by(\"-placed_at\")</text>\n  <text x=\"542\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">query.order_by = (...)</text>\n  <text x=\"542\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">still no SQL</text>\n  <path d=\"M634,108 L658,108\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj10a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"660\" y=\"78\" width=\"184\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"752\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">[:20]</text>\n  <text x=\"752\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">query.set_limits(0, 20)</text>\n  <text x=\"752\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">LIMIT 20 (no negative index)</text>\n  <path d=\"M440,156 L440,180\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj10a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"182\" width=\"840\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"202\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#92400e\" font-weight=\"bold\">EVALUATION triggers &#8212; _fetch_all() runs only if _result_cache is None</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"10\">for x in qs &#183; list(qs) &#183; len(qs) &#183; bool(qs) / if qs &#183; repr(qs) (LIMIT 21) &#183; qs[::2] &#183; pickle &#183; x in qs</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b45309\" font-size=\"10\">NOT triggers: filter/exclude/order_by/annotate/values, qs[:20], qs.query  &#183;  own query: count(), exists(), get(), first()</text>\n  <path d=\"M440,254 L440,278\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj10a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"280\" width=\"270\" height=\"76\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"302\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#5b21b6\" font-weight=\"bold\">SQLCompiler.as_sql()</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"322\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">SQL text with %s placeholders</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"340\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">+ params list &#8594; driver (no injection)</text>\n  <path d=\"M292,318 L316,318\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj10a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"318\" y=\"280\" width=\"250\" height=\"76\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"443\" y=\"302\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#5b21b6\" font-weight=\"bold\">ModelIterable</text>\n  <text x=\"443\" y=\"322\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">rows &#8594; Model.from_db() instances</text>\n  <text x=\"443\" y=\"340\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">(ValuesIterable for values())</text>\n  <path d=\"M570,318 L594,318\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj10b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"596\" y=\"280\" width=\"264\" height=\"76\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"728\" y=\"302\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">qs._result_cache = [...]</text>\n  <text x=\"728\" y=\"322\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">re-iterate same object: 0 queries</text>\n  <text x=\"728\" y=\"340\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">qs.all() / qs.filter(): NEW query</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"370\" width=\"840\" height=\"38\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"393\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">iterator(chunk_size=N) bypasses the cache and streams with a server-side cursor on PostgreSQL &#8212; constant memory for big scans</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Methods that query on their own\n\nSome methods do not evaluate the QuerySet but **run their own query** immediately:\n\n| Method | SQL (PostgreSQL) | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| `count()` | `SELECT COUNT(*) AS \"__count\" FROM ...` | If the QuerySet is already evaluated, returns `len(_result_cache)` without a query |\n| `exists()` | `SELECT 1 AS \"a\" FROM ... LIMIT 1` | Cheapest presence test; uses cache if evaluated |\n| `get(...)` | `SELECT ... WHERE ... LIMIT 21` | Limit lets Django report \"returned more than one (it returned 21!)\" without fetching millions |\n| `first()` / `last()` | `ORDER BY \"id\" ASC/DESC LIMIT 1` if unordered | Returns `None` if empty, never raises |\n| `in_bulk(ids)` | `WHERE id IN (...)` | Returns `{pk: obj}` |\n| `aggregate(...)` | `SELECT SUM(...) ...` | Returns a dict ([Ch 12](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries)) |\n| `update(...)` / `delete()` | `UPDATE ...` / collector + `DELETE` | Bulk; skip `save()` (update) |\n| `contains(obj)` (4.0+) | `SELECT 1 ... WHERE pk = %s LIMIT 1` | Membership without loading all rows |\n\n### Lookups and how they become SQL\n\n| Filter | PostgreSQL SQL fragment |\n|---|---|\n| `title=\"Dune\"` / `title__exact=\"Dune\"` | `\"title\" = 'Dune'` |\n| `title__iexact=\"dune\"` | `UPPER(\"title\"::text) = UPPER('dune')` |\n| `title__icontains=\"dun\"` | `UPPER(\"title\"::text) LIKE UPPER('%dun%')` |\n| `title__startswith=\"Du\"` | `\"title\"::text LIKE 'Du%'` |\n| `price__gte=10` | `\"price\" >= 10` |\n| `id__in=[1, 2, 3]` | `\"id\" IN (1, 2, 3)` |\n| `price__range=(10, 20)` | `\"price\" BETWEEN 10 AND 20` |\n| `deleted_at__isnull=True` / `deleted_at=None` | `\"deleted_at\" IS NULL` |\n| `placed_at__year=2026` | `\"placed_at\" BETWEEN '2026-01-01 00:00:00+00' AND '2026-12-31 23:59:59.999999+00'` |\n| `placed_at__date=date(2026, 10, 25)` | `(\"placed_at\" AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')::date = '2026-10-25'` |\n| `customer__country__code=\"IN\"` | `INNER JOIN customer ... INNER JOIN country ... WHERE country.code = 'IN'` |\n\nNote the contrast between `__year` (rewritten into an index-friendly `BETWEEN`) and `__date` (wraps the column in a cast, which a plain B-tree index on `placed_at` cannot use). `id__in=[]` short-circuits to an empty result without querying (`EmptyResultSet`).\n\n### Q objects: building boolean logic\n\nKeyword arguments to `filter()` are ANDed. For OR and NOT you wrap conditions in `Q`:\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models import Q\n\nOrder.objects.filter(Q(status=\"paid\") | Q(status=\"shipped\"), total__gte=100)\nOrder.objects.filter(~Q(customer__country__code=\"US\"))\nOrder.objects.filter(Q(is_gift=True) ^ Q(gift_note=\"\"))              # XOR, 4.2+\n```\n\n```sql\nWHERE ((\"shop_order\".\"status\" = 'paid' OR \"shop_order\".\"status\" = 'shipped')\n       AND \"shop_order\".\"total\" >= 100)\n```\n\nA `Q` is a tree node (`django.utils.tree.Node`) with a connector (`AND`/`OR`/`XOR`), a `negated` flag and children. `add_q` compiles it recursively into a `WhereNode` with the same shape. Positional `Q` arguments must come **before** keyword arguments in the call. You can build them dynamically:\n\n```python\nimport operator\nfrom functools import reduce\n\nterms = [\"linen\", \"shirt\"]\ncond = reduce(operator.and_, (Q(title__icontains=t) | Q(description__icontains=t) for t in terms))\nProduct.objects.filter(cond)\n```\n\n### F expressions: the database's view of a column\n\n`F(\"field\")` refers to the column value at the moment the database evaluates the statement, not the value in your Python object:\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models import F\n\nProduct.objects.filter(sale_price__lt=F(\"price\") * 0.8)       # compare two columns\nProduct.objects.filter(pk=7).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - 1)     # atomic decrement\nOrder.objects.annotate(net=F(\"subtotal\") - F(\"discount\"))     # computed column\nOrder.objects.filter(shipped_at__gt=F(\"placed_at\") + timedelta(days=3))\nOrder.objects.order_by(F(\"shipped_at\").desc(nulls_last=True))\n```\n\n```sql\nUPDATE \"catalog_product\" SET \"stock\" = (\"catalog_product\".\"stock\" - 1) WHERE \"catalog_product\".\"id\" = 7;\n```\n\nThe update is a single statement executed atomically by the database, so 50 concurrent requests decrement 50 times. The read-modify-write alternative (`p.stock -= 1; p.save()`) loses updates when requests interleave — and also writes every other column back ([Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency)). After assigning an `F()` to an instance attribute and saving, the attribute holds the expression object, not a number: call `refresh_from_db()` before reading it, and do not save twice (the expression would be applied again).\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 360\" width=\"100%\" height=\"360\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj10c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Two requests decrement stock=10: read-modify-write vs F()</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"410\" height=\"300\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef2f2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#991b1b\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">p.stock -= 1; p.save()  &#8212; lost update</text>\n  <text x=\"100\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Request A</text>\n  <text x=\"340\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Request B</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"104\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT &#8594; stock=10</text>\n  <rect x=\"280\" y=\"144\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"345\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT &#8594; stock=10</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"184\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"203\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">UPDATE stock=9</text>\n  <rect x=\"280\" y=\"224\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"345\" y=\"243\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">UPDATE stock=9</text>\n  <path d=\"M225,100 L225,262\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#991b1b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Final stock = 9 (should be 8)</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">both computed 10 - 1 in Python from a stale read;</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"326\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">save() also rewrites every other column</text>\n  <rect x=\"450\" y=\"44\" width=\"410\" height=\"300\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f0fdf4\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15803d\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">update(stock=F(\"stock\") - 1)  &#8212; atomic</text>\n  <text x=\"530\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Request A</text>\n  <text x=\"770\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Request B</text>\n  <rect x=\"465\" y=\"104\" width=\"160\" height=\"40\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"545\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">UPDATE SET stock =</text><text x=\"545\" y=\"134\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">stock - 1 &#8594; 9 (row lock)</text>\n  <rect x=\"690\" y=\"164\" width=\"160\" height=\"40\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"770\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">waits for A's lock, then</text><text x=\"770\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">stock - 1 &#8594; 8</text>\n  <path d=\"M627,140 L688,176\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj10c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M655,100 L655,262\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"1\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">add filter(stock__gt=0) to never go negative:</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"252\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">update() returns rows changed (0 = sold out)</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15803d\" font-weight=\"bold\">Final stock = 8</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">arithmetic happens inside the DB statement;</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"326\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">no stale Python value involved</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### Spanning multi-valued relations: one `filter()` or two?\n\nThis is the most misunderstood rule in the ORM. For **single-valued** relations (forward FK/O2O) it makes no difference. For **multi-valued** relations (reverse FK, M2M), conditions inside **one** `filter()` call must be satisfied by the **same** related row; conditions in **separate chained** `filter()` calls may be satisfied by **different** related rows, because each call adds its own JOIN.\n\n```python\n# Blogs that have an entry which is BOTH about Lennon AND from 2008\nBlog.objects.filter(entry__headline__contains=\"Lennon\", entry__pub_date__year=2008)\n\n# Blogs that have an entry about Lennon, and an entry (maybe another) from 2008\nBlog.objects.filter(entry__headline__contains=\"Lennon\").filter(entry__pub_date__year=2008)\n```\n\n```sql\n-- one filter(): single JOIN, both conditions on the same entry row\nSELECT \"blog\".\"id\", ... FROM \"blog\"\nINNER JOIN \"entry\" ON (\"blog\".\"id\" = \"entry\".\"blog_id\")\nWHERE (\"entry\".\"headline\"::text LIKE '%Lennon%'\n       AND \"entry\".\"pub_date\" BETWEEN '2008-01-01' AND '2008-12-31');\n\n-- chained filter(): two JOINs (T3 is a second alias of entry)\nSELECT \"blog\".\"id\", ... FROM \"blog\"\nINNER JOIN \"entry\" ON (\"blog\".\"id\" = \"entry\".\"blog_id\")\nINNER JOIN \"entry\" T3 ON (\"blog\".\"id\" = T3.\"blog_id\")\nWHERE (\"entry\".\"headline\"::text LIKE '%Lennon%'\n       AND T3.\"pub_date\" BETWEEN '2008-01-01' AND '2008-12-31');\n```\n\n`exclude()` over multi-valued relations is different again: `Blog.objects.exclude(entry__headline__contains=\"Lennon\", entry__pub_date__year=2008)` does **not** mean \"exclude blogs having one entry matching both\"; Django builds a subquery per condition. When you need precise semantics, write an explicit `Exists()`/`~Exists()` subquery ([Ch 12](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries)). Also remember that both forms can return a blog **multiple times** (one row per matching entry) — add `.distinct()` or use `Exists`.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 330\" width=\"100%\" height=\"330\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Multi-valued relations: same row (one filter) vs any rows (chained filters)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"250\" height=\"150\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"145\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Blog \"Beatles Daily\" entries</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"76\" width=\"218\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"145\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">E1: \"Lennon tribute\"</text>\n  <text x=\"145\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">pub 2005</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"126\" width=\"218\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"145\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">E2: \"McCartney tour\"</text>\n  <text x=\"145\" y=\"158\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">pub 2008</text>\n  <rect x=\"290\" y=\"40\" width=\"270\" height=\"150\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"425\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#991b1b\" font-weight=\"bold\">filter(headline~Lennon, year=2008)</text>\n  <text x=\"306\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">one JOIN alias &#8594; the SAME entry row</text>\n  <text x=\"306\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">must match both conditions</text>\n  <text x=\"306\" y=\"128\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">E1: Lennon but 2005 &#10007;</text>\n  <text x=\"306\" y=\"146\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">E2: 2008 but not Lennon &#10007;</text>\n  <text x=\"306\" y=\"174\" fill=\"#991b1b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Blog NOT returned</text>\n  <rect x=\"580\" y=\"40\" width=\"280\" height=\"150\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"720\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">filter(headline~Lennon).filter(year=2008)</text>\n  <text x=\"596\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">two JOIN aliases (entry, T3)</text>\n  <text x=\"596\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">each condition may use a different row</text>\n  <text x=\"596\" y=\"128\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">entry = E1 matches Lennon &#10003;</text>\n  <text x=\"596\" y=\"146\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">T3 = E2 matches 2008 &#10003;</text>\n  <text x=\"596\" y=\"174\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">Blog returned</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"208\" width=\"840\" height=\"108\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Rules of thumb</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"252\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Forward FK / O2O (single-valued): one or many filter() calls are equivalent &#8212; the join is reused.</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"272\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Reverse FK / M2M: conditions that must hold on the same child go in ONE filter() (or one Q, or an Exists subquery).</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"292\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Either way you may get duplicate parents (one per matching child): add .distinct() or use Exists(...).</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"308\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">exclude() across multi-valued relations is subtle; prefer ~Exists(...) for exact semantics.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Choosing the right retrieval method\n\n```text\nNeed exactly one row or an error?           → get()                (DoesNotExist / MultipleObjectsReturned)\nNeed one row or None?                       → filter(...).first()  (adds ORDER BY pk if unordered)\nNeed one row by unique key in a view?       → get_object_or_404(Model, ...)\nNeed to know \"is there any?\"                → exists()             (but if you'll use the rows anyway, just evaluate)\nNeed a number?                              → count()              (or len(qs) if you already evaluated it)\nNeed just a few columns?                    → values() / values_list(flat=True)\nNeed many rows with bounded memory?         → iterator(chunk_size=2000)\nNeed rows by a list of ids?                 → in_bulk(ids)\nCreate if missing?                          → get_or_create() / update_or_create()  (needs a unique constraint to be race-safe)\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: exploring in the shell\n\n```python\n# catalog/models.py\nfrom django.db import models\n\n\nclass Category(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=80)\n\n\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    category = models.ForeignKey(Category, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"products\")\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2)\n    sale_price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2, null=True, blank=True)\n    stock = models.PositiveIntegerField(default=0)\n    is_active = models.BooleanField(default=True)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n```\n\n```python\n>>> from django.db import connection, reset_queries\n>>> from django.db.models import Q, F\n>>> qs = Product.objects.filter(is_active=True, price__lt=50).order_by(\"price\")\n>>> print(qs.query)              # no query executed; approximate SQL with params inlined\nSELECT \"catalog_product\".\"id\", ... FROM \"catalog_product\"\nWHERE (\"catalog_product\".\"is_active\" AND \"catalog_product\".\"price\" < 50) ORDER BY \"catalog_product\".\"price\" ASC\n>>> reset_queries()\n>>> bool(qs); len(qs); list(qs)  # first bool() evaluates; len/list reuse the cache\n>>> len(connection.queries)\n1\n>>> qs.count()                   # already evaluated → len(cache), no query\n>>> Product.objects.filter(is_active=True).count()   # fresh QuerySet → SELECT COUNT(*)\n>>> Product.objects.values_list(\"title\", flat=True)[:3]\n<QuerySet ['Linen shirt', 'Wool scarf', 'Canvas tote']>\n>>> Product.objects.values(\"id\", \"category__name\")[:2]\n<QuerySet [{'id': 1, 'category__name': 'Shirts'}, {'id': 2, 'category__name': 'Scarves'}]>\n>>> Product.objects.get(pk=999)\nProduct.DoesNotExist: Product matching query does not exist.\n>>> Product.objects.get(category__name=\"Shirts\")\nProduct.MultipleObjectsReturned: get() returned more than one Product -- it returned 3!\n```\n\nThe SQL for that `get()` — note the limit and that a single `get()` on a non-unique condition still fetches only up to 21 rows:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"catalog_product\".\"id\", \"catalog_product\".\"category_id\", \"catalog_product\".\"title\", ...\nFROM \"catalog_product\"\nINNER JOIN \"catalog_category\" ON (\"catalog_product\".\"category_id\" = \"catalog_category\".\"id\")\nWHERE \"catalog_category\".\"name\" = 'Shirts'\nLIMIT 21;\n```\n\n### Real-world example: a product search endpoint\n\nRequirements: filter by optional query text, category ids, price range, \"on sale only\" (sale price below price), \"in stock\"; exclude discontinued; sort by a whitelisted field; paginate; and provide a \"buy\" operation that decrements stock safely.\n\n```python\n# catalog/services.py\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.db.models import F, Q, QuerySet\nfrom .models import Product\n\nALLOWED_SORTS = {\"price\": \"price\", \"-price\": \"-price\", \"new\": \"-created_at\", \"title\": \"title\"}\n\n\ndef search_products(*, text: str = \"\", category_ids: list[int] | None = None,\n                    min_price: Decimal | None = None, max_price: Decimal | None = None,\n                    on_sale: bool = False, in_stock: bool = False,\n                    sort: str = \"new\") -> QuerySet[Product]:\n    qs = Product.objects.filter(is_active=True).select_related(\"category\")\n\n    if text:\n        for term in text.split()[:5]:                       # cap terms: each one adds conditions\n            qs = qs.filter(Q(title__icontains=term) | Q(category__name__icontains=term))\n    if category_ids:\n        qs = qs.filter(category_id__in=category_ids)         # *_id: no join needed\n    if min_price is not None:\n        qs = qs.filter(price__gte=min_price)\n    if max_price is not None:\n        qs = qs.filter(price__lte=max_price)\n    if on_sale:\n        qs = qs.filter(sale_price__isnull=False, sale_price__lt=F(\"price\"))\n    if in_stock:\n        qs = qs.filter(stock__gt=0)\n\n    return qs.order_by(ALLOWED_SORTS.get(sort, \"-created_at\"), \"pk\")   # pk tiebreak = stable pages\n\n\nclass OutOfStock(Exception):\n    pass\n\n\ndef buy(product_id: int, qty: int = 1) -> None:\n    with transaction.atomic():\n        updated = (Product.objects\n                   .filter(pk=product_id, stock__gte=qty, is_active=True)\n                   .update(stock=F(\"stock\") - qty))\n        if updated == 0:\n            raise OutOfStock(product_id)\n        # create the order line here, in the same transaction\n```\n\n```python\n# catalog/views.py\nfrom django.core.paginator import Paginator\nfrom django.http import JsonResponse\nfrom .services import search_products\n\n\ndef product_search(request):\n    params = request.GET\n    qs = search_products(\n        text=params.get(\"q\", \"\"),\n        category_ids=[int(c) for c in params.getlist(\"category\") if c.isdigit()],\n        on_sale=params.get(\"on_sale\") == \"1\",\n        in_stock=params.get(\"in_stock\") == \"1\",\n        sort=params.get(\"sort\", \"new\"),\n    )\n    page = Paginator(qs.values(\"id\", \"title\", \"price\", \"sale_price\", \"category__name\"), 24) \\\n        .get_page(params.get(\"page\"))\n    return JsonResponse({\"results\": list(page.object_list), \"num_pages\": page.paginator.num_pages})\n```\n\nA request for `?q=linen&category=3&on_sale=1&in_stock=1&sort=price&page=2` produces exactly two queries — the paginator's count and the page:\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS \"__count\"\nFROM \"catalog_product\"\nINNER JOIN \"catalog_category\" ON (\"catalog_product\".\"category_id\" = \"catalog_category\".\"id\")\nWHERE (\"catalog_product\".\"is_active\"\n       AND (UPPER(\"catalog_product\".\"title\"::text) LIKE UPPER('%linen%')\n            OR UPPER(\"catalog_category\".\"name\"::text) LIKE UPPER('%linen%'))\n       AND \"catalog_product\".\"category_id\" IN (3)\n       AND \"catalog_product\".\"sale_price\" IS NOT NULL\n       AND \"catalog_product\".\"sale_price\" < (\"catalog_product\".\"price\")\n       AND \"catalog_product\".\"stock\" > 0);\n\nSELECT \"catalog_product\".\"id\", \"catalog_product\".\"title\", \"catalog_product\".\"price\",\n       \"catalog_product\".\"sale_price\", \"catalog_category\".\"name\" AS \"category__name\"\nFROM \"catalog_product\"\nINNER JOIN \"catalog_category\" ON (\"catalog_product\".\"category_id\" = \"catalog_category\".\"id\")\nWHERE ( ...same conditions... )\nORDER BY \"catalog_product\".\"price\" ASC, \"catalog_product\".\"id\" ASC\nLIMIT 24 OFFSET 24;\n```\n\nAnd `buy()` is a single conditional, atomic statement — no read, no race, no negative stock:\n\n```sql\nUPDATE \"catalog_product\" SET \"stock\" = (\"catalog_product\".\"stock\" - 1)\nWHERE (\"catalog_product\".\"id\" = 7 AND \"catalog_product\".\"is_active\" AND \"catalog_product\".\"stock\" >= 1);\n```\n\n> **Production story:** the sort parameter is whitelisted. Passing user input straight into `order_by()` lets clients sort by any field, including across relations (`order_by(\"customer__password\")`) — an information leak and a way to force expensive sorts.\n\n### Large exports with `iterator()`\n\n```python\nimport csv\n\ndef export_products(fileobj):\n    writer = csv.writer(fileobj)\n    rows = (Product.objects.filter(is_active=True)\n            .values_list(\"id\", \"title\", \"price\")\n            .order_by(\"pk\")\n            .iterator(chunk_size=5000))       # server-side cursor; no result cache\n    for row in rows:\n        writer.writerow(row)\n```\n\nOn PostgreSQL `iterator()` uses a named server-side cursor and fetches `chunk_size` rows at a time (default 2000), keeping memory flat. Behind PgBouncer in transaction pooling mode, server-side cursors break — set `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS = True` for that database ([Ch 34](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases)). Since 4.1, `prefetch_related()` works with `iterator()` when you pass `chunk_size`.\n\n### `get_or_create` and its race\n\n```python\ntag, created = Tag.objects.get_or_create(slug=\"django\", defaults={\"label\": \"Django\"})\n```\n\nIt runs `SELECT ... LIMIT 21`; if nothing is found it `INSERT`s inside a savepoint; if the insert raises `IntegrityError` (another request inserted first) it retries the `get()`. That retry only works if a **unique constraint** on `slug` exists — without one, two concurrent calls happily create duplicates. `update_or_create()` locks the found row with `select_for_update()` before updating.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Technique | Strength | Cost / Risk |\n|---|---|---|\n| Lazy chaining | Compose queries across functions; one SQL statement | Hidden evaluation points; repeated evaluation if you rebuild |\n| Result cache | Free re-iteration of the same object | Memory for large results; stale if data changes |\n| `values()` / `values_list()` | Less data, no model instantiation | No model methods/properties; dicts leak into templates |\n| `Q` objects | OR/NOT/XOR, dynamic conditions | Complex ORs may defeat indexes (consider UNION) |\n| `F` expressions | DB-side computation; atomic updates | Instance attribute becomes an expression until `refresh_from_db()` |\n| `update()` | One statement, no race | Skips `save()`, signals, `auto_now` |\n| `iterator()` | Constant memory | No cache; server-side cursors vs PgBouncer |\n| `icontains` search | Simple | `LIKE '%x%'` = sequential scan without trigram index |\n| Raw SQL | Anything the DB can do | Lose composability; injection risk if you format strings ([Ch 16](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres)) |\n\n### When to use\n- The ORM for essentially all CRUD and reporting queries; it composes, parameterises and is readable.\n- `F()` for any counter, balance, stock or \"column vs column\" comparison.\n- `values_list(flat=True)` for id lists, `exists()` for guards, `iterator()` for exports and batch jobs.\n\n### When NOT to use\n- Row-by-row Python loops to compute what one `update()` or aggregate could do.\n- `exists()` followed by evaluating the same QuerySet — just evaluate it and check the result.\n- The ORM for very complex analytical SQL (recursive CTEs, vendor-specific features) where `raw()` or a database view is clearer.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Evaluating to check emptiness.**\n\n```python\n# Before: loads every matching row just to test truthiness\nif Order.objects.filter(customer=user, status=\"pending\"):\n    ...\n# After\nif Order.objects.filter(customer=user, status=\"pending\").exists():\n    ...\n```\n\nBut if you are going to use the rows, evaluate once and check the list — `exists()` plus iteration is two queries.\n\n**2. `len(qs)` to count.** Loads all rows. Use `count()` — unless the QuerySet is already evaluated, in which case `len()` is free and `count()` uses the cache anyway.\n\n**3. Re-creating QuerySets in templates/loops.** `{% if user.orders.all %}…{% for o in user.orders.all %}` runs two queries, because each `.all` is a fresh QuerySet. Assign once in the view (or use `{% with %}`).\n\n**4. Read-modify-write counters.** `post.views += 1; post.save()` loses increments under load. Use `Post.objects.filter(pk=post.pk).update(views=F(\"views\") + 1)`.\n\n**5. Using the object after an F assignment.**\n\n```python\nproduct.stock = F(\"stock\") - 1\nproduct.save()\nprint(product.stock)          # CombinedExpression(...), not a number\nproduct.refresh_from_db(fields=[\"stock\"])\n```\n\n**6. Negative indexing / filtering after slicing.** `qs[-1]` raises (`Negative indexing is not supported.`); use `qs.order_by(\"-pk\")[0]` or `last()`. `qs[:10].filter(...)` raises `TypeError: Cannot filter a query once a slice has been taken.` — filter first, slice last.\n\n**7. Expecting `filter(x=None)` and `exclude(x=value)` to behave like Python.** `exclude(status=\"paid\")` does **not** return rows where `status` IS NULL in plain SQL semantics — Django adds an `IS NOT NULL` guard for nullable columns so they are included, which surprises people writing raw SQL equivalents. Be explicit with `Q(status__isnull=True) | ~Q(status=\"paid\")` when it matters.\n\n**8. Using `get()` with a non-unique filter.** Works in testing, raises `MultipleObjectsReturned` in production when duplicates appear. Use `get()` only with unique conditions; otherwise `filter().first()` with an explicit order.\n\n**9. Unordered pagination.** Paginating a QuerySet without `order_by()` gives nondeterministic pages (rows repeated or skipped); Django warns with `UnorderedObjectListWarning`. Always order, with a unique tiebreaker.\n\n**10. Dangerous dynamic filters.** `Model.objects.filter(**request.GET.dict())` lets clients filter on any field and relation (`user__password__startswith=`), a classic data-leak bug. Whitelist filterable fields (django-filter does this well; [Ch 24](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning)).\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Accidental full evaluation.** A view calls `if queryset:` on a 2-million-row table before paginating; memory jumps by gigabytes and the worker is OOM-killed. Root cause: `bool()` evaluates the whole QuerySet. Fix: `exists()` or paginate first. Similar traps: `list(qs)` in a helper \"for convenience\", `random.choice(qs)` (calls `len()`), `qs in some_set`, and logging `repr(qs)` (fetches 21 rows on every log line).\n\n**Leading-wildcard searches.** `icontains` becomes `UPPER(col) LIKE UPPER('%x%')`; on a large table it is a sequential scan. Fix: `pg_trgm` GIN index with `django.contrib.postgres` (`GinIndex(OpClass(Upper(\"title\"), name=\"gin_trgm_ops\"))` to match the `UPPER(...)` expression), or full-text search ([Ch 16](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres)).\n\n**`__date` and functions on indexed columns.** `filter(placed_at__date=today)` casts the column and cannot use a B-tree index on `placed_at`. Rewrite as a range: `placed_at__gte=start, placed_at__lt=end`. `__year` is already rewritten into a range by Django.\n\n**Huge `IN` lists.** `filter(id__in=list_of_100k_ids)` creates a giant statement, slow to parse and plan. Fix: pass a subquery instead of a list (`filter(id__in=Other.objects.filter(...).values(\"id\"))` stays in SQL), batch, or join.\n\n**OR across tables defeats indexes.** `Q(title__icontains=x) | Q(category__name__icontains=x)` often forces a scan. For hot paths, split into two indexed queries combined with `union()`, or denormalise a search column.\n\n**Debugging tools.**\n\n```python\nprint(qs.query)                          # approximate SQL (params inlined, not quoted reliably)\nprint(qs.explain(analyze=True))          # PostgreSQL EXPLAIN ANALYZE — actually runs the query\nfrom django.db import connection\nconnection.queries[-3:]                  # last statements (DEBUG=True only)\n```\n\n```python\n# settings.py — log every SQL statement in development\nLOGGING = {\n    \"version\": 1,\n    \"handlers\": {\"console\": {\"class\": \"logging.StreamHandler\"}},\n    \"loggers\": {\"django.db.backends\": {\"handlers\": [\"console\"], \"level\": \"DEBUG\"}},\n}\n```\n\nIn tests, `with self.assertNumQueries(2): view(request)` pins the query count so a refactor cannot silently add queries. In production, use slow-query logs (`log_min_duration_statement` in PostgreSQL), `pg_stat_statements`, and APM/Sentry performance traces, which group ORM queries by call site.\n\n**Scaling notes.** Prefer keyset (cursor) pagination over large `OFFSET`s — `OFFSET 100000` still scans and discards 100k rows ([REST API · Pagination](../rest-api/topic.html?p=13-pagination)). Use `.only()`/`values()` to avoid fetching wide columns, and route heavy read queries to replicas with `.using(\"replica\")` when staleness is acceptable ([Ch 34](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases)).\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: Are Django QuerySets lazy? When are they evaluated?**\nA: Yes. Creating and chaining a QuerySet (`filter`, `exclude`, `order_by`, `annotate`, `values`, slicing without a step) only builds a `sql.Query` object; no SQL runs. It is evaluated when you iterate it, call `list()`, `len()` or `bool()` on it (including `if qs:`), call `repr()` (shell printing, which fetches up to 21 rows), slice with a step, pickle it, or test membership with `in`. On evaluation `_fetch_all()` runs the query once and stores the rows in `_result_cache`. Methods like `count()`, `exists()`, `get()`, `first()`, `aggregate()`, `update()` and `delete()` run their own query immediately.\n\n**Q: What is the QuerySet result cache and when does it not help?**\nA: After first evaluation, the rows are stored on that QuerySet instance, so iterating it again, or calling `len()`/`count()`/`exists()` on it, costs no query. It does not help when you create a new QuerySet — `qs.all()`, `qs.filter(...)`, or accessing `obj.related.all()` twice — because each is a clone with an empty cache. Indexing or slicing an *unevaluated* QuerySet runs a LIMIT query and does not populate the cache. `iterator()` deliberately bypasses the cache. So assign the QuerySet to a variable and reuse that object.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between get() and filter()?**\nA: `filter()` returns a lazy QuerySet of zero or more objects and never raises for \"no results\". `get()` executes immediately and returns exactly one model instance; it raises `Model.DoesNotExist` if nothing matches and `Model.MultipleObjectsReturned` if more than one does. Internally `get()` adds `LIMIT 21` so it can report how many matched without fetching millions. Use `get()` with unique conditions (pk, unique fields) and `filter().first()` when \"zero or one\" is acceptable; in views, `get_object_or_404` turns `DoesNotExist` into a 404.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between Q and F objects?**\nA: They solve different problems. A `Q` object encapsulates a filter condition so conditions can be combined with `|` (OR), `&` (AND), `~` (NOT) and `^` (XOR, 4.2+) — keyword arguments alone can only AND. An `F` object refers to a column's value inside the database, letting you compare two fields (`filter(sale_price__lt=F(\"price\"))`), compute annotations, or update relative to the current value (`update(stock=F(\"stock\") - 1)`). They are often used together: `Q(stock__lt=F(\"reorder_level\")) | Q(is_discontinued=True)`.\n\n**Q: Why use F() for incrementing a counter instead of obj.count += 1; obj.save()?**\nA: The Python version is a read-modify-write: it reads the value, increments it in Python and writes the result back. Two concurrent requests that read the same value both write value+1, losing one increment, and `save()` also overwrites every other column with possibly stale values. `Model.objects.filter(pk=pk).update(count=F(\"count\") + 1)` sends `SET count = count + 1`, which the database executes atomically under a row lock, so every increment counts. If you assign an F expression to an instance and call `save()`, use `refresh_from_db()` afterwards to read the new value.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between exists() and count(), and when would you use each?**\nA: `exists()` runs `SELECT 1 ... LIMIT 1` and stops at the first matching row, so it is the cheapest way to ask \"is there any?\". `count()` runs `SELECT COUNT(*)`, which on PostgreSQL must visit every matching row — fine for small sets, expensive for millions. Use `exists()` for guards and permission checks and `count()` only when you need the number. If you are going to use the rows anyway, evaluate the QuerySet once and test the result instead of issuing an extra query. On an already-evaluated QuerySet both use the cache.\n\n**Q: What does values() vs values_list() return, and why use them?**\nA: `values(\"id\", \"title\")` returns a QuerySet of dicts; `values_list(\"id\", \"title\")` returns tuples, `values_list(\"id\", flat=True)` returns bare values, and `named=True` returns namedtuples. They select only the named columns and skip model instantiation, which is faster and lighter for exports, id lists and JSON responses. You lose model methods and properties. Combined with `annotate()`, `values()` also defines the `GROUP BY` columns, which is how you do grouped aggregation in the ORM.\n\n**Q: How do field lookups across relationships work, and what SQL do they generate?**\nA: Double underscores follow relations: `Order.objects.filter(customer__country__code=\"IN\")` walks Order → Customer → Country and adds an INNER JOIN for each hop, with the condition on the last table. Reverse relations use the related query name (`Customer.objects.filter(orders__total__gt=100)`), which joins the child table and can return duplicate parents. Lookups like `icontains` or `year` become SQL functions or operators at the end. Use `*_id` fields (`customer_id=5`) to filter on the FK column without a join.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between filter(a, b) and filter(a).filter(b) on a multi-valued relation?**\nA: For single-valued (forward FK) relations, they are the same. For reverse FKs and M2Ms, conditions in a single `filter()` call are applied to the same related row — one JOIN — so the parent is returned only if one child satisfies both. Chained `filter()` calls each add their own JOIN, so different children can satisfy each condition. Interviewers ask this because it silently changes results; for exact semantics, express the condition as an `Exists()` subquery.\n\n**Q: How does get_or_create work and is it safe under concurrency?**\nA: It tries `get()` with the lookup arguments; if nothing is found, it creates the object with the lookups plus `defaults` inside a savepoint. If the insert fails with `IntegrityError` because another process inserted the same row in between, it catches the error and runs `get()` again. That makes it race-safe only when a unique constraint covers the lookup fields; without one, two concurrent calls can both insert duplicates. It returns `(obj, created)`. `update_or_create` additionally locks the found row with `select_for_update()` before updating it.\n\n**Q: A list endpoint got slower and memory usage spikes as the table grows, though it only shows 20 items. How would you investigate? (Senior)**\nA: Suspect accidental full evaluation before the slice: an `if qs:`, `len(qs)`, `list(qs)` or a serializer touching the whole QuerySet before pagination, or logging `repr(qs)`. Measure with debug-toolbar or `connection.queries` locally and APM traces in production to find which statement returns millions of rows. Check for N+1 (one query per item for relations) and for missing indexes on filter and order columns with `qs.explain(analyze=True)`. Fix by slicing/paginating before evaluation, using `exists()` for guards, `select_related`/`prefetch_related`, `values()` for narrow payloads, and keyset pagination for deep pages. Pin the result with an `assertNumQueries` test.\n\n**Q: How would you implement stock decrement for a flash sale so you never oversell? (Senior)**\nA: Make the check and the decrement a single conditional statement: `Product.objects.filter(pk=pk, stock__gte=qty).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - qty)` inside `transaction.atomic()`, and treat a return value of 0 as sold out. The database row lock serialises concurrent updates, and the WHERE guard prevents going negative without any prior read. Create the order line in the same transaction so a failure rolls back both. A `CheckConstraint(stock__gte=0)` is a backstop. If the logic needs to read the row and make decisions (pricing, multiple items), use `select_for_update()` to lock the rows, and for extreme contention consider a Redis counter or queue in front of the database.\n\n**Q: Why might Model.objects.filter(created_at__date=today) be slow on a large table, and how do you fix it? (Senior)**\nA: The `__date` transform renders as a cast of the column (in UTC or the current time zone), and a B-tree index on `created_at` cannot be used for an expression on the column, so PostgreSQL scans the table. Rewrite as a half-open range on the raw column: `created_at__gte=start_of_day, created_at__lt=start_of_next_day`, computed in the correct time zone, which uses the index. Alternatively create a functional index on the same expression, but the range is simpler and portable. The same reasoning applies to `__lower`/`__iexact` without a matching expression index. Confirm with `explain()`.\n\n**Q: How do you safely build dynamic filters from user input? (Senior)**\nA: Never pass raw request parameters as keyword arguments (`filter(**request.GET.dict())`) or into `order_by()` — users could filter or sort by any field, including sensitive ones across relations, and force expensive queries. Map allowed parameters explicitly to lookups, validate and type-convert values (a form, serializer or django-filter FilterSet), whitelist sort keys, and cap the number of terms and page size. Build OR conditions with `Q` objects and `reduce`. Values are always parameterised by the ORM, so injection is not the risk — data exposure and denial of service are.\n\n**Q: How do you see the SQL a QuerySet will run? (Senior)**\nA: `str(qs.query)` prints an approximation with parameters interpolated (not reliably quoted, so do not execute it); `qs.query.sql_with_params()` gives the real SQL and params. `qs.explain()` returns the database's plan, and on PostgreSQL `explain(analyze=True, buffers=True)` actually runs it with timings. With `DEBUG=True`, `connection.queries` lists executed statements, and the `django.db.backends` logger at DEBUG level prints every query. In production, rely on PostgreSQL's `pg_stat_statements`, slow query logs and APM tools rather than DEBUG.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Task | ORM | SQL shape |\n|---|---|---|\n| AND | `filter(a=1, b=2)` | `WHERE a = 1 AND b = 2` |\n| OR / NOT / XOR | `Q(a=1) \\| Q(b=2)`, `~Q(...)`, `Q(...) ^ Q(...)` | `OR`, `NOT (...)`, XOR emulation |\n| Compare columns | `filter(x__lt=F(\"y\"))` | `WHERE x < y` |\n| Atomic update | `update(n=F(\"n\") + 1)` | `SET n = n + 1` |\n| Null | `filter(x__isnull=True)` / `x=None` | `IS NULL` |\n| Range | `filter(d__gte=s, d__lt=e)` | index-friendly range |\n| Across FK | `filter(customer__country__code=\"IN\")` | `INNER JOIN` chain |\n| Presence | `exists()` | `SELECT 1 ... LIMIT 1` |\n| Number | `count()` | `SELECT COUNT(*)` |\n| Exactly one | `get()` | `... LIMIT 21` + exceptions |\n| First or None | `first()` | `ORDER BY pk LIMIT 1` if unordered |\n| Columns only | `values()` / `values_list(flat=True)` | narrow `SELECT` |\n| Streaming | `iterator(chunk_size=2000)` | server-side cursor |\n\n- QuerySets are lazy; evaluation = iterate, `list`, `len`, `bool`, `repr`, step-slice, pickle, `in`.\n- The cache lives on the instance; every `.all()`/`.filter()` is a fresh query.\n- Slicing an unevaluated QuerySet → `LIMIT/OFFSET`; no negative indices; no filtering after slicing.\n- `get()` for unique lookups only; `first()` for \"maybe one\".\n- `Q` = boolean logic; `F` = database-side column values.\n- After `obj.x = F(...)` + `save()`, `refresh_from_db()`.\n- One `filter()` vs chained filters differs for multi-valued relations.\n- `__date` on an indexed datetime defeats the index; use a range.\n- `get_or_create` is race-safe only with a unique constraint.\n- Inspect with `qs.query`, `qs.explain()`, `connection.queries`, `assertNumQueries`.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"psycopg[binary]\" django-debug-toolbar\ndjango-admin startproject qslab . && python manage.py startapp catalog\n```\n\n1. **Count the queries.** In `shell`, with `reset_queries()` and `connection.queries`, test each evaluation trigger: `bool`, `len`, `list`, `repr`, `qs[5]`, `qs[:5]`, `qs[::2]`, `count()` before and after evaluation, and `qs.all()` after evaluation.\n2. **Lookups to SQL.** For ten lookups (`iexact`, `icontains`, `in`, `range`, `isnull`, `year`, `date`, `startswith`, a two-hop relation, a reverse relation) print `qs.query` and run `explain()` with and without an index.\n3. **Same row vs any row.** Build the Blog/Entry example with data where the two `filter` forms differ; then express \"an entry that matches both\" with `Exists()` and compare.\n4. **Race it.** Write a management command that spawns 20 threads each decrementing stock with `p.stock -= 1; p.save()`; observe lost updates. Repeat with `update(stock=F(\"stock\") - 1)` and with the `stock__gte` guard.\n5. **Dynamic search.** Implement `search_products` from §5 with tests for each filter, a whitelist for sorting, and `assertNumQueries(2)` on the view.\n6. **Stream.** Export 500 000 rows to CSV with and without `iterator(chunk_size=5000)` and compare peak memory (`tracemalloc`).\n\n**Mini project — \"Order explorer\":** a JSON endpoint over `Order`/`OrderItem`/`Customer` with whitelisted filters (status list, date range on `placed_at` as a range, min total, customer country, \"has item with SKU X\" via `Exists`), OR text search across order number and customer email using `Q`, keyset pagination by `(placed_at, id)`, an atomic \"cancel order and restock items\" action using `F()` inside `transaction.atomic()`, `explain()` output checked into the README for the three main queries, and tests pinning query counts.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) · [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships) · [Ch 12 · Aggregation, Annotation, Subqueries & Window Functions](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries) · [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) · [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) · [Ch 15 · Custom Managers, QuerySets & Model Inheritance](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance) · [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres) · [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi) (async QuerySet API) · [Ch 39 · ORM Coding Round](topic.html?p=39-orm-coding-challenges)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [SQL · Filtering](../sql/topic.html?p=02-filtering) · [SQL · Joins](../sql/topic.html?p=05-joins) · [SQL · Indexes](../sql/topic.html?p=19-indexes) · [SQL · Execution Plans](../sql/topic.html?p=22-execution-plans) · [REST API · Pagination](../rest-api/topic.html?p=13-pagination)\n\n- **Making queries** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the core guide: lazy QuerySets, lookups, spanning relations, Q and F, caching. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/queries/>\n- **QuerySet API reference** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · every method, what it returns and when it hits the database. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/querysets/>\n- **Query expressions** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · F, Func, Value, ExpressionWrapper and how expressions compose. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/expressions/>\n- **Lookup API reference** — Django docs · *Advanced* · writing custom lookups and transforms. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/lookups/>\n- **Database access optimization** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · official advice on evaluation, caching and query efficiency. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/optimization/>\n- **Use The Index, Luke** — Markus Winand · *Intermediate* · why LIKE '%x%', functions on columns and OFFSET hurt, with PostgreSQL examples. <https://use-the-index-luke.com/>\n- **django-debug-toolbar** — GitHub · *Beginner* · see every query, its duplicates and its stack trace per request. <https://github.com/django-commons/django-debug-toolbar>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 10.*\n", "11-migrations": "# 11 · Migrations\n\n> **In one line:** Migrations are version control for your schema: `makemigrations` diffs your models against the *recorded migration state* (never the live database) and writes Python operations, `migrate` replays the unapplied ones in dependency order, and running them safely in production means designing every change so the old code and the new code can both run against the schema at the same time.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) · [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships)\n\nYour models describe what the database *should* look like. The database on your laptop, on CI, on staging and on the three production shards each describe what the database *does* look like. Something has to move every one of those databases from its current shape to the desired shape, in the same order, with the same result, and without you hand-writing `ALTER TABLE` statements and emailing them to whoever has prod access. That something is the **migration framework** in `django.db.migrations`.\n\nThe naive approach is to edit the database by hand, or to run a \"sync\" tool that inspects the live database and makes it match the models (Django's ancient `syncdb` did a weak version of this). Both fail for the same reasons. A hand edit is not reproducible: a new developer, a fresh CI database and a disaster-recovery restore all end up with different schemas. A \"sync\" tool cannot know your *intent*: if you rename `full_name` to `name`, a diff of before/after sees \"column dropped, column added\" and destroys the data. And neither approach handles *data* changes — splitting a name into first/last, backfilling a new column — which are half of real schema evolution.\n\nDjango's answer is a sequence of small, committed Python files per app, each listing **operations** (`CreateModel`, `AddField`, `AlterField`, `RunPython`…) and the **dependencies** it needs applied first. Together they form a **directed acyclic graph**. Applied migrations are recorded in the `django_migrations` table, so each database knows exactly where it is in the graph.\n\nThe production half of this chapter matters more for senior interviews: a migration that is correct can still take your site down. `AddField(null=False)` on a 200-million-row table, `CREATE INDEX` without `CONCURRENTLY`, a rename that the still-running old pods do not understand — each of these is a correct migration that causes an outage. Zero-downtime migration is a discipline (expand → migrate → contract), not a flag.\n\n> **Why this matters:** Every Django interview past junior level asks some version of \"how do you add a non-nullable column to a big table without downtime?\" and \"what happens if two developers create migration 0007 on different branches?\". Both answers fall out of understanding the graph and the lock behaviour.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Migration file** — a module in `<app>/migrations/` defining `class Migration(migrations.Migration)` with `dependencies` and `operations`. *Why it matters:* it is committed code, reviewed like code, and is the only source of schema truth across environments.\n- **Operation** — a unit of change (`CreateModel`, `AddField`, `RemoveField`, `AlterField`, `RenameField`, `AddIndex`, `AddConstraint`, `RunPython`, `RunSQL`, `SeparateDatabaseAndState`). *Why:* each operation knows how to change both the in-memory *state* and the real *database*, forwards and (usually) backwards.\n- **Migration state (`ProjectState`)** — the in-memory reconstruction of every model as it looked after a given set of migrations, built by replaying operations. *Why:* the autodetector compares your models to *this*, not to the DB, so a hand-edited database is invisible to `makemigrations`.\n- **Autodetector (`MigrationAutodetector`)** — the class that diffs the current models' state against the latest migration state and generates operations. *Why:* it asks interactive questions (\"did you rename this field?\") precisely because a diff cannot infer intent.\n- **Dependencies** — `[(\"app\", \"0006_previous\")]` edges in the graph, including cross-app edges (e.g. to `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL` via `migrations.swappable_dependency`). *Why:* `migrate` performs a topological sort; a missing edge produces \"relation does not exist\" on fresh databases.\n- **Leaf node** — a migration nothing depends on yet. *Why:* two leaves in one app = a conflict; `migrate` refuses until you merge.\n- **`django_migrations` table** — rows of `(app, name, applied)`. *Why:* the recorder; `--fake` writes a row without running SQL.\n- **Data migration** — a migration whose operation is `RunPython` (or `RunSQL`) that transforms rows. *Why:* schema and data change together, in order, everywhere.\n- **Historical models** — models obtained via `apps.get_model(\"app\", \"Model\")` inside `RunPython`, rebuilt from the migration state at that point. *Why:* importing the real model gives you *today's* fields, which may not exist yet in an older migration's world.\n- **Reversibility** — whether an operation has a backwards implementation. *Why:* `migrate app 0005` (rollback) fails with `IrreversibleError` on `RunPython` without a `reverse_code`.\n- **Atomic migrations** — on databases with transactional DDL (PostgreSQL, SQLite) each migration runs inside a transaction by default. *Why:* a half-applied migration rolls back cleanly — but long transactions hold locks, and some DDL (`CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`) cannot run inside one.\n- **Squashing** — collapsing a run of migrations into one with `replaces = [...]`. *Why:* faster test DB creation and fewer files, without breaking databases that applied the originals.\n- **Expand / contract** — the zero-downtime pattern: add compatible schema first, migrate code and data, remove old schema last. *Why:* during a rolling deploy two code versions run against one schema.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 State vs database: the two halves of every operation\n\nEvery operation implements four methods: `state_forwards(app_label, state)`, `database_forwards(app_label, schema_editor, from_state, to_state)`, `database_backwards(...)` and `describe()`. The executor (`MigrationExecutor`) walks the plan; for each migration it calls `Migration.apply()`, which for each operation first mutates a copy of the `ProjectState` and then asks the operation to emit SQL through the backend's **schema editor** (`connection.schema_editor()`, e.g. `DatabaseSchemaEditor` for PostgreSQL). The schema editor turns \"add field X to model Y\" into vendor SQL, defers foreign-key and index creation to the end of the migration where needed, and — when `collect_sql=True` — just records the SQL instead of executing it (that is how `sqlmigrate` works).\n\nKeeping state and database separate is what makes `SeparateDatabaseAndState` possible: you can tell Django \"the state changed, but don't touch the DB\" (e.g. after you already did it by hand, or when moving a model between apps while keeping the table).\n\n### 3.2 How `makemigrations` decides what to write\n\n1. `MigrationLoader` reads every migration file of every installed app and builds a `MigrationGraph` (nodes + dependency edges). It also reads `django_migrations` if a database is available, but `makemigrations` does not need the DB to diff.\n2. The loader replays the graph's leaf states into a `ProjectState` — \"what the models looked like after all migrations\".\n3. `ProjectState.from_apps(apps)` builds the state of your *current* model code.\n4. `MigrationAutodetector(from_state, to_state, questioner).changes(graph)` compares them: created/deleted models, added/removed/altered fields, renamed fields (detected by matching field deconstruction and asking), index/constraint changes, `Meta` option changes.\n5. It orders operations so they are valid (create model before FK to it), splits across files when cross-app cycles require, names them (`0007_order_status_alter_order_total`) and writes them with `MigrationWriter`.\n\nBecause every field implements `deconstruct()` (returning path, args, kwargs), a change to *any* kwarg — even `help_text`, `verbose_name` or `choices` — produces an `AlterField`. Those are no-op SQL on PostgreSQL but they are still state changes; this is why \"makemigrations generates a migration when I changed only choices\" is expected behaviour.\n\n### 3.3 How `migrate` decides what to run\n\n`migrate` loads the graph, reads applied rows from `django_migrations`, computes the **plan** (`executor.migration_plan(targets)`) as a topological ordering of unapplied nodes up to the targets (all app leaves by default), checks consistency (`InconsistentMigrationHistory` if an applied migration has an unapplied dependency), then applies each, writing a row after success. Before and after it emits `pre_migrate` / `post_migrate` signals — `post_migrate` is what creates content types and default permissions.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj11a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj11b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj11c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">The migration graph: dependencies, a cross-app edge, and a conflict</text>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"58\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">accounts</text>\n  <rect x=\"110\" y=\"40\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"175\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">0001_initial (User)</text>\n  <rect x=\"290\" y=\"40\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"355\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">0002_user_phone</text>\n  <path d=\"M290,55 L244,55\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj11a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"148\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">orders</text>\n  <rect x=\"110\" y=\"130\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"175\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">0001_initial</text>\n  <rect x=\"290\" y=\"130\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"355\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">0002_order_user</text>\n  <rect x=\"470\" y=\"130\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"535\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">0003_order_status</text>\n  <path d=\"M290,145 L244,145\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj11a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M470,145 L424,145\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj11a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M330,130 L200,72\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj11a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"104\" fill=\"#7c3aed\" font-size=\"10\">cross-app edge (FK to User)</text>\n  <rect x=\"660\" y=\"95\" width=\"160\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"740\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\">0004_order_coupon (branch A)</text>\n  <rect x=\"660\" y=\"165\" width=\"160\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"740\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\">0004_order_notes (branch B)</text>\n  <path d=\"M660,110 L604,140\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj11b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M660,180 L604,152\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj11b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"740\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">two leaves = conflict</text>\n  <rect x=\"660\" y=\"240\" width=\"160\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"740\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\">0005_merge (makemigrations --merge)</text>\n  <path d=\"M720,240 L720,199\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj11c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M790,240 C850,200 850,140 824,118\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#dj11c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"300\" width=\"800\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"322\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">migrate: plan = topological order of unapplied nodes</text>\n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"342\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"10\">accounts.0001 &#8594; orders.0001 &#8594; orders.0002 (needs accounts.0001) &#8594; accounts.0002 &#8594; orders.0003 &#8594; 0004 A &#8594; 0004 B &#8594; 0005_merge</text>\n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"362\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"10\">Arrows point to the dependency. Each applied node becomes a row in django_migrations(app, name, applied).</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.4 Why migrations are atomic on PostgreSQL — and when that hurts\n\nPostgreSQL supports transactional DDL, so `Migration.atomic = True` (the default) wraps each migration in `BEGIN … COMMIT`. If operation three fails, operations one and two are rolled back too. The cost: every lock taken by every operation is held until the end of the migration. An `ALTER TABLE` takes an `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock; if your migration then runs a `RunPython` that backfills ten million rows, *every query on that table* queues behind the lock until the backfill finishes. That is the single most common migration outage.\n\nWorse, `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` must *wait* for existing locks: if a long-running analytics query holds `ACCESS SHARE` on the table, your `ALTER` queues, and every new query queues behind your `ALTER`. A trivial \"add nullable column\" can freeze the table for as long as that report runs. Production fix: `SET lock_timeout = '5s'` before DDL and retry (see §8). The deeper locking story is in [SQL · Locking & MVCC](../sql/topic.html?p=27-locking-mvcc) and [Database Design · Locking Internals](../database-design/topic.html?p=05-locking-internals).\n\n### 3.5 Historical models and why `RunPython` must use `apps.get_model`\n\nInside a `RunPython(forwards, backwards)` function Django passes `apps` — a registry built from the migration state *at that point in the graph*. `apps.get_model(\"orders\", \"Order\")` gives a class with exactly the fields that existed then, with no custom methods and only managers marked `use_in_migrations = True`. If you `from orders.models import Order` instead, the migration works today, but a year from now — when `Order` has a field that a later migration adds — running migrations on a fresh database will crash because your import references a column that doesn't exist yet. Historical models also don't fire your overridden `save()` logic, so put needed logic inline in the migration.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 The components\n\n```text\nmanage.py makemigrations                   manage.py migrate\n        |                                         |\n  MigrationLoader --- reads */migrations/*.py --- MigrationLoader\n        |                                         |   + MigrationRecorder (django_migrations)\n  MigrationGraph  (nodes + dependencies)    MigrationExecutor.migration_plan()\n        |                                         |\n  ProjectState(graph leaves)  vs                for each Migration:\n  ProjectState.from_apps(current models)         op.state_forwards()  -> new ProjectState\n        |                                         op.database_forwards(schema_editor)\n  MigrationAutodetector.changes()                  -> SQL via DatabaseSchemaEditor\n        |                                         recorder.record_applied()\n  MigrationWriter -> 0007_xxx.py               post_migrate signal (contenttypes, perms)\n```\n\n### 4.2 Zero-downtime: the expand/contract workflow\n\nIn a rolling deploy, old pods (version N) and new pods (N+1) serve traffic simultaneously against one database. Therefore the schema at every moment must be compatible with **both** versions. Any change that breaks old code — dropping a column it selects, renaming one, adding a NOT NULL column without a DB default that old code's INSERTs don't supply — must be split across multiple deploys.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 420\" width=\"100%\" height=\"420\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj11d\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Renaming customer.fullname to customer.name with zero downtime</text>\n  <path d=\"M30,60 L850,60\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj11d)\"/>\n  <text x=\"840\" y=\"52\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">time / deploys</text>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"80\" width=\"190\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e3a8a\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deploy 1: EXPAND</text>\n  <text x=\"42\" y=\"122\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">migration: AddField name (null=True)</text>\n  <text x=\"42\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">code: write BOTH fullname + name</text>\n  <text x=\"42\" y=\"158\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">read: fullname</text>\n  <text x=\"42\" y=\"182\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">old pods: ignore name &#8594; safe</text>\n  <text x=\"42\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">nullable add = metadata only</text>\n  <rect x=\"240\" y=\"80\" width=\"190\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"335\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deploy 2: BACKFILL</text>\n  <text x=\"252\" y=\"122\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">batched job (not one txn):</text>\n  <text x=\"252\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">UPDATE ... SET name=fullname</text>\n  <text x=\"252\" y=\"158\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">WHERE id BETWEEN a AND b</text>\n  <text x=\"252\" y=\"182\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"10\">atomic=False, sleep between</text>\n  <text x=\"252\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"10\">batches to spare replicas</text>\n  <rect x=\"450\" y=\"80\" width=\"190\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"545\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#4c1d95\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deploy 3: SWITCH</text>\n  <text x=\"462\" y=\"122\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">code: read name, write both</text>\n  <text x=\"462\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">migration: name NOT NULL</text>\n  <text x=\"462\" y=\"158\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">(CHECK NOT VALID, VALIDATE,</text>\n  <text x=\"462\" y=\"176\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">then SET NOT NULL)</text>\n  <text x=\"462\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">old pods still write fullname</text>\n  <rect x=\"660\" y=\"80\" width=\"190\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deploy 4+5: CONTRACT</text>\n  <text x=\"672\" y=\"122\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">4: stop using fullname in code</text>\n  <text x=\"672\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">   (SeparateDatabaseAndState</text>\n  <text x=\"672\" y=\"158\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">    removes it from state only)</text>\n  <text x=\"672\" y=\"176\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">5: RemoveField / DROP COLUMN</text>\n  <text x=\"672\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">no running pod references it</text>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"260\" width=\"820\" height=\"140\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"282\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">The one-step version (RenameField) during a rolling deploy</text>\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"306\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">t0: migrate runs ALTER TABLE customer RENAME COLUMN fullname TO name (fast, but instant break)</text>\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"326\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">t0..t1: old pods still running SELECT ... \"customer\".\"fullname\" &#8594; ProgrammingError: column does not exist</text>\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"346\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">If migrate runs AFTER new pods start, the new pods fail instead. There is no ordering that is safe.</text>\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"370\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">Rule: a single deploy may only make schema changes that the PREVIOUS code version tolerates.</text>\n  <text x=\"50\" y=\"388\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">Additive first (expand), destructive last (contract), each in its own release.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 4.3 Where migrations run in the pipeline\n\nRun `migrate` **once per release**, as a dedicated step (a Kubernetes Job, a Heroku release phase, a CI deploy stage) *before* new application pods roll out — never in every container's entrypoint. Ten pods booting simultaneously all running `migrate` race to apply the same migration; PostgreSQL's DDL locks usually serialize them into errors (`relation already exists`, `duplicate key in django_migrations`). The brief rule from [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production): migrations are a release step, gated by `migrate --check` in CI.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example: the everyday loop\n\n```python\n# shop/models.py\nfrom django.db import models\n\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n```\n\n```bash\npython manage.py makemigrations shop          # writes shop/migrations/0001_initial.py\npython manage.py sqlmigrate shop 0001         # print the SQL, run nothing\npython manage.py migrate                      # apply everything unapplied\npython manage.py showmigrations shop          # [X] applied, [ ] pending\npython manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run   # CI: fail if models drifted\npython manage.py migrate --check              # CI/deploy: exit non-zero if unapplied (3.1+)\n```\n\nThe generated file is ordinary Python:\n\n```python\n# shop/migrations/0001_initial.py\nfrom django.db import migrations, models\n\nclass Migration(migrations.Migration):\n    initial = True\n    dependencies = []\n    operations = [\n        migrations.CreateModel(\n            name=\"Product\",\n            fields=[\n                (\"id\", models.BigAutoField(auto_created=True, primary_key=True, serialize=False, verbose_name=\"ID\")),\n                (\"name\", models.CharField(max_length=200)),\n                (\"price\", models.DecimalField(decimal_places=2, max_digits=10)),\n                (\"created_at\", models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)),\n            ],\n        ),\n    ]\n```\n\nAnd `sqlmigrate` shows what PostgreSQL will receive:\n\n```sql\nBEGIN;\n--\n-- Create model Product\n--\nCREATE TABLE \"shop_product\" (\n    \"id\" bigint NOT NULL PRIMARY KEY GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY,\n    \"name\" varchar(200) NOT NULL,\n    \"price\" numeric(10, 2) NOT NULL,\n    \"created_at\" timestamp with time zone NOT NULL\n);\nCOMMIT;\n```\n\n> **Version note:** Since Django 4.1, PostgreSQL auto fields use `GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY` instead of `serial`. Existing `serial` columns are not converted automatically.\n\nNow add a field with a default:\n\n```python\n    stock = models.PositiveIntegerField(default=0)\n```\n\n```sql\n-- sqlmigrate shop 0002 (Django 5.x)\nBEGIN;\nALTER TABLE \"shop_product\" ADD COLUMN \"stock\" integer DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL CHECK (\"stock\" >= 0);\nALTER TABLE \"shop_product\" ALTER COLUMN \"stock\" DROP DEFAULT;\nCOMMIT;\n```\n\nNotice the second statement: a Python-side `default=` is applied to existing rows during the migration, then **dropped** from the database. The DB has no default afterwards, so an INSERT from code that doesn't know the column (old pods, raw SQL, another service) fails with a NOT NULL violation. Django 5.0's `db_default` fixes that:\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models.functions import Now\n\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    ...\n    stock = models.PositiveIntegerField(db_default=0)\n    updated_at = models.DateTimeField(db_default=Now())\n```\n\n```sql\nALTER TABLE \"shop_product\" ADD COLUMN \"stock\" integer DEFAULT 0 NOT NULL CHECK (\"stock\" >= 0);\n-- no DROP DEFAULT: the default lives in the database permanently\n```\n\nOn PostgreSQL 11+ adding a column with a constant default is a metadata-only change (no table rewrite), so this is fast even on large tables — the default is stored in the catalog and returned for old rows.\n\n### 5.2 Data migration with `RunPython`\n\n```bash\npython manage.py makemigrations shop --empty --name backfill_slugs\n```\n\n```python\n# shop/migrations/0004_backfill_slugs.py\nfrom django.db import migrations\nfrom django.utils.text import slugify\n\ndef forwards(apps, schema_editor):\n    Product = apps.get_model(\"shop\", \"Product\")          # historical model\n    db = schema_editor.connection.alias                    # respect --database\n    batch = []\n    for p in Product.objects.using(db).filter(slug=\"\").only(\"id\", \"name\").iterator(chunk_size=2000):\n        p.slug = slugify(p.name)[:50] or f\"product-{p.pk}\"\n        batch.append(p)\n        if len(batch) >= 2000:\n            Product.objects.using(db).bulk_update(batch, [\"slug\"])\n            batch.clear()\n    if batch:\n        Product.objects.using(db).bulk_update(batch, [\"slug\"])\n\ndef backwards(apps, schema_editor):\n    pass  # slugs are derived data; leaving them is harmless\n\nclass Migration(migrations.Migration):\n    dependencies = [(\"shop\", \"0003_product_slug\")]\n    operations = [\n        migrations.RunPython(forwards, backwards, elidable=True),\n    ]\n```\n\nKey points: `apps.get_model`, `using(db)`, `iterator()` to avoid loading every row, `bulk_update` to avoid one UPDATE per row (see [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization)), a reverse function (even a no-op — `migrations.RunPython.noop` exists for exactly this), and `elidable=True` so `squashmigrations` can drop it.\n\n### 5.3 Real-world example: adding a NOT NULL foreign key to a 50M-row table\n\nRequirement: every `Order` must reference a `Store`. `orders_order` has 50 million rows and is written hundreds of times per second. The one-step `AddField(ForeignKey(Store, on_delete=PROTECT))` with a default would: add the column, take `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE`, add an FK constraint that **validates every row while holding a lock**, and create an index — all in one transaction. Here is the safe sequence.\n\n**Release 1 — expand (nullable column, no constraint validation yet):**\n\n```python\n# orders/migrations/0031_order_store.py\nfrom django.db import migrations, models\nimport django.db.models.deletion\n\nclass Migration(migrations.Migration):\n    dependencies = [(\"orders\", \"0030_previous\"), (\"stores\", \"0004_store\")]\n    operations = [\n        migrations.AddField(\n            model_name=\"order\",\n            name=\"store\",\n            field=models.ForeignKey(\n                null=True, db_index=False, db_constraint=False,\n                on_delete=django.db.models.deletion.PROTECT, to=\"stores.store\",\n            ),\n        ),\n    ]\n```\n\n```sql\nALTER TABLE \"orders_order\" ADD COLUMN \"store_id\" bigint NULL;   -- metadata only, milliseconds\n```\n\n**Release 1b — the index, concurrently, in a non-atomic migration:**\n\n```python\n# orders/migrations/0032_order_store_idx.py\nfrom django.contrib.postgres.operations import AddIndexConcurrently\nfrom django.db import migrations, models\n\nclass Migration(migrations.Migration):\n    atomic = False                                   # CONCURRENTLY cannot run in a transaction\n    dependencies = [(\"orders\", \"0031_order_store\")]\n    operations = [\n        AddIndexConcurrently(\n            \"order\", models.Index(fields=[\"store\"], name=\"order_store_idx\"),\n        ),\n    ]\n```\n\nThe model must carry `Meta.indexes = [models.Index(fields=[\"store\"], name=\"order_store_idx\")]` so state and code agree. If a concurrent build fails it leaves an `INVALID` index behind — drop it and retry.\n\n**Release 2 — application code sets `store` on new orders; a backfill fills old rows in batches** (a management command or Celery task, not a migration, because it must be resumable and throttled):\n\n```python\n# orders/management/commands/backfill_order_store.py\nfrom django.core.management.base import BaseCommand\nfrom django.db import connection, transaction\nimport time\n\nclass Command(BaseCommand):\n    def handle(self, *args, **opts):\n        batch = 10_000\n        with connection.cursor() as c:\n            c.execute(\"SELECT min(id), max(id) FROM orders_order\")\n            lo, hi = c.fetchone()\n        for start in range(lo, hi + 1, batch):\n            with transaction.atomic():\n                with connection.cursor() as c:\n                    c.execute(\n                        \"\"\"UPDATE orders_order o SET store_id = b.store_id\n                           FROM orders_branch b\n                           WHERE o.branch_id = b.id AND o.store_id IS NULL\n                             AND o.id >= %s AND o.id < %s\"\"\",\n                        [start, start + batch],\n                    )\n            time.sleep(0.05)   # let replicas and autovacuum keep up\n```\n\n**Release 3 — add the FK constraint `NOT VALID`, validate separately, then enforce NOT NULL via a validated CHECK** (PostgreSQL 12+ uses a valid CHECK to skip the full-table scan in `SET NOT NULL`):\n\n```python\n# orders/migrations/0033_order_store_constraints.py\nfrom django.db import migrations, models\nimport django.db.models.deletion\n\nclass Migration(migrations.Migration):\n    atomic = False\n    dependencies = [(\"orders\", \"0032_order_store_idx\")]\n    operations = [\n        migrations.SeparateDatabaseAndState(\n            database_operations=[\n                migrations.RunSQL(\n                    \"ALTER TABLE orders_order ADD CONSTRAINT order_store_fk \"\n                    \"FOREIGN KEY (store_id) REFERENCES stores_store(id) \"\n                    \"DEFERRABLE INITIALLY DEFERRED NOT VALID\",\n                    reverse_sql=\"ALTER TABLE orders_order DROP CONSTRAINT order_store_fk\",\n                ),\n                migrations.RunSQL(\n                    \"ALTER TABLE orders_order VALIDATE CONSTRAINT order_store_fk\",\n                    reverse_sql=migrations.RunSQL.noop,\n                ),\n                migrations.RunSQL(\n                    \"ALTER TABLE orders_order ADD CONSTRAINT order_store_nn \"\n                    \"CHECK (store_id IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID\",\n                    reverse_sql=\"ALTER TABLE orders_order DROP CONSTRAINT order_store_nn\",\n                ),\n                migrations.RunSQL(\n                    \"ALTER TABLE orders_order VALIDATE CONSTRAINT order_store_nn\",\n                    reverse_sql=migrations.RunSQL.noop,\n                ),\n                migrations.RunSQL(\n                    \"ALTER TABLE orders_order ALTER COLUMN store_id SET NOT NULL\",\n                    reverse_sql=\"ALTER TABLE orders_order ALTER COLUMN store_id DROP NOT NULL\",\n                ),\n                migrations.RunSQL(\n                    \"ALTER TABLE orders_order DROP CONSTRAINT order_store_nn\",\n                    reverse_sql=migrations.RunSQL.noop,\n                ),\n            ],\n            state_operations=[\n                migrations.AlterField(\n                    model_name=\"order\",\n                    name=\"store\",\n                    field=models.ForeignKey(\n                        db_index=False, on_delete=django.db.models.deletion.PROTECT, to=\"stores.store\",\n                    ),\n                ),\n            ],\n        ),\n    ]\n```\n\n`VALIDATE CONSTRAINT` scans the table but takes only a `SHARE UPDATE EXCLUSIVE` lock, so reads and writes continue. `SeparateDatabaseAndState` tells the autodetector \"the field is now non-null with a constraint\" without letting Django emit its own (locking) SQL. Running `makemigrations --check` afterwards must report \"No changes detected\" — that is your proof state and models agree.\n\n### 5.4 Conflicts, squashing, faking, rollback\n\n```bash\n# Two branches both created 0004 -> migrate says \"Conflicting migrations detected\"\npython manage.py makemigrations --merge            # writes 0005_merge_... depending on both leaves\n\n# Roll back to a point (runs backwards operations)\npython manage.py migrate orders 0030\npython manage.py migrate orders zero               # unapply everything in the app\n\n# Tables already exist (legacy DB, or you applied SQL by hand)\npython manage.py migrate --fake-initial            # mark 0001 applied if its tables exist\npython manage.py migrate orders 0033 --fake        # record as applied, run no SQL\n\n# Collapse 0001..0040 into one\npython manage.py squashmigrations orders 0040\n```\n\nThe squashed file carries `replaces = [(\"orders\", \"0001_initial\"), …, (\"orders\", \"0040_x\")]`. Databases that applied the originals treat it as applied; fresh databases run only the squash. Once **every** environment has migrated past the squash, delete the originals, remove `replaces`, and update any migrations elsewhere that depended on the old names.\n\n```text\n$ python manage.py showmigrations orders\norders\n [X] 0001_squashed_0040_cleanup (40 squashed migrations)\n [X] 0041_order_refund_reason\n [ ] 0042_order_store\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Approach | Safe under rolling deploy? | Lock risk | Effort | Use for |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Autogenerated migration, one release | Only if purely additive and nullable / `db_default` | Low–high (depends on op) | Lowest | Small tables, new models, nullable fields |\n| Expand → backfill → contract over several releases | Yes | Low | High (3–5 PRs) | Renames, NOT NULL on big tables, type changes |\n| `AddIndexConcurrently` / `RemoveIndexConcurrently` (`atomic=False`) | Yes | Low (no write block) | Low | Any index on a live table |\n| `RunSQL` + `SeparateDatabaseAndState` | Yes, if SQL is chosen carefully | You control it | Medium–high | `NOT VALID` constraints, custom DDL |\n| `RunPython` in the migration | Risky on large data (one transaction) | High if `atomic` | Low | Small/medium backfills, seed data |\n| Out-of-band batched backfill command | Yes | Lowest | Medium | Large backfills |\n| Squashing | n/a | n/a | Medium (coordination) | Long histories slowing tests |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Autogenerated migrations for every model change — never hand-edit the DB.\n- `db_default` (5.0+) whenever a new NOT NULL column must be written by code that doesn't know about it.\n- Non-atomic migrations for `CONCURRENTLY` operations and for batched `RunPython` that commits per batch.\n- `SeparateDatabaseAndState` when the DB already matches (hand-run DDL, moving models between apps, removing a field from state before dropping the column).\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Don't put a long backfill inside an atomic migration on a hot table.\n- Don't use `RenameField`/`RenameModel`/`RemoveField` in a single release on a table served by a rolling deploy.\n- Don't use `--fake` to \"make an error go away\" — you are desynchronizing state and reality.\n- Don't squash while some environment (a customer's on-prem install, a long-lived staging DB) is still in the middle of the replaced range unless you keep `replaces` around.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Importing real models in a data migration.** People write `from shop.models import Product` because it works today. It breaks a fresh database months later when the model gains a column. Always use `apps.get_model`.\n\n```python\n# Before (time bomb)\nfrom shop.models import Product\ndef forwards(apps, schema_editor):\n    Product.objects.update(active=True)\n\n# After\ndef forwards(apps, schema_editor):\n    Product = apps.get_model(\"shop\", \"Product\")\n    Product.objects.update(active=True)\n```\n\n**2. Not committing migration files, or generating them on the server.** If prod runs `makemigrations`, every server can produce different files. Generate locally, review, commit; CI runs `makemigrations --check --dry-run`.\n\n**3. Editing an applied migration.** Changing `0005` after it ran in prod does nothing there (the row says applied) but changes fresh databases — environments diverge silently. Write a new migration instead. The exception is a migration not yet merged to main.\n\n**4. Deleting migrations to \"reset\" a shared project.** Fine on a throwaway branch; catastrophic on a deployed app — the `django_migrations` rows reference names that no longer exist, and dependencies from other apps break.\n\n**5. Missing reverse code.** `RunPython(forwards)` alone makes the app irreversible past that point. Add `reverse_code=migrations.RunPython.noop` when undoing is harmless, or a real reverse when it isn't.\n\n**6. Forgetting `using(schema_editor.connection.alias)` in multi-DB setups.** The migration writes to `default` even when `migrate --database=analytics` was requested.\n\n**7. Swappable user model added late.** `AUTH_USER_MODEL` must be set before the first `migrate`, because `admin`, `auth` and every FK to the user depend on it (`migrations.swappable_dependency(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL)`). Changing it later means hand-surgery on migration history. See [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user).\n\n**8. Choices/help_text churn.** Every `choices` change generates `AlterField`. That's not a bug; it's state. Keep choices in a `TextChoices` class and accept the migrations (they cost no SQL on PostgreSQL).\n\n**Best practices:**\n- One logical change per migration; name them (`--name add_order_store`).\n- Always read `sqlmigrate` output for migrations on large tables during review.\n- Add a CI check that flags dangerous operations (tools such as `django-migration-linter` or `django-pg-zero-downtime-migrations`, both on GitHub).\n- Keep data migrations idempotent so a retry after partial failure is safe.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: lock queue behind a long query.** *In production* a deploy runs `AddField(null=True)` — supposedly instant. *Symptom:* every request touching `orders_order` hangs for minutes; connection pool exhausts; 502s. *Root cause:* `ALTER TABLE` waited for `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` behind a 10-minute report holding `ACCESS SHARE`; all new queries queued behind the ALTER. *Fix:* set a lock timeout for migration sessions and retry:\n\n```python\n# A first operation in risky migrations (or a custom migrate wrapper)\nmigrations.RunSQL(\"SET lock_timeout = '5s'\", reverse_sql=migrations.RunSQL.noop)\n```\n\n…or set it for the migration role: `ALTER ROLE deployer SET lock_timeout = '5s';` Also set `statement_timeout` for the app role so reports can't hold locks forever.\n\n**Pitfall: index build blocking writes.** `AddIndex` emits `CREATE INDEX`, which blocks INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE for the whole build. *Fix:* `AddIndexConcurrently` with `atomic = False`. Detail on index design in [SQL · Index Design](../sql/topic.html?p=20-index-design).\n\n**Pitfall: type change rewrites the table.** `AlterField` from `IntegerField` to `BigIntegerField` → `ALTER COLUMN TYPE bigint` rewrites every row under `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE`. On a huge table that is hours. *Fix:* new column, dual-write trigger or app code, batched backfill, swap. Plan for int→bigint *before* you exhaust 2^31 IDs — the reason `DEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD` is `BigAutoField`.\n\n**Pitfall: parallel `migrate` on container start.** Two pods apply 0042 simultaneously → one errors and crash-loops. *Fix:* migrate as a single release job; app pods only start after it succeeds.\n\n**Pitfall: old code vs new schema.** Removing a field in the same release that stops using it → old pods `SELECT` the dropped column during rollout. *Fix:* contract in a later release; Django lists every concrete field in SELECTs, so even \"unused\" columns are referenced. A clean trick is `SeparateDatabaseAndState` with `state_operations=[RemoveField(...)]` in release N (code no longer selects it), `RunSQL(\"ALTER TABLE ... DROP COLUMN ...\")` in N+1.\n\n**Pitfall: replication lag from a big backfill.** A single `UPDATE` of 30M rows generates massive WAL; replicas fall minutes behind and read-your-writes breaks (see [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases) and [Database Design · Replication](../database-design/topic.html?p=09-replication)). *Fix:* batch, throttle, watch `pg_stat_replication`.\n\n**Debugging toolkit:**\n\n```bash\npython manage.py showmigrations --plan              # execution order with [X]/[ ]\npython manage.py sqlmigrate orders 0042             # exact SQL\npython manage.py sqlmigrate orders 0042 --backwards # reverse SQL\npython manage.py migrate --plan                     # what WOULD run now\npython manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run -v 3   # show pending diff\n```\n\n```sql\n-- Who is blocking my migration?\nSELECT pid, wait_event_type, state, now() - query_start AS age, left(query, 80)\nFROM pg_stat_activity WHERE datname = current_database() ORDER BY age DESC;\nSELECT pid, pg_blocking_pids(pid) FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE cardinality(pg_blocking_pids(pid)) > 0;\n```\n\n`InconsistentMigrationHistory` means a migration was applied before one of its dependencies — usually someone added a dependency to an old migration, or a `--fake` went wrong. Fix the history explicitly (fake the dependency if the schema really exists), don't delete rows blindly.\n\n**Scaling notes:** for test speed, squash periodically and consider `--keepdb` locally; for many apps, `makemigrations --check` in pre-commit catches drift early. For schema evolution beyond Django (multi-service databases, online schema change tools like `pg_repack`), see [Database Design · Schema Evolution](../database-design/topic.html?p=27-schema-evolution).\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `makemigrations` and `migrate`?**\nA: `makemigrations` is a code generator: it compares your current model definitions against the state reconstructed from existing migration files and writes new migration files describing the difference. It does not touch the database schema. `migrate` is the executor: it reads which migrations are recorded in `django_migrations`, computes a plan of unapplied migrations in dependency order, runs their operations against the database, and records each one. You run `makemigrations` on a developer machine and commit the output; you run `migrate` in every environment.\n\n**Q: How does Django know which migrations have been applied?**\nA: The `MigrationRecorder` stores one row per applied migration in the `django_migrations` table (app, name, applied timestamp). `migrate` loads the migration graph from files, subtracts the recorded set, and plans the rest. That is also why `--fake` works: it inserts the row without running SQL. If you manually alter the schema, Django has no idea — the table records history, not reality.\n\n**Q: Does `makemigrations` look at the database?**\nA: No — the autodetector compares models to the migration *state*, which it rebuilds in memory by replaying every operation in the migration files. This is a frequent trick question. Consequences: hand-made DB changes are invisible to it, and you can generate migrations without a database connection. It only consults the database for consistency checks, not for the diff.\n\n**Q: What is a data migration and how do you write one correctly?**\nA: A migration whose operation transforms rows rather than schema, usually `RunPython(forwards, backwards)`. Inside the function you get the historical model via `apps.get_model(\"app\", \"Model\")` — never import the real model, because it reflects today's fields rather than the fields at that point in history. Use `schema_editor.connection.alias` for multi-database correctness, batch large updates with `iterator()` and `bulk_update`, provide reverse code (`RunPython.noop` if nothing to undo), and keep it idempotent. For very large tables run the backfill outside the migration so it doesn't hold a transaction and locks.\n\n**Q: Two developers each created migration `0007` in the same app. What happens and how do you fix it?**\nA: The graph now has two leaf nodes in that app, and `migrate` refuses with \"Conflicting migrations detected\". If the two migrations touch unrelated things, run `makemigrations --merge`, which creates a merge migration depending on both leaves (with no operations, or interactive resolution). If they conflict semantically — both alter the same field — rewrite one branch's migration on top of the other before merging to main. Many teams add a CI check (or a `max_migration.txt` approach via tools like django-linear-migrations) to force linear history.\n\n**Q: What do `--fake` and `--fake-initial` do, and when are they appropriate?**\nA: `--fake` marks migrations as applied without executing them; `--fake-initial` fakes an app's initial migration only if all the tables/columns it creates already exist. They are appropriate when adopting Django on an existing database, or when the schema change was applied out of band (by a DBA, by `RunSQL` elsewhere). They are dangerous as a \"fix\" for errors, because they make state and reality diverge and the next migration will fail in a more confusing way.\n\n**Q: How do you roll back a migration?**\nA: `python manage.py migrate app 0041` unapplies every migration after 0041 in reverse order, calling each operation's `database_backwards`. Schema ops are reversible automatically; `RunPython`/`RunSQL` need reverse code or the rollback raises `IrreversibleError`. `migrate app zero` unapplies all. In production, rollbacks of destructive changes rarely recover data — a dropped column is gone — so teams prefer roll-forward fixes and rely on expand/contract so that the old code version still works without rolling back the schema.\n\n**Q: What does `squashmigrations` do and what are the risks?**\nA: It combines a range of migrations into one file with optimized operations (e.g. `AddField` then `AlterField` collapses) and a `replaces` list. Databases that applied the originals treat the squash as applied; fresh ones run just the squash. Risks: `RunPython` operations are copied unless marked `elidable=True`, so you may need to clean them up; and you can only delete the old files once every environment has migrated past the squashed range. It mostly buys faster test database setup.\n\n**Q: Why is a migration that adds a column with `default=` different from one with `db_default=`?**\nA: With `default=`, Django uses the Python default to fill existing rows during the migration and then drops the database default, so the column has no default in the DB. Any writer that doesn't supply the column — old application pods during a rolling deploy, raw SQL, other services — gets a NOT NULL violation. `db_default` (Django 5.0+) keeps a database-level `DEFAULT`, so those INSERTs succeed and the model also reads it back. On PostgreSQL 11+ a constant default is metadata-only, so both are fast to add.\n\n**Q: How would you add a NOT NULL column to a 100-million-row table with zero downtime? (Senior)**\nA: Split it across releases. First add the column nullable (or with a constant `db_default`, which is metadata-only on modern PostgreSQL), with no index and no validated constraint. Deploy code that writes the new column for new rows. Backfill old rows in small batched transactions outside the migration, throttled to protect replicas. Then add `CHECK (col IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID`, `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT` (which takes a weak lock and lets traffic continue), `SET NOT NULL` (which PostgreSQL 12+ can do without a rescan thanks to the validated check), and drop the helper check — wrapped in `SeparateDatabaseAndState` so Django's state matches. Any index goes in via `AddIndexConcurrently` in an `atomic = False` migration. Throughout, set `lock_timeout` so a blocked DDL fails fast instead of queueing all traffic.\n\n**Q: How do you rename a column without downtime in a rolling deployment? (Senior)**\nA: Not with `RenameField` — it is instant but breaks whichever code version doesn't know the new name. Expand: add the new column, write both. Backfill. Switch reads to the new column while still writing both. Contract: remove the old field from Django state (`SeparateDatabaseAndState` with `RemoveField` in `state_operations`) so no code selects it, and in the following release drop it in SQL. Alternatively keep the column name and change only the Python attribute with `db_column=\"old_name\"`, which needs no DDL at all — often the pragmatic answer.\n\n**Q: Why should migrations not run in every container's entrypoint? (Senior)**\nA: Because N pods starting together run `migrate` concurrently: they race to apply the same migration, producing errors and crash loops, and long migrations make health checks fail so the orchestrator restarts pods mid-DDL. Migrations also need to run *before* new code serves traffic but *after* the previous release is stable, which is a deployment-ordering concern. Run them once as a release step (Kubernetes Job, release phase), gate rollout on success, and keep `migrate --check` in CI to detect unapplied migrations.\n\n**Q: Your deploy ran a trivial `AddField` and the whole site froze for several minutes. How do you debug and prevent it? (Senior)**\nA: That's the classic lock-queue: `ALTER TABLE` needs `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE`, which waited behind a long-running query, and every subsequent query queued behind the ALTER. Confirm with `pg_stat_activity` and `pg_blocking_pids()` during the incident or via logs (`log_lock_waits`). Prevent with `lock_timeout` on the migration session plus retries, `statement_timeout` for application/report roles, routing analytics to replicas, and running risky migrations at low traffic. Also check the migration wasn't atomic with a heavy `RunPython` that kept the lock held.\n\n**Q: When would you use `SeparateDatabaseAndState`?**\nA: When you need Django's model state and the real database to change differently. Typical cases: you applied DDL manually or via custom `RunSQL` (e.g. `NOT VALID` constraints) and want the state to reflect it; moving a model to another app while keeping its table (`db_table`); or removing a field from state one release before dropping the column. It takes `database_operations` and `state_operations` lists, and afterwards `makemigrations --check` should show no changes.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Command / API | What it does |\n|---|---|\n| `makemigrations [app] --name x` | Diff models vs migration state → write file |\n| `makemigrations --check --dry-run` | CI: fail if models have unmigrated changes |\n| `makemigrations --merge` | Resolve two leaf nodes with a merge migration |\n| `makemigrations app --empty` | Blank file for a data migration |\n| `migrate [app] [target]` | Apply / unapply to target (`zero` = all) |\n| `migrate --plan` / `--check` | Show plan / exit non-zero if unapplied |\n| `migrate --fake` / `--fake-initial` | Record without running SQL |\n| `sqlmigrate app 0007 [--backwards]` | Print SQL |\n| `showmigrations [--plan]` | Applied status / ordered plan |\n| `squashmigrations app 0040` | Collapse with `replaces` |\n| `RunPython(f, reverse, elidable=True)` | Data migration; use `apps.get_model` |\n| `AddIndexConcurrently` + `atomic=False` | Non-blocking index on PostgreSQL |\n| `SeparateDatabaseAndState` | Change state and DB independently |\n| `db_default` (5.0+) | Persistent DB default |\n\n- The autodetector diffs against **migration state**, not the database.\n- `django_migrations` records history; `--fake` edits only history.\n- PostgreSQL migrations are **atomic** by default — locks held until commit.\n- `ALTER TABLE` waits in the lock queue and blocks everyone behind it: use `lock_timeout`.\n- Indexes on live tables: `CONCURRENTLY`, `atomic = False`.\n- Rolling deploy rule: each release's schema must work with the previous release's code.\n- Expand → backfill → switch → contract for renames, NOT NULL, type changes.\n- Never edit an applied migration; never import real models in `RunPython`.\n- Run `migrate` once per release, not per container.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" \"psycopg[binary]\"`, `django-admin startproject lab .`, `python manage.py startapp shop`, point `DATABASES` at a local PostgreSQL.\n\n1. **Read the SQL.** Create `Product(name, price)`, then add `stock = PositiveIntegerField(default=0)`. Run `sqlmigrate` and find the `DROP DEFAULT`. Switch to `db_default=0`, regenerate, compare.\n2. **State vs DB.** Manually `ALTER TABLE shop_product ADD COLUMN sku text;` in `psql`, then add `sku` to the model and run `migrate`. Observe the \"already exists\" error. Fix it with `--fake` *for that migration only*, and explain what you just asserted.\n3. **Conflict.** On two git branches, add different fields and `makemigrations` on each. Merge the branches, run `migrate`, read the error, then `makemigrations --merge`. Inspect the merge file's `dependencies`.\n4. **Data migration.** Add `slug` (blank allowed), write an empty migration that backfills it with `apps.get_model`, `iterator()` and `bulk_update`. Roll back to before it — confirm that without `reverse_code` you get `IrreversibleError`.\n5. **Lock queue.** In `psql` session A: `BEGIN; SELECT * FROM shop_product; ` (leave open). In session B run `migrate` for an `AddField`. In session C run `SELECT 1 FROM shop_product LIMIT 1;` and watch it hang. Repeat with `SET lock_timeout='2s'` in the migration.\n6. **Concurrent index.** Seed a million rows (`generate_series`), add an index with `AddIndexConcurrently` in an `atomic=False` migration, and in parallel run inserts to confirm they aren't blocked.\n\n**Mini project:** implement the full zero-downtime rename of `Customer.fullname` → `Customer.name` as five commits (expand, dual-write code, backfill command, switch reads + NOT NULL, contract), and write a short `DEPLOY.md` stating which commit can be deployed while the previous one's code is still running.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) (field options that drive migrations), [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships) (FKs and cross-app dependencies), [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) (`bulk_update`, `iterator()` for backfills), [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency), [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres), [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user), [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production).\n\n**Other handbooks:** [Database Design · Schema Evolution](../database-design/topic.html?p=27-schema-evolution), [Database Design · Locking Internals](../database-design/topic.html?p=05-locking-internals), [SQL · Locking & MVCC](../sql/topic.html?p=27-locking-mvcc), [SQL · Keys & Constraints](../sql/topic.html?p=29-keys-constraints), [SQL · Index Design](../sql/topic.html?p=20-index-design).\n\n- **Migrations topic guide** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the official walkthrough of the workflow, dependencies, squashing and data migrations. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/migrations/>\n- **Migration operations reference** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · every operation including `RunPython`, `RunSQL` and `SeparateDatabaseAndState`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/migration-operations/>\n- **Writing database migrations (how-to)** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · non-atomic migrations, unique fields on existing rows, moving models. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/writing-migrations/>\n- **PostgreSQL-specific database operations** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `AddIndexConcurrently`, extensions, constraint validation. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/contrib/postgres/operations/>\n- **PostgreSQL ALTER TABLE** — PostgreSQL docs · *Advanced* · which forms rewrite the table and which lock level each takes. <https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/sql-altertable.html>\n- **django-pg-zero-downtime-migrations** — GitHub · *Advanced* · a backend that rewrites dangerous operations into safe ones; read its README for the patterns alone. <https://github.com/tbicr/django-pg-zero-downtime-migrations>\n- **django-migration-linter** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · CI linter for backward-incompatible migrations. <https://github.com/3YOURMIND/django-migration-linter>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 11.*\n", "12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries": "# 12 · Aggregation, Annotation, Subqueries & Window Functions\n\n> **In one line:** `aggregate()` collapses a QuerySet into one dictionary, `annotate()` attaches a computed value to every row, `values().annotate()` is how you write `GROUP BY`, and `Subquery`/`OuterRef`/`Exists`/`Window` let the ORM express almost any reporting query in one round trip — provided you know the SQL each one produces and the join fan-out trap that silently multiplies your counts.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) · [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships)\n\nSooner or later every application needs numbers: revenue per month, the number of books per author, each customer's latest order, the top three products per category, a running balance. The naive approach is to fetch rows into Python and compute there:\n\n```python\n# Naive: pull every order into memory to sum it\ntotal = sum(o.amount for o in Order.objects.filter(status=\"paid\"))\n\n# Naive: N+1 to count books per author\nfor a in Author.objects.all():\n    print(a.name, a.book_set.count())     # one COUNT query per author\n```\n\nThe first moves potentially millions of rows over the network to compute a single number the database could compute in milliseconds with an index. The second issues one query per author. Both are \"correct\" and both fall over the moment data grows. The database is an aggregation engine; your job is to ask it the right question.\n\nDjango's ORM exposes that engine through **expressions** — objects such as `Count`, `Sum`, `F`, `Case`, `Subquery`, `Window`, `Coalesce`, `TruncMonth` — that compile to SQL fragments. You attach them to a QuerySet with `aggregate()`, `annotate()`, `alias()`, `filter()`, `order_by()` and `values()`. Mastering this chapter is mostly mastering *what SQL comes out* — interviewers will show you a QuerySet and ask what it returns, or show you SQL and ask you to write the QuerySet.\n\n> **Why this matters:** \"What's the difference between `aggregate` and `annotate`?\" is in nearly every Django interview. The senior follow-up is \"why does `Book.objects.annotate(Count('authors'), Count('stores'))` return the wrong numbers?\" — and the answer requires understanding joins, not Django.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Aggregate function** — `Count`, `Sum`, `Avg`, `Min`, `Max`, `StdDev`, `Variance` (and PostgreSQL's `ArrayAgg`, `StringAgg`, `JSONBAgg`, `BoolAnd`…). *Why:* they compile to SQL aggregates and accept `distinct=`, `filter=` and (4.0+) `default=`.\n- **`aggregate()`** — terminal; evaluates immediately and returns a `dict` like `{\"price__avg\": 31.5}`. *Why:* use it for \"one number (or a few) about the whole set\".\n- **`annotate()`** — non-terminal; adds a column to each row/object. With an aggregate inside, it triggers a `GROUP BY` on the model's primary key (or on the `values()` fields). *Why:* \"a number per row\".\n- **`values(...).annotate(...)`** — group by the listed fields. *Why:* this is the ORM's `GROUP BY x`. Order matters: `values()` *before* `annotate()` groups; *after* just selects.\n- **`alias()`** (3.2+) — like `annotate()` but the expression isn't selected; usable in `filter()`/`order_by()`. *Why:* avoids computing and transferring a column you only filter on.\n- **`F()` / `ExpressionWrapper` / `output_field`** — column references and arithmetic; `output_field` tells Django the result type when it can't infer it (e.g. mixing `DecimalField` and `IntegerField`). *Why:* wrong or missing output types raise `FieldError: Expression contains mixed types`.\n- **Conditional aggregation** — `Count(\"id\", filter=Q(status=\"paid\"))`. *Why:* many counts in one pass; compiles to `FILTER (WHERE …)` on PostgreSQL, `CASE WHEN` elsewhere.\n- **`Case` / `When`** — SQL `CASE` expression. *Why:* bucketing, custom ordering, conditional updates.\n- **`Subquery` + `OuterRef`** — embed a correlated subquery returning one column (and usually one row). *Why:* \"latest order per customer\" without N+1 or fan-out.\n- **`Exists`** — `EXISTS (subquery)`; boolean; usable directly in `filter()` (3.0+). *Why:* cheapest way to test a related row exists without duplicating rows.\n- **Database functions** — `Coalesce`, `Greatest`, `Lower`, `Concat`, `Cast`, `Now`, `TruncMonth`, `ExtractYear`… *Why:* push computation into SQL so you can filter/group by it.\n- **`Window`** — `Window(expression, partition_by, order_by, frame)` with `RowNumber`, `Rank`, `DenseRank`, `Lag`, `Lead`, `NTile`, or any aggregate. *Why:* rankings and running totals without collapsing rows.\n- **Join fan-out** — when two multi-valued relations are joined in one query, each row of A is repeated once per B per C. *Why:* aggregates over the fanned-out rows are multiplied.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 How expressions become SQL\n\nEvery expression is a subclass of `django.db.models.expressions.BaseExpression` (most via `Expression` / `Func` / `Aggregate`). When you call `qs.annotate(n=Count(\"books\"))`, `QuerySet.annotate()` clones the QuerySet and calls `query.add_annotation(expr, \"n\")`, which **resolves** the expression with `expr.resolve_expression(query)`. Resolution turns `\"books\"` into a `Col` reference, adding the required JOIN to `query.alias_map` (via `Query.setup_joins`). Because the expression `contains_aggregate`, the query's `group_by` is set — to `True` meaning \"group by all selected non-aggregate columns\" for model queries, or to the `values()` fields.\n\nAt evaluation, `SQLCompiler.as_sql()` calls `pre_sql_setup()`, `get_select()`, `get_group_by()` and compiles each expression with `compiler.compile(expr)`, which dispatches to `expr.as_sql()` (or a vendor-specific `as_postgresql()`, `as_sqlite()`…). `Count(\"books\", filter=Q(...))` on PostgreSQL renders `COUNT(...) FILTER (WHERE ...)`; on backends without `FILTER`, `Aggregate.as_sql` rewrites it to `COUNT(CASE WHEN ... THEN ... ELSE NULL END)`.\n\n`aggregate()` is different: it calls `query.get_aggregation(using, aggregate_exprs)`. If the QuerySet already has annotations, a LIMIT, `distinct()` or existing aggregates, Django wraps the existing query as a **subquery** and aggregates over it (`SELECT AVG(n) FROM (SELECT ..., COUNT(...) AS n ... GROUP BY ...) subquery`). That is how `annotate(n=Count(...)).aggregate(Avg(\"n\"))` works.\n\n### 3.2 The three shapes\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj12a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Same table, three result shapes</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"200\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">book rows</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"86\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">id  publisher  price</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">1   Acme       20</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">2   Acme       40</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"142\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">3   Zeta       30</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">4   Zeta       50</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"178\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">5   Zeta       10</text>\n  <path d=\"M222,90 L282,70\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj12a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M222,120 L282,170\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj12a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M222,160 L282,290\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj12a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"286\" y=\"44\" width=\"574\" height=\"58\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"64\" fill=\"#1e3a8a\" font-weight=\"bold\">Book.objects.aggregate(avg=Avg(\"price\"))  &#8594; {\"avg\": 30.0}</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"84\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT AVG(price) FROM book   &#8212; terminal, returns a dict, one row total</text>\n  <rect x=\"286\" y=\"118\" width=\"574\" height=\"112\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"138\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">Book.objects.values(\"publisher\").annotate(avg=Avg(\"price\"))</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"158\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT publisher_id, AVG(price) FROM book GROUP BY publisher_id</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">&#8594; [{\"publisher\": Acme, \"avg\": 30}, {\"publisher\": Zeta, \"avg\": 30}]   one row per group</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">values() BEFORE annotate() = the GROUP BY columns</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"218\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">filter() on an aggregate alias after this = HAVING</text>\n  <rect x=\"286\" y=\"246\" width=\"574\" height=\"138\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"266\" fill=\"#4c1d95\" font-weight=\"bold\">Book.objects.annotate(pub_avg=Window(Avg(\"price\"), partition_by=F(\"publisher\")))</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"286\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT ..., AVG(price) OVER (PARTITION BY publisher_id) FROM book</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"308\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">&#8594; all 5 rows kept, each carries its group&#8217;s value: 1:30  2:30  3:30  4:30  5:30</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"330\" fill=\"#7c3aed\" font-size=\"10\">Contrast: Publisher.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"book\"))  &#8594; one row per publisher,</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"348\" fill=\"#7c3aed\" font-size=\"10\">GROUP BY publisher.id (plus its columns), n is attached to each Publisher instance.</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"370\" fill=\"#7c3aed\" font-size=\"10\">Window functions never collapse rows; GROUP BY always does.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.3 Ordering of clauses is meaning\n\nThe order you chain `values`, `annotate` and `filter` changes the SQL, not just the style:\n\n| Chain | SQL meaning |\n|---|---|\n| `filter(books__rating__gt=4).annotate(n=Count(\"books\"))` | WHERE first: count only highly rated books |\n| `annotate(n=Count(\"books\")).filter(books__rating__gt=4)` | Count *all* books; the second filter adds a **second** join for the filter (for multi-valued relations) |\n| `annotate(n=Count(\"books\")).filter(n__gt=3)` | `HAVING COUNT(...) > 3` |\n| `values(\"x\").annotate(n=Count(\"id\"))` | `GROUP BY x` |\n| `annotate(n=Count(\"id\")).values(\"x\")` | GROUP BY the model pk (plus columns), *then* select `x` — not grouped by x |\n| `values(\"x\").annotate(n=Count(\"id\")).order_by(\"y\")` | `y` is **added to GROUP BY** — groups split unexpectedly |\n\n> **Version note:** Before Django 3.1, a model's `Meta.ordering` leaked into `GROUP BY` and broke `values().annotate()` groupings. Since 3.1 `Meta.ordering` is ignored in such queries — but an explicit `order_by()` on a non-grouped field still adds it to `GROUP BY`. Call `.order_by()` (empty) to clear ordering when in doubt.\n\n### 3.4 The join fan-out trap\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 360\" width=\"100%\" height=\"360\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Book.annotate(Count(\"authors\"), Count(\"stores\")) multiplies across joins</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"250\" height=\"120\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"145\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Book \"Django Deep Dive\"</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">authors: A1, A2, A3       (3)</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"108\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">stores: S1, S2            (2)</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"132\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Expected: authors=3, stores=2</text>\n  <rect x=\"290\" y=\"44\" width=\"300\" height=\"250\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-weight=\"bold\">LEFT JOIN author, LEFT JOIN store: 3 x 2 rows</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">Acme  B1  S1</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">Acme  B1  S2</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">Acme  B2  S1</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"142\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">Acme  B2  S2</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">Acme  B3  S1</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"178\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">Acme  B3  S2</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"206\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">COUNT(author.id) = 6  (wrong)</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"226\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">COUNT(store.id) = 6  (wrong)</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"250\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">Sum(\"stores__rent\") would be tripled too,</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"268\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">and distinct=True cannot fix SUM.</text>\n  <rect x=\"610\" y=\"44\" width=\"250\" height=\"250\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"735\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fixes</text>\n  <text x=\"624\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">1. Count(\"authors\", distinct=True)</text>\n  <text x=\"624\" y=\"108\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">   Count(\"stores\", distinct=True)</text>\n  <text x=\"624\" y=\"126\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">   OK for COUNT only</text>\n  <text x=\"624\" y=\"152\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">2. One Subquery per relation:</text>\n  <text x=\"624\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">   aggregate inside, correlate on</text>\n  <text x=\"624\" y=\"188\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">   OuterRef(\"pk\") &#8212; no fan-out,</text>\n  <text x=\"624\" y=\"206\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">   works for SUM/AVG too</text>\n  <text x=\"624\" y=\"232\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">3. Two queries, merge in Python</text>\n  <text x=\"624\" y=\"258\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">Rule: at most ONE multi-valued</text>\n  <text x=\"624\" y=\"276\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">join per aggregating query</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"310\" width=\"840\" height=\"36\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"333\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"11\">Django docs call this out: combining multiple aggregations with annotate() over different multi-valued relations yields wrong results.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nThis is not a Django bug; it is what SQL `JOIN` + `GROUP BY` mean. The ORM simply joins every relation you mention. `distinct=True` fixes counts (count distinct ids), but `Sum`/`Avg` over duplicated rows can't be \"distincted\" correctly — two books with the same price would collapse. The robust fix is a correlated subquery per relation (§5.3).\n\n### 3.5 Subqueries and `OuterRef`\n\n`Subquery(qs)` wraps an inner QuerySet; `OuterRef(\"pk\")` is a placeholder resolved against the *outer* query when the subquery is embedded (`ResolvedOuterRef`). The inner QuerySet must return exactly **one column** (`.values(\"field\")`) and, when used as a scalar, **at most one row** (`[:1]`). Django compiles it inline into the SELECT, WHERE or ORDER BY. `Exists(qs)` is the same mechanism rendered as `EXISTS(SELECT 1 ... LIMIT 1)`, and since 3.0 can be passed straight into `filter()` / `exclude()` (`~Exists(...)` for NOT EXISTS).\n\nAggregating inside a subquery needs a small idiom, because `aggregate()` is terminal: group the inner query by the correlated column, then select only the aggregate:\n\n```python\nbooks_total = (\n    Book.objects.filter(publisher=OuterRef(\"pk\"))\n    .order_by()                       # clear ordering so it doesn't enter GROUP BY\n    .values(\"publisher\")              # GROUP BY publisher_id\n    .annotate(total=Sum(\"price\"))\n    .values(\"total\")                  # single column\n)\nPublisher.objects.annotate(book_total=Coalesce(Subquery(books_total), Decimal(\"0\")))\n```\n\n### 3.6 Window functions\n\n`Window(expression=RowNumber(), partition_by=[F(\"category\")], order_by=F(\"price\").desc())` compiles to `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY category_id ORDER BY price DESC)`. Frames are expressed with `RowRange(start=None, end=0)` / `ValueRange`. SQL forbids window functions in `WHERE`; since **Django 4.2** you can `filter()` on a window annotation and Django wraps the query in a subquery and filters outside — making top-N-per-group a one-liner. On older versions you had to use `Subquery` or raw SQL. The SQL fundamentals are in [SQL · Window Functions](../sql/topic.html?p=13-window-functions).\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 From QuerySet to SQL with annotations\n\n```text\nqs = Publisher.objects.filter(country=\"IN\")          WHERE added to query.where\n     .annotate(n=Count(\"book\"))                       resolve_expression -> JOIN book\n                                                      query.group_by = True\n     .filter(n__gt=2)                                 n is an aggregate -> query.having\n     .order_by(\"-n\")                                  ORDER BY n DESC\n\nSQLCompiler.as_sql():\n  SELECT publisher.id, publisher.name, publisher.country, COUNT(book.id) AS n\n  FROM publisher LEFT OUTER JOIN book ON book.publisher_id = publisher.id\n  WHERE publisher.country = 'IN'\n  GROUP BY publisher.id                              (PG: pk is enough, functional dependency)\n  HAVING COUNT(book.id) > 2\n  ORDER BY n DESC\n```\n\nOn PostgreSQL Django groups by the primary key only (the backend feature `allows_group_by_selected_pks`), since every other column of that table is functionally dependent on it.\n\n### 4.2 Correlated subquery vs join, visually\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 330\" width=\"100%\" height=\"330\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj12b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj12c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">\"Latest order per customer\" with Subquery + OuterRef</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"260\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e3a8a\" font-weight=\"bold\">Outer query: customer</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">for each customer row c ...</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"104\" width=\"220\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"123\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">c.id = 7, name = Asha</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"144\" width=\"220\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"150\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">c.id = 9, name = Ben</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT c.*, (subquery) AS last_total</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"218\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">FROM customer c</text>\n  <path d=\"M262,119 L346,119\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj12b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"304\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"9\">OuterRef(\"pk\")</text>\n  <rect x=\"350\" y=\"44\" width=\"300\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"500\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#4c1d95\" font-weight=\"bold\">Inner query: order (correlated)</text>\n  <text x=\"364\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT o.total FROM order o</text>\n  <text x=\"364\" y=\"108\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">WHERE o.customer_id = c.id</text>\n  <text x=\"364\" y=\"126\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">ORDER BY o.created_at DESC</text>\n  <text x=\"364\" y=\"144\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">LIMIT 1</text>\n  <text x=\"364\" y=\"172\" fill=\"#7c3aed\" font-size=\"10\">one column (.values), one row ([:1])</text>\n  <text x=\"364\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#7c3aed\" font-size=\"10\">index (customer_id, created_at DESC)</text>\n  <text x=\"364\" y=\"208\" fill=\"#7c3aed\" font-size=\"10\">makes each probe an index seek</text>\n  <path d=\"M652,119 L716,119\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj12c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"720\" y=\"44\" width=\"140\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"790\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">Result</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"96\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Asha  last=120.00</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"120\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Ben   last=NULL</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">one row/customer</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"168\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">no fan-out</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"186\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">one round trip</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"262\" width=\"840\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"284\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"10\">Alternative 1: prefetch all orders and pick max in Python &#8212; transfers every order.</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"304\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"10\">Alternative 2: annotate(Max(\"orders__created_at\")) gives the date, not the order&#8217;s other columns &#8212; you need Subquery for those.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 4.3 Choosing the tool\n\n```text\nNeed one number for the whole set?            -> aggregate()\nNeed a number per object?                     -> annotate(Count/Sum...) on that model\nNeed a number per group of some column(s)?    -> values(cols).annotate(...)  (.order_by() to be safe)\nNeed several aggregates over DIFFERENT\n  multi-valued relations?                     -> one Subquery each (or distinct=True for counts)\nNeed a column from the \"latest/first\" child?  -> Subquery(...order_by(...).values(\"col\")[:1])\nNeed \"has any child matching X\"?              -> filter(Exists(...))\nNeed rank / running total / prev row?         -> Window(...)   (filter on it: 4.2+)\nNeed conditional counts in one pass?          -> Count(\"id\", filter=Q(...))\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example: a bookshop\n\n```python\n# catalog/models.py\nfrom django.db import models\n\nclass Publisher(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n\nclass Author(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    age = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n\nclass Book(models.Model):\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=300)\n    pages = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=8, decimal_places=2)\n    rating = models.FloatField()\n    pubdate = models.DateField()\n    publisher = models.ForeignKey(Publisher, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"books\")\n    authors = models.ManyToManyField(Author, related_name=\"books\")\n\nclass Store(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    books = models.ManyToManyField(Book, related_name=\"stores\")\n```\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models import Avg, Count, Max, Min, Q, Sum, F\n\nBook.objects.aggregate(Avg(\"price\"), max_pages=Max(\"pages\"))\n# {'price__avg': Decimal('31.50'), 'max_pages': 1132}\n\nBook.objects.filter(publisher__name=\"Acme\").aggregate(total=Sum(\"price\", default=0))\n# default= (4.0+) returns 0 instead of None for an empty set\n\npubs = Publisher.objects.annotate(num_books=Count(\"books\")).order_by(\"-num_books\")\npubs[0].num_books        # attribute on each instance\n\n# GROUP BY a column\nBook.objects.values(\"publisher__name\").annotate(n=Count(\"id\"), avg=Avg(\"price\")).order_by(\"-n\")\n\n# HAVING\nAuthor.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"books\")).filter(n__gte=3)\n\n# Conditional aggregation: several counts in one scan\nPublisher.objects.annotate(\n    total=Count(\"books\"),\n    good=Count(\"books\", filter=Q(books__rating__gte=4)),\n    cheap=Count(\"books\", filter=Q(books__price__lt=10)),\n)\n```\n\nThe SQL for the last one on PostgreSQL:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"catalog_publisher\".\"id\", \"catalog_publisher\".\"name\",\n       COUNT(\"catalog_book\".\"id\") AS \"total\",\n       COUNT(\"catalog_book\".\"id\") FILTER (WHERE \"catalog_book\".\"rating\" >= 4.0) AS \"good\",\n       COUNT(\"catalog_book\".\"id\") FILTER (WHERE \"catalog_book\".\"price\" < 10) AS \"cheap\"\nFROM \"catalog_publisher\"\nLEFT OUTER JOIN \"catalog_book\" ON (\"catalog_publisher\".\"id\" = \"catalog_book\".\"publisher_id\")\nGROUP BY \"catalog_publisher\".\"id\";\n```\n\n`Count(\"books\")` produces a **LEFT OUTER JOIN**, so publishers with zero books appear with `total = 0`. Filtering `filter(books__rating__gte=4).annotate(...)` instead produces an INNER JOIN and drops publishers without matching books — a different question.\n\nExpressions and types:\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models import DecimalField, ExpressionWrapper, Case, When, Value, CharField\n\nBook.objects.annotate(\n    price_per_page=ExpressionWrapper(F(\"price\") / F(\"pages\"), output_field=DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=4)),\n    tier=Case(\n        When(price__gte=50, then=Value(\"premium\")),\n        When(price__gte=20, then=Value(\"standard\")),\n        default=Value(\"budget\"),\n        output_field=CharField(),\n    ),\n).values(\"tier\").annotate(n=Count(\"id\")).order_by(\"tier\")\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT CASE WHEN \"catalog_book\".\"price\" >= 50 THEN 'premium'\n            WHEN \"catalog_book\".\"price\" >= 20 THEN 'standard'\n            ELSE 'budget' END AS \"tier\",\n       COUNT(\"catalog_book\".\"id\") AS \"n\"\nFROM \"catalog_book\"\nGROUP BY 1\nORDER BY 1 ASC;\n```\n\nAggregate over annotation (Django builds a subquery):\n\n```python\nPublisher.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"books\")).aggregate(avg_books=Avg(\"n\"))\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT AVG(\"n\") FROM (\n  SELECT \"catalog_publisher\".\"id\" AS \"col1\", COUNT(\"catalog_book\".\"id\") AS \"n\"\n  FROM \"catalog_publisher\" LEFT OUTER JOIN \"catalog_book\" ON (...)\n  GROUP BY 1\n) subquery;\n```\n\n### 5.2 Subquery and Exists\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models import Exists, OuterRef, Subquery\n\nnewest = Book.objects.filter(publisher=OuterRef(\"pk\")).order_by(\"-pubdate\")\n\nPublisher.objects.annotate(\n    newest_title=Subquery(newest.values(\"title\")[:1]),\n    newest_date=Subquery(newest.values(\"pubdate\")[:1]),\n)\n\n# Publishers that have at least one book rated 5 (no duplicates, no DISTINCT needed)\nPublisher.objects.filter(Exists(Book.objects.filter(publisher=OuterRef(\"pk\"), rating=5)))\n\n# Authors with NO books in any store\nAuthor.objects.filter(~Exists(Store.objects.filter(books__authors=OuterRef(\"pk\"))))\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"catalog_publisher\".\"id\", \"catalog_publisher\".\"name\"\nFROM \"catalog_publisher\"\nWHERE EXISTS (\n  SELECT 1 AS \"a\" FROM \"catalog_book\" U0\n  WHERE (U0.\"publisher_id\" = (\"catalog_publisher\".\"id\") AND U0.\"rating\" = 5.0)\n  LIMIT 1\n);\n```\n\nThe `U0`, `U1` aliases are how Django names tables inside subqueries — recognising them helps when reading logs.\n\n### 5.3 Real-world example: a customer analytics endpoint\n\n```python\n# sales/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\n\nclass Category(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=100)\n\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    category = models.ForeignKey(Category, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"products\")\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2)\n\nclass Order(models.Model):\n    class Status(models.TextChoices):\n        PENDING = \"pending\"; PAID = \"paid\"; REFUNDED = \"refunded\"\n    customer = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"orders\")\n    status = models.CharField(max_length=10, choices=Status.choices, db_index=True)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n    total = models.DecimalField(max_digits=12, decimal_places=2)\n\n    class Meta:\n        indexes = [models.Index(fields=[\"customer\", \"-created_at\"], name=\"order_cust_created_idx\")]\n\nclass OrderItem(models.Model):\n    order = models.ForeignKey(Order, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"items\")\n    product = models.ForeignKey(Product, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"order_items\")\n    quantity = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n    unit_price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2)\n```\n\n**(a) Customer list with lifetime value, paid-order count, ticket count and last order — no fan-out:**\n\n```python\nfrom decimal import Decimal\nfrom django.contrib.auth import get_user_model\nfrom django.db.models import Count, DecimalField, OuterRef, Q, Subquery, Sum, Value\nfrom django.db.models.functions import Coalesce\n\nUser = get_user_model()\npaid = Order.objects.filter(customer=OuterRef(\"pk\"), status=Order.Status.PAID).order_by()\n\nltv_sq = paid.values(\"customer\").annotate(s=Sum(\"total\")).values(\"s\")\ncnt_sq = paid.values(\"customer\").annotate(c=Count(\"pk\")).values(\"c\")\nlast_sq = Order.objects.filter(customer=OuterRef(\"pk\")).order_by(\"-created_at\")\n\ncustomers = (\n    User.objects.filter(is_active=True)\n    .annotate(\n        lifetime_value=Coalesce(Subquery(ltv_sq), Value(Decimal(\"0\")), output_field=DecimalField()),\n        paid_orders=Coalesce(Subquery(cnt_sq), 0),\n        last_order_at=Subquery(last_sq.values(\"created_at\")[:1]),\n        last_order_total=Subquery(last_sq.values(\"total\")[:1]),\n    )\n    .order_by(\"-lifetime_value\")[:50]\n)\n```\n\nEach subquery is a correlated index probe on `(customer_id, created_at)`; no relation is joined, so nothing multiplies. Compare the wrong version: `annotate(ltv=Sum(\"orders__total\"), items=Count(\"orders__items\"))` — joining orders *and* items would multiply every order's total by its item count.\n\n**(b) Monthly revenue report (GROUP BY a truncated date):**\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models.functions import TruncMonth\n\nmonthly = (\n    Order.objects.filter(status=Order.Status.PAID, created_at__year=2026)\n    .annotate(month=TruncMonth(\"created_at\"))\n    .values(\"month\")\n    .annotate(revenue=Sum(\"total\"), orders=Count(\"id\"), customers=Count(\"customer\", distinct=True))\n    .order_by(\"month\")\n)\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', \"sales_order\".\"created_at\" AT TIME ZONE 'UTC') AS \"month\",\n       SUM(\"sales_order\".\"total\") AS \"revenue\",\n       COUNT(\"sales_order\".\"id\") AS \"orders\",\n       COUNT(DISTINCT \"sales_order\".\"customer_id\") AS \"customers\"\nFROM \"sales_order\"\nWHERE (\"sales_order\".\"created_at\" BETWEEN '2026-01-01 00:00:00+00:00' AND '2026-12-31 23:59:59.999999+00:00'\n       AND \"sales_order\".\"status\" = 'paid')\nGROUP BY 1\nORDER BY 1 ASC;\n```\n\nWith `USE_TZ=True`, `TruncMonth` converts to the current time zone (`AT TIME ZONE`), so \"month\" means the user's month, not UTC's. Pass `tzinfo=` to control it.\n\n**(c) Top 3 best-selling products per category (window + filter, Django 4.2+):**\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models import F, Sum, Window\nfrom django.db.models.functions import Rank\n\ntop3 = (\n    Product.objects.annotate(units=Coalesce(Sum(\"order_items__quantity\",\n                                                filter=Q(order_items__order__status=\"paid\")), 0))\n    .annotate(rnk=Window(Rank(), partition_by=F(\"category_id\"), order_by=F(\"units\").desc()))\n    .filter(rnk__lte=3)\n    .order_by(\"category_id\", \"rnk\")\n)\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM (\n  SELECT \"sales_product\".\"id\" AS \"col1\", ..., \n         COALESCE(SUM(\"sales_orderitem\".\"quantity\") FILTER (WHERE \"sales_order\".\"status\" = 'paid'), 0) AS \"units\",\n         RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY \"sales_product\".\"category_id\"\n                      ORDER BY COALESCE(SUM(...) FILTER (...), 0) DESC) AS \"rnk\"\n  FROM \"sales_product\"\n  LEFT OUTER JOIN \"sales_orderitem\" ON (...) LEFT OUTER JOIN \"sales_order\" ON (...)\n  GROUP BY \"sales_product\".\"id\"\n) \"qualify\"\nWHERE \"rnk\" <= 3\nORDER BY ...;\n```\n\nDjango names the wrapper `qualify`, after the `QUALIFY` clause some databases offer. Note windows are computed **after** GROUP BY, so a window can rank an aggregate.\n\n**(d) Running balance per account with a frame:**\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models import RowRange\nfrom django.db.models.functions import Lag\n\nLedger.objects.filter(account_id=acct).annotate(\n    balance=Window(Sum(\"amount\"), order_by=[F(\"created_at\").asc(), F(\"id\").asc()],\n                   frame=RowRange(start=None, end=0)),\n    prev_amount=Window(Lag(\"amount\"), order_by=F(\"created_at\").asc()),\n)\n```\n\n`RowRange(start=None, end=0)` = `ROWS BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW`. Always add a unique tiebreaker (`id`) to the window ordering, otherwise ties produce non-deterministic running totals.\n\n**(e) Use the result in DRF without N+1:** annotate in `get_queryset()` and expose the alias as a read-only field (`serializers.DecimalField(read_only=True, max_digits=12, decimal_places=2)`) — see [Ch 21 · DRF Serializers](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers).\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Technique | Rows returned | Good for | Watch out for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `aggregate()` | 1 dict | Totals, KPIs | Terminal; can't chain further |\n| `annotate(Agg)` on model | 1 per object | Per-object counts | Fan-out with ≥2 multi-valued joins; GROUP BY cost |\n| `values().annotate()` | 1 per group | Reports, charts | `order_by` fields join GROUP BY; returns dicts |\n| Conditional aggregate `filter=Q` | same as above | Many metrics in one scan | Needs PG for `FILTER`; CASE elsewhere is fine |\n| `Subquery` | unchanged | Child column, isolated aggregates | Needs index on correlated column; one column/row |\n| `Exists` | unchanged | Semi-joins, permission filters | None significant — usually optimal |\n| `Window` | unchanged | Rank, running totals, lag/lead | Computed after WHERE/GROUP BY; filter needs 4.2+ |\n| Raw SQL / `RawSQL` | any | Truly exotic SQL (`LATERAL`, CTEs) | Loses composability; injection risk if formatted |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Any number you would otherwise compute by looping over model instances.\n- `Subquery` whenever you aggregate across more than one multi-valued relation, or need columns of a \"latest\" child.\n- `Exists` instead of `filter(children__x=...).distinct()` to avoid duplicates and DISTINCT sorts.\n- Windows for leaderboards, rank-within-group, running totals, gaps between events.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Don't annotate heavy aggregates on list endpoints that are paginated over huge tables without an index strategy — the GROUP BY runs over all matched rows before LIMIT. Consider denormalised counters (updated with `F()`; see [Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency)) or a materialized view.\n- Don't build reporting on the primary when replicas exist ([Ch 34](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases)).\n- Don't force the ORM into recursive CTEs — use raw SQL or a package; see [SQL · Recursive CTE](../sql/topic.html?p=12-recursive-cte).\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Confusing `aggregate` and `annotate`.** `Publisher.objects.aggregate(Count(\"books\"))` returns the total number of book links across all publishers, one number. `annotate` gives one number per publisher.\n\n**2. `values()` in the wrong position.**\n\n```python\n# Wrong: grouped by book pk, then projected -> one row per book with n=1\nBook.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"id\")).values(\"publisher\")\n# Right: group by publisher\nBook.objects.values(\"publisher\").annotate(n=Count(\"id\"))\n```\n\n**3. A stray ordering splitting groups.** `values(\"publisher\").annotate(n=Count(\"id\")).order_by(\"title\")` adds `title` to GROUP BY → one row per title. Order by the aggregate or the group key, or clear with `.order_by()`.\n\n**4. Multiple aggregates across different relations.** Covered in §3.4. Before/after:\n\n```python\n# Before (wrong): authors x stores rows per book\nBook.objects.annotate(a=Count(\"authors\"), s=Count(\"stores\"))\n# Before (wrong): each book's price repeated once per author\nPublisher.objects.annotate(n_authors=Count(\"books__authors\"), value=Sum(\"books__price\"))\n\n# After\nBook.objects.annotate(a=Count(\"authors\", distinct=True), s=Count(\"stores\", distinct=True))\n\nbook_value = (Book.objects.filter(publisher=OuterRef(\"pk\")).order_by()\n              .values(\"publisher\").annotate(v=Sum(\"price\")).values(\"v\"))\nPublisher.objects.annotate(\n    n_authors=Count(\"books__authors\", distinct=True),\n    value=Coalesce(Subquery(book_value), Decimal(\"0\")),   # SUM must not see the fanned-out rows\n)\n```\n\n**5. Forgetting `None` for empty sets.** `Sum` over no rows is `NULL` → `None`. Use `default=0` (4.0+) on `aggregate`, or `Coalesce(..., 0)` in `annotate`/subqueries.\n\n**6. Mixed types.** `F(\"price\") * F(\"quantity\")` (Decimal × Integer) may raise `FieldError: Expression contains mixed types` on some combinations — add `output_field=DecimalField(...)` or wrap in `ExpressionWrapper`.\n\n**7. Subquery returning multiple rows/columns.** Missing `[:1]` → `more than one row returned by a subquery used as an expression`. Missing `.values(\"col\")` → `subquery has too many columns`.\n\n**8. Filtering on a related field after annotate unexpectedly adds a join.** For multi-valued relations, `annotate(n=Count(\"books\")).filter(books__rating=5)` creates a *second* join to book, which also fans out the count. Put the filter before the annotate, or use `filter=Q(...)` inside the aggregate.\n\n**Best practices:** print `str(qs.query)` for every non-trivial report while developing; write a test with `assertNumQueries(1)` and known fixture totals; use `alias()` for expressions only needed in filters; index the correlated columns of every subquery.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: dashboard totals off by 2–10×.** *Symptom:* revenue on the admin dashboard doesn't match finance's export. *Root cause:* `annotate(Sum(\"orders__total\"), Count(\"orders__items\"))` — fan-out. *Fix:* subqueries per relation; add a regression test with an order having multiple items.\n\n**Pitfall: annotated admin list view times out.** `ModelAdmin.get_queryset` annotated `Count(\"orders\")` across 5M customers; the admin's `paginator.count` then runs `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM (... GROUP BY ...)`. *Fix:* annotate only in the changelist when needed, use `show_full_result_count = False`, or denormalise a counter. See [Ch 07 · The Django Admin](topic.html?p=07-django-admin).\n\n**Pitfall: correlated subquery without an index.** Each outer row triggers a sequential scan of the inner table — O(N×M). `explain()` shows `SubPlan` with `Seq Scan`. *Fix:* composite index matching the subquery's filter + order, e.g. `(customer_id, created_at DESC)`.\n\n```python\nprint(customers.explain(analyze=True, buffers=True))   # PostgreSQL options pass through\n```\n\n```text\nLimit  (cost=... rows=50)\n  ->  Sort  (... Sort Key: (COALESCE((SubPlan 1), 0)) DESC)\n        ->  Seq Scan on accounts_user ...\n              SubPlan 1\n                ->  GroupAggregate\n                      ->  Index Scan using order_cust_created_idx on sales_order u0\n                            Index Cond: (customer_id = accounts_user.id)\n```\n\n**Pitfall: `count()` on a grouped queryset is slow.** `values(\"x\").annotate(...).count()` becomes `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM (SELECT ... GROUP BY ...) subquery` — it computes every group. Fine for hundreds of groups, not for millions.\n\n**Pitfall: time-zone surprises in date grouping.** Orders at 23:30 IST land in the \"next\" day when the report runs in UTC. Decide the reporting tz explicitly (`TruncDate(\"created_at\", tzinfo=ZoneInfo(\"Asia/Kolkata\"))`).\n\n**Debugging:** `str(qs.query)` (approximate SQL), `connection.queries` with `DEBUG=True`, django-debug-toolbar's SQL panel (shows EXPLAIN per query), and PostgreSQL's `pg_stat_statements` for the heaviest aggregate queries in production.\n\n**Scaling:** heavy reporting belongs on a read replica (`.using(\"replica\")`), in materialized views refreshed on a schedule, or in a warehouse. Hot counters (likes, stock) belong in denormalised columns updated atomically, not recomputed with `Count` per request. Query-level deep dive: [SQL · Group By](../sql/topic.html?p=07-group-by), [SQL · Query Optimization](../sql/topic.html?p=21-query-optimization).\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `aggregate()` and `annotate()`?**\nA: `aggregate()` computes summary values over the entire QuerySet and returns a plain dictionary immediately — it is terminal, so you can't chain filters after it. `annotate()` adds a computed expression to each object in the QuerySet and returns a new lazy QuerySet you can keep filtering and ordering. When the annotation contains an aggregate, Django adds a GROUP BY on the model's primary key (or the `values()` fields), so you get one value per object. Example: `Book.objects.aggregate(Avg(\"price\"))` → `{\"price__avg\": ...}`; `Publisher.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"books\"))` → each publisher has `.n`.\n\n**Q: How do you write a GROUP BY query in the Django ORM?**\nA: Call `values()` with the grouping columns *before* `annotate()`: `Book.objects.values(\"publisher__name\").annotate(n=Count(\"id\"))`. Django groups by the fields in `values()` and returns dictionaries. If you call `values()` after `annotate()`, it only selects columns and the grouping stays on the primary key. Be careful with `order_by()`: an ordering on a field not in `values()` is added to GROUP BY and splits groups, so order by the key or the aggregate, or clear ordering with `.order_by()`.\n\n**Q: How do you express HAVING?**\nA: Filter on an aggregate annotation after it is defined: `Author.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"books\")).filter(n__gt=3)` produces `HAVING COUNT(...) > 3`. A filter placed before the annotate, or on a non-aggregate field, goes into WHERE. So `filter(books__rating__gt=4).annotate(n=Count(\"books\"))` counts only highly rated books, which is a different result from annotating first.\n\n**Q: Why might `Book.objects.annotate(Count(\"authors\"), Count(\"stores\"))` return wrong numbers?**\nA: Because both relations are multi-valued and Django joins them in the same query, producing authors × stores rows per book before grouping. Each COUNT then counts the product, not the individual relation. For counts, `distinct=True` fixes it by counting distinct ids. For `Sum`/`Avg` it does not, so you compute each aggregate in its own correlated `Subquery` (or in separate queries). The rule: at most one multi-valued join per aggregating query.\n\n**Q: What are `Subquery` and `OuterRef` used for?**\nA: `Subquery` embeds a QuerySet as a SQL subquery, and `OuterRef` refers to a column of the enclosing query, making it correlated. Typical use: annotate each customer with their latest order's total — `Subquery(Order.objects.filter(customer=OuterRef(\"pk\")).order_by(\"-created_at\").values(\"total\")[:1])`. The inner query must return one column and, as a scalar, at most one row. It avoids N+1 and avoids join fan-out, and with an index on the correlated column each probe is cheap.\n\n**Q: When would you use `Exists` instead of a join filter?**\nA: When you only need to know whether a related row exists. `filter(books__rating=5)` joins and returns a publisher once per matching book, forcing you to add `.distinct()`, which sorts or hashes the whole result. `filter(Exists(Book.objects.filter(publisher=OuterRef(\"pk\"), rating=5)))` produces a semi-join that stops at the first match and never duplicates rows. `~Exists(...)` gives a clean NOT EXISTS, which is also safer than `NOT IN` with NULLs.\n\n**Q: What is conditional aggregation and how does Django implement it?**\nA: Computing aggregates over subsets of rows in a single pass, e.g. `Count(\"id\", filter=Q(status=\"paid\"))` alongside `Count(\"id\")`. On PostgreSQL Django emits the standard `COUNT(...) FILTER (WHERE ...)`; on databases without `FILTER` support it rewrites to `COUNT(CASE WHEN ... THEN ... END)`. It replaces several separate queries with one table scan, which is the usual way to build a status breakdown or dashboard tile set.\n\n**Q: What does `F()` do in an annotation, and when do you need `ExpressionWrapper` or `output_field`?**\nA: `F(\"field\")` references a column in SQL so arithmetic happens in the database — `annotate(line_total=F(\"quantity\") * F(\"unit_price\"))`. Django infers the output type from the operands; when they differ (decimal times integer, date minus date producing a duration) it may not be able to and raises \"Expression contains mixed types\". You then supply `output_field=DecimalField(...)`, either directly on the expression or via `ExpressionWrapper`. The output field also determines how the value is converted back to Python.\n\n**Q: How do window functions differ from `annotate` with an aggregate, and how do you get the top N per group?**\nA: A window function computes over a partition of rows but keeps every row, while GROUP BY collapses each group into one row. In Django: `annotate(rnk=Window(RowNumber(), partition_by=F(\"category\"), order_by=F(\"price\").desc()))`. SQL doesn't allow window functions in WHERE, but since Django 4.2 you can write `.filter(rnk__lte=3)` and Django wraps the query in a subquery and filters outside it. Before 4.2 you'd use a subquery or raw SQL.\n\n**Q: You need a list of 50 customers sorted by lifetime value, with order count, ticket count and last order date. How would you build it efficiently? (Senior)**\nA: Avoid joining orders and tickets in one aggregating query because of fan-out. Annotate each metric with its own correlated subquery: grouped `Sum(\"total\")` and `Count(\"pk\")` over paid orders with `OuterRef(\"pk\")`, a `Count` over tickets, and `Subquery(...order_by(\"-created_at\").values(\"created_at\")[:1])` for last order, each wrapped in `Coalesce` for customers with none. Make sure each correlated column has a supporting index like `(customer_id, created_at DESC)`, and check `explain(analyze=True)` for index scans in the SubPlans. If the list must sort by LTV across millions of users, that still computes LTV for every user before LIMIT, so at scale I'd denormalise `lifetime_value` onto the customer (updated in the order-paid transaction with `F()`) or precompute it in a materialized view.\n\n**Q: An admin dashboard's revenue numbers are 3× too high, but only for some customers. How do you debug it? (Senior)**\nA: \"Only some\" strongly suggests fan-out: customers with multiple rows in a second joined relation get multiplied. I'd print `str(qs.query)` and look for more than one LEFT JOIN to multi-valued relations under a GROUP BY, then reproduce with a fixture of one order with three items. The fix is to move each aggregate to a subquery or compute `Sum(F(\"quantity\")*F(\"unit_price\"))` at the item level only. I'd add a regression test asserting exact totals for that fixture, and review other reports for the same pattern.\n\n**Q: A correlated subquery annotation made a list endpoint go from 50 ms to 8 s. What happened and what are your options? (Senior)**\nA: Most likely the inner query has no usable index, so PostgreSQL runs a sequential scan per outer row; `explain(analyze=True)` will show a SubPlan with Seq Scan and loops equal to the outer row count. Add a composite index matching the inner filter and order. If the endpoint sorts or filters by the annotation, the subquery is evaluated for every row before LIMIT — then consider a `LATERAL` join via raw SQL, a denormalised column, or computing the annotation only for the page of IDs already selected (two-step: pick IDs, then annotate `filter(pk__in=ids)`). Also confirm the annotation isn't duplicated by both a list and a count query in pagination.\n\n**Q: What is `alias()` and why use it over `annotate()`?**\nA: `alias()` (Django 3.2+) defines an expression you can reference in `filter()`, `exclude()`, `order_by()` or other expressions without adding it to the SELECT list. That saves computing and transferring a column you don't need, and it avoids the expression being part of GROUP BY. If you later need the value, you can `annotate()` it from the alias.\n\n**Q: What does `Sum` return when there are no rows, and how do you handle it?**\nA: SQL aggregates other than COUNT return NULL over an empty set, which Django turns into `None` — a common source of `TypeError` in templates and serializers. Since Django 4.0 aggregates accept `default=`, e.g. `Sum(\"total\", default=0)`, which wraps it in `COALESCE`. In annotations and subqueries, wrap explicitly with `Coalesce(Subquery(...), Value(0))` and specify an `output_field` if types differ.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Want | ORM | SQL |\n|---|---|---|\n| One total | `.aggregate(t=Sum(\"x\", default=0))` | `SELECT COALESCE(SUM(x),0)` |\n| Per object | `.annotate(n=Count(\"rel\"))` | `LEFT JOIN … GROUP BY pk` |\n| Per group | `.values(\"g\").annotate(n=Count(\"id\")).order_by()` | `GROUP BY g` |\n| HAVING | `.annotate(n=...).filter(n__gt=3)` | `HAVING …` |\n| Conditional | `Count(\"id\", filter=Q(...))` | `COUNT(...) FILTER (WHERE …)` |\n| Distinct count | `Count(\"customer\", distinct=True)` | `COUNT(DISTINCT customer_id)` |\n| Latest child col | `Subquery(qs.order_by(\"-t\").values(\"c\")[:1])` | correlated scalar subquery |\n| Has child | `.filter(Exists(qs))` | `WHERE EXISTS(…)` |\n| Bucketing | `Case(When(...), default=...)` | `CASE WHEN … END` |\n| Rank in group | `Window(Rank(), partition_by=…, order_by=…)` | `RANK() OVER (…)` |\n| Running total | `Window(Sum(\"a\"), order_by=…, frame=RowRange(None, 0))` | `SUM() OVER (… ROWS …)` |\n| Date bucket | `TruncMonth(\"created_at\")` | `DATE_TRUNC('month', …)` |\n\n- `aggregate` is terminal and returns a dict; `annotate` returns a QuerySet.\n- `values()` before `annotate()` = GROUP BY; after = projection.\n- Extra `order_by` fields leak into GROUP BY — clear with `.order_by()`.\n- Two multi-valued joins in one aggregating query = multiplied results.\n- `Count` over a relation uses LEFT JOIN (zero counts kept); a prior `filter` on the relation makes it INNER.\n- Subquery: one column (`values`), one row (`[:1]`), index the correlated column.\n- Empty `Sum` is `None`: `default=` or `Coalesce`.\n- Filtering on window annotations works from 4.2 (Django wraps in a subquery).\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" \"psycopg[binary]\"`; create the bookshop models from §5.1 and a seeding command that builds 20 publishers, 200 authors, 5,000 books, 50 stores.\n\n1. Write `aggregate` and `annotate` versions of \"average price\"; print `str(qs.query)` for each and explain the GROUP BY.\n2. Produce `[{publisher, n_books, avg_rating}]` sorted by `n_books` desc. Then add `.order_by(\"title\")` and explain the new result.\n3. Reproduce the fan-out bug: annotate books with `Count(\"authors\")` and `Count(\"stores\")`, and publishers with `Count(\"books__authors\")` plus `Sum(\"books__price\")`; compute the true values in Python; fix with `distinct=True`, then with subqueries.\n4. Annotate each author with the title and date of their newest book using `Subquery`, and filter authors with no book in any store using `~Exists`.\n5. Top 2 highest-rated books per publisher using `Window(RowNumber())` + `filter(rn__lte=2)`; inspect the generated `qualify` subquery.\n6. Add an index that makes exercise 4's subquery an index scan; prove it with `explain(analyze=True)`.\n\n**Mini project:** a `/api/reports/sales/` endpoint (DRF or plain JSON view) returning monthly revenue, distinct customers, refund rate (conditional aggregation) and the top product per month (window), in at most three queries, with a test using `assertNumQueries` and hand-computed fixture totals.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) (Q, F, laziness), [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) (annotations as an N+1 cure), [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) (denormalised counters with `F()`), [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres) (`ArrayAgg`, `StringAgg`, raw SQL), [Ch 39 · ORM Coding Round](topic.html?p=39-orm-coding-challenges).\n\n**Other handbooks:** [SQL · Group By](../sql/topic.html?p=07-group-by), [SQL · HAVING & Grouping Sets](../sql/topic.html?p=08-having-grouping-sets), [SQL · Subqueries](../sql/topic.html?p=10-subqueries), [SQL · Window Functions](../sql/topic.html?p=13-window-functions), [SQL · Ranking & Window Frames](../sql/topic.html?p=14-ranking-window-frames), [SQL · Conditional Logic](../sql/topic.html?p=15-conditional-logic), [SQL · Top-N per Group](../sql/topic.html?p=34-problem-top-n-per-group).\n\n- **Aggregation topic guide** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the canonical explanation of aggregate vs annotate, ordering interactions, and the multiple-aggregation warning. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/aggregation/>\n- **Query expressions reference** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · F, Func, Subquery, OuterRef, Exists, Window and output_field. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/expressions/>\n- **Conditional expressions** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · Case/When and conditional aggregation. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/conditional-expressions/>\n- **Database functions** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · Coalesce, Trunc/Extract, text and math functions, window functions. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/database-functions/>\n- **PostgreSQL-specific aggregates** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · ArrayAgg, StringAgg, JSONBAgg and friends. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/contrib/postgres/aggregates/>\n- **PostgreSQL tutorial: window functions** — PostgreSQL docs · *Beginner* · what OVER/PARTITION BY actually compute. <https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-window.html>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 12.*\n", "13-queryset-optimization": "# 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem\n\n> **In one line:** Most slow Django pages are not slow queries but *too many* queries — one per row, hidden behind attribute access — so optimization means counting queries first, then choosing the right tool: `select_related` (JOIN) for single-valued relations, `prefetch_related` (a second `IN` query) for multi-valued ones, `only`/`values` to fetch less, `bulk_*`/`update()` to write in sets, and `iterator()` to stream.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships) · [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) · [Ch 12 · Aggregation, Annotation, Subqueries](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries)\n\nThe ORM makes related data feel like ordinary Python attributes: `order.customer.email`, `book.authors.all()`. That convenience has a price. Each attribute access on a relation that hasn't been loaded triggers a **lazy query**. In a template looping over 100 orders and printing the customer's name, you run 1 query for the orders and then 100 more for the customers — the **N+1 problem**. Nothing in the code looks like a query. Locally, with 10 rows and a database on localhost, it takes 15 ms and nobody notices. In production, with 500 rows per page and 1 ms network latency per round trip, it's half a second of pure latency — before any query is actually slow.\n\nThe naive fixes fail in their own ways. Caching the page hides the problem until the cache misses. Adding indexes doesn't help: each of the 100 queries is already a primary-key lookup. Writing raw SQL with joins works but throws away the ORM. The real fix is to tell the ORM, up front, which relations you will touch, so it can fetch them in **one JOIN** or **one extra query per relation** instead of one per row.\n\nThe same pattern hides on the write side: calling `save()` in a loop issues one `UPDATE` per object, `create()` in a loop one `INSERT` each. And on the read side, loading full model instances (all 40 columns, including a 50 KB `description` text field) to display three columns wastes memory and bandwidth.\n\n> **Why this matters:** \"Difference between `select_related` and `prefetch_related`\" is the single most-asked Django ORM interview question. Interviewers then probe whether you can *detect* N+1 (not just name it), and whether you know when a prefetch is silently defeated.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **N+1 problem** — 1 query for the parent list + N queries for a relation accessed per row. *Why:* latency scales with page size × round-trip time, not with data complexity.\n- **Lazy relation access** — related objects are fetched by descriptors (`ForwardManyToOneDescriptor.__get__`, `ReverseManyToOneDescriptor`, `ManyToManyDescriptor`) on first access. *Why:* this is where hidden queries come from.\n- **`select_related(*fields)`** — follow forward `ForeignKey`/`OneToOneField` (and reverse one-to-one) with SQL JOINs in the same query. *Why:* zero extra round trips for single-valued relations.\n- **`prefetch_related(*lookups)`** — after the main query, run **one extra query per relation** with `WHERE fk IN (...)` and stitch results in Python. Works for M2M, reverse FK, `GenericForeignKey`, and also forward FK. *Why:* multi-valued relations can't be JOINed without duplicating parent rows.\n- **`Prefetch(lookup, queryset=None, to_attr=None)`** — customise the prefetch query (filter, order, `select_related`, `only`) and optionally store it as a list attribute. *Why:* \"prefetch only the active items, newest first\".\n- **`prefetch_related_objects(instances, *lookups)`** — prefetch onto an already-evaluated list. *Why:* useful when objects came from cache or multiple sources.\n- **`only(*fields)` / `defer(*fields)`** — load a subset of columns into model instances; others become deferred and load lazily on access. *Why:* smaller rows, but accessing a deferred field per row is a new N+1.\n- **`values()` / `values_list(flat=True)`** — return dicts/tuples instead of instances. *Why:* no model instantiation overhead; ideal for exports and IDs.\n- **`bulk_create` / `bulk_update`** — many rows in one (or a few batched) statements; skip `save()` and signals. *Why:* 10,000 inserts become ~10 queries.\n- **`QuerySet.update()` / `delete()`** — set-based writes in SQL. *Why:* one statement, atomic per row, no read-modify-write race.\n- **`iterator(chunk_size=…)`** — stream results without the result cache; uses a server-side cursor on PostgreSQL. *Why:* constant memory for huge exports.\n- **`exists()` / `count()` / `contains()`** — ask the DB for a boolean/number instead of fetching rows. *Why:* don't `len(qs)` just to check emptiness.\n- **`explain()`** — returns the database's query plan. *Why:* tells you whether a single query is slow (index problem) as opposed to too many queries (N+1).\n- **Query counting** — `assertNumQueries`, `django_assert_num_queries` (pytest-django), `connection.queries`, debug toolbar. *Why:* you can't fix what you don't measure, and tests lock fixes in.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 Where the N+1 actually happens\n\n```python\norders = Order.objects.all()[:100]      # lazy, no query yet\nfor o in orders:                          # query 1: SELECT ... FROM order LIMIT 100\n    print(o.customer.name)                # queries 2..101: SELECT ... FROM user WHERE id = %s\n```\n\nWhen you access `o.customer`, `ForwardManyToOneDescriptor.__get__` checks the instance's field cache (`o._state.fields_cache[\"customer\"]`). On a miss it runs `self.get_object(instance)` — a `get()` on the related model by primary key — and caches the result on that instance only. Each `Order` instance has its own cache, so every row misses once. Reverse and M2M managers (`o.items.all()`) work similarly: `ReverseManyToOneDescriptor` returns a `RelatedManager` whose `get_queryset()` first checks `instance._prefetched_objects_cache`; on a miss it builds a fresh filtered QuerySet and runs it.\n\nSo both fixes work by **pre-filling those caches**: `select_related` fills `fields_cache` from joined columns while building each instance; `prefetch_related` fills `_prefetched_objects_cache` (or your `to_attr`) after a batched query.\n\n### 3.2 `select_related` vs `prefetch_related`, mechanically\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 440\" width=\"100%\" height=\"440\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj13a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj13b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj13c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Three ways to load 100 orders with their customer and items</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"270\" height=\"380\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Lazy (N+1): 201 queries</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"78\" width=\"230\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"155\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT * FROM order LIMIT 100</text>\n  <path d=\"M155,108 L155,128\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj13a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"146\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">for each of 100 orders:</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"156\" width=\"230\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"155\" y=\"174\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT * FROM user WHERE id = 7</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"192\" width=\"230\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"155\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT * FROM item WHERE order_id = 1</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">... x 100</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"280\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">Each query is fast (pk/index lookup).</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"298\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">Cost = 201 round trips x latency.</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"316\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">At 1 ms RTT: 200 ms of pure waiting.</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"350\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">Indexes don't help. Only fewer</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"368\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">round trips help.</text>\n  <rect x=\"305\" y=\"40\" width=\"270\" height=\"380\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e3a8a\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">select_related(\"customer\")</text>\n  <rect x=\"320\" y=\"78\" width=\"240\" height=\"56\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"330\" y=\"96\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT order.*, user.*</text>\n  <text x=\"330\" y=\"112\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">FROM order INNER JOIN user</text>\n  <text x=\"330\" y=\"128\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">ON order.customer_id = user.id</text>\n  <path d=\"M440,136 L440,160\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj13b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">one row per order, user columns appended</text>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"198\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">ModelIterable builds Order + User,</text>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"216\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">sets order._state.fields_cache[\"customer\"]</text>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"250\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Forward FK / O2O only</text>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"268\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"10\">nullable FK &#8594; LEFT OUTER JOIN</text>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"286\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"10\">Can't do M2M/reverse FK: would</text>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"304\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"10\">duplicate the order row per item</text>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"338\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"10\">1 query total</text>\n  <text x=\"320\" y=\"356\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"10\">risk: wide rows, many joins</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"40\" width=\"270\" height=\"380\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">prefetch_related(\"items\")</text>\n  <rect x=\"605\" y=\"78\" width=\"240\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"725\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Q1: SELECT * FROM order LIMIT 100</text>\n  <path d=\"M725,108 L725,124\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj13c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"605\" y=\"140\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">collect pks: [1, 2, ... 100]</text>\n  <rect x=\"605\" y=\"150\" width=\"240\" height=\"44\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"615\" y=\"168\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Q2: SELECT * FROM item</text>\n  <text x=\"615\" y=\"184\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">WHERE order_id IN (1, 2, ... 100)</text>\n  <path d=\"M725,196 L725,214\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj13c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"605\" y=\"232\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Python: group items by order_id,</text>\n  <text x=\"605\" y=\"250\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">store in each order&#8217;s</text>\n  <text x=\"605\" y=\"268\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">_prefetched_objects_cache[\"items\"]</text>\n  <text x=\"605\" y=\"302\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">M2M, reverse FK, GFK (and FK)</text>\n  <text x=\"605\" y=\"320\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">1 extra query per relation level</text>\n  <text x=\"605\" y=\"338\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">order.items.all() &#8594; served from cache</text>\n  <text x=\"605\" y=\"356\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">order.items.filter(...) &#8594; NEW query</text>\n  <text x=\"605\" y=\"384\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">Combined: 2 queries for all of it</text>\n  <text x=\"605\" y=\"402\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">(select + prefetch together)</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.3 How prefetching works internally\n\nWhen the main QuerySet evaluates, `QuerySet._fetch_all()` fills `_result_cache` and then, if `_prefetch_related_lookups` is non-empty, calls `_prefetch_related_objects()` → `prefetch_related_objects(self._result_cache, *lookups)`. For each lookup (split on `__` into levels), it:\n\n1. Finds the descriptor for the attribute on the first instance and asks it for a `prefetcher` via `get_prefetcher()`.\n2. Calls `prefetcher.get_prefetch_querysets(instances, querysets)` (Django 5.0+; previously `get_prefetch_queryset`), which returns the related QuerySet filtered with `__in` on the collected keys, plus functions to extract the join key from each related object and each instance.\n3. Evaluates that QuerySet (one query), builds a dict `key → [related objects]`, and assigns each instance's list into `_prefetched_objects_cache[cache_name]` (or `setattr(instance, to_attr, list)`).\n4. Recurses for the next level (`items__product`) using the objects just fetched as the new instance list.\n\nFor M2M the extra query joins the through table and selects the source FK as an extra column (`_prefetch_related_val_<fk>`) to know which parent each row belongs to.\n\nTwo consequences matter. First, the relation manager's `.all()` reads the cache, but `.filter()`, `.order_by()`, `.exclude()` or `.first()` (which adds ordering) on the manager **builds a new QuerySet and queries again** — prefetching is defeated. Use `Prefetch(queryset=…)` to do the filtering in the prefetch itself. Second, the `IN (...)` list contains all parent keys: prefetching from 50,000 parents sends 50,000 parameters. That's legal on PostgreSQL but slow to plan and transfer; paginate or chunk.\n\n### 3.4 Prefetch with `to_attr`, nesting and slicing\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models import Prefetch\n\nOrder.objects.prefetch_related(\n    Prefetch(\n        \"items\",\n        queryset=OrderItem.objects.select_related(\"product\").filter(cancelled=False).order_by(\"id\"),\n        to_attr=\"active_items\",                   # list attribute, not a manager\n    ),\n)\n```\n\n`to_attr` stores a plain `list` — `order.active_items` — making it impossible to accidentally re-query through a manager, and letting you prefetch the same relation twice with different filters. Since Django 4.2, the Prefetch queryset may be **sliced** (`[:3]`), which Django implements with a `ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY fk)` window, giving \"latest 3 comments per post\" in one extra query.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 The optimization workflow\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 300\" width=\"100%\" height=\"300\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj13d\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Measure, classify, fix, lock in</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"150\" height=\"90\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e3a8a\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. Measure</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"92\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">debug toolbar SQL panel</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"108\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">connection.queries</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">APM / Sentry spans</text>\n  <path d=\"M172,95 L208,95\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj13d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"212\" y=\"50\" width=\"200\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"312\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. Classify</text>\n  <text x=\"224\" y=\"96\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Many similar queries</text>\n  <text x=\"224\" y=\"112\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"10\">&#8594; N+1 (relation access)</text>\n  <text x=\"224\" y=\"138\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">One slow query</text>\n  <text x=\"224\" y=\"154\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"10\">&#8594; plan problem: explain()</text>\n  <text x=\"224\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Fast SQL, slow Python</text>\n  <text x=\"224\" y=\"196\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"10\">&#8594; too many instances/columns</text>\n  <text x=\"224\" y=\"222\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Many INSERT/UPDATE</text>\n  <text x=\"224\" y=\"238\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"10\">&#8594; loop of save()</text>\n  <path d=\"M414,95 L450,95\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj13d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"454\" y=\"50\" width=\"230\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"569\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">3. Fix</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"96\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">FK/O2O: select_related</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"112\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">M2M/reverse: prefetch / Prefetch</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"128\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">counts: annotate(Count) / Exists</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"154\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">plan: index, rewrite, Exists</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">only() / values() / iterator()</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"222\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">bulk_create / bulk_update / update()</text>\n  <path d=\"M686,95 L722,95\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj13d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"726\" y=\"50\" width=\"134\" height=\"90\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"793\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#4c1d95\" font-weight=\"bold\">4. Lock in</text>\n  <text x=\"736\" y=\"92\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">assertNumQueries(n)</text>\n  <text x=\"736\" y=\"108\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">test with 2 and 20 rows:</text>\n  <text x=\"736\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">count must not change</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"266\" width=\"840\" height=\"26\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"283\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">The key N+1 test: the number of queries must be constant in the number of rows. If 2 rows = 5 queries and 20 rows = 23, you have N+1.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 4.2 Decision table\n\n```text\nAccessing in a loop...                    Use\n---------------------------------------   ---------------------------------------------------\nobj.fk_field / obj.one_to_one             select_related(\"fk_field\")      (JOIN)\nobj.fk.fk.fk (chain of FKs)               select_related(\"fk__fk__fk\")\nobj.reverse_set.all() / obj.m2m.all()     prefetch_related(\"reverse_set\")  (IN query)\nfiltered/ordered children                 Prefetch(\"rel\", queryset=..., to_attr=\"x\")\nchildren's FK                             Prefetch(\"rel\", queryset=Child.objects.select_related(\"fk\"))\nonly a count of children                  annotate(n=Count(\"rel\"))         (no objects at all)\nwhether any child matches                 annotate(has=Exists(...))\nthe latest child                          Subquery(...[:1]) or Prefetch(queryset=...[:1]) (4.2+)\ngeneric FK                                prefetch_related(\"content_object\") / GenericPrefetch (5.0+)\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example: spotting and fixing N+1 in a shell\n\n```python\n# shop/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\n\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2)\n    description = models.TextField()\n\nclass Tag(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=50, unique=True)\n\nclass Order(models.Model):\n    customer = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"orders\")\n    coupon = models.ForeignKey(\"Coupon\", null=True, blank=True, on_delete=models.SET_NULL)\n    status = models.CharField(max_length=20, db_index=True)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n    tags = models.ManyToManyField(Tag, blank=True)\n\nclass Coupon(models.Model):\n    code = models.CharField(max_length=20, unique=True)\n\nclass OrderItem(models.Model):\n    order = models.ForeignKey(Order, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"items\")\n    product = models.ForeignKey(Product, on_delete=models.PROTECT)\n    quantity = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n```\n\n```python\n# python manage.py shell   (DEBUG=True so connection.queries is populated)\nfrom django.db import connection, reset_queries\nreset_queries()\nfor o in Order.objects.all()[:50]:\n    o.customer.email, [i.product.name for i in o.items.all()]\nlen(connection.queries)\n```\n\n```text\n>>> len(connection.queries)\n301          # 1 orders + 50 customers + 50 item lists + ~200 products\n```\n\n```python\nreset_queries()\nqs = (Order.objects\n      .select_related(\"customer\", \"coupon\")\n      .prefetch_related(Prefetch(\"items\", queryset=OrderItem.objects.select_related(\"product\")))\n      [:50])\nfor o in qs:\n    o.customer.email, [i.product.name for i in o.items.all()]\nlen(connection.queries)\n```\n\n```text\n>>> len(connection.queries)\n2\n>>> for q in connection.queries: print(q[\"sql\"][:120])\nSELECT \"shop_order\".\"id\", ..., \"auth_user\".\"id\", ..., \"shop_coupon\".\"id\", ... FROM \"shop_order\" INNER JOIN \"auth_user\" ON ... LEFT OUTER JOIN \"shop_coupon\" ON ... LIMIT 50\nSELECT \"shop_orderitem\".\"id\", ..., \"shop_product\".\"id\", ... FROM \"shop_orderitem\" INNER JOIN \"shop_product\" ON ... WHERE \"shop_orderitem\".\"order_id\" IN (1, 2, 3, ...)\n```\n\nNotice: `customer` (non-null FK) is an INNER JOIN, `coupon` (nullable) is a LEFT OUTER JOIN, and the prefetch's own `select_related(\"product\")` folds products into the second query.\n\n### 5.2 Fetch less: `only`, `defer`, `values`\n\n```python\n# Load only what the list page needs; description (large TEXT) stays in the DB\nProduct.objects.only(\"id\", \"name\", \"price\")\n# SELECT \"shop_product\".\"id\", \"shop_product\".\"name\", \"shop_product\".\"price\" FROM \"shop_product\"\n\nProduct.objects.defer(\"description\")       # everything except description\n\n# Pitfall: touching a deferred field in a loop is a new N+1\nfor p in Product.objects.only(\"id\", \"name\"):\n    p.price        # SELECT price FROM shop_product WHERE id = %s  -- per row!\n\n# Pure data, no model instances\nProduct.objects.values_list(\"id\", flat=True)                # [1, 2, 3, ...]\nOrder.objects.values(\"id\", \"customer__email\", \"status\")     # JOIN, dicts\n```\n\nWith `select_related`, you can restrict the joined model's columns too: `Order.objects.select_related(\"customer\").only(\"id\", \"status\", \"customer__email\")`.\n\n### 5.3 Write in sets\n\n```python\n# 10,000 INSERTs -> a handful of multi-row INSERTs\nOrderItem.objects.bulk_create(\n    [OrderItem(order_id=oid, product_id=pid, quantity=q) for oid, pid, q in rows],\n    batch_size=1000,\n)\n\n# Upsert (4.1+): insert or update on conflict\nProduct.objects.bulk_create(\n    products,\n    update_conflicts=True,\n    unique_fields=[\"sku\"],\n    update_fields=[\"price\", \"name\"],\n)\n\n# Many different values in one UPDATE ... CASE WHEN\nfor p in products: p.price = p.price * Decimal(\"1.1\")\nProduct.objects.bulk_update(products, [\"price\"], batch_size=500)\n\n# Same change for a set: one UPDATE, no instances loaded\nOrder.objects.filter(status=\"pending\", created_at__lt=cutoff).update(status=\"expired\")\n\n# Atomic increment at the database\nProduct.objects.filter(pk=pid).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - 1)\n```\n\n```sql\n-- bulk_update\nUPDATE \"shop_product\" SET \"price\" = CASE WHEN (\"shop_product\".\"id\" = 1) THEN 11.00\n                                          WHEN (\"shop_product\".\"id\" = 2) THEN 22.00 ... ELSE NULL END\nWHERE \"shop_product\".\"id\" IN (1, 2, ...);\n\n-- update with F\nUPDATE \"shop_product\" SET \"stock\" = (\"shop_product\".\"stock\" - 1) WHERE \"shop_product\".\"id\" = 42;\n```\n\nRemember the trade-off: `bulk_create`, `bulk_update` and `update()` skip `save()`, don't send `pre_save`/`post_save`, and don't update `auto_now` fields unless you include them. `bulk_create` sets primary keys on PostgreSQL (via `RETURNING`). Signals are covered in [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals); the concurrency side of `F()` in [Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency).\n\n### 5.4 Stream big result sets\n\n```python\nimport csv\nfrom django.http import StreamingHttpResponse\n\nclass Echo:\n    def write(self, value): return value\n\ndef export_orders(request):\n    rows = (Order.objects.filter(status=\"paid\")\n            .values_list(\"id\", \"customer__email\", \"created_at\")\n            .iterator(chunk_size=5000))\n    writer = csv.writer(Echo())\n    return StreamingHttpResponse((writer.writerow(r) for r in rows), content_type=\"text/csv\")\n```\n\n`iterator()` skips the result cache, and on PostgreSQL uses a server-side cursor (named cursor) fetching `chunk_size` rows at a time (default 2000). Behind PgBouncer in transaction mode server-side cursors break; set `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS = True` for that database (see [Ch 34](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases)). Since 4.1, `prefetch_related` works with `iterator()` if you pass `chunk_size` — each chunk is prefetched separately.\n\n### 5.5 Real-world example: a DRF order list from 203 queries to 3\n\n```python\n# api/serializers.py\nfrom rest_framework import serializers\nfrom shop.models import Order, OrderItem\n\nclass OrderItemSerializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    product_name = serializers.CharField(source=\"product.name\")\n    class Meta:\n        model = OrderItem\n        fields = [\"id\", \"product_name\", \"quantity\"]\n\nclass OrderSerializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    customer_email = serializers.EmailField(source=\"customer.email\")\n    coupon_code = serializers.CharField(source=\"coupon.code\", default=None)\n    items = OrderItemSerializer(many=True, source=\"active_items\")\n    tags = serializers.SlugRelatedField(many=True, read_only=True, slug_field=\"name\")\n    item_count = serializers.IntegerField(read_only=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        model = Order\n        fields = [\"id\", \"status\", \"created_at\", \"customer_email\", \"coupon_code\", \"items\", \"tags\", \"item_count\"]\n```\n\n```python\n# api/views.py\nfrom django.db.models import Count, Prefetch\nfrom rest_framework import viewsets\nfrom shop.models import Order, OrderItem\nfrom .serializers import OrderSerializer\n\nclass OrderViewSet(viewsets.ReadOnlyModelViewSet):\n    serializer_class = OrderSerializer\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return (\n            Order.objects.filter(customer__is_active=True)\n            .select_related(\"customer\", \"coupon\")\n            .only(\"id\", \"status\", \"created_at\", \"customer__email\", \"coupon__code\")\n            .annotate(item_count=Count(\"items\"))\n            .prefetch_related(\n                Prefetch(\n                    \"items\",\n                    queryset=OrderItem.objects.select_related(\"product\").only(\n                        \"id\", \"order_id\", \"quantity\", \"product__name\"),\n                    to_attr=\"active_items\",\n                ),\n                \"tags\",\n            )\n            .order_by(\"-created_at\")\n        )\n```\n\n```text\nGET /api/orders/?page=1  (page size 50)\nbefore: 203 queries, 410 ms   (1 list + 1 count + 50 customer + 50 coupon + 50 items + 50 tags + N products...)\nafter:    4 queries,  28 ms   (count, orders+customer+coupon JOIN with GROUP BY, items+product, tags)\n```\n\nLock it in:\n\n```python\n# api/tests.py\nfrom django.test import TestCase\nfrom django.urls import reverse\n\nclass OrderListQueryCountTests(TestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpTestData(cls):\n        cls.user = make_user_with_orders(n_orders=30, items_per_order=3)  # your factory\n\n    def test_query_count_is_constant(self):\n        self.client.force_login(self.user)\n        # 2 auth queries (session + user) + count + orders + items + tags\n        with self.assertNumQueries(6):\n            r = self.client.get(reverse(\"order-list\"))\n        self.assertEqual(r.status_code, 200)\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** the `only()` here must include `order_id` in the Prefetch queryset — Django needs the FK to attach items to orders. Leave it out and every item triggers a deferred-field load.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Tool | Queries | Best for | Cost / risk |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `select_related` | 1 (JOIN) | FK, O2O chains | Wide rows; many joins can explode the plan; duplicate data when many children share one parent |\n| `prefetch_related` | 1 + 1 per relation | M2M, reverse FK, GFK | Large `IN` lists; memory for all children; defeated by manager `.filter()` |\n| `Prefetch(queryset, to_attr)` | same | Filtered/ordered/sliced children | More code; to_attr lists aren't managers |\n| `annotate(Count)` | 1 | Counts only | GROUP BY cost; fan-out with several relations ([Ch 12](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries)) |\n| `only` / `defer` | 1 | Skipping big columns | Deferred access = N+1; fragile when templates change |\n| `values` / `values_list` | 1 | Exports, IDs, aggregates | No model methods/properties |\n| `iterator(chunk_size)` | 1 cursor | Huge scans | No result cache; server-side cursor vs PgBouncer |\n| `bulk_create` / `bulk_update` | N/batch | Imports, backfills | No `save()`/signals; `bulk_update` CASE gets slow for huge batches |\n| `update()` / `delete()` | 1 | Set-based changes | No `save()` (update); delete still collects for cascades/signals |\n\n### When to use\n\n- `select_related` for every FK you touch per row in a list; `prefetch_related` for every collection.\n- `only()` when a model has large columns (TEXT, JSON) not shown in the list.\n- `values_list(flat=True)` to feed IDs into another query (or better, pass the QuerySet itself as a subquery: `filter(id__in=qs.values(\"id\"))`).\n- `bulk_*` for imports and migrations; `update()` with `F()` for counters.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Don't `select_related()` with no arguments on models with many FKs — it follows every non-null FK recursively (bounded by depth) and pulls huge rows.\n- Don't prefetch relations you don't render — that's a wasted query and memory.\n- Don't use `bulk_create` when business logic lives in `save()` or signals you depend on (or replicate that logic explicitly).\n- Don't micro-optimise with `only()` in code paths whose templates change often — a new field in the template silently becomes N+1. Pair with query-count tests.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Filtering on a prefetched manager.**\n\n```python\n# Before: prefetch is wasted, one query per order\norders = Order.objects.prefetch_related(\"items\")\nfor o in orders:\n    active = o.items.filter(cancelled=False)      # new query each time\n\n# After\norders = Order.objects.prefetch_related(\n    Prefetch(\"items\", queryset=OrderItem.objects.filter(cancelled=False), to_attr=\"active_items\"))\nfor o in orders:\n    active = o.active_items                        # list, no query\n```\n\nThe same applies to `.first()`, `.last()`, `.order_by()`, `.count()` on the manager — although `.count()` on a prefetched `.all()` uses the cache (`len`), `manager.count()` without prefetch is a query. In templates, prefer `{{ order.items.all|length }}` after prefetching.\n\n**2. N+1 in `__str__`.** `def __str__(self): return f\"{self.order.customer} – {self.product}\"` — harmless on one object, catastrophic in an admin changelist or a select widget with 500 options. Add `list_select_related` in the admin, or keep `__str__` free of relations.\n\n**3. Counting with `len()` or checking with `if qs:`.** `len(qs)` fetches every row; `if qs:` too. Use `qs.count()` / `qs.exists()` — unless you're going to iterate anyway, in which case evaluating once and reusing the cache is the cheapest.\n\n**4. Re-evaluating QuerySets.** `Order.objects.filter(...)` called twice in a template (once for `count`, once for the loop) via a method that builds a new QuerySet each time. Assign once in the view; pass the evaluated list.\n\n**5. `save()` in a loop.**\n\n```python\n# Before: N UPDATEs, and a race if two workers do it\nfor p in Product.objects.filter(category=c):\n    p.price = p.price * Decimal(\"0.9\")\n    p.save()\n\n# After: one UPDATE, computed by the database\nProduct.objects.filter(category=c).update(price=F(\"price\") * Decimal(\"0.9\"))\n```\n\n**6. `IN` with a materialised Python list.** `ids = list(qs.values_list(\"id\", flat=True)); Other.objects.filter(fk__in=ids)` makes two round trips and ships the list both ways. Pass the QuerySet (`fk__in=qs.values(\"id\")`) so Django emits a subquery.\n\n**7. Serializer method fields that query.** `SerializerMethodField` doing `obj.items.count()` per row. Annotate in `get_queryset` instead. See [Ch 21 · DRF Serializers](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers).\n\n**Best practices:** optimise in `get_queryset()` (view or manager), not ad hoc in templates; write query-count tests for every list endpoint; keep django-debug-toolbar in local dev; review `select_related`/`prefetch_related` whenever a serializer or template gains a field.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: endpoint latency grows with page size.** *Symptom:* p95 doubles when the mobile app raises page size from 20 to 50. *Root cause:* a nested serializer added `product.brand.name` without a matching `select_related`. *Fix:* `Prefetch(\"items\", queryset=OrderItem.objects.select_related(\"product__brand\"))` and a query-count test that runs with two different row counts.\n\n**Pitfall: memory spike in an export job.** A Celery task did `for o in Order.objects.all()` on 8M rows; the result cache held every instance and the worker was OOM-killed. *Fix:* `.iterator(chunk_size=2000)` and `values_list`, or keyset batches (`filter(id__gt=last_id).order_by(\"id\")[:5000]`).\n\n**Pitfall: `select_related` made it slower.** Joining five FKs including a wide `profile` with JSON blobs turned a 3 ms query into 300 ms and a 40 MB result. *Fix:* `only()` the joined columns you need, or use `prefetch_related` for the wide relation so it's fetched once per distinct row (prefetch de-duplicates parents — 100 orders from 3 customers fetch 3 customers, whereas a JOIN repeats the customer columns 100 times).\n\n**Pitfall: giant `IN` lists.** Prefetching from 100k parents creates a 100k-parameter query; on some backends (SQLite, Oracle) there are hard parameter limits, and Django batches on those backends, but on PostgreSQL it's one huge query. *Fix:* paginate, or `iterator(chunk_size=…)` with prefetch (4.1+).\n\n**Pitfall: N+1 from permissions.** `has_object_permission` hitting the DB per object, or a template tag checking `user.has_perm` per row with a custom backend. Cache per-request, or precompute in the queryset. See [Ch 26 · Permissions](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization).\n\n**Debugging toolkit:**\n\n```python\n# Local: debug toolbar SQL panel shows duplicates (\"similar\" / \"duplicated\" counts) and EXPLAIN per query.\n# Shell / scripts:\nfrom django.db import connection, reset_queries\nfrom django.test.utils import CaptureQueriesContext\nwith CaptureQueriesContext(connection) as ctx:\n    render_page()\nprint(len(ctx.captured_queries))\nfor q in ctx.captured_queries: print(q[\"time\"], q[\"sql\"][:150])\n\n# Single slow query:\nprint(Order.objects.filter(status=\"paid\").order_by(\"-created_at\")[:50].explain(analyze=True))\n```\n\n```python\n# Log every query in dev (settings.py) — never in production\nLOGGING = {\n    \"version\": 1,\n    \"handlers\": {\"console\": {\"class\": \"logging.StreamHandler\"}},\n    \"loggers\": {\"django.db.backends\": {\"handlers\": [\"console\"], \"level\": \"DEBUG\"}},\n}\n```\n\nIn production, use your APM (Sentry Performance, Datadog, New Relic) which flags N+1 spans automatically, and PostgreSQL `pg_stat_statements` sorted by `calls` — an N+1 shows up as a primary-key query with millions of calls. The `nplusone` package (GitHub) can raise on lazy loads in tests; a `CaptureQueriesContext` middleware that logs requests over a threshold also works.\n\n**Scaling notes:** once queries are few and well-indexed, next steps are caching ([Ch 29 · Caching](topic.html?p=29-caching)), read replicas ([Ch 34](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases)), and denormalisation. Plan analysis is in [SQL · Execution Plans](../sql/topic.html?p=22-execution-plans) and [SQL · Query Optimization](../sql/topic.html?p=21-query-optimization); diagnosing a saturated database in [Database Design · Bottleneck Diagnosis](../database-design/topic.html?p=23-bottleneck-diagnosis).\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the N+1 query problem?**\nA: It's when code runs one query to fetch a list of N objects and then one additional query per object to fetch a related object or collection — N+1 queries in total. In Django it happens because related attributes are loaded lazily by descriptors on first access, so `for o in orders: o.customer.name` silently queries per row. Each query may be fast, but the total cost is N round trips, so latency grows linearly with page size. The fix is to load relations in bulk with `select_related` or `prefetch_related`, or avoid loading them by annotating.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `select_related` and `prefetch_related`?**\nA: `select_related` follows single-valued relations — forward ForeignKey and OneToOne (plus reverse OneToOne) — with SQL JOINs, so related objects come back in the same query. `prefetch_related` runs one additional query per relation using `WHERE fk IN (...)` on the collected keys and joins the results in Python; it handles multi-valued relations (ManyToMany, reverse ForeignKey) and GenericForeignKey, and can also be used for FKs. You can't JOIN a multi-valued relation without duplicating the parent rows, which is why prefetch exists. They combine: `select_related(\"customer\").prefetch_related(\"items__product\")`.\n\n**Q: How do you detect N+1 queries?**\nA: Locally, django-debug-toolbar's SQL panel shows the query count and flags similar/duplicated queries; `connection.queries` (with DEBUG) or `CaptureQueriesContext` lets you count in a shell. In tests, `assertNumQueries` or pytest-django's `django_assert_num_queries` — ideally run with two different data sizes to prove the count is constant. In production, APM tools flag repeated spans, and `pg_stat_statements` shows a primary-key query with an enormous call count. Logging `django.db.backends` at DEBUG in development also makes repeated queries obvious.\n\n**Q: Why might `prefetch_related` not reduce the number of queries?**\nA: Because the code accesses the relation in a way that bypasses the prefetch cache. Calling `.filter()`, `.exclude()`, `.order_by()`, `.first()` or `.latest()` on a related manager builds a new QuerySet and queries the database, even though `.all()` would have been served from cache. The fix is to move that filtering into a `Prefetch(\"items\", queryset=..., to_attr=\"...\")`. Another cause is a prefetched relation whose children then access their own unprefetched FK — nest the lookup (`items__product`) or add `select_related` inside the Prefetch queryset.\n\n**Q: What does the `Prefetch` object give you over a string lookup?**\nA: It lets you supply a custom queryset for the related objects — filtering, ordering, `select_related`, `only`, annotations — and with `to_attr` store the result as a list under a new attribute. That allows prefetching the same relation twice with different filters, makes it impossible to accidentally re-query via a manager, and since Django 4.2 supports sliced querysets for \"top N children per parent\". The string form always prefetches all related rows with the default manager's queryset.\n\n**Q: What's the difference between `only()` and `defer()`, and what's the risk?**\nA: `only(\"a\", \"b\")` loads just those fields (plus the primary key); `defer(\"c\")` loads everything except `c`. Both return model instances whose missing fields are deferred. The risk is that accessing a deferred field triggers a query per instance — reintroducing N+1 — and that this breaks silently when someone adds a field to a template or serializer. Use them for genuinely large columns and back them with query-count tests; if you don't need model behaviour, `values()` is simpler.\n\n**Q: What are the caveats of `bulk_create` and `bulk_update`?**\nA: Both bypass `Model.save()`, so custom save logic doesn't run, and they don't send `pre_save`/`post_save` signals. `bulk_create` sets primary keys on PostgreSQL (and newer SQLite/MariaDB) but not on all backends, doesn't work with multi-table inheritance children, and supports `ignore_conflicts` or, since 4.1, `update_conflicts` with `unique_fields`/`update_fields` for upserts. `bulk_update` builds a `CASE WHEN` per field, which gets slow for very large batches, so set `batch_size`. Neither calls `full_clean()`, so validate beforehand.\n\n**Q: When should you use `QuerySet.update()` instead of `save()`?**\nA: When you're changing a set of rows the same way, or doing arithmetic on the current DB value. `update()` issues one `UPDATE … WHERE`, returns the number of rows matched, and with `F()` expressions (`stock=F(\"stock\") - 1`) is atomic at the row level, avoiding the read-modify-write race of `obj.stock -= 1; obj.save()`. The trade-off is that it skips `save()`, signals and `auto_now` fields. Use `save(update_fields=[...])` when you need per-instance logic but want a narrow UPDATE.\n\n**Q: What's the difference between `count()`, `len()` and `exists()`?**\nA: `count()` runs `SELECT COUNT(*)` unless the QuerySet is already evaluated, in which case it uses the cached length. `len(qs)` evaluates the whole QuerySet, fetching every row, then counts the list — wasteful unless you'll iterate anyway. `exists()` runs a `SELECT 1 … LIMIT 1`, the cheapest way to test presence. If you're going to loop over the results regardless, evaluating once and using `len()` saves a query.\n\n**Q: How would you export 10 million rows to CSV from Django without running out of memory? (Senior)**\nA: Avoid the result cache and model instances: `values_list(...).iterator(chunk_size=5000)` streams through a server-side cursor on PostgreSQL, and a `StreamingHttpResponse` or a background Celery task writing to object storage keeps memory flat. If PgBouncer runs in transaction mode, server-side cursors don't survive, so use keyset pagination instead — `filter(id__gt=last).order_by(\"id\")[:5000]` in a loop — which is also resumable. Run it against a read replica, and for very large exports consider PostgreSQL `COPY` via the driver. I'd also make it asynchronous with a progress record rather than holding an HTTP request open.\n\n**Q: A list endpoint has p95 of 1.2 s. Walk me through how you'd find and fix the cause. (Senior)**\nA: First measure: the APM trace or debug toolbar tells me whether time is in many queries, one slow query, or Python. If I see dozens of near-identical queries, it's N+1 — I trace which serializer field or template access triggers it and add `select_related`/`Prefetch` in `get_queryset`, or replace per-row counts with annotations. If one query dominates, I run `explain(analyze=True)` and look for sequential scans, sort spills or bad join orders, then add an index or rewrite (e.g. `Exists` instead of `distinct()`). If SQL is fast but Python is slow, I reduce instantiated objects and columns with `only`/`values`. Finally I pin the query count with a test and watch the p95 after deploy.\n\n**Q: When can `select_related` be worse than `prefetch_related` for a ForeignKey? (Senior)**\nA: When the related rows are wide and heavily shared. A JOIN repeats the related columns on every parent row — 10,000 order rows referencing 20 merchants with big JSON settings means transferring those settings 10,000 times — while prefetch fetches each distinct merchant once with a small `IN` query. Many joins also increase planning complexity and can lead to poor join orders. Also, `select_related` on a nullable FK uses a LEFT OUTER JOIN, which can prevent some optimizations. So for wide, low-cardinality relations, prefetch (or `only()` on the joined fields) can win; measure with EXPLAIN and payload size.\n\n**Q: How do you prevent N+1 regressions across a team? (Senior)**\nA: Make query counts part of the contract: every list endpoint gets a test asserting a constant number of queries, run with at least two dataset sizes. Centralise queryset construction in `get_queryset()` or custom QuerySet methods (`Order.objects.for_list_api()`), so serializers and querysets change together. In CI or tests, tools such as `nplusone` can fail on lazy loads. In production, APM N+1 detection alerts and `pg_stat_statements` reviews catch what tests miss. Code review checklists should flag new relation accesses in serializers, `__str__` and templates.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Situation | Tool | SQL shape |\n|---|---|---|\n| FK / O2O per row | `select_related(\"fk\")` | JOIN (INNER, or LEFT if nullable) |\n| Reverse FK / M2M per row | `prefetch_related(\"rel\")` | + `WHERE fk IN (...)` |\n| Filtered/ordered children | `Prefetch(\"rel\", queryset=..., to_attr=\"x\")` | + filtered IN query |\n| N children per parent | `Prefetch(queryset=qs[:3])` (4.2+) | ROW_NUMBER window |\n| Only count of children | `annotate(n=Count(\"rel\"))` | GROUP BY |\n| Fewer columns | `only()` / `defer()` / `values()` | narrower SELECT |\n| Huge scans | `iterator(chunk_size=2000)` | server-side cursor |\n| Many inserts | `bulk_create(batch_size=...)` | multi-row INSERT |\n| Many different updates | `bulk_update(objs, fields)` | UPDATE … CASE WHEN |\n| Same update for a set | `filter(...).update(f=F(\"f\")+1)` | single UPDATE |\n| Presence | `exists()` | SELECT 1 … LIMIT 1 |\n| Plan | `qs.explain(analyze=True)` | EXPLAIN ANALYZE |\n\n- N+1 = constant per-row queries; the test is \"query count independent of row count\".\n- `select_related` = JOIN for single-valued; `prefetch_related` = IN query for multi-valued.\n- Manager `.filter()`/`.order_by()`/`.first()` after prefetch = new query.\n- `to_attr` lists can't re-query by accident.\n- `only()` + deferred access = new N+1; include FKs needed for stitching.\n- `bulk_*` and `update()` skip `save()` and signals.\n- Pass QuerySets, not lists, into `__in`.\n- `iterator()` vs PgBouncer transaction mode: `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS`.\n- Pin every list endpoint with `assertNumQueries`.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" \"psycopg[binary]\" django-debug-toolbar djangorestframework`, create the §5.1 models, and seed 200 orders × 3 items with 30 products and 10 tags.\n\n1. Render an order list template showing customer email, coupon code, item names and tags. Count queries with the debug toolbar. Fix to ≤ 3 queries.\n2. Write `assertNumQueries` tests for the page with 5 and with 50 orders; prove the count is identical.\n3. Introduce `o.items.filter(quantity__gt=1)` in the template and watch the prefetch break; fix with `Prefetch(to_attr=...)`.\n4. Use `only(\"id\", \"name\")` on products and access `price` in a loop; count queries; then fix.\n5. Import 50,000 rows from a CSV three ways — `create()` loop, `bulk_create(batch_size=1000)`, and `bulk_create(update_conflicts=True, ...)` on a re-run — and time each.\n6. Export all orders with `iterator(chunk_size=2000)` while watching process memory (`tracemalloc` or `ps`); compare with `list(Order.objects.all())`.\n\n**Mini project:** build a DRF `OrderViewSet` with nested items, products, tags, customer and computed `item_count`/`total`, keep it at ≤ 4 queries per page regardless of page size, add a custom `OrderQuerySet.for_api()` method (see [Ch 15](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance)) holding the optimisations, and a test suite that fails if anyone reintroduces N+1.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships), [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) (laziness, result cache), [Ch 12 · Aggregation, Annotation, Subqueries](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries), [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency), [Ch 15 · Custom Managers, QuerySets & Model Inheritance](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance), [Ch 21 · DRF Serializers](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers), [Ch 29 · Caching in Django](topic.html?p=29-caching), [Ch 33 · Testing](topic.html?p=33-testing), [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases).\n\n**Other handbooks:** [SQL · Joins](../sql/topic.html?p=05-joins), [SQL · Indexes](../sql/topic.html?p=19-indexes), [SQL · Query Optimization](../sql/topic.html?p=21-query-optimization), [SQL · Execution Plans](../sql/topic.html?p=22-execution-plans), [Database Design · Bottleneck Diagnosis](../database-design/topic.html?p=23-bottleneck-diagnosis).\n\n- **Database access optimization** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · the official checklist: profile first, use QuerySets correctly, do work in the DB, retrieve only what you need. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/optimization/>\n- **QuerySet API reference** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · exact semantics of select_related, prefetch_related, Prefetch, only/defer, bulk_*, iterator, explain. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/querysets/>\n- **django-debug-toolbar** — GitHub · *Beginner* · the SQL panel is the fastest way to see N+1 locally. <https://github.com/django-commons/django-debug-toolbar>\n- **nplusone** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · detects lazy loads and unused eager loads in Django tests. <https://github.com/jmcarp/nplusone>\n- **Testing tools: assertNumQueries** — Django docs · *Beginner* · pinning query counts in tests. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/testing/tools/>\n- **PostgreSQL: Using EXPLAIN** — PostgreSQL docs · *Intermediate* · reading the plans `QuerySet.explain()` returns. <https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/using-explain.html>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 13.*\n", "14-transactions-concurrency": "# 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions\n\n> **In one line:** Django runs in autocommit, so every query is its own transaction until you say otherwise; `transaction.atomic()` groups work (nesting with savepoints), `on_commit` defers side effects until the data is durable, and correctness under concurrency comes from pushing the decision into the database — `F()` updates, `select_for_update()` row locks, version checks and unique constraints — never from \"read in Python, then write\".\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) · [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization)\n\nConsider the code almost every e-commerce project writes in week one:\n\n```python\ndef buy(request, product_id):\n    product = Product.objects.get(pk=product_id)\n    if product.stock > 0:\n        product.stock -= 1\n        product.save()\n        Order.objects.create(user=request.user, product=product)\n        send_confirmation_email.delay(request.user.id)\n    ...\n```\n\nIt has four bugs, none of which show up in development:\n\n1. **Lost update.** Two requests read `stock = 1` at the same time, both pass the check, both write `stock = 0`. You sold two items and recorded one decrement.\n2. **Oversell.** Same race, viewed from the business: the check-then-act is not atomic.\n3. **Partial failure.** If `Order.objects.create` raises after `product.save()` committed (autocommit!), stock is decremented with no order.\n4. **Phantom side effect.** The email task is enqueued before anything is guaranteed committed; if a later step rolls back, the customer gets a confirmation for an order that doesn't exist — or the worker runs before the commit and can't find the row.\n\nEach has a precise fix: `atomic()` for (3), `on_commit()` for (4), and for (1)/(2) either an atomic conditional update (`filter(stock__gt=0).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - 1)`) or a row lock (`select_for_update()`). This chapter is about knowing which fix applies, what SQL it produces, and what new failure modes (deadlocks, lock waits, serialization errors) it introduces.\n\n> **Why this matters:** Interviewers love concurrency because it separates people who have run Django under load from those who haven't. Expect: \"what does `atomic` do when nested?\", \"what's `select_for_update` and why must it be inside a transaction?\", \"how do you prevent two users booking the same seat?\", \"why enqueue Celery tasks with `on_commit`?\".\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Autocommit** — Django's default: each SQL statement is committed immediately unless inside `atomic()`. *Why:* `save()` followed by another `save()` is two independent transactions; a crash between them leaves half the work.\n- **`transaction.atomic(using=None, savepoint=True, durable=False)`** — decorator or context manager; outermost block opens a transaction, inner blocks create **savepoints**. *Why:* all-or-nothing units of work, composable across functions.\n- **Savepoint** — a named point inside a transaction you can roll back to without aborting the whole transaction. *Why:* lets an inner failure be recovered while the outer work continues.\n- **`durable=True`** (3.2+) — raises `RuntimeError` if the block is nested in another atomic. *Why:* guarantees \"when this block exits, it's committed\" (e.g. payment ledger writes).\n- **`ATOMIC_REQUESTS`** — per-database setting wrapping each view in `atomic()`; opt out with `@transaction.non_atomic_requests`. *Why:* simple safety net, but holds a transaction for the entire view, including slow external calls.\n- **`transaction.on_commit(func, using=None, robust=False)`** — register a callback to run after the outermost transaction commits; discarded on rollback. `robust=True` (4.2+) logs and continues if the callback raises. *Why:* the correct place to enqueue Celery tasks, send emails, publish events.\n- **`transaction.set_rollback(True)`** — mark the current atomic block for rollback without raising. *Why:* lets you return a response while still discarding work.\n- **Race condition** — correctness depends on interleaving of concurrent operations. *Why:* Gunicorn workers, Celery workers and multiple pods all run concurrently against one database.\n- **Lost update** — two read-modify-write cycles overlap; one write overwrites the other. *Why:* the most common race in Django code (`obj.x += 1; obj.save()`).\n- **`F()` expression update** — `update(x=F(\"x\") + 1)` computes in SQL from the current row value. *Why:* the database serializes row updates, so no update is lost.\n- **`select_for_update(nowait=False, skip_locked=False, of=(), no_key=False)`** — `SELECT … FOR UPDATE` row locks held until the transaction ends. *Why:* pessimistic locking for check-then-act across several statements.\n- **Optimistic locking** — read a `version`, update `WHERE version = v`, treat 0 rows updated as a conflict. *Why:* no locks held while the user thinks; detects concurrent edits.\n- **Isolation level** — READ COMMITTED (PostgreSQL default, and Django's), REPEATABLE READ, SERIALIZABLE. *Why:* determines which anomalies the DB prevents; higher levels raise serialization errors you must retry.\n- **Deadlock** — two transactions each wait for a lock the other holds; PostgreSQL detects it and aborts one. *Why:* the price of row locking; prevented by consistent lock order.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 How `atomic()` works internally\n\n`transaction.atomic` returns an `Atomic` object (a `ContextDecorator`). In `__enter__` it looks at `connection.in_atomic_block`:\n\n- **Outermost block:** sets `connection.set_autocommit(False)` (the driver will `BEGIN` on the next statement), sets `in_atomic_block = True`, and starts collecting `on_commit` callbacks in `connection.run_on_commit`.\n- **Nested block:** if `savepoint=True` (default), calls `connection.savepoint()` which emits `SAVEPOINT \"s140234_x1\"` and pushes the id onto `connection.savepoint_ids`.\n\nIn `__exit__`:\n\n- **No exception, nested:** `RELEASE SAVEPOINT …`.\n- **No exception, outermost:** `connection.commit()`, then run the `on_commit` callbacks, then restore autocommit.\n- **Exception, nested:** `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT …` and re-raise.\n- **Exception, outermost:** `connection.rollback()`, discard callbacks, re-raise.\n\n`connection.needs_rollback` is the flag that ties it together: if a database error happens and isn't handled by a nested atomic, the connection is marked broken, and any further query in that atomic block raises `TransactionManagementError: An error occurred in the current transaction. You can't execute queries until the end of the 'atomic' block.` PostgreSQL itself refuses statements in an aborted transaction (`current transaction is aborted, commands ignored until end of transaction block`), and Django surfaces it early.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj14a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nested atomic(): savepoints, partial rollback, and on_commit</text>\n  <path d=\"M30,60 L850,60\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj14a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"845\" y=\"52\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">time</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"76\" width=\"800\" height=\"230\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"56\" y=\"96\" fill=\"#1e3a8a\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">with atomic():   (outer &#8212; BEGIN)</text>\n  <rect x=\"60\" y=\"108\" width=\"150\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"135\" y=\"127\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">INSERT order</text>\n  <rect x=\"230\" y=\"108\" width=\"270\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"244\" y=\"126\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">try: with atomic():  SAVEPOINT s1</text>\n  <rect x=\"244\" y=\"136\" width=\"110\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"299\" y=\"153\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">INSERT coupon_use</text>\n  <rect x=\"368\" y=\"136\" width=\"120\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"428\" y=\"153\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">IntegrityError!</text>\n  <text x=\"244\" y=\"182\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT s1</text>\n  <text x=\"244\" y=\"200\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">except IntegrityError: (outside inner block)</text>\n  <rect x=\"520\" y=\"108\" width=\"150\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"595\" y=\"127\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">UPDATE stock (F)</text>\n  <rect x=\"520\" y=\"150\" width=\"150\" height=\"40\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"595\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#4c1d95\" font-size=\"10\">on_commit(send_email)</text>\n  <text x=\"595\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#4c1d95\" font-size=\"9\">queued, not run</text>\n  <rect x=\"690\" y=\"108\" width=\"135\" height=\"82\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"757\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">COMMIT</text>\n  <text x=\"757\" y=\"146\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">order + stock durable</text>\n  <text x=\"757\" y=\"164\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">coupon_use absent</text>\n  <text x=\"757\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7c3aed\" font-size=\"10\">then send_email runs</text>\n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"246\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Outer block survives because the inner failure was contained by its own savepoint.</text>\n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"264\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">If the outer block raised instead: ROLLBACK everything, and the on_commit callback is discarded &#8212; no email.</text>\n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"282\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Callbacks registered inside a rolled-back savepoint are discarded too.</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"318\" width=\"800\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"56\" y=\"338\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">Anti-pattern: catching the DB error INSIDE the atomic block without a nested atomic</text>\n  <text x=\"56\" y=\"358\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"10\">with atomic(): try: create_dup() except IntegrityError: pass;  Order.objects.count()</text>\n  <text x=\"56\" y=\"376\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"10\">&#8594; TransactionManagementError: the transaction is aborted; no savepoint existed to roll back to.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.2 The lost update, and the three fixes\n\nUnder READ COMMITTED, each statement sees data committed before *that statement* began. Two transactions doing `SELECT stock` then `UPDATE … SET stock = <python value>` both read 1 and both write 0. The database did nothing wrong: you told it to set `stock = 0` twice.\n\n**Fix 1 — atomic SQL update.** `Product.objects.filter(pk=pid, stock__gt=0).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - 1)` compiles to `UPDATE product SET stock = stock - 1 WHERE id = 42 AND stock > 0`. PostgreSQL takes a row lock for the UPDATE; the second transaction waits, then **re-evaluates the WHERE clause against the new row version** (READ COMMITTED's \"EvalPlanQual\" re-check), sees `stock = 0`, and updates zero rows. The return value (rows affected) tells you whether you won. One statement, no explicit lock, no race. Use it whenever the decision fits in a WHERE clause.\n\n**Fix 2 — pessimistic lock.** `select_for_update()` makes the *read* take the row lock: `SELECT … FOR UPDATE`. The second transaction blocks on its SELECT until the first commits, then reads the fresh value. Use it when the decision needs Python logic or several rows/tables (check balance, check limits, write ledger entries, update status).\n\n**Fix 3 — optimistic lock.** Don't lock; detect. Store a `version` integer, update `WHERE id = 42 AND version = 7` setting `version = 8`; zero rows means someone else changed it — reload and retry or report a conflict. Use it when conflicts are rare and the read happens long before the write (an edit form, an API PATCH with an ETag; see [REST API · Concurrency & Optimistic Locking](../rest-api/topic.html?p=28-concurrency-optimistic-locking)).\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 420\" width=\"100%\" height=\"420\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj14b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj14c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Two buyers, one item in stock</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"410\" height=\"360\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Read-modify-write in Python (lost update)</text>\n  <text x=\"70\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-weight=\"bold\">Worker A</text>\n  <text x=\"380\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-weight=\"bold\">Worker B</text>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"98\" width=\"150\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT stock &#8594; 1</text>\n  <rect x=\"270\" y=\"134\" width=\"150\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"345\" y=\"151\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT stock &#8594; 1</text>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"170\" width=\"150\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"187\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">1 &gt; 0, stock = 0</text>\n  <rect x=\"270\" y=\"206\" width=\"150\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"345\" y=\"223\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">1 &gt; 0, stock = 0</text>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"242\" width=\"150\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"259\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">UPDATE SET stock=0</text>\n  <rect x=\"270\" y=\"278\" width=\"150\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"345\" y=\"295\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">UPDATE SET stock=0</text>\n  <path d=\"M182,111 L268,141\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"4,3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj14b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"340\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">2 orders created, stock 0: oversold by one</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"362\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">atomic() alone does NOT fix this under READ COMMITTED:</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">plain SELECT takes no lock.</text>\n  <rect x=\"450\" y=\"40\" width=\"410\" height=\"360\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">select_for_update() inside atomic()</text>\n  <text x=\"500\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">Worker A</text>\n  <text x=\"810\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-weight=\"bold\">Worker B</text>\n  <rect x=\"460\" y=\"98\" width=\"170\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"545\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT ... FOR UPDATE &#8594; 1</text>\n  <rect x=\"680\" y=\"134\" width=\"170\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/><text x=\"765\" y=\"151\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</text><text x=\"765\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"10\">BLOCKED on row lock</text>\n  <rect x=\"460\" y=\"184\" width=\"170\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"545\" y=\"201\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">UPDATE stock=0, INSERT order</text>\n  <rect x=\"460\" y=\"220\" width=\"170\" height=\"26\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"545\" y=\"237\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">COMMIT (lock released)</text>\n  <path d=\"M632,233 L678,268\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj14c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"680\" y=\"256\" width=\"170\" height=\"40\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"765\" y=\"273\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">unblocks, reads stock = 0</text><text x=\"765\" y=\"288\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">\"sold out\" &#8594; no order</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"330\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">Correct: exactly one order</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"352\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">Equivalent single statement: UPDATE ... SET stock = stock - 1</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"370\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">WHERE id = 42 AND stock &gt; 0   (check rowcount)</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.3 Isolation levels in Django\n\nDjango uses the database's default — READ COMMITTED on PostgreSQL. You can change it per database:\n\n```python\n# settings.py  (psycopg 3)\nfrom psycopg import IsolationLevel\nDATABASES = {\n    \"default\": {\n        \"ENGINE\": \"django.db.backends.postgresql\",\n        # ...\n        \"OPTIONS\": {\"isolation_level\": IsolationLevel.SERIALIZABLE},\n    }\n}\n```\n\nAt REPEATABLE READ and SERIALIZABLE, PostgreSQL never lets you silently overwrite a concurrent change; instead it aborts with a **serialization failure** (`could not serialize access due to concurrent update`, SQLSTATE `40001`), surfaced as `django.db.OperationalError`. That's safer, but every transaction must then be **retried** on that error — which means your atomic blocks must be idempotent and must not trigger external side effects before commit (another reason for `on_commit`). Most Django apps stay on READ COMMITTED and use targeted `F()`/`select_for_update()`/constraints. Background: [SQL · Isolation Levels](../sql/topic.html?p=26-isolation-levels), [Database Design · Isolation Deep Dive](../database-design/topic.html?p=06-isolation-deep-dive), [Database Design · MVCC](../database-design/topic.html?p=03-mvcc).\n\n### 3.4 What `select_for_update` really locks\n\n- It must be **evaluated inside `atomic()`** (or with autocommit disabled); evaluating it in autocommit raises `TransactionManagementError: select_for_update cannot be used outside of a transaction.` Without a transaction the lock would be released immediately after the SELECT, so Django refuses.\n- The lock is acquired when the QuerySet is **evaluated**, not when it's built; it's released at **COMMIT/ROLLBACK** of the outermost transaction — not at the end of the inner `atomic` block.\n- With `select_related`, PostgreSQL locks rows of **all joined tables** unless you restrict it with `of=(\"self\",)` (or names of related fields). Locking the nullable side of an outer join isn't allowed on PostgreSQL; use `of=` to exclude it.\n- `nowait=True` → raise `DatabaseError` immediately if the row is locked. `skip_locked=True` → silently skip locked rows (job queues). `no_key=True` → `FOR NO KEY UPDATE`, a weaker lock that doesn't block inserts of rows with an FK to the locked row — usually what you want when you're not changing the primary key.\n- Under `TestCase` everything runs inside a transaction, so `select_for_update` \"works\" in tests even if your production code forgot `atomic()`. Use `TransactionTestCase` to catch that.\n\nThe PostgreSQL lock modes are covered in [SQL · Locking & MVCC](../sql/topic.html?p=27-locking-mvcc) and [Database Design · Locking Internals](../database-design/topic.html?p=05-locking-internals).\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 Where side effects belong\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 300\" width=\"100%\" height=\"300\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj14d\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">task.delay() inside vs after the transaction</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"400\" height=\"230\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">delay() inside atomic()</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"76\" width=\"130\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">INSERT order 99</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"112\" width=\"130\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">task.delay(99)</text>\n  <path d=\"M172,126 L246,126\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj14d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"250\" y=\"112\" width=\"150\" height=\"44\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"325\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">worker: Order.get(99)</text>\n  <text x=\"325\" y=\"146\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">DoesNotExist (not committed)</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"148\" width=\"130\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">charge fails &#8594; ROLLBACK</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"206\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">Two failures: the worker may run before COMMIT,</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"224\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">and after ROLLBACK the task still runs for an</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"242\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">order that never existed.</text>\n  <rect x=\"460\" y=\"40\" width=\"400\" height=\"230\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"660\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">transaction.on_commit(lambda: task.delay(99))</text>\n  <rect x=\"480\" y=\"76\" width=\"130\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"545\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">INSERT order 99</text>\n  <rect x=\"480\" y=\"112\" width=\"130\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"545\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">register callback</text>\n  <rect x=\"480\" y=\"148\" width=\"130\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"545\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">COMMIT</text>\n  <path d=\"M612,162 L686,162\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj14d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"690\" y=\"140\" width=\"150\" height=\"44\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"765\" y=\"158\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">then delay(99)</text>\n  <text x=\"765\" y=\"174\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">worker finds the row</text>\n  <text x=\"480\" y=\"206\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">On ROLLBACK the callback is dropped. Remaining gap:</text>\n  <text x=\"480\" y=\"224\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">process dies between COMMIT and delay() &#8594; task lost.</text>\n  <text x=\"480\" y=\"242\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">If that matters, use a transactional outbox table.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nThe remaining gap — commit succeeded, process crashed before the broker accepted the task — is the dual-write problem. If losing that message is unacceptable (payments, ledger events), write an outbox row in the same transaction and relay it; see [Messaging · Idempotency & the Outbox Pattern](../messaging/topic.html?p=21-idempotency-outbox) and [Ch 31 · Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks).\n\n### 4.2 Choosing a concurrency strategy\n\n```text\nIs the change expressible as one UPDATE with a WHERE condition?\n   yes -> filter(cond).update(col=F(col) +/- n); check rowcount          (no explicit lock)\n   no  -> Does the decision need several reads/rows, done within one request?\n            yes -> atomic() + select_for_update() (lock in a consistent order; no_key=True)\n            no  -> Long gap between read and write (forms, mobile edits)?\n                     yes -> optimistic version column / ETag + If-Match\nIs the invariant \"only one row like this may exist\"?\n   -> UniqueConstraint (+ get_or_create / IntegrityError handling) — the DB enforces it\nIs it a work queue (claim the next N jobs)?\n   -> select_for_update(skip_locked=True)\nIs it \"only one process may run this job\" (cron, migration, report)?\n   -> pg_advisory_xact_lock or a Redis lock with TTL\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example: `atomic` basics\n\n```python\nfrom django.db import IntegrityError, transaction\n\n# As a decorator: the whole function is one transaction\n@transaction.atomic\ndef transfer(src_id, dst_id, amount):\n    Account.objects.filter(pk=src_id).update(balance=F(\"balance\") - amount)\n    Account.objects.filter(pk=dst_id).update(balance=F(\"balance\") + amount)\n\n# As a context manager, with a recoverable inner step\ndef place_order(user, cart, coupon_code):\n    with transaction.atomic():\n        order = Order.objects.create(user=user, total=cart.total)\n        try:\n            with transaction.atomic():                 # savepoint\n                CouponUse.objects.create(order=order, code=coupon_code)   # unique (user, code)\n        except IntegrityError:\n            order.note = \"coupon already used\"         # outer transaction still healthy\n            order.save(update_fields=[\"note\"])\n        transaction.on_commit(lambda: send_receipt.delay(order.pk))\n    return order\n```\n\nThe SQL Django emits for `place_order` when the coupon is a duplicate:\n\n```sql\nBEGIN;\nINSERT INTO \"shop_order\" (\"user_id\", \"total\", \"note\") VALUES (7, 120.00, '') RETURNING \"shop_order\".\"id\";\nSAVEPOINT \"s139823_x1\";\nINSERT INTO \"shop_couponuse\" (\"order_id\", \"code\") VALUES (99, 'WELCOME');\n-- ERROR: duplicate key value violates unique constraint\nROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT \"s139823_x1\";\nUPDATE \"shop_order\" SET \"note\" = 'coupon already used' WHERE \"shop_order\".\"id\" = 99;\nCOMMIT;\n-- then: send_receipt.delay(99)\n```\n\nAnd the broken version — catching inside without a savepoint:\n\n```python\nwith transaction.atomic():\n    order = Order.objects.create(...)\n    try:\n        CouponUse.objects.create(order=order, code=code)\n    except IntegrityError:\n        pass\n    order.save()     # TransactionManagementError\n```\n\n### 5.2 Atomic counters with `F()`\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models import F\n\n# Increment safely\nArticle.objects.filter(pk=aid).update(views=F(\"views\") + 1)\n\n# On an instance: save() writes the expression, not a value\narticle.views = F(\"views\") + 1\narticle.save(update_fields=[\"views\"])\narticle.refresh_from_db(fields=[\"views\"])     # otherwise article.views is still the F() object\n\n# Conditional decrement (oversell-proof) — the WHERE clause is the guard\nsold = Product.objects.filter(pk=pid, stock__gte=qty).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - qty)\nif sold == 0:\n    raise OutOfStock()\n```\n\n```sql\nUPDATE \"shop_product\" SET \"stock\" = (\"shop_product\".\"stock\" - 2)\nWHERE (\"shop_product\".\"id\" = 42 AND \"shop_product\".\"stock\" >= 2);\n```\n\nPair it with a database constraint so even buggy code paths can't go negative: `models.CheckConstraint(condition=Q(stock__gte=0), name=\"stock_non_negative\")` (in 5.1+ the kwarg is `condition`; `check=` is deprecated). See [Ch 16](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres).\n\n### 5.3 Real-world example: seat booking with `select_for_update`\n\nRequirements: a user holds up to 4 seats for a show; a seat can be held by one user; holds expire after 10 minutes; payments are confirmed later. The decision involves several rows (the seats, the user's existing holds), so a single UPDATE doesn't fit.\n\n```python\n# booking/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.db.models import Q\n\nclass Seat(models.Model):\n    class Status(models.TextChoices):\n        FREE = \"free\"; HELD = \"held\"; SOLD = \"sold\"\n    show = models.ForeignKey(\"Show\", on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"seats\")\n    label = models.CharField(max_length=8)\n    status = models.CharField(max_length=4, choices=Status.choices, default=Status.FREE)\n    held_by = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, null=True, blank=True, on_delete=models.SET_NULL)\n    held_until = models.DateTimeField(null=True, blank=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [\n            models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"show\", \"label\"], name=\"seat_unique_label\"),\n            models.CheckConstraint(\n                condition=Q(status=\"free\") | Q(held_by__isnull=False),\n                name=\"seat_held_has_holder\",\n            ),\n        ]\n```\n\n```python\n# booking/services.py\nfrom datetime import timedelta\nfrom django.db import transaction, DatabaseError\nfrom django.db.models import Q\nfrom django.utils import timezone\nfrom .models import Seat\n\nMAX_SEATS = 4\n\nclass SeatUnavailable(Exception): ...\nclass TooManySeats(Exception): ...\nclass TryAgain(Exception): ...\n\ndef hold_seats(user, show_id, labels):\n    now = timezone.now()\n    try:\n        with transaction.atomic():\n            seats = list(\n                Seat.objects.select_for_update(nowait=True, no_key=True)\n                .filter(show_id=show_id, label__in=labels)\n                .order_by(\"pk\")                 # consistent lock order prevents deadlocks\n            )\n            if len(seats) != len(set(labels)):\n                raise SeatUnavailable(\"unknown seat\")\n            for s in seats:\n                expired = s.status == Seat.Status.HELD and s.held_until and s.held_until < now\n                if s.status != Seat.Status.FREE and not expired:\n                    raise SeatUnavailable(s.label)\n\n            already = (Seat.objects.filter(show_id=show_id, held_by=user, status=Seat.Status.HELD,\n                                           held_until__gte=now)\n                       .exclude(pk__in=[s.pk for s in seats]).count())\n            if already + len(seats) > MAX_SEATS:\n                raise TooManySeats()\n\n            until = now + timedelta(minutes=10)\n            Seat.objects.filter(pk__in=[s.pk for s in seats]).update(\n                status=Seat.Status.HELD, held_by=user, held_until=until)\n            transaction.on_commit(lambda: expire_hold.apply_async((show_id, [s.pk for s in seats]),\n                                                                  eta=until))\n            return seats\n    except DatabaseError as exc:                 # nowait: someone else holds the lock right now\n        if \"could not obtain lock\" in str(exc):\n            raise TryAgain() from exc\n        raise\n```\n\n```sql\nBEGIN;\nSELECT \"booking_seat\".\"id\", \"booking_seat\".\"show_id\", \"booking_seat\".\"label\", ...\nFROM \"booking_seat\"\nWHERE (\"booking_seat\".\"label\" IN ('A1', 'A2') AND \"booking_seat\".\"show_id\" = 3)\nORDER BY \"booking_seat\".\"id\" ASC\nFOR NO KEY UPDATE NOWAIT;\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS \"__count\" FROM \"booking_seat\" WHERE (... \"held_by_id\" = 7 ...);\nUPDATE \"booking_seat\" SET \"status\" = 'held', \"held_by_id\" = 7, \"held_until\" = '...' WHERE \"booking_seat\".\"id\" IN (11, 12);\nCOMMIT;\n```\n\nNotes on the design:\n\n- `order_by(\"pk\")` makes every transaction lock seats in the same order, so two users grabbing overlapping seat sets can't deadlock each other.\n- `nowait=True` turns a contended seat into an instant \"try again\" instead of a request hanging on a lock — good UX on a hot show. Plain blocking is fine for low-contention cases.\n- The user-limit check (`already`) reads rows that aren't locked; two concurrent requests by the same user for different seats could both pass. If that matters, lock a per-user-per-show row (e.g. a `Cart` row) first — always lock a **single parent row** to serialize a user's actions.\n- The DB constraints are the last line of defence.\n\n### 5.4 A job queue with `skip_locked`\n\n```python\ndef claim_jobs(worker_id, n=10):\n    with transaction.atomic():\n        jobs = list(\n            Job.objects.select_for_update(skip_locked=True)\n            .filter(status=\"queued\").order_by(\"priority\", \"id\")[:n]\n        )\n        Job.objects.filter(pk__in=[j.pk for j in jobs]).update(status=\"running\", worker=worker_id)\n    return jobs\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"jobs_job\" WHERE \"jobs_job\".\"status\" = 'queued'\nORDER BY \"jobs_job\".\"priority\" ASC, \"jobs_job\".\"id\" ASC LIMIT 10 FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED;\n```\n\nTen workers running this concurrently each get a disjoint batch without waiting on each other.\n\n### 5.5 Optimistic locking with a version column\n\n```python\nclass Document(models.Model):\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    body = models.TextField()\n    version = models.PositiveIntegerField(default=1)\n\nclass Conflict(Exception): ...\n\ndef save_document(doc_id, expected_version, **changes):\n    updated = Document.objects.filter(pk=doc_id, version=expected_version).update(\n        **changes, version=F(\"version\") + 1)\n    if updated == 0:\n        raise Conflict(\"document changed since you loaded it\")\n```\n\nIn an API, return the version as an `ETag` and require `If-Match`; respond `412 Precondition Failed` on conflict.\n\n### 5.6 `get_or_create` and unique races\n\n`get_or_create(**lookup)` does `get()`, and on `DoesNotExist` tries `create()` inside a savepoint; if that raises `IntegrityError`, it retries the `get()`. This is race-safe **only if a unique constraint covers the lookup fields** — without one, two concurrent calls both create and you get duplicates (and later `MultipleObjectsReturned`). `update_or_create` locks the existing row with `select_for_update()` inside its own `atomic()`, but the *create* branch has the same requirement: without a unique constraint, two callers can both insert. Always back \"only one\" invariants with `UniqueConstraint`.\n\n### 5.7 Testing transactional behaviour\n\n```python\nfrom django.test import TestCase, TransactionTestCase\n\nclass ReceiptTests(TestCase):\n    def test_receipt_enqueued_on_commit(self):\n        with self.captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True) as callbacks:\n            place_order(self.user, self.cart, \"WELCOME\")\n        self.assertEqual(len(callbacks), 1)\n\nclass OversellTests(TransactionTestCase):           # real commits, real locks\n    def test_two_buyers_one_item(self):\n        import threading\n        from django.db import connection\n        product = Product.objects.create(stock=1)\n        results = []\n        def buy():\n            try:\n                results.append(buy_one(product.pk))\n            finally:\n                connection.close()                   # each thread has its own connection\n        threads = [threading.Thread(target=buy) for _ in range(2)]\n        [t.start() for t in threads]; [t.join() for t in threads]\n        self.assertEqual(sorted(results), [False, True])\n```\n\n`TestCase` wraps each test in a transaction that is rolled back, so `on_commit` callbacks never fire unless you use `captureOnCommitCallbacks`, and missing `atomic()` around `select_for_update` isn't detected. See [Ch 33 · Testing](topic.html?p=33-testing).\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Strategy | Prevents | Cost | Failure mode | Best for |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `atomic()` | Partial writes | Holds locks/snapshot until commit | Long transactions block others | Any multi-write unit |\n| `ATOMIC_REQUESTS=True` | Partial writes per view | Transaction spans whole view (incl. HTTP calls, templates) | Pool exhaustion, lock hold time | Small apps; opt-out for heavy views |\n| `F()` conditional update | Lost update, oversell | None (one statement) | Logic limited to WHERE clause | Counters, stock, balances |\n| `select_for_update` | Check-then-act races across rows | Lock waits, deadlock risk | Blocked requests, deadlock errors | Bookings, transfers, state machines |\n| `skip_locked` | Double-claiming | Unfair ordering | Starvation under load | DB-backed job queues |\n| Optimistic version | Lost update over long gaps | Retry/merge UX | Conflicts under high contention | Edit forms, PATCH APIs |\n| `UniqueConstraint` | Duplicates | Index | IntegrityError to handle | \"Only one\" invariants |\n| SERIALIZABLE | All anomalies | Retries on 40001 | Retry storms | Complex invariants, low volume |\n| Advisory lock | Concurrent runs of a job | Session/txn bookkeeping | Lock leak with session locks + poolers | Singletons, cron jobs |\n\n### When to use\n\n- `atomic()` around every sequence of writes that must succeed together — usually in a service function, not the view.\n- `on_commit()` for every side effect outside the database (tasks, emails, webhooks, cache invalidation).\n- `F()` updates for every counter; `select_for_update` when the decision needs Python logic.\n- `durable=True` on functions that must commit independently (e.g. idempotency-key records).\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Don't hold a transaction (or a row lock) across network calls — payment providers, S3, external APIs. Do the external call before (with an idempotency key) or after (via `on_commit`/state machine).\n- Don't use `select_for_update` for simple counters — `F()` is cheaper.\n- Don't enable SERIALIZABLE globally without a retry wrapper.\n- Don't use `ATOMIC_REQUESTS` with streaming responses or long views; the transaction ends when the view returns, not when the stream finishes, and long views hold connections.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Believing `atomic()` prevents races.** It provides atomicity (all-or-nothing), not isolation from concurrent readers under READ COMMITTED. Two atomic blocks reading the same row without `FOR UPDATE` still race.\n\n**2. Catching `DatabaseError` inside atomic without a nested block.** Covered in §5.1. Wrap the risky statement in its own `atomic()` and catch *outside* it.\n\n**3. `select_for_update` outside a transaction.** Raises `TransactionManagementError` — but only when evaluated. Tests using `TestCase` hide it. Wrap in `atomic()`; use `TransactionTestCase` for this code.\n\n**4. Locking more than you think.** `select_for_update().select_related(\"customer\")` also locks the customer row, blocking every other order for that customer. Use `of=(\"self\",)`.\n\n**5. Inconsistent lock order → deadlocks.** Transfer A→B locks A then B; transfer B→A locks B then A. Always lock by sorted primary key:\n\n```python\nwith transaction.atomic():\n    a, b = sorted([src_id, dst_id])\n    accounts = {x.pk: x for x in Account.objects.select_for_update().filter(pk__in=[a, b]).order_by(\"pk\")}\n```\n\n**6. `obj.save()` after using F without refresh.** `obj.views` holds an `F` expression after save; rendering it shows `F(views) + Value(1)`, and saving again increments again. Call `refresh_from_db()`.\n\n**7. Full `save()` overwriting concurrent changes.** `save()` writes every field. If request A changed `email` and B changed `name` from stale copies, B's `save()` reverts A's email. Use `save(update_fields=[...])`.\n\n**8. Enqueuing tasks inside the transaction.** Use `transaction.on_commit(lambda: task.delay(obj.pk))` — or `task.delay_on_commit(...)` in Celery 5.4+.\n\n**9. Lambdas capturing loop variables.** `for o in orders: on_commit(lambda: notify.delay(o.pk))` notifies the last order N times. Use `functools.partial(notify.delay, o.pk)` or a default argument.\n\n**Best practices:** keep transactions short; put business transactions in a service layer; set `statement_timeout`/`lock_timeout` for the app role; back every invariant with a DB constraint; add idempotency keys to retryable endpoints ([REST API · Idempotency Keys](../rest-api/topic.html?p=27-idempotency-keys-retries)).\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: oversold flash sale.** *Symptom:* 1,000 units, 1,060 orders. *Root cause:* `if p.stock >= qty: p.stock -= qty; p.save()` inside `atomic()` — no lock, lost updates. *Fix:* conditional `F()` update + `CheckConstraint(stock >= 0)`; reconcile with a script that compares orders vs inventory movements.\n\n**Pitfall: connection pool exhausted during a payment outage.** With `ATOMIC_REQUESTS=True`, every checkout view held a transaction (and a pool connection) while waiting 30 s on the payment provider. *Symptom:* `OperationalError: too many connections` / pool timeouts on unrelated pages. *Fix:* `@transaction.non_atomic_requests` on the checkout view; short `atomic()` blocks before and after the external call; HTTP timeouts on the client.\n\n**Pitfall: deadlocks under load.** *Symptom:* Sentry shows `deadlock detected` sporadically from the transfer endpoint. *Root cause:* inconsistent lock order, or locking FK-referenced rows implicitly (inserting a child takes `FOR KEY SHARE` on the parent). *Fix:* sorted locking, `no_key=True` when not touching keys, retry once on deadlock (PostgreSQL aborts one side; a retry usually succeeds).\n\n**Pitfall: lock waits pile up.** A hot row (a global counter, a \"settings\" row) locked by every request serializes your whole site. *Symptom:* p99 latency equals queue length × transaction time. *Fix:* shard the counter (N rows, sum on read), move to Redis `INCR` ([Redis · Pipelining, Lua & Transactions](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=22-pipelining-lua-transactions)), or make it eventually consistent.\n\n**Pitfall: idle in transaction.** A Celery task opened `atomic()`, then called an API for minutes. `pg_stat_activity` shows `idle in transaction`; autovacuum can't clean dead tuples older than its snapshot; table bloat grows. *Fix:* set `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` on the role; restructure the task.\n\n**Pitfall: advisory session locks behind PgBouncer.** `pg_advisory_lock` (session-level) under transaction pooling leaks to another client's session. *Fix:* use `pg_advisory_xact_lock` (released at commit), or a Redis lock with TTL ([Redis · Distributed Locks](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=23-distributed-locks-redlock)).\n\n**Debugging:**\n\n```sql\n-- What is waiting on what?\nSELECT a.pid, a.state, now() - a.xact_start AS xact_age, pg_blocking_pids(a.pid) AS blocked_by,\n       left(a.query, 100) AS query\nFROM pg_stat_activity a\nWHERE a.datname = current_database() AND a.state <> 'idle'\nORDER BY xact_age DESC NULLS LAST;\n\n-- Long-running / idle-in-transaction sessions\nSELECT pid, state, now() - xact_start FROM pg_stat_activity WHERE state LIKE 'idle in transaction%';\n```\n\nEnable `log_lock_waits = on` and `deadlock_timeout` (default 1 s) in PostgreSQL to log waits longer than that. In Django, `connection.in_atomic_block` and `connection.savepoint_ids` tell you where you are; logging `django.db.backends` shows `BEGIN`/`SAVEPOINT`/`COMMIT`.\n\n**Scaling notes:** row locks scale with contention, not with traffic — design so hot spots are rare. Beyond one database, there are no cross-database transactions in Django (each alias has its own `atomic(using=...)`); use sagas/outbox ([System Design · Distributed Transactions](../system-design/topic.html?p=21-distributed-transactions)). Async views: transactions aren't supported in async ORM code yet — put the transactional unit in a sync function and call it with `sync_to_async` ([Ch 30](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi)).\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: How does Django handle transactions by default?**\nA: Django runs in autocommit mode: each query is committed as soon as it executes, unless it's inside a `transaction.atomic()` block. So two `save()` calls in a view are two separate transactions, and a failure between them leaves the first committed. You opt into a multi-statement transaction with `atomic()` — or for every view with `ATOMIC_REQUESTS = True`. Internally the outermost `atomic` turns autocommit off on the connection, and the commit or rollback happens when it exits.\n\n**Q: What happens when you nest `atomic()` blocks?**\nA: The outermost block starts a real transaction; each inner block creates a savepoint. If an inner block exits with an exception, Django rolls back to its savepoint and re-raises, so if you catch that exception outside the inner block, the outer transaction continues with everything else intact. If the outer block fails, everything rolls back, including work from inner blocks that \"succeeded\". Only the outermost exit commits; inner blocks just release their savepoints. `savepoint=False` skips the savepoint for performance, at the cost of losing partial rollback.\n\n**Q: Why shouldn't you catch a database exception inside an `atomic` block?**\nA: Because after a database error the transaction is in an aborted state — PostgreSQL rejects further statements until rollback — and Django marks the connection `needs_rollback`. If you catch the error and keep going in the same atomic block, the next query raises `TransactionManagementError`. Worse, on some databases you could commit an inconsistent state. The correct pattern is to wrap the risky operation in its own nested `atomic()` and catch the exception outside that inner block, so the savepoint rollback restores a healthy state.\n\n**Q: What is `transaction.on_commit` and why use it for Celery tasks?**\nA: It registers a callable to run after the outermost transaction successfully commits; if the transaction rolls back, the callback is discarded. If you call `task.delay(order.id)` inside a transaction, the worker may pick it up before commit and not find the row, or the transaction may roll back after the task was already queued. `transaction.on_commit(lambda: task.delay(order.id))` avoids both. It doesn't close the gap where the process crashes after commit but before enqueueing — for that you need an outbox.\n\n**Q: What does `select_for_update()` do, and why must it run inside a transaction?**\nA: It adds `FOR UPDATE` to the SELECT, so the database locks the returned rows; other transactions trying to lock or update them wait until your transaction ends. Locks are released at commit or rollback, so in autocommit mode the lock would be released immediately after the SELECT and protect nothing — Django therefore raises `TransactionManagementError` if it's evaluated outside `atomic()`. Options: `nowait` to fail fast, `skip_locked` to skip locked rows, `of` to limit which joined tables are locked, and `no_key` for the weaker `FOR NO KEY UPDATE`.\n\n**Q: How do you increment a counter safely under concurrency?**\nA: Let the database do the arithmetic with an `F()` expression: `Post.objects.filter(pk=pk).update(likes=F(\"likes\") + 1)`. That compiles to `UPDATE … SET likes = likes + 1`, and since the database applies row updates one at a time with row locks, no increment is lost. The naive `post.likes += 1; post.save()` reads in Python and writes back a stale value. If you use `F()` on an instance and save, call `refresh_from_db()` afterwards because the attribute still holds the expression.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between optimistic and pessimistic locking, and how do you implement each in Django?**\nA: Pessimistic locking assumes conflict and prevents it: `select_for_update()` inside `atomic()` locks rows while you decide, and others wait. Optimistic locking assumes conflicts are rare and detects them: keep a `version` field, update with `filter(pk=pk, version=v).update(..., version=F(\"version\")+1)`, and treat zero affected rows as a conflict to retry or report. Pessimistic suits short, contended operations inside one request (seat booking, transfers); optimistic suits long user think-time between read and write (edit forms, PATCH with ETags), where holding a lock is impossible.\n\n**Q: Is `get_or_create` race-safe?**\nA: Only when the lookup fields are covered by a unique constraint. It tries `get()`, then `create()` in a savepoint, and on `IntegrityError` retries `get()` — so the database's unique index is what actually decides the race. Without a unique constraint, two concurrent calls can both miss the `get` and both insert, leaving duplicates that later cause `MultipleObjectsReturned`. The same applies to `update_or_create`.\n\n**Q: What does `ATOMIC_REQUESTS` do and what are its downsides?**\nA: It wraps each view call in a transaction on that database, committing if the view returns a response and rolling back if it raises. It's a simple guarantee that a view's writes are all-or-nothing. Downsides: the transaction spans the whole view, including slow external calls and template rendering, which holds locks and pool connections longer; it doesn't cover middleware or streaming response generation; and it can hide the need to design proper transactional boundaries. Use `@transaction.non_atomic_requests` to opt out for specific views.\n\n**Q: How would you design a checkout that never oversells, even during a flash sale? (Senior)**\nA: Reserve stock with a single conditional statement: `Product.objects.filter(pk=pid, stock__gte=qty).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - qty)` and check the affected row count, inside an `atomic()` that also creates the order and reservation rows — plus a `CheckConstraint(stock >= 0)` as a backstop. That avoids explicit locks and keeps each transaction to a few milliseconds. The payment call happens outside the transaction with an idempotency key; the order moves through states (reserved → paid / expired) and a scheduled task releases expired reservations with another `F()` update. Side effects go through `on_commit` or an outbox. For extreme hot items, pre-split stock into buckets or front it with Redis `DECR` and reconcile.\n\n**Q: Your transfer endpoint occasionally fails with \"deadlock detected\". How do you diagnose and fix it? (Senior)**\nA: PostgreSQL logs both queries involved in the deadlock; I'd read those to see which rows each transaction locked and in what order. Typical cause: transfer A→B locks A then B while B→A locks B then A. The fix is to always acquire locks in a deterministic order — `select_for_update().filter(pk__in=[a,b]).order_by(\"pk\")` — and to minimise what's locked (`of=(\"self\",)`, `no_key=True`). Since deadlocks can still occur from implicit locks (FK checks), I'd also add a bounded retry on deadlock errors around the whole atomic unit, which is safe as long as side effects are deferred via `on_commit`.\n\n**Q: When would you choose SERIALIZABLE isolation in a Django app, and what must change in your code? (Senior)**\nA: When an invariant spans rows you can't easily lock — e.g. \"at most one on-call doctor per shift\" checked by reading a set of rows — so READ COMMITTED with row locks would require locking phantom rows that don't exist yet. SERIALIZABLE makes PostgreSQL detect such conflicts and abort one transaction with a serialization failure. The code must then retry the whole transaction on SQLSTATE 40001 (surfaced as `OperationalError`), which means transactions must be idempotent and side effects must happen only after commit. I'd usually set it on a separate database alias or connection used for those operations rather than globally, and measure the abort rate. Often a unique or exclusion constraint achieves the same invariant more cheaply.\n\n**Q: How do you process a database-backed job queue with many workers without double-processing? (Senior)**\nA: Claim jobs with `select_for_update(skip_locked=True)` inside a short transaction, ordered by priority, limited to a batch, and mark them as running in the same transaction. Each worker skips rows locked by others, so batches are disjoint and nobody waits. To survive worker crashes, record `claimed_at` and have a reaper requeue jobs stuck in running past a timeout, which means job handlers must be idempotent. Don't hold the lock while processing — claim, commit, then work — otherwise long jobs keep transactions open and block vacuum.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| API | Meaning |\n|---|---|\n| autocommit (default) | each statement commits immediately |\n| `@transaction.atomic` / `with atomic():` | outermost = BEGIN…COMMIT, nested = SAVEPOINT |\n| `atomic(durable=True)` | must be outermost (3.2+) |\n| `atomic(savepoint=False)` | nested without savepoint |\n| `transaction.on_commit(fn, robust=False)` | run after outermost commit; dropped on rollback |\n| `transaction.set_rollback(True)` | roll back without raising |\n| `ATOMIC_REQUESTS` / `@non_atomic_requests` | wrap views / opt out |\n| `filter(...).update(x=F(\"x\")-1)` | atomic DB-side change; check rowcount |\n| `select_for_update()` | `FOR UPDATE`, must be in atomic |\n| `nowait` / `skip_locked` / `of` / `no_key` | fail fast / skip / limit tables / `FOR NO KEY UPDATE` |\n| version column | optimistic: `filter(version=v).update(version=F+1)` |\n| `OPTIONS[\"isolation_level\"]` | change level; retry on 40001 |\n| `captureOnCommitCallbacks` / `TransactionTestCase` | test on_commit / real commits |\n\n- `atomic()` gives all-or-nothing, not race protection.\n- Catch DB errors outside a nested `atomic()`, never inside the failing block.\n- Locks last until the *outermost* commit.\n- Every external side effect: `on_commit` (or outbox).\n- Lock rows in a consistent order (`order_by(\"pk\")`).\n- Never hold a transaction across HTTP calls.\n- `get_or_create` is only safe with a unique constraint.\n- Constraints are the last line of defence for every invariant.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" \"psycopg[binary]\"`; PostgreSQL required (SQLite's locking is database-wide and won't show these behaviours).\n\n1. Reproduce the lost update: a management command that spawns 20 threads each doing `p.stock -= 1; p.save()` on a product with stock 20. Check the final stock. Fix with `F()` and re-run.\n2. Write `place_order` from §5.1; trigger the duplicate coupon; log SQL (`django.db.backends` at DEBUG) and find `SAVEPOINT` / `ROLLBACK TO SAVEPOINT`. Then remove the inner `atomic()` and observe `TransactionManagementError`.\n3. Open two `psql` sessions: in one `BEGIN; SELECT * FROM shop_product WHERE id=1 FOR UPDATE;`. In Django, run `select_for_update(nowait=True)` on the same row and catch the error; then `skip_locked=True` and see an empty result.\n4. Create a deadlock with two threads transferring A→B and B→A with an artificial `time.sleep` between the locks; read the PostgreSQL log; fix with sorted locking.\n5. Write a `TestCase` asserting a Celery task is registered via `on_commit` using `captureOnCommitCallbacks`, and a `TransactionTestCase` that proves only one of two concurrent buyers succeeds.\n6. Implement optimistic locking on a `Document` with a DRF endpoint returning `ETag` and enforcing `If-Match` (412 on mismatch).\n\n**Mini project:** a ticketing service with shows and seats: hold seats (`select_for_update`, consistent order, `nowait`), confirm after a fake payment call made outside the transaction with an idempotency key, release expired holds via a periodic task using `skip_locked`, and a load-test script (100 concurrent clients, 50 seats) proving no seat is sold twice.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) (F expressions), [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) (`update()` vs `save()`), [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres) (constraints, advisory locks via raw SQL), [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals) (signals run inside the transaction), [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi), [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks), [Ch 33 · Testing](topic.html?p=33-testing), [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases), [Ch 40 · Design Rounds](topic.html?p=40-design-rounds).\n\n**Other handbooks:** [SQL · Transactions & ACID](../sql/topic.html?p=25-transactions-acid), [SQL · Isolation Levels](../sql/topic.html?p=26-isolation-levels), [SQL · Locking & MVCC](../sql/topic.html?p=27-locking-mvcc), [Database Design · Concurrency Control](../database-design/topic.html?p=04-concurrency-control), [Database Design · Locking Internals](../database-design/topic.html?p=05-locking-internals), [Database Design · Case: Ticket Booking](../database-design/topic.html?p=33-case-ticket-booking), [REST API · Concurrency & Optimistic Locking](../rest-api/topic.html?p=28-concurrency-optimistic-locking), [Messaging · Idempotency & Outbox](../messaging/topic.html?p=21-idempotency-outbox), [Redis · Distributed Locks](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=23-distributed-locks-redlock).\n\n- **Database transactions** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · atomic, savepoints, on_commit, autocommit and the \"catching exceptions\" warning. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/transactions/>\n- **QuerySet API: select_for_update** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · nowait, skip_locked, of, no_key and backend caveats. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/querysets/>\n- **Avoiding race conditions using F()** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the official explanation of F-based updates. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/expressions/>\n- **PostgreSQL: Transaction Isolation** — PostgreSQL docs · *Intermediate* · exactly what READ COMMITTED re-checks and when SERIALIZABLE aborts. <https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html>\n- **PostgreSQL: Explicit Locking** — PostgreSQL docs · *Advanced* · row lock modes, deadlocks, advisory locks. <https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/explicit-locking.html>\n- **Celery: Django first steps** — Celery docs · *Beginner* · including `delay_on_commit`. <https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/stable/django/first-steps-with-django.html>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 14.*\n", "15-managers-model-inheritance": "# 15 · Custom Managers, QuerySets & Model Inheritance\n\n> **In one line:** A custom `QuerySet` is where reusable, chainable query logic belongs (`Order.objects.paid().for_user(u)`), a `Manager` is just the entry point that hands it out, and the three inheritance styles map to three very different schemas — abstract (no parent table, copy the columns), multi-table (a parent table plus a hidden one-to-one JOIN on every query) and proxy (same table, different Python behaviour).\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) · [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) · [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization)\n\nTwo problems show up in every growing Django codebase.\n\n**Problem one: query logic is copy-pasted.** \"Paid orders from the last 30 days for this tenant, with items prefetched\" appears in a view, a serializer, a Celery task and an admin action — each slightly different. One copy forgets the tenant filter and leaks data; another forgets `prefetch_related` and causes N+1. The naive fix is a helper function `get_recent_paid_orders(user)`, but helpers don't compose: you can't do `get_recent_paid_orders(user).filter(total__gt=100)` *and* reuse the \"paid\" rule elsewhere. The Django answer is a **custom QuerySet** with methods that each return a QuerySet, exposed through a **Manager**, so filters chain: `Order.objects.paid().recent(days=30).for_tenant(t)`.\n\n**Problem two: models share structure.** Every table needs `created_at`/`updated_at`; `Restaurant` \"is a\" `Place`; `Staff` users are users that should appear separately in the admin. The naive approach is copy-paste fields (drift) or a big model with nullable columns and a `type` field (sparse, unvalidated). Django offers three inheritance styles, and choosing wrongly is expensive: multi-table inheritance adds a JOIN to every query and a second INSERT to every save, and it is very hard to undo once data exists.\n\n> **Why this matters:** \"What's the difference between abstract, multi-table and proxy inheritance?\" is a standard Django interview question, as is \"how would you implement soft delete?\" — which is really a question about default managers, base managers and their leaks.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Manager** — the interface (`models.Manager`) through which a model gets QuerySets; class attribute such as `objects`. *Why:* it's the only way to start a query; customising it customises every query entry point.\n- **`get_queryset()`** — the Manager method returning the base QuerySet. *Why:* override it to change what \"all rows\" means (e.g. hide soft-deleted rows).\n- **Custom QuerySet** — a `models.QuerySet` subclass with domain methods that return `self.filter(...)`. *Why:* methods chain in any order and work on related managers too.\n- **`QuerySet.as_manager()`** / **`Manager.from_queryset(QS)`** — build a manager exposing the QuerySet's public methods. *Why:* one definition, available as both `Model.objects.paid()` and `qs.paid()`.\n- **Default manager (`_default_manager`)** — the first manager defined on the model (or `Meta.default_manager_name`). *Why:* used by the admin, generic views, `dumpdata`, reverse related managers and `get_object_or_404(Model, …)`.\n- **Base manager (`_base_manager`)** — plain `Manager` by default (or `Meta.base_manager_name`) used when Django must reach *all* rows: forward relation access (`order.customer`), `refresh_from_db`, saving. *Why:* filtering here can break `save()`/related lookups; not filtering here means soft-deleted rows are reachable via FKs.\n- **`use_in_migrations = True`** — makes a manager available on historical models inside `RunPython`. *Why:* migrations otherwise get a plain manager.\n- **Abstract base class** — `Meta.abstract = True`; no table; fields copied into each child. *Why:* DRY common columns with zero runtime cost.\n- **Multi-table inheritance (MTI)** — a concrete parent with its own table; the child table has a `<parent>_ptr` `OneToOneField(parent_link=True, primary_key=True)`. *Why:* true \"is-a\" with a shared queryable parent table — at the price of JOINs.\n- **Proxy model** — `Meta.proxy = True`; same table as the parent; different managers, methods, `Meta.ordering`, admin and permissions. *Why:* alternative Python behaviour over the same rows.\n- **Polymorphic query** — fetching parent rows and getting the right child class back. *Why:* Django doesn't do it natively; MTI gives you `place.restaurant` access, not automatic downcasting (libraries like django-polymorphic add it).\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 How managers attach and how QuerySet methods get copied\n\nWhen `ModelBase.__new__` builds a model class, every `Manager` instance in the class body goes through `contribute_to_class`, which sets `manager.model` and `manager.name` and registers it in `model._meta.local_managers`. Django wraps managers in a `ManagerDescriptor`, which is why `Order.objects` works but `order_instance.objects` raises `AttributeError: Manager isn't accessible via Order instances`. If a non-abstract model declares no manager, Django adds `objects = models.Manager()` automatically (`Options._prepare` / `ModelBase._prepare`).\n\n`Manager` itself is `BaseManager.from_queryset(QuerySet)`. `from_queryset(qs_class)` creates a new Manager subclass and, via `_get_queryset_methods`, copies every public method of the QuerySet class onto it as a proxy that calls `self.get_queryset().<method>(...)`. Methods starting with an underscore are not copied; you can override with `method.queryset_only = True` (don't copy) or `False` (copy even if private). `QuerySet.as_manager()` is just `Manager.from_queryset(cls)()`.\n\nBecause every QuerySet method calls `_chain()` → `_clone()`, which constructs a new instance of `self.__class__`, a custom QuerySet class survives chaining: `Order.objects.filter(x=1).paid()` works because `filter` returns your `OrderQuerySet`.\n\n### 3.2 Which manager Django uses where\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 420\" width=\"100%\" height=\"420\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj15a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj15b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#d97706\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Soft-delete via the default manager: who sees deleted rows?</text>\n  <rect x=\"330\" y=\"44\" width=\"220\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">class Product</text>\n  <text x=\"344\" y=\"88\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">objects = AliveManager()  (first = default)</text>\n  <text x=\"344\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">    get_queryset: deleted_at IS NULL</text>\n  <text x=\"344\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">all_objects = models.Manager()</text>\n  <text x=\"344\" y=\"142\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">_base_manager: plain Manager</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"190\" width=\"400\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e3a8a\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">_default_manager (filtered): deleted HIDDEN</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"236\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Product.objects.all() / .get(pk=...)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"256\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">category.products.all()  (reverse related manager is built</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"272\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">     from the default manager&#8217;s class)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"292\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Django admin changelist, ModelForm choice fields</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"312\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">generic views / get_object_or_404(Product, ...)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"332\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">dumpdata (unless --all)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"360\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"10\">Good: deleted products disappear from lists</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"378\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"10\">and pickers automatically.</text>\n  <rect x=\"460\" y=\"190\" width=\"400\" height=\"210\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"660\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">_base_manager (unfiltered): deleted VISIBLE</text>\n  <text x=\"476\" y=\"236\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">order_item.product   (forward FK descriptor)</text>\n  <text x=\"476\" y=\"256\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">obj.refresh_from_db(), save() existence check</text>\n  <text x=\"476\" y=\"276\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Product.all_objects / Product._base_manager</text>\n  <text x=\"476\" y=\"296\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">raw SQL, other services, analytics</text>\n  <text x=\"476\" y=\"324\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"10\">Usually right: an old order must still show the</text>\n  <text x=\"476\" y=\"342\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"10\">product it was for, even if that product is deleted.</text>\n  <text x=\"476\" y=\"366\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">Wrong if you used a default manager for SECURITY</text>\n  <text x=\"476\" y=\"384\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">(tenant isolation): FK access bypasses it.</text>\n  <path d=\"M400,156 L260,188\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj15a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M480,156 L620,188\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj15b)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\nRules worth memorising:\n\n- The **first manager defined** (following inheritance from abstract parents) becomes `_default_manager`, unless `Meta.default_manager_name` says otherwise. Putting a filtering manager first changes what the admin and `dumpdata` see.\n- **Forward relation access uses `_base_manager`**, which is unfiltered unless you set `Meta.base_manager_name` — and Django's docs warn the base manager must not filter out rows, because Django relies on it to find objects it already knows exist.\n- **Reverse and many-to-many related managers** (`category.products`) are created by subclassing the related model's **default manager class**, so its `get_queryset()` filtering and custom methods apply: `category.products.paid()` works.\n\n### 3.3 The three inheritance styles, as schemas\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 440\" width=\"100%\" height=\"440\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Abstract vs multi-table vs proxy: tables and SQL</text>\n  <rect x=\"16\" y=\"40\" width=\"276\" height=\"390\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"154\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Abstract base class</text>\n  <text x=\"28\" y=\"84\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">class Timestamped(Model): abstract</text>\n  <text x=\"28\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">class Post(Timestamped), class Comment(...)</text>\n  <rect x=\"28\" y=\"114\" width=\"120\" height=\"74\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"88\" y=\"132\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">blog_post</text>\n  <text x=\"38\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">id, title</text>\n  <text x=\"38\" y=\"166\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">created_at</text>\n  <text x=\"38\" y=\"182\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">updated_at</text>\n  <rect x=\"160\" y=\"114\" width=\"120\" height=\"74\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"132\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">blog_comment</text>\n  <text x=\"170\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">id, body</text>\n  <text x=\"170\" y=\"166\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">created_at</text>\n  <text x=\"170\" y=\"182\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">updated_at</text>\n  <text x=\"28\" y=\"212\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">No Timestamped table exists.</text>\n  <text x=\"28\" y=\"230\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT ... FROM blog_post</text>\n  <text x=\"28\" y=\"248\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">(no join, no overhead)</text>\n  <text x=\"28\" y=\"276\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">Cannot query \"all Timestamped\".</text>\n  <text x=\"28\" y=\"294\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">Cannot FK to the abstract class.</text>\n  <text x=\"28\" y=\"312\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">Meta (ordering etc.) inherited.</text>\n  <text x=\"28\" y=\"330\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">related_name needs %(class)s.</text>\n  <text x=\"28\" y=\"370\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">Use: shared columns/methods</text>\n  <text x=\"28\" y=\"388\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">(the default choice)</text>\n  <rect x=\"302\" y=\"40\" width=\"276\" height=\"390\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Multi-table inheritance</text>\n  <text x=\"314\" y=\"84\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">class Place(Model)   (concrete)</text>\n  <text x=\"314\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">class Restaurant(Place)</text>\n  <rect x=\"314\" y=\"114\" width=\"120\" height=\"58\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"374\" y=\"132\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">geo_place</text>\n  <text x=\"324\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">id, name</text>\n  <text x=\"324\" y=\"166\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">address</text>\n  <rect x=\"446\" y=\"114\" width=\"122\" height=\"58\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"507\" y=\"132\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">geo_restaurant</text>\n  <text x=\"454\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">place_ptr_id (PK,FK)</text>\n  <text x=\"454\" y=\"166\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">serves_pizza</text>\n  <path d=\"M446,150 L436,150\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"314\" y=\"196\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">Restaurant.objects.all():</text>\n  <text x=\"314\" y=\"212\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT ... FROM geo_restaurant</text>\n  <text x=\"314\" y=\"228\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">INNER JOIN geo_place ON place_ptr_id = id</text>\n  <text x=\"314\" y=\"248\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">save(): INSERT place, then INSERT restaurant</text>\n  <text x=\"314\" y=\"276\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">JOIN on every query, 2 writes per save,</text>\n  <text x=\"314\" y=\"294\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">no bulk_create, Place.objects returns Place</text>\n  <text x=\"314\" y=\"312\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">(downcast via place.restaurant = 1 query)</text>\n  <text x=\"314\" y=\"330\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">deep chains = many joins</text>\n  <text x=\"314\" y=\"370\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">Use: rarely; prefer explicit</text>\n  <text x=\"314\" y=\"388\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">OneToOneField composition</text>\n  <rect x=\"588\" y=\"40\" width=\"276\" height=\"390\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"726\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#4c1d95\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">Proxy model</text>\n  <text x=\"600\" y=\"84\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">class Order(Model)</text>\n  <text x=\"600\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">class RefundedOrder(Order): proxy=True</text>\n  <rect x=\"600\" y=\"114\" width=\"252\" height=\"58\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"726\" y=\"132\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#4c1d95\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">shop_order  (one table)</text>\n  <text x=\"610\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">id, status, total, ...</text>\n  <text x=\"610\" y=\"166\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">both classes read/write these rows</text>\n  <text x=\"600\" y=\"196\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">RefundedOrder.objects (custom manager):</text>\n  <text x=\"600\" y=\"212\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT ... FROM shop_order</text>\n  <text x=\"600\" y=\"228\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">WHERE status = 'refunded'</text>\n  <text x=\"600\" y=\"276\" fill=\"#7c3aed\" font-size=\"10\">Can change: managers, methods, ordering,</text>\n  <text x=\"600\" y=\"294\" fill=\"#7c3aed\" font-size=\"10\">admin registration, permissions.</text>\n  <text x=\"600\" y=\"312\" fill=\"#7c3aed\" font-size=\"10\">Cannot add fields. Migration has no SQL.</text>\n  <text x=\"600\" y=\"330\" fill=\"#7c3aed\" font-size=\"10\">Filtering is NOT automatic: write a manager.</text>\n  <text x=\"600\" y=\"370\" fill=\"#4c1d95\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">Use: alternative behaviour /</text>\n  <text x=\"600\" y=\"388\" fill=\"#4c1d95\" font-size=\"11\" font-weight=\"bold\">admin views of the same data</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nInternals worth knowing for MTI: `ModelBase` detects a concrete parent and auto-creates `place_ptr = OneToOneField(Place, parent_link=True, primary_key=True, on_delete=CASCADE, auto_created=True)`. Child field access to parent fields is resolved via `_meta.parents`, and the compiler adds the JOIN through the parent link. On `save()`, `Model.save_base` calls `_save_parents()` first (INSERT/UPDATE on `geo_place`) then `_save_table()` for the child. Deleting a `Restaurant` deletes the `Place` row too (the collector follows parent links). Of the parent's `Meta`, only `ordering` and `get_latest_by` are inherited by an MTI child.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 Layering query logic\n\n```text\n                 views / serializers / tasks / admin\n                               |\n         Order.objects.for_tenant(t).paid().recent(30).with_totals()\n                               |\n   +---------------------------v----------------------------+\n   | OrderManager = Manager.from_queryset(OrderQuerySet)     |\n   |   get_queryset(): base rules (e.g. hide soft-deleted)   |\n   |   create_from_cart(cart): write-side helper (manager-   |\n   |     only, not chainable)                                 |\n   +---------------------------+----------------------------+\n                               |\n   +---------------------------v----------------------------+\n   | OrderQuerySet(models.QuerySet)                          |\n   |   paid()        -> self.filter(status=\"paid\")           |\n   |   recent(days)  -> self.filter(created_at__gte=...)     |\n   |   for_tenant(t) -> self.filter(tenant=t)                |\n   |   with_totals() -> self.annotate(...)  [Ch 12]          |\n   |   for_list_api()-> self.select_related(...).prefetch(.) |\n   +---------------------------+----------------------------+\n                               |\n                      sql.Query -> SQLCompiler -> SQL\n```\n\nPut **read rules** that should compose on the QuerySet; put **table-level operations** that don't make sense mid-chain (create helpers, complex constructors) on the Manager; keep **row behaviour** (`order.mark_paid()`) on the model. Keep **multi-model business transactions** in a service function ([Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency)).\n\n### 4.2 Picking an inheritance strategy\n\n```text\nDo the models just share columns/behaviour, never queried together?   -> abstract base class\nSame rows, different Python behaviour/admin/default filter?           -> proxy model\nNeed one table to query/FK \"any Place\" AND per-type extra columns?\n    few types, few extra columns            -> single table + type field + nullable cols + CheckConstraints\n    many type-specific columns              -> explicit OneToOneField \"profile\" tables (composition)\n    you accept JOINs and want Django's sugar -> multi-table inheritance\nTruly heterogeneous, open-ended attributes                            -> JSONField [Ch 16]\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example: a chainable QuerySet\n\n```python\n# shop/models.py\nfrom datetime import timedelta\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.db.models import Count, F, Sum, DecimalField\nfrom django.utils import timezone\n\nclass OrderQuerySet(models.QuerySet):\n    def paid(self):\n        return self.filter(status=Order.Status.PAID)\n\n    def recent(self, days=30):\n        return self.filter(created_at__gte=timezone.now() - timedelta(days=days))\n\n    def for_user(self, user):\n        return self.filter(customer=user)\n\n    def with_totals(self):\n        return self.annotate(\n            item_count=Count(\"items\"),\n            computed_total=Sum(F(\"items__quantity\") * F(\"items__unit_price\"),\n                               output_field=DecimalField(max_digits=12, decimal_places=2)),\n        )\n\nclass Order(models.Model):\n    class Status(models.TextChoices):\n        PENDING = \"pending\"; PAID = \"paid\"; REFUNDED = \"refunded\"\n\n    customer = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"orders\")\n    status = models.CharField(max_length=10, choices=Status.choices, default=Status.PENDING)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n\n    objects = OrderQuerySet.as_manager()\n```\n\n```python\nOrder.objects.paid().recent(7).for_user(u).with_totals().order_by(\"-created_at\")\nOrder.objects.filter(customer__is_active=True).paid()   # custom methods survive filter()\nrequest.user.orders.paid()                           # works on the reverse related manager\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"shop_order\".\"id\", \"shop_order\".\"customer_id\", \"shop_order\".\"status\", \"shop_order\".\"created_at\",\n       COUNT(\"shop_orderitem\".\"id\") AS \"item_count\",\n       SUM((\"shop_orderitem\".\"quantity\" * \"shop_orderitem\".\"unit_price\")) AS \"computed_total\"\nFROM \"shop_order\"\nLEFT OUTER JOIN \"shop_orderitem\" ON (\"shop_order\".\"id\" = \"shop_orderitem\".\"order_id\")\nWHERE (\"shop_order\".\"status\" = 'paid'\n       AND \"shop_order\".\"created_at\" >= '2026-10-18T10:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz\n       AND \"shop_order\".\"customer_id\" = 7)\nGROUP BY \"shop_order\".\"id\"\nORDER BY \"shop_order\".\"created_at\" DESC;\n```\n\n### 5.2 Manager with extra methods: `from_queryset`\n\n```python\nclass OrderManager(models.Manager.from_queryset(OrderQuerySet)):\n    def create_from_cart(self, cart):\n        order = self.create(customer=cart.user)\n        OrderItem.objects.bulk_create(\n            [OrderItem(order=order, product_id=l.product_id, quantity=l.qty, unit_price=l.price)\n             for l in cart.lines]\n        )\n        return order\n\nclass Order(models.Model):\n    ...\n    objects = OrderManager()\n```\n\n`Order.objects.create_from_cart(cart)` exists; `Order.objects.paid().create_from_cart(cart)` doesn't — which is the point: creation isn't a filter.\n\n### 5.3 Real-world example: soft delete done properly\n\n```python\n# core/models.py\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.utils import timezone\n\nclass SoftDeleteQuerySet(models.QuerySet):\n    def delete(self):                                   # bulk \"delete\" = mark\n        return super().update(deleted_at=timezone.now())\n\n    def hard_delete(self):\n        return super().delete()\n\n    def alive(self):\n        return self.filter(deleted_at__isnull=True)\n\n    def dead(self):\n        return self.filter(deleted_at__isnull=False)\n\nclass AliveManager(models.Manager.from_queryset(SoftDeleteQuerySet)):\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return super().get_queryset().filter(deleted_at__isnull=True)\n\nclass SoftDeleteModel(models.Model):\n    deleted_at = models.DateTimeField(null=True, blank=True, db_index=True)\n\n    objects = AliveManager()                            # first => default manager\n    all_objects = SoftDeleteQuerySet.as_manager()       # escape hatch\n\n    class Meta:\n        abstract = True\n\n    def delete(self, using=None, keep_parents=False):   # instance \"delete\" = mark\n        self.deleted_at = timezone.now()\n        self.save(update_fields=[\"deleted_at\"])\n\n    def restore(self):\n        self.deleted_at = None\n        self.save(update_fields=[\"deleted_at\"])\n```\n\n```python\n# catalog/models.py\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.db.models import Q\nfrom core.models import SoftDeleteModel\n\nclass Product(SoftDeleteModel):\n    sku = models.CharField(max_length=40)\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    category = models.ForeignKey(\"Category\", on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"products\")\n\n    class Meta(SoftDeleteModel.Meta):                   # inherit abstract Meta explicitly\n        constraints = [\n            # SKU unique among live rows only: a deleted SKU can be reused\n            models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"sku\"], condition=Q(deleted_at__isnull=True),\n                                    name=\"product_sku_unique_alive\"),\n        ]\n```\n\n```text\n>>> p = Product.objects.get(sku=\"A1\"); p.delete()\nUPDATE \"catalog_product\" SET \"deleted_at\" = '2026-10-25 09:12:00+00:00' WHERE \"catalog_product\".\"id\" = 5\n>>> Product.objects.filter(sku=\"A1\").exists()\nFalse                                 # SELECT ... WHERE deleted_at IS NULL AND sku = 'A1'\n>>> Product.all_objects.filter(sku=\"A1\").exists()\nTrue\n>>> category.products.count()         # reverse manager uses AliveManager's class -> deleted excluded\n>>> order_item.product                # forward FK uses _base_manager -> deleted product still returned\n```\n\nDecisions this design makes explicitly:\n\n- **Instance and QuerySet `delete()` are both overridden.** Overriding only `Model.delete()` misses `Product.objects.filter(...).delete()` and admin bulk deletes, which go through the QuerySet.\n- **Cascades are not soft.** Real `on_delete=CASCADE` is only triggered by real deletes; soft-deleting a category doesn't soft-delete its products. If you need it, implement it in the service layer (or use a library such as django-safedelete, which models policies).\n- **The unique constraint is partial**, otherwise a deleted row blocks reuse of its SKU.\n- **Forward FK access still returns deleted rows** — the right behaviour for history (old orders), but templates should show \"(deleted)\".\n- **Not a security boundary.** Raw SQL, `_base_manager`, `all_objects`, `dumpdata --all` and other services see everything.\n\n### 5.4 Abstract base with relations\n\n```python\nclass Auditable(models.Model):\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n    updated_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now=True)\n    created_by = models.ForeignKey(\n        settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, null=True, on_delete=models.SET_NULL,\n        related_name=\"%(app_label)s_%(class)s_created\",       # unique per concrete child\n    )\n\n    class Meta:\n        abstract = True\n        ordering = [\"-created_at\"]\n```\n\nWithout the `%(app_label)s_%(class)s` placeholders, two children would both try to create `user.created` reverse accessors and `makemigrations` fails with a clash (fields.E304).\n\n### 5.5 Multi-table inheritance and its SQL\n\n```python\nclass Place(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=100)\n    address = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n\nclass Restaurant(Place):\n    serves_pizza = models.BooleanField(default=False)\n```\n\n```python\nRestaurant.objects.filter(name__startswith=\"Bella\")\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"geo_place\".\"id\", \"geo_place\".\"name\", \"geo_place\".\"address\",\n       \"geo_restaurant\".\"place_ptr_id\", \"geo_restaurant\".\"serves_pizza\"\nFROM \"geo_restaurant\"\nINNER JOIN \"geo_place\" ON (\"geo_restaurant\".\"place_ptr_id\" = \"geo_place\".\"id\")\nWHERE \"geo_place\".\"name\"::text LIKE 'Bella%';\n```\n\n```python\nr = Restaurant.objects.create(name=\"Bella\", address=\"1 Main St\", serves_pizza=True)\n```\n\n```sql\nINSERT INTO \"geo_place\" (\"name\", \"address\") VALUES ('Bella', '1 Main St') RETURNING \"geo_place\".\"id\";\nINSERT INTO \"geo_restaurant\" (\"place_ptr_id\", \"serves_pizza\") VALUES (1, true);\n```\n\nDowncasting from the parent:\n\n```python\nfor place in Place.objects.select_related(\"restaurant\"):      # reverse O2O can be select_related\n    kind = \"restaurant\" if hasattr(place, \"restaurant\") else \"place\"\n```\n\nWithout `select_related(\"restaurant\")`, each `place.restaurant` is a query (and raises `Restaurant.DoesNotExist` for plain places — `hasattr` catches it because `RelatedObjectDoesNotExist` subclasses `AttributeError`).\n\nThe same structure with explicit composition (often preferred — you see the JOIN, and you can make the relation optional or move it later):\n\n```python\nclass Restaurant(models.Model):\n    place = models.OneToOneField(Place, on_delete=models.CASCADE, primary_key=True, related_name=\"restaurant\")\n    serves_pizza = models.BooleanField(default=False)\n```\n\n### 5.6 Proxy models: a separate admin over the same table\n\n```python\nclass RefundedOrderManager(models.Manager):\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return super().get_queryset().filter(status=Order.Status.REFUNDED)\n\nclass RefundedOrder(Order):\n    objects = RefundedOrderManager()\n\n    class Meta:\n        proxy = True\n        ordering = [\"-created_at\"]\n        verbose_name = \"refunded order\"\n\n    def refund_age_days(self):\n        return (timezone.now() - self.created_at).days\n```\n\n```python\n# shop/admin.py\n@admin.register(RefundedOrder)\nclass RefundedOrderAdmin(admin.ModelAdmin):\n    list_display = [\"id\", \"customer\", \"created_at\"]\n    def has_add_permission(self, request): return False\n```\n\nThe proxy's migration is `CreateModel(..., options={\"proxy\": True}, bases=(\"shop.order\",))` with no SQL. Since Django 2.2, a proxy model's permissions (`shop.view_refundedorder`) use the proxy's own content type, so you can grant support staff access to refunds without granting access to all orders. Note `Order.objects.get(pk=x)` returns an `Order`, not a `RefundedOrder` — proxies don't auto-cast.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Technique | Schema effect | Query cost | Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Custom QuerySet (`as_manager`) | none | none | Chainable, works on related managers, testable | Methods must return QuerySets |\n| Manager `get_queryset` filter | none | WHERE on every query | Global default (soft delete, active only) | Surprises: admin, dumpdata, base manager leaks |\n| Abstract base | columns copied per child | none | DRY, zero overhead | No polymorphic queries, no FK to base |\n| Multi-table | parent + child tables, 1:1 PK | JOIN per query, 2 writes per save | Query/FK the parent; \"is-a\" | Joins, no `bulk_create`, hard to refactor, no auto-downcast |\n| Proxy | none | none (+ manager filter) | Separate admin/permissions/behaviour | Can't add fields; no auto-casting |\n| Explicit OneToOne composition | same as MTI | explicit JOIN | Visible, flexible, optional | Slightly more code |\n| Single table + type field | one wide table | none | Simple polymorphism | Nullable columns; enforce with CheckConstraints |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Custom QuerySets for any filter used in more than one place — especially tenant scoping and list-API optimisation (`for_list_api()` with the select/prefetch from [Ch 13](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization)).\n- Abstract bases for timestamps, audit fields, UUID primary keys, soft-delete columns.\n- Proxies for alternative admin screens, role-specific permissions, or state-specific behaviour.\n- MTI only when you genuinely need to query and reference the parent across types and the JOIN is acceptable.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Don't put security-critical filtering (tenant isolation) *only* in a default manager; enforce it in querysets used by views plus database-level controls (row-level security, separate schemas) — see [Database Design · Case: Multi-Tenant SaaS](../database-design/topic.html?p=40-case-multi-tenant-saas).\n- Don't use MTI for deep hierarchies (three levels = three tables joined on every query).\n- Don't filter in a manager that will be the base manager.\n- Don't use a filtering default manager on a model where the admin must show all rows — add an unfiltered manager first, or set `default_manager_name`.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Manager methods that return lists.**\n\n```python\n# Before: not chainable, evaluates immediately\nclass OrderManager(models.Manager):\n    def paid(self):\n        return list(self.filter(status=\"paid\"))\n\n# After: a QuerySet method — lazy and chainable\nclass OrderQuerySet(models.QuerySet):\n    def paid(self):\n        return self.filter(status=\"paid\")\n```\n\n**2. Putting the filtering manager first by accident.** `objects = PublishedManager()` defined before `all_objects = Manager()` makes the admin hide drafts. Decide deliberately; use `Meta.default_manager_name = \"all_objects\"` if the admin must see everything.\n\n**3. Overriding only `Model.delete()` for soft delete.** QuerySet deletes bypass it (admin \"delete selected\" calls `queryset.delete()`). Override both, or block bulk delete.\n\n**4. Expecting `Place.objects.all()` to return `Restaurant` instances.** MTI doesn't downcast. Use `select_related(\"restaurant\")` and check, or a polymorphic library, or redesign.\n\n**5. Forgetting `Meta` inheritance rules.** An abstract child that defines its own `class Meta:` *replaces* the parent's Meta unless it subclasses it (`class Meta(Base.Meta):`) — you lose `ordering`, `indexes`, etc. MTI children inherit only `ordering` and `get_latest_by`.\n\n**6. `related_name` clashes in abstract bases.** Use `%(app_label)s_%(class)s`.\n\n**7. Custom manager methods unavailable in migrations.** Historical models get plain managers unless `use_in_migrations = True` — and even then, avoid importing code that will change; inline logic in `RunPython` ([Ch 11](topic.html?p=11-migrations)).\n\n**8. Changing inheritance later.** Converting MTI to abstract (or vice versa) means moving data between tables and rewriting FKs — a multi-step migration. Choose early; when unsure, start with abstract + explicit OneToOne.\n\n**Best practices:** one QuerySet class per model with domain verbs; test QuerySet methods directly (`Order.objects.paid()` with fixtures, plus `assertNumQueries`); name escape-hatch managers explicitly (`all_objects`, `with_deleted`); document which manager is default.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: deleted rows reappearing.** *Symptom:* customers see a \"deleted\" address on old invoices *and* in a dropdown. *Root cause:* the dropdown used `ModelChoiceField(queryset=Address._base_manager.all())` copied from somewhere, and an FK access in the template. *Fix:* use the default manager for pickers; label deleted rows in history views.\n\n**Pitfall: tenant leak via forward FK.** A multi-tenant app relied on `TenantManager` reading a thread-local tenant. `invoice.customer` uses the base manager, so a crafted invoice ID from another tenant (fetched via an unscoped `get`) exposed a customer. *Fix:* scope every view queryset explicitly (`Invoice.objects.for_tenant(request.tenant)`), add `tenant_id` to every unique constraint and FK checks, and consider PostgreSQL row-level security.\n\n**Pitfall: MTI join explosion.** A `Content` → `Article` → `SponsoredArticle` hierarchy made every list query join three tables; an index on the leaf couldn't help filters on the root. *Symptom:* `explain()` shows nested loops over all three. *Fix:* flatten to abstract base + a type column, migrating data in an expand/contract sequence ([Ch 11](topic.html?p=11-migrations)).\n\n**Pitfall: `bulk_create` on MTI children.** Raises `ValueError: Can't bulk create a multi-table inherited model`. Imports have to create parents first (`bulk_create` parents, then children with `place_ptr_id`), which is fiddly — another reason to prefer composition.\n\n**Pitfall: soft-deleted rows bloating indexes.** Years of soft-deleted rows make every query filter them out. *Fix:* partial indexes `WHERE deleted_at IS NULL` (`models.Index(fields=[...], condition=Q(deleted_at__isnull=True), name=...)`), and archive/purge old dead rows ([Database Design · Data Lifecycle](../database-design/topic.html?p=28-data-lifecycle)).\n\n**Debugging:**\n\n```python\nProduct._default_manager, Product._base_manager            # which managers?\n[m.name for m in Product._meta.managers]                   # declaration order\nRestaurant._meta.parents                                    # {Place: <OneToOneField place_ptr>}\nRestaurant._meta.get_parent_list()\nprint(Restaurant.objects.all().query)                       # see the JOIN\n```\n\n**Scaling notes:** custom QuerySets are the natural home for performance rules (always `select_related` the tenant, always `only()` list columns), so a fix lands everywhere at once. For heavy polymorphic reads, consider a denormalised \"feed\" table maintained on write rather than MTI joins.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is a Manager in Django, and how is it different from a QuerySet?**\nA: A Manager is the class attribute (`objects` by default) through which a model starts queries; it's accessible on the class but not on instances. A QuerySet is the lazy, chainable query object a Manager hands out via `get_queryset()`. Practically, filtering logic belongs on a custom QuerySet so it chains (`Order.objects.paid().recent()`), while the Manager is the entry point and the place for table-level helpers that don't make sense mid-chain. `QuerySet.as_manager()` or `Manager.from_queryset()` connect the two so you write the methods once.\n\n**Q: Why put custom methods on a QuerySet rather than a Manager?**\nA: Manager methods only exist on the manager, so `Order.objects.paid()` works but `Order.objects.filter(x=1).paid()` doesn't. QuerySet methods are available at any point in a chain, because every QuerySet method clones into the same QuerySet class, and they're also available on related managers like `user.orders.paid()`. With `as_manager()` they're also exposed on the manager. So composable read logic goes on the QuerySet.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between the default manager and the base manager?**\nA: The default manager (`_default_manager`) is the first manager defined, or the one named in `Meta.default_manager_name`; Django uses it for the admin, generic views, `dumpdata`, and as the basis of reverse related managers. The base manager (`_base_manager`) is a plain unfiltered `Manager` unless `Meta.base_manager_name` is set, and Django uses it when it must reach any row — forward FK/one-to-one access, `refresh_from_db()`, and saving. Django's docs say a base manager shouldn't filter rows. This distinction is why a soft-delete default manager doesn't hide deleted objects accessed via a ForeignKey.\n\n**Q: What are the three types of model inheritance in Django?**\nA: Abstract base classes (`Meta.abstract = True`) create no table; their fields and Meta are copied into each child, so there's no runtime cost but you can't query or FK the base. Multi-table inheritance subclasses a concrete model: the parent keeps its table, the child gets its own table with a `parent_ptr` one-to-one primary key, and queries on the child JOIN the parent. Proxy models (`Meta.proxy = True`) use the parent's table unchanged and can only change Python behaviour — managers, methods, ordering, admin and permissions — not fields.\n\n**Q: What are the downsides of multi-table inheritance?**\nA: Every query on the child joins the parent table, and every save writes two rows, which adds latency and complicates indexing because filters may span both tables. Querying the parent returns parent instances, not children, so you need extra queries or `select_related(\"child\")` to downcast. `bulk_create` isn't supported for MTI children. Refactoring away from MTI later requires data migrations across tables. Many teams prefer an explicit `OneToOneField` (composition) or an abstract base instead.\n\n**Q: When would you use a proxy model?**\nA: When you want different Python behaviour over the same rows: a separate admin section (e.g. \"Refunded orders\" with its own list display and permissions), a different default ordering, a manager with a default filter, or extra methods for a particular state. Proxy models get their own content type, so permissions can be granted per proxy. They can't add fields, and the parent's manager won't return proxy instances, so the filtering must be in the proxy's own manager.\n\n**Q: How would you implement soft delete in Django?**\nA: Add a nullable `deleted_at` on an abstract base, override `Model.delete()` to set it, and use a custom QuerySet whose `delete()` does `update(deleted_at=now())` so bulk and admin deletes are covered too. Make the default manager filter `deleted_at IS NULL` and add an unfiltered `all_objects` manager. Use partial unique constraints and partial indexes on live rows only. Be aware that forward FK access uses the base manager, so deleted objects remain reachable from history, that cascades don't happen automatically, and that raw SQL and other services see everything.\n\n**Q: What does `use_in_migrations` do on a manager?**\nA: It makes the manager available on historical models inside data migrations. By default, `apps.get_model()` models in `RunPython` get only a plain manager, so custom methods aren't there. Setting `use_in_migrations = True` serializes the manager into migration state so it can be used. The caveat is that the migration then depends on importable manager code, which can change over time, so many teams avoid it and write migration logic inline.\n\n**Q: Why do abstract models often need `related_name=\"%(app_label)s_%(class)s_...\"`?**\nA: Because the fields of an abstract base are copied into every concrete child, a ForeignKey with a fixed `related_name` would create the same reverse accessor name on the target model from each child, which is a clash that system checks reject. The placeholders are substituted per child with its app label and lowercased class name, giving unique accessors like `blog_post_created` and `blog_comment_created`.\n\n**Q: A colleague used a thread-local \"current tenant\" in a default manager for multi-tenancy. What are the risks? (Senior)**\nA: The default manager isn't used everywhere: forward FK access and `refresh_from_db` use the base manager, raw SQL and `_base_manager` bypass it, and Celery tasks, management commands and async contexts may have no tenant set — returning everything or nothing silently. Thread-locals also behave unexpectedly under async views and in thread pools. I'd make tenant scoping explicit in view querysets (`for_tenant(request.tenant)`), include `tenant_id` in unique constraints and composite FKs, add tests that cross-tenant IDs return 404, and consider PostgreSQL row-level security or schema-per-tenant for defence in depth. The manager can still help as a convenience, but not as the only boundary.\n\n**Q: You inherited a three-level multi-table hierarchy that's slow. How would you migrate away from it without downtime? (Senior)**\nA: First decide the target — usually one table with an abstract base and a type column, or composition with explicit OneToOne tables for rare, wide subtypes. Then expand: create the new table, dual-write from the application (or a trigger) on every save, backfill existing rows in batches by joining the old tables. Switch reads endpoint by endpoint behind a flag, comparing results. Repoint ForeignKeys from other models to the new table with the same expand/backfill/switch pattern. Finally contract: stop writing the old tables and drop them in a later release. Primary keys can stay the same since MTI children share the parent's pk, which simplifies the FK move.\n\n**Q: How do custom QuerySets help prevent N+1 and data leaks across a large team? (Senior)**\nA: They give one named place for the rules: `Order.objects.for_tenant(t).for_list_api()` encapsulates the tenant filter plus the `select_related`/`Prefetch`/`only` that the list serializer needs, so every view, task and export gets the same optimised, correctly scoped query. Tests can target these methods with `assertNumQueries` and cross-tenant fixtures. When the serializer changes, the QuerySet method changes with it in the same PR. It also makes code review easier — a raw `Order.objects.filter(...)` in a view becomes a visible smell.\n\n**Q: What happens when you call `save()` on a multi-table child?**\nA: `save_base` first saves the parents — `_save_parents` inserts or updates the parent table row — and then saves the child table row with the parent's primary key as its own `parent_ptr_id`. So creating a child is two INSERTs, wrapped in a transaction by Django to keep them consistent. Updating a child updates both tables only for fields that belong to each. Deleting a child deletes the parent row too, because the collector follows the parent link.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Concept | Key fact |\n|---|---|\n| `objects` | Auto-added if no manager declared |\n| Default manager | First declared or `Meta.default_manager_name`; admin, dumpdata, reverse managers |\n| Base manager | Plain unless `Meta.base_manager_name`; forward FK, refresh, save |\n| `QuerySet.as_manager()` | Manager exposing QuerySet's public methods |\n| `Manager.from_queryset(QS)` | Same, plus your own manager methods |\n| `use_in_migrations` | Manager available in `RunPython` historical models |\n| Abstract | No table; fields copied; Meta inherited (subclass `Base.Meta` to extend) |\n| Multi-table | Parent + child table, `parent_ptr` O2O PK; JOIN per query; 2 writes per save |\n| Proxy | Same table; managers/methods/ordering/admin/perms; no new fields |\n| MTI Meta | Only `ordering`, `get_latest_by` inherited |\n| Downcast | `place.restaurant` (query) or `select_related(\"restaurant\")` |\n\n- Read logic → QuerySet; table helpers → Manager; row behaviour → model; transactions → service.\n- QuerySet methods return QuerySets, never lists.\n- Soft delete: override both instance and QuerySet `delete()`; partial unique constraints.\n- Forward FK access bypasses default-manager filtering.\n- Default managers are not a security boundary.\n- Prefer abstract bases and explicit OneToOne over MTI.\n- Proxies don't auto-cast; give them their own filtering manager.\n- `related_name=\"%(app_label)s_%(class)s_...\"` in abstract bases.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" \"psycopg[binary]\"`, `startproject lab`, apps `shop`, `geo`, `core`.\n\n1. Build `OrderQuerySet` with `paid()`, `recent()`, `for_user()`, `with_totals()`; prove chaining in any order and on `user.orders`. Print SQL.\n2. Implement the soft-delete base from §5.3. Write tests: instance delete, queryset delete, admin \"delete selected\", reverse manager hides, forward FK still returns, partial unique allows SKU reuse.\n3. Swap the order of `objects` and `all_objects`; observe the admin; then fix with `default_manager_name`.\n4. Create `Place`/`Restaurant` MTI; log SQL for create, filter, update and delete; then rewrite as explicit OneToOne and compare.\n5. Add a `RefundedOrder` proxy with its own admin and grant a test user only `shop.view_refundedorder`; verify they can't see the main Order admin.\n6. Try `Restaurant.objects.bulk_create([...])` and read the error; write the two-step workaround.\n\n**Mini project:** a multi-tenant notes app where `Note`, `Notebook` and `Tag` share an abstract `TenantScoped` base (tenant FK, timestamps, soft delete), each with a QuerySet providing `for_tenant()` and `for_list_api()`. Write cross-tenant tests that try to access another tenant's objects via list views, detail views and FK traversal, and fix every leak you find.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields), [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships) (OneToOne composition), [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups), [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations) (`use_in_migrations`, refactoring inheritance), [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization), [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency), [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres) (partial unique constraints), [Ch 07 · The Django Admin](topic.html?p=07-django-admin), [Ch 26 · Permissions, Groups & Authorization](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization), [Ch 36 · Django Internals](topic.html?p=36-django-internals).\n\n**Other handbooks:** [SQL · Schema Design](../sql/topic.html?p=30-schema-design), [SQL · Keys & Constraints](../sql/topic.html?p=29-keys-constraints), [Database Design · Data Lifecycle](../database-design/topic.html?p=28-data-lifecycle), [Database Design · Advanced Database Patterns](../database-design/topic.html?p=29-advanced-database-patterns), [Database Design · Case: Multi-Tenant SaaS](../database-design/topic.html?p=40-case-multi-tenant-saas).\n\n- **Managers** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · custom managers, `from_queryset`, default vs base managers and their rules. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/managers/>\n- **Models: model inheritance** — Django docs · *Beginner* · abstract, multi-table and proxy inheritance with Meta rules. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/models/>\n- **Model Meta options** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `abstract`, `proxy`, `default_manager_name`, `base_manager_name`, constraints. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/options/>\n- **django-polymorphic** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · automatic downcasting for multi-table hierarchies, and a good read on why it's needed. <https://github.com/django-polymorphic/django-polymorphic>\n- **django-safedelete** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · soft-delete policies including cascades, as a reference design. <https://github.com/makinacorpus/django-safedelete>\n- **PostgreSQL: Row Security Policies** — PostgreSQL docs · *Advanced* · database-enforced tenant isolation beyond managers. <https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-rowsecurity.html>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 15.*\n", "16-database-features-postgres": "# 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features\n\n> **In one line:** The ORM is a thin, honest layer over a real database. Declare indexes and constraints in `Meta` so the database enforces your invariants, drop to raw SQL safely (always with parameters) when the ORM runs out of road, and use `django.contrib.postgres` — JSONField, ArrayField, full-text search, GIN indexes, exclusion constraints — to get PostgreSQL's power without giving up migrations.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields), [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups), [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations), [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization).\n\nMost Django projects start with nothing but models and `.filter()` calls. That works until three things happen at once: the table grows to millions of rows, two requests race to create \"the same\" record, and product asks for search. At that point the naive approach — Python-side validation plus whatever indexes Django created by accident — fails in three predictable ways:\n\n1. **Slow queries.** A `WHERE lower(email) = ...` or `WHERE status = 'pending' ORDER BY created_at` scans the whole table because no index matches the predicate. The ORM did not \"do something wrong\"; you never told the database what to index.\n2. **Broken invariants.** You checked `if not Booking.objects.filter(room=r, day=d).exists(): create(...)` in Python. Two concurrent requests both pass the check and both insert. Only a **database constraint** closes that race — application checks are advisory, constraints are law.\n3. **Feature gaps.** You need \"search posts by words, ranked\", \"filter on a key inside a JSON blob\", or \"no two reservations may overlap in time\". The generic ORM cannot express these portably; PostgreSQL can, and Django exposes it through `django.contrib.postgres`.\n\nThis chapter is the home for **indexes and constraints declared in `Meta`**, **raw SQL** (`Manager.raw()`, `connection.cursor()`, `RawSQL`) and its injection safety, and the **PostgreSQL-specific fields, lookups, indexes and constraints**. Query-shape optimization (N+1, `select_related`) lives in [Ch 13](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization); the database theory behind B-trees and planners lives in the SQL handbook ([SQL · Indexes](../sql/topic.html?p=19-indexes), [SQL · Execution Plans](../sql/topic.html?p=22-execution-plans)).\n\n> **Why this matters:** Interviewers use this topic to separate people who \"use Django\" from people who \"run Django on a database\". Expect: \"how do you enforce uniqueness only for active rows?\", \"is `raw()` safe from SQL injection?\", \"JSONField vs a real column?\", and \"how do you add an index to a 50M-row table without downtime?\".\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **`db_index=True` / `unique=True`** — field-level shortcuts that create a single-column index or unique index. *Why it matters:* quick, but they cannot express composite, partial, functional or covering indexes.\n- **Automatic FK index** — every `ForeignKey` gets an index by default (`db_index=True`). *Why it matters:* a composite index that starts with that FK column may make the auto index redundant; you can set `db_index=False` deliberately.\n- **`Meta.indexes`** — a list of `models.Index(...)` (or Postgres index classes). Supports `fields`, expressions (functional indexes), `condition=` (partial), `include=` (covering, PostgreSQL), `opclasses=`. *Why it matters:* this is how you match the index to the query.\n- **`Meta.constraints`** — `UniqueConstraint`, `CheckConstraint`, and Postgres `ExclusionConstraint`. *Why it matters:* the database rejects bad data even when code paths bypass Python (bulk operations, raw SQL, other services).\n- **`UniqueConstraint`** — `fields=` or expressions, optional `condition=` (partial unique), `deferrable=`, `include=`, `nulls_distinct=` (5.0+, PostgreSQL 15+), `violation_error_message`/`violation_error_code`. *Why it matters:* replaces `unique_together`, which the docs now discourage.\n- **`CheckConstraint`** — a `Q`/boolean expression the row must satisfy. In 5.1+ the argument is `condition=`; `check=` is deprecated. *Why it matters:* domain rules like \"price ≥ 0\" or \"end > start\" enforced at the source.\n- **`validate_constraints()`** — since 4.1, `Model.full_clean()` validates `Meta.constraints` (ModelForms therefore surface them as form errors). *Why it matters:* nice messages for users, but `save()` still does not call it — the DB is the final guard.\n- **`Manager.raw(sql, params)`** — returns a `RawQuerySet` of model instances from hand-written SQL. Must select the primary key. *Why it matters:* escape hatch for SQL the ORM cannot build, while still returning models.\n- **`connection.cursor()`** — DB-API cursor for arbitrary SQL; placeholders are `%s` on every backend. *Why it matters:* reporting queries, DDL, stored procedures.\n- **`RawSQL` / `extra()`** — inject SQL fragments into an ORM query. `extra()` is a \"last resort\" API slated for deprecation in spirit. *Why it matters:* most common source of injection in Django codebases.\n- **`JSONField`** — cross-database since 3.1; `jsonb` on PostgreSQL; key/path lookups, `contains`, `has_key`. *Why it matters:* flexible attributes without a schema migration — and a performance trap if abused.\n- **`ArrayField`** (Postgres) — a native array column with `contains`, `overlap`, `len`, index transforms. *Why it matters:* tags without a join table, at the cost of referential integrity.\n- **Full-text search** — `SearchVector`, `SearchQuery`, `SearchRank`, `SearchVectorField`, `TrigramSimilarity`. *Why it matters:* \"good enough\" search inside Postgres before reaching for Elasticsearch.\n- **GIN / GiST / BRIN indexes** — `django.contrib.postgres.indexes`. GIN for \"contains\" queries on jsonb, arrays, tsvector; GiST for ranges/geometry/trigram; BRIN for huge append-only time-ordered tables.\n- **`AddIndexConcurrently`** — migration operation building an index with `CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY`; requires `atomic = False` on the migration. *Why it matters:* indexing a hot table without locking writes.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 From `Meta` to DDL\n\nWhen the `ModelBase` metaclass builds a model, `Options` (`_meta`) stores `indexes` and `constraints`. The migration autodetector compares `_meta` against the *migration state* (not the live DB) and emits `AddIndex`, `AddConstraint`, `RemoveConstraint` operations. At `migrate` time each operation calls the backend's **schema editor** (`connection.schema_editor()`, e.g. `DatabaseSchemaEditor` for PostgreSQL), which asks the index/constraint object for its SQL via `create_sql(model, schema_editor)`. Expressions inside a functional index or check constraint are compiled through the same `SQLCompiler`/`Query` machinery as a normal QuerySet, which is why you can write `Lower(\"email\")` or `Q(price__gte=0)` and get correct SQL.\n\nNames are mandatory for `Meta.indexes` and constraints (`name=\"...\"`), and must be unique per database. In abstract base classes use `%(app_label)s_%(class)s_...` interpolation so each concrete model gets its own name.\n\n### 3.2 How a query picks an index\n\nThe planner can use an index only when the query predicate matches the index *definition*:\n\n- A B-tree on `(tenant_id, created_at)` serves `WHERE tenant_id = ? ORDER BY created_at` and `WHERE tenant_id = ?`, but not `WHERE created_at > ?` alone (leftmost-prefix rule).\n- A functional index on `lower(email)` serves `email__iexact`? Not necessarily: `iexact` compiles to `UPPER(\"email\"::text) = UPPER(%s)` on PostgreSQL. Your index expression must match the SQL Django generates — use `Upper(\"email\")` for `iexact`, or query `annotate(e=Lower(\"email\")).filter(e=...)` / a `Lower` lookup with `Lower(\"email\")` index. Always check with `.explain()`.\n- A partial index `WHERE status = 'pending'` is used only when the query's WHERE implies that condition.\n- `icontains` compiles to `UPPER(col) LIKE UPPER('%x%')` — no B-tree helps a leading wildcard; you need a trigram GIN index.\n\n> **Gotcha:** On PostgreSQL, a `CharField`/`TextField` with `db_index=True` or `unique=True` creates **two** indexes: the normal one and a `..._like` index using `varchar_pattern_ops`/`text_pattern_ops` so that `startswith` can use an index. That is extra write cost you may not need.\n\n### 3.3 Constraints vs validation — where the truth lives\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 330\" width=\"100%\" height=\"330\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj16a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj16b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Which write paths are checked by Python validation vs the database constraint</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"45\" width=\"170\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">ModelForm / Serializer</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"95\" width=\"170\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">obj.full_clean(); save()</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"145\" width=\"170\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">obj.save() alone</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"195\" width=\"170\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"216\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">bulk_create / update()</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"245\" width=\"170\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">raw SQL / other service</text>\n  <rect x=\"300\" y=\"60\" width=\"210\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"405\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Python layer</text>\n  <text x=\"405\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">validate_unique()</text>\n  <text x=\"405\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">validate_constraints() (4.1+)</text>\n  <text x=\"405\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">friendly error messages</text>\n  <text x=\"405\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">racy: check-then-insert</text>\n  <rect x=\"610\" y=\"120\" width=\"230\" height=\"130\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"144\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">PostgreSQL</text>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"164\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">UNIQUE / CHECK / EXCLUDE</text>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">enforced atomically per row</text>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">violation: IntegrityError</text>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"218\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">no bypass, no race</text>\n  <path d=\"M192,62 L298,90\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj16a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M192,112 L298,118\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj16a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M512,120 L608,165\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj16a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M192,162 L608,180\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj16b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M192,212 L608,205\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj16b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M192,262 L608,230\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj16b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"300\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">Red paths skip Python validation entirely. Only the database constraint sees every write.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nThe principle: **validation is for user experience, constraints are for correctness.** Put the rule in `Meta.constraints` so the database enforces it; Django 4.1+ will also run it in `full_clean()` so forms show a helpful message. Catch `IntegrityError` at the race boundary (e.g. `get_or_create` already does this internally — it catches the `IntegrityError` from a concurrent insert and re-fetches).\n\n### 3.4 Raw SQL and parameter binding\n\nEvery database driver separates **SQL text** from **parameters**. When you call `cursor.execute(\"... WHERE id = %s\", [user_id])`, Django passes the list to psycopg, which sends the value through the protocol (or quotes it correctly) — the value can never become SQL syntax. When you write `cursor.execute(f\"... WHERE id = {user_id}\")`, the value *is* SQL syntax. That single difference is the whole story of SQL injection.\n\nInternally, `Manager.raw()` builds a `RawQuery`; iterating the `RawQuerySet` executes it and maps columns to fields by name (`translations=` remaps names). Fields you did not select become **deferred** and trigger one extra query each on access; omitting the primary key raises `FieldDoesNotExist`/`InvalidQuery`-style errors because instances cannot be built.\n\n> **Gotcha:** Never quote the placeholder: `WHERE name = '%s'` is wrong — the driver adds the quotes. And placeholders work only for **values**, never for identifiers (table/column names) or SQL keywords (`ASC`/`DESC`). Those must be whitelisted.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 The PostgreSQL toolbox map\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 360\" width=\"100%\" height=\"360\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj16c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Query shape  ->  Django lookup  ->  index that serves it</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"250\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Query shape</text>\n  <rect x=\"305\" y=\"40\" width=\"250\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"430\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ORM expression</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"40\" width=\"250\" height=\"26\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"715\" y=\"57\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Index</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"80\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">equality / range / ORDER BY</text>\n  <rect x=\"305\" y=\"80\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"430\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">filter(a=1).order_by(\"b\")</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"80\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"715\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B-tree Index(fields=[\"a\",\"b\"])</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"126\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">subset of rows (status=pending)</text>\n  <rect x=\"305\" y=\"126\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"430\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">filter(status=\"pending\")</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"126\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"715\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">partial Index(condition=Q(...))</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"172\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">JSON containment</text>\n  <rect x=\"305\" y=\"172\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"430\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">filter(data__contains={...})</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"172\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"715\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">GinIndex (jsonb_path_ops)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"218\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">words, ranked</text>\n  <rect x=\"305\" y=\"218\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"430\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">filter(search_vector=SearchQuery())</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"218\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"715\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">GinIndex on SearchVectorField</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"264\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">fuzzy / substring match</text>\n  <rect x=\"305\" y=\"264\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"430\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">icontains, trigram_similar</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"264\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"715\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">GinIndex(gin_trgm_ops)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"310\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"145\" y=\"332\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">time-range overlap</text>\n  <rect x=\"305\" y=\"310\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"430\" y=\"332\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">during__overlap=range</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"310\" width=\"250\" height=\"36\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"715\" y=\"332\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">GiST / ExclusionConstraint</text>\n  <path d=\"M272,98 L303,98\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj16c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M557,98 L588,98\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj16c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M272,190 L303,190\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj16c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M557,190 L588,190\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj16c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M272,282 L303,282\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj16c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M557,282 L588,282\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj16c)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 4.2 The workflow for adding an index safely\n\n```text\n1. Find the slow query     -> pg_stat_statements / Sentry / debug-toolbar / slow log\n2. Reproduce in Django     -> qs = Order.objects.filter(...); print(qs.explain(analyze=True))\n3. Design the index        -> match predicate + sort; partial if the hot subset is small\n4. Declare in Meta.indexes -> makemigrations\n5. Make it non-blocking    -> swap AddIndex for AddIndexConcurrently, atomic = False\n6. Review the SQL          -> manage.py sqlmigrate app 0042\n7. Deploy as its own release step; verify with explain() again; watch write latency\n```\n\n### 4.3 Raw SQL decision ladder\n\n```text\nCan the ORM express it?            -> use the ORM (annotate, Subquery, Window, Func)\nNeed one SQL fragment in a query?  -> custom Func/Lookup, or RawSQL(sql, params)\nNeed model instances from SQL?     -> Model.objects.raw(sql, params)\nNeed arbitrary rows / DDL / CTE?   -> with connection.cursor() as c: c.execute(sql, params)\nBuilding identifiers dynamically?  -> whitelist, then connection.ops.quote_name()\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example — indexes and constraints on one model\n\n```python\n# shop/models.py\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.db.models import Q, F\nfrom django.db.models.functions import Lower\n\n\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    sku = models.CharField(max_length=32)\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    email_contact = models.EmailField()\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2)\n    sale_price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2, null=True, blank=True)\n    is_active = models.BooleanField(default=True)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        indexes = [\n            # composite: \"active products, newest first\"\n            models.Index(fields=[\"is_active\", \"-created_at\"], name=\"product_active_recent_idx\"),\n            # functional: case-insensitive lookups on name\n            models.Index(Lower(\"name\"), name=\"product_name_lower_idx\"),\n        ]\n        constraints = [\n            # SKU unique only among active products (partial unique index)\n            models.UniqueConstraint(\n                fields=[\"sku\"],\n                condition=Q(is_active=True),\n                name=\"uniq_active_sku\",\n                violation_error_message=\"An active product with this SKU already exists.\",\n            ),\n            models.CheckConstraint(condition=Q(price__gte=0), name=\"price_non_negative\"),\n            models.CheckConstraint(\n                condition=Q(sale_price__isnull=True) | Q(sale_price__lt=F(\"price\")),\n                name=\"sale_below_price\",\n            ),\n        ]\n\n    def __str__(self):\n        return self.name\n```\n\n> **Version note:** `CheckConstraint(check=...)` was renamed to `condition=` in Django 5.1; `check=` still works in 5.2 with a deprecation warning. On 4.2 LTS you must use `check=`. `index_together` was removed in 5.1 — use `Meta.indexes`.\n\nRun `sqlmigrate` to see what Django will execute:\n\n```bash\npython manage.py makemigrations shop\npython manage.py sqlmigrate shop 0001\n```\n\nThe relevant part of the generated SQL looks like this (PostgreSQL):\n\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX \"product_active_recent_idx\" ON \"shop_product\" (\"is_active\", \"created_at\" DESC);\nCREATE INDEX \"product_name_lower_idx\" ON \"shop_product\" (LOWER(\"name\"));\nCREATE UNIQUE INDEX \"uniq_active_sku\" ON \"shop_product\" (\"sku\") WHERE \"is_active\";\nALTER TABLE \"shop_product\" ADD CONSTRAINT \"price_non_negative\" CHECK (\"price\" >= 0);\nALTER TABLE \"shop_product\" ADD CONSTRAINT \"sale_below_price\"\n  CHECK ((\"sale_price\" IS NULL OR \"sale_price\" < (\"price\")));\n```\n\nNote that a **conditional `UniqueConstraint` becomes a partial unique index**, not a table constraint — which is why it cannot be `deferrable`. Using the index from Python:\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models.functions import Lower\n\nqs = Product.objects.annotate(n=Lower(\"name\")).filter(n=\"blue mug\")\nprint(qs.explain())\n```\n\n```text\nIndex Scan using product_name_lower_idx on shop_product  (cost=0.29..8.30 rows=1 width=...)\n  Index Cond: (lower((name)::text) = 'blue mug'::text)\n```\n\nValidation picks up the constraints as well (4.1+):\n\n```python\np = Product(sku=\"A1\", name=\"x\", email_contact=\"a@b.c\", price=-5)\np.full_clean()\n# ValidationError: {'__all__': ['Constraint “price_non_negative” is violated.']}\n```\n\n### 5.2 Raw SQL, done safely\n\n```python\nfrom django.db import connection\nfrom shop.models import Product\n\n\ndef dictfetchall(cursor):\n    cols = [c[0] for c in cursor.description]\n    return [dict(zip(cols, row)) for row in cursor.fetchall()]\n\n\n# 1) Model instances from SQL — must include the primary key\ncheap = Product.objects.raw(\n    \"SELECT id, name, price FROM shop_product WHERE price < %s AND is_active\",\n    [10],\n)\nfor p in cheap:\n    print(p.name, p.price)        # accessing p.sku would trigger a deferred query per row\n\n# 2) Arbitrary rows, e.g. a reporting CTE\ndef revenue_by_day(since):\n    sql = \"\"\"\n        WITH daily AS (\n            SELECT date_trunc('day', created_at) AS day, sum(price) AS revenue\n            FROM shop_product\n            WHERE created_at >= %(since)s\n            GROUP BY 1\n        )\n        SELECT day, revenue, sum(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY day) AS running\n        FROM daily ORDER BY day\n    \"\"\"\n    with connection.cursor() as cursor:\n        cursor.execute(sql, {\"since\": since})\n        return dictfetchall(cursor)\n\n\n# 3) Dynamic ORDER BY — identifiers cannot be parameters, so whitelist\nALLOWED_SORT = {\"price\": \"price\", \"newest\": \"created_at\"}\n\ndef products_sorted(sort_key, direction):\n    column = ALLOWED_SORT.get(sort_key, \"id\")\n    direction = \"DESC\" if direction == \"desc\" else \"ASC\"\n    qn = connection.ops.quote_name\n    sql = f\"SELECT id, name, price FROM shop_product ORDER BY {qn(column)} {direction}\"\n    return list(Product.objects.raw(sql))\n```\n\nCompare the injection-prone version every code reviewer should reject:\n\n```python\n# DANGEROUS: user input becomes SQL syntax\nname = request.GET[\"q\"]               # \"x' OR 1=1 --\"\nProduct.objects.raw(f\"SELECT * FROM shop_product WHERE name = '{name}'\")\nProduct.objects.extra(where=[f\"name = '{name}'\"])\ncursor.execute(\"SELECT * FROM shop_product WHERE name = '%s'\" % name)\n\n# SAFE: values travel as parameters\nProduct.objects.raw(\"SELECT * FROM shop_product WHERE name = %s\", [name])\nProduct.objects.extra(where=[\"name = %s\"], params=[name])\ncursor.execute(\"SELECT * FROM shop_product WHERE name = %s\", [name])\n```\n\nWhen you only need one SQL fragment, prefer a `RawSQL` expression with params, or better, a tiny `Func` subclass so the ORM keeps composing:\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models import Func, FloatField\nfrom django.db.models.expressions import RawSQL\n\nProduct.objects.annotate(score=RawSQL(\"price * %s\", [1.18]))\n\n\nclass Similarity(Func):           # wraps pg_trgm similarity(a, b)\n    function = \"similarity\"\n    output_field = FloatField()\n\nProduct.objects.annotate(sim=Similarity(\"name\", models.Value(\"mug\"))).filter(sim__gt=0.3)\n```\n\n### 5.3 Real-world example — a searchable catalog with JSON attributes and bookings\n\n```python\n# settings.py\nINSTALLED_APPS = [\n    # ...\n    \"django.contrib.postgres\",     # required for __search, trigram lookups, unaccent, etc.\n    \"catalog\",\n]\n```\n\n```python\n# catalog/models.py\nfrom django.contrib.postgres.constraints import ExclusionConstraint\nfrom django.contrib.postgres.fields import ArrayField, DateTimeRangeField, RangeOperators\nfrom django.contrib.postgres.indexes import GinIndex\nfrom django.contrib.postgres.search import SearchVectorField\nfrom django.db import models\n\n\nclass Listing(models.Model):\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    description = models.TextField()\n    tags = ArrayField(models.CharField(max_length=40), default=list, blank=True)\n    attributes = models.JSONField(default=dict, blank=True)    # {\"color\": \"red\", \"size\": {\"w\": 3}}\n    search_vector = SearchVectorField(null=True, editable=False)\n    city = models.CharField(max_length=80)\n\n    class Meta:\n        indexes = [\n            GinIndex(fields=[\"search_vector\"], name=\"listing_search_gin\"),\n            GinIndex(fields=[\"tags\"], name=\"listing_tags_gin\"),\n            GinIndex(fields=[\"attributes\"], name=\"listing_attrs_gin\", opclasses=[\"jsonb_path_ops\"]),\n            GinIndex(fields=[\"title\"], name=\"listing_title_trgm\", opclasses=[\"gin_trgm_ops\"]),\n        ]\n\n\nclass Booking(models.Model):\n    listing = models.ForeignKey(Listing, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"bookings\")\n    during = DateTimeRangeField()\n    cancelled = models.BooleanField(default=False)\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [\n            # No two active bookings for the same listing may overlap in time\n            ExclusionConstraint(\n                name=\"no_overlapping_bookings\",\n                expressions=[\n                    (\"listing\", RangeOperators.EQUAL),\n                    (\"during\", RangeOperators.OVERLAPS),\n                ],\n                condition=models.Q(cancelled=False),\n            ),\n        ]\n```\n\nThe extensions must exist before the indexes — create them in a migration (needs sufficient DB privileges):\n\n```python\n# catalog/migrations/0001_extensions.py\nfrom django.contrib.postgres.operations import BtreeGistExtension, TrigramExtension\nfrom django.db import migrations\n\n\nclass Migration(migrations.Migration):\n    dependencies = []\n    operations = [TrigramExtension(), BtreeGistExtension()]\n```\n\n`btree_gist` is needed because the exclusion constraint mixes a scalar equality (`listing_id =`) with a range overlap in one GiST index. Keeping the search vector fresh, plus the queries:\n\n```python\n# catalog/search.py\nfrom django.contrib.postgres.search import SearchQuery, SearchRank, SearchVector, TrigramSimilarity\nfrom django.db.models import F\nfrom .models import Listing\n\nVECTOR = SearchVector(\"title\", weight=\"A\", config=\"english\") + \\\n         SearchVector(\"description\", weight=\"B\", config=\"english\")\n\n\ndef refresh_vector(listing_ids):\n    Listing.objects.filter(pk__in=listing_ids).update(search_vector=VECTOR)\n\n\ndef search(q, city=None, tags=None, color=None):\n    query = SearchQuery(q, search_type=\"websearch\", config=\"english\")\n    qs = (Listing.objects\n          .filter(search_vector=query)\n          .annotate(rank=SearchRank(F(\"search_vector\"), query))\n          .order_by(\"-rank\"))\n    if city:\n        qs = qs.filter(city=city)\n    if tags:\n        qs = qs.filter(tags__overlap=tags)                 # any of these tags\n    if color:\n        qs = qs.filter(attributes__contains={\"color\": color})   # served by jsonb_path_ops GIN\n    return qs[:20]\n\n\ndef fuzzy_title(q):\n    return (Listing.objects\n            .annotate(sim=TrigramSimilarity(\"title\", q))\n            .filter(sim__gt=0.3).order_by(\"-sim\")[:10])\n```\n\nThe SQL for the `search()` core (trimmed):\n\n```sql\nSELECT ..., ts_rank(\"catalog_listing\".\"search_vector\",\n                    websearch_to_tsquery('english'::regconfig, 'red sofa')) AS \"rank\"\nFROM \"catalog_listing\"\nWHERE \"catalog_listing\".\"search_vector\" @@ websearch_to_tsquery('english'::regconfig, 'red sofa')\n  AND \"catalog_listing\".\"tags\" && ARRAY['vintage']::varchar(40)[]\n  AND \"catalog_listing\".\"attributes\" @> '{\"color\": \"red\"}'::jsonb\nORDER BY \"rank\" DESC\nLIMIT 20;\n```\n\nCall `refresh_vector()` from a Celery task dispatched with `transaction.on_commit` after a listing is saved, or maintain it with a database trigger. On 5.0+ you can alternatively use `GeneratedField(expression=..., output_field=SearchVectorField(), db_persist=True)`, but the expression must be immutable in PostgreSQL (an explicit text search config is required), so test it on your PG version.\n\nBooking overlap is now impossible at the database level:\n\n```python\nfrom datetime import datetime, timezone\nfrom django.db import IntegrityError, transaction\nfrom psycopg.types.range import Range   # psycopg 3\n\ndef book(listing, start, end):\n    try:\n        with transaction.atomic():\n            return Booking.objects.create(listing=listing, during=Range(start, end))\n    except IntegrityError:\n        raise ValueError(\"Those dates are taken\")\n```\n\nJSON key lookups, and what they compile to:\n\n```python\nListing.objects.filter(attributes__color=\"red\")           # attributes -> 'color' = '\"red\"'\nListing.objects.filter(attributes__size__w__gte=3)         # nested key path\nListing.objects.filter(attributes__has_key=\"warranty\")     # attributes ? 'warranty'\nListing.objects.filter(tags__contains=[\"vintage\", \"oak\"])  # tags @> ARRAY[...]\nListing.objects.filter(tags__len=0)                        # array_length(...) — not index-friendly\n```\n\n### 5.4 Indexing a big table without downtime\n\n```python\n# catalog/migrations/0007_listing_city_idx.py\nfrom django.contrib.postgres.operations import AddIndexConcurrently\nfrom django.db import migrations, models\n\n\nclass Migration(migrations.Migration):\n    atomic = False      # CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY cannot run inside a transaction\n    dependencies = [(\"catalog\", \"0006_previous\")]\n    operations = [\n        AddIndexConcurrently(\"listing\", models.Index(fields=[\"city\"], name=\"listing_city_idx\")),\n    ]\n```\n\nIf a concurrent build fails, PostgreSQL leaves an **INVALID** index behind; drop it (`RemoveIndexConcurrently` or `DROP INDEX CONCURRENTLY`) and retry. Adding a `CheckConstraint` to a large table takes a lock while it scans; for very large tables teams sometimes add it `NOT VALID` via `RunSQL` and `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT` later, keeping the model state in sync with `SeparateDatabaseAndState` (see [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations)).\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Tool | Gains | Costs | Use it for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Meta.indexes` B-tree | Fast equality/range/sort | Write amplification, disk, more WAL | Hot filters and ORDER BYs |\n| Partial index | Small, very fast for a hot subset | Only used when WHERE implies condition | `status='pending'`, soft-delete live rows |\n| Functional index | Case-insensitive / computed lookups | Must exactly match generated SQL | `Lower(email)`, `Upper` for `iexact` |\n| `UniqueConstraint` | Race-proof uniqueness | `IntegrityError` handling needed | Business keys, \"one active per user\" |\n| `CheckConstraint` | Domain rules on every write path | Large-table add needs care | Non-negative amounts, `end > start` |\n| `ExclusionConstraint` | No overlapping ranges, atomically | Postgres-only, GiST + btree_gist | Bookings, schedules, IP ranges |\n| `JSONField` | Schema flexibility, no migration | Weak typing, harder indexing, bloated rows | Truly variable attributes, payload snapshots |\n| `ArrayField` | No join table, GIN-indexable | No FK integrity, awkward updates | Tags, small value lists |\n| Postgres FTS | No extra infrastructure, transactional | Limited relevance tuning, scaling ceiling | Search for up to a few million docs |\n| Raw SQL | Any SQL, full control | Portability, injection risk, no composition | Reporting CTEs, vendor features |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Constraints whenever a rule must hold for **all** writers — including bulk ops, admin, scripts, and other services.\n- Partial unique constraints for \"unique among active/non-deleted rows\" (pairs with soft delete in [Ch 15](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance)).\n- `JSONField` for data you store and return mostly as a blob, or for long-tail attributes queried with `contains`.\n- Postgres FTS when search is a feature, not the product.\n- Raw SQL for recursive CTEs, `LATERAL`, `DISTINCT ON` combos, or DB functions with no ORM equivalent — wrapped in one well-tested function.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Do not index every column \"just in case\": each index slows every INSERT/UPDATE and bloats vacuum.\n- Do not put frequently filtered, typed, relational data in `JSONField` — make it a column (you lose FKs, types, and simple indexes).\n- Do not use `ArrayField` for things that need referential integrity (user IDs) — use M2M.\n- Do not pick Postgres FTS for typo-tolerant, faceted, multilingual search at scale — use OpenSearch/Elasticsearch.\n- Do not reach for `extra()` in new code; it is a legacy API.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Relying on `exists()` checks for uniqueness.** Two requests race and both insert. *Instead:* a `UniqueConstraint` plus `get_or_create`/`update_or_create`, or catch `IntegrityError` in a nested `atomic()`.\n\n**2. Formatting SQL strings.** `raw(f\"... '{q}'\")` or `\"%s\" % q` is SQL injection. *Instead:* pass `params`. Note the difference: `cursor.execute(sql, [q])` is safe, `cursor.execute(sql % q)` is not.\n\n**3. Functional index that the query never uses.**\n\n```python\n# Before: index on Lower(\"email\") but query uses iexact -> UPPER(...) in SQL, index unused\nUser.objects.filter(email__iexact=e)\n\n# After: query the same expression the index was built on\nUser.objects.annotate(e=Lower(\"email\")).filter(e=e.lower())\n# or index Upper(\"email\"), or store a normalized/citext column\n```\n\n**4. Missing `django.contrib.postgres` in `INSTALLED_APPS`.** The `__search`, `__trigram_similar` and `__unaccent` lookups are registered by the app; without it you get `FieldError: Unsupported lookup`.\n\n**5. Catching `IntegrityError` without a savepoint.** Inside `ATOMIC_REQUESTS` or an outer `atomic()`, swallowing the error leaves the transaction broken (`TransactionManagementError` on the next query). *Instead:* wrap just the insert in its own `with transaction.atomic():` (see [Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency)).\n\n**6. `null=True` on a field in a `UniqueConstraint` without thinking about NULLs.** In SQL, NULLs are distinct by default, so many rows with `NULL` pass. On PostgreSQL 15+ use `nulls_distinct=False` (Django 5.0+) if you want at most one NULL.\n\n**7. Plain `AddIndex` on a huge table in a deploy.** It blocks writes for the duration. *Instead:* `AddIndexConcurrently` with `atomic = False`, as its own migration.\n\n**8. Treating `.raw()` like a QuerySet.** It cannot be filtered further or `count()`-ed cheaply, and skipping columns makes deferred loads (an N+1 in disguise). Select every column you will touch.\n\n**Best practices checklist:** name every index/constraint deliberately; review `sqlmigrate` output; check plans with `qs.explain(analyze=True)` on production-like data; keep raw SQL in one module with tests; add `violation_error_message` for user-facing constraints.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: unused indexes eating write throughput.** In production, insert latency on `orders` creeps up after months of \"just add an index\". Root cause: a dozen overlapping indexes. Debug with `pg_stat_user_indexes` (`idx_scan = 0` over weeks) and drop redundant ones — e.g. the auto FK index on `tenant_id` when `(tenant_id, created_at)` exists.\n\n**Pitfall: deploy hangs on migration.** `ALTER TABLE ... ADD CONSTRAINT` waits for an `ACCESS EXCLUSIVE` lock behind a long-running analytics query; meanwhile every request queues behind the waiting lock and the site goes down. Fix: set `lock_timeout` for migrations (e.g. via `RunSQL(\"SET lock_timeout = '5s'\")` or DB role settings), retry, and use concurrent/`NOT VALID` patterns.\n\n**Pitfall: JSONField query is slow despite a GIN index.** `attributes__color=\"red\"` compiles to a `->` key comparison, which a default `jsonb_ops`/`jsonb_path_ops` GIN index does **not** serve; GIN serves containment (`@>`), `?` key existence (`jsonb_ops` only). Fix: rewrite as `attributes__contains={\"color\": \"red\"}`, or add a functional B-tree index on `KeyTextTransform(\"color\", \"attributes\")`, or promote the key to a column.\n\n**Pitfall: Full-text results go stale.** The `SearchVectorField` is only updated when your code updates it; a bulk import via `bulk_create` skipped your signal. Fix: a DB trigger or `GeneratedField`, or a periodic reindex task.\n\n**Pitfall: IntegrityError surfaces as a 500.** A constraint fires during a race and the view does not handle it. Fix: catch `IntegrityError` at the write and translate to a 409/400; log constraint name (`e.__cause__.diag.constraint_name` on psycopg) for metrics.\n\n**Debugging toolkit:**\n\n```python\nfrom django.db import connection, reset_queries\nqs = Listing.objects.filter(attributes__contains={\"color\": \"red\"})\nprint(qs.query)                                   # approximate SQL\nprint(qs.explain(analyze=True, buffers=True))     # PostgreSQL options pass through\n# DEBUG=True: connection.queries holds executed SQL and timings\n```\n\n- `pg_stat_statements` for top queries by total time; Sentry performance spans for per-request SQL.\n- `django-debug-toolbar` SQL panel shows duplicated queries and lets you EXPLAIN in the browser.\n- `manage.py dbshell` then `\\d+ catalog_listing` to confirm what indexes actually exist (migrations state can drift from reality).\n\n**Scaling notes:** Partial and covering indexes (`include=[\"total\"]`) enable index-only scans for hot dashboards. For time-series tables consider `BrinIndex` and declarative partitioning (Django has no native partition API; manage with `RunSQL` or a library). When search outgrows Postgres, keep Postgres as the source of truth and stream changes to a search cluster via an outbox/CDC (see [Messaging · Outbox](../messaging/topic.html?p=21-idempotency-outbox)).\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `db_index=True`, `unique=True`, `Meta.indexes` and `Meta.constraints`?**\nA: `db_index` and `unique` are single-column shortcuts on the field; `unique=True` also implies an index. `Meta.indexes` declares named indexes that can be composite, descending, functional (expressions), partial (`condition=`), covering (`include=`) or use custom operator classes. `Meta.constraints` declares rules the database enforces — `UniqueConstraint`, `CheckConstraint` and, on PostgreSQL, `ExclusionConstraint`. An index is a performance tool; a constraint is a correctness tool that may be implemented with an index (unique, exclusion). Prefer `Meta` declarations for anything beyond a trivial single-column index.\n\n**Q: How do you enforce \"email unique only among non-deleted users\"?**\nA: Use a conditional `UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"email\"], condition=Q(deleted_at__isnull=True), name=\"uniq_live_email\")`. On PostgreSQL this becomes a partial unique index, so deleted rows can share the email while live rows cannot. Pair it with a functional expression (`Lower(\"email\")`) if uniqueness should be case-insensitive. Django 4.1+ validates it in `full_clean()`, so a ModelForm shows a friendly error, while the DB still guarantees correctness under concurrency.\n\n**Q: Why prefer `UniqueConstraint` over `unique_together`?**\nA: `UniqueConstraint` is a superset: it supports conditions, expressions, deferrable constraints, `include`, `nulls_distinct` and custom error messages/codes, and it sits alongside other constraints in `Meta.constraints`. The docs state `unique_together` may be deprecated in the future. Functionally a plain `UniqueConstraint(fields=[a, b])` produces the same unique index, so migrating is low-risk.\n\n**Q: Is `Model.objects.raw()` safe from SQL injection?**\nA: It is safe only if you pass user input through the `params` argument: `raw(\"... WHERE name = %s\", [name])`. The driver then sends the value separately from the SQL text. If you build the string with f-strings, `%` formatting or concatenation, it is injectable — `raw()` does no escaping of the SQL string itself. The same applies to `cursor.execute`, `RawSQL` and `extra()`. Also don't quote placeholders (`'%s'`) and never parameterize identifiers — whitelist them.\n\n**Q: How do you safely build a dynamic ORDER BY or table name in raw SQL?**\nA: Placeholders only work for values, so identifiers must come from a server-side whitelist mapping user choices to known column names; then quote with `connection.ops.quote_name()`. Direction keywords are chosen from a fixed pair (`ASC`/`DESC`). Better still, stay in the ORM: `order_by()` with a whitelisted field name is injection-safe because Django validates field names against the model.\n\n**Q: What does `raw()` require, and what are its limitations?**\nA: The query must return the model's primary key column; Django maps other columns to fields by name (use `translations=` to remap). Columns you don't select become deferred fields that trigger an extra query on access. A `RawQuerySet` can be iterated and indexed but cannot be further filtered, annotated or chained, and `len()` executes the whole query. Extra selected columns become plain attributes on the instances.\n\n**Q: When would you use `JSONField` and when a normal column?**\nA: Use JSONField for genuinely schemaless data: per-tenant custom attributes, third-party webhook payloads, settings blobs — data mostly read and written as a whole or queried by containment. Use real columns for anything typed, frequently filtered or sorted, or relational, because columns get types, constraints, FKs, simple B-tree indexes and accurate planner statistics. A common production smell is a JSON key that every query filters on — promote it to a column (or at least a functional index).\n\n**Q: How do you index a JSONField in PostgreSQL?**\nA: For containment queries (`__contains`, `@>`) add `GinIndex(fields=[\"data\"], opclasses=[\"jsonb_path_ops\"], name=...)`; the default `jsonb_ops` also supports key-existence (`has_key`). For equality/range on one key, add a functional B-tree index on the key expression, e.g. `Index(KeyTextTransform(\"color\", \"data\"), name=...)`, and query with the matching lookup. Always confirm with `explain()`, because `data__color=\"red\"` and `data__contains={\"color\": \"red\"}` compile to different operators served by different indexes.\n\n**Q: How does full-text search work in Django with PostgreSQL?**\nA: `SearchVector` produces a `tsvector` (normalized lexemes, optionally weighted), `SearchQuery` produces a `tsquery` (plain, phrase, raw or websearch syntax), and the `@@` match is what `filter(vector=query)` emits; `SearchRank` orders results. Computing the vector per query is slow, so store it in a `SearchVectorField`, keep it updated (task, trigger or GeneratedField) and put a GIN index on it. For typo tolerance add `pg_trgm` and `TrigramSimilarity`. It requires `django.contrib.postgres` in INSTALLED_APPS.\n\n**Q: You must add an index to a 200M-row table in production. How? (Senior)**\nA: A plain `CREATE INDEX` holds a lock that blocks writes for the whole build, so use `AddIndexConcurrently` from `django.contrib.postgres.operations` in its own migration with `atomic = False`. Run it as a separate release step, set a `lock_timeout`/`statement_timeout` appropriate to the build, and monitor. If it fails it leaves an INVALID index that you must drop and recreate. Confirm usage afterwards with `explain(analyze=True)` and `pg_stat_user_indexes`, and consider whether a partial index on the hot subset would be far smaller. Similar care applies to constraints: add as `NOT VALID` via `RunSQL` then `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT`, with `SeparateDatabaseAndState` to keep Django's state consistent.\n\n**Q: How would you guarantee no double-booking of a room under concurrency? (Senior)**\nA: Application checks (\"is there an overlapping booking?\") are racy under READ COMMITTED. Options: (1) an `ExclusionConstraint` on `(room =, during &&)` with a GiST index and `btree_gist` — the database atomically rejects overlaps and you translate `IntegrityError` to a 409; (2) lock a parent row with `select_for_update()` on the room before checking and inserting, serializing bookings per room; (3) for slot-based systems, a `UniqueConstraint(room, slot)`. The exclusion constraint is the strongest because it protects every write path, not just the code path that remembers to lock. Mention handling cancelled bookings via the constraint's `condition`.\n\n**Q: Your JSONB filter is slow even though there is a GIN index. How do you debug it? (Senior)**\nA: Start with `qs.explain(analyze=True)` and `str(qs.query)` to see the generated operator. Key-path lookups like `data__status=\"x\"` compile to `->`/`->>` comparisons that GIN does not serve; only containment (`@>`) and existence operators use it, and `jsonb_path_ops` doesn't support `?`. Fix by rewriting as `data__contains={...}`, adding a functional B-tree index on the extracted key, or promoting the key to a column. Also check statistics: the planner has poor selectivity estimates for JSON contents, so `ANALYZE` and possibly extended statistics or a real column give better plans.\n\n**Q: When is raw SQL the right call in a Django codebase, and how do you keep it maintainable? (Senior)**\nA: When the ORM cannot express the query efficiently — recursive CTEs for trees, `LATERAL` joins, vendor functions, bulk upserts with complex conflict handling, or hand-tuned reporting queries. First try `Subquery`, `Window`, custom `Func`/`Lookup` classes, which keep composability. If raw SQL wins, isolate it in a repository/service function with a typed return, parameterize everything, cover it with tests against real PostgreSQL, and reference table names via `Model._meta.db_table` so renames don't silently break it. Document why the ORM version wasn't used, with EXPLAIN evidence.\n\n**Q: Do constraints in `Meta.constraints` get validated by `save()`?**\nA: No. `save()` sends the INSERT/UPDATE and the database enforces the constraint, raising `IntegrityError` on violation. `full_clean()` (called by ModelForm and DRF ModelSerializer for unique validators, but not by `save()`) runs `validate_constraints()` since Django 4.1 and raises `ValidationError` with your `violation_error_message`. Python-side validation can still race, so the DB check is the real guarantee.\n\n**Q: What is the extra `_like` index Django creates on PostgreSQL?**\nA: For `CharField`/`TextField` with `db_index=True` or `unique=True`, Django adds a second index using `varchar_pattern_ops`/`text_pattern_ops` so that `LIKE 'abc%'` (`startswith`) can use an index under non-C collations. If you never do prefix searches it is wasted write cost; you can declare the index explicitly via `Meta.indexes` instead of `db_index=True` to avoid it.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Need | Django |\n|---|---|\n| Composite index | `models.Index(fields=[\"a\", \"-b\"], name=...)` |\n| Case-insensitive index | `models.Index(Lower(\"email\"), name=...)` |\n| Partial index | `models.Index(fields=[\"created_at\"], condition=Q(status=\"pending\"), name=...)` |\n| Covering index | `models.Index(fields=[\"user\"], include=[\"total\"], name=...)` |\n| Unique among active | `UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"sku\"], condition=Q(is_active=True), name=...)` |\n| Domain rule | `CheckConstraint(condition=Q(price__gte=0), name=...)` |\n| No overlap | `ExclusionConstraint(expressions=[(\"room\", \"=\"), (\"during\", \"&&\")])` |\n| Non-blocking index | `AddIndexConcurrently(...)` + `atomic = False` |\n| Instances from SQL | `Model.objects.raw(sql, params)` (include PK) |\n| Arbitrary SQL | `with connection.cursor() as c: c.execute(sql, params)` |\n| JSON containment | `filter(data__contains={...})` + `GinIndex(opclasses=[\"jsonb_path_ops\"])` |\n| Full-text | `SearchVectorField` + `GinIndex` + `SearchQuery(search_type=\"websearch\")` |\n\n- Constraints are correctness; validation is UX. Put rules in the DB.\n- Every index costs writes — measure with `pg_stat_user_indexes`.\n- The index must match the SQL Django *generates*; verify with `explain()`.\n- Placeholders are `%s` on all backends, never quoted, values only.\n- `extra()` is legacy; prefer expressions, `RawSQL` with params, or `raw()`.\n- `django.contrib.postgres` must be in `INSTALLED_APPS` for search/trigram lookups.\n- Extensions (`pg_trgm`, `btree_gist`) come first, via migration operations.\n- 5.1+: `CheckConstraint(condition=...)`; `index_together` is gone.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" \"psycopg[binary]\"`, run PostgreSQL locally (e.g. `docker run -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=pw -p 5432:5432 postgres:16`), point `DATABASES` at it.\n\n1. **Partial unique.** Build `Product` from §5.1. Insert two inactive rows with the same SKU (succeeds) and two active ones (fails with `IntegrityError`). Then call `full_clean()` and read the `ValidationError`.\n2. **Index matching.** Seed 200k rows. Compare `explain(analyze=True)` for `name__iexact`, `annotate(n=Lower(\"name\")).filter(n=...)`, with and without the functional index. Write down which index each uses.\n3. **Injection lab.** Write a deliberately vulnerable view using `raw(f\"...\")`, exploit it with `' OR 1=1 --`, then fix it with params and a whitelist for sorting.\n4. **JSON indexing.** Store 100k `attributes` blobs; measure `attributes__color=\"red\"` vs `attributes__contains={\"color\": \"red\"}` with a `jsonb_path_ops` GIN index.\n5. **Concurrent index.** Write a migration with `AddIndexConcurrently`; run `sqlmigrate` to confirm `CONCURRENTLY`; try it without `atomic = False` and read the error.\n\n**Mini project — \"Rentals\":** listings with tags (`ArrayField`), JSON amenities, weighted full-text search with a stored `SearchVectorField` refreshed by a task, trigram \"did you mean\", and bookings guarded by an `ExclusionConstraint`. Add a load test that fires 50 concurrent booking requests for the same dates and prove exactly one succeeds.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) · [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations) · [Ch 12 · Aggregation, Annotation, Subqueries & Window Functions](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries) · [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) · [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) · [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security) · [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [SQL · Indexes](../sql/topic.html?p=19-indexes) · [SQL · Index Design](../sql/topic.html?p=20-index-design) · [SQL · Execution Plans](../sql/topic.html?p=22-execution-plans) · [SQL · JSON & Semi-structured](../sql/topic.html?p=24-json-semistructured) · [SQL · Keys & Constraints](../sql/topic.html?p=29-keys-constraints) · [Database Design · Schema Evolution](../database-design/topic.html?p=27-schema-evolution)\n\n**Free resources:**\n- **Models: Indexes and Constraints reference** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · authoritative option list for `Index`, `UniqueConstraint`, `CheckConstraint`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/indexes/>\n- **Performing raw SQL queries** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `raw()`, cursors, params and the injection warnings. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/sql/>\n- **django.contrib.postgres** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · fields, indexes, constraints, search, operations. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/contrib/postgres/>\n- **Full text search in Django** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · SearchVector/Query/Rank with weighting and performance notes. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/contrib/postgres/search/>\n- **PostgreSQL: Indexes chapter** — PostgreSQL docs · *Intermediate* · partial, expression, GIN/GiST and index-only scans. <https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/indexes.html>\n- **PostgreSQL: Full Text Search** — PostgreSQL docs · *Advanced* · tsvector/tsquery internals and dictionaries. <https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/textsearch.html>\n- **Use The Index, Luke** — Markus Winand · *Intermediate* · the best free explanation of how B-tree indexes serve queries. <https://use-the-index-luke.com/>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 16.*\n", "17-class-based-views": "# 17 · Class-Based & Generic Views\n\n> **In one line:** A class-based view is a function factory: `as_view()` returns a plain function that builds a fresh instance per request and calls `dispatch()`, which routes to `get()`/`post()`; generic views are stacks of small mixins wired together by Python's MRO, so mastering CBVs means knowing which hook (`get_queryset`, `get_object`, `get_context_data`, `form_valid`) each mixin calls and in what order.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 03 · URL Routing & URLconf](topic.html?p=03-urls-routing), [Ch 04 · Views, HttpRequest & HttpResponse](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses), [Ch 05 · Django Template Language](topic.html?p=05-templates), [Ch 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation).\n\nA function-based view (FBV) is just `def view(request, ...) -> HttpResponse`. That is perfect for one-off endpoints, but CRUD-heavy apps repeat the same skeleton dozens of times: fetch an object or 404, branch on `request.method`, bind a form, validate, save, redirect, render a template with a context. Copy-paste of that skeleton is the naive approach, and it fails in the usual ways — subtle inconsistencies (one view forgets the 404, another forgets `login_required`), no reuse beyond helper functions, and changes that must be applied in twenty places.\n\n**Class-based views (CBVs)** factor that skeleton into overridable methods. **Generic views** (`ListView`, `DetailView`, `CreateView`, `UpdateView`, `DeleteView`, `FormView`, `TemplateView`, `RedirectView`, date-based views) are prebuilt combinations of **mixins**: `ListView` = `MultipleObjectTemplateResponseMixin` + `BaseListView` (which is `MultipleObjectMixin` + `View`). You customize behaviour by setting attributes (`model`, `paginate_by`, `template_name`) or overriding hook methods.\n\nThe cost is **indirection**: the code that runs is spread across six classes, and a newcomer cannot see where the template name or the redirect came from. That is why the classic interview question is \"FBV or CBV?\", followed by \"explain the MRO of this view\" and \"where do you put the queryset filtering?\" This chapter is the home for CBV mechanics; DRF's `APIView`/ViewSets build on exactly the same `as_view()`/`dispatch()` design and are covered in [Ch 22](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers).\n\n> **Interview tip:** The answer to \"FBV or CBV?\" is never \"CBV is better\". It is \"CBVs for standard CRUD and shared behaviour via mixins; FBVs for bespoke logic where explicit control flow beats inheritance — and consistency within a codebase matters more than either.\"\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **`View`** — the base class in `django.views.generic.base`. Provides `as_view()`, `setup()`, `dispatch()`, `http_method_not_allowed()`, `options()`. *Why it matters:* every CBV (and DRF's `APIView`) is built on it.\n- **`as_view(**initkwargs)`** — classmethod returning a function `view(request, *args, **kwargs)` for the URLconf. *Why it matters:* the URLconf needs a callable; `as_view()` bridges classes to that contract and validates initkwargs.\n- **Per-request instance** — `view()` creates `cls(**initkwargs)` on **every** request. *Why it matters:* `self.foo = ...` is request-local and thread-safe; class attributes are shared across all requests.\n- **`setup()`** — assigns `self.request`, `self.args`, `self.kwargs` (since 2.2). *Why it matters:* override it to initialize attributes needed by `dispatch()`.\n- **`dispatch()`** — looks up `getattr(self, request.method.lower())` if the method is in `http_method_names`, else `http_method_not_allowed` (405). *Why it matters:* the natural place for cross-cutting checks (permission mixins override it).\n- **Mixin** — a small class that contributes methods and attributes and calls `super()` to cooperate. *Why it matters:* reuse without deep single-purpose hierarchies.\n- **MRO (Method Resolution Order)** — Python's C3 linearization of the class hierarchy, visible as `Cls.__mro__`. *Why it matters:* determines which `dispatch()` or `get_context_data()` runs first; mixin order in the class statement is behaviour.\n- **`TemplateResponseMixin`** — `render_to_response()` returning a lazy `TemplateResponse`; `get_template_names()`. *Why it matters:* template responses can be modified by middleware (`process_template_response`) before rendering.\n- **`ContextMixin.get_context_data(**kwargs)`** — builds the template context, includes `view` and `extra_context`. *Why it matters:* always call `super()` or you lose `object`, `page_obj`, `form`.\n- **`SingleObjectMixin`** — `get_object()` from `pk_url_kwarg`/`slug_url_kwarg` and `get_queryset()`; raises `Http404`. *Why it matters:* override `get_queryset()` to scope objects (e.g. to `request.user`) — the authorization foundation.\n- **`MultipleObjectMixin`** — `get_queryset()`, `paginate_by`, `ordering`, `context_object_name`. *Why it matters:* pagination and listing for free.\n- **`FormMixin` / `ModelFormMixin` / `ProcessFormView`** — `get_form()`, `form_valid()`, `form_invalid()`, `get_success_url()`. *Why it matters:* the POST pipeline of every editing view.\n- **`method_decorator`** — adapts a function decorator to a method: `@method_decorator(login_required, name=\"dispatch\")`. *Why it matters:* reuse FBV decorators on CBVs.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 What `as_view()` actually does\n\nStripped of details, `View.as_view` in Django 5.2 looks like this:\n\n```python\nclass View:\n    http_method_names = [\"get\", \"post\", \"put\", \"patch\", \"delete\", \"head\", \"options\", \"trace\"]\n\n    def __init__(self, **kwargs):\n        for key, value in kwargs.items():\n            setattr(self, key, value)\n\n    @classonlymethod\n    def as_view(cls, **initkwargs):\n        for key in initkwargs:\n            if key in cls.http_method_names:\n                raise TypeError(\"... method name as keyword argument ...\")\n            if not hasattr(cls, key):\n                raise TypeError(\"... received an invalid keyword ...\")\n\n        def view(request, *args, **kwargs):\n            self = cls(**initkwargs)              # NEW instance per request\n            self.setup(request, *args, **kwargs)  # sets self.request/args/kwargs\n            if not hasattr(self, \"request\"):\n                raise AttributeError(\"setup() did not call super()\")\n            return self.dispatch(request, *args, **kwargs)\n\n        view.view_class = cls\n        view.view_initkwargs = initkwargs\n        # async views: view is marked as a coroutine function if handlers are async\n        return view\n\n    def setup(self, request, *args, **kwargs):\n        if hasattr(self, \"get\") and not hasattr(self, \"head\"):\n            self.head = self.get                  # HEAD handled by get()\n        self.request, self.args, self.kwargs = request, args, kwargs\n\n    def dispatch(self, request, *args, **kwargs):\n        if request.method.lower() in self.http_method_names:\n            handler = getattr(self, request.method.lower(), self.http_method_not_allowed)\n        else:\n            handler = self.http_method_not_allowed\n        return handler(request, *args, **kwargs)\n```\n\nThree consequences interviewers like:\n\n1. **Instance per request** — state on `self` cannot leak between requests. But **class attributes are shared**: `queryset = Book.objects.all()` is evaluated lazily and `get_queryset()` calls `.all()` on it to get a fresh clone; a mutable class attribute like `extra = []` that you `.append()` to *will* leak.\n2. **initkwargs are validated** — `as_view(template_name=\"x.html\")` works only because `template_name` is a class attribute; typos raise `TypeError` at import time.\n3. **405 is automatic** — a `ListView` has no `post`, so POST returns `HttpResponseNotAllowed` with an `Allow` header built from the defined handlers. `OPTIONS` is answered by `options()`.\n\n> **Version note:** Since Django 4.1, a CBV may define `async def get(...)` etc. All handlers on a view must be either all sync or all async (`view_is_async` checks this and raises `ImproperlyConfigured` on a mix). Generic views' internals (`get_object`, `get_queryset`) are sync, so async CBVs are mostly useful with plain `View`.\n\n### 3.2 MRO and cooperative `super()`\n\nPython linearizes multiple inheritance with **C3**: a class precedes its bases, and the left-to-right order of bases in the class statement is preserved. Each method calls `super().method()`, which means \"the next class in the MRO of the *instance's* class\", not \"my parent\". That is what lets independent mixins stack.\n\n```python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.mixins import LoginRequiredMixin\nfrom django.views.generic import UpdateView\n\nclass ArticleUpdate(LoginRequiredMixin, UpdateView):\n    model = Article\n    fields = [\"title\", \"body\"]\n\nprint([c.__name__ for c in ArticleUpdate.__mro__])\n```\n\n```text\n['ArticleUpdate', 'LoginRequiredMixin', 'AccessMixin', 'UpdateView',\n 'SingleObjectTemplateResponseMixin', 'TemplateResponseMixin', 'BaseUpdateView',\n 'ModelFormMixin', 'FormMixin', 'SingleObjectMixin', 'ContextMixin',\n 'ProcessFormView', 'View', 'object']\n```\n\nWhen `dispatch()` is called, Python finds `LoginRequiredMixin.dispatch` first. It checks `request.user.is_authenticated`, and either returns `handle_no_permission()` (redirect to login) or calls `super().dispatch()`, which continues down the MRO to `View.dispatch`. Put the mixin on the **right** of `UpdateView` and `View.dispatch` is found first — it never calls `super()`, so **the login check silently never runs**. That is the single most important MRO fact for Django.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 340\" width=\"100%\" height=\"340\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj17a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj17b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">dispatch() lookup walks the MRO left to right: mixin order is behaviour</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"400\" height=\"280\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">class V(LoginRequiredMixin, UpdateView)</text>\n  <rect x=\"60\" y=\"80\" width=\"320\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"220\" y=\"101\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">LoginRequiredMixin.dispatch: check auth</text>\n  <rect x=\"60\" y=\"140\" width=\"320\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"220\" y=\"161\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">super().dispatch() ... skips classes w/o dispatch</text>\n  <rect x=\"60\" y=\"200\" width=\"320\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"220\" y=\"221\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">View.dispatch: pick get()/post()</text>\n  <rect x=\"60\" y=\"260\" width=\"320\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"220\" y=\"281\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">ProcessFormView.post -&gt; form_valid</text>\n  <path d=\"M220,116 L220,138\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M220,176 L220,198\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M220,236 L220,258\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"440\" y=\"40\" width=\"400\" height=\"280\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"640\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">class V(UpdateView, LoginRequiredMixin)</text>\n  <rect x=\"480\" y=\"80\" width=\"320\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"640\" y=\"101\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">View.dispatch found first (in UpdateView chain)</text>\n  <rect x=\"480\" y=\"140\" width=\"320\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"640\" y=\"161\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">View.dispatch never calls super()</text>\n  <rect x=\"480\" y=\"200\" width=\"320\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"640\" y=\"221\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">LoginRequiredMixin.dispatch: never runs</text>\n  <rect x=\"480\" y=\"260\" width=\"320\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"640\" y=\"281\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">anonymous user edits the article</text>\n  <path d=\"M640,116 L640,138\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M640,176 L640,258\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5,4\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17b)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\nRule of thumb: **access-control mixins go leftmost, the generic view goes rightmost**, and your own mixins that tweak hooks go in between.\n\n### 3.3 The hook order inside generic views\n\nFor **`DetailView.get()`**: `get_object()` → (calls `get_queryset()`, filters by pk/slug, `.get()`, 404 on `DoesNotExist`) → `self.object = ...` → `get_context_data(object=...)` (adds `object` and the `context_object_name`, e.g. `article`) → `render_to_response()` → `get_template_names()` (`template_name`, else `<app>/<model>_detail.html`).\n\nFor **`ListView.get()`**: `self.object_list = get_queryset()` (uses `queryset` or `model._default_manager.all()`, applies `ordering`) → `allow_empty` check → `get_context_data()` → `paginate_queryset()` if `paginate_by` (adds `paginator`, `page_obj`, `is_paginated`; an out-of-range page raises `Http404`) → render `<app>/<model>_list.html`.\n\nFor **`CreateView.post()` / `UpdateView.post()`**: `self.object = None` (create) or `get_object()` (update) → `get_form()` (`get_form_class()` from `fields`/`form_class`, `get_form_kwargs()` adds `data`, `files`, `instance`, `initial`, `prefix`) → `form.is_valid()` → `form_valid(form)` (`ModelFormMixin`: `self.object = form.save()`, then `FormMixin.form_valid` redirects to `get_success_url()`) or `form_invalid(form)` (re-render with errors, status 200).\n\n`get_success_url()` uses `success_url` (which may contain `{id}`-style placeholders formatted with `self.object.__dict__`), else `self.object.get_absolute_url()`, else raises `ImproperlyConfigured`.\n\n**`DeleteView`** since Django 4.0 includes `FormMixin`: GET shows the confirmation template (`_confirm_delete.html`); POST validates a (empty) form and `form_valid()` deletes the object and redirects. Custom delete logic now belongs in `form_valid()`, not `delete()`.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 From URLconf to response\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 300\" width=\"100%\" height=\"300\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj17c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">UpdateView POST: the call chain one request takes</text>\n  <rect x=\"15\" y=\"45\" width=\"130\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">URLconf</text><text x=\"80\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">path(..., V.as_view())</text>\n  <rect x=\"175\" y=\"45\" width=\"130\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"240\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">view()</text><text x=\"240\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">cls(**initkwargs)</text>\n  <rect x=\"335\" y=\"45\" width=\"130\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"400\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">setup()</text><text x=\"400\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">self.request/kwargs</text>\n  <rect x=\"495\" y=\"45\" width=\"130\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"560\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">dispatch()</text><text x=\"560\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">mixins, then View</text>\n  <rect x=\"655\" y=\"45\" width=\"190\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"750\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">post()</text><text x=\"750\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">BaseUpdateView</text>\n  <path d=\"M147,67 L173,67\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M307,67 L333,67\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M467,67 L493,67\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M627,67 L653,67\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"655\" y=\"125\" width=\"190\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"750\" y=\"149\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">get_object() / get_queryset()</text>\n  <rect x=\"440\" y=\"125\" width=\"190\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"535\" y=\"149\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">get_form(get_form_kwargs())</text>\n  <rect x=\"225\" y=\"125\" width=\"190\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/><text x=\"320\" y=\"149\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">form.is_valid()</text>\n  <path d=\"M750,91 L750,123\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M653,145 L632,145\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M438,145 L417,145\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"15\" y=\"200\" width=\"300\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"165\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">valid: form_valid(form)</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">self.object = form.save()</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"257\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">302 to get_success_url()</text>\n  <rect x=\"345\" y=\"200\" width=\"300\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"495\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">invalid: form_invalid(form)</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">get_context_data(form=form)</text><text x=\"495\" y=\"257\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">200, re-render template with errors</text>\n  <path d=\"M290,167 L200,198\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M350,167 L450,198\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj17c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Http404 here if the object</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"241\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">is not in get_queryset()</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 4.2 Which hook to override\n\n```text\nWant to...                                   Override\n-------------------------------------------  -----------------------------------\nscope rows to the user / tenant              get_queryset()\nlook up by something other than pk/slug      get_object()  (or slug_field/slug_url_kwarg)\nadd data to the template                     get_context_data(**kwargs) + super()\nset fields the user must not control         form_valid(): form.instance.owner = request.user\npass request/user into the form              get_form_kwargs()\npick template dynamically                    get_template_names()\nredirect somewhere computed                  get_success_url()\ncheck permissions before anything            dispatch() via a mixin (leftmost)\ninitialize attributes from URL kwargs        setup()\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example — a CRUD set for articles\n\n```python\n# blog/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.urls import reverse\n\n\nclass Article(models.Model):\n    author = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE)\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    slug = models.SlugField(unique=True)\n    body = models.TextField()\n    published = models.BooleanField(default=False)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n\n    def get_absolute_url(self):\n        return reverse(\"blog:detail\", kwargs={\"slug\": self.slug})\n```\n\n```python\n# blog/views.py\nfrom django.contrib.auth.mixins import LoginRequiredMixin\nfrom django.urls import reverse_lazy\nfrom django.views.generic import CreateView, DeleteView, DetailView, ListView, UpdateView\nfrom .models import Article\n\n\nclass ArticleList(ListView):\n    model = Article\n    paginate_by = 20\n    ordering = [\"-created_at\"]\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return super().get_queryset().filter(published=True).select_related(\"author\")\n\n\nclass ArticleDetail(DetailView):\n    model = Article                    # looks up by slug from URL kwarg \"slug\"\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return Article.objects.filter(published=True).select_related(\"author\")\n\n\nclass ArticleCreate(LoginRequiredMixin, CreateView):\n    model = Article\n    fields = [\"title\", \"slug\", \"body\", \"published\"]\n\n    def form_valid(self, form):\n        form.instance.author = self.request.user     # never trust a hidden field\n        return super().form_valid(form)              # saves, redirects to get_absolute_url()\n\n\nclass OwnArticlesMixin:\n    \"\"\"Restrict editing views to the current user's articles.\"\"\"\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return super().get_queryset().filter(author=self.request.user)\n\n\nclass ArticleUpdate(LoginRequiredMixin, OwnArticlesMixin, UpdateView):\n    model = Article\n    fields = [\"title\", \"body\", \"published\"]\n\n\nclass ArticleDelete(LoginRequiredMixin, OwnArticlesMixin, DeleteView):\n    model = Article\n    success_url = reverse_lazy(\"blog:list\")          # reverse_lazy: URLconf not loaded at import\n```\n\n```python\n# blog/urls.py\nfrom django.urls import path\nfrom . import views\n\napp_name = \"blog\"\nurlpatterns = [\n    path(\"\", views.ArticleList.as_view(), name=\"list\"),\n    path(\"new/\", views.ArticleCreate.as_view(), name=\"create\"),\n    path(\"<slug:slug>/\", views.ArticleDetail.as_view(), name=\"detail\"),\n    path(\"<slug:slug>/edit/\", views.ArticleUpdate.as_view(), name=\"update\"),\n    path(\"<slug:slug>/delete/\", views.ArticleDelete.as_view(), name=\"delete\"),\n]\n```\n\n```html\n<!-- blog/templates/blog/article_list.html (default name: <app>/<model>_list.html) -->\n{% extends \"base.html\" %}\n{% block content %}\n  {% for article in article_list %}            {# also available as object_list #}\n    <h2><a href=\"{{ article.get_absolute_url }}\">{{ article.title }}</a></h2>\n    <p>by {{ article.author }}</p>\n  {% empty %}\n    <p>No articles yet.</p>\n  {% endfor %}\n\n  {% if is_paginated %}\n    {% if page_obj.has_previous %}<a href=\"?page={{ page_obj.previous_page_number }}\">prev</a>{% endif %}\n    Page {{ page_obj.number }} of {{ paginator.num_pages }}\n    {% if page_obj.has_next %}<a href=\"?page={{ page_obj.next_page_number }}\">next</a>{% endif %}\n  {% endif %}\n{% endblock %}\n```\n\n`CreateView`/`UpdateView` render `blog/article_form.html` (suffix `_form`), `DeleteView` renders `blog/article_confirm_delete.html`. Because `OwnArticlesMixin` filters `get_queryset()`, another user's slug yields **404, not 403** — which also avoids leaking that the article exists. The SQL for an update by a non-owner shows why:\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"blog_article\"\nWHERE (\"blog_article\".\"author_id\" = 7 AND \"blog_article\".\"slug\" = 'hello-world')\nLIMIT 21;   -- get() fetches up to 21 rows to report MultipleObjectsReturned\n```\n\nThe same views as FBVs, for comparison — notice how much is now explicit:\n\n```python\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required\nfrom django.shortcuts import get_object_or_404, redirect, render\nfrom .forms import ArticleForm\n\n@login_required\ndef article_update(request, slug):\n    article = get_object_or_404(Article, slug=slug, author=request.user)\n    form = ArticleForm(request.POST or None, instance=article)\n    if request.method == \"POST\" and form.is_valid():\n        article = form.save()\n        return redirect(article)\n    return render(request, \"blog/article_form.html\", {\"form\": form, \"object\": article})\n```\n\n### 5.2 Real-world example — multi-tenant views with reusable mixins\n\nA SaaS app where every object belongs to an organization from the URL, permissions are per-org, and every list supports search.\n\n```python\n# core/mixins.py\nfrom django.contrib.auth.mixins import LoginRequiredMixin\nfrom django.core.exceptions import PermissionDenied\nfrom django.shortcuts import get_object_or_404\nfrom orgs.models import Membership, Organization\n\n\nclass OrgMixin(LoginRequiredMixin):\n    \"\"\"Resolve the org from the URL once, verify membership, scope querysets.\"\"\"\n    org_role_required = \"member\"\n\n    def setup(self, request, *args, **kwargs):\n        super().setup(request, *args, **kwargs)\n        self.org = None\n\n    def dispatch(self, request, *args, **kwargs):\n        if not request.user.is_authenticated:\n            return self.handle_no_permission()          # LoginRequiredMixin behaviour\n        self.org = get_object_or_404(Organization, slug=kwargs[\"org_slug\"])\n        membership = Membership.objects.filter(org=self.org, user=request.user).first()\n        if membership is None or not membership.has_role(self.org_role_required):\n            raise PermissionDenied\n        self.membership = membership\n        return super().dispatch(request, *args, **kwargs)\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return super().get_queryset().filter(org=self.org)\n\n    def get_context_data(self, **kwargs):\n        kwargs.setdefault(\"org\", self.org)\n        return super().get_context_data(**kwargs)\n\n\nclass SearchMixin:\n    search_fields = ()\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        qs = super().get_queryset()\n        q = self.request.GET.get(\"q\", \"\").strip()\n        if q and self.search_fields:\n            from django.db.models import Q\n            cond = Q()\n            for f in self.search_fields:\n                cond |= Q(**{f\"{f}__icontains\": q})\n            qs = qs.filter(cond)\n        return qs\n\n    def get_context_data(self, **kwargs):\n        ctx = super().get_context_data(**kwargs)\n        ctx[\"q\"] = self.request.GET.get(\"q\", \"\")\n        return ctx\n\n\nclass OrgFormMixin:\n    \"\"\"Inject org into forms and stamp it on save.\"\"\"\n    def get_form_kwargs(self):\n        kwargs = super().get_form_kwargs()\n        kwargs[\"org\"] = self.org\n        return kwargs\n\n    def form_valid(self, form):\n        form.instance.org = self.org\n        form.instance.updated_by = self.request.user\n        return super().form_valid(form)\n```\n\n```python\n# projects/views.py\nfrom django.contrib import messages\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.urls import reverse\nfrom django.views.generic import CreateView, ListView, UpdateView\nfrom core.mixins import OrgFormMixin, OrgMixin, SearchMixin\nfrom .forms import ProjectForm\nfrom .models import Project\n\n\nclass ProjectList(OrgMixin, SearchMixin, ListView):\n    model = Project\n    paginate_by = 25\n    search_fields = (\"name\", \"client__name\")\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return super().get_queryset().select_related(\"client\").order_by(\"-updated_at\")\n\n\nclass ProjectCreate(OrgMixin, OrgFormMixin, CreateView):\n    model = Project\n    form_class = ProjectForm\n    org_role_required = \"editor\"\n\n    @transaction.atomic\n    def form_valid(self, form):\n        response = super().form_valid(form)          # self.object is now saved\n        self.object.members.add(self.request.user)\n        messages.success(self.request, f\"Created {self.object.name}\")\n        return response\n\n    def get_success_url(self):\n        return reverse(\"projects:detail\", kwargs={\"org_slug\": self.org.slug, \"pk\": self.object.pk})\n\n\nclass ProjectUpdate(OrgMixin, OrgFormMixin, UpdateView):\n    model = Project\n    form_class = ProjectForm\n    org_role_required = \"editor\"\n```\n\n```python\n# projects/forms.py\nfrom django import forms\nfrom .models import Client, Project\n\n\nclass ProjectForm(forms.ModelForm):\n    class Meta:\n        model = Project\n        fields = [\"name\", \"client\", \"budget\"]\n\n    def __init__(self, *args, org, **kwargs):\n        super().__init__(*args, **kwargs)\n        # Only clients of this org are selectable — prevents cross-tenant references\n        self.fields[\"client\"].queryset = Client.objects.filter(org=org)\n```\n\nThe MRO for `ProjectList` is `ProjectList → OrgMixin → LoginRequiredMixin → AccessMixin → SearchMixin → ListView → ... → MultipleObjectMixin → ... → View`. `get_queryset()` therefore runs `ProjectList` (select_related, order) → `OrgMixin` (org filter) → `SearchMixin` (q filter) → `MultipleObjectMixin` (base queryset). Each layer refines the result of the next — cooperative inheritance doing its job.\n\nA view test locks the behaviour in:\n\n```python\nfrom django.test import TestCase\nfrom django.urls import reverse\n\nclass ProjectListTests(TestCase):\n    def test_other_org_is_forbidden(self):\n        self.client.force_login(self.alice)\n        r = self.client.get(reverse(\"projects:list\", kwargs={\"org_slug\": self.bob_org.slug}))\n        self.assertEqual(r.status_code, 403)\n\n    def test_query_count(self):\n        self.client.force_login(self.alice)\n        with self.assertNumQueries(6):   # session, user, org, membership, COUNT, page\n            self.client.get(reverse(\"projects:list\", kwargs={\"org_slug\": self.org.slug}))\n```\n\n### 5.3 Decorators on CBVs\n\n```python\nfrom django.utils.decorators import method_decorator\nfrom django.views.decorators.cache import cache_page\nfrom django.views.decorators.csrf import ensure_csrf_cookie\nfrom django.views.generic import TemplateView\n\n@method_decorator([cache_page(60), ensure_csrf_cookie], name=\"dispatch\")\nclass Dashboard(TemplateView):\n    template_name = \"dashboard.html\"\n\n# Or at URL level (decorates the function returned by as_view):\n# path(\"dash/\", cache_page(60)(Dashboard.as_view()))\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Aspect | FBV | CBV / generic views |\n|---|---|---|\n| Readability | Linear, explicit | Indirect; must know the hooks |\n| Boilerplate for CRUD | High | Very low |\n| Reuse | Helpers, decorators | Mixins, inheritance, attributes |\n| HTTP method branching | `if request.method == \"POST\"` | Separate `get()`/`post()` methods; 405 automatic |\n| Testing | Call function | Same via client; `RequestFactory` + `view.setup()` for unit tests |\n| Debugging | Stack trace is your code | Stack trace through Django mixins |\n| Learning curve | Low | Medium (MRO, ccbv.co.uk) |\n| Fit for DRF | `@api_view` | `APIView`, generics, ViewSets |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Standard list/detail/create/update/delete pages, especially many similar ones.\n- Cross-cutting behaviour (tenant scoping, permission checks, audit stamping) shared via mixins.\n- When you want automatic 405, `OPTIONS`, `HEAD` handling and consistent template naming.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- A view that orchestrates several forms, external calls and branching flows — an FBV (or a CBV with plain `View`) reads better than fighting `FormMixin`.\n- Deep inheritance chains (mixin of mixin of mixin) that nobody can trace; flatten or use composition (service functions).\n- When the team is unfamiliar and the app has only a handful of views — consistency and clarity beat cleverness.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Mixin on the wrong side.** `class V(UpdateView, LoginRequiredMixin)` silently skips the login check. *Instead:* access mixins leftmost; add a test that anonymous users get a redirect.\n\n**2. Forgetting `super()` in `get_context_data()`.**\n\n```python\n# Before: object/form/page_obj disappear from the template\ndef get_context_data(self, **kwargs):\n    return {\"stats\": compute_stats()}\n\n# After\ndef get_context_data(self, **kwargs):\n    ctx = super().get_context_data(**kwargs)\n    ctx[\"stats\"] = compute_stats()\n    return ctx\n```\n\n**3. Authorization in the template or in `get()` only.** Checking ownership in `get()` but not `post()` lets a crafted POST through. *Instead:* scope `get_queryset()` — both `get()` and `post()` go through `get_object()`.\n\n**4. Evaluated queryset as a class attribute.** `queryset = list(Book.objects.all())` or `queryset = Book.objects.filter(created__gte=timezone.now() - ...)` freezes the timestamp at import time. *Instead:* compute in `get_queryset()`.\n\n**5. Mutable class attributes.** `extra_context = {}` then `self.extra_context[\"x\"] = ...` mutates the shared dict across requests. *Instead:* override `get_context_data()`.\n\n**6. Setting the owner via a hidden form field.** Users can tamper with it. *Instead:* exclude the field and set `form.instance.owner` in `form_valid()`.\n\n**7. `reverse()` in a class attribute.** `success_url = reverse(\"x\")` runs at import time and may fail with circular URLconf imports. *Instead:* `reverse_lazy()` or `get_success_url()`.\n\n**8. Overriding `post()` in DeleteView/UpdateView to add logic.** You bypass validation. *Instead:* override `form_valid()` (DeleteView since 4.0 too).\n\n**9. N+1 in list templates.** `{{ article.author.name }}` per row. *Instead:* `select_related` in `get_queryset()` (see [Ch 13](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization)).\n\n**Best practices:** keep mixins tiny and single-purpose; always cooperate with `super()`; use [ccbv.co.uk](https://ccbv.co.uk/) to see the flattened methods of any generic view; test access rules per view.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: cross-tenant data leak.** In production, a user edits `/orgs/acme/projects/42/edit/` and changes the URL to project 43 of another org. Root cause: `UpdateView` with `model = Project` and no `get_queryset()` override — `get_object()` finds any project by pk. Fix: tenant-scoped `get_queryset()` in a base mixin every view must inherit, plus a test or system check that enforces it.\n\n**Pitfall: slow list page with `paginate_by`.** The paginator runs `COUNT(*)` on a 10M-row filtered queryset per request. Symptom: list endpoint p95 in seconds while the page query is fast. Fix: an index matching the filter, cache the count, or switch to keyset/cursor pagination for infinite scroll (see [REST API · Pagination](../rest-api/topic.html?p=13-pagination)).\n\n**Pitfall: `PermissionDenied` vs 404 confusion.** Raising 403 for objects outside the user's scope leaks existence (enumeration). Return 404 for \"not yours\", 403 for \"yours but not allowed\".\n\n**Pitfall: thread-safety assumptions.** Developers cache computed data on the class (`cls._cache = ...`) inside a view. Under threaded Gunicorn workers this races and leaks data between users. Keep request data on `self`, process-wide data in the cache framework.\n\n**Debugging:**\n\n- `print(V.__mro__)` or `inspect.getmro` to see the lookup order; ccbv.co.uk for the source of each method.\n- Django debug toolbar's Templates panel shows the resolved template and context (catches a misnamed `context_object_name`).\n- Put a breakpoint in `dispatch()` of your leftmost class and step through `super()` calls.\n- For unit tests: `view = MyView(); view.setup(request, **kwargs); view.get_queryset()` tests one hook in isolation.\n\n**Scaling notes:** CBVs add negligible overhead (one object per request). Real costs are queries and template rendering: `select_related`/`prefetch_related` in `get_queryset()`, fragment caching, and `cache_page` via `method_decorator`. For APIs, migrate to DRF generic views, which reuse the same mental model.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between function-based and class-based views, and when would you choose each?**\nA: Both end up as a callable taking `request` and returning a response — `as_view()` turns a class into such a function. FBVs are explicit and linear, ideal for unique logic and easy to read in a stack trace. CBVs organize the request-handling skeleton into overridable methods, give you per-method handlers, automatic 405/OPTIONS/HEAD, and reuse via mixins; generic CBVs remove CRUD boilerplate. I choose generic CBVs for standard CRUD and shared behaviour, FBVs for bespoke flows. Team consistency matters more than either.\n\n**Q: What does `as_view()` do?**\nA: It is a classmethod that validates the initkwargs (they must be existing class attributes and not HTTP method names) and returns a closure `view(request, *args, **kwargs)`. On each request, that function instantiates the class with the initkwargs, calls `setup()` to store `request`, `args`, `kwargs`, then calls `dispatch()`. It also attaches `view_class` and `view_initkwargs` to the function for introspection. Because a new instance is created per request, instance attributes are request-local.\n\n**Q: How does `dispatch()` decide which method to call?**\nA: It lowercases `request.method` and, if it is in `http_method_names`, looks up a method of that name on the instance; if missing or not allowed it uses `http_method_not_allowed`, returning 405 with an `Allow` header. `HEAD` falls back to `get()` (set up in `setup()`), and `OPTIONS` is answered by the built-in `options()`. Overriding `dispatch()` (usually in a mixin) is the hook for checks that must run before any handler.\n\n**Q: Explain MRO and why mixin order matters in Django views.**\nA: Python computes a C3 linearization of the class hierarchy; attribute lookup and `super()` follow it, and left bases come before right bases. Django's mixins cooperate by calling `super()`, so each mixin's `dispatch()` or `get_queryset()` wraps the next one. `View.dispatch` is the end of the chain and does not call `super()`, so a mixin placed to the right of the generic view never gets its `dispatch()` called. That's why `LoginRequiredMixin` must be leftmost: `class V(LoginRequiredMixin, UpdateView)`.\n\n**Q: Where do you restrict a DetailView/UpdateView to the current user's objects, and why there?**\nA: Override `get_queryset()` to filter by `request.user` (or tenant). `get_object()` uses `get_queryset()` for both GET and POST, so the restriction covers every handler, and objects outside the scope produce a 404 rather than revealing their existence. Checking in `get()` or in the template misses POST and other paths. For role-based checks layer `UserPassesTestMixin` or `PermissionRequiredMixin` on top.\n\n**Q: How do you set the owner of an object in a CreateView?**\nA: Exclude `owner` from `fields`, then override `form_valid()`: `form.instance.owner = self.request.user; return super().form_valid(form)`. `ModelFormMixin.form_valid` saves the form (assigning `self.object`) and redirects to `get_success_url()`. Never rely on a hidden input, because the client controls it.\n\n**Q: What does `get_context_data()` provide by default, and what happens if you don't call `super()`?**\nA: `ContextMixin` adds `view` and merges `extra_context`; `SingleObjectMixin` adds `object` and the named alias (e.g. `article`); `MultipleObjectMixin` adds `object_list`, `<model>_list`, `paginator`, `page_obj`, `is_paginated`; `FormMixin` adds `form`. If you return a new dict without calling `super()`, all of those disappear and the template breaks silently (DTL renders missing variables as empty). Always start with `ctx = super().get_context_data(**kwargs)`.\n\n**Q: How do you apply a function decorator like `login_required` or `cache_page` to a CBV?**\nA: Use `method_decorator`, usually on `dispatch`: `@method_decorator(login_required, name=\"dispatch\")` on the class, or decorate a method directly. A list of decorators can be passed. Alternatively wrap in the URLconf: `login_required(MyView.as_view())`. For auth specifically, `LoginRequiredMixin` is the idiomatic CBV choice; Django 5.1+ also offers `LoginRequiredMiddleware` with `login_not_required` for opt-out.\n\n**Q: How do template names get chosen in generic views?**\nA: `get_template_names()` returns `template_name` if set; otherwise for model-based views it builds `<app_label>/<model_name><template_name_suffix>.html` — `_list`, `_detail`, `_form` (create and update) and `_confirm_delete`. It can also return a list so you can try a specific template before a generic one. `TemplateResponseMixin.render_to_response` returns a lazy `TemplateResponse` that renders at the end of the middleware response phase.\n\n**Q: Why use `reverse_lazy` for `success_url`?**\nA: Class bodies execute at import time, when the URLconf may not be loaded yet (and importing it could be circular because the URLconf imports the views). `reverse_lazy` returns a lazy proxy evaluated when the URL is first used. Alternatively override `get_success_url()`, which runs at request time and can use `self.object`.\n\n**Q: Design reusable multi-tenant behaviour for 40 CBVs. How would you structure it? (Senior)**\nA: A base `TenantMixin` resolves the tenant once (in `dispatch()` or `setup()`), verifies membership, stores it on `self`, scopes `get_queryset()`, injects it into `get_form_kwargs()` and context, and stamps it in `form_valid()`. Access mixins go leftmost and the tenant mixin calls `super()` everywhere so it composes with search/pagination mixins. I'd enforce adoption with a project base class or a test that walks the URLconf and asserts each view's MRO contains the mixin, and keep foreign-key choices in forms tenant-scoped to stop cross-tenant references. Business logic stays in service functions, keeping mixins thin.\n\n**Q: A teammate's CBV has six mixins and nobody knows why a field gets overwritten on save. How do you debug it? (Senior)**\nA: Print `View.__mro__` and identify every class defining `form_valid`, `get_form_kwargs`, `get_initial` and `save` overrides (ccbv.co.uk helps for Django's own classes). Then trace the `super()` chain — a mixin that sets `form.instance.x` after another, or one that doesn't call `super()`, is the usual culprit. A breakpoint in the leftmost `form_valid` and stepping through confirms it. Fix by reordering bases, merging overlapping mixins, or moving the logic into an explicit service call so the order is visible.\n\n**Q: When is a CBV the wrong tool? (Senior)**\nA: When the flow doesn't match the generic shape — multiple forms with conditional steps, heavy orchestration, or many early returns — you end up overriding `post()` entirely and the generic machinery only adds indirection. Also when the inheritance tree becomes too deep for the team to reason about, or when async handlers are needed alongside generic sync internals. Then I use an FBV or a plain `View` subclass with explicit methods, delegating to services. The test is: can a new hire find what runs on POST in under a minute?\n\n**Q: Can CBVs be async?**\nA: Yes, since Django 4.1 a `View` subclass may define `async def get()` etc., and `as_view()` returns a coroutine function. All handlers on the class must be consistently async or sync, otherwise `ImproperlyConfigured` is raised. Generic views' helpers like `get_object()` use the sync ORM, so they don't benefit; use async primarily with plain `View` and the async ORM API, under ASGI (see [Ch 30](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi)).\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Generic view | Key hooks | Default template | Context |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `TemplateView` | `get_context_data` | `template_name` (required) | `view`, URL kwargs |\n| `RedirectView` | `get_redirect_url` | — | — (`permanent=False` default) |\n| `ListView` | `get_queryset`, `paginate_by`, `ordering` | `<app>/<model>_list.html` | `object_list`, `page_obj` |\n| `DetailView` | `get_object`, `get_queryset` | `<app>/<model>_detail.html` | `object`, `<model>` |\n| `CreateView` | `get_form_kwargs`, `form_valid`, `get_success_url` | `<app>/<model>_form.html` | `form` |\n| `UpdateView` | as Create + `get_object` | `<app>/<model>_form.html` | `form`, `object` |\n| `DeleteView` | `form_valid` (4.0+), `success_url` | `<app>/<model>_confirm_delete.html` | `object`, `form` |\n| `FormView` | `form_class`, `form_valid`, `success_url` | `template_name` | `form` |\n\n- `as_view()` → `view()` → `cls(**initkwargs)` → `setup()` → `dispatch()` → handler.\n- New instance per request; class attributes are shared — no mutable ones.\n- Access mixins leftmost; generic view rightmost; always call `super()`.\n- Scope objects in `get_queryset()`; it covers GET and POST.\n- Set server-controlled fields in `form_valid()` via `form.instance`.\n- `reverse_lazy` in class attributes.\n- `method_decorator(dec, name=\"dispatch\")` for FBV decorators.\n- Out-of-range page → 404; `get()` uses `LIMIT 21` for `MultipleObjectsReturned`.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\"`, `django-admin startproject site .`, `python manage.py startapp blog`.\n\n1. **Build CRUD** from §5.1 with only generic views; then rewrite `ArticleUpdate` as an FBV and compare line counts and readability.\n2. **MRO experiment.** Swap `LoginRequiredMixin` to the right of `UpdateView`, log out, and confirm you can reach the edit page. Print `__mro__` for both versions.\n3. **Context bug.** Override `get_context_data` without `super()`, observe the broken template, fix it, and write a test asserting `\"object\" in response.context`.\n4. **Ownership.** Implement `OwnArticlesMixin`, then write a test that user B gets 404 for user A's edit and delete URLs on both GET and POST.\n5. **Unit-test a hook.** Use `RequestFactory` and `view.setup()` to test `get_queryset()` of `ProjectList` without hitting the URLconf.\n\n**Mini project — \"Team Tasks\":** organizations, projects and tasks with tenant-scoped CBVs built from `OrgMixin`, `SearchMixin`, `OrgFormMixin`; a `DeleteView` with a soft-delete `form_valid`; `assertNumQueries` on every list page; and a test that walks all URL patterns and asserts every view class inherits `OrgMixin`.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 04 · Views, HttpRequest & HttpResponse](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses) · [Ch 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation) · [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) · [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) · [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers) · [Ch 26 · Permissions, Groups & Authorization](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization) · [Ch 33 · Testing](topic.html?p=33-testing)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · HTTP Methods & Idempotency](../rest-api/topic.html?p=06-http-methods-idempotency) · [REST API · Status Codes](../rest-api/topic.html?p=07-status-codes) · [REST API · Pagination](../rest-api/topic.html?p=13-pagination)\n\n**Free resources:**\n- **Class-based views (topic guide)** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the official introduction and when to use them. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/class-based-views/>\n- **Using mixins with class-based views** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · how the mixins compose and where it gets tricky. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/class-based-views/mixins/>\n- **Built-in class-based views API** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · reference for every generic view's attributes and methods. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/class-based-views/>\n- **Classy Class-Based Views** — ccbv.co.uk · *Intermediate* · flattened view of every method and attribute with the MRO. <https://ccbv.co.uk/>\n- **Python's super() considered super!** — Raymond Hettinger · *Intermediate* · the definitive explanation of cooperative multiple inheritance. <https://rhettinger.wordpress.com/2011/05/26/super-considered-super/>\n- **The Python 2.3 Method Resolution Order** — python.org · *Advanced* · the C3 algorithm explained. <https://docs.python.org/3/howto/mro.html>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 17.*\n", "18-middleware": "# 18 · Middleware\n\n> **In one line:** Middleware is Django's onion: `BaseHandler.load_middleware()` wraps the view in every class from `MIDDLEWARE` once at startup, so requests flow top→bottom and responses bottom→top; knowing that order, the `process_view` / `process_exception` / `process_template_response` hooks, and when a layer short-circuits is what lets you write correct cross-cutting code — and debug why `request.user` is anonymous.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle), [Ch 04 · Views, HttpRequest & HttpResponse](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses), [Ch 17 · Class-Based & Generic Views](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views).\n\nSome logic belongs to *every* request: attach a request ID, enforce HTTPS, load the session, authenticate the user, add security headers, time the request, route a tenant by hostname. The naive approach is a decorator on every view, or a helper call at the top of each one. It fails fast: someone forgets it on a new view, it doesn't cover 404s or responses from other layers, and ordering between helpers is implicit.\n\n**Middleware** is Django's answer: a chain of callables, configured in the `MIDDLEWARE` setting, that every request passes through on the way in and every response passes through on the way out. Each layer can inspect or modify the request, **short-circuit** by returning a response without calling the inner layers, or modify the response coming back. The order of the list is semantics: `AuthenticationMiddleware` needs `request.session`, so it must come after `SessionMiddleware`; CSRF checks must happen before your view runs; GZip must happen after anything that changes the body.\n\nThis chapter is the home for middleware mechanics, the built-in ordering, and writing custom (including async-capable) middleware. Specific built-ins are explained in their own chapters: sessions ([Ch 27](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies)), CSRF/security headers ([Ch 28](topic.html?p=28-security)), per-site caching ([Ch 29](topic.html?p=29-caching)), WhiteNoise ([Ch 20](topic.html?p=20-static-media-files)).\n\n> **Why this matters:** \"Explain the middleware order\", \"write a middleware that logs request duration\", and \"why is `request.user` anonymous in my middleware when I use JWT?\" are staple questions. They test whether you understand the request lifecycle beyond views.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **`MIDDLEWARE` setting** — ordered list of dotted paths. *Why it matters:* order defines nesting; first = outermost.\n- **Middleware factory** — a callable taking `get_response` and returning a middleware callable; usually a class with `__init__(self, get_response)` and `__call__(self, request)`. *Why it matters:* `__init__` runs **once per process** at startup, `__call__` per request.\n- **`get_response`** — the next layer inward (another middleware or the view-calling core). *Why it matters:* code before calling it is the request phase, code after is the response phase.\n- **Short-circuit** — returning an `HttpResponse` without calling `get_response`. *Why it matters:* inner middleware and the view never run, but outer middleware still process the response.\n- **`process_view(request, view_func, view_args, view_kwargs)`** — called after URL resolution, just before the view. *Why it matters:* the only hook that knows *which* view will run (e.g. check view attributes set by decorators).\n- **`process_exception(request, exception)`** — called when the view raises. *Why it matters:* map domain exceptions to responses, report errors; return `None` to let default handling continue.\n- **`process_template_response(request, response)`** — called when the response has a `render()` method (`TemplateResponse`). *Why it matters:* modify `response.context_data` or template before it renders.\n- **`MiddlewareMixin`** — `django.utils.deprecation.MiddlewareMixin`, supports old-style `process_request`/`process_response`. *Why it matters:* many built-ins and third-party middleware use it; also gives async compatibility.\n- **`MiddlewareNotUsed`** — raise it from `__init__` to remove the middleware from the chain (e.g. feature-flag off). *Why it matters:* zero per-request cost when disabled.\n- **`sync_capable` / `async_capable`** — flags (and decorators `sync_only_middleware`, `async_only_middleware`, `sync_and_async_middleware`) telling the handler which mode the middleware supports. *Why it matters:* a sync middleware in an ASGI stack forces thread hops on every request.\n- **`convert_exception_to_response`** — wraps each layer so exceptions become responses (404, 403, 400, 500) before they reach the outer layer. *Why it matters:* outer middleware see a response, not an exception.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 How the chain is built — `BaseHandler.load_middleware`\n\nWhen `WSGIHandler` (or `ASGIHandler`) is created — at process start, via `get_wsgi_application()` — it calls `load_middleware()`. Simplified:\n\n```python\ndef load_middleware(self, is_async=False):\n    self._view_middleware = []\n    self._template_response_middleware = []\n    self._exception_middleware = []\n\n    get_response = self._get_response_async if is_async else self._get_response\n    handler = convert_exception_to_response(get_response)        # innermost: the view caller\n    handler_is_async = is_async\n\n    for middleware_path in reversed(settings.MIDDLEWARE):         # build from the inside out\n        middleware = import_string(middleware_path)\n        # adapt handler to sync/async as the middleware requires (sync_to_async/async_to_sync)\n        try:\n            mw_instance = middleware(adapted_handler)            # __init__(get_response) ONCE\n        except MiddlewareNotUsed:\n            continue\n        if hasattr(mw_instance, \"process_view\"):\n            self._view_middleware.insert(0, mw_instance.process_view)          # top -> bottom\n        if hasattr(mw_instance, \"process_template_response\"):\n            self._template_response_middleware.append(mw_instance.process_template_response)  # bottom -> top\n        if hasattr(mw_instance, \"process_exception\"):\n            self._exception_middleware.append(mw_instance.process_exception)   # bottom -> top\n        handler = convert_exception_to_response(mw_instance)\n\n    self._middleware_chain = handler                              # outermost layer\n```\n\nThen each request calls `self._middleware_chain(request)`. Iterating `reversed()` means the **last** middleware in the list wraps the view directly and the **first** becomes the outermost layer. The hook lists are ordered so `process_view` runs top→bottom and `process_exception`/`process_template_response` run bottom→top — matching the onion.\n\n### 3.2 The onion\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 380\" width=\"100%\" height=\"380\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj18a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj18b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">The middleware onion: request in top-to-bottom, response out bottom-to-top</text>\n  <rect x=\"150\" y=\"40\" width=\"560\" height=\"320\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"58\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">SecurityMiddleware (outermost)</text>\n  <rect x=\"180\" y=\"66\" width=\"500\" height=\"282\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">SessionMiddleware: request.session</text>\n  <rect x=\"210\" y=\"92\" width=\"440\" height=\"244\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">CommonMiddleware: APPEND_SLASH, ALLOWED_HOSTS via get_host()</text>\n  <rect x=\"240\" y=\"118\" width=\"380\" height=\"206\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">CsrfViewMiddleware: check in process_view</text>\n  <rect x=\"270\" y=\"144\" width=\"320\" height=\"168\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">AuthenticationMiddleware: lazy request.user</text>\n  <rect x=\"300\" y=\"170\" width=\"260\" height=\"130\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"188\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">Messages, XFrameOptions ...</text>\n  <rect x=\"340\" y=\"200\" width=\"180\" height=\"84\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#334155\"/>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">_get_response</text>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">resolve URL</text>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"256\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">process_view chain</text>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"272\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">call view</text>\n  <path d=\"M60,70 L60,250 L336,250\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#dj18a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"60\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-weight=\"bold\">request</text>\n  <path d=\"M524,236 L800,236 L800,70\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#dj18b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"800\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-weight=\"bold\">response</text>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"300\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">code before get_response()</text>\n  <text x=\"660\" y=\"300\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">code after get_response()</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.3 Inside `_get_response`\n\nThe innermost layer, `BaseHandler._get_response(request)`, does:\n\n1. **Resolve** the URL: `resolver_match = resolve(request.path_info)` (using `request.urlconf` if a middleware set it). `Resolver404` becomes a 404 response.\n2. Run each `process_view` in order. The first one that returns a response **stops** the rest and the view.\n3. Call the view (wrapped in `atomic` if `ATOMIC_REQUESTS`). If it raises, run `process_exception` hooks bottom→top; the first non-`None` response wins; if none, re-raise and `convert_exception_to_response` turns it into a 404/403/400/500 response (calling `handler404`/`handler500`).\n4. If the response has `render()` (a `TemplateResponse`), run `process_template_response` hooks bottom→top, then `response.render()`.\n5. Return the response outward through each middleware's post-`get_response` code.\n\nKey subtleties interviewers probe:\n\n- **`process_exception` only sees exceptions from the view** (and from `TemplateResponse` rendering), not from other middleware. If a middleware's `__call__` raises, `convert_exception_to_response` around that layer converts it to an error response, and the outer layers see a *response*.\n- **Short-circuit is symmetric:** if `SessionMiddleware` returned early (it doesn't, but suppose), `SecurityMiddleware`'s response phase would still run; inner layers' response phases would not.\n- **Signals:** `request_started` / `request_finished` are sent by the handler outside the middleware chain; `request_finished` fires when the response is closed, which is when Django closes stale DB connections (`close_old_connections`).\n\n### 3.4 Built-in ordering and why\n\n| Position | Middleware | Why here |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | `SecurityMiddleware` | HTTPS redirect, HSTS etc. before any work; cheap |\n| 2 | `whitenoise.middleware.WhiteNoiseMiddleware` (if used) | Serve static files before sessions/auth do needless work |\n| (cache) | `UpdateCacheMiddleware` | Must be early so its response phase runs **last**, caching the final response |\n| 3 | `SessionMiddleware` | Provides `request.session` for later layers |\n| (i18n) | `LocaleMiddleware` | After Session (and cache), before Common (uses session/cookie for language) |\n| 4 | `CommonMiddleware` | `APPEND_SLASH`/`PREPEND_WWW` redirects, `DISALLOWED_USER_AGENTS` |\n| 5 | `CsrfViewMiddleware` | Before any view middleware that assumes CSRF is checked |\n| 6 | `AuthenticationMiddleware` | Needs session; sets lazy `request.user` |\n| 7 | `MessageMiddleware` | Uses session/cookie storage |\n| 8 | `XFrameOptionsMiddleware` | Response header; position matters little |\n| last | `FetchFromCacheMiddleware` | Late so its request phase runs after Session/Auth have set `Vary` |\n\n`GZipMiddleware` should go before (i.e. above) anything that reads or modifies the response body, so its response phase runs after them. Note the BREACH attack risk when compressing responses containing secrets alongside attacker-controlled input.\n\n> **Version note:** Django 5.1 added `django.contrib.auth.middleware.LoginRequiredMiddleware`: every view requires login unless decorated with `@login_not_required`. Place it after `AuthenticationMiddleware`.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 Hook order for one request\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 330\" width=\"100%\" height=\"330\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj18c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">MIDDLEWARE = [A, B, C]: the exact call order, including hooks</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"45\" width=\"120\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A.__call__ (pre)</text>\n  <rect x=\"160\" y=\"45\" width=\"120\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"220\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B.__call__ (pre)</text>\n  <rect x=\"300\" y=\"45\" width=\"120\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"360\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C.__call__ (pre)</text>\n  <rect x=\"440\" y=\"45\" width=\"120\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/><text x=\"500\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">resolve URL</text>\n  <rect x=\"580\" y=\"45\" width=\"260\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"710\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A.process_view, B.process_view, C.process_view</text>\n  <path d=\"M142,60 L158,60\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj18c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M282,60 L298,60\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj18c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M422,60 L438,60\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj18c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M562,60 L578,60\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj18c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"640\" y=\"110\" width=\"200\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"740\" y=\"131\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">view(request, ...)</text>\n  <path d=\"M740,77 L740,108\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj18c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"330\" y=\"110\" width=\"280\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"470\" y=\"131\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">raises: C.process_exception, B..., A...</text>\n  <path d=\"M638,127 L612,127\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj18c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"580\" y=\"180\" width=\"260\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"710\" y=\"201\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">TemplateResponse: C, B, A .process_template_response</text>\n  <path d=\"M740,146 L740,178\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj18c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"580\" y=\"240\" width=\"260\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/><text x=\"710\" y=\"259\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">response.render()</text>\n  <path d=\"M710,216 L710,238\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj18c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"300\" y=\"285\" width=\"120\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"360\" y=\"304\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">C.__call__ (post)</text>\n  <rect x=\"160\" y=\"285\" width=\"120\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"220\" y=\"304\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">B.__call__ (post)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"285\" width=\"120\" height=\"30\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"80\" y=\"304\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">A.__call__ (post)</text>\n  <path d=\"M640,272 L422,296\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj18c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M298,300 L282,300\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj18c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M158,300 L142,300\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj18c)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 4.2 Short-circuit scenarios\n\n```text\nB returns a response in its pre phase\n    A.pre -> B.pre (returns 403) -> A.post -> client          C and the view never run\n\nB.process_view returns a response\n    A.pre B.pre C.pre -> A.pv -> B.pv (returns) -> C.post B.post A.post   C.pv and view skipped\n\nC.__call__ raises ValueError in its pre phase\n    convert_exception_to_response(C) -> 500 response -> B.post -> A.post   no process_exception\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example — timing + request ID middleware\n\n```python\n# core/middleware.py\nimport logging\nimport time\nimport uuid\n\nlogger = logging.getLogger(\"request\")\n\n\nclass RequestIDTimingMiddleware:\n    header = \"HTTP_X_REQUEST_ID\"\n\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response          # runs once per process\n\n    def __call__(self, request):\n        # ---- request phase ----\n        request.id = request.META.get(self.header) or uuid.uuid4().hex\n        start = time.perf_counter()\n\n        response = self.get_response(request)     # inner layers + view\n\n        # ---- response phase ----\n        duration_ms = (time.perf_counter() - start) * 1000\n        response[\"X-Request-ID\"] = request.id\n        response[\"Server-Timing\"] = f\"app;dur={duration_ms:.1f}\"\n        logger.info(\"%s %s %s %.1fms\", request.method, request.path,\n                    response.status_code, duration_ms, extra={\"request_id\": request.id})\n        return response\n```\n\n```python\n# settings.py\nMIDDLEWARE = [\n    \"core.middleware.RequestIDTimingMiddleware\",     # outermost: times everything below\n    \"django.middleware.security.SecurityMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.sessions.middleware.SessionMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.common.CommonMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.csrf.CsrfViewMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.auth.middleware.AuthenticationMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.messages.middleware.MessageMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.clickjacking.XFrameOptionsMiddleware\",\n]\n```\n\n```text\nINFO GET /articles/ 200 23.4ms  request_id=6f1c0d...\n```\n\nAccept an incoming `X-Request-ID` only from trusted proxies in real deployments, and validate its format so clients can't inject log lines.\n\n### 5.2 All the hooks in one class\n\n```python\nfrom django.http import JsonResponse\nfrom django.core.exceptions import PermissionDenied\n\n\nclass MaintenanceAndErrorsMiddleware:\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response\n\n    def __call__(self, request):\n        return self.get_response(request)\n\n    def process_view(self, request, view_func, view_args, view_kwargs):\n        # Views opt out with an attribute set by a decorator\n        if getattr(view_func, \"maintenance_exempt\", False):\n            return None\n        from django.conf import settings\n        if getattr(settings, \"MAINTENANCE_MODE\", False):\n            return JsonResponse({\"detail\": \"Down for maintenance\"}, status=503)\n        return None                                     # continue to next process_view/view\n\n    def process_exception(self, request, exception):\n        from billing.exceptions import PaymentProviderDown\n        if isinstance(exception, PaymentProviderDown):\n            return JsonResponse({\"detail\": \"Payments temporarily unavailable\"}, status=503)\n        return None                                     # let Django's 500 handling continue\n\n    def process_template_response(self, request, response):\n        response.context_data[\"build_sha\"] = \"abc123\"   # must return the response\n        return response\n\n\ndef maintenance_exempt(view):\n    view.maintenance_exempt = True\n    return view\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** For CBVs, `view_func` in `process_view` is the function returned by `as_view()`. Attributes set by a decorator applied in the URLconf are on that function; class attributes are reachable via `view_func.view_class`.\n\n### 5.3 Real-world example — tenant routing + async-capable middleware + contextvars logging\n\n```python\n# tenants/middleware.py\nimport contextvars\nfrom asgiref.sync import iscoroutinefunction, markcoroutinefunction\nfrom django.http import HttpResponseNotFound\nfrom django.utils.decorators import sync_and_async_middleware\n\ncurrent_tenant = contextvars.ContextVar(\"current_tenant\", default=None)\n\n\ndef _resolve_tenant(host):\n    from django.core.cache import cache\n    from tenants.models import Tenant\n    key = f\"tenant:host:{host}\"\n    tenant_id = cache.get(key)\n    if tenant_id is None:\n        tenant = Tenant.objects.filter(domain=host, is_active=True).only(\"id\").first()\n        tenant_id = tenant.id if tenant else 0\n        cache.set(key, tenant_id, 300)\n    return tenant_id or None\n\n\n@sync_and_async_middleware\ndef tenant_middleware(get_response):\n    \"\"\"Works natively under both WSGI and ASGI — no thread hop either way.\"\"\"\n    if iscoroutinefunction(get_response):\n        from asgiref.sync import sync_to_async\n        resolve = sync_to_async(_resolve_tenant)\n\n        async def middleware(request):\n            tenant_id = await resolve(request.get_host().split(\":\")[0])\n            if tenant_id is None:\n                return HttpResponseNotFound(\"Unknown tenant\")\n            token = current_tenant.set(tenant_id)\n            request.tenant_id = tenant_id\n            try:\n                return await get_response(request)\n            finally:\n                current_tenant.reset(token)\n    else:\n        def middleware(request):\n            tenant_id = _resolve_tenant(request.get_host().split(\":\")[0])\n            if tenant_id is None:\n                return HttpResponseNotFound(\"Unknown tenant\")\n            token = current_tenant.set(tenant_id)\n            request.tenant_id = tenant_id\n            try:\n                return get_response(request)\n            finally:\n                current_tenant.reset(token)\n    return middleware\n```\n\nThe class-based async form, for comparison:\n\n```python\nfrom asgiref.sync import iscoroutinefunction, markcoroutinefunction\n\n\nclass AsyncHeaderMiddleware:\n    async_capable = True\n    sync_capable = False\n\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response\n        if iscoroutinefunction(self.get_response):\n            markcoroutinefunction(self)        # tell Django this instance is async\n\n    async def __call__(self, request):\n        response = await self.get_response(request)\n        response[\"X-Async\"] = \"1\"\n        return response\n```\n\nA logging filter reads the contextvar so every log line in the request — including from deep inside services — carries the tenant:\n\n```python\n# core/logging.py\nimport logging\nfrom tenants.middleware import current_tenant\n\nclass TenantFilter(logging.Filter):\n    def filter(self, record):\n        record.tenant_id = current_tenant.get()\n        return True\n```\n\nUse `contextvars`, not `threading.local`: under ASGI many requests share one thread, and thread-locals bleed across them; contextvars are per-task and also work under WSGI.\n\n### 5.4 Testing middleware in isolation\n\n```python\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse\nfrom django.test import RequestFactory, SimpleTestCase\nfrom core.middleware import RequestIDTimingMiddleware\n\n\nclass RequestIDTests(SimpleTestCase):\n    def test_sets_header(self):\n        mw = RequestIDTimingMiddleware(lambda req: HttpResponse(\"ok\"))\n        request = RequestFactory().get(\"/\", HTTP_X_REQUEST_ID=\"abc\")\n        response = mw(request)\n        self.assertEqual(response[\"X-Request-ID\"], \"abc\")\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Approach | Scope | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Middleware | Every request | Can't be forgotten; sees 404s and all responses; ordered | Runs even where unneeded; global cost; hidden from view code |\n| View decorator | One view | Explicit, opt-in | Easy to forget; no coverage of non-view responses |\n| CBV mixin | Views inheriting it | Reusable, knows view context | Only class-based views; MRO pitfalls |\n| DRF auth/permission classes | DRF views | Per-view config, lazy auth | Not visible to Django middleware |\n| Signals (`request_started`) | Every request | Decoupled | No access to the response; hard to reason about |\n| Reverse proxy (Nginx/Envoy) | Edge | Fast, language-agnostic | Can't use Django state (user, session) |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Truly global concerns: request IDs, timing, security headers, tenant resolution by host, maintenance mode, locale.\n- Response post-processing that must apply to everything (headers, compression).\n- Mapping specific exceptions to responses across the app (`process_exception`).\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Logic that applies to a few views — use a decorator or mixin.\n- Per-request DB queries that most requests don't need (every request pays). Make them lazy (`SimpleLazyObject`) or move them into views.\n- Authentication for token APIs — DRF authentication classes run in the view; duplicating it in middleware causes double work and inconsistencies.\n- Heavy work (calling external APIs) — that's latency on every request; consider the edge or async.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Wrong order.** `AuthenticationMiddleware` above `SessionMiddleware` raises an `ImproperlyConfigured`-style assertion (\"requires session middleware\"). Custom middleware reading `request.user` placed above `AuthenticationMiddleware` gets `AttributeError`. *Instead:* put custom middleware that needs user/session **below** them.\n\n**2. Per-request state on `self`.**\n\n```python\n# Before: shared across threads and requests — data leak / race\nclass Bad:\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response\n    def __call__(self, request):\n        self.user = request.user              # another thread overwrites this\n        return self.get_response(request)\n\n# After: per-request data lives on the request (or a contextvar)\nclass Good:\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response\n    def __call__(self, request):\n        request.audit_actor = getattr(request, \"user\", None)\n        return self.get_response(request)\n```\n\n**3. Forgetting to return the response** (or returning `None` from `process_template_response`). Django raises `ValueError: ... didn't return an HttpResponse object`.\n\n**4. Eagerly evaluating `request.user`** in a middleware for every request (e.g. for logging) — it triggers session + user queries even for static/health endpoints. *Instead:* read it lazily only when needed, or log in a later stage.\n\n**5. Reading `request.body` in middleware.** It consumes the stream; later `request.POST` or file upload parsing may fail, and large bodies get loaded into memory. *Instead:* avoid, or cap with `DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE` and only for specific paths.\n\n**6. Expecting `process_exception` to catch everything.** It won't see exceptions from middleware or from `StreamingHttpResponse` iteration. *Instead:* use Sentry's integration and `got_request_exception` signal for reporting.\n\n**7. Sync middleware in an ASGI app.** Every sync layer forces a `sync_to_async` hop per request. *Instead:* make custom middleware `sync_and_async_middleware`; check the `django.request` debug log for \"Asynchronous handler adapted for middleware ...\" messages.\n\n**Best practices:** keep middleware tiny and fast; make expensive work lazy; set `MiddlewareNotUsed` when disabled; document the ordering assumptions in a comment beside `MIDDLEWARE`; unit test with `RequestFactory`.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: `request.user` is AnonymousUser for JWT/Token API calls inside your middleware.** Root cause: `AuthenticationMiddleware` authenticates via the **session** only; DRF authenticates in `APIView.initial()`, i.e. inside the view, after middleware ran. Fix: don't rely on `request.user` in middleware for API audit; do it in a DRF authentication class, permission, or after the view in the response phase (DRF sets `request.user` on the underlying `HttpRequest` once authenticated — but only after view execution starts).\n\n**Pitfall: all requests suddenly 400.** After a deploy, `CommonMiddleware`/`get_host()` raises `DisallowedHost` because the load balancer's health checks use the pod IP as Host. Fix: add health-check host to `ALLOWED_HOSTS`, or answer health checks in a middleware placed **above** `SecurityMiddleware`/`CommonMiddleware` that short-circuits `/healthz` without calling `get_host()`.\n\n**Pitfall: per-site cache serving one user's page to another.** `UpdateCacheMiddleware`/`FetchFromCacheMiddleware` in the wrong positions bypass `Vary: Cookie` set by `SessionMiddleware`. Fix: Update first, Fetch last, exactly as documented (see [Ch 29](topic.html?p=29-caching)).\n\n**Pitfall: latency regression after adding middleware.** A \"feature flag\" middleware calls Redis on every request including static and health checks: +3 ms p50, more at p99. Fix: cache in-process with a short TTL, skip paths early, or move to views.\n\n**Pitfall: memory/time leak from contextvars or thread-locals not reset.** A thread-local \"current user\" survives into the next request on the same thread. Fix: always reset in `finally`, prefer contextvars with tokens.\n\n**Debugging:**\n\n- `from django.conf import settings; print(settings.MIDDLEWARE)` — and remember third-party packages may document their required position.\n- Add a temporary logging middleware at different positions to see what each layer sees (`request.user`, headers).\n- `django.request` logger at DEBUG prints middleware sync/async adaptation messages.\n- Sentry and APM tools (OpenTelemetry Django instrumentation) show middleware spans so you can measure each layer's cost.\n\n**Scaling notes:** middleware cost is multiplied by every request, including static files if Django serves them. Keep the hot path allocation-free and query-free; put expensive cross-cutting logic at the edge (Nginx, CDN, API gateway) when it doesn't need Django state. Under ASGI, a fully async middleware stack avoids thread pool contention.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is middleware in Django and how does it work?**\nA: Middleware is a framework of hooks that wraps the view: each middleware is a callable built with the next layer (`get_response`) and called per request. Django builds the chain once at startup in `BaseHandler.load_middleware()` by iterating `MIDDLEWARE` in reverse, so the first entry becomes the outermost layer. Code before `get_response(request)` runs in the request phase top→bottom; code after runs in the response phase bottom→top. Any layer can short-circuit by returning a response itself.\n\n**Q: In what order do middleware run for the request and response?**\nA: Request phase follows the `MIDDLEWARE` list top to bottom, then `process_view` hooks top to bottom, then the view. Response phase runs bottom to top, as does `process_exception` and `process_template_response`. If a middleware returns early, only the layers above it see the response. This symmetric nesting is why `UpdateCacheMiddleware` goes first (its response phase runs last) and `FetchFromCacheMiddleware` goes last.\n\n**Q: Why must `AuthenticationMiddleware` come after `SessionMiddleware`?**\nA: `AuthenticationMiddleware` sets `request.user` to a lazy object that reads the user ID from `request.session`. `request.session` is created by `SessionMiddleware`, so it must have run already; otherwise Django raises an error telling you to insert the session middleware before it. Similarly, `MessageMiddleware` with session storage depends on sessions, and any custom middleware using `request.user` must be placed below `AuthenticationMiddleware`.\n\n**Q: How do you write a custom middleware?**\nA: Write a class with `__init__(self, get_response)` storing `get_response`, and `__call__(self, request)` that does request-phase work, calls `response = self.get_response(request)`, does response-phase work and returns the response. Optionally add `process_view`, `process_exception` and `process_template_response` methods. Add its dotted path to `MIDDLEWARE` at the right position. A function-based factory returning an inner `middleware(request)` closure works equally well.\n\n**Q: What are `process_view`, `process_exception` and `process_template_response` for?**\nA: `process_view(request, view_func, view_args, view_kwargs)` runs after URL resolution and before the view; it can inspect the target view (e.g. attributes from decorators) and return a response to skip it — CSRF checking lives here. `process_exception(request, exception)` runs if the view raises; returning a response handles it, `None` defers to the next hook and finally Django's default handling. `process_template_response` runs for responses with a `render()` method and can alter `context_data` or `template_name` before rendering; it must return a response.\n\n**Q: What happens when a middleware returns a response before calling `get_response`?**\nA: The chain short-circuits: inner middleware, `process_view` hooks and the view never run. The returned response travels back out through the response phase of the middlewares *above* it only. Examples: `SecurityMiddleware` returning an HTTPS redirect, `CommonMiddleware` returning an `APPEND_SLASH` redirect, WhiteNoise returning a static file, `FetchFromCacheMiddleware` returning a cached page.\n\n**Q: Is a middleware instance shared between requests?**\nA: Yes. `__init__` runs once per process when the handler loads, and the same instance's `__call__` is invoked for every request, potentially concurrently in threaded workers. Therefore never store per-request data on `self`; attach it to `request` or use a `contextvars.ContextVar`. Expensive one-time setup (compiling regexes, reading settings) belongs in `__init__`, and raising `MiddlewareNotUsed` there removes the middleware entirely.\n\n**Q: Why is `request.user` anonymous in my middleware when the API uses JWT?**\nA: Django's `AuthenticationMiddleware` only knows session authentication. DRF authentication (JWT, Token) runs lazily inside the view — `APIView.initial()` → `perform_authentication()` — after all middleware request phases have run. So in middleware the user is anonymous (or unauthenticated) for token requests. Do per-user logic in DRF authentication/permission classes, or in the middleware's response phase after the view has authenticated, accepting that coupling.\n\n**Q: Does `process_exception` catch exceptions raised inside other middleware?**\nA: No. It only handles exceptions raised by the view (and while rendering a `TemplateResponse`). Each middleware layer is wrapped by `convert_exception_to_response`, so an exception in a middleware becomes an error response (500, or 404/403/400 for the respective exceptions) at that layer, and outer middleware receive a response, not an exception. That's why error reporting should rely on `got_request_exception`/logging/Sentry rather than a custom `process_exception`.\n\n**Q: How do you make middleware work efficiently under ASGI? (Senior)**\nA: Mark it as supporting both modes with `@sync_and_async_middleware` (or `sync_capable`/`async_capable` flags) and branch on `iscoroutinefunction(get_response)`, returning an `async def` middleware in async mode; for class-based async middleware call `markcoroutinefunction(self)`. If any middleware in the chain is sync-only, Django adapts with `sync_to_async`/`async_to_sync` at that boundary, costing a thread hop per request and serializing through the thread-sensitive executor. Use `contextvars` instead of thread-locals, and run blocking calls through `sync_to_async`. Enable DEBUG logging on `django.request` to confirm no adaptation happens.\n\n**Q: Design request-scoped context (request ID, tenant, user) available in logs everywhere. (Senior)**\nA: An outermost middleware generates or validates an incoming request ID, sets a `ContextVar` with a token, and resets it in `finally`; a tenant middleware does the same after resolving the host; a `logging.Filter` copies contextvars onto every record, and the JSON formatter emits them. The request ID is returned in a response header and propagated to Celery tasks via headers and to downstream HTTP calls. Contextvars work across sync, async and threads spawned via `asgiref`, unlike thread-locals. OpenTelemetry instrumentation can replace parts of this with trace IDs.\n\n**Q: After adding a middleware, p99 latency doubled. How do you investigate? (Senior)**\nA: Measure first: APM spans or a temporary timing middleware placed above and below the suspect to isolate its cost. Typical culprits are a DB/cache call per request, eager evaluation of `request.user`/session (triggering queries on static/health paths), reading `request.body`, or a sync middleware forcing thread hops under ASGI. Fix by skipping irrelevant paths early, making work lazy, caching in-process with a short TTL, or moving the concern into views or the edge. Verify with a load test comparing before/after percentiles.\n\n**Q: How does `CsrfViewMiddleware` use the hooks?**\nA: In its request phase it ensures a CSRF secret is available; the actual check happens in `process_view`, because it needs the resolved view to honour `@csrf_exempt` (an attribute on the view function). Safe methods pass; for POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE it validates the masked token from the form field or `X-CSRFToken` header against the cookie or session, and for HTTPS checks `Origin`/`Referer` against trusted origins. In the response phase it sets or rotates the `csrftoken` cookie when used. Details in [Ch 28](topic.html?p=28-security).\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Hook | When | Order | Return |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `__init__(get_response)` | Once per process | — | raise `MiddlewareNotUsed` to disable |\n| `__call__` pre | Each request | top→bottom | response to short-circuit |\n| `process_view` | After URL resolve, before view | top→bottom | `None` or response |\n| `process_exception` | View raised | bottom→top | `None` or response |\n| `process_template_response` | Response has `render()` | bottom→top | response (required) |\n| `__call__` post | Each response | bottom→top | response (required) |\n\n- First in `MIDDLEWARE` = outermost; built by `load_middleware()` iterating reversed.\n- Security → (WhiteNoise) → Session → Common → Csrf → Auth → Messages → XFrame.\n- Cache: `UpdateCacheMiddleware` first, `FetchFromCacheMiddleware` last.\n- Short-circuit: only outer layers see the response.\n- One instance per process: no per-request state on `self`; use `request` or contextvars.\n- `process_exception` sees view exceptions only.\n- DRF auth happens in the view, not middleware.\n- Async: `sync_and_async_middleware`, `markcoroutinefunction`; avoid sync layers under ASGI.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\"`, start a project with one app and two views.\n\n1. **Trace the onion.** Write middleware `A`, `B`, `C` that print on pre, post and every hook. Request a normal view, a view that raises, and a `TemplateResponse` view; write down the printed order and compare with §4.1.\n2. **Short-circuit.** Make `B` return 403 for `/blocked/`. Observe which prints disappear.\n3. **Request ID + JSON logs.** Implement §5.1 plus a contextvar and a logging filter; confirm logs from a service function carry the ID.\n4. **Exception mapping.** Map a custom `RateLimited` exception to a 429 JSON response with `process_exception`, and prove that raising it inside a middleware yields a 500 instead.\n5. **Async.** Run under `uvicorn` with a sync middleware, enable `django.request` DEBUG logging and find the adaptation message; convert the middleware to `sync_and_async_middleware` and confirm it disappears.\n\n**Mini project — \"Edge kit\":** a package with request-ID, tenant-by-host, maintenance-mode (`process_view` with an exempt decorator) and health-check short-circuit middleware, all async-capable, with unit tests using `RequestFactory` and a documented recommended position in `MIDDLEWARE`.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 01 · Architecture & Request Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle) · [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals) · [Ch 20 · Static & Media Files](topic.html?p=20-static-media-files) · [Ch 23 · DRF Authentication, Permissions & Throttling](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling) · [Ch 27 · Sessions, Cookies & Messages](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies) · [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security) · [Ch 29 · Caching](topic.html?p=29-caching) · [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi) · [Ch 36 · Django Internals](topic.html?p=36-django-internals)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · Observability, Logging & Tracing](../rest-api/topic.html?p=38-observability-logging-tracing) · [REST API · TLS, CORS & Security Headers](../rest-api/topic.html?p=22-tls-cors-security-headers) · [System Design · Proxies & Gateway](../system-design/topic.html?p=10-proxies-gateway)\n\n**Free resources:**\n- **Middleware (topic guide)** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · writing middleware, hooks, async support. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/http/middleware/>\n- **Middleware reference and ordering** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · every built-in middleware and the recommended order. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/middleware/>\n- **Asynchronous support** — Django docs · *Advanced* · how sync/async adaptation works and its cost. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/async/>\n- **Django source: django/core/handlers/base.py** — GitHub · *Advanced* · read `load_middleware` and `_get_response` yourself. <https://github.com/django/django/blob/main/django/core/handlers/base.py>\n- **contextvars** — Python docs · *Intermediate* · the right primitive for request-scoped state. <https://docs.python.org/3/library/contextvars.html>\n- **WhiteNoise documentation** — whitenoise.readthedocs.io · *Beginner* · a canonical example of a short-circuiting middleware. <https://whitenoise.readthedocs.io/>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 18.*\n", "19-signals": "# 19 · Signals\n\n> **In one line:** Signals are an in-process, synchronous observer pattern: `Signal.send()` loops over connected receivers in the same thread and the same database transaction as the caller, so they are perfect for decoupling *your* code from *other apps'* events — and an anti-pattern when used to hide core business logic inside `post_save`.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings), [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields), [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency).\n\nWhen a user signs up you want to create a profile, send a welcome email, and record an analytics event. The naive approach puts all of that in the view. Then the admin creates users without a profile, the management command for imports forgets the analytics, and the `User` model — which belongs to `django.contrib.auth`, code you don't own — can't be edited to call your code.\n\n**Signals** solve the \"code I don't own\" part: a sender (Django, or a third-party app) announces \"a `User` was saved\" and any number of **receivers** react, without the sender knowing they exist. That's genuine decoupling and the reason signals exist: `django.contrib.auth` sends `user_logged_in`, the ORM sends `post_save`, the test runner sends `setting_changed`, and your app can listen.\n\nThe problem is that signals are frequently used for *your own* models, where the coupling they \"remove\" was never a problem. The result is **action at a distance**: saving an `Order` mysteriously sends emails, charges cards and updates inventory, nobody can tell from reading `order.save()`, bulk operations silently skip all of it, and the work runs inside the request's transaction. Experienced Django engineers treat signals as a tool of last resort for cross-app hooks — and interviewers love asking \"when are signals an anti-pattern?\"\n\nThis chapter is the home for the signals framework. Background processing lives in [Ch 31 · Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks); `transaction.on_commit` details in [Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency).\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **`Signal`** — `django.dispatch.Signal()`, an object holding a list of receivers. *Why it matters:* every signal, built-in or custom, is an instance of this class.\n- **Receiver** — any callable accepting `sender` and `**kwargs`. *Why it matters:* `**kwargs` is mandatory; Django may add arguments in future versions.\n- **Sender** — usually the class that sent the signal (e.g. the model class for `post_save`). *Why it matters:* filtering by sender (`sender=Order`) avoids running for every model.\n- **`connect(receiver, sender=None, weak=True, dispatch_uid=None)`** / **`@receiver(signal, sender=...)`** — registration. *Why it matters:* `weak=True` by default — a locally defined function can be garbage-collected and silently stop receiving.\n- **`dispatch_uid`** — unique string identifying a registration. *Why it matters:* prevents duplicate connections if the registering module is imported twice.\n- **`send(sender, **kwargs)`** — calls receivers in order; an exception **propagates** and stops remaining receivers. *Why it matters:* a failing receiver breaks the `save()` that triggered it.\n- **`send_robust()`** — catches exceptions per receiver and returns `[(receiver, response_or_exception), ...]`. *Why it matters:* use for optional notifications.\n- **`asend()` / `asend_robust()`** — async variants (Django 5.0+); sync and async receivers can both be connected and Django adapts. *Why it matters:* async views can dispatch signals without blocking.\n- **Model signals** — `pre_init`, `post_init`, `pre_save`, `post_save`, `pre_delete`, `post_delete`, `m2m_changed`, `class_prepared`; migration signals `pre_migrate`, `post_migrate`. *Why it matters:* the most used — and most abused — hooks.\n- **Request signals** — `request_started`, `request_finished`, `got_request_exception`. *Why it matters:* lifecycle hooks used by Django itself (closing DB connections, resetting queries).\n- **Auth signals** — `user_logged_in`, `user_logged_out`, `user_login_failed`. *Why it matters:* audit logging and lockout without touching auth views.\n- **`AppConfig.ready()`** — where to import the signals module. *Why it matters:* receivers must be registered after the app registry is ready and exactly once.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 What `send()` actually does\n\n`Signal` keeps `self.receivers`, a list of `((receiver_id, sender_id), receiver_ref, is_async)` entries, protected by a lock. `send()`:\n\n1. Returns immediately if there are no receivers (cheap fast-path), or if a sender cache says none are connected for this sender (`use_caching=True` for model signals).\n2. Computes the live receivers for the sender via `_live_receivers(sender)`, dereferencing weak refs and dropping dead ones.\n3. Calls each receiver **synchronously, in order of connection**: `response = receiver(signal=self, sender=sender, **named)`. Async receivers are run via `async_to_sync` when sent with `send()`; with `asend()`, sync receivers are run via `sync_to_async`.\n4. Returns `[(receiver, response), ...]`.\n\nNo queue, no thread, no broker: a signal is a **function call loop**. So:\n\n- Receivers run in the **same thread** and, for model signals, inside the **same transaction** as the `save()`. If the outer transaction rolls back, any DB writes by receivers roll back too — but an email they already sent does not un-send.\n- An exception in a receiver (with `send()`) propagates into `save()`; the row may already be written (for `post_save`) while the caller sees an error.\n- Latency of all receivers adds to the request.\n\n### 3.2 Where model signals fire inside `save()` and `delete()`\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 330\" width=\"100%\" height=\"330\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj19a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Model.save(): signals run inline, inside the caller's transaction</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"820\" height=\"200\" rx=\"12\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">transaction.atomic() (ATOMIC_REQUESTS or your block)</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"80\" width=\"140\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"110\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">obj.save()</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">save_base()</text>\n  <rect x=\"210\" y=\"80\" width=\"140\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"280\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">pre_save.send()</text><text x=\"280\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">receivers run now</text>\n  <rect x=\"380\" y=\"80\" width=\"140\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"450\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">INSERT / UPDATE</text><text x=\"450\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">_save_table()</text>\n  <rect x=\"550\" y=\"80\" width=\"140\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"620\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">post_save.send()</text><text x=\"620\" y=\"115\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">created=True/False</text>\n  <rect x=\"720\" y=\"80\" width=\"100\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"770\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">return</text>\n  <path d=\"M182,102 L208,102\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj19a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M352,102 L378,102\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj19a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M522,102 L548,102\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj19a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M692,102 L718,102\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj19a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"470\" y=\"150\" width=\"300\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"620\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">receiver sends email / calls API</text>\n  <text x=\"620\" y=\"188\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">transaction later rolls back</text>\n  <text x=\"620\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">side effect cannot be undone</text>\n  <path d=\"M620,126 L620,148\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj19a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"150\" width=\"380\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">fix: receiver registers on_commit(...)</text>\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"188\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">callback runs only after the outermost COMMIT</text>\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">dropped on rollback</text>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">NOT fired by: QuerySet.update(), bulk_create(), bulk_update(), raw SQL, loaddata sends with raw=True</text>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">QuerySet.delete() DOES send pre/post_delete per collected object (fast-delete only when no receivers)</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nIn `Model.save_base()`, `pre_save` is sent (unless `raw`/`origin` conditions say otherwise), then the parents and the table row are saved, then `post_save` is sent with `created`, `update_fields`, `raw` and `using`. For deletes, the `Collector` gathers all objects to delete (including cascades), sends `pre_delete` per object, executes the DELETEs, then sends `post_delete` per object; since 4.1 the `origin` argument tells receivers which model/queryset initiated the delete.\n\n> **Gotcha:** The `Collector` can use a **fast delete** — a single `DELETE ... WHERE` with no fetching — only when nothing needs per-object handling: no signal receivers, no cascades requiring Python. Connecting even one `pre_delete`/`post_delete` receiver to a model forces Django to load every row into memory before deleting. Signals have a performance cost even when the receiver does nothing.\n\n### 3.3 `m2m_changed`\n\n`m2m_changed` is sent by `add()`, `remove()`, `clear()` and `set()` on a many-to-many manager, with `action` in `pre_add`, `post_add`, `pre_remove`, `post_remove`, `pre_clear`, `post_clear`. The sender is the **through model** (`Pizza.toppings.through`), `instance` is the object whose manager was used, `reverse` tells you which side, `model` is the class of objects added/removed and `pk_set` their primary keys (`None` for clear). Saving a model never fires `m2m_changed` — M2M data is written separately, which is why \"post_save doesn't see my M2M changes from the admin form\" is a classic bug (the admin saves M2M after `save()` via `save_m2m()`; use `m2m_changed` or `ModelAdmin.save_related`).\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 Registration lifecycle\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 280\" width=\"100%\" height=\"280\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj19b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Startup registers receivers once; every send loops over them</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"45\" width=\"180\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"110\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">django.setup()</text><text x=\"110\" y=\"83\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">apps.populate(INSTALLED_APPS)</text>\n  <rect x=\"230\" y=\"45\" width=\"180\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"320\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">import models</text><text x=\"320\" y=\"83\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">all apps' models loaded</text>\n  <rect x=\"440\" y=\"45\" width=\"180\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"530\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">AppConfig.ready()</text><text x=\"530\" y=\"83\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">from . import signals</text>\n  <rect x=\"650\" y=\"45\" width=\"190\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"745\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">@receiver -&gt; connect()</text><text x=\"745\" y=\"83\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">dispatch_uid dedupes</text>\n  <path d=\"M202,70 L228,70\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj19b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M412,70 L438,70\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj19b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M622,70 L648,70\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj19b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"140\" width=\"820\" height=\"110\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Per request: post_save.send(sender=Order, instance=o, created=True)</text>\n  <rect x=\"50\" y=\"175\" width=\"230\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"165\" y=\"198\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">receiver 1 (sender=Order)</text><text x=\"165\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">runs, same thread</text>\n  <rect x=\"315\" y=\"175\" width=\"230\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"430\" y=\"198\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">receiver 2 (sender=None)</text><text x=\"430\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">runs for EVERY model</text>\n  <rect x=\"580\" y=\"175\" width=\"230\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/><text x=\"695\" y=\"198\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">receiver 3 raises</text><text x=\"695\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">send() aborts, save() errors</text>\n  <path d=\"M282,203 L313,203\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj19b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M547,203 L578,203\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj19b)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 4.2 Decision flow: signal or explicit call?\n\n```text\nIs the sender code you don't own (contrib.auth, a third-party app)?\n   yes -> signal receiver is reasonable (keep it thin; defer work with on_commit/Celery)\n   no  -> Is the reaction part of the core use case (charge, reserve stock)?\n             yes -> explicit service function call; no signal\n             no  -> Do several unrelated apps need to react, and must the sender not import them?\n                       yes -> custom signal sent explicitly from the service layer\n                       no  -> just call the function\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example — profile on user creation\n\n```python\n# accounts/signals.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db.models.signals import post_save\nfrom django.dispatch import receiver\nfrom .models import Profile\n\n\n@receiver(post_save, sender=settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, dispatch_uid=\"accounts.create_profile\")\ndef create_profile(sender, instance, created, raw=False, **kwargs):\n    if raw:                       # loaddata: don't touch other tables\n        return\n    if created:\n        Profile.objects.get_or_create(user=instance)\n```\n\n```python\n# accounts/apps.py\nfrom django.apps import AppConfig\n\n\nclass AccountsConfig(AppConfig):\n    default_auto_field = \"django.db.models.BigAutoField\"\n    name = \"accounts\"\n\n    def ready(self):\n        from . import signals  # noqa: F401  — registers receivers\n```\n\n`sender` accepts a lazy `\"app_label.ModelName\"` string for model signals, which is why `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL` works. Behaviour summary:\n\n```python\nUser.objects.create_user(\"ana\")                    # post_save fires -> Profile created\nUser.objects.bulk_create([User(username=\"bo\")])    # no signal -> no Profile!\nUser.objects.filter(pk=1).update(is_active=False)  # no signal\n```\n\n### 5.2 Built-in non-model signals\n\n```python\n# audit/signals.py\nimport logging\nfrom django.contrib.auth.signals import user_logged_in, user_login_failed\nfrom django.core.signals import got_request_exception\nfrom django.dispatch import receiver\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"audit\")\n\n\n@receiver(user_logged_in, dispatch_uid=\"audit.login\")\ndef on_login(sender, request, user, **kwargs):\n    log.info(\"login user=%s ip=%s\", user.pk, request.META.get(\"REMOTE_ADDR\"))\n\n\n@receiver(user_login_failed, dispatch_uid=\"audit.login_failed\")\ndef on_login_failed(sender, credentials, request=None, **kwargs):\n    # credentials has sensitive values scrubbed by Django\n    log.warning(\"login failed username=%s\", credentials.get(\"username\"))\n\n\n@receiver(got_request_exception, dispatch_uid=\"audit.exception\")\ndef on_exception(sender, request=None, **kwargs):\n    log.error(\"unhandled exception path=%s\", getattr(request, \"path\", \"?\"))\n```\n\n### 5.3 Real-world example — a custom domain signal sent explicitly, with deferred side effects\n\nA shop where the `orders` app must not import `loyalty`, `notifications` or `analytics`, yet all three react when an order is paid. The *core* work (marking paid, reserving stock) is an explicit service; the *peripheral* reactions hang off a custom signal sent from the service — not from `post_save`.\n\n```python\n# orders/signals.py\nfrom django.dispatch import Signal\n\n# Documented contract: sent after an order is paid; kwargs: order, payment\norder_paid = Signal()\n```\n\n```python\n# orders/services.py\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.db.models import F\nfrom .models import Order, Stock\nfrom .signals import order_paid\n\n\nclass OutOfStock(Exception):\n    pass\n\n\ndef mark_order_paid(order_id: int, payment) -> Order:\n    with transaction.atomic():\n        order = Order.objects.select_for_update().get(pk=order_id)\n        if order.status == Order.Status.PAID:\n            return order                                   # idempotent\n        for item in order.items.all():\n            updated = (Stock.objects\n                       .filter(product_id=item.product_id, quantity__gte=item.quantity)\n                       .update(quantity=F(\"quantity\") - item.quantity))\n            if not updated:\n                raise OutOfStock(item.product_id)\n        order.status = Order.Status.PAID\n        order.save(update_fields=[\"status\"])\n        # Explicit, visible dispatch; receivers only enqueue work after commit\n        transaction.on_commit(\n            lambda: order_paid.send_robust(sender=Order, order=order, payment=payment)\n        )\n    return order\n```\n\n```python\n# loyalty/receivers.py\nfrom django.dispatch import receiver\nfrom orders.models import Order\nfrom orders.signals import order_paid\nfrom .tasks import award_points\n\n\n@receiver(order_paid, sender=Order, dispatch_uid=\"loyalty.award_points\")\ndef award_points_on_paid(sender, order, **kwargs):\n    award_points.delay(order.pk)          # pass IDs to Celery, not instances\n```\n\n```python\n# notifications/receivers.py\nfrom django.dispatch import receiver\nfrom orders.models import Order\nfrom orders.signals import order_paid\nfrom .tasks import send_receipt_email\n\n\n@receiver(order_paid, sender=Order, dispatch_uid=\"notifications.receipt\")\ndef receipt_on_paid(sender, order, **kwargs):\n    send_receipt_email.delay(order.pk)\n```\n\n```python\n# loyalty/apps.py\nfrom django.apps import AppConfig\n\nclass LoyaltyConfig(AppConfig):\n    name = \"loyalty\"\n    def ready(self):\n        from . import receivers  # noqa: F401\n```\n\nWhy this design is defensible in an interview: the core flow is readable top-to-bottom in `mark_order_paid`; the signal is sent **explicitly** (grep for `order_paid.send` finds every trigger); dispatch happens **after commit** so a rollback never emails a receipt; `send_robust` keeps a failing receiver from breaking the others; and receivers only enqueue idempotent Celery tasks. For guaranteed delivery across crashes between commit and enqueue, replace the signal with a transactional outbox ([Messaging · Outbox](../messaging/topic.html?p=21-idempotency-outbox)).\n\n### 5.4 `m2m_changed` and `pre_save` change detection\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models.signals import m2m_changed, pre_save\nfrom django.dispatch import receiver\nfrom .models import Article\n\n\n@receiver(m2m_changed, sender=Article.tags.through, dispatch_uid=\"blog.tags_changed\")\ndef tags_changed(sender, instance, action, reverse, model, pk_set, **kwargs):\n    if action in {\"post_add\", \"post_remove\", \"post_clear\"} and not reverse:\n        from django.db import transaction\n        transaction.on_commit(lambda: reindex_article.delay(instance.pk))\n\n\n@receiver(pre_save, sender=Article, dispatch_uid=\"blog.detect_publish\")\ndef detect_publish(sender, instance, update_fields=None, **kwargs):\n    if instance.pk is None:\n        instance._became_published = instance.published\n        return\n    old = sender.objects.filter(pk=instance.pk).values_list(\"published\", flat=True).first()\n    instance._became_published = (not old) and instance.published   # extra query per save!\n```\n\nThat `pre_save` pattern costs a query on every save — prefer tracking the original value in `from_db()`/`__init__` or performing the transition in an explicit method (`article.publish()`).\n\n### 5.5 Testing and muting signals\n\n```python\nfrom unittest import mock\nfrom django.db.models.signals import post_save\nfrom django.test import TestCase\nfrom accounts.signals import create_profile\n\n\nclass SignalTests(TestCase):\n    def test_profile_created(self):\n        user = User.objects.create_user(\"ana\")\n        self.assertTrue(hasattr(user, \"profile\"))\n\n    def test_without_signal(self):\n        post_save.disconnect(create_profile, sender=User, dispatch_uid=\"accounts.create_profile\")\n        try:\n            user = User.objects.create_user(\"bo\")\n            self.assertFalse(Profile.objects.filter(user=user).exists())\n        finally:\n            post_save.connect(create_profile, sender=User, dispatch_uid=\"accounts.create_profile\")\n\n    def test_order_paid_dispatch(self):\n        handler = mock.Mock()\n        order_paid.connect(handler, sender=Order, weak=False)\n        with self.captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True):\n            mark_order_paid(self.order.pk, payment=None)\n        handler.assert_called_once()\n        order_paid.disconnect(handler, sender=Order)\n```\n\nNote `weak=False` for the mock and `captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True)`, since `TestCase` wraps tests in a transaction that never commits.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Approach | Coupling | Visibility | Transaction safety | Covers bulk ops | Best for |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `post_save` receiver | Lowest (sender unaware) | Poor — hidden | Runs inside txn | No | Reacting to models you don't own |\n| Override `save()` | Model knows it | Medium | Runs inside txn | No | Invariants tied to the model itself |\n| Explicit service function | Caller imports it | Best | You decide (`on_commit`) | You decide | Core business flows |\n| Custom signal sent explicitly | Sender unaware of receivers | Medium (grep `send`) | Pair with `on_commit` | n/a | Fan-out to optional apps |\n| Celery task via `on_commit` | Low | Good | After commit | n/a | Slow/external side effects |\n| Outbox + relay | Lowest, cross-service | Good | Atomic with data | n/a | Reliable integration events |\n| DB trigger | None in app | Poor in app | Atomic | Yes | Audit/denormalization at DB level |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Hooking into **third-party or contrib** models/events: create a profile when `auth.User` is created, audit `user_logged_in`.\n- Letting optional apps plug into a reusable app's lifecycle (a custom signal is the app's public extension point).\n- Framework-level concerns: cache invalidation on save, `post_migrate` to create default data/permissions, `setting_changed` in tests.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Core business logic of your own domain (payments, stock, state transitions) — call it explicitly.\n- Anything that must happen for bulk operations or raw SQL — signals don't fire.\n- Slow work (HTTP, email) inline — it adds latency and runs before commit.\n- As \"async\": signals are synchronous; for background work use Celery.\n- Chains of signals triggering saves that trigger signals — debugging nightmare and recursion risk.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Registering receivers in `models.py` or forgetting to import them.** Either they're never connected (module never imported) or circular import errors appear. *Instead:* a `signals.py`/`receivers.py` imported in `AppConfig.ready()`, and the app config referenced in `INSTALLED_APPS`.\n\n**2. Duplicate registration.** A module imported twice (different import paths, or `ready()` called twice in some test setups) connects the receiver twice → two welcome emails. *Instead:* `dispatch_uid` on every `@receiver`.\n\n**3. Side effects before commit.**\n\n```python\n# Before: email sent even if the transaction later rolls back; worker may not find the row\n@receiver(post_save, sender=Order)\ndef notify(sender, instance, created, **kwargs):\n    if created:\n        send_confirmation.delay(instance.pk)\n\n# After\n@receiver(post_save, sender=Order, dispatch_uid=\"orders.notify\")\ndef notify(sender, instance, created, **kwargs):\n    if created:\n        transaction.on_commit(lambda: send_confirmation.delay(instance.pk))\n```\n\n**4. Calling `instance.save()` inside `post_save` for the same model.** Infinite recursion or double writes. *Instead:* `Model.objects.filter(pk=instance.pk).update(...)` (doesn't send signals) or compute the value in `pre_save`.\n\n**5. Missing `**kwargs` in the receiver.** Breaks when Django passes extra arguments (`update_fields`, `origin`). Always accept `**kwargs`.\n\n**6. Weak references to local functions.** Connecting a closure inside a function: it is garbage-collected and stops firing. *Instead:* module-level functions or `weak=False`.\n\n**7. No `sender`.** `@receiver(post_save)` runs for **every** model save in the project, including sessions and logs. Always pass `sender`.\n\n**8. Ignoring `raw=True`.** Fixtures loaded with `loaddata` send `post_save(raw=True)`; receivers querying related tables fail because fixture data isn't all there yet. Return early when `raw`.\n\n**9. Assuming `update_fields` is set.** It's `None` for a normal `save()`. Check `update_fields is None or \"status\" in update_fields`.\n\n**Best practices:** keep receivers to a few lines that enqueue or delegate; document custom signals' kwargs; prefer explicit service calls for your own domain; use `send_robust` for optional listeners; log receiver errors.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: \"Celery task can't find the object\".** In production, `post_save` enqueues a task with the ID; the worker picks it up in milliseconds, before the request's `ATOMIC_REQUESTS` transaction commits, and gets `DoesNotExist`. Intermittent, load-dependent. Fix: `transaction.on_commit`.\n\n**Pitfall: a data migration or import silently skips business logic.** A `bulk_create` of 50k users creates no profiles; months later, `user.profile` raises `RelatedObjectDoesNotExist` in random places. Fix: don't rely on signals for invariants; create related rows explicitly in the import, or use `get_or_create` lazily, and add a DB-level check/backfill.\n\n**Pitfall: deletes got slow and memory-hungry.** Someone added a `post_delete` receiver to `LogEntry` for metrics. `LogEntry.objects.filter(created__lt=cutoff).delete()` now loads millions of rows instead of one fast `DELETE`. Fix: remove the receiver, or use `_raw_delete`-style batched raw deletes in a maintenance command (with care), or track metrics differently.\n\n**Pitfall: request latency from inline receivers.** A `post_save` receiver calls a CRM API synchronously; when the CRM slows, saving any customer takes seconds and Gunicorn workers time out. Fix: receivers must only enqueue; set timeouts on external calls in tasks.\n\n**Pitfall: receiver exception after the row is written.** `post_save` raises; the view returns 500; the user retries; with `ATOMIC_REQUESTS` the row rolled back (fine) — without it, the first row exists and the retry creates a duplicate. Fix: idempotency keys, `send_robust` for non-critical receivers, and transactions around the whole use case.\n\n**Debugging:**\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models.signals import post_save\nfrom orders.models import Order\n\nprint(post_save.receivers)                    # raw registry (ids)\nprint(post_save._live_receivers(Order))       # internal API: which receivers fire for Order\nprint(post_save.has_listeners(Order))         # public: any receivers at all?\n```\n\n- `_live_receivers` is private and its return shape changed in 5.0 (sync and async lists) — use it only interactively.\n- Put a breakpoint in `Signal.send` to see every dispatch in a request; or grep the codebase for `@receiver(` and `.connect(`.\n- In Sentry, a stack trace through `django/dispatch/dispatcher.py` tells you a receiver raised.\n\n**Scaling notes:** signals don't scale across processes — they are local to the Python process. For cross-service events use a message broker with an outbox; for fan-out inside a monolith, signals + `on_commit` + Celery is fine. Keep receiver count low on hot models: each one is a function call per save and may disable fast-delete.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What are Django signals and how do they work internally?**\nA: Signals implement the observer pattern: a `Signal` object keeps a list of receivers, `connect()` adds one (optionally filtered by sender), and `send()` iterates the live receivers and calls each with `sender` and keyword arguments, returning their responses. It's an in-process synchronous loop — no queue or thread — so receivers run in the sender's thread and transaction. Built-in signals cover models (`pre_save`, `post_save`, `pre_delete`, `post_delete`, `m2m_changed`), requests, auth and migrations. Receivers use weak references by default.\n\n**Q: Are Django signals synchronous or asynchronous?**\nA: Synchronous. `send()` calls receivers one after another before returning, in the same thread, and model signals execute inside whatever transaction the `save()` is in. Django 5.0 added `asend()` so async code can dispatch without blocking and both sync and async receivers are supported, but that's still not background processing. For background work, use Celery and dispatch with `transaction.on_commit`.\n\n**Q: Where should you register signal receivers and why?**\nA: In a `signals.py` (or `receivers.py`) module imported from the app's `AppConfig.ready()`. `ready()` runs once after the app registry is fully populated, so models are importable and there are no circular-import issues; importing in `models.py` risks import cycles and putting them elsewhere risks them never being imported. Add `dispatch_uid` so duplicate imports don't register twice.\n\n**Q: Which operations do not trigger `pre_save`/`post_save`?**\nA: `QuerySet.update()`, `bulk_create()`, `bulk_update()`, raw SQL and direct `cursor.execute()` don't call `save()`, so no save signals fire. `QuerySet.delete()` is different — the collector sends `pre_delete`/`post_delete` for each object, which is why connected delete receivers disable fast-delete. `loaddata` does send save signals but with `raw=True`. This is the main reason signals can't be used to guarantee invariants.\n\n**Q: What is `dispatch_uid` for?**\nA: It's a unique identifier for a receiver registration. If `connect()` is called again with the same `dispatch_uid` (e.g. the signals module is imported twice through different paths), Django doesn't add a duplicate, so the receiver runs once per send. Without it the deduplication is by receiver identity, which fails for re-created functions.\n\n**Q: How do you avoid sending an email from `post_save` when the transaction later rolls back?**\nA: Wrap the side effect in `transaction.on_commit(lambda: send_email.delay(instance.pk))`. Django queues the callback and runs it after the outermost transaction commits; on rollback it's discarded. In autocommit mode (no active transaction) it runs immediately. Pass the ID, not the instance, to the Celery task, and make the task idempotent.\n\n**Q: What is `m2m_changed` and why doesn't `post_save` see M2M changes?**\nA: M2M relations live in a separate through table written by the related manager's `add()`, `remove()`, `clear()` and `set()`, which send `m2m_changed` with an `action` (pre/post add/remove/clear), `instance`, `model`, `pk_set` and `reverse`. `save()` writes only the model's own row, and ModelForms (including admin) save M2M afterwards via `save_m2m()`. So `post_save` runs before the M2M data is written; listen to `m2m_changed` with `sender=Model.field.through`.\n\n**Q: What's the difference between `send()` and `send_robust()`?**\nA: `send()` lets a receiver's exception propagate immediately, so later receivers don't run and the caller (e.g. `save()`) sees the error. `send_robust()` catches `Exception` per receiver and returns it in the `(receiver, response)` list, so all receivers run. Use `send_robust` for optional listeners where one failure shouldn't affect others — and log the returned exceptions, since otherwise they vanish.\n\n**Q: When are signals an anti-pattern? (Senior)**\nA: When they're used for core business logic in your own code: the behaviour of `order.save()` becomes invisible, ordering between receivers is implicit, bulk ops and raw SQL silently skip it, receivers run inside the transaction and add latency, and tests need to mute them. They also create hidden recursion when receivers save models. I reserve signals for reacting to code I don't own (contrib/third-party) and as explicit extension points of reusable apps; for my own domain, an explicit service function plus `on_commit` and Celery is clearer and testable.\n\n**Q: You inherited a codebase where `post_save` on `Order` triggers 8 receivers. How would you refactor it? (Senior)**\nA: Inventory receivers with grep and `_live_receivers(Order)`, classify them into core (stock, payment state), peripheral (email, analytics) and derived data (caches, search). Move core logic into an explicit `place_order()`/`mark_paid()` service with clear transaction boundaries and tests; convert peripheral ones to Celery tasks dispatched via `on_commit`, possibly from a custom `order_paid` signal sent explicitly by the service; replace derived-data receivers with cache invalidation helpers or DB-level mechanisms. Do it incrementally behind tests that assert side effects per use case, and verify bulk paths now behave correctly.\n\n**Q: How would you guarantee a downstream system learns about every order, even across crashes? (Senior)**\nA: Signals can't guarantee it: the process can crash after commit but before the receiver enqueues, and `on_commit` callbacks are lost then too. Use a transactional outbox — insert an `OutboxEvent` row in the same transaction as the order, then a relay (Celery beat poller or CDC like Debezium) publishes events and marks them sent, with consumers deduplicating by event ID. This gives at-least-once delivery with idempotent consumers. Signals can still be used locally to *write* the outbox row inside the transaction, but explicit service code is clearer.\n\n**Q: Why might adding a `post_delete` receiver make deletes slower?**\nA: Django's deletion `Collector` can do a \"fast delete\" — a single `DELETE ... WHERE` — only when no per-object work is needed, which requires no delete signal receivers for that model and no Python-side cascades. Once a receiver exists, Django must fetch every object to send `pre_delete`/`post_delete` with the instance, loading rows into memory and issuing more queries. On large tables that turns a millisecond delete into minutes.\n\n**Q: How do you test code that depends on signals and `on_commit`?**\nA: For receivers, test behaviour through the public action (create a user, assert the profile exists). To isolate, connect a `mock.Mock()` with `weak=False` and assert calls, disconnecting afterward, or temporarily `disconnect()` the real receiver. Since `TestCase` wraps each test in a transaction that's rolled back, `on_commit` callbacks never fire — use `self.captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True)` (3.2+) or `TransactionTestCase`. Also test that bulk paths behave correctly without signals.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Signal | Sent by | Key kwargs |\n|---|---|---|\n| `pre_save` / `post_save` | `Model.save()` | `instance`, `raw`, `using`, `update_fields`, `created` (post only) |\n| `pre_delete` / `post_delete` | `Model.delete()`, `QuerySet.delete()` | `instance`, `using`, `origin` (4.1+) |\n| `m2m_changed` | related manager add/remove/clear/set | `action`, `instance`, `reverse`, `model`, `pk_set` |\n| `pre_init` / `post_init` | Model `__init__` | `args`, `kwargs` / `instance` |\n| `post_migrate` | `migrate` (per app) | `app_config`, `verbosity`, `plan`, `apps` |\n| `request_started` / `request_finished` | handler | `environ` / — |\n| `got_request_exception` | handler on unhandled exception | `request` |\n| `user_logged_in` / `_out` / `_login_failed` | `contrib.auth` | `request`, `user` / `credentials` |\n\n- Synchronous, same thread, same transaction.\n- Register in `AppConfig.ready()`; always `sender=` and `dispatch_uid=`.\n- Receivers take `sender, **kwargs`.\n- `update()`, `bulk_create()`, `bulk_update()`, raw SQL: no save signals.\n- Delete receivers disable fast-delete.\n- Side effects: `transaction.on_commit` + Celery with IDs.\n- `send_robust` isolates receiver failures; `asend` (5.0+) for async callers.\n- Signals for code you don't own; explicit services for your own domain.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\"`, create a project with `accounts` and `orders` apps.\n\n1. **Profile receiver.** Implement §5.1, then prove `bulk_create` skips it. Write a management command that backfills missing profiles.\n2. **Duplicate registration.** Remove `dispatch_uid` and import the signals module from two places; observe double execution; restore `dispatch_uid`.\n3. **Rollback bug.** Inside `transaction.atomic()`, save an order whose `post_save` logs \"email sent\", then raise. Observe the log line despite the rollback; fix with `on_commit` and confirm.\n4. **Fast-delete.** Create 100k rows, time `QuerySet.delete()` with and without a `post_delete` receiver, and inspect the query count with `connection.queries`.\n5. **m2m_changed.** Edit an article's tags in the admin and log which of `post_save` and `m2m_changed` see the new tags.\n\n**Mini project — \"Refactor the signal soup\":** start from an `Order` model with five `post_save` receivers (stock, payment, email, analytics, cache). Refactor into a `mark_order_paid` service, a custom `order_paid` signal sent after commit, and Celery tasks — with tests proving each side effect happens exactly once and never on rollback.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings) · [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) · [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) · [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) · [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) · [Ch 29 · Caching](topic.html?p=29-caching) · [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks) · [Ch 33 · Testing](topic.html?p=33-testing)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [Messaging · Idempotency & Outbox](../messaging/topic.html?p=21-idempotency-outbox) · [System Design · Event-Driven & CQRS](../system-design/topic.html?p=25-event-driven-cqrs) · [Redis Caching · Cache Invalidation](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=12-cache-invalidation)\n\n**Free resources:**\n- **Signals (topic guide)** — Django docs · *Beginner* · connecting, sending, custom signals and the caveats. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/signals/>\n- **Signals reference** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · every built-in signal and its arguments. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/signals/>\n- **Database transactions: on_commit** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · why side effects must wait for commit. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/transactions/>\n- **Django source: django/dispatch/dispatcher.py** — GitHub · *Advanced* · the whole signal implementation in one readable file. <https://github.com/django/django/blob/main/django/dispatch/dispatcher.py>\n- **Celery: Tasks** — Celery docs · *Intermediate* · the right place for work that signals should only enqueue. <https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/stable/userguide/tasks.html>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 19.*\n", "20-static-media-files": "# 20 · Static & Media Files\n\n> **In one line:** Static files are *your* assets, gathered by finders from app `static/` folders and `STATICFILES_DIRS` into a single `STATIC_ROOT` by `collectstatic` and served by WhiteNoise, Nginx or a CDN with hashed names; media files are *users'* uploads, written through the `STORAGES[\"default\"]` backend (local disk or S3 via django-storages) — and neither should ever be served by Django's development server in production.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings), [Ch 05 · Django Template Language](topic.html?p=05-templates), [Ch 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation), [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware).\n\nEvery web app has two kinds of files that are not Python code:\n\n- **Static files** — CSS, JavaScript, fonts, images that ship with the code. They change only on deploy, are the same for every user, and should be cached aggressively.\n- **Media files** — user uploads: avatars, invoices, documents. They're created at runtime, can be large, can be malicious, and often need access control.\n\nThe naive approach — \"it works with `runserver`\" — breaks the moment you set `DEBUG = False`: CSS disappears, uploads 404, and someone discovers that `django.views.static.serve` \"fixes\" it and ships that to production, where a Python worker now streams megabytes of images, blocking a Gunicorn slot per request. Or the team scales to three containers and uploads written to one container's disk are invisible on the others. Or a deploy changes `app.css` but browsers keep the old cached copy for a year.\n\nDjango's answer is a clear separation. **`django.contrib.staticfiles`** finds static assets across apps, `collectstatic` copies them to one directory (`STATIC_ROOT`), and a storage backend can hash filenames for cache busting. **Media** goes through the file storage API (`FileField`, `default_storage`), whose backend you swap from local disk to object storage without changing model code. Serving is somebody else's job: WhiteNoise, Nginx, a CDN or S3.\n\n> **Why this matters:** \"What's the difference between `STATIC_ROOT` and `STATICFILES_DIRS`?\", \"What does `collectstatic` do?\", \"Why does my CSS 404 with DEBUG=False?\" and \"How do you store uploads with multiple servers?\" come up in almost every Django interview and in every first deployment.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **`STATIC_URL`** — URL prefix for static files (e.g. `/static/` or `https://cdn.example.com/static/`). *Why it matters:* `{% static %}` builds URLs from it; switching to a CDN is a settings change.\n- **App `static/` directories** — each app's `static/<app_name>/...`. *Why it matters:* reusable apps ship their assets; namespacing by app name prevents collisions.\n- **`STATICFILES_DIRS`** — extra *source* directories (project-level assets not in an app). *Why it matters:* it's an input to `collectstatic`, never the output.\n- **`STATIC_ROOT`** — the single *destination* directory `collectstatic` writes to. *Why it matters:* this is what the web server/WhiteNoise serves; it must not be in `STATICFILES_DIRS`.\n- **Finders** — `STATICFILES_FINDERS`: `FileSystemFinder` (searches `STATICFILES_DIRS`) and `AppDirectoriesFinder` (searches each app's `static/`). *Why it matters:* the **first match wins**, so order and namespacing decide which `logo.png` you get.\n- **`collectstatic`** — management command that runs the finders and saves every file through the static storage backend, then `post_process()`. *Why it matters:* a build/deploy step; hashing and compression happen here.\n- **`findstatic`** — `manage.py findstatic css/app.css -v 2` shows which file wins and where it searched. *Why it matters:* debugging \"wrong file served\".\n- **`STORAGES`** (4.2+) — dict with `\"default\"` (media) and `\"staticfiles\"` backends. *Why it matters:* replaced `DEFAULT_FILE_STORAGE`/`STATICFILES_STORAGE`, which were removed in 5.1.\n- **`ManifestStaticFilesStorage`** — saves `app.css` also as `app.3f2a9c1b7e4d.css`, rewrites references in CSS, writes `staticfiles.json`. *Why it matters:* cache-busting so you can set far-future cache headers.\n- **WhiteNoise** — middleware that serves `STATIC_ROOT` efficiently from Python with correct headers, compression and immutable caching. *Why it matters:* the simplest production static setup for containers/PaaS.\n- **`MEDIA_URL` / `MEDIA_ROOT`** — URL prefix and local directory for uploads with `FileSystemStorage`. *Why it matters:* never the same as static settings.\n- **`FileField` / `ImageField`** — store a *path string* (varchar, default `max_length=100`) in the DB; the bytes live in storage. *Why it matters:* deleting the row doesn't delete the file; long paths overflow `max_length`.\n- **`upload_to`** — string (with `strftime` placeholders) or callable `(instance, filename)` returning a path. *Why it matters:* controls layout, avoids hot directories, prevents user-controlled paths.\n- **django-storages** — third-party backends (S3, GCS, Azure). `storages.backends.s3.S3Storage` for S3-compatible stores. *Why it matters:* shared, durable media across many app servers.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 How `collectstatic` works\n\n`collectstatic` (in `django.contrib.staticfiles.management.commands.collectstatic`):\n\n1. Iterates `get_finders()`; each finder's `list(ignore_patterns)` yields `(path, source_storage)` pairs.\n2. For each relative path, the **first finder/location to yield it wins**; later duplicates are skipped (with `-v 2` it reports them).\n3. Copies (or symlinks with `--link`) the file into the **static storage** (`storages[\"staticfiles\"]`) — for `StaticFilesStorage` that's the `STATIC_ROOT` directory; for an S3 backend, a bucket. Unchanged files are skipped by comparing modification times where the storage supports it.\n4. If the storage has `post_process()` (Manifest storage does), calls it: it hashes file contents (MD5, truncated to 12 hex chars), saves hashed copies, rewrites `url(...)` and `@import` references in CSS (and JS module imports when `support_js_module_import_aggregation` is enabled), repeats passes for nested references, and writes `staticfiles.json` mapping `css/app.css → css/app.3f2a9c1b7e4d.css`.\n\nAt runtime, `{% static \"css/app.css\" %}` calls `staticfiles_storage.url(path)`. With Manifest storage and `DEBUG=False` it looks the path up in the manifest and returns the hashed URL; with `manifest_strict = True` (default) a missing entry raises `ValueError` — the famous \"Missing staticfiles manifest entry\" 500. With `DEBUG=True` it returns the unhashed name.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 340\" width=\"100%\" height=\"340\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj20a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">collectstatic: many sources, finders pick first match, one STATIC_ROOT</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"45\" width=\"230\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"135\" y=\"67\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">STATICFILES_DIRS</text><text x=\"135\" y=\"85\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">BASE_DIR / \"assets\"   (FileSystemFinder)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"115\" width=\"230\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"135\" y=\"137\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">blog/static/blog/...</text><text x=\"135\" y=\"155\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">AppDirectoriesFinder</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"185\" width=\"230\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"135\" y=\"207\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">django/contrib/admin/static</text><text x=\"135\" y=\"225\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">AppDirectoriesFinder</text>\n  <rect x=\"300\" y=\"100\" width=\"200\" height=\"90\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"400\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">collectstatic</text>\n  <text x=\"400\" y=\"144\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">first match per path wins</text>\n  <text x=\"400\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">copy via staticfiles storage</text>\n  <text x=\"400\" y=\"176\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">then post_process()</text>\n  <rect x=\"550\" y=\"60\" width=\"290\" height=\"170\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"695\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">STATIC_ROOT (output only)</text>\n  <text x=\"695\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">css/app.css</text>\n  <text x=\"695\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">css/app.3f2a9c1b7e4d.css</text>\n  <text x=\"695\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">css/app.3f2a9c1b7e4d.css.gz / .br</text>\n  <text x=\"695\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">admin/css/base.css ...</text>\n  <text x=\"695\" y=\"178\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">staticfiles.json (manifest)</text>\n  <text x=\"695\" y=\"206\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\">served by WhiteNoise / Nginx / CDN</text>\n  <path d=\"M252,73 L298,125\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj20a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M252,143 L298,145\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj20a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M252,213 L298,165\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj20a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M502,145 L548,145\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj20a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"270\" width=\"820\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Template: {% static \"css/app.css\" %}  -&gt;  manifest lookup  -&gt;  /static/css/app.3f2a9c1b7e4d.css</text>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"308\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">Content changes =&gt; new hash =&gt; new URL =&gt; Cache-Control: max-age=31536000, immutable is safe</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.2 Why hashing makes caching safe\n\nHTTP caching has a dilemma: long `max-age` makes repeat visits fast but means users keep stale CSS after a deploy; short `max-age` forces revalidation round-trips. **Content-hashed filenames** dissolve it: each version of a file has a unique URL, so the URL can be cached forever (`immutable`) and a deploy simply references new URLs from the (uncached, short-lived) HTML. WhiteNoise detects hashed names and sets a one-year `Cache-Control` on them automatically; unhashed names get a short max-age.\n\n### 3.3 The storage API for media\n\n`FileField` is a thin layer over `django.core.files.storage.Storage`. On `save()` of a model with a new uploaded file, the field's `pre_save()` calls `FieldFile.save(name, content)`, which calls `field.generate_filename(instance, filename)` (applies `upload_to`), then `storage.save(name, content)`. The storage calls `get_available_name()` — if a file already exists at that name, it appends a random 7-character suffix rather than overwriting — and returns the final name, which is what gets stored in the DB column. `instance.file.url` calls `storage.url(name)`; `.path` exists only for local filesystem storage (S3 raises `NotImplementedError`), which is why portable code uses `.open()`/`.url`, never `.path`.\n\nUploads arrive via `request.FILES` (only with `method=\"post\"` and `enctype=\"multipart/form-data\"`). Upload handlers stream them: files up to `FILE_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE` (default 2.5 MB) are kept in memory (`InMemoryUploadedFile`), larger ones go to a temp file (`TemporaryUploadedFile`). `DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE` (also 2.5 MB) caps the non-file request body and does **not** cover file uploads — limit file size yourself (and at the proxy, e.g. Nginx `client_max_body_size`).\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 Production topology\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 350\" width=\"100%\" height=\"350\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj20b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj20c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Who serves what in production</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"140\" width=\"110\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#334155\"/><text x=\"75\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Browser</text>\n  <rect x=\"190\" y=\"50\" width=\"170\" height=\"54\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/><text x=\"275\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">CDN</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">/static/* hashed, cached 1y</text>\n  <rect x=\"190\" y=\"140\" width=\"170\" height=\"54\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"275\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Gunicorn + Django</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">HTML, API, upload handling</text>\n  <rect x=\"190\" y=\"240\" width=\"170\" height=\"54\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"275\" y=\"262\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">S3 / object storage</text><text x=\"275\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">media via presigned URL</text>\n  <rect x=\"430\" y=\"50\" width=\"200\" height=\"54\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/><text x=\"530\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">WhiteNoise (origin)</text><text x=\"530\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">serves STATIC_ROOT in-process</text>\n  <rect x=\"430\" y=\"140\" width=\"200\" height=\"54\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"530\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">PostgreSQL</text><text x=\"530\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">FileField stores path only</text>\n  <rect x=\"680\" y=\"50\" width=\"160\" height=\"54\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/><text x=\"760\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">CI build</text><text x=\"760\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">collectstatic in image</text>\n  <path d=\"M132,155 L188,85\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj20b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M132,165 L188,165\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj20b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M132,178 L188,262\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj20c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M362,77 L428,77\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj20b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M362,167 L428,167\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj20b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M275,196 L275,238\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj20c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M678,77 L632,77\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj20b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"222\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">storage.save()</text>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"325\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">Django never streams static or media bytes in the hot path; it only generates URLs and accepts uploads.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 4.2 Dev vs production\n\n```text\n                     DEBUG=True (runserver)                  DEBUG=False (production)\nstatic            staticfiles app serves from finders      collectstatic -> STATIC_ROOT -> WhiteNoise/Nginx/CDN\n                  (no collectstatic needed)                runserver refuses unless --insecure\nmedia             add static(MEDIA_URL, ...) to urls       storage backend URL (S3/CDN) or Nginx alias\n                  helper returns [] when DEBUG=False       private files: presigned URLs or X-Accel-Redirect\n{% static %}      unhashed names                           hashed names from staticfiles.json\n```\n\n### 4.3 Deploy workflow\n\n```text\n1. CI: pip install, then  python manage.py collectstatic --noinput  (inside the Docker build)\n2. Image contains STATIC_ROOT + staticfiles.json  -> every container has the same manifest\n3. (CDN/S3 static) collectstatic uploads to the bucket once per release instead\n4. Release: migrate as a separate step; roll out containers\n5. Old hashed files stay available during rollout (don't --clear S3 static mid-deploy)\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example — settings, templates, uploads in development\n\n```python\n# settings.py\nfrom pathlib import Path\nBASE_DIR = Path(__file__).resolve().parent.parent\n\nINSTALLED_APPS = [\n    \"django.contrib.staticfiles\",\n    # ...\n    \"profiles\",\n]\n\nSTATIC_URL = \"static/\"\nSTATICFILES_DIRS = [BASE_DIR / \"assets\"]          # sources (project-wide)\nSTATIC_ROOT = BASE_DIR / \"staticfiles\"            # destination of collectstatic\n\nMEDIA_URL = \"media/\"\nMEDIA_ROOT = BASE_DIR / \"media\"\n\nSTORAGES = {\n    \"default\": {\"BACKEND\": \"django.core.files.storage.FileSystemStorage\"},\n    \"staticfiles\": {\"BACKEND\": \"django.contrib.staticfiles.storage.StaticFilesStorage\"},\n}\n```\n\n```html\n{# templates/base.html #}\n{% load static %}\n<!doctype html>\n<html>\n<head>\n  <link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"{% static 'css/app.css' %}\">\n</head>\n<body>\n  <img src=\"{% static 'profiles/img/default-avatar.png' %}\" alt=\"\">\n  {% if user.profile.avatar %}\n    <img src=\"{{ user.profile.avatar.url }}\" alt=\"avatar\">\n  {% endif %}\n</body>\n</html>\n```\n\n```python\n# profiles/models.py\nimport uuid\nfrom pathlib import Path\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.core.validators import FileExtensionValidator\nfrom django.db import models\n\n\ndef avatar_path(instance, filename):\n    ext = Path(filename).suffix.lower()\n    # never trust the user's filename; spread files across prefixes\n    return f\"avatars/{instance.user_id}/{uuid.uuid4().hex}{ext}\"\n\n\nclass Profile(models.Model):\n    user = models.OneToOneField(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE)\n    avatar = models.ImageField(                 # requires Pillow\n        upload_to=avatar_path, blank=True, max_length=255,\n        validators=[FileExtensionValidator([\"jpg\", \"jpeg\", \"png\", \"webp\"])],\n    )\n```\n\n```python\n# profiles/views.py\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required\nfrom django.shortcuts import redirect, render\nfrom .forms import AvatarForm\n\n\n@login_required\ndef upload_avatar(request):\n    profile = request.user.profile\n    form = AvatarForm(request.POST or None, request.FILES or None, instance=profile)\n    if request.method == \"POST\" and form.is_valid():\n        form.save()\n        return redirect(\"profile\")\n    return render(request, \"profiles/avatar.html\", {\"form\": form})\n```\n\n```html\n<form method=\"post\" enctype=\"multipart/form-data\">\n  {% csrf_token %}\n  {{ form.as_p }}\n  <button type=\"submit\">Upload</button>\n</form>\n```\n\n```python\n# urls.py — development only; static() returns [] when DEBUG is False\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.conf.urls.static import static\nfrom django.urls import include, path\n\nurlpatterns = [path(\"\", include(\"profiles.urls\"))]\nif settings.DEBUG:\n    urlpatterns += static(settings.MEDIA_URL, document_root=settings.MEDIA_ROOT)\n```\n\n```bash\npython manage.py findstatic css/app.css -v 2\npython manage.py collectstatic --noinput\n```\n\n```text\nFound 'css/app.css' here:\n  /srv/app/assets/css/app.css\nLooking in the following locations:\n  /srv/app/assets\n  /srv/app/.venv/lib/python3.12/site-packages/django/contrib/admin/static\n  /srv/app/profiles/static\n\n132 static files copied to '/srv/app/staticfiles'.\n```\n\n### 5.2 Real-world example — WhiteNoise for static, S3 for media, private documents\n\n```bash\npip install \"whitenoise[brotli]\" \"django-storages[s3]\" boto3 Pillow\n```\n\n```python\n# settings/production.py\nimport os\n\nMIDDLEWARE = [\n    \"django.middleware.security.SecurityMiddleware\",\n    \"whitenoise.middleware.WhiteNoiseMiddleware\",       # right after SecurityMiddleware\n    \"django.contrib.sessions.middleware.SessionMiddleware\",\n    # ...\n]\n\nSTATIC_URL = os.environ.get(\"STATIC_URL\", \"/static/\")  # or https://cdn.example.com/static/\nSTATIC_ROOT = BASE_DIR / \"staticfiles\"\n\nSTORAGES = {\n    \"default\": {                                         # public media (avatars)\n        \"BACKEND\": \"storages.backends.s3.S3Storage\",\n        \"OPTIONS\": {\n            \"bucket_name\": os.environ[\"MEDIA_BUCKET\"],\n            \"location\": \"media\",\n            \"file_overwrite\": False,                     # unique names instead of overwrite\n            \"querystring_auth\": False,                   # public objects; URLs without signature\n            \"custom_domain\": os.environ.get(\"MEDIA_CDN_DOMAIN\"),\n        },\n    },\n    \"staticfiles\": {\n        \"BACKEND\": \"whitenoise.storage.CompressedManifestStaticFilesStorage\",\n    },\n    \"private\": {                                         # named storage for sensitive files\n        \"BACKEND\": \"storages.backends.s3.S3Storage\",\n        \"OPTIONS\": {\n            \"bucket_name\": os.environ[\"PRIVATE_BUCKET\"],\n            \"querystring_auth\": True,                    # presigned URLs\n            \"querystring_expire\": 300,                   # seconds\n            \"file_overwrite\": False,\n        },\n    },\n}\n# Credentials come from the instance/pod IAM role; don't put keys in settings.\n```\n\n```python\n# documents/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.core.files.storage import storages\nfrom django.db import models\n\n\ndef private_storage():\n    return storages[\"private\"]          # callable: resolved lazily, testable, migration-stable\n\n\ndef invoice_path(instance, filename):\n    return f\"invoices/{instance.account_id}/{instance.number}.pdf\"\n\n\nclass Invoice(models.Model):\n    account = models.ForeignKey(\"accounts.Account\", on_delete=models.PROTECT)\n    number = models.CharField(max_length=32, unique=True)\n    pdf = models.FileField(storage=private_storage, upload_to=invoice_path, max_length=255)\n```\n\n```python\n# documents/views.py\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required\nfrom django.shortcuts import get_object_or_404, redirect\nfrom .models import Invoice\n\n\n@login_required\ndef download_invoice(request, number):\n    invoice = get_object_or_404(Invoice, number=number, account__members=request.user)\n    return redirect(invoice.pdf.url)    # 5-minute presigned S3 URL; Django never streams bytes\n```\n\nFor local-disk private files behind Nginx, the equivalent is to authorize in Django and return an empty response with `X-Accel-Redirect: /protected/invoices/...` pointing at an `internal` Nginx location, so Nginx streams the file.\n\nGenerating a file server-side and saving it through storage:\n\n```python\nfrom django.core.files.base import ContentFile\n\ndef attach_pdf(invoice, pdf_bytes):\n    invoice.pdf.save(f\"{invoice.number}.pdf\", ContentFile(pdf_bytes), save=True)\n    # name passes through upload_to; storage may adjust it to avoid collisions\n```\n\nDockerfile excerpt — collect at build time so every container ships the same manifest:\n\n```bash\n# in Dockerfile\nRUN DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=config.settings.production \\\n    SECRET_KEY=build-only MEDIA_BUCKET=x PRIVATE_BUCKET=x \\\n    python manage.py collectstatic --noinput\n```\n\n### 5.3 Cleaning up orphaned files\n\nDjango doesn't delete files when you delete the row or replace the file (by design, since a rollback could leave the row pointing to a deleted file). Handle it explicitly after commit:\n\n```python\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.db.models.signals import post_delete\nfrom django.dispatch import receiver\nfrom .models import Invoice\n\n\n@receiver(post_delete, sender=Invoice, dispatch_uid=\"documents.delete_pdf\")\ndef delete_pdf_file(sender, instance, **kwargs):\n    if instance.pdf:\n        name, storage = instance.pdf.name, instance.pdf.storage\n        transaction.on_commit(lambda: storage.delete(name))\n```\n\nRemember that a delete receiver disables fast-delete for that model (see [Ch 19](topic.html?p=19-signals)); for high-volume tables prefer a periodic cleanup job or S3 lifecycle rules.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Option | Serves | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `runserver` / `static()` | Dev only | Zero setup | Insecure, slow, disabled when DEBUG=False |\n| WhiteNoise | Static | One process, correct headers, gzip/brotli, works on PaaS/containers | Uses app worker for cache misses (put a CDN in front) |\n| Nginx `alias` | Static + local media | Very fast, sendfile | Needs shared volume; another config to manage |\n| CDN (with WhiteNoise/S3 origin) | Static + public media | Global latency, offloads origin | Cache invalidation (solved by hashing), cost |\n| Local `FileSystemStorage` for media | Media | Simple | Breaks with >1 server unless shared volume; backups |\n| S3 via django-storages | Media (and static) | Durable, shared, presigned URLs, lifecycle rules | Latency on save, network dependency, IAM setup |\n| `ManifestStaticFilesStorage` | Static names | Cache busting, immutable caching | Strict manifest errors; `collectstatic` required in every env with DEBUG=False |\n\n### When to use\n\n- WhiteNoise + `CompressedManifestStaticFilesStorage` for nearly every containerized Django app; add a CDN when traffic is global.\n- S3 (or GCS/Azure) for media as soon as you have more than one app server or ephemeral containers.\n- Private storage + presigned URLs (or `X-Accel-Redirect`) for anything that needs authorization.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Don't serve media through WhiteNoise — it only serves files present at startup (`STATIC_ROOT`), not runtime uploads.\n- Don't put user uploads on the same domain as your app without protections; don't serve them via `django.views.static.serve` in production.\n- Don't use the S3 storage for static files if you can avoid it and are on WhiteNoise — it slows `collectstatic` and adds a moving part; use a CDN over WhiteNoise instead.\n- Don't use local disk media on Kubernetes/Heroku-style ephemeral filesystems.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Confusing `STATIC_ROOT` and `STATICFILES_DIRS`.** Putting `STATIC_ROOT` inside `STATICFILES_DIRS` raises `ImproperlyConfigured` (\"The STATICFILES_DIRS setting should not contain the STATIC_ROOT setting\"). Editing files in `STATIC_ROOT` by hand — they get overwritten on the next `collectstatic`. *Instead:* sources in app `static/` and `STATICFILES_DIRS`; `STATIC_ROOT` is build output, git-ignored.\n\n**2. Not namespacing app static files.**\n\n```text\n# Before: two apps both ship static/logo.png -> first finder wins, wrong logo\nblog/static/logo.png\nshop/static/logo.png\n\n# After\nblog/static/blog/logo.png      {% static 'blog/logo.png' %}\nshop/static/shop/logo.png      {% static 'shop/logo.png' %}\n```\n\n**3. Hardcoding `/static/...` in templates.** Breaks hashing and CDN switching. *Instead:* `{% static %}` everywhere (and `static()` from `django.templatetags.static` in Python).\n\n**4. \"Missing staticfiles manifest entry\" in production.** Template references a file that wasn't collected (typo, file added after build, or `collectstatic` not run). *Instead:* run `collectstatic` in CI, run tests with Manifest storage and `DEBUG=False` once, and fix references.\n\n**5. Trusting upload metadata.** `content_type` and the filename are client-controlled. *Instead:* generate names server-side; validate with `FileExtensionValidator` and by inspecting content (Pillow for images via `ImageField`, `python-magic` for others); size limits.\n\n**6. Serving uploads from the main domain.** An uploaded `.html` or `.svg` with script runs as your origin → stored XSS that can read cookies and CSRF tokens. *Instead:* separate domain (`usercontent.example.com`), `Content-Disposition: attachment` for downloads, `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`.\n\n**7. Using `.path` on files.** Works locally, raises `NotImplementedError` on S3. *Instead:* `.open()`, `.read()`, `.url`, `storage.exists()`.\n\n**8. `FileField` default `max_length=100`.** Long `upload_to` paths with UUIDs exceed it, raising a `DataError`/validation error. *Instead:* set `max_length=255`.\n\n**9. Using removed settings.** `DEFAULT_FILE_STORAGE`/`STATICFILES_STORAGE` were removed in 5.1. *Instead:* `STORAGES`.\n\n**Best practices:** collect at build time; long cache on hashed files only; media on object storage; private by default for documents; `upload_to` callable with server-generated names; clean up orphans asynchronously.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: CSS disappears after setting `DEBUG=False`.** Symptom: unstyled admin, 404s for `/static/...`. Root cause: the staticfiles app only serves in DEBUG, and nothing else serves `STATIC_ROOT`. Fix: `collectstatic` + WhiteNoise (or Nginx/CDN). Don't \"fix\" with `runserver --insecure`.\n\n**Pitfall: 500s with `ValueError: Missing staticfiles manifest entry for 'js/new.js'`.** After a deploy, some containers run old images with an old `staticfiles.json`, or `collectstatic` ran in a different container than the one serving. Fix: bake static + manifest into the image; deploy immutable images; if static is on S3, upload new files before rolling out and don't delete old hashed files until the rollout finishes.\n\n**Pitfall: uploads vanish.** Users' avatars disappear after every deploy on Kubernetes. Root cause: `FileSystemStorage` writing to the container's ephemeral filesystem; or uploads land on pod A and pod B can't serve them. Fix: S3 storage; or a ReadWriteMany volume (simpler but slower and riskier).\n\n**Pitfall: Gunicorn workers blocked by large uploads.** A sync worker reads a 500 MB upload from a slow client for minutes; timeouts hit at 30 s; workers exhausted. Fix: Nginx in front buffers request bodies (`client_max_body_size`, default buffering), enforce size limits, or upload **directly to S3 with presigned POST/PUT URLs** and send Django only the object key.\n\n**Pitfall: Stale CSS after deploy.** Without Manifest storage, `app.css` was cached for a year by the CDN. Fix: hashed names; or purge the CDN; never long-cache unhashed URLs.\n\n**Pitfall: slow `collectstatic`.** With S3 static storage, each run compares/upload thousands of files over the network. Fix: WhiteNoise in the image + CDN, or cache `STATIC_ROOT` between CI runs.\n\n**Debugging:**\n\n```bash\npython manage.py findstatic admin/css/base.css -v 2      # which source wins\npython manage.py collectstatic --dry-run --noinput -v 2   # what would be copied\ncurl -I https://example.com/static/css/app.3f2a9c1b7e4d.css   # Cache-Control, Content-Encoding\npython manage.py shell -c \"from django.contrib.staticfiles.storage import staticfiles_storage as s; print(s.url('css/app.css'))\"\npython manage.py shell -c \"from django.core.files.storage import default_storage as d; print(type(d), d.url('x'))\"\n```\n\n- Check `staticfiles.json` in the running container to confirm a file is in the manifest.\n- `manage.py check --deploy` warns about some static/media misconfigurations alongside security settings.\n- S3 403s on `.url`: check `querystring_auth`, bucket policy (public access block), object ACLs (prefer bucket policy over per-object `default_acl`).\n\n**Scaling notes:** put a CDN in front of both static (origin WhiteNoise) and public media (origin S3). Use direct-to-S3 uploads for large files, processing (thumbnails, virus scan) in Celery tasks triggered after commit or by S3 events. Use S3 lifecycle rules to expire temp uploads. Keep media paths prefix-distributed (by user ID/UUID) for listing and for operational sanity.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `STATIC_ROOT`, `STATICFILES_DIRS` and `STATIC_URL`?**\nA: `STATICFILES_DIRS` lists extra *source* directories that the `FileSystemFinder` searches, in addition to each app's `static/` folder found by `AppDirectoriesFinder`. `STATIC_ROOT` is the single *destination* directory where `collectstatic` copies everything for deployment, and it's what the web server or WhiteNoise serves. `STATIC_URL` is the URL prefix used to build links (e.g. `/static/` or a CDN URL). `STATIC_ROOT` must not be one of the `STATICFILES_DIRS`, and you never edit it by hand.\n\n**Q: What does `collectstatic` do?**\nA: It runs each configured finder, gathers every static file across apps and `STATICFILES_DIRS` (first match per relative path wins), and saves them through the static storage backend — into `STATIC_ROOT` for filesystem storage, or a bucket for remote storage. Then it calls the storage's `post_process()`, which for Manifest storage hashes files, rewrites CSS references, and writes `staticfiles.json`. It's a deploy/build-time step, usually run with `--noinput` in CI.\n\n**Q: Why does static stop working when `DEBUG=False`?**\nA: The `django.contrib.staticfiles` app serves files via `runserver` only when `DEBUG=True`, as a development convenience; Django is not meant to serve static files in production because it's inefficient and insecure. In production you must run `collectstatic` and serve `STATIC_ROOT` with WhiteNoise, Nginx or a CDN. Also, `static()` URL helper for media returns no patterns when DEBUG is off.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between static and media files?**\nA: Static files are part of the codebase — CSS, JS, images shipped by developers — the same for all users and changed only on deploy, so they're collected and cached aggressively. Media files are uploaded by users at runtime through `FileField`/`ImageField` and the default storage backend; they're untrusted, may need access control, and must live on shared storage when you have multiple servers. They have separate settings (`STATIC_*` vs `MEDIA_*`, `STORAGES[\"staticfiles\"]` vs `STORAGES[\"default\"]`) and should be served from different paths or domains.\n\n**Q: How does `ManifestStaticFilesStorage` work and why use it?**\nA: During `collectstatic` post-processing it computes an MD5-based hash of each file's content, saves a copy named like `app.3f2a9c1b7e4d.css`, rewrites `url()`/`@import` references in CSS to the hashed names, and stores the mapping in `staticfiles.json`. `{% static %}` then emits hashed URLs (when DEBUG is False). Because a content change produces a new URL, you can set year-long immutable cache headers without ever serving stale assets. The catch: references to files missing from the manifest raise `ValueError` with `manifest_strict` on.\n\n**Q: What does WhiteNoise do and where does it go in `MIDDLEWARE`?**\nA: WhiteNoise is a middleware that serves files from `STATIC_ROOT` directly from the Python process with proper headers: far-future caching for hashed files, gzip/brotli pre-compressed variants, `Content-Type` and range support. It goes immediately after `SecurityMiddleware` and above everything else, so static requests short-circuit before sessions, CSRF or auth do work. Its `CompressedManifestStaticFilesStorage` combines hashing with compression at `collectstatic` time. Put a CDN in front for global traffic; it isn't for user uploads.\n\n**Q: How do you store user uploads when you run several app servers?**\nA: Use a shared storage backend — typically S3 or another object store through django-storages, configured as `STORAGES[\"default\"]` — so any server can write and any server (or the CDN) can serve. The model's `FileField` stores only the object key; `.url` returns a public or presigned URL. Alternatives like an NFS/RWX volume work but are slower and less durable. Also avoid `.path` in code because remote storages don't support it.\n\n**Q: What is the `STORAGES` setting?**\nA: Introduced in Django 4.2, it's a dict of named storage configurations, each with a `BACKEND` and `OPTIONS`; the `\"default\"` alias is used for `FileField` and `default_storage`, the `\"staticfiles\"` alias for static files. You can add more aliases (e.g. `\"private\"`) and access them via `django.core.files.storage.storages[\"private\"]`. It replaced `DEFAULT_FILE_STORAGE` and `STATICFILES_STORAGE`, which were deprecated in 4.2 and removed in 5.1.\n\n**Q: How would you serve private files that only authorized users may download? (Senior)**\nA: Keep them in non-public storage and authorize in Django, but don't stream bytes through the Python worker. With S3, the view checks permissions and redirects to a short-lived presigned URL (`querystring_auth=True`, a few minutes' expiry), possibly with `ResponseContentDisposition` to force download. With local disk behind Nginx, return `X-Accel-Redirect` to an `internal` location (or `X-Sendfile` on Apache) so the web server sends the file. Log access, avoid predictable keys, and serve from a separate domain to contain any content-type tricks.\n\n**Q: What are the security risks of user uploads and how do you mitigate them? (Senior)**\nA: Risks: stored XSS via HTML/SVG served from your origin, path traversal through crafted filenames, oversized uploads exhausting disk/memory/workers, malware distribution, and content-type spoofing. Mitigate by generating filenames server-side in `upload_to`, validating extension and actual content (Pillow, libmagic), enforcing size limits in Django and at the proxy, serving from a separate cookieless domain with `nosniff` and `Content-Disposition: attachment` for non-images, keeping sensitive files private with presigned URLs, and scanning asynchronously before making files available. Django's storage already prevents writing outside the storage root and avoids overwriting via `get_available_name`.\n\n**Q: A deploy causes intermittent 500s: \"Missing staticfiles manifest entry\". Diagnose it. (Senior)**\nA: Manifest storage raises when `{% static %}` references a path absent from `staticfiles.json` of the process handling the request. Intermittency suggests version skew — some instances have the new templates but an old manifest, or the manifest is generated at container start in some pods and not others, or static lives in a shared bucket that was overwritten/cleared mid-rollout. Fix by running `collectstatic` at image build so templates and manifest ship together, using immutable images, uploading new static before rollout and keeping old hashed files, and catching missing references in CI by running tests or a smoke render with `DEBUG=False`.\n\n**Q: How would you handle 1 GB video uploads in a Django app? (Senior)**\nA: Don't route the bytes through Django: a sync worker would be tied up for the whole transfer and hit timeouts. Issue a presigned S3 multipart upload (or presigned POST) from an API endpoint after authorization, let the browser upload directly to S3, then have the client notify Django (or use an S3 event) with the object key. A Celery task validates, transcodes and creates thumbnails, updating the model after commit. Enforce size and content-type in the presigned policy, and use lifecycle rules to clean up abandoned uploads.\n\n**Q: Does deleting a model instance delete its file?**\nA: No. Since Django 1.3, `FileField` files are not deleted when the instance is deleted or when the file is replaced, because a transaction rollback could leave a row pointing to a missing file and other rows might reference it. If you want cleanup, delete explicitly — e.g. in a `post_delete` receiver using `transaction.on_commit(lambda: storage.delete(name))` — or with a periodic orphan sweep or object-storage lifecycle rules. Packages like django-cleanup automate it.\n\n**Q: How does Django handle an uploaded file in memory vs on disk?**\nA: Upload handlers process the multipart stream: by default `MemoryFileUploadHandler` keeps files up to `FILE_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE` (2.5 MB) in memory as `InMemoryUploadedFile`, and `TemporaryFileUploadHandler` streams larger ones to a temp file (`TemporaryUploadedFile`, in `FILE_UPLOAD_TEMP_DIR`). Both appear in `request.FILES` and are iterable in chunks via `.chunks()`. `DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE` limits the non-file body only, so total upload size must be limited in validation and at the proxy.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Setting / tool | Role |\n|---|---|\n| `STATIC_URL` | URL prefix for static (`/static/` or CDN) |\n| `STATICFILES_DIRS` | Extra source dirs (input) |\n| app `static/<app>/` | Per-app sources (input) |\n| `STATIC_ROOT` | `collectstatic` output; served in prod |\n| `STATICFILES_FINDERS` | FileSystemFinder, AppDirectoriesFinder; first match wins |\n| `STORAGES[\"staticfiles\"]` | `StaticFilesStorage`, `ManifestStaticFilesStorage`, WhiteNoise storage |\n| `MEDIA_URL` / `MEDIA_ROOT` | Uploads URL / local dir |\n| `STORAGES[\"default\"]` | Media backend (`FileSystemStorage`, `storages.backends.s3.S3Storage`) |\n| `findstatic -v 2` | Which file wins |\n| `collectstatic --noinput` | Build-time gather + hash |\n\n- `STATIC_ROOT` is output, `STATICFILES_DIRS` is input; never overlap.\n- Namespace app static files: `static/<app>/...`.\n- Always `{% static %}`; hashed names only when DEBUG=False with Manifest storage.\n- WhiteNoise right after `SecurityMiddleware`; CDN in front for scale.\n- Media on object storage with multiple servers; never `.path`.\n- `STORAGES` replaced the old storage settings (removed in 5.1).\n- Uploads are untrusted: server-side names, validate content, separate domain, size limits.\n- Files are not deleted with rows; clean up after commit.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" whitenoise Pillow`.\n\n1. **Finder precedence.** Put `css/app.css` in both `STATICFILES_DIRS` and an app's `static/`; run `findstatic -v 2` and `collectstatic -v 2` to see which wins; then namespace and fix.\n2. **DEBUG=False.** Flip `DEBUG=False`, observe broken CSS; add WhiteNoise and `CompressedManifestStaticFilesStorage`, run `collectstatic`, and inspect headers with `curl -I` for hashed and unhashed URLs.\n3. **Manifest error.** Reference a non-existent file in a template with Manifest storage and DEBUG=False; read the error; write a test that renders all templates to catch it.\n4. **Avatar upload.** Implement §5.1 with a UUID `upload_to`, extension validator and a 2 MB size check in `clean_avatar()`; try uploading an SVG renamed to `.png`.\n5. **S3 locally.** Run MinIO in Docker, configure `S3Storage` with `endpoint_url`, upload a file, and compare `.url` with `querystring_auth` on and off.\n\n**Mini project — \"Docs vault\":** public avatars on an S3 `default` storage behind a CDN domain, private invoices on a `private` storage served via presigned URLs after a permission check, direct-to-S3 uploads for large files with a Celery post-processing task, orphan cleanup after commit, and a Docker build that runs `collectstatic` with WhiteNoise.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 05 · Django Template Language](topic.html?p=05-templates) · [Ch 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation) · [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) · [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals) · [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security) · [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks) · [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [System Design · CDN](../system-design/topic.html?p=11-cdn) · [REST API · HTTP Caching & ETags](../rest-api/topic.html?p=25-http-caching-etags) · [Docker · Multi-stage Builds](../docker/topic.html?p=07-multistage-builds) · [Docker · Volumes & Storage](../docker/topic.html?p=13-volumes-storage) · [System Design · Design Dropbox](../system-design/topic.html?p=40-design-dropbox)\n\n**Free resources:**\n- **Managing static files (how-to)** — Django docs · *Beginner* · setup, `{% static %}`, development serving. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/static-files/>\n- **How to deploy static files** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · production strategies, CDN, remote storage. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/static-files/deployment/>\n- **The staticfiles app reference** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · finders, `collectstatic`, Manifest storage options. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/contrib/staticfiles/>\n- **Managing files** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `FileField`, `File`, storage API, `STORAGES`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/files/>\n- **File uploads** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `request.FILES`, upload handlers, size settings. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/http/file-uploads/>\n- **WhiteNoise documentation** — whitenoise.readthedocs.io · *Beginner* · Django integration, compression, CDN setup. <https://whitenoise.readthedocs.io/>\n- **django-storages documentation** — django-storages.readthedocs.io · *Intermediate* · S3/GCS/Azure backends and their options. <https://django-storages.readthedocs.io/>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 20.*\n", "21-drf-serializers": "# 21 · DRF Serializers\n\n> **In one line:** A serializer is the single, declarative boundary between untrusted JSON and your models — it converts in both directions, validates in a fixed and knowable order, and decides which fields can be written; mastering that order, `ModelSerializer`'s generated fields, nested writes and N+1 traps is most of what \"knowing DRF\" means in an interview.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation) (the same validation pipeline idea), [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields), [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships), [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization).\n\nEvery JSON API has to do the same three jobs on every request: turn a model instance (or a queryset of them) into JSON-safe primitives, turn incoming JSON back into Python values, and refuse anything that is malformed, out of range, or not allowed to be written by this client. The naive way is to do it by hand in the view: `json.loads(request.body)`, pick keys out of a dict, `int()` a few of them, write `if not data.get(\"title\"): return JsonResponse({...}, status=400)`, and then build the response with a dict comprehension over `Book.objects.all()`.\n\nThat approach fails in predictable ways. Validation logic gets copy-pasted between the create view and the update view and drifts apart. Error formats differ endpoint by endpoint, so the frontend team writes a parser per screen. Someone adds `is_staff` to the model and the \"update profile\" endpoint happily writes it because it did `for k, v in data.items(): setattr(user, k, v)` — a **mass-assignment** vulnerability (OWASP API3). The list endpoint touches `book.author.name` in a loop and fires one query per row. And nothing is self-documenting, so there is no schema.\n\nDjango already solved the form version of this problem with `Form`/`ModelForm` ([Ch 06](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation)). Django REST Framework's **serializers** are the API equivalent, deliberately modelled on forms: declare fields, call `is_valid()`, read `validated_data`, call `save()`. A `ModelSerializer` is to `Serializer` what `ModelForm` is to `Form` — it introspects the model to generate fields, validators and default `create()`/`update()` implementations.\n\nThe difference from forms is that serializers are *bidirectional* and *nestable*. They render output (`to_representation`) as well as parse input (`to_internal_value`), and a serializer is itself a field, so you can nest them to represent relationships. That power is also where most production bugs come from: nested serializers that cause N+1 queries, nested writes that DRF refuses to do for you, and `SerializerMethodField`s that quietly hit the database once per object.\n\n> **Why this matters:** In DRF interviews, \"walk me through what happens when you call `is_valid()`\" and \"how do you write a nested serializer that can create children\" are near-certain questions. They test whether you understand the framework or just copied a tutorial.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **`Serializer`** — a declarative class of fields with `to_representation` (instance → primitives) and `to_internal_value` (primitives → validated Python). *Why it matters:* it is the one place input is trusted, so every write must go through one.\n- **`ModelSerializer`** — a `Serializer` that generates fields from `Meta.model` + `Meta.fields`, adds model-derived validators (`UniqueValidator`, `UniqueTogetherValidator`), and implements `create()`/`update()`. *Why:* removes boilerplate, but generated behaviour must be inspected (`print(repr(MySerializer()))`).\n- **`initial_data` / `validated_data` / `data` / `errors`** — the raw input, the cleaned dict after `is_valid()`, the rendered output, and the error dict. *Why:* accessing them in the wrong order raises assertions DRF deliberately added.\n- **Field-level validation** — `validate_<field_name>(self, value)` methods plus `validators=[...]` on a field. *Why:* runs per field, errors keyed by field name.\n- **Object-level validation** — `validate(self, attrs)` for cross-field rules. *Why:* runs only after all fields individually passed.\n- **Validators** — reusable callables (`UniqueValidator`, `UniqueTogetherValidator`, custom callables or classes with `requires_context = True`). *Why:* they can be attached to fields or `Meta.validators`.\n- **`read_only` / `write_only` / `read_only_fields` / `extra_kwargs`** — control the direction a field participates in. *Why:* this is your mass-assignment defence.\n- **`source`** — where a field reads from: `source=\"author.name\"`, `source=\"get_status_display\"`, `source=\"*\"` (the whole object). *Why:* reshapes output without new methods.\n- **`SerializerMethodField`** — read-only field computed by `get_<name>(self, obj)`. *Why:* flexible, but the most common source of hidden N+1 queries.\n- **Relational fields** — `PrimaryKeyRelatedField`, `SlugRelatedField`, `StringRelatedField`, `HyperlinkedRelatedField`, or a nested serializer. *Why:* each has a different query cost and write story.\n- **`ListSerializer`** — what you actually get when you pass `many=True`; it wraps a `child` serializer. *Why:* bulk update/create customisation goes on it via `Meta.list_serializer_class`.\n- **`partial=True`** — skip \"required\" checks for absent fields (PATCH). *Why:* object-level `validate()` still runs, with only the provided keys in `attrs`.\n- **`context`** — a dict (usually `{\"request\": ..., \"view\": ..., \"format\": ...}`) passed by generic views. *Why:* hyperlinked fields and `CurrentUserDefault` need `request`.\n- **`HiddenField` + `CurrentUserDefault`** — inject server-side values (owner) that the client cannot set. *Why:* clean way to satisfy unique-together validators involving the user.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### Serializers are fields, fields are serializers\n\nIn DRF's design, `Serializer` subclasses `BaseSerializer`, which subclasses `Field`. A serializer declared with `SerializerMetaclass` collects its declared fields into `_declared_fields` (just as Django's `ModelBase` collects model fields). When you access `serializer.fields`, DRF calls `get_fields()` — for a plain `Serializer` that is a deep copy of the declared fields; for `ModelSerializer` it also runs `build_field()` for every name in `Meta.fields`, using `serializer_field_mapping` (e.g. `models.CharField → serializers.CharField`, `models.ForeignKey → PrimaryKeyRelatedField`) and `get_extra_kwargs()` to apply `max_length`, `required`, `allow_null`, `validators` and your `extra_kwargs`. Each field is then **bound** to the parent (`field.bind(field_name, parent)`), which is how a field knows its name, its `source_attrs`, and its root's `context`.\n\nBecause a serializer is a field, nesting is just composition: `author = AuthorSerializer()` on `BookSerializer` means \"to represent `book.author`, call `AuthorSerializer.to_representation(book.author)`\".\n\n### The validation pipeline — the exact order\n\n`is_valid()` calls `self.run_validation(self.initial_data)` and stores the result in `_validated_data` (or the caught `ValidationError.detail` in `_errors`). `Serializer.run_validation` does:\n\n1. **`validate_empty_values(data)`** — handles `None` / empty for the serializer itself.\n2. **`to_internal_value(data)`** — for every writable field, in declaration order:\n   1. `primitive = field.get_value(data)` (reads the key, honours `partial` and HTML form quirks),\n   2. `value = field.run_validation(primitive)` → the field's own `to_internal_value()` (type coercion: `\"42\"` → `42`) then `field.run_validators(value)` (`max_length`, `UniqueValidator`, your `validators=[...]`),\n   3. if the serializer has `validate_<field_name>`, `value = validate_<field_name>(value)`.\n   Errors are *collected* across all fields, then raised together as one `ValidationError({field: [...]})`.\n3. **`run_validators(value)`** — serializer-level validators from `Meta.validators` (e.g. `UniqueTogetherValidator`).\n4. **`validate(attrs)`** — your object-level hook; its return value becomes `validated_data`.\n\nConsequence: if any field fails in step 2, steps 3 and 4 never run. So `validate()` can safely assume every field is individually valid — and you will not see cross-field errors until field errors are fixed.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 330\" width=\"100%\" height=\"330\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj21a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj21b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">serializer.is_valid(): the validation pipeline</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"120\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">initial_data</text>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">request.data (dict)</text>\n  <path d=\"M140,75 L170,75\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"172\" y=\"40\" width=\"330\" height=\"140\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"337\" y=\"58\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. to_internal_value(data): per field, in order</text>\n  <rect x=\"186\" y=\"70\" width=\"96\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"234\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">field.get_value</text>\n  <text x=\"234\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">read key / partial</text>\n  <path d=\"M282,92 L292,92\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"294\" y=\"70\" width=\"96\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"342\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">run_validation</text>\n  <text x=\"342\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">coerce + validators</text>\n  <path d=\"M390,92 L400,92\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"402\" y=\"70\" width=\"92\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"448\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">validate_x()</text>\n  <text x=\"448\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">your field hook</text>\n  <text x=\"337\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">errors from ALL fields are collected,</text>\n  <text x=\"337\" y=\"156\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">then raised together as {field: [msgs]}</text>\n  <path d=\"M502,110 L532,110\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"534\" y=\"80\" width=\"150\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"609\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. run_validators</text>\n  <text x=\"609\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Meta.validators</text>\n  <text x=\"609\" y=\"133\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">UniqueTogetherValidator</text>\n  <path d=\"M684,110 L714,110\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"716\" y=\"80\" width=\"146\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"789\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">3. validate(attrs)</text>\n  <text x=\"789\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">cross-field rules</text>\n  <text x=\"789\" y=\"133\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">returns validated_data</text>\n  <path d=\"M337,180 L337,226\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M609,140 L609,226\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M789,140 L789,226\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"200\" y=\"228\" width=\"660\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"530\" y=\"246\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">ValidationError at any stage: stop, store in serializer.errors, is_valid() returns False</text>\n  <text x=\"530\" y=\"261\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">raise_exception=True re-raises it; the view's exception handler turns it into HTTP 400</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"284\" width=\"840\" height=\"34\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"305\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Not in the pipeline: Model.clean() and Model.full_clean(). ModelSerializer only runs field-level and unique validators.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### What `ModelSerializer` does and does not validate\n\n`ModelSerializer` copies model field constraints into serializer field arguments: `max_length`, `max_digits`/`decimal_places`, `choices`, `null → allow_null`, `blank → allow_blank`, `default`/`blank → required=False`, model field `validators`, and `unique=True → UniqueValidator(queryset=Model.objects.all())`. From `Meta.unique_together` (and, in recent DRF releases, simple `UniqueConstraint`s) it builds `UniqueTogetherValidator`s. What it does **not** do is call `Model.clean()` or `full_clean()`. This was a deliberate DRF 3 decision: validation lives in the serializer, explicitly. If your invariants live in `Model.clean()`, either duplicate them into `validate()` or call `full_clean()` yourself (and translate `django.core.exceptions.ValidationError` into DRF's).\n\nUnique validators are also **not race-free**: `UniqueValidator` does a `SELECT ... WHERE email = %s` before the insert. Two concurrent requests both pass and one hits `IntegrityError` at the database. The database constraint is the real guarantee; the validator only provides a nice error message in the common case.\n\n### Saving: `save()` dispatches to `create()` or `update()`\n\n`serializer.save(**kwargs)` asserts `is_valid()` was called and there are no errors, merges `kwargs` into `validated_data` (this is how `serializer.save(owner=request.user)` works), then calls `self.update(self.instance, validated_data)` if an instance was passed to the constructor, else `self.create(validated_data)`. It stores the result in `self.instance`. `ModelSerializer.create()` pops many-to-many fields, does `Model._default_manager.create(**validated_data)`, then sets the M2M relations. If `validated_data` contains a nested dict/list from a nested serializer, `create()` raises an assertion — DRF refuses to guess how to write nested objects (`raise_errors_on_nested_writes`).\n\n### Representation: `to_representation`\n\nOn `serializer.data`, DRF calls `to_representation(instance)`: for each readable field, `attribute = field.get_attribute(instance)` (walks `source_attrs`, e.g. `instance.author.name` — which triggers the FK descriptor and potentially a query), then `field.to_representation(attribute)`. Results are collected into a `ReturnDict` (or `ReturnList` for `many=True`) that remembers its serializer for the browsable API. This is the phase where N+1 queries happen, because attribute access on unprefetched relations goes to the database per object.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### The two directions\n\nThe same class runs two opposite pipelines; keep them separate in your head, because their costs live in different places.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 300\" width=\"100%\" height=\"300\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj21c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj21d\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Serializer: deserialize (write path) vs serialize (read path)</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"48\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-weight=\"bold\">WRITE: POST / PUT / PATCH</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"60\" width=\"130\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"85\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">JSON body</text>\n  <text x=\"85\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">parsed by JSONParser</text>\n  <path d=\"M150,85 L190,85\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"192\" y=\"60\" width=\"150\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"267\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">is_valid()</text>\n  <text x=\"267\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">to_internal_value + validate</text>\n  <path d=\"M342,85 L382,85\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"384\" y=\"60\" width=\"140\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"454\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">validated_data</text>\n  <text x=\"454\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Python values, safe</text>\n  <path d=\"M524,85 L564,85\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"566\" y=\"60\" width=\"140\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"636\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">save(**kwargs)</text>\n  <text x=\"636\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">create() or update()</text>\n  <path d=\"M706,85 L746,85\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"748\" y=\"60\" width=\"112\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"804\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Model row</text>\n  <text x=\"804\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">INSERT / UPDATE</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-weight=\"bold\">READ: GET (and the response after a write)</text>\n  <rect x=\"748\" y=\"172\" width=\"112\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"804\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">instance / qs</text>\n  <text x=\"804\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">prefetched!</text>\n  <path d=\"M748,197 L708,197\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"526\" y=\"172\" width=\"180\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"616\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">field.get_attribute(obj)</text>\n  <text x=\"616\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">walks source: obj.author.name</text>\n  <path d=\"M526,197 L486,197\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"306\" y=\"172\" width=\"178\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"395\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">field.to_representation</text>\n  <text x=\"395\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">date to ISO string, Decimal...</text>\n  <path d=\"M306,197 L266,197\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"116\" y=\"172\" width=\"148\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">serializer.data</text>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">ReturnDict / ReturnList</text>\n  <path d=\"M116,197 L76,197\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj21d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"10\" y=\"172\" width=\"64\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"42\" y=\"201\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">JSON</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"240\" width=\"840\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"259\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Read path cost = 1 query for the queryset + (per row x per unprefetched relation or SerializerMethodField query)</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"276\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">fix in the VIEW: get_queryset().select_related(...).prefetch_related(...), not inside the serializer</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Who calls what in a generic view\n\n```text\nCreateAPIView.post(request)\n  └─ CreateModelMixin.create()\n       serializer = self.get_serializer(data=request.data)     # context = {request, view, format}\n       serializer.is_valid(raise_exception=True)               # → 400 via exception handler\n       self.perform_create(serializer)                         # → serializer.save()  (override to pass owner=)\n       headers = self.get_success_headers(serializer.data)\n       return Response(serializer.data, status=201)            # read path runs on the saved instance\n\nUpdateAPIView.patch(request, pk)\n  └─ UpdateModelMixin.partial_update() → update(partial=True)\n       instance = self.get_object()                            # object permissions checked here\n       serializer = self.get_serializer(instance, data=request.data, partial=True)\n       serializer.is_valid(raise_exception=True)\n       self.perform_update(serializer)                         # → serializer.save() → update(instance, data)\n```\n\nViews are covered in [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers); for now the important part is that the view owns the queryset (and therefore query optimisation) and the serializer owns shape and validation.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Setup\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"djangorestframework>=3.15\"\ndjango-admin startproject config . && python manage.py startapp library\n```\n\n```python\n# library/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\n\n\nclass Author(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=120)\n    email = models.EmailField(unique=True)\n\n\nclass Tag(models.Model):\n    name = models.SlugField(unique=True)\n\n\nclass Book(models.Model):\n    class Status(models.TextChoices):\n        DRAFT = \"draft\"\n        PUBLISHED = \"published\"\n\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    author = models.ForeignKey(Author, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"books\")\n    tags = models.ManyToManyField(Tag, blank=True, related_name=\"books\")\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=8, decimal_places=2)\n    status = models.CharField(max_length=10, choices=Status.choices, default=Status.DRAFT)\n    published_on = models.DateField(null=True, blank=True)\n    owner = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE)\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [\n            models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"author\", \"title\"], name=\"uniq_author_title\"),\n        ]\n```\n\n### Simple example: a plain `Serializer`\n\n```python\n# library/serializers.py\nfrom rest_framework import serializers\n\n\nclass ContactSerializer(serializers.Serializer):\n    name = serializers.CharField(max_length=80)\n    email = serializers.EmailField()\n    message = serializers.CharField(min_length=10)\n    urgent = serializers.BooleanField(default=False)\n\n    def validate_name(self, value):                 # field-level hook\n        return value.strip().title()\n\n    def validate(self, attrs):                      # object-level hook\n        if attrs[\"urgent\"] and \"refund\" not in attrs[\"message\"].lower():\n            raise serializers.ValidationError(\"Urgent is reserved for refund requests.\")\n        return attrs\n```\n\nRunning it in a shell shows the error format and the ordering:\n\n```python\n>>> s = ContactSerializer(data={\"name\": \"  ada lovelace \", \"email\": \"not-an-email\", \"message\": \"hi\"})\n>>> s.is_valid()\nFalse\n>>> s.errors\n{'email': [ErrorDetail(string='Enter a valid email address.', code='invalid')],\n 'message': [ErrorDetail(string='Ensure this field has at least 10 characters.', code='min_length')]}\n>>> # validate() did NOT run — field errors short-circuit it\n>>> s = ContactSerializer(data={\"name\": \" ada \", \"email\": \"a@b.co\", \"message\": \"please refund me\", \"urgent\": True})\n>>> s.is_valid(), s.validated_data\n(True, {'name': 'Ada', 'email': 'a@b.co', 'message': 'please refund me', 'urgent': True})\n```\n\nNon-field errors from `validate()` appear under the key `non_field_errors` (configurable with `NON_FIELD_ERRORS_KEY`). Raise `ValidationError({\"field\": \"msg\"})` from `validate()` to attach a cross-field error to a specific field instead.\n\n### `ModelSerializer` and inspecting what it generated\n\n```python\nclass TagSerializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    class Meta:\n        model = Tag\n        fields = [\"id\", \"name\"]\n\n\nclass BookSerializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    owner = serializers.HiddenField(default=serializers.CurrentUserDefault())\n    author_name = serializers.CharField(source=\"author.name\", read_only=True)\n    status_label = serializers.CharField(source=\"get_status_display\", read_only=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        model = Book\n        fields = [\"id\", \"title\", \"author\", \"author_name\", \"tags\", \"price\",\n                  \"status\", \"status_label\", \"published_on\", \"owner\"]\n        read_only_fields = [\"status\"]          # status changes via a dedicated action only\n        extra_kwargs = {\"price\": {\"min_value\": 0}}\n\n    def validate(self, attrs):\n        # On PATCH, attrs only has submitted keys — fall back to the instance.\n        status = attrs.get(\"status\", getattr(self.instance, \"status\", None))\n        published_on = attrs.get(\"published_on\", getattr(self.instance, \"published_on\", None))\n        if status == Book.Status.PUBLISHED and published_on is None:\n            raise serializers.ValidationError({\"published_on\": \"Required when published.\"})\n        return attrs\n```\n\nAlways print the generated serializer once; it is the fastest way to see what DRF inferred:\n\n```text\n>>> print(repr(BookSerializer()))\nBookSerializer():\n    id = IntegerField(label='ID', read_only=True)\n    title = CharField(max_length=200)\n    author = PrimaryKeyRelatedField(queryset=Author.objects.all())\n    author_name = CharField(read_only=True, source='author.name')\n    tags = PrimaryKeyRelatedField(allow_empty=False, many=True, queryset=Tag.objects.all(), required=False)\n    price = DecimalField(decimal_places=2, max_digits=8, min_value=0)\n    status = ChoiceField(choices=[('draft', 'Draft'), ('published', 'Published')], read_only=True)\n    ...\n    owner = HiddenField(default=CurrentUserDefault())\n    class Meta:\n        validators = [<UniqueTogetherValidator(queryset=Book.objects.all(), fields=('author', 'title'))>]\n```\n\nNote the `UniqueTogetherValidator` derived from the constraint (exact output varies a little by DRF version). It requires every field it names to be present in input, which is why `HiddenField` is the idiomatic way to include `owner` in such a validator: the client cannot send it, but the validator still sees it.\n\n> **Gotcha:** `fields = \"__all__\"` silently exposes every new column you add later — including `is_internal`, `cost_price`, or `password`. Prefer an explicit list. DRF requires you to set `fields` or `exclude`; it raises an `ImproperlyConfigured` otherwise.\n\n### Validators: field, reusable, and context-aware\n\n```python\nfrom rest_framework.validators import UniqueValidator\n\n\ndef no_profanity(value):\n    if \"darn\" in value.lower():\n        raise serializers.ValidationError(\"Keep it clean.\")\n\n\nclass NotInPast:\n    requires_context = True                     # receives the field as 2nd arg\n\n    def __call__(self, value, serializer_field):\n        from django.utils import timezone\n        if value and value < timezone.localdate():\n            raise serializers.ValidationError(\"Date cannot be in the past.\")\n\n\nclass AuthorSerializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    email = serializers.EmailField(\n        validators=[UniqueValidator(queryset=Author.objects.all(), lookup=\"iexact\")]\n    )\n    name = serializers.CharField(max_length=120, validators=[no_profanity])\n\n    class Meta:\n        model = Author\n        fields = [\"id\", \"name\", \"email\"]\n```\n\nDeclaring `email` explicitly replaces the auto-generated field, so you must restate `UniqueValidator` yourself — a frequent source of \"why did uniqueness stop being validated?\" bugs.\n\n### Real-world example: writable nested serializer with an order and line items\n\nThe interview classic: \"accept an order with its line items in one POST, and update them in a PUT.\" DRF will not do this automatically; you override `create()` and `update()` and wrap them in a transaction ([Ch 14 · Transactions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency)).\n\n```python\n# shop/models.py\nclass Order(models.Model):\n    customer = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.PROTECT)\n    note = models.CharField(max_length=200, blank=True)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n\n\nclass OrderItem(models.Model):\n    order = models.ForeignKey(Order, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"items\")\n    sku = models.CharField(max_length=40)\n    quantity = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n    unit_price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2)\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"order\", \"sku\"], name=\"uniq_order_sku\")]\n```\n\n```python\n# shop/serializers.py\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom rest_framework import serializers\n\nfrom .models import Order, OrderItem\n\n\nclass OrderItemSerializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    id = serializers.IntegerField(required=False)          # writable so updates can match rows\n\n    class Meta:\n        model = OrderItem\n        fields = [\"id\", \"sku\", \"quantity\", \"unit_price\"]\n        extra_kwargs = {\"quantity\": {\"min_value\": 1}}\n\n\nclass OrderSerializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    customer = serializers.HiddenField(default=serializers.CurrentUserDefault())\n    items = OrderItemSerializer(many=True)\n    total = serializers.SerializerMethodField()\n\n    class Meta:\n        model = Order\n        fields = [\"id\", \"customer\", \"note\", \"items\", \"total\", \"created_at\"]\n        read_only_fields = [\"created_at\"]\n\n    def get_total(self, obj):\n        # Uses the prefetched items — no extra query if the view prefetched them.\n        return str(sum(i.quantity * i.unit_price for i in obj.items.all()))\n\n    def validate_items(self, items):\n        if not items:\n            raise serializers.ValidationError(\"An order needs at least one item.\")\n        skus = [i[\"sku\"] for i in items]\n        if len(skus) != len(set(skus)):\n            raise serializers.ValidationError(\"Duplicate SKU in items.\")\n        return items\n\n    @transaction.atomic\n    def create(self, validated_data):\n        items = validated_data.pop(\"items\")\n        order = Order.objects.create(**validated_data)\n        OrderItem.objects.bulk_create(\n            [OrderItem(order=order, **{k: v for k, v in item.items() if k != \"id\"}) for item in items]\n        )\n        return order\n\n    @transaction.atomic\n    def update(self, instance, validated_data):\n        items = validated_data.pop(\"items\", None)\n        for attr, value in validated_data.items():\n            setattr(instance, attr, value)\n        instance.save()\n\n        if items is not None:                                # PATCH without items: leave them alone\n            existing = {i.id: i for i in instance.items.all()}\n            keep_ids = []\n            for item in items:\n                item_id = item.pop(\"id\", None)\n                if item_id is not None:\n                    if item_id not in existing:              # never let a client touch another order's row\n                        raise serializers.ValidationError({\"items\": f\"Unknown item id {item_id}.\"})\n                    row = existing[item_id]\n                    for attr, value in item.items():\n                        setattr(row, attr, value)\n                    row.save()\n                    keep_ids.append(item_id)\n                else:\n                    keep_ids.append(OrderItem.objects.create(order=instance, **item).id)\n            instance.items.exclude(id__in=keep_ids).delete()  # PUT semantics: replace the set\n        return instance\n```\n\nThe SQL a create produces (PostgreSQL) is exactly three statements inside one transaction:\n\n```sql\nBEGIN;\nINSERT INTO \"shop_order\" (\"customer_id\", \"note\", \"created_at\") VALUES (7, 'gift', '2026-10-25T10:00:00Z') RETURNING \"id\";\nINSERT INTO \"shop_orderitem\" (\"order_id\", \"sku\", \"quantity\", \"unit_price\")\n  VALUES (42, 'BK-1', 2, 19.99), (42, 'BK-2', 1, 5.00) RETURNING \"id\";\nCOMMIT;\n```\n\nBut the 201 response renders `serializer.data` on the new instance: `items` and `get_total` each do `order.items.all()`, i.e. **two** extra SELECTs (no prefetch cache on a freshly created object). That is fine for one object. On the list endpoint it would be per row — so the view must prefetch:\n\n```python\nclass OrderViewSet(viewsets.ModelViewSet):\n    serializer_class = OrderSerializer\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return (Order.objects.filter(customer=self.request.user)\n                .prefetch_related(\"items\")\n                .order_by(\"-created_at\"))\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"shop_order\" WHERE \"shop_order\".\"customer_id\" = 7 ORDER BY \"created_at\" DESC LIMIT 20;\nSELECT ... FROM \"shop_orderitem\" WHERE \"shop_orderitem\".\"order_id\" IN (42, 41, 40, ...);\n```\n\nTwo queries for any page size instead of 1 + 2N.\n\n### Read and write with different shapes\n\nA common pattern: accept a PK on write, return a nested object on read.\n\n```python\nclass BookReadSerializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    author = AuthorSerializer(read_only=True)\n    tags = serializers.SlugRelatedField(slug_field=\"name\", many=True, read_only=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        model = Book\n        fields = [\"id\", \"title\", \"author\", \"tags\", \"price\"]\n\n\nclass BookWriteSerializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    class Meta:\n        model = Book\n        fields = [\"title\", \"author\", \"tags\", \"price\"]\n\n    def to_representation(self, instance):\n        # respond with the rich shape after a write\n        return BookReadSerializer(instance, context=self.context).data\n```\n\nAlternatively override `get_serializer_class()` in the viewset by `self.action`. Overriding `to_representation` is also how you rename keys, drop `None` values or add envelope data:\n\n```python\n    def to_representation(self, instance):\n        data = super().to_representation(instance)\n        return {k: v for k, v in data.items() if v is not None}\n```\n\n### Bulk writes with `ListSerializer`\n\n```python\nclass BulkTagListSerializer(serializers.ListSerializer):\n    def create(self, validated_data):\n        return Tag.objects.bulk_create([Tag(**item) for item in validated_data])\n\n\nclass BulkTagSerializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    class Meta:\n        model = Tag\n        fields = [\"id\", \"name\"]\n        list_serializer_class = BulkTagListSerializer\n\n# view: s = BulkTagSerializer(data=request.data, many=True); s.is_valid(raise_exception=True); s.save()\n```\n\nWithout this, `ListSerializer.create` calls `child.create()` once per item — one INSERT each. Note that `UniqueValidator` on `name` still runs one SELECT per item during validation.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Choice | Pros | Cons | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `Serializer` | Explicit, not tied to a model, full control | You write every field and `create/update` | Input that is not a model (login, search, RPC-ish actions) |\n| `ModelSerializer` | Little code, validators from model, default create/update | Hidden behaviour, `__all__` leaks, no `Model.clean()` | CRUD on a model |\n| `HyperlinkedModelSerializer` | HATEOAS-style URLs | Needs `request` in context, URL reversing cost | Public, browsable APIs |\n| Nested read serializer | One round-trip for client | N+1 unless prefetched; bigger payloads | Detail views, small lists |\n| PK / Slug related field | Cheap, simple writes | Client needs a second call for details | Write paths, large lists |\n| `SerializerMethodField` | Arbitrary computed output | Read-only; per-object code, query traps | Derived values from prefetched data or annotations |\n| Writable nested (custom create/update) | One atomic request for aggregate | You own matching, deletes, permissions | Aggregates (order + items) |\n| Separate endpoints for children | Simple, RESTful, cacheable | More requests, partial states | Independently managed children |\n\n### When to use\n- Use `ModelSerializer` for straightforward model CRUD, with an explicit `fields` list and `read_only_fields`.\n- Use a plain `Serializer` for actions and inputs that do not map onto one model (password change, checkout request, filters).\n- Use separate read/write serializers when input and output shapes legitimately differ.\n\n### When NOT to use\n- Not for hot, read-only endpoints returning thousands of rows where serializer overhead dominates — use `.values()` plus a lightweight dict build, or annotate in SQL and use a thin serializer.\n- Not as a place to fetch data: queries belong in the view's `get_queryset()`.\n- Not for business workflows with side effects (sending emails, charging cards) hidden in `create()` — put those in a service function the serializer or view calls explicitly, dispatched with `transaction.on_commit`.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. `fields = \"__all__\"` on write serializers.** What people do: it's quick. Why it hurts: mass assignment — a new `is_verified` column becomes client-writable the day it ships. Instead: explicit `fields`, `read_only_fields`, and a test that asserts the field list.\n\n**2. Expecting `Model.clean()` to run.** Your invariant in `clean()` is bypassed by the API. Instead: put rules in `validate()` (or a model method both call), and keep a DB `CheckConstraint` as the backstop.\n\n**3. Forgetting `partial` in `validate()`.** On PATCH, `attrs[\"status\"]` raises `KeyError`. Instead: `attrs.get(\"status\", getattr(self.instance, \"status\", None))`.\n\n**4. Querying inside `SerializerMethodField`.**\n\n```python\n# before: 1 query per book\ndef get_review_count(self, obj):\n    return obj.reviews.count()\n\n# after: annotate in the view, read the attribute\n# view: Book.objects.annotate(review_count=Count(\"reviews\"))\nreview_count = serializers.IntegerField(read_only=True)\n```\n\n**5. Nesting serializers without prefetching.** The serializer cannot prefetch for you; the view must `select_related` (FK/O2O) and `prefetch_related` (reverse FK/M2M). Assert with `assertNumQueries` in tests.\n\n**6. Calling `.save()` after accessing `.data`.** DRF raises an assertion; if you need the output, call `save()` first, then read `.data`.\n\n**7. Passing `request.data` straight to `Model.objects.create(**request.data)`.** That is the naive approach the serializer exists to prevent. Always go through `validated_data`.\n\n**8. Re-declaring a field and losing its validator.** Explicit `email = serializers.EmailField()` drops the generated `UniqueValidator`. Restate it, or tweak via `extra_kwargs` instead of redeclaring.\n\n**9. Trusting nested `id`s on update.** Matching child rows by client-supplied `id` without scoping to the parent lets a client modify another user's rows. Always resolve ids against `instance.items`.\n\n**Best practices:** keep serializers thin and declarative; one serializer per direction when shapes differ; push heavy logic to services; use `HiddenField(default=CurrentUserDefault())` or `serializer.save(owner=...)` for server-set fields; use `raise_exception=True` everywhere; write a query-count test for every list endpoint.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: list endpoint gets slower as data grows.** Symptom: p95 latency of `/api/books/` climbs from 80 ms to 2 s. Root cause: `AuthorSerializer` nested plus `tags` — `1 + 2N` queries per page. Fix: `select_related(\"author\").prefetch_related(\"tags\")` in `get_queryset`, and a regression test:\n\n```python\ndef test_book_list_query_count(self):\n    BookFactory.create_batch(30)\n    with self.assertNumQueries(3):   # COUNT for pagination, books+authors JOIN, tags prefetch\n        self.client.get(\"/api/books/\")\n```\n\n**Pitfall: write validation does N queries.** `tags = PrimaryKeyRelatedField(many=True, queryset=Tag.objects.all())` validates each pk with its own `queryset.get(pk=...)`. A POST with 500 tag ids is 500 SELECTs. Fix: accept a `ListField(child=IntegerField())`, then one `Tag.objects.filter(pk__in=ids)` and compare counts in `validate_tags`.\n\n**Pitfall: `IntegrityError` → 500 despite `UniqueValidator`.** Two concurrent sign-ups pass the SELECT check. Fix: keep the DB constraint, catch `IntegrityError` in `perform_create` and raise `ValidationError`, or rely on a custom exception handler mapping it to 409/400.\n\n**Pitfall: CPU-bound serialization.** DRF serializers are pure Python and do per-field work for each object; serializing tens of thousands of objects is CPU-heavy. Symptoms: high CPU on API pods with low DB time in your APM. Fixes: paginate ([Ch 24](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning)); for internal exports use `.values()`/`.values_list()` and stream; cache rendered payloads ([Ch 29 · Caching](topic.html?p=29-caching)); avoid `HyperlinkedRelatedField` on large lists (a `reverse()` per link).\n\n**Pitfall: huge `DecimalField` strings vs floats.** DRF renders `DecimalField` as a string by default (`COERCE_DECIMAL_TO_STRING=True`) to avoid precision loss — frontends that expect a number break. Decide once, document in the schema.\n\n**Debugging toolkit.**\n- `print(repr(MySerializer()))` — see generated fields and validators.\n- `serializer.errors` in a shell with real payloads; `ErrorDetail.code` for machine-readable codes.\n- `django-debug-toolbar` on the browsable API, or `from django.db import connection; len(connection.queries)` with `DEBUG=True`.\n- Sentry or logs for `AssertionError` from nested writes — usually a missing `create()` override.\n\n**Scaling notes.** The serializer is rarely the bottleneck until queries are fixed; once they are, look at payload size (sparse fieldsets via a `fields` query param, or a dynamic-fields serializer) and at caching. Keep response shapes stable — changing a serializer is changing a public contract ([REST API · Backward Compatibility](../rest-api/topic.html?p=32-backward-compatibility-deprecation)).\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `Serializer` and `ModelSerializer`?**\nA: Both are bidirectional converters with the same validation pipeline. `Serializer` requires you to declare every field and implement `create()`/`update()` yourself. `ModelSerializer` introspects `Meta.model` to generate fields with matching types and constraints (`max_length`, `choices`, `allow_null`), adds `UniqueValidator`/`UniqueTogetherValidator` from model uniqueness, and provides default `create()`/`update()` that handle M2M fields. It is the `ModelForm` analogue. It does not call `Model.clean()`, and it refuses nested writes by default.\n\n**Q: Walk me through what happens when you call `serializer.is_valid()`?**\nA: It runs `run_validation(initial_data)`. First `to_internal_value` iterates writable fields: each field reads its value, coerces it via its own `to_internal_value`, runs its validators, then the serializer's `validate_<field>` method runs if defined. Errors from all fields are collected and raised together. Then serializer-level `Meta.validators` run (e.g. `UniqueTogetherValidator`), then `validate(attrs)`. The result is stored as `validated_data`; on failure, `errors` is populated and `is_valid()` returns False (or raises with `raise_exception=True`, which views turn into a 400).\n\n**Q: Does `validate()` run if a field fails validation?**\nA: No. Field errors are raised from `to_internal_value` before serializer-level validators and `validate()` execute. That is useful — `validate()` can assume each field is individually valid — but it also means clients see cross-field errors only after fixing field errors. If you need both reported at once, do the cross-field check in a field validator with access to `self.initial_data`, though that is rarely worth it.\n\n**Q: Does `ModelSerializer` call the model's `clean()` method?**\nA: No. DRF 3 moved away from that deliberately; validation is the serializer's job and `Model.clean()`/`full_clean()` are not called. It does use the model field validators and uniqueness. If you rely on `clean()`, call `instance.full_clean()` in `validate()` (on a temp instance) and convert `django.core.exceptions.ValidationError` to `serializers.ValidationError`, or move the rule into a shared function.\n\n**Q: How do you write a nested serializer that can create related objects?**\nA: Declare the child serializer (`items = ItemSerializer(many=True)`), then override `create()` to pop `items` from `validated_data`, create the parent, and create the children with the parent FK — ideally with `bulk_create` and inside `transaction.atomic()`. For `update()` you must decide semantics: replace the whole set, upsert by id, or reject children on update. Default `ModelSerializer.create()` raises an assertion for nested data precisely because these choices are domain-specific. Scope any child ids to the parent to avoid touching other users' rows.\n\n**Q: What is `SerializerMethodField` and what is the risk?**\nA: It is a read-only field whose value is returned by `get_<name>(self, obj)`. It is handy for derived values, but it runs once per object, so any query inside it (`obj.reviews.count()`) becomes an N+1. Prefer annotating in the view's queryset and exposing the annotation as a plain read-only field, or compute from prefetched data (`obj.items.all()` hitting the prefetch cache).\n\n**Q: How do you make a field read-only or write-only?**\nA: `read_only=True` / `write_only=True` on a declared field, or `Meta.read_only_fields = [...]` and `extra_kwargs = {\"password\": {\"write_only\": True}}` for generated fields. Read-only fields are ignored on input (silently, not an error), write-only fields are omitted from output. `read_only_fields` does not apply to fields you declare explicitly — you must pass `read_only=True` on them. This is how you prevent mass assignment of fields like `is_staff` or `owner`.\n\n**Q: How do you set the current user as the owner when creating an object?**\nA: Either `serializer.save(owner=self.request.user)` in the view's `perform_create`, which merges into `validated_data`, or `owner = serializers.HiddenField(default=serializers.CurrentUserDefault())` on the serializer. `HiddenField` is preferable when `owner` participates in a `UniqueTogetherValidator`, because the validator needs the value present during validation, while `save(owner=...)` only adds it afterwards. Both require `request` in the serializer context (generic views supply it).\n\n**Q: What does `partial=True` change?**\nA: Required fields that are absent from the input are not errors, and only submitted keys appear in `validated_data`. Field validators still run on submitted fields, and `validate()` still runs — so it must handle missing keys by falling back to `self.instance`. Generic views set it for PATCH via `partial_update`.\n\n**Q: How do serializers relate to the N+1 problem, and where should you fix it?**\nA: Rendering walks `source` attributes on each object, so nested serializers, `source=\"author.name\"` and related fields trigger a lazy-load query per row unless the data is already loaded. The serializer cannot fix this; the view must `select_related` forward FKs and `prefetch_related` reverse FKs/M2M (with `Prefetch` objects for filtered relations). Lock it in with `assertNumQueries`. Some teams add a `setup_eager_loading` classmethod on the serializer that the view calls, keeping the knowledge next to the fields.\n\n**Q: Your unique email validator passes but you still get `IntegrityError` in production. Why, and how do you fix it? (Senior)**\nA: `UniqueValidator` is a check-then-act SELECT before INSERT, so two concurrent requests both see \"no row\" and both insert; the database unique index rejects the second. That is a race, not a bug in DRF. The constraint must stay as the source of truth. Handle the `IntegrityError` (in `perform_create` or a custom exception handler) and translate it into a 400/409 with the same error shape; optionally normalise emails (lowercase) before both the check and the insert, and use a case-insensitive unique constraint (`UniqueConstraint(Lower(\"email\"), ...)`) so the validator and DB agree.\n\n**Q: How would you design serializers for an aggregate like Order + OrderItems that supports create, full replace and partial update? (Senior)**\nA: Use a writable nested `items` serializer with an optional writable `id`. In `create()`, pop items, create the order, `bulk_create` items, all inside `transaction.atomic()`. In `update()`, apply scalar fields, then if `items` is present: load existing items scoped to the order, update matched ids, create new ones, delete missing ones (PUT semantics), and reject unknown ids. Validate invariants (non-empty, unique SKU, stock availability) in `validate_items`/`validate`, and keep DB constraints as backstops. Push side effects (stock reservation events, emails) to `transaction.on_commit`. If business rules grow, move the write into a service function and keep the serializer as an input validator only.\n\n**Q: A list endpoint serializing 5,000 objects spends most of its time in Python, not the DB. What do you do? (Senior)**\nA: First confirm with profiling (py-spy, APM spans) that time is in `to_representation`. Then reduce the work: paginate, trim fields (sparse fieldsets), avoid hyperlinked fields and `SerializerMethodField`s that compute per object, move computation into SQL annotations. For read-only bulk endpoints, bypass `ModelSerializer` with `.values(...)` and a hand-built dict or a minimal `Serializer` with plain fields; cache the rendered response keyed by a version/updated_at; and consider streaming exports via a background job instead of a request. Measure each step; serializer overhead is per-field-per-object, so fewer fields times fewer objects is the lever.\n\n**Q: How do you implement bulk create or bulk update with DRF? (Senior)**\nA: Pass `many=True`, which yields a `ListSerializer`. Its default `create()` loops `child.create()` (one INSERT each) and it has no default `update()` because matching instances to items is ambiguous. Set `Meta.list_serializer_class` to a subclass that implements `create()` with `bulk_create` and `update()` by mapping ids to instances and using `bulk_update` with explicit fields. Watch validation cost too — per-item `UniqueValidator` and `PrimaryKeyRelatedField` each query — and validate in bulk in `validate()` instead. Remember `bulk_create`/`bulk_update` skip `save()` and signals.\n\n**Q: When would you override `to_representation` versus `to_internal_value`?**\nA: Override `to_representation` to change output shape globally for that serializer: renaming keys, removing nulls, embedding a richer read serializer after a write, adding computed envelopes. Override `to_internal_value` to accept an input format that does not match fields one-to-one — e.g. accepting a legacy flat payload and reshaping it before validation — calling `super()` with the normalised dict. Keep both small; heavy logic there is hard to test and invisible in the schema.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Thing | Remember |\n|---|---|\n| Validation order | field `to_internal_value` → field validators → `validate_<f>` → `Meta.validators` → `validate()` |\n| Short-circuit | any field error ⇒ `validate()` does not run |\n| `save()` | asserts valid; `save(**kw)` merges into `validated_data`; `create` vs `update` by `instance` |\n| ModelSerializer | generates fields + unique validators; no `Model.clean()`; nested writes need overrides |\n| `read_only_fields` | only affects generated fields, not declared ones |\n| `partial=True` | absent fields OK; `validate()` still runs |\n| `SerializerMethodField` | read-only; per-object; N+1 risk |\n| N+1 fix | in the view's `get_queryset` (`select_related`/`prefetch_related`) |\n| Bulk | `many=True` → `ListSerializer`; customise via `list_serializer_class` |\n| Server-set fields | `HiddenField(default=CurrentUserDefault())` or `save(owner=...)` |\n\n- Never `fields = \"__all__\"` on a write serializer.\n- `validated_data` is the only trusted input.\n- Errors are a dict keyed by field; `non_field_errors` for `validate()`.\n- Unique validators are not race-free; the DB constraint is the guarantee.\n- `.data` before `.save()` is an assertion error.\n- Decimal renders as a string by default.\n- Explicitly declared fields lose auto-generated validators.\n- Wrap nested writes in `transaction.atomic()`.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"djangorestframework>=3.15\" django-debug-toolbar\n```\n\n1. **Validation order probe.** Write a serializer whose field validator, `validate_x`, `Meta.validators` and `validate()` each `print()` their name. Submit valid and invalid payloads and record which hooks run.\n2. **Inspect generation.** Create the `Book` model above and `print(repr(BookSerializer()))`. Change `null`, `blank`, `default` and `unique` on fields and observe how `required`, `allow_null` and validators change.\n3. **N+1 hunt.** Build a `BookViewSet` with a nested `AuthorSerializer` and a `SerializerMethodField` counting reviews. Seed 50 books, count queries, then fix with `select_related` + `annotate` and assert with `assertNumQueries`.\n4. **Nested write.** Implement the `OrderSerializer` create/update and test: create with 3 items, PUT replacing items, PATCH changing only `note`, and PATCH with another order's item id (must 400).\n5. **Concurrency.** Run two threads posting the same author email with a `UniqueValidator`; reproduce the `IntegrityError`, then handle it and return a 400.\n\n**Mini project — \"Bookshelf API\".** Models: Author, Book, Tag, Review. Requirements: explicit field lists; read/write serializer split for Book; writable nested Review creation under a book; `average_rating` via annotation; bulk tag creation via `list_serializer_class`; every list endpoint has a query-count test; an OpenAPI schema generated later in [Ch 24](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning).\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation) · [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships) · [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) · [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) · [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers) · [Ch 23 · DRF Authentication, Permissions & Throttling](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling) · [Ch 24 · DRF Pagination, Filtering, Versioning & Docs](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning) · [Ch 38 · Interview: DRF, Security & Scale](topic.html?p=38-interview-drf-advanced)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · Payload Design & JSON](../rest-api/topic.html?p=08-payload-design-json) · [REST API · Validation & Input Sanitization](../rest-api/topic.html?p=17-validation-input-sanitization) · [REST API · Error Handling & Problem Details](../rest-api/topic.html?p=16-error-handling-problem-details) · [REST API · PUT, PATCH & JSON Patch](../rest-api/topic.html?p=14-put-patch-json-patch)\n\n- **Serializers (API Guide)** — Django REST framework · *Beginner–Intermediate* · the canonical reference for fields, validation and nested writes. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/serializers/>\n- **Serializer fields** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · every field type, `source`, `read_only`/`write_only` semantics. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/fields/>\n- **Validators** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · Unique validators, `requires_context`, and their limitations. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/validators/>\n- **Serializer relations** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · related fields and nested relationships. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/relations/>\n- **DRF Tutorial 1: Serialization** — Django REST framework · *Beginner* · builds a Serializer then a ModelSerializer step by step. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/tutorial/1-serialization/>\n- **DRF source code** — encode/django-rest-framework on GitHub · *Advanced* · `serializers.py` and `fields.py` are readable and answer most \"what really happens\" questions. <https://github.com/encode/django-rest-framework>\n- **Django ModelForms** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the conceptual ancestor of ModelSerializer. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/forms/modelforms/>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 21.*\n", "22-drf-views-viewsets-routers": "# 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers\n\n> **In one line:** Every DRF endpoint is an `APIView` whose `dispatch()` wraps the request, negotiates content, authenticates, checks permissions and throttles, runs your handler and turns exceptions into responses; generic views and ViewSets are layers of mixins on top of that one pipeline, and routers just map HTTP verbs and URLs onto ViewSet actions.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 04 · Views, Requests & Responses](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses), [Ch 17 · Class-Based & Generic Views](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views), [Ch 21 · DRF Serializers](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers).\n\nA plain Django view can serve JSON: `return JsonResponse(list(Book.objects.values()), safe=False)`. That works until you need the things every real API needs. You have to parse JSON *and* form data *and* multipart uploads. You need consistent authentication across endpoints, a permission check before the handler runs, a throttle, and an error format that is the same whether the failure is a missing object, bad input, or an unauthenticated user. You need `405 Method Not Allowed` with an `Allow` header, `406` when the client asks for XML you cannot produce, and an `OPTIONS` response. Doing that by hand in every view means hundreds of lines of copy-paste that drift, and the first endpoint someone forgets to protect is your data breach.\n\nDjango REST Framework's answer is to put all of that in **one** class, `APIView`, and make every endpoint inherit it. Then, because 80% of endpoints are \"list/create/retrieve/update/delete a model\", DRF layers on **`GenericAPIView`** (queryset, serializer, lookup, pagination, filtering hooks) and **mixins** (`ListModelMixin`, `CreateModelMixin`, ...) that implement those actions. **ViewSets** go one step further: one class holds all the actions for a resource, and a **router** generates the URL patterns.\n\nThe trade-off is the usual one with abstraction: you write very little code, but when something goes wrong (\"why is my permission not checked on the list endpoint?\", \"why does my queryset return stale data?\", \"why do I get 403 instead of 401?\") you need to know exactly which method in the stack does what. That is what this chapter teaches, and it is exactly what interviewers probe with \"APIView vs ViewSet vs ModelViewSet\" and \"what happens when a request hits a DRF view?\".\n\n> **Why this matters:** Knowing the `dispatch()` → `initial()` → handler → `finalize_response()` sequence lets you answer almost every DRF debugging question from first principles instead of from memory.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **`APIView`** — DRF's base class, a subclass of Django's `View`. *Why:* centralises parsing, auth, permissions, throttling, content negotiation and exception handling.\n- **`Request`** — `rest_framework.request.Request` wrapping Django's `HttpRequest`; adds `request.data` (parsed body for any method), `request.query_params` (alias of `GET`), `request.user` and `request.auth` (lazy). *Why:* one interface regardless of content type.\n- **`Response`** — a `SimpleTemplateResponse` that holds *unrendered* data; the chosen renderer serialises it after the view returns. *Why:* the same view can return JSON or the browsable API.\n- **Parsers / Renderers** — `JSONParser`, `FormParser`, `MultiPartParser`; `JSONRenderer`, `BrowsableAPIRenderer`. *Why:* control accepted and produced media types.\n- **Content negotiation** — picks a renderer from the `Accept` header (or `?format=`/suffix). *Why:* returns 406 when nothing matches.\n- **`@api_view`** — decorator that turns a function into an `APIView`. *Why:* FBV style with the full DRF pipeline.\n- **`GenericAPIView`** — adds `queryset`, `serializer_class`, `lookup_field`, `get_queryset()`, `get_object()`, `get_serializer()`, `filter_queryset()`, `paginate_queryset()`. *Why:* the hooks you override in real projects.\n- **Mixins** — `List/Create/Retrieve/Update/DestroyModelMixin` implement `list()`, `create()`, `retrieve()`, `update()`/`partial_update()`, `destroy()`. *Why:* composable behaviour; `perform_create/update/destroy` are the extension points.\n- **Concrete generics** — `ListCreateAPIView`, `RetrieveUpdateDestroyAPIView`, etc. *Why:* mixin + `GenericAPIView` + method handlers pre-wired.\n- **`ViewSet`** — groups actions (`list`, `retrieve`, ...) instead of HTTP methods. `ViewSet`, `GenericViewSet`, `ModelViewSet`, `ReadOnlyModelViewSet`. *Why:* one class per resource; consistent URLs.\n- **`self.action`** — name of the current ViewSet action. *Why:* switch serializers/permissions per action.\n- **`@action`** — extra routes on a ViewSet, `detail=True` (`/books/{pk}/publish/`) or `detail=False` (`/books/recent/`). *Why:* non-CRUD operations without a new class.\n- **Routers** — `SimpleRouter`, `DefaultRouter` (adds an API root view and format-suffix routes). *Why:* generate URL patterns and names (`book-list`, `book-detail`).\n- **Exception handler** — `EXCEPTION_HANDLER` setting; converts `APIException`, `Http404`, Django `PermissionDenied` into `Response`s. *Why:* one error envelope for the whole API.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### How `APIView` processes a request\n\n`APIView.as_view()` calls Django's `View.as_view()` and then wraps the result in `csrf_exempt`. That surprises people: DRF disables Django's CSRF middleware for its views *because* CSRF is enforced, only when needed, by `SessionAuthentication` itself (see [Ch 23](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling)). Token/JWT clients do not need it.\n\nWhen a request arrives, `APIView.dispatch(request, *args, **kwargs)` runs:\n\n1. **`initialize_request()`** wraps the `HttpRequest` in a `Request` with `parsers=self.get_parsers()`, `authenticators=self.get_authenticators()` and `negotiator=self.get_content_negotiator()`. Nothing is parsed or authenticated yet.\n2. **`initial()`**:\n   - `perform_content_negotiation()` chooses renderer + media type (stored on `request.accepted_renderer`), raising `NotAcceptable` (406) if none fit,\n   - `determine_version()` sets `request.version` / `request.versioning_scheme` ([Ch 24](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning)),\n   - `perform_authentication()` simply accesses `request.user`, which triggers the lazy authenticators,\n   - `check_permissions()` calls `has_permission(request, view)` on each permission class,\n   - `check_throttles()` calls `allow_request()` on each throttle; failures raise `Throttled` (429) with a `Retry-After`.\n3. **Handler lookup**: `getattr(self, request.method.lower(), self.http_method_not_allowed)` — so `get()`, `post()`... or 405.\n4. **`handle_exception(exc)`** on any exception: for `NotAuthenticated`/`AuthenticationFailed` it decides 401 vs 403 by asking the first authenticator for an `authenticate_header()`; then calls the configured exception handler; if that returns `None`, the exception is re-raised (→ Django 500).\n5. **`finalize_response()`** attaches the renderer and media type to the `Response`, adds `Vary: Accept` (and `Allow`), and Django renders it on the way out.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 380\" width=\"100%\" height=\"380\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj22a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj22b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">APIView.dispatch(): one pipeline for every DRF endpoint</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"150\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">HttpRequest</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">after Django middleware</text>\n  <path d=\"M170,67 L200,67\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj22a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"202\" y=\"44\" width=\"170\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"287\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">initialize_request()</text>\n  <text x=\"287\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Request(parsers, authenticators)</text>\n  <path d=\"M372,67 L402,67\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj22a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"404\" y=\"40\" width=\"456\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"632\" y=\"58\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">initial()</text>\n  <rect x=\"416\" y=\"68\" width=\"104\" height=\"52\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"468\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">negotiation</text>\n  <text x=\"468\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Accept, 406</text>\n  <rect x=\"528\" y=\"68\" width=\"104\" height=\"52\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"580\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">version</text>\n  <text x=\"580\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">request.version</text>\n  <rect x=\"640\" y=\"68\" width=\"104\" height=\"52\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"692\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">authenticate</text>\n  <text x=\"692\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">request.user (lazy)</text>\n  <rect x=\"752\" y=\"68\" width=\"98\" height=\"52\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"801\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">permissions</text>\n  <text x=\"801\" y=\"103\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">has_permission</text>\n  <rect x=\"640\" y=\"130\" width=\"210\" height=\"48\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"745\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">throttles: allow_request()</text>\n  <text x=\"745\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">429 + Retry-After</text>\n  <text x=\"520\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">runs BEFORE your handler,</text>\n  <text x=\"520\" y=\"174\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">for every request</text>\n  <path d=\"M632,190 L632,214\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj22a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"404\" y=\"216\" width=\"456\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"632\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">handler = getattr(self, method.lower()) or ViewSet action</text>\n  <text x=\"632\" y=\"254\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">get_object() calls check_object_permissions() (detail routes only)</text>\n  <path d=\"M632,272 L632,300\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj22a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"404\" y=\"302\" width=\"456\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"632\" y=\"322\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">finalize_response()</text>\n  <text x=\"632\" y=\"340\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">attach renderer, Vary: Accept, Allow; Django renders on the way out</text>\n  <path d=\"M404,140 L230,200\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj22b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M404,244 L230,244\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj22b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"196\" width=\"210\" height=\"96\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"216\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">handle_exception(exc)</text>\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"234\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">401 vs 403 via authenticate_header</text>\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"250\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">EXCEPTION_HANDLER(exc, context)</text>\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">returns None: re-raise, Django 500</text>\n  <text x=\"125\" y=\"282\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">set_rollback() if ATOMIC_REQUESTS</text>\n  <path d=\"M230,280 L402,320\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj22b)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### The class hierarchy\n\n```text\ndjango.views.View\n └─ APIView                              dispatch, initial, handle_exception, finalize_response\n     └─ GenericAPIView                   queryset, serializer_class, get_queryset, get_object, get_serializer,\n         │                               filter_queryset, paginate_queryset, get_paginated_response\n         ├─ ListAPIView        = ListModelMixin     + GenericAPIView  (get → list)\n         ├─ CreateAPIView      = CreateModelMixin   + GenericAPIView  (post → create)\n         ├─ ListCreateAPIView, RetrieveUpdateDestroyAPIView, ...\n         └─ GenericViewSet     = ViewSetMixin + GenericAPIView\n             ├─ ReadOnlyModelViewSet = Retrieve + List mixins\n             └─ ModelViewSet         = Create + Retrieve + Update + Destroy + List mixins\nViewSet = ViewSetMixin + APIView   (no queryset/serializer machinery)\n```\n\n### `get_queryset()` and why `.all()` matters\n\n`GenericAPIView.get_queryset()` returns `self.queryset.all()` if you set a class attribute — and asserts if you set neither attribute nor override. The `.all()` is important: a class attribute is created once at import time and shared across all requests and threads. If DRF evaluated it directly, its result cache would hold rows from the first request forever. `.all()` clones it into a fresh, unevaluated QuerySet per request ([Ch 10 · QuerySets & Lookups](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups)). The same reasoning means you should **override `get_queryset()`** whenever the queryset depends on the request (the user, tenant, query params) — never filter `self.queryset` in place.\n\n### `get_object()`\n\n`get_object()` runs `queryset = self.filter_queryset(self.get_queryset())`, builds a filter from `lookup_field` (default `pk`) and `lookup_url_kwarg`, calls `get_object_or_404(queryset, **filter)`, then **`self.check_object_permissions(request, obj)`**. Two consequences: (1) object-level permissions only run where `get_object()` runs — retrieve, update, destroy, and your detail `@action`s that call it — never on `list`; (2) because the lookup uses the *filtered* queryset, scoping `get_queryset()` to the user automatically turns \"someone else's object\" into a 404 rather than a 403, which avoids leaking existence.\n\n### ViewSets: how actions become handlers\n\n`ViewSetMixin.as_view(actions)` *requires* an `actions` mapping such as `{\"get\": \"list\", \"post\": \"create\"}`. The returned view function, at request time, does `setattr(self, method, getattr(self, action))` for each pair — literally binding `self.get = self.list` — and sets `self.action` to the action name. After that, the normal `APIView.dispatch` runs unchanged. That is why `self.action` is available in `get_permissions()`, `get_serializer_class()` and `get_queryset()`, and why it can be `None` for an `OPTIONS` request (the metadata handler) — guard for that.\n\nRouters are the thing that calls `as_view(actions)` for you, once for the list route and once for the detail route, plus one per `@action`.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### Router → URL → action\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 340\" width=\"100%\" height=\"340\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj22c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">router.register(\"books\", BookViewSet, basename=\"book\")</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"200\" height=\"270\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">BookViewSet</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">list()</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"112\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">create()</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"132\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">retrieve()</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">update() / partial_update()</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"172\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">destroy()</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7c3aed\">@action(detail=True)</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"220\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">publish()</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"252\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7c3aed\">@action(detail=False)</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"268\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">recent()</text>\n  <rect x=\"290\" y=\"56\" width=\"560\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"570\" y=\"78\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">^books/$   name=\"book-list\"</text>\n  <text x=\"570\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">as_view({\"get\": \"list\", \"post\": \"create\"})</text>\n  <rect x=\"290\" y=\"134\" width=\"560\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"570\" y=\"156\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">^books/{pk}/$   name=\"book-detail\"</text>\n  <text x=\"570\" y=\"176\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">as_view({\"get\": \"retrieve\", \"put\": \"update\", \"patch\": \"partial_update\", \"delete\": \"destroy\"})</text>\n  <rect x=\"290\" y=\"212\" width=\"560\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"570\" y=\"238\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">^books/{pk}/publish/$   name=\"book-publish\"   as_view({\"post\": \"publish\"})</text>\n  <rect x=\"290\" y=\"266\" width=\"560\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"570\" y=\"292\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">^books/recent/$   name=\"book-recent\"   as_view({\"get\": \"recent\"})</text>\n  <path d=\"M220,100 L288,88\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj22c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M220,150 L288,166\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj22c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M220,214 L288,234\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj22c)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M220,262 L288,288\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj22c)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\nRoute names are `<basename>-<url_name>`. `basename` defaults to the lowercased model name of `viewset.queryset`; if you only override `get_queryset()` (no `queryset` attribute) you **must** pass `basename=`, or registration fails with an assertion.\n\n### Request walk-through: `PATCH /api/books/7/`\n\n```text\nURLResolver → book-detail → view = BookViewSet.as_view({..., \"patch\": \"partial_update\"})\n  ViewSetMixin.view(): self.action = \"partial_update\"; self.patch = self.partial_update\n  APIView.dispatch():\n    initialize_request → Request(JSONParser, ...)\n    initial(): negotiate → JSON; version → None; request.user → JWTAuthentication.authenticate()\n               check_permissions → IsAuthenticated.has_permission ✓ ; throttles ✓\n    partial_update(): update(partial=True)\n        instance = get_object()  → get_queryset().filter(...) → get_object_or_404(pk=7)\n                                  → check_object_permissions → IsOwner.has_object_permission ✓\n        serializer = get_serializer(instance, data=request.data, partial=True)  # request.data parsed NOW\n        serializer.is_valid(raise_exception=True)\n        perform_update(serializer)  → serializer.save()\n        return Response(serializer.data)\n    finalize_response → JSONRenderer → 200\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: `APIView` and `@api_view`\n\n```python\n# library/views.py\nfrom django.shortcuts import get_object_or_404\nfrom rest_framework import status\nfrom rest_framework.decorators import api_view, permission_classes\nfrom rest_framework.permissions import IsAuthenticated\nfrom rest_framework.response import Response\nfrom rest_framework.views import APIView\n\nfrom .models import Book\nfrom .serializers import BookSerializer\n\n\nclass BookList(APIView):\n    permission_classes = [IsAuthenticated]\n\n    def get(self, request):\n        books = Book.objects.select_related(\"author\").order_by(\"id\")[:50]\n        return Response(BookSerializer(books, many=True).data)\n\n    def post(self, request):\n        serializer = BookSerializer(data=request.data, context={\"request\": request})\n        serializer.is_valid(raise_exception=True)\n        serializer.save()\n        return Response(serializer.data, status=status.HTTP_201_CREATED)\n\n\nclass BookDetail(APIView):\n    def get(self, request, pk):\n        book = get_object_or_404(Book, pk=pk)\n        self.check_object_permissions(request, book)   # APIView does NOT do this for you\n        return Response(BookSerializer(book).data)\n\n\n@api_view([\"GET\"])\n@permission_classes([IsAuthenticated])\ndef health(request):\n    return Response({\"status\": \"ok\", \"user\": request.user.get_username()})\n```\n\nNote the `check_object_permissions` call: with raw `APIView` nothing calls it for you. That single line is a frequent security bug in hand-written views.\n\n### The same thing with generics\n\n```python\nfrom rest_framework import generics\n\n\nclass BookListCreate(generics.ListCreateAPIView):\n    serializer_class = BookSerializer\n    permission_classes = [IsAuthenticated]\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return Book.objects.select_related(\"author\").prefetch_related(\"tags\").order_by(\"id\")\n\n    def perform_create(self, serializer):\n        serializer.save(owner=self.request.user)\n\n\nclass BookRetrieveUpdateDestroy(generics.RetrieveUpdateDestroyAPIView):\n    serializer_class = BookSerializer\n    lookup_field = \"pk\"\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return Book.objects.filter(owner=self.request.user)   # others' books → 404\n```\n\n### Real-world example: a `ModelViewSet` with per-action behaviour\n\n```python\n# library/views.py\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.db.models import Count\nfrom django.utils import timezone\nfrom rest_framework import mixins, permissions, serializers, status, viewsets\nfrom rest_framework.decorators import action\nfrom rest_framework.response import Response\n\nfrom .models import Book\nfrom .permissions import IsOwnerOrReadOnly\nfrom .serializers import BookReadSerializer, BookWriteSerializer, PublishSerializer\nfrom .tasks import notify_followers\n\n\nclass BookViewSet(viewsets.ModelViewSet):\n    permission_classes = [permissions.IsAuthenticatedOrReadOnly, IsOwnerOrReadOnly]\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        qs = (Book.objects.select_related(\"author\", \"owner\")\n              .prefetch_related(\"tags\")\n              .annotate(review_count=Count(\"reviews\")))\n        if self.action == \"list\":\n            qs = qs.filter(status=Book.Status.PUBLISHED)\n        return qs.order_by(\"-id\")\n\n    def get_serializer_class(self):\n        if self.action in (\"create\", \"update\", \"partial_update\"):\n            return BookWriteSerializer\n        if self.action == \"publish\":\n            return PublishSerializer\n        return BookReadSerializer\n\n    def get_permissions(self):\n        if self.action == \"destroy\":\n            return [permissions.IsAdminUser()]\n        return super().get_permissions()\n\n    def perform_create(self, serializer):\n        serializer.save(owner=self.request.user)\n\n    def perform_destroy(self, instance):\n        instance.status = Book.Status.DRAFT        # soft \"unpublish\" instead of DELETE\n        instance.save(update_fields=[\"status\"])\n\n    @action(detail=True, methods=[\"post\"])\n    def publish(self, request, pk=None):\n        book = self.get_object()                    # object permissions run here\n        serializer = self.get_serializer(book, data=request.data)\n        serializer.is_valid(raise_exception=True)\n        with transaction.atomic():\n            book.status = Book.Status.PUBLISHED\n            book.published_on = serializer.validated_data.get(\"published_on\", timezone.localdate())\n            book.save(update_fields=[\"status\", \"published_on\"])\n            transaction.on_commit(lambda: notify_followers.delay(book.id))\n        return Response(BookReadSerializer(book, context=self.get_serializer_context()).data)\n\n    @action(detail=False, methods=[\"get\"], url_path=\"recent\", permission_classes=[permissions.AllowAny])\n    def recent(self, request):\n        qs = self.filter_queryset(self.get_queryset())[:10]\n        return Response(BookReadSerializer(qs, many=True, context=self.get_serializer_context()).data)\n```\n\n```python\n# config/urls.py\nfrom django.urls import include, path\nfrom rest_framework.routers import DefaultRouter\n\nfrom library.views import BookViewSet\n\nrouter = DefaultRouter()\nrouter.register(r\"books\", BookViewSet, basename=\"book\")   # basename needed: no queryset attr\n\nurlpatterns = [\n    path(\"api/\", include(router.urls)),\n]\n```\n\n```bash\npython manage.py show_urls   # needs django-extensions; otherwise inspect router.urls in a shell\n```\n\n```text\n/api/                         api-root\n/api/books/                   book-list\n/api/books/<pk>/              book-detail\n/api/books/<pk>/publish/      book-publish\n/api/books/recent/            book-recent\n```\n\nThe list query the router-backed `list` action issues (with pagination configured, see [Ch 24](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning)):\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM (SELECT ... FROM \"library_book\" WHERE \"status\" = 'published' GROUP BY ...) subquery;\nSELECT \"library_book\".*, \"library_author\".*, \"auth_user\".*, COUNT(\"library_review\".\"id\") AS \"review_count\"\nFROM \"library_book\"\nINNER JOIN \"library_author\" ON (...) INNER JOIN \"auth_user\" ON (...)\nLEFT OUTER JOIN \"library_review\" ON (\"library_book\".\"id\" = \"library_review\".\"book_id\")\nWHERE \"library_book\".\"status\" = 'published'\nGROUP BY \"library_book\".\"id\", \"library_author\".\"id\", \"auth_user\".\"id\"\nORDER BY \"library_book\".\"id\" DESC LIMIT 20;\nSELECT ... FROM \"library_book_tags\" INNER JOIN \"library_tag\" ... WHERE \"book_id\" IN (...);\n```\n\n### Pick only the actions you need\n\n```python\nclass ReviewViewSet(mixins.CreateModelMixin,\n                    mixins.ListModelMixin,\n                    viewsets.GenericViewSet):\n    \"\"\"No update/delete: the router will only generate GET/POST on /reviews/.\"\"\"\n    serializer_class = ReviewSerializer\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return Review.objects.filter(book_id=self.kwargs[\"book_pk\"])\n```\n\nNested URLs like `/books/{book_pk}/reviews/` are not built into DRF routers; use `drf-nested-routers` or add a `path()` manually with `ReviewViewSet.as_view({\"get\": \"list\", \"post\": \"create\"})`.\n\n### A plain `ViewSet` for non-model resources\n\n```python\nclass StatsViewSet(viewsets.ViewSet):\n    permission_classes = [permissions.IsAdminUser]\n\n    def list(self, request):\n        return Response({\"books\": Book.objects.count(), \"authors\": Author.objects.count()})\n\n    def retrieve(self, request, pk=None):\n        return Response({\"author\": pk, \"books\": Book.objects.filter(author_id=pk).count()})\n\nrouter.register(r\"stats\", StatsViewSet, basename=\"stats\")\n```\n\n### Custom exception handler: one error envelope\n\n```python\n# config/exceptions.py\nimport logging\n\nfrom django.db import IntegrityError\nfrom rest_framework import status\nfrom rest_framework.response import Response\nfrom rest_framework.views import exception_handler\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\n\ndef api_exception_handler(exc, context):\n    response = exception_handler(exc, context)        # handles APIException, Http404, PermissionDenied\n\n    if response is None and isinstance(exc, IntegrityError):\n        response = Response({\"detail\": \"Conflict with existing data.\"}, status=status.HTTP_409_CONFLICT)\n\n    if response is None:\n        log.exception(\"Unhandled API error\", extra={\"view\": context[\"view\"].__class__.__name__})\n        return None                                     # let Django return 500 (and Sentry capture it)\n\n    detail = response.data\n    response.data = {\n        \"error\": {\n            \"status\": response.status_code,\n            \"code\": getattr(exc, \"default_code\", \"error\"),\n            \"detail\": detail,\n        }\n    }\n    return response\n```\n\n```python\n# settings.py\nREST_FRAMEWORK = {\n    \"EXCEPTION_HANDLER\": \"config.exceptions.api_exception_handler\",\n    \"DEFAULT_RENDERER_CLASSES\": [\"rest_framework.renderers.JSONRenderer\"],   # no browsable API in prod\n    \"DEFAULT_PARSER_CLASSES\": [\"rest_framework.parsers.JSONParser\"],\n}\n```\n\nCustom domain exceptions are just `APIException` subclasses:\n\n```python\nfrom rest_framework.exceptions import APIException\n\n\nclass OutOfStock(APIException):\n    status_code = 409\n    default_detail = \"Item is out of stock.\"\n    default_code = \"out_of_stock\"\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** catching `IntegrityError` in the exception handler while `ATOMIC_REQUESTS=True` is fine — DRF's handler calls `set_rollback()` to mark the request transaction for rollback. But if you catch it *inside* an `atomic()` block in your view without a nested savepoint, the transaction is broken ([Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency)).\n\n### Testing a ViewSet\n\n```python\nfrom django.urls import reverse\nfrom rest_framework.test import APITestCase\n\n\nclass BookApiTests(APITestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpTestData(cls):\n        cls.user = User.objects.create_user(\"ann\", password=\"pw\")\n\n    def test_publish_requires_owner(self):\n        book = BookFactory(owner=UserFactory())\n        self.client.force_authenticate(self.user)\n        resp = self.client.post(reverse(\"book-publish\", args=[book.pk]), {})\n        self.assertEqual(resp.status_code, 403)\n\n    def test_list_query_count(self):\n        BookFactory.create_batch(25, status=\"published\")\n        with self.assertNumQueries(3):\n            self.client.get(reverse(\"book-list\"))\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Style | Code | Control | Best for | Pitfall |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `@api_view` FBV | Explicit, short | Full | One-off endpoints, webhooks | Easy to forget object permissions |\n| `APIView` | Explicit per method | Full | Non-CRUD, RPC-like, aggregations | Repeated boilerplate |\n| Generic views | Very little | Via hooks | One or two operations on a model | Hook names must be learned |\n| `GenericViewSet` + mixins | Little | Pick actions | Resources with a subset of CRUD | Mixin order / MRO confusion |\n| `ModelViewSet` | Least | Via hooks | Full CRUD resources | Exposes DELETE/PUT you may not want |\n| `ViewSet` | Medium | Full, no generics | Non-model resources with router URLs | No `get_object()`/pagination for free |\n\n| Router | Adds |\n|---|---|\n| `SimpleRouter` | list/detail/action routes only |\n| `DefaultRouter` | + browsable API root view, + `.json` format-suffix routes |\n\n### When to use\n- `ModelViewSet` when the resource genuinely supports all five operations and its URL layout is standard.\n- `GenericViewSet` + chosen mixins when it supports some — this is the most common production choice.\n- `APIView` for workflows: `/checkout/`, `/auth/login/`, `/reports/sales/`.\n\n### When NOT to use\n- Don't use `ModelViewSet` \"because it's shorter\" if clients must not delete or replace — every inherited action is a public endpoint.\n- Don't cram unrelated workflows into a ViewSet as ten `@action`s; that is an RPC service in disguise — give it its own view.\n- Don't use routers for deeply nested resources; write explicit `path()`s or use a dedicated package.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Filtering the class-level `queryset` attribute.** `queryset = Book.objects.filter(owner=???)` cannot see the request. Override `get_queryset()`.\n\n**2. Relying on `has_object_permission` for lists.** It never runs on `list`. Scope the queryset (`filter(owner=request.user)`) for list views. See [Ch 23](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling).\n\n**3. Skipping `get_object()` in a custom detail action.** `Book.objects.get(pk=pk)` bypasses filtering and object permissions. Always use `self.get_object()`.\n\n**4. Logic in `create()` instead of `perform_create()`.** Overriding `create()` means re-implementing serializer/response handling. Use `perform_create(serializer)` to add `owner=`, enqueue tasks (with `on_commit`), or audit-log.\n\n**5. Assuming `self.action` is always set.** On `OPTIONS` it is `None` (metadata); `if self.action == \"list\"` is fine, but `self.action.startswith(...)` crashes.\n\n**6. Missing `basename`.** Registering a viewset without a `queryset` attribute raises at import; pass `basename=\"book\"`.\n\n**7. Shipping the browsable API to production.** It is great for development but renders forms, exposes field lists and costs CPU. Restrict `DEFAULT_RENDERER_CLASSES` in production settings.\n\n**8. Forgetting `context` when instantiating serializers manually.** Hyperlinked fields and `CurrentUserDefault` need `request`. Use `self.get_serializer(...)` or pass `context=self.get_serializer_context()`.\n\n**9. Returning Django `JsonResponse` from DRF views.** It bypasses renderers and content negotiation. Return `Response`.\n\n**Best practices:** keep views thin — queryset selection, permission wiring, serializer selection, calling a service; one viewset per resource; explicit mixins; a project-wide exception handler; query-count tests for every list action.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: data leak through an `@action`.** Symptom: a pen test finds `/api/invoices/123/pdf/` returns other tenants' invoices. Root cause: the action did `Invoice.objects.get(pk=pk)` instead of `self.get_object()`, skipping tenant filtering and object permission. Fix: always `get_object()`; add a test per action asserting 404 for foreign objects.\n\n**Pitfall: 403 when the client expected 401.** Symptom: the SPA's token refresh never triggers. Root cause: the first class in `DEFAULT_AUTHENTICATION_CLASSES` is `SessionAuthentication`, which has no `authenticate_header`, so DRF returns 403 for unauthenticated requests. Fix: put the token/JWT authenticator first so 401 + `WWW-Authenticate` is returned.\n\n**Pitfall: stale data from a cached queryset.** Symptom: list endpoint shows rows that were deleted hours ago on one worker. Root cause: someone overrode `get_queryset()` to return `self.queryset` (no `.all()`) after evaluating it in `__init__`, or cached a list on the class. Fix: always return a fresh QuerySet.\n\n**Pitfall: unhandled exceptions return HTML.** With `DEBUG=False`, an exception the handler does not recognise becomes Django's HTML 500 page. Clients choke on it. Fix: set `handler500` to a JSON view for API paths, and make sure Sentry captures the original exception.\n\n**Pitfall: DELETE that cascades.** `ModelViewSet.destroy` calls `instance.delete()`; with `on_delete=CASCADE` one request can delete thousands of rows (and their signals). Override `perform_destroy` for soft-delete or restrict the action.\n\n**Debugging.**\n- `print(router.urls)` or `django-extensions`' `show_urls` to confirm generated routes and names.\n- DRF's browsable API in dev shows the allowed methods, filters and serializer form for a view.\n- `django-debug-toolbar` works with the browsable API to show SQL per request.\n- Log `view.action`, `request.user.pk` and status in a middleware or the exception handler for structured request logs ([REST API · Observability](../rest-api/topic.html?p=38-observability-logging-tracing)).\n\n**Scaling.** DRF views are synchronous; under ASGI they run in a thread pool. Throughput comes from fixing queries, paginating, caching (`cache_page` on list actions with `vary_on_headers(\"Authorization\")` for per-user data, see [Ch 29](topic.html?p=29-caching)), moving slow work to Celery ([Ch 31](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks)), and horizontal scaling of stateless workers ([Ch 35](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production)).\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `APIView`, generic views and ViewSets?**\nA: `APIView` is the base: you write `get()`, `post()` etc. and get DRF's request parsing, auth, permissions, throttling and exception handling. Generic views (`GenericAPIView` + mixins, e.g. `ListCreateAPIView`) add queryset/serializer/lookup/pagination hooks and implement standard CRUD so you mostly override hooks. ViewSets group actions (`list`, `retrieve`, `create`...) for one resource in one class and are bound to HTTP methods by a router via `as_view(actions)`. `ModelViewSet` is a `GenericViewSet` with all five mixins. Choose by how standard the resource is.\n\n**Q: What happens when a request hits a DRF view?**\nA: `as_view()` (CSRF-exempted) calls `dispatch()`. It wraps the `HttpRequest` in a DRF `Request`, then `initial()` performs content negotiation, determines the version, authenticates (by touching `request.user`), checks `has_permission` for each permission class, and checks throttles. It then calls the handler for the method (or the bound ViewSet action). Any exception goes through `handle_exception`, which uses the configured exception handler to build a `Response`. Finally `finalize_response` attaches the renderer and headers and Django renders it.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `ViewSet` and `ModelViewSet`?**\nA: `ViewSet` is `ViewSetMixin + APIView`: action-based routing but no `queryset`, `get_object()`, serializer helpers or default actions — you implement `list()`/`retrieve()` yourself. `ModelViewSet` is `GenericViewSet` plus `Create/Retrieve/Update/Destroy/ListModelMixin`, giving full CRUD from `queryset` and `serializer_class`. `ReadOnlyModelViewSet` gives only list and retrieve. In between, you compose `GenericViewSet` with just the mixins you want.\n\n**Q: How do you add a custom endpoint to a ViewSet?**\nA: Use `@action(detail=True|False, methods=[...])`. `detail=True` routes to `/{prefix}/{lookup}/{url_path}/` and should call `self.get_object()`; `detail=False` routes to `/{prefix}/{url_path}/`. You can override `permission_classes`, `serializer_class`, `url_path` and `url_name` per action. The route name is `<basename>-<url_name>`, e.g. `book-publish`.\n\n**Q: Why does DRF call `.all()` on the `queryset` attribute in `get_queryset()`?**\nA: The class attribute is created once and shared across requests. Evaluating it directly would populate its result cache once and serve stale rows forever. `.all()` returns a fresh clone per request. Override `get_queryset()` whenever filtering depends on the request.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `perform_create` and `create` in a generic view?**\nA: `create()` is the mixin's full handler: build serializer from `request.data`, validate, call `perform_create`, build the 201 response with headers. `perform_create(serializer)` is just the save step (`serializer.save()`), designed as the hook to inject server-side values (`owner=request.user`), audit, or schedule work. Override `perform_create` for these; override `create` only if you need a different response.\n\n**Q: What does a router do, and `SimpleRouter` vs `DefaultRouter`?**\nA: A router inspects a ViewSet's actions and generates URL patterns: a list route mapping GET/POST to `list`/`create`, a detail route mapping GET/PUT/PATCH/DELETE, and one per `@action`, all named `<basename>-<name>`. `DefaultRouter` also adds a root view listing the registered endpoints and format-suffix patterns. `basename` comes from `queryset.model` or must be supplied.\n\n**Q: How does DRF handle exceptions and how do you customise the error format?**\nA: Views catch exceptions in `handle_exception`. The default `exception_handler` converts `APIException` subclasses (ValidationError 400, NotAuthenticated/AuthenticationFailed 401 or 403, PermissionDenied 403, NotFound 404, MethodNotAllowed 405, Throttled 429...), plus Django's `Http404` and `PermissionDenied`, into `Response`s; anything else returns `None` and becomes a 500. Point `EXCEPTION_HANDLER` at a function that calls the default and reshapes `response.data` into your envelope, and maps extra exceptions like `IntegrityError` to 409.\n\n**Q: Why might DRF return 403 instead of 401 for an unauthenticated request?**\nA: 401 requires a `WWW-Authenticate` header. DRF asks the **first** authentication class for `authenticate_header()`; `SessionAuthentication` returns none, so DRF responds 403. `TokenAuthentication`/JWT return a header, so with them first you get 401. Order authenticators deliberately.\n\n**Q: You must expose list, create and retrieve for a resource but never update or delete. How do you build it, and how do you prove nothing else is exposed? (Senior)**\nA: Compose `mixins.ListModelMixin, mixins.CreateModelMixin, mixins.RetrieveModelMixin, viewsets.GenericViewSet` rather than subclassing `ModelViewSet` and blocking methods. The router then only maps GET/POST on the list route and GET on detail; PUT/PATCH/DELETE return 405 with a correct `Allow` header. Prove it with tests asserting 405 for each disallowed method, and with the OpenAPI schema (drf-spectacular) diffed in CI so new operations show up in review. Avoid `http_method_names` hacks — they are easy to forget when adding actions.\n\n**Q: How do you make permissions, serializers and querysets differ per action without the ViewSet becoming a mess? (Senior)**\nA: Use the hooks keyed by `self.action`: `get_permissions()`, `get_serializer_class()`, `get_queryset()`, and per-`@action` kwargs (`permission_classes=`, `serializer_class=`). For many variants, use declarative maps (`serializer_classes = {\"list\": ..., \"create\": ...}` with a fallback) in a small base class. Keep heavy logic out of the view — the action calls a service function that encapsulates the transaction and side effects. Guard for `self.action is None` (OPTIONS). If a ViewSet grows many unrelated actions, split it: actions are for operations on the resource, not a dumping ground.\n\n**Q: An `@action` in production leaked other tenants' data. How did it happen and how do you prevent the class of bug? (Senior)**\nA: Typically the action fetched the object directly (`Model.objects.get(pk=pk)`) or built its own queryset, bypassing `get_queryset()` tenant scoping and `check_object_permissions()`. Prevention: tenant-scope in one place (`get_queryset()` in a base class, or a tenant-aware manager), always use `self.get_object()`/`self.get_queryset()` in actions, set restrictive default permission classes globally (`IsAuthenticated`), and add a parametrised test that hits every route in `router.urls` as a user from another tenant and expects 404/403. Code review checklists and a lint rule against `.objects.` in view modules help too.\n\n**Q: How do `ATOMIC_REQUESTS` and DRF's exception handling interact?**\nA: With `ATOMIC_REQUESTS=True` Django wraps the view in `transaction.atomic()`. Because DRF converts exceptions into responses (the exception never propagates out of the view), the atomic block would otherwise commit. DRF's default handler calls `set_rollback()`, marking the transaction for rollback when an error response is produced. Custom handlers that call the default inherit that; one that builds responses without it should call `rest_framework.views.set_rollback()` itself.\n\n**Q: How do you version or route nested resources like `/books/{id}/reviews/`?**\nA: DRF's built-in routers do not nest. Options: `drf-nested-routers` (`NestedSimpleRouter`) which passes `book_pk` in `self.kwargs`; or explicit `path(\"books/<int:book_pk>/reviews/\", ReviewViewSet.as_view({...}))`. In the nested viewset, filter `get_queryset()` by `self.kwargs[\"book_pk\"]` and verify access to the parent (otherwise you leak children of books the user cannot see). Keep nesting to one level; deeper hierarchies are better as top-level resources with filters.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Concept | Remember |\n|---|---|\n| `dispatch` order | initialize_request → negotiation → version → auth → permissions → throttles → handler → finalize |\n| CSRF | `APIView.as_view` is `csrf_exempt`; `SessionAuthentication` re-enforces it |\n| `get_queryset` | returns `queryset.all()`; override for request-dependent filtering |\n| `get_object` | filter_queryset + lookup + 404 + `check_object_permissions` |\n| Object perms | only via `get_object()` / `check_object_permissions` — never on list |\n| Hooks | `perform_create/update/destroy`, `get_serializer_class`, `get_permissions` |\n| ViewSet binding | `as_view({\"get\": \"list\"})` → `self.get = self.list`, `self.action = \"list\"` |\n| Route names | `<basename>-list`, `<basename>-detail`, `<basename>-<action>` |\n| `basename` | required when no `queryset` attribute |\n| 401 vs 403 | decided by first authenticator's `authenticate_header()` |\n\n- Prefer `GenericViewSet` + chosen mixins over `ModelViewSet` when not all CRUD is allowed.\n- Use `self.get_object()` in every detail action.\n- Return `Response`, not `JsonResponse`.\n- One `EXCEPTION_HANDLER` for a consistent error envelope.\n- Disable the browsable API renderer in production.\n- `self.action` can be `None`.\n- Nested routes need `drf-nested-routers` or manual paths.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"djangorestframework>=3.15\" django-extensions\n```\n\n1. **Trace dispatch.** Subclass `APIView`, override `initial`, `check_permissions`, `handle_exception` and `finalize_response` to log, and call it with good and bad credentials. Write down the order.\n2. **Same endpoint four ways.** Implement \"list + create books\" as `@api_view`, `APIView`, `ListCreateAPIView` and a `GenericViewSet` with mixins. Compare lines of code and behaviour of `OPTIONS`.\n3. **Router inspection.** Register a viewset with two `@action`s and print `router.urls`; reverse each route name in a test.\n4. **Security test.** Write a test that iterates every detail route as a foreign user and asserts 404 — then introduce a buggy `@action` using `Model.objects.get` and watch the test catch it.\n5. **Error envelope.** Implement the custom exception handler above, map `IntegrityError` to 409, and verify `ATOMIC_REQUESTS` rolls back on a 400.\n\n**Mini project — \"Library API v1\".** Resources: books (full CRUD for owners, read for all), reviews (create/list only, nested under books), stats (`ViewSet`, admin only). Requirements: per-action serializers and permissions, `publish` action with `on_commit` Celery dispatch, consistent error envelope, query-count tests, no browsable API in production settings.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 04 · Views, Requests & Responses](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses) · [Ch 17 · Class-Based & Generic Views](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views) · [Ch 21 · DRF Serializers](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers) · [Ch 23 · DRF Authentication, Permissions & Throttling](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling) · [Ch 24 · DRF Pagination, Filtering, Versioning & Docs](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning) · [Ch 33 · Testing Django Applications](topic.html?p=33-testing) · [Ch 38 · Interview: DRF, Security & Scale](topic.html?p=38-interview-drf-advanced)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · Resource Modeling & URI Design](../rest-api/topic.html?p=05-resource-modeling-uri-design) · [REST API · HTTP Methods & Idempotency](../rest-api/topic.html?p=06-http-methods-idempotency) · [REST API · Status Codes](../rest-api/topic.html?p=07-status-codes) · [REST API · Content Negotiation](../rest-api/topic.html?p=11-content-negotiation) · [REST API · Error Handling & Problem Details](../rest-api/topic.html?p=16-error-handling-problem-details)\n\n- **Views (API Guide)** — Django REST framework · *Beginner* · `APIView`, `@api_view` and the policy attributes. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/views/>\n- **Generic views** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · every hook on `GenericAPIView` and the mixins. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/generic-views/>\n- **ViewSets** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · actions, `@action`, and per-action configuration. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/viewsets/>\n- **Routers** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · generated routes, basenames, custom routers. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/routers/>\n- **Exceptions** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · the exception classes and writing a custom handler. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/exceptions/>\n- **Classy DRF** — cdrf.co · *Intermediate* · every method of every DRF class view, with the MRO laid out. <https://www.cdrf.co/>\n- **drf-nested-routers** — alanjds on GitHub · *Intermediate* · nested routes for ViewSets. <https://github.com/alanjds/drf-nested-routers>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 22.*\n", "23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling": "# 23 · DRF Authentication, Permissions & Throttling\n\n> **In one line:** In DRF, authentication only answers *who are you* (lazily, by trying each authenticator in order), permissions answer *may you do this* (view-level for every request, object-level only when `get_object()` runs), and throttles answer *how often* (cache-backed counters) — and almost every API security bug is one of those three being misunderstood.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers) (the `initial()` pipeline), [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user) (users, passwords, backends), [Ch 27 · Sessions, Cookies & Messages](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies).\n\nDjango's own auth system was built for server-rendered sites: a login form, a session cookie, `request.user` filled in by `AuthenticationMiddleware`, and `@login_required`. An API has different clients — a React SPA on another origin, a mobile app that cannot keep a cookie jar sensibly, a partner's cron job, another microservice — and each wants a different credential: a cookie, a long-lived API key, a short-lived bearer token.\n\nThe naive approach is to check a header at the top of every view: `if request.headers.get(\"X-Api-Key\") != settings.KEY: return 403`. Then you add per-user tokens, then \"only the owner can edit\", then \"admins can do anything\", and soon every view has a slightly different hand-rolled `if` ladder. One forgotten ladder is an IDOR (insecure direct object reference — OWASP API1, *Broken Object Level Authorization*), the most common API vulnerability there is ([REST API · OWASP API Security](../rest-api/topic.html?p=23-owasp-api-security)).\n\nDRF separates the problem into three pluggable policies that run for every request in a fixed order inside `APIView.initial()`: **authentication classes** produce `request.user`/`request.auth`, **permission classes** allow or deny, and **throttle classes** rate-limit. Each is a list you set globally in `REST_FRAMEWORK` and override per view. The power is composability; the danger is the defaults — DRF's default permission is **`AllowAny`**. A new endpoint with no `permission_classes` is public unless you changed the global default.\n\n> **Why this matters:** \"JWT vs session vs token auth\", \"`has_permission` vs `has_object_permission`\", and \"why doesn't my object permission run on the list endpoint?\" are among the most-asked DRF interview questions, and the last one is also among the most common real-world data leaks.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Authentication class** — implements `authenticate(request) -> (user, auth) | None` and optionally `authenticate_header(request)`. *Why:* `None` means \"not my credential, try the next\"; raising `AuthenticationFailed` means \"your credential is bad, stop\".\n- **`request.user` / `request.auth`** — the authenticated user (or `AnonymousUser`) and the credential object (token instance, decoded JWT, `None`). *Why:* `request.auth` lets you read token scopes/claims.\n- **Lazy authentication** — authenticators run on first access to `request.user`; `initial()` forces it via `perform_authentication()`. *Why:* explains odd ordering bugs in middleware.\n- **`SessionAuthentication`** — uses Django's session; **enforces CSRF** for authenticated requests. *Why:* the right choice for a same-site SPA.\n- **`TokenAuthentication`** — `Authorization: Token <key>`, one DB-stored key per user, no expiry (`rest_framework.authtoken`). *Why:* simple server-to-server keys; weak for browsers.\n- **`JWTAuthentication`** (SimpleJWT) — `Authorization: Bearer <jwt>`; signed, self-contained, short-lived access tokens + refresh tokens. *Why:* stateless verification, mobile/SPA friendly, hard to revoke.\n- **Permission class** — `has_permission(request, view)` and `has_object_permission(request, view, obj)`, both default `True`. *Why:* all classes in the list must pass (AND).\n- **Object-level permission** — checked only in `get_object()` / `check_object_permissions()`. *Why:* never runs on list views — scope querysets instead.\n- **Built-ins** — `AllowAny`, `IsAuthenticated`, `IsAdminUser` (`is_staff`), `IsAuthenticatedOrReadOnly`, `DjangoModelPermissions`, `DjangoModelPermissionsOrAnonReadOnly`, `DjangoObjectPermissions`. *Why:* covers most needs; compose with `&`, `|`, `~`.\n- **`SAFE_METHODS`** — `(\"GET\", \"HEAD\", \"OPTIONS\")`. *Why:* the read-only check in every \"OrReadOnly\" permission.\n- **Throttle** — `allow_request(request, view)` + `wait()`. `AnonRateThrottle` (by IP), `UserRateThrottle` (by user id), `ScopedRateThrottle` (by `throttle_scope`). *Why:* stores request timestamps in the cache.\n- **CORS** — browser-enforced cross-origin rules, added by `django-cors-headers`. *Why:* CORS is not authentication; it only controls which origins' JavaScript can read responses.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### How authentication runs internally\n\n`Request.user` is a property. On first access it calls `self._authenticate()`, which loops over `self.authenticators` (instances of `get_authenticators()`, i.e. your `authentication_classes`):\n\n```text\nfor authenticator in self.authenticators:\n    try:\n        user_auth_tuple = authenticator.authenticate(self)\n    except exceptions.APIException:\n        self._not_authenticated()      # set AnonymousUser, then re-raise → 401/403\n        raise\n    if user_auth_tuple is not None:\n        self._authenticator = authenticator\n        self.user, self.auth = user_auth_tuple\n        return\nself._not_authenticated()              # AnonymousUser, auth = None\n```\n\nThree rules fall out. **First match wins** — order matters. **Returning `None` is not failure** — the request just stays anonymous; it is the *permission* classes that then deny it. **Raising stops the chain** — a malformed or expired JWT raises `AuthenticationFailed` even if a valid session cookie is also present.\n\nWhether the client sees 401 or 403 is decided in `APIView.permission_denied()` and `handle_exception()`: if the request is unauthenticated and the **first** authenticator provides `authenticate_header()` (Token: `Token`, JWT: `Bearer realm=\"api\"`, Basic: `Basic realm=\"api\"`), the response is **401** with `WWW-Authenticate`; `SessionAuthentication` has no header, so you get **403**.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 360\" width=\"100%\" height=\"360\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj23a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj23b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj23c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">request.user: authenticators tried in order (first non-None wins)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"140\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"90\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">access request.user</text>\n  <text x=\"90\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">perform_authentication()</text>\n  <path d=\"M160,75 L196,75\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"198\" y=\"50\" width=\"160\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"278\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">1. JWTAuthentication</text>\n  <text x=\"278\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Authorization: Bearer ...</text>\n  <path d=\"M358,75 L394,75\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"376\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">None</text>\n  <rect x=\"396\" y=\"50\" width=\"160\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"476\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">2. SessionAuthentication</text>\n  <text x=\"476\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">sessionid cookie + CSRF</text>\n  <path d=\"M556,75 L592,75\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"574\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">None</text>\n  <rect x=\"594\" y=\"50\" width=\"266\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"727\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">no match: AnonymousUser, auth=None</text>\n  <text x=\"727\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">not an error by itself</text>\n  <path d=\"M278,100 L278,150\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"290\" y=\"128\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"9\">(user, token)</text>\n  <path d=\"M476,100 L476,150\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"488\" y=\"128\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"9\">(user, None)</text>\n  <rect x=\"198\" y=\"152\" width=\"358\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"377\" y=\"178\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">request.user, request.auth set; stop the loop</text>\n  <path d=\"M727,100 L727,150\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"594\" y=\"152\" width=\"266\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"727\" y=\"172\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">permissions decide next</text>\n  <text x=\"727\" y=\"187\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">IsAuthenticated denies anonymous</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"226\" width=\"400\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"246\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">authenticate() RAISES AuthenticationFailed</text>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">expired / bad signature JWT, inactive user, bad token</text>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"284\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">chain stops even if a valid cookie exists</text>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"302\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">response: 401 if FIRST authenticator has</text>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"318\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">authenticate_header(), else 403</text>\n  <path d=\"M278,100 L220,224\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"450\" y=\"226\" width=\"410\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"246\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">SessionAuthentication.enforce_csrf()</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">only after a session user is found</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"284\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">unsafe methods need X-CSRFToken header</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"302\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">anonymous requests are not CSRF-checked here</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"318\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">(APIView itself is csrf_exempt)</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Session vs Token vs JWT\n\n| | Session (`SessionAuthentication`) | DRF Token (`TokenAuthentication`) | JWT (SimpleJWT) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Credential | `sessionid` cookie | opaque key in `Authorization: Token` | signed JSON in `Authorization: Bearer` |\n| Server state | session row/cache entry | `authtoken_token` row | none for access tokens (optional blacklist for refresh) |\n| Per-request cost | session lookup + user lookup | token + user lookup (one JOIN) | signature check + user lookup (by default) |\n| Expiry | `SESSION_COOKIE_AGE` (2 weeks default) | none built-in | access: 5 min default; refresh: 1 day default |\n| Revocation | delete session / logout | delete token | hard: wait for expiry, or denylist |\n| CSRF | required (cookies auto-sent) | not needed | not needed (if not in a cookie) |\n| XSS exposure | `HttpOnly` cookie not readable by JS | token in JS storage is readable | same, if in `localStorage` |\n| Best for | same-site SPA, admin, browsable API | internal/server clients, simple keys | mobile apps, cross-domain SPAs, multi-service |\n\nThe interview-grade nuance: **JWT is not \"more secure\"**; it is **stateless**. You trade revocation and simplicity for not needing a shared session store. Most monolithic Django APIs serving a first-party SPA on the same site are better served by session auth with CSRF. Details of JWT structure and pitfalls live in [REST API · JWT](../rest-api/topic.html?p=20-jwt).\n\n### SimpleJWT mechanics\n\n`TokenObtainPairView` authenticates username/password via `authenticate()` ([Ch 25](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user)) and returns an **access** and a **refresh** token, both signed with `SIGNING_KEY` (defaults to `SECRET_KEY`, HS256). On each request `JWTAuthentication` reads the header, validates signature and `exp`, checks the `token_type` is access, then by default **loads the user from the database** by the `user_id` claim (and rejects inactive users). `TokenRefreshView` exchanges a refresh token for a new access token; with `ROTATE_REFRESH_TOKENS=True` it also issues a new refresh token, and with `BLACKLIST_AFTER_ROTATION=True` plus the `token_blacklist` app the old refresh token is recorded in `BlacklistedToken` so it cannot be reused. Only refresh tokens are blacklisted; an issued access token is valid until it expires — which is why access lifetimes are short.\n\n### Permissions: two hooks, two moments\n\n`APIView.check_permissions(request)` runs in `initial()` for **every** request and calls `has_permission` on each permission instance; the first `False` calls `self.permission_denied(request, message=..., code=...)`. `check_object_permissions(request, obj)` calls `has_object_permission` on each — but it is only called from `GenericAPIView.get_object()` (or explicitly by you). So:\n\n- list / create → `has_permission` only,\n- retrieve / update / destroy / detail actions that call `get_object()` → `has_permission`, then `has_object_permission`.\n\nBecause `BasePermission.has_object_permission` returns `True`, a class implementing only `has_permission` passes the object stage; a class implementing only `has_object_permission` passes the view stage. Composition with `&`, `|`, `~` (DRF 3.9+) builds `OperandHolder` objects that combine both hooks — OR across two permissions where one only implements the object hook has had surprising semantics in past versions, so test composed permissions explicitly.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 330\" width=\"100%\" height=\"330\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj23d\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">When each permission hook runs</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"400\" height=\"270\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">GET /api/invoices/   (list)</text>\n  <rect x=\"50\" y=\"82\" width=\"340\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">initial(): has_permission(request, view)</text>\n  <path d=\"M220,122 L220,144\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"50\" y=\"146\" width=\"340\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">list(): filter_queryset(get_queryset())</text>\n  <path d=\"M220,186 L220,208\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"50\" y=\"210\" width=\"340\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"228\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">has_object_permission NEVER called</text>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"243\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">every row the queryset returns is exposed</text>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fix: get_queryset().filter(tenant=...)</text>\n  <text x=\"220\" y=\"298\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">authorization for lists = queryset scoping</text>\n  <rect x=\"460\" y=\"44\" width=\"400\" height=\"270\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"660\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">PATCH /api/invoices/42/   (detail)</text>\n  <rect x=\"490\" y=\"82\" width=\"340\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"660\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">initial(): has_permission(request, view)</text>\n  <path d=\"M660,122 L660,144\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"490\" y=\"146\" width=\"340\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"660\" y=\"164\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">get_object(): get_object_or_404(scoped qs)</text>\n  <text x=\"660\" y=\"179\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">not in scope: 404, existence not leaked</text>\n  <path d=\"M660,186 L660,208\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"490\" y=\"210\" width=\"340\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"660\" y=\"228\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">check_object_permissions: has_object_permission</text>\n  <text x=\"660\" y=\"243\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">fails: 403 (or 401 if anonymous)</text>\n  <path d=\"M660,250 L660,272\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"490\" y=\"274\" width=\"340\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"660\" y=\"294\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">serializer validate + save</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Throttling internals\n\n`SimpleRateThrottle` parses a rate like `\"100/hour\"` into `(num_requests, duration)`, builds a cache key via `get_cache_key()` (`throttle_user_<pk>` or `throttle_anon_<ident>`), loads the **list of request timestamps** from the default cache, drops those older than the window, and allows the request if fewer than `num_requests` remain — a sliding-window log. It then writes the list back with `cache.set`. Two properties matter in production: it is **not atomic** (concurrent requests read the same list and both pass), and it is only as shared as your cache — with the default `LocMemCache`, every Gunicorn worker has its own counter, so a \"100/hour\" limit becomes roughly 100 × workers × pods. Use Redis ([Ch 29 · Caching](topic.html?p=29-caching)) and treat DRF throttles as fair-use limits, not as a security boundary; brute-force protection and DDoS defence belong at the gateway/WAF too ([REST API · Rate Limiting](../rest-api/topic.html?p=24-rate-limiting-throttling)).\n\nAnonymous identity comes from `get_ident()`: with `NUM_PROXIES` unset it uses the **whole** `X-Forwarded-For` header (if present) as the key, which a client can set to anything — a trivially bypassable limit. Set `NUM_PROXIES` to the number of trusted proxies in front of Django.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### JWT login, use and refresh\n\n```text\nSPA/mobile                    Django + SimpleJWT                       DB\n   │ POST /api/token/ {username,password}\n   │──────────────────────────────▶ TokenObtainPairView\n   │                                 authenticate() → ModelBackend ──────▶ SELECT user; check hash\n   │◀──────── 200 {access (5 min), refresh (1 day)}\n   │ GET /api/orders/  Authorization: Bearer <access>\n   │──────────────────────────────▶ JWTAuthentication: verify sig + exp\n   │                                 get_user(user_id) ────────────────────▶ SELECT user (is_active)\n   │                                 IsAuthenticated ✓ → queryset scoped to user\n   │◀──────── 200 [...]\n   │ ... 5 minutes later: 401 {\"code\": \"token_not_valid\"}\n   │ POST /api/token/refresh/ {refresh}\n   │──────────────────────────────▶ TokenRefreshView (ROTATE_REFRESH_TOKENS)\n   │                                 blacklist old refresh ────────────────▶ INSERT blacklistedtoken\n   │◀──────── 200 {access, refresh(new)}\n   │ POST /api/token/blacklist/ {refresh}   (logout)\n```\n\n### Where each policy sits\n\nAuthentication, permissions and throttles are only three of the layers between a client and your handler; the picture below places them next to the edge and CORS so you can see what each one can and cannot stop.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 250\" width=\"100%\" height=\"250\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj23e\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Layers a request passes before your handler</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"120\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Browser /</text>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">mobile</text>\n  <path d=\"M140,85 L160,85\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23e)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"162\" y=\"50\" width=\"130\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"227\" y=\"74\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Gateway / WAF</text>\n  <text x=\"227\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">global rate limit</text>\n  <text x=\"227\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">TLS, IP blocks</text>\n  <path d=\"M292,85 L312,85\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23e)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"314\" y=\"50\" width=\"130\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"379\" y=\"74\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">CorsMiddleware</text>\n  <text x=\"379\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">preflight OPTIONS</text>\n  <text x=\"379\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Access-Control-*</text>\n  <path d=\"M444,85 L464,85\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23e)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"466\" y=\"50\" width=\"120\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"526\" y=\"74\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Authentication</text>\n  <text x=\"526\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">who are you?</text>\n  <text x=\"526\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">401</text>\n  <path d=\"M586,85 L606,85\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23e)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"608\" y=\"50\" width=\"120\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"668\" y=\"74\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Permissions</text>\n  <text x=\"668\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">may you?</text>\n  <text x=\"668\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">403</text>\n  <path d=\"M728,85 L748,85\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23e)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"750\" y=\"50\" width=\"110\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"805\" y=\"74\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Throttles</text>\n  <text x=\"805\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">how often?</text>\n  <text x=\"805\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">429 Retry-After</text>\n  <rect x=\"466\" y=\"150\" width=\"394\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"663\" y=\"172\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Handler: queryset scoping + get_object()</text>\n  <text x=\"663\" y=\"190\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">has_object_permission on detail routes</text>\n  <text x=\"663\" y=\"206\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">tenant / owner filter on every list</text>\n  <path d=\"M805,120 L805,148\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj23e)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"150\" width=\"424\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"232\" y=\"172\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">CORS is NOT access control</text>\n  <text x=\"232\" y=\"190\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">curl, mobile apps and servers ignore it entirely;</text>\n  <text x=\"232\" y=\"206\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">it only stops other sites' JS from reading responses</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: global defaults that fail closed\n\n```bash\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"djangorestframework>=3.15\" djangorestframework-simplejwt django-cors-headers\n```\n\n```python\n# settings.py\nINSTALLED_APPS += [\n    \"rest_framework\",\n    \"rest_framework_simplejwt.token_blacklist\",\n    \"corsheaders\",\n]\n\nMIDDLEWARE = [\n    \"django.middleware.security.SecurityMiddleware\",\n    \"corsheaders.middleware.CorsMiddleware\",          # as high as possible, before CommonMiddleware\n    \"django.contrib.sessions.middleware.SessionMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.common.CommonMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.csrf.CsrfViewMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.auth.middleware.AuthenticationMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.messages.middleware.MessageMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.clickjacking.XFrameOptionsMiddleware\",\n]\n\nREST_FRAMEWORK = {\n    \"DEFAULT_AUTHENTICATION_CLASSES\": [\n        \"rest_framework_simplejwt.authentication.JWTAuthentication\",   # first → 401 + WWW-Authenticate\n        \"rest_framework.authentication.SessionAuthentication\",         # admin/browsable API\n    ],\n    \"DEFAULT_PERMISSION_CLASSES\": [\n        \"rest_framework.permissions.IsAuthenticated\",                  # fail closed (default is AllowAny!)\n    ],\n    \"DEFAULT_THROTTLE_CLASSES\": [\n        \"rest_framework.throttling.AnonRateThrottle\",\n        \"rest_framework.throttling.UserRateThrottle\",\n    ],\n    \"DEFAULT_THROTTLE_RATES\": {\n        \"anon\": \"60/min\",\n        \"user\": \"1000/hour\",\n        \"login\": \"5/min\",\n        \"exports\": \"10/day\",\n    },\n    \"NUM_PROXIES\": 1,                     # exactly one trusted load balancer in front\n}\n\nfrom datetime import timedelta\n\nSIMPLE_JWT = {\n    \"ACCESS_TOKEN_LIFETIME\": timedelta(minutes=5),\n    \"REFRESH_TOKEN_LIFETIME\": timedelta(days=1),\n    \"ROTATE_REFRESH_TOKENS\": True,\n    \"BLACKLIST_AFTER_ROTATION\": True,\n    \"AUTH_HEADER_TYPES\": (\"Bearer\",),\n    \"SIGNING_KEY\": env(\"JWT_SIGNING_KEY\"),   # separate from SECRET_KEY so you can rotate independently\n}\n\nCORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS = [\"https://app.example.com\"]\nCORS_ALLOW_CREDENTIALS = False             # True only if you send cookies cross-origin\n\nCACHES = {\n    \"default\": {\n        \"BACKEND\": \"django.core.cache.backends.redis.RedisCache\",\n        \"LOCATION\": env(\"REDIS_URL\"),       # shared counters across workers and pods\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```python\n# config/urls.py\nfrom rest_framework_simplejwt.views import (\n    TokenBlacklistView, TokenObtainPairView, TokenRefreshView,\n)\n\nurlpatterns += [\n    path(\"api/token/\", TokenObtainPairView.as_view(), name=\"token_obtain_pair\"),\n    path(\"api/token/refresh/\", TokenRefreshView.as_view(), name=\"token_refresh\"),\n    path(\"api/token/blacklist/\", TokenBlacklistView.as_view(), name=\"token_blacklist\"),\n]\n```\n\n```bash\npython manage.py migrate      # creates token_blacklist tables\ncurl -s -X POST localhost:8000/api/token/ -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\\n     -d '{\"username\":\"ann\",\"password\":\"s3cret!\"}'\n```\n\n```text\n{\"refresh\":\"eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...\",\"access\":\"eyJhbGciOiJIUzI1NiIsInR5cCI6IkpXVCJ9...\"}\n```\n\n### Custom claims (and what not to put in them)\n\n```python\nfrom rest_framework_simplejwt.serializers import TokenObtainPairSerializer\nfrom rest_framework_simplejwt.views import TokenObtainPairView\n\n\nclass ClaimsSerializer(TokenObtainPairSerializer):\n    @classmethod\n    def get_token(cls, user):\n        token = super().get_token(user)\n        token[\"tenant_id\"] = user.tenant_id      # fine: non-secret, stable\n        token[\"role\"] = user.role                # stale until token expires — keep TTL short\n        return token\n\n\nclass LoginView(TokenObtainPairView):\n    serializer_class = ClaimsSerializer\n    throttle_scope = \"login\"\n    throttle_classes = [ScopedRateThrottle]     # brute-force friction, not a full defence\n```\n\nJWT payloads are **base64-encoded, not encrypted** — never put emails you consider private, permissions you cannot afford to be stale, or secrets in them.\n\n### Real-world example: multi-tenant permissions\n\n```python\n# billing/permissions.py\nfrom rest_framework.permissions import SAFE_METHODS, BasePermission\n\n\nclass IsTenantMember(BasePermission):\n    message = \"You are not a member of this workspace.\"\n\n    def has_permission(self, request, view):\n        return bool(request.user and request.user.is_authenticated and request.user.tenant_id)\n\n    def has_object_permission(self, request, view, obj):\n        return obj.tenant_id == request.user.tenant_id\n\n\nclass IsOwnerOrReadOnly(BasePermission):\n    def has_object_permission(self, request, view, obj):\n        if request.method in SAFE_METHODS:\n            return True\n        return obj.created_by_id == request.user.id\n\n\nclass HasScope(BasePermission):\n    \"\"\"Checks a 'scope' claim on the JWT (request.auth is the validated token).\"\"\"\n\n    def has_permission(self, request, view):\n        required = getattr(view, \"required_scope\", None)\n        if required is None:\n            return True\n        token = request.auth\n        scopes = token.get(\"scope\", \"\").split() if token is not None else []\n        return required in scopes\n```\n\n```python\n# billing/views.py\nfrom rest_framework import permissions, viewsets\nfrom rest_framework.decorators import action\nfrom rest_framework.throttling import ScopedRateThrottle\n\nfrom .models import Invoice\nfrom .permissions import IsOwnerOrReadOnly, IsTenantMember\nfrom .serializers import InvoiceSerializer\n\n\nclass InvoiceViewSet(viewsets.ModelViewSet):\n    serializer_class = InvoiceSerializer\n    permission_classes = [IsTenantMember & (IsOwnerOrReadOnly | permissions.IsAdminUser)]\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        # THE authorization for list endpoints: scope rows to the tenant.\n        return (Invoice.objects\n                .filter(tenant_id=self.request.user.tenant_id)\n                .select_related(\"customer\")\n                .order_by(\"-issued_at\"))\n\n    def perform_create(self, serializer):\n        serializer.save(tenant_id=self.request.user.tenant_id, created_by=self.request.user)\n\n    @action(detail=False, methods=[\"get\"])\n    def export(self, request):\n        ...   # expensive CSV build; throttled via get_throttles() below\n```\n\nThe SQL for a cross-tenant detail request shows why scoping matters — the row simply is not found:\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"billing_invoice\"\nINNER JOIN \"billing_customer\" ON (...)\nWHERE (\"billing_invoice\".\"tenant_id\" = 3 AND \"billing_invoice\".\"id\" = 42)\nLIMIT 21;   -- get() fetches up to 21 rows to detect MultipleObjectsReturned → 0 rows → 404\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** `ScopedRateThrottle` reads `view.throttle_scope`. For a ViewSet that is one attribute for all actions; per-action scopes need `get_throttles()` keyed on `self.action`, or a small view for the expensive endpoint.\n\n```python\n    def get_throttles(self):\n        if self.action == \"export\":\n            self.throttle_scope = \"exports\"\n            return [ScopedRateThrottle()]\n        return super().get_throttles()\n```\n\n### `DjangoModelPermissions`\n\n```python\nclass ProductViewSet(viewsets.ModelViewSet):\n    queryset = Product.objects.all()            # required: it reads the model from the queryset\n    serializer_class = ProductSerializer\n    permission_classes = [permissions.DjangoModelPermissions]\n```\n\nIt maps POST → `add_product`, PUT/PATCH → `change_product`, DELETE → `delete_product`; GET/HEAD/OPTIONS need no model permission by default (subclass and extend `perms_map` to require `view_product`). It uses `user.has_perms()`, i.e. Django's group/permission system covered in [Ch 26 · Permissions, Groups & Authorization](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization). `DjangoObjectPermissions` additionally needs an object-permission backend such as django-guardian.\n\n### A custom authentication class (API keys for partners)\n\n```python\nimport hashlib\n\nfrom rest_framework import authentication, exceptions\n\nfrom .models import ApiKey\n\n\nclass ApiKeyAuthentication(authentication.BaseAuthentication):\n    keyword = \"Api-Key\"\n\n    def authenticate(self, request):\n        header = authentication.get_authorization_header(request).decode()\n        if not header.startswith(self.keyword + \" \"):\n            return None                                     # not ours — let others try\n        raw = header[len(self.keyword) + 1:].strip()\n        digest = hashlib.sha256(raw.encode()).hexdigest()   # store only hashes of keys\n        try:\n            key = ApiKey.objects.select_related(\"owner\").get(digest=digest, revoked=False)\n        except ApiKey.DoesNotExist:\n            raise exceptions.AuthenticationFailed(\"Invalid API key.\")\n        if not key.owner.is_active:\n            raise exceptions.AuthenticationFailed(\"User inactive.\")\n        return (key.owner, key)\n\n    def authenticate_header(self, request):\n        return self.keyword                                 # enables 401 responses\n```\n\n### Testing auth\n\n```python\nfrom rest_framework.test import APITestCase\n\n\nclass InvoiceAuthTests(APITestCase):\n    def test_anonymous_gets_401(self):\n        self.assertEqual(self.client.get(\"/api/invoices/\").status_code, 401)\n\n    def test_other_tenant_gets_404(self):\n        inv = InvoiceFactory(tenant=self.tenant_a)\n        self.client.force_authenticate(UserFactory(tenant=self.tenant_b))\n        self.assertEqual(self.client.get(f\"/api/invoices/{inv.pk}/\").status_code, 404)\n\n    def test_jwt_flow(self):\n        resp = self.client.post(\"/api/token/\", {\"username\": \"ann\", \"password\": \"pw\"}, format=\"json\")\n        self.client.credentials(HTTP_AUTHORIZATION=f\"Bearer {resp.data['access']}\")\n        self.assertEqual(self.client.get(\"/api/invoices/\").status_code, 200)\n```\n\n`force_authenticate` skips authenticators entirely — keep at least one test per scheme that goes through real credentials.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Decision | Option A | Option B | Guidance |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Browser SPA, same site | Session + CSRF | JWT in memory + refresh in HttpOnly cookie | Session is simpler and revocable; choose JWT only for cross-site or multi-service needs |\n| Mobile app | DRF Token (no expiry) | JWT with refresh rotation | JWT/expiring tokens (or django-rest-knox) |\n| Server-to-server | API key (hashed) | OAuth2 client credentials | API key for few partners; OAuth2 at scale ([REST API · OAuth2/OIDC](../rest-api/topic.html?p=19-oauth2-oidc)) |\n| Token storage in browser | `localStorage` | `HttpOnly` cookie | localStorage is XSS-readable; cookies need CSRF and SameSite |\n| Authorization of lists | Queryset scoping | Object permission | Always scoping; object permission is for detail routes |\n| Rate limiting | DRF throttles | Gateway / Redis atomic limiter | DRF for per-user fair use; gateway for abuse |\n\n### When to use\n- `IsAuthenticated` as the global default; loosen per view with explicit `AllowAny`.\n- `SessionAuthentication` for first-party web apps on the same registrable domain and for the admin.\n- SimpleJWT with short access tokens and rotating, blacklisted refresh tokens for mobile and cross-domain clients.\n- `ScopedRateThrottle` for expensive or sensitive endpoints (login, exports, OTP).\n\n### When NOT to use\n- Do not use DRF `TokenAuthentication` for public browser clients — tokens never expire and must live in JS-readable storage.\n- Do not rely on `has_object_permission` for list security, or on CORS for any security.\n- Do not use LocMem cache for throttles in a multi-process deployment.\n- Do not embed rapidly changing authorization data in JWT claims.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Leaving the default `AllowAny`.** A new view without `permission_classes` is public. Set `IsAuthenticated` globally, and add a test that enumerates URL patterns and checks anonymous access is denied except for an allowlist.\n\n**2. Expecting object permissions on lists.** Covered above; fix with `get_queryset()` scoping, ideally in a shared base class or a tenant-aware manager.\n\n**3. Overriding `permission_classes` and dropping the default.** `permission_classes = [IsOwner]` *replaces* `IsAuthenticated`; an anonymous user then reaches `has_object_permission` with `AnonymousUser`. Include both: `[IsAuthenticated, IsOwner]`.\n\n**4. Returning `False` from `authenticate()` instead of `None`.** DRF expects a tuple or `None`; anything else breaks unpacking. Raise `AuthenticationFailed` for bad credentials.\n\n**5. `@csrf_exempt` sprinkled on API views to \"fix\" 403s.** APIView is already csrf-exempt; the 403 comes from `SessionAuthentication.enforce_csrf`. Send `X-CSRFToken` from the SPA (read from the `csrftoken` cookie) and configure `CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS` — or use token auth for that client.\n\n**6. `CORS_ALLOW_ALL_ORIGINS = True` with credentials.** Browsers reject `*` with credentials anyway; teams then reflect any origin, which lets any site make authenticated reads. Use an explicit allowlist.\n\n**7. Long-lived access tokens \"to avoid refresh complexity\".** A leaked 30-day access token is a 30-day breach with no revocation. Keep access tokens minutes long.\n\n**8. Throttle key spoofing.** `NUM_PROXIES` unset with a load balancer: clients rotate `X-Forwarded-For` to dodge anon limits. Set it correctly.\n\n**Best practices:** fail closed globally; one authorization helper for tenant scoping; hash stored API keys; separate JWT signing key; logout = blacklist refresh + delete client copy; log authentication failures with user/IP for alerting; test 401/403/404 matrices per endpoint.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: throttles that don't throttle.** Symptom: a scraper does 5,000 req/hour against a \"1000/hour\" limit. Root cause: `LocMemCache` default — 8 workers × 4 pods = 32 independent counters, plus non-atomic read-modify-write. Fix: Redis cache, gateway limit for hard caps; for exact limits, a Redis `INCR`+`EXPIRE` or Lua-based throttle ([Redis · Sorted Sets & Rate Limiting](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=19-sorted-sets-rate-limiting)).\n\n**Pitfall: every request hits the DB for the user.** `JWTAuthentication` loads the user per request; at high RPS that is a hot query. Usually fine (PK lookup), but if it matters use `JWTStatelessUserAuthentication` (a `TokenUser` built from claims, no DB) for read-only services, accepting that deactivation takes effect only at token expiry.\n\n**Pitfall: \"logged out\" user still has access.** Blacklisting the refresh token does not kill an issued access token. Expected behaviour; keep the access TTL short, or keep a small Redis denylist of `jti`s checked by a custom authenticator for high-risk cases.\n\n**Pitfall: rotating `SECRET_KEY` logs everyone out and invalidates JWTs.** Since SimpleJWT signs with `SECRET_KEY` by default, a rotation invalidates all tokens and sessions. Use a dedicated `SIGNING_KEY`, and for `SECRET_KEY` use `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS` (Django 4.1+) during rotation.\n\n**Pitfall: CSRF failures behind a proxy.** Symptom: SPA POSTs fail with \"CSRF Failed: Origin checking failed\". Root cause: `CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS` missing the scheme-qualified SPA origin, or `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` not set so Django thinks the request is HTTP. See [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security).\n\n**Debugging.**\n- `request.successful_authenticator` tells you which class authenticated the request — log it.\n- Decode a JWT locally (`jwt.io` or `python -c \"import jwt; print(jwt.decode(t, options={'verify_signature': False}))\"`) to inspect `exp`, `token_type`, `user_id`.\n- Inspect throttle state: `cache.get(\"throttle_user_42\")` returns the timestamp list.\n- `curl -i` to see `WWW-Authenticate`, `Retry-After` and CORS headers; browser devtools for preflight responses.\n\n**Scaling.** Stateless JWT verification scales without a session store; sessions scale fine on Redis (`cached_db` or `cache` engine). Authorization cost is dominated by queryset scoping — index `tenant_id` (often composite with the ordering column). Move abuse-oriented rate limiting to the edge so Django workers are not spent rejecting traffic.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between authentication and permissions in DRF?**\nA: Authentication identifies the caller: authentication classes inspect the request and set `request.user` and `request.auth`, or leave the request anonymous. Permissions authorise: permission classes look at the user, the view and possibly the object and allow or deny. They run in that order in `APIView.initial()`, followed by throttles. A request with no credentials is not rejected by authentication — it is the `IsAuthenticated` permission that rejects it.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `has_permission` and `has_object_permission`?**\nA: `has_permission(request, view)` runs for every request in `initial()`, before the handler. `has_object_permission(request, view, obj)` runs only when `check_object_permissions()` is called — which `GenericAPIView.get_object()` does for retrieve, update, destroy and detail actions. It never runs on list or create. Both default to `True` in `BasePermission`, and all permission classes must pass.\n\n**Q: Why doesn't my object-level permission protect the list endpoint, and how do you fix it?**\nA: Because list views never call `get_object()`, so `has_object_permission` is never invoked; every row of the queryset is serialised. The fix is to filter `get_queryset()` by the user/tenant so unauthorised rows are not in the result at all. That filtering also makes detail lookups of foreign objects return 404, which avoids leaking existence.\n\n**Q: Compare session, token and JWT authentication. When would you use each?**\nA: Session auth stores state server-side and uses an `HttpOnly` cookie; it is revocable and simple but needs CSRF protection and works best same-site. DRF's TokenAuthentication is an opaque DB-backed key with no expiry — fine for internal or server clients. JWT is a signed, self-contained token verified without a session store; it suits mobile and cross-domain clients and multi-service setups, but cannot easily be revoked, so access tokens must be short-lived with refresh tokens rotated and blacklisted. JWT is about statelessness, not extra security.\n\n**Q: How does SimpleJWT's refresh flow work, and what does blacklisting do?**\nA: Login returns an access token (default 5 minutes) and a refresh token (default 1 day). When access expires the client posts the refresh token to the refresh endpoint and gets a new access token; with `ROTATE_REFRESH_TOKENS` it also gets a new refresh token. With `BLACKLIST_AFTER_ROTATION` and the `token_blacklist` app installed, the old refresh token is recorded as blacklisted so it cannot be reused; logout blacklists the current refresh token. Access tokens are not blacklisted — they live until `exp`.\n\n**Q: Why does DRF return 403 instead of 401 for an unauthenticated request?**\nA: A 401 must carry `WWW-Authenticate`. DRF uses the first authentication class's `authenticate_header()`; `SessionAuthentication` doesn't provide one, so the response becomes 403. Put `JWTAuthentication` or `TokenAuthentication` first to get 401, which SPAs typically use to trigger a token refresh.\n\n**Q: Does DRF enforce CSRF?**\nA: `APIView.as_view()` is `csrf_exempt`, so Django's middleware does not check DRF views. `SessionAuthentication` re-applies Django's CSRF check itself, but only when the request was authenticated by session — so cookie-authenticated unsafe requests need the `X-CSRFToken` header. Token and JWT auth in headers need no CSRF protection because browsers do not attach them automatically.\n\n**Q: How does DRF throttling work and what are its limitations?**\nA: Throttle classes run after permissions. `SimpleRateThrottle` keeps a list of request timestamps per key (user id, IP, or scope + id) in Django's cache and allows the request if fewer than N fall within the window; otherwise it raises `Throttled` (429, `Retry-After`). It is non-atomic and only as shared as the cache — with LocMem each worker counts separately — and anonymous keys depend on `X-Forwarded-For` handling via `NUM_PROXIES`. Treat it as fair-use limiting and enforce hard limits at the gateway.\n\n**Q: What is CORS and is it a security mechanism for your API?**\nA: CORS is a browser mechanism: the server's `Access-Control-Allow-*` headers tell a browser whether JavaScript from another origin may read a response (and whether non-simple requests may be sent after a preflight). It protects users' browsers from other sites reading their authenticated data, but it does nothing against curl, scripts or mobile apps. Your API's protection is authentication and authorization. In Django use `django-cors-headers` with an explicit `CORS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS`.\n\n**Q: Design authentication for a Django API serving a web SPA, a mobile app and partner integrations. (Senior)**\nA: For the web SPA on the same site, session auth with CSRF (or, if cross-site, JWT access token in memory plus refresh token in an `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite` cookie used only on the refresh endpoint). For mobile, SimpleJWT with short access TTL, refresh rotation and blacklisting, plus device-bound refresh token records so a user can revoke a device. For partners, hashed API keys with scopes and per-key throttles, or OAuth2 client credentials if partners are many. Order authenticators so the header-based ones come first (proper 401s), set `IsAuthenticated` globally, scope querysets by tenant, use a dedicated JWT signing key with a rotation plan, and log/alert on authentication failures and unusual token refresh rates.\n\n**Q: Your rate limit of 100/min is being exceeded by some clients by 10x. Diagnose. (Senior)**\nA: First check the cache backend — LocMem gives each worker its own counters, so effective limit = limit × workers × pods. Then check the key: for anonymous traffic `get_ident()` uses `X-Forwarded-For`; with `NUM_PROXIES` unset clients can spoof it. Then concurrency: the throttle reads and writes the timestamp list non-atomically, so bursts slip through. Fixes: Redis-backed cache, correct `NUM_PROXIES`, an atomic Redis counter throttle for strict endpoints, and a gateway limit as the outer bound. Verify by logging the throttle key and inspecting the cache entry.\n\n**Q: How do you revoke access immediately for a compromised user when you use JWTs? (Senior)**\nA: Deactivate the user (`is_active=False`): SimpleJWT's default authenticator loads the user per request and rejects inactive users, so access stops immediately — that's a reason not to use stateless user auth for user-facing APIs. Blacklist all their outstanding refresh tokens (`OutstandingToken` → `BlacklistedToken`). If you must keep the user active but kill sessions, add a `token_version` on the user, include it as a claim, and reject tokens whose version is stale in a custom authenticator — trading one cache/DB read for revocability. Also shorten access TTLs and rotate the signing key in a true key-compromise scenario.\n\n**Q: How would you implement role-based plus object-based permissions cleanly in DRF? (Senior)**\nA: Keep three layers: coarse role checks in `has_permission` (e.g. `HasRole(\"billing_admin\")`), row visibility in `get_queryset()` (tenant/team scoping), and fine object rules in `has_object_permission` (owner, status-based rules like \"cannot edit a paid invoice\"). Compose with `&`/`|` and test the composition. For per-object ACLs use Django's permission framework with django-guardian or `rules` predicates so the logic is reusable in admin and templates too ([Ch 26](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization)). Put rules in one module, not inlined in views, and drive tests from a permission matrix.\n\n**Q: What does `request.auth` contain?**\nA: The second element of the tuple returned by the successful authenticator: the `Token` instance for TokenAuthentication, the validated `AccessToken` for SimpleJWT, `None` for session auth, or whatever a custom authenticator returns (e.g. an ApiKey row). Use it to read scopes, claims or key metadata in permissions.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Item | Value / rule |\n|---|---|\n| Default auth classes | `SessionAuthentication`, `BasicAuthentication` |\n| Default permission | **`AllowAny`** — change it to `IsAuthenticated` |\n| Order in `initial()` | authenticate → permissions → throttles |\n| Auth result | tuple wins; `None` = try next; raise = stop |\n| 401 vs 403 | first authenticator's `authenticate_header()` |\n| Object permission | only via `get_object()`; never on list |\n| `SAFE_METHODS` | GET, HEAD, OPTIONS |\n| SimpleJWT defaults | access 5 min, refresh 1 day, HS256, `Bearer` |\n| Blacklist | refresh tokens only; needs `token_blacklist` app |\n| Throttles | cache-backed timestamp lists; use Redis; set `NUM_PROXIES` |\n| CORS | `CorsMiddleware` high in the stack; allowlist origins |\n\n- Scope every queryset by user/tenant — that is list authorization.\n- Overriding `permission_classes` replaces the defaults; restate `IsAuthenticated`.\n- SessionAuthentication enforces CSRF only for session-authenticated requests.\n- JWT is stateless, not magic; keep access tokens short.\n- Never put secrets or fast-changing permissions in JWT claims.\n- Hash API keys at rest.\n- CORS protects browsers, not your API.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"djangorestframework>=3.15\" djangorestframework-simplejwt django-cors-headers redis\n```\n\n1. **Authenticator order.** Configure `[SessionAuthentication, JWTAuthentication]`, call an endpoint anonymously and note 403; swap the order and get 401. Send an expired JWT with a valid session cookie and observe the chain stopping.\n2. **List leak.** Build an `InvoiceViewSet` with only `IsOwner.has_object_permission`; show that `/invoices/` lists everyone's invoices; fix with `get_queryset()` and write tests.\n3. **JWT lifecycle.** Obtain, use, let expire (set access lifetime to 10 s), refresh with rotation, reuse the old refresh token (expect 401), and blacklist on logout.\n4. **Throttle accuracy.** Run Gunicorn with 4 workers and LocMem cache; hammer an endpoint limited to `10/min` with `hey` or a loop and count 200s. Switch to Redis and repeat.\n5. **API key auth.** Implement the `ApiKeyAuthentication` above with hashed keys and a `revoked` flag; add a management command to issue keys.\n\n**Mini project — \"Multi-tenant Billing API\".** Tenants, users with roles, invoices. Requirements: JWT with tenant claim and rotation; session auth for the admin; global `IsAuthenticated`; tenant scoping in a base viewset; owner/role object permissions with a test matrix; scoped throttles for login and export; CORS allowlist; a test that asserts every registered route denies anonymous access.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers) · [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user) · [Ch 26 · Permissions, Groups & Authorization](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization) · [Ch 27 · Sessions, Cookies & Messages](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies) · [Ch 28 · Security: CSRF, XSS, SQL Injection & Hardening](topic.html?p=28-security) · [Ch 29 · Caching in Django](topic.html?p=29-caching) · [Ch 38 · Interview: DRF, Security & Scale](topic.html?p=38-interview-drf-advanced)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · Authentication Basics](../rest-api/topic.html?p=18-authentication-basics) · [REST API · OAuth 2.0 & OIDC](../rest-api/topic.html?p=19-oauth2-oidc) · [REST API · JWT](../rest-api/topic.html?p=20-jwt) · [REST API · Authorization: RBAC & ABAC](../rest-api/topic.html?p=21-authorization-rbac-abac) · [REST API · TLS, CORS & Security Headers](../rest-api/topic.html?p=22-tls-cors-security-headers) · [REST API · Rate Limiting](../rest-api/topic.html?p=24-rate-limiting-throttling)\n\n- **Authentication (API Guide)** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · every built-in scheme and how to write your own. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/authentication/>\n- **Permissions (API Guide)** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · view vs object permissions, composition, custom classes. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/permissions/>\n- **Throttling (API Guide)** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · rates, scopes, client identification and caveats. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/throttling/>\n- **Simple JWT documentation** — jazzband · *Intermediate* · settings, rotation, blacklist app, custom claims. <https://django-rest-framework-simplejwt.readthedocs.io/>\n- **django-cors-headers** — adamchainz on GitHub · *Beginner* · configuration and middleware placement. <https://github.com/adamchainz/django-cors-headers>\n- **OWASP API Security Top 10** — OWASP · *Intermediate* · BOLA, broken auth and resource consumption explained. <https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/>\n- **Django CSRF protection** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · how the token and Origin checks work, AJAX usage. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/csrf/>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 23.*\n", "24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning": "# 24 · DRF Pagination, Filtering, Versioning & Docs\n\n> **In one line:** A list endpoint is a query you let strangers write — pagination bounds how much it returns (and cursor pagination keeps it fast and stable at depth), filter backends decide which `WHERE` and `ORDER BY` clauses clients may add, versioning lets the contract evolve without breaking old clients, and an OpenAPI schema makes all of it discoverable.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 21 · DRF Serializers](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers), [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers), [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization), [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres).\n\nThe first version of every list endpoint is `return Response(Serializer(Model.objects.all(), many=True).data)`. It works in development with 40 rows. In production the table has four million rows, a mobile client calls the endpoint on launch, and the worker serialises the whole table into memory until Gunicorn kills it at the 30-second timeout. That is the first lesson: **every list endpoint must be bounded**.\n\nThen the clients want \"only my open orders\", \"sorted by price\", \"search by name\". The naive implementation reads `request.GET` by hand: `qs.filter(**request.GET.dict())`. That is an injection of a different kind — a client can send `?owner__password__startswith=pbkdf2` and binary-search a hash, or `?ordering=some_unindexed_column` and force a sequential scan with a sort on four million rows. The second lesson: **clients choose from a whitelist of filters and orderings, and every whitelisted option needs an index**.\n\nThen the mobile app ships v1 and cannot be force-upgraded, while the web team wants to rename `price` to `price_cents`. The third lesson: **the response shape is a contract**, and you need a versioning scheme to change it. And for partners to integrate at all you need the fourth piece: an **OpenAPI schema** generated from the same code, so documentation cannot drift.\n\nDRF covers all four with pluggable classes: **pagination classes** (`PageNumberPagination`, `LimitOffsetPagination`, `CursorPagination`), **filter backends** (django-filter's `DjangoFilterBackend`, `SearchFilter`, `OrderingFilter`), **versioning classes** (`URLPathVersioning`, `AcceptHeaderVersioning`, ...), and schema generation (in practice, **drf-spectacular**). The HTTP-level design of each is covered in the REST API handbook ([Pagination](../rest-api/topic.html?p=13-pagination), [Filtering, Sorting & Searching](../rest-api/topic.html?p=12-filtering-sorting-searching), [Versioning](../rest-api/topic.html?p=31-api-versioning)); this chapter is about how DRF implements them and what SQL they generate.\n\n> **Why this matters:** \"Offset vs cursor pagination — which and why?\" is a near-universal backend interview question, and \"why is page 5,000 slow?\" is a near-universal production incident.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **`DEFAULT_PAGINATION_CLASS` + `PAGE_SIZE`** — global defaults; both must be set for pagination to apply. *Why:* pagination is off unless configured, and applies only to generic list actions (or where you call `paginate_queryset` yourself).\n- **`PageNumberPagination`** — `?page=3`; response `{count, next, previous, results}`; uses Django's `Paginator` (a `COUNT(*)` + `LIMIT/OFFSET`). *Why:* human-friendly, jumpable, expensive at depth.\n- **`LimitOffsetPagination`** — `?limit=50&offset=200`; same SQL shape, client controls the window. *Why:* flexible; same deep-offset cost.\n- **`CursorPagination`** — `?cursor=<opaque>`; keyset pagination on an ordering; no `count`, no page jumping. *Why:* constant cost per page and stable results while rows are inserted.\n- **`max_page_size` / `max_limit`** — server caps on client-chosen sizes. *Why:* without them `?page_size=1000000` is a DoS.\n- **Filter backend** — a class with `filter_queryset(request, queryset, view)`; listed in `filter_backends`. *Why:* applied in order by `GenericAPIView.filter_queryset()` for list *and* detail.\n- **`DjangoFilterBackend` / `FilterSet`** — django-filter; `filterset_fields` or a `FilterSet` class with typed, validated filters. *Why:* explicit whitelist, invalid input → 400.\n- **`SearchFilter`** — `?search=term` across `search_fields`; `icontains` by default, prefixes `^` (startswith), `=` (exact), `@` (PostgreSQL full-text), `$` (regex). *Why:* convenient, but `icontains` defeats B-tree indexes.\n- **`OrderingFilter`** — `?ordering=-price,name` restricted to `ordering_fields`; `ordering` sets the default. *Why:* every allowed ordering is a potential sort; index the hot ones.\n- **Versioning class** — determines `request.version` in `initial()`: `URLPathVersioning`, `NamespaceVersioning`, `AcceptHeaderVersioning`, `HostNameVersioning`, `QueryParameterVersioning`. *Why:* lets views branch on version.\n- **`ALLOWED_VERSIONS` / `DEFAULT_VERSION`** — validation of requested versions. *Why:* unknown versions → 404/406 instead of silent defaults.\n- **OpenAPI schema** — machine-readable contract. *Why:* client generation, docs, contract tests.\n- **drf-spectacular** — OpenAPI 3 generator for DRF; `@extend_schema` to annotate. *Why:* the de-facto standard; DRF's own schema generation is minimal and drf-yasg targets OpenAPI 2.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### Where these hooks live in `list()`\n\n`ListModelMixin.list()` is short, and every concept in this chapter is a line of it:\n\n```python\ndef list(self, request, *args, **kwargs):\n    queryset = self.filter_queryset(self.get_queryset())   # filter backends, in order\n    page = self.paginate_queryset(queryset)                 # pagination class, or None\n    if page is not None:\n        serializer = self.get_serializer(page, many=True)\n        return self.get_paginated_response(serializer.data) # envelope with next/previous\n    serializer = self.get_serializer(queryset, many=True)\n    return Response(serializer.data)\n```\n\n`filter_queryset()` loops `for backend in list(self.filter_backends): queryset = backend().filter_queryset(self.request, queryset, self)`. Note that **`get_object()` also calls `filter_queryset()`**, so a filter like \"status=published\" applied by a backend also restricts detail lookups — usually what you want, occasionally a surprise. `paginate_queryset()` returns `None` if `pagination_class` is `None`, which is why unpaginated endpoints silently return everything. Versioning happens earlier, in `initial()` (`determine_version`), so `request.version` is available to `get_queryset()` and `get_serializer_class()`.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 300\" width=\"100%\" height=\"300\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj24a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">GET /api/v2/products/?category=3&amp;search=lamp&amp;ordering=-price&amp;cursor=cD0xOTk</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"150\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">initial()</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">determine_version</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">request.version = \"v2\"</text>\n  <path d=\"M170,76 L196,76\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj24a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"198\" y=\"44\" width=\"130\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"263\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">get_queryset()</text>\n  <text x=\"263\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">tenant scope +</text>\n  <text x=\"263\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">select_related</text>\n  <path d=\"M328,76 L354,76\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj24a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"356\" y=\"36\" width=\"330\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"521\" y=\"54\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">filter_queryset(): backends in order</text>\n  <rect x=\"366\" y=\"64\" width=\"100\" height=\"42\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"416\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">DjangoFilter</text>\n  <text x=\"416\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">category_id = 3</text>\n  <rect x=\"472\" y=\"64\" width=\"100\" height=\"42\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"522\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">SearchFilter</text>\n  <text x=\"522\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">name ILIKE %lamp%</text>\n  <rect x=\"578\" y=\"64\" width=\"100\" height=\"42\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"628\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">OrderingFilter</text>\n  <text x=\"628\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">ORDER BY price DESC</text>\n  <path d=\"M686,76 L712,76\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj24a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"714\" y=\"44\" width=\"146\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"787\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">paginate_queryset()</text>\n  <text x=\"787\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">decode cursor</text>\n  <text x=\"787\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">WHERE + LIMIT n+1</text>\n  <path d=\"M787,108 L787,148\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj24a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"456\" y=\"150\" width=\"404\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"658\" y=\"172\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">get_serializer(page, many=True) then get_paginated_response</text>\n  <text x=\"658\" y=\"190\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">{\"next\": \"...?cursor=...\", \"previous\": ..., \"results\": [...]}</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"150\" width=\"410\" height=\"128\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">One SQL statement is built from all of it</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"192\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">WHERE tenant_id = 9 AND category_id = 3</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"208\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">AND name ILIKE '%lamp%'</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"224\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">AND (price, id) &lt; (cursor position)</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">ORDER BY price DESC, id DESC LIMIT 21</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"262\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">every allowed combination needs a plan you have checked</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Why OFFSET gets slow, and why COUNT is expensive\n\n`PageNumberPagination` builds a Django `Paginator(queryset, page_size)`. `paginator.count` runs `queryset.count()` → `SELECT COUNT(*) ...`, and `paginator.page(n)` slices the queryset → `LIMIT page_size OFFSET (n-1)*page_size`. PostgreSQL implements `OFFSET k` by producing and **discarding** k rows, so page 5,000 of 20 means reading 100,000 rows to return 20. `COUNT(*)` on PostgreSQL must visit every visible row (MVCC — no cached row count; see [Database Design · MVCC](../database-design/topic.html?p=03-mvcc)), so on large filtered tables the count can cost more than the page itself. `LimitOffsetPagination` has the same two queries.\n\nThere is also a correctness problem: if rows are inserted at the head of the ordering between two page requests, every row shifts down and the client sees a duplicate on the next page; deletes cause skipped rows.\n\n### How `CursorPagination` works\n\n`CursorPagination` implements **keyset** (seek) pagination. It requires an `ordering` (default `\"-created\"`, which fails if your model has no `created` field — always set it). On a page request it decodes the cursor — a base64-encoded querystring holding `o` (offset), `r` (reverse) and `p` (position: the ordering field value of the boundary row) — and filters `WHERE ordering_field < position` (or `>` depending on direction), then `LIMIT page_size + 1` to know whether a next page exists. The cost is an index range scan from the position, independent of depth, and inserts at the head do not shift later pages.\n\nRequirements and limits: the ordering should be on a **unique, immutable** field (or the first field should be mostly unique — DRF's cursor stores the position of the *first* ordering field and uses the small offset `o` to step past ties, which degrades if many rows share a value); you cannot jump to page N; there is no total count; and clients cannot choose arbitrary orderings unless you also allow them via `OrderingFilter` (each allowed ordering then needs an index). Cursors are opaque but not encrypted — they are not a security boundary.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 320\" width=\"100%\" height=\"320\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Page 5,000 of 20 rows: OFFSET vs cursor (keyset)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"410\" height=\"262\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef2f2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#b91c1c\" font-weight=\"bold\">PageNumber / LimitOffset</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20 OFFSET 99980</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"96\" width=\"370\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"113\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">scan + discard 99,980 index entries / rows</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"126\" width=\"370\" height=\"16\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"138\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"9\">return 20</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">+ SELECT COUNT(*) over the whole filtered set</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"190\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">cost grows linearly with page depth</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"214\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">insert at head between requests:</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">rows shift: duplicate or skipped items</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">but: total count, jump to any page</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"278\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">good for admin tables and small sets</text>\n  <rect x=\"450\" y=\"40\" width=\"410\" height=\"262\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f0fdf4\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15803d\" font-weight=\"bold\">CursorPagination</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">WHERE id &lt; 400021 ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 21</text>\n  <rect x=\"470\" y=\"96\" width=\"60\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"500\" y=\"113\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">skipped</text>\n  <text x=\"560\" y=\"113\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">seek</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"96\" width=\"250\" height=\"26\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"715\" y=\"113\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"10\">index range scan: read 21 entries</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">no COUNT query</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"174\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">cost constant at any depth</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"198\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">insert at head: later pages unaffected</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"10\">needs unique, immutable ordering + index</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"254\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"10\">no page numbers, no total count</text>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"278\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#15803d\" font-size=\"10\">good for feeds, infinite scroll, sync, exports</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Filtering: whitelists and types\n\ndjango-filter's `DjangoFilterBackend` builds a `FilterSet` (from `filterset_fields` or your `filterset_class`), binds it to `request.query_params` and the queryset, and if the filterset form is invalid (e.g. `?price__gte=abc`) raises a `ValidationError` → **400**. Only declared filters are honoured; unknown params are ignored. That whitelist is the defence against lookup injection.\n\n`SearchFilter` splits the `search` param into terms; for each term it ORs `icontains` lookups across `search_fields` and ANDs across terms. When a search field traverses a to-many relation (`tags__name`), DRF checks whether the lookup can produce duplicate rows and applies `.distinct()` when needed. The SQL is `UPPER(col) LIKE UPPER('%term%')` — a leading wildcard that cannot use a normal B-tree index. For real search, use `@` (PostgreSQL full-text, requires `django.contrib.postgres`), a trigram GIN index, or an external engine ([Ch 16](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres)).\n\n`OrderingFilter` reads `?ordering=` (comma-separated, `-` for descending), keeps only terms in `ordering_fields` (if `ordering_fields` is not set it falls back to the serializer's readable fields — set it explicitly; `\"__all__\"` allows any model field), and applies `order_by()`; otherwise it applies the view's `ordering`.\n\n### Versioning\n\nIn `initial()`, `determine_version()` instantiates `versioning_class` and calls `determine_version(request, *args, **kwargs)`, storing `request.version` and `request.versioning_scheme`. `URLPathVersioning` reads a URL kwarg (`<version>` in the pattern); `NamespaceVersioning` uses the URL namespace; `AcceptHeaderVersioning` reads `Accept: application/json; version=2`; `QueryParameterVersioning` reads `?version=2`; `HostNameVersioning` reads `v2.api.example.com`. With `ALLOWED_VERSIONS` set, an unknown version raises `NotFound` (path, namespace, hostname) or `NotAcceptable` (Accept header). The versioning scheme also participates in `reverse()`: `rest_framework.reverse.reverse(\"book-detail\", args=[1], request=request)` produces a URL for the same version, so hyperlinks stay within the client's version.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### Choosing a pagination class\n\n```text\nIs the dataset small and bounded (< ~10k rows after filtering)?\n ├─ yes → Do humans need \"page 7 of 23\"?  yes → PageNumberPagination (+ max_page_size)\n │                                         no  → LimitOffsetPagination (tables with resize)\n └─ no  → Is it a feed / infinite scroll / sync / export?  → CursorPagination on (created, id)\n          Do you still need a total?  → approximate count (pg_class.reltuples / cached count)\n                                         or \"has_more\" only\n```\n\n### Versioning a breaking change\n\n```text\nv1 client  GET /api/v1/products/7/  → request.version = \"v1\" → ProductV1Serializer {\"price\": \"19.99\"}\nv2 client  GET /api/v2/products/7/  → request.version = \"v2\" → ProductV2Serializer {\"price_cents\": 1999}\n                                        same view, same queryset, serializer chosen by version\nDeprecation: v1 responses carry Deprecation + Sunset headers; usage of v1 tracked in logs per client id\n```\n\n### Schema pipeline\n\n```text\nviews + serializers + filters + pagination  ──▶  drf-spectacular AutoSchema\n   @extend_schema / @extend_schema_field         │\n                                                 ▼\n                         manage.py spectacular --file schema.yml   (CI artifact, diffed per PR)\n                                                 │\n                    ┌────────────────────────────┼────────────────────────────┐\n                    ▼                            ▼                            ▼\n             Swagger UI / Redoc         client SDK generation          contract tests\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: global settings\n\n```bash\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"djangorestframework>=3.15\" django-filter drf-spectacular\n```\n\n```python\n# settings.py\nINSTALLED_APPS += [\"rest_framework\", \"django_filters\", \"drf_spectacular\"]\n\nREST_FRAMEWORK = {\n    \"DEFAULT_PAGINATION_CLASS\": \"config.pagination.DefaultPagination\",\n    \"PAGE_SIZE\": 20,\n    \"DEFAULT_FILTER_BACKENDS\": [\n        \"django_filters.rest_framework.DjangoFilterBackend\",\n        \"rest_framework.filters.SearchFilter\",\n        \"rest_framework.filters.OrderingFilter\",\n    ],\n    \"DEFAULT_VERSIONING_CLASS\": \"rest_framework.versioning.URLPathVersioning\",\n    \"DEFAULT_VERSION\": \"v1\",\n    \"ALLOWED_VERSIONS\": [\"v1\", \"v2\"],\n    \"DEFAULT_SCHEMA_CLASS\": \"drf_spectacular.openapi.AutoSchema\",\n}\n\nSPECTACULAR_SETTINGS = {\n    \"TITLE\": \"Shop API\",\n    \"VERSION\": \"2.0.0\",\n    \"SERVE_INCLUDE_SCHEMA\": False,\n}\n```\n\n```python\n# config/pagination.py\nfrom rest_framework.pagination import CursorPagination, PageNumberPagination\n\n\nclass DefaultPagination(PageNumberPagination):\n    page_size = 20\n    page_size_query_param = \"page_size\"   # opt-in: off by default in DRF\n    max_page_size = 100                   # never let clients ask for a million rows\n\n\nclass FeedCursorPagination(CursorPagination):\n    page_size = 25\n    ordering = (\"-created_at\", \"-id\")     # newest first; id breaks ties\n    page_size_query_param = \"page_size\"\n    max_page_size = 100\n```\n\n```text\nGET /api/v1/products/?page=2\n{\n  \"count\": 1342,\n  \"next\": \"https://api.example.com/api/v1/products/?page=3\",\n  \"previous\": \"https://api.example.com/api/v1/products/?page=1\",\n  \"results\": [ {...}, ... ]\n}\n```\n\n### Real-world example: a product catalogue\n\n```python\n# catalog/models.py\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.contrib.postgres.indexes import GinIndex\n\n\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    tenant = models.ForeignKey(\"tenants.Tenant\", on_delete=models.CASCADE)\n    category = models.ForeignKey(\"Category\", on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"products\")\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    sku = models.CharField(max_length=40)\n    price_cents = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n    in_stock = models.BooleanField(default=True)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True, db_index=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"tenant\", \"sku\"], name=\"uniq_tenant_sku\")]\n        indexes = [\n            models.Index(fields=[\"tenant\", \"-created_at\", \"-id\"], name=\"prod_tenant_feed\"),\n            models.Index(fields=[\"tenant\", \"category\", \"price_cents\"], name=\"prod_tenant_cat_price\"),\n            GinIndex(fields=[\"name\"], name=\"prod_name_trgm\", opclasses=[\"gin_trgm_ops\"]),  # needs pg_trgm\n        ]\n```\n\n```python\n# catalog/filters.py\nimport django_filters\n\nfrom .models import Product\n\n\nclass ProductFilter(django_filters.FilterSet):\n    min_price = django_filters.NumberFilter(field_name=\"price_cents\", lookup_expr=\"gte\")\n    max_price = django_filters.NumberFilter(field_name=\"price_cents\", lookup_expr=\"lte\")\n    category = django_filters.NumberFilter(field_name=\"category_id\")\n    created_after = django_filters.IsoDateTimeFilter(field_name=\"created_at\", lookup_expr=\"gte\")\n\n    class Meta:\n        model = Product\n        fields = [\"in_stock\"]          # exact match; plus the declared filters above\n```\n\n```python\n# catalog/views.py\nfrom django_filters.rest_framework import DjangoFilterBackend\nfrom drf_spectacular.types import OpenApiTypes\nfrom drf_spectacular.utils import OpenApiParameter, extend_schema, extend_schema_view\nfrom rest_framework import filters, viewsets\n\nfrom config.pagination import FeedCursorPagination\nfrom .filters import ProductFilter\nfrom .models import Product\nfrom .serializers import ProductV1Serializer, ProductV2Serializer\n\n\n@extend_schema_view(\n    list=extend_schema(\n        summary=\"List products\",\n        parameters=[OpenApiParameter(\"search\", OpenApiTypes.STR, description=\"Name or SKU contains\")],\n    ),\n)\nclass ProductViewSet(viewsets.ReadOnlyModelViewSet):\n    filter_backends = [DjangoFilterBackend, filters.SearchFilter, filters.OrderingFilter]\n    filterset_class = ProductFilter\n    search_fields = [\"name\", \"=sku\"]                 # icontains on name, exact on sku\n    ordering_fields = [\"price_cents\", \"created_at\"]  # explicit whitelist, both indexed\n    ordering = [\"-created_at\", \"-id\"]\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return (Product.objects\n                .filter(tenant=self.request.user.tenant)\n                .select_related(\"category\"))\n\n    def get_serializer_class(self):\n        return ProductV2Serializer if self.request.version == \"v2\" else ProductV1Serializer\n\n    @property\n    def paginator(self):\n        # Feed-style cursor pagination unless the client asked for a custom ordering.\n        if not hasattr(self, \"_paginator\"):\n            from config.pagination import DefaultPagination\n            use_cursor = \"ordering\" not in self.request.query_params\n            self._paginator = FeedCursorPagination() if use_cursor else DefaultPagination()\n        return self._paginator\n```\n\n```python\n# catalog/serializers.py\nclass ProductV1Serializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    price = serializers.SerializerMethodField()\n\n    class Meta:\n        model = Product\n        fields = [\"id\", \"name\", \"sku\", \"price\", \"in_stock\", \"category\"]\n\n    @extend_schema_field(OpenApiTypes.STR)\n    def get_price(self, obj):\n        return f\"{obj.price_cents / 100:.2f}\"\n\n\nclass ProductV2Serializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    class Meta:\n        model = Product\n        fields = [\"id\", \"name\", \"sku\", \"price_cents\", \"in_stock\", \"category\", \"created_at\"]\n```\n\n```python\n# config/urls.py\nfrom drf_spectacular.views import SpectacularAPIView, SpectacularRedocView, SpectacularSwaggerView\nfrom rest_framework.routers import DefaultRouter\n\nrouter = DefaultRouter()\nrouter.register(r\"products\", ProductViewSet, basename=\"product\")\n\nurlpatterns = [\n    path(\"api/<str:version>/\", include(router.urls)),          # URLPathVersioning reads `version`\n    path(\"api/schema/\", SpectacularAPIView.as_view(), name=\"schema\"),\n    path(\"api/docs/\", SpectacularSwaggerView.as_view(url_name=\"schema\"), name=\"swagger-ui\"),\n    path(\"api/redoc/\", SpectacularRedocView.as_view(url_name=\"schema\"), name=\"redoc\"),\n]\n```\n\nNow the generated SQL for `GET /api/v2/products/?category=3&min_price=1000&cursor=...`:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"catalog_product\".*, \"catalog_category\".*\nFROM \"catalog_product\"\nINNER JOIN \"catalog_category\" ON (\"catalog_product\".\"category_id\" = \"catalog_category\".\"id\")\nWHERE \"catalog_product\".\"tenant_id\" = 9\n  AND \"catalog_product\".\"category_id\" = 3\n  AND \"catalog_product\".\"price_cents\" >= 1000\n  AND \"catalog_product\".\"created_at\" < '2026-10-20T08:15:00+00:00'\nORDER BY \"catalog_product\".\"created_at\" DESC, \"catalog_product\".\"id\" DESC\nLIMIT 26;\n```\n\nNo `COUNT`, and the `(tenant, -created_at, -id)` index serves the order. The page-number variant with `?ordering=price_cents&page=40` instead issues:\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*) AS \"__count\" FROM \"catalog_product\"\nWHERE \"tenant_id\" = 9 AND \"category_id\" = 3 AND \"price_cents\" >= 1000;\nSELECT ... ORDER BY \"catalog_product\".\"price_cents\" ASC LIMIT 20 OFFSET 780;\n```\n\nA `search=lamp` adds `AND (UPPER(\"name\"::text) LIKE UPPER('%lamp%') OR \"sku\" = 'lamp')`; with the trigram GIN index PostgreSQL can serve the `LIKE` instead of scanning every row. Confirm with `queryset.explain(analyze=True)` ([Ch 13](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization)).\n\n```bash\npython manage.py spectacular --file schema.yml --validate\n```\n\n### Paginating a custom action or `APIView`\n\nPagination only runs automatically in `ListModelMixin.list()`. In an `@action` you must call it yourself:\n\n```python\n@action(detail=False)\ndef discounted(self, request):\n    qs = self.filter_queryset(self.get_queryset()).filter(discount__gt=0)\n    page = self.paginate_queryset(qs)\n    if page is not None:\n        return self.get_paginated_response(self.get_serializer(page, many=True).data)\n    return Response(self.get_serializer(qs, many=True).data)\n```\n\n### Cheaper counts for big tables\n\n```python\nfrom django.core.paginator import Paginator\nfrom django.db import connection\nfrom django.utils.functional import cached_property\n\n\nclass EstimatedCountPaginator(Paginator):\n    @cached_property\n    def count(self):\n        if self.object_list.query.where:        # filtered: fall back to the real count\n            return super().count\n        with connection.cursor() as cur:\n            cur.execute(\"SELECT reltuples::bigint FROM pg_class WHERE relname = %s\",\n                        [self.object_list.model._meta.db_table])\n            return cur.fetchone()[0]\n\n\nclass EstimatedPagination(PageNumberPagination):\n    django_paginator_class = EstimatedCountPaginator\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Pagination | Total count | Jump to page | Deep-page cost | Stable under inserts | Best for |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| PageNumber | yes (COUNT) | yes | O(offset) | no | Admin UIs, small sets |\n| LimitOffset | yes (COUNT) | yes (by offset) | O(offset) | no | Data tables with variable sizes |\n| Cursor | no | no | O(page) | yes | Feeds, infinite scroll, sync, exports |\n\n| Versioning scheme | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| URL path `/api/v2/` | Visible, cacheable, easy routing and logs | URLs change per version |\n| Accept header | Clean URLs, \"proper\" content negotiation | Harder to test in a browser; caches need `Vary: Accept` |\n| Query param `?version=2` | Easy | Easily dropped; mixes with filters |\n| Hostname `v2.api...` | Separate deploys possible | DNS/TLS per version |\n| Namespace | Uses Django URL namespaces | Duplicated URL includes |\n\n| Filtering tool | Strength | Weakness |\n|---|---|---|\n| django-filter | typed, validated, whitelisted | Another dependency; need to define FilterSets |\n| SearchFilter | One line | `icontains`, poor index use, no relevance |\n| OrderingFilter | One line | Each allowed field is a potential sort scan |\n\n### When to use\n- Cursor pagination for anything a client scrolls or syncs, or any table that will exceed a few hundred thousand rows.\n- Page-number pagination when users genuinely need page numbers and totals on modest datasets.\n- URL path versioning for public APIs — it is what most clients and gateways expect.\n- drf-spectacular for every API with external consumers; check the schema into CI.\n\n### When NOT to use\n- Not page-number pagination over a growing multi-million-row feed.\n- Not `SearchFilter` as a search engine — use PostgreSQL full-text/trigram or a dedicated engine.\n- Not versioning for additive changes — adding a field is backwards compatible; version only for breaking changes ([REST API · Backward Compatibility](../rest-api/topic.html?p=32-backward-compatibility-deprecation)).\n- Not `ordering_fields = \"__all__\"` on large tables.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. No pagination on some list endpoints.** Setting `pagination_class` on one view but not globally, or setting `DEFAULT_PAGINATION_CLASS` without `PAGE_SIZE`. Configure both globally; opt out explicitly with `pagination_class = None` only for genuinely tiny lists.\n\n**2. Unbounded `page_size_query_param`.** Allowing client page size without `max_page_size`. Always set the cap.\n\n**3. Cursor pagination on a non-unique or mutable field.** Ordering by `-updated_at` alone: rows move between pages as they are edited, and ties step through slowly. Order by an immutable column plus `id`.\n\n**4. Hand-rolled filters from `request.GET`.**\n\n```python\n# before: lookup injection, no validation\nqs = Product.objects.filter(**request.query_params.dict())\n\n# after: whitelisted, typed, validated\nfilterset_class = ProductFilter\n```\n\n**5. Unindexed orderings and filters.** Each `ordering_fields` entry and each filter combination you expect should be backed by an index whose leading column matches the tenant scope.\n\n**6. Forgetting that filter backends also apply to `get_object()`.** A detail request with `?in_stock=true` on an out-of-stock product returns 404. Usually fine; document it.\n\n**7. Branching on `request.version` everywhere.** Scattered `if request.version == \"v2\"` in views rots quickly. Centralise: select serializer classes via a map keyed by version, and keep one queryset.\n\n**8. Documenting by hand.** A hand-written Markdown API doc is wrong within a sprint. Generate the schema; annotate gaps with `@extend_schema`; fail CI on schema warnings.\n\n**Best practices:** global pagination with caps; cursor for feeds; FilterSets for every filterable list; explicit `ordering_fields`; index per access path; version only on breaking changes; deprecation headers and usage metrics for old versions; schema generated in CI and diffed in code review.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: deep pages time out.** Symptom: crawlers walking `?page=1..N` push DB CPU to 100%; p99 for late pages is seconds. Root cause: `OFFSET` discarding hundreds of thousands of rows, plus `COUNT(*)` per request. Fix: cursor pagination for crawler-visible lists, cap the maximum page (`if page > 500: raise NotFound`), and estimated counts.\n\n**Pitfall: COUNT dominates the request.** Symptom: `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` shows the count taking 800 ms and the page 5 ms. Fix: estimated counts, cached counts (Redis, invalidated or refreshed periodically), or drop totals in favour of `has_more`.\n\n**Pitfall: search melts the database.** `?search=a` on a 10M-row table → `ILIKE '%a%'` sequential scan on every keystroke from the frontend's type-ahead. Fix: minimum search length, debounce in the client, trigram GIN index, or PostgreSQL full-text search with a `SearchVector` index; throttle the endpoint ([Ch 23](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling)).\n\n**Pitfall: duplicates in results.** Filtering across an M2M (`?tag=red&tag=blue` or `search_fields = [\"tags__name\"]`) yields joined duplicate rows; `count` disagrees with `results`. Fix: `distinct=True` on django-filter filters for to-many fields, or use `Exists()` subqueries.\n\n**Pitfall: caches mix versions.** With `AcceptHeaderVersioning`, a CDN caching on URL alone returns v2 bodies to v1 clients. Fix: ensure `Vary: Accept` (DRF sets it), or prefer URL versioning when a CDN is involved ([Ch 29 · Caching](topic.html?p=29-caching)).\n\n**Pitfall: the schema endpoint is slow or leaks internals.** Generating the schema walks every view on each request; exposing `/api/schema/` publicly reveals admin-only endpoints. Fix: generate at build time with `manage.py spectacular`, serve a static file, and restrict the live endpoint (`SERVE_PERMISSIONS`).\n\n**Debugging.** Use django-debug-toolbar on the browsable API, `print(view.filter_queryset(view.get_queryset()).query)` in a shell, `qs.explain(analyze=True)` on PostgreSQL, and `pg_stat_statements` to find which filter combinations are hot. Log the query params of slow requests (with PII scrubbed) to see real access patterns.\n\n**Scaling.** Pagination is the main lever on response size; indexes per access path are the main lever on DB time; caching first pages of public lists is the cheapest win. For very large exports, do not paginate through the API in a loop from the client — run a Celery job ([Ch 31](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks)) that streams with `.iterator(chunk_size=...)` to object storage and returns a download link.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What pagination styles does DRF provide and how do they differ?**\nA: `PageNumberPagination` (`?page=n`), `LimitOffsetPagination` (`?limit=&offset=`) and `CursorPagination` (`?cursor=`). The first two produce `COUNT(*)` plus `LIMIT/OFFSET`, give totals and random access, but get slower with depth and shift under inserts. Cursor pagination uses keyset filtering (`WHERE ordering_field < last_seen`) on a fixed ordering, so each page costs the same and stays stable while data changes, but there is no total and no jumping. All are configured via `DEFAULT_PAGINATION_CLASS` and `PAGE_SIZE` or per view.\n\n**Q: Why is offset pagination slow on deep pages?**\nA: The database must produce and discard `OFFSET` rows before returning `LIMIT` rows, so cost is linear in page depth — page 5,000 of 20 reads ~100k rows. PostgreSQL also has to count all matching rows for `COUNT(*)` because MVCC means there is no cheap exact count. Indexes help the ordering but do not let the engine skip the discarded rows.\n\n**Q: What are the requirements for `CursorPagination`?**\nA: A fixed `ordering` on a field that is unique (or nearly so) and never changes after creation — typically `-created_at` with `-id` as a tie-breaker — backed by an index. The default ordering is `-created`, so you must override it if your model lacks that field. Clients cannot jump to page N and there is no count. Arbitrary client orderings would each need their own index and break cursor stability, so restrict them.\n\n**Q: Why must you set `max_page_size`?**\nA: If `page_size_query_param` is enabled, clients choose page size; without a cap, `?page_size=10000000` makes the server load and serialise the whole table — an easy denial of service. `max_page_size` (and `max_limit` for limit/offset) clamps it.\n\n**Q: How does filtering work in DRF and how do you prevent clients from filtering on arbitrary fields?**\nA: `GenericAPIView.filter_queryset()` passes the queryset through each class in `filter_backends`. With django-filter's `DjangoFilterBackend`, only fields declared in `filterset_fields` or a `FilterSet` are applied, values are validated (bad input → 400), and lookups are fixed by you. Never pass `request.query_params` straight into `.filter(**...)` — it allows arbitrary lookups across relations, including sensitive columns.\n\n**Q: What does `SearchFilter` do and what are its limitations?**\nA: It applies `?search=` terms across `search_fields` using `icontains` by default (or `^` startswith, `=` exact, `@` full-text on PostgreSQL, `$` regex), ORing fields and ANDing terms, and adds `distinct()` when to-many lookups can create duplicates. `icontains` compiles to `UPPER(col) LIKE UPPER('%term%')`, which cannot use B-tree indexes, so it scans on large tables and has no relevance ranking. Use trigram indexes, PostgreSQL full-text search, or an external engine for real search.\n\n**Q: How do you implement API versioning in DRF?**\nA: Set `DEFAULT_VERSIONING_CLASS` (e.g. `URLPathVersioning`) with `ALLOWED_VERSIONS` and `DEFAULT_VERSION`; DRF sets `request.version` in `initial()`. Views then choose serializers or behaviour by version, typically in `get_serializer_class()`. URL path versioning is explicit and cache-friendly; Accept-header versioning keeps URLs stable but needs `Vary: Accept`. Version only for breaking changes and give old versions deprecation headers and a sunset date.\n\n**Q: How do you generate API documentation for a DRF project?**\nA: Use drf-spectacular: set `DEFAULT_SCHEMA_CLASS` to its `AutoSchema`, mount `SpectacularAPIView` plus Swagger/Redoc views, and annotate what introspection cannot infer with `@extend_schema` (parameters, responses, examples) and `@extend_schema_field` on `SerializerMethodField`s. Generate the file in CI with `manage.py spectacular --validate` and diff it in PRs so contract changes are reviewed. drf-yasg only supports OpenAPI 2, and DRF's built-in generator is basic.\n\n**Q: Why is `count` sometimes different from the number of results you can page through?**\nA: Usually duplicates from joins across to-many relations (filters or search on M2M/reverse FK): `COUNT(*)` counts joined rows while the client sees repeats, or `distinct()` is applied inconsistently. Also concurrent inserts/deletes between the count and page queries. Fix with `distinct=True`, `Exists()` subqueries, and cursor pagination for live data.\n\n**Q: A public listing endpoint's DB CPU spikes whenever a crawler visits. Walk me through the fix. (Senior)**\nA: Confirm with `pg_stat_statements` that deep `OFFSET` queries and `COUNT(*)` dominate. Short term: cap the page number, throttle anonymous traffic, and cache early pages. Structural fix: switch the endpoint (or a crawler-facing variant) to cursor pagination ordered by an indexed `(created_at, id)`, so each request is an index range scan with no count. Replace exact totals with estimates or `has_more`. Make sure every permitted filter/order combination has a supporting composite index starting with the scope column, and verify with `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`. Version the change if clients depend on `page` and `count`.\n\n**Q: How would you support cursor pagination while letting clients sort by price? (Senior)**\nA: Make the ordering part of the cursor contract: allow a small whitelist of orderings, each with a unique tie-breaker (`price_cents, id`) and a composite index (`tenant, price_cents, id`). DRF's `CursorPagination` reads `OrderingFilter`'s ordering if present, but it encodes only the first field's position plus an offset for ties, so heavy ties (many products at the same price) cost extra steps; for exact keyset behaviour write a custom paginator that encodes the full tuple and filters with a row comparison (`(price_cents, id) > (%s, %s)`). A cursor must be rejected if the client changes ordering mid-stream. Document that cursors are opaque and short-lived.\n\n**Q: How do you evolve an API from v1 to v2 without breaking mobile clients you cannot force-upgrade? (Senior)**\nA: Prefer additive, backwards-compatible changes; reserve a new version for breaking ones. Implement v2 with URL path versioning, sharing querysets and services and differing only in serializers (selected by a version→serializer map). Keep v1 frozen with contract tests from its published OpenAPI schema, return `Deprecation`/`Sunset` headers, track v1 usage per client id/app version in logs, and coordinate a minimum supported app version with the mobile team. Remove v1 only when traffic is near zero, after a communicated sunset date.\n\n**Q: Where does pagination run, and why doesn't my `@action` paginate?**\nA: Only `ListModelMixin.list()` calls `paginate_queryset()` and `get_paginated_response()`. Custom actions and plain `APIView`s must call them explicitly (`page = self.paginate_queryset(qs)`), or instantiate a paginator and call `paginate_queryset(qs, request, view=self)`.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Topic | Remember |\n|---|---|\n| Enable pagination | `DEFAULT_PAGINATION_CLASS` **and** `PAGE_SIZE` |\n| PageNumber/LimitOffset SQL | `COUNT(*)` + `LIMIT/OFFSET` |\n| Cursor SQL | `WHERE key < position ORDER BY key LIMIT n+1`, no count |\n| Cursor needs | unique, immutable ordering + index; default `-created` |\n| Caps | `max_page_size`, `max_limit` |\n| Filter order | `filter_backends` in sequence; also applied to `get_object()` |\n| django-filter | whitelisted, typed, 400 on bad input |\n| SearchFilter | `icontains`; `^ = @ $` prefixes; distinct on to-many |\n| OrderingFilter | `ordering_fields` whitelist; `ordering` default |\n| Versioning | `request.version` set in `initial()`; URL path most common |\n| Docs | drf-spectacular, `@extend_schema`, `manage.py spectacular` |\n\n- Every list endpoint must be bounded.\n- Cursor for feeds, page numbers for small admin lists.\n- Never `.filter(**request.query_params)`.\n- Every allowed filter/ordering is an access path — index it.\n- `COUNT(*)` is not free on PostgreSQL.\n- Actions don't paginate automatically.\n- Version only for breaking changes; deprecate with headers and data.\n- Generate the schema in CI and review diffs.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"djangorestframework>=3.15\" django-filter drf-spectacular \"psycopg[binary]\"\n```\n\n1. **Measure offset.** Seed 2M products in PostgreSQL. Time `?page=1`, `?page=1000`, `?page=50000` with page-number pagination and capture `EXPLAIN ANALYZE` for each.\n2. **Switch to cursor.** Configure `CursorPagination` on `(-created_at, -id)` with a matching index; repeat the deep-page measurement by following `next` links (script it).\n3. **Lookup injection.** Write the naive `.filter(**request.query_params.dict())` view and demonstrate filtering on `owner__email__startswith`; replace with a `FilterSet` and show the 400 on bad input.\n4. **Search performance.** Compare `SearchFilter` `icontains` with and without a `pg_trgm` GIN index; then implement `@` full-text search.\n5. **Versioning + schema.** Add v1/v2 serializers, URL path versioning, generate the schema with `spectacular --validate`, and add a CI step that fails if the schema changes without a committed update.\n\n**Mini project — \"Catalogue API\".** Products with categories and tags for multiple tenants. Requirements: cursor pagination for the feed, page numbers for an admin list with estimated counts, a FilterSet with price range/category/in_stock, trigram search, whitelisted orderings each with an index, v1/v2 with a renamed field, deprecation headers on v1, and Swagger UI at `/api/docs/` served from a build-time schema.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) · [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres) · [Ch 21 · DRF Serializers](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers) · [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers) · [Ch 23 · DRF Authentication, Permissions & Throttling](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling) · [Ch 29 · Caching in Django](topic.html?p=29-caching) · [Ch 38 · Interview: DRF, Security & Scale](topic.html?p=38-interview-drf-advanced)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · Pagination](../rest-api/topic.html?p=13-pagination) · [REST API · Filtering, Sorting & Searching](../rest-api/topic.html?p=12-filtering-sorting-searching) · [REST API · API Versioning](../rest-api/topic.html?p=31-api-versioning) · [REST API · Backward Compatibility & Deprecation](../rest-api/topic.html?p=32-backward-compatibility-deprecation) · [REST API · OpenAPI Specification](../rest-api/topic.html?p=33-openapi-specification) · [SQL · Sorting & Pagination](../sql/topic.html?p=03-sorting-pagination) · [SQL · Index Design](../sql/topic.html?p=20-index-design)\n\n- **Pagination (API Guide)** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · all three classes, their settings and cursor caveats. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/pagination/>\n- **Filtering (API Guide)** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · filter backends, SearchFilter, OrderingFilter. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/filtering/>\n- **Versioning (API Guide)** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · each scheme and how `reverse` interacts with versions. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/versioning/>\n- **django-filter documentation** — django-filter · *Intermediate* · FilterSets, lookups and the DRF integration. <https://django-filter.readthedocs.io/>\n- **drf-spectacular documentation** — tfranzel · *Intermediate* · OpenAPI 3 generation, `@extend_schema`, customisation. <https://drf-spectacular.readthedocs.io/>\n- **Use the Index, Luke — Paging Through Results** — Markus Winand · *Advanced* · why OFFSET is slow and how keyset pagination works. <https://use-the-index-luke.com/no-offset>\n- **PostgreSQL full-text search** — PostgreSQL docs · *Advanced* · `tsvector`, ranking and GIN indexes behind the `@` search prefix. <https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/textsearch.html>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 24.*\n", "25-authentication-custom-user": "# 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model\n\n> **In one line:** `django.contrib.auth` gives you a user model, a pluggable chain of authentication backends, salted slow password hashing that upgrades itself, and session-based login that rotates keys — and the single most consequential decision you make with it is defining a custom user model with `AUTH_USER_MODEL` *before* your first migration.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings), [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields), [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations), [Ch 15 · Custom Managers, QuerySets & Model Inheritance](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance).\n\nEvery application needs to answer \"who is this?\" and every hand-rolled answer gets the same things wrong. Passwords stored as plain text or as unsalted `md5(password)` — cracked in seconds once a backup leaks. Login that compares strings with `==`, leaking timing information. A session ID that stays the same before and after login, so an attacker who planted it (session fixation) is now logged in as the victim. A \"forgot password\" link containing the user ID and nothing else. A `User` table with `username` as the identity when the product wants email login, discovered three years in.\n\nDjango's auth framework (`django.contrib.auth`) solves these once: a `User` model with a hashed `password` column, `authenticate()` that delegates to a configurable list of **backends**, `login()`/`logout()` that manage the session correctly, password validators, a password-reset flow with self-expiring tokens, and permissions/groups (covered in [Ch 26](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization)).\n\nThe trap is the default `User` model. It is fine — until you need email as the login identifier, a `tenant` FK, or to drop `username`. Every model in your project has an FK to the user table, and the user table's identity is baked into the very first migration of `auth`. Swapping it later means rewriting migration history across apps. The Django docs therefore say plainly: **when starting a new project, set up a custom user model, even if the default is sufficient for you.** Interviewers ask this because it separates people who have run a project for years from those who have only done tutorials.\n\n> **Why this matters:** \"AbstractUser vs AbstractBaseUser\", \"why set `AUTH_USER_MODEL` on day one\", \"how does Django store passwords\" and \"what does `login()` actually do\" are standard Django interview questions — and the answers are also what keeps your users' credentials safe.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **`AUTH_USER_MODEL`** — `\"app_label.ModelName\"` of the active user model. *Why:* must be set before the first `migrate`; every FK to users depends on it.\n- **`AbstractBaseUser`** — minimal base: `password`, `last_login`, `is_active`, plus `set_password`, `check_password`, `get_username`, `USERNAME_FIELD`. *Why:* full control over fields; you supply a manager.\n- **`AbstractUser`** — the full default user (`username`, `first_name`, `last_name`, `email`, `is_staff`, `is_active`, `date_joined`) + `PermissionsMixin`, as an abstract class. *Why:* subclass to add fields while keeping all default behaviour.\n- **`PermissionsMixin`** — `is_superuser`, `groups`, `user_permissions`, `has_perm()`. *Why:* needed with `AbstractBaseUser` if you want Django permissions and admin access.\n- **`BaseUserManager`** — manager base with `normalize_email`; you implement `create_user` and `create_superuser`. *Why:* `createsuperuser` and your code call them.\n- **`USERNAME_FIELD` / `REQUIRED_FIELDS` / `EMAIL_FIELD`** — identity field (must be unique), extra fields `createsuperuser` prompts for, and which field is the email. *Why:* drive auth and management commands.\n- **`get_user_model()` vs `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL`** — runtime model class vs string reference for FKs. *Why:* avoids import-time app-registry errors and hard-coding `auth.User`.\n- **Password hasher** — `PASSWORD_HASHERS`; first entry hashes new passwords, the rest verify old ones. Default PBKDF2-SHA256; Argon2 recommended. *Why:* hashes upgrade transparently on next login.\n- **Authentication backend** — class with `authenticate(request, **credentials)` and `get_user(user_id)`; listed in `AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS`. *Why:* add email login, LDAP, SSO without touching views.\n- **`authenticate()` / `login()` / `logout()`** — verify credentials / attach user to session (rotating the key) / flush the session. *Why:* the three calls every login flow uses.\n- **`AuthenticationMiddleware`** — sets `request.user` lazily from the session. *Why:* `request.user` costs a query only when accessed.\n- **Password validators** — `AUTH_PASSWORD_VALIDATORS`, run by `validate_password()`. *Why:* forms and `createsuperuser` use them; `set_password()` does not.\n- **`PasswordResetTokenGenerator`** — HMAC token over user state + timestamp. *Why:* stateless, expires, and is invalidated when the password changes or the user logs in.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### The user model hierarchy\n\nThree abstract building blocks combine into the concrete user model; which ones you inherit from decides how much you get for free and how much you must write.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 330\" width=\"100%\" height=\"330\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj25a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">django.contrib.auth model classes (arrows point to the parent)</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"44\" width=\"250\" height=\"96\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">AbstractBaseUser</text>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">password, last_login, is_active</text>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">set_password, check_password</text>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">USERNAME_FIELD, REQUIRED_FIELDS</text>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">get_session_auth_hash()</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"44\" width=\"250\" height=\"96\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"715\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">PermissionsMixin</text>\n  <text x=\"715\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">is_superuser</text>\n  <text x=\"715\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">groups, user_permissions (M2M)</text>\n  <text x=\"715\" y=\"114\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">has_perm, has_perms, has_module_perms</text>\n  <rect x=\"315\" y=\"170\" width=\"250\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"190\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">AbstractUser (abstract)</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"208\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">username, first_name, last_name, email</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"224\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">is_staff, date_joined, UserManager</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">USERNAME_FIELD = \"username\"</text>\n  <path d=\"M380,170 L220,142\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj25a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M500,170 L660,142\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj25a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"200\" y=\"272\" width=\"200\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"292\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">auth.User</text>\n  <text x=\"300\" y=\"308\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">swappable = \"AUTH_USER_MODEL\"</text>\n  <rect x=\"480\" y=\"272\" width=\"200\" height=\"46\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"580\" y=\"292\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">accounts.User(AbstractUser)</text>\n  <text x=\"580\" y=\"308\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">option 1: keep fields, add more</text>\n  <path d=\"M300,272 L400,252\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj25a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M580,272 L480,252\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj25a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"170\" width=\"240\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"190\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">accounts.User(AbstractBaseUser,</text>\n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"206\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">PermissionsMixin)</text>\n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"224\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">option 2: full control, email login,</text>\n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"238\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">own manager + fields</text>\n  <path d=\"M120,170 L130,142\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj25a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M250,190 C420,160 560,150 610,142\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" fill=\"none\" marker-end=\"url(#dj25a)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n`auth.User` declares `class Meta: swappable = \"AUTH_USER_MODEL\"`. When `AUTH_USER_MODEL` points elsewhere, Django marks `auth.User` as swapped out: its table is not created, and any FK declared as `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL` (including those in `admin`, `auth.Permission` relations, and third-party apps) resolves to your model. The migration framework records a **swappable dependency** (`migrations.swappable_dependency(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL)`), so every app with a user FK depends on whatever app defines the user model's first migration. That is why changing `AUTH_USER_MODEL` after tables exist is so hard: the initial migrations of many apps (yours and third-party) were applied against `auth_user`, and their FKs physically reference it.\n\n### How passwords are stored and checked\n\n`user.set_password(raw)` calls `make_password(raw)`, which uses the first hasher in `PASSWORD_HASHERS` (default `PBKDF2PasswordHasher`) with a fresh random salt and stores a string of the form:\n\n```text\npbkdf2_sha256$<iterations>$<salt>$<base64 hash>\nargon2$argon2id$v=19$m=102400,t=2,p=8$<salt>$<hash>\n```\n\nThe algorithm name prefix lets Django pick the right hasher for verification. PBKDF2 runs on the order of a million SHA-256 iterations in current Django releases (the number is raised every release), which deliberately makes each guess slow. **Argon2** (`Argon2PasswordHasher`, requires `argon2-cffi`) is memory-hard and the recommended choice; `ScryptPasswordHasher` exists too. Never use `MD5PasswordHasher` outside tests (where it makes the test suite much faster).\n\n`user.check_password(raw)` calls `hashers.check_password(raw, encoded, setter)`: it identifies the hasher from the prefix, recomputes, and compares with `constant_time_compare` to avoid timing leaks. If the password is correct **and** the stored hash uses a non-preferred algorithm or an outdated iteration count (`hasher.must_update(encoded)`), the `setter` re-hashes with the current preferred hasher and saves — so raising iterations or switching to Argon2 upgrades users transparently on their next login. `ModelBackend` also runs the hasher once for a non-existent username, so \"user not found\" and \"wrong password\" take similar time (mitigating user enumeration by timing).\n\n`set_password()` does **not** call password validators and does **not** save; call `validate_password(raw, user)` first and `user.save()` after.\n\n### `authenticate()`: the backend chain\n\n`django.contrib.auth.authenticate(request=None, **credentials)` iterates `AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS` (default `[\"django.contrib.auth.backends.ModelBackend\"]`). For each backend whose `authenticate()` signature accepts the given credentials, it calls it: a returned user wins and gets `user.backend = \"<dotted path>\"`; `None` moves on; raising `PermissionDenied` stops the chain immediately. If nothing matches it sends `user_login_failed` and returns `None`. `ModelBackend.authenticate` does `UserModel._default_manager.get_by_natural_key(username)`, `user.check_password(password)`, and `user_can_authenticate(user)` (rejects `is_active=False`).\n\n### `login()`: what actually happens\n\n`login(request, user, backend=None)`:\n\n1. computes `session_auth_hash = user.get_session_auth_hash()` (an HMAC of the password hash);\n2. if the session already belongs to a *different* user, or the stored hash differs, `request.session.flush()`; otherwise `request.session.cycle_key()` — **a new session key either way**, which defeats session fixation;\n3. stores `_auth_user_id`, `_auth_user_backend`, `_auth_user_hash` in the session;\n4. sets `request.user = user`, calls `rotate_token(request)` to rotate the CSRF token;\n5. sends `user_logged_in`, whose built-in receiver updates `last_login`.\n\nOn later requests, `AuthenticationMiddleware` sets `request.user = SimpleLazyObject(lambda: get_user(request))`. `get_user()` reads the user id and backend from the session, loads the user via `backend.get_user(id)`, and verifies `_auth_user_hash` against the current `get_session_auth_hash()`. Because that hash derives from the password hash, **changing a password invalidates all other sessions**; `update_session_auth_hash(request, user)` keeps the current one alive after a password change. `logout(request)` sends `user_logged_out`, flushes the session, and sets `AnonymousUser`.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 360\" width=\"100%\" height=\"360\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj25b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj25c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">POST /login/: authenticate() then login()</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"140\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"90\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">LoginView</text>\n  <text x=\"90\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">AuthenticationForm.clean()</text>\n  <path d=\"M160,72 L190,72\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj25b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"192\" y=\"40\" width=\"380\" height=\"140\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"382\" y=\"58\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">authenticate(request, username, password)</text>\n  <rect x=\"204\" y=\"68\" width=\"170\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"289\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">EmailBackend</text>\n  <text x=\"289\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">returns None: try next</text>\n  <rect x=\"390\" y=\"68\" width=\"170\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"475\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">ModelBackend</text>\n  <text x=\"475\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">get_by_natural_key</text>\n  <path d=\"M374,93 L388,93\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj25b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"382\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">check_password: constant-time compare,</text>\n  <text x=\"382\" y=\"156\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">rehash if hasher outdated; is_active check</text>\n  <text x=\"382\" y=\"172\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">sets user.backend = \"dotted.path\"</text>\n  <path d=\"M572,110 L602,110\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj25b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"604\" y=\"40\" width=\"256\" height=\"190\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"58\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">login(request, user)</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">1. session.cycle_key() or flush()</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">new session id: no fixation</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">2. store _auth_user_id,</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"134\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">_auth_user_backend, _auth_user_hash</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"156\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">3. rotate_token(): new CSRF secret</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"178\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">4. user_logged_in signal</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">updates last_login</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"216\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Set-Cookie: sessionid=NEW; HttpOnly</text>\n  <path d=\"M382,180 L382,250\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj25c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"192\" y=\"252\" width=\"380\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"382\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">all backends None: user_login_failed signal</text>\n  <text x=\"382\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">a backend raising PermissionDenied stops the chain</text>\n  <rect x=\"604\" y=\"252\" width=\"256\" height=\"92\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"272\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">next request</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">AuthenticationMiddleware:</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"306\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">request.user = lazy get_user()</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"322\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">verifies _auth_user_hash vs password hash</text>\n  <text x=\"732\" y=\"336\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">password changed elsewhere: logged out</text>\n  <path d=\"M732,230 L732,250\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj25b)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Password reset tokens\n\n`PasswordResetTokenGenerator.make_token(user)` produces `<timestamp_b36>-<hmac>` where the HMAC (keyed with `SECRET_KEY`) covers the user's pk, **password hash**, **`last_login`**, the timestamp and the email. Nothing is stored in the database. `check_token()` recomputes and checks the age against `PASSWORD_RESET_TIMEOUT` (default 3 days, in seconds). Because the password hash and `last_login` are inputs, the token is single-use in practice: once the password changes (or the user logs in), the token no longer validates. Rotating `SECRET_KEY` invalidates outstanding tokens — use `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS` (4.1+) during rotation.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### Day-one setup order\n\n```text\n1. django-admin startproject config .\n2. python manage.py startapp accounts\n3. accounts/models.py: class User(AbstractUser) (or AbstractBaseUser + PermissionsMixin)\n4. settings.py: AUTH_USER_MODEL = \"accounts.User\"; add \"accounts\" to INSTALLED_APPS\n5. python manage.py makemigrations accounts      ← accounts/0001_initial creates the user table\n6. python manage.py migrate                       ← FIRST migrate, after the above\n7. accounts/admin.py: register with UserAdmin (customised fieldsets)\n```\n\nIf step 6 has already happened with `auth.User`, see the Senior question in §9 — it is a migration project, not a settings change.\n\n### Where the user model is referenced\n\n```text\nmodels.py   owner = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=...)   # string, import-safe\nservices    User = get_user_model(); User.objects.filter(...)                     # runtime class\nsignals     @receiver(post_save, sender=settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL)                 # string sender works\nforms       class SignUpForm(UserCreationForm): class Meta(UserCreationForm.Meta): model = get_user_model()\ntests       User = get_user_model(); User.objects.create_user(...)\nNEVER       from django.contrib.auth.models import User                           # breaks when swapped\n```\n\n### Login options at a glance\n\n| Flow | Built-in piece |\n|---|---|\n| Username/password HTML login | `LoginView` + `AuthenticationForm` |\n| Logout | `LogoutView` (POST only since Django 5.0) |\n| Require login | `@login_required`, `LoginRequiredMixin`, `LoginRequiredMiddleware` + `@login_not_required` (5.1+) |\n| Password change | `PasswordChangeView` (calls `update_session_auth_hash`) |\n| Password reset | `PasswordResetView` → email → `PasswordResetConfirmView` |\n| API login | DRF session/JWT ([Ch 23](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling)) |\n| Social / SSO / MFA | django-allauth, mozilla-django-oidc, django-otp |\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: `AbstractUser` (recommended default)\n\n```python\n# accounts/models.py\nfrom django.contrib.auth.models import AbstractUser\nfrom django.db import models\n\n\nclass User(AbstractUser):\n    \"\"\"Start here on every new project, even with no extra fields yet.\"\"\"\n    display_name = models.CharField(max_length=80, blank=True)\n    tenant = models.ForeignKey(\"tenants.Tenant\", null=True, blank=True, on_delete=models.PROTECT)\n```\n\n```python\n# settings.py\nINSTALLED_APPS = [..., \"accounts\"]\nAUTH_USER_MODEL = \"accounts.User\"\n\nPASSWORD_HASHERS = [\n    \"django.contrib.auth.hashers.Argon2PasswordHasher\",   # new hashes\n    \"django.contrib.auth.hashers.PBKDF2PasswordHasher\",   # still verifies old ones, upgrades on login\n    \"django.contrib.auth.hashers.PBKDF2SHA1PasswordHasher\",\n    \"django.contrib.auth.hashers.ScryptPasswordHasher\",\n]\n\nAUTH_PASSWORD_VALIDATORS = [\n    {\"NAME\": \"django.contrib.auth.password_validation.UserAttributeSimilarityValidator\"},\n    {\"NAME\": \"django.contrib.auth.password_validation.MinimumLengthValidator\", \"OPTIONS\": {\"min_length\": 12}},\n    {\"NAME\": \"django.contrib.auth.password_validation.CommonPasswordValidator\"},\n    {\"NAME\": \"django.contrib.auth.password_validation.NumericPasswordValidator\"},\n]\n\nLOGIN_URL = \"login\"\nLOGIN_REDIRECT_URL = \"dashboard\"\n```\n\n```python\n# accounts/admin.py\nfrom django.contrib import admin\nfrom django.contrib.auth.admin import UserAdmin\n\nfrom .models import User\n\n\n@admin.register(User)\nclass CustomUserAdmin(UserAdmin):\n    fieldsets = UserAdmin.fieldsets + ((\"Profile\", {\"fields\": (\"display_name\", \"tenant\")}),)\n```\n\n```bash\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"argon2-cffi\"\npython manage.py makemigrations accounts && python manage.py migrate\npython manage.py createsuperuser\n```\n\n```text\n>>> from django.contrib.auth import get_user_model\n>>> u = get_user_model().objects.get(username=\"admin\")\n>>> u.password\n'argon2$argon2id$v=19$m=102400,t=2,p=8$c2FsdHNhbHQ$Zm9vYmFyYmF6...'\n>>> u.check_password(\"wrong\"), u.check_password(\"correct horse battery\")\n(False, True)\n```\n\n### Real-world example: email login with `AbstractBaseUser`\n\n```python\n# accounts/models.py\nfrom django.contrib.auth.base_user import AbstractBaseUser, BaseUserManager\nfrom django.contrib.auth.models import PermissionsMixin\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.db.models.functions import Lower\nfrom django.utils import timezone\n\n\nclass UserManager(BaseUserManager):\n    use_in_migrations = True\n\n    def _create_user(self, email, password, **extra):\n        if not email:\n            raise ValueError(\"Email is required\")\n        email = self.normalize_email(email).lower()\n        user = self.model(email=email, **extra)\n        user.set_password(password)          # None → unusable password (SSO-only users)\n        user.save(using=self._db)\n        return user\n\n    def create_user(self, email, password=None, **extra):\n        extra.setdefault(\"is_staff\", False)\n        extra.setdefault(\"is_superuser\", False)\n        return self._create_user(email, password, **extra)\n\n    def create_superuser(self, email, password=None, **extra):\n        extra.setdefault(\"is_staff\", True)\n        extra.setdefault(\"is_superuser\", True)\n        if extra[\"is_staff\"] is not True or extra[\"is_superuser\"] is not True:\n            raise ValueError(\"Superuser must have is_staff=True and is_superuser=True.\")\n        return self._create_user(email, password, **extra)\n\n    def get_by_natural_key(self, email):\n        return self.get(email__iexact=email)    # case-insensitive login\n\n\nclass User(AbstractBaseUser, PermissionsMixin):\n    email = models.EmailField(unique=True)\n    full_name = models.CharField(max_length=150, blank=True)\n    is_staff = models.BooleanField(default=False)        # admin site access\n    is_active = models.BooleanField(default=True)        # soft-disable instead of delete\n    date_joined = models.DateTimeField(default=timezone.now)\n    tenant = models.ForeignKey(\"tenants.Tenant\", null=True, blank=True, on_delete=models.PROTECT)\n\n    objects = UserManager()\n\n    USERNAME_FIELD = \"email\"\n    EMAIL_FIELD = \"email\"\n    REQUIRED_FIELDS = []                 # prompted by createsuperuser besides USERNAME_FIELD + password\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [\n            models.UniqueConstraint(Lower(\"email\"), name=\"user_email_ci_unique\"),\n        ]\n\n    def get_full_name(self):\n        return self.full_name\n\n    def get_short_name(self):\n        return self.full_name.split(\" \")[0] if self.full_name else self.email\n```\n\nThe SQL behind a login with this model:\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"accounts_user\" WHERE UPPER(\"accounts_user\".\"email\"::text) = UPPER('Ann@Example.com') LIMIT 21;\n-- on success, user_logged_in → update_last_login:\nUPDATE \"accounts_user\" SET \"last_login\" = '2026-10-25T10:00:00+00:00' WHERE \"accounts_user\".\"id\" = 7;\n```\n\nUse a functional index matching the lookup (`Lower(\"email\")` with `email__iexact` may need `Upper` on some versions — check `explain()`), or normalise to lowercase at write time and query `email=normalized` for a plain B-tree lookup.\n\n> **Gotcha:** `REQUIRED_FIELDS` must not contain `USERNAME_FIELD` or `password`. And `is_active` on `AbstractBaseUser` is a class attribute `True` — declare a real field if you want to deactivate users.\n\n### A custom authentication backend\n\nSometimes you keep `username` as the identifier but want users to log in with either username or email:\n\n```python\n# accounts/backends.py\nfrom django.contrib.auth import get_user_model\nfrom django.contrib.auth.backends import ModelBackend\nfrom django.db.models import Q\n\n\nclass UsernameOrEmailBackend(ModelBackend):\n    def authenticate(self, request, username=None, password=None, **kwargs):\n        if username is None or password is None:\n            return None\n        User = get_user_model()\n        try:\n            user = User._default_manager.get(Q(username__iexact=username) | Q(email__iexact=username))\n        except User.DoesNotExist:\n            User().set_password(password)      # equalise timing with the \"found\" path\n            return None\n        except User.MultipleObjectsReturned:\n            return None\n        if user.check_password(password) and self.user_can_authenticate(user):\n            return user\n        return None\n```\n\n```python\nAUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS = [\n    \"accounts.backends.UsernameOrEmailBackend\",\n    \"django.contrib.auth.backends.ModelBackend\",   # keeps has_perm() from ModelBackend\n]\n```\n\nPermission checks (`user.has_perm`) also iterate all backends, so subclassing `ModelBackend` keeps that behaviour.\n\n### Login, logout and reset views\n\n```python\n# accounts/urls.py\nfrom django.contrib.auth import views as auth_views\nfrom django.urls import path\n\nurlpatterns = [\n    path(\"login/\", auth_views.LoginView.as_view(redirect_authenticated_user=True), name=\"login\"),\n    path(\"logout/\", auth_views.LogoutView.as_view(), name=\"logout\"),        # POST only (5.0+)\n    path(\"password-change/\", auth_views.PasswordChangeView.as_view(), name=\"password_change\"),\n    path(\"password-reset/\", auth_views.PasswordResetView.as_view(), name=\"password_reset\"),\n    path(\"reset/<uidb64>/<token>/\", auth_views.PasswordResetConfirmView.as_view(),\n         name=\"password_reset_confirm\"),\n]\n# or simply: path(\"accounts/\", include(\"django.contrib.auth.urls\"))\n```\n\n```html\n<!-- logout must be a POST form since Django 5.0 -->\n<form method=\"post\" action=\"{% url 'logout' %}\">{% csrf_token %}<button>Log out</button></form>\n```\n\nA programmatic login, e.g. after sign-up:\n\n```python\nfrom django.contrib.auth import authenticate, login, update_session_auth_hash\nfrom django.contrib.auth.password_validation import validate_password\n\n\ndef signup(request):\n    form = SignUpForm(request.POST or None)\n    if request.method == \"POST\" and form.is_valid():\n        user = form.save()                              # UserCreationForm runs validators + set_password\n        login(request, user, backend=\"django.contrib.auth.backends.ModelBackend\")  # needed with >1 backend\n        return redirect(\"dashboard\")\n    return render(request, \"accounts/signup.html\", {\"form\": form})\n\n\ndef change_password(request):\n    new = request.POST[\"new_password\"]\n    validate_password(new, user=request.user)          # set_password does NOT validate\n    request.user.set_password(new)\n    request.user.save(update_fields=[\"password\"])\n    update_session_auth_hash(request, request.user)   # keep this session; others are logged out\n```\n\n### Requiring login everywhere (Django 5.1+)\n\n```python\nMIDDLEWARE = [..., \"django.contrib.auth.middleware.AuthenticationMiddleware\",\n              \"django.contrib.auth.middleware.LoginRequiredMiddleware\", ...]\n\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_not_required\n\n@login_not_required\ndef healthcheck(request):\n    return HttpResponse(\"ok\")\n```\n\n### Extending without swapping: a profile model\n\n```python\nclass Profile(models.Model):\n    user = models.OneToOneField(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"profile\")\n    avatar = models.ImageField(upload_to=\"avatars/\", blank=True)\n    timezone = models.CharField(max_length=40, default=\"UTC\")\n```\n\nKeep authentication data on the user model and app-specific data on profiles — but note each `request.user.profile` is an extra query unless you `select_related(\"profile\")`.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Approach | Pros | Cons | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Default `auth.User` | Zero work | Cannot change later without pain | Throwaway prototypes only |\n| `AbstractUser` subclass | Keeps admin, forms, username; easy to add fields | Still has `username` (can be repurposed) | Almost every new project |\n| `AbstractBaseUser` + `PermissionsMixin` | Exact fields, email login, no username | Write manager, admin forms, more code | Email/phone identity, strict schemas |\n| Profile `OneToOne` | No swap needed; separates concerns | Extra join/query, sync on create | Adding data to an existing project |\n| Custom backend | Any credential source (email, LDAP, SSO) | Must handle timing, is_active, perms | Alternative login identifiers |\n\n| Hasher | Notes |\n|---|---|\n| PBKDF2-SHA256 (default) | Standard, no extra deps, iterations raised each release |\n| Argon2id | Memory-hard, recommended; needs `argon2-cffi` |\n| scrypt | Memory-hard, built in (4.0+) |\n| bcrypt (`BCryptSHA256PasswordHasher`) | Needs `bcrypt`; 72-byte limit handled by SHA256 pre-hash |\n| MD5 | Only for test settings, to speed tests |\n\n### When to use\n- A custom user model (`AbstractUser`) on every new project, set before the first migrate.\n- `AbstractBaseUser` when the identity is email/phone and you want no username column.\n- Argon2 as the first hasher in production.\n- Custom backends for alternate identifiers or external identity providers.\n\n### When NOT to use\n- Don't store app-specific, frequently changing data on the user if many apps touch it — a profile can reduce contention and migrations on a hot table.\n- Don't write your own password hashing or session handling.\n- Don't use `AbstractBaseUser` \"just because\"; you take on manager, forms and admin work.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Running `migrate` before setting `AUTH_USER_MODEL`.** The `auth_user` table and all FKs to it now exist. Set it at project creation.\n\n**2. Importing `User` directly.**\n\n```python\n# before — breaks when the model is swapped\nfrom django.contrib.auth.models import User\nowner = models.ForeignKey(User, on_delete=models.CASCADE)\n\n# after\nfrom django.conf import settings\nowner = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE)\n```\n\n**3. Calling `get_user_model()` at module import time in `models.py`.** It can raise `AppRegistryNotReady`/`ImproperlyConfigured`. Use the setting string for relations.\n\n**4. `User.objects.create(password=\"secret\")`.** Stores the raw string, which `check_password` can never match (and leaks it). Use `create_user()` or `set_password()`.\n\n**5. Assuming `set_password()` validates or saves.** It does neither. Call `validate_password()` and `save()`.\n\n**6. Case-sensitive email uniqueness.** `Ann@x.com` and `ann@x.com` become two accounts. Normalise and use a `Lower(\"email\")` unique constraint.\n\n**7. Deleting users.** Deleting cascades through orders, audit logs and more. Set `is_active=False`; `ModelBackend` will refuse login.\n\n**8. GET logout links.** Removed in Django 5.0 — logout must be a POST (CSRF-protected), so a malicious `<img src=\"/logout/\">` can't log users out.\n\n**9. Leaking account existence in reset/login flows.** Show the same message for \"no such user\" and \"wrong password\"; `PasswordResetView` already responds identically whether or not the email exists.\n\n**Best practices:** custom user from day one; reference via `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL`/`get_user_model()`; Argon2 first; validators with a 12+ minimum length; throttle login endpoints; `is_active` instead of delete; MFA for staff (django-otp or allauth); `MD5PasswordHasher` in test settings only.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Pitfall: login is slow and CPU-bound.** Symptom: `/login/` p95 of 400 ms and high CPU during morning peaks or credential-stuffing attacks. Root cause: password hashing is *designed* to be slow; an attacker trying 1,000 passwords/second costs you 1,000 hashes/second. Fix: rate-limit login per IP and per account (django-axes or a gateway), keep hashing cost (do not lower it), scale login capacity separately if needed.\n\n**Pitfall: everyone logged out after a deploy.** Root cause: `SECRET_KEY` changed (session auth hash, signed cookies, reset tokens all depend on it), or the session backend changed. Fix: rotate with `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS`; never generate `SECRET_KEY` at boot.\n\n**Pitfall: hasher upgrade causes write spike.** After switching to Argon2, every user's next login re-hashes and saves the password — a burst of UPDATEs on the user table. Usually harmless; on huge user bases, expect it and watch replication lag.\n\n**Pitfall: mid-project swap to a custom user.** Symptom: `ValueError: Related model 'accounts.user' cannot be resolved` or `InconsistentMigrationHistory`. Root cause: changing `AUTH_USER_MODEL` after migrations ran. Fix: the documented manual path (see Senior Q below), on a staging copy first.\n\n**Pitfall: N+1 on `request.user` relations.** Templates that touch `request.user.profile`, `request.user.tenant` on every request add queries per request. Fix: a custom `AuthenticationMiddleware`/backend `get_user()` with `select_related`, or cache what you need in the session.\n\n**Debugging.**\n- `python manage.py shell`: `from django.contrib.auth.hashers import identify_hasher; identify_hasher(u.password)`.\n- Inspect a session: `Session.objects.get(pk=key).get_decoded()` shows `_auth_user_id`/`_auth_user_backend`.\n- Connect to `user_login_failed` to log failed attempts (without the password) for alerting.\n- `manage.py check --deploy` flags insecure session/CSRF cookie settings ([Ch 28](topic.html?p=28-security)).\n\n**Scaling.** Store sessions in Redis (`cached_db` or `cache` engine) to remove a DB read per request ([Ch 27](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies)); keep the user table narrow and indexed on the login identifier; move SSO/MFA to a dedicated identity provider (OIDC) when you have many apps sharing users.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: Why should you create a custom user model at the start of a project?**\nA: Because `AUTH_USER_MODEL` determines the table every user FK points to, and the swappable dependency is baked into the initial migrations of your apps and third-party apps. Switching after the first `migrate` requires rewriting migration history and data migrations. Creating `class User(AbstractUser): pass` on day one costs nothing and lets you add fields or change the identifier later with normal migrations. The Django docs explicitly recommend it.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `AbstractUser` and `AbstractBaseUser`?**\nA: `AbstractUser` is the complete default user as an abstract model — username, names, email, `is_staff`, `is_active`, `date_joined`, `PermissionsMixin` and `UserManager` — so you subclass it to add fields. `AbstractBaseUser` only provides password handling, `last_login` and identity plumbing; you declare all fields, `USERNAME_FIELD`, `REQUIRED_FIELDS`, a `BaseUserManager` with `create_user`/`create_superuser`, and add `PermissionsMixin` if you want Django permissions. Use `AbstractUser` unless you need a different identity model such as email-only.\n\n**Q: When do you use `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL` versus `get_user_model()`?**\nA: Use the `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL` string for model relations (FK, O2O, M2M) and signal senders, because models.py is imported while the app registry is loading and the string is resolved lazily. Use `get_user_model()` at runtime — in views, services, forms, tests — to get the actual class. Never import `django.contrib.auth.models.User` directly in reusable code.\n\n**Q: How does Django store passwords?**\nA: As `algorithm$parameters$salt$hash`, e.g. `pbkdf2_sha256$<iterations>$<salt>$<hash>`, produced by the first hasher in `PASSWORD_HASHERS`. Each password gets a random salt, and the algorithm is deliberately slow (PBKDF2 with a very high iteration count, or memory-hard Argon2/scrypt) to make offline cracking expensive. Verification identifies the hasher from the prefix and uses a constant-time comparison. Plain text is never stored.\n\n**Q: How do you migrate users to a stronger hasher like Argon2?**\nA: Install `argon2-cffi` and put `Argon2PasswordHasher` first in `PASSWORD_HASHERS`, keeping the old hashers after it. New passwords use Argon2; on each successful `check_password`, Django sees the stored hash uses a non-preferred hasher (or outdated parameters) and re-hashes it with Argon2 transparently. For users who never log in, you can wrap old hashes (hash the existing hash) with a custom wrapper hasher, as the docs describe for legacy SHA1/MD5.\n\n**Q: What does `authenticate()` do and how do authentication backends work?**\nA: It iterates `AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS`, calling each backend's `authenticate(request, **credentials)` whose signature matches. The first backend returning a user wins (Django records which via `user.backend`); `None` means try the next; raising `PermissionDenied` stops the chain. `ModelBackend` looks up by `USERNAME_FIELD`, checks the password and that `is_active` is true. It only verifies — it doesn't log in; `login()` does that.\n\n**Q: What does `login()` do, and how does Django prevent session fixation?**\nA: `login()` cycles the session key (or flushes the session if it belonged to another user), stores the user id, backend path and session auth hash in the session, rotates the CSRF token, sets `request.user`, and fires `user_logged_in`, which updates `last_login`. Cycling the key means a session ID an attacker planted before login is useless afterwards — that is the fixation defence. `logout()` flushes the session entirely.\n\n**Q: Why does changing a password log a user out of other devices?**\nA: The session stores `_auth_user_hash`, derived from the user's password hash via `get_session_auth_hash()`. On each request `get_user()` compares it with the current value; after a password change they differ, so other sessions are invalidated. To keep the current session alive call `update_session_auth_hash(request, user)`, which `PasswordChangeView` does for you.\n\n**Q: How do password reset tokens work without being stored?**\nA: `PasswordResetTokenGenerator` creates a timestamp plus an HMAC (keyed with `SECRET_KEY`) over the user's pk, password hash, last login, email and timestamp. To verify, Django recomputes it and checks the age against `PASSWORD_RESET_TIMEOUT`. Changing the password or logging in changes the inputs, so a used token stops validating — effectively single-use with no database state. Rotating `SECRET_KEY` invalidates outstanding tokens.\n\n**Q: How do you implement email-based login?**\nA: Either make email the identifier on a custom `AbstractBaseUser` (or `AbstractUser`) model with `USERNAME_FIELD = \"email\"` and a unique, case-insensitive email, or keep usernames and add a backend that looks users up by email. Normalise emails on write, enforce case-insensitive uniqueness with a `Lower(\"email\")` unique constraint, make `get_by_natural_key` case-insensitive, and avoid leaking existence in error messages and reset flows.\n\n**Q: Your project has been running on `auth.User` for two years and now needs a custom user model. How do you migrate? (Senior)**\nA: Avoid if you can (a profile model may suffice). If necessary: create `accounts.User` with `db_table = \"auth_user\"` (and the M2M tables) so it reuses the existing table, matching `auth.User` fields exactly; create its initial migration and mark it applied with `migrate --fake` or by inserting into `django_migrations`; update `django_content_type` so the content type points at the new app/model; then switch `AUTH_USER_MODEL`, and only afterwards add new fields in normal migrations. Third-party apps' migrations reference `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL` already, so they keep working. Rehearse on a production snapshot, freeze deploys, and keep a rollback plan — it is a migration-history surgery, not a schema change.\n\n**Q: Design authentication for a SaaS product with email login, SSO for enterprise customers and MFA for admins. (Senior)**\nA: Custom user model with email identity, case-insensitive unique, `tenant` FK, `is_active` soft-disable. Backends: `ModelBackend`-based email backend for password users; an OIDC backend (e.g. mozilla-django-oidc or allauth) for enterprise tenants, with users provisioned just-in-time and `set_unusable_password()`; a tenant flag forcing SSO so password login is refused (a backend raising `PermissionDenied`). Argon2 hashing, length-based validators, login throttling per IP and account, and `user_login_failed` alerting. MFA via django-otp/allauth required for `is_staff`, and session security (`SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE`, short admin session age). APIs use JWT or session per [Ch 23](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling) on top of the same user model.\n\n**Q: Login requests are saturating CPU during an attack. What do you do? (Senior)**\nA: Hashing is deliberately expensive, so each guess costs you CPU; the answer is to stop guesses before hashing, not to weaken the hasher. Add per-IP and per-username rate limits at the gateway and in Django (django-axes, or a Redis counter in a custom backend that raises `PermissionDenied` before `check_password`), CAPTCHA after failures, and block known-bad IP ranges at the WAF. Make sure unknown usernames still hash once (timing parity) but are rate-limited too. Scale or isolate login workers so the rest of the site is unaffected, and alert on `user_login_failed` rates.\n\n**Q: Why does Django run the password hasher even when the user doesn't exist?**\nA: To make the response time for \"unknown user\" similar to \"wrong password\". If unknown users returned instantly, an attacker could enumerate valid usernames by timing. `ModelBackend` calls `UserModel().set_password(password)` in the `DoesNotExist` branch to spend the same hashing time. Custom backends should do the same.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Topic | Remember |\n|---|---|\n| Custom user | Always; set `AUTH_USER_MODEL` before first `migrate` |\n| `AbstractUser` | full default fields — add more |\n| `AbstractBaseUser` | password + last_login only; you add fields, manager, `PermissionsMixin` |\n| `USERNAME_FIELD` | unique identifier; `REQUIRED_FIELDS` excludes it and password |\n| References | FK: `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL`; runtime: `get_user_model()` |\n| Hash format | `algorithm$params$salt$hash`; first hasher hashes, others verify |\n| Upgrade | automatic re-hash on successful login |\n| `authenticate()` | backends in order; user wins, `None` next, `PermissionDenied` stops |\n| `login()` | cycle session key, store id/backend/hash, rotate CSRF, update last_login |\n| Password change | invalidates other sessions; `update_session_auth_hash` keeps current |\n| Reset token | HMAC of pk + password hash + last_login + email + timestamp; `PASSWORD_RESET_TIMEOUT` 3 days |\n| Logout | POST-only since 5.0 |\n\n- `set_password()` neither validates nor saves.\n- `create_user()`, never `create(password=...)`.\n- Deactivate with `is_active=False`; don't delete users.\n- Argon2 first in `PASSWORD_HASHERS`.\n- Case-insensitive unique email with `Lower(\"email\")`.\n- `LoginRequiredMiddleware` (5.1+) for login-by-default sites.\n- Rate-limit login; never weaken the hasher.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\" argon2-cffi\ndjango-admin startproject config . && python manage.py startapp accounts\n```\n\n1. **Day-one user.** Create `accounts.User(AbstractUser)`, set `AUTH_USER_MODEL`, migrate, and inspect the created tables (`\\dt` in psql or `sqlmigrate accounts 0001`).\n2. **Hasher upgrade.** Create a user with PBKDF2 first in `PASSWORD_HASHERS`, then put Argon2 first, log in, and watch the stored hash prefix change.\n3. **Email identity.** Build the `AbstractBaseUser` email model with its manager; run `createsuperuser`; add the `Lower(\"email\")` constraint and prove `Ann@x.com` and `ann@x.com` cannot both register.\n4. **Session fixation.** Log the session key before and after `login()` in a view and confirm it changes; change the password in another browser and confirm the first session is logged out.\n5. **Reset tokens.** Generate a reset token in the shell, verify it, log the user in (updating `last_login`), and verify the token now fails.\n\n**Mini project — \"Accounts service\".** Email-login custom user with tenant FK; sign-up, login, POST logout, password change and reset (console email backend); Argon2; validators; a username-or-email backend; login throttling via a Redis counter that raises `PermissionDenied`; `user_login_failed` logging; `LoginRequiredMiddleware` with `@login_not_required` on public pages; tests for every flow using `get_user_model()`.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations) · [Ch 15 · Custom Managers, QuerySets & Model Inheritance](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance) · [Ch 23 · DRF Authentication, Permissions & Throttling](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling) · [Ch 26 · Permissions, Groups & Authorization](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization) · [Ch 27 · Sessions, Cookies & Messages](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies) · [Ch 28 · Security: CSRF, XSS, SQL Injection & Hardening](topic.html?p=28-security) · [Ch 37 · Interview: Core & ORM](topic.html?p=37-interview-core-orm)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · Authentication Basics](../rest-api/topic.html?p=18-authentication-basics) · [REST API · OAuth 2.0 & OIDC](../rest-api/topic.html?p=19-oauth2-oidc) · [REST API · OWASP API Security](../rest-api/topic.html?p=23-owasp-api-security)\n\n- **User authentication in Django** — Django docs · *Beginner* · overview of users, login, logout and views. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/auth/>\n- **Customizing authentication in Django** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · custom user models, backends, and the \"substituting a custom User model\" guidance. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/auth/customizing/>\n- **Password management in Django** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · hashers, upgrading, validators, wrapping legacy hashes. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/auth/passwords/>\n- **Using the Django authentication system** — Django docs · *Beginner* · `authenticate`, `login`, decorators, built-in views. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/auth/default/>\n- **OWASP Password Storage Cheat Sheet** — OWASP · *Intermediate* · why Argon2id/scrypt/PBKDF2 and how to tune them. <https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Password_Storage_Cheat_Sheet.html>\n- **django-allauth** — pennersr on GitHub · *Intermediate* · email verification, social login, MFA. <https://github.com/pennersr/django-allauth>\n- **django-axes** — jazzband on GitHub · *Intermediate* · login failure tracking and lockout. <https://github.com/jazzband/django-axes>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 25.*\n", "26-permissions-authorization": "# 26 · Permissions, Groups & Authorization\n\n> **In one line:** Authentication tells you *who* is calling; authorization decides *what they may do to which object* — Django gives you model-level permissions, groups and a pluggable backend chain for free, but object-level and tenant-level rules are your job, and the safest place to enforce them is the queryset, not the template.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user) (users, backends, `AUTH_USER_MODEL`), [Ch 17 · Class-Based Views](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views) (mixins and `dispatch`), [Ch 07 · The Django Admin](topic.html?p=07-django-admin) (where model permissions are most visible).\n\nEvery non-trivial app eventually needs to answer the question \"may this user do this thing?\". The naive answer is a scattering of `if request.user.is_staff:` checks in views and `{% if user.is_superuser %}` in templates. It works for the first week. Then a product manager asks for \"editors who can publish but not delete\", a support role that can *view* orders but not refund them, and customers who must only ever see *their own* invoices. The boolean flags cannot express any of that, the checks drift apart between views, templates and the API, and one forgotten `if` in a new endpoint becomes an **IDOR** (insecure direct object reference) — the single most common authorization bug in web applications.\n\nDjango's answer is a small, layered system inside `django.contrib.auth`:\n\n- A **`Permission`** table with one row per *(model, action)* — `add`, `change`, `delete` and `view` are generated automatically for every model, and you can declare custom ones like `publish_article`.\n- **Groups** that bundle permissions so you assign *roles*, not individual rights — this is Django's built-in **RBAC**.\n- **`user.has_perm(\"app_label.codename\")`**, which asks every configured **authentication backend** in turn. The default `ModelBackend` answers from the user's own permissions plus their groups' permissions.\n- Decorators and mixins (`@permission_required`, `PermissionRequiredMixin`, `LoginRequiredMixin`, `UserPassesTestMixin`) to enforce checks at the view boundary, plus the `perms` proxy in templates for hiding UI.\n\nWhat Django does **not** do out of the box is **object-level permissions** — \"Alice may edit *this* article but not *that* one\". The `has_perm(perm, obj)` signature exists, but `ModelBackend` returns `False` for any call that passes an object. You fill that gap with ownership rules in `get_queryset()`, with a rules engine such as **django-rules**, or with a per-row permission table such as **django-guardian**. In multi-tenant SaaS, the most important authorization rule of all — \"never show tenant A's data to tenant B\" — is not a permission at all; it is a mandatory filter on every queryset.\n\nThis chapter covers the mechanism end to end: how permissions are created, how `has_perm` resolves them (and caches them), how to enforce them in function views, CBVs, templates, the admin and DRF, how to do object-level and tenant-scoped authorization correctly, and the production failures (stale permission caches, missing permissions in data migrations, 403-vs-404 leaks) interviewers love to probe.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Authentication vs authorization** — authentication establishes identity (`request.user`); authorization decides whether that identity may perform an action. *Why it matters:* interviewers open with this distinction; mixing them up is a red flag.\n- **`Permission` model** — rows of `(name, content_type, codename)`, e.g. codename `change_article` for content type `blog.article`. *Why:* permissions are data, so you can assign them at runtime without a deploy.\n- **Default permissions** — `add_`, `change_`, `delete_`, `view_` per model, created by a `post_migrate` handler. `view` was added in Django 2.1. *Why:* the admin and `DjangoModelPermissions` rely on them; `Meta.default_permissions = ()` disables them.\n- **Custom permissions** — `Meta.permissions = [(\"publish_article\", \"Can publish article\")]`. *Why:* model actions beyond CRUD (\"publish\", \"refund\", \"export\") deserve named rights.\n- **Group** — a named set of permissions; users get the union of their direct and group permissions. *Why:* assign roles once instead of 40 permissions per new hire.\n- **`is_active`, `is_staff`, `is_superuser`** — inactive users have no permissions; `is_staff` only gates admin *login*; an active superuser has *every* permission. *Why:* `is_staff` is not an authorization system.\n- **`has_perm` / `has_perms` / `has_module_perms`** — methods on `PermissionsMixin` that delegate to backends. Permission strings are `\"<app_label>.<codename>\"`. *Why:* the most common bug is passing `\"change_article\"` without the app label — it silently returns `False`.\n- **Authentication backend** — any class in `AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS`; it may implement `has_perm`, `get_all_permissions`, etc. *Why:* this is the extension point for object permissions (guardian, rules).\n- **`PermissionDenied`** — raising it in a backend's `has_perm` short-circuits the chain to `False`; raising it in a view produces a 403. *Why:* explicit deny beats \"nobody said yes\".\n- **Object-level permission** — a rule about a specific row. Not supported by `ModelBackend`. *Why:* almost every real app needs it.\n- **Queryset scoping** — filtering `get_queryset()` by owner or tenant so unauthorized rows are simply not reachable. *Why:* it fixes IDOR at the root and protects list endpoints that never call an object check.\n- **RBAC vs ABAC** — role-based (groups) vs attribute-based (rules over user, object and context attributes). *Why:* design rounds ask which you would choose; see [REST API · Authorization: RBAC & ABAC](../rest-api/topic.html?p=21-authorization-rbac-abac).\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### How permissions come into existence\n\nPermissions are not created by `makemigrations`. After every `migrate`, Django fires the `post_migrate` signal, and `django.contrib.auth`'s handler `create_permissions()` walks every installed model, computes the default and `Meta.permissions` codenames, and `bulk_create`s whatever rows are missing. It **never deletes** rows. Consequences:\n\n1. In a fresh test database permissions exist only after migrations finish — a `RunPython` data migration that does `Permission.objects.get(codename=\"publish_article\")` can fail with `DoesNotExist` because `post_migrate` has not run yet for that app.\n2. Renaming a model or a codename leaves the old `Permission` row behind and creates a new one; groups still point at the old row. You must migrate assignments yourself.\n3. Permissions are keyed by `ContentType`, which is keyed by `(app_label, model)`. Proxy models get their own content type and permissions (since Django 2.2).\n\n### How `user.has_perm()` resolves\n\n`PermissionsMixin.has_perm(perm, obj=None)` does three things:\n\n1. If the user `is_active` and `is_superuser`, return `True` immediately — **even when `obj` is passed**.\n2. Otherwise call `django.contrib.auth.models._user_has_perm(user, perm, obj)`, which iterates `auth.get_backends()` in `AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS` order.\n3. For each backend that defines `has_perm`: if it returns `True`, the answer is `True`; if it raises `PermissionDenied`, the answer is `False` and remaining backends are skipped; otherwise continue. If nobody says yes, `False`.\n\n`ModelBackend.has_perm` checks `user_obj.is_active` and then whether `perm` is in `get_all_permissions(user_obj, obj)`. That method returns an **empty set whenever `obj is not None`** — which is exactly why `ModelBackend` \"does not support object permissions\". For `obj=None`, it builds the set from two queries (user permissions and group permissions) and caches them on the user instance as `_user_perm_cache`, `_group_perm_cache` and `_perm_cache`.\n\nThe diagram shows the resolution chain with a third-party object backend plugged in.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj26a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj26b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj26c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">user.has_perm(\"blog.change_article\", obj) resolution</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"190\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"115\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">PermissionsMixin.has_perm</text>\n  <text x=\"115\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">perm string + optional obj</text>\n  <path d=\"M210,72 L262,72\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj26a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"264\" y=\"46\" width=\"200\" height=\"52\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"364\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">is_active and is_superuser?</text>\n  <text x=\"364\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">checked before any backend</text>\n  <path d=\"M464,72 L560,72\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj26b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"512\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">yes</text>\n  <rect x=\"562\" y=\"50\" width=\"270\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"697\" y=\"70\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">return True</text>\n  <text x=\"697\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">even for object checks</text>\n  <path d=\"M364,98 L364,130\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj26a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">no: _user_has_perm iterates AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"134\" width=\"370\" height=\"130\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"154\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. ModelBackend</text>\n  <text x=\"56\" y=\"176\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">obj is not None: get_all_permissions returns empty set</text>\n  <text x=\"56\" y=\"194\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">obj is None: user_permissions UNION group permissions</text>\n  <text x=\"56\" y=\"212\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">2 queries first time, then cached in _perm_cache</text>\n  <text x=\"56\" y=\"230\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">cache lives on THIS user instance only</text>\n  <text x=\"56\" y=\"250\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"10\">returns True or falls through</text>\n  <rect x=\"450\" y=\"134\" width=\"380\" height=\"130\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"640\" y=\"154\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. Object backend (guardian / rules)</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"176\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">guardian: looks up UserObjectPermission rows</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"194\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">  and GroupObjectPermission rows for (perm, obj)</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"212\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">rules: evaluates a predicate, e.g. is_author(user, obj)</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"230\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">may raise PermissionDenied = hard deny</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"250\" fill=\"#7c3aed\" font-size=\"10\">returns True, None/False, or raises</text>\n  <path d=\"M410,200 L448,200\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj26a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M225,264 L225,310\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj26b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M640,264 L640,310\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj26c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"312\" width=\"370\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"336\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">first backend returning True wins</text>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"356\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">order of AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS matters</text>\n  <rect x=\"450\" y=\"312\" width=\"380\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"640\" y=\"336\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">PermissionDenied or nobody says yes: False</text>\n  <text x=\"640\" y=\"356\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">default is deny</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Principles worth stating in an interview\n\n- **Default deny.** Nothing is allowed unless a backend affirmatively says yes. Your own checks should follow the same shape: start from \"no\", add explicit allows.\n- **Enforce on the server, at the narrowest point.** Hiding a button in a template is UX, not security. The authoritative check belongs in the view (and ideally in the queryset), so that the API, the admin and a hand-crafted `curl` all hit it.\n- **Model permissions are coarse; object rules are domain logic.** \"Can change articles\" is a role; \"can change *this* article because they wrote it and it is unpublished\" is a rule. Keep both, and combine them: model permission as the gate, object rule as the refinement.\n- **Scope before you check.** A list endpoint never calls an object check. If `get_queryset()` returns every row, `has_object_permission` on the detail view protects nothing on the list view.\n- **Least privilege.** Superuser is a break-glass account, not a role. Staff + specific groups for humans; service accounts with only the permissions their job needs.\n\n### Permission caching semantics\n\nBecause `ModelBackend` caches on the *instance*, adding a permission to a user during a request does not take effect on that same `user` object:\n\n```python\nuser.user_permissions.add(Permission.objects.get(codename=\"publish_article\"))\nuser.has_perm(\"blog.publish_article\")   # False - _perm_cache already populated\nuser = User.objects.get(pk=user.pk)     # re-fetch to drop the cache\nuser.has_perm(\"blog.publish_article\")   # True\n```\n\nAcross requests this is a non-issue: `AuthenticationMiddleware` loads a fresh user from the session on every request (lazily, via `SimpleLazyObject`). It does mean every request that calls `has_perm` pays two permission queries on first use. That is fine for most apps; at high request rates you can cache the permission set yourself (keyed on user id plus a version you bump on role changes).\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\nAuthorization is not a single check but a set of layers, each catching a different class of bug. A well-built Django request passes through all of them.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 860 430\" width=\"100%\" height=\"430\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj26d\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Layered authorization for GET/PATCH /articles/42/</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"560\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"66\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Layer 1 - Authentication (middleware)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"86\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">AuthenticationMiddleware sets request.user; LoginRequiredMiddleware (5.1+) or LoginRequiredMixin</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"114\" width=\"560\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"136\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Layer 2 - Model / role permission (view entry)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"156\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">PermissionRequiredMixin: has_perm(\"blog.change_article\") via groups; fail = 403</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"184\" width=\"560\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"206\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Layer 3 - Queryset scoping (get_queryset)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"226\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Article.objects.filter(tenant=request.tenant); other tenants' rows are unreachable = 404</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"254\" width=\"560\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"276\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Layer 4 - Object rule (get_object + check)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"296\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">has_perm(\"blog.change_article\", article): author, or editor while unpublished</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"324\" width=\"560\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"346\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Layer 5 - Field / state rules (form or serializer)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"366\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">only editors may set status=published; read-only fields per role</text>\n  <path d=\"M600,72 L600,352\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj26d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"620\" y=\"44\" width=\"220\" height=\"336\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"730\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">What each layer catches</text>\n  <text x=\"634\" y=\"92\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">1: anonymous access</text>\n  <text x=\"634\" y=\"162\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">2: wrong role entirely</text>\n  <text x=\"634\" y=\"226\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">3: cross-tenant IDOR,</text>\n  <text x=\"634\" y=\"240\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">   list-endpoint leaks</text>\n  <text x=\"634\" y=\"296\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">4: same tenant, not owner</text>\n  <text x=\"634\" y=\"366\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">5: privilege escalation</text>\n  <text x=\"634\" y=\"380\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">   via extra fields</text>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"410\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Templates ({% if perms.blog.change_article %}) only hide buttons - they are never a layer.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Where the check lives in each Django surface\n\n| Surface | Gate (role) | Object / scope | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Function view | `@login_required`, `@permission_required(\"blog.change_article\", raise_exception=True)` | `get_object_or_404(Article.objects.filter(owner=request.user), pk=pk)` | `user_passes_test` for arbitrary predicates |\n| Class-based view | `LoginRequiredMixin`, `PermissionRequiredMixin` | override `get_queryset()`; `has_permission()` | mixins must come **left** of the view class |\n| Template | `{% if perms.blog.change_article %}` | `{% if article.author_id == user.id %}` | cosmetic only |\n| Admin | model perms automatically; `has_change_permission(request, obj)` | `get_queryset(request)` | `is_staff` needed to log in at all |\n| DRF | `permission_classes`, `DjangoModelPermissions` | `get_queryset()`, `has_object_permission` | object check skipped for list; see [Ch 23](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling) |\n\n### Decorator and mixin flow\n\n```text\nrequest ──▶ LoginRequiredMixin.dispatch\n              └─ not authenticated? ──▶ handle_no_permission() ──▶ 302 to LOGIN_URL?next=...\n          ──▶ PermissionRequiredMixin.dispatch\n              └─ not has_permission()? ──▶ handle_no_permission()\n                       authenticated + raise_exception=False ──▶ 403 PermissionDenied\n          ──▶ View.dispatch ──▶ get()/post() ──▶ get_object() ──▶ get_queryset() (scoped)\n```\n\n`AccessMixin.handle_no_permission()` redirects anonymous users to the login page and raises `PermissionDenied` (403) for authenticated users when `raise_exception` is false — the sensible default since Django 2.1. The function decorator `@permission_required` is cruder: without `raise_exception=True` it redirects *authenticated* users to the login page too, which creates login loops.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: custom permissions, groups, and view checks\n\n```python\n# blog/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\n\n\nclass Article(models.Model):\n    class Status(models.TextChoices):\n        DRAFT = \"draft\", \"Draft\"\n        PUBLISHED = \"published\", \"Published\"\n\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    body = models.TextField()\n    author = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"articles\")\n    status = models.CharField(max_length=10, choices=Status, default=Status.DRAFT)\n\n    class Meta:\n        permissions = [\n            (\"publish_article\", \"Can publish article\"),\n            (\"export_article\", \"Can export articles\"),\n        ]\n```\n\nCreate roles in a data migration. Because default and custom permissions are created by `post_migrate`, the migration must create them explicitly first (or depend on a later step). The reliable trick is to call `create_permissions` for the app inside the migration:\n\n```python\n# blog/migrations/0003_roles.py\nfrom django.contrib.auth.management import create_permissions\nfrom django.db import migrations\n\n\ndef create_roles(apps, schema_editor):\n    for app_config in apps.get_app_configs():\n        app_config.models_module = True          # required by create_permissions\n        create_permissions(app_config, apps=apps, verbosity=0)\n        app_config.models_module = None\n\n    Group = apps.get_model(\"auth\", \"Group\")\n    Permission = apps.get_model(\"auth\", \"Permission\")\n    perms = lambda *codes: Permission.objects.filter(content_type__app_label=\"blog\", codename__in=codes)\n\n    writer, _ = Group.objects.get_or_create(name=\"Writer\")\n    writer.permissions.set(perms(\"add_article\", \"change_article\", \"view_article\"))\n\n    editor, _ = Group.objects.get_or_create(name=\"Editor\")\n    editor.permissions.set(perms(\"add_article\", \"change_article\", \"view_article\",\n                                 \"delete_article\", \"publish_article\"))\n\n\nclass Migration(migrations.Migration):\n    dependencies = [(\"blog\", \"0002_article_status\"), (\"auth\", \"0012_alter_user_first_name_max_length\"),\n                    (\"contenttypes\", \"0002_remove_content_type_name\")]\n    operations = [migrations.RunPython(create_roles, migrations.RunPython.noop)]\n```\n\nEnforce in views:\n\n```python\n# blog/views.py\nfrom django.contrib.auth.decorators import login_required, permission_required\nfrom django.contrib.auth.mixins import LoginRequiredMixin, PermissionRequiredMixin\nfrom django.shortcuts import get_object_or_404, redirect\nfrom django.views.generic import UpdateView\n\nfrom .models import Article\n\n\n@login_required\n@permission_required(\"blog.publish_article\", raise_exception=True)\ndef publish(request, pk):\n    article = get_object_or_404(Article, pk=pk)\n    if request.method == \"POST\":\n        article.status = Article.Status.PUBLISHED\n        article.save(update_fields=[\"status\"])\n    return redirect(\"article-detail\", pk=pk)\n\n\nclass ArticleUpdate(LoginRequiredMixin, PermissionRequiredMixin, UpdateView):\n    model = Article\n    fields = [\"title\", \"body\"]\n    permission_required = \"blog.change_article\"\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        qs = super().get_queryset()\n        if self.request.user.has_perm(\"blog.publish_article\"):   # editors see all\n            return qs\n        return qs.filter(author=self.request.user)               # writers: own only -> others 404\n```\n\nAnd in templates (the `auth` context processor provides `perms`):\n\n```html\n{% if perms.blog.publish_article and article.status == \"draft\" %}\n  <form method=\"post\" action=\"{% url 'article-publish' article.pk %}\">\n    {% csrf_token %}<button>Publish</button>\n  </form>\n{% endif %}\n{% if perms.blog %}<a href=\"{% url 'article-dashboard' %}\">Blog dashboard</a>{% endif %}\n```\n\nQuick shell check of the resolution behaviour:\n\n```text\n>>> u = User.objects.get(username=\"wendy\"); u.groups.add(Group.objects.get(name=\"Writer\"))\n>>> u = User.objects.get(pk=u.pk)\n>>> u.has_perm(\"blog.change_article\")\nTrue\n>>> u.has_perm(\"change_article\")          # missing app label\nFalse\n>>> u.has_perm(\"blog.change_article\", some_article)   # ModelBackend ignores objects\nFalse\n>>> u.get_all_permissions()\n{'blog.add_article', 'blog.change_article', 'blog.view_article'}\n```\n\n### Real-world example: object rules with django-rules, plus tenant scoping\n\ndjango-rules gives you object-level permissions as **predicates** — plain Python functions — without any extra tables. It plugs in as a backend, so `has_perm(perm, obj)`, `PermissionRequiredMixin` and DRF's object checks all just work.\n\n```bash\npip install \"django~=5.2\" django-rules\n```\n\n```python\n# settings.py\nINSTALLED_APPS += [\"rules.apps.AutodiscoverRulesConfig\"]   # imports each app's rules.py\nAUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS = [\n    \"rules.permissions.ObjectPermissionBackend\",\n    \"django.contrib.auth.backends.ModelBackend\",\n]\n```\n\n```python\n# blog/rules.py\nimport rules\n\n\n@rules.predicate\ndef is_author(user, article):\n    return article is not None and article.author_id == user.id\n\n\n@rules.predicate\ndef is_draft(user, article):\n    return article is not None and article.status == \"draft\"\n\n\nis_editor = rules.is_group_member(\"Editor\")\nsame_tenant = rules.predicate(lambda user, obj: obj is None or obj.tenant_id == user.tenant_id)\n\nrules.add_perm(\"blog.view_article\", same_tenant)\nrules.add_perm(\"blog.change_article\", same_tenant & (is_editor | (is_author & is_draft)))\nrules.add_perm(\"blog.delete_article\", same_tenant & is_editor)\nrules.add_perm(\"blog.publish_article\", same_tenant & is_editor)\n```\n\nNote the `obj is None` handling: `PermissionRequiredMixin` calls `has_perms` *without* an object at dispatch time. Predicates that crash on `None` break the whole view. With django-rules you can make CBVs check against the object by using its mixin:\n\n```python\n# blog/views.py\nfrom rules.contrib.views import PermissionRequiredMixin as ObjectPermissionRequiredMixin\n\n\nclass TenantScopedMixin:\n    \"\"\"Every queryset is filtered by the request's tenant. Non-negotiable.\"\"\"\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return super().get_queryset().filter(tenant_id=self.request.user.tenant_id)\n\n\nclass ArticleEdit(LoginRequiredMixin, TenantScopedMixin, ObjectPermissionRequiredMixin, UpdateView):\n    model = Article\n    fields = [\"title\", \"body\"]\n    permission_required = \"blog.change_article\"   # checked against self.get_object()\n    raise_exception = True\n```\n\nThe generated SQL for the scoped detail lookup shows why cross-tenant guessing returns 404 rather than 403 — the row is never fetched:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"blog_article\".\"id\", \"blog_article\".\"title\", \"blog_article\".\"body\",\n       \"blog_article\".\"author_id\", \"blog_article\".\"status\", \"blog_article\".\"tenant_id\"\nFROM \"blog_article\"\nWHERE (\"blog_article\".\"tenant_id\" = 7 AND \"blog_article\".\"id\" = 42)\nLIMIT 21;\n```\n\nThe same rules power DRF. `has_object_permission` delegates to `has_perm(perm, obj)`, and the list view relies on `get_queryset()`:\n\n```python\n# blog/api.py\nfrom rest_framework import permissions, viewsets\nfrom .models import Article\nfrom .serializers import ArticleSerializer\n\n\nclass RulesObjectPermission(permissions.BasePermission):\n    action_map = {\"retrieve\": \"view\", \"update\": \"change\", \"partial_update\": \"change\", \"destroy\": \"delete\"}\n\n    def has_permission(self, request, view):\n        return request.user.is_authenticated\n\n    def has_object_permission(self, request, view, obj):\n        action = self.action_map.get(view.action, \"view\")\n        return request.user.has_perm(f\"blog.{action}_article\", obj)\n\n\nclass ArticleViewSet(viewsets.ModelViewSet):\n    serializer_class = ArticleSerializer\n    permission_classes = [RulesObjectPermission]\n\n    def get_queryset(self):   # list endpoint never calls has_object_permission\n        return Article.objects.filter(tenant_id=self.request.user.tenant_id).select_related(\"author\")\n\n    def perform_create(self, serializer):\n        serializer.save(author=self.request.user, tenant_id=self.request.user.tenant_id)\n```\n\n### When you need per-row grants: django-guardian\n\nRules are computed; sometimes grants are *data* — \"share this document with Bob\". django-guardian stores `(user|group, permission, content_type, object_pk)` rows:\n\n```python\n# settings: AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS = [\"django.contrib.auth.backends.ModelBackend\",\n#                                      \"guardian.backends.ObjectPermissionBackend\"]\nfrom guardian.shortcuts import assign_perm, get_objects_for_user, remove_perm\n\nassign_perm(\"docs.view_document\", bob, doc)\nbob.has_perm(\"docs.view_document\", doc)        # True\nvisible = get_objects_for_user(bob, \"docs.view_document\", klass=Document)  # queryset, use for lists\nremove_perm(\"docs.view_document\", bob, doc)\n```\n\nUse `get_objects_for_user` (a subquery, not a Python loop) for list views. Guardian's generic `object_pk` is a `CharField`, so for large tables use its **direct foreign key** models (`UserObjectPermissionBase` subclasses) to get real FKs and indexes.\n\n### Admin integration\n\n```python\n# blog/admin.py\nfrom django.contrib import admin\nfrom .models import Article\n\n\n@admin.register(Article)\nclass ArticleAdmin(admin.ModelAdmin):\n    list_display = [\"title\", \"author\", \"status\"]\n\n    def get_queryset(self, request):\n        qs = super().get_queryset(request)\n        return qs if request.user.is_superuser else qs.filter(tenant_id=request.user.tenant_id)\n\n    def has_change_permission(self, request, obj=None):\n        if not super().has_change_permission(request, obj):\n            return False\n        return obj is None or request.user.has_perm(\"blog.change_article\", obj)\n\n    def get_readonly_fields(self, request, obj=None):\n        return [] if request.user.has_perm(\"blog.publish_article\") else [\"status\"]\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Approach | Strengths | Weaknesses | Best for |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `is_staff` / `is_superuser` flags | Zero setup | Two roles, no granularity | Admin login, break-glass only |\n| Model permissions + Groups (built-in) | Data-driven, admin UI, works with DRF `DjangoModelPermissions` | No object level; permission rows drift on renames | Role-based internal tools |\n| Ownership in `get_queryset()` | Fast (one indexed filter), fixes IDOR, protects lists | Logic spread across views unless shared mixin | Every app, always |\n| django-rules (predicates) | No DB tables, readable, testable, object-aware | Not queryable: cannot turn a predicate into a filter automatically | Rule-based ABAC, \"author or editor while draft\" |\n| django-guardian (per-row grants) | Ad-hoc sharing, admin integration, queryable via `get_objects_for_user` | Extra table grows with rows x users; generic FK slow without direct FK models | Document sharing, explicit ACLs |\n| External policy engine (OPA, Oso, OpenFGA) | Central policy across services, relationship graphs | Network hop, operational cost, new language | Many services, Zanzibar-style sharing |\n\n### When to use\n\n- **Built-in permissions + groups** whenever you have staff roles and the admin; it is free and auditors understand it.\n- **Queryset scoping** on every endpoint that returns user- or tenant-owned data, without exception.\n- **django-rules** when access depends on attributes (author, status, tenant, time) and grants are not stored per row.\n- **django-guardian** when users explicitly share specific objects with other users or groups.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Do not use guardian to express \"owner can edit\" — that is a predicate, and storing a row per object per owner wastes space and drifts.\n- Do not use model permissions as tenant isolation — `has_perm(\"blog.view_article\")` is true for every tenant's articles.\n- Do not reach for an external policy engine in a single monolith with a handful of roles; it adds latency to every request.\n- Do not rely on template checks or hidden form fields for anything security-relevant.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Permission string without the app label.** People write `has_perm(\"change_article\")`; it never matches because permission strings are `\"app_label.codename\"`. It fails closed, so you see mysterious 403s. Always use `\"blog.change_article\"`, and add a test that asserts a role *can* do its job, not only that strangers cannot.\n\n**2. Checking the object in the detail view only.** The detail endpoint calls `get_object()` and an object check; the list endpoint returns `Article.objects.all()`. Attackers just call the list. Fix: scope `get_queryset()`, which both views share.\n\n```python\n# Before: IDOR on the list, object check only on detail\nclass InvoiceViewSet(viewsets.ReadOnlyModelViewSet):\n    queryset = Invoice.objects.all()\n    permission_classes = [IsOwner]           # has_object_permission only\n\n# After: unauthorized rows are unreachable everywhere\nclass InvoiceViewSet(viewsets.ReadOnlyModelViewSet):\n    permission_classes = [IsAuthenticated]\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return Invoice.objects.filter(customer__user=self.request.user)\n```\n\n**3. Mixin order.** `class V(UpdateView, LoginRequiredMixin)` — `UpdateView.dispatch` runs first and the mixin's `dispatch` is never reached before the handler. Access mixins go **leftmost**.\n\n**4. `@permission_required` without `raise_exception=True`.** An authenticated user without the permission is redirected to the login page, logs in, is redirected back, and loops. Use `raise_exception=True` for authenticated areas.\n\n**5. Using `is_staff` as \"admin role\".** `is_staff` only lets a user log into the admin; what they can do there is controlled by permissions. Granting `is_staff` + superuser to support agents is how a support account deletes production data.\n\n**6. Data migration referencing permissions that do not exist yet.** In a fresh database `Permission.objects.get(...)` fails. Call `create_permissions` inside the migration as shown, or create the roles in a management command run after `migrate`.\n\n**7. Expecting a just-granted permission to apply to the same user instance.** `_perm_cache` holds the old set. Re-fetch the user (`User.objects.get(pk=...)`) after changing permissions in the same request or test.\n\n**8. Returning 403 for other tenants' objects.** A 403 confirms the object exists, enabling enumeration. For resources the user should not know about, return 404 (which scoping gives you for free); return 403 when the user can see the object but not perform the action.\n\n**9. Field-level escalation.** A writer's edit form includes `status`, so a writer POSTs `status=published`. Per-role `fields` / `get_form_class()` / serializer `read_only_fields`, and validate state transitions server-side.\n\n**Best practices:** one `TenantScopedMixin` (or custom manager method `for_user(user)`) used everywhere; roles defined in code (migration or command) so every environment matches; deny-by-default DRF setting `DEFAULT_PERMISSION_CLASSES = [\"rest_framework.permissions.IsAuthenticated\"]`; consider `LoginRequiredMiddleware` (Django 5.1+) with `@login_not_required` on public views so new views are private by default; test authorization with a matrix of (role x action x object owner).\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Production story — the list endpoint leak.** A B2B app had `has_object_permission` checks on every detail route and a pentest passed. Six months later a mobile developer added `?ids=1,2,3` bulk fetching to the list route. *Symptom:* customer reports seeing another company's invoices. *Root cause:* the list `get_queryset()` filtered only by `ids`, never by tenant; object checks never ran. *Fix:* a base viewset that raises `ImproperlyConfigured` if a subclass's queryset is not filtered by tenant, plus a test that hits every registered route as a user from another tenant and asserts 404/empty.\n\n**Stale permissions after a rename.** Renaming `Post` to `Article` created `change_article` while groups still held `change_post`. *Symptom:* editors suddenly get 403 after deploy. *Fix:* in the rename migration, update the existing `Permission` rows' codename and the `ContentType.model` instead of letting new ones be created; run `manage.py remove_stale_contenttypes` afterwards to clean leftovers (it cascades to their permissions — check first).\n\n**Permission query overhead.** Each request that calls `has_perm` costs two queries (user perms, group perms) on first call. In a template loop over 50 rows calling a custom `can_edit` that re-fetches the user, it becomes 100. *Debug:* django-debug-toolbar's SQL panel, or `assertNumQueries` in tests. *Fix:* call `has_perm` once per request and pass the boolean down; never re-fetch the user per row.\n\n**Object-permission tables at scale.** guardian's `UserObjectPermission` table is `rows x grantees`. With millions of documents shared to whole organizations, grant to *groups*, use direct-FK permission models, and index `(permission_id, user_id, object_pk)`. When sharing becomes graph-shaped (folders containing documents shared via teams), move to a relationship-based system (Zanzibar-style, e.g. OpenFGA/SpiceDB) rather than exploding rows.\n\n**Debugging a 403.**\n\n```python\nfrom django.contrib.auth import get_backends\nu = User.objects.get(username=\"wendy\")\nprint(u.is_active, u.is_superuser, list(u.groups.values_list(\"name\", flat=True)))\nprint(sorted(u.get_all_permissions()))\nfor b in get_backends():\n    print(type(b).__name__, getattr(b, \"has_perm\", None) and b.has_perm(u, \"blog.change_article\", article))\n```\n\nLog authorization denials (user id, permission, object id, route) at INFO with a structured logger; a spike in denials after a deploy is the earliest signal of a broken role migration, and a spike from one user is an enumeration attempt.\n\n**Auditing.** Regulated environments need \"who granted Alice refunds and when\". Permission and group M2M changes fire `m2m_changed`; record them to an audit table, or use django-simple-history / django-auditlog on your role models.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between authentication and authorization in Django?**\nA: Authentication answers \"who are you?\" — `authenticate()` iterates `AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS` to verify credentials, `login()` stores the user id in the session, and `AuthenticationMiddleware` puts the user on `request.user`. Authorization answers \"may you do this?\" — `has_perm`, groups, decorators, mixins and your own object rules. They share the backend mechanism: a backend can implement both `authenticate()` and `has_perm()`. A 401 means \"not authenticated\", a 403 means \"authenticated but not allowed\".\n\n**Q: What default permissions does Django create, and when are they created?**\nA: For every model, `add_<model>`, `change_<model>`, `delete_<model>` and `view_<model>` (view since 2.1), plus anything in `Meta.permissions`. They are created by the `create_permissions` handler connected to the `post_migrate` signal, not by a migration file. That is why they do not exist in the middle of a migration run on a fresh database, and why they are never deleted automatically when models are renamed or removed. `Meta.default_permissions` lets you change or disable the defaults.\n\n**Q: How does `user.has_perm()` work internally?**\nA: `PermissionsMixin.has_perm` first returns `True` for an active superuser. Otherwise it calls `_user_has_perm`, which loops over the backends in `AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS` order: the first backend returning `True` grants, a backend raising `PermissionDenied` denies immediately, and if nobody grants the result is `False`. `ModelBackend` builds the set of the user's direct and group permissions with two queries and caches it on the user instance. Permission strings must be `\"app_label.codename\"`.\n\n**Q: Does Django support object-level permissions out of the box?**\nA: The API does — `has_perm(perm, obj)` and `get_all_permissions(obj)` accept an object — but the default `ModelBackend` returns an empty set whenever an object is passed, so object checks return `False` (except for superusers, who short-circuit to `True`). You implement object level by scoping querysets by ownership, by adding a backend like django-rules (predicates) or django-guardian (per-row grant table), or by writing your own backend. Always combine it with queryset scoping, because list views never perform object checks.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `is_staff`, `is_superuser` and permissions?**\nA: `is_staff` only controls whether the user can log into the admin site. `is_superuser` makes an active user pass every `has_perm` check without any explicit assignment. Permissions are the granular grants that decide what a staff user can actually do in the admin and what your views allow. A staff user with no permissions can log into the admin and sees nothing. Inactive users (`is_active=False`) have no permissions at all from `ModelBackend`, even if they are superusers.\n\n**Q: How do `LoginRequiredMixin`, `PermissionRequiredMixin` and `UserPassesTestMixin` differ, and why does mixin order matter?**\nA: All three extend `AccessMixin` and override `dispatch`. `LoginRequiredMixin` checks `is_authenticated`; `PermissionRequiredMixin` checks `has_perms(self.get_permission_required())` (override `has_permission()` for custom logic); `UserPassesTestMixin` runs your `test_func()`. On failure, `handle_no_permission()` redirects anonymous users to login and raises 403 for authenticated users. They must appear to the left of the view class in the bases, because Python's MRO calls the leftmost `dispatch` first; put them on the right and the view's own dispatch runs the handler before your check.\n\n**Q: How would you prevent users from accessing other users' objects (IDOR)?**\nA: Make unauthorized objects unreachable by filtering the queryset: `get_queryset()` returns `Invoice.objects.filter(owner=request.user)`, and `get_object_or_404` operates on that. Then guessing another id yields a 404, the list endpoint is protected by the same code, and no separate check can be forgotten. Add object checks only for finer rules (an owner can view but only an editor can delete). Test it explicitly: log in as user B and request user A's URLs.\n\n**Q: How do permissions work in Django templates?**\nA: The `django.contrib.auth.context_processors.auth` context processor exposes `user` and `perms`. `{% if perms.blog.change_article %}` calls `user.has_perm(\"blog.change_article\")`, and `{% if perms.blog %}` calls `has_module_perms(\"blog\")`. The `perms` proxy has no object-level syntax. Template checks are only for hiding UI; the view must enforce the same rule because anyone can send the request directly.\n\n**Q: How do DRF permissions relate to Django permissions?**\nA: DRF has its own permission classes with `has_permission(request, view)` (always runs in `initial()`) and `has_object_permission(request, view, obj)` (runs only when `get_object()` calls `check_object_permissions`). `DjangoModelPermissions` maps HTTP methods to Django model permissions — POST to `add`, PUT/PATCH to `change`, DELETE to `delete` — and by default does not require `view` for GET. `DjangoObjectPermissions` calls `has_perm(perm, obj)` and needs an object-capable backend. Details are in [Ch 23](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling).\n\n**Q: You added a permission to a user and `has_perm` still returns False. Why?**\nA: Most likely the permission cache on that user instance: `ModelBackend` stores the computed set in `_perm_cache` the first time you call `has_perm`, and adding a permission does not invalidate it. Re-fetch the user from the database. Other causes: missing app label in the string, the user is inactive, the permission belongs to a different content type (for example a proxy model), or a backend earlier in the chain raised `PermissionDenied`.\n\n**Q: How would you design authorization for a multi-tenant SaaS application in Django? (Senior)**\nA: Treat tenant isolation as a data-layer invariant, not a permission: resolve the tenant in middleware (from subdomain, header or the user's membership), and route every query through a tenant-aware manager or base viewset that filters by `tenant_id`, failing loudly if a view bypasses it. Within a tenant, use groups or a `Membership(user, tenant, role)` model for RBAC, and django-rules predicates for attribute rules (\"owner or admin of this tenant\"). Every unique constraint and index includes `tenant_id`. For defence in depth on PostgreSQL, add row-level security policies keyed on a session variable set per request, so a forgotten filter still returns nothing. Test with a cross-tenant crawler over every URL, and log denials.\n\n**Q: When would you choose django-guardian over django-rules, and what are the scaling concerns? (Senior)**\nA: Choose rules when access is *computed* from attributes already on the data — ownership, status, group membership, tenant — because predicates need no storage and never drift. Choose guardian when access is *granted* explicitly per object, like sharing a document with a colleague, because that fact must be stored. Guardian's tables grow with objects times grantees, and its generic `object_pk` CharField makes joins slow, so use direct-FK permission models, grant to groups rather than users, and use `get_objects_for_user` (a subquery) for lists. Rules' weakness is that predicates cannot be translated to SQL, so you still need a parallel queryset filter for list views. When sharing becomes hierarchical (folders, teams, inheritance), a relationship-based authorization service is a better fit than either.\n\n**Q: How do you handle permission changes when renaming a model, and how do you keep roles consistent across environments? (Senior)**\nA: Renaming a model changes the content type and the default codenames; `post_migrate` creates new permissions but leaves the old ones attached to groups, so users silently lose access. In the rename migration, update `ContentType.model` and the `Permission.codename`/`name` rows in place with `RunPython` using `apps.get_model`, so group assignments survive. Define roles in code — a data migration calling `create_permissions` first, or an idempotent `sync_roles` management command run in the release step — so dev, staging and prod have identical groups. Never assign permissions by hand in production except through an audited process.\n\n**Q: Should an unauthorized object request return 403 or 404? (Senior)**\nA: It depends on whether the user is allowed to know the object exists. For objects in another tenant or owned by another user, return 404 — a 403 confirms existence and enables enumeration of ids, which is why scoped querysets that naturally 404 are preferred. For objects the user can see but cannot modify (a viewer trying to delete), 403 is correct and more helpful. GitHub's private repositories are the classic example: they return 404 to non-members. Whatever you choose, be consistent and make sure timing and error bodies do not leak the difference.\n\n**Q: How would you implement \"editors can publish, writers can only edit their own drafts\"?**\nA: Two layers. Groups: Writer has `add/change/view_article`, Editor additionally has `publish_article` and `delete_article`. Object rule: `change_article` is allowed if the user is an editor, or is the author and the article is a draft — expressed as a django-rules predicate or as a `get_queryset()` filter plus a status check. The publish action requires `blog.publish_article`, and the writer's form excludes the `status` field so a crafted POST cannot publish. Test each role against each article state.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Need | Tool |\n|---|---|\n| Must be logged in | `@login_required`, `LoginRequiredMixin`, `LoginRequiredMiddleware` (5.1+) |\n| Role check | `@permission_required(\"app.codename\", raise_exception=True)`, `PermissionRequiredMixin` |\n| Arbitrary predicate | `@user_passes_test(fn)`, `UserPassesTestMixin.test_func` |\n| Custom permission | `Meta.permissions = [(\"publish_article\", \"Can publish article\")]` |\n| Role | `Group` + `group.permissions.set(...)`, `user.groups.add(...)` |\n| Ownership / tenant | `get_queryset().filter(owner=request.user)` |\n| Object rules | django-rules predicates + `rules.permissions.ObjectPermissionBackend` |\n| Per-object grants | django-guardian `assign_perm`, `get_objects_for_user` |\n| Template | `{% if perms.app.codename %}` (cosmetic) |\n| DRF | `has_permission` always; `has_object_permission` only in `get_object()` |\n\n- Permission strings are `\"app_label.codename\"`; wrong format returns `False` silently.\n- Permissions are created in `post_migrate`, never deleted automatically.\n- Active superusers pass every check, including object checks; inactive users pass none.\n- `ModelBackend` returns `False` for any object check — object level is your job.\n- Permission sets are cached per user instance; re-fetch after changing them.\n- Access mixins go leftmost in the class bases.\n- List views never run object checks: scope the queryset.\n- Prefer 404 for objects the user must not know exist; 403 for visible-but-forbidden.\n- `is_staff` = admin login only; it is not a role.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" django-rules djangorestframework`, `django-admin startproject site .`, `python manage.py startapp blog`.\n\n1. **Permission inventory.** Create `Article` with `Meta.permissions`, migrate, then in the shell print `Permission.objects.filter(content_type__app_label=\"blog\").values_list(\"codename\", flat=True)`. Set `default_permissions = (\"view\",)`, migrate again, and observe that old rows are not deleted.\n2. **Cache trap.** In a test, give a user `blog.publish_article` and assert `has_perm` is `False` on the same instance and `True` after re-fetching. Then write a role-matrix test (Writer/Editor/anonymous x view/change/publish/delete).\n3. **IDOR hunt.** Build an `InvoiceListView` and `InvoiceDetailView` that use `Invoice.objects.all()` with an ownership check only on detail. Write a test as user B that reads user A's invoices via the list; then fix with a shared `OwnedQuerysetMixin` and watch the test pass.\n4. **Mixin order.** Write `class Broken(UpdateView, LoginRequiredMixin)` and confirm anonymous users reach the form; swap the bases and confirm a redirect.\n5. **Rules.** Implement the Writer/Editor rules from §5 with django-rules, and verify `has_perm(\"blog.change_article\", article)` across the four combinations of author/editor x draft/published.\n6. **Data migration.** Write the roles migration from §5, run `python manage.py flush` and `migrate` on a fresh SQLite DB, and confirm the groups exist with the right permissions.\n\n**Mini project — multi-tenant notes.** `Tenant`, `Membership(user, tenant, role)` and `Note(tenant, author, body, shared_with M2M)`. Requirements: tenant resolved from a header in middleware; a base CBV mixin and a DRF base viewset that scope by tenant; owners and tenant admins can edit; notes can be shared with specific members (model the grant either as the M2M or with django-guardian); a test that crawls every URL as a member of another tenant and asserts 404 or an empty list.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user) · [Ch 23 · DRF Authentication, Permissions & Throttling](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling) · [Ch 17 · Class-Based Views](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views) · [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) · [Ch 07 · The Django Admin](topic.html?p=07-django-admin) · [Ch 27 · Sessions, Cookies & Messages](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies) · [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security) · [Ch 15 · Managers & Model Inheritance](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · Authorization: RBAC & ABAC](../rest-api/topic.html?p=21-authorization-rbac-abac) · [REST API · OWASP API Security](../rest-api/topic.html?p=23-owasp-api-security)\n\n- **Using the Django authentication system — Permissions and Authorization** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the official reference for permissions, groups, decorators and mixins. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/auth/default/>\n- **Customizing authentication — custom backends and permissions** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · how backends implement `has_perm` and object permissions. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/auth/customizing/>\n- **django-rules** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · predicate-based object permissions with no database tables. <https://github.com/dfunckt/django-rules>\n- **django-guardian** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · per-object permission grants with admin integration. <https://github.com/django-guardian/django-guardian>\n- **DRF Permissions guide** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · `has_permission` vs `has_object_permission` and the built-in classes. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/permissions/>\n- **OWASP API Security Top 10** — OWASP · *Intermediate* · broken object level authorization is the number-one API risk. <https://owasp.org/www-project-api-security/>\n- **Zanzibar: Google's Consistent, Global Authorization System** — USENIX ATC 2019 · *Advanced* · the relationship-based model behind modern authorization services. <https://research.google/pubs/zanzibar-googles-consistent-global-authorization-system/>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 26.*\n", "27-sessions-cookies": "# 27 · Sessions, Cookies & Messages\n\n> **In one line:** HTTP is stateless, so Django gives each browser an opaque random session key in a cookie and keeps the real state server-side (or signed inside the cookie) — understanding how `SessionMiddleware` loads lazily, saves only on modification, rotates the key on login and flags the cookie is what separates \"it logs in\" from \"it survives a security review and a Redis outage\".\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) (the request/response onion `SessionMiddleware` lives in), [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user) (`login()` and `logout()` are session operations), [Ch 04 · Views, Requests & Responses](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses) (`HttpRequest.COOKIES`, `HttpResponse.set_cookie`).\n\nEvery HTTP request arrives with no memory of the previous one. Yet a shopping cart, a logged-in user, a multi-step form and a \"Your changes were saved\" banner all require state that spans requests. The browser's only built-in mechanism is the **cookie**: a small name/value pair the server sets with `Set-Cookie` and the browser echoes back with `Cookie`.\n\nThe naive approach is to put the state itself into cookies: `user_id=42`, `is_admin=1`, `cart=[...]`. That fails immediately. Cookies are fully controlled by the client, so `user_id=1` makes anyone the first user. They are limited to roughly 4 KB each, they are sent on *every* request to the domain (bloating static asset requests too), and once issued you cannot revoke them — a stolen cookie stays valid until it expires.\n\nDjango's **sessions framework** (`django.contrib.sessions`) solves this with the classic design: the cookie carries only an unguessable random **session key**, and the data lives server-side — in the database by default, or in a cache, a file, or (with a deliberate trade-off) signed inside the cookie. `request.session` behaves like a dictionary; `SessionMiddleware` loads it lazily on first access and writes it back only when you modify it. The authentication system is built on top: `login()` stores the user id in the session, and `AuthenticationMiddleware` reads it back on each request.\n\nAround sessions sit two related tools. The **signing framework** (`django.core.signing`) produces tamper-proof values with HMAC, used by signed-cookie sessions, password-reset tokens and anything you want to hand to a client and trust later. The **messages framework** (`django.contrib.messages`) provides one-time \"flash\" notifications that survive a redirect, stored in a cookie or the session.\n\nThis chapter covers how sessions work internally, the engine choice and its production consequences, cookie flags and session fixation, signing, and messages — and the interview questions that come up every time (\"how do Django sessions work?\", \"what is session fixation?\", \"why did my session change not save?\").\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Session key** — a 32-character random string (lowercase letters and digits) stored in the `sessionid` cookie. *Why it matters:* it is a bearer credential; whoever holds it *is* the user.\n- **`SessionStore`** — the engine class (`SESSION_ENGINE`) implementing load/save/exists/delete/create. *Why:* swapping the engine changes durability, latency and revocability without code changes.\n- **`request.session`** — a `SessionBase` dict-like object, loaded on first access. *Why:* views that never touch it cost nothing.\n- **`modified` / `accessed` flags** — set by `__setitem__`/`__delitem__` and by any read. *Why:* only `modified` triggers a save; mutations of nested objects do not set it.\n- **Session serializer** — `JSONSerializer` (the only built-in one since `PickleSerializer` was removed in Django 5.0). *Why:* you can only store JSON-serializable values — no datetimes, Decimals or model instances without converting.\n- **Expiry** — `SESSION_COOKIE_AGE` (default 1,209,600 s = 2 weeks), `SESSION_EXPIRE_AT_BROWSER_CLOSE`, `request.session.set_expiry()`. *Why:* idle timeout and absolute timeout are compliance requirements in finance and health.\n- **Cookie flags** — `Secure`, `HttpOnly`, `SameSite`, `Domain`, `Path`. *Why:* they decide whether the cookie leaks over HTTP, to JavaScript, or on cross-site requests.\n- **Session fixation** — an attacker plants a known session key in the victim's browser before login, then rides the authenticated session. *Why:* Django defeats it with `cycle_key()` in `login()`; custom login flows must not skip it.\n- **Session auth hash** — `_auth_user_hash`, an HMAC of the user's password hash stored in the session. *Why:* changing the password invalidates every other session automatically.\n- **Signing** — `Signer`, `TimestampSigner`, `signing.dumps/loads` producing `value:signature` with HMAC keyed by `SECRET_KEY`. *Why:* integrity without server storage; *not* encryption.\n- **`SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS`** (4.1+) — older keys still accepted for verification. *Why:* lets you rotate `SECRET_KEY` without logging everyone out.\n- **Messages** — `messages.success(request, \"Saved\")` stored by `FallbackStorage` (cookie first, session if too large), consumed when iterated. *Why:* the post/redirect/get pattern needs a place to put feedback.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### What `SessionMiddleware` does on every request\n\n`django.contrib.sessions.middleware.SessionMiddleware` is a small class with two hooks:\n\n**`process_request`** reads `request.COOKIES.get(settings.SESSION_COOKIE_NAME)` and assigns `request.session = self.SessionStore(session_key)`. Nothing is loaded yet. `SessionBase` stores the key and defers loading to the `_session` property, which calls `self.load()` the first time any key is read or written. An invalid or expired key loads as an empty session, and the key is discarded so a new one is generated on save — Django never trusts a client-chosen key.\n\n**`process_response`** inspects the flags:\n\n1. If the session was **accessed**, add `Vary: Cookie` to the response (caches must not share a response across users).\n2. If the session is **empty** but the request had a cookie, delete the cookie.\n3. If the session was **modified**, or `SESSION_SAVE_EVERY_REQUEST` is `True`, and the response status is not 500: call `request.session.save()` and emit `Set-Cookie` with the key, `max_age`/`expires` derived from the expiry policy, and the configured flags.\n\nThe diagram traces this lifecycle.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 420\" width=\"100%\" height=\"420\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj27a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj27b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">SessionMiddleware: lazy load in, conditional save out</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"120\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"74\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Browser</text>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Cookie: sessionid=k3x...</text>\n  <path d=\"M140,80 L196,80\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj27a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"198\" y=\"44\" width=\"220\" height=\"72\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"308\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">process_request</text>\n  <text x=\"212\" y=\"84\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">key = COOKIES[\"sessionid\"]</text>\n  <text x=\"212\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">request.session = SessionStore(key)</text>\n  <path d=\"M418,80 L474,80\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj27a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"476\" y=\"44\" width=\"384\" height=\"72\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"668\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">view: request.session[\"cart_id\"]</text>\n  <text x=\"490\" y=\"84\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">first access: _session property calls load()</text>\n  <text x=\"490\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">db: SELECT session_data WHERE key AND expire_date &gt; now</text>\n  <rect x=\"476\" y=\"140\" width=\"384\" height=\"96\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"668\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Store (SESSION_ENGINE)</text>\n  <text x=\"490\" y=\"182\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">db: django_session (key, data, expire_date)</text>\n  <text x=\"490\" y=\"198\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">cache: \"django.contrib.sessions.cache\" + key</text>\n  <text x=\"490\" y=\"214\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">cached_db: cache first, DB on miss, write both</text>\n  <text x=\"490\" y=\"230\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">signed_cookies: data is the cookie itself</text>\n  <path d=\"M668,116 L668,138\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj27a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"198\" y=\"260\" width=\"662\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"529\" y=\"280\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">process_response</text>\n  <text x=\"214\" y=\"302\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">accessed? add Vary: Cookie</text>\n  <text x=\"214\" y=\"320\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">empty and cookie present? delete cookie</text>\n  <text x=\"214\" y=\"338\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">modified or SESSION_SAVE_EVERY_REQUEST (and status != 500)?</text>\n  <text x=\"234\" y=\"356\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">save() then Set-Cookie: sessionid=...; HttpOnly; SameSite=Lax; Secure; Max-Age</text>\n  <path d=\"M198,316 L82,316 L82,112\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj27b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Reads alone never write. Nested mutation (session[\"cart\"].append(x)) does not set modified.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### The modification rule\n\n`SessionBase.__setitem__` sets `self.modified = True`. Mutating an object *stored in* the session does not go through `__setitem__`:\n\n```python\nrequest.session[\"cart\"] = {\"items\": []}       # modified = True, saved\nrequest.session[\"cart\"][\"items\"].append(17)   # modified stays False -> NOT saved\nrequest.session.modified = True               # explicit fix\n```\n\nThis is the single most-asked session gotcha.\n\n### How login, logout and session fixation fit together\n\n`django.contrib.auth.login(request, user)` does, in order:\n\n1. If the session already holds a *different* user id (or a mismatched auth hash), `flush()` it; otherwise call **`cycle_key()`**, which keeps the data but moves it to a brand-new key and deletes the old record.\n2. Store `_auth_user_id`, `_auth_user_backend` and `_auth_user_hash`.\n3. Rotate the CSRF token (`rotate_token`) and send `user_logged_in`.\n\n`cycle_key()` is the session-fixation defence: an attacker who planted key `ATTACKER` in your browser (via a subdomain cookie or a URL in old frameworks) finds that key dead the moment you log in. `logout()` calls `flush()`, which deletes the stored record and issues a new empty session, so a stolen pre-logout key is useless — **except** with the `signed_cookies` engine, where there is no stored record to delete.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 300\" width=\"100%\" height=\"300\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj27c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Session fixation and how cycle_key() defeats it</text>\n  <line x1=\"40\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"840\" y2=\"70\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj27c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"58\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">time</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"86\" width=\"180\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"130\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. Attacker plants key</text>\n  <text x=\"130\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">sessionid=ATTACKER set via</text>\n  <text x=\"130\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">a sibling subdomain cookie</text>\n  <rect x=\"240\" y=\"86\" width=\"180\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"330\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. Victim logs in</text>\n  <text x=\"330\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">POST /login with</text>\n  <text x=\"330\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Cookie: sessionid=ATTACKER</text>\n  <rect x=\"440\" y=\"86\" width=\"190\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"535\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">3. login() cycle_key()</text>\n  <text x=\"535\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">data copied to key NEW123,</text>\n  <text x=\"535\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">record ATTACKER deleted</text>\n  <rect x=\"650\" y=\"86\" width=\"190\" height=\"64\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"745\" y=\"106\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">4. Attacker replays</text>\n  <text x=\"745\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">sessionid=ATTACKER loads as</text>\n  <text x=\"745\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">empty: anonymous user</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"180\" width=\"800\" height=\"96\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"56\" y=\"202\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Without rotation (custom login that sets session[\"user_id\"] directly):</text>\n  <text x=\"56\" y=\"222\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">the ATTACKER key now maps to an authenticated session and step 4 succeeds - full account takeover.</text>\n  <text x=\"56\" y=\"244\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Also rotate on privilege change:</text>\n  <text x=\"56\" y=\"264\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">call request.session.cycle_key() after MFA step-up or sudo mode; after password change call update_session_auth_hash().</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Signing: integrity, not secrecy\n\n`django.core.signing.Signer` computes an HMAC (SHA-256 by default) of the value with a key derived from `SECRET_KEY` and a **salt** (a namespace so a signature from one feature cannot be replayed in another). `Signer().sign(\"42\")` yields `\"42:<base64 signature>\"`. `TimestampSigner` appends a timestamp so `unsign(value, max_age=3600)` can reject old values with `SignatureExpired`. `signing.dumps(obj)` JSON-encodes, optionally compresses, base64s and signs. Anyone can base64-decode and *read* the payload; they just cannot change it. Never put secrets in signed values.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### Choosing a session engine\n\n| Engine (`SESSION_ENGINE`) | Where data lives | Read cost | Durable | Server-side revoke | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `django.contrib.sessions.backends.db` (default) | `django_session` table | 1 indexed query | Yes | Yes | Needs `clearsessions` cron; write per login |\n| `...backends.cache` | Cache (`SESSION_CACHE_ALIAS`) | Cache GET | No — eviction/restart logs users out | Yes | Use Redis/Memcached, never `locmem` with multiple workers |\n| `...backends.cached_db` | Cache + DB (write-through) | Cache GET, DB on miss | Yes | Yes | Best general default for production |\n| `...backends.file` | Files in `SESSION_FILE_PATH` | File read | On that host only | Yes | Single-server only |\n| `...backends.signed_cookies` | The cookie itself | None | N/A | **No** | ~4 KB limit; readable by client; logout cannot kill copies |\n\nThe request topology with `cached_db` and Redis:\n\n```text\nbrowser ─Cookie: sessionid─▶ LB ─▶ gunicorn worker N\n                                     │ SessionMiddleware\n                                     ├─ cache.get(\"django.contrib.sessions.cached_db\" + key) ──▶ Redis   (hit: done)\n                                     └─ miss ─▶ SELECT ... FROM django_session WHERE session_key=%s AND expire_date > now\n                                                 └─ cache.set(...) repopulates\n                         on save:   UPDATE/INSERT django_session  +  cache.set(...)\n```\n\nBecause the store is shared, **any worker on any host** can serve any user — no sticky sessions needed. That is the key architectural property: Django's session state is externalized, so app servers stay stateless and horizontally scalable.\n\n### Cookie flags and what each defends against\n\n| Setting | Default | Production value | Defends against |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE` | `False` | `True` | Cookie sent over plain HTTP and sniffed |\n| `SESSION_COOKIE_HTTPONLY` | `True` | `True` | XSS reading `document.cookie` |\n| `SESSION_COOKIE_SAMESITE` | `\"Lax\"` | `\"Lax\"` (or `\"Strict\"`) | Cookie sent on cross-site POSTs (CSRF) |\n| `SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN` | `None` (host-only) | `None` unless SSO across subdomains | Sibling subdomains reading/setting it |\n| `SESSION_COOKIE_AGE` | 1209600 | Policy-driven | Long-lived stolen sessions |\n| `SESSION_COOKIE_NAME` | `\"sessionid\"` | Change per app on shared domains | Two Django apps on one domain clobbering each other |\n| `CSRF_COOKIE_SECURE` | `False` | `True` | Same as above for the CSRF cookie |\n\n`SameSite=None` requires `Secure` and is only needed for cross-site embeds (an app in an iframe on a partner site). `SameSite=Lax` still sends the cookie on top-level GET navigations, so links from email work while cross-site form POSTs do not carry it.\n\n### Messages workflow (post/redirect/get)\n\n```text\nPOST /profile ──▶ form valid ──▶ messages.success(request, \"Profile saved\")\n                                   └─ FallbackStorage: try CookieStorage (\"messages\" cookie, signed)\n                                                      too big? overflow to SessionStorage\n              ◀── 302 Location: /profile\nGET /profile  ──▶ template: {% for m in messages %} ... {% endfor %}\n                                   └─ iteration marks storage as used; process_response clears them\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: a session cart and cookie helpers\n\n```python\n# settings.py (relevant parts)\nINSTALLED_APPS = [\n    \"django.contrib.sessions\",\n    \"django.contrib.messages\",\n    # ...\n]\nMIDDLEWARE = [\n    \"django.middleware.security.SecurityMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.sessions.middleware.SessionMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.common.CommonMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.csrf.CsrfViewMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.auth.middleware.AuthenticationMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.messages.middleware.MessageMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.clickjacking.XFrameOptionsMiddleware\",\n]\n```\n\n```python\n# shop/views.py\nfrom django.contrib import messages\nfrom django.shortcuts import redirect, render\nfrom django.views.decorators.http import require_POST\n\nCART_KEY = \"cart\"\n\n\n@require_POST\ndef add_to_cart(request, product_id):\n    cart = request.session.get(CART_KEY, {})          # JSON: keys must be strings\n    key = str(product_id)\n    cart[key] = cart.get(key, 0) + 1\n    request.session[CART_KEY] = cart                   # reassign -> modified = True\n    messages.success(request, \"Added to cart.\")\n    return redirect(\"cart\")\n\n\ndef cart_view(request):\n    cart = request.session.get(CART_KEY, {})\n    return render(request, \"shop/cart.html\", {\"cart\": cart})\n\n\ndef remember_theme(request):\n    response = redirect(\"home\")\n    response.set_signed_cookie(\"theme\", request.GET.get(\"t\", \"light\"), salt=\"ui.theme\",\n                               max_age=60 * 60 * 24 * 365, secure=True, httponly=True, samesite=\"Lax\")\n    return response\n\n\ndef read_theme(request):\n    return request.get_signed_cookie(\"theme\", default=\"light\", salt=\"ui.theme\")   # tampered -> default\n```\n\n```html\n{# templates/base.html #}\n{% if messages %}\n  <ul class=\"messages\">\n    {% for message in messages %}\n      <li class=\"{{ message.tags }}\">{{ message }}</li>\n    {% endfor %}\n  </ul>\n{% endif %}\n```\n\nWhat the database row looks like:\n\n```sql\nSELECT session_key, expire_date, length(session_data) FROM django_session LIMIT 1;\n```\n\n```text\n session_key                      | expire_date                   | length\n----------------------------------+-------------------------------+-------\n q8k3m1z0w7n2c5v9b4x6l1j0h3g8f2d5 | 2026-11-08 10:14:03.512+00    |   112\n```\n\n```python\n>>> from django.contrib.sessions.models import Session\n>>> Session.objects.get(pk=\"q8k3m1z0w7n2c5v9b4x6l1j0h3g8f2d5\").get_decoded()\n{'cart': {'17': 2}, '_auth_user_id': '42', '_auth_user_backend': 'django.contrib.auth.backends.ModelBackend', '_auth_user_hash': '5f1c...'}\n```\n\n### Real-world example: production session settings, idle timeout, step-up auth\n\n```python\n# settings/production.py\nSESSION_ENGINE = \"django.contrib.sessions.backends.cached_db\"\nSESSION_CACHE_ALIAS = \"sessions\"            # dedicated Redis DB so cache flushes do not log users out\nCACHES = {\n    \"default\":  {\"BACKEND\": \"django.core.cache.backends.redis.RedisCache\", \"LOCATION\": \"redis://redis:6379/1\"},\n    \"sessions\": {\"BACKEND\": \"django.core.cache.backends.redis.RedisCache\", \"LOCATION\": \"redis://redis:6379/2\"},\n}\nSESSION_COOKIE_SECURE = True\nSESSION_COOKIE_HTTPONLY = True\nSESSION_COOKIE_SAMESITE = \"Lax\"\nSESSION_COOKIE_AGE = 60 * 60 * 8            # absolute: 8 hours\nSESSION_COOKIE_NAME = \"acme_sid\"\nCSRF_COOKIE_SECURE = True\nSECRET_KEY = env(\"SECRET_KEY\")\nSECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS = env.list(\"OLD_SECRET_KEYS\", default=[])   # rotation window\n```\n\nAn idle-timeout middleware (absolute expiry comes from the cookie age; idle expiry must be tracked):\n\n```python\n# accounts/middleware.py\nimport time\nfrom django.contrib.auth import logout\nfrom django.shortcuts import redirect\n\nIDLE_SECONDS = 15 * 60\nTOUCH_EVERY = 60          # avoid a session write on every single request\n\n\nclass IdleTimeoutMiddleware:\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response\n\n    def __call__(self, request):\n        if request.user.is_authenticated:\n            now = int(time.time())\n            last = request.session.get(\"last_seen\", now)\n            if now - last > IDLE_SECONDS:\n                logout(request)                       # flush(): deletes record, new key\n                return redirect(\"login\")\n            if now - last > TOUCH_EVERY:\n                request.session[\"last_seen\"] = now    # throttled write\n        return self.get_response(request)\n```\n\nStep-up authentication for sensitive actions, with key rotation on privilege change:\n\n```python\n# accounts/views.py\nimport time\nfrom django.contrib.auth import update_session_auth_hash\nfrom django.contrib.auth.forms import PasswordChangeForm\n\n\ndef confirm_password(request):\n    if request.method == \"POST\" and request.user.check_password(request.POST[\"password\"]):\n        request.session.cycle_key()                      # new key for the elevated session\n        request.session[\"sudo_until\"] = int(time.time()) + 300\n        return redirect(request.GET.get(\"next\", \"/\"))\n    return render(request, \"accounts/confirm.html\")\n\n\ndef change_password(request):\n    form = PasswordChangeForm(request.user, request.POST or None)\n    if form.is_valid():\n        user = form.save()\n        update_session_auth_hash(request, user)          # keep THIS session; others die via auth hash\n        messages.success(request, \"Password changed. Other devices were signed out.\")\n        return redirect(\"profile\")\n    return render(request, \"accounts/change_password.html\", {\"form\": form})\n```\n\n\"Log out all other devices\" for the db-backed engines (the session table is not indexed by user, so store a mapping on login):\n\n```python\n# accounts/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\n\n\nclass UserSession(models.Model):\n    user = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE)\n    session_key = models.CharField(max_length=40, unique=True)\n    created = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n\n\n# accounts/signals.py  (connected in AppConfig.ready)\nfrom django.contrib.auth.signals import user_logged_in\nfrom django.contrib.sessions.models import Session\nfrom django.dispatch import receiver\nfrom importlib import import_module\nfrom django.conf import settings\n\nSessionStore = import_module(settings.SESSION_ENGINE).SessionStore\n\n\n@receiver(user_logged_in, dispatch_uid=\"track_user_session\")\ndef track(sender, request, user, **kwargs):\n    UserSession.objects.create(user=user, session_key=request.session.session_key)\n\n\ndef logout_everywhere_else(request):\n    keep = request.session.session_key\n    for us in UserSession.objects.filter(user=request.user).exclude(session_key=keep):\n        SessionStore(session_key=us.session_key).delete()   # clears cache and DB for cached_db\n        us.delete()\n```\n\nExpiry hygiene for the db engines:\n\n```bash\npython manage.py clearsessions        # deletes expired rows; schedule daily (cron or Celery beat)\n```\n\nAsync views (Django 5.1+) have async session methods:\n\n```python\nasync def async_cart(request):\n    cart = await request.session.aget(\"cart\", {})\n    await request.session.aset(\"visited\", True)\n    return JsonResponse({\"cart\": cart})\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Concern | Server-side sessions (db / cache / cached_db) | Signed-cookie sessions | Stateless JWT in a cookie/header |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Revocation (logout, ban) | Immediate — delete the record | Impossible until expiry (unless you add a server-side denylist) | Impossible until expiry (needs denylist) |\n| Read cost | Query or cache hit per request | None | Signature check |\n| Payload size | Unlimited server-side | About 4 KB, sent on every request | Grows with claims |\n| Confidentiality | Opaque key only | Payload readable (base64) | Payload readable |\n| Horizontal scaling | Needs shared store | Nothing shared | Nothing shared |\n| Best for | Browser apps, admin, anything with logout | Tiny, low-risk state | Service-to-service, mobile APIs — see [REST API · JWT](../rest-api/topic.html?p=20-jwt) |\n\n### When to use\n\n- **`cached_db`** as the production default: fast reads from Redis, durable through cache restarts.\n- **`cache`** with a dedicated Redis instance (persistence on) when you accept losing sessions in a disaster and want zero DB writes.\n- **`db`** for small apps without a cache tier.\n- **Signed cookies** (via `set_signed_cookie`) for small, non-sensitive preferences.\n- **Messages** for any feedback across a redirect.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Do not use `signed_cookies` sessions where you need real logout, bans or \"sign out all devices\".\n- Do not use the `cache` engine on `LocMemCache` with more than one process — each worker has its own memory, so users appear randomly logged out.\n- Do not store large objects (querysets, full carts with product data) in the session; store ids.\n- Do not use sessions for API clients that are not browsers; use token auth.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Mutating nested session data.** `request.session[\"cart\"][\"17\"] += 1` is not saved. Reassign the top-level key or set `request.session.modified = True`.\n\n**2. Storing non-JSON values.** Putting a `datetime`, `Decimal` or model instance in the session raises `TypeError: Object of type X is not JSON serializable` at response time (inside middleware, far from your view). Store ISO strings, ints and ids.\n\n```python\n# Before\nrequest.session[\"last_order\"] = order                  # TypeError in process_response\n# After\nrequest.session[\"last_order_id\"] = order.pk\nrequest.session[\"last_seen\"] = timezone.now().isoformat()\n```\n\n**3. Writing a custom login that bypasses `login()`.** `request.session[\"user_id\"] = user.pk` skips `cycle_key()`, the auth hash, CSRF rotation and the `user_logged_in` signal. Always call `django.contrib.auth.login` (or `alogin`).\n\n**4. Never running `clearsessions`.** The `django_session` table grows by one row per new visitor (anonymous sessions too, once anything is stored). Teams find a 200 GB table years later. Schedule it.\n\n**5. Using `SESSION_SAVE_EVERY_REQUEST = True` to implement idle timeout.** It writes the session on every request, including every AJAX poll, turning reads into writes and multiplying DB load. Throttle as in the middleware above.\n\n**6. `SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN = \".example.com\"` without need.** Every subdomain — including a marketing site on a third-party CMS — now receives the session cookie. Keep cookies host-only unless you deliberately share sessions.\n\n**7. Rotating `SECRET_KEY` without fallbacks.** Every session (for signed engines and the auth hash), password-reset link and signed cookie becomes invalid at once. Put the old key in `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS`, deploy, wait out the longest token lifetime, then remove it.\n\n**8. Messages that never show.** `messages.debug()` is dropped because `MESSAGE_LEVEL` defaults to `INFO`; messages added in a view that renders (not redirects) appear on the *next* page if the template does not iterate them. Iterate `messages` in the base template.\n\n**Best practices:** keep sessions small and id-based; dedicated cache alias for sessions; `Secure` cookies in production (`check --deploy` warns otherwise); rotate keys on login and privilege changes; one cookie name per app on shared domains.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Production story — random logouts after scaling to four workers.** *Symptom:* users are logged out every few clicks. *Root cause:* `SESSION_ENGINE = \"...cache\"` with the default `LocMemCache`; each Gunicorn worker has a private dict, so a session created in worker 1 does not exist in worker 3. *Fix:* Redis cache backend (or `cached_db`). Same symptom appears with the `file` engine on multiple hosts and with multiple Django apps sharing a domain and cookie name.\n\n**Production story — cache flush logs out everyone.** An engineer ran `cache.clear()` to fix stale product pages; sessions lived in the same Redis DB under the `cache` engine. *Fix:* separate `SESSION_CACHE_ALIAS`, or `cached_db` so the DB repopulates the cache.\n\n**Login loop behind a proxy.** *Symptom:* login succeeds, redirect returns to login. *Root cause:* `SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE=True` but TLS terminates at the load balancer and Django sees HTTP — the browser still accepts the cookie over HTTPS, but `SecurityMiddleware` redirects or `request.is_secure()` is wrong and CSRF fails. Also common: `SESSION_COOKIE_DOMAIN` set to a domain not matching the host so the browser rejects the cookie. *Fix:* `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER = (\"HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO\", \"https\")` with a proxy that sets it, and correct domain settings. Debug by inspecting the `Set-Cookie` header in browser devtools — a rejected cookie shows a warning icon with the reason.\n\n**Session writes as a hidden load source.** Every authenticated request with idle tracking, \"last activity\" or cart touches writes the session. On the `db` engine that is an UPDATE per request; at thousands of requests per second the `django_session` table becomes the hottest table in the database, with vacuum pressure on PostgreSQL. *Fix:* `cached_db` (reads from cache) and throttled writes; for pure-read requests make sure nothing sets a key.\n\n**`Vary: Cookie` kills page caching.** Any view that *reads* the session (including via `request.user`, which touches the session) gets `Vary: Cookie`, so shared caches and `cache_page` store per-cookie variants. Anonymous landing pages should avoid touching the session; see [Ch 29 · Caching in Django](topic.html?p=29-caching).\n\n**Debugging toolkit.**\n\n```python\nfrom importlib import import_module\nfrom django.conf import settings\nstore = import_module(settings.SESSION_ENGINE).SessionStore(session_key=\"q8k3...\")\nstore.load()                 # {} if expired, missing or tampered\nstore.get_expiry_date()\n```\n\nLog the session key prefix (never the full key — it is a credential) alongside user id in request logs to correlate issues. Sentry scrubs cookies by default; keep it that way.\n\n**Scaling.** Session reads are key lookups, ideal for Redis. A single Redis instance handles very large session volumes; the concerns are memory (keys x average size — another reason to keep sessions small), persistence (enable AOF if you use the pure `cache` engine) and failover (Sentinel or a managed service). See [Redis Caching · Security Hardening](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=29-security-hardening) for protecting the instance that now holds every user's credentials.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: How do sessions work in Django?**\nA: `SessionMiddleware` reads the `sessionid` cookie and attaches `request.session`, a `SessionStore` for that key, without loading anything. On first access the store loads the data from the configured engine (the `django_session` table by default) where the row is decoded from signed JSON. Views use it like a dict. In the response phase, if the session was modified the middleware saves it and sends `Set-Cookie` with the key and the configured flags. The cookie holds only a random key; the data stays server-side, which is why the client cannot tamper with it.\n\n**Q: What session backends does Django provide and how do you choose?**\nA: `db` (default), `cache`, `cached_db`, `file` and `signed_cookies`. `db` is durable but costs a query per request; `cache` is fast but loses sessions on eviction or restart; `cached_db` writes to both and reads from cache first, which is the best production default; `file` only works on a single host; `signed_cookies` stores data in the cookie, needs no server storage but cannot be revoked and is limited to about 4 KB. For multi-worker deployments the store must be shared — never `locmem`.\n\n**Q: Why didn't my change to the session get saved?**\nA: Django only saves when `request.session.modified` is `True`, which is set by assigning or deleting a top-level key. Mutating a nested object — `request.session[\"cart\"].append(x)` — changes the dict in memory but does not touch `__setitem__`, so the change is lost. Reassign the key or set `request.session.modified = True`. Other causes: the response was a 500 (Django skips the save), or the value is not JSON-serializable.\n\n**Q: What is session fixation and how does Django prevent it?**\nA: The attacker gets the victim to use a session key the attacker knows — for example by setting a cookie from a sibling subdomain — then waits for the victim to log in, and reuses that key as an authenticated session. Django's `login()` calls `cycle_key()`, moving the session data to a new random key and deleting the old one, so the planted key is dead after login. `logout()` calls `flush()`, deleting the data and issuing a new key. Custom login code that writes the user id into the session directly reintroduces the bug.\n\n**Q: What do the `HttpOnly`, `Secure` and `SameSite` cookie flags do, and what are Django's defaults?**\nA: `HttpOnly` hides the cookie from JavaScript, limiting what XSS can steal; Django's session cookie is `HttpOnly` by default. `Secure` makes the browser send the cookie only over HTTPS; it defaults to `False` and must be enabled in production (`check --deploy` warns). `SameSite` controls whether the cookie is sent on cross-site requests; Django defaults to `Lax`, which blocks it on cross-site POSTs while allowing top-level link navigations. The CSRF cookie is not `HttpOnly` by default because some JavaScript setups read it.\n\n**Q: Why does changing a password log out other sessions?**\nA: `login()` stores `_auth_user_hash`, an HMAC of the user's password hash (`get_session_auth_hash()`). On every request `get_user()` recomputes it and compares; after a password change the hash differs, so other sessions are flushed and become anonymous. `update_session_auth_hash(request, user)` updates the current session so the user who changed the password stays logged in. Rotating `SECRET_KEY` without `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS` has the same effect on everyone.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between signing and encryption, and how is Django's signing framework used?**\nA: Signing proves a value was produced by someone with the key and has not been changed; encryption hides the value. `django.core.signing` signs with HMAC (SHA-256) using `SECRET_KEY` and a salt; the payload stays readable. It is used for signed-cookie sessions, `set_signed_cookie`, the messages cookie, and password-reset-style tokens. `TimestampSigner` and `signing.loads(max_age=...)` add expiry. Use different salts per purpose so a signature cannot be replayed in another context.\n\n**Q: How do session expiry settings work?**\nA: `SESSION_COOKIE_AGE` (default two weeks) sets the cookie max-age and the stored expiry. `SESSION_EXPIRE_AT_BROWSER_CLOSE = True` makes it a browser-session cookie (though browsers restoring tabs often keep them). `request.session.set_expiry(value)` overrides per session: an int is seconds of inactivity, a datetime is absolute, `0` means at browser close, and `None` falls back to the global policy. Expired rows in the db engine remain until `clearsessions` deletes them.\n\n**Q: How does the messages framework work?**\nA: `MessageMiddleware` attaches a storage backend to the request; the default `FallbackStorage` tries `CookieStorage` (a signed cookie) and overflows to `SessionStorage`. `messages.success(request, \"...\")` adds a message with a level and tags. When a template iterates `messages`, the storage is marked used and the messages are removed in the response phase; anything not iterated persists to the next request. The default `MESSAGE_LEVEL` is `INFO`, so debug messages are dropped.\n\n**Q: Should a REST API use sessions or tokens?**\nA: For a browser SPA on the same site, session authentication with `HttpOnly` cookies is often the safer choice: no token in JavaScript-accessible storage, real server-side logout, and CSRF protection via DRF's `SessionAuthentication`. For mobile apps, third-party clients and service-to-service calls, tokens (DRF tokens or JWT) fit better because there is no cookie jar and no CSRF concern. The trade-off is revocation versus statelessness. See [REST API · Authentication Basics](../rest-api/topic.html?p=18-authentication-basics).\n\n**Q: Users are randomly logged out after you added more Gunicorn workers. How do you debug it? (Senior)**\nA: Random logouts that scale with worker count mean the session store is not shared. Check `SESSION_ENGINE` and the cache it uses: `cache` engine on `LocMemCache`, or `file` on several hosts, gives each process its own store. Confirm by logging worker pid and whether `session.load()` returned empty for a known key. Other suspects: cache eviction (Redis `maxmemory` with `allkeys-lru` evicting session keys), a shared domain with another app using the same cookie name, or the secret key differing between hosts. Fix with a shared Redis or `cached_db`, a dedicated cache alias, and identical settings across hosts.\n\n**Q: How would you implement \"sign out of all other devices\"? (Senior)**\nA: With the db engine, sessions are not indexed by user, so scanning and decoding every row is too slow at scale. Record the mapping on `user_logged_in` in a `UserSession(user, session_key)` table, then delete those keys through the `SessionStore` (which also clears the cache for `cached_db`). A cheaper alternative that needs no table: bump a per-user \"session generation\" counter and store it in the session at login; middleware logs out sessions whose stored generation is stale. Changing the password also works via the auth hash. With signed-cookie sessions only the generation-counter approach is possible, since there is no record to delete.\n\n**Q: How do you rotate `SECRET_KEY` without logging everyone out? (Senior)**\nA: Since Django 4.1, set the new value in `SECRET_KEY` and move the old one into `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS`. New signatures use the new key while verification also accepts the fallbacks, so existing sessions' auth hashes, signed cookies, messages and password-reset tokens keep working. After the longest-lived artifact expires (two weeks for default sessions, `PASSWORD_RESET_TIMEOUT` for reset links), remove the fallback. On 4.2 and later this is a routine operation; before 4.1 rotation invalidated everything at once.\n\n**Q: What are the trade-offs of the cache vs cached_db session engines in a high-traffic system? (Senior)**\nA: The `cache` engine never touches the database, so login storms and per-request writes cost only Redis operations, but a Redis failover without persistence, eviction under memory pressure, or a `cache.clear()` logs users out. `cached_db` reads from the cache and falls back to the DB, so cache loss is survivable, but every save writes the database too, which matters if you write sessions often. Choose `cached_db` unless DB write volume from sessions is a real problem, then reduce writes (throttled touches) before switching to pure `cache` with a dedicated, persistent, non-evicting Redis.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Item | Value / API |\n|---|---|\n| Cookie | `sessionid`, HttpOnly, SameSite=Lax, Secure off by default |\n| Default engine | `django.contrib.sessions.backends.db` |\n| Default age | 1,209,600 s (2 weeks) |\n| Serializer | `JSONSerializer` only (Pickle removed in 5.0) |\n| Save trigger | `modified` or `SESSION_SAVE_EVERY_REQUEST`; not on 500 |\n| Fixation defence | `login()` calls `cycle_key()`; `logout()` calls `flush()` |\n| Password change | `update_session_auth_hash(request, user)` |\n| Expiry | `set_expiry(int/datetime/0/None)`, `clearsessions` |\n| Signing | `Signer`, `TimestampSigner`, `signing.dumps/loads(salt=, max_age=)` |\n| Signed cookie | `response.set_signed_cookie()`, `request.get_signed_cookie()` |\n| Key rotation | `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS` (4.1+) |\n| Messages | `messages.success/info/warning/error`, `FallbackStorage` |\n| Async | `aget`, `aset`, `asave`, `aflush`, `acycle_key` (5.1+) |\n\n- The cookie holds only a random key; data lives in the store.\n- Nested mutations need `request.session.modified = True`.\n- Sessions must be shared across workers: Redis or DB, never locmem.\n- Signed-cookie sessions cannot be revoked.\n- Always use `login()`/`logout()`; never hand-roll session auth.\n- Run `clearsessions` on a schedule for db/file engines.\n- Session access adds `Vary: Cookie`, which fragments caches.\n- Signing is integrity, not secrecy.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" redis`, start a project, run `migrate` and `createsuperuser`.\n\n1. **Watch the lifecycle.** Add a view that only *reads* `request.session.get(\"x\")` and another that sets it. Use `curl -i` to show that only the second emits `Set-Cookie`, and both emit `Vary: Cookie`.\n2. **Nested mutation bug.** Implement a cart with `request.session[\"cart\"][\"items\"].append(...)`, show it fails to persist across requests, then fix it two ways.\n3. **Fixation demo.** Log in through the admin while noting the `sessionid` before and after; confirm it changes. Then write a naive login view that sets `session[\"user_id\"]` and observe the key does not change.\n4. **Engines.** Switch between `db`, `cache` (locmem), `cached_db` (Redis) and `signed_cookies`. Run `gunicorn -w 4` with locmem and reproduce random logouts. With signed cookies, copy the cookie, log out, and replay it with `curl` to see the session still valid.\n5. **Signing.** In the shell, sign a value with `TimestampSigner(salt=\"invite\")`, tamper with one character, and catch `BadSignature`; wait past `max_age` and catch `SignatureExpired`. Rotate `SECRET_KEY` with and without `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS`.\n6. **Messages.** Add messages at each level, set `MESSAGE_LEVEL = messages.DEBUG`, and add a message larger than the cookie limit to see it overflow to the session.\n\n**Mini project — device management page.** Build a \"Your sessions\" page listing each active login (device user agent, IP, created, last seen) with \"sign out this device\" and \"sign out all others\" buttons, using a `UserSession` table populated on `user_logged_in`, throttled last-seen updates, an idle timeout middleware and `cached_db` on Redis. Add tests for fixation (key rotates on login) and for the password-change behaviour.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user) · [Ch 26 · Permissions, Groups & Authorization](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization) · [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security) · [Ch 29 · Caching in Django](topic.html?p=29-caching) · [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) · [Ch 23 · DRF Authentication, Permissions & Throttling](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling) · [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · Authentication Basics](../rest-api/topic.html?p=18-authentication-basics) · [REST API · JWT](../rest-api/topic.html?p=20-jwt) · [Redis Caching · Security Hardening](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=29-security-hardening)\n\n- **How to use sessions** — Django docs · *Beginner* · engines, settings, the session API and `clearsessions`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/http/sessions/>\n- **Cryptographic signing** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `Signer`, `TimestampSigner`, salts and `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/signing/>\n- **The messages framework** — Django docs · *Beginner* · storage backends, levels and template usage. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/contrib/messages/>\n- **Settings reference (SESSION_*, CSRF_COOKIE_*)** — Django docs · *Beginner* · every cookie flag and its default. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/settings/>\n- **OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet** — OWASP · *Intermediate* · idle/absolute timeouts, rotation and cookie attributes. <https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html>\n- **MDN: Using HTTP cookies** — MDN · *Beginner* · cookie attributes and SameSite behaviour across browsers. <https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Cookies>\n- **django-user-sessions** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · a db session backend that records user, IP and user agent for device management. <https://github.com/jazzband/django-user-sessions>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 27.*\n", "28-security": "# 28 · Security: CSRF, XSS, SQL Injection & Hardening\n\n> **In one line:** Django is secure by default against the classic web attacks — CSRF tokens, template autoescaping, parameterized SQL, `X-Frame-Options: DENY`, `ALLOWED_HOSTS` — but every one of those defences has an explicit off switch, and real Django breaches come from developers flipping them (`|safe`, `@csrf_exempt`, f-strings in `raw()`, `DEBUG=True`) or from never turning on the production settings that `check --deploy` lists.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) (security features are middleware), [Ch 05 · Templates](topic.html?p=05-templates) (autoescaping), [Ch 27 · Sessions, Cookies & Messages](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies) (cookie flags, signing, `SECRET_KEY`), [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user) (password hashing).\n\nA web application is a program that executes instructions from strangers. Every request carries attacker-controlled data — URL, headers, cookies, body — and every place that data is *interpreted* is an injection point: HTML (XSS), SQL (injection), the browser's ambient credentials (CSRF), frames (clickjacking), the `Host` header (cache and password-reset poisoning), redirects (open redirect). The naive approach — \"validate inputs carefully\" — fails because there are hundreds of sinks and one forgotten one is enough.\n\nDjango's philosophy is **secure by default with explicit, greppable opt-outs**:\n\n| Attack | Django's default defence | The opt-out that reopens it |\n|---|---|---|\n| Cross-site request forgery | `CsrfViewMiddleware` + `{% csrf_token %}` + `SameSite=Lax` cookies | `@csrf_exempt`, removing the middleware |\n| Cross-site scripting | Template autoescaping of `< > ' \" &` | `|safe`, `mark_safe()`, `{% autoescape off %}`, building HTML in Python |\n| SQL injection | ORM parameterizes every value | f-strings in `raw()`, `extra()`, `RawSQL`, `cursor.execute()` |\n| Clickjacking | `XFrameOptionsMiddleware`, `X_FRAME_OPTIONS = \"DENY\"` | `@xframe_options_exempt` |\n| Host header attacks | `ALLOWED_HOSTS` checked by `request.get_host()` | `ALLOWED_HOSTS = [\"*\"]` |\n| Session theft over HTTP | `SecurityMiddleware` + `Secure`/HSTS settings | Off by default — you must enable them |\n| Information disclosure | `DEBUG = False` hides tracebacks | Shipping `DEBUG = True` |\n\nNotice the last two rows: some protections are *off* by default because they depend on your deployment (HTTPS, proxy). That is what `python manage.py check --deploy` is for.\n\nThis chapter explains how each defence works *internally* — what `CsrfViewMiddleware.process_view` actually compares, why autoescaping does not protect `<script>` blocks or `href=\"javascript:...\"`, which ORM APIs are injection-safe — and the production hardening list interviewers expect: `SECRET_KEY` handling, HTTPS/HSTS, CSP, uploads, admin hardening and the holes developers open by accident.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **CSRF (cross-site request forgery)** — a malicious site makes the victim's browser send an authenticated request to your site using its cookies. *Why it matters:* any cookie-authenticated, state-changing endpoint is a target.\n- **CSRF token** — a secret in the `csrftoken` cookie plus a *masked* copy in the form (`csrfmiddlewaretoken`) or the `X-CSRFToken` header; the middleware unmasks and compares. *Why:* a cross-site attacker cannot read your cookie or page, so cannot produce the matching value.\n- **Origin / Referer checking** — for unsafe requests Django checks `Origin` (and, on HTTPS, `Referer` when `Origin` is absent) against the host and `CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS`. *Why:* defence in depth; also why reverse-proxy and subdomain setups fail with 403.\n- **XSS (cross-site scripting)** — attacker-controlled data rendered as active HTML/JS in your origin. *Why:* runs with the victim's session; can do anything the user can.\n- **Autoescaping** — templates HTML-escape every variable unless marked safe. *Why:* converts the default from \"vulnerable\" to \"safe\"; context-insensitive, so it has gaps.\n- **`SafeString` / `mark_safe`** — a string subclass that tells the template engine \"already escaped\". *Why:* the opt-out; every use is an audit point.\n- **SQL injection** — user data interpreted as SQL syntax. *Why:* full data exfiltration or destruction.\n- **Parameterization** — sending SQL and values separately to the driver (`%s` placeholders). *Why:* values can never become syntax.\n- **Clickjacking** — your page framed invisibly under a decoy so clicks land on your buttons. *Why:* bypasses CSRF because the real user really clicks.\n- **Host header poisoning** — a forged `Host` used to build absolute URLs (password-reset links, cache keys). *Why:* reset emails pointing to the attacker's domain leak tokens.\n- **`SECRET_KEY`** — HMAC key for sessions' auth hash, signed cookies, messages, password-reset tokens. *Why:* leaking it lets attackers forge all of those.\n- **HSTS** — `Strict-Transport-Security` tells browsers to use HTTPS only for a period. *Why:* stops SSL-stripping on first plain-HTTP request.\n- **CSP (Content Security Policy)** — a header whitelisting script/style/frame sources. *Why:* the best mitigation when an XSS slips through; via django-csp on 5.2 (built into Django 6.0).\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### How CSRF protection works inside Django\n\n`django.middleware.csrf.CsrfViewMiddleware` participates in two phases.\n\n**When a token is needed** (a template uses `{% csrf_token %}` or code calls `get_token(request)`), Django ensures a **secret** exists for the client and returns a **masked token**: the secret combined with a fresh random mask, so the value in the HTML changes on every render. Masking is the defence against BREACH-style compression attacks, which can recover a constant secret from compressed HTTPS responses. In the response phase the middleware sets the `csrftoken` cookie (or stores the secret in the session when `CSRF_USE_SESSIONS = True`).\n\n**When a request arrives**, `process_view` runs after URL resolution, just before the view:\n\n1. If the view is marked `csrf_exempt`, or the method is safe (`GET`, `HEAD`, `OPTIONS`, `TRACE`), accept. Safe methods must therefore never change state.\n2. If an `Origin` header is present, it must match the request's own origin or one in `CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS` (which must include the scheme since Django 4.0, e.g. `https://app.example.com`); otherwise reject.\n3. If the request is HTTPS and there is no `Origin`, a `Referer` is required and must be same-origin or trusted (blocks a man-in-the-middle on HTTP from forging HTTPS posts).\n4. Read the secret from the cookie (or session). Missing: reject (\"CSRF cookie not set\").\n5. Read the submitted token from `request.POST[\"csrfmiddlewaretoken\"]` or the `X-CSRFToken` header (`CSRF_HEADER_NAME`). Unmask it and compare to the secret with a **constant-time** comparison. Mismatch: reject.\n6. Rejection calls `CSRF_FAILURE_VIEW`, a 403 page whose DEBUG version names the exact reason.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 450\" width=\"100%\" height=\"450\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj28a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj28b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">CsrfViewMiddleware.process_view decision path</text>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"44\" width=\"250\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">POST /transfer (cookie auth)</text>\n  <path d=\"M155,84 L155,104\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"106\" width=\"250\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"124\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">csrf_exempt view or safe method?</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"139\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">GET HEAD OPTIONS TRACE</text>\n  <path d=\"M155,146 L155,166\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"166\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">no</text>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"168\" width=\"250\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"186\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Origin header present?</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"201\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">must equal own origin or CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS</text>\n  <path d=\"M155,208 L155,228\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"230\" width=\"250\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"248\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">HTTPS and no Origin?</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"263\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Referer required, same-origin or trusted</text>\n  <path d=\"M155,270 L155,290\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"292\" width=\"250\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">secret from csrftoken cookie</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"325\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">(or session if CSRF_USE_SESSIONS)</text>\n  <path d=\"M155,332 L155,352\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"30\" y=\"354\" width=\"250\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"372\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">unmask POST token or X-CSRFToken</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"388\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">constant_time_compare(secret, unmasked)</text>\n  <path d=\"M280,126 L400,126\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"340\" y=\"118\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">yes</text>\n  <rect x=\"402\" y=\"106\" width=\"200\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"502\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">accept: call the view</text>\n  <path d=\"M280,376 L400,376\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"340\" y=\"368\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">match</text>\n  <rect x=\"402\" y=\"356\" width=\"200\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"502\" y=\"380\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">accept: call the view</text>\n  <path d=\"M280,188 L640,188\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M280,250 L640,250\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M280,312 L640,312\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"460\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">untrusted Origin</text>\n  <text x=\"460\" y=\"244\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">missing or bad Referer</text>\n  <text x=\"460\" y=\"306\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">CSRF cookie not set</text>\n  <rect x=\"642\" y=\"168\" width=\"218\" height=\"164\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"751\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">reject</text>\n  <text x=\"656\" y=\"218\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">CSRF_FAILURE_VIEW</text>\n  <text x=\"656\" y=\"236\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">403 Forbidden</text>\n  <text x=\"656\" y=\"254\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">DEBUG page states reason</text>\n  <text x=\"656\" y=\"290\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">attacker site cannot read</text>\n  <text x=\"656\" y=\"306\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">your cookie or page, so it</text>\n  <text x=\"656\" y=\"322\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">cannot forge the token</text>\n  <path d=\"M600,380 L750,380 L750,334\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"680\" y=\"400\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">mismatch</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"436\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">SameSite=Lax session cookies add a second, browser-side layer; tokens remain the primary defence.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### How autoescaping works — and where it stops\n\nWhen the template engine renders `{{ comment.body }}`, it calls `conditional_escape(value)`: if the value is a `SafeData` instance it is output as-is; otherwise `<`, `>`, `&`, `'`, `\"` become entities. Filters are marked `is_safe` or `needs_autoescape` so the engine knows whether their output stays safe. In Python, `django.utils.html.format_html()` is the safe way to build HTML: it escapes every argument and marks the result safe.\n\nAutoescaping is **HTML-body escaping**. It does not understand context:\n\n- **Inside `<script>`**: `var name = \"{{ name }}\";` — escaping quotes helps, but `</script>` breaking and JS string rules make this fragile. Use `{{ data|json_script:\"data-id\" }}` and `JSON.parse(document.getElementById(\"data-id\").textContent)`.\n- **Unquoted attributes**: `<div class={{ cls }}>` — a space plus `onmouseover=...` injects an attribute without needing any escaped character. Always quote attributes.\n- **URL attributes**: `<a href=\"{{ user.website }}\">` — `javascript:alert(1)` contains nothing to escape. Validate the scheme (`URLField` with `URLValidator` restricts to http/https/ftp/ftps by default) on input.\n- **Markdown or rich text**: rendering user HTML requires a sanitizer such as `nh3` (or bleach, now deprecated) with an allowlist, then `mark_safe` on the sanitized output only.\n\n### How the ORM prevents SQL injection\n\nA QuerySet compiles to SQL via `SQLCompiler.as_sql()`, which returns `(sql_string, params)` with `%s` placeholders. The database driver (psycopg) sends parameters separately or quotes them safely; a value can never become syntax. `filter(name=user_input)`, `raw(\"... WHERE id = %s\", [user_input])`, `cursor.execute(sql, params)` and `RawSQL(\"...\", params)` are all parameterized. What parameterization *cannot* cover is **identifiers** — column names, table names, `ORDER BY` directions — so those must come from an allowlist, never from the user.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\nSecurity in a Django deployment is layered from the edge inward. The diagram maps each threat to the component that stops it and the configuration it depends on.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 440\" width=\"100%\" height=\"440\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj28c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Defence layers from the edge to the database</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"130\" height=\"380\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"85\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Browser</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"86\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">honours HSTS,</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">SameSite, CSP,</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"114\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">X-Frame-Options,</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"128\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">nosniff, COOP</text>\n  <path d=\"M150,230 L176,230\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"178\" y=\"40\" width=\"150\" height=\"380\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"253\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Proxy / LB</text>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"86\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">TLS termination</text>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">sets X-Forwarded-Proto</text>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"114\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">rejects unknown Host</text>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"128\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">body size limits</text>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"142\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">WAF, rate limits</text>\n  <path d=\"M328,230 L354,230\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"356\" y=\"40\" width=\"250\" height=\"380\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"481\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Django middleware</text>\n  <rect x=\"368\" y=\"74\" width=\"226\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"92\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">SecurityMiddleware (first)</text>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">SSL redirect, HSTS, nosniff,</text>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"120\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Referrer-Policy, COOP</text>\n  <rect x=\"368\" y=\"138\" width=\"226\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"156\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">CommonMiddleware / get_host()</text>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">ALLOWED_HOSTS: 400 on bad Host</text>\n  <rect x=\"368\" y=\"186\" width=\"226\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"204\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">CsrfViewMiddleware</text>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"218\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Origin/Referer + token</text>\n  <rect x=\"368\" y=\"234\" width=\"226\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"252\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">XFrameOptionsMiddleware</text>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"266\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">X-Frame-Options: DENY</text>\n  <rect x=\"368\" y=\"282\" width=\"226\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"300\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">django-csp (5.2) / built-in (6.0)</text>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"314\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Content-Security-Policy</text>\n  <text x=\"368\" y=\"350\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">order: Security, Session, Common,</text>\n  <text x=\"368\" y=\"364\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Csrf, Auth, Messages, XFrame</text>\n  <path d=\"M606,230 L632,230\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj28c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"634\" y=\"40\" width=\"226\" height=\"180\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"747\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">View + template</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"86\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">forms/serializers validate</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">explicit fields (no mass assign)</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"114\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">authorization (Ch 26)</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"128\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">autoescape, json_script</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"142\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">safe redirects</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"170\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">holes: |safe, mark_safe,</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"184\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">csrf_exempt, fields=\"__all__\"</text>\n  <rect x=\"634\" y=\"240\" width=\"226\" height=\"180\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"747\" y=\"262\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ORM / database</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"286\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">parameterized SQL</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"300\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">least-privilege DB role</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"314\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">TLS to database</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"342\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">holes: f-strings in raw(),</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"356\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">extra(), cursor.execute,</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"370\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">user-chosen identifiers,</text>\n  <text x=\"646\" y=\"384\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">**user_dict into values()</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### What `check --deploy` inspects\n\n```bash\nDJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=config.settings.production python manage.py check --deploy --fail-level WARNING\n```\n\n```text\nSystem check identified some issues:\n\nWARNINGS:\n?: (security.W004) You have not set a value for the SECURE_HSTS_SECONDS setting. ...\n?: (security.W008) Your SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT setting is not set to True. ...\n?: (security.W009) Your SECRET_KEY has less than 50 characters, less than 5 unique characters, or it's prefixed with 'django-insecure' ...\n?: (security.W012) SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE is not set to True. ...\n?: (security.W016) You have 'django.middleware.csrf.CsrfViewMiddleware' in your MIDDLEWARE, but you have not set CSRF_COOKIE_SECURE to True. ...\n?: (security.W018) You should not have DEBUG set to True in deployment.\n```\n\nRun it in CI against the production settings module with `--fail-level WARNING` so a regression fails the build.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: the safe and unsafe versions of each sink\n\n**CSRF in a form and in fetch:**\n\n```html\n<form method=\"post\" action=\"{% url 'transfer' %}\">\n  {% csrf_token %}\n  <input name=\"amount\"><button>Send</button>\n</form>\n\n{{ config|json_script:\"page-config\" }}\n<script>\n  function getCookie(name) {\n    return document.cookie.split(\"; \").find(r => r.startsWith(name + \"=\"))?.split(\"=\")[1];\n  }\n  fetch(\"/api/transfer/\", {\n    method: \"POST\",\n    headers: {\"X-CSRFToken\": getCookie(\"csrftoken\"), \"Content-Type\": \"application/json\"},\n    credentials: \"same-origin\",\n    body: JSON.stringify({amount: 10}),\n  });\n</script>\n```\n\nFor a SPA that never renders a Django form, decorate the page view (or a `/csrf/` endpoint) with `@ensure_csrf_cookie` so the cookie is set.\n\n**XSS — building HTML in Python:**\n\n```python\nfrom django.utils.html import format_html, format_html_join\nfrom django.utils.safestring import mark_safe\n\n# Vulnerable: the name is inserted raw and then trusted\ndef badge_bad(user):\n    return mark_safe(f'<span class=\"badge\">{user.display_name}</span>')\n\n# Safe: format_html escapes arguments and marks the whole result safe\ndef badge(user):\n    return format_html('<span class=\"badge\">{}</span>', user.display_name)\n\ndef tag_list(tags):\n    return format_html_join(\", \", '<a href=\"/t/{}/\">{}</a>', ((t.slug, t.name) for t in tags))\n```\n\n**SQL — the parameterized and the injectable:**\n\n```python\nfrom django.db import connection\nfrom django.db.models.expressions import RawSQL\n\n# Injectable: q = \"x' OR '1'='1\"\nProduct.objects.raw(f\"SELECT * FROM shop_product WHERE name = '{q}'\")\n\n# Safe: params are sent separately\nProduct.objects.raw(\"SELECT * FROM shop_product WHERE name = %s\", [q])\nwith connection.cursor() as cur:\n    cur.execute(\"UPDATE shop_product SET views = views + 1 WHERE id = %s\", [pid])\nProduct.objects.annotate(score=RawSQL(\"similarity(name, %s)\", (q,)))\n\n# Note: do NOT quote placeholders yourself: \"WHERE name = '%s'\" breaks parameterization\n```\n\nThe ORM's own SQL shows the separation:\n\n```python\n>>> qs = Product.objects.filter(name=\"x' OR '1'='1\")\n>>> qs.query.sql_with_params()\n('SELECT \"shop_product\".\"id\", \"shop_product\".\"name\" FROM \"shop_product\" WHERE \"shop_product\".\"name\" = %s',\n (\"x' OR '1'='1\",))\n```\n\n**Identifiers from an allowlist:**\n\n```python\nSORTABLE = {\"price\": \"price\", \"-price\": \"-price\", \"new\": \"-created_at\"}\n\ndef product_list(request):\n    order = SORTABLE.get(request.GET.get(\"sort\"), \"-created_at\")\n    return render(request, \"shop/list.html\", {\"products\": Product.objects.order_by(order)})\n```\n\n`order_by(user_input)` will not inject SQL (unknown names raise `FieldError`), but without an allowlist a user can order by `password` or `supplier__cost_price` and infer hidden values by observing order — an information leak.\n\n**Clickjacking:**\n\n```python\nfrom django.views.decorators.clickjacking import xframe_options_sameorigin\n\n@xframe_options_sameorigin          # allow our own pages to embed this widget only\ndef embeddable_chart(request): ...\n```\n\n### Real-world example: a production security settings module\n\n```python\n# config/settings/production.py\nimport os\nfrom .base import *  # noqa\n\nDEBUG = False\nSECRET_KEY = os.environ[\"DJANGO_SECRET_KEY\"]                       # never in git\nSECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS = [k for k in os.environ.get(\"DJANGO_OLD_SECRET_KEYS\", \"\").split(\",\") if k]\n\nALLOWED_HOSTS = [\"app.example.com\", \"api.example.com\"]\nCSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS = [\"https://app.example.com\", \"https://admin.example.com\"]\n\n# TLS terminates at the load balancer, which sets X-Forwarded-Proto.\n# Only safe if the proxy strips client-supplied X-Forwarded-Proto.\nSECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER = (\"HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO\", \"https\")\nSECURE_SSL_REDIRECT = True\nSECURE_REDIRECT_EXEMPT = [r\"^healthz$\"]                          # LB health check over HTTP\n\nSECURE_HSTS_SECONDS = 60 * 60 * 24 * 365      # start with 300, raise after verifying\nSECURE_HSTS_INCLUDE_SUBDOMAINS = True\nSECURE_HSTS_PRELOAD = True\n\nSESSION_COOKIE_SECURE = True\nCSRF_COOKIE_SECURE = True\nSESSION_COOKIE_SAMESITE = \"Lax\"\nSECURE_CONTENT_TYPE_NOSNIFF = True            # default True\nSECURE_REFERRER_POLICY = \"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\"\nSECURE_CROSS_ORIGIN_OPENER_POLICY = \"same-origin\"\nX_FRAME_OPTIONS = \"DENY\"                      # default since 3.0\n\nDATA_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE = 5 * 1024 * 1024\nFILE_UPLOAD_PERMISSIONS = 0o640\n\nPASSWORD_HASHERS = [\n    \"django.contrib.auth.hashers.Argon2PasswordHasher\",          # pip install argon2-cffi\n    \"django.contrib.auth.hashers.PBKDF2PasswordHasher\",          # verifies existing hashes, upgraded on login\n]\n\nMIDDLEWARE.insert(1, \"csp.middleware.CSPMiddleware\")              # django-csp\n\n# django-csp 4.x dict-style configuration\nCONTENT_SECURITY_POLICY = {\n    \"DIRECTIVES\": {\n        \"default-src\": [\"'self'\"],\n        \"script-src\": [\"'self'\"],\n        \"style-src\": [\"'self'\"],\n        \"img-src\": [\"'self'\", \"data:\", \"https://cdn.example.com\"],\n        \"frame-ancestors\": [\"'none'\"],\n        \"form-action\": [\"'self'\"],\n    }\n}\n\nLOGGING[\"loggers\"][\"django.security\"] = {\"handlers\": [\"console\"], \"level\": \"WARNING\", \"propagate\": False}\n```\n\nHiding secrets from error reports and logs:\n\n```python\nfrom django.views.decorators.debug import sensitive_post_parameters, sensitive_variables\n\n@sensitive_variables(\"card_number\", \"cvv\")\n@sensitive_post_parameters(\"card_number\", \"cvv\", \"password\")\ndef checkout(request):\n    card_number = request.POST[\"card_number\"]\n    ...\n```\n\nSafe redirects after login (open-redirect defence):\n\n```python\nfrom django.utils.http import url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme\n\ndef post_login_redirect(request):\n    nxt = request.POST.get(\"next\", \"/\")\n    if not url_has_allowed_host_and_scheme(nxt, allowed_hosts={request.get_host()},\n                                           require_https=request.is_secure()):\n        nxt = \"/\"\n    return redirect(nxt)\n```\n\nMass assignment — explicit fields only:\n\n```python\n# Vulnerable: a user can POST is_staff=on or balance=1000000\nclass ProfileForm(forms.ModelForm):\n    class Meta:\n        model = User\n        fields = \"__all__\"\n\n# Safe\nclass ProfileForm(forms.ModelForm):\n    class Meta:\n        model = User\n        fields = [\"first_name\", \"last_name\", \"email\"]\n```\n\nUser-generated rich text, sanitized before `mark_safe`:\n\n```python\nimport nh3   # pip install nh3\nALLOWED = {\"p\", \"b\", \"i\", \"em\", \"strong\", \"a\", \"ul\", \"ol\", \"li\", \"code\", \"pre\"}\n\ndef clean_html(dirty: str) -> str:\n    return nh3.clean(dirty, tags=ALLOWED, attributes={\"a\": {\"href\"}}, url_schemes={\"http\", \"https\", \"mailto\"})\n```\n\nAdmin hardening: move it off `/admin/`, require 2FA (django-otp), rate-limit login attempts (django-axes), and restrict by IP at the proxy if you can.\n\n```python\n# urls.py\nurlpatterns = [path(os.environ.get(\"ADMIN_URL\", \"control-7f3a/\"), admin.site.urls), ...]\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Defence | Cost | Gaps | Alternative / complement |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CSRF tokens | Token plumbing for AJAX, 403 debugging behind proxies | Useless if XSS exists (script reads the token) | SameSite cookies; token auth for non-browser clients |\n| SameSite=Lax | None | Same-site subdomains can still attack; GET state changes | Keep tokens; never change state on GET |\n| Autoescaping | Occasional `|safe` temptation | Context-insensitive: JS, URLs, unquoted attributes | `json_script`, `format_html`, CSP |\n| ORM parameterization | None | Raw SQL, identifiers | Allowlists, least-privilege DB user |\n| X-Frame-Options DENY | Breaks legit embeds | Legacy header | CSP `frame-ancestors` |\n| HSTS + preload | Hard to undo; all subdomains must support HTTPS | First visit before preload | Start with short max-age |\n| CSP | Inline scripts must be refactored or nonce'd | Misconfigured `unsafe-inline` nullifies it | Report-only mode first |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Always: default middleware, `check --deploy` in CI, explicit form fields, `ALLOWED_HOSTS`, HTTPS settings.\n- CSP in report-only mode on day one of a new app (cheap) and enforce once violations are understood.\n- `CSRF_USE_SESSIONS = True` when you must not have a JS-readable CSRF cookie (strict compliance), accepting that the token must be rendered into pages.\n- HSTS preload only when every subdomain is permanently HTTPS.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Do not `@csrf_exempt` an endpoint to \"fix\" a 403 — find which check failed (DEBUG page or `django.security.csrf` log). Webhooks from third parties are the legitimate exemption, and they must be authenticated by a signature instead.\n- Do not set `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` unless your proxy always sets/overwrites that header; otherwise clients can claim HTTPS.\n- Do not enable long HSTS before confirming HTTPS works everywhere — browsers will refuse HTTP for the whole max-age.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. `DEBUG = True` in production.** The technical 500 page shows settings (Django masks names containing KEY, SECRET, PASS, TOKEN, API, SIGNATURE, but not your custom names like `DATABASE_URL`), local variables, SQL and paths. Bots scan for it. Load `DEBUG` from the environment with a default of `False`.\n\n**2. `ALLOWED_HOSTS = [\"*\"]`.** Enables Host-header poisoning: `PasswordResetView` builds the reset link from `request.get_host()`, so a forged `Host: evil.com` sends the victim a link to evil.com containing a valid token. List exact hosts; reject unknown hosts at the proxy too.\n\n**3. `|safe` on user data.** Usually added to render line breaks or links. Use `|linebreaks` / `|urlize` (which escape), or sanitize and then mark safe.\n\n**4. Variables inside `<script>`.**\n\n```html\n<!-- Before: XSS if title contains </script> or quotes in some contexts -->\n<script>const title = \"{{ article.title }}\";</script>\n<!-- After -->\n{{ article.title|json_script:\"title\" }}\n<script>const title = JSON.parse(document.getElementById(\"title\").textContent);</script>\n```\n\n**5. Changing state on GET.** `GET /logout/` or `GET /delete/42/` bypasses CSRF (safe methods skip the check) and SameSite=Lax allows top-level GET navigations, so an `<img src>` or link triggers it. Since Django 5.0, `LogoutView` only accepts POST for this reason.\n\n**6. Committing `SECRET_KEY`.** Anyone with the repo can forge password-reset tokens, signed cookies and messages. If it leaked: rotate immediately (old key into `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS` only if you are sure it was not exposed to attackers; otherwise accept the logout), and consider every signed artifact compromised.\n\n**7. Serving user uploads from your main origin.** An uploaded `.html` or SVG with script served from `app.example.com/media/` is stored XSS. Serve media from a separate domain (S3/CDN bucket on another origin), set `Content-Disposition: attachment` for downloads, validate file types by content, not extension.\n\n**8. Splatting user dicts into ORM kwargs.** `Model.objects.values(**request.GET.dict())` or `annotate(**user_dict)` lets users choose aliases and lookups. Several Django CVEs (2022 and 2024) were SQL injection through user-controlled column aliases or JSON keys in exactly these APIs. Never pass user-controlled keys as kwargs, and stay on a patched release.\n\n**Best practices:** keep Django on the latest patch of an LTS (security releases are announced in advance on the django-announce list); `pip-audit` or Dependabot in CI; run `bandit` for Python sinks; one security review checklist per PR touching auth, templates with `safe`, raw SQL or exemptions; grep-able opt-outs mean you can audit them: `grep -rn \"csrf_exempt\\|mark_safe\\||safe\\|raw(\\|extra(\\|cursor.execute\" .`.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Production story — 403 CSRF verification failed after moving behind a load balancer.** *Symptom:* every login POST fails with \"Origin checking failed - https://app.example.com does not match any trusted origins.\" *Root cause:* TLS terminated at the LB, Django saw `http://` and computed its own origin as `http://app.example.com`, which does not equal the browser's `https://` Origin. *Fix:* `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` with a proxy that sets `X-Forwarded-Proto`, and `USE_X_FORWARDED_HOST` only if the proxy controls that header. For cross-subdomain posts (admin on another subdomain), add the exact origin with scheme to `CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS`.\n\n**Production story — \"Invalid HTTP_HOST header\" floods.** Sentry fills with `DisallowedHost` errors from scanners hitting the IP directly. *Fix:* reject unknown Host headers at Nginx (`server_name _; return 444;` default server) and silence the `django.security.DisallowedHost` logger rather than allowing `*`.\n\n**Stored XSS via the admin.** A staff-editable \"announcement banner\" rendered with `|safe` because marketing wanted bold text. A compromised low-privilege staff account then injected script that ran in every superuser's browser. *Fix:* sanitize with an allowlist on save, CSP that forbids inline script, and treat staff input as untrusted.\n\n**HSTS lockout.** A team enabled `SECURE_HSTS_SECONDS = 31536000` with `INCLUDE_SUBDOMAINS` while an internal HTTP-only subdomain existed; browsers refused it for a year. *Fix:* ramp HSTS (300 s, then a day, a week, a month) and inventory subdomains first.\n\n**Debugging tools.**\n\n- The DEBUG CSRF failure page states the exact reason; in production log `django.security.csrf` at WARNING.\n- `curl -I https://app.example.com/` to verify headers: `Strict-Transport-Security`, `X-Frame-Options`, `X-Content-Type-Options`, `Referrer-Policy`, `Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy`, `Content-Security-Policy`.\n- Mozilla Observatory or securityheaders.com for an external header scan.\n- `python manage.py check --deploy` in CI; `pip-audit` for known CVEs in dependencies.\n- Brute force: django-axes logs and locks out; also rate limit at the edge.\n\n**Scaling security.** At scale, push generic defences to the edge (WAF, rate limits, bot detection, Host filtering at the CDN) so Django workers spend time on real traffic, but keep application-level defences (CSRF, escaping, authorization) in Django because only the app understands the semantics. Secrets go into a secret manager injected as environment variables, rotated on a schedule with `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS`. Database credentials should be a least-privilege role without DDL rights for the web process; migrations run with a separate role.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: How does Django prevent CSRF attacks?**\nA: `CsrfViewMiddleware` requires every unsafe request (POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE) to carry a token that matches a secret stored in the `csrftoken` cookie (or the session). Templates insert a masked copy with `{% csrf_token %}`; JavaScript sends it in the `X-CSRFToken` header. A malicious site can make the browser send your cookies but cannot read your cookie or page, so it cannot produce the token. On HTTPS Django also checks `Origin`/`Referer` against the host and `CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS`. SameSite=Lax session cookies add a browser-side layer.\n\n**Q: Why is the CSRF token in the form different on every page load?**\nA: The cookie holds a constant secret, but the token rendered into HTML is the secret combined with a random mask, regenerated each time. The middleware unmasks it before comparing. This defeats BREACH-style attacks, which recover a repeated secret from the size of compressed HTTPS responses; a value that changes every render leaks nothing useful.\n\n**Q: Does a DRF API need CSRF protection?**\nA: Only if it authenticates with cookies. DRF's `SessionAuthentication` enforces CSRF for authenticated requests (it runs Django's check explicitly, because `APIView` is `csrf_exempt` by default). `TokenAuthentication` and JWT in an `Authorization` header do not need CSRF, because a cross-site attacker cannot make the browser attach that header. If you store a JWT in a cookie, you are back to needing CSRF protection.\n\n**Q: How does Django prevent XSS, and where does autoescaping fail?**\nA: The template engine HTML-escapes `< > & ' \"` in every variable unless it is a `SafeString`. It fails when you opt out (`|safe`, `mark_safe`, `autoescape off`), and in contexts where HTML escaping is the wrong encoding: inside `<script>` blocks, in unquoted attributes, and in URL attributes where `javascript:` URLs contain nothing to escape. Use `json_script` for data to JavaScript, always quote attributes, validate URL schemes, sanitize rich text with an allowlist, and add CSP as a backstop.\n\n**Q: How does the Django ORM prevent SQL injection, and when can you still be vulnerable?**\nA: The compiler produces SQL with `%s` placeholders and a separate parameter list, and the driver transmits values without letting them become syntax. You are vulnerable when you build SQL with string formatting in `raw()`, `extra()`, `RawSQL` or `cursor.execute()`, when you quote a placeholder yourself (`'%s'`), and when user input chooses identifiers like column names or sort fields, which cannot be parameterized. Use params everywhere and allowlists for identifiers.\n\n**Q: What is clickjacking and how does Django defend against it?**\nA: The attacker loads your page in a transparent iframe over a decoy and tricks the user into clicking your real buttons; CSRF tokens do not help because the real page submits the real form. `XFrameOptionsMiddleware` sends `X-Frame-Options: DENY` by default (since Django 3.0), so browsers refuse to frame the page. `@xframe_options_sameorigin` and `@xframe_options_exempt` relax it per view. The modern equivalent is CSP `frame-ancestors`.\n\n**Q: What does `ALLOWED_HOSTS` protect against?**\nA: Host header attacks. Django uses `request.get_host()` to build absolute URLs, for example in password-reset emails and redirects; if any Host is accepted, an attacker can request a reset for a victim with `Host: evil.com`, and the email contains a valid token linking to the attacker's domain. With `DEBUG=False`, Django returns 400 for hosts not in `ALLOWED_HOSTS`. It also protects caches keyed by host from poisoning.\n\n**Q: What does `python manage.py check --deploy` do?**\nA: It runs the system checks tagged for deployment against the current settings: `DEBUG`, `SECRET_KEY` strength, `ALLOWED_HOSTS`, `SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT`, HSTS settings, `SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE`, `CSRF_COOKIE_SECURE`, missing security middleware, `X_FRAME_OPTIONS` and so on, and prints warnings with ids like `security.W004`. Run it against the production settings module in CI with `--fail-level WARNING`. It does not scan your code for `mark_safe` or raw SQL; it only checks configuration.\n\n**Q: What is `SECRET_KEY` used for, and what happens if it leaks?**\nA: It is the HMAC key for Django's cryptographic signing: the session auth hash, signed-cookie sessions, the messages cookie, `signing.dumps`, password-reset tokens and anything using `Signer`. It is not used to hash passwords. If it leaks, attackers can forge password-reset tokens for any user, forge signed cookies and messages, and with the signed-cookie session engine, forge whole sessions. Rotate it, revoke sessions, and treat signed data issued before the leak as untrusted.\n\n**Q: What HTTPS-related settings must you set in production?**\nA: `SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT = True`, `SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE = True`, `CSRF_COOKIE_SECURE = True`, `SECURE_HSTS_SECONDS` (ramped up), optionally `SECURE_HSTS_INCLUDE_SUBDOMAINS` and `SECURE_HSTS_PRELOAD`, and `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` if TLS terminates at a proxy that sets `X-Forwarded-Proto`. `SecurityMiddleware` must be first so the redirect happens before any other work. Without the proxy header setting, Django thinks requests are HTTP, which causes redirect loops and CSRF origin failures.\n\n**Q: You inherit a Django codebase. How do you audit its security? (Senior)**\nA: Start with configuration: `check --deploy` against production settings, Django and dependency versions against advisories (`pip-audit`), and where `SECRET_KEY` and credentials live. Then grep the explicit opt-outs, because Django makes them greppable: `csrf_exempt`, `mark_safe`, `|safe`, `autoescape off`, `raw(`, `extra(`, `RawSQL`, `cursor.execute`, `fields = \"__all__\"`, `xframe_options_exempt`, `ALLOWED_HOSTS`. Review authorization next — querysets not scoped by owner or tenant are the most common real bug (IDOR). Check file upload handling and where media is served, redirects using `next`, and admin exposure (URL, 2FA, rate limiting). Finally scan response headers externally and add CSP in report-only mode to see what an XSS would be able to do.\n\n**Q: How would you deploy CSP on a large existing Django app without breaking it? (Senior)**\nA: Install django-csp on 5.2 (Django 6.0 has built-in CSP support) and ship the policy in `Content-Security-Policy-Report-Only` mode with a `report-uri`/`report-to` endpoint so browsers report violations without blocking. Inventory violations by directive and source, move inline scripts to static files or add per-request nonces (django-csp exposes `request.csp_nonce` for templates), and replace inline event handlers. Tighten progressively — `frame-ancestors` and `object-src 'none'` first, then `script-src` — and only switch to enforcing mode when reports are quiet. Avoid `'unsafe-inline'` in `script-src`, which defeats most of CSP's XSS protection.\n\n**Q: How do you handle third-party webhooks that cannot send a CSRF token? (Senior)**\nA: Exempting the view from CSRF is correct here, because webhooks are not browser requests and carry no user cookies; but you must replace CSRF with real authentication. Verify the provider's HMAC signature over the raw body with `hmac.compare_digest`, check the timestamp to reject replays outside a window, optionally restrict source IPs, and make processing idempotent on the event id because providers retry. In DRF, use an `APIView` with no `SessionAuthentication` and a custom authentication class that verifies the signature, so the endpoint is never cookie-authenticated.\n\n**Q: How does Django protect against brute-force login and password attacks?**\nA: Password storage is strong: PBKDF2-SHA256 by default with a high iteration count, or Argon2 if configured, with per-password salts and hashes upgraded on login. Password validators (`AUTH_PASSWORD_VALIDATORS`) reject common, numeric, short and user-similar passwords. Django does not rate-limit login attempts itself; add django-axes or edge rate limiting, and 2FA for staff. `login()` rotates the session key, and changing the password invalidates other sessions via the session auth hash.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Threat | Default protection | Opt-out to audit | Extra hardening |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| CSRF | `CsrfViewMiddleware`, `{% csrf_token %}`, SameSite=Lax | `@csrf_exempt` | `CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS` with scheme, POST-only state changes |\n| XSS | Autoescape | `|safe`, `mark_safe`, `autoescape off` | `json_script`, `format_html`, CSP, nh3 |\n| SQLi | Parameterized ORM | f-strings in `raw`/`extra`/`cursor` | Identifier allowlists, least-privilege DB role |\n| Clickjacking | `X_FRAME_OPTIONS = \"DENY\"` | `@xframe_options_exempt` | CSP `frame-ancestors` |\n| Host header | `ALLOWED_HOSTS` | `[\"*\"]` | Reject at proxy |\n| Transport | — | — | SSL redirect, HSTS, Secure cookies, proxy header |\n| Disclosure | — | `DEBUG = True` | `sensitive_variables`, Sentry scrubbing |\n| Mass assignment | — | `fields = \"__all__\"` | Explicit fields / serializer `read_only_fields` |\n\n- Security features are mostly middleware; order starts with `SecurityMiddleware`.\n- Safe methods skip CSRF, so GET must never change state.\n- Autoescaping is HTML-body escaping only.\n- Parameterize values; allowlist identifiers.\n- `check --deploy --fail-level WARNING` in CI.\n- `CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS` entries need a scheme (4.0+).\n- `SECRET_KEY` signs things; it does not hash passwords. Rotate with `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS`.\n- CSP: django-csp on 5.2, built-in from 6.0; start report-only.\n- Serve user uploads from another origin.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" django-csp nh3`, `django-admin startproject site .`.\n\n1. **Forge a CSRF.** Build a POST `/transfer/` view. Serve a separate HTML page on another port with an auto-submitting form to it. Observe the 403; add `@csrf_exempt` and watch the attack succeed with your logged-in session (note SameSite=Lax may still block it — set `SESSION_COOKIE_SAMESITE = None` with Secure on localhost HTTPS to see why both layers matter). Remove the exemption.\n2. **XSS gallery.** Render a comment containing `<script>alert(1)</script>` normally and with `|safe`; then a website field `javascript:alert(1)` in an `href`; then a value inside `<script>`. Fix each with escaping, `URLValidator`, and `json_script`.\n3. **SQL injection.** Write a search using `raw(f\"... '{q}'\")` and extract all users with a crafted `q`. Rewrite with params and show `qs.query.sql_with_params()`.\n4. **Host header.** Set `ALLOWED_HOSTS = [\"*\"]`, trigger a password reset with `curl -H \"Host: evil.test\"` and the console email backend; inspect the link. Fix and confirm the 400.\n5. **Deploy check.** Create a `production.py` settings module, run `check --deploy`, and drive warnings to zero. Verify headers with `curl -I`.\n6. **CSP.** Add django-csp in report-only mode, add an inline script, and watch the browser console report; move it to a static file.\n\n**Mini project — security baseline package.** Create a reusable `security_baseline` app: a production settings mixin, a CI script that runs `check --deploy`, `pip-audit`, and a grep for opt-outs that fails on any un-annotated `mark_safe`/`csrf_exempt`/raw SQL; a signed-webhook `APIView` base class with HMAC and timestamp verification; and a test suite asserting response headers and CSRF enforcement on every POST route.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 27 · Sessions, Cookies & Messages](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies) · [Ch 26 · Permissions, Groups & Authorization](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization) · [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user) · [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) · [Ch 05 · Templates](topic.html?p=05-templates) · [Ch 20 · Static & Media Files](topic.html?p=20-static-media-files) · [Ch 23 · DRF Authentication, Permissions & Throttling](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling) · [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · TLS, CORS & Security Headers](../rest-api/topic.html?p=22-tls-cors-security-headers) · [REST API · OWASP API Security](../rest-api/topic.html?p=23-owasp-api-security) · [REST API · Validation & Input Sanitization](../rest-api/topic.html?p=17-validation-input-sanitization)\n\n- **Security in Django** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the official overview of every built-in protection. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/security/>\n- **Cross Site Request Forgery protection** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · how the token, Origin checks and AJAX usage work. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/csrf/>\n- **Deployment checklist** — Django docs · *Beginner* · the settings `check --deploy` inspects, explained. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/deployment/checklist/>\n- **Django security releases archive** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · real CVEs and what they teach about risky APIs. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/dev/releases/security/>\n- **OWASP Cheat Sheet Series (CSRF, XSS, SQLi)** — OWASP · *Intermediate* · framework-neutral prevention guidance. <https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/>\n- **django-csp** — GitHub (Mozilla) · *Intermediate* · Content Security Policy middleware for Django 5.2. <https://github.com/mozilla/django-csp>\n- **MDN: Content Security Policy** — MDN · *Intermediate* · directives, nonces and report-only mode. <https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/CSP>\n- **PortSwigger Web Security Academy** — PortSwigger · *Intermediate* · free hands-on labs for CSRF, XSS, SQLi and Host header attacks. <https://portswigger.net/web-security>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 28.*\n", "29-caching": "# 29 · Caching in Django\n\n> **In one line:** Django's cache framework is one API (`django.core.cache`) over pluggable backends, exposed at four granularities — whole site, per view, template fragment and low-level keys — plus HTTP caching headers that let browsers and CDNs skip Django entirely; the hard part is never storing data, it is choosing the right layer, keying it correctly (`Vary`!) and invalidating it without serving stale or private data.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) (fix the queries before you cache them), [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) (per-site caching is a middleware pair), [Ch 27 · Sessions, Cookies & Messages](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies) (sessions add `Vary: Cookie`), [Ch 05 · Templates](topic.html?p=05-templates) (fragment caching).\n\nA product page that takes 180 ms — 12 queries, a pricing service call, a template with 400 loop iterations — is fine at ten requests per second and a disaster at a thousand. Most of that work produces the same bytes for every visitor for minutes at a time. **Caching** stores the result of expensive work and reuses it, trading freshness and memory for latency and throughput.\n\nThe naive approaches fail in predictable ways. A module-level dict (`_cache = {}`) works on `runserver` and silently diverges across Gunicorn workers, grows forever, and is wiped on every deploy. Caching an entire page \"because it is slow\" leaks one user's name and cart into everyone else's page the moment the page contains anything personal. Caching without an invalidation plan turns \"the price changed\" into a support ticket about why the site still shows the old one for an hour.\n\nDjango's **cache framework** gives you:\n\n- **Backends** behind one interface: Redis (built in since Django 4.0), Memcached, database, file system, local memory and a dummy backend for development.\n- **Four granularities:** the **per-site cache** (`UpdateCacheMiddleware` + `FetchFromCacheMiddleware`), the **per-view cache** (`@cache_page`), **template fragment caching** (`{% cache %}`), and the **low-level API** (`cache.get/set/get_or_set/delete/incr`).\n- **HTTP caching** helpers — `@cache_control`, `@never_cache`, `@vary_on_headers`, `@condition`/`@etag`/`@last_modified`, `ConditionalGetMiddleware` — so browsers and CDNs can cache or revalidate without your code running at all.\n\nThis chapter shows how each layer works internally (how `cache_page` builds keys, what `Vary` does to them), how to pick one, how to invalidate correctly (TTL, explicit delete on commit, versioned keys), and the production failure modes: per-process `locmem`, stampedes, cached private data, pickle pitfalls and Redis memory pressure. Redis itself — eviction policies, persistence, clustering — is covered in depth in the [Redis Caching handbook](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=03-redis-as-a-cache).\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **`CACHES` setting** — a dict of aliases (`\"default\"` required) each with `BACKEND`, `LOCATION`, `TIMEOUT`, `OPTIONS`, `KEY_PREFIX`, `VERSION`. *Why it matters:* you can have separate caches for pages, sessions and throttling with different eviction rules.\n- **`TIMEOUT`** — default 300 seconds; `None` means never expire; `0` means expire immediately (do not cache). *Why:* forgetting it means everything lives five minutes, which is either too long or too short.\n- **Cache key** — the final key is `KEY_FUNCTION(key, KEY_PREFIX, VERSION)`, by default `\"<prefix>:<version>:<key>\"`. *Why:* prefixes isolate environments sharing one Redis; versions invalidate whole namespaces.\n- **Serialization** — values are pickled (Redis, Memcached, db, file backends). *Why:* any picklable object can be cached, but class changes can break old entries and a writable cache becomes a code-execution vector.\n- **Cache-aside** — read cache, on miss compute and set. Django's `get_or_set` is the one-liner. *Why:* the default pattern; see [Redis Caching · Cache-Aside](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=08-cache-aside).\n- **Per-view cache (`cache_page`)** — caches the full `HttpResponse` keyed by URL and `Vary` headers. *Why:* biggest win for anonymous, read-heavy pages.\n- **Per-site cache** — the same mechanism applied to every GET/HEAD via middleware. *Why:* blunt, rarely right for apps with logged-in users.\n- **Fragment cache** — `{% cache 600 \"sidebar\" user.id %}` caches a rendered block. *Why:* caches the expensive, shared parts of a personalized page.\n- **`Vary` header** — names request headers that change the response; Django's page cache folds them into the key. *Why:* `Vary: Cookie` makes the cache per-user; missing `Vary` serves one user's page to another.\n- **Invalidation** — expire by TTL, delete on change, or bump a version. *Why:* the hard problem; see [Redis Caching · Cache Invalidation](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=12-cache-invalidation).\n- **Stampede (dog-pile)** — many requests miss simultaneously and all recompute. *Why:* can take down the database exactly when a hot key expires; see [Redis Caching · Cache Stampede](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=15-cache-stampede).\n- **Conditional GET** — `ETag`/`Last-Modified` with `If-None-Match`/`If-Modified-Since` lets the server answer `304 Not Modified`. *Why:* saves bandwidth and rendering even when content must be revalidated.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### The backend abstraction\n\nEvery backend subclasses `django.core.cache.backends.base.BaseCache`, which implements key construction (`make_and_validate_key`), default timeouts and the async methods (`aget`, `aset`, ... — by default thin `sync_to_async` wrappers). `django.core.cache.caches[\"alias\"]` returns a per-thread backend instance (connections are not shared across threads); `django.core.cache.cache` is a proxy for `caches[\"default\"]`.\n\n| Backend | Shared across processes | Atomic `incr` | Notes |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `django.core.cache.backends.redis.RedisCache` | Yes | Yes | Built in since 4.0; uses redis-py; a list `LOCATION` = first is primary for writes, others replicas for reads |\n| `django.core.cache.backends.memcached.PyMemcacheCache` | Yes | Yes | Keys max 250 bytes, no spaces; values default max about 1 MB; no persistence |\n| `django.core.cache.backends.db.DatabaseCache` | Yes | No | Needs `createcachetable`; adds DB load — usually the wrong place |\n| `django.core.cache.backends.filebased.FileBasedCache` | Same host only | No | Culls by scanning; slow with many entries |\n| `django.core.cache.backends.locmem.LocMemCache` (default) | **No — per process** | Within process | LRU with `MAX_ENTRIES` (default 300); fine for tests and single-process dev |\n| `django.core.cache.backends.dummy.DummyCache` | — | — | Implements the API, stores nothing |\n\nThe third-party **django-redis** backend predates the built-in one and adds features the built-in lacks: `cache.lock()`, `delete_pattern()`, `ttl()`, compression and pluggable serializers. Many production codebases still use it.\n\n### How `cache_page` builds its key and decides what to store\n\n`@cache_page(timeout)` wraps the view with `CacheMiddleware` (the combined fetch/update middleware). On a request:\n\n1. **Fetch phase** (`FetchFromCacheMiddleware.process_request`): only for `GET` and `HEAD`. It calls `get_cache_key(request, key_prefix, \"GET\", cache)`, which first looks up a **header list** stored under a key derived from the full URL (`views.decorators.cache.cache_header.<prefix>.<md5 of absolute URL>`). That list records which request headers the cached response varies on. It then builds the **page key** (`views.decorators.cache.cache_page.<prefix>.GET.<md5 url>.<md5 of those header values>`). Hit: return the stored response; the view never runs.\n2. **Update phase** (`UpdateCacheMiddleware.process_response`): stores the response only if it is a non-streaming 200 (or 304) to a GET/HEAD, the response is not `Cache-Control: private`, and the timeout is not zero. It also skips responses that set cookies on cookie-less requests while varying on `Cookie` (e.g. the first CSRF cookie). It calls `learn_cache_key()`, which saves the response's `Vary` header list under the header-list key and then stores the response under the page key. It also **patches the response headers** with `Cache-Control: max-age=<timeout>` and `Expires`, so browsers cache it too.\n\nTwo consequences everyone misses. First, if the response has `Vary: Cookie`, the page key includes the cookie value, so the cache is effectively per-session — safe, but with a hit rate near zero for logged-in traffic. Second, **where** the caching runs decides which `Vary` headers it sees. `SessionMiddleware` adds `Vary: Cookie` in *its* response phase. With the per-site middleware, `UpdateCacheMiddleware` is outermost and sees it. With the `@cache_page` **decorator**, the response is stored *inside* the middleware stack — before `SessionMiddleware` has added `Vary: Cookie` — so a decorated view that renders `request.user` can be cached once and served to everyone unless you add `@vary_on_cookie` (or never decorate personalized views).\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 430\" width=\"100%\" height=\"430\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj29a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj29b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">cache_page: two keys, Vary-aware lookup</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"46\" width=\"200\" height=\"54\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">GET /products/?page=2</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Accept-Language: de</text>\n  <path d=\"M220,72 L262,72\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj29a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"264\" y=\"40\" width=\"300\" height=\"66\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"414\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. header-list key</text>\n  <text x=\"276\" y=\"78\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">cache_header.prefix.md5(absolute URL)</text>\n  <text x=\"276\" y=\"94\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">value: [\"HTTP_ACCEPT_LANGUAGE\", \"HTTP_COOKIE\"]</text>\n  <path d=\"M414,106 L414,132\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj29a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"264\" y=\"134\" width=\"300\" height=\"66\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"414\" y=\"154\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. page key</text>\n  <text x=\"276\" y=\"172\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">cache_page.prefix.GET.md5(URL).md5(values</text>\n  <text x=\"276\" y=\"188\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">of the listed headers in THIS request)</text>\n  <path d=\"M564,166 L620,166\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj29b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"592\" y=\"158\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">hit</text>\n  <rect x=\"622\" y=\"134\" width=\"238\" height=\"66\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"741\" y=\"158\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">return stored response</text>\n  <text x=\"741\" y=\"176\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">view, ORM, template skipped</text>\n  <path d=\"M414,200 L414,226\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj29a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"426\" y=\"218\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">miss</text>\n  <rect x=\"264\" y=\"228\" width=\"300\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"414\" y=\"250\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">3. view runs</text>\n  <text x=\"414\" y=\"266\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">response carries Vary: Accept-Language, Cookie</text>\n  <path d=\"M414,278 L414,304\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj29a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"164\" y=\"306\" width=\"500\" height=\"82\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"414\" y=\"326\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">4. UpdateCacheMiddleware: store only if safe</text>\n  <text x=\"178\" y=\"346\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">GET/HEAD, status 200, not streaming, not Cache-Control: private, timeout &gt; 0</text>\n  <text x=\"178\" y=\"362\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">learn_cache_key(): save header list, then response under the page key</text>\n  <text x=\"178\" y=\"378\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">patch headers: Cache-Control: max-age=timeout, Expires</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"134\" width=\"220\" height=\"140\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"130\" y=\"154\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Why Vary matters</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"176\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Vary: Cookie puts the session</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"192\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">cookie into the page key:</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"208\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">safe, but one entry per user.</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"232\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">No Vary on a personalized</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"248\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">page: user A's HTML is served</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"264\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">to user B.</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"414\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">The header-list key is why changing Vary on a deployed view needs a key_prefix bump to take effect cleanly.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Invalidation strategies\n\nThere are three families; pick per data item.\n\n1. **Time-based (TTL).** Accept staleness up to N seconds. Simple, self-healing, and the only option for `cache_page` (which has no per-URL delete API you would want to rely on). Use short TTLs for volatile data.\n2. **Event-based (delete on write).** When the source changes, delete the key. In Django, do it in `transaction.on_commit()` — deleting inside the transaction lets a concurrent reader repopulate the cache from the *old* committed row before your commit lands.\n3. **Version-based (namespacing).** Store a version counter (`cache.incr(\"catalog:v\")`) and include it in every catalog key. Bumping it invalidates millions of keys in O(1); the old ones age out by TTL. Django's `version=` argument and `VERSION` setting are the built-in form of this.\n\nThe delete-on-commit race, step by step:\n\n```text\nwriter: BEGIN; UPDATE product SET price=9 ...; cache.delete(\"p:1\")   (inside txn)\nreader:                            cache miss on \"p:1\" -> SELECT -> sees OLD price 10 (not committed yet)\nreader:                            cache.set(\"p:1\", price=10, 1h)\nwriter: COMMIT\nresult: cache holds the old price for an hour.\nfix:    transaction.on_commit(lambda: cache.delete(\"p:1\"))\n```\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\nA request can be satisfied at any of several layers; each layer closer to the user is cheaper and harder to invalidate.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj29c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Caching layers in a Django deployment</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"130\" height=\"300\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"85\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Browser</text>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Cache-Control</text>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"104\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">max-age, ETag</text>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">304 revalidation</text>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"318\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">cannot purge</text>\n  <path d=\"M150,190 L172,190\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj29c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"174\" y=\"44\" width=\"130\" height=\"300\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"239\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">CDN / Nginx</text>\n  <text x=\"184\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">public, s-maxage</text>\n  <text x=\"184\" y=\"104\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">stale-while-revalidate</text>\n  <text x=\"184\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">static files</text>\n  <text x=\"184\" y=\"318\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"10\">purge API, slow</text>\n  <path d=\"M304,190 L326,190\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj29c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"328\" y=\"44\" width=\"380\" height=\"300\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"518\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Django process</text>\n  <rect x=\"340\" y=\"80\" width=\"356\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"98\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Per-site: UpdateCacheMiddleware ... FetchFromCacheMiddleware</text>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"114\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">every anonymous GET/HEAD; first and last in MIDDLEWARE</text>\n  <rect x=\"340\" y=\"132\" width=\"356\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"150\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Per-view: @cache_page(60 * 15)</text>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"166\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">whole response for one URL, keyed with Vary</text>\n  <rect x=\"340\" y=\"184\" width=\"356\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"202\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fragment: {% cache 600 \"nav\" request.LANGUAGE_CODE %}</text>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"218\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">shared parts of a personalized page</text>\n  <rect x=\"340\" y=\"236\" width=\"356\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"254\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Low-level: cache.get_or_set(\"p:42:v3\", fn, 300)</text>\n  <text x=\"350\" y=\"270\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">data, computed values, API responses; precise invalidation</text>\n  <text x=\"340\" y=\"304\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">also in-process: @cached_property (per instance),</text>\n  <text x=\"340\" y=\"318\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">functools.lru_cache (per process), cached template loader</text>\n  <path d=\"M708,190 L730,190\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj29c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"732\" y=\"44\" width=\"128\" height=\"140\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"796\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Redis</text>\n  <text x=\"742\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">shared by all</text>\n  <text x=\"742\" y=\"104\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">workers and hosts</text>\n  <text x=\"742\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">TTL + eviction</text>\n  <rect x=\"732\" y=\"204\" width=\"128\" height=\"140\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"796\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">PostgreSQL</text>\n  <text x=\"742\" y=\"250\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">source of truth</text>\n  <text x=\"742\" y=\"264\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">reached only</text>\n  <text x=\"742\" y=\"278\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">on a miss</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"378\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Left = cheapest per hit, hardest to invalidate. Right = most precise, costs a network round trip.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Choosing a layer\n\n| Layer | Granularity | Invalidation | Personalization | Typical use |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Browser / CDN (headers) | URL | TTL, revalidation, CDN purge | Only with `private` / `Vary` | Static assets, public pages, public API GETs |\n| Per-site middleware | Every URL | TTL only | Dangerous | Mostly-anonymous content sites |\n| `cache_page` | One view's URLs | TTL (or key_prefix bump) | Via `Vary` (low hit rate) | Anonymous landing pages, public listings |\n| Fragment | Template block | TTL, `make_template_fragment_key` delete | Pass vary-on args | Navigation, sidebars, expensive widgets |\n| Low-level | Any value | Exact delete, versions | Key per user if needed | Query results, computed aggregates, third-party API calls |\n\n### Per-site middleware order\n\n```python\nMIDDLEWARE = [\n    \"django.middleware.cache.UpdateCacheMiddleware\",      # FIRST: runs last on the response\n    \"django.middleware.security.SecurityMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.sessions.middleware.SessionMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.common.CommonMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.csrf.CsrfViewMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.auth.middleware.AuthenticationMiddleware\",\n    \"django.contrib.messages.middleware.MessageMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.clickjacking.XFrameOptionsMiddleware\",\n    \"django.middleware.cache.FetchFromCacheMiddleware\",   # LAST: runs last on the request\n]\nCACHE_MIDDLEWARE_ALIAS = \"pages\"\nCACHE_MIDDLEWARE_SECONDS = 600\nCACHE_MIDDLEWARE_KEY_PREFIX = \"site\"\n```\n\n`UpdateCacheMiddleware` must be outermost so it sees the response after every other middleware has added its headers (especially `Vary: Cookie` from sessions); `FetchFromCacheMiddleware` must be innermost so the session and auth middleware have run and the `Vary`-based key can be computed.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: configure Redis and use every layer\n\n```bash\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"redis>=5\" hiredis\n```\n\n```python\n# settings.py\nCACHES = {\n    \"default\": {\n        \"BACKEND\": \"django.core.cache.backends.redis.RedisCache\",\n        \"LOCATION\": \"redis://127.0.0.1:6379/1\",\n        \"TIMEOUT\": 300,\n        \"KEY_PREFIX\": \"shop-prod\",\n    },\n    \"pages\": {\n        \"BACKEND\": \"django.core.cache.backends.redis.RedisCache\",\n        \"LOCATION\": \"redis://127.0.0.1:6379/3\",\n        \"TIMEOUT\": 600,\n    },\n}\n```\n\nPer-view:\n\n```python\n# catalog/views.py\nfrom django.views.decorators.cache import cache_page\nfrom django.views.decorators.vary import vary_on_headers\n\n\n@cache_page(60 * 15, cache=\"pages\", key_prefix=\"catalog-v1\")\n@vary_on_headers(\"Accept-Language\")\ndef category(request, slug):\n    ...\n\n# URLconf form, useful for cache-ing a CBV or a third-party view\npath(\"about/\", cache_page(60 * 60)(AboutView.as_view())),\n```\n\nFragment:\n\n```html\n{% load cache %}\n{% cache 600 category_sidebar category.pk request.LANGUAGE_CODE %}\n  {% for c in category.children.all %}<a href=\"{{ c.get_absolute_url }}\">{{ c.name }}</a>{% endfor %}\n{% endcache %}\n```\n\n```python\nfrom django.core.cache import cache\nfrom django.core.cache.utils import make_template_fragment_key\n\ncache.delete(make_template_fragment_key(\"category_sidebar\", [category.pk, \"en\"]))\n```\n\nLow-level API tour:\n\n```python\n>>> from django.core.cache import cache\n>>> cache.set(\"greeting\", \"hi\", timeout=30)\n>>> cache.get(\"greeting\"), cache.get(\"missing\", \"default\")\n('hi', 'default')\n>>> cache.add(\"greeting\", \"other\")          # only if absent -> False\nFalse\n>>> cache.get_or_set(\"answer\", lambda: 6 * 7, timeout=60)\n42\n>>> cache.set(\"hits\", 0); cache.incr(\"hits\"); cache.incr(\"hits\", 10)\n11\n>>> cache.set_many({\"a\": 1, \"b\": 2}); cache.get_many([\"a\", \"b\", \"c\"])\n{'a': 1, 'b': 2}\n>>> cache.touch(\"a\", 120)                   # extend TTL\nTrue\n>>> cache.set(\"cfg\", \"x\", version=2); cache.get(\"cfg\"), cache.get(\"cfg\", version=2)\n(None, 'x')\n```\n\nIn async views use the `a`-prefixed methods: `await cache.aget(key)`, `await cache.aset(key, value)`.\n\n### Real-world example: product detail with cache-aside, on-commit invalidation, versioned lists and stampede protection\n\n```python\n# catalog/cache.py\nimport random\nfrom django.core.cache import cache\n\nPRODUCT_TTL = 60 * 30\nLIST_TTL = 60 * 5\n_MISS = object()                       # sentinel: distinguishes \"cached None\" from \"not cached\"\n\n\ndef product_key(pk: int) -> str:\n    return f\"product:{pk}\"\n\n\ndef catalog_version() -> int:\n    v = cache.get(\"catalog:version\")\n    if v is None:\n        cache.add(\"catalog:version\", 1, timeout=None)   # add() is atomic: only one writer wins\n        v = cache.get(\"catalog:version\", 1)\n    return v\n\n\ndef bump_catalog_version() -> None:\n    try:\n        cache.incr(\"catalog:version\")\n    except ValueError:                   # key missing\n        cache.add(\"catalog:version\", 1, timeout=None)\n\n\ndef jittered(ttl: int) -> int:\n    return int(ttl * random.uniform(0.9, 1.1))           # spread expiries: avoid synchronized misses\n```\n\n```python\n# catalog/services.py\nfrom django.core.cache import cache\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom .cache import PRODUCT_TTL, LIST_TTL, _MISS, product_key, catalog_version, bump_catalog_version, jittered\nfrom .models import Product\n\n\ndef get_product_card(pk: int) -> dict | None:\n    key = product_key(pk)\n    data = cache.get(key, _MISS)\n    if data is not _MISS:\n        return data\n    # Simple stampede guard: one process recomputes, others briefly serve nothing or retry\n    lock_key = f\"lock:{key}\"\n    if cache.add(lock_key, 1, timeout=10):\n        try:\n            p = (Product.objects.select_related(\"brand\").only(\"id\", \"name\", \"price\", \"brand__name\")\n                 .filter(pk=pk).first())\n            data = None if p is None else {\"id\": p.id, \"name\": p.name, \"price\": str(p.price), \"brand\": p.brand.name}\n            cache.set(key, data, jittered(PRODUCT_TTL))   # caches \"not found\" too (negative caching)\n            return data\n        finally:\n            cache.delete(lock_key)\n    # Lost the race: fall back to the DB rather than block\n    p = Product.objects.filter(pk=pk).values(\"id\", \"name\", \"price\").first()\n    return p\n\n\ndef featured_products() -> list[dict]:\n    key = f\"featured:v{catalog_version()}\"\n    return cache.get_or_set(\n        key,\n        lambda: list(Product.objects.filter(featured=True).order_by(\"-score\").values(\"id\", \"name\", \"price\")[:20]),\n        jittered(LIST_TTL),\n    )\n\n\n@transaction.atomic\ndef update_price(pk: int, new_price) -> None:\n    Product.objects.filter(pk=pk).update(price=new_price)\n    transaction.on_commit(lambda: cache.delete(product_key(pk)))\n    transaction.on_commit(bump_catalog_version)        # every list keyed on the version is now stale\n```\n\nGeneric invalidation from signals (remember `QuerySet.update()` and `bulk_update()` do **not** send `post_save`, so services must still invalidate explicitly):\n\n```python\n# catalog/signals.py  (connected in CatalogConfig.ready)\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.db.models.signals import post_delete, post_save\nfrom django.dispatch import receiver\nfrom django.core.cache import cache\nfrom .cache import product_key, bump_catalog_version\nfrom .models import Product\n\n\n@receiver([post_save, post_delete], sender=Product, dispatch_uid=\"product_cache_invalidate\")\ndef invalidate_product(sender, instance, **kwargs):\n    transaction.on_commit(lambda: cache.delete(product_key(instance.pk)))\n    transaction.on_commit(bump_catalog_version)\n```\n\nHTTP caching for a public API endpoint with ETag revalidation:\n\n```python\n# catalog/api.py\nimport hashlib\nfrom django.http import JsonResponse\nfrom django.views.decorators.cache import cache_control\nfrom django.views.decorators.http import condition\nfrom .models import Product\n\n\ndef product_etag(request, pk):\n    row = Product.objects.filter(pk=pk).values_list(\"updated_at\", flat=True).first()\n    return None if row is None else hashlib.md5(row.isoformat().encode()).hexdigest()\n\n\n@cache_control(public=True, max_age=60, s_maxage=300, stale_while_revalidate=30)\n@condition(etag_func=product_etag)\ndef product_json(request, pk):\n    p = Product.objects.values(\"id\", \"name\", \"price\").get(pk=pk)\n    p[\"price\"] = str(p[\"price\"])\n    return JsonResponse(p)\n```\n\n```text\n$ curl -i https://shop.example.com/api/products/42/\nHTTP/1.1 200 OK\nCache-Control: public, max-age=60, s-maxage=300, stale-while-revalidate=30\nETag: \"9b2c...\"\n$ curl -i -H 'If-None-Match: \"9b2c...\"' https://shop.example.com/api/products/42/\nHTTP/1.1 304 Not Modified\n```\n\nThe `etag_func` costs a single indexed one-column query, far cheaper than rendering; the CDN serves most requests for 300 s without reaching Django. Personal pages get the opposite: `@never_cache` or `@cache_control(private=True, no_store=True)`. The HTTP side is covered in depth in [REST API · HTTP Caching & ETags](../rest-api/topic.html?p=25-http-caching-etags).\n\n### Caching expensive aggregates in DRF\n\n```python\nfrom rest_framework.decorators import api_view\nfrom rest_framework.response import Response\nfrom django.core.cache import cache\nfrom django.db.models import Count, Sum\n\n\n@api_view([\"GET\"])\ndef dashboard_stats(request):\n    key = f\"stats:tenant:{request.user.tenant_id}\"     # tenant in key: never share across tenants\n    stats = cache.get_or_set(\n        key,\n        lambda: Order.objects.filter(tenant_id=request.user.tenant_id)\n                .aggregate(n=Count(\"id\"), revenue=Sum(\"total\")),\n        timeout=120,\n    )\n    return Response(stats)\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Choice | Pros | Cons |\n|---|---|---|\n| Redis (built-in backend) | Shared, fast, atomic incr, persistence options, also usable for sessions and throttling | Another stateful service; memory sizing and eviction to manage |\n| django-redis | Locks, `delete_pattern`, compression, TTL inspection | Third-party dependency |\n| Memcached | Simple, multi-threaded, predictable LRU | No persistence, 250-byte keys, about 1 MB values, fewer data structures |\n| LocMem | Zero setup | Per process: inconsistent across workers, invisible to other hosts |\n| DatabaseCache | No extra service | Adds load to the thing you are trying to protect |\n| Long TTLs | High hit rate | Staleness |\n| Delete-on-write | Fresh data | Every write path must remember; missed paths (`update()`) cause stale reads |\n| Versioned keys | O(1) mass invalidation | Old keys linger until TTL/eviction, using memory |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Cache **after** fixing N+1 and indexes — caching a bad query hides it until the cache is cold (deploys, evictions) and then it hurts more.\n- Low-level cache for data read far more often than written, expensive to compute, and tolerant of seconds-to-minutes staleness.\n- `cache_page` / CDN for anonymous, public, identical-for-everyone responses.\n- Fragment caching for shared pieces of personalized pages.\n- ETags for resources clients poll.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Not for data that must be strongly consistent (balances, inventory decrements) — read the DB, use `select_for_update` or atomic `F()` updates (see [Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency)).\n- Not `cache_page` or per-site caching on pages containing user-specific content without correct `Vary` / `private`.\n- Not as the only copy of data — caches evict.\n- Not `locmem` in any multi-process deployment for anything that must be consistent (sessions, rate limits, locks).\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. `LocMemCache` in production.** It is the default when `CACHES` is unset. With 8 Gunicorn workers you have 8 independent caches: invalidation in one worker leaves 7 stale, DRF throttles allow 8x the rate, and `cache.add`-based locks do not lock. Configure Redis or Memcached.\n\n**2. Caching personalized responses.** `@cache_page` on a view that renders `request.user.username` serves one user's page to others: the decorator stores the response before `SessionMiddleware` adds `Vary: Cookie`, so the cookie is not part of the key. Prefer fragment caching around the shared parts and keep the personal parts uncached; or use `@vary_on_cookie` explicitly.\n\n**3. Not distinguishing \"cached None\" from a miss.**\n\n```python\n# Before: a product that doesn't exist is re-queried on every request\ndata = cache.get(key)\nif data is None:\n    data = load()          # returns None for missing products\n    cache.set(key, data)\n# After: sentinel default\ndata = cache.get(key, _MISS)\nif data is _MISS:\n    data = load()\n    cache.set(key, data, 60)\n```\n\n**4. Invalidating inside the transaction.** See the race in §3. Use `transaction.on_commit`.\n\n**5. Caching QuerySets or model instances.** Pickling a QuerySet evaluates it and stores all rows; pickled model instances break or misbehave after a field is added and old entries are unpickled by new code. Cache plain dicts/lists (`values()`), and include a schema version in the key prefix.\n\n**6. Unbounded key cardinality.** `cache_page` on a search view caches every distinct `?q=` permanently-until-TTL; bots generate millions of keys and evict everything useful. Do not page-cache high-cardinality URLs; normalize parameters for low-level keys.\n\n**7. Forgetting `cache_page` sets browser headers.** It adds `Cache-Control: max-age=<timeout>`. Even after you clear Redis, browsers keep their copy. For server-side-only caching use the low-level API or `cache_page` combined with `@cache_control(max_age=0)` (inner decorator) — or accept it deliberately.\n\n**8. `cache.clear()` on a shared Redis.** It flushes the whole Redis DB, including other apps' and sessions' keys if they share it. Separate DBs/instances per purpose, and invalidate by version instead.\n\n**Best practices:** one key-builder module per domain (no ad-hoc f-strings scattered through views); include tenant/user in keys for scoped data; TTL on everything (no `timeout=None` except version counters); jitter TTLs; measure hit rate before and after; never cache what you have not first profiled.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Production story — the post-deploy stampede.** A homepage aggregate took 2 s to compute and was cached for 10 minutes. A deploy changed `KEY_PREFIX`, so every key missed at once; 400 concurrent requests computed it simultaneously and saturated the database connection pool, causing a site-wide outage. *Fixes:* a lock (`cache.add(lock_key)` or django-redis `cache.lock()`) so one request recomputes while others serve a stale copy; warm critical keys from a post-deploy job; never change prefixes during peak; jittered TTLs. For hot keys, refresh-ahead from a Celery beat task so users never see a miss. Background: [Redis Caching · Cache Stampede](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=15-cache-stampede) and [Hot Keys & Avalanche](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=17-hot-keys-avalanche).\n\n**Production story — stale prices after a bulk import.** An admin action used `Product.objects.filter(...).update(price=F(\"price\") * 1.1)`. No `post_save` fired, so per-product keys stayed stale for 30 minutes. *Fix:* bulk paths call an explicit invalidation (`cache.delete_many([...])` plus a version bump) in `on_commit`.\n\n**Production story — user data leak through the CDN.** An API returned `Cache-Control: public, max-age=60` from a shared decorator, including `/api/me/`. The CDN cached one user's profile and served it to others. *Fix:* default `private, no-store` for authenticated endpoints, and an allowlist for public ones; CDN configured to never cache responses to requests with `Authorization` or session cookies.\n\n**Redis memory pressure.** Symptoms: rising latency, `OOM command not allowed` errors, or (with an eviction policy) mysteriously low hit rates. Check `INFO memory` and `INFO stats` (`evicted_keys`, `keyspace_hits`/`keyspace_misses`). Set `maxmemory` and `maxmemory-policy allkeys-lru` (or `volatile-lru` if non-expiring keys must survive) on a cache-only instance. See [Redis Caching · Memory & Eviction](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=07-memory-and-eviction).\n\n**Debugging.**\n\n- django-debug-toolbar's **Cache panel** lists every cache call, hit/miss and time per request.\n- `redis-cli MONITOR` (briefly, never on a busy production node) or `redis-cli --bigkeys` / `--memkeys` to find heavy keys.\n- Log hit/miss counters per key family to your metrics system; a hit rate below about 80% for a supposedly hot family means the keying or TTL is wrong.\n- `curl -I` to confirm `Cache-Control`, `Vary`, `ETag`, `Age` and CDN hit headers.\n\n**Scaling.** One Redis primary handles a very large request rate for small values; scale out reads with replicas (the built-in backend sends reads to the replicas listed after the first `LOCATION`), shard with Redis Cluster for memory, and keep values small (compress large blobs with django-redis or store ids). Network round trips dominate: batch with `get_many`/`set_many`, and consider a small per-request in-memory memo for keys read many times in one request.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What cache backends does Django support, and which would you use in production?**\nA: Built-in: Redis (since 4.0), Memcached (`PyMemcacheCache`, `PyLibMCCache`), database, file-based, local memory and dummy. In production use Redis or Memcached because they are shared across all worker processes and hosts and support atomic increments. Redis adds persistence options and richer data types and can also back sessions, throttling and Celery. Local memory is per process, so it is only suitable for tests or single-process development.\n\n**Q: What are the different levels of caching in Django?**\nA: Per-site caching with `UpdateCacheMiddleware` (first) and `FetchFromCacheMiddleware` (last) caches every GET/HEAD response. Per-view caching with `@cache_page(timeout)` caches one view's full response. Template fragment caching with `{% cache timeout name vary_args %}` caches part of a page. The low-level API (`cache.get`, `set`, `get_or_set`, `delete`, `incr`, `get_many`) caches arbitrary values. On top of that, HTTP headers (`Cache-Control`, `ETag`) let browsers and CDNs cache without hitting Django.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `cache_page` and the low-level cache API?**\nA: `cache_page` stores an entire `HttpResponse` keyed by URL, method and `Vary` headers; the view does not run on a hit, which is maximal savings but coarse — it cannot be personalized cheaply, invalidates only by TTL in practice, and also sets browser `Cache-Control` headers. The low-level API caches specific values you choose — a query result, a computed aggregate, an external API response — with keys you control, so you can invalidate precisely on writes and still render per-user templates around cached data. Use `cache_page` for public pages and low-level caching for data.\n\n**Q: How does Django build the cache key for `cache_page`, and why does `Vary` matter?**\nA: It hashes the full absolute URL (including the query string) together with the method and the values of the request headers listed in the response's `Vary` header; the list of varying headers is itself cached under a URL-based key by `learn_cache_key`. If a response says `Vary: Cookie`, the session cookie is part of the key, so each user gets their own entry. If a personalized response does not vary on the right header, one user's page can be served to another. A classic trap: the `@cache_page` decorator caches inside the middleware stack, before `SessionMiddleware` adds `Vary: Cookie`, so decorated views that use `request.user` need an explicit `@vary_on_cookie`. `vary_on_headers` and `vary_on_cookie` add headers explicitly.\n\n**Q: How do you invalidate cached data when the underlying model changes?**\nA: Delete or overwrite the affected keys when the data changes, from the service layer or a `post_save`/`post_delete` receiver, and do it in `transaction.on_commit` so a concurrent reader cannot repopulate the key with pre-commit data. For collections, include a version number in the key and bump it on change, invalidating all of them at once. Remember that `QuerySet.update()`, `bulk_create` and `bulk_update` bypass signals, so those paths must invalidate explicitly. Always keep a TTL as a safety net.\n\n**Q: What is the default cache timeout and what do `None` and `0` mean?**\nA: The default `TIMEOUT` is 300 seconds. Passing `timeout=None` stores the value with no expiry; `timeout=0` expires it immediately, effectively not caching. The `TIMEOUT` in a `CACHES` entry sets the default for that alias; `cache_page` and `{% cache %}` take their own timeouts. `cache_page` also uses its timeout for the `max-age` it writes into the response headers.\n\n**Q: Why is LocMemCache a problem in production?**\nA: Each process has its own private in-memory dict, so with multiple Gunicorn workers or multiple hosts each one has a different cache. Invalidating a key in one worker leaves the others stale, cache-based locks do not exclude each other, and DRF throttling or rate limiting counts per process, allowing many times the configured rate. It also disappears on every restart. Use Redis or Memcached for anything shared.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `cache.add`, `cache.set` and `cache.get_or_set`?**\nA: `set` always writes. `add` writes only if the key does not exist and returns whether it did — on Redis and Memcached it is atomic, which makes it usable as a simple lock or \"first writer wins\" primitive. `get_or_set(key, default, timeout)` returns the cached value, or computes the default (a callable is called) and stores it with `add` semantics. `get_or_set` does not lock the computation, so many concurrent misses still compute concurrently.\n\n**Q: How does HTTP caching with ETags work in Django?**\nA: The server sends an `ETag` (a fingerprint of the representation) and the client sends it back as `If-None-Match`; if it still matches, the server returns `304 Not Modified` with no body. `ConditionalGetMiddleware` adds ETags to responses and returns 304s automatically, but the view still runs. The `@condition(etag_func=..., last_modified_func=...)` decorator, or `@etag` / `@last_modified`, compute the validator cheaply before the view, so a match skips the view entirely. Combine with `@cache_control(max_age=...)` to control how long clients may reuse without revalidating.\n\n**Q: How would you prevent a cache stampede on an expensive key? (Senior)**\nA: When a hot key expires, every concurrent request misses and recomputes, overloading the database. Mitigations: a recompute lock (`cache.add(lock_key, 1, timeout)` or django-redis `cache.lock`) so one request rebuilds while others serve a stale copy kept under a second key with a longer TTL; refresh-ahead from a Celery beat task so the key never expires under load; probabilistic early recomputation (recompute with rising probability as expiry nears); and jittered TTLs so many keys do not expire together. After deploys, warm critical keys before taking traffic and avoid changing key prefixes at peak.\n\n**Q: Design a caching strategy for a product catalog with frequent price updates and a high read rate. (Senior)**\nA: Separate data by volatility. Product descriptions and images change rarely: cache product cards with long, jittered TTLs and delete on commit when edited. Prices and stock change often and matter: either keep them out of the cached card and read them with one indexed query (or a tiny separate key with a short TTL), or delete on every price change via `on_commit`. Category and search listings use versioned keys (`catalog:v{n}`) bumped on any change, with short TTLs. Public pages go behind a CDN with `s-maxage` and `stale-while-revalidate`, and checkout always reads the database with row locks. Bulk import paths invalidate explicitly because `update()` does not send signals, and hit rates and staleness are measured per key family.\n\n**Q: What are the risks of caching pickled objects, and how do you mitigate them? (Senior)**\nA: Django's backends pickle values. Model instances and QuerySets pickled by one code version may fail to unpickle, or unpickle with missing attributes, after a deploy changes the class — producing errors right after deploys while old entries remain. Pickled QuerySets also store all evaluated rows and are large. Unpickling is code execution, so anyone who can write to your cache can run code in your app; the cache must be private and authenticated. Mitigate by caching plain JSON-like data (`values()`, dicts), versioning the key prefix with a schema version, and securing Redis (network isolation, ACLs, TLS).\n\n**Q: How do you cache a page that is mostly shared but has a personalized header? (Senior)**\nA: Do not cache the whole response per user. Cache the expensive shared parts as template fragments (`{% cache 600 \"product_body\" product.pk product.updated_at %}`) or as low-level data, and render the personalized header normally — it is usually cheap (the user is already loaded). Alternatively serve the page fully cached and anonymous from a CDN, and fetch the personalized bits with a small authenticated API call or edge-side includes. The key insight is to separate content by audience, not to accept `Vary: Cookie` and a near-zero hit rate.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Task | API |\n|---|---|\n| Configure Redis | `\"BACKEND\": \"django.core.cache.backends.redis.RedisCache\"` |\n| Default timeout | 300 s; `None` = forever; `0` = do not cache |\n| Per-view | `@cache_page(60 * 15, cache=\"pages\", key_prefix=\"v2\")` |\n| Per-site | `UpdateCacheMiddleware` first, `FetchFromCacheMiddleware` last |\n| Fragment | `{% load cache %}{% cache 600 name arg1 arg2 %}...{% endcache %}` |\n| Delete fragment | `cache.delete(make_template_fragment_key(name, [args]))` |\n| Low-level | `get`, `set`, `add`, `get_or_set`, `get_many`, `set_many`, `delete`, `delete_many`, `incr`, `decr`, `touch`, `clear` |\n| Async | `aget`, `aset`, `aget_or_set`, `adelete`, ... |\n| Versions | `cache.set(k, v, version=2)`, `cache.incr_version(k)`, `VERSION`, `KEY_PREFIX` |\n| HTTP | `@cache_control`, `@never_cache`, `@vary_on_headers`, `@vary_on_cookie`, `@condition`, `@etag`, `ConditionalGetMiddleware` |\n\n- Fix queries first; cache second.\n- `locmem` is per process — never for shared state.\n- `Vary: Cookie` makes page caches per user; the `@cache_page` decorator runs before `SessionMiddleware` adds it, so add `@vary_on_cookie` yourself.\n- Invalidate in `transaction.on_commit`.\n- `update()` / `bulk_*` skip signals — invalidate explicitly.\n- Use a sentinel to cache \"not found\".\n- Jitter TTLs and lock hot recomputes to avoid stampedes.\n- `cache_page` also sets browser `max-age`.\n- Cache plain data, not model instances or QuerySets.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" redis django-debug-toolbar`, run Redis with `docker run -p 6379:6379 redis:7`.\n\n1. **Backends side by side.** Configure `locmem`, then run `gunicorn -w 4` with a view that increments `cache.incr(\"hits\")` and returns the value. Hit it 20 times and observe divergent counters; switch to Redis and repeat.\n2. **cache_page and Vary.** Cache a view that shows `request.user`. Log in as two users and see whether pages leak; inspect `Vary`, then fix with fragment caching. Use `redis-cli KEYS \"*cache_page*\"` to see one key per session.\n3. **Invalidation race.** Write a test with `TransactionTestCase` and two threads reproducing stale data when deleting inside `atomic()`; fix with `on_commit`.\n4. **Versioned lists.** Implement `featured_products()` with a version key; bump it from an admin save and verify the new list appears while old keys remain until TTL.\n5. **ETag.** Add `@condition(etag_func=...)` to a JSON endpoint and use `curl -H 'If-None-Match: ...'` to receive a 304. Check in debug-toolbar that the view body did not run.\n6. **Stampede.** Make a view whose computation sleeps 2 s with a 5 s TTL; hit it with `hey -c 50` (or `ab`) and count computations; add the lock pattern and compare.\n\n**Mini project — cached storefront.** A catalog with categories and products: CDN-friendly public category pages with `cache_control(public, s_maxage)`, fragment-cached product bodies keyed on `updated_at`, low-level cached product cards with on-commit invalidation, versioned featured lists, a stampede lock for the homepage aggregate, a Celery beat refresh-ahead task, and a hit/miss metrics counter displayed on a staff page.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) · [Ch 14 · Transactions & Concurrency](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) · [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) · [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals) · [Ch 27 · Sessions, Cookies & Messages](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies) · [Ch 23 · DRF Authentication, Permissions & Throttling](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling) · [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks) · [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [Redis Caching · Redis as a Cache](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=03-redis-as-a-cache) · [Cache-Aside](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=08-cache-aside) · [Cache Invalidation](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=12-cache-invalidation) · [Cache Stampede](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=15-cache-stampede) · [Memory & Eviction](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=07-memory-and-eviction) · [REST API · HTTP Caching & ETags](../rest-api/topic.html?p=25-http-caching-etags)\n\n- **Django's cache framework** — Django docs · *Beginner* · backends, all four levels, keys, versions and the low-level API. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/cache/>\n- **Conditional View Processing** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `@condition`, ETags and Last-Modified done right. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/conditional-view-processing/>\n- **django-redis** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · the feature-rich Redis backend with locks, patterns and compression. <https://github.com/jazzband/django-redis>\n- **MDN: HTTP caching** — MDN · *Beginner* · Cache-Control, Vary, validators and shared caches. <https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/HTTP/Caching>\n- **Redis: key eviction** — Redis docs · *Intermediate* · maxmemory policies for a cache-only Redis. <https://redis.io/docs/latest/develop/reference/eviction/>\n- **web.dev: Prevent unnecessary network requests with the HTTP Cache** — web.dev · *Beginner* · practical Cache-Control and revalidation recipes for the browser layer. <https://web.dev/articles/http-cache>\n- **django-debug-toolbar** — GitHub · *Beginner* · the cache panel shows every call and hit/miss per request. <https://github.com/django-commons/django-debug-toolbar>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 29.*\n", "30-async-django-asgi": "# 30 · Async Django & ASGI\n\n> **In one line:** ASGI lets Django serve requests from an event loop, so an `async def` view can wait on many network calls, WebSockets or streams at once without holding a thread — but the ORM still runs in threads behind `sync_to_async`, transactions are sync-only, and one blocking call stalls the whole loop, so async pays off for I/O fan-out and long-lived connections, not for ordinary CRUD.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle) (WSGI/ASGI handlers and `BaseHandler`), [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) (the middleware chain that must now be sync- or async-capable), [Ch 04 · Views, Requests & Responses](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses), [Ch 14 · Transactions & Concurrency](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency).\n\nFor most of its life Django spoke only **WSGI**: the server calls `application(environ, start_response)`, the call blocks until the response is ready, and concurrency comes from running many worker processes and threads. That model is simple and robust, and it is still the right model for the majority of Django apps. It breaks down in three situations:\n\n1. **I/O fan-out.** A view calls four external APIs, each taking 300 ms. Sequentially that is 1.2 s per request, and the worker thread sits idle for all of it. At 200 concurrent requests you need 200 threads doing nothing but waiting.\n2. **Long-lived connections.** WebSockets, Server-Sent Events and long polling hold a connection open for minutes. In WSGI each one pins a thread, so 10,000 connected clients means 10,000 threads.\n3. **Slow clients and streaming.** Streaming a large response to a slow mobile client holds a worker the whole time.\n\nThe naive fix — \"add more threads\" — fails because threads cost memory (stack per thread), context-switching and, crucially, database connections (one per thread). **ASGI** (Asynchronous Server Gateway Interface) replaces the blocking callable with `async def application(scope, receive, send)`. One process runs an **event loop** that interleaves thousands of in-flight requests: whenever one awaits I/O, the loop runs another.\n\nDjango has been adding async support since 3.0: the `ASGIHandler` (3.0), async views and middleware (3.1), an async ORM *interface* (4.1), async model and related-manager methods (4.2), async auth helpers and signals (5.0), and async session methods (5.1). The important word is *interface*: the ORM and database drivers underneath are still synchronous, so `await Book.objects.aget(pk=1)` runs the query in a thread via `sync_to_async`. Async Django is therefore a hybrid, and using it well means knowing exactly where the sync/async boundaries are, what each crossing costs, and which workloads actually benefit.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **WSGI** — synchronous interface: one request occupies one thread until it finishes. *Why it matters:* the baseline; `gunicorn` sync workers are WSGI.\n- **ASGI** — asynchronous interface with `scope`, `receive`, `send`; also carries WebSockets and lifespan events. *Why:* required for async views to deliver concurrency, and for Channels.\n- **Event loop** — the `asyncio` scheduler that runs coroutines and switches at every `await`. *Why:* one blocking call (`time.sleep`, `requests.get`, sync ORM) freezes every request on that loop.\n- **Coroutine / `async def` view** — a view Django detects with `asyncio.iscoroutinefunction`. *Why:* under ASGI it runs on the loop directly; under WSGI Django wraps it in `async_to_sync` and gains nothing.\n- **`sync_to_async`** (asgiref) — runs a sync function in a thread and awaits the result. *Why:* the bridge from async code to the ORM, third-party sync libraries and transactions.\n- **`async_to_sync`** — runs a coroutine from sync code, using the outer loop if one exists. *Why:* calling async APIs (e.g. a Channels layer) from sync views and Celery tasks.\n- **`thread_sensitive=True`** (default) — sync functions run in one dedicated thread per request context (and on the main thread when called under an outer `async_to_sync`). *Why:* the ORM's connections are thread-local, so all DB work for a request must stay on the same thread.\n- **`SynchronousOnlyOperation`** — raised when sync-only Django code (the ORM, lazy related access) is called directly from an async context. *Why:* the guard that stops you from blocking the loop with a DB query.\n- **Async ORM API** — `aget`, `acreate`, `acount`, `aexists`, `afirst`, `aupdate`, `adelete`, `ain_bulk`, `aiterator`, `aaggregate`, `async for`, `Model.asave()`, `arefresh_from_db()`, related `aadd`/`aset`/`aclear`. *Why:* readable async code; same threaded execution underneath.\n- **Async middleware** — middleware declaring `async_capable`; Django adapts mismatched middleware with sync/async wrappers per request. *Why:* one sync-only middleware in an async stack forces thread hops on every request.\n- **ASGI servers** — uvicorn, Daphne, Hypercorn; often `gunicorn -k uvicorn.workers.UvicornWorker` for process management. *Why:* `manage.py runserver` is WSGI unless Daphne is in `INSTALLED_APPS`.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### Concurrency models side by side\n\nUnder WSGI, a Gunicorn sync worker handles exactly one request at a time; threaded workers (`gthread`) handle as many as they have threads. Waiting on I/O is cheap for the CPU but expensive for capacity: the thread is reserved. Under ASGI, a single worker process runs one event loop; a request that awaits a network response yields, and the loop serves other requests in the meantime. Capacity for waiting is limited by memory and file descriptors, not threads.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 380\" width=\"100%\" height=\"380\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Three requests, each waiting 300 ms on an external API</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"410\" height=\"300\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">WSGI: one thread per in-flight request</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"92\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">thread 1</text>\n  <rect x=\"90\" y=\"80\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <rect x=\"110\" y=\"80\" width=\"240\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"93\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"9\">blocked: waiting for API</text>\n  <rect x=\"350\" y=\"80\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"132\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">thread 2</text>\n  <rect x=\"90\" y=\"120\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <rect x=\"110\" y=\"120\" width=\"240\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"133\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"9\">blocked</text>\n  <rect x=\"350\" y=\"120\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"172\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">thread 3</text>\n  <rect x=\"90\" y=\"160\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <rect x=\"110\" y=\"160\" width=\"240\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"9\">blocked</text>\n  <rect x=\"350\" y=\"160\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"212\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">thread 4</text>\n  <rect x=\"90\" y=\"200\" width=\"280\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"213\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#78350f\" font-size=\"9\">4th request queues if only 3 threads</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"252\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Cost: N waiting requests = N threads,</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"268\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">N stacks, up to N DB connections.</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"292\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Simple, debuggable, any library works.</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"316\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">Fine when requests are short and CPU/DB bound.</text>\n  <rect x=\"450\" y=\"40\" width=\"410\" height=\"300\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f0fdf4\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ASGI: one event loop interleaves requests</text>\n  <text x=\"464\" y=\"92\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">loop</text>\n  <rect x=\"500\" y=\"80\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <rect x=\"520\" y=\"80\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <rect x=\"540\" y=\"80\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <rect x=\"780\" y=\"80\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <rect x=\"800\" y=\"80\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <rect x=\"820\" y=\"80\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"670\" y=\"93\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">idle: all three awaiting</text>\n  <text x=\"464\" y=\"132\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">req A</text>\n  <rect x=\"520\" y=\"120\" width=\"260\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"650\" y=\"133\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e3a8a\" font-size=\"9\">await httpx (suspended)</text>\n  <text x=\"464\" y=\"172\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">req B</text>\n  <rect x=\"540\" y=\"160\" width=\"260\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"670\" y=\"173\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#4c1d95\" font-size=\"9\">await (suspended)</text>\n  <text x=\"464\" y=\"212\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">req C</text>\n  <rect x=\"560\" y=\"200\" width=\"260\" height=\"18\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"690\" y=\"213\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#14532d\" font-size=\"9\">await (suspended)</text>\n  <text x=\"464\" y=\"252\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Cost: a coroutine object per request;</text>\n  <text x=\"464\" y=\"268\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">thousands of waits on one thread.</text>\n  <text x=\"464\" y=\"292\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">Any blocking call (sync ORM, requests, sleep)</text>\n  <text x=\"464\" y=\"308\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">freezes A, B and C together.</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"364\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Blue/purple/green blocks = CPU work for requests A, B, C. Waiting is free on the loop, expensive on threads.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### How Django runs a request under ASGI\n\n`django.core.handlers.asgi.ASGIHandler.__call__(scope, receive, send)`:\n\n1. Rejects non-`http` scopes (WebSockets need Channels), reads the body from `receive` into a spooled temporary file, and builds an `ASGIRequest`.\n2. Sends `request_started` and calls `await self.get_response_async(request)`. The middleware chain was built once in `load_middleware(is_async=True)`; for each middleware, `adapt_method_mode` wraps it with `sync_to_async` or `async_to_sync` if its capabilities do not match the mode of the layer around it.\n3. Resolves the URL and calls the view. An `async def` view is awaited directly on the loop. A **sync view** is wrapped in `sync_to_async(thread_sensitive=True)` and runs in a thread.\n4. Each request is wrapped in an asgiref `ThreadSensitiveContext`, so all thread-sensitive sync code *for that request* (sync middleware, sync views, ORM calls from `aget` and friends) runs on one per-request thread — which keeps the thread-local database connection consistent within the request.\n5. Sends the response through `send` (streaming bodies chunk by chunk; async iterators are supported by `StreamingHttpResponse` since 4.2).\n\nUnder **WSGI**, `WSGIHandler` calls `get_response` synchronously; an `async def` view is executed via `async_to_sync`, which spins up an event loop for that call. It works, which is convenient for gradual migration, but each request still holds its thread for the whole duration — no concurrency is gained, and the extra loop costs a little.\n\n### The async ORM: an async interface over sync execution\n\n`QuerySet.aget()` is essentially:\n\n```python\nasync def aget(self, *args, **kwargs):\n    return await sync_to_async(self.get)(*args, **kwargs)\n```\n\nThe query runs in the request's sync thread through the normal compiler and psycopg connection. You get non-blocking *semantics* — the loop keeps serving other requests while your query runs — but each query costs a thread hop, and the number of concurrent queries is bounded by the threads and connections available. What does **not** work from async code:\n\n- Lazy evaluation that would hit the DB implicitly: iterating a queryset with plain `for`, `len(qs)`, accessing an unloaded FK (`book.author`), or touching `request.user` attributes that lazy-load. These raise `SynchronousOnlyOperation`. Use `async for`, `acount()`, `select_related`, or `await request.auser()` (5.0+).\n- **Transactions.** `transaction.atomic()` is not supported in async code. Put the transactional unit in a sync function and call it with `sync_to_async`.\n- Anything in third-party sync libraries that queries the DB (most DRF internals, many packages).\n\nAlthough psycopg 3 itself offers an async API, Django 5.2's ORM does not use it — truly async database I/O remains future work, so plan around the threaded model.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\nThe diagram shows where each piece of code runs in a typical ASGI deployment and where thread hops happen.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 440\" width=\"100%\" height=\"440\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj30a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj30b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#d97706\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Inside one uvicorn worker: event loop thread vs per-request sync thread</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"150\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">gunicorn master</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">-k UvicornWorker</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">-w (cores)</text>\n  <path d=\"M170,80 L196,80\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj30a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"198\" y=\"40\" width=\"420\" height=\"250\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"408\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Event loop thread</text>\n  <rect x=\"212\" y=\"72\" width=\"392\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"93\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">ASGIHandler: build ASGIRequest, ThreadSensitiveContext per request</text>\n  <rect x=\"212\" y=\"114\" width=\"392\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"135\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">async-capable middleware (Security, Common, Csrf ... ) run on the loop</text>\n  <rect x=\"212\" y=\"156\" width=\"392\" height=\"44\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"174\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">async def view</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">await asyncio.gather(httpx calls)   runs here, non-blocking</text>\n  <rect x=\"212\" y=\"208\" width=\"392\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"229\" fill=\"#7f1d1d\" font-size=\"10\">requests.get() / time.sleep() / sync ORM here = loop frozen</text>\n  <text x=\"222\" y=\"270\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">sync-only middleware forces a hop each way on every request</text>\n  <rect x=\"660\" y=\"40\" width=\"200\" height=\"250\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"760\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Per-request sync thread</text>\n  <text x=\"672\" y=\"86\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">thread_sensitive=True code:</text>\n  <text x=\"672\" y=\"104\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">- await qs.aget() bodies</text>\n  <text x=\"672\" y=\"120\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">- sync views under ASGI</text>\n  <text x=\"672\" y=\"136\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">- sync middleware</text>\n  <text x=\"672\" y=\"152\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">- sync_to_async(atomic fn)</text>\n  <text x=\"672\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">owns the thread-local</text>\n  <text x=\"672\" y=\"196\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">DB connection</text>\n  <path d=\"M604,178 L658,178\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj30b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"630\" y=\"170\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"9\">hop</text>\n  <path d=\"M760,290 L760,326\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj30a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"660\" y=\"328\" width=\"200\" height=\"54\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"760\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">PostgreSQL</text>\n  <text x=\"760\" y=\"368\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">via pool or PgBouncer</text>\n  <path d=\"M408,290 L408,326\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj30a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"198\" y=\"328\" width=\"420\" height=\"54\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"408\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">External APIs, Redis (async clients), WebSockets</text>\n  <text x=\"408\" y=\"368\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">true non-blocking I/O on the loop</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"416\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Async gives concurrency for awaiting network I/O; DB work still costs a thread hop and a connection.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Middleware capability and adaptation\n\nEvery middleware has `sync_capable` (default `True`) and `async_capable` (default `False`) attributes. When Django builds the chain in async mode, it checks each layer: an async-capable middleware is called directly; a sync-only one is wrapped so that the request crosses into a thread and back. Docs describe the penalty as on the order of a millisecond per switch — negligible once, but several sync-only middlewares on a hot endpoint add up and also serialize on the per-request thread. All of Django's built-in middleware is async-capable; audit third-party ones.\n\n```python\nfrom asgiref.sync import iscoroutinefunction, markcoroutinefunction\nfrom django.utils.decorators import sync_and_async_middleware\n\n\n@sync_and_async_middleware\ndef timing_middleware(get_response):\n    import time\n    if iscoroutinefunction(get_response):\n        async def middleware(request):\n            t = time.perf_counter()\n            response = await get_response(request)\n            response[\"Server-Timing\"] = f\"app;dur={(time.perf_counter() - t) * 1000:.1f}\"\n            return response\n    else:\n        def middleware(request):\n            t = time.perf_counter()\n            response = get_response(request)\n            response[\"Server-Timing\"] = f\"app;dur={(time.perf_counter() - t) * 1000:.1f}\"\n            return response\n    return middleware\n```\n\nClass-based form: set `async_capable = True`, `sync_capable = False` (or both), and in `__init__` call `markcoroutinefunction(self)` when `get_response` is a coroutine function, implementing `async def __call__`.\n\n### Choosing a server\n\n| Server | Protocols | Notes |\n|---|---|---|\n| uvicorn | HTTP/1.1, WebSockets | Fast (uvloop, httptools); run standalone or as a Gunicorn worker class |\n| Daphne | HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, WebSockets | Reference server from the Channels project; adding `daphne` to `INSTALLED_APPS` makes `runserver` ASGI |\n| Hypercorn | HTTP/1.1, HTTP/2, HTTP/3 (experimental) | Trio or asyncio |\n| Gunicorn + `uvicorn.workers.UvicornWorker` (or the `uvicorn-worker` package) | as uvicorn | Gunicorn's process supervision, graceful reloads and timeouts |\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: an async view that fans out\n\n```bash\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"uvicorn[standard]\" httpx gunicorn\n```\n\n```python\n# config/asgi.py  (generated by startproject)\nimport os\nfrom django.core.asgi import get_asgi_application\n\nos.environ.setdefault(\"DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE\", \"config.settings\")\napplication = get_asgi_application()\n```\n\n```python\n# dashboard/views.py\nimport asyncio\nimport httpx\nfrom django.http import JsonResponse\n\nSERVICES = {\n    \"weather\": \"https://api.example.com/weather?city=berlin\",\n    \"fx\": \"https://api.example.com/fx?base=EUR\",\n    \"news\": \"https://api.example.com/news?top=5\",\n}\n\n\nasync def dashboard(request):\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=2.0) as client:\n        results = await asyncio.gather(\n            *(client.get(url) for url in SERVICES.values()),\n            return_exceptions=True,                     # one failing service must not fail the page\n        )\n    payload = {}\n    for name, res in zip(SERVICES, results):\n        payload[name] = res.json() if isinstance(res, httpx.Response) and res.is_success else None\n    return JsonResponse(payload)\n```\n\n```bash\nuvicorn config.asgi:application --workers 4 --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000\n# or, with Gunicorn's supervision:\ngunicorn config.asgi:application -k uvicorn.workers.UvicornWorker -w 4 --timeout 30\n```\n\nThree 300 ms calls now take about 300 ms total, not 900 ms, and the worker serves other requests while they are in flight.\n\n### Async ORM usage\n\n```python\n# library/views.py\nfrom django.http import Http404, JsonResponse\nfrom .models import Book\n\n\nasync def book_detail(request, pk):\n    try:\n        book = await Book.objects.select_related(\"author\").aget(pk=pk)   # FK loaded in the same query\n    except Book.DoesNotExist:\n        raise Http404\n    return JsonResponse({\"title\": book.title, \"author\": book.author.name})\n\n\nasync def recent_books(request):\n    qs = Book.objects.filter(published=True).order_by(\"-created_at\")[:20]\n    items = [{\"id\": b.id, \"title\": b.title} async for b in qs]            # async for, not for\n    total = await Book.objects.filter(published=True).acount()\n    return JsonResponse({\"total\": total, \"items\": items})\n\n\nasync def rename(request, pk):\n    book = await Book.objects.aget(pk=pk)\n    book.title = request.POST[\"title\"]\n    await book.asave(update_fields=[\"title\"])\n    user = await request.auser()                                          # 5.0+: async user access\n    return JsonResponse({\"ok\": True, \"by\": user.pk})\n```\n\nThe mistake every team makes first:\n\n```text\n>>> async def bad(request, pk):\n...     book = await Book.objects.aget(pk=pk)\n...     return JsonResponse({\"author\": book.author.name})     # lazy FK load from async context\ndjango.core.exceptions.SynchronousOnlyOperation: You cannot call this from an async context - use a thread or sync_to_async.\n```\n\n### Transactions and sync-only code from async views\n\n```python\nfrom asgiref.sync import sync_to_async\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.db.models import F\n\n\ndef _place_order_sync(user_id, product_id, qty):\n    with transaction.atomic():\n        product = Product.objects.select_for_update().get(pk=product_id)\n        if product.stock < qty:\n            raise ValueError(\"out of stock\")\n        Product.objects.filter(pk=product_id).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - qty)\n        order = Order.objects.create(user_id=user_id, product_id=product_id, qty=qty)\n        transaction.on_commit(lambda: send_confirmation.delay(order.pk))\n        return order.pk\n\n\nplace_order = sync_to_async(_place_order_sync)       # thread_sensitive=True by default\n\n\nasync def checkout(request):\n    user = await request.auser()\n    if not user.is_authenticated:\n        return JsonResponse({\"detail\": \"login required\"}, status=401)\n    order_id = await place_order(user.pk, int(request.POST[\"product\"]), int(request.POST[\"qty\"]))\n    return JsonResponse({\"order\": order_id}, status=201)\n```\n\nNever use `thread_sensitive=False` for ORM code: it runs in a shared thread pool, so the connection, transaction state and `select_for_update` lock could end up on a different thread than the rest of the request.\n\n### Real-world example: Server-Sent Events with an async streaming response\n\nLong-lived streams are where ASGI shines: each open stream is a suspended coroutine, not a thread.\n\n```python\n# notifications/views.py\nimport asyncio\nimport json\nimport redis.asyncio as aioredis\nfrom django.http import HttpResponse, StreamingHttpResponse\n\nr = aioredis.from_url(\"redis://redis:6379/4\")\n\n\nasync def event_stream(user_id):\n    pubsub = r.pubsub()\n    await pubsub.subscribe(f\"notify:{user_id}\")\n    try:\n        yield \"retry: 5000\\n\\n\"\n        while True:\n            msg = await pubsub.get_message(ignore_subscribe_messages=True, timeout=15)\n            if msg is None:\n                yield \": keep-alive\\n\\n\"                 # comment line keeps proxies from timing out\n                continue\n            yield f\"data: {json.dumps(json.loads(msg['data']))}\\n\\n\"\n    except asyncio.CancelledError:                       # client disconnected\n        raise\n    finally:\n        await pubsub.unsubscribe(f\"notify:{user_id}\")\n        await pubsub.aclose()\n\n\nasync def notifications(request):\n    user = await request.auser()\n    if not user.is_authenticated:\n        return HttpResponse(status=401)\n    response = StreamingHttpResponse(event_stream(user.pk), content_type=\"text/event-stream\")\n    response[\"Cache-Control\"] = \"no-cache\"\n    response[\"X-Accel-Buffering\"] = \"no\"                 # tell Nginx not to buffer\n    return response\n```\n\nAny sync code (a Celery task, a sync view) can publish to the user with a plain sync Redis client: `redis.Redis(...).publish(f\"notify:{uid}\", json.dumps(payload))`. For bidirectional real-time, see [Ch 32 · Django Channels & Real-Time](topic.html?p=32-channels-websockets).\n\n### Calling async code from sync code\n\n```python\nfrom asgiref.sync import async_to_sync\nfrom channels.layers import get_channel_layer\n\n\ndef order_shipped(order):                          # sync: a Celery task or sync view\n    async_to_sync(get_channel_layer().group_send)(\n        f\"user_{order.user_id}\", {\"type\": \"order.shipped\", \"order_id\": order.pk}\n    )\n```\n\n### Testing async views\n\n```python\nfrom django.test import TestCase\n\n\nclass DashboardTests(TestCase):\n    async def test_recent_books(self):\n        await Book.objects.acreate(title=\"Dune\", published=True, author=await Author.objects.acreate(name=\"FH\"))\n        response = await self.async_client.get(\"/books/recent/\")\n        self.assertEqual(response.status_code, 200)\n        self.assertEqual(response.json()[\"total\"], 1)\n```\n\n`TestCase` methods can be `async def`; `self.async_client` is an `AsyncClient`. Use `respx` or `pytest-httpx` to mock httpx calls.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Dimension | WSGI (sync views, Gunicorn sync/gthread) | ASGI + async views |\n|---|---|---|\n| Concurrency for waiting | Threads/processes; bounded and memory-heavy | Thousands of coroutines per process |\n| CPU-bound work | Same | Same (one loop per process; GIL) — no gain |\n| DB-bound CRUD | Direct calls, no hops | Thread hop per query; no gain, slight overhead |\n| External API fan-out | Sequential or manual thread pools | `asyncio.gather` — big latency and capacity win |\n| WebSockets / SSE / long polling | Impractical | Natural fit |\n| Ecosystem | Everything works (DRF, all packages) | Sync-only libraries need wrappers; DRF views are sync |\n| Debugging | Simple stack traces | Blocking calls hide; need loop-blocking detection |\n| Transactions | Native | Must wrap in `sync_to_async` |\n| Failure blast radius | One stuck request = one thread | One blocking call = every request on that loop |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Views that call several slow external services and can do so concurrently.\n- Streaming responses, SSE, long polling and WebSockets (with Channels).\n- Gateways/BFF endpoints that mostly proxy and aggregate other services.\n- Workloads with many slow clients where thread count is the capacity limit.\n- Mixed apps: run under ASGI, keep most views sync, and make only the I/O-heavy ones async.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Typical CRUD and admin traffic dominated by database queries — async adds hops and complexity with no throughput gain; scale with more WSGI workers and a connection pool.\n- CPU-heavy work (image processing, PDF generation, ML inference) — offload to Celery ([Ch 31](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks)).\n- Codebases heavily dependent on sync-only libraries in the request path.\n- Background jobs that must survive restarts — an `asyncio.create_task` in a view dies with the worker; use a task queue.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Blocking calls inside `async def`.** `requests.get()`, `time.sleep()`, `boto3`, sync Redis clients and file I/O block the loop. Symptom: latency spikes for *unrelated* requests. Use async clients (`httpx.AsyncClient`, `redis.asyncio`, `aiobotocore`) or wrap with `sync_to_async`.\n\n```python\n# Before: freezes every request on this worker for up to 2 s\nasync def view(request):\n    data = requests.get(URL, timeout=2).json()\n# After\nasync def view(request):\n    async with httpx.AsyncClient(timeout=2) as c:\n        data = (await c.get(URL)).json()\n```\n\n**2. Async views under WSGI and expecting a speed-up.** Under Gunicorn sync workers an async view runs in a per-request event loop; it still holds the worker. Deploy with an ASGI server.\n\n**3. Making every view async.** Converting CRUD views to `aget`/`asave` adds a thread hop per query and makes code harder to read for zero gain. Convert only where concurrency helps.\n\n**4. Lazy relations and `request.user` in async code.** Accessing `book.author` or `request.user.email` without preloading raises `SynchronousOnlyOperation`. Use `select_related`/`prefetch_related` in the async query, `await request.auser()`, and serialize everything inside a sync function if the template or serializer touches many relations.\n\n**5. Silencing the guard with `DJANGO_ALLOW_ASYNC_UNSAFE`.** It exists for Jupyter notebooks. In a server it lets sync DB calls run on the loop thread, blocking it and risking connection misuse.\n\n**6. Persistent DB connections under ASGI.** `CONN_MAX_AGE > 0` assumes long-lived worker threads; under ASGI the per-request threads come and go, so persistent connections leak or pile up. Django's docs recommend disabling persistent connections in async deployments and using connection pooling instead — Django 5.1's native psycopg 3 pool (`OPTIONS: {\"pool\": True}`) or PgBouncer.\n\n**7. Fire-and-forget tasks.** `asyncio.create_task(send_email())` in a view is not durable, has no retries, and may be garbage-collected if no reference is kept. Use Celery with `transaction.on_commit`.\n\n**8. Creating an HTTP client per call in hot paths.** `httpx.AsyncClient` holds a connection pool; creating one per request loses keep-alive. Create a module-level client (closed on shutdown) or one per request that does multiple calls, as in the example.\n\n**Best practices:** keep business logic in sync service functions and call them via `sync_to_async` from the few async views; set timeouts on every await (`asyncio.timeout()` / client timeouts); use `return_exceptions=True` or `TaskGroup` with error handling for fan-out; enable asyncio debug mode in development (`PYTHONASYNCIODEBUG=1`) to log slow callbacks that block the loop.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Production story — p99 latency explodes after \"going async\".** A team moved a DRF API to uvicorn and rewrote a few views as async. *Symptom:* p99 rose from 200 ms to 3 s although CPU was low. *Root cause:* one async view used the sync `stripe` SDK, blocking the loop for 400–800 ms per call; every concurrent request on that worker waited. *Debug:* `PYTHONASYNCIODEBUG=1` with `loop.slow_callback_duration = 0.1` logged \"Executing <Task ...> took 0.6 seconds\"; py-spy showed the loop thread inside `ssl.read`. *Fix:* wrap the SDK call in `sync_to_async` (or use an async client), and add a CI lint rule flagging known blocking calls inside `async def`.\n\n**Production story — database connection exhaustion.** Under ASGI with `CONN_MAX_AGE = 600`, connections grew until PostgreSQL hit `max_connections`. *Root cause:* per-request sync threads each opened a connection that was not reused or closed promptly. *Fix:* `CONN_MAX_AGE = 0` plus Django 5.1 pooling (`\"OPTIONS\": {\"pool\": {\"min_size\": 2, \"max_size\": 10}}`) or PgBouncer in transaction mode (with `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS = True`). See [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases) and [Database Design · Application Architecture](../database-design/topic.html?p=20-database-application-architecture).\n\n**Production story — SSE streams cut at 60 s.** *Symptom:* clients reconnect every minute. *Root cause:* Nginx `proxy_read_timeout` default and response buffering. *Fix:* `X-Accel-Buffering: no`, keep-alive comments every 15 s, `proxy_read_timeout 1h` on the stream location, and Gunicorn `--timeout` raised or a dedicated uvicorn deployment for streaming routes.\n\n**Capacity planning.** An ASGI worker can hold thousands of idle streams, but DB-touching requests are still limited by the sync thread pool and the connection pool. Size: processes ≈ CPU cores; DB pool per process sized so `processes x pool_max` stays below the database's budget; external-call concurrency bounded with `asyncio.Semaphore` so a slow upstream does not create unbounded in-flight requests. Split traffic: a WSGI deployment for CRUD/admin and an ASGI deployment for streaming and fan-out endpoints is a common and pragmatic architecture.\n\n**Debugging toolkit.**\n\n- `PYTHONASYNCIODEBUG=1` and `loop.slow_callback_duration` for blocking detection.\n- `py-spy dump --pid <worker>` to see what the loop thread is doing right now.\n- Structured logs with request ids propagated via `contextvars` (thread-locals do not follow coroutines; asgiref preserves contextvars across `sync_to_async`).\n- APM tools (Sentry, OpenTelemetry) have async-aware instrumentation for Django; verify spans cross the sync/async boundary.\n- Graceful shutdown: long streams must handle `CancelledError`; set server graceful timeouts so deploys do not hang.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between WSGI and ASGI?**\nA: WSGI is a synchronous interface: the server calls `application(environ, start_response)` and the call blocks until the response is complete, so each in-flight request holds a thread or process. ASGI is asynchronous: `async def application(scope, receive, send)` lets a server run many requests concurrently on an event loop and also supports long-lived protocols like WebSockets and a lifespan protocol. Django provides `WSGIHandler` and `ASGIHandler`, both built on `BaseHandler`. ASGI is required to get concurrency from async views and for Channels.\n\n**Q: Do async views make Django faster?**\nA: Not by themselves. Async improves *concurrency* when a request spends most of its time waiting on I/O that can be awaited — several external API calls, streaming, long polling. It does not make CPU work or database queries faster; the ORM still runs queries in a thread via `sync_to_async`, adding a small overhead per query. Under WSGI, async views run in a per-request event loop and give no benefit at all. For ordinary DB-bound CRUD, sync views under WSGI with enough workers perform as well or better.\n\n**Q: What do `sync_to_async` and `async_to_sync` do?**\nA: `sync_to_async(fn)` returns an awaitable that runs a synchronous function in a thread, so async code can call the ORM or a sync library without blocking the event loop. `async_to_sync(coro_fn)` lets synchronous code call a coroutine function, running it on the outer event loop if there is one or a new loop otherwise — for example calling a Channels layer's `group_send` from a Celery task. Both come from asgiref and propagate contextvars across the boundary.\n\n**Q: What does `thread_sensitive=True` mean, and why is it the default?**\nA: Thread-sensitive sync functions run in a single dedicated thread rather than an arbitrary pool thread: under Django's ASGI handler, one thread per request (via `ThreadSensitiveContext`), and on the main thread when called beneath an outer `async_to_sync`. It is the default because Django's database connections and transaction state are thread-local; all ORM work for a request must happen on the same thread to reuse the connection and keep transactions coherent. `thread_sensitive=False` is fine only for thread-safe code with no Django DB access.\n\n**Q: What is `SynchronousOnlyOperation` and how do you fix it?**\nA: Django raises it when sync-only code, chiefly the ORM, is called directly from a thread running an event loop, because a blocking query would freeze the loop. Common triggers are iterating a queryset with plain `for`, accessing an unloaded foreign key, or using a sync library that queries the database inside an async view. Fix it by using the async ORM methods (`aget`, `async for`, `acount`), preloading relations with `select_related`/`prefetch_related`, or wrapping the sync code with `sync_to_async`. Do not set `DJANGO_ALLOW_ASYNC_UNSAFE` in servers.\n\n**Q: Is the Django ORM truly asynchronous?**\nA: Its interface is async since 4.1 (`aget`, `acreate`, `async for`, `aaggregate`, and `asave`, `adelete`, related-manager methods since 4.2), but execution is not: each async method calls the sync implementation through `sync_to_async`, and the database driver call blocks a worker thread. The event loop stays free while the query runs, which is what matters for concurrency, but you pay a thread switch per query and remain limited by threads and connections. Native async database drivers in the ORM are still future work.\n\n**Q: Can you use transactions in async Django code?**\nA: Not directly — `transaction.atomic()` is not supported in async contexts in Django 5.2. Put the transactional logic, including `select_for_update` and `on_commit` registration, in a synchronous function and call it with `sync_to_async` (thread-sensitive), so the entire transaction runs on one thread with one connection. This also tends to produce cleaner code: a sync service layer called from thin async views.\n\n**Q: How do middleware work with async views?**\nA: Each middleware declares `sync_capable` and `async_capable`. When building the chain for ASGI, Django calls async-capable middleware directly and wraps sync-only middleware with adapters so each request hops into a thread and back, costing on the order of a millisecond per switch. All built-in middleware supports both modes; third-party sync-only middleware in the chain erodes async benefits. Write dual-mode middleware with `@sync_and_async_middleware` or the class flags plus `markcoroutinefunction`.\n\n**Q: Which ASGI servers can run Django, and how do you deploy one?**\nA: Uvicorn, Daphne and Hypercorn. A common production setup is Gunicorn as the process manager with the uvicorn worker class — `gunicorn config.asgi:application -k uvicorn.workers.UvicornWorker -w 4` — behind Nginx or a load balancer. Daphne is the Channels reference server, and adding `daphne` to `INSTALLED_APPS` makes `runserver` serve ASGI in development. Workers are sized roughly to CPU cores, since each process runs one event loop.\n\n**Q: When would you choose async Django over sync, and how would you migrate an existing app? (Senior)**\nA: Choose async for I/O-bound concurrency: aggregating several upstream APIs, SSE or WebSockets, long polling, or many slow clients where thread count limits capacity. Keep DB-bound CRUD sync. To migrate, first switch the server to ASGI with all views unchanged — sync views keep working in per-request threads — and verify middleware is async-capable and `CONN_MAX_AGE` is replaced with pooling. Then convert only the endpoints that benefit, keep business logic in sync service functions called via `sync_to_async`, swap blocking clients for async ones in those paths, and add blocking-call detection and load tests comparing p50/p99. A split deployment (WSGI for CRUD, ASGI for streaming/fan-out) is a valid end state.\n\n**Q: Your async endpoint has fine p50 but terrible p99 under load. How do you debug it? (Senior)**\nA: That pattern suggests the event loop is being blocked intermittently, so every request queued behind a blocking call suffers. Enable asyncio debug mode with a low `slow_callback_duration` to log callbacks that run too long, and use `py-spy dump` on a worker to see what the loop thread is executing. Look for sync HTTP SDKs, sync Redis/S3 clients, CPU-heavy JSON or image work, or ORM calls hidden behind `sync_to_async` that serialize on the per-request thread or wait for a connection from an exhausted pool. Fix with async clients, `sync_to_async` wrappers, offloading CPU work, connection pool sizing and concurrency limits on upstream calls.\n\n**Q: How do database connections behave under ASGI, and how should you configure them? (Senior)**\nA: Django connections are per thread. Under ASGI, sync code runs in per-request threads, so persistent connections tied to threads (`CONN_MAX_AGE > 0`) are not reliably reused and can accumulate until PostgreSQL hits `max_connections`. Django's guidance is to disable persistent connections for async deployments and use pooling: the native psycopg 3 pool in Django 5.1+ (`OPTIONS: {\"pool\": True}` or explicit sizes), or PgBouncer in transaction mode with `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS = True`. Size `workers x pool_max` against the database's connection budget, and remember that async does not increase how many queries can run concurrently — the pool does.\n\n**Q: How do you call an external API from a Django view without blocking, and handle failures?**\nA: In an async view under ASGI, use an async client such as `httpx.AsyncClient` with explicit timeouts, and run independent calls concurrently with `asyncio.gather(..., return_exceptions=True)` or a `TaskGroup` so one failure does not cancel or fail the page. Bound concurrency with a semaphore, add retries with backoff for idempotent calls, and degrade gracefully when a dependency is down. If the work is not needed for the response, enqueue it in Celery instead. In a sync view, the same calls block the worker; a thread pool is the sync alternative.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Item | Detail |\n|---|---|\n| Entry points | `config/wsgi.py` (`get_wsgi_application`), `config/asgi.py` (`get_asgi_application`) |\n| Async view | `async def view(request)`; real concurrency only under ASGI |\n| Sync view under ASGI | Runs via `sync_to_async(thread_sensitive=True)` in a per-request thread |\n| Async view under WSGI | Runs via `async_to_sync`; no gain |\n| Async ORM | `aget`, `acreate`, `aget_or_create`, `acount`, `aexists`, `afirst`, `aupdate`, `adelete`, `aaggregate`, `ain_bulk`, `aiterator`, `async for`; `asave`, `adelete`, `arefresh_from_db` |\n| Async auth/session | `await request.auser()`, `alogin`, `alogout` (5.0); `session.aget/aset` (5.1) |\n| Transactions | Not in async — wrap a sync function with `sync_to_async` |\n| Guard | `SynchronousOnlyOperation` |\n| Middleware flags | `sync_capable`, `async_capable`, `@sync_and_async_middleware` |\n| Servers | uvicorn, Daphne, Hypercorn; `gunicorn -k uvicorn.workers.UvicornWorker` |\n| DB connections | `CONN_MAX_AGE = 0` + pool (5.1+) or PgBouncer |\n| Tests | `async def test_...`, `self.async_client`, `AsyncClient` |\n\n- Async helps waiting, not computing.\n- The ORM is an async API over threaded execution.\n- One blocking call in `async def` stalls the whole worker.\n- Preload relations; lazy loads raise in async code.\n- Keep logic sync; make only I/O-heavy edges async.\n- Do not use `thread_sensitive=False` for ORM code.\n- Long-lived work belongs in Celery, not `create_task`.\n- Bound upstream concurrency and set timeouts on every await.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" \"uvicorn[standard]\" gunicorn httpx`.\n\n1. **Sync vs async fan-out.** Create a slow stub endpoint that sleeps 300 ms (`await asyncio.sleep(0.3)`). Write a sync view that calls it three times with `httpx.Client` and an async view using `asyncio.gather`. Benchmark both under `gunicorn -w 2` (WSGI) and `uvicorn --workers 2` with `hey -c 50 -n 500`, and tabulate latency and throughput.\n2. **Block the loop.** Add `time.sleep(1)` to the async view and measure the effect on a second, trivial async endpoint on the same worker. Replace it with `await asyncio.sleep(1)` and compare. Enable `PYTHONASYNCIODEBUG=1` to see the warning.\n3. **SynchronousOnlyOperation.** Trigger it with a lazy FK access in an async view; fix it with `select_related`, and separately with `sync_to_async`.\n4. **Transactions.** Implement a stock-decrement checkout from an async view using a sync `atomic()` function wrapped in `sync_to_async`; write an async test proving stock never goes negative under concurrent requests.\n5. **Dual-mode middleware.** Write the timing middleware from §4 and confirm (with a breakpoint or `threading.get_ident()`) which thread it runs on for a sync view and an async view under ASGI.\n6. **SSE.** Build the notifications stream, publish from the Django shell, and watch messages arrive in `curl -N`. Kill the client and confirm the `finally` block runs.\n\n**Mini project — travel search aggregator.** An async endpoint that queries five simulated provider APIs concurrently with per-provider timeouts and a global semaphore, caches results in Redis via the async cache API (`await cache.aget`), streams partial results to the browser over SSE as each provider responds, stores the final search in PostgreSQL through a sync service function, and is deployed with Gunicorn + UvicornWorker and Django 5.1+ connection pooling. Include a load test report comparing it to a sequential sync implementation.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters:** [Ch 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle) · [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) · [Ch 14 · Transactions & Concurrency](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) · [Ch 29 · Caching in Django](topic.html?p=29-caching) · [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks) · [Ch 32 · Django Channels & Real-Time](topic.html?p=32-channels-websockets) · [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases) · [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production)\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · Streaming, SSE & WebSockets](../rest-api/topic.html?p=30-streaming-sse-websockets) · [REST API · Async APIs & Webhooks](../rest-api/topic.html?p=29-async-apis-webhooks)\n\n- **Asynchronous support** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · async views, the sync/async boundary, `sync_to_async` and thread sensitivity. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/async/>\n- **How to deploy with ASGI** — Django docs · *Beginner* · uvicorn, Daphne and Hypercorn setup. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/deployment/asgi/>\n- **Asynchronous queries** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · the async ORM interface and its limits. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/queries/#asynchronous-queries>\n- **ASGI specification** — asgiref docs · *Advanced* · scope, receive/send and the HTTP and WebSocket sub-protocols. <https://asgi.readthedocs.io/en/latest/>\n- **asgiref** — GitHub · *Advanced* · the source of `sync_to_async`, `async_to_sync` and `ThreadSensitiveContext`. <https://github.com/django/asgiref>\n- **Uvicorn deployment docs** — Uvicorn · *Intermediate* · running with Gunicorn, workers and proxies. <https://www.uvicorn.org/deployment/>\n- **Python asyncio — developing with asyncio** — Python docs · *Intermediate* · debug mode and detecting blocking calls. <https://docs.python.org/3/library/asyncio-dev.html>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 30.*\n", "31-celery-background-tasks": "# 31 · Background Tasks with Celery\n\n> **In one line:** Anything slow, flaky or not needed to build the HTTP response belongs in a background task — and with Celery that means passing IDs not objects, dispatching only after the transaction commits, and writing every task so that running it twice is harmless, because at-least-once is the delivery you actually get.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) (atomic, `on_commit`), [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals), [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi), [Ch 29 · Caching in Django](topic.html?p=29-caching).\n\nA user clicks \"Sign up\". Your view creates the `User`, sends a welcome email through an SMTP provider, generates a PDF invoice, resizes the avatar and pings a CRM webhook. Each of those external calls takes between 200 ms and several seconds, and any of them can time out. Done inline, the request takes eight seconds, a Gunicorn worker is pinned for all eight, and when the CRM is down the *signup itself* returns a 500 — even though the user row was created fine. Under load, ten slow signups occupy ten workers and your whole site stops answering.\n\nThe naive fixes fail in instructive ways. **Spawning a thread** inside the view (`threading.Thread(target=send_email).start()`) looks like it works in development, but the thread dies when Gunicorn recycles or kills the worker, there is no retry, no visibility, and nothing survives a deploy. **`asyncio.create_task` in an async view** has the same problem: fire-and-forget work lives only as long as the process. **A cron job that scans a table every minute** works but adds latency, duplicates logic and needs its own locking. What you actually need is a **durable queue**: the web process writes a small message (\"send welcome email to user 42\") into a broker, returns immediately, and a separate pool of **worker** processes pulls messages, executes them, retries failures with backoff, and acknowledges completion.\n\n**Celery** is the de-facto Python implementation of that pattern. It is a distributed task queue: you decorate a function with `@shared_task`, call `.delay()` from Django, and a Celery worker (a separate process started with `celery -A proj worker`) runs it. It brings retries, rate limits, time limits, scheduling (`celery beat`), workflow primitives (chain, group, chord) and monitoring (Flower, events). It also brings distributed-systems problems straight into your Django code: messages can be delivered twice, tasks can run before the database row they refer to is committed, and a model instance pickled into a message is stale by the time it runs. This chapter is about using Celery correctly — the part interviewers probe.\n\n> **Version note:** Django 6.0 adds a built-in Tasks framework (`django.tasks`) that standardises the \"enqueue a function\" API with pluggable backends. On Django 5.2 LTS, Celery (or RQ/Dramatiq/Huey) is still how production systems do it, and the principles in this chapter carry over unchanged.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Task** — a Python function registered with Celery (`@shared_task` / `@app.task`). *Why it matters:* the unit of retry, timeout and routing; its arguments must be serialisable (JSON by default).\n- **Producer / client** — the code that enqueues (`task.delay()`, `task.apply_async()`), usually a Django view. *Why:* producing is cheap (one broker write) — that is the whole latency win.\n- **Broker** — the message transport: **RabbitMQ** (AMQP, real acks, priorities) or **Redis** (simpler, visibility-timeout based). *Why:* it determines durability and redelivery semantics.\n- **Worker** — a long-running process (`celery worker`) with a pool of child processes/threads that execute tasks. *Why:* you scale throughput by adding workers, independent of web servers.\n- **Pool** — `prefork` (default, multiprocessing), `threads`, `gevent`/`eventlet` (greenlets for I/O), `solo`. *Why:* CPU-bound work wants prefork; thousands of HTTP calls want gevent.\n- **Result backend** — optional store for return values/states (Redis, database via `django-celery-results`). *Why:* only needed if someone reads results; leaving it on for every task wastes memory.\n- **AsyncResult** — handle returned by `.delay()` (has `.id`, `.state`, `.get()`). *Why:* calling `.get()` inside a web request (or another task) re-introduces blocking and can deadlock.\n- **Acknowledgement (ack)** — telling the broker a message is done. Default: acked *before* execution (early ack). With `acks_late=True`: acked *after* the task returns. *Why:* early ack loses the task if the worker dies mid-run; late ack can run it twice.\n- **Prefetch multiplier** — how many messages each worker process reserves in advance (default 4). *Why:* with long tasks, prefetching starves other workers; set `worker_prefetch_multiplier=1` for long jobs.\n- **Retry** — re-enqueue on failure, via `self.retry()` or `autoretry_for`, with `retry_backoff` and `max_retries`. *Why:* transient failures (timeouts, 503s) are normal; retries without idempotency multiply side effects.\n- **Idempotency** — running the task twice has the same effect as once. *Why:* at-least-once delivery (late acks, redelivery after visibility timeout, manual replays) guarantees duplicates eventually.\n- **`transaction.on_commit`** — Django hook that runs a callback after the outermost transaction commits. *Why:* dispatching inside an open transaction lets the worker run before the row exists.\n- **Celery beat** — the scheduler process that enqueues periodic tasks; `django-celery-beat` stores schedules in the DB. *Why:* replaces cron; must run as exactly **one** instance.\n- **Canvas** — workflow primitives: `chain` (sequential, result piped), `group` (parallel), `chord` (group + callback when all finish). *Why:* fan-out/fan-in without hand-rolled coordination; chords need a result backend.\n- **Routing / queues** — send tasks to named queues (`task_routes`) consumed by dedicated workers. *Why:* a flood of slow report jobs must not delay password-reset emails.\n- **Time limits** — `soft_time_limit` (raises `SoftTimeLimitExceeded` inside the task) and `time_limit` (hard kill). *Why:* a hung HTTP call otherwise blocks a worker slot forever.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### The life of a task message\n\nWhen you call `send_welcome_email.delay(user_id=42)`, Celery's client does not run anything. It builds a message — task name (`\"accounts.tasks.send_welcome_email\"`), a UUID task id, args/kwargs serialised to JSON, retry count, ETA, headers — and publishes it to the broker under the task's queue (default `celery`). That is one network round trip, typically well under a millisecond on a local network.\n\nA worker process, started with `celery -A proj worker -l info`, holds a connection to the broker and **consumes** from one or more queues. The main worker process (the *consumer*) reserves up to `concurrency × prefetch_multiplier` messages, hands each to a child in its pool, and the child looks up the task by name in its registry (populated at startup by `app.autodiscover_tasks()` importing every installed app's `tasks.py`), deserialises the arguments, and calls the function. On success, it stores the result (if a backend is configured) and the message is acknowledged. On exception, the task either retries (a *new* message with `retries+1` and an ETA is published) or is marked `FAILURE`.\n\nThe crucial detail is **when the ack happens**. By default Celery acks the message *when the worker receives it, before running the task*. If the worker is OOM-killed halfway through, the message is already gone: the task is lost (at-most-once). With `acks_late=True` the ack is sent *after* the function returns; if the worker dies, the broker redelivers the message to another worker (RabbitMQ when the channel closes; Redis after the **visibility timeout**, default one hour). That is at-least-once — and it means your task can run twice. There is no setting that gives you exactly-once; you get it *in effect* by making the task idempotent. See [Messaging · Delivery Guarantees](../messaging/topic.html?p=04-delivery-guarantees) and [Messaging · Idempotency & Outbox](../messaging/topic.html?p=21-idempotency-outbox).\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj31a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj31b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Celery topology: Django produces, broker stores, workers consume</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"170\" height=\"130\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"105\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Django web (Gunicorn)</text>\n  <text x=\"105\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">view: create Order</text>\n  <text x=\"105\" y=\"112\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">on_commit(lambda:</text>\n  <text x=\"105\" y=\"128\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">send_receipt.delay(id))</text>\n  <text x=\"105\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-weight=\"bold\">returns 201 in ~50 ms</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"220\" width=\"170\" height=\"80\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"105\" y=\"244\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">celery beat (x1)</text>\n  <text x=\"105\" y=\"264\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">every 5 min: enqueue</text>\n  <text x=\"105\" y=\"282\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">expire_abandoned_carts</text>\n  <rect x=\"300\" y=\"60\" width=\"240\" height=\"250\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"420\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Broker (RabbitMQ / Redis)</text>\n  <rect x=\"320\" y=\"100\" width=\"200\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"420\" y=\"117\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">queue \"default\"</text>\n  <text x=\"420\" y=\"132\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">[receipt:7] [receipt:8] ...</text>\n  <rect x=\"320\" y=\"155\" width=\"200\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"420\" y=\"172\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">queue \"reports\" (slow)</text>\n  <text x=\"420\" y=\"187\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">[monthly_report:3]</text>\n  <rect x=\"320\" y=\"210\" width=\"200\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"420\" y=\"227\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">unacked (in flight)</text>\n  <text x=\"420\" y=\"242\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">redelivered if worker dies</text>\n  <text x=\"420\" y=\"285\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#92400e\">message = name + id + JSON args</text>\n  <rect x=\"650\" y=\"50\" width=\"210\" height=\"120\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">worker -Q default</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">prefork pool, concurrency 8</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"112\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">Order.objects.get(pk=id)</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">send email, mark sent</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\">ack (late) after return</text>\n  <rect x=\"650\" y=\"190\" width=\"210\" height=\"100\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">worker -Q reports</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"234\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">prefetch_multiplier=1</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"252\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">soft_time_limit=600</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"272\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">isolated from emails</text>\n  <rect x=\"650\" y=\"310\" width=\"210\" height=\"60\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"334\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Result backend (optional)</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"352\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">states / return values</text>\n  <path d=\"M190,115 L298,120\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj31a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M190,260 L298,230\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj31a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M520,120 L648,110\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj31a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M520,175 L648,235\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj31a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M755,290 L755,308\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj31a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M650,160 L524,232\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj31b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"560\" y=\"210\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">crash</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"350\" fill=\"#334155\">Web and workers share the same codebase and settings, but are separate processes,</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"368\" fill=\"#334155\">scaled separately. Workers talk to the same PostgreSQL as web.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Why you pass IDs, not model instances\n\n`send_receipt.delay(order)` fails with the default JSON serializer (a model instance is not JSON-serialisable). People \"fix\" it by switching to pickle, and that is worse: pickle is a remote-code-execution risk if anyone can write to your broker, and the pickled object is a **snapshot** taken at enqueue time. If the task runs 30 seconds later, after an admin changed the order's address, the task sends the old address. If it is retried an hour later, the snapshot is an hour old. Passing `order.pk` forces the task to re-read the current row with `Order.objects.get(pk=order_id)` — fresh data, small messages, language-neutral JSON. It also gives you a natural place to handle \"the row was deleted in the meantime\" (`DoesNotExist` → log and return).\n\n### Why you dispatch on commit\n\nHere is the classic race. Inside `transaction.atomic()` (or with `ATOMIC_REQUESTS=True`) your view does `order = Order.objects.create(...)` then `send_receipt.delay(order.pk)`. The message hits the broker immediately. A worker picks it up in two milliseconds and runs `Order.objects.get(pk=...)` — on its own database connection, in its own transaction, under READ COMMITTED — and the view's transaction **has not committed yet**. The worker gets `DoesNotExist`. Or worse: the view later raises and rolls back, and the worker already emailed a receipt for an order that never existed.\n\n`transaction.on_commit(callback)` fixes both. Django stores the callback on the connection's list of pending callbacks; when the **outermost** `atomic` block commits, it runs them in order; if the transaction rolls back, they are discarded. Outside any transaction (plain autocommit) the callback runs immediately. The idiom is:\n\n```python\nfrom functools import partial\nfrom django.db import transaction\n\ntransaction.on_commit(lambda: send_receipt.delay(order.pk))\n# or, avoiding late-binding surprises:\ntransaction.on_commit(partial(send_receipt.delay, order.pk))\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** a lambda inside a loop captures the variable, not the value: `for o in orders: on_commit(lambda: task.delay(o.pk))` enqueues the *last* order N times. Use `partial(task.delay, o.pk)` or `lambda pk=o.pk: task.delay(pk)`.\n\n`on_commit` closes the \"task runs before commit\" gap but leaves a smaller one: the commit succeeds and then the process dies before the callback publishes. The task is never sent. For most emails that is acceptable; for money it is not, and you use a **transactional outbox** — write an `OutboxEvent` row in the same transaction, and a relay (a beat task or CDC) publishes it. That pattern lives in [Messaging · Idempotency & Outbox](../messaging/topic.html?p=21-idempotency-outbox). Django 5.0+ also accepts `on_commit(fn, robust=True)` so that one failing callback (say, the broker is down) does not prevent later callbacks from running.\n\n### Idempotency: the property that makes retries safe\n\nDuplicates come from many directions: `acks_late` redelivery after a worker crash, Redis visibility timeout expiring for a task that ran longer than an hour, a retry after a timeout where the remote call actually succeeded, a human re-running a failed task from Flower, or the producer enqueuing twice. A task is idempotent when its *effect* converges no matter how many times it runs. Techniques, cheapest first:\n\n1. **Make the effect a state, not a delta.** `Order.objects.filter(pk=id).update(status=\"shipped\")` is naturally idempotent; `F(\"balance\") + 10` is not.\n2. **Guard with a state check under a lock.** `select_for_update()` the row, return if `receipt_sent_at` is set, otherwise send and set it, all inside `atomic()`.\n3. **Record a processed key.** Insert `(task_name, business_key)` into a table with a unique constraint; an `IntegrityError` means \"already done\".\n4. **Pass an idempotency key to the external API** (Stripe and most payment gateways support it) so the provider dedupes retries of a call that may have succeeded.\n\n### Retries and backoff\n\nA task that calls an HTTP API will see timeouts. `autoretry_for=(requests.Timeout, requests.ConnectionError)` with `retry_backoff=True` makes Celery re-enqueue with exponential delays (1 s, 2 s, 4 s … capped by `retry_backoff_max`, default 600 s) and `retry_jitter=True` (default) randomises them to avoid thundering herds. `max_retries` (default 3) bounds it; after that the exception propagates and the task is `FAILURE`. Manual control: `raise self.retry(exc=exc, countdown=30)` inside a `bind=True` task. Retry only **transient** errors; retrying a `ValidationError` five times just produces five failures.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### The request-to-task sequence\n\nThe diagram below is the sequence you should be able to draw on a whiteboard: the publish happens only after COMMIT, so the worker's SELECT always finds the row.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 420\" width=\"100%\" height=\"420\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj31c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">on_commit dispatch: the task can only see committed data</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"40\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/><text x=\"105\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">View</text>\n  <rect x=\"260\" y=\"40\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"325\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">PostgreSQL</text>\n  <rect x=\"480\" y=\"40\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/><text x=\"545\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Broker</text>\n  <rect x=\"700\" y=\"40\" width=\"130\" height=\"30\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"765\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Worker</text>\n  <line x1=\"105\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"105\" y2=\"380\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/>\n  <line x1=\"325\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"325\" y2=\"380\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/>\n  <line x1=\"545\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"545\" y2=\"380\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/>\n  <line x1=\"765\" y1=\"70\" x2=\"765\" y2=\"380\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\"/>\n  <rect x=\"95\" y=\"90\" width=\"240\" height=\"120\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" opacity=\"0.6\"/>\n  <text x=\"215\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#5b21b6\" font-size=\"10\">atomic(): uncommitted, invisible to others</text>\n  <path d=\"M105,125 L320,125\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj31c)\"/><text x=\"210\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">INSERT order 7</text>\n  <text x=\"215\" y=\"152\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">on_commit(cb) registered</text>\n  <text x=\"215\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">(nothing sent yet)</text>\n  <path d=\"M105,195 L320,195\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj31c)\"/><text x=\"210\" y=\"190\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">COMMIT</text>\n  <path d=\"M105,235 L540,235\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj31c)\"/><text x=\"320\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2563eb\">cb(): send_receipt.delay(7) publishes</text>\n  <path d=\"M545,265 L760,265\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj31c)\"/><text x=\"655\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">deliver message</text>\n  <path d=\"M765,295 L330,295\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj31c)\"/><text x=\"545\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">SELECT order 7 (row is visible)</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"330\" text-anchor=\"end\" fill=\"#334155\">send email, set receipt_sent_at</text>\n  <path d=\"M765,355 L550,355\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj31c)\"/><text x=\"655\" y=\"350\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\">ack (acks_late)</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"405\" fill=\"#dc2626\">Without on_commit: the publish happens inside the purple box and the worker SELECT finds nothing.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Project layout and wiring\n\n```text\nproj/\n├── proj/\n│   ├── __init__.py        # imports celery app so @shared_task binds to it\n│   ├── celery.py          # Celery('proj'), config_from_object, autodiscover_tasks\n│   └── settings.py        # CELERY_* settings\n├── orders/\n│   ├── models.py\n│   ├── services.py        # place_order(): atomic + on_commit\n│   └── tasks.py           # @shared_task send_receipt\n└── manage.py\n\nprocesses in production:\n  gunicorn proj.wsgi                         (N replicas)\n  celery -A proj worker -Q default           (M replicas)\n  celery -A proj worker -Q reports -c 2 --prefetch-multiplier=1\n  celery -A proj beat                        (exactly 1)\n  celery -A proj flower                      (optional, behind auth)\n```\n\n### Workflow primitives (canvas)\n\n- `chain(a.s(1), b.s(), c.s())` — runs `a`, passes its return value as the first argument to `b`, then `c`. Sequential pipeline.\n- `group(resize.s(img_id, size) for size in SIZES)` — runs in parallel across workers.\n- `chord(group(...), finalize.s())` — when every member of the group finishes, calls `finalize` with the list of results. Requires a result backend and tracks completion with a counter; a single failing member means the callback never runs (it errors instead).\n- `.si()` (immutable signature) when you do *not* want the previous result passed in.\n\nUse canvas sparingly. A chord of 10,000 tasks puts 10,000 results into the backend; often a simpler design is \"each task updates its own row; a periodic task checks whether all rows are done\".\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: a welcome email\n\n```bash\npython -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"celery[redis]~=5.4\"\n```\n\nThe Celery application module lives next to `settings.py`:\n\n```python\n# proj/celery.py\nimport os\nfrom celery import Celery\n\nos.environ.setdefault(\"DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE\", \"proj.settings\")\n\napp = Celery(\"proj\")\n# Read every setting that starts with CELERY_ from Django settings.\napp.config_from_object(\"django.conf:settings\", namespace=\"CELERY\")\n# Import tasks.py from every INSTALLED_APP.\napp.autodiscover_tasks()\n```\n\n```python\n# proj/__init__.py\nfrom .celery import app as celery_app\n\n__all__ = (\"celery_app\",)\n```\n\n```python\n# proj/settings.py (excerpt)\nimport os\n\nCELERY_BROKER_URL = os.environ.get(\"CELERY_BROKER_URL\", \"redis://localhost:6379/0\")\nCELERY_RESULT_BACKEND = None          # we don't read results\nCELERY_TASK_SERIALIZER = \"json\"\nCELERY_ACCEPT_CONTENT = [\"json\"]\nCELERY_TASK_ACKS_LATE = True          # at-least-once; tasks must be idempotent\nCELERY_TASK_REJECT_ON_WORKER_LOST = True\nCELERY_WORKER_PREFETCH_MULTIPLIER = 1\nCELERY_TASK_TIME_LIMIT = 300          # hard kill after 5 min\nCELERY_TASK_SOFT_TIME_LIMIT = 240\nCELERY_TIMEZONE = \"UTC\"\n```\n\n```python\n# accounts/tasks.py\nimport logging\nfrom celery import shared_task\nfrom django.contrib.auth import get_user_model\nfrom django.core.mail import send_mail\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\n@shared_task\ndef send_welcome_email(user_id: int) -> None:\n    User = get_user_model()\n    try:\n        user = User.objects.get(pk=user_id)\n    except User.DoesNotExist:\n        log.warning(\"welcome email: user %s vanished\", user_id)\n        return\n    send_mail(\"Welcome!\", f\"Hi {user.first_name}\", None, [user.email])\n```\n\n```python\n# accounts/views.py\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.shortcuts import redirect, render\nfrom .forms import SignupForm\nfrom .tasks import send_welcome_email\n\ndef signup(request):\n    form = SignupForm(request.POST or None)\n    if form.is_valid():\n        with transaction.atomic():\n            user = form.save()\n            transaction.on_commit(lambda: send_welcome_email.delay(user.pk))\n        return redirect(\"home\")\n    return render(request, \"signup.html\", {\"form\": form})\n```\n\n```bash\ncelery -A proj worker -l info          # terminal 1\npython manage.py runserver              # terminal 2\n```\n\n```text\n[2026-10-25 10:00:01,203: INFO/MainProcess] Task accounts.tasks.send_welcome_email[3f2c...] received\n[2026-10-25 10:00:01,451: INFO/ForkPoolWorker-1] Task accounts.tasks.send_welcome_email[3f2c...] succeeded in 0.24s: None\n```\n\n### Real-world example: idempotent payment capture with retries\n\nThe order has been placed; now you must capture the payment with an external gateway, which times out occasionally and supports idempotency keys.\n\n```python\n# payments/models.py\nimport uuid\nfrom django.db import models\n\nclass Payment(models.Model):\n    class Status(models.TextChoices):\n        PENDING = \"pending\"\n        CAPTURED = \"captured\"\n        FAILED = \"failed\"\n\n    order = models.OneToOneField(\"orders.Order\", on_delete=models.PROTECT)\n    amount_cents = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n    status = models.CharField(max_length=16, choices=Status, default=Status.PENDING)\n    gateway_charge_id = models.CharField(max_length=64, blank=True)\n    idempotency_key = models.UUIDField(default=uuid.uuid4, unique=True, editable=False)\n    captured_at = models.DateTimeField(null=True, blank=True)\n    receipt_sent_at = models.DateTimeField(null=True, blank=True)\n```\n\n```python\n# payments/tasks.py\nimport logging\nfrom functools import partial\n\nimport requests\nfrom celery import shared_task\nfrom celery.exceptions import SoftTimeLimitExceeded\nfrom django.core.mail import send_mail\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.utils import timezone\n\nfrom .gateway import GatewayClient, CardDeclined, GatewayServerError\nfrom .models import Payment\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(__name__)\nTRANSIENT = (requests.Timeout, requests.ConnectionError, GatewayServerError)\n\n@shared_task(\n    bind=True,\n    acks_late=True,\n    autoretry_for=TRANSIENT,\n    retry_backoff=True,        # 1s, 2s, 4s, ... with jitter\n    retry_backoff_max=300,\n    max_retries=8,\n    soft_time_limit=30,\n    queue=\"payments\",\n)\ndef capture_payment(self, payment_id: int) -> None:\n    # 1. Cheap pre-check outside the lock: already done? (duplicate delivery)\n    if Payment.objects.filter(pk=payment_id, status=Payment.Status.CAPTURED).exists():\n        log.info(\"payment %s already captured, skipping\", payment_id)\n        return\n\n    with transaction.atomic():\n        # 2. Lock the row so two concurrent duplicates cannot both call the gateway.\n        payment = Payment.objects.select_for_update().get(pk=payment_id)\n        if payment.status != Payment.Status.PENDING:\n            return\n        try:\n            # 3. The gateway dedupes on idempotency_key: if a previous attempt\n            #    succeeded but we timed out reading the response, it returns\n            #    the original charge instead of charging twice.\n            charge = GatewayClient().capture(\n                amount=payment.amount_cents,\n                idempotency_key=str(payment.idempotency_key),\n                timeout=10,\n            )\n        except CardDeclined as exc:\n            payment.status = Payment.Status.FAILED\n            payment.save(update_fields=[\"status\"])\n            log.warning(\"payment %s declined: %s\", payment_id, exc)\n            return                     # business failure: do NOT retry\n        except SoftTimeLimitExceeded:\n            raise self.retry(countdown=60)\n\n        payment.status = Payment.Status.CAPTURED\n        payment.gateway_charge_id = charge.id\n        payment.captured_at = timezone.now()\n        payment.save(update_fields=[\"status\", \"gateway_charge_id\", \"captured_at\"])\n        transaction.on_commit(partial(send_payment_receipt.delay, payment.pk))\n\n\n@shared_task(acks_late=True)\ndef send_payment_receipt(payment_id: int) -> None:\n    # Idempotent by state: an atomic conditional UPDATE \"claims\" the send.\n    # Only one execution can flip receipt_sent_at from NULL; duplicates see 0 rows.\n    claimed = Payment.objects.filter(\n        pk=payment_id, receipt_sent_at__isnull=True\n    ).update(receipt_sent_at=timezone.now())\n    if not claimed:\n        return\n    payment = Payment.objects.select_related(\"order__customer\").get(pk=payment_id)\n    send_mail(\n        \"Payment received\",\n        f\"We received {payment.amount_cents / 100:.2f} for order {payment.order_id}.\",\n        None,\n        [payment.order.customer.email],\n    )\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** claim-then-send makes the email *at-most-once*: if the worker dies after the UPDATE but before `send_mail`, no email goes out. Send-then-claim makes it *at-least-once*. Choose consciously — for receipts, a rare missing email is better than duplicates; for a payout, you would record the external call's idempotency key instead.\n\n> **Gotcha:** holding `select_for_update()` across a network call keeps the row lock for up to the gateway timeout. That is acceptable here because the lock is per-payment and the timeout is bounded; never hold a lock on a *hot* row (a shared counter) across I/O.\n\nThe SQL the worker issues for the locking read and the claim:\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"payments_payment\".\"id\", \"payments_payment\".\"order_id\", ...\nFROM \"payments_payment\"\nWHERE \"payments_payment\".\"id\" = 42\nLIMIT 21\nFOR UPDATE;\n\nUPDATE \"payments_payment\"\nSET \"receipt_sent_at\" = '2026-10-25T10:00:03+00:00'::timestamptz\nWHERE (\"payments_payment\".\"id\" = 42 AND \"payments_payment\".\"receipt_sent_at\" IS NULL);\n```\n\n### Periodic tasks with beat\n\n```python\n# settings.py\nfrom celery.schedules import crontab\n\nCELERY_BEAT_SCHEDULE = {\n    \"expire-carts\": {\n        \"task\": \"orders.tasks.expire_abandoned_carts\",\n        \"schedule\": 300.0,                     # every 5 minutes\n    },\n    \"nightly-report\": {\n        \"task\": \"reports.tasks.build_daily_report\",\n        \"schedule\": crontab(hour=2, minute=0),\n        \"options\": {\"queue\": \"reports\"},\n    },\n}\n```\n\n```python\n# orders/tasks.py\nfrom datetime import timedelta\nfrom celery import shared_task\nfrom django.utils import timezone\nfrom .models import Cart\n\n@shared_task\ndef expire_abandoned_carts() -> int:\n    cutoff = timezone.now() - timedelta(hours=2)\n    # Set-based and idempotent: running twice expires nothing extra.\n    return Cart.objects.filter(status=\"open\", updated_at__lt=cutoff).update(status=\"expired\")\n```\n\nWith `django-celery-beat`, schedules live in the database and are editable in the admin: run `celery -A proj beat -S django`.\n\n### Fan-out with a chord\n\n```python\nfrom celery import chord, shared_task\nfrom .models import Image\n\n@shared_task\ndef resize(image_id: int, width: int) -> str:\n    ...  # load, resize, upload; return the storage key of the variant\n\n@shared_task\ndef mark_ready(keys: list[str], image_id: int) -> None:\n    Image.objects.filter(pk=image_id).update(variants=keys, ready=True)\n\ndef process_upload(image_id: int) -> None:\n    chord(resize.s(image_id, w) for w in (320, 640, 1280))(mark_ready.s(image_id))\n```\n\n### Routing to separate queues\n\n```python\nCELERY_TASK_ROUTES = {\n    \"payments.tasks.*\": {\"queue\": \"payments\"},\n    \"reports.tasks.*\": {\"queue\": \"reports\"},\n}\nCELERY_TASK_DEFAULT_QUEUE = \"default\"\n```\n\n```bash\ncelery -A proj worker -Q default,payments -c 8 -n web@%h\ncelery -A proj worker -Q reports -c 2 --prefetch-multiplier=1 -n reports@%h\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Option | Strengths | Weaknesses | Pick when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Celery + RabbitMQ | Real acks, priorities, mature routing, quorum queues | Another stateful service to run | Critical tasks, many queues, high volume |\n| Celery + Redis | One dependency you likely already have; simple | Visibility-timeout redelivery quirks; data loss if Redis is not persistent | Most small/medium apps |\n| RQ / Dramatiq / Huey | Simpler APIs, fewer knobs; Dramatiq acks late by default | Smaller ecosystem, fewer canvas features | Small teams who want less magic |\n| Threads / `asyncio.create_task` | Zero infra | Lost on restart, no retries, no visibility | Never for anything that matters |\n| Cron + management command | Trivial, no broker | Minute granularity, overlapping runs need locking | Nightly batch jobs only |\n| Django 6.0 Tasks framework | Standard API, swappable backend | Not in 5.2; needs a backend for real work | New projects on 6.x |\n\n### When to use\n\n- External I/O that the response does not need: emails, SMS, webhooks, push notifications, CRM sync.\n- CPU-heavy work: image/video processing, PDF generation, report building, ML inference.\n- Retries of flaky dependencies with backoff.\n- Scheduled maintenance (beat): expiring carts, sending digests, refreshing materialised views.\n- Fan-out of one event into many independent jobs.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- When the user needs the result in the same response (validate a coupon) — do it inline, fast.\n- To \"speed up\" a slow query — fix the query ([Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization)).\n- For real-time pushes to a browser — that is [Ch 32 · Django Channels & Real-Time](topic.html?p=32-channels-websockets) (often a Celery task *triggers* a channel-layer send).\n- As an event bus between microservices with replay needs — that is Kafka's job ([Messaging · Kafka vs RabbitMQ](../messaging/topic.html?p=03-kafka-vs-rabbitmq)).\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**Passing model instances.** People call `task.delay(user)`. It fails under JSON or, with pickle, ships a stale snapshot and opens an RCE hole. Pass `user.pk` and re-fetch.\n\n**Dispatching inside the transaction.** The worker races the commit and sees `DoesNotExist`, or acts on rolled-back data. Wrap in `transaction.on_commit`. This includes dispatching from a `post_save` signal — the signal fires *inside* the transaction.\n\n```python\n# Before\n@receiver(post_save, sender=Order)\ndef on_order(sender, instance, created, **kw):\n    if created:\n        send_receipt.delay(instance.pk)          # races the commit\n\n# After\n@receiver(post_save, sender=Order)\ndef on_order(sender, instance, created, **kw):\n    if created:\n        transaction.on_commit(partial(send_receipt.delay, instance.pk))\n```\n\n**Non-idempotent tasks with `acks_late`.** `Account.objects.filter(pk=id).update(balance=F(\"balance\") + amount)` in a retried task credits twice. Record a processed key or make the effect absolute.\n\n**Retrying everything.** `autoretry_for=(Exception,)` retries bugs and validation errors, hammering dependencies with doomed calls. List transient exceptions explicitly.\n\n**Calling `.get()` on a result inside a task.** A task that waits on another task can deadlock the pool when all slots are waiting. Celery raises a `RuntimeError` for this by default; use chains/chords instead.\n\n**One queue for everything.** A 20-minute report blocks password resets behind it. Route slow work to its own queue and workers.\n\n**Running several beat instances.** Two beats (e.g. beat embedded in every worker pod via `-B`) enqueue every periodic task twice. Run exactly one beat process (a Kubernetes Deployment with `replicas: 1` and `strategy: Recreate`), and still keep periodic tasks idempotent.\n\n**Leaving the result backend on for fire-and-forget tasks.** Millions of result keys accumulate in Redis. Set `ignore_result=True` or no backend.\n\n**Querying the database at import time in `celery.py` or `tasks.py`.** The connection is opened in the parent before fork and the socket is shared by all children, causing corrupted protocol errors. Keep imports side-effect free.\n\n**Best practices**\n\n- Small, explicit arguments (IDs, primitive values); keep message payloads tiny.\n- Every task idempotent; every task with a time limit.\n- Log task id and business key on every line (use `self.request.id` in bound tasks).\n- Celery's Django fixup calls `close_old_connections` around each task; long-running loops inside one task should call `django.db.close_old_connections()` themselves.\n- Version task signatures: add new kwargs with defaults so old in-flight messages still deserialise during a rolling deploy.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n> **Production story:** After a deploy, receipts went out for orders that customers said they never placed. Root cause: a refactor moved `send_receipt.delay()` above a validation step inside `atomic()`. When validation failed the transaction rolled back, but the message was already published. Fix: `on_commit`, plus a guard in the task (`if not Order.objects.filter(pk=id).exists(): return`).\n\n**Tasks \"disappearing\" after a worker OOM.** Symptom: some jobs never complete, no error logged. Root cause: default early ack — the message was acked before the child was SIGKILLed. Fix: `acks_late=True` and `task_reject_on_worker_lost=True`, plus idempotency.\n\n**Tasks running twice every hour with Redis.** Symptom: long tasks (over an hour) duplicate. Root cause: Redis transport redelivers unacked messages after `visibility_timeout` (one hour default). Fix: raise `broker_transport_options={\"visibility_timeout\": 43200}` above your longest task + ETA, split the task, or use RabbitMQ.\n\n**ETA/countdown tasks piling up in worker memory.** Tasks scheduled far in the future are held by the worker (reserved) until due, inflating memory and interacting with the visibility timeout. Fix: for long delays, store a `run_at` row and let a beat task enqueue due items.\n\n**Memory growth in workers.** Python processes fragment memory over time. Use `worker_max_tasks_per_child` (recycle a child after N tasks) or `worker_max_memory_per_child` (KiB).\n\n**Database connection exhaustion.** Each prefork child holds its own DB connection: 10 worker pods × concurrency 16 = 160 connections, on top of web. Size Postgres `max_connections` or put PgBouncer in front — see [Ch 34 · Scaling Django: Connections, Replicas & Multiple Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases).\n\n**Graceful shutdown during deploys.** SIGTERM triggers a warm shutdown: the worker stops consuming and finishes current tasks. Kubernetes' `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` must exceed your longest task, otherwise SIGKILL follows; with `acks_late` the task is redelivered, without it, lost. See [Kubernetes · Jobs & CronJobs](../kubernetes/topic.html?p=08-jobs-cronjobs) for one-off batch alternatives.\n\n**Poison messages.** A task that always crashes the worker (segfault in an image library, huge payload) is redelivered forever with `acks_late`. Cap retries, detect repeated redelivery (`self.request.delivery_info.get(\"redelivered\")`) and park it in a dead-letter table or queue — see [Messaging · Backpressure & Poison Messages](../messaging/topic.html?p=23-backpressure-poison-messages).\n\n**Debugging toolkit**\n\n```bash\ncelery -A proj inspect active          # what is running right now\ncelery -A proj inspect reserved        # prefetched, not yet started\ncelery -A proj inspect scheduled       # ETA tasks held by workers\ncelery -A proj inspect registered      # task names the worker knows\ncelery -A proj purge -Q reports        # drop queued messages (careful)\ncelery -A proj flower --port=5555      # web UI: rates, failures, retries\n```\n\n- Queue depth is the key metric: alert on a growing backlog of the `default` queue (RabbitMQ management API, `LLEN default` in Redis).\n- Sentry's Celery integration captures task exceptions with task name and arguments.\n- `Received unregistered task` means the worker runs older code or `autodiscover_tasks` did not import the module — check `inspect registered` and that worker and web images are the same version.\n\n**Scaling.** Throughput scales with workers × concurrency until you hit the database or the external API. Scale workers on queue length (KEDA in Kubernetes, see [Kubernetes · Autoscaling](../kubernetes/topic.html?p=20-autoscaling)), give each queue its own deployment, use `rate_limit=\"10/s\"` per task to respect API quotas (it is enforced per worker, not globally), and use gevent pools for thousands of concurrent outbound HTTP calls.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: Why do you pass a model's ID to a Celery task instead of the model instance?**\nA: Task arguments are serialised into the broker message, JSON by default, and a model instance is not JSON-serialisable. Switching to pickle \"works\" but ships a snapshot that is stale by the time the task runs (and even staler on retries), bloats messages, and is a remote-code-execution risk if the broker is ever writable by an attacker. Passing the primary key makes the task re-read the current row with `Model.objects.get(pk=...)`, so it acts on fresh data. It also forces you to handle the case where the row was deleted in between, which is a real event in production.\n\n**Q: Why should you enqueue tasks with `transaction.on_commit` rather than calling `.delay()` directly?**\nA: Inside `atomic()` (or with `ATOMIC_REQUESTS`) your writes are not visible to other connections until commit. `.delay()` publishes immediately, so a fast worker can run the task before the commit and get `DoesNotExist`, or act on data that is later rolled back. `transaction.on_commit(fn)` registers the callback on the connection and runs it only after the outermost transaction commits, discarding it on rollback. Outside a transaction it runs immediately, so it is always safe to use. The residual risk — the process dies between commit and publish — is solved with an outbox when you cannot afford to lose the task.\n\n**Q: What does `acks_late=True` do and what is its trade-off?**\nA: By default Celery acknowledges a message when the worker receives it, before execution, so a crash mid-task loses it (at-most-once). `acks_late=True` acknowledges after the task finishes; if the worker dies, the broker redelivers to another worker (at-least-once). The trade-off is duplicates: the task may have done some or all of its side effects before the crash. So `acks_late` is only correct for idempotent tasks. Pair it with `task_reject_on_worker_lost=True` so a SIGKILLed child's message is requeued rather than acknowledged.\n\n**Q: What does it mean for a task to be idempotent, and how do you make one?**\nA: Running it N times has the same effect as running it once. You make the effect a target state rather than a delta (`update(status=\"shipped\")` instead of incrementing), guard with a state check under `select_for_update()`, record a processed key in a table with a unique constraint, and pass an idempotency key to external APIs so the provider dedupes a call that succeeded but timed out. Retries, late acks, visibility timeouts and manual replays all produce duplicates, so idempotency is a requirement, not an optimisation.\n\n**Q: How do you configure retries in Celery?**\nA: Either declaratively with `@shared_task(autoretry_for=(Timeout,), retry_backoff=True, retry_backoff_max=600, max_retries=5)` or manually in a `bind=True` task with `raise self.retry(exc=exc, countdown=30)`. `retry_backoff` gives exponential delays, and jitter is on by default to avoid synchronised retry storms. Retry only transient errors — network timeouts, 5xx, deadlocks — never validation or business errors like a declined card. After `max_retries` the original exception propagates and the task is marked failed, which should alert.\n\n**Q: What are the broker and the result backend, and do you always need a result backend?**\nA: The broker transports task messages from producers to workers (RabbitMQ or Redis). The result backend stores task state and return values so a caller can poll `AsyncResult`. Most Django tasks are fire-and-forget — email, webhooks — and nobody reads the result, so you set `ignore_result=True` or configure no backend at all. You need one for chords, for `AsyncResult.get()`, or for progress polling. Leaving it on everywhere fills Redis with millions of keys.\n\n**Q: What is Celery beat and what goes wrong if you run two of them?**\nA: Beat is the scheduler process that enqueues tasks on a schedule (interval or crontab), from `CELERY_BEAT_SCHEDULE` or the database with `django-celery-beat`. It does not execute tasks; workers do. Running two beats — common when you add `-B` to every worker replica — enqueues every periodic task twice. Run exactly one beat instance and still make periodic tasks idempotent and set-based so an accidental double run is harmless.\n\n**Q: Explain chain, group and chord.**\nA: A `chain` runs tasks sequentially, passing each result as the first argument of the next. A `group` runs tasks in parallel and returns a `GroupResult`. A `chord` is a group plus a callback that receives the list of results once every member has finished; it needs a result backend to count completions. Use `.si()` for immutable signatures when the next task should not receive the previous result. Large chords are expensive and fragile — one failure blocks the callback — so for big fan-outs track completion in your own table.\n\n**Q: Why does dispatching a task from a `post_save` signal cause intermittent `DoesNotExist` errors?**\nA: Signals are synchronous and fire inside the same transaction as the `save()`, before commit. If the request is wrapped in `atomic()` or `ATOMIC_REQUESTS`, the worker can pick up the task before the row is committed and visible to its connection. It is intermittent because it depends on the race between the remaining view code and the worker's speed. Wrap the dispatch in `transaction.on_commit` inside the receiver, or better, dispatch from the service function that owns the transaction.\n\n**Q: Your task queue backs up every morning and password-reset emails take 20 minutes. How do you fix it? (Senior)**\nA: First measure: queue depth per queue, task runtimes, and which tasks dominate (Flower or broker metrics). The usual cause is mixing slow batch jobs with latency-sensitive ones in one queue, plus the default prefetch of 4 letting one worker hoard long tasks. Route tasks to separate queues (`task_routes`), give the latency-sensitive queue its own worker deployment so it can never be starved, and set `worker_prefetch_multiplier=1` with `acks_late` on the slow queue. Then scale workers on queue length, add time limits so hung tasks free their slots, and check whether the batch job can be made set-based instead of one task per row.\n\n**Q: A task that charges a card occasionally charges customers twice. Walk me through diagnosing and fixing it. (Senior)**\nA: Duplicates mean the task ran more than once or retried after a partial success. Sources: `acks_late` redelivery after a crash or deploy, the Redis visibility timeout on a long task, an `autoretry_for` on a timeout where the gateway actually succeeded, or a producer enqueuing twice (dispatch in a signal plus in the view). Fixes in layers: pass a stable idempotency key (stored on the Payment row at creation) to the gateway so it dedupes; lock the row with `select_for_update()` and check status before calling; persist the outcome in the same transaction; and dispatch with `on_commit` in exactly one place. Add an alert on any Payment with two gateway charges to catch regressions.\n\n**Q: How would you guarantee a task is never lost even if the web process crashes right after commit? (Senior)**\nA: `on_commit` cannot guarantee it, because the publish is a separate step after commit. Use the transactional outbox: in the same `atomic()` that writes the order, insert an `OutboxEvent(task=\"send_receipt\", payload={\"order_id\": 7})`. A relay — a beat task every few seconds using `select_for_update(skip_locked=True)`, or Debezium CDC on the table — publishes pending rows and marks them sent. The relay can publish twice if it crashes after publishing but before marking, so the consuming task stays idempotent. This gives \"published if and only if committed\" plus at-least-once delivery.\n\n**Q: How do Celery workers interact with Django's database connections, and what can go wrong? (Senior)**\nA: Each prefork child opens its own connection on first query and reuses it across tasks; Celery's Django fixup closes stale or errored connections before and after each task, honouring `CONN_MAX_AGE`. Problems: connection counts multiply (pods × concurrency), so Postgres runs out of slots; a connection broken by a DB failover stays broken inside a long-running loop unless you call `close_old_connections()`; and opening a connection at import time in the parent shares one socket across forked children. Size the pool budget explicitly, consider PgBouncer, and with gevent pools remember Django connections are per thread (and greenlets patch threading), so many greenlets mean many connections.\n\n**Q: How do you test Celery tasks?**\nA: Test the task function directly as a plain call (`capture_payment(payment.pk)` or `capture_payment.apply(args=[...])`) for the business logic, with external APIs mocked. For the dispatch, assert it is enqueued after commit using `TestCase.captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True)` and a mocked `.delay`. `CELERY_TASK_ALWAYS_EAGER` runs tasks inline but hides serialization and transaction issues, so use it sparingly. See [Ch 33 · Testing Django Applications](topic.html?p=33-testing).\n\n**Q: What is the visibility timeout and why does it matter with Redis?**\nA: Redis has no native acks, so Celery's Redis transport keeps a reserved message in an unacked set and, if it is not acknowledged within the visibility timeout (one hour by default), redelivers it. A task that legitimately runs longer than that, or an ETA task scheduled further out than that, will be delivered again to another worker and run twice. Raise the timeout above your longest task plus ETA, avoid long ETAs, or use RabbitMQ, which redelivers only when the consumer's channel closes.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Concern | Setting / API | Rule of thumb |\n|---|---|---|\n| Arguments | `task.delay(obj.pk)` | IDs and primitives, JSON serializer |\n| Dispatch timing | `transaction.on_commit(partial(t.delay, pk))` | Always, including from signals |\n| At-least-once | `acks_late=True`, `task_reject_on_worker_lost=True` | Only with idempotent tasks |\n| Retries | `autoretry_for`, `retry_backoff`, `max_retries` | Transient errors only |\n| Long tasks | `worker_prefetch_multiplier=1`, own queue | Prevent hoarding and starvation |\n| Hung tasks | `soft_time_limit`, `time_limit` | Every task has a limit |\n| Results | `ignore_result=True` | Backend only when someone reads it |\n| Scheduling | `celery beat` (one instance) | Periodic tasks set-based and idempotent |\n| Memory | `worker_max_tasks_per_child` | Recycle children |\n| Redis redelivery | `visibility_timeout` | Greater than longest task + ETA |\n\n- `.delay()` only publishes a message; a separate worker process runs the code.\n- Default ack is early (at-most-once); `acks_late` makes it at-least-once — there is no exactly-once delivery.\n- `post_save` runs inside the transaction: dispatch from it via `on_commit`.\n- Lambdas in loops capture the variable — use `partial`.\n- Never call `AsyncResult.get()` inside a request or another task.\n- Separate queues for latency-sensitive and bulk work.\n- Beat schedules, workers execute; exactly one beat.\n- Use an outbox when a lost task is unacceptable.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSetup: `python -m venv .venv && pip install \"django~=5.2\" \"celery[redis]~=5.4\"` and run Redis with `docker run -p 6379:6379 redis:7`.\n\n1. **The commit race.** Write a view that creates an `Order` inside `atomic()`, calls `task.delay(order.pk)`, then `time.sleep(2)` before leaving the block. Watch the worker log `DoesNotExist`. Switch to `on_commit` and confirm it works.\n2. **Lost vs duplicated.** Write a task that sleeps 30 s then writes a row. Start it, `kill -9` the worker child mid-sleep. Observe it is lost with default settings; enable `acks_late` + `task_reject_on_worker_lost` and observe redelivery.\n3. **Make it idempotent.** Write `credit_account(account_id, amount, txn_id)` and run it three times with the same `txn_id`. Add a `ProcessedTransaction` model with a unique constraint so the balance changes once.\n4. **Backoff.** Write a task that calls a fake API failing 3 times then succeeding; configure `autoretry_for` and `retry_backoff` and read the retry timings in the log.\n5. **Queue isolation.** Route a 60-second `slow_report` task to a `reports` queue; flood it with 20 jobs and show that `send_email` on `default` still completes instantly.\n\n**Mini project:** an image-upload pipeline. The upload view saves `Image` and dispatches on commit; a chord resizes to three widths in parallel and a callback marks the image ready; a beat task every 10 minutes re-enqueues images stuck in \"processing\" for more than 15 minutes; all tasks are idempotent; Flower shows the flow.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n- [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) — `atomic`, `on_commit`, `select_for_update`.\n- [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals) — why signals run inside the transaction.\n- [Ch 32 · Django Channels & Real-Time](topic.html?p=32-channels-websockets) — pushing task results to browsers.\n- [Ch 33 · Testing Django Applications](topic.html?p=33-testing) — `captureOnCommitCallbacks`, mocking `.delay`.\n- [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production) — running worker and beat processes.\n- [Messaging · Delivery Guarantees](../messaging/topic.html?p=04-delivery-guarantees), [Messaging · Acks, Prefetch & QoS](../messaging/topic.html?p=08-acks-prefetch-qos), [Messaging · Idempotency & Outbox](../messaging/topic.html?p=21-idempotency-outbox).\n- [System Design · Message Queues](../system-design/topic.html?p=23-message-queues).\n\n- **First Steps with Django** — Celery docs · *Beginner* · the official wiring of `celery.py`, `autodiscover_tasks` and settings. <https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/stable/django/first-steps-with-django.html>\n- **Tasks guide** — Celery docs · *Intermediate* · retries, acks_late, time limits and the \"avoid launching synchronous subtasks\" advice. <https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/stable/userguide/tasks.html>\n- **Canvas: Designing Work-flows** — Celery docs · *Intermediate* · chain, group, chord and signatures in depth. <https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/stable/userguide/canvas.html>\n- **Optimizing** — Celery docs · *Advanced* · prefetch limits, acks_late and worker tuning. <https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/stable/userguide/optimizing.html>\n- **Database transactions** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · the exact semantics of `on_commit`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/transactions/>\n- **django-celery-beat** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · database-backed periodic task schedules editable in the admin. <https://github.com/celery/django-celery-beat>\n- **Flower** — GitHub · *Beginner* · real-time monitoring UI for Celery. <https://github.com/mher/flower>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 31.*\n", "32-channels-websockets": "# 32 · Django Channels & Real-Time\n\n> **In one line:** Channels extends Django beyond request/response by running long-lived ASGI connections (WebSockets) in consumers, and connects every process through a channel layer so that any piece of code — a view, a Celery task, another socket — can broadcast to a group of browsers; the hard parts are authentication, the layer's best-effort delivery, and scaling thousands of open connections.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi) (ASGI, `sync_to_async`), [Ch 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle), [Ch 27 · Sessions, Cookies & Messages](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies), [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks).\n\nClassic Django answers one HTTP request with one response and forgets the client. That model cannot express \"tell every user watching order 42 that it just shipped\", \"show a new chat message in under 100 ms\", or \"stream build logs as they are produced\". The naive approach is **polling**: the browser asks `/api/orders/42/status` every two seconds. With 10,000 open tabs that is 5,000 requests per second, almost all returning \"no change\", each paying for middleware, authentication and a database query. Long-polling helps latency but still pins a worker per waiting client under WSGI, which is exactly the resource you have least of.\n\nWhat you want is a **persistent connection** the server can push into. The WebSocket protocol provides it: an HTTP request with `Upgrade: websocket` turns into a full-duplex TCP stream that stays open for minutes or hours. WSGI cannot host that — a WSGI callable must return a response and finish. **ASGI** can, because an ASGI application is an async callable that receives and sends events for as long as the connection lives. **Django Channels** is the official project that builds on ASGI to give you WebSocket (and other protocol) handling in Django style: routing like URLconf, **consumers** like views, Django's auth and sessions on the socket, and a **channel layer** — a cross-process message bus, usually Redis — so that a message produced anywhere can reach a socket held by any server process.\n\nThat last piece is the one people miss. With four Daphne/uvicorn processes behind a load balancer, user A's socket lives in process 1 and user B's in process 3. When A sends a chat message, process 1 must somehow deliver it to B's socket in process 3. The channel layer solves this with **groups**: every consumer for room \"lobby\" joins group `chat_lobby`; `group_send(\"chat_lobby\", event)` publishes to Redis; each process subscribed for members of that group receives the event and calls the matching handler on its local consumers, which write to their sockets.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **ASGI** — the async successor to WSGI: an app is `async def app(scope, receive, send)`. *Why it matters:* a connection can stay open and receive/send many events, which WebSockets need.\n- **Scope** — the connection's metadata dict: `type` (`\"http\"`/`\"websocket\"`), `path`, `headers`, `query_string`, and (after middleware) `user`, `session`, `url_route`. *Why:* the WebSocket equivalent of `request`, fixed for the lifetime of the connection.\n- **ASGI server** — **Daphne** (from the Channels project), **uvicorn**, or **hypercorn**. *Why:* Gunicorn sync workers cannot hold sockets; you need an async server.\n- **`ProtocolTypeRouter`** — top-level router that dispatches by `scope[\"type\"]`: `\"http\"` to Django's ASGI app, `\"websocket\"` to your WebSocket stack. *Why:* one ASGI entrypoint serves both.\n- **`URLRouter`** — path-based routing for WebSocket URLs, using `path()`/`re_path()`. *Why:* familiar URLconf patterns, captured kwargs land in `scope[\"url_route\"][\"kwargs\"]`.\n- **`AuthMiddlewareStack`** — `CookieMiddleware` + `SessionMiddleware` + `AuthMiddleware`, populating `scope[\"user\"]` lazily from the session cookie. *Why:* the same login works for HTTP and sockets.\n- **`AllowedHostsOriginValidator`** — rejects WebSocket handshakes whose `Origin` is not in `ALLOWED_HOSTS`. *Why:* browsers send cookies on cross-site WebSocket connects; without it you are open to cross-site WebSocket hijacking.\n- **Consumer** — a class handling one connection: `WebsocketConsumer` (sync), `AsyncWebsocketConsumer`, `AsyncJsonWebsocketConsumer`. *Why:* one instance per connection, so instance attributes are per-socket state.\n- **Channel** — a named mailbox unique to one consumer instance (`self.channel_name`). *Why:* the address the layer uses to reach that specific socket.\n- **Group** — a named set of channels (`group_add`, `group_discard`, `group_send`). *Why:* broadcast without knowing which process holds which socket.\n- **Channel layer** — the cross-process transport: `channels_redis.core.RedisChannelLayer` (production), `RedisPubSubChannelLayer`, `InMemoryChannelLayer` (tests/dev only). *Why:* without it, groups only work inside one process.\n- **Event `type`** — a message sent through the layer must have `\"type\": \"chat.message\"`; Channels calls the consumer method `chat_message` (dots → underscores). *Why:* this is how one event fans into handler methods.\n- **`database_sync_to_async`** — Channels' wrapper that runs sync ORM code in a thread and cleans up old DB connections. *Why:* calling the ORM directly from an async consumer raises `SynchronousOnlyOperation`.\n- **SSE (Server-Sent Events)** — one-way server→browser stream over plain HTTP (`text/event-stream`). *Why:* often all you need for notifications, with simpler infra than WebSockets.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### What happens on a WebSocket connect\n\n1. The browser sends `GET /ws/chat/lobby/` with `Upgrade: websocket`, `Sec-WebSocket-Key`, `Origin` and the session cookie.\n2. The ASGI server (Daphne/uvicorn) builds a scope with `type: \"websocket\"` and calls your `application(scope, receive, send)`.\n3. `ProtocolTypeRouter` sees `\"websocket\"` and passes the call to `AllowedHostsOriginValidator`, which checks the `Origin` header against `ALLOWED_HOSTS` and closes the handshake if it does not match.\n4. `AuthMiddlewareStack` parses cookies, loads the session from the session engine, and puts a lazy `scope[\"user\"]` in place.\n5. `URLRouter` matches the path, adds `url_route` kwargs, and instantiates the consumer via the ASGI app returned by `ChatConsumer.as_asgi()` — **one consumer instance per connection**.\n6. The server delivers a `websocket.connect` event; the consumer's `connect()` runs. It must call `self.accept()`; calling `self.close()` *before* accepting rejects the handshake (Daphne answers HTTP 403 and the browser sees a failed connection with close code 1006, without any custom code).\n7. On accept, the consumer registers its unique `channel_name` with the channel layer and typically calls `group_add`.\n8. From then on, `websocket.receive` events call `receive(text_data=...)`, layer events call the method named by `type`, and `websocket.disconnect` calls `disconnect(code)`.\n\nInternally, a consumer runs **two event sources** concurrently: the socket (`receive` from the server) and its channel-layer mailbox. Channels' base `AsyncConsumer.__call__` subscribes the instance's channel to the layer and uses an `await_many_dispatch` loop that waits on both and dispatches each message to a handler chosen from `message[\"type\"]` by `get_handler_name` (replace `.` with `_`, reject names starting with `_`). That is why a layer event with `\"type\": \"chat.message\"` ends up calling `self.chat_message(event)`.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj32a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">One ASGI entrypoint, two protocol stacks</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"170\" width=\"120\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Browser</text>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"213\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">HTTP + WS</text>\n  <rect x=\"170\" y=\"160\" width=\"130\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Daphne / uvicorn</text>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"203\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">builds scope</text>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"221\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">calls application</text>\n  <rect x=\"330\" y=\"160\" width=\"140\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"400\" y=\"185\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ProtocolTypeRouter</text>\n  <text x=\"400\" y=\"205\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">scope[\"type\"]</text>\n  <text x=\"400\" y=\"221\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">http | websocket</text>\n  <rect x=\"510\" y=\"50\" width=\"350\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"74\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">\"http\": get_asgi_application()</text>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">normal Django: middleware, URLconf, views</text>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">(created before importing models)</text>\n  <rect x=\"510\" y=\"150\" width=\"350\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"172\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">AllowedHostsOriginValidator: Origin in ALLOWED_HOSTS?</text>\n  <rect x=\"510\" y=\"194\" width=\"350\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"216\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">AuthMiddlewareStack: cookie, session, scope[\"user\"]</text>\n  <rect x=\"510\" y=\"238\" width=\"350\" height=\"34\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"260\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">URLRouter: ws/chat/&lt;room&gt;/ to url_route kwargs</text>\n  <rect x=\"510\" y=\"282\" width=\"350\" height=\"60\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"304\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ChatConsumer.as_asgi()</text>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"322\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">new instance per connection: connect, receive,</text>\n  <text x=\"685\" y=\"336\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">chat_message (layer events), disconnect</text>\n  <path d=\"M140,200 L168,200\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M300,200 L328,200\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M470,185 L508,90\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M470,210 L508,167\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M685,184 L685,192\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M685,228 L685,236\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M685,272 L685,280\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"375\" fill=\"#334155\">Each layer can close the connection before the consumer exists: bad Origin, no route, or a rejected</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"391\" fill=\"#334155\">connect() all appear to the browser as a failed handshake.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### The channel layer: what it guarantees and what it does not\n\nThe Redis channel layer implements two operations. `send(channel, message)` pushes to one consumer's mailbox (a Redis list keyed by the channel name, with the process-specific prefix so the owning process reads it). `group_send(group, message)` looks up the group's members (a Redis sorted set of channel names, scored by join time so stale entries expire after `group_expiry`, default one day) and pushes the message to each member's mailbox in one Lua script. Each server process runs a receive loop that pulls messages for the channels it owns and hands them to the right consumer instance.\n\nThe guarantees are deliberately weak. Delivery is **at-most-once**: if a process is down or the consumer disconnected, the message is lost. Messages **expire** (default 60 s) and each channel has a **capacity** (default 100); when a channel is full, `send` raises `ChannelFull`, and `group_send` silently drops for that member. There is **no persistence and no replay**. That is the right design for presence, typing indicators and \"something changed, refresh\" pings; it is the wrong design for anything a user must not miss. The pattern for important events is: **persist to the database first, then push a notification**; on reconnect, the client fetches what it missed via a normal REST endpoint (for example `GET /api/messages?after=<last_id>`).\n\n`RedisPubSubChannelLayer` uses Redis Pub/Sub instead of lists. It is lighter for pure broadcast, but loses even more under disconnects (Pub/Sub has no buffering at all). Both are documented in the channels_redis README. For the underlying Redis primitives, see [Redis · Streams, Pub/Sub & Invalidation](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=21-streams-pubsub-invalidation).\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### Broadcasting across processes\n\nThe diagram shows why the channel layer exists: Alice and Bob are connected to different server processes, and a Celery task with no socket at all also needs to reach Bob.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 420\" width=\"100%\" height=\"420\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj32b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj32c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">group_send: one event, every member, any process</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"60\" width=\"100\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"87\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Alice tab</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"170\" width=\"100\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"197\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Bob tab</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"280\" width=\"100\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"70\" y=\"307\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Carol tab</text>\n  <rect x=\"170\" y=\"45\" width=\"200\" height=\"80\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"67\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ASGI process 1</text>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"87\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">ChatConsumer (Alice)</text>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"105\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">channel specific.p1!a1</text>\n  <rect x=\"170\" y=\"155\" width=\"200\" height=\"180\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"177\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ASGI process 2</text>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"199\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">ChatConsumer (Bob)</text>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">channel specific.p2!b7</text>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"295\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">ChatConsumer (Carol)</text>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"311\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">channel specific.p2!c3</text>\n  <rect x=\"460\" y=\"120\" width=\"210\" height=\"150\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"565\" y=\"143\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Redis channel layer</text>\n  <text x=\"565\" y=\"166\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">group chat_lobby =</text>\n  <text x=\"565\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">{p1!a1, p2!b7, p2!c3}</text>\n  <text x=\"565\" y=\"206\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">per-channel lists</text>\n  <text x=\"565\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">expiry 60 s, capacity 100</text>\n  <text x=\"565\" y=\"250\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#92400e\" font-weight=\"bold\">at-most-once, no replay</text>\n  <rect x=\"720\" y=\"300\" width=\"140\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"790\" y=\"324\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Celery task / view</text>\n  <text x=\"790\" y=\"342\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">async_to_sync(</text>\n  <text x=\"790\" y=\"358\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">layer.group_send)</text>\n  <path d=\"M120,80 L168,80\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">\"hi\"</text>\n  <path d=\"M370,85 L458,150\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"430\" y=\"105\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"10\">1. group_send</text>\n  <path d=\"M460,185 L372,205\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M460,235 L372,295\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M460,140 L372,110\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"395\" y=\"262\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"10\">2. fan out</text>\n  <path d=\"M170,200 L122,195\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M170,302 L122,302\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">3. chat_message</text>\n  <text x=\"140\" y=\"252\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">sends to socket</text>\n  <path d=\"M720,320 L672,250\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M370,330 L458,262\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj32c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"380\" y=\"370\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">Bob offline? message is simply dropped:</text>\n  <text x=\"380\" y=\"386\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">persist first, push second, refetch on reconnect</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Deployment topology\n\n```text\n              ┌────────── Nginx / ALB (TLS, Upgrade headers, long read timeout) ─────────┐\n              │                                                                          │\n     /api/*, /admin/*  ──►  Gunicorn (WSGI, sync workers)   ── PostgreSQL ──┐            │\n     /ws/*             ──►  Daphne or uvicorn (ASGI)  ◄──►  Redis channel layer ◄── Celery workers\n              │                                                                          │\n              └──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┘\n```\n\nMany teams split WSGI (regular traffic) and ASGI (only `/ws/`) so that a pile of open sockets cannot affect request latency, and so the ASGI fleet can scale on connection count while the WSGI fleet scales on CPU. A single ASGI fleet serving both is also fine if your views are well behaved.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: a chat room\n\n```bash\npip install \"django~=5.2\" \"channels~=4.1\" \"daphne~=4.1\" \"channels-redis~=4.2\"\n```\n\n```python\n# settings.py\nINSTALLED_APPS = [\n    \"daphne\",                 # must be first: makes runserver serve ASGI\n    \"django.contrib.admin\",\n    \"django.contrib.auth\",\n    \"django.contrib.contenttypes\",\n    \"django.contrib.sessions\",\n    \"django.contrib.messages\",\n    \"django.contrib.staticfiles\",\n    \"channels\",\n    \"chat\",\n]\n\nASGI_APPLICATION = \"proj.asgi.application\"\n\nCHANNEL_LAYERS = {\n    \"default\": {\n        \"BACKEND\": \"channels_redis.core.RedisChannelLayer\",\n        \"CONFIG\": {\"hosts\": [(\"127.0.0.1\", 6379)]},\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```python\n# proj/asgi.py\nimport os\nfrom django.core.asgi import get_asgi_application\n\nos.environ.setdefault(\"DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE\", \"proj.settings\")\n# Initialise Django (apps registry) BEFORE importing anything that imports models.\ndjango_asgi_app = get_asgi_application()\n\nfrom channels.auth import AuthMiddlewareStack                     # noqa: E402\nfrom channels.routing import ProtocolTypeRouter, URLRouter        # noqa: E402\nfrom channels.security.websocket import AllowedHostsOriginValidator  # noqa: E402\nimport chat.routing                                               # noqa: E402\n\napplication = ProtocolTypeRouter({\n    \"http\": django_asgi_app,\n    \"websocket\": AllowedHostsOriginValidator(\n        AuthMiddlewareStack(URLRouter(chat.routing.websocket_urlpatterns))\n    ),\n})\n```\n\n```python\n# chat/routing.py\nfrom django.urls import path\nfrom . import consumers\n\nwebsocket_urlpatterns = [\n    path(\"ws/chat/<slug:room>/\", consumers.ChatConsumer.as_asgi()),\n]\n```\n\n```python\n# chat/consumers.py\nimport json\nfrom channels.generic.websocket import AsyncWebsocketConsumer\n\nclass ChatConsumer(AsyncWebsocketConsumer):\n    async def connect(self):\n        self.room = self.scope[\"url_route\"][\"kwargs\"][\"room\"]\n        self.group = f\"chat_{self.room}\"          # ASCII letters, digits, - _ . only\n        await self.channel_layer.group_add(self.group, self.channel_name)\n        await self.accept()\n\n    async def disconnect(self, code):\n        await self.channel_layer.group_discard(self.group, self.channel_name)\n\n    async def receive(self, text_data=None, bytes_data=None):\n        payload = json.loads(text_data)\n        await self.channel_layer.group_send(\n            self.group,\n            {\"type\": \"chat.message\", \"text\": payload[\"text\"]},  # -> chat_message()\n        )\n\n    async def chat_message(self, event):\n        await self.send(text_data=json.dumps({\"text\": event[\"text\"]}))\n```\n\n```html\n<script>\n  const ws = new WebSocket(`${location.protocol === \"https:\" ? \"wss\" : \"ws\"}://${location.host}/ws/chat/lobby/`);\n  ws.onmessage = (e) => console.log(JSON.parse(e.data).text);\n  ws.onopen = () => ws.send(JSON.stringify({text: \"hello\"}));\n</script>\n```\n\n```bash\npython manage.py runserver          # with \"daphne\" in INSTALLED_APPS this is an ASGI server\n```\n\n```text\nStarting ASGI/Daphne version 4.1.2 development server at http://127.0.0.1:8000/\nWebSocket HANDSHAKING /ws/chat/lobby/ [127.0.0.1:52144]\nWebSocket CONNECT /ws/chat/lobby/ [127.0.0.1:52144]\n```\n\n### Real-world example: authenticated order-status notifications\n\nRequirements: only the order's owner may subscribe; status changes come from a Celery task; messages must not be lost across reconnects; the ORM is used from the consumer.\n\n```python\n# orders/consumers.py\nfrom channels.db import database_sync_to_async\nfrom channels.generic.websocket import AsyncJsonWebsocketConsumer\nfrom .models import Order\n\nclass OrderStatusConsumer(AsyncJsonWebsocketConsumer):\n    async def connect(self):\n        user = self.scope[\"user\"]\n        self.order_id = int(self.scope[\"url_route\"][\"kwargs\"][\"order_id\"])\n        if not user.is_authenticated or not await self.owns_order(user.pk, self.order_id):\n            # Accept, then close with an app-defined code (4000-4999) so the client\n            # can tell \"not allowed\" (do not retry) from a network drop (retry).\n            await self.accept()\n            await self.close(code=4403 if user.is_authenticated else 4401)\n            return\n        self.group = f\"order_{self.order_id}\"\n        await self.channel_layer.group_add(self.group, self.channel_name)\n        await self.accept()\n        # Send current state immediately: covers anything missed while disconnected.\n        await self.send_json({\"type\": \"snapshot\", **await self.current_state()})\n\n    async def disconnect(self, code):\n        if hasattr(self, \"group\"):\n            await self.channel_layer.group_discard(self.group, self.channel_name)\n\n    async def receive_json(self, content, **kwargs):\n        if content.get(\"action\") == \"ping\":\n            await self.send_json({\"type\": \"pong\"})\n\n    async def order_status(self, event):              # type \"order.status\"\n        await self.send_json({\"type\": \"status\", \"status\": event[\"status\"],\n                              \"version\": event[\"version\"]})\n\n    @database_sync_to_async\n    def owns_order(self, user_id: int, order_id: int) -> bool:\n        return Order.objects.filter(pk=order_id, customer_id=user_id).exists()\n\n    @database_sync_to_async\n    def current_state(self) -> dict:\n        o = Order.objects.only(\"status\", \"version\").get(pk=self.order_id)\n        return {\"status\": o.status, \"version\": o.version}\n```\n\n```python\n# orders/routing.py\nfrom django.urls import path\nfrom .consumers import OrderStatusConsumer\n\nwebsocket_urlpatterns = [path(\"ws/orders/<int:order_id>/\", OrderStatusConsumer.as_asgi())]\n```\n\nThe producer is synchronous code (a Celery task), so it bridges into the async layer API with `async_to_sync`:\n\n```python\n# orders/tasks.py\nfrom functools import partial\nfrom asgiref.sync import async_to_sync\nfrom celery import shared_task\nfrom channels.layers import get_channel_layer\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.db.models import F\nfrom .models import Order\n\ndef _notify(order_id: int, status: str, version: int) -> None:\n    async_to_sync(get_channel_layer().group_send)(\n        f\"order_{order_id}\",\n        {\"type\": \"order.status\", \"status\": status, \"version\": version},\n    )\n\n@shared_task(acks_late=True)\ndef mark_shipped(order_id: int) -> None:\n    with transaction.atomic():\n        updated = Order.objects.filter(pk=order_id).exclude(status=\"shipped\").update(\n            status=\"shipped\", version=F(\"version\") + 1\n        )\n        if not updated:\n            return                                    # idempotent: already shipped\n        version = Order.objects.values_list(\"version\", flat=True).get(pk=order_id)\n        # Push only after commit, so a client that refetches sees the new state.\n        transaction.on_commit(partial(_notify, order_id, \"shipped\", version))\n```\n\nThe client keeps the highest `version` it has seen, ignores older events, and reconnects with exponential backoff; on reconnect, the server's `snapshot` message resynchronises it. That turns the channel layer's at-most-once delivery into an eventually consistent UI.\n\n### Token authentication for non-browser clients\n\nBrowsers send cookies on WebSocket handshakes, but mobile apps usually hold a JWT. Browsers cannot set custom headers on `new WebSocket()`, so tokens travel in the query string (logged by proxies — keep them short-lived) or in the `Sec-WebSocket-Protocol` header. A minimal middleware:\n\n```python\n# api/ws_auth.py\nfrom urllib.parse import parse_qs\nfrom channels.db import database_sync_to_async\nfrom channels.middleware import BaseMiddleware\nfrom django.contrib.auth.models import AnonymousUser\nfrom rest_framework_simplejwt.authentication import JWTAuthentication\n\n@database_sync_to_async\ndef user_from_token(raw: str):\n    auth = JWTAuthentication()\n    try:\n        return auth.get_user(auth.get_validated_token(raw))\n    except Exception:\n        return AnonymousUser()\n\nclass JWTAuthMiddleware(BaseMiddleware):\n    async def __call__(self, scope, receive, send):\n        token = parse_qs(scope[\"query_string\"].decode()).get(\"token\", [None])[0]\n        scope[\"user\"] = await user_from_token(token) if token else AnonymousUser()\n        return await super().__call__(scope, receive, send)\n```\n\n### Server-Sent Events: the simpler alternative\n\nFor one-way updates you do not need Channels at all. Under ASGI (Django 4.2+), `StreamingHttpResponse` accepts an async iterator:\n\n```python\n# notifications/views.py\nimport asyncio, json\nfrom django.http import StreamingHttpResponse\n\nasync def events(request):\n    async def stream():\n        last = 0\n        while True:\n            # In production read from Redis pub/sub instead of polling.\n            async for n in Notification.objects.filter(user_id=request.user.pk, pk__gt=last).order_by(\"pk\"):\n                last = n.pk\n                yield f\"id: {n.pk}\\ndata: {json.dumps({'text': n.text})}\\n\\n\"\n            await asyncio.sleep(2)\n    resp = StreamingHttpResponse(stream(), content_type=\"text/event-stream\")\n    resp[\"Cache-Control\"] = \"no-cache\"\n    resp[\"X-Accel-Buffering\"] = \"no\"      # tell Nginx not to buffer\n    return resp\n```\n\nThe browser's `EventSource` reconnects automatically and sends `Last-Event-ID`, which gives you resume-from-offset for free. See [REST API · Streaming, SSE & WebSockets](../rest-api/topic.html?p=30-streaming-sse-websockets).\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Approach | Direction | Infra | Strengths | Weaknesses |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Short polling | client → server | none | Trivial, cacheable, works everywhere | Wasteful, latency = interval |\n| Long polling | client → server | async server ideal | Near real-time, plain HTTP | Reconnect per message, holds connections |\n| SSE | server → client | ASGI server | Auto-reconnect, `Last-Event-ID` resume, plain HTTP | One-way; browser per-domain connection limits on HTTP/1.1 |\n| WebSockets + Channels | both | ASGI + Redis layer | Full duplex, low latency, groups | Stateful connections, auth/scaling complexity, at-most-once layer |\n| Managed service (Pusher, Ably, AWS API Gateway WS) | both | external | No socket fleet to run | Cost, vendor lock-in, data leaves your infra |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Chat, collaborative editing, multiplayer/live dashboards where the client also sends frequently.\n- Push notifications to logged-in users where latency matters (sub-second).\n- Progress of background jobs (Celery task → group → browser).\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- One-way feeds: SSE is simpler and resumes natively.\n- Low-frequency updates (once a minute): polling with HTTP caching is cheaper to run.\n- Guaranteed delivery of business events: persist and use a real queue; the channel layer drops.\n- When you cannot run an ASGI fleet and Redis: use a managed WebSocket service.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**Calling the ORM directly from an async consumer.** `Order.objects.get(...)` inside `async def connect` raises `SynchronousOnlyOperation`. Use `database_sync_to_async` (or the async ORM methods like `aget()` for simple reads; Channels' wrapper additionally closes stale connections).\n\n**Importing models before `get_asgi_application()`.** `asgi.py` that imports `chat.routing` (which imports consumers, which import models) at the top raises `AppRegistryNotReady`. Call `get_asgi_application()` first.\n\n**Forgetting `AllowedHostsOriginValidator`.** The browser attaches the session cookie to a WebSocket opened by *any* site; without an Origin check, evil.example can open a socket as your user (cross-site WebSocket hijacking). CSRF middleware does not run on WebSockets.\n\n**Using `InMemoryChannelLayer` in production.** Groups work only within one process; with two replicas, half your users never see messages. It is for tests.\n\n**Treating the layer as a queue.** Sending important data only via `group_send` loses it whenever a client is disconnected or a channel is full. Persist first, push second, resync on connect.\n\n**Blocking the event loop.** `time.sleep`, `requests.get` or a heavy JSON parse inside an async consumer stalls every connection in that process. Use async libraries (`httpx.AsyncClient`) or offload to a thread/Celery.\n\n**Group names with invalid characters.** `f\"chat_{user.email}\"` contains `@`, which is invalid (group names must be ASCII alphanumerics, hyphens, underscores or periods, under 100 characters). Use IDs.\n\n**Not discarding groups on disconnect.** Stale memberships linger until `group_expiry`, and `group_send` keeps writing to dead channels until they expire, wasting Redis memory.\n\n**Best practices**\n\n- Authorise in `connect()`. Rejecting before `accept()` gives the client only a generic failure; accept-then-close with an application code (4000–4999) lets the client distinguish \"forbidden, stop retrying\" from a network drop.\n- Keep consumers thin: they translate between socket and domain services.\n- Version or sequence-number events so clients can drop stale ones and detect gaps.\n- Send an application-level heartbeat; idle-timeout proxies silently kill quiet sockets.\n- Push from `transaction.on_commit` so clients that refetch see committed state.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n> **Production story:** Every deploy caused a five-minute Redis CPU spike and a flood of 502s. Root cause: 40,000 sockets disconnected at once, every client reconnected instantly, and each `connect()` ran two database queries and a `group_add`. Fix: exponential backoff with jitter on the client, staggered rollout of ASGI pods, a connection-rate limit at the load balancer, and caching the ownership check.\n\n**Sockets dropping after 60 seconds.** Symptom: clients disconnect at a suspiciously round interval. Root cause: Nginx `proxy_read_timeout` defaults to 60 s, and load balancers have idle timeouts. Fix: raise the timeouts for `/ws/` and send pings more often than the shortest idle timeout.\n\n```text\nlocation /ws/ {\n    proxy_pass http://asgi_upstream;\n    proxy_http_version 1.1;\n    proxy_set_header Upgrade $http_upgrade;\n    proxy_set_header Connection \"upgrade\";\n    proxy_set_header Host $host;\n    proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;\n    proxy_read_timeout 3600s;\n}\n```\n\n**Handshake works locally, fails behind the proxy.** Missing `Upgrade`/`Connection` headers or HTTP/1.0 proxying. In Kubernetes, the Ingress controller needs WebSocket support configured — see [Kubernetes · Ingress](../kubernetes/topic.html?p=11-ingress).\n\n**`ChannelFull` exceptions or silently missing messages.** A slow client's mailbox fills (capacity 100 by default). Raise `capacity` per channel pattern in `CONFIG`, reduce message rate (coalesce updates), or drop slow consumers.\n\n**Database connection exhaustion.** Each `database_sync_to_async` call runs in a thread pool thread with its own connection. Thousands of sockets doing frequent queries create many connections; keep socket-path queries rare and cached, and budget connections ([Ch 34 · Scaling Django: Connections, Replicas & Multiple Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases)).\n\n**Graceful deploys.** A rolling restart closes every socket on the old pod. Clients must reconnect with jittered backoff, and the server must send a snapshot on connect. Use `preStop` hooks and generous termination grace periods so pods drain.\n\n**Debugging**\n\n- Browser DevTools → Network → WS shows frames, close codes and handshake headers.\n- `websocat ws://localhost:8000/ws/chat/lobby/` from a terminal.\n- Log `connect`/`disconnect` with user id, path and close code; count open connections as a gauge metric.\n- `redis-cli MONITOR` (development only) to watch `group_send` traffic; `redis-cli --bigkeys` to spot huge groups.\n- Test with `channels.testing.WebsocketCommunicator` ([Ch 33 · Testing Django Applications](topic.html?p=33-testing)).\n\n**Scaling.** An async process can hold thousands of mostly idle sockets; the limits are memory per connection, file descriptors (`ulimit -n`), and how much work each message triggers. Scale ASGI pods on connection count. The Redis layer becomes the bottleneck for very large groups (`group_send` to 100,000 members is 100,000 list pushes); shard with multiple Redis hosts in `CONFIG` (channels_redis consistently hashes channels across them), avoid giant groups (fan out per region or per shard), or move to `RedisPubSubChannelLayer` for broadcast-heavy workloads. Beyond that, dedicated real-time gateways (or a managed service) take over. Background reading: [System Design · Real-Time Protocols](../system-design/topic.html?p=08-realtime-protocols).\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: Why can't you serve WebSockets with Gunicorn's default sync workers?**\nA: WSGI is a synchronous call-and-return interface: the application receives `environ`, returns an iterable response, and the request is over. A WebSocket is a long-lived, bidirectional connection that must receive and send many messages over minutes or hours. A sync worker would be pinned for the whole connection lifetime, so a handful of sockets would exhaust the server. ASGI defines an async `(scope, receive, send)` interface that supports long-lived connections, and an ASGI server (Daphne, uvicorn) multiplexes thousands of them on an event loop.\n\n**Q: What is a channel layer and why do you need one?**\nA: It is a cross-process messaging system, normally `channels_redis.core.RedisChannelLayer`, that lets any code send a message to a specific consumer's channel or to a named group. You need it because sockets are spread across many server processes and machines; a consumer in process 1 cannot reach a socket held in process 3 without a shared bus. It is also how code without a socket — a view, a Celery task — pushes to users, via `async_to_sync(channel_layer.group_send)`. Without a layer (or with the in-memory one) groups only work inside one process.\n\n**Q: How do groups and the `type` key work?**\nA: A group is a named set of channel names; consumers join with `group_add(group, self.channel_name)` and leave with `group_discard`. `group_send(group, {\"type\": \"chat.message\", ...})` delivers the dict to every member's channel. Each receiving consumer dispatches on `type`, replacing dots with underscores, so `chat.message` calls `self.chat_message(event)`. The handler typically forwards the data to its socket with `send()` or `send_json()`.\n\n**Q: How do you authenticate WebSocket connections in Channels?**\nA: For browsers, wrap the router in `AuthMiddlewareStack`, which reads the session cookie sent on the handshake and populates `scope[\"user\"]` exactly like `request.user`. Check `self.scope[\"user\"].is_authenticated` and authorisation in `connect()` and close if it fails. Wrap everything in `AllowedHostsOriginValidator` because CSRF protection does not apply to WebSockets and browsers send cookies cross-site. For mobile/API clients, write a middleware that validates a short-lived token from the query string or subprotocol header, since browsers cannot set custom headers on WebSocket handshakes.\n\n**Q: What happens if you call the Django ORM inside an `AsyncWebsocketConsumer`?**\nA: Django detects the running event loop and raises `SynchronousOnlyOperation`, because the ORM is synchronous and would block the loop for every connection in the process. Wrap ORM code in `channels.db.database_sync_to_async`, which runs it in a worker thread and also closes stale database connections around the call. For simple reads the async ORM API (`aget`, `afirst`, `async for`) also works, but it still runs the query in a thread under the hood. Alternatively use the sync `WebsocketConsumer`, which Channels runs in a thread for you.\n\n**Q: What delivery guarantees does the Redis channel layer provide?**\nA: At-most-once with no persistence. Messages expire (default 60 seconds), channels have a capacity (default 100) after which sends fail or are dropped, and a disconnected consumer simply never receives what was sent while it was away. There is no replay. So you never use the layer as the source of truth: write the event to the database, then push a notification, and have clients fetch missed data on reconnect.\n\n**Q: When would you choose Server-Sent Events over WebSockets?**\nA: When updates flow only from server to client — notifications, live scores, progress bars, log tails. SSE is plain HTTP, works through most proxies and caches without special config, and `EventSource` reconnects automatically and sends `Last-Event-ID` so you can resume. It needs an ASGI server (or it pins sync workers) and does not support client-to-server messages, which you send with normal POSTs. Pick WebSockets when the client sends frequent messages too, like chat or collaborative editing.\n\n**Q: Why does `asgi.py` call `get_asgi_application()` before importing your routing module?**\nA: `get_asgi_application()` calls `django.setup()`, which configures settings and populates the app registry. Your routing imports consumers, which import models, and importing models before the registry is ready raises `AppRegistryNotReady`. Creating the Django ASGI app first guarantees the registry is loaded. That is why the documented `asgi.py` has imports after that line, usually with `noqa` comments.\n\n**Q: How would you push the progress of a Celery task to the user's browser?**\nA: The consumer for the task page joins a group like `task_<id>` on connect, after verifying the user owns the task. The Celery task, which is synchronous, calls `async_to_sync(get_channel_layer().group_send)(\"task_<id>\", {\"type\": \"task.progress\", \"pct\": 40})` at checkpoints. The consumer's `task_progress` method forwards it to the socket. Also store progress in the DB or cache so a client that connects late gets the current value immediately in `connect()`.\n\n**Q: Your chat app works with one server but users miss messages after you scale to four pods. What is wrong? (Senior)**\nA: The most likely cause is the in-memory channel layer (or no layer), so groups exist per process and `group_send` only reaches consumers in the sender's pod. Configure `RedisChannelLayer` pointing all pods to the same Redis. If it is already Redis, check for different `prefix` or Redis DB settings between deployments, `ChannelFull` drops for slow clients, and dropped connections due to proxy idle timeouts. Even when fixed, design for loss: persist messages and have the client fetch messages after its last-seen ID on reconnect.\n\n**Q: How would you design real-time notifications for a million connected users? (Senior)**\nA: Split the problem. A stateless ASGI connection tier holds sockets (each process tens of thousands of mostly idle connections), scaled on connection count with high file descriptor limits; regular HTTP stays on the WSGI fleet. Use per-user groups (`user_<id>`) rather than giant broadcast groups, shard the channel layer across several Redis instances, and coalesce high-frequency updates. Notifications are persisted first, fanned out by background workers, and pushed best-effort; clients resync via REST on reconnect with a cursor. Plan reconnect storms during deploys with jittered backoff and LB rate limits — or recognise when a managed real-time service is cheaper than running this.\n\n**Q: How do you secure a Channels deployment against cross-site WebSocket hijacking and abuse? (Senior)**\nA: Wrap the WebSocket stack in `AllowedHostsOriginValidator` (or `OriginValidator` with an explicit list) so foreign origins cannot open authenticated sockets with the user's cookies. Authorise in `connect()` per resource, not just \"is logged in\", and re-check permissions on sensitive actions because the scope is fixed for the connection's life (a revoked user stays connected until you close them — keep a per-user group and send a \"force logout\" event). Validate and size-limit every incoming message, rate-limit messages per connection, and cap connections per user at the edge. Use `wss://` only, and never put long-lived tokens in query strings that end up in access logs.\n\n**Q: How do you test a consumer?**\nA: Use `channels.testing.WebsocketCommunicator(application, \"/ws/chat/lobby/\")` inside an async test (`pytest-asyncio` or Django's async test support): `connected, _ = await communicator.connect()`, `await communicator.send_json_to({...})`, `await communicator.receive_json_from()`, then `await communicator.disconnect()`. Configure `InMemoryChannelLayer` in test settings. Wrap the application with the same middleware as production when testing auth, or set `communicator.scope[\"user\"]` directly. Database access from these tests needs `transaction=True` style tests because the consumer's ORM calls run in other threads.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Piece | Name | Remember |\n|---|---|---|\n| Entrypoint | `ProtocolTypeRouter` in `asgi.py` | `get_asgi_application()` first |\n| Routing | `URLRouter([...path(..., Consumer.as_asgi())])` | kwargs in `scope[\"url_route\"][\"kwargs\"]` |\n| Auth | `AuthMiddlewareStack` | `scope[\"user\"]`, session cookie |\n| Origin check | `AllowedHostsOriginValidator` | CSRF does not cover WebSockets |\n| Consumer | `AsyncWebsocketConsumer` / `AsyncJsonWebsocketConsumer` | one instance per connection |\n| Layer | `channels_redis.core.RedisChannelLayer` | at-most-once, expiry, capacity |\n| Broadcast | `group_add` / `group_send` / `group_discard` | `type` \"a.b\" calls `a_b()` |\n| From sync code | `async_to_sync(layer.group_send)` | Celery tasks, views, signals |\n| ORM | `database_sync_to_async` | else `SynchronousOnlyOperation` |\n| Server | Daphne / uvicorn | `\"daphne\"` first in INSTALLED_APPS for runserver |\n\n- WebSockets need ASGI; WSGI workers would be pinned.\n- The channel layer connects processes; in-memory layer = single process only.\n- Persist first, push second, resync on reconnect.\n- Authorise in `connect()`; close with 4xxx codes.\n- Proxies need `Upgrade`/`Connection` headers and long read timeouts.\n- Never block the event loop in an async consumer.\n- Reconnect storms on deploy: jittered backoff on clients.\n- SSE is the simpler choice for one-way updates.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSetup: `python -m venv .venv && pip install \"django~=5.2\" \"channels~=4.1\" \"daphne~=4.1\" \"channels-redis~=4.2\"`, Redis via `docker run -p 6379:6379 redis:7`.\n\n1. **Chat room.** Build the simple example, open two browser tabs and chat. Then switch to `InMemoryChannelLayer`, run two Daphne processes on different ports, and observe that cross-process messages vanish. Switch back to Redis.\n2. **Auth and Origin.** Require login in `connect()`. From a page served on another port, try to open the socket and confirm `AllowedHostsOriginValidator` rejects it.\n3. **Celery to browser.** Write a task that sleeps 10 times for 1 s and pushes progress to `task_<id>`; render a progress bar.\n4. **Lossy layer.** Disconnect a client, send five messages, reconnect. Then add persistence and a \"since last id\" snapshot on connect so nothing is missed.\n5. **SSE.** Implement the same notifications as an async `StreamingHttpResponse` endpoint and compare code size and proxy configuration.\n\n**Mini project:** a live order tracker. REST endpoint to change status (staff), Celery task that updates status and pushes on commit, `OrderStatusConsumer` with ownership checks, versioned events and snapshot-on-connect, Nginx config with WebSocket headers, and a `WebsocketCommunicator` test suite.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n- [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi) — ASGI, `sync_to_async`, async ORM.\n- [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks) — tasks that push to groups.\n- [Ch 28 · Security: CSRF, XSS, SQL Injection & Hardening](topic.html?p=28-security) — Origin checks and CSRF.\n- [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production) — running an ASGI fleet.\n- [REST API · Streaming, SSE & WebSockets](../rest-api/topic.html?p=30-streaming-sse-websockets), [System Design · Real-Time Protocols](../system-design/topic.html?p=08-realtime-protocols), [Redis · Streams, Pub/Sub & Invalidation](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=21-streams-pubsub-invalidation).\n\n- **Channels documentation** — Channels · *Beginner* · the official tutorial (chat app) and topic guides on consumers, routing and layers. <https://channels.readthedocs.io/>\n- **channels_redis** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · layer configuration: capacity, expiry, group_expiry, sharding, Pub/Sub layer. <https://github.com/django/channels_redis>\n- **How to deploy with ASGI** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `get_asgi_application`, Daphne, uvicorn and Hypercorn. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/deployment/asgi/>\n- **Asynchronous support** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · async views, `SynchronousOnlyOperation`, sync_to_async semantics. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/async/>\n- **ASGI specification** — asgiref docs · *Advanced* · scope, events and the WebSocket sub-protocol in detail. <https://asgi.readthedocs.io/>\n- **The WebSocket API** — MDN · *Beginner* · browser-side API, close codes and events. <https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/WebSockets_API>\n- **Using server-sent events** — MDN · *Beginner* · `EventSource`, event format and reconnection. <https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Server-sent_events/Using_server-sent_events>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 32.*\n", "33-testing": "# 33 · Testing Django Applications\n\n> **In one line:** Django's test classes buy speed by wrapping every test in a transaction that is rolled back — which is exactly why you must know when that isolation lies to you (on_commit callbacks, real commits, other threads) and why the most valuable assertions in a Django suite are about behaviour and query counts, not implementation details.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency), [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization), [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers), [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks).\n\nEvery Django project eventually hits the same wall: a refactor of a serializer silently adds 200 queries to a list endpoint, a migration breaks a data invariant, a Celery task fires for orders that were rolled back, and nobody notices until production. Tests are the only scalable defence, but naive tests are slow (each one recreating data from scratch), flaky (depending on test order or the clock), or worse, green while the real system is broken — because the test ran inside a transaction that never committed, so the code path that only runs on commit was never exercised.\n\nDjango ships a serious test framework on top of `unittest`. `manage.py test` creates a **separate test database** (your DB name prefixed with `test_`), runs migrations into it, discovers tests, and gives you a hierarchy of test classes with different isolation strategies: `SimpleTestCase` (no database at all), `TestCase` (each test inside a transaction that is rolled back — fast), `TransactionTestCase` (real commits, tables flushed after each test — slow but truthful), and `LiveServerTestCase` (a real HTTP server in a thread for browser tests). Around that you get the test `Client`, `RequestFactory`, `override_settings`, `mail.outbox`, and the assertion every senior Django engineer uses: `assertNumQueries`.\n\nMost teams today run those same classes through **pytest-django**, add **factory_boy** for test data and **DRF's `APIClient`** for API tests. This chapter covers how each piece works internally, how to choose between them, and the testing questions interviewers ask: *TestCase vs TransactionTestCase*, *how do you test on_commit*, *how do you catch N+1 in CI*, *fixtures or factories*, *what do you mock and where*.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Test database** — created at the start of a run (`test_<NAME>`), migrated, destroyed at the end (keep with `--keepdb`). *Why it matters:* tests never touch your dev data, but migrations run every time unless you keep the DB.\n- **`SimpleTestCase`** — no database access allowed (queries raise an error). *Why:* fastest; for forms without models, utilities, URL resolution, templates.\n- **`TestCase`** — wraps the class in an atomic block and each test in a nested atomic (savepoint) that is rolled back. *Why:* isolation without truncating tables — the default for nearly all tests.\n- **`setUpTestData`** — classmethod run once per class inside the class-level transaction. *Why:* create shared data once instead of per test; since Django 3.2 instances assigned on `cls` are deep-copied for each test so in-memory mutations do not leak.\n- **`TransactionTestCase`** — no wrapping transaction; tables are **flushed** (truncated) after each test. *Why:* the only way to test real commit behaviour, `select_for_update` across connections, or code that manages its own transactions.\n- **`LiveServerTestCase` / `StaticLiveServerTestCase`** — starts a live server thread on a free port. *Why:* Selenium/Playwright tests; it is a `TransactionTestCase` because the server thread uses its own connection.\n- **`captureOnCommitCallbacks`** — context manager on `TestCase` that captures (and optionally executes) `transaction.on_commit` callbacks. *Why:* inside `TestCase` the outer transaction never commits, so on_commit callbacks never run otherwise.\n- **`assertNumQueries(n)`** — asserts exactly `n` queries were executed inside the block. *Why:* turns \"no N+1\" into a regression test.\n- **Test `Client`** — simulates requests through the full stack (middleware, URLconf, views, templates) without a network. *Why:* integration-level tests of views; `force_login` skips authentication backends.\n- **`RequestFactory`** — builds `HttpRequest` objects to call a view function directly; no middleware runs. *Why:* unit tests of view logic; you set `request.user` yourself.\n- **`APIClient` / `APITestCase` / `APIRequestFactory`** — DRF equivalents with `force_authenticate`, JSON encoding by `format=\"json\"`. *Why:* API tests without hand-building auth headers.\n- **`override_settings` / `modify_settings`** — decorators/context managers that temporarily change settings and send `setting_changed`. *Why:* never mutate `settings` directly in tests.\n- **Fixtures** — JSON/YAML dumps loaded via `fixtures = [...]`. *Why:* simple but brittle — they bypass `save()` and break silently when models change.\n- **factory_boy** — Python factories (`DjangoModelFactory`, `SubFactory`, `Sequence`, `Faker`, traits). *Why:* data defined in code, refactors with the models, creates only what a test needs.\n- **pytest-django** — pytest plugin: `@pytest.mark.django_db`, fixtures `client`, `rf`, `admin_client`, `settings`, `django_assert_num_queries`, `django_capture_on_commit_callbacks`, `--reuse-db`. *Why:* pytest's fixtures, parametrisation and output with Django's DB isolation.\n- **Mocking** — `unittest.mock.patch(\"module.where.used.name\")`. *Why:* isolate external services; patching the wrong import path is the number one mocking bug.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### How the test runner sets up\n\n`manage.py test` instantiates `DiscoverRunner` (`TEST_RUNNER` setting). `run_tests()` does: `setup_test_environment()` (switches `EMAIL_BACKEND` to the locmem backend, so emails land in `django.core.mail.outbox`, and sets `ALLOWED_HOSTS` to include `testserver`), `build_suite()` (unittest discovery of `test*.py`), `setup_databases()` (for each alias in `DATABASES`, `connection.creation.create_test_db()` creates the test DB and runs `migrate`; with `--keepdb` it reuses it; with `--parallel` it clones it per worker process), runs the suite, then tears down. `DEBUG` is forced to `False` regardless of your settings (use `--debug-mode` to override), so tests see production-like error handling.\n\n### How `TestCase` isolates tests\n\n`TestCase.setUpClass` opens `transaction.atomic()` on every database the class uses (`databases = {\"default\"}` by default), then calls `setUpTestData()` *inside* that atomic. For each test method, `_fixture_setup` opens a *second*, nested atomic — a **savepoint** — runs `setUp`, the test, `tearDown`, and then rolls back to the savepoint. After the last test, the class-level atomic is rolled back too. Nothing is ever committed, so isolation costs one savepoint per test instead of truncating every table.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 380\" width=\"100%\" height=\"380\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj33a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">TestCase: one class transaction, one savepoint per test, nothing commits</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"45\" width=\"840\" height=\"230\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"66\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">setUpClass: BEGIN (class-level atomic)</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"78\" width=\"240\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">setUpTestData()</text>\n  <text x=\"160\" y=\"111\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">cls.author = Author(...) once</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"135\" width=\"250\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"155\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">test_a: SAVEPOINT s1</text>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"175\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">setUp, test body, tearDown</text>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"193\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">writes rows, on_commit(cb)</text>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-weight=\"bold\">ROLLBACK TO s1</text>\n  <text x=\"165\" y=\"233\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\">cb never runs</text>\n  <rect x=\"315\" y=\"135\" width=\"250\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"155\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">test_b: SAVEPOINT s2</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"175\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">sees setUpTestData rows</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"193\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">does NOT see test_a rows</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-weight=\"bold\">ROLLBACK TO s2</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"233\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">cls.author deep-copied</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"135\" width=\"250\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"715\" y=\"155\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">test_c: captureOnCommit</text>\n  <text x=\"715\" y=\"175\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">with self.captureOnCommitCallbacks(</text>\n  <text x=\"715\" y=\"191\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">execute=True) as cbs:</text>\n  <text x=\"715\" y=\"211\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-weight=\"bold\">callbacks run on exit</text>\n  <text x=\"715\" y=\"231\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">assert len(cbs) == 1</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"265\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">tearDownClass: ROLLBACK (class-level atomic)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"295\" width=\"840\" height=\"70\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"317\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">What TestCase cannot show you</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"337\" fill=\"#334155\">Real COMMIT behaviour, a second connection seeing your rows, select_for_update blocking another thread,</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"353\" fill=\"#334155\">code relying on durable commits. For those use TransactionTestCase (truncate after each test).</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nThree consequences follow, and interviewers probe all three:\n\n1. **`transaction.on_commit` callbacks never fire** in a plain `TestCase`, because the outer transaction never commits. Since Django 3.2, `self.captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True)` collects callbacks registered inside the block and runs them when the block exits. Without it, a test of \"order placement sends a receipt task\" passes even if the dispatch is broken — or fails confusingly.\n2. **Everything happens on one connection.** If your code opens a thread (or a live server, or `database_sync_to_async` in some setups) that uses a *different* connection, it cannot see uncommitted test data. Use `TransactionTestCase`.\n3. **Durable blocks do not really commit.** `atomic(durable=True)` normally raises `RuntimeError` when nested; `TestCase` marks its own wrapping blocks so durable blocks are allowed inside them, but they still end in a rollback. Code whose correctness depends on the commit actually happening needs `TransactionTestCase`.\n\n### How `TransactionTestCase` isolates tests\n\nIt does not wrap anything. Your code commits for real. After each test, `_fixture_teardown` calls the `flush` management command (TRUNCATE all tables on PostgreSQL), which is much slower — on the order of tens to hundreds of milliseconds per test depending on schema size, versus well under a millisecond for a savepoint rollback. Data created by migrations (e.g. a data migration that inserts default groups) is wiped by the flush; set `serialized_rollback = True` to have Django serialise the initial DB state and reload it after each flush. Django runs all `TestCase` subclasses before `TransactionTestCase` ones so a flush cannot damage expected state.\n\n### How `assertNumQueries` works\n\n`assertNumQueries(num, func=None, *args, using=\"default\")` returns a `_AssertNumQueriesContext`, a subclass of `CaptureQueriesContext`. On enter, it sets `connection.force_debug_cursor = True` (so queries are recorded even though `DEBUG=False`) and remembers `len(connection.queries)`; on exit it computes the slice of `connection.queries_log` executed inside, and fails with the full list of SQL if the count differs. Savepoint statements are not counted. Because it captures the SQL, its failure message is also your best N+1 debugging tool. pytest-django exposes the same as `django_assert_num_queries` and `django_assert_max_num_queries`.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### Choosing the test class\n\nThe decision tree below is what you should reach for in an interview answer: start from the cheapest class and only pay for real commits when the behaviour under test needs them.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj33b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Which Django test class? Cheapest first</text>\n  <rect x=\"330\" y=\"40\" width=\"220\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"65\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Does the test touch the DB?</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"120\" width=\"220\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"143\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">SimpleTestCase</text>\n  <text x=\"150\" y=\"163\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">forms, utils, URLs, templates</text>\n  <rect x=\"330\" y=\"120\" width=\"220\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"141\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Needs real COMMIT or a</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"158\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">second connection/thread?</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"220\" width=\"300\" height=\"100\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"243\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">TestCase (default choice)</text>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"263\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">savepoint rollback per test, fast</text>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"281\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">setUpTestData for shared rows</text>\n  <text x=\"190\" y=\"299\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">captureOnCommitCallbacks for on_commit</text>\n  <rect x=\"400\" y=\"220\" width=\"200\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"500\" y=\"241\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Needs a real browser /</text>\n  <text x=\"500\" y=\"258\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">HTTP server?</text>\n  <rect x=\"400\" y=\"300\" width=\"200\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"500\" y=\"323\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">TransactionTestCase</text>\n  <text x=\"500\" y=\"343\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">real commits, TRUNCATE after</text>\n  <text x=\"500\" y=\"361\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">locks, durable atomic, threads</text>\n  <rect x=\"650\" y=\"300\" width=\"210\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"323\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">StaticLiveServerTestCase</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"343\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">server thread on a free port</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"361\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">Playwright / Selenium, slowest</text>\n  <path d=\"M380,80 L200,118\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj33b)\"/><text x=\"270\" y=\"94\" fill=\"#334155\">no</text>\n  <path d=\"M440,80 L440,118\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj33b)\"/><text x=\"450\" y=\"102\" fill=\"#334155\">yes</text>\n  <path d=\"M400,170 L250,218\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj33b)\"/><text x=\"300\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#334155\">no</text>\n  <path d=\"M470,170 L490,218\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj33b)\"/><text x=\"490\" y=\"195\" fill=\"#334155\">yes</text>\n  <path d=\"M500,270 L500,298\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj33b)\"/><text x=\"510\" y=\"288\" fill=\"#334155\">no</text>\n  <path d=\"M600,250 L740,298\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj33b)\"/><text x=\"680\" y=\"268\" fill=\"#334155\">yes</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Client vs RequestFactory vs calling the service\n\n```text\n                     middleware  URLconf  auth   templates  speed     use for\nself.client.get()        yes       yes    yes      yes      medium    view integration tests\nAPIClient (DRF)          yes       yes    yes      n/a      medium    API contract tests\nRequestFactory + view    no        no     manual   yes      fast      view logic in isolation\nservice function call    no        no     n/a      n/a      fastest   business rules (most tests)\n```\n\nA healthy suite puts most assertions on **service functions and model methods** (pure business logic, fast), a solid layer of **Client/APIClient** tests per endpoint (permissions, status codes, response shape, query count), and a thin layer of browser tests for critical flows.\n\n### A typical CI pipeline\n\n```text\n1. pip install -r requirements.txt\n2. python manage.py makemigrations --check --dry-run   # fail if models changed without a migration\n3. python manage.py check --deploy --fail-level WARNING  (with prod-like settings)\n4. pytest -n auto --reuse-db --cov=. --cov-report=xml    # or: manage.py test --parallel\n5. coverage threshold gate; upload report\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: models, views and the test client\n\n```python\n# library/tests/test_views.py\nfrom django.test import TestCase\nfrom django.urls import reverse\nfrom library.models import Author, Book\n\nclass BookListViewTests(TestCase):\n    @classmethod\n    def setUpTestData(cls):\n        cls.author = Author.objects.create(name=\"Ursula\")\n        Book.objects.bulk_create(\n            [Book(title=f\"Book {i}\", author=cls.author) for i in range(5)]\n        )\n\n    def test_lists_books(self):\n        response = self.client.get(reverse(\"library:book-list\"))\n        self.assertEqual(response.status_code, 200)\n        self.assertTemplateUsed(response, \"library/book_list.html\")\n        self.assertContains(response, \"Book 3\")\n        self.assertEqual(len(response.context[\"books\"]), 5)\n\n    def test_no_n_plus_one(self):\n        # 1 query for books JOIN author (select_related), regardless of row count.\n        # (Add 1-2 more if the view touches the session/user.)\n        with self.assertNumQueries(1):\n            self.client.get(reverse(\"library:book-list\"))\n\n    def test_create_requires_login(self):\n        response = self.client.post(reverse(\"library:book-create\"), {\"title\": \"X\"})\n        self.assertRedirects(response, \"/accounts/login/?next=/books/new/\")\n```\n\n```bash\npython manage.py test library --keepdb -v 2\npython manage.py test library.tests.test_views.BookListViewTests.test_no_n_plus_one\npython manage.py test --parallel auto --shuffle     # catch order-dependent tests\n```\n\nWhen the query count is wrong, the failure lists the SQL — the fastest way to see an N+1:\n\n```text\nAssertionError: 6 != 1 : 6 queries executed, 1 expected\nCaptured queries were:\n1. SELECT \"library_book\".\"id\", \"library_book\".\"title\", \"library_book\".\"author_id\" FROM \"library_book\"\n2. SELECT ... FROM \"library_author\" WHERE \"library_author\".\"id\" = 1 LIMIT 21\n3. SELECT ... FROM \"library_author\" WHERE \"library_author\".\"id\" = 1 LIMIT 21\n...\n```\n\n### Real-world example: an order API with factories, pytest, mocks and on_commit\n\n```bash\npip install pytest pytest-django factory-boy pytest-xdist coverage\n```\n\n```ini\n# pytest.ini\n[pytest]\nDJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE = proj.settings.test\npython_files = tests.py test_*.py\naddopts = --reuse-db -p no:cacheprovider\n```\n\n```python\n# proj/settings/test.py\nfrom .base import *  # noqa\n\nPASSWORD_HASHERS = [\"django.contrib.auth.hashers.MD5PasswordHasher\"]  # fast, tests only\nCELERY_TASK_ALWAYS_EAGER = False      # we assert dispatch explicitly\nCACHES = {\"default\": {\"BACKEND\": \"django.core.cache.backends.locmem.LocMemCache\"}}\n```\n\n```python\n# orders/tests/factories.py\nimport factory\nfrom django.contrib.auth import get_user_model\nfrom orders.models import Order, OrderItem, Product\n\nclass UserFactory(factory.django.DjangoModelFactory):\n    class Meta:\n        model = get_user_model()\n        skip_postgeneration_save = True\n\n    username = factory.Sequence(lambda n: f\"user{n}\")\n    email = factory.LazyAttribute(lambda o: f\"{o.username}@example.com\")\n    password = factory.django.Password(\"pw\")      # hashed via set_password\n\nclass ProductFactory(factory.django.DjangoModelFactory):\n    class Meta:\n        model = Product\n\n    name = factory.Faker(\"word\")\n    price_cents = 1999\n    stock = 10\n\nclass OrderFactory(factory.django.DjangoModelFactory):\n    class Meta:\n        model = Order\n\n    customer = factory.SubFactory(UserFactory)\n    status = \"pending\"\n\n    class Params:\n        shipped = factory.Trait(status=\"shipped\")\n\nclass OrderItemFactory(factory.django.DjangoModelFactory):\n    class Meta:\n        model = OrderItem\n\n    order = factory.SubFactory(OrderFactory)\n    product = factory.SubFactory(ProductFactory)\n    quantity = 1\n```\n\n```python\n# orders/tests/test_api.py\nfrom unittest import mock\n\nimport pytest\nfrom django.urls import reverse\nfrom rest_framework.test import APIClient\n\nfrom orders.models import Order, Product\nfrom .factories import OrderFactory, OrderItemFactory, ProductFactory, UserFactory\n\npytestmark = pytest.mark.django_db\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef api():\n    return APIClient()\n\n@pytest.fixture\ndef user():\n    return UserFactory()\n\ndef test_list_only_own_orders(api, user):\n    OrderFactory.create_batch(3, customer=user)\n    OrderFactory.create_batch(2)                      # someone else's\n    api.force_authenticate(user)\n    resp = api.get(reverse(\"order-list\"))\n    assert resp.status_code == 200\n    assert resp.json()[\"count\"] == 3\n\ndef test_list_query_count_is_constant(api, user, django_assert_num_queries):\n    for _ in range(10):\n        OrderItemFactory.create_batch(3, order=OrderFactory(customer=user))\n    api.force_authenticate(user)\n    # count + orders page + prefetch items + prefetch products = 4, for any N\n    with django_assert_num_queries(4):\n        api.get(reverse(\"order-list\"))\n\ndef test_anonymous_gets_401(api):\n    assert api.get(reverse(\"order-list\")).status_code == 401\n\n@pytest.mark.parametrize(\"qty,expected\", [(1, 201), (10, 201), (11, 409)])\ndef test_place_order_stock_rules(api, user, qty, expected):\n    product = ProductFactory(stock=10)\n    api.force_authenticate(user)\n    resp = api.post(reverse(\"order-list\"),\n                    {\"items\": [{\"product\": product.pk, \"quantity\": qty}]}, format=\"json\")\n    assert resp.status_code == expected\n\n# Patch where the name is LOOKED UP (orders.services imports the task), not where defined.\n@mock.patch(\"orders.services.send_receipt.delay\")\ndef test_receipt_dispatched_after_commit(delay, api, user, django_capture_on_commit_callbacks):\n    product = ProductFactory()\n    api.force_authenticate(user)\n    with django_capture_on_commit_callbacks(execute=True) as callbacks:\n        resp = api.post(reverse(\"order-list\"),\n                        {\"items\": [{\"product\": product.pk, \"quantity\": 1}]}, format=\"json\")\n    assert resp.status_code == 201\n    assert len(callbacks) == 1\n    delay.assert_called_once_with(resp.json()[\"id\"])\n\n@mock.patch(\"orders.services.send_receipt.delay\")\ndef test_no_receipt_when_order_fails(delay, api, user, django_capture_on_commit_callbacks):\n    product = ProductFactory(stock=0)\n    api.force_authenticate(user)\n    with django_capture_on_commit_callbacks(execute=True):\n        api.post(reverse(\"order-list\"),\n                 {\"items\": [{\"product\": product.pk, \"quantity\": 1}]}, format=\"json\")\n    delay.assert_not_called()\n    assert not Order.objects.exists()\n```\n\n### Testing the Celery task itself\n\n```python\n# orders/tests/test_tasks.py\nfrom unittest import mock\nimport pytest\nimport requests\nfrom orders.tasks import send_receipt\nfrom .factories import OrderFactory\n\npytestmark = pytest.mark.django_db\n\n@mock.patch(\"orders.tasks.email_client.send\")\ndef test_send_receipt_is_idempotent(send):\n    order = OrderFactory()\n    send_receipt(order.pk)            # call the function body directly\n    send_receipt(order.pk)            # duplicate delivery\n    send.assert_called_once()\n    order.refresh_from_db()\n    assert order.receipt_sent_at is not None\n\n@mock.patch(\"orders.tasks.email_client.send\", side_effect=requests.Timeout)\ndef test_send_receipt_retries_on_timeout(send):\n    order = OrderFactory()\n    result = send_receipt.apply(args=[order.pk])   # runs locally, honours retry logic\n    assert result.state in {\"RETRY\", \"FAILURE\"}\n```\n\n### Testing real concurrency with `TransactionTestCase`\n\n```python\n# orders/tests/test_concurrency.py\nimport threading\nfrom django.db import connection\nfrom django.test import TransactionTestCase\nfrom orders.services import reserve_stock\nfrom orders.models import Product\n\nclass ReserveStockConcurrencyTests(TransactionTestCase):\n    def test_no_oversell_under_concurrency(self):\n        product = Product.objects.create(name=\"x\", price_cents=1, stock=1)\n        results = []\n\n        def worker():\n            try:\n                results.append(reserve_stock(product.pk, 1))   # select_for_update inside\n            finally:\n                connection.close()        # each thread has its own connection\n\n        threads = [threading.Thread(target=worker) for _ in range(5)]\n        for t in threads: t.start()\n        for t in threads: t.join()\n\n        product.refresh_from_db()\n        self.assertEqual(product.stock, 0)\n        self.assertEqual(results.count(True), 1)\n```\n\nUnder `TestCase` this test would hang or fail: the product row is created inside the test's uncommitted transaction, and the threads' separate connections cannot see it.\n\n### RequestFactory, settings overrides and email\n\n```python\nfrom django.core import mail\nfrom django.test import RequestFactory, TestCase, override_settings\nfrom accounts.views import profile\nfrom .factories import UserFactory\n\nclass ProfileViewTests(TestCase):\n    def test_view_logic_without_middleware(self):\n        request = RequestFactory().get(\"/profile/\")\n        request.user = UserFactory()          # no AuthenticationMiddleware here\n        response = profile(request)\n        self.assertEqual(response.status_code, 200)\n\n    @override_settings(FEATURE_NEW_SIGNUP=True)\n    def test_feature_flag(self):\n        ...\n\n    def test_password_reset_sends_email(self):\n        UserFactory(email=\"a@example.com\")\n        self.client.post(\"/accounts/password_reset/\", {\"email\": \"a@example.com\"})\n        self.assertEqual(len(mail.outbox), 1)\n        self.assertIn(\"password\", mail.outbox[0].subject.lower())\n```\n\n### Testing a Channels consumer\n\n```python\nimport pytest\nfrom channels.testing import WebsocketCommunicator\nfrom proj.asgi import application\n\n@pytest.mark.asyncio\n@pytest.mark.django_db(transaction=True)\nasync def test_chat_echo(settings):\n    settings.CHANNEL_LAYERS = {\"default\": {\"BACKEND\": \"channels.layers.InMemoryChannelLayer\"}}\n    comm = WebsocketCommunicator(application, \"/ws/chat/lobby/\",\n                                 headers=[(b\"origin\", b\"http://testserver\")])\n    connected, _ = await comm.connect()\n    assert connected\n    await comm.send_json_to({\"text\": \"hi\"})\n    assert (await comm.receive_json_from())[\"text\"] == \"hi\"\n    await comm.disconnect()\n```\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Tool | Speed | Realism | Best for | Watch out |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `SimpleTestCase` | fastest | no DB | forms, utils, URL resolution | DB queries raise |\n| `TestCase` | fast | single connection, no commit | 90% of tests | on_commit, threads, real commits |\n| `TransactionTestCase` | slow (truncate) | real commits | locking, concurrency, durable atomics | wipes migration data unless `serialized_rollback` |\n| `LiveServerTestCase` + browser | slowest | full stack | critical user journeys | flaky waits, keep few |\n| JSON fixtures | fast to load | frozen data | small reference data | bypass `save()`, break on schema change |\n| factory_boy | fast enough | data per test | almost everything | SubFactory chains create hidden rows |\n| `CELERY_TASK_ALWAYS_EAGER` | fast | low | quick smoke tests | hides serialization and on_commit bugs |\n| Mocks | fast | depends | external services | over-mocking tests the mock, not the code |\n\n### When to use\n\n- `TestCase` + factories + `APIClient` as the default stack.\n- `assertNumQueries` on every list endpoint and every admin changelist you care about.\n- `TransactionTestCase` for code that relies on commits, row locks across connections, or `on_commit` with real commits.\n- Mock at the boundary: HTTP clients, payment SDKs, email providers, the clock (`time-machine`/`freezegun`).\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Do not mock the ORM: a test with `mock.patch(\"Order.objects.filter\")` proves nothing about your SQL. Use the test database.\n- Do not use SQLite in tests when production is PostgreSQL if you rely on Postgres features (JSONField lookups, `select_for_update`, constraints, `DISTINCT ON`).\n- Do not reach for `TransactionTestCase` \"just in case\" — it can multiply suite time.\n- Do not snapshot entire HTML pages; assert on the behaviour that matters.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**Testing on_commit code in `TestCase` without capturing callbacks.** The task dispatch silently never runs; tests pass while production breaks (or vice versa). Use `captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True)`.\n\n**Patching the wrong path.** `mock.patch(\"orders.tasks.send_receipt\")` does nothing if `orders.services` did `from orders.tasks import send_receipt` — the name is already bound in `services`. Patch `orders.services.send_receipt`.\n\n**Creating data in `setUp` for every test.** Fifty tests × ten factory objects is five hundred inserts. Move shared data to `setUpTestData`. Do not mutate class data in a test expecting the change to persist — each test gets a deep copy.\n\n**Fixtures as the main test data strategy.** A JSON fixture with 400 objects breaks every time a field is added, loads data that tests do not need, and hides which data a test depends on. Use factories; keep fixtures for small static reference data.\n\n**Asserting implementation instead of behaviour.** `assert serializer.validate_email.called` couples the test to structure. Assert status code, response body, DB state, side effects.\n\n**Order-dependent tests.** A test that only passes because a previous one created a user. Run `--shuffle` (Django 4.0+) or `pytest-randomly` in CI.\n\n**Time-dependent tests.** `timezone.now()` near midnight or month end flips results. Freeze time with `time-machine`.\n\n**Slow password hashing.** Every `create_user` with PBKDF2 at around a million iterations costs real time. Use `MD5PasswordHasher` in test settings only.\n\n**Real network calls.** A test that hits Stripe sandbox is slow and flaky. Mock the client or use `responses`/`respx`; add a separate, opt-in contract test suite.\n\n**Best practices**\n\n- Name tests by behaviour: `test_shipped_orders_cannot_be_cancelled`.\n- One factory per model, sensible defaults, traits for states.\n- `makemigrations --check` in CI so missing migrations fail the build.\n- Measure coverage but gate on critical modules, not a global vanity number.\n- Keep the suite fast enough to run before every push; parallelise with `--parallel` or `pytest -n auto`.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n> **Production story:** A list endpoint went from 120 ms to 9 s after a \"harmless\" serializer change that added a nested `owner.profile.avatar_url`. The test suite had full coverage of the endpoint but only created two objects per test, so the extra queries were invisible. Fix: an `assertNumQueries` test with 20 objects for every list endpoint, so any query that scales with rows fails CI.\n\n**Suite slowed from 3 to 25 minutes.** Root causes, in order of frequency: migrations running on every run (use `--keepdb` / `--reuse-db`), `TransactionTestCase` overuse, data created in `setUp` instead of `setUpTestData`, slow password hashers, real network calls. Profile with `pytest --durations=20`.\n\n**Flaky tests in CI only.** Usually parallelism (tests sharing a cache, file paths or a Redis DB across workers), time zones, or ordering. Give each worker its own cache key prefix, use `tmp_path`, freeze time, run with `--shuffle` locally to reproduce.\n\n**Tests pass with SQLite, prod fails on PostgreSQL.** SQLite ignores `max_length`, has different `LIKE` case rules, weaker type enforcement and limited locking. Run CI on the same PostgreSQL major version (a service container) — see [Docker · Compose](../docker/topic.html?p=18-compose).\n\n**`Database access not allowed` in pytest.** The test is missing `@pytest.mark.django_db` (or `pytestmark`). For tests using threads or async consumers, use `django_db(transaction=True)`.\n\n**Migrations untested.** Data migrations break on real data shapes. Use `django-test-migrations` (or a `TransactionTestCase` that migrates to a previous state with `MigrationExecutor`, inserts rows, migrates forward and asserts), and run `migrate` against a production snapshot in staging.\n\n**Debugging tools**\n\n- `manage.py test --debug-sql` prints SQL for failing tests; `--pdb` drops into the debugger on failure.\n- `CaptureQueriesContext(connection)` to print queries in an ad-hoc test.\n- `pytest -x --lf` re-runs only last failures; `-k pattern` selects tests.\n- `coverage run -m pytest && coverage html` to find untested branches.\n\n**Scaling the suite.** Parallelise by process (`--parallel`, `pytest-xdist`); Django clones the test DB per worker. Split slow browser tests into a separate CI job. Tag tests (`@tag(\"slow\")`, `--exclude-tag slow`) for fast local runs.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `TestCase` and `TransactionTestCase`?**\nA: `TestCase` wraps the whole class in a transaction and each test in a savepoint, rolling back after every test, so isolation is cheap and tests are fast. `TransactionTestCase` does not wrap tests; code commits for real and Django truncates all tables after each test, which is slower. You need `TransactionTestCase` when the behaviour depends on a real commit: `on_commit` with actual commits, other connections or threads seeing the data, `select_for_update` contention, or code managing its own transactions. Everything else should use `TestCase`.\n\n**Q: Why don't `transaction.on_commit` callbacks run in a `TestCase`, and how do you test them?**\nA: Because the test's outer transaction is always rolled back, so the commit that would trigger the callbacks never happens. Since Django 3.2, `TestCase.captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True)` captures callbacks registered inside the `with` block and executes them on exit; it also returns the list so you can assert how many were registered. pytest-django offers `django_capture_on_commit_callbacks`. Alternatively use `TransactionTestCase`, where commits are real, at the cost of speed.\n\n**Q: What is `setUpTestData` and how is it different from `setUp`?**\nA: `setUpTestData` is a classmethod that runs once per test class inside the class-level transaction, so data is created once and shared by all tests in the class. `setUp` runs before every test. Since Django 3.2, model instances assigned to `cls` in `setUpTestData` are deep-copied for each test, so a test mutating `self.author` in memory does not affect other tests; database changes are rolled back by the per-test savepoint anyway. Using `setUpTestData` for shared data is one of the biggest speed wins in a Django suite.\n\n**Q: How do you catch N+1 queries in tests?**\nA: Wrap the request in `self.assertNumQueries(n)` (or pytest-django's `django_assert_num_queries`) with enough rows that a per-row query would change the count — at least a handful, ideally more than the page size you care about. The assertion records queries via `CaptureQueriesContext` and on failure prints every SQL statement, which shows the repeated query. A good pattern asserts the same count for 2 rows and 20 rows. This turns \"we optimised with `select_related`\" into a regression test that fails CI when someone adds a nested serializer field.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between the test `Client` and `RequestFactory`?**\nA: The `Client` sends a simulated request through the full Django stack — middleware, URL resolution, the view, templates — and returns the response with extras like `response.context` and `templates`. `RequestFactory` only builds an `HttpRequest`; you call the view function directly, and no middleware runs, so you must attach `request.user`, sessions or messages yourself. Use the `Client` for integration tests of endpoints and `RequestFactory` for fast unit tests of view logic or middleware.\n\n**Q: Fixtures or factory_boy — which do you prefer and why?**\nA: Factories, for almost everything. JSON fixtures are static dumps loaded via `loaddata`: they bypass `save()` and signals, break when a field is added, load more data than each test needs, and hide which data a test depends on. Factories define data in Python, so they refactor with your models, each test creates exactly what it needs with explicit overrides, and traits express states like `shipped=True`. Fixtures remain fine for small, stable reference data such as countries.\n\n**Q: How do you mock an external API call in a Django test, and what is the classic mistake?**\nA: Use `unittest.mock.patch` on the name as it is looked up by the code under test, e.g. `@mock.patch(\"orders.services.stripe_client.charge\")`, or HTTP-level mocking with `responses`/`respx`. The classic mistake is patching where the object is defined instead of where it is imported: if `services.py` did `from payments.client import charge`, patching `payments.client.charge` has no effect on the already-bound name. Also avoid mocking the ORM — use the test database so the SQL is real.\n\n**Q: How do you test DRF APIs with authentication?**\nA: Use `rest_framework.test.APIClient` (or `APITestCase`) and call `client.force_authenticate(user=user)` to bypass the authentication classes, or `client.credentials(HTTP_AUTHORIZATION=\"Bearer ...\")` when you want to test the auth path itself. Send JSON with `format=\"json\"`. Assert status codes for anonymous, unauthorised and authorised users, response shape, DB state and query count. For unit-testing a view, `APIRequestFactory` plus `force_authenticate(request, user=user)` works without the URLconf.\n\n**Q: How do you test Celery tasks?**\nA: Test the task body directly as a function call (or `task.apply()` to run it locally with retry semantics) with external services mocked, and assert idempotency by calling it twice. Test dispatch separately: mock `task.delay` at the import path the code uses and assert it was called inside `captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True)`, plus a negative test that a failing transaction does not dispatch. `CELERY_TASK_ALWAYS_EAGER` is convenient but hides serialization errors (passing a model instance works eagerly) and on_commit ordering, so it should not be your only coverage.\n\n**Q: Your test suite takes 30 minutes. How do you bring it under 5? (Senior)**\nA: Measure first with `pytest --durations=50` to find the slowest tests and fixtures. Common wins: reuse the test database (`--keepdb`/`--reuse-db`) so migrations do not run each time, move shared data from `setUp` to `setUpTestData`, use the MD5 password hasher in tests, replace real network calls with mocks, and convert unnecessary `TransactionTestCase` classes to `TestCase` with `captureOnCommitCallbacks`. Then parallelise with `--parallel` or `pytest -n auto`, ensuring workers do not share caches or files. Finally split browser and slow integration tests into a separate CI job, and keep the fast suite runnable locally.\n\n**Q: How would you test that your stock-reservation code cannot oversell under concurrency? (Senior)**\nA: A `TestCase` cannot do it, because all code shares one uncommitted connection and other threads cannot see the data. Use `TransactionTestCase`: create a product with stock 1, start several threads that each call the reservation service (which uses `select_for_update()` or a conditional `UPDATE ... WHERE stock >= qty`), close each thread's connection at the end, join, and assert exactly one success and stock zero. Run it against PostgreSQL, not SQLite, because locking semantics differ. Add a deterministic unit test for the conditional update as well, since thread tests can be timing-sensitive.\n\n**Q: How do you test a data migration safely? (Senior)**\nA: Test it against migration state rather than current models: use a `TransactionTestCase` (or `django-test-migrations`) that migrates to the migration before yours with `MigrationExecutor`, gets historical models from the resulting state via `apps.get_model`, inserts representative rows, migrates forward to your migration, and asserts the transformed data. Also test the reverse if you wrote one. In CI run `makemigrations --check` to catch missing migrations, and in staging run the full migration against a production-sized snapshot to measure lock duration and runtime — correctness on ten rows says nothing about a 50-million-row table.\n\n**Q: What does `override_settings` do and why not just assign to `settings`?**\nA: `override_settings` (decorator or context manager) temporarily replaces settings for the test and restores them afterwards, and it sends the `setting_changed` signal so Django clears caches that depend on settings (template engines, static storage, password hashers and others). Assigning `settings.FOO = ...` directly leaks the change into later tests and skips those cache resets, causing order-dependent failures. pytest-django's `settings` fixture does the same thing with automatic restore.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Need | Use |\n|---|---|\n| No DB | `SimpleTestCase` |\n| Normal DB test | `TestCase` + `setUpTestData` |\n| Real commits / threads / locks | `TransactionTestCase` |\n| Browser | `StaticLiveServerTestCase` + Playwright |\n| on_commit in TestCase | `self.captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True)` |\n| N+1 regression | `assertNumQueries(n)` / `django_assert_num_queries` |\n| Full-stack request | `self.client`, `APIClient` |\n| View in isolation | `RequestFactory` / `APIRequestFactory` |\n| Auth shortcut | `client.force_login(u)`, `api.force_authenticate(u)` |\n| Settings | `@override_settings`, pytest `settings` fixture |\n| Email | `django.core.mail.outbox` |\n| Test data | factory_boy `DjangoModelFactory`, `SubFactory`, traits |\n| Speed | `--keepdb`, `--parallel`, MD5 hasher |\n\n- `TestCase` never commits: on_commit callbacks need capturing.\n- Other threads/connections cannot see `TestCase` data.\n- `TransactionTestCase` truncates tables — and migration-created data with them.\n- Patch where the name is used, not where it is defined.\n- Do not mock the ORM; do mock the network.\n- Assert query counts with more rows than you think you need.\n- Run tests on the same database engine as production.\n- `makemigrations --check` in CI.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSetup: `python -m venv .venv && pip install \"django~=5.2\" pytest pytest-django factory-boy djangorestframework`.\n\n1. **Isolation.** Write two `TestCase` tests that each create a `User` with the same username and show both pass. Then write a test that creates a row and reads it from a new thread — watch it fail — and convert the class to `TransactionTestCase`.\n2. **N+1 guard.** Build a `Book` list view that loops `book.author.name`. Write an `assertNumQueries` test with 10 books, read the failure output, fix with `select_related`, and watch it pass.\n3. **on_commit.** Write a service that creates an order and dispatches a mocked task on commit. Show a plain `TestCase` never sees the call; fix with `captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True)`.\n4. **Factories.** Replace a JSON fixture with factory_boy factories including a `SubFactory` and a `Trait`; measure the suite time difference.\n5. **Mock path.** Deliberately patch the wrong import path for an external client and observe the real function being called; fix the path.\n6. **Speed.** Time the suite, then add `--keepdb`, the MD5 hasher and `setUpTestData`; record the improvement.\n\n**Mini project:** a fully tested order API — factories for all models, permission matrix tests (anonymous/owner/other/staff) with `pytest.mark.parametrize`, query-count tests for list and detail endpoints, an on_commit dispatch test, an idempotent Celery task test, a `TransactionTestCase` oversell test, and a GitHub Actions workflow with a PostgreSQL service container and `makemigrations --check`.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n- [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) — what `assertNumQueries` protects.\n- [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) — why TestCase isolation works and where it lies.\n- [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks) — idempotent tasks and on_commit dispatch.\n- [Ch 32 · Django Channels & Real-Time](topic.html?p=32-channels-websockets) — `WebsocketCommunicator`.\n- [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations) — testing data migrations.\n- [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production) — CI/CD pipeline.\n- [REST API · Testing REST APIs](../rest-api/topic.html?p=36-testing-rest-apis), [REST API · Mocking, Stubs & Sandboxes](../rest-api/topic.html?p=37-mocking-stubs-sandboxes), [Docker · CI/CD](../docker/topic.html?p=26-ci-cd).\n\n- **Testing in Django** — Django docs · *Beginner* · overview, test runner, test database and the test class hierarchy. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/testing/>\n- **Testing tools** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · Client, RequestFactory, TestCase features, `captureOnCommitCallbacks`, `assertNumQueries`. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/testing/tools/>\n- **DRF Testing** — Django REST framework · *Intermediate* · APIClient, APIRequestFactory, force_authenticate. <https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/testing/>\n- **pytest-django documentation** — pytest-django · *Intermediate* · `django_db` marker, fixtures, `--reuse-db`. <https://pytest-django.readthedocs.io/>\n- **factory_boy documentation** — factory_boy · *Intermediate* · DjangoModelFactory, SubFactory, traits, post-generation hooks. <https://factoryboy.readthedocs.io/>\n- **unittest.mock — where to patch** — Python docs · *Intermediate* · the official explanation of patching the lookup location. <https://docs.python.org/3/library/unittest.mock.html#where-to-patch>\n- **Coverage.py** — coverage.py docs · *Beginner* · measuring line and branch coverage. <https://coverage.readthedocs.io/>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 33.*\n", "34-scaling-databases": "# 34 · Scaling Django: Connections, Replicas & Multiple Databases\n\n> **In one line:** Django opens one database connection per thread and, by default, closes it at the end of every request — so the first scaling problems are connection churn and connection *count*, solved with `CONN_MAX_AGE`, Django 5.1's native pool or PgBouncer; after that come read replicas via database routers (and the read-your-writes bugs they cause), and finally partitioning data by tenant or shard.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization), [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency), [Ch 29 · Caching in Django](topic.html?p=29-caching), [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks).\n\nYour Django app is getting real traffic. The database CPU is fine, the slow-query log is clean (you already fixed the N+1s), and yet p99 latency is climbing and every few days PostgreSQL logs `FATAL: sorry, too many clients already`. Two different problems are hiding there. First, **connection churn**: with the default `CONN_MAX_AGE = 0`, every request opens a fresh TCP connection to PostgreSQL, negotiates TLS, authenticates, and PostgreSQL forks a backend process — several milliseconds of overhead per request, and on a busy server a large share of database CPU. Second, **connection count**: 20 pods × 4 Gunicorn workers × 4 threads + 10 Celery pods × 8 processes = 400 potential connections, and each PostgreSQL backend costs memory and contends for locks; `max_connections` defaults to 100.\n\nThe naive fixes make it worse. Raising `max_connections` to 2,000 trades a connection error for a memory and context-switch problem — PostgreSQL performance degrades well before such numbers because each connection is a process. Setting `CONN_MAX_AGE = None` (persistent forever) removes churn but pins a connection per thread even when idle, so the count problem gets *worse*. The right answer is layered: reuse connections within a process (persistent connections or Django 5.1's native **psycopg pool**), multiplex many client connections onto few server connections with **PgBouncer**, and budget connections deliberately.\n\nOnce connections are under control, the next limit is read throughput on a single primary. Django's **multiple-database** support and **database routers** let you send reads to **read replicas** while writes go to the primary — at the cost of replication lag, which turns into \"I saved my profile and the page still shows the old name\". Beyond replicas, write scaling requires partitioning data: per-tenant schemas or databases, or application-level sharding. This chapter walks that path in the order you will hit it. Database-side theory lives in [Database Design · Replication](../database-design/topic.html?p=09-replication) and [Database Design · Pools, Timeouts & Storms](../database-design/topic.html?p=20-database-application-architecture); here is the Django side.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **Connection per thread** — `django.db.connections` is a `ConnectionHandler` that keeps one `DatabaseWrapper` per alias *per thread* (asgiref `Local`). *Why it matters:* connection count = processes × threads per alias, not \"number of servers\".\n- **Lazy connect** — the wrapper connects on the first query, not at startup. *Why:* a request that never touches the DB costs no connection.\n- **`CONN_MAX_AGE`** — seconds to keep a connection open after a request: `0` (default) closes at the end of each request; `None` keeps it forever; `60` reuses for a minute. *Why:* removes per-request connect overhead.\n- **`close_old_connections`** — handler for `request_started` and `request_finished` signals; closes connections that exceeded `CONN_MAX_AGE` or are unusable after errors. *Why:* this is the only place persistent connections are recycled — outside requests (Celery, management commands) you call it yourself.\n- **`CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS`** (4.1+) — when `True`, a reused persistent connection is pinged at the start of a request and replaced if dead. *Why:* avoids the first query of a request failing after a DB failover or idle timeout.\n- **Native pooling** (Django 5.1+, PostgreSQL + psycopg 3) — `OPTIONS: {\"pool\": True}` or pool kwargs, using `psycopg_pool.ConnectionPool` per process. *Why:* threads borrow connections only for the duration of a request, so idle threads hold none.\n- **PgBouncer** — external connection pooler. **Session mode** maps a client to a server connection for the whole session; **transaction mode** only for each transaction. *Why:* transaction mode lets thousands of client connections share tens of server connections — but breaks session state.\n- **`DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS`** — make `.iterator()` use client-side cursors. *Why:* server-side cursors (named cursors used by `iterator()` on PostgreSQL) do not survive PgBouncer transaction mode outside a transaction.\n- **`DATABASES` aliases** — multiple entries (`default`, `replica`, `analytics`). *Why:* each alias is a separate connection; querysets choose one via routers or `.using()`.\n- **Database router** — a class in `DATABASE_ROUTERS` implementing `db_for_read`, `db_for_write`, `allow_relation`, `allow_migrate`. *Why:* central policy for where each query goes.\n- **`.using(alias)` / `save(using=)` / `Manager.db_manager(alias)`** — explicit per-query routing. *Why:* override the router for specific cases (force primary after a write).\n- **Instance `_state.db`** — every model instance remembers which alias it was loaded from. *Why:* related lookups from that instance default to the same database (\"stickiness\").\n- **Replication lag** — the delay between a commit on the primary and its visibility on a replica. *Why:* reading your own write from a replica can return stale data.\n- **Multi-tenancy** — isolating customers via a shared schema with `tenant_id`, schema-per-tenant (PostgreSQL `search_path`, e.g. django-tenants), or database-per-tenant. *Why:* determines isolation, migration cost and how you shard later.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### How Django manages connections internally\n\n`connections[\"default\"]` returns the `DatabaseWrapper` for the current thread (creating it from `settings.DATABASES` on first use). When you evaluate a QuerySet, the compiler calls `connection.cursor()`, which calls `ensure_connection()` → `connect()` if `self.connection is None`. `connect()` records `self.close_at = now + CONN_MAX_AGE` (or `None` for unlimited). At the start and end of each HTTP request, `BaseHandler` sends `request_started` / `request_finished`, and `django.db.close_old_connections` calls `close_if_unusable_or_obsolete()` on every connection, which closes it if it errored, if autocommit was left off, or if `close_at` has passed. With `CONN_MAX_AGE = 0`, `close_at` is \"now\", so every request ends with a close.\n\nTwo consequences matter for interviews. **Persistent connections are per thread**, so with Gunicorn `--threads 8`, each worker process can hold eight connections. And **persistent connections only get recycled at request boundaries**: a Celery worker or a long-running management command never sends `request_finished`, so a connection broken by a failover stays broken until you call `close_old_connections()` (Celery's Django fixup does this around each task).\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj34a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Three ways to hold connections: churn, persistent, pooled</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"270\" height=\"250\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">CONN_MAX_AGE = 0 (default)</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">request 1: connect, query, close</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">request 2: connect, query, close</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">request 3: connect, query, close</text>\n  <rect x=\"45\" y=\"140\" width=\"220\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">each connect: TCP + TLS + auth</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"178\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">+ Postgres forks a backend</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-weight=\"bold\">cost: ms per request, DB CPU</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"235\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">count: only threads mid-request</text>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">fine at low traffic</text>\n  <rect x=\"305\" y=\"40\" width=\"270\" height=\"250\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">CONN_MAX_AGE = 60 / None</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">thread keeps its connection</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">across requests until close_at</text>\n  <rect x=\"330\" y=\"122\" width=\"220\" height=\"68\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">worker: T1 conn, T2 conn,</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">T3 conn, T4 conn</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"178\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">even when T2..T4 are idle</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-weight=\"bold\">no churn</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"235\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\">count = processes x threads</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"255\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">add CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS=True</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"40\" width=\"270\" height=\"250\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Pool (Django 5.1+) / PgBouncer</text>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"86\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">threads borrow on first query,</text>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"104\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">return at request end</text>\n  <rect x=\"615\" y=\"122\" width=\"220\" height=\"68\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"142\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">psycopg pool: per process</text>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">PgBouncer: shared across</text>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"178\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">all pods (txn mode)</text>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"215\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-weight=\"bold\">no churn, bounded count</text>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"235\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">busy threads only</text>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"255\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">txn mode: no session state</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"310\" width=\"840\" height=\"74\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"332\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Connection budget (per DB alias)</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"352\" fill=\"#334155\">web pods x Gunicorn workers x threads  +  Celery pods x concurrency  +  beat, cron, migrations, admins</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"372\" fill=\"#334155\">must stay below max_connections minus reserved slots; PgBouncer turns the left side into \"client\" connections</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Native pooling in Django 5.1+\n\nWith `psycopg[pool]` installed and `\"OPTIONS\": {\"pool\": True}`, the PostgreSQL backend creates one `psycopg_pool.ConnectionPool` per process per alias. `connect()` borrows a connection from the pool; `close()` returns it instead of closing the socket. Because Django still \"closes\" at the end of each request (pooling requires `CONN_MAX_AGE = 0`; Django raises `ImproperlyConfigured` if you combine pooling with persistent connections), an idle thread holds nothing. You can pass pool options as a dict: `{\"pool\": {\"min_size\": 2, \"max_size\": 10, \"timeout\": 10}}`. When all connections are busy, a thread waits up to `timeout` seconds and then raises `PoolTimeout` — backpressure instead of overwhelming PostgreSQL.\n\nThe pool is still **per process**. With 80 Gunicorn processes and `max_size=10`, the ceiling is 800. Pooling solves churn and idle waste inside a process; PgBouncer solves the fleet-wide count.\n\n### PgBouncer and transaction mode\n\nIn **transaction pooling** mode, PgBouncer assigns a server connection to a client only for the duration of a transaction; between transactions (and in autocommit, between statements) the server connection may serve someone else. That breaks anything that relies on session state persisting: `SET` statements, session-level advisory locks, `LISTEN/NOTIFY`, temporary tables, and **server-side cursors** — which Django uses for `QuerySet.iterator()` on PostgreSQL. A named cursor declared outside a transaction is gone when the next `FETCH` lands on a different server connection, and you see `cursor \"_django_curs_...\" does not exist`. The fix is `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS = True` on that alias (iterator then streams via client-side fetching in chunks, using more memory). Django's own per-connection setup (time zone, isolation level) is applied on connect, which with PgBouncer means on the *client* connection — so make sure PgBouncer's server connections are consistent (same `timezone` in `postgresql.conf` and UTC everywhere is the safe default).\n\n### Read replicas and routers\n\nDjango sends every query through `django.db.router`, a `ConnectionRouter` that iterates `DATABASE_ROUTERS`. For reads, `QuerySet.db` asks `router.db_for_read(model, **hints)`; for writes (`save`, `delete`, `update`, `bulk_create`), `router.db_for_write`. The first router that returns a non-`None` value wins; if all return `None`, Django falls back to the instance's `_state.db` (when an instance hint is present) or `\"default\"`. Explicit `.using()` always overrides the router.\n\nA QuerySet created inside `transaction.atomic()` still asks the router; if your router sends reads to a replica, reads inside a transaction go to a different connection that is not part of that transaction and cannot see its uncommitted writes. Good routers check whether the primary is inside an atomic block and pin reads to it.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 420\" width=\"100%\" height=\"420\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj34b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj34c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Router decisions and the read-your-writes trap</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"74\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">QuerySet / save()</text>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">.using()? wins</text>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">else ask the router</text>\n  <rect x=\"250\" y=\"40\" width=\"300\" height=\"150\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"400\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">PrimaryReplicaRouter</text>\n  <text x=\"265\" y=\"86\" fill=\"#334155\">db_for_write: \"default\"</text>\n  <text x=\"265\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#334155\">db_for_read:</text>\n  <text x=\"280\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#334155\">in atomic() on default?  \"default\"</text>\n  <text x=\"280\" y=\"142\" fill=\"#334155\">pinned (wrote recently)? \"default\"</text>\n  <text x=\"280\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#334155\">else random replica</text>\n  <text x=\"265\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#334155\">allow_migrate: only \"default\"</text>\n  <rect x=\"620\" y=\"40\" width=\"240\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"740\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Primary (\"default\")</text>\n  <text x=\"740\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">all writes, pinned reads</text>\n  <rect x=\"620\" y=\"130\" width=\"240\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"740\" y=\"154\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Replica(s)</text>\n  <text x=\"740\" y=\"174\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">most reads, lag 10 ms .. seconds</text>\n  <path d=\"M200,85 L248,95\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj34b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M550,80 L618,72\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj34b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M550,150 L618,158\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj34b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M740,100 L740,128\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj34b)\"/>\n  <text x=\"750\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">WAL streaming</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"220\" width=\"840\" height=\"180\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"242\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Without pinning: user edits profile, redirect shows the old name</text>\n  <rect x=\"50\" y=\"260\" width=\"170\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"135\" y=\"285\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">POST /profile</text>\n  <rect x=\"270\" y=\"260\" width=\"170\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/><text x=\"355\" y=\"285\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">UPDATE on primary</text>\n  <rect x=\"490\" y=\"260\" width=\"170\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/><text x=\"575\" y=\"285\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">302, GET /profile</text>\n  <rect x=\"690\" y=\"260\" width=\"150\" height=\"40\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/><text x=\"765\" y=\"285\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">SELECT on replica</text>\n  <path d=\"M220,280 L268,280\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj34b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M440,280 L488,280\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj34b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M660,280 L688,280\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj34c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"765\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-weight=\"bold\">old row: lag not caught up</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"350\" fill=\"#334155\">Fixes: pin the user to the primary for N seconds after a write (cookie or cache key), read from the primary inside</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"368\" fill=\"#334155\">transactions and for \"my own data\" pages, or use .using(\"default\") explicitly on read-after-write paths.</text>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"386\" fill=\"#334155\">Monitor lag (pg_stat_replication / replay_lag) and drop replicas that fall too far behind.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### The scaling ladder\n\n```text\nStage 0  single Postgres, CONN_MAX_AGE=0          fix N+1, add indexes first (Ch 13, Ch 16)\nStage 1  reuse connections                         CONN_MAX_AGE=60 + CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS, or \"pool\": True (5.1+)\nStage 2  bound the fleet's connections             PgBouncer (transaction mode) + DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS\nStage 3  cache hot reads                           Redis cache, per-view / low-level (Ch 29)\nStage 4  read replicas                             DATABASE_ROUTERS, pinning after writes, lag monitoring\nStage 5  split by function                         analytics DB, separate DB for audit/events (allow_migrate per app)\nStage 6  partition data                            Postgres declarative partitioning, tenant-per-schema/DB, sharding\n```\n\nDo not skip stages. Most Django apps never need stage 6, and a replica added before connections are pooled just doubles the connection problem.\n\n### Request flow with PgBouncer and replicas\n\n```text\n Gunicorn pod ─┐                       ┌──► PgBouncer-primary  (pool_mode=transaction, default_pool_size≈20) ──► Postgres primary\n Gunicorn pod ─┼── DATABASES aliases ──┤\n Celery pod   ─┘   default / replica   └──► PgBouncer-replica  ───────────────────────────────────────────────► Postgres replica(s)\n```\n\nEach Django alias points at a PgBouncer host/port. Django sees \"a database\"; PgBouncer multiplexes many client connections to a small number of server connections per database/user pair.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: persistent connections and the native pool\n\n```python\n# settings.py — Option A: persistent connections (any Django version)\nDATABASES = {\n    \"default\": {\n        \"ENGINE\": \"django.db.backends.postgresql\",\n        \"NAME\": \"shop\",\n        \"USER\": \"shop\",\n        \"PASSWORD\": os.environ[\"DB_PASSWORD\"],\n        \"HOST\": \"db.internal\",\n        \"PORT\": \"5432\",\n        \"CONN_MAX_AGE\": 60,              # reuse for up to 60 s\n        \"CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS\": True,      # ping before reuse (4.1+)\n        \"OPTIONS\": {\"connect_timeout\": 5},\n    }\n}\n```\n\n```python\n# settings.py — Option B: native pool (Django 5.1+, pip install \"psycopg[binary,pool]\")\nDATABASES = {\n    \"default\": {\n        \"ENGINE\": \"django.db.backends.postgresql\",\n        \"NAME\": \"shop\",\n        \"USER\": \"shop\",\n        \"PASSWORD\": os.environ[\"DB_PASSWORD\"],\n        \"HOST\": \"db.internal\",\n        \"CONN_MAX_AGE\": 0,               # required with pooling\n        \"OPTIONS\": {\n            \"pool\": {\"min_size\": 2, \"max_size\": 8, \"timeout\": 10},\n        },\n    }\n}\n```\n\nVerify what is happening from PostgreSQL's side:\n\n```sql\nSELECT usename, application_name, state, count(*)\nFROM pg_stat_activity\nWHERE datname = 'shop'\nGROUP BY 1, 2, 3\nORDER BY 4 DESC;\n```\n\n```text\n usename | application_name |        state        | count\n---------+------------------+---------------------+-------\n shop    |                  | idle                |   212\n shop    |                  | active              |    14\n shop    |                  | idle in transaction |     3\n```\n\n212 idle connections and 14 active ones is the classic signature of persistent connections with too many threads — a pool or PgBouncer would hold perhaps 20. \"idle in transaction\" rows are worse: a transaction left open (often a long `atomic()` around an external call) holding locks and preventing vacuum.\n\n### Real-world example: primary + replicas with pinning\n\n```python\n# settings.py\nDATABASES = {\n    \"default\": {  # primary, via PgBouncer\n        \"ENGINE\": \"django.db.backends.postgresql\",\n        \"NAME\": \"shop\", \"USER\": \"shop\", \"PASSWORD\": os.environ[\"DB_PASSWORD\"],\n        \"HOST\": \"pgbouncer-primary\", \"PORT\": \"6432\",\n        \"CONN_MAX_AGE\": 60, \"CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS\": True,\n        \"DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS\": True,           # PgBouncer transaction mode\n    },\n    \"replica\": {\n        \"ENGINE\": \"django.db.backends.postgresql\",\n        \"NAME\": \"shop\", \"USER\": \"shop_ro\", \"PASSWORD\": os.environ[\"DB_RO_PASSWORD\"],\n        \"HOST\": \"pgbouncer-replica\", \"PORT\": \"6432\",\n        \"CONN_MAX_AGE\": 60, \"CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS\": True,\n        \"DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS\": True,\n        \"TEST\": {\"MIRROR\": \"default\"},                 # tests: replica = primary\n    },\n}\nDATABASE_ROUTERS = [\"core.db.PrimaryReplicaRouter\"]\nREPLICA_PIN_SECONDS = 5\n```\n\n```python\n# core/db.py\nimport random\nfrom contextvars import ContextVar\nfrom django.db import connections\n\n# Set per request by middleware; contextvars work for both WSGI threads and ASGI tasks.\n_pinned: ContextVar[bool] = ContextVar(\"pinned_to_primary\", default=False)\n\nREPLICAS = [\"replica\"]\n\ndef pin_to_primary() -> None:\n    _pinned.set(True)\n\nclass PrimaryReplicaRouter:\n    def db_for_read(self, model, **hints):\n        if _pinned.get() or connections[\"default\"].in_atomic_block:\n            return \"default\"            # read-your-writes, and consistency inside transactions\n        return random.choice(REPLICAS)\n\n    def db_for_write(self, model, **hints):\n        pin_to_primary()                # any write pins the rest of this request\n        return \"default\"\n\n    def allow_relation(self, obj1, obj2, **hints):\n        # Primary and replica hold the same data, so relations between them are fine.\n        return {obj1._state.db, obj2._state.db} <= {\"default\", *REPLICAS}\n\n    def allow_migrate(self, db, app_label, model_name=None, **hints):\n        return db == \"default\"          # never run migrations against replicas\n```\n\n```python\n# core/middleware.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom .db import _pinned, pin_to_primary\n\nPIN_COOKIE = \"pin_primary\"\n\nclass ReplicaPinningMiddleware:\n    \"\"\"Keep a user on the primary for a few seconds after they write.\"\"\"\n\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response\n\n    def __call__(self, request):\n        token = _pinned.set(False)\n        try:\n            if request.COOKIES.get(PIN_COOKIE) or request.method not in (\"GET\", \"HEAD\", \"OPTIONS\"):\n                pin_to_primary()\n            response = self.get_response(request)\n            if _pinned.get() and request.method not in (\"GET\", \"HEAD\", \"OPTIONS\"):\n                response.set_cookie(PIN_COOKIE, \"1\", max_age=settings.REPLICA_PIN_SECONDS,\n                                    httponly=True, samesite=\"Lax\", secure=True)\n            return response\n        finally:\n            _pinned.reset(token)\n```\n\nExplicit routing where intent matters more than policy:\n\n```python\n# Reports can tolerate lag: always replica.\nmonthly = Order.objects.using(\"replica\").filter(created_at__gte=start).aggregate(total=Sum(\"total\"))\n\n# Payment confirmation must see the latest state: always primary.\npayment = Payment.objects.using(\"default\").select_for_update().get(pk=pid)\n\n# Instances remember their DB; saving an object loaded from the replica still goes through\n# db_for_write, which returns \"default\" here.\norder = Order.objects.using(\"replica\").get(pk=7)\norder._state.db      # 'replica'\norder.save()          # router.db_for_write -> 'default'\n```\n\nMigrations per alias:\n\n```bash\npython manage.py migrate                     # default only (router allow_migrate)\npython manage.py migrate --database=analytics\npython manage.py dbshell --database=replica\n```\n\n### Functional split: a separate analytics database\n\n```python\nclass AnalyticsRouter:\n    app = \"analytics\"\n\n    def db_for_read(self, model, **hints):\n        return \"analytics\" if model._meta.app_label == self.app else None\n\n    def db_for_write(self, model, **hints):\n        return \"analytics\" if model._meta.app_label == self.app else None\n\n    def allow_relation(self, obj1, obj2, **hints):\n        a, b = obj1._meta.app_label == self.app, obj2._meta.app_label == self.app\n        return None if not (a or b) else a and b     # no FKs across databases\n\n    def allow_migrate(self, db, app_label, model_name=None, **hints):\n        if app_label == self.app:\n            return db == \"analytics\"\n        return None if db == \"default\" else False\n\nDATABASE_ROUTERS = [\"core.db.AnalyticsRouter\", \"core.db.PrimaryReplicaRouter\"]\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** Django does not support foreign keys or joins across databases. A `ForeignKey` from an `analytics` model to `auth.User` in `default` cannot be enforced by the DB and cannot be traversed in a single query; store `user_id` as a plain integer (`db_constraint=False` on the FK at minimum) and join in Python.\n\n### Multi-tenancy patterns\n\n```python\n# Pattern 1: shared schema, tenant column (simplest, scales to many tenants)\nclass TenantScopedManager(models.Manager):\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return super().get_queryset().filter(tenant_id=current_tenant_id())\n\nclass Invoice(models.Model):\n    tenant = models.ForeignKey(\"tenants.Tenant\", on_delete=models.CASCADE, db_index=True)\n    number = models.CharField(max_length=32)\n    objects = TenantScopedManager()\n    all_tenants = models.Manager()\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"tenant\", \"number\"], name=\"uniq_invoice_per_tenant\")]\n        indexes = [models.Index(fields=[\"tenant\", \"-id\"])]\n```\n\n```python\n# Pattern 3: database per tenant (or per shard), chosen per request\nfrom contextvars import ContextVar\n_tenant_db: ContextVar[str] = ContextVar(\"tenant_db\", default=\"default\")\n\nclass TenantShardRouter:\n    def db_for_read(self, model, **hints):\n        return _tenant_db.get() if model._meta.app_label in TENANT_APPS else None\n    db_for_write = db_for_read\n\n    def allow_migrate(self, db, app_label, **hints):\n        if app_label in TENANT_APPS:\n            return db.startswith(\"shard_\")\n        return db == \"default\"\n\n# middleware: tenant = resolve(request.get_host()); _tenant_db.set(tenant.shard_alias)\n```\n\n| Pattern | Isolation | Migrations | Tenant count | Cross-tenant queries |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Shared schema + `tenant_id` | Row-level (app enforced; add Postgres RLS for defence in depth) | One run | Unlimited | Easy |\n| Schema per tenant (django-tenants) | Schema (`search_path`) | Once per schema — slow at thousands | Hundreds to low thousands | Hard |\n| Database per tenant / shard | Strong | Per database | Limited by ops | Very hard |\n\nSee [Database Design · Case: Multi-Tenant SaaS](../database-design/topic.html?p=40-case-multi-tenant-saas) and [Database Design · Sharding](../database-design/topic.html?p=12-sharding).\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Technique | Solves | Costs | Gotchas |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `CONN_MAX_AGE > 0` | Connect overhead | Connections = processes × threads | Stale connections after failover (use health checks) |\n| Native pool (5.1+) | Churn + idle threads | Per-process ceiling, needs psycopg 3 | Not with persistent connections; `PoolTimeout` under load |\n| PgBouncer transaction mode | Fleet-wide connection count | Extra hop, ops | No session state; disable server-side cursors |\n| PgBouncer session mode | Connection storms on deploy | Little multiplexing | Same count as clients when all busy |\n| Read replicas + router | Read throughput, isolation of reports | Lag, more connections, router complexity | Read-your-writes, reads inside atomic |\n| Functional split | Noisy workloads | No cross-DB joins/FKs | `allow_migrate` mistakes |\n| Sharding / DB-per-tenant | Write throughput, data size | Huge complexity | Cross-shard queries, rebalancing |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Persistent connections or the pool for any production deployment with steady traffic.\n- PgBouncer when total connections across pods approach a few hundred, or with autoscaling fleets.\n- Replicas when read load dominates *after* caching and query fixes, or to isolate heavy reporting.\n- Shared-schema multi-tenancy by default; schemas or databases only for strong isolation requirements.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Replicas to fix slow queries — a slow query is slow on a replica too.\n- Transaction-mode PgBouncer with code that needs `SET`, advisory locks held across transactions, `LISTEN`, or server-side cursors.\n- `CONN_MAX_AGE = None` with many threads and no pooler.\n- Sharding before exhausting vertical scaling, partitioning and caching.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**Persistent connections with threaded or async servers and no budget.** `--threads 16` × 40 pods × `CONN_MAX_AGE=None` = 640 mostly idle connections. Do the arithmetic; use the pool or PgBouncer.\n\n**Expecting `CONN_MAX_AGE` to work in async views.** Under ASGI, each request's sync ORM work runs via `sync_to_async`, and persistent connections have historically been unreliable there; the Django docs recommend `CONN_MAX_AGE = 0` under ASGI and an external pooler or the native pool instead.\n\n**Long transactions around external calls.** `with atomic(): charge_card(); order.save()` holds a connection (and locks) for the duration of the HTTP call — in PgBouncer transaction mode, it also holds a *server* connection. Do I/O outside transactions.\n\n**Router that ignores transactions.** Reading from the replica inside `atomic()` returns data that does not include your own uncommitted writes, and `select_for_update()` routed to a replica fails (replicas are read-only). Check `in_atomic_block` and route locking reads to the primary.\n\n**Running migrations against replicas.** Without `allow_migrate`, `migrate --database=replica` will try DDL on a read-only server. Restrict migrations to the primary alias.\n\n**Assuming objects carry their DB on save.** With no router, an object loaded via `.using(\"replica\")` saves back to `\"replica\"` (its `_state.db`) and fails. Always have `db_for_write` return the primary.\n\n**Forgetting `TEST: {\"MIRROR\": \"default\"}`.** The test runner creates a separate test DB for the replica alias, which is empty — tests reading via the router see nothing.\n\n**Best practices**\n\n- Write down the connection budget per alias and alert at 70–80% of `max_connections`.\n- Set `statement_timeout` and `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout` in PostgreSQL (or via `OPTIONS: {\"options\": \"-c statement_timeout=5000\"}`, which in PgBouncer transaction mode should be set on the role instead).\n- Monitor replica lag; remove replicas that exceed a threshold from rotation.\n- Route by *intent* in code that cares (`using(\"default\")` for read-after-write, `using(\"replica\")` for reports).\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n> **Production story:** After moving to PgBouncer transaction mode, a nightly export job failed with `cursor \"_django_curs_140234_sync_1\" does not exist`. Root cause: `QuerySet.iterator()` uses a named server-side cursor, and the next `FETCH` landed on a different server connection. Fix: `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS = True`, or wrap the iteration in `atomic()` so the whole loop is one transaction pinned to one server connection (at the cost of a long transaction).\n\n**`too many clients already` during deploys.** Rolling deploys briefly run old and new pods together, doubling connections. Budget for surge (`maxSurge`), and put PgBouncer in front so client spikes queue instead of failing.\n\n**First request after a failover errors.** Persistent connections point at the dead primary; the first query raises `OperationalError`. Enable `CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS`, use a DNS name or PgBouncer that follows the failover, and keep connect timeouts short.\n\n**Pool exhaustion (`PoolTimeout`).** All pool connections are busy — usually slow queries or long transactions, not too few connections. Find them in `pg_stat_activity` (`state`, `xact_start`, `query`) before raising `max_size`.\n\n**Stale reads reported by users.** Measure lag (`SELECT now() - pg_last_xact_replay_timestamp();` on the replica; `replay_lag` in `pg_stat_replication` on the primary). Correlate with write bursts (bulk imports, big migrations). Pin sessions after writes; send critical reads to the primary.\n\n**Celery workers holding broken connections.** Workers do not get `request_finished`; Celery's fixup closes connections around tasks, but long loops inside a task should call `close_old_connections()` periodically.\n\n**Debugging toolkit**\n\n- `connection.queries` / `connection.vendor` / `connections.all()` in a shell to see which alias was used.\n- `QuerySet.db` tells you which alias a queryset will use *before* evaluating it.\n- `pg_stat_activity`, `pg_stat_statements`, and PgBouncer's `SHOW POOLS; SHOW CLIENTS;` admin console.\n- django-debug-toolbar's SQL panel shows the alias per query.\n- See [Database Design · Bottleneck Diagnosis](../database-design/topic.html?p=23-bottleneck-diagnosis) and [Database Design · Database Scaling](../database-design/topic.html?p=21-database-scaling).\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What does `CONN_MAX_AGE` do, and what are the trade-offs of 0, 60 and `None`?**\nA: It controls how long Django keeps a database connection open after a request. `0` (the default) closes the connection at the end of every request, so each request pays connect overhead (TCP, TLS, auth, a new PostgreSQL backend process). A positive number reuses the connection for that many seconds, removing churn; `None` keeps it forever. The cost of persistence is that every thread holds a connection even when idle, so the total count becomes processes × threads, and a connection broken by a failover is only discovered at use unless `CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS` is on. Recycling happens only at request start/end, so non-request code must call `close_old_connections()`.\n\n**Q: How many database connections can a Django deployment open?**\nA: Django keeps one connection per thread per database alias, opened lazily. So the ceiling for web is pods × Gunicorn workers × threads per worker per alias, plus Celery pods × concurrency, plus beat, cron jobs, management commands and admin sessions. With persistent connections that ceiling is approached even at low load; with `CONN_MAX_AGE = 0` or pooling, only threads that are mid-request hold one. You compare that total against PostgreSQL's `max_connections` minus reserved slots and use PgBouncer when it does not fit.\n\n**Q: What connection pooling options exist for Django on PostgreSQL?**\nA: Django 5.1+ has native pooling with psycopg 3: `\"OPTIONS\": {\"pool\": True}` or a dict of `psycopg_pool` options like `min_size`, `max_size`, `timeout`. It is per process and requires `CONN_MAX_AGE = 0`. Before 5.1 there was no in-process pool, only persistent connections. The external option is PgBouncer, which pools across the whole fleet; transaction mode gives the most multiplexing but forbids session state. Many production setups use both: a small per-process pool or persistent connections to PgBouncer, and PgBouncer to PostgreSQL.\n\n**Q: What breaks when you put PgBouncer in transaction mode in front of Django?**\nA: Anything that assumes the same server connection across transactions: session-level `SET` commands, session advisory locks, `LISTEN/NOTIFY`, temporary tables and server-side cursors. Django uses named server-side cursors for `QuerySet.iterator()` on PostgreSQL, which fail with \"cursor does not exist\" when the next fetch lands on a different backend, so you set `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS = True`. Settings Django applies at connect time should be consistent at the server level. Long transactions also pin server connections, so keep transactions short.\n\n**Q: How do database routers work?**\nA: `DATABASE_ROUTERS` is a list of classes; Django asks each in order and uses the first non-`None` answer. `db_for_read(model, **hints)` and `db_for_write(model, **hints)` choose an alias for reads and writes, `allow_relation(obj1, obj2)` decides whether a relation between objects on different databases is permitted, and `allow_migrate(db, app_label, model_name)` controls which migrations run on which alias. Hints include `instance` for operations on an existing object. Explicit `.using()` and `save(using=...)` override routers.\n\n**Q: How would you set up read replicas in Django?**\nA: Add a second alias for each replica in `DATABASES` (read-only user, `TEST: {\"MIRROR\": \"default\"}` so tests use the primary), and a router whose `db_for_write` returns `\"default\"`, `db_for_read` returns a replica, `allow_relation` returns `True` for objects in the same replica set, and `allow_migrate` only allows the primary. Then deal with lag: read from the primary inside transactions and for a few seconds after a user writes (pinning via cookie or contextvar), and route reports explicitly to replicas with `.using()`. Monitor lag and remove lagging replicas.\n\n**Q: What is the read-your-writes problem with replicas and how do you fix it?**\nA: A user writes to the primary, gets redirected, and the next request reads from a replica that has not yet replayed the change, so the page shows stale data. Replication is asynchronous by default, so lag of milliseconds to seconds is normal. Fixes: pin the user to the primary for a short window after any write (cookie, session or cache flag consulted by the router), always read the user's own data from the primary, read from the primary inside `atomic()`, or in extreme cases use synchronous replication or wait for a specific WAL position. The cheapest robust fix is time-based pinning.\n\n**Q: Can you have a ForeignKey between models on different databases?**\nA: No — Django does not support cross-database relations. The database cannot enforce a constraint across servers and the ORM cannot JOIN across connections, so a router's `allow_relation` should return `False` for such pairs. Store the foreign ID as a plain field (or a FK with `db_constraint=False` if both live in the same physical database under different aliases) and resolve it with a second query. This is the main reason functional splits must be planned along domain boundaries.\n\n**Q: How does Django handle connections in Celery workers or management commands?**\nA: The same per-thread connection model applies, but there are no `request_started`/`request_finished` signals, so `CONN_MAX_AGE` and health checks are never applied automatically. Celery's Django fixup closes stale connections before and after each task. A long-running command or loop should call `django.db.close_old_connections()` periodically so that a connection killed by a failover or idle timeout is replaced. Budget Celery's connections too: each prefork child has its own.\n\n**Q: Your PostgreSQL primary has 900 connections, mostly idle, and new pods fail to connect. Walk me through fixing it. (Senior)**\nA: First quantify from `pg_stat_activity`: grouped by application, state and client address, which shows persistent connections from many threads plus maybe some `idle in transaction`. Short term: reduce `CONN_MAX_AGE` or threads, kill idle-in-transaction sessions, and set `idle_in_transaction_session_timeout`. Structural fix: put PgBouncer in transaction mode in front (with `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS`), size its server pool to what PostgreSQL handles well (on the order of a few times CPU cores for active work), and optionally enable Django 5.1's pool for per-process reuse. Then write the connection budget into the deployment config, budget deploy surges, and alert at 70–80% of `max_connections`.\n\n**Q: Design a router that sends reads to replicas without ever showing users stale data for their own actions. (Senior)**\nA: Writes always go to the primary. Reads go to the primary when the primary connection is inside an atomic block, when the request is a write method, or when the user is pinned; otherwise to a healthy replica. Pinning is set on every write and persisted for a few seconds beyond the expected lag via a cookie (stateless) or a cache key per user; state for the current request lives in a `ContextVar` so it is safe under threads and ASGI. Health checking removes replicas whose lag exceeds a threshold. Celery tasks triggered by a write read from the primary (the task was dispatched on commit, but replicas may lag), and reports use `.using(\"replica\")` explicitly. Tests use `MIRROR` so routing does not change behaviour.\n\n**Q: How would you scale writes beyond a single PostgreSQL primary in a Django app? (Senior)**\nA: First make sure it is really a write problem: batch writes (`bulk_create`, `update()`), move non-critical writes to async pipelines, partition large tables (PostgreSQL declarative partitioning, invisible to the ORM aside from constraints), and scale the primary vertically. If it still does not fit, partition data along a key that most requests are scoped to — usually tenant or user. In Django, implement it with a router reading the shard alias from a `ContextVar` set by middleware, one `DATABASES` alias per shard, `allow_migrate` applying tenant apps to every shard, and a directory (tenant → shard) in a small global database. Accept that cross-shard queries, uniqueness and transactions become application problems, and plan rebalancing from day one — or adopt Citus, which shards PostgreSQL behind one endpoint.\n\n**Q: What are the multi-tenancy options in Django and how do you choose? (Senior)**\nA: Shared schema with a `tenant_id` column on every tenant table, enforced by managers/middleware and ideally PostgreSQL row-level security, is simplest, scales to huge tenant counts and keeps one migration run. Schema-per-tenant (django-tenants sets `search_path` per request) gives stronger isolation and easy per-tenant backup, but migrations run once per schema and catalog bloat limits you to hundreds or low thousands of tenants. Database-per-tenant gives the strongest isolation and noisy-neighbour protection but the highest ops cost. Choose by isolation and compliance requirements, tenant count and size skew; a common hybrid is shared schema for most tenants and dedicated databases for the largest.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Setting / API | Meaning | Default / note |\n|---|---|---|\n| `CONN_MAX_AGE` | Seconds to keep a connection after a request | `0` (close each request); `None` = forever |\n| `CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS` | Ping persistent connection before reuse | `False`; Django 4.1+ |\n| `OPTIONS[\"pool\"]` | Native psycopg 3 pool | Django 5.1+, PostgreSQL only, needs `CONN_MAX_AGE=0` |\n| `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS` | `iterator()` without named cursors | Needed with PgBouncer transaction mode |\n| `DATABASE_ROUTERS` | Routing policy | First non-None answer wins |\n| `db_for_read` / `db_for_write` | Alias per operation | Fallback: instance's `_state.db` or `default` |\n| `allow_relation` / `allow_migrate` | Cross-DB relations / where migrations run | Forbid cross-DB FKs; migrate primary only |\n| `.using()` / `save(using=)` | Explicit alias | Overrides routers |\n| `TEST[\"MIRROR\"]` | Replica alias uses primary's test DB | Prevents empty replica in tests |\n| `close_old_connections()` | Recycle stale/broken connections | Call in non-request loops |\n\n- One connection per thread per alias; count = processes × threads.\n- Persistent connections remove churn but increase idle count.\n- Pool (5.1+) is per process; PgBouncer is fleet-wide.\n- Transaction-mode PgBouncer: no session state, no server-side cursors.\n- Replicas mean lag: pin after writes, primary inside transactions.\n- No FKs or joins across databases.\n- Fix queries and cache before adding replicas; replicas before shards.\n- Write down and alert on the connection budget.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSetup: `python -m venv .venv && pip install \"django~=5.2\" \"psycopg[binary,pool]\"`; run PostgreSQL with `docker run -e POSTGRES_PASSWORD=pw -p 5432:5432 postgres:16`.\n\n1. **Churn vs reuse.** Load-test a view doing one query with `CONN_MAX_AGE=0`, then `60`, then `\"pool\": True`; compare latency and watch `pg_stat_activity` counts.\n2. **Thread math.** Run Gunicorn with `--workers 2 --threads 8` and `CONN_MAX_AGE=None`; hit it concurrently and count connections. Switch to the pool and count again.\n3. **PgBouncer.** Run PgBouncer in transaction mode (e.g. the `edoburu/pgbouncer` image), iterate a large queryset with `.iterator()`, reproduce the cursor error, fix with `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS`.\n4. **Replica router.** Simulate a replica with a second alias pointing at the same DB but a different user; implement the router and log which alias each query uses (`QuerySet.db`). Add pinning middleware and test it with the test client.\n5. **Cross-DB FK.** Put a model in an `analytics` alias with a FK to `User` and observe the error; refactor to a plain `user_id`.\n\n**Mini project:** a multi-tenant notes app with shared-schema tenancy (tenant resolved from subdomain, scoped manager, unique constraints including tenant), a primary/replica router with pinning, PgBouncer in docker-compose, a documented connection budget, and tests proving that a user always sees their own edits immediately.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n- [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) — fix queries before scaling infrastructure.\n- [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) — `atomic(using=...)`, locks on the primary.\n- [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres) — partial indexes, partitioning-friendly constraints.\n- [Ch 29 · Caching in Django](topic.html?p=29-caching) — the cheaper alternative to replicas.\n- [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production) — worker counts drive connection counts.\n- [Database Design · Replication](../database-design/topic.html?p=09-replication), [Database Design · Consistency Models](../database-design/topic.html?p=10-consistency-models), [Database Design · Pools, Timeouts & Storms](../database-design/topic.html?p=20-database-application-architecture), [Database Design · Sharding](../database-design/topic.html?p=12-sharding), [Database Design · Case: Multi-Tenant SaaS](../database-design/topic.html?p=40-case-multi-tenant-saas).\n- [System Design · Database Scaling](../system-design/topic.html?p=16-database-scaling).\n\n- **Databases: persistent connections and pooling** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `CONN_MAX_AGE`, health checks, the PostgreSQL connection pool and server-side cursor notes. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/databases/>\n- **Multiple databases** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · routers, `using()`, cross-database limitations, the primary/replica example. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/multi-db/>\n- **PgBouncer features** — PgBouncer · *Intermediate* · what works in session vs transaction pooling. <https://www.pgbouncer.org/features.html>\n- **psycopg 3 connection pools** — psycopg docs · *Advanced* · pool sizing, timeouts and behaviour. <https://www.psycopg.org/psycopg3/docs/advanced/pool.html>\n- **PostgreSQL: Monitoring Database Activity** — PostgreSQL docs · *Intermediate* · `pg_stat_activity` and replication views. <https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/monitoring-stats.html>\n- **django-tenants** — GitHub · *Advanced* · schema-per-tenant multi-tenancy for PostgreSQL. <https://github.com/django-tenants/django-tenants>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 34.*\n", "35-deployment-production": "# 35 · Deployment & Production Operations\n\n> **In one line:** Production Django is a small fleet of cooperating processes — an application server sized to its workload, a proxy in front, static files off the app server, migrations as a separate release step, and settings, logging, health checks and graceful shutdown designed so that deploys are boring and failures are visible.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings), [Ch 20 · Static & Media Files](topic.html?p=20-static-media-files), [Ch 28 · Security: CSRF, XSS, SQL Injection & Hardening](topic.html?p=28-security), [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi), [Ch 34 · Scaling Django: Connections, Replicas & Multiple Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases).\n\n`python manage.py runserver` is a single-process development server: it auto-reloads, serves static files when `DEBUG=True`, has not been security-audited, and the Django docs say plainly not to use it in production. The first \"deployment\" many people make is exactly that — `runserver 0.0.0.0:8000` on a VM — and it fails in predictable ways: one slow request blocks others, a crash takes the site down until someone SSHes in, `DEBUG=True` leaks settings and stack traces to anyone who triggers an error, and static files are served by Python at a fraction of a proper server's speed.\n\nA production deployment replaces each piece with something designed for the job. An **application server** (Gunicorn for WSGI, uvicorn/Daphne for ASGI) runs several worker processes, restarts crashed ones, and enforces timeouts. A **reverse proxy** (Nginx, or a cloud load balancer / Kubernetes Ingress) terminates TLS, buffers slow clients so they do not tie up Python workers, and serves or forwards static files. **Static files** are collected at build time and served by WhiteNoise, Nginx or a CDN; **media files** go to object storage. **Settings** come from the environment, with `DEBUG=False`, a strict `ALLOWED_HOSTS` and the HTTPS hardening flags. **Migrations** run once per release as a separate step, not on every container start. **Logs** go to stdout as structured lines, **errors** go to Sentry, and **health checks** tell the orchestrator when a process is alive and when it is ready for traffic.\n\nNone of this is exotic, which is exactly why interviewers ask about it: \"how many Gunicorn workers would you run?\", \"why not run migrations in the container entrypoint?\", \"what does `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` do?\", \"how do you deploy without downtime?\". This chapter answers those with the mechanics behind them. Container and cluster depth lives in the [Docker](../docker/topic.html?p=30-lab-production-dockerfile) and [Kubernetes](../kubernetes/topic.html?p=29-lab-zero-downtime-rollout) handbooks.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **WSGI / ASGI application** — `proj.wsgi.application` (`WSGIHandler`) or `proj.asgi.application` (`ASGIHandler`). *Why it matters:* this callable is what the application server imports; choose by whether you need async/WebSockets.\n- **Gunicorn** — pre-fork WSGI server: a master process that forks N workers and supervises them. *Why:* crash recovery, timeouts, graceful reloads and signals for free.\n- **Worker class** — `sync` (default, one request at a time per process), `gthread` (threads per process), `gevent` (greenlets), `uvicorn_worker.UvicornWorker` (ASGI). *Why:* determines concurrency per process and how blocking I/O behaves.\n- **Worker count** — starting point `2 × CPU cores + 1` for sync workers. *Why:* enough to overlap I/O waits without thrashing; tune by measurement.\n- **`--timeout`** — seconds of silence before the master kills a worker (default 30). *Why:* stops hung requests, and is the reason long work belongs in Celery.\n- **`--max-requests` / `--max-requests-jitter`** — recycle a worker after N requests. *Why:* contains slow memory leaks without restarts all at once.\n- **Reverse proxy** — Nginx / ALB / Ingress in front of the app server. *Why:* TLS, buffering slow clients, request size limits, static files, connection limits.\n- **`SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER`** — tells Django which header signals the original request was HTTPS (e.g. `(\"HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO\", \"https\")`). *Why:* behind a TLS-terminating proxy Django otherwise thinks every request is HTTP — redirect loops, insecure URLs, CSRF origin failures.\n- **`DEBUG = False`** — disables debug pages and requires `ALLOWED_HOSTS`. *Why:* debug pages leak settings and code; Django refuses to serve unknown hosts.\n- **`STORAGES`** (4.2+) — the setting configuring the `default` (media) and `staticfiles` storages, replacing `DEFAULT_FILE_STORAGE`/`STATICFILES_STORAGE`. *Why:* manifest-hashed static files and S3 media are configured here.\n- **WhiteNoise** — middleware serving compressed, cache-busted static files from the app. *Why:* simplest correct static serving in containers; pair with a CDN for scale.\n- **Release step** — a one-off job that runs `migrate` (and other one-time tasks) before new code takes traffic. *Why:* concurrent migrations from many containers race and lock tables.\n- **Expand/contract migrations** — split breaking schema changes into backwards-compatible steps across deploys. *Why:* during a rolling deploy, old and new code run against the same schema.\n- **Liveness vs readiness probes** — \"is the process alive?\" vs \"can it serve traffic now?\". *Why:* a DB outage should stop traffic (readiness), not restart every pod (liveness).\n- **Graceful shutdown** — on SIGTERM, stop accepting, finish in-flight requests, exit. *Why:* zero-downtime deploys depend on it.\n- **Structured logging** — JSON lines to stdout via `LOGGING`. *Why:* containers collect stdout; JSON is searchable.\n- **Sentry** — error tracking with stack traces, request context and releases. *Why:* emails from `ADMINS` do not scale; you need grouping, alerting and release correlation.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### How Gunicorn serves Django\n\nWhen you run `gunicorn proj.wsgi:application --workers 5`, the **master** process imports nothing by default (unless `--preload`), binds the socket, and forks five **workers**. Each worker imports `proj.wsgi`, which calls `get_wsgi_application()` → `django.setup()` (load settings, configure logging, populate the app registry) and builds a `WSGIHandler`, whose `BaseHandler.load_middleware()` builds the middleware chain once. Each worker then loops: `accept()` a connection from the shared socket, parse the HTTP request, call `application(environ, start_response)`, write the response. With the sync worker class, one worker handles exactly one request at a time.\n\nThe master supervises: workers send a heartbeat by touching a temp file; if a worker is silent for longer than `--timeout`, the master kills it (you see `WORKER TIMEOUT` and the request gets a 502 from the proxy) and forks a replacement. On SIGTERM, the master stops accepting and gives workers up to `--graceful-timeout` (default 30 s) to finish; on SIGHUP it reloads configuration and replaces workers gracefully.\n\n**Sizing.** For sync workers the classic formula `2 × cores + 1` assumes requests are a mix of CPU and I/O waits (database, cache): while one worker waits on PostgreSQL, another uses the CPU. It is a starting point, not a law. Measure: if CPU is saturated at peak, more workers will not help; if CPU is idle while requests queue (most time in DB/HTTP waits), add workers or threads (`gthread`: e.g. 4 workers × 4 threads). Remember each worker is a full Python process with Django loaded (commonly on the order of 100–300 MB), and each thread holds its own database connection — so worker count drives both memory and the connection budget from [Ch 34](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases). In containers, `cores` means the CPU limit of the container, not the host's core count.\n\n**ASGI.** For async views or Channels, run uvicorn workers under Gunicorn — `gunicorn proj.asgi:application -k uvicorn_worker.UvicornWorker` (the `uvicorn-worker` package; older docs use `uvicorn.workers.UvicornWorker`) — or uvicorn directly with `--workers`. Each process runs an event loop and handles many concurrent connections; sync views still run in a thread via `sync_to_async`, so ASGI is not automatically faster for a sync codebase.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 420\" width=\"100%\" height=\"420\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj35a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Production request path: proxy, Gunicorn master and workers, backing services</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"170\" width=\"100\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"70\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Client</text>\n  <text x=\"70\" y=\"213\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">HTTPS</text>\n  <rect x=\"150\" y=\"60\" width=\"170\" height=\"280\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nginx / LB / Ingress</text>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">TLS termination</text>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">buffers slow clients</text>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"144\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">client_max_body_size</text>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"162\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">sets X-Forwarded-Proto</text>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">and X-Forwarded-For</text>\n  <rect x=\"170\" y=\"200\" width=\"130\" height=\"50\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"221\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">/static/ via CDN</text>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"238\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">or WhiteNoise</text>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">health checks</text>\n  <text x=\"235\" y=\"308\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">drains on deploy</text>\n  <rect x=\"360\" y=\"50\" width=\"300\" height=\"300\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"510\" y=\"74\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Gunicorn (one container / pod)</text>\n  <rect x=\"380\" y=\"88\" width=\"260\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"510\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">master</text>\n  <text x=\"510\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">binds :8000, forks, kills silent workers</text>\n  <text x=\"510\" y=\"139\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">(--timeout 30), SIGTERM: graceful</text>\n  <rect x=\"380\" y=\"160\" width=\"120\" height=\"80\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">worker 1</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"198\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">django.setup()</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"214\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">WSGIHandler</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">1 req (sync)</text>\n  <rect x=\"520\" y=\"160\" width=\"120\" height=\"80\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"580\" y=\"180\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">worker N</text>\n  <text x=\"580\" y=\"198\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">2 x cores + 1</text>\n  <text x=\"580\" y=\"214\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">or gthread</text>\n  <text x=\"580\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">or uvicorn</text>\n  <text x=\"510\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">each worker: own memory, own DB connection(s)</text>\n  <text x=\"510\" y=\"290\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">--max-requests 1000 --max-requests-jitter 100</text>\n  <text x=\"510\" y=\"310\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">logs to stdout (JSON)</text>\n  <rect x=\"700\" y=\"60\" width=\"160\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"780\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">PostgreSQL / PgBouncer</text>\n  <rect x=\"700\" y=\"125\" width=\"160\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"780\" y=\"155\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Redis (cache, broker)</text>\n  <rect x=\"700\" y=\"190\" width=\"160\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"780\" y=\"220\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">S3 media (STORAGES)</text>\n  <rect x=\"700\" y=\"255\" width=\"160\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"780\" y=\"285\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\">Sentry / log pipeline</text>\n  <path d=\"M120,200 L148,200\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj35a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M320,200 L358,200\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj35a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M660,90 L698,85\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj35a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M660,150 L698,150\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj35a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M660,215 L698,215\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj35a)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M660,280 L698,280\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj35a)\"/>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"385\" fill=\"#334155\">Without the proxy's buffering, a client on a slow mobile link holds a sync worker for the whole upload or download;</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"403\" fill=\"#334155\">with it, the worker hands the response to Nginx in milliseconds and moves on.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Why the proxy and `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` matter\n\nTLS ends at the proxy, which then speaks plain HTTP to Gunicorn. From Django's point of view, `request.is_secure()` is `False` and `request.build_absolute_uri()` produces `http://` URLs. With `SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT = True`, Django redirects to HTTPS, the proxy forwards it as HTTP again, and you have an infinite redirect loop. CSRF origin checks against `https://` trusted origins fail too. Setting `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER = (\"HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO\", \"https\")` tells Django to trust that header — **only safe if your proxy always sets or strips it**, otherwise any client can claim HTTPS. Similarly, `REMOTE_ADDR` is the proxy's IP; the real client IP is in `X-Forwarded-For`, which you parse taking into account how many trusted proxies are in front (never trust the leftmost value blindly — clients can forge it).\n\n### Migrations in a rolling deploy\n\nDuring a rolling update, old pods and new pods serve traffic at the same time, both against the same database. That has two consequences. First, migrations must run **exactly once, before** the new code serves traffic — as a Kubernetes Job, an init step in the pipeline, or a Heroku-style release phase — not in every container's entrypoint, where ten pods would race (Django's migration recorder is not a distributed lock, and on PostgreSQL concurrent DDL either blocks or fails). Second, every migration must be **compatible with the old code** still running. Dropping or renaming a column the old code reads breaks it mid-deploy. The **expand/contract** pattern solves it: release 1 adds the new column (nullable or with `db_default`) and writes both; a backfill copies data; release 2 reads the new column; release 3 removes the old field from the model and drops the column. See [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations) and [Database Design · Schema Evolution](../database-design/topic.html?p=27-schema-evolution).\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### The CI/CD pipeline\n\nThe pipeline below is the release sequence you should be able to describe in an interview, with the migrate step separated from the rollout.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 360\" width=\"100%\" height=\"360\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj35b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#334155\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Build once, migrate once, roll out gradually</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"150\" height=\"120\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. CI</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">lint, tests</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"112\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">makemigrations --check</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">check --deploy</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">pip-audit</text>\n  <rect x=\"195\" y=\"50\" width=\"150\" height=\"120\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. Build image</text>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">multi-stage, non-root</text>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"112\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">collectstatic at build</text>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">tag = git SHA</text>\n  <text x=\"270\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">push to registry</text>\n  <rect x=\"370\" y=\"50\" width=\"150\" height=\"120\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"445\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">3. Release job</text>\n  <text x=\"445\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">same image</text>\n  <text x=\"445\" y=\"112\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">manage.py migrate</text>\n  <text x=\"445\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">runs exactly once</text>\n  <text x=\"445\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">fail: stop pipeline</text>\n  <rect x=\"545\" y=\"50\" width=\"150\" height=\"120\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"620\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">4. Rolling update</text>\n  <text x=\"620\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">new pod starts</text>\n  <text x=\"620\" y=\"112\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">readiness OK: traffic</text>\n  <text x=\"620\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">old pod: SIGTERM,</text>\n  <text x=\"620\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">drain, exit</text>\n  <rect x=\"720\" y=\"50\" width=\"140\" height=\"120\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\"/>\n  <text x=\"790\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">5. Verify</text>\n  <text x=\"790\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">Sentry release</text>\n  <text x=\"790\" y=\"112\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">error rate, p95</text>\n  <text x=\"790\" y=\"130\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">auto rollback</text>\n  <text x=\"790\" y=\"148\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">to previous SHA</text>\n  <path d=\"M170,110 L193,110\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj35b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M345,110 L368,110\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj35b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M520,110 L543,110\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj35b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M695,110 L718,110\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj35b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"200\" width=\"840\" height=\"140\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f1f5f9\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"40\" y=\"222\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">During step 4 old and new code share one schema, so every migration must be backwards compatible</text>\n  <rect x=\"40\" y=\"236\" width=\"190\" height=\"80\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"135\" y=\"256\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deploy A: expand</text>\n  <text x=\"135\" y=\"274\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">add nullable column</text>\n  <text x=\"135\" y=\"292\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">code writes old + new</text>\n  <rect x=\"250\" y=\"236\" width=\"190\" height=\"80\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"345\" y=\"256\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Backfill</text>\n  <text x=\"345\" y=\"274\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">batched, in Celery or</text>\n  <text x=\"345\" y=\"292\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">a management command</text>\n  <rect x=\"460\" y=\"236\" width=\"190\" height=\"80\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"555\" y=\"256\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deploy B: switch</text>\n  <text x=\"555\" y=\"274\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">read new column</text>\n  <text x=\"555\" y=\"292\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">stop using old one</text>\n  <rect x=\"670\" y=\"236\" width=\"170\" height=\"80\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"256\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Deploy C: contract</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"274\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">remove field,</text>\n  <text x=\"755\" y=\"292\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\">drop column</text>\n  <path d=\"M230,276 L248,276\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj35b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M440,276 L458,276\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj35b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M650,276 L668,276\" stroke=\"#334155\" stroke-width=\"1.5\" marker-end=\"url(#dj35b)\"/>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### The processes you actually run\n\n```text\nrole          command                                                         replicas   scales on\nweb           gunicorn proj.wsgi -c gunicorn.conf.py                          2..N       CPU / latency\nasgi (opt.)   gunicorn proj.asgi -k uvicorn_worker.UvicornWorker              2..N       connections\nworker        celery -A proj worker -Q default -c 8                           1..N       queue depth\nbeat          celery -A proj beat                                             exactly 1  -\nrelease       python manage.py migrate --noinput                              1 per deploy (Job)\ncron          python manage.py clearsessions                                  scheduled Job\n```\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Simple example: production settings\n\n```python\n# proj/settings/prod.py\nimport os\nfrom .base import *  # noqa\n\nDEBUG = False\nSECRET_KEY = os.environ[\"DJANGO_SECRET_KEY\"]                 # fail fast if missing\nALLOWED_HOSTS = os.environ[\"DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS\"].split(\",\")  # \"shop.example.com,.internal\"\nCSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS = [\"https://shop.example.com\"]          # scheme required (4.0+)\n\n# Behind a TLS-terminating proxy that ALWAYS sets X-Forwarded-Proto\nSECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER = (\"HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO\", \"https\")\nSECURE_SSL_REDIRECT = True\nSESSION_COOKIE_SECURE = True\nCSRF_COOKIE_SECURE = True\nSECURE_HSTS_SECONDS = 60 * 60 * 24 * 365   # start low (e.g. 3600) and raise once verified\nSECURE_HSTS_INCLUDE_SUBDOMAINS = True\nSECURE_HSTS_PRELOAD = True\nSECURE_CONTENT_TYPE_NOSNIFF = True          # default True\nSECURE_REFERRER_POLICY = \"same-origin\"\nSECURE_REDIRECT_EXEMPT = [r\"^healthz/$\", r\"^readyz/$\"]   # probes hit pods over HTTP\n\nSTATIC_ROOT = BASE_DIR / \"staticfiles\"\nSTATIC_URL = \"/static/\"\nSTORAGES = {\n    \"default\": {\"BACKEND\": \"storages.backends.s3.S3Storage\"},           # django-storages\n    \"staticfiles\": {\"BACKEND\": \"whitenoise.storage.CompressedManifestStaticFilesStorage\"},\n}\n\nMIDDLEWARE.insert(1, \"whitenoise.middleware.WhiteNoiseMiddleware\")  # right after SecurityMiddleware\n\nDATABASES[\"default\"][\"CONN_MAX_AGE\"] = 60\nDATABASES[\"default\"][\"CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS\"] = True\n\nCACHES = {\"default\": {\"BACKEND\": \"django.core.cache.backends.redis.RedisCache\",\n                      \"LOCATION\": os.environ[\"REDIS_URL\"]}}\n```\n\n```bash\nDJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=proj.settings.prod python manage.py check --deploy --fail-level WARNING\n```\n\n```text\nSystem check identified no issues (0 silenced).\n```\n\n### Gunicorn configuration\n\n```python\n# gunicorn.conf.py\nimport multiprocessing\nimport os\n\nbind = \"0.0.0.0:8000\"\ncpu = int(os.environ.get(\"WEB_CPU\", multiprocessing.cpu_count()))   # set to the container CPU limit\nworkers = int(os.environ.get(\"WEB_CONCURRENCY\", cpu * 2 + 1))\nworker_class = \"gthread\"\nthreads = int(os.environ.get(\"WEB_THREADS\", 2))\ntimeout = 30                 # kill a worker silent for 30 s\ngraceful_timeout = 30        # time to finish in-flight requests on SIGTERM\nkeepalive = 75               # must exceed the load balancer's idle timeout (see gotcha)\nmax_requests = 1000\nmax_requests_jitter = 100    # avoid all workers recycling at once\naccesslog = \"-\"              # stdout\nerrorlog = \"-\"\nforwarded_allow_ips = \"*\"    # trust X-Forwarded-* from the proxy network only (restrict if possible)\npreload_app = False          # True saves memory via copy-on-write but breaks per-worker init and reloads\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** if a cloud load balancer keeps idle connections to Gunicorn longer than Gunicorn's `keepalive`, Gunicorn closes them first and the LB occasionally sends a request on a closed socket, producing sporadic 502s. Set the app server's keep-alive *longer* than the load balancer's idle timeout, or disable keep-alive between them.\n\n### Nginx in front (VM deployments)\n\n```text\nupstream django { server 127.0.0.1:8000; keepalive 32; }\n\nserver {\n    listen 443 ssl http2;\n    server_name shop.example.com;\n    ssl_certificate     /etc/letsencrypt/live/shop/fullchain.pem;\n    ssl_certificate_key /etc/letsencrypt/live/shop/privkey.pem;\n\n    client_max_body_size 20m;\n\n    location /static/ {\n        alias /srv/shop/staticfiles/;\n        expires 1y;\n        add_header Cache-Control \"public, immutable\";   # safe: ManifestStaticFilesStorage hashes names\n    }\n\n    location / {\n        proxy_pass http://django;\n        proxy_http_version 1.1;\n        proxy_set_header Connection \"\";\n        proxy_set_header Host $host;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-For $proxy_add_x_forwarded_for;\n        proxy_set_header X-Forwarded-Proto $scheme;     # overwrites any client-sent value\n        proxy_read_timeout 35s;                         # a bit above Gunicorn's timeout\n    }\n}\nserver { listen 80; server_name shop.example.com; return 301 https://$host$request_uri; }\n```\n\n### Real-world example: container image, health checks, logging, Sentry\n\n```text\n# Dockerfile\nFROM python:3.12-slim AS build\nENV PIP_NO_CACHE_DIR=1 PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1\nWORKDIR /app\nRUN pip install --upgrade pip\nCOPY requirements.txt .\nRUN pip wheel --wheel-dir /wheels -r requirements.txt\n\nFROM python:3.12-slim\nENV PYTHONUNBUFFERED=1 PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=proj.settings.prod\nRUN useradd --create-home --uid 10001 app\nWORKDIR /app\nCOPY --from=build /wheels /wheels\nRUN pip install --no-cache-dir /wheels/* && rm -rf /wheels\nCOPY --chown=app:app . .\n# collectstatic needs settings importable without real secrets\nRUN DJANGO_SECRET_KEY=build DJANGO_ALLOWED_HOSTS=localhost REDIS_URL=redis://x \\\n    python manage.py collectstatic --noinput\nUSER app\nEXPOSE 8000\nCMD [\"gunicorn\", \"proj.wsgi:application\", \"-c\", \"gunicorn.conf.py\"]\n```\n\nThe image runs only the web server. Migrations run from the same image as a separate command (`python manage.py migrate --noinput`) in a release job. More in [Docker · Multi-stage Builds](../docker/topic.html?p=07-multistage-builds) and [Docker · Lab: Production Dockerfile](../docker/topic.html?p=30-lab-production-dockerfile).\n\n```python\n# core/health.py\nfrom django.core.cache import cache\nfrom django.db import connection\nfrom django.http import JsonResponse\n\ndef healthz(request):\n    \"\"\"Liveness: the process can run Python and answer. No dependencies.\"\"\"\n    return JsonResponse({\"status\": \"ok\"})\n\ndef readyz(request):\n    \"\"\"Readiness: can this pod serve real traffic right now?\"\"\"\n    checks = {}\n    try:\n        with connection.cursor() as cur:\n            cur.execute(\"SELECT 1\")\n        checks[\"db\"] = \"ok\"\n    except Exception as exc:          # noqa: BLE001\n        checks[\"db\"] = f\"error: {exc.__class__.__name__}\"\n    try:\n        cache.set(\"readyz\", \"1\", 5)\n        checks[\"cache\"] = \"ok\"\n    except Exception as exc:          # noqa: BLE001\n        checks[\"cache\"] = f\"error: {exc.__class__.__name__}\"\n    ok = all(v == \"ok\" for v in checks.values())\n    return JsonResponse({\"status\": \"ok\" if ok else \"degraded\", **checks}, status=200 if ok else 503)\n```\n\n```python\n# proj/urls.py (excerpt)\nfrom core.health import healthz, readyz\nurlpatterns += [path(\"healthz/\", healthz), path(\"readyz/\", readyz)]\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** probes connect to the pod IP with a `Host` header like `10.1.2.3:8000`, which fails `ALLOWED_HOSTS` with a 400 (`DisallowedHost`). Either configure the probe's `Host` header, add the pod network pattern, or serve health endpoints from a tiny middleware placed before `CommonMiddleware`'s host validation. Also exempt them from `SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT` as above.\n\n```python\n# proj/settings/prod.py — logging: JSON to stdout\nLOGGING = {\n    \"version\": 1,\n    \"disable_existing_loggers\": False,\n    \"formatters\": {\n        \"json\": {\"()\": \"pythonjsonlogger.json.JsonFormatter\",\n                 \"fmt\": \"%(asctime)s %(levelname)s %(name)s %(message)s\"},\n    },\n    \"handlers\": {\"stdout\": {\"class\": \"logging.StreamHandler\", \"formatter\": \"json\"}},\n    \"root\": {\"handlers\": [\"stdout\"], \"level\": \"INFO\"},\n    \"loggers\": {\n        \"django.request\": {\"level\": \"WARNING\"},          # 4xx/5xx details\n        \"django.security\": {\"level\": \"WARNING\"},         # DisallowedHost, CSRF failures\n        \"django.db.backends\": {\"level\": \"WARNING\"},      # never DEBUG in prod: logs every SQL\n    },\n}\n```\n\n```python\n# proj/settings/prod.py — Sentry\nimport sentry_sdk\n\nsentry_sdk.init(\n    dsn=os.environ.get(\"SENTRY_DSN\"),\n    environment=os.environ.get(\"APP_ENV\", \"production\"),\n    release=os.environ.get(\"GIT_SHA\"),       # correlate errors with deploys\n    traces_sample_rate=0.05,                  # sample performance traces\n    send_default_pii=False,\n)\n```\n\nThe Django and Celery integrations are enabled automatically when the packages are installed.\n\n### Kubernetes essentials\n\n```text\n# deployment.yaml (excerpt)\nspec:\n  replicas: 4\n  strategy: {type: RollingUpdate, rollingUpdate: {maxSurge: 1, maxUnavailable: 0}}\n  template:\n    spec:\n      terminationGracePeriodSeconds: 45\n      containers:\n      - name: web\n        image: registry.example.com/shop:3f9c2ab\n        envFrom: [{secretRef: {name: shop-env}}]\n        resources: {requests: {cpu: \"1\", memory: 768Mi}, limits: {cpu: \"2\", memory: 1Gi}}\n        env: [{name: WEB_CPU, value: \"2\"}]\n        readinessProbe: {httpGet: {path: /readyz/, port: 8000}, periodSeconds: 5}\n        livenessProbe:  {httpGet: {path: /healthz/, port: 8000}, periodSeconds: 10, failureThreshold: 6}\n        lifecycle:\n          preStop: {exec: {command: [\"sleep\", \"10\"]}}   # let endpoints/LB deregister first\n```\n\nThe `preStop` sleep matters: when a pod is terminated, removal from Service endpoints and SIGTERM happen concurrently. Without a short sleep, Gunicorn may stop accepting while the load balancer still sends requests, causing connection-refused errors during every deploy. See [Kubernetes · Probes & Health](../kubernetes/topic.html?p=22-probes-health) and [Kubernetes · Rollouts](../kubernetes/topic.html?p=21-rollouts).\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Choice | Pros | Cons | Use when |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Gunicorn sync workers | Simple, robust, predictable | One request per process; slow I/O blocks workers | CPU-light, DB-bound apps with short requests |\n| Gunicorn gthread | More concurrency per MB of RAM | GIL; thread-safety of your code and libs | I/O-heavy sync apps |\n| Gunicorn gevent | Thousands of concurrent I/O waits | Monkey-patching surprises; C drivers may block | Many outbound HTTP calls |\n| uvicorn (ASGI) | Async views, WebSockets, streaming | Sync code still threads; ecosystem caveats | Async or Channels workloads |\n| WhiteNoise | No extra service, correct cache headers | App processes serve bytes (fine behind a CDN) | Containers, PaaS |\n| Nginx static | Fast, sendfile | Shared volume between build and Nginx | VM deployments |\n| CDN + S3 | Global edge, offloads origin | Invalidation, cost, CORS for fonts | Public sites at scale |\n| PaaS (Heroku-like, Render, Fly) | Release phase, logs, TLS built in | Less control, cost at scale | Small teams |\n| Kubernetes | Rollouts, autoscaling, jobs | Operational complexity | Many services, platform team |\n\n### When to use\n\n- Gunicorn + Nginx/LB is the default for WSGI Django; add uvicorn workers only for async needs.\n- WhiteNoise for static files in containers; a CDN in front for high traffic.\n- A release step for migrations on every platform.\n- Readiness checks that test the database; liveness checks that do not.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- `runserver` in production, ever.\n- `--preload` when workers need per-process state initialised after fork, or when you rely on graceful code reloads with SIGHUP.\n- Liveness probes that hit the database (an outage restarts every pod and turns degradation into downtime).\n- Huge Gunicorn timeouts to \"fix\" slow endpoints — move the work to Celery.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**`DEBUG = True` in production.** Leaks settings, SQL and source in error pages, and Django stores every query in memory (`connection.queries`), which grows per request. Read `DEBUG` from the environment with a safe default of `False`.\n\n**Running migrations in the entrypoint.** Every pod runs `migrate` at start; parallel runs block each other on locks or fail, and a long migration delays pod readiness so the rollout times out. Use a release job.\n\n**Non-backwards-compatible migrations.** Renaming a field in one deploy breaks old pods still serving traffic. Use expand/contract; Django's `RenameField` is a breaking change for running code.\n\n**Wrong worker count in containers.** `multiprocessing.cpu_count()` returns the host's cores (e.g. 64) inside a container limited to 2 CPUs, so Gunicorn starts 129 workers and the pod OOMs. Set `WEB_CONCURRENCY` from the CPU limit.\n\n**Trusting `X-Forwarded-For`/`X-Forwarded-Proto` from anywhere.** If the app is reachable without the proxy, clients can forge them. Ensure only the proxy can reach Gunicorn and that it overwrites the headers.\n\n**Secrets in the image or repository.** `SECRET_KEY` committed to git or baked into a Docker layer. Inject from the environment / secret manager at runtime ([Docker · Secrets](../docker/topic.html?p=24-secrets), [Kubernetes · ConfigMaps & Secrets](../kubernetes/topic.html?p=05-configmaps-secrets)).\n\n**Serving media from the app server's disk.** Pods are ephemeral and not shared; uploads disappear or are visible on one pod only. Use object storage via `STORAGES[\"default\"]`.\n\n**Logging SQL in production.** `django.db.backends` at DEBUG logs every query (only when DEBUG is on, but people force it) — huge volume and possibly sensitive data.\n\n**Best practices**\n\n- One image, many roles: web, worker, beat, release job all from the same tag.\n- Twelve-factor config: everything that varies between environments comes from env vars.\n- `check --deploy` and `makemigrations --check` in CI.\n- Tag images with the git SHA and pass it to Sentry as `release`.\n- Recycle workers with `max_requests` + jitter; keep timeouts tight.\n- Run `clearsessions` periodically if you use database sessions.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n> **Production story:** Every deploy produced a burst of 502s for about ten seconds. Root cause: on SIGTERM, Gunicorn stopped accepting immediately while the cloud load balancer still routed to the pod for several seconds. Fix: a `preStop` sleep of 10 s, `terminationGracePeriodSeconds` greater than the sleep plus Gunicorn's `graceful_timeout`, and `maxUnavailable: 0`.\n\n**`WORKER TIMEOUT` and 502s under load.** Symptom: Gunicorn logs `[CRITICAL] WORKER TIMEOUT (pid:...)`, Nginx logs `upstream prematurely closed connection`. Root cause: requests exceeding `--timeout` — slow queries, external API calls without timeouts, or all workers busy so requests queue. Fix: find the slow endpoints (APM/Sentry performance, slow query log), add timeouts to every outbound call, move long work to Celery, and only then revisit worker counts.\n\n**`DisallowedHost` flooding the logs.** Scanners hit the IP with random `Host` headers. That is `ALLOWED_HOSTS` working; filter the `django.security.DisallowedHost` logger and reject unknown hosts at the proxy (a default `server` block returning 444).\n\n**Static files 404 after deploy with `DEBUG=False`.** `collectstatic` did not run, or `ManifestStaticFilesStorage` raises `ValueError: Missing staticfiles manifest entry` for a file referenced in a template but not collected. Run `collectstatic` in the build and fail the build on errors.\n\n**Memory creeping until OOM kill.** A leak in a library or caching in module globals. `max_requests` with jitter contains it; find it with `tracemalloc` or memray in staging.\n\n**Mixed-content and redirect loops.** Missing `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` (loops with `SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT`) or a proxy that does not set `X-Forwarded-Proto`. Check `request.is_secure()` in a debug endpoint in staging.\n\n**HSTS lockout.** Enabling `SECURE_HSTS_SECONDS = 31536000` with `include_subdomains` while some subdomain lacks HTTPS makes it unreachable for browsers for a year. Start with a small value and raise it.\n\n**Debugging toolkit**\n\n- `py-spy dump --pid <worker pid>` shows what a stuck worker is doing without restarting it; `py-spy top` for live profiling.\n- Gunicorn access logs with request time (`%(D)s` in `access_log_format`) to spot slow endpoints.\n- Sentry issues grouped per release to tell whether a deploy introduced errors.\n- `manage.py shell` inside a running pod (`kubectl exec`) with production settings — read-only investigations only.\n- Correlation IDs: a middleware that reads or creates `X-Request-ID`, adds it to logs and returns it in the response.\n\n**Scaling.** Scale horizontally first: more pods behind the load balancer, since Django web processes are stateless if sessions and cache live in Redis/DB and media in S3. Autoscale on CPU or request latency ([Kubernetes · Autoscaling](../kubernetes/topic.html?p=20-autoscaling)), watching the database connection budget as replicas grow. Put a CDN in front of static assets and cacheable pages. Separate traffic classes (public API, admin, webhooks) into different deployments when one can starve another.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: Why shouldn't you use `runserver` in production?**\nA: It is a development server: single process with threads, an auto-reloader, static file serving tied to `DEBUG`, and no hardening or security audit — the Django docs explicitly say not to use it in production. It has no worker supervision, no timeouts for hung requests and no graceful restarts. A production setup uses Gunicorn or uvicorn behind a reverse proxy that handles TLS, buffering and static files. `runserver` is for local development only.\n\n**Q: How many Gunicorn workers would you run, and how do you decide?**\nA: The documented starting point for sync workers is `2 × CPU cores + 1`, where cores means the CPUs actually available to the container. Then measure: if CPU saturates at peak, more workers will not help; if CPU is low but requests queue because workers wait on the database or external APIs, add workers or switch to `gthread` with a few threads. Constraints are memory (each worker is a full Django process) and database connections (each worker thread may hold one). Long-running work does not belong in web workers at all — it goes to Celery.\n\n**Q: What are Gunicorn's `--timeout` and `--max-requests` for?**\nA: `--timeout` (default 30 seconds) is how long a worker may be silent before the master kills and replaces it; it protects you from hung requests but also kills legitimately slow ones, which the proxy turns into 502s. `--max-requests` restarts a worker after it has served N requests, which contains slow memory leaks; `--max-requests-jitter` randomises that number so workers do not all restart at once. Both are safety nets: the real fixes are bounding slow work and finding leaks.\n\n**Q: What does `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` do and when is it dangerous?**\nA: Behind a TLS-terminating proxy, Django receives plain HTTP and `request.is_secure()` is `False`, causing redirect loops with `SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT`, `http://` absolute URLs and CSRF origin mismatches. Setting `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER = (\"HTTP_X_FORWARDED_PROTO\", \"https\")` makes Django treat requests with that header as secure. It is dangerous when clients can reach Django directly or the proxy passes through a client-supplied header, because then anyone can claim HTTPS. Only set it when the proxy always overwrites the header and is the only path to the app.\n\n**Q: Why should migrations run as a separate release step instead of in the container entrypoint?**\nA: With many replicas, every container would run `migrate` at the same time; Django has no distributed lock for migrations, so they block each other on DDL locks or fail mid-way, and long migrations delay readiness until the rollout times out. A single release job (Kubernetes Job, pipeline step, PaaS release phase) runs once from the same image before new pods take traffic and stops the deploy if it fails. Migrations must also be backwards compatible, because old pods keep serving during the rollout.\n\n**Q: How do you serve static files in production?**\nA: Run `collectstatic` at build time to gather files into `STATIC_ROOT` with hashed filenames (`ManifestStaticFilesStorage` or WhiteNoise's `CompressedManifestStaticFilesStorage`, configured in `STORAGES[\"staticfiles\"]`). Then serve them with WhiteNoise middleware (simplest in containers), Nginx from a shared directory, or upload to S3 behind a CDN. Hashed names allow far-future cache headers because a content change changes the URL. Never rely on `django.contrib.staticfiles` serving with `DEBUG=True`.\n\n**Q: What settings do you change for production?**\nA: `DEBUG = False`; `SECRET_KEY` from the environment; explicit `ALLOWED_HOSTS` and `CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS` with schemes; HTTPS hardening (`SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT`, `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` behind a proxy, `SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE`, `CSRF_COOKIE_SECURE`, HSTS); `STORAGES` for static and media; persistent connections or pooling; a shared cache like Redis rather than locmem; JSON logging to stdout; and error tracking such as Sentry. Run `manage.py check --deploy` in CI to catch omissions.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between liveness and readiness probes for a Django app?**\nA: Liveness answers \"is the process healthy enough to keep running?\" and should be cheap and dependency-free — if it fails, the orchestrator restarts the container. Readiness answers \"can this instance serve traffic now?\" and can check the database and cache — if it fails, the instance is removed from the load balancer but not restarted. Putting the database in the liveness check means a database blip restarts every pod at once, turning a partial outage into a full one and causing a reconnection storm.\n\n**Q: How do you get the real client IP in Django behind a proxy?**\nA: `REMOTE_ADDR` is the proxy's address. The proxy appends the client address to `X-Forwarded-For`, so you read the value added by your trusted proxy — counting from the right by the number of proxies you control — not the leftmost value, which clients can forge. Django does not parse it for you (the old `SetRemoteAddrFromForwardedFor` middleware was removed); use a small middleware or a library such as django-ipware configured with your proxy count. Rate limiting and audit logs depend on getting this right.\n\n**Q: How do you deploy a Django app with zero downtime? (Senior)**\nA: Build an immutable image once, run backwards-compatible migrations in a release job, then do a rolling update with `maxUnavailable: 0` so capacity never drops. New pods only receive traffic once readiness passes; old pods get a `preStop` delay so the load balancer deregisters them, then SIGTERM triggers Gunicorn's graceful shutdown within `graceful_timeout`, all inside `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`. Schema changes follow expand/contract across releases so old and new code coexist. Celery workers drain with warm shutdown and tasks are idempotent with `acks_late`; static assets have hashed names so old and new pages both find their files. Finally, watch error rate and latency per release and roll back automatically on regression.\n\n**Q: You need to rename a heavily used column on a 200-million-row table. How do you ship it? (Senior)**\nA: Never with a plain `RenameField` in one deploy, because pods running old code will query the old name during the rollout. Expand: add the new column as nullable (fast on PostgreSQL), and deploy code that writes both columns. Backfill in batches (by primary key ranges, in a management command or Celery task, with sleeps to limit replication lag and lock time). Then deploy code that reads the new column, add constraints with `NOT VALID` then `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT`, or build indexes with `AddIndexConcurrently` in a non-atomic migration. Finally contract: remove the old field from the model and drop the column in a later release. Alternatively keep the DB column name and change only the Python name via `db_column`, which needs no data movement.\n\n**Q: Your Django pods get OOM-killed under traffic spikes. How do you investigate and fix it? (Senior)**\nA: Check how many workers each pod starts — a common cause is `cpu_count()` seeing the host's cores, spawning far too many processes — and set `WEB_CONCURRENCY` from the CPU limit. Then look at per-request memory: views that load whole querysets into lists, large file uploads held in memory (`FILE_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE`), unbounded serializer responses, or `DEBUG=True` accumulating `connection.queries`. Profile with memray or tracemalloc in staging under replayed traffic, stream big responses, use `iterator()` and pagination, and use `max_requests` with jitter to contain leaks. Finally set memory requests/limits from measured usage and autoscale on load rather than surviving spikes by luck.\n\n**Q: How would you set up observability for a production Django service? (Senior)**\nA: Three signals. Logs: JSON to stdout with request ID, user ID, path, status and duration, shipped to a central store; `django.request` and `django.security` at WARNING. Metrics: request rate, error rate and latency percentiles per endpoint (django-prometheus or OpenTelemetry), Gunicorn worker saturation, database connections and slow queries, Celery queue depth. Traces and errors: Sentry or OpenTelemetry tracing with a sampled rate, releases tagged with the git SHA so errors correlate with deploys. Alerts on SLOs (error rate, p95 latency) rather than raw CPU, and dashboards per release for quick rollback decisions.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Area | Setting / tool | Rule |\n|---|---|---|\n| App server | `gunicorn proj.wsgi:application` | Never `runserver` |\n| Workers | `2 × cores + 1` (sync) | Cores = container CPU limit; measure |\n| Timeouts | `--timeout 30`, `--graceful-timeout 30` | Slow work goes to Celery |\n| Leaks | `--max-requests 1000 --max-requests-jitter 100` | Recycle gradually |\n| ASGI | `-k uvicorn_worker.UvicornWorker` | Only if you need async |\n| Proxy | Nginx / LB / Ingress | TLS, buffering, body size |\n| HTTPS behind proxy | `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` | Only if proxy overwrites the header |\n| Hosts | `ALLOWED_HOSTS`, `CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS` (with scheme) | Required with `DEBUG=False` |\n| Static | `collectstatic` at build + WhiteNoise / CDN | Hashed names, long cache |\n| Media | `STORAGES[\"default\"]` → S3 | Never pod disk |\n| Migrations | Release job, expand/contract | Exactly once, backwards compatible |\n| Probes | `/healthz` (no deps), `/readyz` (DB, cache) | DB never in liveness |\n| Shutdown | `preStop` sleep + graceful timeout | Grace period > sleep + drain |\n| Errors | Sentry with `release=GIT_SHA` | Correlate with deploys |\n| Check | `manage.py check --deploy` | In CI |\n\n- The proxy protects sync workers from slow clients.\n- Worker count drives memory and database connections.\n- Old and new code coexist during a rolling deploy.\n- Liveness cheap, readiness honest.\n- Secrets from the environment, never in the image.\n- One image for web, workers, beat and release job.\n- Start HSTS small; raise once HTTPS is verified everywhere.\n- Logs to stdout as JSON; add request IDs.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSetup: `python -m venv .venv && pip install \"django~=5.2\" gunicorn whitenoise sentry-sdk python-json-logger`.\n\n1. **check --deploy.** Run `manage.py check --deploy` on a fresh project, then fix every warning in a `prod.py` settings module until it is clean.\n2. **Worker sizing.** Write a view that sleeps 200 ms (simulated DB wait). Load-test with `hey` or `wrk` using 1, 5 and 9 sync workers, then 3 workers × 4 gthread threads; plot throughput vs memory.\n3. **Timeouts.** Add a view sleeping 40 s; observe `WORKER TIMEOUT` with the default timeout and the resulting 502 behind Nginx.\n4. **Proxy headers.** Put Nginx with TLS (self-signed) in front, enable `SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT` without `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` and reproduce the redirect loop; then fix it.\n5. **Graceful shutdown.** Run Gunicorn, start a 10 s request, send SIGTERM to the master and confirm the request completes before exit.\n6. **Expand/contract.** Rename a field across three commits (add, backfill + switch, drop) while a script hits the API continuously; confirm zero errors.\n\n**Mini project:** a production-ready deployment of a small shop app with docker-compose: multi-stage Dockerfile, Gunicorn config from env, Nginx with TLS and static caching (or WhiteNoise), PostgreSQL + PgBouncer, Redis, a Celery worker and beat, a one-shot `migrate` service that the web service depends on, `/healthz` and `/readyz`, JSON logs and Sentry. Add a GitHub Actions pipeline that runs tests, `makemigrations --check`, `check --deploy`, builds and tags the image with the git SHA.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n- [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings) — settings per environment, env vars.\n- [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations) — safe schema changes, `AddIndexConcurrently`.\n- [Ch 20 · Static & Media Files](topic.html?p=20-static-media-files) — `collectstatic`, `STORAGES`, WhiteNoise.\n- [Ch 28 · Security: CSRF, XSS, SQL Injection & Hardening](topic.html?p=28-security) — `check --deploy`, HSTS.\n- [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks) and [Ch 34 · Scaling Django: Connections, Replicas & Multiple Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases).\n- [Docker · Dockerfile](../docker/topic.html?p=05-dockerfile), [Docker · Healthchecks](../docker/topic.html?p=17-healthchecks), [Docker · Compose in Production](../docker/topic.html?p=20-compose-production), [Docker · CI/CD](../docker/topic.html?p=26-ci-cd).\n- [Kubernetes · Deployments](../kubernetes/topic.html?p=03-deployments), [Kubernetes · Probes & Health](../kubernetes/topic.html?p=22-probes-health), [Kubernetes · Lab: Zero-Downtime Rollout](../kubernetes/topic.html?p=29-lab-zero-downtime-rollout).\n- [REST API · Deployment & CI/CD for APIs](../rest-api/topic.html?p=41-deployment-cicd-apis).\n\n- **Deployment checklist** — Django docs · *Beginner* · every setting `check --deploy` cares about, with explanations. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/deployment/checklist/>\n- **How to deploy Django** — Django docs · *Beginner* · WSGI and ASGI deployment guides, Gunicorn and uvicorn. <https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/deployment/>\n- **Gunicorn design and settings** — Gunicorn docs · *Intermediate* · worker types, sizing advice, timeouts and signals. <https://docs.gunicorn.org/en/stable/design.html>\n- **WhiteNoise documentation** — WhiteNoise · *Beginner* · Django integration, compression and caching headers. <https://whitenoise.readthedocs.io/>\n- **The Twelve-Factor App** — 12factor.net · *Beginner* · config, logs, processes and dev/prod parity principles. <https://12factor.net/>\n- **Sentry for Django** — Sentry docs · *Beginner* · setup, releases and performance tracing. <https://docs.sentry.io/platforms/python/integrations/django/>\n- **django-storages** — GitHub · *Intermediate* · S3 and other storage backends for media files. <https://github.com/jschneier/django-storages>\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 35.*\n", "36-django-internals": "# 36 · Django Internals: How It Really Works\n\n> **In one line:** Django is a few hundred lines of carefully ordered startup code (lazy settings → `django.setup()` → the app registry), a metaclass that turns your model classes into `_meta` plus descriptors, a lazy `QuerySet` that builds a `sql.Query` tree and hands it to a `SQLCompiler` only when you iterate, and a handler that compiles `MIDDLEWARE` into one nested callable at boot. Know these four machines and nearly every confusing Django error explains itself.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle), [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings), [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields), [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups), [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware).\n\nMost Django developers learn the framework from the outside in: write a model, write a view, it works. That is fine until one of these lands on your desk:\n\n- `django.core.exceptions.AppRegistryNotReady: Apps aren't loaded yet.` from a Celery worker or a standalone script.\n- `ImproperlyConfigured: Requested setting DEBUG, but settings are not configured.` in a test runner.\n- `RuntimeError: Model class shop.models.Book doesn't declare an explicit app_label and isn't in an application in INSTALLED_APPS.`\n- A page that issues 400 queries even though \"there's only one loop\" — because a template touched `book.author.name`.\n- A middleware that \"doesn't run\" for some requests, or whose exception handler never sees an exception.\n- A `QuerySet` you \"filtered\" that still returns everything, because you forgot that `filter()` returns a *new* object.\n\nThe naive approach to such bugs is cargo-cult: move the import, add `django.setup()` somewhere, sprinkle `select_related`, reorder middleware until it works. That gets you through the afternoon but leaves you unable to predict the next failure — and it collapses in a senior interview, where \"what happens when you call `Book.objects.filter(...)`?\" or \"how does `book.author` know to run a query?\" are standard probes.\n\nThis chapter opens the box. It is the **home chapter** for Django's internal machinery; the concept chapters it builds on explain *how to use* each piece. Here you learn *how each piece is built*, in four parts:\n\n1. **Startup** — `LazySettings`, `django.setup()`, and the three-phase `Apps.populate()`.\n2. **Models** — the `ModelBase` metaclass, `Options` (`_meta`), `contribute_to_class`, and the descriptors that power attribute access.\n3. **Queries** — `Manager` → `QuerySet` → `sql.Query` → `SQLCompiler` → cursor → `ModelIterable` → instances.\n4. **Requests** — `WSGIHandler`/`ASGIHandler`, `BaseHandler.load_middleware()`, `get_response()`, URL resolution and exception conversion.\n\n> **Why this matters:** Every one of these machines is *lazy* or *built once*. Settings load on first attribute access, models register at import time, queries compile on iteration, the middleware chain is compiled at the first request. Most \"mysterious\" bugs are a mismatch between when you *think* something happens and when it actually does.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n- **`LazySettings` / `LazyObject`** — `django.conf.settings` is a proxy that imports `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE` on first attribute access. *Why it matters:* importing `settings` is free; *reading* one before configuration raises `ImproperlyConfigured`.\n- **`django.setup()`** — configures logging, sets the URL script prefix, and calls `apps.populate(settings.INSTALLED_APPS)`. *Why it matters:* every entry point (`manage.py`, `get_wsgi_application()`, `get_asgi_application()`, Celery's Django fixup, pytest-django) calls it; your standalone scripts must too.\n- **App registry (`django.apps.apps`)** — the global `Apps` instance holding `AppConfig`s and models. Flags `apps_ready`, `models_ready`, `ready`. *Why it matters:* `apps.get_model()` and anything that walks models only work after population.\n- **`AppConfig`** — per-app object (`label`, `name`, `path`, `models_module`) with the `ready()` hook. *Why it matters:* the right place to connect signals and register checks, because all models exist by then.\n- **`ModelBase`** — the metaclass of `models.Model`. *Why it matters:* the moment Python executes `class Book(models.Model):`, `ModelBase.__new__` builds `_meta`, attaches fields, creates `DoesNotExist`, adds a default manager and registers the class.\n- **`Options` (`Model._meta`)** — the model's metadata: `fields`, `get_fields()`, `get_field()`, `pk`, `db_table`, `label`, `app_label`, `ordering`, `indexes`, `constraints`. *Why it matters:* admin, forms, serializers, migrations and the ORM all introspect models through `_meta` — and so can your generic code.\n- **`contribute_to_class(cls, name)`** — the hook a field or manager uses to install itself on a model class. *Why it matters:* it is why a field declaration becomes both a `_meta` entry *and* a class-level descriptor.\n- **Descriptors** — objects with `__get__`/`__set__` placed on the class: `DeferredAttribute` (plain fields), `ForwardManyToOneDescriptor` (`book.author`), `ReverseManyToOneDescriptor` (`author.books`), `ManyToManyDescriptor`, `ReverseOneToOneDescriptor`, `ManagerDescriptor`. *Why it matters:* every implicit query (lazy FK load, deferred field load) is triggered inside a descriptor's `__get__`.\n- **`ModelState` (`instance._state`)** — `adding`, `db`, `fields_cache`. *Why it matters:* `fields_cache` is where `select_related` results live; `adding` decides INSERT vs UPDATE.\n- **`Manager`** — `BaseManager.from_queryset(QuerySet)`; a factory for QuerySets. *Why it matters:* `Book.objects.filter` is literally `Book.objects.get_queryset().filter`.\n- **`QuerySet`** — lazy, chainable wrapper around a `sql.Query` plus a `_result_cache`. *Why it matters:* every chain method clones; nothing hits the DB until evaluation.\n- **`sql.Query`** — the backend-neutral query tree: `alias_map` (tables and joins), `where` (a `WhereNode` of lookups), `annotations`, `group_by`, `order_by`, `low_mark`/`high_mark`. *Why it matters:* this is what `filter()` actually mutates.\n- **`SQLCompiler`** — turns a `Query` into `(sql, params)` for one connection; subclasses for INSERT/UPDATE/DELETE/aggregate, and per-backend variants. *Why it matters:* it is where vendor differences (`as_postgresql`) are applied and where parameterization protects you from SQL injection.\n- **Iterable classes** — `ModelIterable`, `ValuesIterable`, `ValuesListIterable`, `FlatValuesListIterable`, `NamedValuesListIterable`. *Why it matters:* `.values()` is cheaper than model instances because it swaps the iterable, skipping `Model.from_db()`.\n- **`BaseHandler`** — parent of `WSGIHandler` and `ASGIHandler`; `load_middleware()` builds `_middleware_chain`; `get_response()` runs it. *Why it matters:* middleware ordering, short-circuiting and exception conversion are all decided here.\n- **`convert_exception_to_response`** — wraps each middleware layer so exceptions become responses (404/403/400/500) before reaching the outer layer. *Why it matters:* outer middleware sees a 500 *response*, not an exception.\n- **`ConnectionHandler` (`django.db.connections`)** — lazily opens one `DatabaseWrapper` per alias per thread (per async context). *Why it matters:* connections are not shared across threads, and `CONN_MAX_AGE` is enforced by signal handlers at request boundaries.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### 3.1 Settings are a lazy proxy\n\n`django/conf/__init__.py` ends with `settings = LazySettings()`. `LazySettings` subclasses `LazyObject`, whose `_wrapped` attribute starts as the sentinel `empty`. The first time you read *any* attribute, `LazySettings.__getattr__` calls `self._setup(name)`, which:\n\n1. Reads `os.environ[\"DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE\"]`. If missing, it raises `ImproperlyConfigured(\"Requested setting X, but settings are not configured. You must either define the environment variable DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE or call settings.configure() ...\")`.\n2. Builds `Settings(settings_module)`: copies every UPPERCASE name from `django.conf.global_settings` (the defaults), then `importlib.import_module(settings_module)` and copies every UPPERCASE name from your module over them. Lower-case names in `settings.py` are silently ignored — a classic bug.\n3. Validates a few things (for example, tuple/list types for `ALLOWED_HOSTS`-style settings, `SECRET_KEY` non-empty when read).\n\n`__getattr__` then **caches the value in the proxy's own `__dict__`**, so the second read of `settings.DEBUG` is a plain attribute lookup. `__setattr__` clears that cache, which is how `override_settings` in tests can swap `_wrapped` and send the `setting_changed` signal so caches that depend on settings (template engines, URL resolver, password hashers) reset.\n\n`settings.configure(**overrides)` is the alternative for scripts and libraries: it wraps a `UserSettingsHolder` around `global_settings` and must be called before the first read, or you get `RuntimeError: Settings already configured.`\n\n### 3.2 `django.setup()` and the three phases of `Apps.populate()`\n\n`django.setup(set_prefix=True)` does three things: `configure_logging(settings.LOGGING_CONFIG, settings.LOGGING)`, `set_script_prefix(...)` (so `reverse()` works outside requests), and `apps.populate(settings.INSTALLED_APPS)`. `populate()` holds a lock, is idempotent (returns if `ready`) and **not reentrant** — if code running during population calls `populate()` again, it raises `RuntimeError(\"populate() isn't reentrant\")`. It runs in three strict phases:\n\n- **Phase 1 — app configs.** For each `INSTALLED_APPS` entry, `AppConfig.create(entry)` imports the app *package* (not its models), finds its `AppConfig` (an explicit dotted path, or since 3.2 the single `AppConfig` subclass in `apps.py`, or the one marked `default = True`), and checks label and name uniqueness (\"Application labels aren't unique\"). Then `apps_ready = True`.\n- **Phase 2 — models.** For each config, `import_models()` imports `<app>.models`. Importing executes the class bodies, so `ModelBase.__new__` runs for every model and calls `apps.register_model()`. Then `models_ready = True`.\n- **Phase 3 — ready().** Each `AppConfig.ready()` is called in `INSTALLED_APPS` order. Then `ready = True`.\n\nThe infamous `AppRegistryNotReady: Apps aren't loaded yet.` is raised by `apps.check_apps_ready()` when something asks the registry for information it doesn't have yet — most commonly, a module imported in Phase 1 (an app's `__init__.py`) imports `models`, or a script imports `myapp.models` before calling `django.setup()`. `ModelBase` itself calls `apps.get_containing_app_config(module)` to find a model's `app_label`, which requires `apps_ready`.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 420\" width=\"100%\" height=\"420\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj36a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj36b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Startup: from DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE to a ready app registry</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"180\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Entry point</text>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">manage.py / wsgi.py / asgi.py</text>\n  <text x=\"110\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">celery worker / pytest</text>\n  <path d=\"M200,79 L240,79\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"242\" y=\"44\" width=\"190\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"337\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">LazySettings._setup()</text>\n  <text x=\"337\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">global_settings defaults</text>\n  <text x=\"337\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">+ UPPERCASE names of your module</text>\n  <path d=\"M432,79 L472,79\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"474\" y=\"44\" width=\"190\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"569\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">django.setup()</text>\n  <text x=\"569\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">configure_logging()</text>\n  <text x=\"569\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">set_script_prefix()</text>\n  <path d=\"M664,79 L704,79\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"706\" y=\"44\" width=\"154\" height=\"70\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"783\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">apps.populate()</text>\n  <text x=\"783\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">lock, idempotent,</text>\n  <text x=\"783\" y=\"100\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">not reentrant</text>\n  <path d=\"M783,114 L783,140\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"146\" width=\"270\" height=\"130\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"155\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Phase 1: AppConfig.create(entry)</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">import the app package (not models)</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"208\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">pick AppConfig from apps.py</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"226\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">check labels / names unique</text>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"254\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">apps_ready = True</text>\n  <rect x=\"305\" y=\"146\" width=\"270\" height=\"130\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Phase 2: import_models()</text>\n  <text x=\"319\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">import app.models &#8594; class bodies run</text>\n  <text x=\"319\" y=\"208\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">ModelBase.__new__ builds _meta</text>\n  <text x=\"319\" y=\"226\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">apps.register_model(label, cls)</text>\n  <text x=\"319\" y=\"254\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">models_ready = True</text>\n  <rect x=\"590\" y=\"146\" width=\"270\" height=\"130\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"725\" y=\"168\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Phase 3: AppConfig.ready()</text>\n  <text x=\"604\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">connect signals, register checks</text>\n  <text x=\"604\" y=\"208\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">every model already exists</text>\n  <text x=\"604\" y=\"226\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">avoid DB queries here</text>\n  <text x=\"604\" y=\"254\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">ready = True</text>\n  <path d=\"M290,211 L303,211\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36b)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M575,211 L588,211\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"296\" width=\"840\" height=\"106\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"318\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Where the classic errors come from</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"340\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Read a setting before DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE is set &#8594; ImproperlyConfigured: settings are not configured</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"358\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Import models in an app __init__.py, or before django.setup() &#8594; AppRegistryNotReady: Apps aren't loaded yet</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"376\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Model module not inside an INSTALLED_APPS app &#8594; RuntimeError: doesn't declare an explicit app_label</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"394\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Code in ready() triggers populate() again &#8594; RuntimeError: populate() isn't reentrant</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.3 `ModelBase`: what `class Book(models.Model)` actually executes\n\nWhen Python finishes reading a `class` statement it calls the metaclass: `ModelBase.__new__(mcs, \"Book\", (models.Model,), attrs)`. Simplified, it:\n\n1. **Skips** the bare `Model` base itself (only subclasses get processed).\n2. **Splits `attrs`**: attributes *with* a `contribute_to_class` method (fields, managers, `Meta`-free descriptors) are held back; plain attributes (methods, constants) go straight into `type.__new__`.\n3. **Resolves `app_label`** from `Meta.app_label` or `apps.get_containing_app_config(module)`; failing both, it raises the \"doesn't declare an explicit app_label\" `RuntimeError`.\n4. **Builds `Options`**: `new_class.add_to_class(\"_meta\", Options(meta, app_label))`. `Options.contribute_to_class` reads your inner `Meta` (`db_table`, `ordering`, `indexes`, `constraints`, `abstract`, `proxy`…) and computes defaults like `db_table = \"shop_book\"`.\n5. **Creates exceptions**: `Book.DoesNotExist` (subclass of the parent's or `ObjectDoesNotExist`) and `Book.MultipleObjectsReturned` — which is why `except Book.DoesNotExist` works and catches child-model misses too.\n6. **Contributes fields** by calling `add_to_class(name, value)` → `value.contribute_to_class(cls, name)` for each held-back attribute. For a `Field` this sets `field.name`, `field.attname` (`author_id` for an FK), `field.column`, `field.model = cls`, calls `cls._meta.add_field(field)`, and installs a descriptor on the class (`DeferredAttribute` for plain fields; relation fields install their own).\n7. **Handles inheritance**: abstract parents' fields are *copied* onto the child; multi-table inheritance auto-adds a `parent_ptr` `OneToOneField(parent_link=True)`; proxies share the concrete parent's table.\n8. **`_prepare()`**: `Options._prepare()` adds an auto primary key (`DEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD`, typically `BigAutoField`) if you didn't declare one, adds a default `objects = Manager()` if the class has no managers, and sends the `class_prepared` signal.\n9. **Registers** the class: `apps.register_model(app_label, new_class)`. Registration also flushes **pending lazy operations** — a `ForeignKey(\"shop.Author\")` string reference declared before `Author` existed is resolved now via `lazy_related_operation`.\n\nAll of this happens at **import time**, once per process. That is why defining a model in a module that never gets imported (say, `shop/models/book.py` not re-exported from `shop/models/__init__.py`) means \"the model doesn't exist\" — no migration, no admin, nothing.\n\n### 3.4 Descriptors: how attribute access runs queries\n\nAfter `ModelBase` runs, the *class* `Book` holds descriptor objects; *instances* hold raw values in `__dict__`.\n\n- **`DeferredAttribute`** (plain fields). `__get__` returns `instance.__dict__[\"title\"]` if present; if the field was deferred via `only()`/`defer()`, it calls `instance.refresh_from_db(fields=[\"title\"])` — **one query per deferred field access**.\n- **`ForwardManyToOneDescriptor`** (`book.author`). `__get__` checks `instance._state.fields_cache[\"author\"]` (filled by `select_related` or a previous access). If absent and `author_id` is not `None`, it runs `Author._base_manager.get(pk=author_id)` and caches it. That is the N+1 query, located precisely. `__set__` sets both the cache and `author_id`.\n- **`ReverseManyToOneDescriptor`** (`author.books`). `__get__` returns a **related manager** — a dynamically created subclass of the default manager whose `get_queryset()` adds `filter(author=instance)`. Before querying, it checks `instance._prefetched_objects_cache[\"books\"]`; if `prefetch_related` populated it, `.all()` returns the cached list. A further `.filter()` builds a new query and ignores the cache.\n- **`ManyToManyDescriptor`** — subclass of the reverse descriptor; its manager joins through the `through` table and implements `add/remove/set/clear` (sending `m2m_changed`).\n- **`ReverseOneToOneDescriptor`** (`user.profile`) — raises `RelatedObjectDoesNotExist` (a subclass of both `Profile.DoesNotExist` and `AttributeError`) when there is no row, which is why `hasattr(user, \"profile\")` works.\n- **`ManagerDescriptor`** — `Book.objects` returns the manager; `book.objects` raises `AttributeError: Manager isn't accessible via Book instances`, a deliberate design choice separating table-level and row-level operations.\n\n> **Interview tip:** \"How does `book.author` work?\" — answer with the descriptor: class attribute `ForwardManyToOneDescriptor`, cache in `_state.fields_cache`, otherwise a `get(pk=author_id)` query. Then mention that `book.author_id` never queries because it's a plain `DeferredAttribute` on the FK column.\n\n### 3.5 The query pipeline: from `filter()` to model instances\n\n`Book.objects.filter(price__gt=20)` is: `ManagerDescriptor.__get__` → `Manager` → `get_queryset()` returns `QuerySet(model=Book, using=self._db)` → `.filter()` → `_filter_or_exclude()` → `clone = self._chain()` → `clone._query.add_q(Q(price__gt=20))`.\n\n`add_q()` → `_add_q()` → `build_filter()`, which splits `\"price__gt\"` with `solve_lookup_type()`, walks relations with `names_to_path()`/`setup_joins()` (adding `Join` objects to `alias_map` and reusing aliases where it can), resolves the right-hand side (an `F()` becomes a `Col`), finds the registered `Lookup` class (`GreaterThan`) and appends it to the `WhereNode`. **No SQL exists yet** — only a tree.\n\nEvaluation (iteration, `len()`, `list()`, `bool()`, `repr()`, pickling, step-slicing) calls `QuerySet._fetch_all()`: if `_result_cache is None`, it runs `list(self._iterable_class(self))`, then performs `prefetch_related` lookups if any. `ModelIterable.__iter__`:\n\n1. `compiler = queryset.query.get_compiler(using=db)` — looks up `connection.ops.compiler(\"SQLCompiler\")`, so PostgreSQL gets `django.db.backends.postgresql.compiler.SQLCompiler`.\n2. `results = compiler.execute_sql(chunked_fetch=..., chunk_size=...)` → `as_sql()` → `pre_sql_setup()` (`get_select()`, `get_order_by()`, `get_group_by()`), then each node compiled via `compiler.compile(node)`, which prefers a vendor method like `as_postgresql()` over `as_sql()`. The result is `(sql, params)` — **parameters stay separate** until the DB driver binds them.\n3. `cursor.execute(sql, params)` on the connection's cursor; rows fetched in chunks (`GET_ITERATOR_CHUNK_SIZE`, 100 rows).\n4. For each row, `Model.from_db(db, field_names, values)` builds an instance (`_state.adding = False`, `_state.db = db`); `select_related` rows are split by `RelatedPopulator`s into related instances stored in `fields_cache`; annotations are `setattr` onto the instance.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj36c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj36d\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Build lazily, compile on evaluation: the QuerySet pipeline</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"48\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">BUILD (no database access)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"58\" width=\"150\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Book.objects</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Manager.get_queryset()</text>\n  <path d=\"M170,89 L200,89\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"202\" y=\"58\" width=\"170\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"287\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">.filter(price__gt=20)</text>\n  <text x=\"287\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">_chain() clones QuerySet</text>\n  <text x=\"287\" y=\"112\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">query.add_q(Q(...))</text>\n  <path d=\"M372,89 L402,89\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"404\" y=\"58\" width=\"456\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"632\" y=\"78\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">sql.Query tree</text>\n  <text x=\"418\" y=\"96\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">alias_map: shop_book, shop_author (Join)   where: WhereNode(AND: GreaterThan(Col, 20))</text>\n  <text x=\"418\" y=\"112\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">select / annotations / group_by / order_by / low_mark, high_mark (slicing)</text>\n  <text x=\"20\" y=\"152\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"12\" font-weight=\"bold\">EVALUATE (iteration, len, list, bool, repr, pickling)</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"162\" width=\"190\" height=\"72\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"115\" y=\"184\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">QuerySet._fetch_all()</text>\n  <text x=\"115\" y=\"202\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">_result_cache is None?</text>\n  <text x=\"115\" y=\"218\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">list(ModelIterable(qs))</text>\n  <path d=\"M210,198 L240,198\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"242\" y=\"162\" width=\"200\" height=\"72\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"342\" y=\"184\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">query.get_compiler(using)</text>\n  <text x=\"342\" y=\"202\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">connection.ops.compiler(...)</text>\n  <text x=\"342\" y=\"218\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">backend SQLCompiler</text>\n  <path d=\"M442,198 L472,198\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"474\" y=\"162\" width=\"190\" height=\"72\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"569\" y=\"184\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">as_sql() &#8594; (sql, params)</text>\n  <text x=\"569\" y=\"202\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">compile(node): as_postgresql</text>\n  <text x=\"569\" y=\"218\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">or as_sql per expression</text>\n  <path d=\"M664,198 L694,198\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"696\" y=\"162\" width=\"164\" height=\"72\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"778\" y=\"184\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">execute_sql()</text>\n  <text x=\"778\" y=\"202\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">cursor.execute(sql, params)</text>\n  <text x=\"778\" y=\"218\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">fetchmany in chunks</text>\n  <path d=\"M778,234 L778,262\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"404\" y=\"266\" width=\"456\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"632\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Row &#8594; instance</text>\n  <text x=\"418\" y=\"304\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Model.from_db(db, names, values): _state.adding=False, _state.db=db</text>\n  <text x=\"418\" y=\"320\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">select_related: RelatedPopulator fills _state.fields_cache; annotations setattr</text>\n  <path d=\"M404,297 L372,297\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"266\" width=\"350\" height=\"62\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"195\" y=\"286\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">_result_cache = [Book, Book, ...]</text>\n  <text x=\"195\" y=\"304\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">then _prefetch_related_objects(): one IN (...)</text>\n  <text x=\"195\" y=\"320\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">query per lookup into _prefetched_objects_cache</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"344\" width=\"840\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"362\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">.values() swaps ModelIterable for ValuesIterable (no from_db); .iterator() skips _result_cache;</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"378\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\">str(qs.query) interpolates params for display only &#8212; the real call keeps them separate.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 3.6 What `save()` does internally\n\n`Model.save()` → `save_base()` sends `pre_save`, then (inside an atomic block if there are MTI parents) `_save_parents()` and `_save_table()`. `_save_table()` decides between UPDATE and INSERT: if the PK is set and you didn't pass `force_insert`, it tries `_do_update()` — `UPDATE ... WHERE id = %s` via `SQLUpdateCompiler` — and if **zero rows** matched, falls back to `_do_insert()` (`SQLInsertCompiler`, with `RETURNING id` on PostgreSQL). Since Django 3.0 it **skips the UPDATE attempt** when `_state.adding` is `True` and the PK field has a default (e.g. a `UUIDField(default=uuid4)`), saving a wasted query. `update_fields=[...]` restricts the UPDATE column list. Afterwards it sets `_state.adding = False`, `_state.db = using`, and sends `post_save(created=...)`. `full_clean()` is never called.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### 4.1 The handler builds the middleware chain once\n\n`get_wsgi_application()` calls `django.setup(set_prefix=False)` and returns `WSGIHandler()`, whose `__init__` calls `self.load_middleware()`. (`ASGIHandler` calls `load_middleware(is_async=True)`.) `BaseHandler.load_middleware()`:\n\n1. Starts with `handler = convert_exception_to_response(self._get_response)` — the innermost callable, which resolves the URL and calls the view.\n2. Iterates `reversed(settings.MIDDLEWARE)`. For each entry it imports the class, adapts sync/async mode (`adapt_method_mode`, using `sync_capable`/`async_capable` flags), and instantiates `mw_instance = middleware(handler)`. A middleware can opt out by raising `MiddlewareNotUsed` in `__init__`.\n3. Collects hooks: `process_view` is **inserted at index 0** (so they run in top-to-bottom order), `process_template_response` and `process_exception` are appended (so they run bottom-to-top).\n4. Wraps: `handler = convert_exception_to_response(mw_instance)`.\n5. Stores the outermost callable as `self._middleware_chain`.\n\nBecause the list is iterated in reverse, the **first** item in `MIDDLEWARE` ends up as the **outermost** wrapper — it sees the request first and the response last. Each layer is wrapped in `convert_exception_to_response`, so if the view raises `Http404`, the layer just outside the view receives a 404 `HttpResponse`, not an exception. `response_for_exception()` maps `Http404` → 404, `PermissionDenied` → 403, `BadRequest`/`SuspiciousOperation` → 400 (and logs security events), and everything else → `handle_uncaught_exception()` → 500 (the yellow `technical_500_response` when `DEBUG=True`), firing `got_request_exception` (which Sentry hooks).\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 430\" width=\"100%\" height=\"430\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj36e\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj36f\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">BaseHandler: one request through the compiled _middleware_chain</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"840\" height=\"300\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"34\" y=\"58\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">convert_exception_to_response( SecurityMiddleware )  &#8212; first in MIDDLEWARE = outermost</text>\n  <rect x=\"50\" y=\"68\" width=\"780\" height=\"260\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"64\" y=\"86\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">convert_exception_to_response( SessionMiddleware )</text>\n  <rect x=\"80\" y=\"96\" width=\"720\" height=\"222\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"94\" y=\"114\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">... CommonMiddleware, CsrfViewMiddleware, AuthenticationMiddleware ...</text>\n  <rect x=\"110\" y=\"124\" width=\"660\" height=\"184\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"124\" y=\"142\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">convert_exception_to_response( BaseHandler._get_response )</text>\n  <rect x=\"130\" y=\"154\" width=\"140\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"176\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">resolve_request()</text>\n  <text x=\"200\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">URLResolver.resolve(path_info)</text>\n  <path d=\"M270,182 L296,182\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36e)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"298\" y=\"154\" width=\"140\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"368\" y=\"176\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">process_view hooks</text>\n  <text x=\"368\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">top &#8594; bottom; may return</text>\n  <path d=\"M438,182 L464,182\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36e)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"466\" y=\"154\" width=\"130\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"531\" y=\"176\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">view(request)</text>\n  <text x=\"531\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">make_view_atomic()</text>\n  <path d=\"M596,182 L622,182\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36e)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"624\" y=\"154\" width=\"136\" height=\"56\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"692\" y=\"176\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">response.render()</text>\n  <text x=\"692\" y=\"194\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">template_response hooks</text>\n  <rect x=\"130\" y=\"226\" width=\"630\" height=\"70\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"445\" y=\"246\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">view raises? process_exception hooks (bottom &#8594; top) get first chance</text>\n  <text x=\"445\" y=\"264\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">none returns a response &#8594; re-raise &#8594; convert_exception_to_response()</text>\n  <text x=\"445\" y=\"282\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Http404 &#8594; 404, PermissionDenied &#8594; 403, SuspiciousOperation &#8594; 400, other &#8594; 500</text>\n  <path d=\"M20,370 L860,370\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36e)\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"362\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"10\">request phase: __call__ of each layer, outer to inner (a layer that returns a response short-circuits)</text>\n  <path d=\"M860,400 L20,400\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj36f)\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"420\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\">response phase: code after get_response(request) in each layer, inner to outer &#8212; always an HttpResponse</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### 4.2 One WSGI request, end to end\n\n```text\ngunicorn worker thread\n  └─ WSGIHandler.__call__(environ, start_response)\n       ├─ set_script_prefix(get_script_name(environ))\n       ├─ signals.request_started.send()      -> close_old_connections() (CONN_MAX_AGE / health checks)\n       ├─ request = WSGIRequest(environ)       (GET/POST/body parsed lazily)\n       ├─ response = self.get_response(request)\n       │     ├─ set_urlconf(settings.ROOT_URLCONF)\n       │     ├─ response = self._middleware_chain(request)\n       │     │     └─ ... _get_response(): resolve -> process_view -> view -> render\n       │     └─ log_response() if status >= 400\n       ├─ start_response(status, headers)\n       └─ return response  (server iterates it, then calls response.close())\n                              └─ signals.request_finished -> close_old_connections()\n```\n\nTwo details worth noticing. First, `request_finished` fires from `HttpResponse.close()`, which the WSGI server calls *after* sending the body — so for a `StreamingHttpResponse`, DB connections stay open until the last chunk is sent. Second, `request.urlconf` set by a middleware overrides `ROOT_URLCONF` for that request (the basis of per-host routing in multi-tenant apps).\n\n### 4.3 The ASGI variant\n\n`ASGIHandler.__call__(scope, receive, send)` accepts only `scope[\"type\"] == \"http\"` (Channels' `ProtocolTypeRouter` handles websockets), reads the body into a `SpooledTemporaryFile`, sends `request_started` via `sync_to_async`, builds an `ASGIRequest`, and awaits `get_response_async()`. The chain was built with `is_async=True`, so any sync-only middleware is wrapped in `sync_to_async` (thread-sensitive), costing a thread hop per layer — which is why one sync middleware in an otherwise async stack quietly erodes async benefits. The ORM is still sync underneath: `aget()`/`acount()` are `sync_to_async` wrappers (see [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi)).\n\n### 4.4 Database connections\n\n`django.db.connections` is a `ConnectionHandler` keyed by alias; `connection` is a proxy to `connections[\"default\"]`. Each thread (or async context, via `asgiref.local.Local`) gets its own `DatabaseWrapper`, created lazily and connected on the first `cursor()` call (`ensure_connection()`). With `DEBUG=True`, cursors are `CursorDebugWrapper`s that append to `connection.queries` (a bounded deque) — handy, but also a memory cost in long scripts. `connection.execute_wrapper(fn)` lets you instrument every query without DEBUG, which is how you build a \"queries per request\" budget in production. Pooling, `CONN_MAX_AGE` and replicas are covered in [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases).\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### 5.1 Simple example: poke the machinery in a shell\n\n```python\n# python manage.py shell\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.apps import apps\nfrom shop.models import Book\n\ntype(settings), type(settings._wrapped)\n# (<class 'django.conf.LazySettings'>, <class 'django.conf.Settings'>)\n\ntype(Book)                       # <class 'django.db.models.base.ModelBase'>\nBook._meta.label, Book._meta.db_table, Book._meta.pk\n# ('shop.Book', 'shop_book', <django.db.models.fields.BigAutoField: id>)\n[f.name for f in Book._meta.get_fields()]\n# ['order_items', 'reviews', 'id', 'title', 'author', 'publisher', ...]\n\ntype(Book.__dict__[\"author\"]).__name__   # 'ForwardManyToOneDescriptor'\ntype(Book.__dict__[\"title\"]).__name__    # 'DeferredAttribute'\ntype(Book.__dict__[\"objects\"]).__name__  # 'ManagerDescriptor'\n\nqs = Book.objects.filter(price__gt=20, author__name=\"Ana\").order_by(\"-published_on\")[:5]\nq = qs.query\nq.alias_map.keys()       # dict_keys(['shop_book', 'shop_author'])\nq.where                  # (AND: Exact(Col(shop_author, shop.Author.name), 'Ana'), GreaterThan(...))\nq.low_mark, q.high_mark  # (0, 5)\n\ncompiler = q.get_compiler(\"default\")\ntype(compiler)           # <class 'django.db.backends.postgresql.compiler.SQLCompiler'>\ncompiler.as_sql()\n```\n\nThe real output of `as_sql()` keeps parameters separate:\n\n```text\n('SELECT \"shop_book\".\"id\", \"shop_book\".\"title\", ... FROM \"shop_book\"\n  INNER JOIN \"shop_author\" ON (\"shop_book\".\"author_id\" = \"shop_author\".\"id\")\n  WHERE (\"shop_author\".\"name\" = %s AND \"shop_book\".\"price\" > %s)\n  ORDER BY \"shop_book\".\"published_on\" DESC LIMIT 5',\n ('Ana', Decimal('20')))\n```\n\n`str(qs.query)` shows `WHERE (\"shop_author\".\"name\" = Ana ...)` — unquoted, because it naively interpolates for display. Never copy it into psql and never treat it as the executed SQL. For the exact statement, use `connection.queries`, the debug toolbar, or `qs.explain()`.\n\n```python\nb = Book.objects.first()\nb._state.adding, b._state.db, b._state.fields_cache   # (False, 'default', {})\nb.author                                              # runs SELECT ... FROM shop_author WHERE id = ...\nb._state.fields_cache.keys()                          # dict_keys(['author'])\nb.objects                                             # AttributeError: Manager isn't accessible via Book instances\n```\n\n### 5.2 Standalone script done right\n\n```python\n# scripts/reprice.py — run with: python scripts/reprice.py\nimport os\n\nimport django\n\nos.environ.setdefault(\"DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE\", \"config.settings.prod\")\ndjango.setup()  # MUST run before importing any models\n\nfrom decimal import Decimal  # noqa: E402\n\nfrom django.db.models import F  # noqa: E402\n\nfrom shop.models import Book  # noqa: E402\n\nupdated = Book.objects.filter(publisher__name=\"Penguin\").update(price=F(\"price\") * Decimal(\"1.10\"))\nprint(f\"repriced {updated} books\")\n```\n\nBetter still, make it a management command (`shop/management/commands/reprice.py`), because `manage.py` → `execute_from_command_line()` → `ManagementUtility.execute()` already calls `django.setup()` and runs system checks for commands that require them.\n\n### 5.3 Real-world example 1: a generic \"model to CSV\" exporter built on `_meta`\n\n```python\n# core/exporters.py\nimport csv\nfrom collections.abc import Iterable\n\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.http import StreamingHttpResponse\n\n\nclass Echo:\n    def write(self, value):\n        return value\n\n\ndef concrete_columns(model: type[models.Model]) -> list[models.Field]:\n    \"\"\"Forward, concrete, non-M2M fields in declaration order.\"\"\"\n    return [f for f in model._meta.concrete_fields]\n\n\ndef export_csv(queryset: models.QuerySet, filename: str) -> StreamingHttpResponse:\n    fields = concrete_columns(queryset.model)\n    header = [str(f.verbose_name) for f in fields]\n    attnames = [f.attname for f in fields]  # 'author_id', not 'author' -> no FK queries\n\n    def rows() -> Iterable[list]:\n        yield header\n        # values_list swaps the iterable class: tuples, no Model.from_db()\n        for row in queryset.values_list(*attnames).iterator(chunk_size=2000):\n            yield list(row)\n\n    writer = csv.writer(Echo())\n    resp = StreamingHttpResponse((writer.writerow(r) for r in rows()), content_type=\"text/csv\")\n    resp[\"Content-Disposition\"] = f'attachment; filename=\"{filename}\"'\n    return resp\n```\n\nThree internals are doing the work: `_meta.concrete_fields` gives a correct column list for *any* model (including inherited fields); `f.attname` uses the raw FK column so there is no descriptor access and no N+1; `values_list().iterator()` skips both model construction and the result cache, so memory stays flat for a million rows.\n\n### 5.4 Real-world example 2: a custom field and a custom lookup\n\nFields and lookups plug into the same pipeline the compiler walks. A field controls Python ⇄ DB conversion; a lookup controls the WHERE fragment.\n\n```python\n# core/fields.py\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.db.models import Lookup\n\n\nclass LowercaseEmailField(models.EmailField):\n    \"\"\"Normalises on the way in; get_prep_value runs for saves AND for filter() values.\"\"\"\n\n    def get_prep_value(self, value):\n        value = super().get_prep_value(value)\n        return value.lower() if isinstance(value, str) else value\n\n\n@models.CharField.register_lookup\nclass NotEqual(Lookup):\n    lookup_name = \"ne\"\n\n    def as_sql(self, compiler, connection):\n        lhs, lhs_params = self.process_lhs(compiler, connection)\n        rhs, rhs_params = self.process_rhs(compiler, connection)\n        return f\"{lhs} <> {rhs}\", (*lhs_params, *rhs_params)\n```\n\n```python\n# usage\nfrom django.contrib.auth.models import User\n\nUser.objects.filter(username__ne=\"admin\")\n```\n\nThe generated SQL shows the lookup compiled in place, still parameterized:\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"auth_user\" WHERE \"auth_user\".\"username\" <> %s\n-- params: ('admin',)\n```\n\nBecause `NotEqual.as_sql` returns params rather than formatting the value into the string, it inherits the ORM's SQL-injection safety. A vendor-specific variant would be an `as_postgresql(self, compiler, connection)` method — `compiler.compile()` picks it automatically.\n\n### 5.5 Real-world example 3: a query-count budget without DEBUG\n\n```python\n# core/middleware.py\nimport logging\nimport time\n\nfrom django.db import connection\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(\"perf\")\n\n\nclass QueryBudgetMiddleware:\n    \"\"\"Counts SQL per request in production via connection.execute_wrapper (no DEBUG needed).\"\"\"\n\n    BUDGET = 30\n\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response  # called once, at load_middleware() time\n\n    def __call__(self, request):\n        stats = {\"n\": 0, \"ms\": 0.0}\n\n        def counter(execute, sql, params, many, context):\n            start = time.perf_counter()\n            try:\n                return execute(sql, params, many, context)\n            finally:\n                stats[\"n\"] += 1\n                stats[\"ms\"] += (time.perf_counter() - start) * 1000\n\n        with connection.execute_wrapper(counter):\n            response = self.get_response(request)\n        if stats[\"n\"] > self.BUDGET:\n            log.warning(\"query budget exceeded\", extra={\n                \"path\": request.path, \"queries\": stats[\"n\"], \"db_ms\": round(stats[\"ms\"], 1),\n                \"view\": getattr(request.resolver_match, \"view_name\", None),\n            })\n        response[\"Server-Timing\"] = f'db;dur={stats[\"ms\"]:.1f};desc=\"{stats[\"n\"]} queries\"'\n        return response\n```\n\nNote `request.resolver_match`: it is set by `resolve_request()` *inside* the chain, so it is available in the response phase of any middleware but not before `get_response()` is called. Also note that a streaming response's queries (inside the generator) run after this middleware returns — the wrapper misses them.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n| Design choice | What you gain | What it costs |\n|---|---|---|\n| Lazy settings | Import `settings` anywhere; tests swap settings with `override_settings` | Errors surface at first read, far from the cause; lower-case names silently ignored |\n| Global app registry populated at startup | One source of truth for models; admin, migrations, contenttypes all introspect it | Import-order sensitivity; `AppRegistryNotReady`; slow startup for huge projects |\n| Metaclass + `contribute_to_class` | Declarative models; fields self-register; generic tooling via `_meta` | \"Magic\" — attributes on the class are not what you wrote; hard to debug metaclass errors |\n| Descriptors for relations | `book.author` reads like Python | Hidden queries (N+1), invisible in code review |\n| Lazy, cloning QuerySets | Composable, reusable query fragments; no wasted queries | Every chain call allocates; easy to evaluate twice or not at all; cache surprises |\n| Backend-neutral `Query` + per-vendor compilers | Portable ORM; vendor tweaks via `as_<vendor>` | Generated SQL is not always the SQL you'd write; some features PostgreSQL-only |\n| Middleware compiled once at boot | Zero per-request setup cost | Settings changes need a reload; `__init__` side effects run once per process |\n| Exceptions converted to responses per layer | Outer middleware always receives a response | Outer middleware can't `try/except` view errors — must use `process_exception` or signals |\n\n### When to use (internals knowledge)\n\n- Writing reusable apps, admin extensions, serializers or exporters that work across models → `_meta` APIs (`get_fields()`, `get_field()`, `concrete_fields`).\n- Writing custom fields, lookups, transforms, expressions or database functions → the compiler protocol (`as_sql`, `as_<vendor>`, `resolve_expression`).\n- Debugging import-time errors, query explosions, middleware ordering, connection leaks.\n- Instrumenting production: `execute_wrapper`, `got_request_exception`, `request_started/finished`.\n\n### When NOT to use\n\n- Don't use private attributes (`qs.query.where`, `_state.fields_cache`, `_prefetched_objects_cache`) in application code — they change between releases. Read them in a debugger, not in features.\n- Don't subclass `ModelBase` or monkeypatch the compiler to \"fix\" a query; write a custom `Func`/`Lookup` or use `RawSQL`/`raw()`.\n- Don't add logic to `AppConfig.ready()` that queries the database — it runs during `migrate` on an empty DB and on every process start.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n**1. Importing models at module import time of an app package.** People put `from .models import Book` in `shop/__init__.py` or `apps.py` top-level. Phase 1 imports app packages before `apps_ready`, so model class creation calls `get_containing_app_config()` too early → `AppRegistryNotReady`. Instead, import models inside `ready()` or inside functions:\n\n```python\n# shop/apps.py — before (breaks)\nfrom django.apps import AppConfig\nfrom .models import Book  # executed during Phase 1\n\n# after\nclass ShopConfig(AppConfig):\n    name = \"shop\"\n    default_auto_field = \"django.db.models.BigAutoField\"\n\n    def ready(self):\n        from . import signals  # noqa: F401  (imports models safely; all models exist now)\n```\n\n**2. Lower-case or conditional settings.** `debug = True` in `settings.py` does nothing — only UPPERCASE names are copied. Similarly `if os.environ.get(\"X\"): CACHES = ...` executed at import is fine, but reading `settings.X` at import time of *another* module freezes the value before `override_settings` can patch it. Read settings inside functions.\n\n**3. Treating `str(qs.query)` as the executed SQL.** It interpolates params without quoting. Use `connection.queries` (DEBUG), `qs.explain(analyze=True)` on PostgreSQL, or the debug toolbar.\n\n**4. Assuming chain methods mutate.** `qs.filter(active=True)` on its own line does nothing; QuerySets are immutable-by-clone. Assign the result: `qs = qs.filter(active=True)`.\n\n**5. Accessing an FK object when you only need its id.** `if book.author.id == user.author_id` triggers a query; `book.author_id` doesn't. Same for serializers: `PrimaryKeyRelatedField` reads `author_id` only if you use `source=\"author_id\"` or it can — prefer the attname.\n\n**6. Filtering a prefetched relation.** `author.books.filter(genre=\"tech\")` inside a loop ignores `_prefetched_objects_cache` and queries every time. Use `Prefetch(\"books\", queryset=Book.objects.filter(genre=\"tech\"), to_attr=\"tech_books\")`.\n\n**7. Expensive work in middleware `__init__`.** It runs once per process at `load_middleware()`, which is fine for config — but people expect it per request (it isn't) or do network calls there (slowing every worker boot, and failing boot when the dependency is down).\n\n**8. Catching view exceptions with `try/except` around `get_response`.** Because each layer is wrapped by `convert_exception_to_response`, your `except` never fires — you get a 500 response. Use `process_exception(request, exception)` or the `got_request_exception` signal.\n\n**9. Calling `django.setup()` inside a library or inside `ready()`.** Libraries shouldn't configure Django; `ready()` calling it triggers the reentrancy `RuntimeError`.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Celery workers crash with `AppRegistryNotReady`.** *Symptom:* worker boot traceback. *Root cause:* `celery.py` imports a tasks module (which imports models) before `app.config_from_object(\"django.conf:settings\", namespace=\"CELERY\")` and the Django fixup has run setup, or a task module is imported at the top of `config/__init__.py`. *Fix:* set `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE` first, create the `Celery` app, call `app.autodiscover_tasks()`; never import models at the top of `config/celery.py`.\n\n**Memory growth in a long data migration or script.** *Symptom:* RSS climbs into gigabytes. *Root cause:* `DEBUG=True` makes every cursor a `CursorDebugWrapper` that logs queries into `connection.queries` (bounded, but still large), *and* iterating a giant QuerySet fills `_result_cache`. *Fix:* run with `DEBUG=False`, use `.iterator(chunk_size=...)`, call `django.db.reset_queries()` periodically if you must keep DEBUG.\n\n**Queries \"outside\" requests leak connections.** *Symptom:* `FATAL: too many connections` from cron containers or threads. *Root cause:* `close_old_connections()` is wired to `request_started`/`request_finished`; scripts, threads and Celery tasks don't fire those signals, so `CONN_MAX_AGE` is never enforced. *Fix:* call `django.db.close_old_connections()` at the start/end of long-running loops; use `connection.close()` in threads you spawn; Celery's Django fixup handles this for tasks.\n\n**Slow cold start on autoscaling.** *Symptom:* the first request of each new pod takes seconds. *Root cause:* `get_wsgi_application()` does `setup()` (imports every app's models) at import, but URLconf import, `load_middleware`, template engine setup and the first DB connection all happen lazily at the first request. *Fix:* warm up with a readiness probe that hits a cheap view that touches the URL resolver and DB; avoid heavy work at module import in `urls.py`.\n\n**Middleware \"doesn't run\" for some responses.** *Root cause:* an earlier (outer) middleware short-circuited — e.g. `CommonMiddleware` returning an `APPEND_SLASH` redirect, `SecurityMiddleware` an HTTPS redirect, or `FetchFromCacheMiddleware` a cached response — so inner layers never saw the request. *Fix:* reason from `load_middleware`'s order: outer = earlier in the list.\n\n**Debugging toolkit:**\n\n```python\nfrom django.db import connection, reset_queries\nfrom django.test.utils import CaptureQueriesContext\n\nwith CaptureQueriesContext(connection) as ctx:     # works without DEBUG\n    list(Book.objects.select_related(\"author\")[:10])\nprint(len(ctx.captured_queries), ctx.captured_queries[0][\"sql\"])\n\nprint(Book.objects.filter(genre=\"tech\").explain(analyze=True))  # PostgreSQL EXPLAIN ANALYZE\n\nimport logging\nlogging.getLogger(\"django.db.backends\").setLevel(logging.DEBUG)  # logs SQL when DEBUG=True\n```\n\n```bash\npython manage.py check --deploy          # system checks framework (registered in ready())\npython -X importtime manage.py check 2> imports.log   # find slow app imports at startup\npython manage.py shell -c \"from django.apps import apps; print(len(apps.get_models()))\"\n```\n\n**Scaling note.** Everything in this chapter is per-process: settings, the app registry, the compiled middleware chain, URL resolver caches (`get_resolver` is `functools.cache`d), template loaders. Horizontal scaling multiplies processes, so keep module-level state immutable and push shared state into the DB or cache.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What happens, step by step, when Django starts up?**\nA: The entry point (`manage.py`, `wsgi.py`, `asgi.py`) sets `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE` and calls `django.setup()` (directly, or via `get_wsgi_application()`). The first settings read makes `LazySettings` import your settings module over `global_settings`. `setup()` configures logging, sets the script prefix and calls `apps.populate(INSTALLED_APPS)`, which runs three phases: create `AppConfig`s by importing app packages, import every `models` module (each class body runs `ModelBase`, which registers the model), then call every `AppConfig.ready()`. The handler is then constructed and `load_middleware()` compiles the middleware chain. The URLconf itself is imported lazily on the first request.\n\n**Q: Why do you get `AppRegistryNotReady: Apps aren't loaded yet`, and how do you fix it?**\nA: Something asked the app registry for models before population finished — usually a standalone script importing models before `django.setup()`, or an app's `__init__.py`/top-level `apps.py` importing `models` during phase 1. Model class creation needs `apps.get_containing_app_config()`, which requires `apps_ready`. Fix by calling `django.setup()` before model imports in scripts, using management commands instead of raw scripts, and moving model imports into `ready()` or function bodies. Using `apps.get_model(\"app\", \"Model\")` at call time instead of import time also helps.\n\n**Q: How is `django.conf.settings` implemented?**\nA: It's a `LazySettings` instance, a `LazyObject` proxy whose `_wrapped` is empty until first attribute access. `_setup()` reads `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE`, builds a `Settings` object from the uppercase names of `global_settings` and then your module, and `__getattr__` caches each value in the proxy's `__dict__`. `settings.configure()` is the manual alternative. Only uppercase names count, which is why `debug = True` silently does nothing. `override_settings` works by replacing `_wrapped` and sending `setting_changed`.\n\n**Q: What does the `ModelBase` metaclass do when you define a model?**\nA: It runs `ModelBase.__new__` at import time: separates attributes with `contribute_to_class` from plain ones, resolves `app_label`, creates `Options` as `_meta` from your `Meta`, creates `DoesNotExist`/`MultipleObjectsReturned`, calls `contribute_to_class` on every field and manager (which registers the field in `_meta` and installs a descriptor), handles abstract/MTI/proxy inheritance, adds an auto PK and a default `objects` manager in `_prepare()`, and finally calls `apps.register_model()`, which also resolves pending string references like `ForeignKey(\"shop.Author\")`. That's why a model in a module that's never imported simply doesn't exist.\n\n**Q: How does `book.author` know to run a query, and why doesn't `book.author_id`?**\nA: `Book.author` on the class is a `ForwardManyToOneDescriptor`. Its `__get__` checks `book._state.fields_cache[\"author\"]`, which `select_related` or a previous access fills; on a miss it runs a `get(pk=book.author_id)` and caches the result. `author_id` is the FK's attname — a plain column value stored in the instance `__dict__`, served by a `DeferredAttribute` without any query. This descriptor is exactly where the N+1 problem lives. Reverse relations use `ReverseManyToOneDescriptor`, which returns a related manager rather than an object.\n\n**Q: Walk me through what happens when you call `Book.objects.filter(price__gt=20)` and then iterate it.**\nA: `Book.objects` is a `ManagerDescriptor` returning the manager; `get_queryset()` creates a `QuerySet` wrapping a fresh `sql.Query`. `filter()` clones the QuerySet (`_chain()`), and `Query.add_q()` → `build_filter()` resolves `price__gt` into a `GreaterThan(Col(price), 20)` lookup in the `WhereNode`, adding joins to `alias_map` if relations are spanned. No SQL runs. Iteration calls `_fetch_all()`, which runs `ModelIterable`: `query.get_compiler(using)` picks the backend's `SQLCompiler`, `as_sql()` produces `(sql, params)`, `execute_sql()` runs the cursor and fetches rows in chunks, and each row becomes an instance via `Model.from_db()`. The list is stored in `_result_cache` and prefetches run afterwards.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `str(queryset.query)` and the SQL actually executed?**\nA: `str(query)` calls `sql_with_params()` and then does plain `%` interpolation for display, so string params appear unquoted and types may render differently; it can even be invalid SQL. The executed statement keeps placeholders and passes params separately to the driver, which handles quoting and escaping — that's the ORM's SQL-injection protection. To see the real thing use `connection.queries`, `CaptureQueriesContext`, the debug toolbar or `explain()`. Interviewers ask this to see whether you understand parameterization.\n\n**Q: In what order do middleware run, and how does Django implement that?**\nA: Request phase runs top to bottom of `MIDDLEWARE`; response phase bottom to top. `load_middleware()` iterates `reversed(MIDDLEWARE)`, instantiating each class with the handler built so far, so the first entry becomes the outermost wrapper. `process_view` hooks are inserted at position 0 (top-to-bottom), and `process_exception`/`process_template_response` hooks are appended (bottom-to-top). Any middleware that returns a response from `__call__` without calling `get_response` short-circuits all inner layers and the view.\n\n**Q: Why can't a middleware catch a view's exception with try/except around `get_response`? (Senior)**\nA: Because `load_middleware()` wraps every layer — and the innermost `_get_response` — with `convert_exception_to_response`. When the view raises, the `process_exception` hooks get a chance, and if none returns a response, the wrapper calls `response_for_exception()`, turning `Http404` into a 404, `PermissionDenied` into 403, `SuspiciousOperation` into 400 and anything else into a 500 response (sending `got_request_exception`). So the outer layer receives an `HttpResponse` with a status code, never the exception. To act on exceptions, implement `process_exception`, connect to `got_request_exception`, or inspect `response.status_code`. This design guarantees every middleware's response phase runs even when a view crashes.\n\n**Q: What happens internally when you call `save()` on a model instance? (Senior)**\nA: `save()` → `save_base()` sends `pre_save`, saves MTI parents, then `_save_table()`. If the PK is set and not forced, it tries an UPDATE via `SQLUpdateCompiler`; zero affected rows makes it fall back to an INSERT via `SQLInsertCompiler` (with `RETURNING` on PostgreSQL). If `_state.adding` is true and the PK has a default (like a UUID), it skips the UPDATE attempt and inserts directly. It then sets `_state.adding = False` and `_state.db`, and sends `post_save` with `created`. It never calls `full_clean()`, and `update_fields` limits the columns — important for avoiding lost updates when two processes save different fields of the same row.\n\n**Q: How would you add database-specific SQL for a custom expression without breaking other backends? (Senior)**\nA: Subclass `Func` (or `Lookup`/`Transform`) and implement `as_sql()` as the portable default, then add `as_postgresql(self, compiler, connection)` or `as_sqlite` for vendor variants. `SQLCompiler.compile(node)` looks for `as_<connection.vendor>` first and falls back to `as_sql`. Always return SQL with placeholders and a params tuple; never format values into the string. For lookups, register them on the field class with `register_lookup`. This is the same mechanism Django's built-in functions use to cover vendor differences.\n\n**Q: How do the ORM's connections behave across threads and async code, and what leaks? (Senior)**\nA: `connections` is a `ConnectionHandler` storing one `DatabaseWrapper` per alias in an `asgiref` `Local`, so each thread (or thread-sensitive async context) has its own connection, opened lazily on first cursor. Lifetime management (`CONN_MAX_AGE`, `CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS`) is enforced by `close_old_connections()`, connected to `request_started` and `request_finished`. Threads you spawn, scripts and custom daemons don't emit those signals, so their connections live until the process dies or the server drops them — then you get \"connection already closed\" errors or connection exhaustion. Call `close_old_connections()` around units of work, and close connections when threads finish. Under ASGI the sync ORM runs on the thread-sensitive executor, so async views share one thread's connection.\n\n**Q: Where should you connect signals and why not in `models.py`? (Senior)**\nA: In `AppConfig.ready()`, typically by importing a `signals` module there. `ready()` runs in phase 3, after every model in every app is registered, so receivers can reference any model by class. Connecting at import time of `models.py` works by accident but can register twice if the module is imported under two paths, and it couples model import to side effects; `dispatch_uid` guards duplicates. `ready()` may run more than once in tests, so keep it idempotent and never query the database there — it also runs during `migrate` on an empty schema.\n\n**Q: Why are QuerySets lazy, and what triggers evaluation?**\nA: Laziness lets you compose queries across functions — a manager method adds a filter, the view adds ordering, the paginator adds LIMIT — and run exactly one query at the end. Each chain method returns a clone with a modified `Query`; nothing hits the DB until `_fetch_all()` runs. Iteration, `len()`, `list()`, `bool()`, `repr()`, pickling and slicing with a step evaluate; plain slicing adds LIMIT/OFFSET lazily. `count()`, `exists()`, `aggregate()`, `get()`, `first()` run their own queries immediately. The result cache lives on that QuerySet instance only.\n\n**Q: How do `prefetch_related` results get attached to instances?**\nA: After the main query fills `_result_cache`, `_prefetch_related_objects()` calls `prefetch_related_objects()`, which, per lookup, asks the descriptor's manager for `get_prefetch_querysets()` (5.0+, previously `get_prefetch_queryset`), runs one `WHERE fk IN (...)` query, groups rows in Python by key and stores each group in `instance._prefetched_objects_cache[name]` (or on `to_attr`). The related manager's `get_queryset()` checks that cache first. That's why `.all()` is free afterwards but `.filter()` re-queries — it builds a new query instead of reading the cache.\n\n**Q: What's the difference between a Manager and a QuerySet internally?**\nA: `Manager` is `BaseManager.from_queryset(QuerySet)` — a class generated by copying QuerySet's public methods onto the manager as proxies that call `self.get_queryset().<method>()`. The manager is attached to the model class via `contribute_to_class` and guarded by `ManagerDescriptor`, which blocks instance access. A QuerySet holds state (query, result cache); a manager holds none beyond its model and database hints. `from_queryset` or `QuerySet.as_manager()` is how custom QuerySet methods become chainable from `objects`.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Machine | Key class / function | Happens when | Classic error |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Settings | `LazySettings._setup()` | First attribute read | `ImproperlyConfigured: settings are not configured` |\n| Setup | `django.setup()` → `apps.populate()` | Entry point boot | `AppRegistryNotReady`, `populate() isn't reentrant` |\n| Model class | `ModelBase.__new__`, `Options`, `contribute_to_class` | `models.py` import (phase 2) | \"doesn't declare an explicit app_label\" |\n| Attribute access | `DeferredAttribute`, `ForwardManyToOneDescriptor`, related managers | `instance.attr` | N+1 queries, deferred-field queries |\n| Query build | `QuerySet._chain()`, `Query.add_q()`, `build_filter()` | Each chain call | `FieldError: Cannot resolve keyword` |\n| Query run | `_fetch_all()`, `get_compiler()`, `as_sql()`, `execute_sql()`, `ModelIterable` | Evaluation | Evaluating twice, huge `_result_cache` |\n| Save | `save_base()`, `_save_table()`, `_do_update`/`_do_insert` | `save()` | Lost updates without `update_fields` |\n| Request | `WSGIHandler`, `load_middleware()`, `get_response()` | Boot / per request | Wrong middleware order, swallowed exceptions |\n\n- Settings: only UPPERCASE names are read; values are cached on first access.\n- `populate()` = app configs → models → `ready()`. Never import models in an app's `__init__.py`.\n- A model exists only if its module is imported during phase 2.\n- `_meta` is the public introspection API: `get_fields()`, `get_field()`, `concrete_fields`, `label`.\n- Relation access is a descriptor; `fields_cache` (select_related) and `_prefetched_objects_cache` (prefetch) are the two caches it checks.\n- Every chain method clones; SQL is built only in `SQLCompiler.as_sql()` at evaluation.\n- `str(qs.query)` is for eyes only; params are sent separately.\n- First in `MIDDLEWARE` = outermost; each layer is wrapped by `convert_exception_to_response`.\n- Connections are per thread; lifetime is managed by request signals only.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" && django-admin startproject lab . && python manage.py startapp shop`.\n\n1. **Break startup on purpose.** Write `scripts/peek.py` that imports `shop.models` before `django.setup()`; read the traceback and find `check_apps_ready` in it. Then add `from .models import Book` to `shop/__init__.py` and observe the same error from `runserver`. Fix both.\n2. **Trace the metaclass.** In `shop/models.py`, connect a `class_prepared` receiver (defined above the models) that prints `sender._meta.label` and `[f.attname for f in sender._meta.concrete_fields]`. Run `manage.py check` and note the order models are prepared in.\n3. **Walk a Query.** In the shell, build `Book.objects.filter(author__name__startswith=\"A\").exclude(price__lt=10).order_by(\"title\")[10:20]`. Print `qs.query.alias_map`, `qs.query.where`, `qs.query.low_mark/high_mark`, then `qs.query.get_compiler(\"default\").as_sql()`. Compare with `str(qs.query)`.\n4. **Catch the descriptor.** Use `CaptureQueriesContext` to count queries for `[b.author.name for b in Book.objects.all()[:20]]`, then with `select_related(\"author\")`. Inspect `b._state.fields_cache` in both cases.\n5. **Write a lookup.** Implement and register the `ne` lookup from §5.4 plus an `as_sqlite` variant that prints a marker comment; confirm `compiler.compile()` picks the vendor method on SQLite.\n6. **Middleware ordering lab.** Write two middleware classes that print on request and response, plus `process_view` and `process_exception`. Put them in both orders, raise `Http404` and `ValueError` in a view, and draw the printed sequence; then add a `try/except` around `get_response` and prove it never catches.\n\n**Mini project — \"django-introspect\" management command:** `python manage.py introspect` prints, for every installed model, its table, PK type, every relation with the descriptor class installed on each side, the default manager, indexes and constraints — and, with `--sql \"shop.Book\"`, compiles a sample `.all()[:1]` through `get_compiler()` and prints the SQL and params. Add a `QueryBudgetMiddleware` (§5.5) and a test using `assertNumQueries` that fails when a view exceeds its budget.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters**\n- [Ch 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle) — the user-level view of the request flow.\n- [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings) — `INSTALLED_APPS`, `AppConfig.ready`, settings layout.\n- [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) and [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships) — what the metaclass and descriptors are building.\n- [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) — laziness and evaluation from the user's side.\n- [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) — fixing what descriptors cause.\n- [Ch 15 · Custom Managers, QuerySets & Model Inheritance](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance) — `from_queryset`, MTI parent links.\n- [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) — writing middleware on top of `load_middleware`.\n- [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals) — `class_prepared`, `request_started`, receivers in `ready()`.\n- [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi) and [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases) — the async handler and connection management.\n- [Ch 37 · Most-Asked Django Interview Questions: Core & ORM](topic.html?p=37-interview-core-orm) — rapid-fire practice on these internals.\n\n**Other handbooks**\n- [SQL · Execution Plans](../sql/topic.html?p=22-execution-plans) — reading what `explain()` returns.\n- [Database Design · Database Application Architecture](../database-design/topic.html?p=20-database-application-architecture) — ORMs, connection handling and the app/DB boundary.\n\n**Free resources**\n- **Django source code** — GitHub · *Advanced* · `django/db/models/base.py`, `sql/query.py`, `sql/compiler.py` and `core/handlers/base.py` are surprisingly readable. https://github.com/django/django\n- **Applications (the app registry)** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `AppConfig`, `ready()`, and the population process. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/applications/\n- **Model `_meta` API** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · the supported introspection surface. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/meta/\n- **How to write custom lookups** — Django docs · *Advanced* · the compiler protocol in practice. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/custom-lookups/\n- **How to create custom model fields** — Django docs · *Advanced* · `contribute_to_class`, `from_db_value`, `get_prep_value`. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/custom-model-fields/\n- **Middleware** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · official semantics of ordering, hooks and async support. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/http/middleware/\n- **Database instrumentation** — Django docs · *Advanced* · `execute_wrapper` for production query metrics. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/instrumentation/\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 36.*\n", "37-interview-core-orm": "# 37 · Most-Asked Django Interview Questions: Core & ORM\n\n> **In one line:** The Django core and ORM questions interviewers actually ask, from \"MVT vs MVC\" to \"why is this count wrong?\", with answers that start from the reason and end with the production pitfall, so you can show depth instead of reciting definitions.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** every concept chapter from [Ch 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle) to [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals). This chapter is a question bank. Each answer is short on purpose and links the chapter that teaches the topic in depth.\n\nDjango interviews follow a predictable path, and most of the time goes on two areas: the **request pipeline** (how a request becomes a response) and the **ORM** (how Python turns into SQL, and how that SQL goes wrong). This chapter covers both. [Ch 38 · Most-Asked Django Interview Questions: DRF, Security & Scale](topic.html?p=38-interview-drf-advanced) covers DRF, security, async, Celery and scaling. [Ch 39 · ORM Coding Round: Query Challenges](topic.html?p=39-orm-coding-challenges) is the live-coding round. [Ch 40 · Django Design Rounds: Building Real Systems](topic.html?p=40-design-rounds) is the system-design round.\n\n### How a Django interview is structured\n\n| Stage | Length | What happens | What they are really testing |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Recruiter / screening | 15–30 min | Rapid-fire basics: MVT, project vs app, `null` vs `blank`, `get` vs `filter` | Have you actually built something with Django? |\n| Technical deep-dive | 45–60 min | One topic at a time, then \"why?\" follow-ups: QuerySet laziness, N+1, transactions, migrations | Do you know the mechanism, or only the API? |\n| Live coding | 45–60 min | Write models plus QuerySets, sometimes a view or serializer; \"what SQL does this run?\" | Can you get the right result with a small, fixed number of queries? |\n| Design | 45–60 min | \"Design checkout / multi-tenant SaaS / notifications in Django\" | Data modelling, concurrency, async work, scaling path |\n| Behavioural / incident | 30 min | \"Tell me about a production bug you fixed\" | Debugging method, ownership, judgement |\n\n### What each level is expected to know\n\n- **Junior (0–2 years):** the request lifecycle at a high level, models and fields, basic QuerySets, forms, templates, the admin, running migrations. You should be able to say what happens and roughly why.\n- **Mid (2–5 years):** QuerySet laziness and caching, N+1 and `select_related`/`prefetch_related`, `F()` and `Q()`, aggregation, `atomic()`, migration conflicts, custom managers, CBV internals, signals and their pitfalls. You should be able to say which SQL runs and how many queries.\n- **Senior (5+ years):** the internals (`ModelBase`, `sql.Query`, the compiler, descriptors), concurrency (`select_for_update`, lock ordering, optimistic locking, `on_commit`), zero-downtime migrations on large tables, constraints as invariants, query plans and indexes, admin and ORM at tens of millions of rows. You should be able to explain the trade-off, how it fails, and how you would prove it.\n\n> **Interview tip:** Interviewers rarely care about the first answer. They care about the second and third \"why?\". Every answer below is built so that you can keep going when the follow-up comes.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\nThe topic map below lists what this chapter covers, which chapter teaches each area, and how often the topic comes up in interviews.\n\n| Area | Typical questions | Home chapter | Frequency |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Architecture & lifecycle | MVT vs MVC, what happens on a request, WSGI vs ASGI | [Ch 01](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle) | Very high |\n| Projects, apps, settings | project vs app, `INSTALLED_APPS`, `AppConfig.ready()` | [Ch 02](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings) | High |\n| URLs | `path` vs `re_path`, `reverse`, namespaces, ordering | [Ch 03](topic.html?p=03-urls-routing) | Medium |\n| Views | FBV vs CBV, `HttpRequest`, `JsonResponse`, `as_view()` | [Ch 04](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses), [Ch 17](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views) | High |\n| Templates | inheritance, autoescape, context processors, custom tags | [Ch 05](topic.html?p=05-templates) | Medium |\n| Forms | validation order, `clean()` vs `clean_<field>()`, `commit=False` | [Ch 06](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation) | High |\n| Admin | customisation, performance on big tables | [Ch 07](topic.html?p=07-django-admin) | Medium |\n| Models & fields | `null` vs `blank`, choices, `Meta`, `auto_now` | [Ch 08](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) | Very high |\n| Relationships | `on_delete`, `related_name`, `through` | [Ch 09](topic.html?p=09-relationships) | Very high |\n| QuerySets | laziness, caching, `Q`/`F`, `get` vs `filter`, `exists` vs `count` | [Ch 10](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) | Very high |\n| Migrations | how they work, conflicts, data migrations, zero downtime | [Ch 11](topic.html?p=11-migrations) | High |\n| Aggregation | `aggregate` vs `annotate`, subqueries, window functions | [Ch 12](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries) | High |\n| Optimization | N+1, `select_related` vs `prefetch_related`, `only`, bulk ops | [Ch 13](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) | Very high |\n| Transactions | `atomic`, savepoints, `select_for_update`, races | [Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) | Very high (mid+) |\n| Managers & inheritance | custom managers, abstract vs multi-table vs proxy | [Ch 15](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance) | Medium |\n| DB features | indexes, constraints, raw SQL, PostgreSQL fields | [Ch 16](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres) | Medium (senior) |\n| Middleware | order, hooks, writing one | [Ch 18](topic.html?p=18-middleware) | High |\n| Signals | when to use them, sync behaviour, pitfalls | [Ch 19](topic.html?p=19-signals) | High |\n| Internals | `ModelBase`, `LazySettings`, compiler | [Ch 36](topic.html?p=36-django-internals) | Senior |\n\nSome vocabulary that comes up in nearly every answer:\n\n- **Lazy evaluation**: a QuerySet does not hit the database until something needs its rows. *Why it matters:* most \"too many queries\" bugs come from evaluating a QuerySet repeatedly or at the wrong moment.\n- **Result cache**: once a QuerySet is evaluated, its rows are stored on that instance (`_result_cache`). *Why it matters:* reusing the same instance avoids a second query, while calling `.all()` or `.filter()` again creates a new QuerySet with an empty cache.\n- **N+1**: one query for the list, then one more query for each row's relation. *Why it matters:* this is the most common Django performance bug, and the most common question asked about it.\n- **Autocommit**: Django's default mode, in which each query commits on its own. *Why it matters:* without `atomic()`, the two writes in a \"transfer\" are not all-or-nothing.\n- **Savepoint**: a nested `atomic()` block. *Why it matters:* an inner failure can roll back only the inner work.\n- **Migration state**: the model state Django rebuilds by replaying the migration files. *Why it matters:* `makemigrations` compares your models to this state, not to the database.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### The answer framework\n\nA strong answer to almost any Django question has five beats. Interviewers grade the later beats, because that is where the difference between junior and senior shows.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 300\" width=\"100%\" height=\"300\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj37a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">The five-beat answer: each beat earns the right to the next</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"150\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. Direct answer</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">One sentence.</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">\"select_related is a</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">JOIN; prefetch is a</text>\n  <text x=\"95\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">second query.\"</text>\n  <path d=\"M172,105 L192,105\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"194\" y=\"50\" width=\"150\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"269\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. Why it exists</text>\n  <text x=\"269\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">The problem it solves.</text>\n  <text x=\"269\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">\"Accessing book.author</text>\n  <text x=\"269\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">in a loop runs one</text>\n  <text x=\"269\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">query per row.\"</text>\n  <path d=\"M346,105 L366,105\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"368\" y=\"50\" width=\"150\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"443\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">3. Mechanism</text>\n  <text x=\"443\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">How Django does it.</text>\n  <text x=\"443\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">\"Prefetch runs WHERE</text>\n  <text x=\"443\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">id IN (...) and joins</text>\n  <text x=\"443\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">in Python.\"</text>\n  <path d=\"M520,105 L540,105\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"542\" y=\"50\" width=\"150\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"617\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">4. Example</text>\n  <text x=\"617\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Code or SQL.</text>\n  <text x=\"617\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">\"Book list with</text>\n  <text x=\"617\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">authors and tags:</text>\n  <text x=\"617\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">101 queries down to 3.\"</text>\n  <path d=\"M694,105 L714,105\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"716\" y=\"50\" width=\"150\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"791\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">5. Trade-off</text>\n  <text x=\"791\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Where it breaks.</text>\n  <text x=\"791\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">\"JOIN on a to-many</text>\n  <text x=\"791\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">duplicates rows; filter()</text>\n  <text x=\"791\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">after prefetch re-queries.\"</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"190\" width=\"324\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"182\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Junior stops here</text>\n  <text x=\"182\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Correct, but sounds like the docs.</text>\n  <rect x=\"368\" y=\"190\" width=\"498\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"617\" y=\"210\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Senior signal lives here</text>\n  <text x=\"617\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Internals, numbers (query counts), and a production failure you have seen.</text>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"270\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"11\">Aim for 30–90 seconds. Then stop and let them choose the follow-up. Do not lecture.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\nThree principles behind the framework:\n\n1. **Lead with the answer, not the history.** \"It depends\" is only acceptable when you immediately say *on what*.\n2. **Talk in queries and SQL.** For any ORM question, the most convincing evidence you can give is \"this runs N queries, and this is the SQL\". The interviewer can check it, and it shows you have actually looked at what your ORM does.\n3. **Name a failure mode.** Every Django feature has a well-known way of going wrong in production: the prefetch that gets re-queried, the signal that fires on `save()` but not on `update()`, `locmem` caches that are not shared between workers. Mentioning one is the quickest way to show experience.\n\n### How to handle \"I don't know\"\n\nSay what you *do* know and reason towards the answer out loud: \"I haven't used `RESTRICT`, but I know `PROTECT` blocks any delete that would orphan rows, so I'd expect `RESTRICT` to be a softer version. I believe it allows the delete when the referenced object is itself being removed by a CASCADE in the same operation.\" Interviewers score your reasoning. A confident wrong answer costs you more than an honest, reasoned guess.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### The request-lifecycle whiteboard (redraw this in the interview)\n\n\"What happens when a request hits Django?\" is the most-asked Django question there is. Practise drawing this diagram in about 60 seconds.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj37b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj37c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">One request through Django: the whiteboard version</text>\n  <rect x=\"16\" y=\"60\" width=\"90\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"61\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Client</text>\n  <text x=\"61\" y=\"98\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">GET /books/7/</text>\n  <path d=\"M108,85 L128,85\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"130\" y=\"60\" width=\"110\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"185\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Nginx + Gunicorn</text>\n  <text x=\"185\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">or Uvicorn (ASGI)</text>\n  <path d=\"M242,85 L262,85\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"264\" y=\"60\" width=\"130\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"329\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">WSGIHandler</text>\n  <text x=\"329\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">builds HttpRequest</text>\n  <path d=\"M396,85 L416,85\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"418\" y=\"46\" width=\"200\" height=\"200\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"518\" y=\"66\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Middleware onion</text>\n  <rect x=\"434\" y=\"76\" width=\"168\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"518\" y=\"91\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">SecurityMiddleware</text>\n  <rect x=\"434\" y=\"102\" width=\"168\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"518\" y=\"117\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">SessionMiddleware</text>\n  <rect x=\"434\" y=\"128\" width=\"168\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"518\" y=\"143\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">CommonMiddleware</text>\n  <rect x=\"434\" y=\"154\" width=\"168\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"518\" y=\"169\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">CsrfViewMiddleware</text>\n  <rect x=\"434\" y=\"180\" width=\"168\" height=\"22\" rx=\"4\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"518\" y=\"195\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">AuthenticationMiddleware</text>\n  <text x=\"518\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">request: top to bottom</text>\n  <text x=\"518\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">response: bottom to top</text>\n  <path d=\"M620,85 L650,85\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"652\" y=\"60\" width=\"212\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"758\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">URL resolver</text>\n  <text x=\"758\" y=\"96\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">ROOT_URLCONF, in order, first match</text>\n  <path d=\"M758,112 L758,138\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"652\" y=\"140\" width=\"212\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"758\" y=\"160\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">process_view, then the view</text>\n  <text x=\"758\" y=\"176\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">FBV, or CBV via as_view() and dispatch()</text>\n  <path d=\"M758,192 L758,218\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"652\" y=\"220\" width=\"100\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"702\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ORM</text>\n  <text x=\"702\" y=\"256\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">QuerySet to SQL</text>\n  <rect x=\"764\" y=\"220\" width=\"100\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"814\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Template</text>\n  <text x=\"814\" y=\"256\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">render to HTML</text>\n  <path d=\"M702,272 L702,300\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"652\" y=\"302\" width=\"100\" height=\"40\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"702\" y=\"326\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">PostgreSQL</text>\n  <path d=\"M760,350 L520,350 L520,250\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"5 3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37c)\"/>\n  <text x=\"640\" y=\"366\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#166534\" font-size=\"10\">HttpResponse back out through middleware (bottom to top), then to the server</text>\n  <rect x=\"16\" y=\"150\" width=\"380\" height=\"120\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"206\" y=\"172\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Talking points that score</text>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"194\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Middleware chain is built once, by BaseHandler.load_middleware()</text>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"212\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">A middleware that returns a response short-circuits the inner layers</text>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"230\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">No URL match raises Http404 (technical 404 page when DEBUG)</text>\n  <text x=\"30\" y=\"248\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">request.user is lazy; the session is saved only if modified</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### The ORM evaluation pipeline (redraw for \"when does a QuerySet hit the DB?\")\n\nThe second diagram interviewers like to see explains why chaining methods costs nothing and evaluating costs a query.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 280\" width=\"100%\" height=\"280\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj37d\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">QuerySet: build lazily, evaluate once, cache on the instance</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"50\" width=\"200\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Chain (no SQL)</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">filter(), exclude(), order_by()</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">each clones and mutates sql.Query</text>\n  <path d=\"M222,90 L250,90\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"252\" y=\"50\" width=\"190\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"347\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Evaluation trigger</text>\n  <text x=\"347\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">iterate, list(), len(), bool()</text>\n  <text x=\"347\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">repr(), pickling, step slicing</text>\n  <path d=\"M444,90 L472,90\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"474\" y=\"50\" width=\"190\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"569\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">_fetch_all()</text>\n  <text x=\"569\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">get_compiler(using)</text>\n  <text x=\"569\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">as_sql(), execute_sql()</text>\n  <path d=\"M666,90 L694,90\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj37d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"696\" y=\"50\" width=\"168\" height=\"80\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"780\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">ModelIterable</text>\n  <text x=\"780\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">rows become instances</text>\n  <text x=\"780\" y=\"108\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">stored in _result_cache</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"160\" width=\"410\" height=\"96\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"225\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Reuses the cache (no new query)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"204\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">qs = Book.objects.all(); list(qs); list(qs)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"222\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">len(qs) after evaluation; qs.count() after evaluation</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"240\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">qs[0] after the QuerySet is evaluated</text>\n  <rect x=\"450\" y=\"160\" width=\"414\" height=\"96\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"657\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">New query every time</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"204\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">qs.all(), qs.filter(...): a new QuerySet with an empty cache</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"222\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">qs[0] on an unevaluated QuerySet: LIMIT 1 each time</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"240\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">qs.exists() / qs.count() before evaluation: separate SQL</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### A 45-minute technical round, minute by minute\n\n```text\n00–05  Warm-up: \"Tell me about a Django project you shipped.\" (They are choosing topics to dig into.)\n05–20  Request pipeline: lifecycle, middleware, CBV vs FBV, forms.\n20–40  ORM deep-dive: laziness, N+1, aggregation, transactions; \"what SQL does this run?\"\n40–45  Your questions. (Ask about their Django version, DB size, how they deploy migrations.)\n```\n\nBefore the interview, choose two production stories you can reuse: one about performance (an N+1 or a missing index) and one about correctness (a race condition or a migration that locked a table). Most follow-ups lead to one of them.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\nThis is the question bank. Every answer gives the reasoning first and links the chapter that teaches the topic in depth. Read each answer aloud, then try to defend it against a \"why?\".\n\n### 5.1 Django basics & architecture\n\n**Q: What is MVT, and how does it differ from MVC?**\nA: MVT stands for Model, View, Template. The **model** holds the data and business rules. The **view** is a Python callable that takes a request and returns a response, so it does what an MVC *controller* does. The **template** is the presentation layer, which MVC calls the *view*. The piece that does the dispatching, which MVC also attributes to the controller, is Django itself: the URLconf maps a URL to a view. The only real difference is naming. If you say \"the Django view is the controller\", most interviewers will be happy. See [Ch 01 · Architecture & Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle).\n\n**Q: Walk me through what happens when a request hits a Django application.**\nA: The web server (Gunicorn or Uvicorn) calls Django's entry point, `WSGIHandler` or `ASGIHandler`. Both subclass `BaseHandler`, which built the middleware chain once at startup with `load_middleware()`. The handler creates an `HttpRequest` and passes it through the middleware request phase from top to bottom. The URL resolver then tries the patterns in `ROOT_URLCONF` in order and uses the first match; if nothing matches, it raises `Http404`. Next, `process_view` hooks run and the view is called. The view usually queries the ORM and renders a template. The `HttpResponse` then travels back out through the middleware from bottom to top, and the server sends it to the client. If any middleware returns a response early, for example CSRF rejecting a POST, the inner layers and the view never run. See [Ch 01](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle) and [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware).\n\n**Q: What is the difference between a project and an app?**\nA: A **project** is the deployable unit: settings, the root URLconf, and the WSGI/ASGI entry points. An **app** is a reusable Python package with one area of responsibility, such as models, views, templates and migrations for `orders` or `billing`. A project is a collection of apps listed in `INSTALLED_APPS`. Apps let you split a codebase by domain, and they are also how Django discovers models, migrations, templates, admin modules and management commands. Interviewers often follow up with \"how do you split apps?\" A good answer is: by bounded context, keeping relationships between apps few and pointing in one direction. See [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings).\n\n**Q: What is `AppConfig.ready()` used for?**\nA: `ready()` runs once, after the app registry is fully populated, meaning all models are imported. It is the right place to register signal receivers (`from . import signals`), run system-check registrations, or do any start-up wiring that needs models. Do not run database queries in it. It runs in every process, including `migrate` and `collectstatic`, and the tables may not exist yet. Also bear in mind that it can run more than once in tests, which is one reason receivers use `dispatch_uid`. See [Ch 02](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings).\n\n**Q: How does `django.conf.settings` work, and why can't you change settings at runtime?**\nA: `settings` is a `LazySettings` object, a `LazyObject` that imports the module named in `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE` the first time any attribute is read. It is a process-wide global that many components read once and cache, such as the middleware chain, database connections and template engines. Changing a value at runtime is therefore unreliable, and it does not work across processes anyway. In tests, use `override_settings`, which also sends `setting_changed` so that caches are reset. In production, settings come from environment variables at start-up. See [Ch 36 · Django Internals](topic.html?p=36-django-internals).\n\n**Q: How do you structure settings for development, staging and production?**\nA: There are two common patterns. One is a `settings/` package with `base.py`, plus `dev.py` and `prod.py` that each do `from .base import *` and override values. The other is a single settings file driven entirely by environment variables, using `django-environ` or `os.environ`, which follows 12-factor. Either way, secrets such as `SECRET_KEY` and database URLs never go into git, `DEBUG` defaults to False, and `manage.py check --deploy` runs in CI. Most senior engineers prefer the environment-variable approach because the same image is promoted unchanged from staging to production. See [Ch 02](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings).\n\n**Q: What does `manage.py` do, and how do you write a custom management command?**\nA: `manage.py` sets `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE` and calls `execute_from_command_line`, which runs `django.setup()` and dispatches to the command. To add your own, create `yourapp/management/commands/<name>.py` with a `Command(BaseCommand)` class that implements `add_arguments()` and `handle()`. Commands are what you use for cron jobs, backfills and one-off maintenance. Make them idempotent and process work in batches so they are safe to re-run. See [Ch 02](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings).\n\n### 5.2 URLs & views\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `path()` and `re_path()`?**\nA: `path()` uses simple route strings with **converters** such as `<int:pk>` and `<slug:slug>`. The converter does the matching and also converts the type, so the view receives an `int`. `re_path()` takes a regular expression, and every captured value arrives as a `str`. Use `path()` by default. When you need a pattern `path()` cannot express, a custom converter (a class with `regex`, `to_python` and `to_url`) is usually cleaner than `re_path()`, and it also works with `reverse()`. See [Ch 03 · URL Routing](topic.html?p=03-urls-routing).\n\n**Q: Why use `reverse()` and URL names instead of hard-coded URLs?**\nA: A URL name separates *what* you link to from *where* it lives. With `reverse(\"orders:detail\", kwargs={\"pk\": 7})` or `{% url %}` in templates, you can change the route string without breaking any links. Namespaces (`app_name` in the app's `urls.py`, plus `include()`) prevent name clashes between apps, for example two apps that both have a URL named `detail`. Some follow-ups: `reverse_lazy` is for places that are evaluated at import time, such as `success_url` on a CBV, and URL patterns are matched in order with the first match winning. See [Ch 03](topic.html?p=03-urls-routing).\n\n**Q: FBV vs CBV: which do you prefer, and why?**\nA: Function-based views are explicit and easy to read top to bottom. They are good for one-off endpoints and for logic that does not fit the generic CRUD shape. Class-based views give you reuse through inheritance and mixins. The generic views (`ListView`, `UpdateView`) remove boilerplate for standard CRUD, but the behaviour is spread across the MRO, so it is harder to see what actually runs. A balanced answer is: CBVs for standard CRUD and shared behaviour such as a tenant-scoping mixin, FBVs when the flow is unusual. In DRF, the same trade-off applies between `APIView` and viewsets. See [Ch 04 · Views](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses) and [Ch 17 · Class-Based Views](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views).\n\n**Q: What does `as_view()` actually do?**\nA: `as_view()` is a classmethod that returns a plain function. URLconf needs a callable, and it cannot hold an instance shared across requests. On each request, that function creates a **new instance** of the class, calls `setup()` (which sets `self.request`, `self.args` and `self.kwargs`), and then calls `dispatch()`. `dispatch()` looks up the handler named after the HTTP method (`get`, `post` and so on), or returns `http_method_not_allowed` if there is none. Because each request gets a fresh instance, it is safe to store per-request state on `self`. Keyword arguments passed to `as_view(template_name=...)` override class attributes. See [Ch 17](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views).\n\n**Q: In a CBV, why do mixins go on the left?**\nA: Python resolves methods left to right along the MRO. A mixin such as `LoginRequiredMixin` overrides `dispatch()` and must run before the base view's `dispatch()`. Putting it first, `class OrderList(LoginRequiredMixin, ListView)`, means its `dispatch()` runs first and calls `super()`. Put it on the right and the base view handles the request before the login check runs, so it does nothing. This question is a check that you understand `super()` and the MRO, not only Django.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `HttpResponse`, `JsonResponse`, `StreamingHttpResponse` and `FileResponse`?**\nA: `HttpResponse` holds a body in memory. `JsonResponse` serialises a dict with `DjangoJSONEncoder` and sets the content type. You need `safe=False` to pass a list, a guard that dates from old JSON-hijacking attacks. `StreamingHttpResponse` takes an iterator and sends chunks as they are produced, which suits large CSV exports. Middleware that reads `.content` cannot work with it, and under WSGI it ties up a worker for the whole stream. `FileResponse` is a streaming response tuned for file objects and can use `wsgi.file_wrapper`. See [Ch 04](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses).\n\n**Q: What are `request.GET`, `request.POST`, `request.body` and `request.META`?**\nA: `GET` and `POST` are `QueryDict` objects. They are immutable and multi-valued, so use `getlist()` for repeated keys. `POST` is only populated for form-encoded and multipart bodies; a JSON body is not parsed into it, so read `request.body` and call `json.loads`. `request.FILES` holds uploads for multipart forms. `META` holds WSGI environ values such as `REMOTE_ADDR` and `HTTP_*` headers. The cleaner modern API for headers is `request.headers`, which is case-insensitive. A common gotcha: behind a proxy, `REMOTE_ADDR` is the proxy's IP. See [Ch 04](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses).\n\n### 5.3 Templates & forms\n\n**Q: How does template inheritance work?**\nA: A base template defines `{% block %}` regions. A child template starts with `{% extends \"base.html\" %}` and overrides only the blocks it needs. `{{ block.super }}` adds to the parent's content instead of replacing it. `{% include %}` is a different thing: it composes a fragment rather than inheriting a layout. The engine looks up templates through the configured loaders: `DIRS` first, then each app's `templates/` folder in `INSTALLED_APPS` order. That is why namespacing app templates (`orders/templates/orders/list.html`) matters. See [Ch 05 · Templates](topic.html?p=05-templates).\n\n**Q: How does Django protect templates against XSS, and when does that protection fail?**\nA: Autoescaping is on by default and escapes `< > ' \" &` in every variable. It stops being applied when you use `|safe`, `mark_safe()` or `{% autoescape off %}`. It is also not enough in some contexts: inside `<script>` blocks, in unquoted attributes, and in `href=\"{{ url }}\"` where the value could be `javascript:...`. To get data into JavaScript safely, use `{{ data|json_script:\"id\" }}`. See [Ch 05](topic.html?p=05-templates) and [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security).\n\n**Q: What is a context processor?**\nA: A context processor is a function that takes the request and returns a dict that is merged into the context of every template rendered with a `RequestContext` (which is what `render()` uses). Built-in examples add `request`, `user`, `perms` and `messages`. They run on *every* render, so a context processor that queries the database adds that query to every page. Keep them cheap, or make them lazy. See [Ch 05](topic.html?p=05-templates).\n\n**Q: Explain the form validation order.**\nA: `form.is_valid()` triggers `full_clean()`. For each field, `field.clean()` runs `to_python()` (convert the type), then `validate()` (field-level rules such as required), then `run_validators()`. Next, the form's `clean_<fieldname>()` runs, if defined, and must return the cleaned value. After all fields are processed, `Form.clean()` runs for checks across fields. In a ModelForm, `_post_clean()` then builds the instance and calls `instance.full_clean()`, which covers model field validation, `Model.clean()` and unique checks. Errors collect in `form.errors`, and `cleaned_data` holds only the fields that passed. See [Ch 06 · Forms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation).\n\n**Q: When do you use `clean_<field>()` versus `clean()`?**\nA: Use `clean_<field>()` for a rule about one field, such as \"the username must not be reserved\". It runs after that field's own validation, so its value is already in `cleaned_data`. Use `clean()` for rules that involve several fields, such as \"end date after start date\". In `clean()`, any field that failed validation is missing from `cleaned_data`, so use `.get()`, and attach errors to fields with `self.add_error(\"end\", ...)`. Raising `ValidationError` from `clean()` creates a non-field error.\n\n**Q: What does `form.save(commit=False)` do, and what is the catch?**\nA: It builds the model instance from the cleaned data without saving it, so you can set extra fields such as `obj.owner = request.user` before calling `obj.save()`. The catch is many-to-many data: an M2M row cannot be written until the instance has a primary key, so Django attaches a `save_m2m()` method to the form. You must call `form.save_m2m()` after `obj.save()`. If you forget, the M2M selections are silently lost. See [Ch 06](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation).\n\n### 5.4 Models & fields\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `null=True` and `blank=True`?**\nA: `null` works at the **database** level: the column allows NULL. `blank` works at the **validation** level: forms and `full_clean()` accept an empty value. They are independent, and the common combinations are `null=True, blank=True` for an optional FK, date or number, and `blank=True` alone for an optional `CharField`. Avoid `null=True` on `CharField` and `TextField`, because then both NULL and `\"\"` mean \"empty\", and every query has to check both. The exception is a unique optional string, where NULL is needed because many NULLs do not conflict but many `\"\"` values do. See [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields).\n\n**Q: How do you define choices, and what does `get_FOO_display()` do?**\nA: Modern Django uses enumeration types:\n\n```python\nclass Order(models.Model):\n    class Status(models.TextChoices):\n        PENDING = \"pending\", \"Pending\"\n        PAID = \"paid\", \"Paid\"\n        SHIPPED = \"shipped\", \"Shipped\"\n\n    status = models.CharField(max_length=16, choices=Status, default=Status.PENDING)\n```\n\nThe database stores the value (`\"paid\"`). `order.get_status_display()` returns the human label. Choices are validated only by `full_clean()` and forms, not by the database. If you need the database to enforce them, add a `CheckConstraint`. Since Django 5.0 you can pass the enum class directly to `choices=` without `.choices`.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `auto_now` and `auto_now_add`?**\nA: `auto_now_add=True` sets the value once, on creation. `auto_now=True` sets it on every `save()`. Both make the field non-editable in forms. The gotcha is that they are applied in the field's `pre_save()`, so `QuerySet.update()` does **not** touch an `auto_now` field. If you bulk-update rows, set `updated_at=timezone.now()` yourself. Another gotcha: `save(update_fields=[...])` only writes `updated_at` if it is in the list. A frequent alternative is `default=timezone.now` (pass the callable, do not call it), which still lets you set the value explicitly.\n\n**Q: What are the most important `Meta` options?**\nA: `ordering` sets the default sort, and it costs a sort on every query, so use it carefully. `indexes` and `constraints` hold `Index`, `UniqueConstraint` and `CheckConstraint`; these are preferred over `unique_together` and `index_together`, and `index_together` has been removed. `db_table`, `verbose_name` and `verbose_name_plural` do what their names say. `abstract = True` and `proxy = True` control inheritance. `get_latest_by` and `default_related_name` are less common. `permissions` adds custom permissions. A senior answer mentions that `Meta.ordering` is not applied to `GROUP BY` queries (since 3.1), and that a default ordering on a huge table can turn a cheap query into a sort. See [Ch 08](topic.html?p=08-models-fields).\n\n**Q: What primary key type does Django use, and what is `DEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD`?**\nA: Since 3.2, new projects set `DEFAULT_AUTO_FIELD = \"django.db.models.BigAutoField\"`, a 64-bit auto-increment. Older projects used `AutoField`, which is 32-bit and can run out at about 2.1 billion rows. Moving a large existing table from int to bigint is a slow rewrite, which is why the default changed. UUID primary keys (`UUIDField(primary_key=True, default=uuid.uuid4)`) hide row counts and allow ids to be generated offline. The cost is larger indexes, and random UUIDv4 values give poor insert locality. Time-ordered UUIDs (v7) fix that.\n\n**Q: What happens when you call `model.save()`? (Senior)**\nA: Django sends `pre_save`. It calls each field's `pre_save()`, which is where `auto_now` gets applied, then decides between INSERT and UPDATE. If the instance is new (`_state.adding`) and the pk has a default, it INSERTs directly. Otherwise, when a pk is set, it tries an UPDATE first, and if no rows were affected it INSERTs. The UPDATE writes **every** field unless you pass `update_fields`. After that it sets `_state.adding = False` and sends `post_save` with `created`. It does **not** call `full_clean()`, so model validation is skipped unless you call it yourself. Saving every column causes lost updates when two processes save the same row: the last full save overwrites the other's change. That is why `update_fields` and `F()` matter. See [Ch 08](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) and [Ch 36](topic.html?p=36-django-internals).\n\n**Q: Why doesn't `save()` call `full_clean()`?**\nA: For backward compatibility and performance, and because validation belongs at the input boundary: forms, serializers and admin. Saving internal data you already trust should not pay for validation queries such as unique checks. The consequence is that invariants which must *always* hold belong in the database, as `UniqueConstraint` or `CheckConstraint`, not only in `clean()`. Some teams call `full_clean()` in an overridden `save()`, but that does nothing for `update()` or `bulk_create()`. Since Django 4.1, `full_clean()` also validates `Meta.constraints`.\n\n### 5.5 Relationships\n\n**Q: Explain the `on_delete` options.**\nA: `CASCADE` deletes the dependent rows. `PROTECT` raises `ProtectedError` and blocks the delete. `RESTRICT` (3.1+) blocks the delete too, *unless* the referenced object is also being deleted through a CASCADE path in the same operation. `SET_NULL` needs `null=True`. `SET_DEFAULT` and `SET(value_or_callable)` replace the reference. `DO_NOTHING` leaves the integrity problem to the database, which will raise a foreign-key error unless you have defined DB-level behaviour. The key point that impresses interviewers: Django **emulates** these in Python with its deletion `Collector`. The foreign-key constraint Django creates has no `ON DELETE CASCADE`, so raw SQL deletes do not cascade, and a CASCADE over large child tables loads and deletes those children through Python (with batching and fast-delete where it can). See [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships).\n\n**Q: What is `related_name`, and what is `related_query_name`?**\nA: `related_name` is the reverse accessor on the target model. With `author = ForeignKey(Author, related_name=\"books\", ...)`, you write `author.books.all()` instead of `author.book_set.all()`. `related_query_name` is the name used in reverse lookups such as `Author.objects.filter(books__title=...)`; it defaults to `related_name` if that is set. You must set `related_name` when a model has two FKs to the same target, such as `sender` and `recipient` both pointing at User, or the reverse names clash. `related_name=\"+\"` disables the reverse relation. See [Ch 09](topic.html?p=09-relationships).\n\n**Q: When do you need a `through` model on a ManyToManyField?**\nA: When the relationship itself has data: when a student enrolled, their grade, a membership role. You define an explicit model with two FKs and point the M2M at it with `through=`. Since Django 2.2, `.add()`, `.create()` and `.set()` work with through models, and you pass extra fields with `through_defaults={...}`. Add a `UniqueConstraint` on the two FKs, because an explicit through table does not get uniqueness automatically. Many teams start with an explicit through model from the beginning, because converting an auto-created M2M later needs `SeparateDatabaseAndState`. See [Ch 09](topic.html?p=09-relationships).\n\n**Q: ForeignKey vs OneToOneField: what's the difference?**\nA: A `OneToOneField` is a `ForeignKey(unique=True)` whose reverse side returns a single object (`user.profile`) instead of a manager. If the related row does not exist, the reverse access raises `RelatedObjectDoesNotExist`. It is used for profile tables, for splitting rarely-used columns into a separate table, and implicitly by multi-table inheritance. `select_related` works in both directions for O2O, and forward only for FK.\n\n**Q: What is a GenericForeignKey, and why are seniors wary of it?**\nA: It uses the `contenttypes` framework to point at *any* model, with a `content_type` FK plus an `object_id`. It suits comments or tags that attach to many models. The costs: there is no real foreign-key constraint, so orphan rows appear; you cannot JOIN or `filter()` through it; and `select_related` does not work, although `prefetch_related` does (and Django 5.0 adds `GenericPrefetch` for querysets per content type). Often a better design is separate nullable FKs with a `CheckConstraint` that exactly one is set, or one table per target. See [Ch 09](topic.html?p=09-relationships).\n\n### 5.6 QuerySets\n\n**Q: What does it mean that QuerySets are lazy, and when are they evaluated?**\nA: Building a QuerySet (`filter`, `exclude`, `order_by`, `annotate`) only builds an internal `sql.Query`, and no database call is made. It is evaluated when something needs rows: iteration, `list()`, `len()`, `bool()` or `if qs:`, `repr()` (the shell does this), pickling, or slicing with a step. Slicing without a step on an unevaluated QuerySet returns a new QuerySet with LIMIT/OFFSET, and negative indexes are not supported. Some methods always run a query immediately: `get()`, `count()`, `exists()`, `aggregate()`, `first()` and `last()`. Laziness lets you compose queries across function calls without any cost until the end. See [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups).\n\n**Q: How does QuerySet caching work?**\nA: The first evaluation stores the instances in that QuerySet's `_result_cache`, and iterating the *same* object again reuses them. Any method that returns a new QuerySet (`.all()`, `.filter()`) starts with an empty cache. Indexing or slicing an *unevaluated* QuerySet does not fill the cache; it runs a LIMIT query each time. `count()` and `exists()` reuse the cache only if the QuerySet has already been evaluated.\n\n```python\nqs = Book.objects.filter(published=True)\nfor b in qs: ...       # query 1, fills qs._result_cache\nfor b in qs: ...       # no query\nqs.all()[0]            # new QuerySet: query 2 (LIMIT 1)\n```\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `get()` and `filter()`?**\nA: `filter()` returns a lazy QuerySet of zero or more rows and never raises for \"not found\". `get()` runs immediately and returns exactly one instance. It raises `Model.DoesNotExist` if there are none and `MultipleObjectsReturned` if there are several. Internally it adds a small LIMIT so it can detect duplicates without fetching everything. Use `get()` when exactly one row must exist (often through `get_object_or_404`), and `filter().first()` when \"zero or one\" is fine. Calling `get()` on a non-unique column is a latent bug: it works until a duplicate appears.\n\n**Q: `exists()` vs `count()` vs `len()`: when do you use each?**\nA: `exists()` runs `SELECT 1 ... LIMIT 1`, the cheapest way to ask \"is there at least one?\". `count()` runs `SELECT COUNT(*)`, which you need for the number itself, and which can be slow on big PostgreSQL tables because of MVCC. `len(qs)` fetches and builds every row, so use it only if you are going to use the rows anyway. In that case it is the best choice, because the later loop reuses the cache. The pattern to avoid is `if qs.count() > 0:` followed by a loop, which runs two queries; either `if qs.exists()` alone, or evaluate once and check the list.\n\n**Q: What are Q objects for?**\nA: Keyword arguments to `filter()` are combined with AND. `Q` objects let you build OR (`|`), NOT (`~`) and nested boolean logic, and you can build them dynamically, for example by folding a list of search terms with `|`. `filter(Q(a=1) | Q(b=2), c=3)` means `(a=1 OR b=2) AND c=3`, and positional `Q` arguments must come before keyword arguments. `~Q()` with multi-valued relations can produce subqueries that surprise people, so check the SQL. See [Ch 10](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups).\n\n**Q: What are F expressions, and why do they matter for concurrency?**\nA: `F(\"field\")` refers to a column's value *inside the database* rather than a Python value. `Product.objects.filter(pk=1).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - 1)` produces `UPDATE ... SET stock = stock - 1`, which is atomic, with no read-modify-write race. They also allow comparisons between fields (`filter(sold__gt=F(\"stock\"))`) and arithmetic in annotations. Gotcha: after `obj.stock = F(\"stock\") - 1; obj.save()`, the attribute still holds the expression, so call `obj.refresh_from_db()` before you read it, and do not save twice or the decrement happens twice. See [Ch 10](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) and [Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency).\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `values()`, `values_list()` and `only()`?**\nA: `values()` returns dicts and `values_list()` returns tuples (`flat=True` for a single column, `named=True` for namedtuples). Neither builds model instances, so they are faster and use less memory, but you lose model methods and relation descriptors. `only()` and `defer()` still return model instances with some columns not loaded. If you then access a deferred field, Django runs **one query per instance** to load it, which is an N+1 you have created yourself. Use `values_list` for exports and IDs, and `only()` when you need instances but the table has heavy columns. See [Ch 13](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization).\n\n**Q: How do lookups across relationships work, and what is the trap with multi-valued relations?**\nA: Double underscores follow relationships: `Book.objects.filter(author__country=\"IN\")` becomes a JOIN. For multi-valued relations, `filter(a, b)` in **one** call means both conditions must match the *same* related row. Chained `filter(a).filter(b)` allows *different* related rows. For example, \"authors with a book published in 2024 that is also a bestseller\" needs a single `filter(books__year=2024, books__bestseller=True)`, while chaining the two finds authors with any 2024 book and any bestseller. JOINs on to-many relations can also duplicate parent rows, so `.distinct()` may be needed.\n\n**Q: What does `get_or_create()` do, and is it race-safe? (Senior)**\nA: It tries `get(**lookup)`, and on `DoesNotExist` it creates the row with the lookup plus `defaults` inside an atomic block. On an `IntegrityError` it retries the `get()`. That retry only protects you if the lookup fields have a **unique constraint**. Without one, two concurrent requests can both miss and both insert, and you end up with duplicates and later `MultipleObjectsReturned`. So the honest answer is \"race-safe only with a DB uniqueness guarantee\". `update_or_create()` has the same requirement, and it takes a row lock with `select_for_update()` on the get path. Since 5.0, `create_defaults` lets the create use different values from the update. See [Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency).\n\n### 5.7 Aggregation & annotation\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `aggregate()` and `annotate()`?**\nA: `aggregate()` summarises the whole QuerySet into a single dict, such as `{\"total\": 1234}`. It ends the chain and runs immediately. `annotate()` adds a computed value to **each** row, and with an aggregate function inside, it adds `GROUP BY`. For example, `Author.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"books\"))` gives each author an `n`. `values(\"country\").annotate(n=Count(\"id\"))` groups by country. The position of `values()` relative to `annotate()` changes the meaning: before `annotate()` it sets the GROUP BY columns, after it only selects columns. See [Ch 12 · Aggregation, Annotation & Subqueries](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries).\n\n**Q: Why does combining two `Count` annotations give wrong numbers? (Senior)**\nA: Each to-many annotation adds a JOIN, and two JOINs on different to-many relations multiply rows together. An author with 3 books and 4 awards produces 12 joined rows, so `Count(\"books\")` and `Count(\"awards\")` both come out as 12. `Count(..., distinct=True)` fixes counts. It does **not** fix `Sum`, because duplicated rows inflate the sum. The robust fix is a correlated subquery for each relation:\n\n```python\nfrom django.db.models import OuterRef, Subquery, Count, IntegerField\nfrom django.db.models.functions import Coalesce\n\nbooks = (Book.objects.filter(author=OuterRef(\"pk\"))\n         .values(\"author\").annotate(c=Count(\"id\")).values(\"c\"))\nAuthor.objects.annotate(n_books=Coalesce(Subquery(books, output_field=IntegerField()), 0))\n```\n\nInterviewers love this one because it separates people who read the SQL from people who don't.\n\n**Q: How do you filter on an aggregate, the equivalent of `HAVING`?**\nA: Call `filter()` after the `annotate()` that defines the alias: `Author.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"books\")).filter(n__gt=5)` becomes `HAVING COUNT(...) > 5`. A `filter()` *before* the annotate is a `WHERE`, which restricts the rows that get counted. To count only some related rows, use the `filter=` argument: `Count(\"books\", filter=Q(books__year=2024))`, which compiles to `COUNT(...) FILTER (WHERE ...)` on PostgreSQL.\n\n**Q: When do you use `Subquery`/`OuterRef` and `Exists`?**\nA: Use `Subquery` when you need one value from a related table per row, such as \"latest order date per customer\" or \"price of the newest variant\", without a JOIN that multiplies rows. Use `Exists` for \"has any related row that matches\". It is usually faster than `filter(...).distinct()` because PostgreSQL can turn it into a semi-join. Since 3.0, an `Exists` expression can go straight into `filter()`. Use `OuterRef(\"pk\")` to refer to the outer query's row. See [Ch 12](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries).\n\n**Q: How would you get the top 3 books per author with the ORM?**\nA: Use a window function. Annotate `rank=Window(RowNumber(), partition_by=F(\"author\"), order_by=F(\"sales\").desc())` and filter `rank__lte=3`. Since Django 4.2, filtering on a window annotation is supported: Django wraps the query in an outer SELECT for you. On older versions, use a raw query or a `Subquery` with a `[:3]` slice and `pk__in`. This is the classic \"top-N per group\" problem, also covered in [SQL · Top N Per Group](../sql/topic.html?p=34-problem-top-n-per-group) and [Ch 39](topic.html?p=39-orm-coding-challenges).\n\n### 5.8 N+1 & optimization\n\n**Q: What is the N+1 query problem, and how do you detect it?**\nA: You load N objects with one query and then touch a relation on each one inside a loop or template (`{{ book.author.name }}`), which runs N more queries. It is invisible in development with ten rows and serious in production with ten thousand. To detect it: django-debug-toolbar's SQL panel (look for \"similar/duplicate queries\"), `connection.queries` with `DEBUG=True`, `assertNumQueries` in tests, `nplusone` or `django-zeal` in development, and APM traces in production that show the same SELECT repeated. See [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization).\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `select_related` and `prefetch_related`?**\nA: `select_related` follows **single-valued** relations (forward FK, OneToOne in either direction) with a SQL **JOIN**, so everything comes back in one query. `prefetch_related` handles **multi-valued** relations (M2M, reverse FK, GenericForeignKey) with **a separate query per relation**, `WHERE fk IN (...)`, and joins the results in Python, attaching them to each object's cache. A JOIN for to-many would duplicate the parent rows, which is why prefetch exists. Rule of thumb: \"one\" relation, use `select_related`; \"many\" relation, use `prefetch_related`; and you can combine them, as in `prefetch_related(Prefetch(\"books\", queryset=Book.objects.select_related(\"publisher\")))`.\n\n```sql\n-- select_related(\"author\")\nSELECT book.*, author.* FROM book INNER JOIN author ON book.author_id = author.id;\n-- prefetch_related(\"tags\")\nSELECT * FROM book;\nSELECT tag.*, book_tags.book_id FROM tag INNER JOIN book_tags ON ... WHERE book_tags.book_id IN (1, 2, 3, ...);\n```\n\n**Q: Why does my prefetch not work? I added `prefetch_related` but the queries are still there.**\nA: The usual cause is calling a QuerySet method on the prefetched manager in the loop, such as `author.books.filter(published=True)` or `.order_by()`. The prefetch cache only answers `.all()`, so anything else is a new query. The fix is to push that condition into a `Prefetch` object, ideally with `to_attr`:\n\n```python\nauthors = Author.objects.prefetch_related(\n    Prefetch(\"books\", queryset=Book.objects.filter(published=True).order_by(\"-year\"),\n             to_attr=\"published_books\"))\nfor a in authors:\n    a.published_books   # a plain list, no query\n```\n\nOther causes: the prefetch is applied to a different QuerySet object than the one you iterate, or a serializer accesses a field you did not prefetch.\n\n**Q: When is `select_related` the wrong choice?**\nA: When the JOIN is wide or deep and you do not need most of the columns, because you transfer far more data than necessary. When the joined table is huge and the join gets a bad plan. And it simply cannot be used on to-many relations. Calling `select_related()` with no arguments follows every non-null FK, which is rarely what you want. For a list page that shows only `author.name`, `select_related(\"author\").only(\"title\", \"author__name\")` keeps the payload small.\n\n**Q: How do `bulk_create`, `bulk_update` and `update()` differ from calling `save()` in a loop?**\nA: `save()` in a loop is one query per object plus signals. `bulk_create(objs, batch_size=...)` does a multi-row INSERT, `bulk_update(objs, [\"field\"])` does a `CASE WHEN` UPDATE, and `QuerySet.update()` runs a single UPDATE statement. All three **skip `save()`** and the `pre_save`/`post_save` signals, so any logic that lives in `save()` or a receiver does not run, and `auto_now` is not applied by `update()`. `bulk_create` sets PKs on PostgreSQL and supports `update_conflicts=True` (an upsert, 4.1+) and `ignore_conflicts=True`. A good answer mentions batching for memory, and that `bulk_update` is slower than people expect on very large sets. See [Ch 13](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization).\n\n**Q: How do you process ten million rows without running out of memory? (Senior)**\nA: Do not let the result cache hold them all. `qs.iterator(chunk_size=2000)` streams rows. On PostgreSQL it uses a server-side cursor, and it bypasses the result cache. `prefetch_related` works with `iterator()` only when you give a `chunk_size` (4.1+). Combine it with `values_list` or `only()` to shrink each row. For batch *updates*, iterate by primary-key ranges (keyset: `pk__gt=last_pk` ordered by pk, limited to a batch) so each batch is a short transaction, and avoid OFFSET pagination, which gets slower as the offset grows. Behind PgBouncer in transaction mode, server-side cursors break, so set `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS=True`, and then key-range batching is the safe approach. See [Ch 13](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) and [Ch 34](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases).\n\n**Q: How do you see the SQL a QuerySet will run?**\nA: `print(qs.query)` shows an approximation: parameters are interpolated without proper quoting, so it is not always valid SQL. `qs.explain(analyze=True)` on PostgreSQL shows the real plan. `connection.queries` lists executed SQL when `DEBUG=True`. `sqlmigrate` shows migration SQL. For tests, `CaptureQueriesContext` or `assertNumQueries`. In production, `pg_stat_statements` and the slow-query log are where you actually find the problem.\n\n### 5.9 Transactions & concurrency\n\n**Q: How does Django handle transactions by default?**\nA: Django runs in **autocommit** mode: each query commits as soon as it runs, unless you are inside `transaction.atomic()`. `atomic()` opens a transaction and commits on a normal exit or rolls back on an exception. Nested `atomic()` blocks create **savepoints**, so an inner failure rolls back only to the savepoint. `ATOMIC_REQUESTS = True` wraps every view in a transaction. It is easy, but it holds a transaction open for the whole request, including slow external calls. Most teams prefer explicit `atomic()` around the critical section. See [Ch 14 · Transactions & Concurrency](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency).\n\n**Q: What happens if you catch a database error inside an `atomic()` block?**\nA: On PostgreSQL, a failed statement puts the transaction into an aborted state, and Django marks the block as needing rollback. Any further query in the same block raises `TransactionManagementError`. The correct pattern is to wrap the statement that might fail in its own nested `atomic()`, so the failure rolls back only that savepoint and the outer transaction stays usable:\n\n```python\nwith transaction.atomic():\n    order.save()\n    try:\n        with transaction.atomic():       # savepoint\n            Coupon.objects.create(code=code, order=order)\n    except IntegrityError:\n        pass                              # outer transaction is still valid\n```\n\n**Q: What is `transaction.on_commit()`, and why is it important?**\nA: It registers a callback that runs only after the outermost transaction commits successfully. If the transaction rolls back, the callback is discarded. The classic case is enqueueing a Celery task or sending an email. If you call `task.delay(order.id)` inside the transaction, the worker can pick it up before the commit and fail to find the row, or the transaction can roll back after the email has gone. `transaction.on_commit(lambda: send_receipt.delay(order.id))` closes that gap. In tests, `TestCase` never really commits, so use `captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True)`. See [Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) and [Ch 31](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks).\n\n**Q: How do you prevent two requests from overselling the last item in stock? (Senior)**\nA: There are three tools, depending on the logic. (1) A conditional atomic update when the rule fits in SQL: `Product.objects.filter(pk=pid, stock__gte=qty).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - qty)`, and if it returns 0 rows, the item is sold out. This is a single statement with no explicit lock. (2) Pessimistic locking when you must read, decide in Python, then write: inside `atomic()`, `select_for_update()` locks the row until commit, so the second request waits. (3) A `CheckConstraint(condition=Q(stock__gte=0))` as the last line of defence. Lock rows in a consistent order (sort the ids) to avoid deadlocks, and keep network calls out of the locked section. See [Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) and [Ch 40](topic.html?p=40-design-rounds).\n\n**Q: What are the options of `select_for_update()`?**\nA: It must run inside `atomic()`, or Django raises `TransactionManagementError`. `nowait=True` raises `DatabaseError` immediately if the row is locked instead of waiting. `skip_locked=True` skips locked rows, which is ideal for job queues where workers take different rows. `of=(\"self\",)` restricts locking to certain tables when you use `select_related`. `no_key=True` (PostgreSQL) takes `FOR NO KEY UPDATE`, a weaker lock that does not block inserts of rows that reference this one. The QuerySet is evaluated lazily, so the lock is taken when it is evaluated, not when it is built.\n\n**Q: Optimistic vs pessimistic locking in Django?**\nA: Pessimistic locking (`select_for_update`) blocks other writers while you hold the lock. It is simple and correct but reduces throughput, and it can deadlock. Optimistic locking adds a `version` field and updates conditionally: `Doc.objects.filter(pk=pk, version=v).update(body=new, version=F(\"version\") + 1)`. If 0 rows are updated, someone else won, and you re-read or return a 409 conflict to the client. Use optimistic locking when conflicts are rare and transactions are long, such as edits made through a UI. Use pessimistic locking when conflicts are common and the critical section is short, such as inventory or balances. See [REST API · Optimistic Locking](../rest-api/topic.html?p=28-concurrency-optimistic-locking).\n\n**Q: What isolation level does Django use on PostgreSQL, and why does it matter?**\nA: Django uses READ COMMITTED, the PostgreSQL default. Each statement sees data committed before that statement started, so two SELECTs in the same transaction can see different data, and read-check-write logic races unless you lock or use conditional updates. You can set `OPTIONS: {\"isolation_level\": ...}` to REPEATABLE READ or SERIALIZABLE. Those raise serialization failures that your code must catch and **retry**, which Django does not do for you. See [SQL · Isolation Levels](../sql/topic.html?p=26-isolation-levels).\n\n### 5.10 Migrations\n\n**Q: How do migrations work internally?**\nA: `makemigrations` replays the existing migration files to build a **project state** in memory, compares it with your current models (the autodetector), and writes operations such as `AddField` and `AlterField` for the differences. It never looks at the database. `migrate` reads the `django_migrations` table to see what has been applied, builds the dependency graph, and runs each unapplied migration's operations as SQL. On PostgreSQL each migration runs in a transaction by default. Because the state comes from the files, editing an applied migration or deleting migrations creates drift between files and database. See [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations).\n\n**Q: How do you write a data migration correctly?**\nA: Use `migrations.RunPython(forwards, backwards)`, and inside the functions get models with `apps.get_model(\"app\", \"Model\")`, the **historical** model, never a direct import. A directly imported model reflects today's code, which may have fields that do not exist yet at that point in the history, so the migration breaks later. Historical models also have no custom methods and use only managers marked `use_in_migrations`. Process large tables in batches, provide a reverse function (or `RunPython.noop`), and for large backfills consider a management command that runs outside the deploy's migration transaction.\n\n**Q: Two developers created migrations `0005_a` and `0005_b` on different branches. What do you do?**\nA: Both depend on `0004`, so the graph has two leaf nodes, and `migrate` refuses to run with a \"conflicting migrations\" error. Run `makemigrations --merge`, which creates a merge migration that depends on both. That is fine when the two touch unrelated fields. If they conflict, for example both alter the same field, resolve it by hand, or rebase one branch and regenerate its migration. CI should run `makemigrations --check --dry-run` to catch missing migrations.\n\n**Q: What do `--fake` and `--fake-initial` do, and when are they appropriate?**\nA: `--fake` records a migration as applied without running its SQL. It is for repairing state after you made a change by hand, and it is dangerous otherwise. `--fake-initial` skips an initial migration if all of its tables already exist, which helps when adopting Django on an existing database. Related commands: `showmigrations` (status), `sqlmigrate app 0007` (see the SQL; do this before running anything risky in production), `migrate app 0006` (roll back to 0006), `migrate app zero` (unapply everything), and `squashmigrations` (collapse a long history).\n\n**Q: How do you add a NOT NULL column to a 100-million-row table without downtime? (Senior)**\nA: Do it in expand-and-contract steps. (1) Add the column as nullable, or with `db_default` (5.0+), which PostgreSQL 11+ can add without rewriting the table. (2) Deploy code that writes the new column. (3) Backfill in batches with a management command, with short transactions and throttling. (4) Add the NOT NULL constraint. On PostgreSQL you can first add a `CHECK (col IS NOT NULL) NOT VALID`, then `VALIDATE CONSTRAINT`, which takes a weaker lock, then set NOT NULL. Create indexes with `AddIndexConcurrently` in a migration with `atomic = False`. Set `lock_timeout` so a migration that is stuck waiting for a lock does not queue up all other traffic behind it. Never rename a column in place while old code is running: add the new column, dual-write, switch reads, then drop the old one. See [Ch 11](topic.html?p=11-migrations) and [Database Design · Schema Evolution](../database-design/topic.html?p=27-schema-evolution).\n\n**Q: What is `SeparateDatabaseAndState` used for? (Senior)**\nA: It lets you change Django's migration *state* and the *database* separately. It is used when the two must diverge temporarily, or when the database already matches. Examples: moving a model to another app without dropping and recreating the table (state operations delete and create the model, database operations do nothing or rename), converting an auto-created M2M to an explicit `through` model, or adopting a table created by hand. It is powerful and easy to get wrong, so always check the result with `sqlmigrate` and a fresh `migrate` on an empty database.\n\n### 5.11 Managers & model inheritance\n\n**Q: What is a manager, and how do you write a custom one?**\nA: A manager is the interface for table-level queries; `objects` is the default. Put reusable query logic in a custom **QuerySet** and expose it with `QuerySet.as_manager()` or `Manager.from_queryset()`. That way the methods can be chained: `Order.objects.paid().recent()`. Writing a `Manager` subclass alone only gives you methods that run first, at the start of the chain. Overriding `get_queryset()` in a manager changes its base set, for example to exclude soft-deleted rows. See [Ch 15 · Managers & Inheritance](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance).\n\n```python\nclass OrderQuerySet(models.QuerySet):\n    def paid(self):\n        return self.filter(status=Order.Status.PAID)\n    def for_user(self, user):\n        return self.filter(customer=user)\n\nclass Order(models.Model):\n    objects = OrderQuerySet.as_manager()\n```\n\n**Q: What is the danger of overriding the default manager to filter rows, for example for soft delete?**\nA: The first manager defined on a model becomes `_default_manager`. Django uses it in places such as `dumpdata`, admin and some generic code. Related-object access uses `_base_manager`, which is a plain `Manager` and does *not* apply your filter. So if `objects` hides deleted rows, `order.customer` can still return a soft-deleted customer, and uniqueness still covers deleted rows. Safer: keep `objects` unfiltered, add `active = ActiveManager()` explicitly, or make the soft-delete visible everywhere with partial unique constraints (`UniqueConstraint(..., condition=Q(deleted_at__isnull=True))`).\n\n**Q: Compare abstract base classes, multi-table inheritance and proxy models.**\nA: An **abstract** base (`Meta.abstract = True`) creates no table and copies its fields into each child's table. This is the usual choice for shared columns such as timestamps. **Multi-table** inheritance creates a table for the parent and for each child, linked by an implicit `OneToOneField` (`parent_link`). Every child query joins to the parent, and every save writes two tables, so it gets expensive and is rarely the right design. **Proxy** models (`Meta.proxy = True`) share the parent's table and change only Python behaviour: managers, methods, admin, ordering. They are useful for showing filtered views in the admin. See [Ch 15](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance).\n\n**Q: When would you actually choose multi-table inheritance? (Senior)**\nA: Rarely. It fits when you genuinely need to query across all subtypes through the parent table with shared FKs pointing at it, for example a `Payment` parent that a refund FK references, with `CardPayment` and `BankPayment` children that each have very different columns. The costs are a JOIN on every child query, a downcasting problem (from a `Payment` you cannot easily get the concrete subclass without `django-polymorphic` or a type column), and two-table writes. Alternatives: a single table with nullable type-specific columns plus a `CheckConstraint` per type, a JSONField for the variable part, or separate tables with a GFK or a union view. Say which one you would pick and why.\n\n### 5.12 Signals\n\n**Q: What are Django signals, and are they asynchronous?**\nA: Signals are an in-process observer pattern: senders call `signal.send()`, and every connected receiver runs. They are **synchronous**. Receivers run in the same thread and the same database transaction as the sender, before `save()` returns. A slow receiver slows the request, and an exception in a receiver propagates up to the sender and can roll back its transaction (`send_robust` catches exceptions). Register receivers in `AppConfig.ready()`, and use `dispatch_uid` to avoid connecting the same receiver twice. Async support (`asend`) arrived in 5.0, but it still does not make them background tasks. See [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals).\n\n**Q: When should you use signals, and when should you avoid them?**\nA: Use them for **decoupling** when the sender cannot know about the receiver: reacting to a third-party app's model (creating a `Profile` when `User` is created), or invalidating a cache on save. Avoid them for your own business logic. They hide control flow (someone reading `order.save()` has no idea it sends email), they do not fire for `update()`, `bulk_create()` or raw SQL, and they make ordering and transactions hard to reason about. Prefer an explicit service function (`place_order()`) that does the work and calls `on_commit` for side effects.\n\n**Q: Which ORM operations do *not* send `post_save`?**\nA: `QuerySet.update()`, `bulk_create()`, `bulk_update()`, raw SQL, and fixture loading with `raw=True` (the receivers get `raw=True` and should usually skip). `QuerySet.delete()` *does* send `pre_delete` and `post_delete` for each collected object, unless Django can fast-delete because no signals or cascades are involved. `m2m_changed` fires for `.add()`, `.remove()`, `.clear()` and `.set()`, not `post_save`. That is why a receiver on M2M data needs `m2m_changed` with `action=\"post_add\"`.\n\n### 5.13 Admin\n\n**Q: How do you customise the Django admin?**\nA: Use `ModelAdmin` options: `list_display`, `list_filter`, `search_fields`, `readonly_fields`, `fieldsets`, `inlines` (Tabular or Stacked), `actions` for bulk operations, `prepopulated_fields`, and `get_queryset()` for row-level scoping. You can override `save_model()`, `has_change_permission()` and similar hooks, as well as templates. Remember that the admin is an internal tool for trusted staff. It is not a customer-facing CRUD builder, and access should be locked down (2FA, a non-obvious URL, network restrictions). See [Ch 07 · The Django Admin](topic.html?p=07-django-admin).\n\n**Q: The admin change list for a 50-million-row table takes 30 seconds. How do you fix it? (Senior)**\nA: Work through the usual causes. (1) The paginator's `COUNT(*)`: use a custom paginator that estimates the count, or set `show_full_result_count = False`. (2) N+1 from `list_display` showing FKs: set `list_select_related`. (3) FK dropdowns on the change form that load millions of options: `raw_id_fields` or `autocomplete_fields`. (4) `search_fields` with `icontains` across joins: limit the fields, use `^` (startswith) prefixes, or add trigram indexes. (5) `list_filter` on high-cardinality fields builds large lists. (6) Ordering on an unindexed column. Profile with the debug toolbar, which works in the admin. See [Ch 07](topic.html?p=07-django-admin).\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `list_select_related` and overriding `get_queryset`?**\nA: `list_select_related` is a shortcut that applies `select_related` to the change list only. `get_queryset()` is the general hook: you can add `prefetch_related`, annotations (so a column like \"order count\" comes from one query instead of one per row), and permission filtering (`qs.filter(tenant=request.user.tenant)`). Annotations in `get_queryset` combined with a method in `list_display` that sets `ordering` make computed columns sortable.\n\n### 5.14 Middleware (core pipeline)\n\n**Q: How do you write a custom middleware?**\nA: Write a factory that receives `get_response` once at start-up and returns a callable that receives each request:\n\n```python\nimport time, logging\nlog = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\nclass TimingMiddleware:\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response          # runs once per process\n\n    def __call__(self, request):\n        start = time.perf_counter()               # request phase\n        response = self.get_response(request)     # inner layers + view\n        response[\"Server-Timing\"] = f\"app;dur={(time.perf_counter()-start)*1000:.1f}\"\n        return response                           # response phase\n```\n\nOptional hooks are `process_view`, `process_exception` and `process_template_response`. Add it to `MIDDLEWARE`; its position decides what it can see. See [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware).\n\n**Q: Why does middleware order matter? Give an example.**\nA: Request handling runs top to bottom and responses run bottom to top, and each middleware depends on what has run before it. `AuthenticationMiddleware` reads the session, so it must come after `SessionMiddleware`. `SecurityMiddleware` goes first so that HTTPS redirects happen before any work is done. Per-site caching needs `UpdateCacheMiddleware` first and `FetchFromCacheMiddleware` last. If your middleware needs `request.user`, it must come after `AuthenticationMiddleware`.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\nThe same question gets graded differently at each level. Use this table to aim your answer.\n\n| Topic | Junior answer (passes screening) | Mid answer (passes technical) | Senior answer (gets the offer) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Request lifecycle | URL to view to template | Middleware order, `as_view()`, `dispatch()` | `BaseHandler.load_middleware`, short-circuiting, WSGI vs ASGI and sync/async adaptation |\n| `null` vs `blank` | DB vs form | No `null` on strings; the unique-string exception | Constraints as invariants; `full_clean()` is not called by `save()` |\n| QuerySets | \"They're lazy\" | Triggers for evaluation, result cache, `.all()` clones | `sql.Query`, compiler, `_fetch_all`, iterator and server-side cursors |\n| N+1 | \"Use select_related\" | `select_related` vs `prefetch_related`, `Prefetch(to_attr)` | Detecting it in CI with `assertNumQueries`, JOIN explosion vs IN-list size, plan analysis |\n| Aggregation | `Count`, `Sum` | `annotate` vs `aggregate`, the `values()` position | JOIN multiplication; Subquery fix; window functions |\n| Transactions | \"Use atomic\" | Savepoints, `on_commit`, `select_for_update` | Lock ordering, isolation and retries, optimistic vs pessimistic choice |\n| Migrations | `makemigrations` / `migrate` | Conflicts, `RunPython` with `apps.get_model` | Zero-downtime expand/contract, lock timeouts, concurrent indexes |\n| Signals | \"Run code on save\" | Synchronous; registered in `ready()` | Why to avoid them for domain logic; `on_commit`; bypass paths |\n\n### When to use this chapter\n\n- Use it the week before an interview: read a section, cover the answers, answer aloud, then compare.\n- Use it after reading the concept chapters, as a self-test of whether you can *explain* the material and not only recognise it.\n- Use it to prepare interviewers: the follow-ups in §8 and §9 make good probing questions.\n\n### When NOT to use this chapter\n\n- Don't use it as a first introduction. Each answer is compressed, and the linked chapter is where you actually learn the topic.\n- Don't memorise answers word for word. Interviewers notice recited answers and immediately ask an unusual follow-up. Learn the mechanism instead.\n- For DRF, security, async, Celery and scaling, go to [Ch 38](topic.html?p=38-interview-drf-advanced).\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\nThese are the mistakes candidates make when *answering*, as opposed to when coding.\n\n- **Reciting the docs without the why.** \"`select_related` follows foreign keys\" is true and earns nothing. Add *why* (to avoid N+1 queries), *how* (a JOIN) and *when not* (to-many relations, wide joins).\n- **Saying \"Django handles it\" for concurrency.** It does not. Django runs in autocommit with no locking. If you claim `save()` is safe under concurrency, expect a follow-up that shows the lost update.\n- **Mixing up `select_related` and `prefetch_related`.** Use the memory aid \"select = SQL JOIN = single object; prefetch = Python join = plural\".\n- **Forgetting that bulk operations bypass `save()` and signals.** This is the follow-up interviewers ask most often to \"you put the logic in `save()`\" or \"you used a `post_save` signal\".\n- **Claiming `null=True` on a CharField is fine.** It shows you have not dealt with data that has both NULL and `\"\"`.\n- **Not giving query counts.** \"That's 1 + N queries, with `prefetch_related` it's 2\" is concrete and checkable. \"It's faster\" is neither.\n- **Talking for five minutes.** Stop after about 90 seconds. Let the interviewer steer, because they have a rubric and you are using up their time.\n- **Bluffing about internals.** \"I believe `get()` adds a LIMIT so it can detect multiple rows. I'd confirm with `qs.query`\" is better than inventing a class name.\n- **Ignoring the version.** Mention it when it matters: native connection pooling in 5.1, `db_default` in 5.0, filtering on window functions in 4.2, async ORM methods in 4.1. Staying current is a real signal.\n\nBest practice for any ORM question: say the SQL. Before and after, compare:\n\n```text\nWeak:   \"I'd optimize it with select_related.\"\nStrong: \"Right now that's 1 query for orders + 1 per order for customer, so 51 for a page of 50.\n         select_related('customer') makes it one JOIN. Items are reverse FK, so prefetch_related('items')\n         adds one IN query: 2 queries total, and I'd pin that with assertNumQueries(2).\"\n```\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n### Tricky and gotcha questions\n\nThese questions are designed to catch candidates who know the API but not how it behaves. Each one is a real production bug in disguise.\n\n**Q: `if queryset:` versus `if queryset.exists():` — which is better?**\nA: It depends on what comes next. `if queryset:` evaluates the *whole* QuerySet and fills the cache, which is wasteful if you only wanted to know whether it is empty, but ideal if you then loop over it, because the loop reuses the cache. `exists()` is a cheap `LIMIT 1` query, but a following loop runs a second, full query. Choose based on whether you will use the rows.\n\n**Q: Why does `Book.objects.all()[0]` inside a loop run a query every iteration?**\nA: Indexing an *unevaluated* QuerySet compiles to `LIMIT 1 OFFSET n` and runs immediately without filling the result cache. Each `qs[0]` is a new query. Evaluate first (`books = list(qs)`) and index the list, or use `first()` once outside the loop.\n\n**Q: A colleague overrode `save()` to update `slug`, but some rows have empty slugs. Why?**\nA: Those rows were written by a path that skips `save()`: `QuerySet.update()`, `bulk_create()`, `bulk_update()`, a data migration using historical models (which have no custom `save()`), raw SQL, or a fixture load. If the slug must always exist, make the column non-blank at the DB level with a `CheckConstraint`, and set it in every write path. Better still, generate it in one service function.\n\n**Q: Why does `Author.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"books\")).order_by(\"name\")` return each author several times when you add `.values(\"name\", \"books__title\")`?**\nA: `values()` *after* `annotate()` selects columns, and including a to-many path (`books__title`) adds a JOIN and makes each book a separate row. Every combination of author and book becomes its own row, and the GROUP BY now includes the book title. Decide what you want: either one row per author (drop the to-many column), or a flat list of rows (drop the annotation).\n\n**Q: You set `CONN_MAX_AGE` and now see \"server closed the connection unexpectedly\" errors. Why?**\nA: With persistent connections, Django reuses a connection across requests, and something in between (PgBouncer, a load balancer, the database's idle timeout) has closed it in the meantime. Set `CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS = True` (4.1+) so Django checks the connection before reuse, keep `CONN_MAX_AGE` below the proxy's idle timeout, or use the native pool (5.1+) or PgBouncer. See [Ch 34](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases).\n\n**Q: You prefetched `items`, so why does `order.items.filter(shipped=True).count()` in a template still run a query per order?**\nA: The prefetch stores an evaluated QuerySet for `order.items.all()`. A plain `order.items.count()` or `.exists()` is answered from that cache, because `count()` on an evaluated QuerySet returns `len(_result_cache)`. Adding `.filter()`, `.exclude()` or `.order_by()` creates a new QuerySet with no cache, so you get one `COUNT(*)` per order, which is an N+1. Fix it by annotating in the main query (`annotate(n_shipped=Count(\"items\", filter=Q(items__shipped=True)))`), or with `Prefetch(\"items\", queryset=Item.objects.filter(shipped=True), to_attr=\"shipped_items\")` and `len()`. Verify with the debug toolbar rather than assuming.\n\n**Q: Why does `Model.objects.filter(created__date=today)` miss rows or not use your index?**\nA: With `USE_TZ=True`, the `__date` lookup converts the timestamp to the current time zone before taking the date. The SQL wraps the column in a function, so a plain B-tree index on `created` cannot be used, and the result depends on the active time zone. Filter on a range instead: `created__gte=start_of_day, created__lt=start_of_next_day`, both aware datetimes. That is index-friendly and has explicit time-zone handling.\n\n**Q: Why is `exclude(tags__name=\"x\")` slower and different from what you expected?**\nA: `exclude()` across a multi-valued relation means \"has no tag named x\", and Django compiles it to a `NOT IN` / `NOT EXISTS` subquery, not to `tags.name != 'x'` on a JOIN. That is usually the semantics you want, but it surprises people who expected the JOIN version (which would wrongly keep rows that have both \"x\" and another tag). Use `~Exists(...)` explicitly when you want control over the plan.\n\n### Debugging toolkit to mention in interviews\n\n| Symptom | First tool | What you are looking for |\n|---|---|---|\n| Slow page | django-debug-toolbar SQL panel | Duplicate queries (N+1), slow single queries |\n| Slow query | `qs.explain(analyze=True)` | Seq scan on a big table, a sort spilling to disk, bad row estimates |\n| Query regression | `assertNumQueries` in tests | A count that crept up in a PR |\n| Production hot spots | `pg_stat_statements`, APM (Sentry, Datadog) | Highest total time, not only highest mean |\n| Lock waits | `pg_locks` / `pg_stat_activity` | Long-running transactions holding row locks |\n| Migration risk | `sqlmigrate`, a `lock_timeout` setting | Table rewrites, ACCESS EXCLUSIVE locks |\n\n> **Production story:** A team added `ordering = [\"-created\"]` to `Meta` on a 40M-row events table \"for the admin\". Every `Event.objects.filter(user=u)[:20]` began sorting on `created`, and there was no `(user, created)` index, so p95 went from 8 ms to 900 ms. The fix was a composite index and removing the default ordering. Mention this when someone asks about `Meta.ordering`.\n\n### Scaling notes for ORM-heavy answers\n\n- Count queries per request and per page, and set a budget enforced in CI.\n- A big IN list from `prefetch_related` (tens of thousands of ids) can be slower than a JOIN. Paginate first.\n- `count()` on huge PostgreSQL tables is a sequential scan. Use estimates (`pg_class.reltuples`) or cursor pagination.\n- Heavy reporting queries belong on a replica (`.using(\"replica\")`) or in a warehouse. See [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases).\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\nThese are the hardest follow-ups, the ones that come third or fourth in a chain. Each one is tagged Senior.\n\n**Q: Trace `list(Book.objects.filter(author__name=\"Ann\").select_related(\"author\")[:10])` from Python to model instances. (Senior)**\nA: `Book.objects` is a manager that returns a `QuerySet` wrapping a `sql.Query`. `filter()` clones the QuerySet and adds a `WhereNode` with a lookup resolved through `_meta` (the JOIN to author is created by `setup_joins`). `select_related` sets `query.select_related`, and the slice sets `low_mark` and `high_mark`. Nothing has run yet. `list()` calls `__iter__`, then `_fetch_all()`, which runs `ModelIterable`. That calls `query.get_compiler(using=db)`, and `SQLCompiler.as_sql()` builds the SQL and params (`SELECT ... INNER JOIN author ... WHERE author.name = %s LIMIT 10`). `execute_sql()` sends it through the DB-API cursor. For each row, `ModelIterable` builds a `Book` with `from_db()` and fills the related `Author` instance into the field cache. Results go into `_result_cache`. Being able to name the compiler and `from_db` shows you have read the source. See [Ch 36 · Internals](topic.html?p=36-django-internals).\n\n**Q: How does `prefetch_related` work internally, and what are its limits? (Senior)**\nA: After the main QuerySet is evaluated, `prefetch_related_objects()` walks the lookups. For each one it asks the relation descriptor for a `get_prefetch_queryset(s)` (for example, `ReverseManyToOneDescriptor` returns `Book.objects.filter(author__in=instances)`). It runs that one query, groups the results by the join key in Python, and stores them in each instance's `_prefetched_objects_cache` (or as a list on `to_attr`). Nested lookups (`books__tags`) repeat this one level down. Limits: a large IN list; the cache is answered only for `.all()`; memory use is proportional to all the related rows; and a `Prefetch` queryset with slicing was only supported from 4.2 (using a window function).\n\n**Q: Two concurrent transfers between the same two accounts deadlock. Explain and fix. (Senior)**\nA: Transfer A locks account 1 then requests 2, while transfer B locks 2 then requests 1. Each waits for the other, and PostgreSQL detects the cycle and aborts one with a deadlock error. The fix is to lock in a **consistent order**: `Account.objects.select_for_update().filter(pk__in=sorted([a, b])).order_by(\"pk\")`, evaluated in one statement inside `atomic()`. Then the debit and credit use `F()` updates. Also keep transactions short with no network calls inside, and retry the aborted transaction, since deadlock aborts are safe to retry. See [Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency).\n\n**Q: A `post_save` receiver sends an email, and users sometimes get emails for orders that don't exist. Why? (Senior)**\nA: `post_save` fires inside the transaction, before the commit. If the outer transaction later rolls back (a payment failure, or `ATOMIC_REQUESTS` with an exception afterwards), the email has already gone out for a row that never existed. If the receiver enqueues a Celery task instead, the worker may run before the commit and find nothing. Fix: `transaction.on_commit(lambda: send_email.delay(order.pk))` inside the receiver or, better, in an explicit service function. For at-least-once delivery across crashes, use an outbox table written in the same transaction. See [Ch 19](topic.html?p=19-signals) and [Messaging · Idempotency & Outbox](../messaging/topic.html?p=21-idempotency-outbox).\n\n**Q: How do you enforce \"a user can have only one active subscription\" reliably? (Senior)**\nA: Not with an `exists()` check followed by `create()`, which races. Use a partial unique constraint: `UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"user\"], condition=Q(status=\"active\"), name=\"one_active_sub_per_user\")`. The database rejects the second active row atomically. In code, catch `IntegrityError` (inside a nested `atomic`) and return a clean error. Since 4.1, `full_clean()` validates constraints too, so forms show a friendly message. The general principle: invariants live in the database, and application checks only improve the error message. See [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints & PostgreSQL](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres).\n\n**Q: A page made 3 queries yesterday and 400 today, with no change to the view. How do you find the cause? (Senior)**\nA: Something outside the view changed which attributes are accessed. Likely candidates: a template or serializer that now shows a new relation (`{{ item.product.brand }}`), a model `__str__` that touches an FK (used by the admin or logs), a property that queries, a context processor, or `only()` deferring a field the template now reads. I'd reproduce it with the debug toolbar, look at the repeated SQL, which shows the table, and use the stack trace option to find the line. Then I'd fix it with `select_related`/`prefetch_related` and add `assertNumQueries` so it cannot regress silently. For PRs, the `git log` of templates, serializers and models is often faster than profiling.\n\n**Q: How would you implement soft delete correctly? (Senior)**\nA: Add a `deleted_at` field, a custom QuerySet with `alive()`/`dead()`, and a `delete()` override on the QuerySet and the model that sets `deleted_at` (and a real `hard_delete()`). Make it safe: (1) uniqueness becomes partial (`condition=Q(deleted_at__isnull=True)`); (2) related access through `_base_manager` still sees deleted rows, so decide whether that is acceptable; (3) cascades are now your responsibility; (4) indexes should be partial on alive rows; (5) for privacy compliance (GDPR), soft delete is not deletion, so you also need a purge job. Many teams use an archive table instead, because then \"deleted\" really means gone from the hot table.\n\n**Q: `count()` on a 200-million-row table takes 20 seconds. What do you do? (Senior)**\nA: PostgreSQL's MVCC means `COUNT(*)` must check row visibility, which is effectively a scan; an index-only scan helps only if the visibility map is current. Options: avoid exact counts (cursor pagination, \"50+\" UI), use the planner's estimate (`SELECT reltuples FROM pg_class WHERE relname=...`) for unfiltered totals, maintain a counter table updated with `F()` or triggers, or cache the count with a TTL. For filtered counts, a matching partial index can make it fast enough. In the admin, `show_full_result_count = False` and a custom paginator. See [Ch 13](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization).\n\n**Q: Explain `select_for_update()` with `select_related` and the `of=` argument. (Senior)**\nA: `select_for_update()` on a joined query locks rows in *all* joined tables by default, which can lock far more than intended. For example, locking an order also locks its customer row, and other order operations for that customer then queue up behind it. `of=(\"self\",)` restricts the lock to the base table's rows. On PostgreSQL, rows on the nullable side of an outer join cannot be locked, so Django requires `of=` in that case. Combine with `no_key=True` when you only update non-key columns, so inserts of child rows that reference the locked row are not blocked.\n\n**Q: How do you make a migration that renames a heavily used column with zero downtime? (Senior)**\nA: Don't rename in place. Old pods running during the rollout would query the old name and fail. Use expand and contract: (1) add the new column (nullable), (2) deploy code that writes both columns and still reads the old one, (3) backfill in batches, (4) deploy code that reads the new column, (5) stop writing the old one, (6) drop it in a later release. If you only want the Python name changed, set `db_column=\"old_name\"` on the renamed field; that is a state-only change with no database operation. See [Ch 11](topic.html?p=11-migrations).\n\n**Q: When does the ORM stop being the right tool, and what do you use instead? (Senior)**\nA: When the query needs features the ORM cannot express cleanly (recursive CTEs, complex lateral joins, vendor-specific hints), when the ORM's generated SQL gets a bad plan and you need exact control, or for bulk ETL where building model instances is the bottleneck. Step down gradually: expressions and `Func` subclasses, then `RawSQL` inside annotations, then `Model.objects.raw()` (which still maps to instances), then `connection.cursor()` with **parameters, never f-strings**. Keep raw SQL in one module with tests, so schema changes break tests and not production. See [Ch 16](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres).\n\n**Q: How do you design a `Meta`/constraint set for an `OrderItem` table that will get 1 billion rows? (Senior)**\nA: Start from the queries. \"Items of an order\" means an index on `order_id` (Django creates FK indexes automatically). \"Units sold per product per day\" probably goes to a reporting replica or rollup table, not to `OrderItem` directly. Constraints: `CheckConstraint(condition=Q(quantity__gt=0))` and a unique `(order, product)` if that is the business rule. A BigAutoField pk, no `Meta.ordering`, `DecimalField` for money (never float), and a `unit_price` stored on the row (a price snapshot, not a JOIN to the current price). At that scale also plan for partitioning by time, which Django does not manage, so you would use migrations with `RunSQL` or a library, and keep FKs into the partitioned table to a minimum. See [Database Design · Partitioning](../database-design/topic.html?p=11-partitioning).\n\n**Q: How would you test that a view is free of N+1 regardless of page size? (Senior)**\nA: Write the test at two sizes, so a hidden per-row query shows up as a difference. Create 2 rows and capture the query count, then create 20 and assert the count is the same. With `assertNumQueries(n)` at one size, an N+1 is only caught if the size is large enough. With pytest-django, use `django_assert_num_queries` or `django_assert_max_num_queries`. Use `setUpTestData` or factories for the data, and include the related objects the serializer or template touches.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Question | 10-second answer |\n|---|---|\n| MVT vs MVC | View = controller; template = view; URLconf dispatches |\n| `null` vs `blank` | DB vs validation; no `null` on strings |\n| `get` vs `filter` | One object or raise vs lazy QuerySet |\n| `exists` vs `count` | `LIMIT 1` vs `COUNT(*)`; `len()` if you will use the rows |\n| QuerySet evaluation | iter, `list`, `len`, `bool`, `repr`, step-slice, pickle |\n| `select_related` vs `prefetch_related` | JOIN for FK/O2O vs IN query for M2M/reverse FK |\n| Prefetch re-query | `.filter()` on the manager defeats it; use `Prefetch(to_attr=)` |\n| `F()` | DB-side value; atomic `update(stock=F(\"stock\")-1)` |\n| `Q()` | OR/NOT/dynamic filters |\n| `annotate` vs `aggregate` | Per row (GROUP BY) vs one dict |\n| Two `Count`s wrong | JOIN multiplication; `distinct=True` or Subquery |\n| Bulk ops | Skip `save()` and signals |\n| `save()` | `pre_save`, UPDATE else INSERT, writes all fields, `post_save`, no `full_clean` |\n| Default transactions | Autocommit; `atomic` with savepoints |\n| `on_commit` | Enqueue tasks and emails only after commit |\n| Oversell | Conditional `F()` update, or `select_for_update` plus lock order |\n| Data migration | `RunPython` with `apps.get_model` |\n| Zero-downtime column | Expand, backfill, contract; `db_default`; concurrent indexes |\n| Signals | Synchronous, same transaction, register in `ready()` |\n| Inheritance | Abstract (no table), MTI (JOIN), proxy (same table) |\n\n**Remember this**\n\n- Say the query count and the SQL. That is the most convincing evidence you can give.\n- Laziness and caching: chaining is free, evaluation runs a query, and the cache lives on that QuerySet instance.\n- \"One\" relation, `select_related`; \"many\" relation, `prefetch_related`; anything other than `.all()` on a prefetched relation runs a query.\n- Anything that bypasses `save()` bypasses your `save()` logic and signals.\n- Correctness under concurrency comes from the database: constraints, conditional updates, row locks.\n- Side effects go in `on_commit`, never inline in a transaction.\n- Migrations compare models to the migration *files*, not to the database.\n- Every invariant that must hold belongs in a constraint; `clean()` only gives a better error message.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up a scratch project: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" django-debug-toolbar psycopg[binary]`, then `django-admin startproject mock . && python manage.py startapp shop`.\n\n1. **Lifecycle drill.** Without notes, draw the request-lifecycle diagram from §4 in 60 seconds. Then add a middleware that logs `request.path` in its request phase and the status code in its response phase, and confirm the order by adding a second middleware.\n2. **N+1 hunt.** Create `Author`, `Book` (FK) and `Tag` (M2M), and seed 50 books. Write a template loop that shows author and tags. Read the query count from the debug toolbar, fix it with `select_related` plus `Prefetch(to_attr=...)`, and lock it in with `assertNumQueries` at two data sizes.\n3. **Wrong count.** Add `Award` (FK to Author). Annotate both `Count(\"books\")` and `Count(\"awards\")`, show the inflated numbers, then fix them with `distinct=True` and with the `Subquery` version. Compare the SQL with `print(qs.query)`.\n4. **Race it.** Write a view that decrements stock with read-modify-write and fire 50 concurrent requests (`xargs -P 20 curl`). Observe the oversell, then fix it with the conditional `F()` update, and again with `select_for_update`.\n5. **Migration conflict.** On two git branches, add different fields and create a migration on each. Merge the branches, see the conflict, and resolve it with `makemigrations --merge`. Then write a `RunPython` backfill using `apps.get_model`.\n6. **Signal trap.** Add a `post_save` receiver that prints. Call `save()`, `update()` and `bulk_create()`, and note which ones fire it. Then move the side effect into `on_commit` and roll back a transaction to prove it does not run.\n\n### Mock-interview drills\n\n- **Rapid fire (10 minutes):** a partner reads 20 random questions from §5. You get 30 seconds for each. Score 1 point for the direct answer and 1 for the why.\n- **Follow-up ladder (15 minutes):** start with \"select_related vs prefetch_related?\" and have your partner ask \"why?\" four times in a row. Go on to internals, then to failure modes.\n- **Whiteboard (5 minutes):** \"What happens when I hit /orders/7/?\" Draw it, name every component, and stop at 90 seconds.\n- **Incident (10 minutes):** \"Our order list p95 went from 80 ms to 2 s after Tuesday's deploy.\" Walk through the debugging method out loud.\n\n### Mini project\n\nBuild a small \"library\" app with the models above, and write a `tests/test_interview.py` file with one test for each classic gotcha: lazy evaluation (`assertNumQueries(0)` while chaining), prefetch re-query, JOIN multiplication, bulk ops skipping signals, the `on_commit` rollback, and `get_or_create` with and without a unique constraint under threads (`TransactionTestCase`). Each test shows the behaviour as a failing assertion first, then the fix. This repository is a strong thing to talk about in an interview.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Concept chapters each answer comes from:**\n\n- [Ch 01 · Django Architecture & the Request/Response Lifecycle](topic.html?p=01-architecture-request-lifecycle) — MVT, handlers, the lifecycle whiteboard.\n- [Ch 02 · Projects, Apps & Settings](topic.html?p=02-projects-apps-settings) — apps, `ready()`, settings, commands.\n- [Ch 03 · URL Routing & URLconf](topic.html?p=03-urls-routing) — `path`, converters, `reverse`, namespaces.\n- [Ch 04 · Views, HttpRequest & HttpResponse](topic.html?p=04-views-requests-responses) — FBVs and response classes.\n- [Ch 05 · Django Template Language](topic.html?p=05-templates) — inheritance, autoescape, context processors.\n- [Ch 06 · Forms, ModelForms & Validation](topic.html?p=06-forms-validation) — validation order, `commit=False`.\n- [Ch 07 · The Django Admin](topic.html?p=07-django-admin) — customisation and performance.\n- [Ch 08 · Models & Fields](topic.html?p=08-models-fields) — `null`/`blank`, choices, `Meta`, `save()`.\n- [Ch 09 · Relationships](topic.html?p=09-relationships) — `on_delete`, `related_name`, `through`, GFK.\n- [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) — laziness, caching, lookups.\n- [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations) — autodetector, data migrations, zero downtime.\n- [Ch 12 · Aggregation, Annotation, Subqueries & Window Functions](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries) — GROUP BY, Subquery, windows.\n- [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) — select/prefetch, only/defer, bulk, iterator.\n- [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) — atomic, savepoints, locks, `on_commit`.\n- [Ch 15 · Custom Managers, QuerySets & Model Inheritance](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance) — managers, MTI, proxy.\n- [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres) — constraints as invariants, raw SQL.\n- [Ch 17 · Class-Based & Generic Views](topic.html?p=17-class-based-views) — `as_view()`, `dispatch()`, MRO.\n- [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) — order and hooks.\n- [Ch 19 · Signals](topic.html?p=19-signals) — synchronous receivers and pitfalls.\n- [Ch 34 · Scaling Django](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases) — connections, replicas.\n- [Ch 36 · Django Internals](topic.html?p=36-django-internals) — `ModelBase`, compiler, settings.\n- Next rounds: [Ch 38 · DRF, Security & Scale questions](topic.html?p=38-interview-drf-advanced), [Ch 39 · ORM Coding Round](topic.html?p=39-orm-coding-challenges), [Ch 40 · Design Rounds](topic.html?p=40-design-rounds).\n\n**Other handbooks:** [SQL · Isolation Levels](../sql/topic.html?p=26-isolation-levels), [SQL · Execution Plans](../sql/topic.html?p=22-execution-plans), [SQL · Indexes](../sql/topic.html?p=19-indexes), [Database Design · MVCC](../database-design/topic.html?p=03-mvcc), [Database Design · Schema Evolution](../database-design/topic.html?p=27-schema-evolution), [REST API · Optimistic Locking](../rest-api/topic.html?p=28-concurrency-optimistic-locking).\n\n**Free resources**\n\n- **Django documentation: Making queries** — Django Project · *Beginner* · the reference for lookups, laziness, Q and F. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/queries/\n- **Django documentation: Database access optimization** — Django Project · *Intermediate* · the official checklist behind half of the ORM questions. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/optimization/\n- **Django documentation: QuerySet API reference** — Django Project · *Intermediate* · exactly what each method does and when it evaluates. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/querysets/\n- **Django documentation: Database transactions** — Django Project · *Intermediate* · atomic, savepoints, `on_commit`, autocommit. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/transactions/\n- **Django documentation: Migrations** — Django Project · *Intermediate* · how the autodetector and historical models work. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/migrations/\n- **Django source code** — GitHub · *Advanced* · read `django/db/models/query.py` and `sql/compiler.py` once, and every internals question gets easier. https://github.com/django/django\n- **django-debug-toolbar** — Jazzband · *Beginner* · the tool to demo when they ask how you find N+1 queries. https://github.com/jazzband/django-debug-toolbar\n- **Classy Class-Based Views** — ccbv.co.uk · *Intermediate* · the MRO and method list for every generic view, the best preparation for CBV questions. https://ccbv.co.uk/\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 37.*\n", "38-interview-drf-advanced": "# 38 · Most-Asked Django Interview Questions: DRF, Security & Scale\n\n> **In one line:** The DRF, security, async, Celery, testing and scaling questions that make up the second half of every backend Django interview, plus the scenario questions (\"the API got slow\", \"users see each other's data\", \"duplicate orders on retry\") where you are judged on how you debug, not on what you remember.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 37 · Most-Asked Django Interview Questions: Core & ORM](topic.html?p=37-interview-core-orm) for the request lifecycle and the ORM, plus the concept chapters from [Ch 21 · DRF Serializers](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers) to [Ch 36 · Django Internals](topic.html?p=36-django-internals). Each answer here is compressed and links the chapter that teaches the topic.\n\nMost Django jobs today are API jobs, so once an interviewer is satisfied with your ORM knowledge, they move to DRF, then to how you secure the API, then to how it behaves under load. Questions at mid and senior level increasingly take the form of **scenarios**: an incident description with no stated topic. You are expected to form hypotheses, name the tools you would use, and explain the fix and how you would prevent it happening again.\n\n### How the second half of the interview usually goes\n\n| Stage | Typical prompt | What a strong answer shows |\n|---|---|---|\n| DRF fundamentals | \"Serializer vs ModelSerializer?\", \"ViewSet vs APIView?\" | You know the DRF request flow (`dispatch`, `initial`, handler) |\n| Auth & permissions | \"Session or JWT for our SPA?\", \"How do object permissions work?\" | Threat models and trade-offs, not a favourite library |\n| Security | \"How does Django prevent CSRF?\", \"What does `check --deploy` flag?\" | Exact mechanisms and the settings that turn them on |\n| Performance & async | \"When do async views help?\", \"How do you cache this endpoint?\" | Knowing where time actually goes (I/O vs CPU vs DB) |\n| Background work | \"How do you send 1M emails?\", \"A task ran twice — why?\" | At-least-once thinking, idempotency, `on_commit` |\n| Scale & ops | \"We're running out of DB connections\", \"Zero-downtime deploys?\" | Numbers: workers × threads × pods vs `max_connections` |\n| Scenarios | \"The API got slow after Tuesday's release\" | A method: scope, measure, hypothesise, verify, fix, guard against recurrence |\n\n### What each level is expected to know\n\n- **Junior:** write a ModelSerializer and a ModelViewSet with a router, use `IsAuthenticated`, explain CSRF at a high level, run tests with `APIClient`.\n- **Mid:** validation hooks, `get_queryset` scoping, object permissions and the list-view gap, JWT vs session trade-offs, pagination types, throttling scopes, Celery retries and `on_commit`, cache layers, `assertNumQueries` on API endpoints.\n- **Senior:** auth architecture for web plus mobile, token revocation, multi-tenant isolation that can't be bypassed by accident, idempotency keys, async vs threads vs workers sizing, connection budgets and pooling, read replicas with read-your-writes, safe deploys, and a clear incident-debugging method.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\nThe topic map below lists what this chapter covers, which chapter teaches each area, and how often it comes up.\n\n| Area | Typical questions | Home chapter | Frequency |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Serializers | validation order, nested writes, `SerializerMethodField` N+1 | [Ch 21](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers) | Very high |\n| Views, viewsets, routers | `APIView` vs `GenericAPIView` vs `ModelViewSet`, `@action`, `get_queryset` | [Ch 22](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers) | Very high |\n| Auth, permissions, throttling | session/token/JWT, `has_object_permission`, rate scopes | [Ch 23](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling) | Very high |\n| Pagination, filtering, versioning | cursor vs offset, django-filter, versioning schemes | [Ch 24](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning) | High |\n| Custom user & auth | `AbstractUser` vs `AbstractBaseUser`, backends, hashing | [Ch 25](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user) | Very high |\n| Authorization | permissions, groups, object-level, multi-tenant | [Ch 26](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization) | High |\n| Sessions & cookies | session engines, cookie flags | [Ch 27](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies) | Medium |\n| Security | CSRF, XSS, SQLi, clickjacking, deploy checklist | [Ch 28](topic.html?p=28-security) | Very high |\n| Caching | per-view, low-level, invalidation, stampede | [Ch 29](topic.html?p=29-caching) | High |\n| Async & ASGI | async views, async ORM, `sync_to_async` | [Ch 30](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi) | High (rising) |\n| Celery | retries, idempotency, `on_commit`, beat, canvas | [Ch 31](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks) | Very high |\n| Channels | consumers, groups, channel layer | [Ch 32](topic.html?p=32-channels-websockets) | Medium |\n| Testing | `TestCase` vs `TransactionTestCase`, factories, mocking | [Ch 33](topic.html?p=33-testing) | High |\n| Scaling databases | pooling, replicas, routers, multi-DB | [Ch 34](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases) | High (senior) |\n| Deployment | Gunicorn/Uvicorn, static, settings, migrations on deploy | [Ch 35](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production) | High |\n\nVocabulary the answers rely on:\n\n- **Authentication vs authorization**: *who are you* vs *what may you do*. *Why it matters:* DRF runs them as separate steps (`perform_authentication`, then `check_permissions`), and most data leaks are authorization bugs.\n- **Object-level permission**: a check against a specific instance. *Why it matters:* DRF calls it only from `get_object()`, so list endpoints need queryset scoping instead.\n- **At-least-once**: the delivery guarantee you actually get from Celery with `acks_late`, from retries, and from clients retrying. *Why it matters:* every handler that has side effects must be idempotent.\n- **Idempotency key**: a client-supplied unique key that makes a retried POST return the original result. *Why it matters:* it is the standard answer to \"duplicate orders on retry\".\n- **Connection budget**: processes × threads × pods, compared with what the database can accept. *Why it matters:* \"too many connections\" is the most common scaling outage in Django.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### The DRF request flow (draw this for any DRF question)\n\nAlmost every DRF question, whether about auth, permissions, throttling or serializers, is answered by knowing where in `APIView.dispatch()` the thing happens.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 380\" width=\"100%\" height=\"380\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj38a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj38b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">APIView.dispatch(): where auth, permissions, throttles and serializers run</text>\n  <rect x=\"16\" y=\"50\" width=\"130\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"81\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Django request</text>\n  <text x=\"81\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">after middleware and URL</text>\n  <path d=\"M148,78 L170,78\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"172\" y=\"50\" width=\"130\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"237\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">initialize_request</text>\n  <text x=\"237\" y=\"90\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">wraps in DRF Request</text>\n  <path d=\"M304,78 L326,78\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"328\" y=\"40\" width=\"300\" height=\"140\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"478\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">initial()</text>\n  <rect x=\"344\" y=\"70\" width=\"268\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"478\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">1. perform_authentication: request.user (lazy)</text>\n  <rect x=\"344\" y=\"104\" width=\"268\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"478\" y=\"122\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">2. check_permissions: has_permission()</text>\n  <rect x=\"344\" y=\"138\" width=\"268\" height=\"28\" rx=\"5\" fill=\"#ffffff\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"478\" y=\"156\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">3. check_throttles: rate in the cache</text>\n  <path d=\"M630,110 L652,110\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"654\" y=\"40\" width=\"212\" height=\"140\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"760\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Handler (list / create...)</text>\n  <text x=\"670\" y=\"84\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">get_queryset(): scope rows here</text>\n  <text x=\"670\" y=\"102\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">get_object(): object permissions</text>\n  <text x=\"670\" y=\"120\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">serializer.is_valid(): validate</text>\n  <text x=\"670\" y=\"138\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">perform_create(): save(owner=...)</text>\n  <text x=\"670\" y=\"156\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">paginate, serializer.data</text>\n  <path d=\"M760,182 L760,214\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"654\" y=\"216\" width=\"212\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"760\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">finalize_response</text>\n  <text x=\"760\" y=\"252\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">content negotiation, renderer</text>\n  <rect x=\"328\" y=\"216\" width=\"300\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"478\" y=\"236\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">handle_exception</text>\n  <text x=\"478\" y=\"252\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">401 / 403 / 429 / 400, via EXCEPTION_HANDLER</text>\n  <path d=\"M478,182 L478,214\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" stroke-dasharray=\"4 3\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"16\" y=\"290\" width=\"850\" height=\"74\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"441\" y=\"312\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">The three facts interviewers probe</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"332\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">has_object_permission runs only when get_object() runs, so list endpoints are protected only by get_queryset() filtering.</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"350\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Authentication is lazy, and failing to authenticate is not an error: the user becomes AnonymousUser, and permissions decide.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### The scenario-question method\n\nScenario questions are scored on method. Use the same six steps every time, and say each one out loud.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 250\" width=\"100%\" height=\"250\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj38c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">Debugging a production scenario out loud: six beats</text>\n  <rect x=\"16\" y=\"46\" width=\"132\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"82\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. Scope</text>\n  <text x=\"82\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Which endpoints?</text>\n  <text x=\"82\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">All users or some?</text>\n  <text x=\"82\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Since when?</text>\n  <path d=\"M150,100 L162,100\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"164\" y=\"46\" width=\"132\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. Stop the bleed</text>\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Roll back, flip a flag,</text>\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">disable the cache.</text>\n  <text x=\"230\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Mitigate first.</text>\n  <path d=\"M298,100 L310,100\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"312\" y=\"46\" width=\"132\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">3. Measure</text>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">APM traces, Sentry,</text>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">pg_stat_statements,</text>\n  <text x=\"378\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">deploy diff.</text>\n  <path d=\"M446,100 L458,100\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"460\" y=\"46\" width=\"132\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"526\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">4. Hypothesise</text>\n  <text x=\"526\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Two or three ranked</text>\n  <text x=\"526\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">causes, each with</text>\n  <text x=\"526\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">a test that rules it out.</text>\n  <path d=\"M594,100 L606,100\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"608\" y=\"46\" width=\"124\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"670\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">5. Fix</text>\n  <text x=\"670\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Smallest safe change,</text>\n  <text x=\"670\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">then verify the</text>\n  <text x=\"670\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">metric recovers.</text>\n  <path d=\"M734,100 L746,100\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"748\" y=\"46\" width=\"118\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"807\" y=\"68\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">6. Prevent</text>\n  <text x=\"807\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Test, alert,</text>\n  <text x=\"807\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">constraint, or</text>\n  <text x=\"807\" y=\"116\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">guard-rail in CI.</text>\n  <rect x=\"16\" y=\"180\" width=\"850\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"441\" y=\"200\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">The senior signal is beats 2 and 6</text>\n  <text x=\"441\" y=\"218\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Juniors jump straight to a fix. Seniors mitigate first and finish with a structural guard so the bug class cannot return.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Principles for this half of the interview\n\n1. **Security answers name the mechanism and the setting.** \"Django has CSRF protection\" earns nothing. \"`CsrfViewMiddleware` compares a masked token from the form or the `X-CSRFToken` header with the `csrftoken` cookie on unsafe methods, and checks `Origin` against `CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS` on HTTPS\" earns the point.\n2. **Scale answers come with arithmetic.** \"4 pods × 9 Gunicorn workers × 1 connection = 36, plus Celery 4 × 8 = 32, plus beat and cron, against `max_connections=100`\" is the answer interviewers are looking for.\n3. **Every side effect is at-least-once.** Retries from clients, Celery redeliveries and webhook replays all mean the handler must be idempotent. Say so before they ask.\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### The production topology to draw for \"how would you deploy and scale this?\"\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 330\" width=\"100%\" height=\"330\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj38d\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj38e\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"14\" font-weight=\"bold\">A typical production Django stack and where each question lives</text>\n  <rect x=\"16\" y=\"50\" width=\"110\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"71\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">CDN</text>\n  <text x=\"71\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">static, cached GETs</text>\n  <path d=\"M128,75 L148,75\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"150\" y=\"50\" width=\"120\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Load balancer</text>\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">TLS ends here</text>\n  <path d=\"M272,75 L292,75\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"294\" y=\"40\" width=\"250\" height=\"120\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"419\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Web pods (N replicas)</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"82\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Gunicorn: 2 x cores + 1 sync workers</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">or Uvicorn workers for ASGI</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"118\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER set</text>\n  <text x=\"310\" y=\"136\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">1 DB connection per worker thread</text>\n  <path d=\"M546,100 L580,100\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"582\" y=\"70\" width=\"120\" height=\"60\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"642\" y=\"94\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">PgBouncer</text>\n  <text x=\"642\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">or native pool (5.1+)</text>\n  <path d=\"M704,100 L730,100\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"732\" y=\"50\" width=\"134\" height=\"50\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"799\" y=\"72\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Postgres primary</text>\n  <text x=\"799\" y=\"88\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">writes, max_connections</text>\n  <rect x=\"732\" y=\"116\" width=\"134\" height=\"44\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"799\" y=\"136\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Read replica</text>\n  <text x=\"799\" y=\"150\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">router, lag</text>\n  <path d=\"M799,102 L799,114\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38e)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"294\" y=\"190\" width=\"160\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"374\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Redis</text>\n  <text x=\"374\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">cache, throttles, broker, layer</text>\n  <path d=\"M374,162 L374,188\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"490\" y=\"190\" width=\"200\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"590\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Celery workers + beat</text>\n  <text x=\"590\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">own connection budget, acks_late</text>\n  <path d=\"M456,218 L488,218\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38d)\"/>\n  <path d=\"M692,218 L740,218 L740,164\" stroke=\"#d97706\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"16\" y=\"190\" width=\"250\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"141\" y=\"212\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Channels (ASGI) pods</text>\n  <text x=\"141\" y=\"230\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">WebSockets via the Redis channel layer</text>\n  <path d=\"M268,218 L292,218\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj38d)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"16\" y=\"268\" width=\"850\" height=\"48\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"441\" y=\"288\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Release step (separate job): migrate, then roll out pods; collectstatic at build time</text>\n  <text x=\"441\" y=\"305\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Every arrow is a question: cache headers, proxy SSL, worker sizing, pooling, replica lag, broker reliability.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### The request path for a typical authenticated API call\n\n```text\nClient --(Authorization: Bearer <jwt>)--> LB --> Gunicorn worker\n  SecurityMiddleware -> SessionMiddleware -> CommonMiddleware -> CsrfViewMiddleware (view is csrf_exempt via APIView)\n  -> AuthenticationMiddleware -> URL resolver -> OrderViewSet.as_view({\"get\": \"list\"})\n  APIView.dispatch -> initial: JWTAuthentication (lazy) -> IsAuthenticated -> UserRateThrottle (Redis)\n  list(): get_queryset() scoped to request.user -> filter_queryset -> paginate_queryset -> serializer -> Response\n  finalize_response -> JSONRenderer -> back out through middleware\n```\n\n> **Gotcha:** `APIView.as_view()` wraps the view in `csrf_exempt`. CSRF is then enforced again *only* by `SessionAuthentication`, for requests it authenticates. That is why token- and JWT-authenticated calls don't need a CSRF token, while browser session calls still do.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\nThis is the question bank. Answer each one aloud before reading the answer. Every group links its home chapter.\n\n### 5.1 DRF serializers\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `Serializer` and `ModelSerializer`?**\nA: A `Serializer` declares every field explicitly and requires you to write `create()` and `update()`. A `ModelSerializer` inspects the model to generate fields, includes validators for model field constraints and `unique` / `unique_together` / `UniqueConstraint` checks, and provides default `create()` and `update()`. Use `ModelSerializer` for CRUD on a model. Use a plain `Serializer` for input that does not map onto one model, such as a \"checkout\" command or a search request. Interviewers often follow up: ModelSerializer does **not** call `Model.clean()`, so validation in `clean()` is skipped unless you call it yourself. See [Ch 21 · DRF Serializers](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers).\n\n**Q: Explain the serializer validation order.**\nA: `is_valid()` calls `run_validation(data)`. First, `to_internal_value()` runs each field's own validation (type conversion, `required`, `max_length`, field validators), followed by that field's `validate_<field>(value)` method. Next, serializer-level `Meta.validators` run, such as `UniqueTogetherValidator`. Finally, `validate(attrs)` runs for checks across fields. Errors collect in `serializer.errors`, and `validated_data` is available only after validation passes. `is_valid(raise_exception=True)` raises `ValidationError`, which DRF turns into a 400. Calling `.save()` dispatches to `create()` or `update()`, depending on whether an `instance` was passed.\n\n**Q: How do you write a nested writable serializer?**\nA: DRF does not write nested data automatically, because it cannot know your intent: should missing children be deleted? Should the parent's id be trusted? You override `create()` (and `update()`), pop the nested data, create the parent, then create the children, all inside `transaction.atomic()`:\n\n```python\nclass OrderSerializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    items = OrderItemSerializer(many=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        model = Order\n        fields = [\"id\", \"items\", \"note\"]\n\n    @transaction.atomic\n    def create(self, validated_data):\n        items = validated_data.pop(\"items\")\n        order = Order.objects.create(customer=self.context[\"request\"].user, **validated_data)\n        OrderItem.objects.bulk_create([OrderItem(order=order, **i) for i in items])\n        return order\n```\n\nFor updates, decide explicitly whether to replace, merge or reject changes to the children. Many teams use separate endpoints for child resources instead.\n\n**Q: Why can `SerializerMethodField` cause N+1 queries, and how do you fix it?**\nA: The method runs once per object during `to_representation`, so if it queries the database (`obj.reviews.count()`), a list of 100 objects runs 100 queries. The fix is to move the computation into the viewset's `get_queryset()`: `annotate(review_count=Count(\"reviews\"))` and read it with `serializers.IntegerField(read_only=True)`, or prefetch and compute from the cache. The same applies to nested serializers. Every relation a serializer touches needs a matching `select_related` or `prefetch_related` in `get_queryset()`. See [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization).\n\n**Q: How do you set the owner of an object from the request, not from client input?**\nA: Never accept `owner` as a writable field. Either mark it `read_only` and set it in the view with `perform_create(self, serializer): serializer.save(owner=self.request.user)`, or use `owner = serializers.HiddenField(default=serializers.CurrentUserDefault())`. Both prevent **mass assignment**, where a client sets fields it should not control. Related point: `fields = \"__all__\"` is risky on any model with sensitive fields. List fields explicitly.\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `read_only`, `write_only`, `read_only_fields` and `extra_kwargs`?**\nA: `read_only=True` fields appear in output but are ignored on input (ids, computed values). `write_only=True` fields are accepted on input but never returned (passwords). `Meta.read_only_fields` is a shortcut for marking generated fields read-only, and `extra_kwargs` sets options on generated fields without redeclaring them, for example `{\"password\": {\"write_only\": True}}`. Note that `read_only_fields` does not apply to fields you declare explicitly on the serializer.\n\n**Q: How do you handle PATCH with serializers?**\nA: Pass `partial=True` (`UpdateModelMixin.partial_update` does this), so required fields are not enforced and only the fields provided are validated and updated. Gotcha: in `validate(attrs)`, fields that were not sent are missing from `attrs`, so cross-field checks must fall back to `self.instance` values. Also, `update()` saves the whole model by default, so concurrent PATCHes to different fields can overwrite each other. Use `save(update_fields=...)` or optimistic locking if that matters.\n\n**Q: How do you make a list endpoint that serializes 10,000 rows faster? (Senior)**\nA: First, don't return 10,000 rows: paginate. If you really must (an export), measure where the time goes. It is usually (1) queries, so fix N+1 with `select_related`/`prefetch_related` and `only()`; then (2) serializer overhead, because DRF field-by-field serialization is pure Python and slow per object. Options: use `values()` plus a lightweight serializer or plain dicts for read-only lists, cache the serialized output, stream a CSV with `StreamingHttpResponse` and `iterator()`, or move the export to a Celery task that writes a file and notifies the user. Profile with `cProfile` or py-spy rather than guessing.\n\n### 5.2 Views, viewsets & routers\n\n**Q: `APIView` vs `GenericAPIView` vs `ViewSet` vs `ModelViewSet`?**\nA: `APIView` is the base: you write `get()`/`post()` by hand and get DRF's request, auth, permissions, throttling and exception handling. `GenericAPIView` adds `queryset`, `serializer_class`, `get_queryset()`, `get_object()`, filtering and pagination. Combined with mixins (`ListModelMixin`, `CreateModelMixin`...), it gives the concrete generic views (`ListCreateAPIView`). `ViewSet` maps actions (`list`, `retrieve`) instead of HTTP methods, so one class serves a resource and a router generates the URLs. `ModelViewSet` is `GenericViewSet` plus all five CRUD mixins. Use viewsets for standard resources and `APIView` for actions that do not fit CRUD. See [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers).\n\n**Q: Why override `get_queryset()` instead of setting `queryset`?**\nA: The `queryset` attribute is static, defined once at class level. `get_queryset()` runs per request, so it can scope by `self.request.user` or tenant, choose `select_related` per action, or filter by query parameters. DRF calls `.all()` on the `queryset` attribute to avoid sharing a result cache between requests, but any per-user filtering *must* happen in `get_queryset()`. It is also the primary authorization mechanism for list endpoints, since object permissions do not run there.\n\n**Q: How do you add a custom endpoint to a ViewSet?**\nA: Use `@action`. `@action(detail=True, methods=[\"post\"])` on `def cancel(self, request, pk=None)` creates `POST /orders/{pk}/cancel/`, and `detail=False` creates a collection route. You can set `permission_classes`, `serializer_class` and `url_path` per action. In `get_serializer_class()` and `get_permissions()`, branch on `self.action` to vary behaviour by action.\n\n**Q: What do routers do, and `SimpleRouter` vs `DefaultRouter`?**\nA: A router takes registered viewsets and generates URL patterns for the list and detail routes plus any `@action`s, binding HTTP methods to actions: `GET` to `list`, `POST` to `create`, and so on. `DefaultRouter` also adds an API root view and optional format-suffix patterns. `SimpleRouter` does not. Use `basename=` when the viewset has no `queryset` attribute, because the router derives URL names from it.\n\n**Q: Where should business logic live in a DRF app?**\nA: Not spread across serializers, signals and `perform_create`. A common, defensible structure: serializers validate and shape data, views handle HTTP concerns (auth, status codes), and a **service layer** (plain functions such as `place_order(user, cart, idempotency_key)`) holds the transactional logic, called from the view. Services can be tested without HTTP and reused by Celery tasks and management commands. Model methods are fine for logic about a single object.\n\n### 5.3 Authentication: session, token, JWT\n\n**Q: Which authentication classes does DRF provide, and how does DRF choose one?**\nA: Built in: `SessionAuthentication` (Django sessions, enforces CSRF), `BasicAuthentication` (only over HTTPS, mostly for testing), `TokenAuthentication` (`Authorization: Token <key>`, a single static token per user in the `authtoken` table) and `RemoteUserAuthentication`. JWT comes from `djangorestframework-simplejwt`. DRF tries each class in `DEFAULT_AUTHENTICATION_CLASSES` in order. The first one that returns `(user, auth)` wins, a class that returns `None` passes to the next, and one that raises `AuthenticationFailed` stops the request. If none succeed, `request.user` is `AnonymousUser`. See [Ch 23 · DRF Auth, Permissions & Throttling](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling).\n\n**Q: Session authentication vs JWT: which would you use for a React SPA?**\nA: If the SPA is served from the same site as the API, **session cookies** are usually the better default. They are HttpOnly (JavaScript cannot steal them through XSS), revocable instantly on the server, and Django and DRF support them fully. The cost is CSRF handling (`X-CSRFToken`) and same-site constraints. **JWT** suits mobile apps, third-party clients and service-to-service calls, or cross-domain setups where cookies are awkward. Its costs are that revocation is hard, where you store the token in the browser is a security decision (localStorage is exposed to XSS), and tokens get large. A balanced answer: sessions for the first-party web app, JWT for mobile and machine clients, behind the same permissions. See [REST API · JWT](../rest-api/topic.html?p=20-jwt).\n\n**Q: How does SimpleJWT work, and how do you handle logout or revocation?**\nA: Login returns a short-lived **access** token (5 minutes by default) and a longer **refresh** token (1 day by default), both signed JWTs. The API validates the access token's signature and expiry without a database lookup. The client uses the refresh token to get new access tokens. For revocation, enable `ROTATE_REFRESH_TOKENS` and `BLACKLIST_AFTER_ROTATION` with the `token_blacklist` app, so a used or logged-out refresh token is rejected. Access tokens stay valid until they expire, which is why they are kept short. For \"log out everywhere now\", add a per-user token version claim, checked against the database or cache, which brings back a lookup per request.\n\n**Q: What is wrong with DRF's built-in `TokenAuthentication` for production?**\nA: Each user gets a single static token with no expiry, and the token is stored in the database as-is, so a database leak exposes usable credentials. There is also no per-device tokens or scopes. It is fine for internal tools. For production, consider `django-rest-knox` (hashed, per-device, expiring tokens), SimpleJWT, or OAuth2 via `django-oauth-toolkit` for third-party access.\n\n**Q: Why do I get 403 instead of 401 when not logged in?**\nA: DRF returns 401 only if the first authentication class provides `authenticate_header()`, which supplies the `WWW-Authenticate` header that a 401 response requires. `SessionAuthentication` has no such header, so when it is first in the list, unauthenticated requests get **403**. Put `TokenAuthentication` or JWT first if your clients rely on 401 to trigger a token refresh. A small detail, but it shows you have actually run into it.\n\n### 5.4 Permissions & throttling\n\n**Q: How do `has_permission` and `has_object_permission` differ?**\nA: `has_permission(request, view)` runs for every request in `initial()`. `has_object_permission(request, view, obj)` runs only when the view calls `check_object_permissions()`, which `GenericAPIView.get_object()` does for retrieve, update and destroy. **List endpoints never call it.** So an `IsOwner` object permission does nothing for `GET /orders/`, and that endpoint lists everyone's orders unless `get_queryset()` filters by user. This is the most common DRF authorization bug, and interviewers know it well. Note too that the permission classes are combined with AND, and both methods must return True.\n\n```python\nclass IsOwner(BasePermission):\n    def has_object_permission(self, request, view, obj):\n        return obj.owner_id == request.user.id\n\nclass OrderViewSet(ModelViewSet):\n    permission_classes = [IsAuthenticated, IsOwner]\n    def get_queryset(self):                      # protects list; IsOwner protects detail\n        return Order.objects.filter(owner=self.request.user)\n```\n\n**Q: How do you compose permissions?**\nA: DRF supports `&`, `|` and `~` on permission classes: `permission_classes = [IsAuthenticated & (IsOwner | IsAdminUser)]`. Use `get_permissions()` to vary them by `self.action`, for example allowing reads for anyone and writes only for staff. `DjangoModelPermissions` maps HTTP methods to model permissions (`add_`, `change_`, `delete_`). `DjangoObjectPermissions` needs an object-permission backend such as django-guardian. See [Ch 26 · Permissions & Authorization](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization).\n\n**Q: How does DRF throttling work, and what are its limits?**\nA: Throttle classes (`AnonRateThrottle`, `UserRateThrottle`, `ScopedRateThrottle`) store request timestamps per key in Django's **cache**, and `allow_request()` checks the count in the window (\"100/hour\"). Blocked requests get 429 with `Retry-After`. Limits: with the `locmem` cache, each process counts separately, so 10 workers allow about 10 times the rate. Use Redis. The check-then-write is not atomic, so it is approximate under concurrency. Anonymous identity is the client IP from `X-Forwarded-For` / `REMOTE_ADDR`, governed by `NUM_PROXIES`. Treat it as fair-use control, and put real abuse protection at the gateway or WAF. See [REST API · Rate Limiting](../rest-api/topic.html?p=24-rate-limiting-throttling).\n\n**Q: How do you rate-limit login attempts specifically?**\nA: Put a `ScopedRateThrottle` with `throttle_scope = \"login\"` on the token or login view, keyed by IP. Also keep a lockout counter per account in Redis (or use `django-axes`) to slow credential stuffing that rotates IPs. Return the same error for \"unknown user\" and \"wrong password\" to avoid user enumeration, and alert on spikes. The throttle protects the endpoint, and the per-account counter protects the account.\n\n**Q: How do you implement multi-tenant isolation so one tenant can never see another's data? (Senior)**\nA: Make scoping structural, not something each developer has to remember. Resolve the tenant once (from subdomain, token claim or membership) in middleware or authentication, and attach it to `request.tenant`. Put a `TenantScopedViewSet` base class whose `get_queryset()` always filters `tenant=request.tenant`, and a serializer base class that sets the tenant on create and validates that every FK in the input (`project_id`) belongs to the same tenant. Add database guard rails: composite FKs or `UniqueConstraint`s that include `tenant_id`, or PostgreSQL row-level security with a session variable. Test it: one test per endpoint that creates two tenants and asserts a 404 across tenants. Schema-per-tenant (`django-tenants`) is stronger isolation but costs operational complexity. See [Database Design · Multi-Tenant SaaS](../database-design/topic.html?p=40-case-multi-tenant-saas).\n\n### 5.5 Pagination, filtering, versioning\n\n**Q: Compare PageNumberPagination, LimitOffsetPagination and CursorPagination.**\nA: Page-number pagination (`?page=3`) and limit-offset pagination (`?limit=50&offset=100`) both run `OFFSET`, which gets slower as the offset grows because the database scans and discards the skipped rows. They also run a `COUNT(*)` for the total, and they show duplicates or skip rows when data changes between pages. **CursorPagination** encodes the position in an opaque cursor (`WHERE created < x`). It is stable under inserts and fast at any depth, but it requires a unique, unchanging ordering, gives no total count and no \"jump to page 50\". Use cursor pagination for feeds and large tables, and page numbers for small admin-style lists. See [Ch 24](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning) and [REST API · Pagination](../rest-api/topic.html?p=13-pagination).\n\n**Q: How do you add filtering, search and ordering?**\nA: Set `filter_backends = [DjangoFilterBackend, SearchFilter, OrderingFilter]`, with `filterset_fields` or a `FilterSet` class (from django-filter), `search_fields = [\"title\", \"^sku\"]`, and an **explicit** `ordering_fields` whitelist. Without the whitelist, clients can order by any field, including sensitive ones or unindexed columns that cause slow sorts. Every field you expose for filtering or ordering needs an index plan, so that a client can't trigger a full scan of the table.\n\n**Q: How would you version a DRF API?**\nA: DRF provides `URLPathVersioning` (`/v1/orders/`), `NamespaceVersioning`, `AcceptHeaderVersioning` (`Accept: application/json; version=2`), `HostNameVersioning` and `QueryParameterVersioning`. The view reads `request.version` and can pick a serializer per version in `get_serializer_class()`. URL path versioning is the most visible and the easiest to cache. In practice, version rarely: make additive, backward-compatible changes, and introduce a new version only for breaking changes, with a deprecation window. See [REST API · Versioning](../rest-api/topic.html?p=31-api-versioning).\n\n**Q: How do you generate API docs for DRF?**\nA: Use `drf-spectacular` to generate an OpenAPI 3 schema from your viewsets and serializers, served with Swagger UI or Redoc. Annotate unusual endpoints with `@extend_schema`. DRF's own `coreapi` schema support is deprecated. Run schema generation in CI and fail on warnings so the docs stay accurate, and you can also diff the schema to catch breaking changes.\n\n### 5.6 Custom user model & authentication\n\n**Q: Why should you set a custom user model at the start of every project?**\nA: Because changing `AUTH_USER_MODEL` after the first migration is very painful. Every FK to the user, the admin log, permissions and the content types all reference the original table, and Django has no automatic migration for it. Starting with `class User(AbstractUser): pass` costs nothing and leaves room to add fields or switch to email login later. Always reference it with `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL` in FKs and `get_user_model()` in code, never `from django.contrib.auth.models import User`. See [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user).\n\n**Q: `AbstractUser` vs `AbstractBaseUser`: when do you use each?**\nA: Subclass `AbstractUser` when Django's fields (username, email, first and last name, `is_staff`, groups) are fine and you only want to add more. Subclass `AbstractBaseUser` with `PermissionsMixin` when you need full control, such as email as the login with no username field. Then you define `USERNAME_FIELD`, `REQUIRED_FIELDS` and a custom manager with `create_user()` / `create_superuser()`, and you adapt the admin forms. Profile data that is not about authentication often belongs in a separate `Profile` table (OneToOne) to keep the user row small.\n\n**Q: How does Django store and check passwords?**\nA: `make_password()` hashes with the first hasher in `PASSWORD_HASHERS` (PBKDF2-SHA256 by default, with on the order of a million iterations in recent versions) and stores `algorithm$iterations$salt$hash`. `check_password()` reads the algorithm from the stored string, so old hashes keep working, and if the hasher or its iteration count is outdated, Django **upgrades** the hash on successful login. Argon2 is recommended (`argon2-cffi`, put `Argon2PasswordHasher` first). Passwords are never stored in reversible form, which is why \"password reset\" exists and \"password recovery\" does not.\n\n**Q: How do `authenticate()` and `login()` work, and what does `login()` do for security?**\nA: `authenticate(request, **credentials)` tries each backend in `AUTHENTICATION_BACKENDS` in order, and the first one that returns a user wins. That is how email login, LDAP or SSO plug in. `login(request, user)` stores the user id and backend in the session and **rotates the session key** (`cycle_key`), which prevents session fixation. It also rotates the CSRF token. `logout()` flushes the session. In DRF, token and JWT views call `authenticate()` but not `login()`, because there is no session.\n\n**Q: How would you add social login or SSO?**\nA: Use `django-allauth` for social providers and email verification, or `mozilla-django-oidc` / `social-auth-app-django` for OpenID Connect against Okta, Azure AD or Google Workspace. Behind the scenes it is an extra authentication backend plus the OAuth2 authorization-code flow (with PKCE for public clients). Key points: link accounts by verified email only, map IdP groups to Django groups at login, and decide what happens when a user is deprovisioned at the IdP. See [REST API · OAuth2 & OIDC](../rest-api/topic.html?p=19-oauth2-oidc).\n\n### 5.7 Security\n\n**Q: How does Django prevent CSRF?**\nA: `CsrfViewMiddleware` enforces a token on unsafe methods (POST, PUT, PATCH, DELETE). The server sets a `csrftoken` cookie holding a secret, or stores it in the session if `CSRF_USE_SESSIONS`. Forms include a **masked** version of that secret via `{% csrf_token %}`, or JavaScript sends it in the `X-CSRFToken` header. The middleware unmasks the token and compares it with the cookie. A cross-site attacker can make the browser *send* the cookie but cannot *read* it to forge the matching token. On HTTPS, it also checks that `Origin` (or `Referer`) matches the host or `CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS`, which needs a scheme since 4.0. The masking changes per request, which defeats BREACH-style compression attacks. `SameSite=Lax` cookies add defence in depth. See [Ch 28 · Security](topic.html?p=28-security).\n\n**Q: When is `@csrf_exempt` acceptable?**\nA: Only for endpoints that are not authenticated by an ambient browser credential: webhooks from Stripe or GitHub, which you verify with an HMAC signature instead, or APIs authenticated purely by `Authorization` headers, which browsers don't send automatically. Making a cookie-session endpoint exempt reopens CSRF. DRF already exempts its views and re-applies CSRF only inside `SessionAuthentication`, so you rarely need to add `@csrf_exempt` yourself.\n\n**Q: How does the ORM protect against SQL injection, and where can it still happen?**\nA: The ORM always sends values as **parameters** separately from the SQL text, and the database driver handles them, so input cannot change the query structure. Injection happens when you build SQL with string formatting: `raw(f\"... {name}\")`, `cursor.execute(\"...\" % x)`, `extra()`, `RawSQL()` with interpolated input, or by interpolating *identifiers* such as column names for ordering, which parameters cannot cover. Fix: always pass `params=[...]`, and whitelist identifiers against a fixed set.\n\n```python\n# vulnerable\nBook.objects.raw(f\"SELECT * FROM shop_book WHERE title = '{q}'\")\n# safe\nBook.objects.raw(\"SELECT * FROM shop_book WHERE title = %s\", [q])\n```\n\n**Q: What is clickjacking, and how does Django prevent it?**\nA: Clickjacking is when an attacker loads your page in an invisible iframe and tricks the user into clicking buttons on it. `XFrameOptionsMiddleware` sends `X-Frame-Options: DENY` by default (since 3.0), so browsers refuse to frame the page. Use `@xframe_options_sameorigin` or `@xframe_options_exempt` per view for legitimate embedding. The modern equivalent is CSP `frame-ancestors`. Django 5.2 needs `django-csp` for CSP, and newer Django versions add built-in CSP support.\n\n**Q: What does `manage.py check --deploy` look for?**\nA: It flags insecure production settings: `DEBUG=True`; a weak or default `SECRET_KEY`; missing `SECURE_HSTS_SECONDS`; `SECURE_SSL_REDIRECT` not set; `SESSION_COOKIE_SECURE` and `CSRF_COOKIE_SECURE` not set; missing `SecurityMiddleware`; `X_FRAME_OPTIONS` not DENY; and others. Run it in CI against the production settings module. Things it can't check, which you still own: `ALLOWED_HOSTS` correctness, `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` behind a TLS-terminating proxy, admin exposure and dependency CVEs.\n\n**Q: What is `SECRET_KEY` used for, and what happens if it leaks?**\nA: It signs data: sessions when you use the signed-cookie backend, password-reset tokens, `django.core.signing` values, messages and CSRF-related values. It is **not** used to hash passwords. If it leaks, attackers can forge signed values, such as password-reset links or signed cookie sessions, which can lead to account takeover. Rotate it with `SECRET_KEY_FALLBACKS` (4.1+): put the new key in `SECRET_KEY` and the old one in fallbacks, so existing sessions keep working during the transition, then remove the old key.\n\n**Q: Why does `ALLOWED_HOSTS` exist?**\nA: To stop **Host header** attacks. Django builds absolute URLs, including password-reset links in emails, from the request's Host header. An attacker who sends `Host: evil.com` could get a reset email that points to their domain. With `DEBUG=False`, Django rejects hosts not in `ALLOWED_HOSTS` with a 400. Never set `[\"*\"]` in production. Behind a load balancer, also make sure health checks send a valid Host.\n\n**Q: What is an IDOR, and how does it show up in Django APIs?**\nA: An Insecure Direct Object Reference is accessing another user's object by changing an id: `GET /api/invoices/124/`. In DRF it shows up when `get_queryset()` returns `Invoice.objects.all()` and nothing checks ownership, or when a serializer accepts a FK id (`project_id`) without checking that the project belongs to the user. Fix: scope `get_queryset()` by user or tenant (out-of-scope ids then become a 404), validate FK ownership in the serializer (`queryset=` on `PrimaryKeyRelatedField` limited per request), and write cross-user tests. UUIDs make ids hard to guess, but they are not authorization. See [REST API · OWASP API Security](../rest-api/topic.html?p=23-owasp-api-security).\n\n### 5.8 Caching\n\n**Q: What caching options does Django offer?**\nA: Per-site caching (`UpdateCacheMiddleware` first plus `FetchFromCacheMiddleware` last), per-view caching (`@cache_page(60 * 5)`), template fragment caching (`{% cache 600 sidebar request.user.id %}`) and the low-level API (`cache.get`, `set`, `get_or_set`, `delete`, `incr`, `get_many`). Backends: Redis (built in since 4.0), Memcached (`PyMemcacheCache`), database, file, `locmem` and dummy. `locmem` is per-process, so it is not shared between Gunicorn workers. The default `TIMEOUT` is 300 seconds. In APIs, low-level caching of computed data is usually more useful than whole-page caching. See [Ch 29 · Caching](topic.html?p=29-caching).\n\n**Q: How do you invalidate a cached product detail when the product changes?**\nA: Use cache-aside with explicit deletion on write. Read with `cache.get_or_set(f\"product:{pk}:v1\", compute, timeout)`. In the write path, call `transaction.on_commit(lambda: cache.delete(key))`, after commit so that a concurrent reader can't re-cache the old value before the commit. Keep a TTL as a safety net. For data that many keys depend on, use a **version key** (`cache.incr(\"catalog_version\")`, included in every key) so one increment invalidates everything. Signals can trigger invalidation, but they miss `update()`, so a service-layer write function is more reliable. See [Redis Caching · Cache Invalidation](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=12-cache-invalidation).\n\n**Q: What is a cache stampede, and how do you prevent it in Django?**\nA: When a hot key expires, hundreds of concurrent requests miss at the same moment and all recompute, hammering the database. Mitigations: add random jitter to TTLs; take a lock so only one request recomputes (`cache.add(lock_key, 1, 30)` returns True for one caller only) while others serve the stale value; refresh ahead of expiry in a Celery task; or keep \"stale-while-revalidate\" metadata in the cached value. See [Redis Caching · Cache Stampede](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=15-cache-stampede).\n\n**Q: Why is `cache_page` dangerous on authenticated endpoints?**\nA: The cache key is built from the URL plus the headers listed in `Vary`. If the response varies by user but the cache key doesn't, user A's page is served to user B. Django adds `Vary: Cookie` when the session is accessed, but token-authenticated APIs send `Authorization`, which is not in `Vary` unless you add `@vary_on_headers(\"Authorization\")`. And a CDN in front may ignore `Vary`. Cache per-user data with user-specific low-level keys, or mark responses `Cache-Control: private`.\n\n**Q: How do ETags and conditional requests work in Django?**\nA: `ConditionalGetMiddleware`, or the `@condition(etag_func=..., last_modified_func=...)` decorator, lets Django answer `If-None-Match` / `If-Modified-Since` with `304 Not Modified` and no body. With `@condition`, the cheap ETag function runs first, and the expensive view doesn't run at all on a match. That saves bandwidth and, with `@condition`, server work. It also enables optimistic concurrency: `If-Match` on PUT returns 412 if the resource has changed. See [REST API · HTTP Caching & ETags](../rest-api/topic.html?p=25-http-caching-etags).\n\n### 5.9 Async & ASGI\n\n**Q: When do async views actually help?**\nA: When the view spends its time **waiting on I/O** that has an async client: calling several external HTTP APIs at once with `httpx.AsyncClient` and `asyncio.gather`, long-polling, streaming, or many concurrent slow connections. They do not speed up CPU-bound work. They give little benefit for plain database CRUD, because the async ORM still runs queries in a thread pool. Under WSGI they run in a one-off event loop per request, so they gain nothing. You need an ASGI server (Uvicorn, Daphne, Hypercorn) to benefit. See [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi).\n\n**Q: What is `SynchronousOnlyOperation`, and how do you fix it?**\nA: Django raises it when you call sync-only code, typically the synchronous ORM (`Book.objects.get()`, or a lazy relation access), from inside a running event loop. Blocking the loop would stall every other coroutine. Fixes: use the async ORM methods (`await Book.objects.aget()`, `async for b in qs`, `acount`, `acreate`), or wrap sync code with `await sync_to_async(fn)()`. Remember that lazy attribute access (`book.author`) is also a sync query, so use `select_related` up front.\n\n**Q: How does the async ORM work under the hood, and what are its limits?**\nA: The async methods (4.1+) are wrappers that run the sync query code through `sync_to_async`, so the database call still happens in a thread, with a sync driver. The main limit is that **transactions are not supported in async code**: `atomic()` does not work across awaits, so put the transactional unit in a sync function and call it with `sync_to_async`. With `thread_sensitive=True`, the default, those calls run in a single shared thread, which keeps connection handling safe but serialises them.\n\n**Q: Explain `sync_to_async` and `async_to_sync`.**\nA: Both come from `asgiref`. `sync_to_async(fn)` runs blocking code in a thread so the event loop isn't blocked. With `thread_sensitive=True`, the default, it uses one shared thread, which matters for code that uses thread-locals or database connections. `async_to_sync(coro)` runs a coroutine from sync code by using or creating an event loop, and is used for things like `channel_layer.group_send` from a sync view or a Celery task. Each crossing costs a thread hop, so mixing sync and async within one request adds latency. Keep each request path mostly one or the other.\n\n**Q: How does Django handle sync middleware in an async stack?**\nA: Each middleware declares `sync_capable` / `async_capable`. When a sync-only middleware sits in an ASGI stack, Django adapts it with `sync_to_async`, which adds a thread switch for each sync middleware the request passes through. To get the real benefit of ASGI, every middleware in the chain should be async-capable. Check third-party middleware before you assume that ASGI makes your app async.\n\n### 5.10 Celery & background tasks\n\n**Q: Why use Celery, and what are its components?**\nA: To move slow or unreliable work out of the request: emails, reports, third-party calls, image processing. Components: the **broker** (Redis or RabbitMQ) queues task messages; **workers** consume and run them; an optional **result backend** stores return values; **beat** schedules periodic tasks (django-celery-beat keeps schedules in the database). A task call such as `send_receipt.delay(order_id)` serialises the name and arguments to the broker and returns immediately. See [Ch 31 · Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks).\n\n**Q: Why should you pass IDs to tasks, not model instances?**\nA: Serialised instances go stale: the task runs seconds or minutes later, and the row may have changed or been deleted. JSON can't serialise model instances anyway, and pickle is a security risk. Pass the primary key, re-fetch inside the task, and handle `DoesNotExist`. Combine this with dispatching via `transaction.on_commit` so the row is guaranteed to be committed when the worker looks for it.\n\n**Q: How do you make Celery tasks reliable: retries, acks and idempotency?**\nA: Use `@shared_task(bind=True, autoretry_for=(RequestException,), retry_backoff=True, retry_jitter=True, max_retries=5)` for transient failures. `acks_late=True` acknowledges the message only after the task finishes, so a worker crash redelivers it instead of losing it. That makes delivery **at-least-once**, so the task must be **idempotent**: check a status before acting, use unique constraints or an idempotency record, and pass a stable key to external APIs (Stripe idempotency keys). Also set `task_reject_on_worker_lost` deliberately, and use time limits (`soft_time_limit`) so a hung task does not hold a worker forever.\n\n```python\n@shared_task(bind=True, acks_late=True, autoretry_for=(PaymentGatewayTimeout,),\n             retry_backoff=True, max_retries=5)\ndef capture_payment(self, order_id):\n    order = Order.objects.get(pk=order_id)\n    if order.payment_status == Order.PaymentStatus.CAPTURED:\n        return \"already captured\"                      # idempotent on redelivery\n    gateway.capture(order.payment_ref, idempotency_key=f\"capture-{order_id}\")\n    Order.objects.filter(pk=order_id).update(payment_status=Order.PaymentStatus.CAPTURED)\n```\n\n**Q: What are chain, group and chord?**\nA: Celery's **canvas** primitives. `chain(a.s(), b.s(), c.s())` runs tasks in sequence, passing each result to the next. `group(t.s(i) for i in ids)` runs tasks in parallel. `chord(group, callback)` runs the group, then the callback with all the results, which needs a result backend and can be fragile at scale. For very large fan-outs, prefer chunking (`t.chunks(items, 100)`) and a counter or status table over a giant chord.\n\n**Q: How would you send a newsletter to one million users?**\nA: Don't create one task per user from the request, and don't create one giant task either. A beat-triggered or admin-triggered \"dispatcher\" task iterates over user ids in key-range batches (`iterator()` or pk ranges) and enqueues batch tasks of, say, 500 users. Each batch task sends through the email provider's bulk API, records sends in a `NewsletterDelivery` table with a unique `(campaign, user)` constraint (so a redelivered batch skips users already sent), and respects provider rate limits (`rate_limit` or token buckets). Use a dedicated queue and workers so the newsletter can't starve transactional email. Monitor progress from the delivery table.\n\n**Q: What does `worker_prefetch_multiplier` do, and when do you change it?**\nA: Each worker process reserves `prefetch_multiplier × concurrency` messages ahead of time (the default multiplier is 4). That is good for many short tasks and bad for long ones: one worker can sit on 4 long tasks while other workers are idle, and with `acks_late` the reserved tasks are redelivered if the worker dies. Set it to 1 for long-running tasks. Routing long and short tasks to separate queues helps more.\n\n### 5.11 Channels & real-time\n\n**Q: How does Django Channels work?**\nA: Channels runs Django under ASGI and adds protocol routing: `ProtocolTypeRouter({\"http\": django_asgi_app, \"websocket\": AuthMiddlewareStack(URLRouter(ws_patterns))})`. Each WebSocket connection is a **consumer** instance (`AsyncWebsocketConsumer`) with `connect`, `receive` and `disconnect` handlers. A **channel layer** (`channels_redis.core.RedisChannelLayer`) lets processes talk to each other: consumers join **groups** with `group_add`, and any code can `group_send(group, {\"type\": \"chat.message\", ...})`. The `type` is mapped to a handler method (`chat_message`) on every consumer in the group. See [Ch 32 · Channels](topic.html?p=32-channels-websockets).\n\n**Q: How do you push a notification to a user's WebSocket from a Django view or a Celery task?**\nA: On connect, the consumer joins a per-user group (`f\"user_{user.id}\"`). The view or task calls `async_to_sync(channel_layer.group_send)(\"user_42\", {\"type\": \"notify\", \"payload\": data})`, ideally inside `transaction.on_commit`. Whichever server holds that user's sockets receives the message through Redis and calls `notify()`, which sends to the client. Channel layers are fire-and-forget with no persistence, so store the notification in the database as well and let clients fetch missed ones on reconnect.\n\n**Q: How do you access the ORM from an async consumer?**\nA: Wrap ORM calls in `database_sync_to_async` (from `channels.db`). It runs them in a thread and also closes stale connections, which plain `sync_to_async` does not. Alternatively, use the async ORM methods for simple queries. Never call the sync ORM directly in `async def` handlers, as that raises `SynchronousOnlyOperation`. Keep the work per message small, because long handlers block that consumer's message processing.\n\n**Q: How do you authenticate WebSocket connections?**\nA: `AuthMiddlewareStack` reads the Django session cookie, which works for same-site browser clients, and sets `scope[\"user\"]`. Check `self.scope[\"user\"].is_authenticated` in `connect()` and `close()` if not. Validate the `Origin` header too (`AllowedHostsOriginValidator`), because WebSockets are not protected by CSRF. For token clients, write a small middleware that validates a short-lived token passed in the query string or the first message. Avoid long-lived JWTs in URLs, because they end up in logs.\n\n### 5.12 Testing\n\n**Q: `TestCase` vs `TransactionTestCase` vs `SimpleTestCase`?**\nA: `SimpleTestCase` blocks database access, for pure logic and fast tests. `TestCase` wraps each test in a transaction that is rolled back at the end (fast), and supports `setUpTestData` for data created once per class. Because nothing is committed, `on_commit` callbacks don't run unless you use `captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True)`, and you can't test real concurrency or commit behaviour. `TransactionTestCase` truncates tables after each test. It is slower, but it is needed for testing `select_for_update` across threads, real commits, or code that manages its own transactions. See [Ch 33 · Testing](topic.html?p=33-testing).\n\n**Q: How do you test a DRF endpoint?**\nA: Use `APITestCase` / `APIClient`. `self.client.force_authenticate(user)` skips the auth mechanics when they are not under test. Post JSON with `format=\"json\"`, assert `status_code` and `response.json()`, and cover the cases that matter: unauthenticated (401/403), another user's object (404), invalid input (400 with field errors), success, and a query-count assertion. With pytest-django, use fixtures and `@pytest.mark.django_db`, plus factory_boy factories instead of fixture files.\n\n```python\ndef test_cannot_read_other_users_order(api_client, user_factory, order_factory):\n    me, other = user_factory(), user_factory()\n    order = order_factory(owner=other)\n    api_client.force_authenticate(me)\n    assert api_client.get(f\"/api/orders/{order.id}/\").status_code == 404\n```\n\n**Q: How do you mock external services in Django tests?**\nA: Isolate them behind a thin client module, and patch that module (`unittest.mock.patch(\"payments.client.capture\")`) rather than `requests` deep inside your code. For HTTP-level mocking, use `responses` or `respx` (httpx). For Celery, set `CELERY_TASK_ALWAYS_EAGER=True` in unit tests to run tasks inline (it hides serialisation and broker behaviour, so keep a few integration tests), or patch `.delay` and assert it was called with the right ids. Use `freezegun` or `time-machine` for time.\n\n**Q: How do you keep a large Django test suite fast?**\nA: Use `setUpTestData` instead of `setUp` for shared data. Use factories that build only what each test needs. Run in parallel (`manage.py test --parallel` or `pytest -n auto`). Use `--keepdb` locally. Switch to the MD5 password hasher in test settings, because the default hasher is deliberately slow and dominates user-creation time. Avoid `TransactionTestCase` unless you need it, and mock network calls. Track the slowest tests (`--durations=20`) in CI.\n\n### 5.13 DB scaling & connection pooling\n\n**Q: How does Django manage database connections?**\nA: Django uses one connection per thread, opened lazily on first query. `CONN_MAX_AGE` controls reuse: `0` (the default) closes the connection at the end of each request, a number keeps it for that many seconds, and `None` keeps it open indefinitely. `CONN_HEALTH_CHECKS = True` (4.1+) checks a reused connection before the request uses it. Persistent connections save the connect cost (TLS plus auth, which can take milliseconds) but hold a server connection per worker even while idle. See [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases).\n\n**Q: What is new about connection pooling in Django 5.1+?**\nA: Django 5.1 added native pooling for PostgreSQL with psycopg 3: `\"OPTIONS\": {\"pool\": True}`, or a dict of `psycopg_pool` options such as `min_size`, `max_size` and `timeout`. Each process keeps a pool, and connections are borrowed per request. It is a process-local pool, so total connections are still pools × processes. For many pods, or for serverless, an external pooler (PgBouncer in transaction mode) is still how you cap total server connections. Don't combine the native pool with a non-zero `CONN_MAX_AGE`.\n\n**Q: What breaks when you put PgBouncer in transaction mode in front of Django?**\nA: In transaction mode, a server connection is assigned to a client only for the length of a transaction, so anything that depends on session state across transactions breaks: server-side cursors used by `iterator()` (set `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS = True`), session-level `SET` statements, advisory locks held across transactions, `LISTEN/NOTIFY`, and, depending on the PgBouncer version, prepared statements. Recent PgBouncer versions support protocol-level prepared statements. Keep `CONN_MAX_AGE` reasonable, and let PgBouncer do the pooling.\n\n**Q: How do you route reads to a replica, and what goes wrong?**\nA: Configure a `replica` entry in `DATABASES` and a router class implementing `db_for_read` (return `\"replica\"`), `db_for_write` (`\"default\"`), `allow_relation` and `allow_migrate`, listed in `DATABASE_ROUTERS`. Or route explicitly with `.using(\"replica\")`. The problem is **replication lag**: a user creates an object, is redirected, and the read hits a replica that doesn't have it yet, so they see a 404. Fixes: pin a user's reads to the primary for a few seconds after they write (a cookie or a request flag), always read from the primary inside transactions, and send only lag-tolerant queries (reports, public lists) to replicas. See [Database Design · Replication](../database-design/topic.html?p=09-replication).\n\n**Q: You hit \"FATAL: too many connections\" after scaling out. Walk through the numbers. (Senior)**\nA: Count every client. Web: pods × Gunicorn workers × threads, with each thread holding one connection when `CONN_MAX_AGE` is non-zero. For example, 10 pods × 9 workers × 1 = 90. Celery: pods × concurrency, say 4 × 8 = 32. Plus beat, cron jobs, migrations, admin shells and monitoring. Against PostgreSQL's `max_connections` of 100 (with a few reserved for superusers), you are out. Each PostgreSQL connection is a process using several MB, so raising `max_connections` far is not the answer. Fixes, in order: put PgBouncer in transaction mode in front, so thousands of client connections share tens of server connections; size the native per-process pool small; reduce idle connections (`CONN_MAX_AGE` lower, or 0 behind PgBouncer); and give Celery its own pool limit. Then alert on the active-connection count.\n\n### 5.14 Deployment & operations\n\n**Q: How do you run Django in production?**\nA: Use a WSGI server (Gunicorn) or an ASGI server (Uvicorn workers via `uvicorn.workers.UvicornWorker` or the `uvicorn-worker` package) behind a reverse proxy or load balancer. Never `runserver`. Set `DEBUG=False`, a correct `ALLOWED_HOSTS`, secrets from the environment, and `SECURE_PROXY_SSL_HEADER` if TLS terminates at the proxy. Serve static files with WhiteNoise or a CDN after `collectstatic` at build time. Configure the `STORAGES` setting (4.2+) for media on S3. Add structured logging, Sentry and health-check endpoints. Run migrations as a separate release step. See [Ch 35 · Deployment](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production).\n\n**Q: How many Gunicorn workers should you run?**\nA: The usual starting point is `2 × cores + 1` sync workers, then tune by measuring. Sync workers handle one request at a time, so concurrency equals the worker count. If requests mostly wait on I/O, add threads (`--threads 4`, gthread) or move to ASGI. Each worker is a full Python process with its own memory and database connection, so memory and the database connection budget usually set the ceiling before CPU does. Set `--timeout` (30 s by default) above your slowest legitimate request, and `--max-requests` with jitter to recycle workers that leak memory.\n\n**Q: How do you deploy with zero downtime when there are migrations?**\nA: Old and new code run at the same time during a rolling deploy, so every migration must be **backward compatible** with the code currently running. Run `migrate` once as a release job (not in every container's start command, where parallel runs race), then roll out the new pods. Additive changes go first (nullable columns, new tables, concurrent indexes). Destructive changes (drop, rename, NOT NULL) go in a later release, after no running code uses the old shape. Set a `lock_timeout` so a migration waiting on a lock fails fast instead of blocking traffic. See [Ch 11 · Migrations](topic.html?p=11-migrations).\n\n**Q: How do you serve static and media files correctly?**\nA: Static files (your CSS and JS) are collected at build time with `collectstatic` into `STATIC_ROOT`, and served by WhiteNoise (with compression and hashed file names via `CompressedManifestStaticFilesStorage`) or uploaded to a CDN. Media files (user uploads) go to object storage (`django-storages` with S3) through `STORAGES[\"default\"]`, served from the bucket or a CDN, with private files behind signed URLs. Never serve uploads from the app server's disk in a multi-pod deployment, because pods don't share disks. See [Ch 20 · Static & Media Files](topic.html?p=20-static-media-files).\n\n**Q: What do you monitor for a Django service?**\nA: Use the RED metrics per endpoint: rate, errors and duration, especially p95 and p99. Track database query count and time per request (APM), connection-pool usage, Celery queue depth and task latency, worker restarts and memory, cache hit rate, and 4xx/5xx breakdowns. Send exceptions to Sentry with release tags, so a regression maps to a deploy. Use structured JSON logs with a request id that is propagated to Celery tasks. Define SLOs and alert on them, not on CPU. See [REST API · Observability](../rest-api/topic.html?p=38-observability-logging-tracing).\n\n### 5.15 Scenario & debugging questions\n\n**Q: The API got slow after a release. Walk me through how you'd investigate. (Senior)**\nA: **Scope:** which endpoints, all users or some, how much slower (p50 vs p99), and does it line up with the deploy time? **Mitigate:** if it is serious and clearly tied to the release, roll back first and investigate after. **Measure:** APM traces for the slow endpoint show whether the time is spent in the database, an external call or Python. Compare query counts before and after the release, because an N+1 shows up as 40 queries becoming 400. `pg_stat_statements` shows new expensive statements. **Hypotheses**, from most to least likely: a serializer or template change that touches a relation without `select_related`/`prefetch_related`; a new filter or ordering on an unindexed column (seq scan, check with `EXPLAIN ANALYZE`); a migration that left a table locked or dropped an index; a cache whose key changed so the hit rate collapsed; a new synchronous external call in the request path. **Fix** the specific cause, then **prevent** recurrence: `assertNumQueries` on the endpoint, a query budget in CI, and an alert on p95.\n\n**Q: Users report seeing each other's data. What could cause that? (Senior)**\nA: Treat it as a security incident: mitigate first (disable the suspected cache or feature, roll back), then investigate. Likely causes: (1) **caching** without the user in the key, such as `cache_page` on an authenticated endpoint, a CDN caching responses that vary by `Authorization`, or a low-level cache key missing the user id; (2) **shared mutable state** in a process, such as a module-level dict or a mutable class attribute on a view or serializer that is changed per request, or a thread-local not cleared between requests under thread workers; (3) **missing queryset scoping**, where list endpoints return `objects.all()` because object permissions never run on lists; (4) a session or auth bug, such as a proxy caching `Set-Cookie`, or a custom auth backend that returns the wrong user. Confirm the cause from logs, which user ids were served which objects, then fix it. Add a cross-user test for every endpoint, `Cache-Control: private` on user-specific responses, and a review rule against module-level mutable state. Assess the exposure for disclosure obligations.\n\n**Q: Clients retrying a POST created duplicate orders. How do you fix it? (Senior)**\nA: The network made the POST at-least-once: the client timed out after the server had committed, then retried. Fix it with an **idempotency key**. The client generates a UUID per logical operation and sends `Idempotency-Key`. The server stores `(user, key)` with a unique constraint, the request fingerprint, and the response, in the same transaction as the order. On a repeat with the same key and the same body, return the stored response. With a different body, return 422. If the first request is still in progress, return 409. Add a natural guard too, such as a unique `(cart_id)` on orders, so that even a client without the key can't double-submit the same cart.\n\n```python\n@transaction.atomic\ndef place_order(user, cart, key, fingerprint):\n    rec, created = IdempotencyRecord.objects.select_for_update().get_or_create(\n        user=user, key=key, defaults={\"fingerprint\": fingerprint})\n    if not created:\n        if rec.fingerprint != fingerprint:\n            raise Conflict(\"key reused with a different body\")\n        return rec.response                      # replay the original result\n    order = Order.objects.create_from_cart(user, cart)\n    rec.response = OrderSerializer(order).data\n    rec.save(update_fields=[\"response\"])\n    transaction.on_commit(lambda: send_confirmation.delay(order.id))\n    return rec.response\n```\n\nSee [REST API · Idempotency Keys](../rest-api/topic.html?p=27-idempotency-keys-retries).\n\n**Q: A Celery task sometimes runs twice. Why? (Senior)**\nA: Celery is at-least-once, so expect it. Common causes: with `acks_late`, a worker crash or OOM kill redelivers the task; with the Redis broker, a task not acknowledged within `visibility_timeout` (1 hour by default) is redelivered to another worker, which hits long tasks and ETA/countdown tasks scheduled further out than that; autoretry after a partial side effect; the dispatching code ran twice (a retried request, or a signal connected twice without `dispatch_uid`); beat running in two places at once. Fix: make the task idempotent (state checks, unique constraints, external idempotency keys), raise the visibility timeout above your longest ETA, run a single beat instance, and dispatch through `on_commit` from an idempotent service.\n\n**Q: Gunicorn workers are getting killed with \"WORKER TIMEOUT\" under load. What do you check?**\nA: A sync worker was busy longer than `--timeout` (30 s by default), so the master killed it. Find out why the requests are slow: a slow external API called without a client timeout (always set `timeout=` on `requests`), a lock wait in the database, a slow query, or a large export done in the request. Under load, if all workers are blocked on I/O, requests queue up and time out together. Fixes: set client timeouts, move slow work to Celery, add threads or move I/O-heavy endpoints to ASGI, and use `statement_timeout` on the database. Raising `--timeout` only hides the problem.\n\n**Q: Memory in your web workers keeps growing until they are OOM-killed. How do you approach it?**\nA: Common Django causes: `DEBUG=True` in production (`connection.queries` keeps every query); evaluating huge QuerySets into memory (`list(qs)` on millions of rows, or iterating without `iterator()`); module-level caches that grow without limit (unbounded `lru_cache`, global dicts); and large response bodies built in memory. Use `--max-requests` with jitter as an immediate mitigation, then profile with `tracemalloc` or `memray` on a staging worker under the same traffic. Look at which endpoint or task precedes the growth, using per-request memory logging.\n\n**Q: The same view is fast locally and slow in production. What are the usual suspects?**\nA: Data volume (10 rows locally, 10 million in production, so an N+1 or a missing index only bites there), network latency to the database (each query costs 1–2 ms in production instead of 0.05 ms locally, so 300 queries cost a lot more), cold caches, a different database plan due to stale statistics (`ANALYZE`), connection setup with `CONN_MAX_AGE=0` over TLS, and production-only middleware or logging. Reproduce with production-like data volume and measure with APM before changing anything.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\nThe trade-offs that come up again and again in this half of the interview:\n\n| Decision | Option A | Option B | Rule of thumb |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Web auth | Session cookie (HttpOnly, revocable, needs CSRF) | JWT (stateless, hard to revoke, storage risk) | Sessions for a first-party web app; JWT for mobile and machine clients |\n| Pagination | Page number / offset (totals, jump to page) | Cursor (stable, fast at depth, no total) | Cursor for feeds and big tables |\n| Authorization on lists | `get_queryset()` scoping | Object permissions | Both: scoping for lists, object permissions for detail |\n| Background work | Celery (mature, retries, canvas) | Inline / thread (simple, no reliability) | Anything slow, retryable or bursty goes to Celery |\n| Concurrency model | WSGI sync workers (simple, predictable) | ASGI async (high I/O concurrency) | ASGI only if the hot paths are I/O-bound and async end to end |\n| Caching | Per-view `cache_page` | Low-level keys with explicit invalidation | Low-level for APIs and per-user data |\n| Pooling | Native pool (5.1+, per process) | PgBouncer (global cap) | PgBouncer once pods × workers approaches `max_connections` |\n| Reads | Primary only (consistent) | Replica (scales reads, lags) | Replicas for lag-tolerant reads; pin to primary after writes |\n\n### Expectations by level\n\n| Topic | Junior | Mid | Senior |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| DRF | ModelSerializer + ModelViewSet + router | Validation hooks, `get_queryset`, `@action`, N+1 in serializers | Service layer, nested writes in transactions, serialization performance |\n| Auth | `IsAuthenticated`, token login | Session vs JWT, refresh and blacklist, 401 vs 403 | Auth architecture for web plus mobile, revocation, SSO |\n| Security | \"Django has CSRF and escapes HTML\" | CSRF mechanism, `check --deploy`, SQLi in `raw()` | IDOR and tenant isolation, secret rotation, threat models |\n| Async & Celery | \"Use Celery for emails\" | Retries, `on_commit`, idempotent tasks | Visibility timeouts, queue design, fan-out at scale |\n| Scale | \"Add caching\" | Cache invalidation, replicas | Connection budgets, pooling, read-your-writes, safe deploys |\n\n### When to use this chapter\n\n- Use it after [Ch 37](topic.html?p=37-interview-core-orm), for any API-backend role.\n- Use the scenarios in §5.15 and §9 for incident-style rounds and behavioural \"tell me about a time\" questions.\n\n### When NOT to use this chapter\n\n- Don't learn DRF from it. Read [Ch 21](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers) to [Ch 24](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning) first.\n- For full system designs, use [Ch 40 · Design Rounds](topic.html?p=40-design-rounds). This chapter answers the questions *within* a design.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\nAnswering mistakes that cost candidates offers:\n\n- **\"JWT is more secure than sessions.\"** Neither is inherently more secure. They have different threat models. Say what you are defending against (XSS stealing tokens, CSRF, revocation, scaling).\n- **Relying on object permissions to protect lists.** Saying `IsOwner` alone protects `GET /orders/` is a known red flag. Always mention `get_queryset()` scoping.\n- **\"Celery guarantees the task runs once.\"** It doesn't. Say \"at-least-once, so I make it idempotent\" before you're asked.\n- **\"Async makes Django faster.\"** Only for I/O-bound work with async clients, under ASGI. Database CRUD gains little, and CPU-bound work gains nothing.\n- **Suggesting `@csrf_exempt` to \"fix\" a 403.** That disables a security control instead of sending `X-CSRFToken`, and interviewers notice.\n- **Scaling with no numbers.** \"Add more pods\" without the connection arithmetic leads straight to the \"too many connections\" follow-up.\n- **Jumping to a fix in scenario questions.** Mitigate, measure, hypothesise, verify. A guessed fix with no evidence scores lower than a slower answer that follows the method.\n- **Caching per-user data in shared keys.** If you suggest `cache_page` on an authenticated endpoint, expect \"what if two users hit it?\"\n\nBefore and after for a security answer:\n\n```text\nWeak:   \"Django is secure by default, it handles CSRF and SQL injection.\"\nStrong: \"CsrfViewMiddleware checks a masked token against the csrftoken cookie on unsafe methods and\n         Origin against CSRF_TRUSTED_ORIGINS on HTTPS. DRF only enforces it through SessionAuthentication,\n         so our JWT endpoints don't need it. The ORM parameterizes; the risk is raw()/cursor with f-strings,\n         and we lint for that.\"\n```\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n### Tricky and gotcha questions\n\n**Q: Throttling is set to 100/hour, but a client made 600 requests. How?**\nA: The throttle state lives in the cache. With `locmem`, each Gunicorn worker has its own counter, so 6 workers allow about 600 requests. It can also be IP misidentification: behind a proxy without `NUM_PROXIES`, every client appears as the proxy's IP (over-throttling), or a client spoofs `X-Forwarded-For` (bypass). Use Redis as the throttle cache, configure `NUM_PROXIES` correctly, and enforce hard limits at the gateway.\n\n**Q: Your `IsOwner` permission works on retrieve, but a user updated someone else's object via a custom `@action`. Why?**\nA: The action looked up the object itself (`Order.objects.get(pk=pk)`) instead of calling `self.get_object()`, so `check_object_permissions()` never ran. Always use `get_object()` in detail actions, or call `self.check_object_permissions(request, obj)` explicitly, and scope `get_queryset()` so out-of-scope ids return 404 anyway.\n\n**Q: A ModelSerializer accepted data that violated `Model.clean()`. Why?**\nA: DRF serializers run field validators and unique validators, but not `Model.clean()` or `full_clean()`. DRF deliberately keeps validation in the serializer. Move the rule into `validate()` (or a shared validator function used by both), or enforce it with a database `CheckConstraint`, so every write path is covered.\n\n**Q: Tests pass, but the email-sending `on_commit` hook never fires in tests. Why?**\nA: `TestCase` wraps each test in a transaction that is rolled back, so the outermost commit never happens and `on_commit` callbacks are discarded. Use `with self.captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True):` (3.2+), or `TransactionTestCase` when you need real commit semantics.\n\n**Q: After moving to ASGI, the app got slower. Why might that be?**\nA: Most of the stack was still sync: sync middleware, sync views and the sync ORM. Under ASGI each sync piece is wrapped with `sync_to_async`, which adds thread switches, and `thread_sensitive=True` puts those calls through one thread per process. You paid the cost of adaptation without any async I/O to benefit from. Either go async end to end on the I/O-heavy paths, or stay on WSGI with threads for those endpoints.\n\n**Q: JWT logout \"doesn't work\": the user can still call the API after logging out. Why?**\nA: An access token is self-contained and valid until it expires. Logout can only blacklist the refresh token. That is expected behaviour, and the mitigations are short access lifetimes, refresh rotation with blacklisting, and a per-user token version checked per request when immediate revocation is required.\n\n**Q: You enabled `CONN_MAX_AGE=None` with PgBouncer in transaction mode and now `iterator()` fails intermittently. Why?**\nA: `iterator()` uses a named server-side cursor, which lives on one server connection across several fetches. In transaction mode PgBouncer can give later statements a different server connection, so the cursor \"does not exist\". Set `DISABLE_SERVER_SIDE_CURSORS = True` for that database alias, and use key-range batching for big scans.\n\n### Debugging toolkit for this half\n\n| Problem | Tool | Signal |\n|---|---|---|\n| Slow endpoint | APM traces (Sentry, Datadog, OpenTelemetry) | Time split between DB, HTTP and Python |\n| Query explosion | django-debug-toolbar (browsable API), `assertNumQueries` | Repeated SELECTs per row |\n| Data leak | Access logs with user id, cross-tenant tests | Object ids served to the wrong user |\n| Duplicates | Unique-constraint violations, idempotency table | Same key, two effects |\n| Celery stuck | `celery inspect active/reserved`, Flower, queue depth | Prefetched long tasks, a dead worker |\n| Connection exhaustion | `pg_stat_activity` grouped by `application_name`/state | Idle connections per pod |\n\n> **Production story:** After a team turned on Uvicorn \"for performance\", p95 rose by 30%. All their middleware was sync, and each request crossed the sync/async boundary five times. They went back to Gunicorn with gthread for the CRUD API and ran a separate ASGI deployment only for the endpoints that call several external services at once. That is a good real-world answer to \"should we go async?\"\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\nThe hardest follow-ups, all Senior:\n\n**Q: Design authentication for a product with a React web app, iOS and Android apps, and a public partner API. (Senior)**\nA: Use one identity, with a different credential for each client type. Web app on the same site: Django session cookies (HttpOnly, Secure, SameSite=Lax), CSRF via `X-CSRFToken`, and `SessionAuthentication`. Mobile: short-lived JWT access tokens plus rotating refresh tokens stored in the platform keychain, with the blacklist on rotation and logout. Partners: OAuth2 client credentials or per-partner API keys that are hashed at rest, scoped and rate-limited separately (`ScopedRateThrottle`). The same permission classes and `get_queryset()` scoping apply to all three, so authorization logic is never duplicated. Add MFA for staff and admin, and SSO via OIDC for enterprise customers. Explain why not JWT everywhere: browser token storage and revocation.\n\n**Q: How would you implement \"log out of all devices\" immediately with JWT? (Senior)**\nA: Add a `token_version` integer on the user, embed it as a claim at issue, and have a custom `JWTAuthentication.get_user()` reject tokens whose claim is lower than the current value. Cache the version in Redis to avoid a database hit per request. \"Log out everywhere\" increments it and blacklists the outstanding refresh tokens. This makes JWT partly stateful again (one cache read per request), which is the honest trade-off: immediate revocation always needs server state.\n\n**Q: Make a payment-capture flow correct when Django, Celery and the payment gateway can all fail independently. (Senior)**\nA: Write the order in state `PENDING_CAPTURE` with an idempotency record in one transaction, and enqueue capture through `on_commit`. Better still, write an outbox row in the same transaction and have a relay enqueue it, so a crash between commit and enqueue does not lose the task. The task is `acks_late`, retries with backoff, and calls the gateway with a stable idempotency key (`capture-{order_id}`), so a retry never double-charges. It moves the order to `CAPTURED` with a conditional update (`filter(status=PENDING_CAPTURE).update(...)`). Gateway webhooks are verified by signature and handled idempotently (a unique event id table). A periodic reconciliation task queries the gateway for orders stuck in pending and repairs them. Every step is at-least-once, and every effect is idempotent. See [Messaging · Idempotency & Outbox](../messaging/topic.html?p=21-idempotency-outbox).\n\n**Q: How do you guarantee read-your-writes with read replicas? (Senior)**\nA: Several layers. A router that sends reads to the primary when the request is in a transaction or has written in the last N seconds (set a short-lived cookie or a cache key per user on write, and have the router check a context variable populated by middleware). Explicit `.using(\"default\")` on the post-write redirect path. Replicas only for lag-tolerant views. Monitor replica lag, and have the router stop using a replica when lag exceeds a threshold. Interviewers want to hear the failure (a 404 right after create) and a session-sticky solution, not \"replication is fast\".\n\n**Q: Your API serves 5k requests per second at peak. Size the Gunicorn workers, DB connections and Celery for it. (Senior)**\nA: Start from latency. If the mean is 40 ms, each sync worker handles about 25 requests per second, so roughly 200 concurrent workers, plus headroom gives about 300. On 4-core pods with 9 workers each, that is about 34 pods. Each worker holds a DB connection, so about 300 web connections, which is too many for one PostgreSQL. Put PgBouncer in transaction mode with a server pool of around 50–100, because only a fraction of workers are in a query at any moment. Celery is sized by queue throughput and task duration, on separate queues for fast and slow tasks. Then validate with a load test, because these numbers are estimates and the real limit is usually database CPU or a hot row. Show the arithmetic, then say you would measure.\n\n**Q: How would you cache a product listing with filters for anonymous and logged-in users? (Senior)**\nA: Split the response. The product data, which is the same for everyone, is cached under a key derived from the normalised filter parameters plus a catalog version (`catalog:v{n}:{hash(params)}`). Personal data (price tier, wishlist flags) is computed per request from a small per-user lookup or cached per user. Invalidate by incrementing the catalog version on product writes, in `on_commit`. Add a short TTL with jitter, lock-based recomputation on hot keys, and HTTP caching (`Cache-Control: public, max-age=60` with ETags) only on the anonymous variant at the CDN. Never let a personalised response reach a shared cache.\n\n**Q: An endpoint must call three external services, each with about 300 ms latency. How do you build it in Django? (Senior)**\nA: Sequential sync calls take about 900 ms and hold a worker. Options: (1) an async view under ASGI with `httpx.AsyncClient` and `asyncio.gather`, so the wall time is about 300 ms and no thread is blocked, with timeouts and fallbacks per call; database work in that view uses the async ORM or a `sync_to_async` unit; (2) under WSGI, a thread pool (`concurrent.futures`) for the three calls, which works but uses threads; (3) if the result can be eventually consistent, precompute it in Celery and serve from cache. Add circuit breakers and per-dependency timeouts in all cases, and degrade gracefully when one service is down.\n\n**Q: How do you prevent and detect N+1 regressions across a large DRF codebase? (Senior)**\nA: Prevent: a convention that every viewset's `get_queryset()` declares `select_related`/`prefetch_related` matching its serializer, and annotations instead of per-object `SerializerMethodField` queries. Detect: a query-count test per list endpoint at two data sizes (equal counts mean no N+1), `nplusone` or `django-zeal` raising errors in CI, and per-endpoint query-count metrics in APM with alerts on jumps after a deploy. The two-size test is the important idea, because it catches N+1 without hard-coding fragile exact counts.\n\n**Q: Your Celery queue is growing faster than workers drain it. What do you do? (Senior)**\nA: First find out whether it is a backlog or a stall. Check the workers are alive and consuming (`inspect active`), check whether one task type dominates, and check whether tasks are slow because of a downstream dependency (a database lock, a rate-limited API). Then: scale workers if the work is CPU or I/O and the downstream can take more; separate queues so slow tasks don't starve urgent ones; set `prefetch_multiplier=1` for long tasks; batch tiny tasks into chunks; add `rate_limit` if a downstream is being overwhelmed. Shed or defer non-critical work. Long term, add alerts on queue depth and age of the oldest message, and set expiry (`expires=`) on tasks that are useless when late.\n\n**Q: How would you secure file uploads in a Django API? (Senior)**\nA: Validate size (`DATA_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE` and `FILE_UPLOAD_MAX_MEMORY_SIZE`, plus limits at the proxy), validate type by content rather than extension or the client's `Content-Type`, and generate storage names yourself (never trust the filename, to avoid path traversal and overwriting). Store files in object storage, not on the web root, and serve them from a separate domain or with `Content-Disposition: attachment` so uploaded HTML or SVG cannot run as your origin (stored XSS). Use signed URLs for private files. Scan files (antivirus) asynchronously before making them available. For large files, use direct-to-S3 presigned uploads so the bytes never pass through your workers.\n\n**Q: You must ship a breaking change to an API response field used by mobile apps you cannot force to update. How? (Senior)**\nA: Don't break it in place. Add the new field alongside the old one (additive and backward compatible), and switch new app versions to it. Track usage of the old field by app version from request headers. Announce deprecation (a `Deprecation`/`Sunset` header and docs), and remove it only when usage falls below a threshold or behind a new API version (`URLPathVersioning`, with the old serializer kept for v1). Add contract tests for the old shape so it doesn't break by accident before removal. See [REST API · Backward Compatibility](../rest-api/topic.html?p=32-backward-compatibility-deprecation).\n\n**Q: Explain how you'd make a Django deploy safe end to end, from PR to production. (Senior)**\nA: CI runs tests (including query-count and cross-tenant tests), `makemigrations --check`, `check --deploy` against production settings, linting for raw SQL, and a dependency audit. Build one immutable image with `collectstatic` done at build time. The release job runs `migrate` once, with migrations written to be backward compatible and with a `lock_timeout`. Then a rolling deploy with readiness probes and graceful shutdown (Gunicorn `--graceful-timeout`, Celery warm shutdown so in-flight tasks finish). Feature flags for risky behaviour. Sentry release tracking and SLO alerts to catch regressions, and a rehearsed rollback, which only works if the migrations were backward compatible. See [Ch 35 · Deployment](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production).\n\n**Q: How do you prove to an auditor that tenant A can never read tenant B's data? (Senior)**\nA: Show the controls in layers, with evidence for each. Structural: a single base viewset and queryset manager that scope by tenant, with a lint or test that fails if any viewset doesn't inherit it. Database: `tenant_id` on every tenant-owned table, composite constraints, and optionally PostgreSQL row-level security policies keyed on a session variable set per request (with care around pooling). Tests: an automated suite that creates two tenants and checks every endpoint and action for a 404 across tenants. Runtime: audit logs of access with tenant and user ids, and alerts on anomalies. Defence in depth means that a single developer mistake doesn't become a breach.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Question | 10-second answer |\n|---|---|\n| DRF flow | `dispatch`, then `initial` (authenticate, permissions, throttles), then handler, then `finalize_response` |\n| Serializer validation | Fields and `validate_<field>`, then Meta validators, then `validate()`; no `Model.clean()` |\n| Object permissions | Only via `get_object()`; lists need `get_queryset()` scoping |\n| 401 vs 403 | 401 needs `authenticate_header`; `SessionAuthentication` gives 403 |\n| Session vs JWT | Sessions for a first-party web app; JWT for mobile and machine clients; revocation is the catch |\n| SimpleJWT | Access 5 min, refresh 1 day, rotation plus blacklist |\n| Throttling | Cache-based; `locmem` is per process; set `NUM_PROXIES` |\n| Pagination | Offset is slow at depth; cursor is stable but has no total |\n| Custom user | Set `AUTH_USER_MODEL` before the first migrate; use `settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL` in FKs |\n| Passwords | PBKDF2 by default, Argon2 recommended, upgraded on login |\n| CSRF | Masked token vs cookie on unsafe methods, Origin check on HTTPS |\n| SQLi | Parameters always; danger in f-strings in `raw()`/cursor |\n| Clickjacking | `XFrameOptionsMiddleware`, `DENY` by default |\n| Caching | Redis backend; `on_commit` delete; version keys; `Vary` on auth |\n| Async | Helps I/O fan-out under ASGI; no async transactions; `SynchronousOnlyOperation` |\n| Celery | IDs not instances; `on_commit(delay)`; `acks_late` plus idempotent; prefetch 1 for long tasks |\n| Channels | Groups plus a Redis layer; `database_sync_to_async`; validate Origin |\n| Testing | `TestCase` rolls back (use `captureOnCommitCallbacks`); `TransactionTestCase` for real commits |\n| Connections | One per thread; `CONN_MAX_AGE`; native pool 5.1+; PgBouncer transaction mode breaks server-side cursors |\n| Deploy | `2 × cores + 1` workers to start; migrate as a release step; backward-compatible migrations |\n\n**Remember this**\n\n- Authorization bugs, not authentication bugs, cause most data leaks: scope `get_queryset()` everywhere.\n- Everything is at-least-once: client retries, Celery, webhooks. Idempotency keys and unique constraints absorb the duplicates.\n- Side effects go in `transaction.on_commit`.\n- Shared caches must never hold per-user responses unless the user is part of the key.\n- Async is a tool for I/O fan-out, not a general speed-up.\n- Show the connection arithmetic for any scaling answer.\n- Scenario answers: scope, mitigate, measure, hypothesise, fix, prevent.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nScratch project: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" \"djangorestframework~=3.15\" djangorestframework-simplejwt django-filter celery redis pytest-django factory-boy`.\n\n1. **The list-view leak.** Build an `Order` ModelViewSet with an `IsOwner` object permission only. Show that `GET /orders/` leaks every order, fix it with `get_queryset()` scoping, and write the two-user test.\n2. **Auth comparison.** Set up `SessionAuthentication` and SimpleJWT on the same endpoint. Show that the session POST needs `X-CSRFToken` and the JWT POST does not. Observe 403 vs 401 by reordering the authentication classes.\n3. **Throttle across workers.** Run Gunicorn with 4 workers and `locmem`, and hammer an endpoint throttled at 10/min. Count the successes, then switch to Redis and repeat.\n4. **Duplicate orders.** Simulate a client timeout plus a retry against `POST /orders/`. Show the duplicate, then add the `Idempotency-Key` table and replay logic from §5.15 and show the retry returning the original response.\n5. **Celery twice.** Write a task with `acks_late=True`, kill the worker (`kill -9`) mid-task, and watch it redeliver. Make it idempotent with a status check and a unique constraint.\n6. **Connection budget.** Run 3 Gunicorn instances × 5 workers with `CONN_MAX_AGE=60`, load them, and count the connections in `pg_stat_activity`. Add PgBouncer in transaction mode and count again.\n\n### Mock-interview drills\n\n- **Rapid fire (10 minutes):** 20 random questions from §5.1–§5.14, 30 seconds each.\n- **Scenario round (20 minutes):** a partner picks one of the §5.15 scenarios and plays the incident commander, revealing data (APM traces, logs) only when you ask the right question.\n- **Security probe (10 minutes):** \"Here is our settings.py\" (have a partner plant `DEBUG=True`, `ALLOWED_HOSTS=[\"*\"]`, `CORS_ALLOW_ALL_ORIGINS=True`, `fields=\"__all__\"`). Find them all and explain the risk of each.\n- **Scaling whiteboard (10 minutes):** redraw the §4 topology and do the connection arithmetic for 3 different traffic numbers.\n\n### Mini project\n\nBuild an \"orders API\" that passes a hostile test suite you write first: cross-user and cross-tenant 404s on every endpoint and action; JWT refresh rotation and blacklist on logout; idempotent `POST /orders/` with replay; a capture task that survives `kill -9` without double-charging (a mocked gateway counts calls); `assertNumQueries` at two sizes for every list endpoint; and `check --deploy` passing in CI. Each test maps to one question in this chapter, which gives you a concrete repository to walk an interviewer through.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Concept chapters each answer comes from:**\n\n- [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) — serializer N+1, `iterator()`.\n- [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) — `on_commit`, locks, idempotent writes.\n- [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware) — the sync/async middleware chain, ordering.\n- [Ch 20 · Static & Media Files](topic.html?p=20-static-media-files) — WhiteNoise, `STORAGES`, uploads.\n- [Ch 21 · DRF Serializers](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers) — validation order, nested writes, mass assignment.\n- [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers) — generic views, `get_queryset`, `@action`.\n- [Ch 23 · DRF Authentication, Permissions & Throttling](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling) — auth classes, object permissions, throttles.\n- [Ch 24 · DRF Pagination, Filtering, Versioning & Docs](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning) — cursor pagination, django-filter, drf-spectacular.\n- [Ch 25 · Authentication & the Custom User Model](topic.html?p=25-authentication-custom-user) — `AUTH_USER_MODEL`, hashers, backends.\n- [Ch 26 · Permissions, Groups & Authorization](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization) — model and object permissions, tenancy.\n- [Ch 27 · Sessions, Cookies & Messages](topic.html?p=27-sessions-cookies) — session engines and cookie flags.\n- [Ch 28 · Security: CSRF, XSS, SQL Injection & Hardening](topic.html?p=28-security) — every security answer here.\n- [Ch 29 · Caching in Django](topic.html?p=29-caching) — backends, invalidation, stampedes.\n- [Ch 30 · Async Django & ASGI](topic.html?p=30-async-django-asgi) — async views, async ORM, adapters.\n- [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks) — retries, acks, canvas, beat.\n- [Ch 32 · Django Channels & Real-Time](topic.html?p=32-channels-websockets) — consumers, groups, layers.\n- [Ch 33 · Testing Django Applications](topic.html?p=33-testing) — test classes, APIClient, mocking.\n- [Ch 34 · Scaling Django: Connections, Replicas & Multiple Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases) — pooling, routers, lag.\n- [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production) — Gunicorn/Uvicorn, release steps, monitoring.\n- [Ch 36 · Django Internals](topic.html?p=36-django-internals) — handlers and the app registry.\n- Other rounds: [Ch 37 · Core & ORM questions](topic.html?p=37-interview-core-orm), [Ch 39 · ORM Coding Round](topic.html?p=39-orm-coding-challenges), [Ch 40 · Design Rounds](topic.html?p=40-design-rounds).\n\n**Other handbooks:** [REST API · JWT](../rest-api/topic.html?p=20-jwt), [REST API · Idempotency Keys](../rest-api/topic.html?p=27-idempotency-keys-retries), [REST API · Rate Limiting](../rest-api/topic.html?p=24-rate-limiting-throttling), [REST API · OWASP API Security](../rest-api/topic.html?p=23-owasp-api-security), [Redis Caching · Cache Stampede](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=15-cache-stampede), [Messaging · Idempotency & Outbox](../messaging/topic.html?p=21-idempotency-outbox), [Database Design · Replication](../database-design/topic.html?p=09-replication), [System Design · Rate Limiting](../system-design/topic.html?p=26-rate-limiting).\n\n**Free resources**\n\n- **Django REST framework documentation** — DRF · *Beginner* · the API guide for serializers, views, auth, permissions and throttling. https://www.django-rest-framework.org/\n- **Django documentation: Security in Django** — Django Project · *Intermediate* · the official overview of CSRF, XSS, SQLi, clickjacking and host validation. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/security/\n- **Django documentation: Deployment checklist** — Django Project · *Intermediate* · exactly what `check --deploy` and production settings require. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/howto/deployment/checklist/\n- **Django documentation: Asynchronous support** — Django Project · *Advanced* · async views, the async ORM and its limits, adapters. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/async/\n- **Celery documentation** — Celery Project · *Intermediate* · tasks, retries, acks_late, routing and canvas. https://docs.celeryq.dev/\n- **Django Channels documentation** — Channels · *Intermediate* · consumers, routing, channel layers and auth. https://channels.readthedocs.io/\n- **Simple JWT documentation** — Jazzband · *Intermediate* · token lifetimes, rotation and blacklisting. https://django-rest-framework-simplejwt.readthedocs.io/\n- **OWASP API Security Top 10** — OWASP · *Intermediate* · the threat list behind the IDOR, mass-assignment and rate-limit questions. https://owasp.org/API-Security/\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 38.*\n", "39-orm-coding-challenges": "# 39 · ORM Coding Round: Query Challenges\n\n> **In one line:** The ORM live-coding round tests one skill — translating a business question into a single, correct, index-friendly QuerySet and being able to say what SQL it produces — so this chapter drills 35 problems on one shared schema, each with the solution, the real generated PostgreSQL, an index note and the wrong answer interviewers watch for.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups), [Ch 12 · Aggregation, Annotation, Subqueries & Window Functions](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries), [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization), [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency), [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres).\n\nMost Django interviews for mid and senior roles include a 30–45 minute **ORM round**. You get a few models (on a shared doc, CoderPad, or a whiteboard) and a sequence of questions that escalate: \"list the books by this publisher\" → \"count books per publisher, including publishers with none\" → \"top three books per genre\" → \"users whose gap between orders exceeded 90 days\". Sometimes there's a Django shell; often there isn't, and you're judged on whether your QuerySet *would* work and whether you can describe the SQL.\n\nCandidates fail this round in predictable ways:\n\n- **Python loops instead of SQL.** `[a for a in Author.objects.all() if a.books.count() == 0]` gets the right answer with N+1 queries. The interviewer stops listening.\n- **Right shape, wrong semantics.** Two `Count`s over two different joins (the fan-out bug), `exclude()` with two conditions on a multi-valued relation, `annotate()` before `values()` when you meant GROUP BY. These return plausible-looking wrong numbers — the worst kind of bug.\n- **No idea what SQL is produced.** The follow-up \"what query does that generate, and what index helps?\" is where seniority shows.\n- **Not knowing the limits.** The ORM cannot put a window over an aggregate, cannot write a recursive CTE, and duplicates correlated subqueries when you filter on them. Knowing when to reach for `raw()` is a senior signal.\n\nEvery solution below was run against PostgreSQL with Django 5.2; the SQL shown is what Django emitted (long column lists trimmed with `...`). Problems escalate through §3 (warm-up), §4 (aggregation), §5 (subqueries), §6 (window functions), §7 (writes and data quality) and §8 (performance and the ORM's limits).\n\n> **Interview tip:** Narrate in this order: *shape of the result* (one row per what?) → *which table drives* → *joins or subqueries* → *QuerySet* → *SQL* → *index*. Saying \"one row per publisher, so I start from `Publisher` and LEFT JOIN books\" before typing is worth more than typing fast.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\n### 2.1 The shared schema (used by every problem)\n\n```python\n# shop/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\n\n\nclass Publisher(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200, unique=True)\n    country = models.CharField(max_length=2)  # ISO code\n\n\nclass Author(models.Model):\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    born = models.DateField(null=True, blank=True)\n    mentor = models.ForeignKey(\"self\", null=True, blank=True,\n                               on_delete=models.SET_NULL, related_name=\"mentees\")\n\n\nclass Tag(models.Model):\n    name = models.SlugField(unique=True)\n\n\nclass Book(models.Model):\n    class Genre(models.TextChoices):\n        FICTION = \"fiction\"\n        TECH = \"tech\"\n        HISTORY = \"history\"\n\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=300)\n    author = models.ForeignKey(Author, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"books\")\n    publisher = models.ForeignKey(Publisher, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"books\")\n    genre = models.CharField(max_length=20, choices=Genre.choices)\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=8, decimal_places=2)\n    stock = models.PositiveIntegerField(default=0)\n    published_on = models.DateField()\n    tags = models.ManyToManyField(Tag, related_name=\"books\", blank=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        indexes = [models.Index(fields=[\"genre\", \"-published_on\"])]\n\n\nclass Order(models.Model):\n    class Status(models.TextChoices):\n        PENDING = \"pending\"\n        PAID = \"paid\"\n        REFUNDED = \"refunded\"\n\n    user = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE,\n                             related_name=\"orders\")\n    status = models.CharField(max_length=10, choices=Status.choices, default=Status.PENDING)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True, db_index=True)\n    total = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2, default=0)\n\n\nclass OrderItem(models.Model):\n    order = models.ForeignKey(Order, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"items\")\n    book = models.ForeignKey(Book, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"order_items\")\n    quantity = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n    unit_price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=8, decimal_places=2)\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"order\", \"book\"], name=\"uniq_order_book\")]\n\n\nclass Review(models.Model):\n    book = models.ForeignKey(Book, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"reviews\")\n    user = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE,\n                             related_name=\"reviews\")\n    rating = models.PositiveSmallIntegerField()  # 1..5\n    body = models.TextField(blank=True)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n```\n\nAll snippets assume these imports:\n\n```python\nimport datetime as dt\nfrom decimal import Decimal\n\nfrom django.contrib.auth.models import User\nfrom django.db.models import (Avg, Count, DecimalField, Exists, F, Max, Min, OuterRef,\n                              Prefetch, Q, Subquery, Sum, Value, Window)\nfrom django.db.models.functions import (Coalesce, DenseRank, Lag, Lower, Rank, RowNumber,\n                                        TruncDate, TruncMonth)\nfrom shop.models import Author, Book, Order, OrderItem, Publisher, Review, Tag\n```\n\n### 2.2 The toolbox, by question shape\n\n- **\"Rows matching a condition\"** → `filter()`, `Q` for OR/NOT, `F` to compare columns. *Why it matters:* no GROUP BY, one pass.\n- **\"One row per X with a number\"** → start from X, `annotate(Count/Sum/...)`. *Why it matters:* the model you start from decides LEFT vs INNER join and whether zero-rows appear.\n- **\"Group by an expression\"** → `values(expr).annotate(agg)` — **values first, then annotate**. *Why it matters:* `annotate()` then `values()` is per-row, not grouped.\n- **\"Count only some children\"** → `Count(\"rel\", filter=Q(...))` → `COUNT(...) FILTER (WHERE ...)` on PostgreSQL.\n- **\"Has / has-not related row\"** → `Exists(...)` / `~Exists(...)`. *Why it matters:* semi-joins don't duplicate rows and short-circuit.\n- **\"Value from the latest/first related row\"** → `Subquery(qs.order_by(...).values(\"col\")[:1])` with `OuterRef`.\n- **\"Rank / top-N per group / running total / previous row\"** → `Window(RowNumber() | Rank() | DenseRank() | Sum() | Lag(), partition_by=..., order_by=...)`; since Django 4.2 you can `.filter()` on a window annotation.\n- **\"Change many rows safely\"** → `update(col=F(...))`, `filter(guard).update(...)`, `bulk_create(update_conflicts=True, ...)`.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\nWarm-up problems — what a screening round asks. The theory behind every one of them: **a QuerySet is a SQL statement you build incrementally**, and every `__` that crosses a relation adds a JOIN (forward FK → INNER JOIN when the filter requires a match; reverse/M2M → a join that can multiply rows).\n\n### Problem 1 — Filter across a relation and a date part\n\n**Question:** Penguin books published in 2024 that cost less than 40.\n\n```python\nBook.objects.filter(publisher__name=\"Penguin\", published_on__year=2024, price__lt=40)\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"shop_book\"\nINNER JOIN \"shop_publisher\" ON (\"shop_book\".\"publisher_id\" = \"shop_publisher\".\"id\")\nWHERE (\"shop_book\".\"price\" < 40\n   AND \"shop_book\".\"published_on\" BETWEEN '2024-01-01'::date AND '2024-12-31'::date\n   AND \"shop_publisher\".\"name\" = 'Penguin')\n```\n\n**Index note:** `__year` on a `DateField` compiles to a `BETWEEN` range, so an index on `published_on` is usable — unlike `EXTRACT(YEAR ...)`. `publisher.name` is already indexed by `unique=True`.\n**Common wrong answer:** `Book.objects.filter(publisher=Publisher.objects.get(name=\"Penguin\"))` — two queries and a `DoesNotExist` crash when the publisher is missing; or `published_on__contains=\"2024\"` on a date.\n\n### Problem 2 — OR and NOT with Q\n\n**Question:** Books whose title contains \"django\" (any case) or that were written by Ana, excluding history books.\n\n```python\nBook.objects.filter(\n    (Q(title__icontains=\"django\") | Q(author__name=\"Ana\")) & ~Q(genre=Book.Genre.HISTORY)\n)\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"shop_book\" INNER JOIN \"shop_author\" ON (...)\nWHERE ((UPPER(\"shop_book\".\"title\"::text) LIKE UPPER('%django%') OR \"shop_author\".\"name\" = 'Ana')\n   AND NOT (\"shop_book\".\"genre\" = 'history'))\n```\n\n**Index note:** a leading-wildcard `LIKE` can't use a B-tree; for real search use a `pg_trgm` GIN index (`GinIndex(OpClass(Upper(\"title\"), name=\"gin_trgm_ops\"))`) or full-text search (Ch 16).\n**Common wrong answer:** `filter(title__icontains=\"django\").filter(author__name=\"Ana\")` — that's AND, not OR. Another: `Q(...) or Q(...)` with Python's `or`, which silently returns just the first `Q`.\n\n### Problem 3 — Compare two columns with F\n\n**Question:** Order items sold below the book's current list price.\n\n```python\nOrderItem.objects.filter(unit_price__lt=F(\"book__price\")).select_related(\"book\")\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"shop_orderitem\"\nINNER JOIN \"shop_book\" ON (\"shop_orderitem\".\"book_id\" = \"shop_book\".\"id\")\nWHERE \"shop_orderitem\".\"unit_price\" < (\"shop_book\".\"price\")\n```\n\n**Index note:** a column-vs-column predicate can't use an index on either column alone; the planner will hash-join and filter. Fine for analytics, slow on a hot path.\n**Common wrong answer:** a Python loop `[i for i in OrderItem.objects.all() if i.unit_price < i.book.price]` — N+1 and full table into memory.\n\n### Problem 4 — Rows with no related rows (anti-join)\n\n**Question:** Authors who have no books.\n\n```python\nAuthor.objects.filter(books__isnull=True)\n# equivalent, often clearer:  Author.objects.filter(~Exists(Book.objects.filter(author=OuterRef(\"pk\"))))\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"shop_author\"\nLEFT OUTER JOIN \"shop_book\" ON (\"shop_author\".\"id\" = \"shop_book\".\"author_id\")\nWHERE \"shop_book\".\"id\" IS NULL\n```\n\n**Index note:** every `ForeignKey` gets an index on its column by default (`db_index=True`), which is what makes this anti-join cheap; PostgreSQL usually plans it as a hash anti-join.\n**Common wrong answer:** `[a for a in Author.objects.all() if not a.books.exists()]` (N+1), or `Author.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"books\")).filter(n=0)` — correct but aggregates every author's books just to find zeros.\n\n### Problem 5 — The `exclude()` multi-valued trap\n\n**Question:** Users who did **not** place a *paid* order in 2025.\n\n```python\npaid_2025 = Order.objects.filter(user=OuterRef(\"pk\"), status=\"paid\", created_at__year=2025)\nUser.objects.filter(~Exists(paid_2025))\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"auth_user\"\nWHERE NOT EXISTS(SELECT 1 AS \"a\" FROM \"shop_order\" U0\n                 WHERE (U0.\"created_at\" BETWEEN '2025-01-01 00:00:00+00:00'::timestamptz\n                                            AND '2025-12-31 23:59:59.999999+00:00'::timestamptz\n                        AND U0.\"status\" = 'paid' AND U0.\"user_id\" = (\"auth_user\".\"id\")) LIMIT 1)\n```\n\n**Index note:** composite `Index(fields=[\"user\", \"status\", \"created_at\"])` turns each probe into an index-only lookup.\n**Common wrong answer:** `User.objects.exclude(orders__status=\"paid\", orders__created_at__year=2025)`. The docs warn that conditions inside one `exclude()` on a multi-valued relation **need not refer to the same row**; Django emits two independent `EXISTS` clauses (\"has *some* 2025 order\" AND \"has *some* paid order\") and negates the conjunction. On our test data the correct query returned 8 users; the `exclude()` version returned 2.\n\n```sql\n-- what the wrong answer generates\nWHERE NOT (EXISTS(SELECT 1 FROM \"shop_order\" U1 WHERE U1.\"created_at\" BETWEEN ... AND U1.\"user_id\" = \"auth_user\".\"id\")\n       AND EXISTS(SELECT 1 FROM \"shop_order\" U1 WHERE U1.\"status\" = 'paid' AND U1.\"user_id\" = \"auth_user\".\"id\"))\n```\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\nAggregation problems. The architecture to keep in your head: **the model you start from is the GROUP BY key**; `annotate()` on a model groups by its primary key; `values(...).annotate(...)` groups by the listed values; and every join to a *many* side multiplies rows *before* aggregation runs.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 400\" width=\"100%\" height=\"400\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj39a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#dc2626\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj39b\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">The join fan-out: two Count()s over two many-relations</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"200\" height=\"120\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Book #2</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"84\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">reviews: R1 R2 R3 R4 R5</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"102\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">order items: 2 rows</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"120\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">(quantity 3 + 4 = 7)</text>\n  <text x=\"120\" y=\"144\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">truth: 5 reviews, 7 units</text>\n  <path d=\"M220,100 L262,100\" stroke=\"#dc2626\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj39a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"264\" y=\"40\" width=\"300\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"414\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">LEFT JOIN review LEFT JOIN orderitem</text>\n  <text x=\"414\" y=\"82\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">every review paired with every item</text>\n  <text x=\"290\" y=\"106\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">R1 x item(3)   R1 x item(4)</text>\n  <text x=\"290\" y=\"124\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">R2 x item(3)   R2 x item(4)</text>\n  <text x=\"290\" y=\"142\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">R3 x item(3)   R3 x item(4)</text>\n  <text x=\"290\" y=\"160\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">R4 x item(3)   R4 x item(4)</text>\n  <text x=\"290\" y=\"178\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">R5 x item(3)   R5 x item(4)</text>\n  <text x=\"414\" y=\"204\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">5 x 2 = 10 rows before GROUP BY</text>\n  <text x=\"414\" y=\"224\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\">COUNT(review) = 10, SUM(quantity) = 35</text>\n  <path d=\"M564,100 L606,100\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj39b)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"608\" y=\"40\" width=\"252\" height=\"200\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"734\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Fixes</text>\n  <text x=\"622\" y=\"86\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Count(\"reviews\", distinct=True)</text>\n  <text x=\"622\" y=\"102\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">fixes COUNT only (not SUM)</text>\n  <text x=\"622\" y=\"130\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Subquery per relation:</text>\n  <text x=\"622\" y=\"146\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">(SELECT SUM(qty) ... WHERE</text>\n  <text x=\"622\" y=\"162\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">book_id = outer.id)</text>\n  <text x=\"622\" y=\"190\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">one join path per aggregate</text>\n  <text x=\"622\" y=\"208\" fill=\"#16a34a\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">= correct numbers</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"260\" width=\"840\" height=\"124\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"282\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">How the start model and values() decide GROUP BY</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"304\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Publisher.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"books\"))                    &#8594; GROUP BY publisher.id, LEFT JOIN: zero-book publishers appear</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"324\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Book.objects.values(\"publisher__name\").annotate(n=Count(\"id\"))  &#8594; GROUP BY name, INNER JOIN from books: zero-book publishers vanish</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"344\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">qs.values(\"month\").annotate(Sum(...))                             &#8594; GROUP BY month (values BEFORE annotate)</text>\n  <text x=\"36\" y=\"364\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">qs.annotate(Sum(...)).values(\"month\")                             &#8594; GROUP BY pk: one row per object, not per month</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Problem 6 — Count per parent, including zeros\n\n**Question:** Number of books per publisher, most first, including publishers with no books.\n\n```python\nPublisher.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"books\")).order_by(\"-n\")\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"shop_publisher\".\"name\", COUNT(\"shop_book\".\"id\") AS \"n\" FROM \"shop_publisher\"\nLEFT OUTER JOIN \"shop_book\" ON (\"shop_publisher\".\"id\" = \"shop_book\".\"publisher_id\")\nGROUP BY \"shop_publisher\".\"id\" ORDER BY 2 DESC\n```\n\nResult on test data: `[('Manning', 10), ('OReilly', 10), ('Penguin', 10), ('Empty Press', 0)]`.\n**Index note:** the FK index on `book.publisher_id` drives the join. `COUNT(\"shop_book\".\"id\")` (not `COUNT(*)`) is what makes the zero correct under a LEFT JOIN.\n**Common wrong answer:** `Book.objects.values(\"publisher__name\").annotate(n=Count(\"id\"))` — starts from books, so \"Empty Press\" is missing.\n\n### Problem 7 — Group by an expression (monthly revenue)\n\n**Question:** Revenue and order count per calendar month, paid orders only.\n\n```python\n(Order.objects.filter(status=\"paid\")\n    .annotate(month=TruncMonth(\"created_at\"))\n    .values(\"month\")\n    .annotate(revenue=Sum(\"total\"), orders=Count(\"id\"))\n    .order_by(\"month\"))\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT DATE_TRUNC('month', \"shop_order\".\"created_at\" AT TIME ZONE 'UTC') AS \"month\",\n       SUM(\"shop_order\".\"total\") AS \"revenue\", COUNT(\"shop_order\".\"id\") AS \"orders\"\nFROM \"shop_order\" WHERE \"shop_order\".\"status\" = 'paid'\nGROUP BY 1 ORDER BY 1 ASC\n```\n\n**Index note:** with `USE_TZ=True` the truncation happens in the *current* time zone (`AT TIME ZONE`) — activate the business time zone or pass `tzinfo=` or your month boundaries are wrong for non-UTC customers. A partial index `Index(fields=[\"created_at\"], condition=Q(status=\"paid\"))` serves the filter.\n**Common wrong answer:** `.annotate(revenue=Sum(\"total\")).values(\"month\")` — annotate before values groups by `id`, giving one row per order.\n\n### Problem 8 — Conditional aggregation\n\n**Question:** For every author: total books, tech books, and books published since 2023 — in one query.\n\n```python\nAuthor.objects.annotate(\n    total=Count(\"books\"),\n    tech=Count(\"books\", filter=Q(books__genre=\"tech\")),\n    recent=Count(\"books\", filter=Q(books__published_on__gte=dt.date(2023, 1, 1))),\n)\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"shop_author\".\"name\", COUNT(\"shop_book\".\"id\") AS \"total\",\n       COUNT(\"shop_book\".\"id\") FILTER (WHERE \"shop_book\".\"genre\" = 'tech') AS \"tech\",\n       COUNT(\"shop_book\".\"id\") FILTER (WHERE \"shop_book\".\"published_on\" >= '2023-01-01'::date) AS \"recent\"\nFROM \"shop_author\" LEFT OUTER JOIN \"shop_book\" ON (\"shop_author\".\"id\" = \"shop_book\".\"author_id\")\nGROUP BY \"shop_author\".\"id\"\n```\n\n**Index note:** one scan of the join produces all three numbers. On databases without `FILTER`, Django emits `COUNT(CASE WHEN ... THEN ... END)`.\n**Common wrong answer:** three separate queries, or `Sum(Case(When(books__genre=\"tech\", then=1), default=0))` — works but is the pre-2.0 idiom; `filter=` is clearer.\n\n### Problem 9 — HAVING, and why filter order matters\n\n**Question:** Users with at least three **paid** orders, with their average paid order value.\n\n```python\n(User.objects.filter(orders__status=\"paid\")\n    .annotate(n=Count(\"orders\"), aov=Avg(\"orders__total\"))\n    .filter(n__gte=3))\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"auth_user\".\"username\", COUNT(\"shop_order\".\"id\") AS \"n\", AVG(\"shop_order\".\"total\") AS \"aov\"\nFROM \"auth_user\" INNER JOIN \"shop_order\" ON (\"auth_user\".\"id\" = \"shop_order\".\"user_id\")\nWHERE \"shop_order\".\"status\" = 'paid'\nGROUP BY \"auth_user\".\"id\" HAVING COUNT(\"shop_order\".\"id\") >= 3\n```\n\n**Index note:** `(user_id, status)` composite index. The filter *before* `annotate()` becomes `WHERE` and restricts the rows being aggregated; the filter *after* becomes `HAVING`.\n**Common wrong answer:** `User.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"orders\")).filter(orders__status=\"paid\", n__gte=3)` — the status filter after annotate adds a **second** join, and `n` counts all orders, not paid ones. Equivalent correct alternative: `annotate(n=Count(\"orders\", filter=Q(orders__status=\"paid\")))`.\n\n### Problem 10 — Two aggregates over two relations (fan-out)\n\n**Question:** For each book, the number of reviews and total units sold.\n\n```python\nunits_sold = (OrderItem.objects.filter(book=OuterRef(\"pk\"))\n              .values(\"book\").annotate(s=Sum(\"quantity\")).values(\"s\"))\nBook.objects.annotate(\n    n_reviews=Count(\"reviews\", distinct=True),\n    units=Coalesce(Subquery(units_sold), 0),\n)\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"shop_book\".\"id\", COUNT(DISTINCT \"shop_review\".\"id\") AS \"n_reviews\",\n       COALESCE((SELECT SUM(U0.\"quantity\") AS \"s\" FROM \"shop_orderitem\" U0\n                 WHERE U0.\"book_id\" = (\"shop_book\".\"id\") GROUP BY U0.\"book_id\"), 0) AS \"units\"\nFROM \"shop_book\" LEFT OUTER JOIN \"shop_review\" ON (\"shop_book\".\"id\" = \"shop_review\".\"book_id\")\nGROUP BY 1, 3\n```\n\nOn test data book #2 correctly shows `(5 reviews, 7 units)`.\n**Index note:** the subquery is a per-book index probe on `orderitem.book_id`. The `.values(\"book\").annotate(...).values(\"s\")` dance forces a GROUP BY inside the subquery so it returns exactly one value.\n**Common wrong answer:** `annotate(n_reviews=Count(\"reviews\"), units=Sum(\"order_items__quantity\"))` returned `(20, 35)` for the same book — both joins in one FROM multiply each other (see the diagram above). `distinct=True` rescues `Count`, but there is no safe `distinct` for `Sum`.\n\n### Problem 11 — Top-N by an aggregate\n\n**Question:** The ten best-selling books by units in paid orders, with revenue.\n\n```python\n(Book.objects.filter(order_items__order__status=\"paid\")\n    .annotate(units=Sum(\"order_items__quantity\"),\n              revenue=Sum(F(\"order_items__quantity\") * F(\"order_items__unit_price\")))\n    .order_by(\"-units\", \"id\")[:10])\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"shop_book\".\"title\", SUM(\"shop_orderitem\".\"quantity\") AS \"units\",\n       SUM((\"shop_orderitem\".\"quantity\" * \"shop_orderitem\".\"unit_price\")) AS \"revenue\"\nFROM \"shop_book\"\nINNER JOIN \"shop_orderitem\" ON (\"shop_book\".\"id\" = \"shop_orderitem\".\"book_id\")\nINNER JOIN \"shop_order\" ON (\"shop_orderitem\".\"order_id\" = \"shop_order\".\"id\")\nWHERE \"shop_order\".\"status\" = 'paid'\nGROUP BY \"shop_book\".\"id\" ORDER BY 2 DESC, \"shop_book\".\"id\" ASC LIMIT 10\n```\n\n**Index note:** both sums use the *same* join path, so there is no fan-out. `Integer * Decimal` resolves to `DecimalField` automatically in modern Django; for exotic mixes pass `output_field=DecimalField(...)`. The `\"id\"` tiebreaker makes the top 10 deterministic.\n**Common wrong answer:** `.order_by(\"-units\")[:10]` without a tiebreak — pagination and \"top N\" become non-deterministic on ties.\n\n### Problem 12 — Aggregating through a many-to-many\n\n**Question:** Tags in use, by number of books.\n\n```python\nTag.objects.annotate(n=Count(\"books\")).filter(n__gt=0).order_by(\"-n\")\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"shop_tag\".\"name\", COUNT(\"shop_book_tags\".\"book_id\") AS \"n\" FROM \"shop_tag\"\nLEFT OUTER JOIN \"shop_book_tags\" ON (\"shop_tag\".\"id\" = \"shop_book_tags\".\"tag_id\")\nGROUP BY \"shop_tag\".\"id\" HAVING COUNT(\"shop_book_tags\".\"book_id\") > 0 ORDER BY 2 DESC\n```\n\n**Index note:** Django counts the through table's `book_id` directly — it never joins `shop_book` because it doesn't need any book column. The auto-created through table has a unique index on `(book_id, tag_id)` plus an index on `tag_id`.\n**Common wrong answer:** `Tag.objects.filter(books__isnull=False).annotate(n=Count(\"books\"))` — the extra filter adds a second join and doubles counts.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\nSubquery problems. `OuterRef(\"col\")` refers to the outer query's row; `Subquery(qs)` must return **one column** and, in a SELECT position, **at most one row** (slice `[:1]`); `Exists(qs)` is a boolean you can filter on directly.\n\n### Problem 13 — Latest row per group (three ways)\n\n**Question:** The latest order of each user (the whole row, not just the date).\n\n```python\n# (a) correlated subquery — portable\nlatest_id = (Order.objects.filter(user=OuterRef(\"user\"))\n             .order_by(\"-created_at\", \"-id\").values(\"pk\")[:1])\nOrder.objects.filter(pk=Subquery(latest_id))\n\n# (b) PostgreSQL DISTINCT ON — usually fastest\nOrder.objects.order_by(\"user_id\", \"-created_at\", \"-id\").distinct(\"user_id\")\n\n# (c) just the timestamp\nUser.objects.annotate(last_order_at=Max(\"orders__created_at\"))\n```\n\n```sql\n-- (a)\nSELECT ... FROM \"shop_order\" WHERE \"shop_order\".\"id\" =\n  (SELECT U0.\"id\" FROM \"shop_order\" U0 WHERE U0.\"user_id\" = (\"shop_order\".\"user_id\")\n   ORDER BY U0.\"created_at\" DESC, U0.\"id\" DESC LIMIT 1)\n-- (b)\nSELECT DISTINCT ON (\"shop_order\".\"user_id\") ... FROM \"shop_order\" ORDER BY 1 ASC, 3 DESC, 2 DESC\n```\n\n**Index note:** `Index(fields=[\"user\", \"-created_at\", \"-id\"])` makes (a) one index probe per user and (b) a single ordered index scan. `distinct(*fields)` requires the `order_by` to *start* with the same fields.\n**Common wrong answer:** `Order.objects.values(\"user\").annotate(Max(\"created_at\"))` and then \"join back on the timestamp\" — two users' orders can share a timestamp, and you've lost the order id.\n\n### Problem 14 — Pull a column from the latest child\n\n**Question:** Each book with the text and rating of its most recent review.\n\n```python\nlatest = Review.objects.filter(book=OuterRef(\"pk\")).order_by(\"-created_at\", \"-id\")\nBook.objects.annotate(\n    latest_body=Subquery(latest.values(\"body\")[:1]),\n    latest_rating=Subquery(latest.values(\"rating\")[:1]),\n)\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"shop_book\".\"id\",\n  (SELECT U0.\"body\" FROM \"shop_review\" U0 WHERE U0.\"book_id\" = (\"shop_book\".\"id\")\n   ORDER BY U0.\"created_at\" DESC, U0.\"id\" DESC LIMIT 1) AS \"latest_body\",\n  (SELECT U0.\"rating\" FROM \"shop_review\" U0 WHERE U0.\"book_id\" = (\"shop_book\".\"id\")\n   ORDER BY U0.\"created_at\" DESC, U0.\"id\" DESC LIMIT 1) AS \"latest_rating\"\nFROM \"shop_book\"\n```\n\n**Index note:** two correlated subqueries means two probes per book; index `(book_id, created_at DESC)`. If you need many columns, `Prefetch(\"reviews\", queryset=Review.objects.order_by(\"book_id\", \"-created_at\").distinct(\"book_id\"), to_attr=\"latest\")` fetches whole rows in one extra query.\n**Common wrong answer:** `Subquery(latest.values(\"body\"))` without `[:1]` → \"more than one row returned by a subquery used as an expression\" at runtime.\n\n### Problem 15 — Never-ordered books (NOT EXISTS vs NOT IN)\n\n**Question:** Books that have never appeared in any order.\n\n```python\nBook.objects.filter(~Exists(OrderItem.objects.filter(book=OuterRef(\"pk\"))))\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"shop_book\" WHERE NOT EXISTS(\n  SELECT 1 AS \"a\" FROM \"shop_orderitem\" U0 WHERE U0.\"book_id\" = (\"shop_book\".\"id\") LIMIT 1)\n```\n\n**Index note:** PostgreSQL plans this as a hash/merge anti-join using the FK index.\n**Common wrong answer:** `Book.objects.exclude(id__in=list(OrderItem.objects.values_list(\"book_id\", flat=True)))` — pulls every order item into Python and sends a giant literal list. Without `list()`, `exclude(id__in=<subquery>)` is fine here, but `NOT IN` returns *no rows at all* if the subquery yields a `NULL` — `NOT EXISTS` has no such trap.\n\n### Problem 16 — Compare a row to its group's aggregate\n\n**Question:** Books priced above their publisher's average price.\n\n```python\npub_avg = (Book.objects.filter(publisher=OuterRef(\"publisher\"))\n           .values(\"publisher\").annotate(a=Avg(\"price\")).values(\"a\"))\nBook.objects.alias(pub_avg=Subquery(pub_avg)).filter(price__gt=F(\"pub_avg\"))\n\n# window alternative (Django 4.2+ can filter on windows):\nBook.objects.annotate(pub_avg=Window(Avg(\"price\"), partition_by=F(\"publisher\"))\n                      ).filter(price__gt=F(\"pub_avg\"))\n```\n\n```sql\n-- window version\nSELECT * FROM ( SELECT \"shop_book\".\"id\", \"shop_book\".\"price\",\n       AVG(\"shop_book\".\"price\") OVER (PARTITION BY \"shop_book\".\"publisher_id\") AS \"pub_avg\"\n       FROM \"shop_book\" ) \"qualify\"\nWHERE \"price\" > (\"pub_avg\")\n```\n\n**Index note:** the subquery version runs one grouped probe per book (index on `publisher_id`); the window version scans once and sorts by publisher — usually better for \"all rows\". Using `alias()` instead of `annotate()` keeps the subquery out of the SELECT list; with `annotate()` Django repeats the subquery in both SELECT and WHERE.\n**Common wrong answer:** `Book.objects.filter(price__gt=Avg(\"price\"))` — `FieldError`; aggregates can't appear in a WHERE.\n\n### Problem 17 — Relational division (\"bought all of them\")\n\n**Question:** Users who bought **every** history book published in 2024.\n\n```python\ntarget = Book.objects.filter(genre=\"history\", published_on__year=2024)\nn = target.count()\n(User.objects.filter(orders__status=\"paid\", orders__items__book__in=target)\n    .annotate(k=Count(\"orders__items__book\", distinct=True))\n    .filter(k=n))\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"auth_user\"\nINNER JOIN \"shop_order\" ON (...) INNER JOIN \"shop_orderitem\" ON (...)\nWHERE (\"shop_orderitem\".\"book_id\" IN (SELECT U0.\"id\" FROM \"shop_book\" U0\n       WHERE (U0.\"genre\" = 'history' AND U0.\"published_on\" BETWEEN ... AND ...))\n   AND \"shop_order\".\"status\" = 'paid')\nGROUP BY \"auth_user\".\"id\" HAVING COUNT(DISTINCT \"shop_orderitem\".\"book_id\") = 2\n```\n\n**Index note:** two queries (the count, then the division); the `IN (subquery)` stays in the database. `distinct=True` is essential — buying the same book in two orders must count once.\n**Common wrong answer:** `User.objects.filter(orders__items__book__in=target)` — that's \"bought *any* of them\", plus duplicate users without `.distinct()`.\n\n### Problem 18 — Conditions on *different* related rows (retention)\n\n**Question:** Users who ordered in **both** January and February 2026.\n\n```python\ndef ordered_between(start, end):\n    return Exists(Order.objects.filter(user=OuterRef(\"pk\"), created_at__gte=start, created_at__lt=end))\n\njan, feb, mar = (dt.datetime(2026, m, 1, tzinfo=dt.UTC) for m in (1, 2, 3))\nUser.objects.filter(ordered_between(jan, feb), ordered_between(feb, mar))\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"auth_user\"\nWHERE (EXISTS(SELECT 1 FROM \"shop_order\" U0 WHERE (U0.\"created_at\" >= '2026-01-01 ...' AND U0.\"created_at\" < '2026-02-01 ...' AND U0.\"user_id\" = (\"auth_user\".\"id\")) LIMIT 1)\n   AND EXISTS(SELECT 1 FROM \"shop_order\" U0 WHERE (U0.\"created_at\" >= '2026-02-01 ...' AND U0.\"created_at\" < '2026-03-01 ...' AND U0.\"user_id\" = (\"auth_user\".\"id\")) LIMIT 1))\n```\n\n**Index note:** `(user_id, created_at)` index; half-open ranges (`>=`/`<`) avoid the 23:59:59.999999 edge.\n**Common wrong answer:** putting both ranges inside **one** `filter()` call: within a single `filter()`, conditions on a multi-valued relation apply to the *same* order row, so \"an order in January that is also in February\" → always empty. Chaining `.filter(jan range).filter(feb range)` is correct (it creates two joins — `INNER JOIN \"shop_order\" ... INNER JOIN \"shop_order\" T3`) but multiplies rows; `Exists` is cleaner and duplicate-free.\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\nWindow-function problems. A `Window` computes a value per row over a *partition* without collapsing rows. Since Django 4.2 you can `filter()` on a window annotation: Django wraps the query in an outer `SELECT * FROM (...) \"qualify\" WHERE ...`, because SQL forbids window functions in WHERE.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 360\" width=\"100%\" height=\"360\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj39c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Top-3 per genre: ROW_NUMBER over partitions, then the \"qualify\" wrapper</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"380\" height=\"300\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"210\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Inner SELECT: window per partition</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"72\" width=\"348\" height=\"118\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"90\" fill=\"#2563eb\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">PARTITION genre = fiction (ORDER BY price DESC, id)</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"110\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Book 27   59.00   rn = 1</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"128\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Book 15   57.00   rn = 2</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"146\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Book 12   53.00   rn = 3</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"164\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"10\">Book 18   40.00   rn = 4</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"180\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"10\">...</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"200\" width=\"348\" height=\"124\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"218\" fill=\"#d97706\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">PARTITION genre = history</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"238\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Book 20   54.00   rn = 1</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"256\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Book 17   47.00   rn = 2</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"274\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Book 26   42.00   rn = 3</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"292\" fill=\"#94a3b8\" font-size=\"10\">Book 23   41.00   rn = 4</text>\n  <text x=\"48\" y=\"310\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">numbering restarts in every partition</text>\n  <path d=\"M400,190 L448,190\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj39c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"450\" y=\"40\" width=\"410\" height=\"150\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Outer SELECT added by Django 4.2+</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"84\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">SELECT * FROM ( inner ... ) \"qualify\"</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"102\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">WHERE \"rn\" &lt;= 3</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"128\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">SQL evaluates windows AFTER WHERE, so a window</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"146\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">cannot be filtered in the same SELECT. The wrapper</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"164\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">plays the role of QUALIFY (absent in PostgreSQL).</text>\n  <rect x=\"450\" y=\"206\" width=\"410\" height=\"134\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"655\" y=\"226\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Pick the ranking function deliberately</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"248\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">RowNumber: 1,2,3,4 &#8212; exactly N per group (ties broken by id)</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"268\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">Rank: 1,2,2,4 &#8212; ties share, gaps follow (may return more than N)</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"288\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">DenseRank: 1,2,2,3 &#8212; ties share, no gaps (\"Nth highest value\")</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"314\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">Always add a unique tiebreaker to order_by,</text>\n  <text x=\"466\" y=\"330\" fill=\"#dc2626\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">or results change between runs.</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n### Problem 19 — Top-N per group\n\n**Question:** The three most expensive books in each genre.\n\n```python\nBook.objects.annotate(\n    rn=Window(RowNumber(), partition_by=F(\"genre\"), order_by=[F(\"price\").desc(), F(\"id\").asc()])\n).filter(rn__lte=3)\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM ( SELECT \"shop_book\".\"genre\", \"shop_book\".\"title\", \"shop_book\".\"price\",\n       ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY \"shop_book\".\"genre\"\n                          ORDER BY \"shop_book\".\"price\" DESC, \"shop_book\".\"id\" ASC) AS \"rn\"\n       FROM \"shop_book\" ) \"qualify\"\nWHERE \"rn\" <= 3\n```\n\n**Index note:** an index on `(genre, price DESC, id)` lets PostgreSQL feed the window pre-sorted. On Django < 4.2, filtering on `rn` raised `NotSupportedError`; you'd use a `Subquery` per group or raw SQL.\n**Common wrong answer:** looping over genres with `Book.objects.filter(genre=g).order_by(\"-price\")[:3]` — fine for 3 genres, an N+1 for 3,000 categories.\n\n### Problem 20 — Rank over an aggregate\n\n**Question:** Top two books per genre by average rating, considering only books with at least two reviews.\n\n```python\nBook.objects.annotate(\n    avg_rating=Avg(\"reviews__rating\"),\n    n=Count(\"reviews\"),\n    rk=Window(Rank(), partition_by=F(\"genre\"), order_by=F(\"avg_rating\").desc(nulls_last=True)),\n).filter(n__gte=2, rk__lte=2)\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM ( SELECT \"shop_book\".\"genre\", \"shop_book\".\"title\",\n       AVG(\"shop_review\".\"rating\") AS \"avg_rating\",\n       RANK() OVER (PARTITION BY \"shop_book\".\"genre\"\n                    ORDER BY AVG(\"shop_review\".\"rating\") DESC NULLS LAST) AS \"rk\"\n       FROM \"shop_book\" LEFT OUTER JOIN \"shop_review\" ON (...)\n       GROUP BY \"shop_book\".\"id\" HAVING COUNT(\"shop_review\".\"id\") >= 2 ) \"qualify\"\nWHERE \"rk\" <= 2\n```\n\n**Index note:** Django places `n__gte` in HAVING (inside) and `rk__lte` in the wrapper (outside), so ranking happens only among qualifying books — exactly the intended semantics. A window *ranking by* an aggregate is fine; a window *aggregating* an aggregate is not (Problem 22).\n**Common wrong answer:** forgetting `nulls_last=True` — books with no reviews (`AVG = NULL`) sort *first* in PostgreSQL's `DESC` order.\n\n### Problem 21 — Running total per user\n\n**Question:** For each paid order, the user's cumulative spend up to and including it.\n\n```python\nOrder.objects.filter(status=\"paid\").annotate(\n    running=Window(Sum(\"total\"), partition_by=F(\"user\"),\n                   order_by=[F(\"created_at\").asc(), F(\"id\").asc()])\n)\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"shop_order\".\"user_id\", \"shop_order\".\"total\",\n       SUM(\"shop_order\".\"total\") OVER (PARTITION BY \"shop_order\".\"user_id\"\n            ORDER BY \"shop_order\".\"created_at\" ASC, \"shop_order\".\"id\" ASC) AS \"running\"\nFROM \"shop_order\" WHERE \"shop_order\".\"status\" = 'paid'\n```\n\n**Index note:** index `(user_id, created_at, id)`. With `ORDER BY` in the window, the default frame is `RANGE BETWEEN UNBOUNDED PRECEDING AND CURRENT ROW` — rows with the *same* order key are peers and get the same total; the unique `id` tiebreak prevents that surprise. Use `frame=RowRange(start=None, end=0)` to be explicit.\n**Common wrong answer:** a Python accumulator over `Order.objects.all()` — correct, but loads the table; or omitting `partition_by`, which gives a global running total.\n\n### Problem 22 — Running total of a daily aggregate (an ORM limit)\n\n**Question:** Daily paid revenue with a cumulative running total.\n\n```python\n(Order.objects.filter(status=\"paid\")\n    .annotate(day=TruncDate(\"created_at\"))\n    .annotate(revenue=Window(Sum(\"total\"), partition_by=F(\"day\")),\n              running=Window(Sum(\"total\"), order_by=F(\"day\").asc()))\n    .values(\"day\", \"revenue\", \"running\")\n    .distinct()\n    .order_by(\"day\"))\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT DISTINCT (\"shop_order\".\"created_at\" AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')::date AS \"day\",\n  SUM(\"shop_order\".\"total\") OVER (PARTITION BY (\"shop_order\".\"created_at\" AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')::date) AS \"revenue\",\n  SUM(\"shop_order\".\"total\") OVER (ORDER BY (\"shop_order\".\"created_at\" AT TIME ZONE 'UTC')::date ASC) AS \"running\"\nFROM \"shop_order\" WHERE \"shop_order\".\"status\" = 'paid' ORDER BY 1 ASC\n```\n\n**Why this shape:** the natural answer — `values(\"day\").annotate(revenue=Sum(\"total\")).annotate(running=Window(Sum(\"revenue\"), ...))` — raises `FieldError: Cannot compute Sum('revenue'): 'revenue' is an aggregate`. The ORM cannot nest a window around a GROUP BY aggregate. The trick: compute both numbers as windows over raw rows (the default `RANGE` frame makes all rows of a day peers, so each gets the cumulative total through that day), then `distinct()` collapses to one row per day.\n**Common wrong answer:** `Window(Sum(Sum(\"total\")))` — `FieldError` too. For anything more elaborate, write the CTE with `raw()` (Problem 35).\n\n### Problem 23 — Share of a partition total\n\n**Question:** Each paid order's percentage of that user's total paid spend.\n\n```python\nOrder.objects.filter(status=\"paid\").annotate(\n    user_total=Window(Sum(\"total\"), partition_by=F(\"user\")),\n    share=F(\"total\") * 100 / F(\"user_total\"),\n)\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"shop_order\".\"user_id\", \"shop_order\".\"total\",\n       SUM(\"shop_order\".\"total\") OVER (PARTITION BY \"shop_order\".\"user_id\") AS \"user_total\",\n       ((\"shop_order\".\"total\" * 100) / SUM(\"shop_order\".\"total\") OVER (PARTITION BY \"shop_order\".\"user_id\")) AS \"share\"\nFROM \"shop_order\" WHERE \"shop_order\".\"status\" = 'paid'\n```\n\n**Index note:** no `order_by` in the window → the frame is the whole partition. Arithmetic referencing a window annotation is inlined, which is legal in a SELECT.\n**Common wrong answer:** a `Subquery` that sums the user's orders per row — correct but one probe per order instead of one sort.\n\n### Problem 24 — Previous row and gaps (Lag)\n\n**Question:** Orders placed more than 90 days after the same user's previous order.\n\n```python\n(Order.objects\n    .annotate(prev=Window(Lag(\"created_at\"), partition_by=F(\"user\"), order_by=F(\"created_at\").asc()))\n    .annotate(gap=F(\"created_at\") - F(\"prev\"))\n    .filter(gap__gt=dt.timedelta(days=90)))\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT * FROM ( SELECT \"shop_order\".\"user_id\", ...,\n       (\"shop_order\".\"created_at\" - LAG(\"shop_order\".\"created_at\", 1) OVER\n         (PARTITION BY \"shop_order\".\"user_id\" ORDER BY \"shop_order\".\"created_at\" ASC)) AS \"gap\"\n       FROM \"shop_order\" ) \"qualify\"\nWHERE \"gap\" > '90 days 0:00:00'::interval\n```\n\n**Index note:** `(user_id, created_at)`. `gap` is a `timedelta` on PostgreSQL (`interval`). The first order of every user has `prev = NULL`, so `gap` is NULL and drops out naturally.\n**Common wrong answer:** a self-join \"order o2 where o2.created_at < o1.created_at, take the max\" with `Subquery` per row — works, but quadratic-ish and far slower than one `LAG`.\n\n### Problem 25 — Dense rank over a filtered aggregate\n\n**Question:** Rank authors by paid revenue (ties share a rank, no gaps); authors with no sales get 0.\n\n```python\nrevenue = Sum(F(\"books__order_items__quantity\") * F(\"books__order_items__unit_price\"),\n              filter=Q(books__order_items__order__status=\"paid\"))\nAuthor.objects.annotate(\n    revenue=Coalesce(revenue, Value(Decimal(\"0\"))),\n    rnk=Window(DenseRank(), order_by=F(\"revenue\").desc()),\n)\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"shop_author\".\"name\",\n  COALESCE(SUM((\"shop_orderitem\".\"quantity\" * \"shop_orderitem\".\"unit_price\"))\n           FILTER (WHERE \"shop_order\".\"status\" = 'paid'), 0) AS \"revenue\",\n  DENSE_RANK() OVER (ORDER BY COALESCE(SUM(...) FILTER (WHERE ...), 0) DESC) AS \"rnk\"\nFROM \"shop_author\" LEFT OUTER JOIN \"shop_book\" ON (...)\nLEFT OUTER JOIN \"shop_orderitem\" ON (...) LEFT OUTER JOIN \"shop_order\" ON (...)\nGROUP BY \"shop_author\".\"id\"\n```\n\n**Index note:** the status condition lives in `FILTER`, not `WHERE`, so authors with zero paid sales survive the LEFT JOINs.\n**Common wrong answer:** `Author.objects.filter(books__order_items__order__status=\"paid\").annotate(...)` — the WHERE turns the LEFT JOINs into inner semantics and silently removes unsold authors.\n\n### Problem 26 — The Nth highest distinct value\n\n**Question:** The third-highest distinct book price.\n\n```python\nBook.objects.order_by(\"-price\").values_list(\"price\", flat=True).distinct()[2]\n# or, to get the books at that price:\nBook.objects.annotate(r=Window(DenseRank(), order_by=F(\"price\").desc())).filter(r=3)\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT DISTINCT \"shop_book\".\"price\" FROM \"shop_book\" ORDER BY 1 DESC LIMIT 1 OFFSET 2\n```\n\n**Index note:** an index on `price` serves the ordered distinct scan.\n**Common wrong answer:** `Book.objects.order_by(\"-price\")[2]` — ignores duplicates (two books at the same price make \"third\" wrong). Also note `[2]` raises `IndexError` when fewer than three distinct prices exist; `.distinct()[2:3]` returns an empty list instead.\n\n### Problem 27 — Month-over-month change (know when to stop)\n\n**Question:** Monthly paid revenue and the change versus the previous month.\n\n```python\nmonthly = list(\n    Order.objects.filter(status=\"paid\")\n    .annotate(month=TruncMonth(\"created_at\")).values(\"month\")\n    .annotate(revenue=Sum(\"total\")).order_by(\"month\")\n)\nprev = None\nfor row in monthly:                     # at most a few dozen rows\n    row[\"delta\"] = None if prev is None else row[\"revenue\"] - prev\n    prev = row[\"revenue\"]\n```\n\n**Why:** `annotate(prev=Window(Lag(Sum(\"total\")), order_by=F(\"month\")))` compiles but PostgreSQL rejects it — `ProgrammingError: aggregate functions are not allowed in GROUP BY` — because Django adds the window expression to the GROUP BY. With a handful of aggregated rows, finishing in Python is the senior answer; for heavy reporting, a `raw()` CTE (`WITH m AS (SELECT ... GROUP BY 1) SELECT month, revenue, revenue - LAG(revenue) OVER (ORDER BY month) FROM m`) or a reporting view mapped with `managed = False`.\n**Common wrong answer:** fighting the ORM for twenty minutes. Say the limit out loud and move on.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\nWrites and data-quality problems. These are the ones where a wrong answer is not just slow but **corrupts data**, so interviewers probe concurrency.\n\n### Problem 28 — Find case-insensitive duplicates\n\n**Question:** Emails used by more than one user (ignoring case), then the users involved.\n\n```python\ndups = (User.objects.annotate(email_l=Lower(\"email\"))\n        .values(\"email_l\").annotate(n=Count(\"id\")).filter(n__gt=1))\nUser.objects.annotate(email_l=Lower(\"email\")).filter(email_l__in=dups.values(\"email_l\")).order_by(\"email_l\", \"id\")\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT LOWER(\"auth_user\".\"email\") AS \"email_l\", \"auth_user\".\"id\" FROM \"auth_user\"\nWHERE LOWER(\"auth_user\".\"email\") IN (SELECT LOWER(U0.\"email\") AS \"email_l\" FROM \"auth_user\" U0\n                                     GROUP BY 1 HAVING COUNT(U0.\"id\") > 1)\nORDER BY 1 ASC, 2 ASC\n```\n\n**Index note:** a functional index `Index(Lower(\"email\"), name=\"user_email_lower\")` serves both; prevent recurrence with `UniqueConstraint(Lower(\"email\"), name=\"uniq_email_ci\")` (Ch 16).\n**Common wrong answer:** `values(\"email\")` — misses `Ann@x.com` vs `ann@x.com`, which is exactly how duplicates sneak in.\n\n### Problem 29 — Delete duplicates, keep the newest\n\n**Question:** A bug let users review the same book several times. Delete all but each user's latest review per book.\n\n```python\nfrom django.db import transaction\n\nextra = Review.objects.annotate(\n    rn=Window(RowNumber(), partition_by=[F(\"user\"), F(\"book\")],\n              order_by=[F(\"created_at\").desc(), F(\"id\").desc()])\n).filter(rn__gt=1)\n\nwith transaction.atomic():\n    deleted, per_model = Review.objects.filter(pk__in=extra.values(\"pk\")).delete()\n```\n\n```sql\nDELETE FROM \"shop_review\" WHERE \"shop_review\".\"id\" IN (\n  SELECT \"pk\" FROM ( SELECT * FROM ( SELECT U0.\"id\" AS \"pk\", ROW_NUMBER() OVER (\n      PARTITION BY U0.\"user_id\", U0.\"book_id\" ORDER BY U0.\"created_at\" DESC, U0.\"id\" DESC) AS \"qual0\"\n    FROM \"shop_review\" U0 ) \"qualify\" WHERE \"qual0\" > 1 ) \"qualify_mask\")\n```\n\n**Index note:** then add `UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"user\", \"book\"], name=\"one_review_per_user_book\")` in a migration so it can't recur. Because `Review` has no dependants and no signal receivers, Django can fast-delete in a single statement; with receivers or cascades it collects rows first.\n**Common wrong answer:** `for r in Review.objects.all(): ...` comparing in Python, deleting one by one — thousands of queries, and racy without a lock.\n\n### Problem 30 — Bulk update relative to the current value\n\n**Question:** Raise all Manning book prices by 10% atomically.\n\n```python\nBook.objects.filter(publisher__name=\"Manning\").update(price=F(\"price\") * Decimal(\"1.10\"))\n```\n\n```sql\nUPDATE \"shop_book\" SET \"price\" = (\"shop_book\".\"price\" * 1.10)\nWHERE \"shop_book\".\"id\" IN (SELECT U0.\"id\" FROM \"shop_book\" U0\n  INNER JOIN \"shop_publisher\" U1 ON (U0.\"publisher_id\" = U1.\"id\") WHERE U1.\"name\" = 'Manning')\n```\n\n**Index note:** Django doesn't emit `UPDATE ... FROM`, so a filter across a relation becomes `id IN (subquery)`. The `numeric(8,2)` column rounds the result. `update()` returns the row count, skips `save()` and sends no `pre_save`/`post_save` signals.\n**Common wrong answer:** `for b in qs: b.price *= Decimal(\"1.1\"); b.save()` — N queries, and a lost update if another request changes a price between your read and write.\n\n### Problem 31 — Conditional decrement (no overselling)\n\n**Question:** Reserve `qty` copies of a book; fail if stock is insufficient, safely under concurrency.\n\n```python\nclass OutOfStock(Exception):\n    pass\n\n\ndef reserve(book_id: int, qty: int) -> None:\n    updated = Book.objects.filter(pk=book_id, stock__gte=qty).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - qty)\n    if updated == 0:\n        raise OutOfStock(book_id)\n```\n\n```sql\nUPDATE \"shop_book\" SET \"stock\" = (\"shop_book\".\"stock\" - 3)\nWHERE (\"shop_book\".\"id\" = 1 AND \"shop_book\".\"stock\" >= 3)\n```\n\n**Index note:** primary-key lookup; the row lock taken by `UPDATE` serialises concurrent reservations, and the guard is re-evaluated against the committed value under READ COMMITTED. As a backstop, `PositiveIntegerField` already creates a `CHECK (\"stock\" >= 0)` on PostgreSQL; on a plain `IntegerField` add `CheckConstraint(condition=Q(stock__gte=0), ...)` (`check=` before 5.1).\n**Common wrong answer:** `b = Book.objects.get(pk=id); if b.stock >= qty: b.stock -= qty; b.save()` — read-modify-write race: two requests both see 1 in stock and both sell it. See [Ch 14](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) and the checkout round in [Ch 40](topic.html?p=40-design-rounds).\n\n### Problem 32 — Upsert in bulk\n\n**Question:** Sync a feed of publishers: insert new names, update the country of existing ones, in one statement.\n\n```python\nfeed = [Publisher(name=\"Penguin\", country=\"US\"), Publisher(name=\"NoStarch\", country=\"US\")]\nPublisher.objects.bulk_create(\n    feed, update_conflicts=True, unique_fields=[\"name\"], update_fields=[\"country\"]\n)\n```\n\n```sql\nINSERT INTO \"shop_publisher\" (\"name\", \"country\")\nSELECT * FROM UNNEST(('{Penguin,NoStarch}')::varchar[], ('{US,US}')::varchar[])\nON CONFLICT(\"name\") DO UPDATE SET \"country\" = EXCLUDED.\"country\"\nRETURNING \"shop_publisher\".\"id\"\n```\n\n**Index note:** `ON CONFLICT` needs a unique index or constraint on `unique_fields`. Django 5.2 on PostgreSQL sends the rows via `UNNEST` arrays (fewer bind parameters). Use `ignore_conflicts=True` when existing rows should be left alone (PKs are then not set on the objects).\n**Common wrong answer:** `update_or_create()` in a loop — two queries per row and racy without a unique constraint.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\nPerformance problems and the ORM's hard limits — the problems that separate \"it returns the right rows\" from \"it survives production traffic\".\n\n### Problem 33 — Keyset (seek) pagination\n\n**Question:** Page through orders newest-first, 50 at a time, given the last row of the previous page — without `OFFSET`.\n\n```python\ndef next_page(last_created_at, last_id, size=50):\n    return (Order.objects\n            .filter(Q(created_at__lt=last_created_at) |\n                    Q(created_at=last_created_at, id__lt=last_id))\n            .order_by(\"-created_at\", \"-id\")[:size])\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... FROM \"shop_order\"\nWHERE (\"shop_order\".\"created_at\" < '2025-11-09 06:21:06.840954+00:00'::timestamptz\n    OR (\"shop_order\".\"created_at\" = '2025-11-09 06:21:06.840954+00:00'::timestamptz AND \"shop_order\".\"id\" < 59))\nORDER BY \"shop_order\".\"created_at\" DESC, 1 DESC LIMIT 50\n```\n\n**Index note:** `Index(fields=[\"-created_at\", \"-id\"])`. Cost is constant per page; `OFFSET 100000` scans and discards 100,000 rows. This is what DRF's `CursorPagination` does for you ([Ch 24](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning)).\n**Common wrong answer:** `Paginator(qs, 50).page(2000)` on a large table — `COUNT(*)` plus a deep `OFFSET`, both linear.\n\n### Problem 34 — Kill the N+1 in an order listing\n\n**Question:** List the latest 20 orders with the user, each item, its book and the book's publisher, plus an item count — with a constant number of queries.\n\n```python\norders = (Order.objects\n          .select_related(\"user\")\n          .prefetch_related(Prefetch(\"items\", queryset=OrderItem.objects.select_related(\"book__publisher\")))\n          .annotate(item_count=Count(\"items\"))\n          .order_by(\"-created_at\")[:20])\n```\n\n```sql\nSELECT \"shop_order\".*, COUNT(\"shop_orderitem\".\"id\") AS \"item_count\", \"auth_user\".* FROM \"shop_order\"\nLEFT OUTER JOIN \"shop_orderitem\" ON (...) INNER JOIN \"auth_user\" ON (...)\nGROUP BY \"shop_order\".\"id\", \"auth_user\".\"id\" ORDER BY \"shop_order\".\"created_at\" DESC LIMIT 20;\n\nSELECT \"shop_orderitem\".*, \"shop_book\".*, \"shop_publisher\".* FROM \"shop_orderitem\"\nINNER JOIN \"shop_book\" ON (...) INNER JOIN \"shop_publisher\" ON (...)\nWHERE \"shop_orderitem\".\"order_id\" IN (9, 32, 41, ...)\n```\n\n**Index note:** two queries regardless of page size. Prove it in a test with `self.assertNumQueries(2)` ([Ch 33](topic.html?p=33-testing)). Use `only()` on the prefetch queryset if `auth_user`'s password hash and other wide columns aren't needed.\n**Common wrong answer:** `prefetch_related(\"items__book__publisher\")` — correct but three extra queries instead of one; or `select_related(\"items\")` — `FieldError`, because `select_related` only follows single-valued relations.\n\n### Problem 35 — Hierarchies: when the answer is raw SQL\n\n**Question:** Given an author, return their mentor chain upward (mentor, mentor's mentor, ...).\n\n```python\nchain = Author.objects.raw(\n    \"\"\"\n    WITH RECURSIVE chain AS (\n        SELECT id, name, mentor_id, 0 AS depth FROM shop_author WHERE id = %s\n        UNION ALL\n        SELECT a.id, a.name, a.mentor_id, c.depth + 1\n        FROM shop_author a JOIN chain c ON a.id = c.mentor_id\n    )\n    SELECT * FROM chain ORDER BY depth\n    \"\"\",\n    [author_id],\n)\n[(a.name, a.depth) for a in chain]   # extra columns become attributes\n```\n\n**Index note:** the PK serves every step. Parameters go in the second argument — never f-string `author_id` into the SQL. Alternatives: `django-cte` for CTEs as QuerySets, `django-treebeard`/`django-mptt` (materialised path, nested sets) when you query trees constantly, or PostgreSQL `ltree`.\n**Common wrong answer:** a `while author.mentor:` loop — one query per level (fine for depth 3, terrible for a category tree of depth 12 per page row), or `raw(f\"... WHERE id = {author_id}\")` — SQL injection.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: What is the difference between `annotate()` and `aggregate()`?**\nA: `aggregate()` collapses the whole QuerySet into a dict of values — one SQL row, terminal, it returns data, not a QuerySet. `annotate()` adds a computed column per row of the result and returns a QuerySet you can keep filtering, ordering and slicing. On a model QuerySet, `annotate(Count(...))` groups by the model's primary key; after `values(...)`, it groups by those values. Filters on annotations become `HAVING`.\n\n**Q: Why does the order of `values()` and `annotate()` matter?**\nA: `values(\"x\").annotate(agg)` tells Django to GROUP BY `x` and aggregate per group. `annotate(agg).values(\"x\")` computes the aggregate per object (grouped by pk) and then merely selects `x` — so you get one row per object with repeated `x` values. Interviewers use this to test whether you think in SQL. When grouping by an expression, `annotate(month=TruncMonth(...))` first, then `values(\"month\")`, then the aggregate.\n\n**Q: How do you count only some related rows?**\nA: Use `Count(\"rel\", filter=Q(...))`, which compiles to `COUNT(...) FILTER (WHERE ...)` on PostgreSQL and `COUNT(CASE WHEN ...)` elsewhere. It lets one query return several conditional counts. Avoid putting the condition in `.filter()` if you also need rows with zero matches — the WHERE removes them before grouping.\n\n**Q: Why can two `Count`s in one `annotate()` return inflated numbers, and how do you fix it?**\nA: Each relation adds a join, and joining two independent one-to-many relations produces a cross product per parent row before GROUP BY: 5 reviews × 2 order items = 10 rows, so both aggregates are wrong. `Count(..., distinct=True)` fixes counts because it counts distinct child ids, but it can't fix `Sum`/`Avg`. The general fix is a correlated `Subquery` per relation (or separate queries), so each aggregate has its own join path.\n\n**Q: When would you use `Exists` instead of a join or `__in`?**\nA: For \"has / has not a related row\" questions. `Exists` is a semi-join: it never duplicates the outer rows (no need for `distinct()`), it can stop at the first match, and `~Exists` doesn't have `NOT IN`'s NULL trap. It's also the only reliable way to express several conditions that must hold on the *same* related row inside an exclusion, because `exclude()` with multiple multi-valued conditions doesn't guarantee they refer to one row.\n\n**Q: Explain `Subquery` and `OuterRef` with an example.**\nA: `OuterRef(\"pk\")` is a placeholder for a column of the outer query's current row, resolved when the inner QuerySet is embedded. `Subquery(Review.objects.filter(book=OuterRef(\"pk\")).order_by(\"-created_at\").values(\"body\")[:1])` annotates each book with its latest review text. The inner QuerySet must return one column (`values(\"col\")`) and, used as a value, one row (`[:1]`). For per-group aggregates inside a subquery, use `.values(\"fk\").annotate(x=Agg(...)).values(\"x\")` to force a GROUP BY.\n\n**Q: How do you get the top N rows per group in Django?**\nA: Annotate a `Window(RowNumber(), partition_by=F(\"group\"), order_by=[...])` and filter on it — supported since Django 4.2, which wraps the query in an outer SELECT because SQL can't filter windows in WHERE. Include a unique tiebreaker in `order_by`. Use `Rank`/`DenseRank` if ties should share a position. Before 4.2, the options were a correlated subquery per group, `DISTINCT ON` for N=1 on PostgreSQL, or raw SQL.\n\n**Q: How would you decrement stock without overselling under concurrent requests? (Senior)**\nA: Make the check and the write a single atomic statement: `Book.objects.filter(pk=id, stock__gte=qty).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - qty)` and treat a return value of 0 as \"insufficient stock\". The UPDATE takes a row lock, concurrent updates queue behind it, and under READ COMMITTED PostgreSQL re-evaluates the WHERE against the newly committed row, so the guard can't be bypassed. Back it with a `CheckConstraint(stock >= 0)`. If you must read first (multiple rows, business rules), use `select_for_update()` inside `atomic()` and lock rows in a consistent order (by id) to avoid deadlocks.\n\n**Q: Your annotated report is correct in tests but slow in production. How do you investigate? (Senior)**\nA: Capture the real SQL (`connection.queries`, debug toolbar, or `CaptureQueriesContext`), then run `qs.explain(analyze=True, buffers=True)` on production-like data. Look for sequential scans on large tables, row-estimate errors, nested loops over correlated subqueries, and sorts spilling to disk. Typical fixes: a composite index matching filter + order columns, partial indexes for status filters, `alias()` instead of `annotate()` so a subquery isn't evaluated twice, replacing per-row subqueries with a window or a join, and dropping wide columns with `only()`. Then lock the query count with `assertNumQueries` so it doesn't regress.\n\n**Q: What can't the ORM express, and what do you do then? (Senior)**\nA: Recursive CTEs, windows over GROUP BY aggregates (running totals of daily sums, `LAG` of monthly totals), `LATERAL` joins, some set operations combined with ordering, and vendor features without wrappers. First check whether a reformulation works (window over raw rows plus `distinct()`, finishing small result sets in Python). Otherwise use `Model.objects.raw(sql, params)` for model instances, `connection.cursor()` for arbitrary rows, a `managed = False` model over a database view for reusable reports, or libraries like `django-cte`. Always pass parameters separately to stay safe from SQL injection.\n\n**Q: How would you design pagination for a feed with tens of millions of rows? (Senior)**\nA: Keyset pagination: order by a unique, indexed key such as `(created_at, id)` and fetch `WHERE (created_at, id) < (last_created_at, last_id) ORDER BY created_at DESC, id DESC LIMIT n`, expressed with `Q` objects in the ORM. Cost is constant per page, while `OFFSET` grows linearly and `COUNT(*)` for page numbers is itself a full scan. In DRF, `CursorPagination` implements this with opaque cursors, at the price of no total count and no jumping to page 500. Index the ordering columns in the same direction.\n\n**Q: What's the difference between `filter(a__x=1, a__y=2)` and `filter(a__x=1).filter(a__y=2)` on a reverse FK?**\nA: In a single `filter()` call, both conditions apply to the *same* related row — \"has an `a` that is both x=1 and y=2\". Chained `filter()` calls on a multi-valued relation create separate joins, so each condition can be satisfied by a *different* related row — \"has an `a` with x=1 and some `a` with y=2\". The second form can also duplicate parent rows. For forward FKs and one-to-one there's no difference, because there's only one related row.\n\n**Q: How do you find and remove duplicate rows with the ORM?**\nA: Find with `values(key_expr).annotate(n=Count(\"id\")).filter(n__gt=1)`, using `Lower()` for case-insensitive keys. Remove by ranking within each duplicate group with `Window(RowNumber(), partition_by=[...], order_by=[...])`, filtering `rn > 1`, and deleting `filter(pk__in=ranked.values(\"pk\"))` inside a transaction. Then add a `UniqueConstraint` (functional if needed) so it can't recur, and make the writing code handle `IntegrityError`.\n\n**Q: When is `bulk_create(update_conflicts=True)` better than `update_or_create()`?**\nA: For syncing many rows. `bulk_create` with `update_conflicts=True, unique_fields=..., update_fields=...` issues one `INSERT ... ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` per batch and is atomic per row at the database level. `update_or_create()` does a SELECT (with `select_for_update`) and then an INSERT or UPDATE per row, which is far slower for large sets. The trade-offs: no `save()` calls, no signals, and a unique constraint on the conflict fields is required.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Question shape | ORM tool | SQL produced | Classic trap |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Has / has no child | `Exists` / `~Exists`, `rel__isnull=True` | `EXISTS`, `LEFT JOIN ... IS NULL` | `exclude()` with two multi-valued conditions |\n| Count per parent incl. zero | `Parent.objects.annotate(Count(\"children\"))` | `LEFT JOIN ... GROUP BY parent.id` | starting from the child table |\n| Group by expression | `annotate(e=...).values(\"e\").annotate(agg)` | `GROUP BY 1` | `annotate()` before `values()` |\n| Conditional count | `Count(\"rel\", filter=Q(...))` | `COUNT(...) FILTER (WHERE ...)` | condition in `.filter()` drops zeros |\n| Two aggregates, two relations | `Subquery` per relation, `distinct=True` for counts | correlated subqueries | join fan-out |\n| Latest child value | `Subquery(qs.order_by(...).values(\"c\")[:1])` | `(SELECT ... LIMIT 1)` | missing `[:1]` |\n| Latest row per group | `distinct(\"fk\")` (PG) or `pk=Subquery(...)` | `DISTINCT ON` | joining back on a timestamp |\n| Top-N per group | `Window(RowNumber(), partition_by=...)` + `filter` | wrapper `\"qualify\"` | no tiebreaker |\n| Running total | `Window(Sum(...), order_by=...)` | `SUM() OVER (ORDER BY ...)` | RANGE peers on ties |\n| Safe decrement | `filter(guard).update(F(...))` | `UPDATE ... WHERE stock >= n` | read-modify-write |\n| Upsert | `bulk_create(update_conflicts=True)` | `ON CONFLICT DO UPDATE` | `update_or_create` loop |\n| Deep pagination | keyset `Q(...)` filter | `WHERE (ts, id) < ...` | `OFFSET` |\n\n- Say the *result grain* first: \"one row per ___\".\n- The model you start from is your GROUP BY and your LEFT/INNER JOIN decision.\n- `values()` before `annotate()` to group; after to just select.\n- Each `__` into a many-relation multiplies rows; two of them multiply each other.\n- Filters on windows work since 4.2; windows over aggregates don't.\n- Always add a unique tiebreaker to orderings used for ranking or pagination.\n- Writes: `F()` + guard in one `UPDATE`; constraints as the backstop.\n- When the ORM can't, use `raw()` with params — and say so confidently.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" \"psycopg[binary]\" factory-boy`, create a project with the §2 schema pointing at a local PostgreSQL, and write a seed command that generates a few thousand rows with `factory_boy` (include ties in prices and timestamps on purpose).\n\n1. **Assert, don't eyeball.** For Problems 6, 10, 17 and 24, write a `TestCase` that computes the answer in plain Python from `Model.objects.all()` and asserts the ORM result matches. Watch Problem 10's naive version fail.\n2. **Read the plans.** Run `explain(analyze=True)` for Problems 5, 13(a), 13(b) and 19 before and after adding the suggested indexes; record the plan node types that change.\n3. **Break the semantics.** Write the `exclude()` version of Problem 5 and the single-`filter()` version of Problem 18; find a dataset where each returns a different answer than the correct one.\n4. **Port to 4.2-less.** Rewrite Problem 19 without filtering on a window (correlated `Subquery` with `pk__in`), and compare plans.\n5. **Concurrency drill.** In a `TransactionTestCase`, run Problem 31's `reserve()` from 20 threads against a book with 10 copies; assert exactly 10 succeed. Then try the naive read-modify-write and show it oversells.\n6. **Raw where needed.** Implement Problem 27 as a `raw()` CTE and as a `managed = False` model over a `CREATE VIEW` migration (`RunSQL`); compare ergonomics.\n\n**Mini project — \"ORM kata\" test suite:** a Django app containing all 35 problems as functions in `shop/queries.py`, each with a test asserting both the result (against a Python oracle) and the query count (`assertNumQueries`). Add a CI job that runs it against PostgreSQL so regressions in query shape fail the build.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters**\n- [Ch 10 · QuerySets, Lookups, Q & F Expressions](topic.html?p=10-querysets-lookups) — lookups, `Q`, `F`, multi-valued filter semantics.\n- [Ch 12 · Aggregation, Annotation, Subqueries & Window Functions](topic.html?p=12-aggregation-annotation-subqueries) — the concept chapter behind §4–§6.\n- [Ch 13 · QuerySet Optimization & the N+1 Problem](topic.html?p=13-queryset-optimization) — Problem 34 in depth.\n- [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency) — Problems 29–31.\n- [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres) — every index note, functional constraints, `raw()`.\n- [Ch 24 · DRF Pagination, Filtering, Versioning & Docs](topic.html?p=24-drf-pagination-filtering-versioning) — cursor pagination.\n- [Ch 33 · Testing Django Applications](topic.html?p=33-testing) — `assertNumQueries`, factories.\n- [Ch 36 · Django Internals](topic.html?p=36-django-internals) — how these QuerySets compile.\n- [Ch 37 · Most-Asked Django Interview Questions: Core & ORM](topic.html?p=37-interview-core-orm) and [Ch 40 · Django Design Rounds](topic.html?p=40-design-rounds) — the rest of the interview loop.\n\n**Other handbooks**\n- [SQL · Top-N per Group](../sql/topic.html?p=34-problem-top-n-per-group), [SQL · Running Total](../sql/topic.html?p=35-problem-running-total), [SQL · Gaps & Islands](../sql/topic.html?p=36-problem-gaps-islands) — the same problems in raw SQL.\n- [SQL · Window Functions](../sql/topic.html?p=13-window-functions) and [SQL · Index Design](../sql/topic.html?p=20-index-design) — the theory behind the index notes.\n- [REST API · Pagination](../rest-api/topic.html?p=13-pagination) — offset vs cursor at the API layer.\n\n**Free resources**\n- **Aggregation** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · the canonical explanation of `values()`/`annotate()` ordering and join interactions. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/aggregation/\n- **Query Expressions** — Django docs · *Advanced* · `F`, `Subquery`, `OuterRef`, `Exists`, `Window` and frames. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/expressions/\n- **Making queries** — Django docs · *Beginner* · spanning multi-valued relationships, the `filter()`/`exclude()` semantics. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/queries/\n- **QuerySet API reference** — Django docs · *Intermediate* · `distinct(*fields)`, `bulk_create` conflicts, `explain()`. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/querysets/\n- **PostgreSQL: Window Functions tutorial** — PostgreSQL docs · *Intermediate* · frames and peers explained by the database itself. https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/tutorial-window.html\n- **Use The Index, Luke** — Markus Winand · *Intermediate* · why keyset pagination and composite index order matter. https://use-the-index-luke.com/\n- **django-cte** — GitHub · *Advanced* · CTEs (including recursive) as QuerySets. https://github.com/dimagi/django-cte\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 39.*\n", "40-design-rounds": "# 40 · Django Design Rounds: Building Real Systems\n\n> **In one line:** A Django design round is judged on whether your models, transactions and background jobs keep their promises under concurrency and failure, not on how many boxes you draw. This chapter works through five full rounds (checkout without overselling, multi-tenant SaaS, a notification fan-out pipeline, a rate-limited public API and a URL shortener) with real model, serializer, view and Celery code, and says where each design breaks.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Overview\n\n> **Builds on:** [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency), [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers), [Ch 23 · DRF Authentication, Permissions & Throttling](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling), [Ch 29 · Caching in Django](topic.html?p=29-caching), [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks), [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases).\n\nGeneric system design interviews ask you to draw load balancers and pick databases. A **Django design round** is different. The interviewer already knows you'll use Django, PostgreSQL, Redis and Celery. What they want to see is whether you can turn a product requirement into *specific* Django code that stays correct when two requests arrive in the same millisecond, when a worker dies halfway through a task, or when one tenant's query could leak into another's response. A typical 45–60 minute round runs like this:\n\n1. **Clarify** (5 min): users, read/write ratio, peak numbers, and *what must never happen*: an oversell, a double charge, a data leak.\n2. **Model** (10 min): models with constraints and indexes derived from the queries.\n3. **API** (5–10 min): DRF endpoints, serializers, error codes.\n4. **Correctness** (10–15 min): transactions, locks, idempotency, `on_commit`. This is where most of the scoring happens.\n5. **Async work** (5 min): which Celery tasks there are, what happens on retry, and how each task stays idempotent.\n6. **Scale and failure** (10 min): caching, replicas, hot rows, queue backlogs, and what happens when Redis or the payment provider is down.\n\nThe naive approach fails in a predictable way. The candidate designs a clean model, writes `if product.stock >= qty: product.stock -= qty; product.save()`, enqueues `send_email.delay(order.id)` inside the transaction, and caches with `locmem`. Every one of those lines is a production incident: a lost update, a task that runs before the row is committed, and a cache that isn't shared between workers. The rounds below are written to show the correct version of each move and to explain *why* it's correct.\n\n> **Interview tip:** Say the invariant out loud before writing code: \"stock never goes below zero, and an order is charged at most once.\" Then point at the line of code that enforces each invariant. Interviewers score that more than diagrams.\n\n## 2. Core Concepts\n\nThe toolkit every round reuses. Each item has a home chapter. Here it is in one line, with the reason it matters.\n\n- **Invariant**: a property that must hold no matter how requests interleave (stock ≥ 0, one charge per order). *Why it matters:* you design the locking around invariants, not around endpoints.\n- **`transaction.atomic()`**: all-or-nothing writes; nested blocks become savepoints. *Why:* partial writes are how inventory and money drift.\n- **`select_for_update()`**: row locks held until commit; `skip_locked` for work queues, `nowait` to fail fast, `of=` to lock only some tables. *Why:* it serialises read-modify-write on the same rows.\n- **Conditional `update()` with `F()`**: `filter(stock__gte=n).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - n)` puts the check and the write in one statement. *Why:* it's the cheapest correct way to decrement a counter.\n- **Idempotency key**: a client-supplied key stored under a `UniqueConstraint`, so a retried POST returns the original result. *Why:* mobile clients and load balancers retry.\n- **`transaction.on_commit()`**: runs a callback only after the outermost commit. *Why:* a Celery task enqueued inside the transaction can run before the row is visible, or run for a row that was rolled back.\n- **Transactional outbox**: write an event row in the same transaction as the domain change, and have a relay publish it later. *Why:* `on_commit` callbacks are lost if the process dies right after commit, while an outbox row is durable.\n- **Idempotent task**: a task that can run twice with the same final effect, enforced by a status guard or a unique constraint. *Why:* with `acks_late=True`, Celery delivers at least once.\n- **Tenant scoping**: every query filtered by the tenant, ideally enforced in one place (a manager, or PostgreSQL row-level security). *Why:* a single forgotten `.filter(tenant=...)` is a data breach.\n- **Shared rate limiting**: counters in Redis, updated atomically with Lua, rather than per-process `locmem`. *Why:* DRF throttles that use the default per-process cache let each worker grant the full quota.\n- **Back-of-envelope numbers**: 1M requests/day is about 12 requests/second on average, and peaks run 5–10× the average. A PostgreSQL primary on decent hardware handles thousands of simple indexed writes per second. One Gunicorn sync worker handles about 1 / (mean latency) requests per second. *Why:* the numbers decide whether you need caching or queues at all.\n\n## 3. Theory & Principles\n\n### Round 1: E-commerce checkout without overselling\n\n**Requirements.** Logged-in users check out a cart of up to 20 products. Normal load is about 50 checkouts per minute. A flash sale peaks around 3,000 per minute on a few hot products. The service must never oversell, never charge twice, and must release stock if payment fails or is abandoned within 15 minutes. Payment uses an external provider (Stripe-like) that supports idempotency keys.\n\n**The two invariants.** (1) `Product.stock` never goes negative, and units sold never exceed units stocked. (2) Each order produces at most one successful charge.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 290\" width=\"100%\" height=\"290\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj40a\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n      <marker id=\"dj40c\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#7c3aed\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Checkout: one transaction for stock + order, payment after commit</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"40\" width=\"120\" height=\"56\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"62\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Client</text>\n  <text x=\"80\" y=\"80\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">Idempotency-Key: k1</text>\n  <path d=\"M140,68 L178,68\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj40a)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"180\" y=\"40\" width=\"680\" height=\"232\" rx=\"10\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"520\" y=\"60\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">CheckoutView.post: inside transaction.atomic()</text>\n  <rect x=\"196\" y=\"72\" width=\"150\" height=\"74\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"271\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">1. INSERT order</text>\n  <text x=\"271\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">UNIQUE(user, idem_key)</text>\n  <text x=\"271\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">duplicate: IntegrityError</text>\n  <text x=\"271\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">return original order</text>\n  <path d=\"M346,108 L370,108\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj40c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"372\" y=\"72\" width=\"160\" height=\"74\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"452\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">2. SELECT ... FOR UPDATE</text>\n  <text x=\"452\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">products ORDER BY id</text>\n  <text x=\"452\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">same lock order in every</text>\n  <text x=\"452\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">request: no deadlock cycle</text>\n  <path d=\"M532,108 L556,108\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj40c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"558\" y=\"72\" width=\"140\" height=\"74\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"628\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">3. check stock</text>\n  <text x=\"628\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">short: raise OutOfStock</text>\n  <text x=\"628\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">whole block rolls back</text>\n  <text x=\"628\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">409 to client</text>\n  <path d=\"M698,108 L722,108\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj40c)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"724\" y=\"72\" width=\"124\" height=\"74\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"786\" y=\"92\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">4. write</text>\n  <text x=\"786\" y=\"110\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">lines + stock</text>\n  <text x=\"786\" y=\"126\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">status=AWAITING</text>\n  <text x=\"786\" y=\"140\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">_PAYMENT</text>\n  <rect x=\"196\" y=\"162\" width=\"652\" height=\"96\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"522\" y=\"182\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">5. transaction.on_commit(lambda: charge_order.delay(order.id))</text>\n  <text x=\"522\" y=\"202\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">registered now, runs only after COMMIT; discarded on rollback</text>\n  <text x=\"522\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">locks are released at COMMIT, so hold them for milliseconds and never across the payment HTTP call</text>\n  <text x=\"522\" y=\"242\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">concurrent buyer of the same product waits at step 2, then re-reads the committed stock</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n**Models.**\n\n```python\n# shop/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.db.models import Q\n\n\nclass Product(models.Model):\n    sku = models.CharField(max_length=40, unique=True)\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2)\n    stock = models.PositiveIntegerField()        # PostgreSQL CHECK (stock >= 0) is created for you\n    is_active = models.BooleanField(default=True)\n\n\nclass Order(models.Model):\n    class Status(models.TextChoices):\n        AWAITING_PAYMENT = \"awaiting_payment\"\n        PAID = \"paid\"\n        CANCELLED = \"cancelled\"\n        EXPIRED = \"expired\"\n\n    user = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"orders\")\n    idempotency_key = models.CharField(max_length=64)\n    request_hash = models.CharField(max_length=64)  # sha256 of the normalised payload\n    status = models.CharField(max_length=20, choices=Status.choices, default=Status.AWAITING_PAYMENT)\n    total = models.DecimalField(max_digits=12, decimal_places=2)\n    payment_ref = models.CharField(max_length=100, blank=True)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [\n            models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"user\", \"idempotency_key\"], name=\"uniq_order_idem_key\"),\n        ]\n        indexes = [\n            models.Index(fields=[\"created_at\"], name=\"order_awaiting_idx\",\n                         condition=Q(status=\"awaiting_payment\")),\n        ]\n\n\nclass OrderLine(models.Model):\n    order = models.ForeignKey(Order, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"lines\")\n    product = models.ForeignKey(Product, on_delete=models.PROTECT, related_name=\"+\")\n    quantity = models.PositiveIntegerField()\n    unit_price = models.DecimalField(max_digits=10, decimal_places=2)  # price snapshot\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"order\", \"product\"], name=\"uniq_line_product\")]\n```\n\nThe model decisions follow from the invariants. `PositiveIntegerField` gives a database CHECK as a backstop for stock. `UNIQUE(user, idempotency_key)` makes duplicate submission a database error rather than a race. `unit_price` snapshots the price so later price changes don't rewrite history. The partial index serves the expiry sweep without indexing the paid orders, which are the vast majority.\n\n**Service layer (the part that's scored).**\n\n```python\n# shop/services.py\nimport hashlib\nimport json\nfrom decimal import Decimal\n\nfrom django.db import IntegrityError, transaction\n\nfrom .models import Order, OrderLine, Product\nfrom .tasks import charge_order\n\n\nclass OutOfStock(Exception):\n    def __init__(self, product_ids):\n        self.product_ids = product_ids\n\n\nclass IdempotencyConflict(Exception):\n    \"\"\"Same key reused with a different payload.\"\"\"\n\n\ndef _hash(items) -> str:\n    canonical = json.dumps(sorted((i[\"product_id\"], i[\"quantity\"]) for i in items))\n    return hashlib.sha256(canonical.encode()).hexdigest()\n\n\ndef place_order(user, key: str, items: list[dict]) -> tuple[Order, bool]:\n    \"\"\"Returns (order, created). Safe under concurrent duplicates and concurrent buyers.\"\"\"\n    req_hash = _hash(items)\n    try:\n        with transaction.atomic():\n            order = Order.objects.create(user=user, idempotency_key=key,\n                                         request_hash=req_hash, total=Decimal(\"0\"))\n            wanted = {i[\"product_id\"]: i[\"quantity\"] for i in items}\n            products = list(\n                Product.objects.select_for_update()\n                .filter(pk__in=wanted, is_active=True)\n                .order_by(\"pk\")                      # consistent lock order: no deadlocks\n            )\n            short = [p.pk for p in products if p.stock < wanted[p.pk]]\n            missing = set(wanted) - {p.pk for p in products}\n            if short or missing:\n                raise OutOfStock(sorted(short) + sorted(missing))   # rolls back the order row too\n\n            lines, total = [], Decimal(\"0\")\n            for p in products:\n                p.stock -= wanted[p.pk]\n                lines.append(OrderLine(order=order, product=p, quantity=wanted[p.pk], unit_price=p.price))\n                total += p.price * wanted[p.pk]\n            Product.objects.bulk_update(products, [\"stock\"])\n            OrderLine.objects.bulk_create(lines)\n            order.total = total\n            order.save(update_fields=[\"total\"])\n            transaction.on_commit(lambda: charge_order.delay(order.pk))\n            return order, True\n    except IntegrityError:\n        # Another request with the same key committed first (our INSERT waited on its unique entry).\n        order = Order.objects.get(user=user, idempotency_key=key)\n        if order.request_hash != req_hash:\n            raise IdempotencyConflict(key)\n        return order, False\n```\n\nWhy this is correct:\n\n- **Duplicate submissions.** Two requests with key `k1` both try the INSERT. The second blocks on the first's uncommitted unique-index entry. When the first commits, the second gets `IntegrityError`. Because the error is raised inside `atomic()`, the block rolls back cleanly, and the `except` outside the block can still query safely. It returns the original order, and the view answers `200` instead of `201`. A different payload under the same key becomes a `422`.\n- **Concurrent buyers.** `select_for_update()` locks the product rows, so a second buyer waits and then reads the *committed* stock. Locking in `pk` order means two carts `{A, B}` and `{B, A}` can't deadlock each other.\n- **Atomic failure.** `OutOfStock` raised inside the block undoes the order INSERT. The client never sees a half-created order.\n- **Task timing.** `on_commit` enqueues the charge only after commit. Under `TestCase` you must use `captureOnCommitCallbacks` to see it fire.\n\n> **Version note:** a lock-free alternative for single-product carts is `Product.objects.filter(pk=p, stock__gte=q).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - q)`, checking that the row count is 1. For multi-product carts, apply the updates in `pk` order inside `atomic()` and raise on the first 0, so the earlier decrements roll back.\n\n**API.**\n\n```python\n# shop/api.py\nfrom rest_framework import serializers, status\nfrom rest_framework.permissions import IsAuthenticated\nfrom rest_framework.response import Response\nfrom rest_framework.views import APIView\n\nfrom .services import IdempotencyConflict, OutOfStock, place_order\n\n\nclass CartItemSerializer(serializers.Serializer):\n    product_id = serializers.IntegerField(min_value=1)\n    quantity = serializers.IntegerField(min_value=1, max_value=10)\n\n\nclass CheckoutSerializer(serializers.Serializer):\n    items = CartItemSerializer(many=True, allow_empty=False, max_length=20)\n\n    def validate_items(self, items):\n        if len({i[\"product_id\"] for i in items}) != len(items):\n            raise serializers.ValidationError(\"Duplicate product in cart.\")\n        return items\n\n\nclass CheckoutView(APIView):\n    permission_classes = [IsAuthenticated]\n    throttle_scope = \"checkout\"\n\n    def post(self, request):\n        key = request.headers.get(\"Idempotency-Key\", \"\")\n        if not 8 <= len(key) <= 64:\n            return Response({\"detail\": \"Idempotency-Key header required.\"}, status=400)\n        ser = CheckoutSerializer(data=request.data)\n        ser.is_valid(raise_exception=True)\n        try:\n            order, created = place_order(request.user, key, ser.validated_data[\"items\"])\n        except OutOfStock as exc:\n            return Response({\"detail\": \"Insufficient stock.\", \"product_ids\": exc.product_ids},\n                            status=status.HTTP_409_CONFLICT)\n        except IdempotencyConflict:\n            return Response({\"detail\": \"Idempotency-Key reused with a different payload.\"},\n                            status=status.HTTP_422_UNPROCESSABLE_ENTITY)\n        body = {\"order_id\": order.pk, \"status\": order.status, \"total\": str(order.total)}\n        return Response(body, status=status.HTTP_201_CREATED if created else status.HTTP_200_OK)\n```\n\n**Celery tasks.** Every task here can run twice without harm.\n\n```python\n# shop/tasks.py\nfrom datetime import timedelta\n\nfrom celery import shared_task\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.db.models import F\nfrom django.utils import timezone\n\nfrom .models import Order, Product\nfrom .payments import PaymentDeclined, ProviderUnavailable, provider\n\n\n@shared_task(bind=True, acks_late=True, autoretry_for=(ProviderUnavailable,),\n             retry_backoff=True, retry_backoff_max=300, retry_jitter=True, max_retries=10)\ndef charge_order(self, order_id: int) -> None:\n    order = Order.objects.get(pk=order_id)\n    if order.status != Order.Status.AWAITING_PAYMENT:\n        return                                         # already paid / cancelled / expired\n    try:\n        ref = provider.charge(customer=order.user_id, amount=order.total,\n                              idempotency_key=f\"order-{order.pk}\")   # provider dedupes retries\n    except PaymentDeclined:\n        cancel_order(order.pk, Order.Status.CANCELLED)\n        return\n    marked = (Order.objects.filter(pk=order.pk, status=Order.Status.AWAITING_PAYMENT)\n              .update(status=Order.Status.PAID, payment_ref=ref))\n    if not marked:\n        provider.refund(ref, idempotency_key=f\"refund-order-{order.pk}\")  # expired meanwhile\n\n\ndef cancel_order(order_id: int, new_status: str) -> bool:\n    with transaction.atomic():\n        order = (Order.objects.select_for_update()\n                 .filter(pk=order_id, status=Order.Status.AWAITING_PAYMENT).first())\n        if order is None:\n            return False                                # someone else already finalised it\n        for line in order.lines.order_by(\"product_id\"):\n            Product.objects.filter(pk=line.product_id).update(stock=F(\"stock\") + line.quantity)\n        order.status = new_status\n        order.save(update_fields=[\"status\"])\n        return True\n\n\n@shared_task\ndef expire_stale_orders(batch: int = 200) -> int:\n    cutoff = timezone.now() - timedelta(minutes=15)\n    ids = list(Order.objects.filter(status=Order.Status.AWAITING_PAYMENT, created_at__lt=cutoff)\n               .values_list(\"pk\", flat=True)[:batch])\n    return sum(cancel_order(pk, Order.Status.EXPIRED) for pk in ids)\n```\n\nThe status guard on every transition (`filter(status=AWAITING_PAYMENT)`) turns the state machine into compare-and-swap. A late \"paid\" result for an order the sweeper already expired is detected because the update affects zero rows, and the charge is refunded rather than silently kept. The provider's idempotency key makes a retried `charge` return the original charge. Without that key, `acks_late` plus a worker crash after the HTTP call would charge the customer twice.\n\n**Caching.** Product pages and prices are cached (`cache_page` or low-level caching with a version bumped on update). The checkout path never reads stock from cache. A cached number is only a hint; the locked row is the truth.\n\n**Scaling path and failure modes.**\n\n- **Hot row in a flash sale.** 3,000 checkouts per minute on one product serialise on one row lock. With about 5 ms of lock time per transaction that's still within budget, but lock time grows with transaction length. Keep the transaction short and do no HTTP inside it. Past that point, put a Redis gate in front of the database (an atomic `DECRBY` in a Lua script that refuses to go below zero) so that only requests holding a reservation reach PostgreSQL. Or split the inventory into N bucket rows (`ProductStockShard(product, shard, stock)`) and decrement a random shard that has stock.\n- **Connections.** Waiting on locks holds connections. Use pooling (the Django 5.1+ `OPTIONS[\"pool\"]` setting or PgBouncer in transaction mode) and set `lock_timeout` so a pile-up fails fast as `409`/`503` rather than exhausting workers.\n- **Broker down.** `on_commit` → `.delay()` raises after the order committed. The order stays `AWAITING_PAYMENT`, so add a beat task that re-enqueues `charge_order` for awaiting orders older than a minute (the task is idempotent), or move to an outbox (Round 3).\n- **Provider down.** `autoretry_for` with backoff, and the sweeper expires anything left too long. The UI shows \"payment pending\".\n\n## 4. Architecture & Workflow\n\n### Round 2: Multi-tenant SaaS (project management)\n\n**Requirements.** B2B project tracker with 5,000 tenant organisations. Most are small; the 20 largest have 10k users. Users can belong to several tenants. URLs look like `acme.tracker.app`. A tenant must never see another tenant's data. Enterprise customers may later demand physical isolation. Peak load is about 2,000 requests per second, and 90% are reads.\n\n**Isolation options (the first question the interviewer asks).**\n\n| Model | How | Isolation | Ops cost | Fits |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Shared schema, `tenant_id` column | FK on every table + scoped manager (+ RLS) | Logical | Lowest: one schema, one migration | Thousands of small tenants (our case) |\n| Schema per tenant | PostgreSQL `search_path` per request (e.g. django-tenants) | Stronger | Migrations × N schemas; catalog bloat past a few thousand | Hundreds of mid-size tenants |\n| Database per tenant | DB router picks the alias per request | Physical | Highest: N databases, pools, backups | A few enterprise tenants, compliance |\n\nThe answer here is a shared schema with defence in depth, plus a router-based escape hatch that can move an enterprise tenant to its own database later.\n\n**Models and the scoped manager.**\n\n```python\n# tenancy/context.py\nfrom contextlib import contextmanager\nfrom contextvars import ContextVar\n\n_current_tenant: ContextVar[\"Tenant | None\"] = ContextVar(\"current_tenant\", default=None)\n\n\nclass NoTenant(RuntimeError):\n    pass\n\n\ndef get_current_tenant():\n    tenant = _current_tenant.get()\n    if tenant is None:\n        raise NoTenant(\"Tenant-scoped query without a tenant in context.\")\n    return tenant\n\n\n@contextmanager\ndef tenant_context(tenant):\n    token = _current_tenant.set(tenant)\n    try:\n        yield tenant\n    finally:\n        _current_tenant.reset(token)\n```\n\n```python\n# tenancy/models.py\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\n\nfrom .context import get_current_tenant\n\n\nclass Tenant(models.Model):\n    slug = models.SlugField(unique=True)\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    plan = models.CharField(max_length=20, default=\"team\")\n    db_alias = models.CharField(max_length=40, default=\"default\")\n\n\nclass Membership(models.Model):\n    class Role(models.TextChoices):\n        OWNER = \"owner\"\n        ADMIN = \"admin\"\n        MEMBER = \"member\"\n\n    tenant = models.ForeignKey(Tenant, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"memberships\")\n    user = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"memberships\")\n    role = models.CharField(max_length=10, choices=Role.choices, default=Role.MEMBER)\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"tenant\", \"user\"], name=\"uniq_membership\")]\n\n\nclass TenantQuerySet(models.QuerySet):\n    pass\n\n\nclass TenantManager(models.Manager.from_queryset(TenantQuerySet)):\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return super().get_queryset().filter(tenant=get_current_tenant())\n\n\nclass TenantScoped(models.Model):\n    tenant = models.ForeignKey(Tenant, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"+\", editable=False)\n\n    objects = TenantManager()            # default manager: scoped, fails closed\n    unscoped = models.Manager()          # explicit, grep-able escape hatch for admin/ops\n\n    class Meta:\n        abstract = True\n\n    def save(self, *args, **kwargs):\n        if self.tenant_id is None:\n            self.tenant = get_current_tenant()\n        elif self.tenant_id != get_current_tenant().pk:\n            raise PermissionError(\"Cross-tenant write.\")\n        super().save(*args, **kwargs)\n\n\nclass Project(TenantScoped):\n    slug = models.SlugField()\n    name = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    archived = models.BooleanField(default=False)\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"tenant\", \"slug\"], name=\"uniq_project_slug\")]\n        indexes = [models.Index(fields=[\"tenant\", \"archived\", \"name\"])]\n```\n\nThe `tenant` column leads every composite index and every unique constraint. Every query starts with `tenant_id = ?`, and two tenants can both have a project called `roadmap`. Related managers (`tenant.projects`, `project.tasks`) are built from the related model's default manager, so they're scoped too. Django uses `_base_manager` (a plain `Manager`) for forward FK access like `task.project`, and that path is safe because the FK came from a row that was already scoped.\n\n**Middleware, permission and view.**\n\n```python\n# tenancy/middleware.py\nfrom django.core.cache import cache\nfrom django.http import Http404\n\nfrom .context import tenant_context\nfrom .models import Tenant\n\n\nclass TenantMiddleware:\n    def __init__(self, get_response):\n        self.get_response = get_response\n\n    def __call__(self, request):\n        slug = request.get_host().split(\":\")[0].split(\".\")[0]\n        tenant = cache.get(f\"tenant:{slug}\")\n        if tenant is None:\n            tenant = Tenant.objects.filter(slug=slug).first()\n            if tenant is None:\n                raise Http404(\"Unknown tenant\")\n            cache.set(f\"tenant:{slug}\", tenant, 300)\n        request.tenant = tenant\n        with tenant_context(tenant):\n            return self.get_response(request)\n```\n\n```python\n# tenancy/permissions.py\nfrom rest_framework.permissions import BasePermission\n\nfrom .models import Membership\n\n\nclass IsTenantMember(BasePermission):\n    def has_permission(self, request, view):\n        if not (request.user and request.user.is_authenticated):\n            return False\n        role = (Membership.objects.filter(tenant=request.tenant, user=request.user)\n                .values_list(\"role\", flat=True).first())\n        request.tenant_role = role\n        return role is not None\n```\n\n```python\n# projects/api.py\nfrom rest_framework import serializers, viewsets\n\nfrom tenancy.permissions import IsTenantMember\nfrom .models import Project\n\n\nclass ProjectSerializer(serializers.ModelSerializer):\n    class Meta:\n        model = Project\n        fields = [\"id\", \"slug\", \"name\", \"archived\"]\n\n\nclass ProjectViewSet(viewsets.ModelViewSet):\n    serializer_class = ProjectSerializer\n    permission_classes = [IsTenantMember]\n\n    def get_queryset(self):\n        return Project.objects.filter(archived=False).order_by(\"name\")\n```\n\n`ModelSerializer` would normally add a `UniqueTogetherValidator` for `(tenant, slug)`, but `tenant` isn't a serializer field. So add a `validate_slug()` that checks `Project.objects.filter(slug=value).exists()` against the scoped manager (excluding `self.instance` on update), and let the database constraint catch the race between two simultaneous creates (`IntegrityError` → 409 in a custom exception handler). Because `get_queryset` uses the scoped manager, `get_object()` on `/projects/<id>/` returns 404 for another tenant's id. That's the correct response: never 403, which would confirm the id exists.\n\n**Celery and tenant context.** A task has no request, so it receives `tenant_id`, loads the tenant and wraps its body in `with tenant_context(tenant):`. The scoped manager then works unchanged, and a task that forgets to do this fails with `NoTenant` instead of leaking data.\n\n**Row-level security backstop.** A `RunSQL` migration runs `ALTER TABLE projects_project ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY`, then `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` so the table owner is filtered too, and `CREATE POLICY tenant_isolation ON projects_project USING (tenant_id = current_setting('app.tenant_id')::bigint)`. With `ATOMIC_REQUESTS=True`, a small middleware placed after `TenantMiddleware` runs `SET LOCAL app.tenant_id = %s` through `connection.cursor()` at the start of each request. `SET LOCAL` is scoped to the transaction, which makes it safe with PgBouncer in transaction mode. A plain `SET` would leak the tenant to the next client of a pooled connection. If `app.tenant_id` was never set, `current_setting` raises an error, so a missing tenant fails closed. Ops and migration roles use a role with `BYPASSRLS`.\n\n**Caching and scaling.** Every cache key is built through a helper `tkey(name) -> f\"t{tenant.pk}:{name}\"`. Reads go to a replica via a router for list endpoints that tolerate a second of lag. Read-your-writes views pin to the primary for a few seconds after a write (a session flag or cookie). The 20 big tenants dominate load, so per-tenant throttling (`ScopedRateThrottle` keyed by tenant, on a Redis cache) stops one tenant's batch job from starving everyone else. That's the noisy-neighbour problem.\n\n**Failure modes.** The worst bug is a `NoTenant` check that someone turns into a silent no-op, so the manager returns unscoped data. Write a test that runs every endpoint as tenant A against data seeded for tenant B and asserts 404 or empty lists. Run it in CI for every new viewset.\n\n## 5. Implementation\n\n### Round 3: Notifications and fan-out pipeline\n\n**Requirements.** A social learning platform has 8M users. Events include \"someone replied to your thread\" (one recipient), \"a course you follow published a lesson\" (up to 500k recipients), and transactional mail like password resets and receipts. Channels are in-app (persisted, with a websocket push to online users), email and mobile push. Users set per-category, per-channel preferences. Each notification is delivered at most once per channel. Transactional mail must arrive within seconds even while a 500k fan-out is running. Volume is about 30M notifications per day, roughly 350 per second on average and several thousand per second during fan-out bursts.\n\n```svg\n<svg viewBox=\"0 0 880 296\" width=\"100%\" height=\"296\" font-family=\"ui-sans-serif,system-ui,sans-serif\" font-size=\"11\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\">\n  <defs>\n    <marker id=\"dj40f\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#2563eb\"/></marker>\n    <marker id=\"dj40g\" markerWidth=\"9\" markerHeight=\"9\" refX=\"7\" refY=\"3\" orient=\"auto\"><path d=\"M0,0 L7,3 L0,6 Z\" fill=\"#16a34a\"/></marker>\n  </defs>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"22\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\">Notification pipeline: outbox, chunked fan-out, idempotent per-channel delivery</text>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"44\" width=\"190\" height=\"90\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"115\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Domain transaction</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"84\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">INSERT lesson</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">INSERT outbox_event</text>\n  <text x=\"32\" y=\"116\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">COMMIT (both or neither)</text>\n  <path d=\"M210,89 L244,89\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj40f)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"246\" y=\"44\" width=\"190\" height=\"90\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"341\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">relay_outbox (beat, 2 s)</text>\n  <text x=\"258\" y=\"84\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</text>\n  <text x=\"258\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">fan_out.delay(event_id)</text>\n  <text x=\"258\" y=\"116\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">mark published_at</text>\n  <path d=\"M436,89 L470,89\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj40f)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"472\" y=\"44\" width=\"190\" height=\"90\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#ede9fe\" stroke=\"#7c3aed\"/>\n  <text x=\"567\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">fan_out(event)</text>\n  <text x=\"484\" y=\"84\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">stream recipient ids</text>\n  <text x=\"484\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">keyset batches of 1,000</text>\n  <text x=\"484\" y=\"116\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">one task per batch</text>\n  <path d=\"M662,89 L696,89\" stroke=\"#2563eb\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj40f)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"698\" y=\"44\" width=\"162\" height=\"90\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"779\" y=\"64\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">materialise(batch)</text>\n  <text x=\"710\" y=\"84\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">filter preferences</text>\n  <text x=\"710\" y=\"100\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">bulk_create, ignore</text>\n  <text x=\"710\" y=\"116\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"10\">conflicts (unique)</text>\n  <path d=\"M779,134 L779,166\" stroke=\"#16a34a\" stroke-width=\"2\" marker-end=\"url(#dj40g)\"/>\n  <rect x=\"20\" y=\"170\" width=\"840\" height=\"110\" rx=\"8\" fill=\"#f8fafc\" stroke=\"#94a3b8\"/>\n  <text x=\"440\" y=\"190\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-weight=\"bold\">Per-channel delivery: separate queues so bulk never delays transactional</text>\n  <rect x=\"36\" y=\"202\" width=\"190\" height=\"66\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dcfce7\" stroke=\"#16a34a\"/>\n  <text x=\"131\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">in-app (queue: realtime)</text>\n  <text x=\"131\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">row exists = delivered</text>\n  <text x=\"131\" y=\"256\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">group_send(\"user_42\")</text>\n  <rect x=\"244\" y=\"202\" width=\"190\" height=\"66\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#dbeafe\" stroke=\"#2563eb\"/>\n  <text x=\"339\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">email (queue: bulk_email)</text>\n  <text x=\"339\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">claim PENDING to SENDING</text>\n  <text x=\"339\" y=\"256\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">provider key = delivery id</text>\n  <rect x=\"452\" y=\"202\" width=\"190\" height=\"66\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fee2e2\" stroke=\"#dc2626\"/>\n  <text x=\"547\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">transactional (queue: tx)</text>\n  <text x=\"547\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">dedicated workers</text>\n  <text x=\"547\" y=\"256\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">never behind a fan-out</text>\n  <rect x=\"660\" y=\"202\" width=\"186\" height=\"66\" rx=\"6\" fill=\"#fef3c7\" stroke=\"#d97706\"/>\n  <text x=\"753\" y=\"222\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#1e293b\" font-size=\"10\" font-weight=\"bold\">push (queue: push)</text>\n  <text x=\"753\" y=\"240\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">batch per provider API</text>\n  <text x=\"753\" y=\"256\" text-anchor=\"middle\" fill=\"#334155\" font-size=\"9\">prune dead tokens</text>\n</svg>\n```\n\n**Models.**\n\n```python\n# notifications/models.py\nimport uuid\n\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.db.models import Q\n\n\nclass OutboxEvent(models.Model):\n    id = models.UUIDField(primary_key=True, default=uuid.uuid4)\n    topic = models.CharField(max_length=60)              # \"lesson.published\"\n    payload = models.JSONField()\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n    published_at = models.DateTimeField(null=True, blank=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        indexes = [models.Index(fields=[\"created_at\"], name=\"outbox_unpublished\",\n                                condition=Q(published_at__isnull=True))]\n\n\nclass Notification(models.Model):\n    event = models.ForeignKey(OutboxEvent, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"+\")\n    recipient = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE,\n                                  related_name=\"notifications\")\n    category = models.CharField(max_length=40)\n    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)\n    url = models.CharField(max_length=500)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n    read_at = models.DateTimeField(null=True, blank=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"event\", \"recipient\"], name=\"uniq_event_recipient\")]\n        indexes = [\n            models.Index(fields=[\"recipient\", \"-created_at\"], name=\"notif_inbox\"),\n            models.Index(fields=[\"recipient\"], name=\"notif_unread\", condition=Q(read_at__isnull=True)),\n        ]\n\n\nclass Delivery(models.Model):\n    class Status(models.TextChoices):\n        PENDING = \"pending\"\n        SENDING = \"sending\"\n        SENT = \"sent\"\n        FAILED = \"failed\"\n\n    notification = models.ForeignKey(Notification, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"deliveries\")\n    channel = models.CharField(max_length=10)\n    status = models.CharField(max_length=10, choices=Status.choices, default=Status.PENDING)\n    attempts = models.PositiveSmallIntegerField(default=0)\n    claimed_at = models.DateTimeField(null=True, blank=True)\n    provider_id = models.CharField(max_length=100, blank=True)\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"notification\", \"channel\"], name=\"uniq_delivery\")]\n        indexes = [models.Index(fields=[\"claimed_at\"], name=\"delivery_sending\",\n                                condition=Q(status=\"sending\"))]\n```\n\n`Preference(user, category, channel, enabled)` with a unique constraint on the first three fields is stored *sparsely*: only opt-outs are rows, and the default is enabled. A million users don't need a million preference rows to receive their defaults.\n\n**Producing the event (outbox, not `on_commit`).** `publish_lesson()` creates the `Lesson` and an `OutboxEvent(topic=\"lesson.published\", payload={\"lesson_id\": ..., \"course_id\": ...})` inside one `transaction.atomic()` block.\n\nAn `on_commit(lambda: fan_out.delay(...))` would also work and be faster, but if the web process dies between COMMIT and the broker publish, the event is lost. A lost \"new lesson\" notification is a business bug. The outbox row survives the crash, and the relay publishes it within seconds. Combining both is common: a fast path through `on_commit`, with the relay as the safety net. That's safe because downstream is idempotent. The pattern itself is covered in depth in [Messaging · Idempotency & Outbox](../messaging/topic.html?p=21-idempotency-outbox).\n\n**Tasks.**\n\n```python\n# notifications/tasks.py\nfrom itertools import batched\n\nfrom celery import group, shared_task\nfrom django.db import transaction\nfrom django.db.models import F\nfrom django.utils import timezone\n\nfrom courses.models import Follow, Lesson\nfrom .channels import ProviderError, push_in_app, send_email\nfrom .models import Delivery, Notification, OutboxEvent, Preference\n\nBATCH = 1000\n\n\n@shared_task\ndef relay_outbox(limit: int = 500) -> int:\n    with transaction.atomic():\n        events = list(OutboxEvent.objects.select_for_update(skip_locked=True)\n                      .filter(published_at__isnull=True).order_by(\"created_at\")[:limit])\n        for e in events:\n            fan_out.delay(str(e.pk))            # at-least-once: duplicates absorbed downstream\n        OutboxEvent.objects.filter(pk__in=[e.pk for e in events]).update(published_at=timezone.now())\n    return len(events)\n\n\n@shared_task(acks_late=True)\ndef fan_out(event_id: str) -> None:\n    event = OutboxEvent.objects.get(pk=event_id)\n    follower_ids = (Follow.objects.filter(course_id=event.payload[\"course_id\"])\n                    .order_by(\"user_id\").values_list(\"user_id\", flat=True))\n    for chunk in batched(follower_ids.iterator(chunk_size=5000), BATCH):\n        materialise.delay(event_id, list(chunk))\n\n\n@shared_task(acks_late=True, rate_limit=\"200/s\")\ndef materialise(event_id: str, user_ids: list[int]) -> None:\n    event = OutboxEvent.objects.get(pk=event_id)\n    lesson = Lesson.objects.select_related(\"course\").get(pk=event.payload[\"lesson_id\"])\n    opted_out_email = set(Preference.objects.filter(\n        user_id__in=user_ids, category=\"lessons\", channel=\"email\", enabled=False\n    ).values_list(\"user_id\", flat=True))\n\n    Notification.objects.bulk_create(\n        [Notification(event_id=event_id, recipient_id=uid, category=\"lessons\",\n                      title=f\"New lesson in {lesson.course.title}: {lesson.title}\",\n                      url=lesson.get_absolute_url()) for uid in user_ids],\n        ignore_conflicts=True,                  # replayed batch: existing rows are skipped\n    )\n    notifs = list(Notification.objects.filter(event_id=event_id, recipient_id__in=user_ids)\n                  .values_list(\"pk\", \"recipient_id\"))\n    Delivery.objects.bulk_create(\n        [Delivery(notification_id=nid, channel=\"email\")\n         for nid, uid in notifs if uid not in opted_out_email],\n        ignore_conflicts=True,\n    )\n    push_in_app.delay(event_id, user_ids)       # websocket push to online users, best effort\n    pending = Delivery.objects.filter(notification_id__in=[n for n, _ in notifs], channel=\"email\",\n                                      status=Delivery.Status.PENDING).values_list(\"pk\", flat=True)\n    group(deliver_email.s(pk) for pk in pending).apply_async(queue=\"bulk_email\")\n\n\n@shared_task(bind=True, acks_late=True, max_retries=8)\ndef deliver_email(self, delivery_id: int) -> None:\n    claimed = (Delivery.objects\n               .filter(pk=delivery_id, status=Delivery.Status.PENDING)\n               .update(status=Delivery.Status.SENDING, attempts=F(\"attempts\") + 1,\n                       claimed_at=timezone.now()))\n    if not claimed:\n        return                                   # sent, failed, or another worker has it\n    d = Delivery.objects.select_related(\"notification__recipient\").get(pk=delivery_id)\n    try:\n        provider_id = send_email(to=d.notification.recipient.email, subject=d.notification.title,\n                                 url=d.notification.url, idempotency_key=f\"delivery-{d.pk}\")\n    except ProviderError as exc:\n        final = self.request.retries >= self.max_retries\n        Delivery.objects.filter(pk=d.pk).update(\n            status=Delivery.Status.FAILED if final else Delivery.Status.PENDING)\n        if final:\n            return\n        raise self.retry(exc=exc, countdown=min(600, 2 ** self.request.retries * 5))\n    Delivery.objects.filter(pk=d.pk).update(status=Delivery.Status.SENT, provider_id=provider_id)\n```\n\nNotes the interviewer will probe:\n\n- **Claiming without holding locks across I/O.** `deliver_email` claims the row with a compare-and-swap `UPDATE ... WHERE status='pending'` instead of keeping a `select_for_update` lock open during an SMTP or HTTP call. A worker that dies mid-send leaves the row in `SENDING`. A beat sweeper resets `SENDING` rows older than 10 minutes back to `PENDING` (served by the partial index), and the provider's idempotency key prevents a duplicate email when the task re-runs.\n- **Chunking.** `iterator(chunk_size=...)` streams follower ids without loading 500k rows into memory, and `itertools.batched` (Python 3.12) cuts them into task-sized batches. Pass ids, never model instances, as task arguments.\n- **Queue isolation.** `CELERY_TASK_ROUTES = {\"notifications.tasks.deliver_email\": {\"queue\": \"bulk_email\"}, \"accounts.tasks.send_password_reset\": {\"queue\": \"tx\"}}`, with separate worker pools. A 500k fan-out can then never delay a password reset. Use `worker_prefetch_multiplier=1` and `acks_late` for slow tasks so one worker doesn't hoard a large prefetch of messages.\n- **In-app real time.** The `Notification` row *is* the in-app delivery. `push_in_app` does `async_to_sync(channel_layer.group_send)(f\"user_{uid}\", {\"type\": \"notify\", ...})` only for users present in a Redis \"online\" set, and offline users read their inbox on next load ([Ch 32 · Channels](topic.html?p=32-channels-websockets)).\n- **Read API.** `GET /api/notifications/?cursor=...` uses `CursorPagination(ordering=\"-created_at\")` served by the `(recipient, -created_at)` index. The unread badge count is `Notification.objects.filter(recipient=u, read_at__isnull=True).count()` on the partial index, cached for 30 s with explicit invalidation on mark-read.\n\n**Scaling path.** The `Notification` table grows by about 30M rows per day. Partition it by month with raw SQL in migrations (or `django-postgres-extra`), and drop partitions past retention instead of running a giant `DELETE`. When Celery-on-Redis becomes the bottleneck for fan-out bursts, move the event stream to Kafka and keep Celery for per-delivery work ([Messaging · Kafka vs RabbitMQ](../messaging/topic.html?p=03-kafka-vs-rabbitmq)). Celebrity fan-out (a course with millions of followers) eventually switches from write-time fan-out to read-time merging. The inbox query then unions the personal notifications with \"course announcements since your last visit\", which is the classic push-vs-pull trade-off from [System Design · Notification System](../system-design/topic.html?p=39-design-notification).\n\n## 6. Advantages, Disadvantages & Trade-offs\n\n### Round 4: Rate-limited public API\n\n**Requirements.** A public REST API for third-party developers. Organisations create API keys. The Free plan allows 60 requests per minute with bursts up to 20; Pro allows 1,200 per minute with bursts up to 200. The limit must be accurate across 40 Gunicorn pods. Responses carry rate-limit headers, and an exceeded limit returns `429` with `Retry-After`. Usage is metered per key per day for billing. Keys can be rotated and revoked, and revocation takes effect within a minute. Peak traffic is about 15k requests per second.\n\n**Why DRF's built-in throttles aren't enough.** `UserRateThrottle` and `ScopedRateThrottle` keep a list of timestamps per key in the Django cache, using a read-modify-write that isn't atomic. Two pods reading the same history at the same time can both allow the request. If the cache is `locmem`, every process keeps its own counters, so 40 pods × 4 workers each grant the full quota. Those throttles are fine for abuse protection on an internal API. A limit that customers pay for needs one atomic counter in shared storage.\n\n**Models.**\n\n```python\n# api_keys/models.py\nimport hashlib\nimport hmac\nimport secrets\n\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import models\nfrom django.utils import timezone\n\n\ndef _digest(secret: str) -> str:\n    return hmac.new(settings.API_KEY_PEPPER.encode(), secret.encode(), hashlib.sha256).hexdigest()\n\n\nclass ApiKey(models.Model):\n    class Plan(models.TextChoices):\n        FREE = \"free\"\n        PRO = \"pro\"\n\n    organization = models.ForeignKey(\"orgs.Organization\", on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"api_keys\")\n    service_user = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"+\")\n    prefix = models.CharField(max_length=12, unique=True)       # shown in dashboards and logs\n    digest = models.CharField(max_length=64)                    # HMAC-SHA256 of the secret part\n    plan = models.CharField(max_length=10, choices=Plan.choices, default=Plan.FREE)\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n    revoked_at = models.DateTimeField(null=True, blank=True)\n    last_used_at = models.DateTimeField(null=True, blank=True)\n\n    @classmethod\n    def issue(cls, organization, service_user, plan=\"free\") -> tuple[\"ApiKey\", str]:\n        prefix, secret = secrets.token_hex(6), secrets.token_urlsafe(32)\n        key = cls.objects.create(organization=organization, service_user=service_user,\n                                 prefix=prefix, digest=_digest(secret), plan=plan)\n        return key, f\"sk_{prefix}_{secret}\"                      # shown once, never stored\n\n    def verify(self, secret: str) -> bool:\n        return self.revoked_at is None and hmac.compare_digest(self.digest, _digest(secret))\n\n\nclass UsageDaily(models.Model):\n    api_key = models.ForeignKey(ApiKey, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"usage\")\n    day = models.DateField()\n    requests = models.PositiveBigIntegerField()\n\n    class Meta:\n        constraints = [models.UniqueConstraint(fields=[\"api_key\", \"day\"], name=\"uniq_usage_day\")]\n```\n\nA fast HMAC is the right choice here, not Argon2. API secrets are 256-bit random values, so slow hashing adds nothing against brute force, and at 15k requests per second you can't spend 100 ms per request hashing. The prefix allows an indexed lookup and lets users identify a leaked key without the secret ever being logged.\n\n**Authentication.**\n\n```python\n# api_keys/auth.py\nfrom django.core.cache import cache\nfrom rest_framework import authentication, exceptions\n\nfrom .models import ApiKey\n\n\nclass ApiKeyAuthentication(authentication.BaseAuthentication):\n    keyword = \"Bearer\"\n\n    def authenticate(self, request):\n        header = authentication.get_authorization_header(request).decode()\n        if not header.startswith(f\"{self.keyword} sk_\"):\n            return None                                     # let other authenticators try\n        try:\n            _, prefix, secret = header.split(\" \", 1)[1].split(\"_\", 2)\n        except ValueError:\n            raise exceptions.AuthenticationFailed(\"Malformed API key.\")\n        key = cache.get(f\"apikey:{prefix}\")\n        if key is None:\n            key = ApiKey.objects.select_related(\"service_user\").filter(prefix=prefix).first()\n            if key is None:\n                raise exceptions.AuthenticationFailed(\"Invalid API key.\")\n            cache.set(f\"apikey:{prefix}\", key, 60)          # revocation visible within 60 s\n        if not key.verify(secret):\n            raise exceptions.AuthenticationFailed(\"Invalid API key.\")\n        return key.service_user, key                        # request.user, request.auth\n\n    def authenticate_header(self, request):\n        return self.keyword                                 # makes DRF answer 401 (not 403)\n```\n\nRevocation deletes the cache entry immediately (`cache.delete(f\"apikey:{prefix}\")` in `revoke()`). The 60 s TTL is only the bound for a missed invalidation. `last_used_at` isn't written on every request, since that would mean 15k UPDATEs per second. It's derived from the usage counters instead.\n\n**Token bucket in Redis (atomic Lua).**\n\n```python\n# api_keys/ratelimit.py\nfrom dataclasses import dataclass\n\nimport redis\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.utils import timezone\n\nr = redis.Redis.from_url(settings.RATELIMIT_REDIS_URL, socket_timeout=0.05)\n\nTOKEN_BUCKET = r.register_script(\"\"\"\nlocal cap   = tonumber(ARGV[1])\nlocal rate  = tonumber(ARGV[2])          -- tokens per second\nlocal cost  = tonumber(ARGV[3])\nlocal t     = redis.call('TIME')\nlocal now   = tonumber(t[1]) * 1000 + math.floor(tonumber(t[2]) / 1000)\nlocal b     = redis.call('HMGET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', 'ts')\nlocal tokens = tonumber(b[1]) or cap\nlocal ts     = tonumber(b[2]) or now\ntokens = math.min(cap, tokens + (now - ts) / 1000 * rate)\nlocal allowed, retry_ms = 0, 0\nif tokens >= cost then\n  tokens = tokens - cost\n  allowed = 1\nelse\n  retry_ms = math.ceil((cost - tokens) / rate * 1000)\nend\nredis.call('HSET', KEYS[1], 'tokens', tostring(tokens), 'ts', now)\nredis.call('PEXPIRE', KEYS[1], math.ceil(cap / rate * 1000) + 1000)\nreturn {allowed, math.floor(tokens), retry_ms}\n\"\"\")\n\n\n@dataclass(frozen=True)\nclass Plan:\n    burst: int\n    per_second: float\n\n\nPLANS = {\"free\": Plan(burst=20, per_second=1.0), \"pro\": Plan(burst=200, per_second=20.0)}\n\n\ndef take(prefix: str, plan: str, cost: int = 1) -> tuple[bool, int, float]:\n    p = PLANS[plan]\n    allowed, remaining, retry_ms = TOKEN_BUCKET(keys=[f\"rl:{{{prefix}}}\"], args=[p.burst, p.per_second, cost])\n    return bool(allowed), int(remaining), retry_ms / 1000\n\n\ndef meter_usage(api_key_id: int) -> None:\n    day_key = f\"usage:{timezone.localdate().isoformat()}\"\n    pipe = r.pipeline(transaction=False)\n    pipe.hincrby(day_key, api_key_id, 1)\n    pipe.expire(day_key, 3 * 86400)\n    try:\n        pipe.execute()\n    except redis.RedisError:\n        pass                                  # metering is best effort; never fail the request\n```\n\nThe script reads Redis's own clock (`TIME`) so that clock skew between pods can't distort the refill. It's a single round trip and runs atomically on the shard that owns the key. The `{prefix}` hash tag keeps any future per-key companion keys on the same Redis Cluster slot. Tokens are stored as strings so fractional refill survives, because Lua numbers returned to Redis are truncated to integers.\n\n**DRF throttle and headers.**\n\n```python\n# api_keys/throttling.py\nimport logging\n\nimport redis\nfrom rest_framework.throttling import BaseThrottle\n\nfrom .ratelimit import take\n\nlog = logging.getLogger(__name__)\n\n\nclass PlanThrottle(BaseThrottle):\n    def allow_request(self, request, view):\n        key = request.auth\n        if key is None:\n            return True                       # anonymous traffic handled by AnonRateThrottle\n        try:\n            allowed, remaining, wait = take(key.prefix, key.plan)\n        except redis.RedisError:\n            log.warning(\"ratelimit backend down; failing open\", extra={\"prefix\": key.prefix})\n            return True                       # availability over strictness (a business decision)\n        request.ratelimit = {\"remaining\": remaining}\n        self._wait = wait\n        return allowed\n\n    def wait(self):\n        return self._wait\n```\n\n```python\n# api/views.py\nfrom rest_framework.views import APIView\n\nfrom api_keys.auth import ApiKeyAuthentication\nfrom api_keys.ratelimit import meter_usage\nfrom api_keys.throttling import PlanThrottle\n\n\nclass PublicAPIView(APIView):\n    authentication_classes = [ApiKeyAuthentication]\n    throttle_classes = [PlanThrottle]\n\n    def finalize_response(self, request, response, *args, **kwargs):\n        response = super().finalize_response(request, response, *args, **kwargs)\n        info = getattr(request, \"ratelimit\", None)\n        if info is not None:\n            response[\"RateLimit-Remaining\"] = str(info[\"remaining\"])\n        if request.auth is not None and response.status_code != 429:\n            meter_usage(request.auth.pk)      # one pipelined HINCRBY; rejected calls aren't billed\n        return response\n```\n\nWhen `allow_request` returns `False`, DRF calls `throttled()`, which raises `Throttled(wait)`. The default exception handler turns that into a `429` with a `Retry-After` header. Usage metering does `HINCRBY usage:2026-10-25 <key_id> 1` with a 3-day TTL. A beat task every 5 minutes reads the hash with `HGETALL` and upserts *absolute* daily totals with `UsageDaily.objects.bulk_create(rows, update_conflicts=True, unique_fields=[\"api_key\", \"day\"], update_fields=[\"requests\"])`. Because Redis holds the running total for the day, the flush is idempotent. Replaying it writes the same numbers, which isn't true if you flush deltas. A final flush shortly after midnight closes yesterday's numbers for billing.\n\n**Trade-offs of the decisions across rounds.**\n\n| Decision | Option A | Option B | Choose A when | Choose B when |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Stock decrement | `select_for_update` + check | conditional `update(F())` | multi-row business rules, need to read first | single counter, highest throughput |\n| Dedupe a POST | `UNIQUE(user, idem_key)` | Redis `SET NX` with TTL | the result is a DB row (orders) | non-DB side effects, short windows |\n| Trigger async work | `on_commit(task.delay)` | transactional outbox | losing a rare event is tolerable | every event must be delivered |\n| Tenant isolation | scoped manager | + PostgreSQL RLS | small team, strong tests | compliance, raw SQL in the codebase |\n| Rate limit algorithm | token bucket (Lua) | fixed-window `INCR` + `EXPIRE` | bursts allowed, smooth limits | simplicity; 2× boundary burst acceptable |\n| Limiter outage | fail open | fail closed | paid API, availability first | security-sensitive (login, OTP) |\n| Fan-out | on write (materialise rows) | on read (merge at query time) | bounded audiences | celebrity audiences in the millions |\n\n### When to use these patterns\n\n- Locks, idempotency keys and outboxes: whenever money, inventory or user-visible messages are involved.\n- Scoped managers and RLS: from day one of any B2B product. Retrofitting tenancy costs far more than starting with it.\n- Redis-backed limiters: whenever limits are part of a contract, or your pods number more than one.\n\n### When NOT to use them\n\n- A personal blog or an internal admin tool doesn't need an outbox, sharded counters or RLS. Plain `atomic()` and `on_commit` are enough.\n- Don't reach for schema-per-tenant or database-per-tenant without a compliance or noisy-neighbour reason. It multiplies migration time and operational surface.\n- Don't build a custom limiter if API-gateway rate limiting (Kong, Envoy, a cloud gateway) already enforces the contract. Keep a DRF throttle only as a second line.\n\n## 7. Common Mistakes & Best Practices\n\n### Round 5 (optional): URL shortener with click analytics\n\n**Requirements.** 50M links. 100M redirects per day, about 1,200 per second on average with 10k per second at peak. Redirect p99 latency under 20 ms. Per-link click counts and daily charts. Custom aliases for paid users. Links can expire.\n\n```python\n# links/models.py\nimport secrets\nimport string\n\nfrom django.conf import settings\nfrom django.db import IntegrityError, models, transaction\n\nALPHABET = string.ascii_letters + string.digits          # base62\n\n\nclass Link(models.Model):\n    code = models.CharField(max_length=16, unique=True)\n    target_url = models.URLField(max_length=2048)\n    owner = models.ForeignKey(settings.AUTH_USER_MODEL, on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name=\"links\")\n    created_at = models.DateTimeField(auto_now_add=True)\n    expires_at = models.DateTimeField(null=True, blank=True)\n    clicks = models.PositiveBigIntegerField(default=0)\n\n    @classmethod\n    def shorten(cls, owner, url: str, alias: str | None = None) -> \"Link\":\n        for _ in range(5):\n            code = alias or \"\".join(secrets.choice(ALPHABET) for _ in range(7))\n            try:\n                with transaction.atomic():               # savepoint: a collision doesn't poison an outer txn\n                    return cls.objects.create(owner=owner, target_url=url, code=code)\n            except IntegrityError:\n                if alias:\n                    raise\n        raise RuntimeError(\"Could not allocate a short code\")\n```\n\nSeven random base62 characters give about 3.5 × 10¹² codes, so collisions at 50M links are rare and a retry handles them. Codes derived from a sequential id are guessable and enumerable, which leaks how many links exist and lets anyone crawl them.\n\nThe redirect view reads `cache.get(f\"link:{code}\")`, falls back to `Link.objects.filter(code=code).values(\"target_url\", \"expires_at\").first()`, caches the result for an hour, and caches misses for 60 s (a negative cache, so scanners can't hammer the database). It raises `Http404` for missing or expired links, calls `record_click()` and returns `HttpResponseRedirect(target_url)`.\n\nUse `302`, or `307` to preserve the method. A `301` is cached by browsers, so repeat clicks never reach you and analytics go blind. `record_click` does `INCR clicks:{code}` and `XADD clicks * code ... ts ... ref ...` on a Redis stream. A beat task drains the counters atomically with `GETDEL` and applies `Link.objects.filter(code=c).update(clicks=F(\"clicks\") + n)`. A stream consumer batches events into a monthly-partitioned `ClickEvent` table or a column store for the charts. Validate `target_url` against a scheme allow-list (`http`/`https` only, no `javascript:`) and against a malware-domain list, or the shortener becomes a phishing tool. The redirect view runs with no session middleware and no database query on a cache hit, which is how it stays under 20 ms.\n\n### Mistakes candidates make in Django design rounds\n\n**1. Read-modify-write on counters.** People write `p.stock -= 1; p.save()`. Two requests read the same value and one decrement is lost. Use `update(stock=F(\"stock\") - 1)` with a guard, or lock with `select_for_update()`.\n\n**2. Enqueuing tasks inside the transaction.** People write `order.save(); send_receipt.delay(order.id)` inside `atomic()`. The worker can run before the commit and get `DoesNotExist`, or run for an order that was later rolled back. Use `transaction.on_commit(lambda: send_receipt.delay(order.id))`, or an outbox.\n\n**3. Holding locks across network calls.** People call `select_for_update()` and then the payment provider inside the same transaction. The lock is held for the length of the HTTP call, typically 300 ms to 30 s, and every concurrent buyer queues behind it. Commit the reservation first and charge afterwards.\n\n**4. Non-idempotent tasks with `acks_late`.** If a worker crashes after the side effect and before the ack, the task is redelivered and the side effect happens twice. Guard with a state compare-and-swap, a unique constraint or a provider idempotency key.\n\n**5. Tenant filters sprinkled per view.** `Project.objects.filter(tenant=request.tenant)` in 140 places means the 141st will be forgotten. Make the default manager scoped and fail closed, and test for cross-tenant access.\n\n**6. Using the default per-process cache for throttles or idempotency.** `locmem` isn't shared across Gunicorn workers. Use `RedisCache` (built in since Django 4.0) or a dedicated Redis client.\n\n**7. Passing model instances to Celery.** The serialised instance is stale by the time the task runs, and pickling is unsafe. Pass primary keys and re-fetch inside the task.\n\n**8. Designing without numbers.** \"We'll add caching\" doesn't answer anything. \"Redirects are 10k per second, a cache hit costs about 1 ms, so a single Redis primary absorbs it with headroom\" does.\n\n## 8. Production: Pitfalls, Debugging & Scaling\n\n**Lock pile-up during a flash sale.** *Symptom:* checkout p99 jumps from 80 ms to 9 s, and Gunicorn workers time out. *Root cause:* every request waits on the same product row lock, and some transactions also do slow work while holding it. *Fix:* move slow work out of the transaction, set `SET LOCAL lock_timeout = '2s'` so excess requests return 409 or 503 instead of hanging, and add the Redis inventory gate. *Debug:* query `pg_stat_activity` with `wait_event_type = 'Lock'` and `pg_locks`, and correlate with Sentry transaction traces.\n\n**Deadlocks between checkouts.** *Symptom:* `deadlock detected` errors in the PostgreSQL log, with the matching exceptions in Sentry. *Root cause:* rows locked in different orders, for example iterating over the cart in the order the client sent it. *Fix:* always lock with `.order_by(\"pk\")`, and retry a deadlocked transaction once.\n\n**Celery backlog hides a fan-out.** *Symptom:* password-reset emails arrive 40 minutes late. *Root cause:* transactional and bulk tasks share one queue, and a 500k fan-out filled it. *Fix:* separate queues and worker pools, `rate_limit` on bulk tasks, and alerts on queue depth and on the age of the oldest message rather than on task failures alone.\n\n**Tenant leak through the cache.** *Symptom:* a customer sees another workspace's dashboard numbers. *Root cause:* `cache_page` keyed only by URL, while tenants share paths like `/dashboard/`. *Fix:* include the host in the cache key, or use `vary_on_headers(\"Host\")`, or build keys only through a tenant-aware helper. Add the cross-tenant test suite to CI.\n\n**Rate limiter blocks everyone after a Redis failover.** *Symptom:* 429s across the board, or no limiting at all. *Root cause:* the client timeout is too long (every request waits on a dead Redis), or there's no explicit fail-open/fail-closed decision. *Fix:* a 50 ms socket timeout, a circuit breaker, a deliberate fail-open policy for the paid API, and fail-closed for authentication endpoints.\n\n**Idempotency rows grow forever.** Orders keep their key forever, which is fine. For generic idempotency tables, store `created_at` and purge after 24–72 hours, matching the retry window you document for clients.\n\n**Debugging kit for these designs.**\n\n```python\nfrom concurrent.futures import ThreadPoolExecutor\nfrom unittest import mock\n\nfrom django.contrib.auth.models import User\nfrom django.db import connection\nfrom django.test import TransactionTestCase\n\nfrom shop.models import Product\nfrom shop.services import OutOfStock, place_order\n\n\nclass OversellTest(TransactionTestCase):           # real commits: needed to test locking\n    @mock.patch(\"shop.services.charge_order\")      # on_commit really fires here; keep the broker out\n    def test_ten_buyers_one_unit(self, _charge):\n        product = Product.objects.create(sku=\"X\", name=\"X\", price=10, stock=1)\n        users = [User.objects.create(username=f\"u{i}\") for i in range(10)]\n\n        def buy(u):\n            try:\n                place_order(u, f\"key-{u.pk}\", [{\"product_id\": product.pk, \"quantity\": 1}])\n                return True\n            except OutOfStock:\n                return False\n            finally:\n                connection.close()                  # each thread owns its own connection\n\n        with ThreadPoolExecutor(max_workers=10) as pool:\n            results = list(pool.map(buy, users))\n        self.assertEqual(sum(results), 1)\n        product.refresh_from_db()\n        self.assertEqual(product.stock, 0)\n```\n\nRun concurrency tests with `TransactionTestCase`, because `TestCase` wraps everything in one transaction and can't show real lock behaviour. Run them against PostgreSQL, not SQLite. Use `captureOnCommitCallbacks(execute=True)` in `TestCase` to assert that tasks were enqueued only after a successful commit.\n\n**Scaling ladder (say it in this order).** Query fixes and indexes first. Then caching with explicit invalidation. Then connection pooling. Then read replicas with a router and read-your-writes pinning. Then moving work to Celery with queue isolation. Then partitioning the big tables. Then splitting hot domains into separate services or databases (checkout, notifications). Each step has a measurable trigger, such as p99 latency, CPU, connection count or replication lag. Name the trigger, not just the step.\n\n## 9. Interview Questions\n\n**Q: Why isn't \"check the stock, then decrement it\" safe in Django, and what are the correct options?**\nA: Two requests can both read `stock = 1` before either writes, and both sell. PostgreSQL's default READ COMMITTED isolation doesn't prevent this read-modify-write race. The options are an atomic conditional update, `filter(pk=..., stock__gte=q).update(stock=F(\"stock\") - q)` with a check on the affected row count, or `select_for_update()` inside `atomic()` so the second request waits and re-reads. A `CHECK (stock >= 0)` constraint is the last line of defence. For multi-product carts, lock or update in primary-key order to avoid deadlocks.\n\n**Q: How does an idempotency key prevent a double order when the client retries at the same moment?**\nA: Store the key in the order row under `UniqueConstraint(user, idempotency_key)`. The first request's INSERT holds an uncommitted unique-index entry. The concurrent duplicate's INSERT blocks on it, then raises `IntegrityError` after the first commits. Catch that error outside the atomic block, load the original order and return it with 200. Store a hash of the payload too, so that a key reused with a different body gets a 422 instead of someone else's result. Checking for the key with a SELECT first is only an optimisation, never the guarantee.\n\n**Q: Why use `transaction.on_commit` to enqueue Celery tasks?**\nA: A task enqueued inside the transaction can be picked up before the commit. The worker then can't see the row (`DoesNotExist`) or sees stale data. If the transaction rolls back, the task runs for something that never happened. `on_commit` defers the callback until the outermost commit and discards it on rollback. Its remaining gap is a crash between commit and publish, which loses the task. When that's unacceptable, use a transactional outbox.\n\n**Q: The payment provider charged the card, but the worker crashed before marking the order paid. What happens in your design? (Senior)**\nA: With `acks_late=True` the message wasn't acknowledged, so the broker redelivers it, and the task calls the provider again with the same idempotency key, `order-<id>`. The provider returns the original charge instead of charging again. The task then compare-and-swaps the status from `AWAITING_PAYMENT` to `PAID`. If the expiry sweeper had already expired the order, the swap affects zero rows and the task issues a refund. I'd also reconcile from provider webhooks, which are verified and deduplicated by event id, and run a nightly job that compares provider charges with orders. External side effects become safe through provider idempotency plus state-machine guards, never through hoping tasks run once.\n\n**Q: How would you handle a flash sale where 100k users try to buy 500 units of one product? (Senior)**\nA: The database row becomes a lock hotspot, so you protect it. Use a Redis gate: an atomic Lua `DECRBY` that refuses to go below zero, initialised to 500, and admit only requests that win a token. Losers get an immediate \"sold out\" without touching PostgreSQL. Winners proceed to the normal locked checkout, which stays the source of truth. A reservation that fails or expires returns its token. Add a virtual waiting room or queue in front if the traffic is extreme, keep the transaction tiny, set `lock_timeout`, and pre-scale workers. An alternative to the Redis gate is splitting the stock across N shard rows to spread the lock contention.\n\n**Q: How do you guarantee that one tenant can never read another tenant's data? (Senior)**\nA: Defence in depth. Middleware resolves the tenant from the host and sets a contextvar. A DRF permission verifies membership, because DRF authentication is lazy and middleware doesn't see JWT users. A default manager scopes every query and fails closed when no tenant is set. PostgreSQL row-level security, driven by `SET LOCAL app.tenant_id`, acts as a backstop that also covers raw SQL. Unique constraints and indexes are tenant-prefixed, cache keys include the tenant, and Celery tasks take `tenant_id` and enter the context explicitly. Finally, an automated cross-tenant test suite hits every endpoint as tenant A against tenant B's data and expects 404s.\n\n**Q: When would you choose schema-per-tenant or database-per-tenant over a shared schema?**\nA: A shared schema with a `tenant_id` column scales to many thousands of small tenants with one migration path, so it's the default. Schema-per-tenant, for example with django-tenants and `search_path`, gives stronger isolation and easy per-tenant backups, but migrations run once per schema and the PostgreSQL catalog bloats past a few thousand schemas. Database-per-tenant is for a few large or regulated customers: a router maps the tenant to a DB alias, at the cost of N connection pools, backups and migration runs. A hybrid is common, with a shared schema for most tenants and dedicated databases for the enterprise tier.\n\n**Q: How do you fan a notification out to 500,000 followers without hurting the rest of the system?**\nA: Never do it in the request. The request writes an outbox event in its transaction. A relay enqueues a fan-out task that streams follower ids with `iterator()` and cuts them into batches of about 1,000, one task per batch. Each batch task bulk-creates notification rows with `ignore_conflicts` on a unique `(event, recipient)` constraint, and creates delivery rows for the channels the users haven't opted out of. Delivery runs on dedicated queues with rate limits, so transactional email is never stuck behind bulk work. For truly huge audiences, switch to fan-out on read, merging announcements into the inbox query.\n\n**Q: How do you make email delivery effectively exactly-once?**\nA: You can't get exactly-once delivery, but you can get an exactly-once effect. A `Delivery` row with `UNIQUE(notification, channel)` ensures one logical send per channel. The task claims the row with a conditional update from `PENDING` to `SENDING`, so concurrent duplicates exit early. The provider call carries `delivery-<id>` as its idempotency key, so a retry after a crash doesn't send twice. A sweeper resets rows stuck in `SENDING`. Status transitions are compare-and-swap updates, so retries can't move a `SENT` row backwards.\n\n**Q: Why are DRF's built-in throttles insufficient for a paid public API, and what do you build instead?**\nA: They store request histories in the Django cache with a non-atomic read-modify-write. With `locmem` each process counts separately, and even with Redis, concurrent requests can both pass. For contractual limits, implement a custom `BaseThrottle` that calls one atomic Redis Lua script: a token bucket keyed by API key, using Redis `TIME` so skew between pods doesn't matter. Return `wait()` so DRF sends `429` with `Retry-After`, and add remaining-quota headers in `finalize_response`. Decide explicitly whether to fail open or fail closed when Redis is down.\n\n**Q: In a URL shortener, should redirects be 301 or 302, and how do you count clicks at 10k per second?**\nA: Use 302 (or 307). A 301 is cached permanently by browsers, so repeat clicks skip your server and you lose analytics and the ability to change or expire the link. Serve the redirect from cache with no database write. Count with Redis `INCR` and append raw events to a stream. A periodic task drains the counters atomically with `GETDEL` and applies `update(clicks=F(\"clicks\") + n)`, and a consumer batches the events into a partitioned table or a column store for charts.\n\n**Q: How would you evolve the checkout from a monolith into services without losing consistency? (Senior)**\nA: First split the code into modules with service functions (`place_order`, `cancel_order`) and an outbox, still within one database. When inventory and payments move to separate services, there's no shared transaction any more. Use a saga: the order is created as `PENDING`, an `InventoryReserved` event leads to a payment command, and failures trigger compensating actions such as releasing the stock or refunding. Each step is idempotent and keyed by order id. Events flow through the outbox and Kafka or RabbitMQ, with consumers deduplicating through an inbox table. The trade is eventual consistency and more moving parts in exchange for independent deploys and scaling, so I'd only do it when team or scale pressure justifies it.\n\n**Q: How do you test that your concurrency design actually works?**\nA: Use `TransactionTestCase` against PostgreSQL, because `TestCase`'s single wrapping transaction hides real lock behaviour. Spawn threads that each call the service and close their own DB connection, then assert the invariant: exactly N successful orders and stock at zero. Test idempotency by firing the same key from two threads and asserting one order. Use `captureOnCommitCallbacks` to assert that tasks are enqueued only on commit. In staging, run load tests with production-like contention and watch `pg_locks` and deadlock counts.\n\n## 10. Quick Revision & Cheat Sheet\n\n| Round | Invariant | Key Django mechanism | Async piece | First scaling move |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| Checkout | stock ≥ 0, one charge per order | `select_for_update` in pk order, `UNIQUE(user, idem_key)`, `on_commit` | `charge_order` (provider key + status CAS), expiry sweeper | short transactions, Redis inventory gate |\n| Multi-tenant | no cross-tenant reads or writes | contextvar + scoped manager (fail closed), membership permission, RLS | tasks take `tenant_id`, enter context | per-tenant throttles, replicas, route big tenants to own DB |\n| Notifications | each notification once per channel | outbox in the domain transaction, unique `(event, recipient)` and `(notification, channel)` | relay → fan-out batches → per-channel deliver | queue isolation, partitioning, fan-out on read |\n| Public API | quota honoured across all pods | custom authentication (prefix + HMAC), `BaseThrottle` + Redis Lua | usage flush (absolute totals upsert) | Redis Cluster, gateway limits |\n| Shortener | codes unique and unguessable | random base62 + retry on `IntegrityError` in savepoint | click counter drain (`GETDEL` + `F()`) | cache-only redirect path, CDN |\n\n- State the invariants first, then point at the line that enforces each one.\n- The database enforces uniqueness and ranges. Application checks are optimisations.\n- Locks: short, in a consistent order, never across network I/O.\n- Every task must be idempotent. Use `acks_late` for at-least-once delivery, and pass ids, not instances.\n- Use `on_commit` for convenience and an outbox when delivery must be guaranteed.\n- Scope tenants in one place, fail closed, and test for leaks automatically.\n- Throttles that customers pay for need Redis plus Lua, not `locmem`, and an explicit failure policy.\n- Quote numbers: requests per second, rows per day, lock duration, queue depth.\n\n## 11. Hands-On Exercises\n\nSet up: `python -m venv .venv && source .venv/bin/activate && pip install \"django~=5.2\" djangorestframework \"psycopg[binary]\" \"celery[redis]\" redis`, then run PostgreSQL and Redis locally (Docker is easiest).\n\n1. **Prove the oversell.** Implement a naive `place_order` that does `p.stock -= q; p.save()`, run the 10-thread test from §8, and watch it oversell. Switch to the §3 version and make the test pass. Then reverse the lock order for two-product carts and reproduce a deadlock.\n2. **Idempotency under fire.** Send the same `Idempotency-Key` from 20 threads, assert exactly one order exists, and confirm that a different payload with the same key gets a 422.\n3. **Break and fix tenancy.** Build `TenantScoped` and `Project`, write the cross-tenant test that hits list, detail, update and delete, then deliberately add an unscoped `objects = models.Manager()` and watch the test catch it. Finish by adding the RLS migration and showing that a raw `cursor.execute(\"SELECT * FROM projects_project\")` is still filtered.\n4. **Fan-out with replay.** Implement `relay_outbox`, `fan_out` and `materialise`, run `fan_out` twice for the same event, and assert the notification and delivery counts don't change.\n5. **Limiter accuracy.** Run the token-bucket throttle behind 4 Gunicorn workers, fire 500 requests concurrently with a key on the Free plan, and count the 200s. Compare with `UserRateThrottle` on `locmem`.\n6. **Shortener hot path.** Build `redirect_view` with the negative cache, load-test it with `wrk` or `locust`, confirm zero DB queries on cache hits using `CaptureQueriesContext`, and implement the `GETDEL` drain task.\n\n**Mini project, \"Shop in a weekend\":** combine Rounds 1 and 4 into one repository. Build a DRF checkout API protected by API keys with plan limits, Celery payment and expiry tasks against a fake provider that randomly times out, an outbox-driven receipt email, Sentry for errors, and a CI job that runs the concurrency tests against PostgreSQL.\n\n## 12. Related Topics & Free Learning Resources\n\n**Sibling chapters**\n- [Ch 14 · Transactions, Locking & Race Conditions](topic.html?p=14-transactions-concurrency): `atomic`, savepoints, `select_for_update`, `on_commit`.\n- [Ch 15 · Custom Managers, QuerySets & Model Inheritance](topic.html?p=15-managers-model-inheritance): the scoped-manager pattern.\n- [Ch 16 · Indexes, Constraints, Raw SQL & PostgreSQL Features](topic.html?p=16-database-features-postgres): partial indexes, unique and check constraints, `RunSQL`.\n- [Ch 18 · Middleware](topic.html?p=18-middleware): tenant middleware ordering.\n- [Ch 21 · DRF Serializers](topic.html?p=21-drf-serializers) and [Ch 22 · DRF Views, ViewSets & Routers](topic.html?p=22-drf-views-viewsets-routers): the API layer.\n- [Ch 23 · DRF Authentication, Permissions & Throttling](topic.html?p=23-drf-auth-permissions-throttling): custom authentication and throttles.\n- [Ch 26 · Permissions, Groups & Authorization](topic.html?p=26-permissions-authorization): object-level and role checks.\n- [Ch 29 · Caching in Django](topic.html?p=29-caching), [Ch 31 · Background Tasks with Celery](topic.html?p=31-celery-background-tasks), [Ch 32 · Django Channels & Real-Time](topic.html?p=32-channels-websockets).\n- [Ch 33 · Testing Django Applications](topic.html?p=33-testing), [Ch 34 · Scaling Databases](topic.html?p=34-scaling-databases), [Ch 35 · Deployment & Production Operations](topic.html?p=35-deployment-production).\n- [Ch 38 · Most-Asked Django Interview Questions: DRF, Security & Scale](topic.html?p=38-interview-drf-advanced) and [Ch 39 · ORM Coding Round](topic.html?p=39-orm-coding-challenges): the rest of the interview loop.\n\n**Other handbooks**\n- [Messaging · Idempotency, Deduplication & the Outbox Pattern](../messaging/topic.html?p=21-idempotency-outbox): the outbox and inbox in depth.\n- [Redis Caching · Sorted Sets & Rate Limiting](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=19-sorted-sets-rate-limiting) and [Redis Caching · Pipelining, Lua & Transactions](../redis-caching/topic.html?p=22-pipelining-lua-transactions): limiter internals.\n- [REST API · Idempotency Keys & Retries](../rest-api/topic.html?p=27-idempotency-keys-retries) and [REST API · Rate Limiting & Throttling](../rest-api/topic.html?p=24-rate-limiting-throttling): the HTTP contract.\n- [Database Design · Multi-Tenant SaaS case study](../database-design/topic.html?p=40-case-multi-tenant-saas) and [Database Design · E-commerce case study](../database-design/topic.html?p=31-case-ecommerce): the data-layer view.\n- [System Design · URL Shortener](../system-design/topic.html?p=32-design-url-shortener), [System Design · Notification System](../system-design/topic.html?p=39-design-notification), [System Design · Rate Limiter](../system-design/topic.html?p=35-design-rate-limiter).\n\n**Free resources**\n- **Database transactions**, Django docs (*Intermediate*): `atomic`, savepoints and `on_commit` semantics, from the source. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/topics/db/transactions/\n- **QuerySet API: select_for_update**, Django docs (*Intermediate*): `nowait`, `skip_locked`, `of`, and backend caveats. https://docs.djangoproject.com/en/5.2/ref/models/querysets/\n- **Throttling**, Django REST framework (*Intermediate*): the `BaseThrottle` contract and the documented caveats of the built-in throttles. https://www.django-rest-framework.org/api-guide/throttling/\n- **Celery: Tasks**, Celery docs (*Intermediate*): `acks_late`, retries, and why tasks must be idempotent. https://docs.celeryq.dev/en/stable/userguide/tasks.html\n- **PostgreSQL: Row Security Policies**, PostgreSQL docs (*Advanced*): RLS, `FORCE ROW LEVEL SECURITY` and `BYPASSRLS`. https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/ddl-rowsecurity.html\n- **django-tenants**, GitHub (*Advanced*): schema-per-tenant on PostgreSQL, useful for comparing against the shared-schema design. https://github.com/django-tenants/django-tenants\n- **Stripe API: Idempotent requests**, Stripe docs (*Intermediate*): how a real payment provider implements idempotency keys. https://docs.stripe.com/api/idempotent_requests\n\n---\n\n*Django Handbook — chapter 40.*\n"};
